 certainly since Bush v. Gore, 22 years ago, arguably since ever? The six- three conservative majority court handed on huge decisions just within the last week of its term, limiting the authority of the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  to regulate carbon emissions, making it harder for states to keep guns off the streets by massively expanding the reach of the Second Amendment and overturning 50 years of precedent by ending the federal constitutional right for a woman to control her own reproductive health.\par \par \uc2 The court also agreed to hear a case that could give state legislatures new powers over federal elections. Potentially setting up exactly the kind of power grab that Trump and his cronies were trying to pull off in 2020.\par \par \uc2 This group of right-wing justices, five of them elevated to the Supreme Court by a president who came to office by losing the popular vote are now in a position to fundamentally change the country.\par \par \uc2 As Elie Mystal, the Justice Correspondent for The Nation Magazine put it yesterday: "The Supreme Court as an institution defines its own power. It`s now run by people who think the federal government was a mistake. And yet, people in the federal government are not willing to check this institution. What do you think is going to happen?"\par \par \uc2 [20:55:06]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And Elie Mystal, author of the bestselling book Allow Me to Retort: A Black Guy`s Guide to the Constitution joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Well, Elie, you take no pleasure in being right about what this court would be. But this was -- basically, this term was the nightmare scenario, the nightmare that all of us felt like it was possibly leading to, the nightmare that felt like a little bit put off, because they were being a little careful that first term, and there were these decisions that people thought, oh, well, maybe this was it. This was just about as bad as it could possibly be, right?\par \par \uc2 ELIE MYSTAL, JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT, THE NATION: Yes, I have this as the worst Supreme Court term since 1857. That`s the court that unleashed the Dred Scott decision which basically unleashed the Civil War, but I don`t think we have to go back to 165 years ago. I think we want to go back to 30 years ago, to really understand what`s happened.\par \par \uc2 30 years ago in 1992, Planned Parenthood V. Casey, Roe v. Wade is upheld albeit with new restrictions in a five to four vote. Those five votes in the majority were justices all appointed by Republican presidents.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the 1992 court was appointed by justices, eight of whom were appointed by Republican president. And yet, they upheld Roe. Why? It`s not like they liked abortion. It`s not like they liked states` rights, they had a confederate view of states` rights.\par \par \uc2 The difference is that conservatives 30 years ago believed in practicality. They said repeatedly in Casey that the practical realities of abortion of what the government can do and can`t do were such that they had to uphold the law just because of the facts on the ground, to defeat Roe, the modern conservatives.\par \par \uc2 What the Republicans realize is that they had to start appointing judges who did not care about the practicalities, who did not care about facts, who did not care about reality itself. And that is how they found justices who are now willing to overturn Roe and that decision finding justices who are willing to do that, that`s what explains everything else.\par \par \uc2 And once you have a justice that`s not willing to look at the practical realities of abortion, that`s how you also have a judge who wasn`t willing to look at the practical realities of climate change, who isn`t willing to look at the practical reality of gun violence.\par \par \uc2 What we have, it`s like a bad science fiction novel, Chris, it`s like, when -- you know, they release an invasive predator to defeat some other predator. We`re at the release the bat stage, right?\par \par \uc2 And the problem with those science fiction novels is that once the thing finishes eating, whatever you sent it to eat, it keeps feeding. And that`s all these conservatives are going to do. They`re going to keep feeding on the rights and dignity of others until somebody in the government, somebody in the other branches of government stops them.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, let`s talk about that. And that`s an apt metaphor. I think \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  himself is probably going to be on the table pretty soon for their -- for their delectation.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, you mentioned Dred Scott, we quoted from Lincoln`s first inaugural, which is a fascinating text, because Dred Scott, of course, which basically says that black people have no rights, that you can escape slavery and be whisked back. But not only that, but that just the Constitution was meant for white people, essentially.\par \par \uc2 That is a radicalizing moment. It helps to precipitate the Civil War, it helps to precipitate formation of the Republican Party, and one of the first things that Lincoln has to do is bring the reactionary court to heel, he expands the court.\par \par \uc2 The other big example of this, of course, is the Lochner era court, which in the new deal is striking down legislation over and over again. FDR famously tries to expand the court, there`s a huge fight over it, he loses that fight. But there`s some evidence to suggest the court was intimidated by it and basically reverses their jurisprudence.\par \par \uc2 What`s the lesson there about what checks a court that is sort of dead set on this kind of like reactionary marauding?\par \par \uc2 MYSTAL: The court has no money, it has no power to tax, and it has no army. The person who can stop the court is the executive of the United States, the President of the United States.\par \par \uc2 But right now, and I`m not -- I want to say that I`m angry at Biden right now. I am sad. I am sad that right now, because you know, who remembers 30 years ago, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ? \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  remembers the hokey past of bipartisanship, where he could reach out to conservatives who were concerned about practical governance. Those people don`t exist now but \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  never got the memo.\par \par \uc2 And so, I`m sad that in this crucial moment, we have a president who is unwilling, who is so ossified in his past thinking that he won`t stand with his people at this critical moment to take power back, not for himself, because he doesn`t matter, but to take power back for the American people from these unelected, unaccountable judges.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, and for constitutional governance, Elie Mystal, thank you, have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:01]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Before we go, quick programming note on Sunday, NBC presents "UKRAINE: ANSWERING THE CALL", an hour long special executive, produced by our own MSNBC`s Nicole Wallace. Special guests like Alicia Keys, Brad Paisley, Brandi Carlile and more.\par \par \uc2 It`s going to be raising funds for those affected by the ongoing war, so be sure to tune in this Sunday 7:00 p.m. on NBC, 10:00 p.m. on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 And finally, finally, before we go, we have a lot of people come and go on this show, turnover is natural, and I don`t do stuff on the show about it every time but a very, very special member of our team Tiffany Champion, who has been with us from the beginning is leaving, and it`s a huge blow.\par \par \uc2 And for the old timers, the ones who were here from the first day who spent the whole career before us. If you`ve liked this show through the years, Tiffany was a huge part of the reason that you did and we`re going to miss her a lot.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN for this week, "MSNBC PRIME" starts now with Ali Velshi. Good evening Ali and I`m sorry I`m throwing to you late.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220702ei7100001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for July 1, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Joe Fryer, Frank Figliuzzi, Allie Raffa, Tim Miller, Josh  Lederman, Joe Fryer, Megan Vespa, Sam Brock \par \uc2 8,794 \uc2 words\par \uc2 1 July 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOE FRYER, NBC NEWS ANCHOR: We start with the January 6 investigation and growing questions about the influence that Trump world is trying to exert behind the scenes, reported efforts to block the committee from getting to the truth. That includes new evidence that Cassidy Hutchinson herself was reportedly the target of messages seemingly designed to influence her testimony.\par \par \uc2 It`s according to CNN based on at least three sources. NBC News has not confirmed that reporting. And it`s not clear who in Trump World actually sent the messages. At least one member of the committee Congressman Pete Aguilar has said that`s evidence of witness tampering, and that the Justice Department should act on it. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  reportedly told ABC the committee is considering a criminal referral to the DOJ to do just that.\par \par \uc2 But none of this may be news to people in Trump`s orbit. According to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , "Evidence across multiple state federal and congressional investigations points to a similar pattern. Trump and his close allies privately shower potential witnesses with flattery and attention, extending vague assurances that staying loyal to Trump would be better than crossing him."\par \par \uc2 It`s a sentiment echoed by Trump`s former attorney \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 . He says the messages revealed during Tuesday`s hearing are identical to the ones he received before he flipped on his former boss.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL COHEN\uc2 , FORMER TRUMP ATTORNEY: It is a mob-style scenario where you want to make sure that these folks stay in your orbit. And if in fact that you step out of the orbit, understand that there are people there that are going to potentially do some pretty bad things to you. You put yourself into that -- into that spot, and a lot of the actions that came with me, I`m sorry to say, I`m sure she`s going to experience the same and he wants it that way.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: I want to bring in NBC`S Allie Raffa on Capitol Hill. Frank Figliuzzi, served as assistant director for counterintelligence at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . He`s also an MSNBC national security analyst. And Tim Miller is an MSNBC political analyst and writer-at-large for The Bulwark. He`s also the author of the brand new book, Why We Did It: A Travelogue from the Republican Road to Hell. Good to have all of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for delaying the start of your holiday weekend to chat with us. Frank, I want to start with you. And I want to remind people of some of the actual messages that Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  read at the end of that hearing Tuesday that she says were relayed the witnesses. Aong other things, it says, as long as you stay on the team and do the right thing, you`ll stay in good graces in Trump world.\par \par \uc2 Another quote here, he wants me to let you know he`s thinking about you. He -- you know -- he knows you`re loyal and you`re going to do the right thing. Now, we`re not sure who he is in that second message. But bottom line, Frank, what do you take away from this? If true, could it be witness tampering or no?\par \par \uc2 FRANK FIGLIUZZI, MSNBC NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Oh, there`s little doubt here. This is law school first year 101 Criminal Procedure. At the very least, DOJ now has enough to open a witness tampering investigation. We can talk about whether they would win or not, who did it or not, reasonable doubt or not. But there is no question in my mind that what you just read is an attempt to intimidate a witness. No question about it.\par \par \uc2 When you then add that to the fact that it appears that they provided her initial attorney to her Cassidy Hutchinson. You now have a -- without a doubt predication to open a federal witness tampering investigation.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Allie, it is Friday, and we are still digesting Tuesday`s testimony, but we know there`s more to come. So what do we know about where the January 6 investigation stands and what is next?\par \par \uc2 ALLIE RAFFA, NBC NEWS CAPITOL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Joe, we know this suspicions that this message could have come from Cassidy Hutchinson`s former boss, former White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  certainly piqued the interest of the committee and speaking with him again, but we know he`s been willing to face possible criminal charges for not complying with their subpoenas that they`ve issued already.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s a dead end there. But we`re hearing from a spokesperson for Meadows in the last couple of hours who says in a new statement, "No one from Meadows` camp, himself or otherwise has ever attempted to intimidate or shape Miss Hutchinson`s testimony to the committee." So, during this next week of recess, we know committee members are talking about this.\par \par \uc2 They`re talking about whether these messages have coerced or influenced any of the witnesses that we`ve heard, speak with them both privately or publicly. Remember, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  mentioned two witnesses that received messages like this or whether possibly witnesses who they`re still trying to speak with. Whether they`ve been coerced or influenced in any way by these messages.\par \par \uc2 And so, while we`re waiting for these next couple of hearings to begin the second week of July, we know that they`re still trying to get \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , the former White House counsel to testify anything on the record really after subpoenaing him this. We also know that there`s a tug of war going on now between Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` wife Ginni Thomas and her lawyer as well as Congressman Mo Brooks.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:09]\par \par \uc2 He`s one of the House GOP congressmen accused of requesting a presidential pardon from President Trump after January 6. Still several people the committee wants to speak with. So it may be a quiet recess here on the Hill but certainly not for committee members working remotely from back home in their districts, Joe.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Tim, you just wrote a whole book that dives into this very idea of why people embrace the former president, Why does this tactic, this idea of staying in Trump`s good graces still -- a year and a half after he left office, still carry so much weight?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: So two things, Joe. One of them is there is just this compulsion among political operatives, the people, you know, in politics in Washington who want to continue to be in the mix, want to continue to be in the club, do want to continue to get invited back down to Mar-a-Lago and this might that might seem cheap, might seem silly, but it`s a reality.\par \par \uc2 And when you do something, you show the bravery that Cassidy Hutchison did to step forward this week. And I`m just so impressed that she was able to kind of break free from these complexes that I read about in the book. You`re immediately shown that, you can already see if she`s getting physical threats, threats of sexual violence. She`s not going to be invited to any, you know, parties, from her old colleagues and friends.\par \par \uc2 I think that her career, she -- in my view, she`s going to be fine, but I`m sure she has some doubts. She doesn`t know what her next step is going to be now. There`s an uncertainty. So, you know, it is because of that, because of what she`s experiencing now that all these cowards are not acting, you know, they want to be in the mix. They want to feel secure in their career, you know, they want to have another chance to get back in there if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  runs again, which is very possible. And so, all of these things are preventing them from showing the courage that Cassidy did.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Our little pre holiday party is growing here. I also want to bring in Asha Rangappa. She is a former \uc2 FBI\uc2  special agent, a lawyer and senior lecturer at \uc2 Yale University\uc2 . Asha, as we`ve been saying, there is a pattern of this kind of behavior across multiple investigations that involve Trump and his allies. As \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2  has also pointed out, they`re never cut and dry, where Trump says, say this, or you`re in trouble. It`s always implied. How much harder does it make it to charge anyone as a crime?\par \par \uc2 ASHA RANGAPPA, FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Well, Trump in particular, I think is a master at this. And I worry that with him, you know, he famously doesn`t use e-mail. I don`t think he texts. So he -- there`s never a paper trail. So, we are incredibly reliant on people talking about their conversations with him. And, you know, revealing what he said. And as you noted, the structure of complicity around him is incredibly strong.\par \par \uc2 He wants loyalty, we saw this going back as far as Comey and the loyalty oath. Or, as, you know, your previous guests just noted, or you get kicked out of the club, and you pay a tremendous price for it. So, it is pretty important because he -- there are not as many other means of getting to what he has done besides his own words.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Frank, we learned this week that Trump`s political organization or his allies are paying for lawyers for some of the January 6 Committee witnesses. According to \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  that includes Stefan Passantino who was getting paid by Trump groups while representing Cassidy Hutchinson. The Post reports that he was telling Hutchinson not to testify publicly. She then fired him and hired the lawyer that she has now.\par \par \uc2 What should the public make of this? Is it unusual? And does the law say anything about this?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: So it`s not unusual to have a third party pay for your legal services. This happens every day across the country. Family members help out someone who`s in trouble. Even at the major Fortune 100 corporate level of companies will pay for employees to have an attorney because the attorney is under scrutiny for something they did with regard to their employment. But here`s the mob analogy continued on here.\par \par \uc2 But a lawyer knows where the paychecks coming from and even on a subconscious level, that knowledge of who`s paying could impact how they represent your individual interests. Even at the corporate level, when a company comes to you and says, hey, we`re going to help out you know, we have an attorney here for you because we`re all under investigation. They have to also tell you they give you a letter that says, understand, this attorney is our attorney.\par \par \uc2 It is not your attorney. We`re helping you out but he really needs to represent our interests, just want you to know. The same thing happens with the mob. They provide someone who`s jammed up a mob attorney, but the allegiance is generally to the capo, not to the client.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Obviously, there are ongoing investigations on many levels. Asha, can you give us any insight on why you think when people ask, you know, why hasn`t the Department of Justice acted to bring more charges against people who were in charge on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:09]\par \par \uc2 Why do we think that is?\par \par \uc2 RANGAPPA: I think it`s because there`s a misalignment between the evidence that we`re hearing in public, which is coming out of a congressional committee and the fact that DOJ is approaching this from a completely different avenue. They are moving from the bottom up. They have, you know, they`re prosecuting the foot soldiers moving up to the Proud Boys and out - - Oath Keepers which they`ve done and we see them now zeroing in on, you know, Eastman and Jeffrey Clark.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s just going to take more time. So, I think that DOJ is just going to be slower than Congress which doesn`t have to deal with many of the admissibility, witness credibility, all of these kinds of evidentiary concerns in impeaching a witness, adversarial cross examination in the same way that prosecutors do.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: And so, when we talk about the impact of all of this, I mean, Tim, CNBC interviewed a number of Republican mega donors, that consensus appears to be that they`re moving away from Trump looking at backing other Republican candidates (INAUDIBLE) buy that and how much do you think that matters right now them looking for others?\par \par \uc2 FIGLIUZZI: I do buy it. And you know why it sounds very familiar. These same Republican mega donors were funding one of my groups back in 2016 when we were running Republicans against Trump effort. An effort to try to stop Trump. And what happened when Trump ended up winning the nomination, all of those mega donors went on to support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  which I found rather odd story that I wrote about in the book.\par \par \uc2 So look, these guys want access, they want power, they see that Trump is erratic, they see his legal problems. Unlike a lot of the voters in the Republican side, they know we actually lost. They don`t believe the big lie. And so, it`s not surprising that they would rather move on to \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  or \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . The question is when the rubber hits the road, if Trump decides to run again, are they really going to throw money in against him and risk his ire?\par \par \uc2 Would they really oppose him in a general election? I`m deeply skeptical of that. So, I do think this is something I`m encouraged that I hope, you know, if Trump is one that somebody primaries that, I think he`s uniquely dangerous. But I don`t think that there should be any awards for courage going around for these guys at this point.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Great conversation to kick us off this hour. Allie Raffa, Frank Figliuzzi and Asha Rangappa, thank you all. Tim, you`re sticking with us for another important conversation coming up a little later this hour.\par \par \uc2 Right now we are keeping a close eye on the White House. President Biden is about to meet virtually with a group of governors. The topic, what states can do and are doing after the Supreme Court abortion rule. And we`ll go there live once it starts.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the legislation that critics called the Don`t Say Gay law takes effect today. The notifications about LGBTQ children one school district is already required. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:17:08]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Right now President Biden is meeting with governors to talk about the future of abortion. He just started speaking moments ago. Let`s listen in.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: A terrible extreme decision in my view, upending lives and impacting on the health and safety of millions of women. And I share the public outrage at this. Extremist court is committed to moving America backwards with fewer rights, less autonomy and politicians invading the most personal decisions that not only women, but we`ll find if they expand on this decision, men as well.\par \par \uc2 But as I`ve said last week, this is not over. Last week. I announced to specific actions. First, if extremist governors try to block a woman from traveling from her state, that prohibits her from seeking medical help she needs to a state that provides that care. The federal government will act to protect her bedrock rights through the attorney general`s office. Second, if states try to block a woman from getting medication, the FADA has already approved and has been available for more than 20 years, my administration will act and protect that woman`s right to that medication.\par \par \uc2 There are many other unlawful actions in my view that states are preparing to take that will have -- will have to address as well. But ultimately, congress is going to have to act to codify the Roe into federal law. As I said yesterday, the filibuster should not stand in the way of us being able to do that. But right now we don`t have the votes in the Senate to change the filibuster on at the moment.\par \par \uc2 That means we need two more votes now, not now -- when we vote probably after November, more senators and House majority and the House majority- elected November to get this bill to my desk. So, the choice is clear, we either elect federal senators and representatives who will codify Roe or Republicans who will elect the House and Senate will try to ban abortions nationwide, nationwide.\par \par \uc2 This is going to go one way or the other after November. So let`s remember, the reasoning of this decision has an impact much beyond Roe and to the right to privacy more generally. Justice Thomas himself said that. Under the reasoning of this decision that the court should reconsider marriage equality and contraception. And there`s a lot of steak here. In the meantime, I want to hear what the governors are doing.\par \par \uc2 Talk about my plans and discuss what we can do until Congress acts. This is not over. It`s not over. I`m going to turn it over to Jen Klein, the head of the White House gender policy council and we`ll proceed with the discussions.\par \par \uc2 JEN KLEIN, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR AND CO-CHAIR OF THE WHITE HOUSE GENDER POLICY COUNCIL: Thank you, Mr. President. And I`d like to add my thanks to each of you for joining and for your leadership.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:03]\par \par \uc2 We`d -- as the president just said really like to hear from you about both what you`ve done and further action that you`re planning to take. So we`re going to start with you, Governor Hochul from New York. Over to you.\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Thank you, Jen. And thank you, Mr. President for convening us with the sense of urgency that I feel that you are conveying because this is a frightening time for women all across our nation. A lot of fear and anxiety out there. And I hear it from women all across the state or they come up to me at Subway stops in New York City at farmers markets. Literally young women who never even had to think about this rioter coming up and putting their arms around my neck and sobbing.\par \par \uc2 So, there is such stress out there. So, we in the state of New York, this came the day after the Supreme Court also told us that we did not have the right to protect our citizens from people carrying concealed weapons in places like Subways and Times Square, et cetera. So, what I had to do was to convene an extraordinary session because they weren`t due back until January. I brought them back and literally at 2:00 a.m. this morning while we`re still working on the gun legislation, I changed my proclamation to say, we`ll also include to give further protections for women in the state, what we call the Equality Agenda.\par \par \uc2 This will prohibit discrimination on the basis of pregnancy and pregnancy outcomes and reproductive health care and autonomy. So, we`re one-step closer as of this afternoon. I think it`s going to be passed by both houses to enshrine abortion rights in our state constitution. So, as long as I`m governor, these rights will not be rolled up back. But we also are prepared to serve as a destination for women who`ll be looking to a place like New York and other states and my colleagues on the call as a safe harbor.\par \par \uc2 So, even before the Supreme Court decision, I knew I had to beef up our opportunities for women to come from other states. We allocated $35 million right off the bat to go to providers so they could hire more people, expand their space and make sure that they can keep their staff safe from violence as well. And we also decided to mandate all insurance companies doing business in the state of New York now have to cover abortion.\par \par \uc2 And I signed six bills related to protecting our providers after the decision came down, we will stop the extradition of any search for one of our providers or a woman who`s in our state. We`re wanted under criminal charges, that`s not happening in New York. And we`re also defending our abortion providers from malpractice and other lawsuits. We also launched a public awareness campaign because there`s a lot of misinformation.\par \par \uc2 Women in New York are seeing the national news, they don`t know whether their rights are protected here in New York. So we`re going to continue to be leaders in this fight, protect our providers, protect them from the vigilante justice which has been unleashed by the states. They`re going to allow these private rights of action where they`re going to hunt down women and providers. This is, you know, chaos. It`s frightening.\par \par \uc2 But also we`re doing what we can to make sure that, you know, we are protected. What`s happening now, the rights of millions of women across this country are now falling on the shoulders of just a handful of states. Just a handful of states are now going to have to take care of the health care of women from other states. So, we believe as you do, Mr. President that what`s available to New Yorkers and the other enlightened states should be available to all Americans.\par \par \uc2 And no one should have to travel and that`s why as you agree with us, Congress has to act. And we sent a letter way back to Congress saying, please pass the Women`s Health Protection Act, let`s get it through the Senate. So what we`re doing at the state level is key, but I completely agree with you and I commend you, Mr. President for standing up and talking about the fact that it is the filibuster that`s preventing the majority of senators and it is the majority of senators who want to speak on behalf of the majority of Americans.\par \par \uc2 But we understand our options are limited until next January with a new Senate. And we`re all going to be focusing very hard on that outcome because it is a matter of life and death for American women because we do not want to go back to the days anywhere in this country of the back-alley abortions which are real. I talked to one of my neighbors down the hall whose husband used to perform these abortions before abortion was legal in New York State in 1970.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is not just hyperbole. It is real. So, what we`re asking to help continue at the federal level, we believe that more can be done to fund family planning services more broadly to allow the providers and their clinics to have state -- federal resources for these services and they can focus on private dollars for abortion services. And I`m really grateful that you`re putting an emphasis on what`s going to happen for women to be able to continue to receive abortion services by mail.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be a battle that when that people would not have foreseen we want to make sure that there`s no unlawful interference and we have to do that to ease public concerns.\par \par \uc2 [13:25::08]\par \par \uc2 And also, Mr. President, we`d ask that you consider your ability to use federal facilities. What am I talking about? Veterans hospitals, military bases, and other places where the federal government controls the jurisdiction in some of the states that are hostile to women`s rights and make sure that those services can be available to other women. So, those are just a few of the ideas that we encourage you to look at.\par \par \uc2 An idea of what we`re doing at the New York state level. But literally before our close of business today, we will have the first step toward the process of changing our constitution which is the boldest step we can take. And we just started that today. So, thank you for convening us. We are there to stand with you to do something that protect the rights that my mother`s generation had to fight for.\par \par \uc2 I fought for, my daughter who`s in her thirties had a fight for and now Mr. President, I have a brand new -- brand new granddaughter. And thank you for your note acknowledging that. I didn`t think we`d have to fight this battle for her generation as well. But apparently we do. But we are ready to take on that fight. So, thank you.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, I didn`t think we would either. I was worried we would. But, you know, I think that a lot of the folks don`t understand what this decision means. It means, if you got a 11-year-old child who`s a victim of incest and finds herself pregnant, which occurs that, you know, she can`t get choices. She can`t -- her health can be protected. You find, you know, if you`re raped, there`s no exception.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there`s so many things that people really haven`t focused on yet beyond the fundamental right of a woman to be able to choose. And so, I just think that as you pointed out, Kathy, we`re going to -- it`s just going to be a big deal that we get people out to vote because here`s what`s going to happen I predict. If we don`t, if we don`t take this -- keep the Senate increase it in the house, we`re going to be in a situation where the Republicans are going to pass a nationwide prohibition consistent with what the Supreme Court ruled. And so, there`s a lot at stake here. But I`m sure glad you`re leaving New York for real.\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Thank you, Mr. President.\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Thank you, Governor Hochul. I`m now going to turn to Governor Cooper.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: You`re watching as President Biden right now with a group of Democratic governors talking about abortion rights and reproductive rights at the state level. I want to bring in NBC`s Josh Lederman who is at the White House. I also want to bring in Robert Gibbs, former White House press secretary in the Obama White House and an MSNBC political analyst. And Lauren Leader, CEO of All in Together. All to continue this conversation.\par \par \uc2 So Josh, we heard the president there talk a little bit about what the federal government can do but really acknowledging this is so much a state issue right now. So, what are the actual steps the White House can take in working with these governors?\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Really the biggest action that the federal government can take are the ones that the White House announced within hours of that decision, Joe, to try to preserve the ability for women to cross state lines, to seek abortions and other states and to work with the health agencies to make sure that there is wide access to the abortion medication.\par \par \uc2 But the White House sees the next battleground on this issue as the states and that is the whole purpose of what President Biden is doing today, allowing the press to watch some of this meeting he`s doing with Democratic governors in states that are trying to protect the ability of women to get abortions. Of course, the bigger problem is the states that are not doing that. And that, according to the White House is where they need to see more action.\par \par \uc2 They want voters to go out and make clear to their state and local leaders that they want to see these abortion rights codified into law. But the president also in his remarks here, I think gave the most frank outline of what the plan is going forward as the White House continues to say this is a job that Congress needs to take up because we know that the president came out just at the end of his foreign trip in support of a filibuster exception for codifying Roe versus Wade into law.\par \par \uc2 The president acknowledging today that Democrats don`t have the votes to do that, because of Democratic Senators Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  who are standing firm in their opposition to a filibuster carve out even for these abortion and privacy issues that the president is referencing. But President Biden said here, OK, so what we need to do is elect two more Democratic senators, have two Democratic pickups in the Senate in the midterm elections, which if that were to happen would bring Democrats to 52 Democratic senators.\par \par \uc2 Which would mean they could still lose Manchin and Sinema and ostensibly carve out this filibuster exception and pass something. So essentially, the president putting Democrats on notice that he would be willing to go around the objections of Senators Sinema and Manchin if it means that they can get some type of federal legislation on Roe versus Wade. Of course that is a big if because it would mean Democrats would have to pick up two seats in midterm elections where -- which many Democrats are bracing for Democrats to have a really difficult time.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:09]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Yes, and the president also noting there they also have to keep the House majority there as well. I mean, Robert, this is a generational earthquake in terms of U.S. policy. Do you think right now the White House is matching the seismic stakes of this moment?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, I think it`s a great question and I think today`s event is going to have to go a long way toward demonstrating the commitment that this White House has. A lot of women around this country, and a lot of Democrats, want to know that this White House is ready to meet the moment that the political establishment in Washington is ready to meet this scary moment.\par \par \uc2 So I think it`s important that the president is out there, that he`s reemphasizing the steps that they have announced around travel and medication that was just talk about. But I think what Democrats really need to do is feel two things. One, that there`s a passion in this White House for making this happen. And secondly, that this idea of electing two more Democrats is not just a talking point for right now but becomes a rallying cry to a larger campaign message in the fall.\par \par \uc2 This isn`t something that the president is going to have to do once on a Friday. This is something that the entire Democratic establishment is going to have to go and talk about repeatedly and loudly if this is going to really bleed in.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: And the question is, is this a big enough issue to get voters to the polls.\par \par \uc2 Lauren, your organization is out this morning with some interesting polling showing how women view voting after Roe versus Wade was overturned. You found a 6 percent jump among all women now compared to September. And when you look at all voters, not just women, it`s just a percentage point jump. So that`s the difference there. And then if you break it down by party, you saw a 14 percentage point jump among Democratic women but it`s only moving the dial a few percentage points among independents.\par \par \uc2 So do you think that`s enough to narrow the margins for Democrats in November especially when you consider the major headwinds they were already facing? And what more are you hearing from female voters about this?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN LEADER, ALL IN TOGETHER CEO: Right, and the data point that you didn`t include which I think is the most dramatic is that among young women ages 18 to 29, it was a 26 point jump from September in terms of their commitment and their motivation to vote in September. And excuse me, in November. And that is just incredibly powerful. We`ve been seeing depressed engagement going into the midterm elections from Democrats and that has been particularly true of younger voters.\par \par \uc2 And yet the Roe issue really seems to be changing that. It is a galvanizing issue. And for Democrats to win in the midterms across all of these races it requires their critical coalition of women, especially younger women, suburban women, independent women, those are the voters that have turned -- that turned the majority in 2018, and then the White House in 2020. If they don`t turn out, if they`re not galvanized, it`s incredibly difficult for Democrats to overcome what has been a Republican enthusiasm advantage.\par \par \uc2 So we actually see this as a significant change. And the Dobbs decision is a mobilizer and has the potential to really create tectonic shifts in the landscape of the midterm election.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: So, Robert, what do you think are the points that the Democrats should hammer home right now heading into the midterm elections and just what`s your reaction when you see those polling numbers? Does that tell you this is a big enough issue to motivate voters or no?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: I do think it`s a big enough issue. I think those numbers demonstrate it. I think focus groups that I`ve heard about over the past week demonstrate it. And look, I think we all think that a lot of these races are going to come down to being pretty close races. And so we`re not talking about needing, you know, 10 point gaps to win these races. A lot of these are going to be won in two-point races, and so even the types of changes that we`re beginning to see and feel in some of the polling are going to be important.\par \par \uc2 Look, I think in many ways sophisticated campaigns are going to take all of what has happened at the Supreme Court in the past, you know, 10 days and all of what has been happening in Washington and kind of put that together. If you think about it, in the last week, you know, Governor Hochul in New York said it. The idea that there is reasonable gun safety legislation in this country is going to be hard to prosecute now because of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 We know what they`ve done on Roe versus Wade. And despite the fact that 80 percent of the country wants to see more stringent application on powerplant emissions, the Supreme Court did away with that. So what you`re beginning to see in Washington from conservatives and Republicans is something that is extreme and out of step with the concerns and cares that people have in this country.\par \par \uc2 That larger narrative is going to have to be put together and prosecuted in this campaign. It`s not just going to be one issue here and one issue there. It`s going to be that amalgamation of issues into a larger narrative about extremism that I think can move the needle in November.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:10]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: And Lauren, quickly, I want to ask you about that idea. I mean, Republican Governor Glenn Youngkin of Virginia, that`s a state that has gone blue in recent presidential cycles, he said he would sign a 15 or 20- week abortion ban. I mean, do you think Republican governors might be overestimating this issue among voters right now?\par \par \uc2 LEADER: Right. And I think as we start to see during the summer, the real world effects of some of the most extreme legislation, I really think we`re going to see increasing shifts. When even pro-life voters have difficulty accessing IVF or basic birth control because of this ruling, I do think these many voters will see this as a bridge too far. Even those who maybe agree in principle and on moral grounds around abortion.\par \par \uc2 And that is really going to be the problem for Republicans. Robert is right. This extremism, the fact that these rulings are so out of step with what the majority of Americans want is highly galvanizing. It`s the existential threat that really has motivated Democrats to come out against Trump and against the Republican agenda in 2018 and I think could motivate them again in some pretty substantial ways in each of those individual races. If they see a threat on an existential level, the key rights and values, I think they turn out.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Josh Lederman, Robert Gibbs, Lauren Leader, thank you all for joining us on this Friday, we appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 You may have heard of some states suspending the gas tax to fight high prices. Well, Illinois has a new plan taking effect today that targets another set of staples hit by soaring inflation. The idea to save folks some money at the grocery store. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:36]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Right now nearly 50 million Americans are hitting the roads and the skies to kick off this Fourth of July weekend. For those folks who are flying, airlines are already warning of slashed routes and big delays today, much like we saw over the last two holiday weekends. On the roads, even though the national average has dropped slightly as of late, gas prices are still around historic highs.\par \par \uc2 And if you`re planning on keeping the party at home or hosting a barbecue is going to cost you with traditional food prices up 11 percent this year.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is NBC`s Maggie Vespa who`s at a grocery store in Chicago as residents there stock up on food for the holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 And Maggie, in an effort to help with inflation pain, the state of Illinois is actually suspending its 1 percent grocery tax ahead of the Fourth. That means the average customer will really only save a dollar on a $100 grocery bill this weekend. So you`re talking to shoppers there. Are they happy about the move or is it too small to really notice?\par \par \uc2 MAGGIE VESPA, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: You know, I mean, 1 percent is 1 percent, right, so a lot of them are noticing it, but we`ve gotten some mixed reviews. I mean, like you said, you`re talking about $100 bill, this is a $60 bill we went shopping for some sort of Fourth of July staples. Isn`t that lovely? And on a $60 bill, we saved about $.60 on our total. So knowing that kind of knowing how high also food prices are right now, we just asked shoppers here, does a 1 percent reduction really register on your radar? Here`s what they said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s a nice gesture but I don`t think it`s going to make that much of a difference. It didn`t seem to make that much of a difference in my bill today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I appreciated it. We appreciate all the help we can get.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: For right now, it`s going to work but I know eventually it`s going to go right back up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Yes, so like everybody said, they told us, you know, kind of getting any relief is really welcomed. They`re not going to turn it down obviously. But again, 1 percent is 1 percent. And you also mentioned gas by the way. The state of Illinois also waving their inflation adjustment hike on their gas tax. That also sounds like a great headline and relief if great, that it also boils down to about 2.2 cents per gallon. Obviously gas also hurting families in Illinois and across America. Joe?\par \par \uc2 FRYER: That is right. Maggie Vespa, getting ready to host a parking lot barbecue there in the Jewel Osco in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 Maggie, thanks so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 VESPA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: The trial for American basketball star, two-time Olympic gold medalist Brittney Griner has started in Moscow. The 31-year-old appeared in court in handcuffs this morning. This is a video of her leaving the courtroom after her appearance. Griner is charged with drug smuggling for allegedly trying to leave the country with cannabis oil vape canisters in her luggage. She faces up to 10 years in prison.\par \par \uc2 U.S. State Department\uc2  says she is being wrongfully detained by the Russian government. An official classification means the special presidential for envoy for hostage affairs is working to secure her release. The next hearing in her case is now set for July 7th.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, alerting parents if a student is, quote, "open about their gender identity. Flagging book that even mentions sexual orientation." These are some of the policies being rolled out as Florida`s so-called "Don`t Say Gay" law officially takes effect today. What this means for students and teachers in the state next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:47:54]\par \par \uc2 FRYER: The so-called "Don`t Say Gay" law signed by Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is now in effect as of today and it is already changing the way some teachers do their jobs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I absolutely feel that we are moving backward with regard to civil rights and social justice. It`s frightening to be a citizen in Florida.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Representatives of the Orange County Classroom Teachers Association say before today school officials have been verbally warning educators not to wear rainbow articles of clothing. Another school board in Leon County unanimously approved its LGBTQ inclusive school guide. One provision in that, alerting parents if a student who is open about their gender identity is in their child`s gym class or on an overnight school trip.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Sam Brock has more from Florida.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 SAM BROCK, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: One of the most polarizing pieces of legislation in Florida in recent memory takes effect today as many schools across the state right now are trying to figure out how does this affect them.\par \par \uc2 We do know this. The state of Florida says instruction will definitely be limited for kindergarten to third graders like at the elementary school behind me when it comes to topics related to sexual orientation and gender identity. But the state admits right now they have no blueprint for how to address this kind of material and content when it comes to older kids. They say they have about a year to figure that question out, but it`s one of the factors driving uncertainty and fear around this law which critics call "Don`t Say Gay."\par \par \uc2 Now in terms of a lawsuit that is proceeding right now against the state of Florida, Florida responded with a motion this week trying to dispel misconceptions it says about what the law does and does not do. It say what it does is restrict instruction on those subject matters. What it does not do, according to the state, is prevent teachers who are gay or transgender from posting pictures of their family, talking about their spouses or even their children or answering questions from students about their families.\par \par \uc2 All of that right now, though, does appear to be open to interpretation as we hear different stories from different schools. There has been uproar in the state of Florida as protests have broken out at schools all over the state in the months after Governor DeSantis signed this into law back in March.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 Where do we stand here? We know there`s been a high-profile feud with DeSantis and \uc2 Disney\uc2  with this \uc2 Disney\uc2  voicing its opposition to "Don`t Say Gay" and losing its special status in Florida. That legal battle continues. And I spoke with a teacher here at Pembrooke Pines Elementary in Broward County. She told me that she is gay and that she has lived for 34 years watching the evolution of civil rights that when she was a student here, people couldn`t talk about their sexual orientation.\par \par \uc2 But it feels like there`s been so much progress made and yet now civil rights, she says, is moving backwards. And it`s not just Florida, because there are six other states that have passed similar laws with 33 others that have introduced legislation within the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 In Pembroke Pines, Sam Brock, NBC News.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 FRYER: All right, thank you, Sam.\par \par \uc2 Back for me now with more reaction on all this is MSNBC political analyst Tim Miller. His new book is "Why We Did It: A Travelogue from the Republican Road to Hell."\par \par \uc2 So, Tim, you have an interesting perspective here. You`re part of the LGBTQ community. You`re married with a family. And you worked for one of Florida`s former Republican governors, Jeb Bush. And you`ve said, this is basically "Don`t Ask Don`t Tell" part two but it`s 25 years later. So help us understand from your perspective what are the politics at play here?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, the politics at play is that \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and the right-wing governors, the other ones that have put this into place, have seen an opportunity that it is not any different from anti-gay bigotry that goes all the way back to the Briggs Initiative in California in the `70s. It`s this notion that well, I`m OK with gay people, I just don`t want them to be exposed to kids.\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 ` spokesperson said, they might be groomers if they`re around kids. Parents are concerned in schools about kind of the changing language and norms and morals around gender identity. Some of those concerns I think of parents are in good faith. Others of them are based on bigotry. But what is happening here is that politicians are now taking advantage of those concerns about the changing, you know, kind of language and discussion and increasing inclusivity of families like mine, of people that have -- you know, are gender nonbinary, of different types of gender expression.\par \par \uc2 And they`re using those, you know, that change for a short-term political advantage. And these bills are going to be extremely harmful, particularly to kids in red districts because to that last report, here`s the key thing. It was written so vague, it`s such a poorly written bill, that it`s going to be interpreted different school district to school district. So if you have a hard line antigay principal or superintendent in a certain school or if you have a very loud parent-teacher association that`s very anti-gay at your school on the parent side of it, then the schools are going to be much more harsher -- much more harsh in how they interpret the law.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: And keeping in mind that one of the issues here is school districts are worried about being sued by parents who may have an issue with the LGBTQ community and it may not take much for those lawsuits to be filed and school districts may not want to spend a lot of money to depend them.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Tim, you talked about this. There`s been a lot of chatter about \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  running for president in 2024. So is what we`re seeing in Florida really the blueprint for this party`s future right now with laws like this?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Look, there`s \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  imitations everywhere. You could see Greg Abbott in Texas is jealous of \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  and he`s trying to do things like this. Yes, this gets you positive coverage on the \uc2 Ben Shapiro\uc2  show and the big right-wing conservative media shows. This is very popular there because it`s in this ethos of they`re taking down a peg the cultural left, taking down gays and lesbians.\par \par \uc2 And I think, just going back to this point on the lawsuits. This is very important because the lawsuit is also tied to the lawsuit bounties in Texas. That is how they`re enforcing these laws, through the courts. And so if you`re a lawyer representing a school district, you may not be anti-gay but you just might want to not have to deal with getting sued so you might be making advice to teachers, and saying you can`t do certain types of projects.\par \par \uc2 I have one example right here. This is what my daughter brought home from school for Valentine`s Day. It says "Papa Dad to Lose." They asked each kid to make a valentine. Is that instruction? That seems like instruction to me, if you`re telling a kid make a valentine about your family, and it`s a gay family or an LGBTQ family of any kind. That seems like that`s instruction. So if you have one teacher -- or excuse me one parent of another kid in that class who is uncomfortable with that, they could sue the teacher.\par \par \uc2 So you could see a lawyer reasonably, just because of the nature of the law, saying that you can`t do this in that classroom. And they`re not saying that because they`re bigoted. They`re saying that because we just need to be safe, we don`t want the school to get sued, we don`t want to lose money. And so that is all of just the, you know, the negative impacts of this on kids who come from nontraditional families.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: And those are some of the key questions that are really I guess going to play out over the next several weeks and months.\par \par \uc2 Tim Miller, your daughter is a very talented artist, so thank you for sharing that with us. We appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: Have a great holiday weekend.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: You too.\par \par \uc2 FRYER: There are more possible changes for our school. I want you to listen to these words. Involuntary relocation.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 What could a student think if they heard that? Well, the "Texas Tribune" reports that earlier this month a group of Texas educators proposed to the Texas State Board of Education that slavery should be taught as involuntary relocation during second grade social 