e the election results there. And Alex Holder who`s documentary series Unprecedented premieres this summer on Discovery Plus joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Alex, can you explain to me how the committee made contact with you or you made kind of the committee in terms of them learning about the existence of this footage which seemed like a fairly late-breaking revelation given that it`s existed for a long time?\par \par \uc2 ALEX HOLDER, DOCUMENTARY FILMMAKER: Sure. I mean, I probably have about 1000 different theories as to how they became aware of it. I mean, at the end of the day, we were very visible throughout the process. I mean, some people on the Trump side seemed to think that they didn`t know about the project. And we certainly did know about it -- I mean, I don`t know how they could have possibly not known.\par \par \uc2 At one point, my cameraman was on the stage with Ivanka and I think CNN actually called to say, who is this random guy on the stage during the campaign? He`s interrupting our shot. So, we were there. We were very visible. And obviously, on the January 6 as well, that terrible day, we were also filming throughout the -- that tragic events. So, I imagine there were lots of different ways they could have found out and they reached out and we`re cooperating with them as they requested.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Did they -- they did not subpoena you, they just -- they asked for cooperation?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: No, no, no. They did. The January 6 Commission did subpoena me, me and the material, and we`ve cooperated. Yes, absolutely.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: This is sort of random question. But 11 hours doesn`t sound like a lot actually, given how much footage goes into documentary filmmaking, particularly if it`s longer than, you know, than just an hour or two hours. Like is that -- was there a narrow ask from them on the -- on the footage they wanted from you?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Sure. So, we actually have about over 100 hours` worth of footage, which includes all the campaign rallies and the behind-the-scenes and some of the verities that we filmed throughout the campaign, as well as off the campaign as well, on the -- during the Stop the Steal, the sort of the Stop the Steal rallies that took place after the election had finished. So, they specifically asked for raw interviews, which is what we gave them.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And those raw interviews, as I understand it, are with people like the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself, \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , others like that?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Absolutely. Yes, exactly. It was -- the president, President Trump -- former President Trump, his three children, as well as the interview that we did with the vice president as well, and \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I suspect that you can`t tell me what`s in those interviews if I -- if I asked you or you`re not going to for a variety of reasons. But can you describe the extent of your cooperation with the committee? I understand that you sat and spoke to them as well, right?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Yes, I sat with him for two hours. We went through some of -- all their questions. I answered them very directly and honestly, and they were very professional. And if you want to see what`s in those -- in that -- in that material, you have to wait for the series to come out.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There has been reporting that in subsequent to this, as the news has broken that you have had security, that you`ve hired security, that there`s maybe some fear about your security situation. I know that when people end up in the sort of crosshairs of some people in the MAGA movement, that can end up being the case. What is your experience been like?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Well, apparently, I`m an undercover \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent with a funny accent, or I am an MI6 spy. But yes, I mean, there`s all sorts of conspiracy theories being bandied about by people online. And I think after the various opinions I`ve received, including the one recently from Georgia, from Fulton County, you know, I`d rather be sort of safe than sorry, which is an unfortunate situation but I think in the circumstances, it`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Were you shooting in Georgia? I know that subpoena happened. Do you have footage from that -- from that state? I know, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  was there. Obviously, Trump was there for that big rally. Did you guys shoot down there?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: We did, yes. For two rallies in fact, yes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And you`ve -- and you`ve provided them or cooperated with that subpoena as well?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Yes, we`re in the process of cooperating and providing them with the materials that they`ve asked for? Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Did you talk to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  at all during that period between the election and January 6?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: I think I met him for about 13 seconds on the steps going up to Air Force One. And then I saw him again on Air Force One when he told us that they had to reschedule the interview that we were going to do on the plane with the former president.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:10]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: And you were filming on the day of the sixth as well or no?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Yes. So, we were there at the rally in the Ellipse, and then also for the event -- well, for the -- for the riot that took place outside the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What was going through your mind as you were -- as you were watching that unfold in real-time and capturing it, documenting it?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: I mean, it was obviously horrific. I mean, it looked like a warzone. It was -- it was terrifying. My close friend, Michael, who was our DP for the project is there immersed in this insanity. But on the other hand, it was also wasn`t surprising. I mean, this was something that I actually had predicted the night before.\par \par \uc2 I mean, at the end of the day, when the rhetoric and the belligerence that has come out from the campaign all the way through past the election, and then you have these conspiracy theories and these lies about having actually won the election, when you didn`t win the election and you`re telling 75 million people that you won when in fact you didn`t, I mean, it was sort of an inevitable result which is terribly sad. But I sort of -- it was -- it wasn`t surprising which is -- which is tragic.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Could you feel that violent energy in the crowd that morning?\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Yes, I mean, it was -- there was this fervor, though it changed all the way through that day. There was this sort of this fervor, this passion, this almost religious belief in that something might happen. This sort of Hail Mary that the President might be able to actually succeed in this venture of trying to interrupt this ceremonial process and remain in office.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it was -- it was really quite incredible. And there was a whole load of people as well that were there. It was all different types. There were children. There were parents. There was sort of this like very odd carnival like atmosphere that then turned into something that was just -- I mean, it was like -- it was like a warzone.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Alex Holder, thank you so much for making time for us. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 HOLDER: Pleasure. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Coming up, the fight to save democracy vote by vote, state by state. \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  joins me ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, arguably the most consequential and disastrous Supreme Court term in recent memory ended. And it was also the beginning of a new chapter, one that will include the first Black woman to sit on the court. At noon, Justice Stephen Breyer`s resignation went into effect with conclusion of the term and Judge \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  was sworn in.\par \par \uc2 I really got to wonder what was going through Justice Jackson`s mind as that was happening now particularly as she takes her seat on a court with only two other liberal justices and a majority that is completely recklessly politicized and apparently out for blood. All you can do right now, if you are Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  is put your head down, attend to the work of doing what you can to preserve American democracy and constitutional governance and have faith that things will change, and that others will come to your aid because for all of their prodigious talents, she Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Justice Elena Kagan, cannot do it alone.\par \par \uc2 And I truly hope that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and the Democratic members of Congress who have all rightfully cheered Justice Jackson on understand that simple yet inescapable fact.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s been less than a week since the Supreme Court took away the 50- year-old right to an abortion and the ramifications of that decision are now playing out state by state, hour by hour really across the country, with Democrats trying to protect access to reproductive health care and Republicans trying to end it.\par \par \uc2 Take for example, what`s happening right in Georgia -- right now in Georgia. The current governor is Republican Brian Kemp. He signed a bill in 2019 that bans abortions before most people even know they`re pregnant. That bill is currently being held up in a court of appeals. And Kemp is now asking the court to let the band go into effect immediately pursuant to the Supreme Court`s decision.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s what Brian Kemp`s stance on abortion. But Kemp is up for reelection this November. His opponent is Democrat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . She not only supports abortion rights, she`s also pledging to pass legislation to protect a woman`s right to an abortion if she becomes the next Governor of Georgia. Those are the very real stakes, the very real consequences in a post Roe America. And \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  joins me now.\par \par \uc2 I`m wondering if you could start just with an explanation of where things stand in your state right now for women and their reproductive health care.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS (D-GA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: Right now, women are in extreme danger. Georgia Governor Brian Kemp passed a law in 2019 that he gleefully signed that eliminates access to the right to an abortion after six weeks. Most women don`t know they`re pregnant before six weeks.\par \par \uc2 And what exacerbates this crisis is that 82 of the 159 counties, so half of the state, does not have an OBGYN. 18 counties don`t have a general medical -- general family doctor, nine counties have no physicians at all. We refuse to expand Medicaid. We have the second-highest maternal mortality rate in the nation. So, pregnant women die more in Georgia. And if you`re a Black woman, then your likelihood of dying is three times higher. And Black people comprise 33% of the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And so if you`re a woman, you are in danger. If you are a Black woman, you are in extreme danger. And if you are a person in this state, Brian Kemp has no interest in actually serving your needs. And that will be the law of the land as soon as the very conservative 11th circuit sends down its edict and says to the state of Georgia that forced pregnancy is now the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I just want to make sure I understood what you said at the beginning of that, that basically half of the counties in your state -- you`re not talking about abortion clinics are actually abortion. You`re saying half the counties don`t have an OBGYN practicing in the county?\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: They do not have an OBGYN in the County. Georgia is facing an extreme doctor shortage. We are facing a nursing shortage. And what this law will do, it say that you have to secure services that do not exist in order to protect your health to make medical decisions. And they know they`re setting an impossible standard that cannot be met. The one possible way of meeting that standard would be expanding Medicaid so we could draw down more dollars and encourage more doctors to come to Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But Brian Kemp both refuses to expand Medicaid in an act of cruelty that defies understanding. And he has signed this law that is going to deny access to women who cannot get service without going 203 miles outside of the state assuming they have the resources and the context to get outside of the state of Georgia to get access to health care.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, I was looking at polling on this -- on the issue of abortion in the state of Georgia. Some stuff from the Atlanta Journal- Constitution now, but this is back in January, although I think things probably even gotten more along the lines of what they saw. And I was -- I was pretty struck.\par \par \uc2 This is a question about that 2019-2020 law that Kemp signed, right, the six-week ban. Do you support or oppose that law going into effect? Support: 37.9 percent, opposed: 53.9 percent, don`t know: eight. Would you like to see the Supreme Court overturns Roe v Wade decision or not? Yes, overturn Roe v. Wade: 23.9, no: 68 percent. Those are really striking majorities in a state that is very much a 50-50 state as we all know.\par \par \uc2 ADAMS: Absolutely. And that is one of the reasons this decision is both perplexing and so dangerous. It goes against the will of the people. But this is part of a national trend that we are seeing with this devolution of constitutional rights to states that have pledged not to abide by those constitutional rights. And that is why Governor Kemp is so dangerous to Georgia. He`s dangerous to the women of Georgia. He`s dangerous to the families of Georgia. He`s dangerous to the economy of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 And he has no interest in solving the problem. In fact, he`s indicated he would expand that law to make certain that the exemptions that exist for incest and rape are removed. And let`s be clear, this would criminalize women. It would prosecute them. It would put doctors in prison for up to 10 years. And this could include a miscarriage that gets misread or investigated as an act of abortion. It would also outlaw abortion pills, medical abortion. This law is dangerous.\par \par \uc2 We know that women die in pregnancy. We know that women need the ability to make medical decisions. And Brian Kemp has said that the right to make these medical decisions will not exist for most women in the state of Georgia.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You know, something else we`ve seen in other states. I haven`t seen any one specifically in Georgia yet proposed this, although I imagine it will -- it will be sooner. Our increasing efforts to criminalize travel to other states, you know, use jurisdictional discretion to prosecute things that happen outside of state lines. There`s been talk about criminalizing speech. I mean, a clear First Amendment problem of counseling women where they can obtain abortion care.\par \par \uc2 I got to imagine given the record of your state`s state legislature both in the House and the Senate, these are the kinds of bills that are going to be coming towards a governor`s desk after this Midterm election.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: They not only will be coming, they`ve already started. There are those who are calling for a special session. During the last session, there was an attempt to outlaw being able to receive abortion pills through the mail. And I encourage people to go to my website, StaceyAbrams.com, to learn more about our reproductive health platform.\par \par \uc2 But we need people to understand that Georgia is the tip of the spear. And if we can stop these laws here in Georgia, if we can reverse this trend, we can do so much not only to protect the women of Georgia, but to protect women in the south. We need folks to pay attention. We need people in Georgia to understand that the governor is the last hope that we have. And this current governor has no interest in serving the women of Georgia, serving the families of Georgia, and he should not have this job.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We got some news today from the Supreme Court about taking up a case about gerrymandering. I don`t have time to get into that with you, but I`m raising it because I would like to speak with you about it because your state has also been ground zero for all kinds of different means of essentially, massaging, rigging, hijacking democratic processes so as to barricade Republicans inside a majority in what we all know is a very evenly divided state. So, maybe I can have you back to talk about that.\par \par \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  who is running for governor in the state of Georgia, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 ABRAMS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "MSNBC PRIME" starts now with Ali Velshi. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Chris. Have yourself a great evening. And thanks to you at home for joining us this hour. It was an historic moment at the Supreme Court today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 , ASSOCIATE JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: I \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  do solemnly swear --\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS\uc2 , CHIEF JUSTICE, U.S. SUPREME COURT: That I will support and defend the Constitution of the United States against all enemies foreign and domestic.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220701ei6u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for June 30, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Ali Vitali, Juanita Tolliver, Glenn Kirschner, Ken  Dilanian, Maria Teresa Kumar, Gabe Gutierrez, Mike Memoli, Cal Perry \par \uc2 7,992 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good afternoon, I`m Chris Jansing at MSNBC headquarters in New York City. On another day when multiple stories are developing as we speak, we begin at the Supreme Court where less than an hour ago, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  was sworn in as the newest justice and the first black woman in the court`s history, replacing \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  who officially stepped down at noon.\par \par \uc2 But while she brings new diversity to the court, she won`t change the ideological makeup. And that`s a big issue for those concerned that a string of decisions by the conservative majority Roe v. Wade, religious freedom, and gun rights are just the start of a broader push reshaping the cultural face of America.\par \par \uc2 And it was Roe today that pushed President Biden to reverse his long-held position, now calling on Congress to carve out an exception to the 60-vote filibuster and pass new laws that would protect abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I believe we have to codify Roe v. Wade in the law. And the way to do that is to make sure that Congress votes to do that. And if the filibuster gets in the way it`s like voting rights, it should be -- we provide an exception for this. They accept -- the -- requiring exception to the filibuster for this action.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: His speech came just hours before two new decisions, the first actually delivering a win for the Biden administration, ruling that it acted properly when it ended Trump`s Remain in Mexico policy for asylum seekers. But the court also dealt a blow to the fight against climate change limiting the EPA`s power to regulate harmful greenhouse gases. We`ll have more on those in just a minute.\par \par \uc2 But all of that is happening as the January 6 investigation is posing potential new perils for Republicans following Cassidy Hutchinson`s explosive testimony. Committee Member \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  speaking at the Reagan Library last night received sustained applause and a standing ovation as she stepped to the podium to deliver a scathing assessment of where her party stands right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R-WY): At this moment, we are confronting a domestic threat that we have never faced before. And that is a former president who is attempting to unravel the foundations of our constitutional republic. And he is aided by Republican leaders and elected officials who made themselves willing hostages to this dangerous and irrational man.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Her speech comes in two big developments for the 1/6 committee. The first, the decision to issue a subpoena to compel Trump`s former White House Counsel \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  to testify next Wednesday, July 6. The second, from Cheney herself who told ABC is John Carrol that the committee may make a criminal referral to DOJ recommending that anyone who attempted to influence witness testimony be prosecuted for witness tampering.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve got lots to get to and I want to bring in NBC`s Ali Vitali on Capitol Hill, Glenn Kirschner is a former federal prosecutor, Brendan Buck worked for two Republican White House -- two Republican House speakers, \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 , and Juanita Tolliver is an MSNBC political analyst, so good to have all of you here.\par \par \uc2 Look, Glenn, the committee has always shied away from issuing criminal referrals, at least up to this point. Why might this time be different?\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, you know, this time probably will be different, Chris. You know, we can all understand that the legislature is a separate equal branch of government to the executive branch where the Department of Justice resides. They want to do their work thoroughly and professionally, they want to issue a report, and that`s when they will probably begin to make criminal referrals.\par \par \uc2 However, when it comes to matters such as witness tampering, some of which we`re now seeing, that`s the kind of thing that needs to be referred in real-time because you know, when somebody tries to tamper with an investigation, whether congressional or criminal, that strikes right at the heart of the integrity of the investigation.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s the kind of thing that I would urge the Department of Justice to jump on with both feet, figuratively speaking in real-time, so the J-six committee can continue to do the work that is so important in documenting and exposing what went on during the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And you could certainly argue, Juanita, this really raises the stakes. But the committee`s concern, and we`ve known this for a while is that a criminal referral would put too much of a political lens on any conclusion that Justice Department would reach about charging. So is there an argument to be made that the committee should simply let DOJ do its work?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Look, I think we`ve seen a lot of tensions between DOJ and the select committee as of late in terms of transferring over transcripts and video depositions, but I do think that the select committee is maybe overstating how political it`ll be especially based on the explosive evidence that they`ve been laying out in real-time. Think about Cassidy Hutchison`s testimony which connected the fake electors` plot with the premeditated effort to attack and target the Capitol building, with the premeditated effort to have Trump on site of that same of tech, right?\par \par \uc2 Like, I think that they need to absolutely extend and exhaust everything that they have in their power to try to make sure that the DOJ gets this right because this attempted coup cannot be glossed over. This unprecedented action by a sitting president cannot be glossed over. And so I think it`s important that they recognize and zoom out a little bit and refocus on the reality of the continued threat that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continues to present, as well as the long-term impact this will have on our democracy if it goes without any degree of serious accountability or even prosecution.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Which was exactly the point that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  was making tonight. The other big move, Ali, is the subpoena for \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 . Clearly, the testimony of Cassidy Hutchinson showed us all what a key player he was in the days before and after the insurrection. What do you think? Will he be willing to testify? And what would be most important to hear from him?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, what I had heard yesterday from my sources when the committee was thinking about issuing the subpoena was the fact that in order for Cipollone to even come back before them, they probably needed this as the precursor there for optics reasons and others. The fact that he was subpoenaed now gives his legal team the ability to consider what they`re going to do with that.\par \par \uc2 We know, for example, that one of his key concerns is the idea of executive privilege. He also has to contend with the idea of attorney-client privilege. The way that other people in this orbit who are at his level from the Trump White House, and the person I`m thinking of specifically, is former chief of staff, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , is this idea of executive privilege is obviously stronger for them as actual White House officials than it was for someone like \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  whose case is currently in front of the courts.\par \par \uc2 The way that Cipollone is thinking about this, of course, is going to be important. And then, of course, the way that it actually manifests is going to be important too. And the committee has already spoken to Cipollone. They mentioned this in the subpoena that they talked to him behind closed doors back in April. And the reason that they want him before them now, again, is because they have more questions.\par \par \uc2 He`s probably if he decides to cooperate, going to testify, again, behind closed doors, our sources tell us that that would just be a transcribed deposition. No less important, though, to the committee, ultimately getting to the bottom line here, which is that Cipollone within many of these meetings that Cassidy Hutchinson was talking about, but also that those top former officials from the Department of Justice were talking about when they testified.\par \par \uc2 Cipollone can shed light, not just on who said what in those meetings, but also, his view as the \uc2 White\uc2  House`s top lawyer there. And the committee has gone to painstaking lengths to try to show that the former president and his top allies were moving ahead not just with these false electoral strategies and attempts to overturn the election, but also what the White House was talking about during the insurrection showing that they did these things even as people were telling them, they were probably illegal.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Brendan, we heard what \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  said basically, that Republicans can support the Constitution or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but not both. And really interesting, an AP poll was just released, and it shows while nearly half of the public thinks Trump should face criminal charges for his role on January 6, it`s almost completely driven by Democrats, just 10 percent of Republicans think he should be charged. But here`s what`s interesting. That was taken before Hutchinson`s testimony. Would you expect that that changes the dynamic in any way? And we still have at least a couple more hearings to go.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER CHIEF COMMUNICATIONS ADVISER TO FORMER HOUSE SPEAKER PAUL RYAN: I think it`s chipping away at him a little bit but I`m skeptical to say that this is going to dramatically change Trump`s fortunes within the Republican Party. And the reason for that is many of them are tuned out. Many of them have already made up their minds. Because remember, there`s been a year-long effort to whitewash what happened there, to tell people that this was agitators from the left or whatever it may be. So there`s not a lot of room for Republicans to have an open mind.\par \par \uc2 But what \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is doing is going to have a political impact. And -- but I`m looking more ahead at a general election. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is probably still the nominee for the Republican Party if he decides to go for it. But he`s becoming awfully risky as a Republican. And I -- you got to imagine that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  or \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is now looking at this a little differently than they did before.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 But I think \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is actually the center of the entire effort. You`re going to have a different nominee. So many Republicans want to dance around this. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  wants to talk about the Trump-Pence agenda. \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  is the only one who is willing to go right at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , to attack him by name, to raise the stakes on this, and it may cost her her seat. But I think that is -- makes her probably the most selfless politician I`ve ever seen. She`s willing to run a political kamikaze mission, lose her seat because she thinks she`s doing the right thing, and you rarely really see that in politics these days.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Glenn, the DOJ meantime, is basically laying low, which is what they do. And I want to play a little bit of what Lisa Monaco, Deputy Attorney General, had to say last night about how the Justice Department is handling the January 6 investigation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LISA MONACO, DEPUTY U.S. ATTORNEY GENERAL: We are doing what we should do, which is following the facts and the law wherever they lead. And the Attorney General and I have been very clear about this as well. We will continue to do that, the prosecutors on these cases and in these investigations will continue to do that and hold perpetrators to account no matter what level they may have occupied and whether they were there on January 6 or not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Look, you`ve spent years inside the Justice Department. You know the way it works. For those wondering why the DOJ, why Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  have not brought charges against people like, include -- and including \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , what do you tell them?\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: So, Chris, people might not understand that -- you know, the federal prosecutors investigate crime proactively. What does that mean? It means before any arrest is made, there in the Grand Jury, presenting witnesses and evidence, and they do that thoroughly and sometimes, exhaustively. In my experience, sometimes they do it too exhaustively and they take too long to reach a prosecutorial decision, whereas state prosecutors work reactively an arrest is made, and they are immediately in court prosecuting.\par \par \uc2 We now know courtesy of this most recent January 6 hearing that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  intended to lead not just an attack on the Capitol, but an attack that he knew was going to be an armed attack. So what I would urge the Department of Justice to do is to -- you know when the house is on fire, I think it`s a fair observation that our democracy is on fire, you don`t take a lot of time to sort of measure the pH balance of the water, you unravel the investigative hose, you deploy it full blast, and you begin to hold people accountable to stop the clear and present danger to our democracy that I would contend \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  still represents. So I expect that the Department of Justice is doing it that they`re picking up the pace given what we just learned.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And the other thing you do, \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  argues, is you step up, right? And so, Juanita, I want to play something else that \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  said last night about the example that Hutchinson set through her willingness to indeed step up and testify.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Little girls all across this great nation are seeing what it really means to love this country and what it really means to be a patriot. And let me also say this to the little girls and to the young women who are watching tonight, these days, for the most part, men are running the world and it is really not going that well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Take applause line there, Juanita. But look, there are people who could do what she did. Some who frankly could fill in the gaps without likely exposing themselves to any criminal liability, but they haven`t. Do any of them do you think, look at what she`s done and are saying and having conversations with their lawyers, maybe it`s time?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Well, it depends on who their lawyer is because just like Cassidy Hutchison had, she got rid of her trump affiliated lawyer switched attorneys, and after four depositions now comes out with a more complete story with new details. So I think that`s a big part of it, too. But the -- I was absolutely stunned that this 25-year-old woman is standing here when you have folks like Meadows, Cipollone, and Vice President Pence even whose life was directly targeted, sitting back and letting their staffers take this on.\par \par \uc2 I think it also says a lot about the select committee strategy and focusing on mid-level staffers who were less tied to Trump and less drinking his Kool-Aid if you will, for fewer decades because they were willing to go out and say the truth and speak to the select committee, unlike the other people and advisors who were on Trump`s team and Trump`s campaign. But I do think that Chairman Thompson is right in saying hey, if you found any courage come talk to us and the next person they`re targeting with that is Cipollone, who, again, as Ali mentioned, spoke to the select committee in April informally, but now it`s time to step up, take an oath and testify under oath.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s all about being under oath. Ali and Glenn, thank you. Brendan and Juanita, you`re sticking around.\par \par \uc2 Also in Washington, Ketanji Brown Jackson`s historic appointment to the Supreme Court, but given the court`s divide, what is the new Justice getting herself into?\par \par \uc2 Plus, Biden 2024? The president has begun pitching himself as the rightful nominee in the upcoming presidential election. But is his party convinced? And who else might run against him?\par \par \uc2 And if you`re headed to the airport this Fourth of July weekend, will it be smooth flying or stress-inducing nightmares? Answers to your questions ahead.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: From Judge to justice. Today, we`re entering a new era for the Supreme Court because just in the last hour, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  officially joined the bench becoming the first black female justice on the High Court and longtime Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  hung up his robes after a roller coaster term.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 We also just got the final rulings for the term, a big loss for the federal government`s ability to regulate climate change and emissions, but a win for the Biden administration on border policy. And we`ll have more on that ahead.\par \par \uc2 All of it, though, only crystallizing the weight of the 6-3 conservative majority court Justice Jackson is joining and we`ve seen life-changing rulings on everything from the rollback of abortion rights, expansion of Second Amendment rights over concealed carry, even the fraying of the barrier between church and state with cases about prayer and public funds for religious schools. So, what will this new justice and Americans face next term?\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ken Dilanian joins me, also here Maria Teresa Kumar, an MSNBC contributor and President and CEO of the grassroots organization, Voto Latino, and back with me, Brendan Buck, who worked for both former Republican House speakers, \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, Maria, I think the blunt question is, what is \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  getting yourself into because look, just one of three in the minority on the court?\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Well, her being there actually is going to represent a large swath of Americans that often have been voiceless, and that is, African American women, in particular, an African American community. And her life experience demonstrates the need to have her voice there alongside that of \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 . You could counter that it was the life experience of the current Justice that is allowing them to make such extreme decisions themselves on the other side but fundamentally, it`s a matter of having a diverse court that increasingly looks like America. And that also means reminding voters that voting matters.\par \par \uc2 The reason that we are seeing such dissents on what the majority of Americans want is when we`re talking about abortion bans when we`re talking about climate change legislation, whether we`re talking even about the idea of who should have a gun and concealed weapons is because Trump delivered to his voters that he was going to appoint justices as they came about.\par \par \uc2 And I believe that the Democrats need to learn -- take that lesson and vote as aggressively and mobilized as aggressively along what is at stake when a lot of these judgments are coming to the Supreme Court. And they`re legislating increasingly on behalf of a Congress that is at a standstill.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s talk one of the most controversial judgments, Brendan. Earlier, President Biden made some big news overseas by setting down a marker for how to codify abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: I believe we have to qualify Roe v. Wade and the law. And the way to do that is to make sure that Congress votes to do that. And if the filibuster gets in the way it`s like voting rights, it should be -- we provide an exception for this. They accept -- the -- requiring an exception to the filibuster for this action. And if you think this decision by the court was an outrage or a significant stake, vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It does tell you, Brendan, about the urgency that a lot of Democrats feel in this moment. But what do you look -- what are you going to look for and what do you think the political implications might be?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes, what he said there at the end, the voting is obviously the most important. Democrats have tried this already. They don`t have the votes. They don`t even have 50 votes to change this, let alone get over the filibuster. It`s a lot of conversation about the filibuster, but the votes don`t exist anyway. Joe Manchin, Democrat in West Virginia voted with Republicans. So I know he has to say this. But ultimately, this is going to come down to the American people voting and who they want to send through the Senate.\par \par \uc2 Look, there was a much more liberal court for many years. And I think Democrats got complacent about it. And Republicans, on the other hand, took it very seriously. And they organized. And they worked for years and years and years to get to this point. The court was always much more important to Republicans than it was for Democrats. And I think maybe that`s probably going to change.\par \par \uc2 But I think what he`s doing is raising the bar for the next election in the Senate and for the years to come. That`s where this is going to happen. There are no shortcuts. There`s no going around the filibuster. There`s no stacking the court if you want to change it, you have to vote.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: You have to vote but also we`re looking at what a lot of people have been talking about, Maria, which is what they see is a chaotic situation from state to state, right? And we just learned literally in the last couple of minutes this breaking news that a Florida federal judge has halted a new law that would have prohibited all abortions in that state after 15 weeks of pregnancy. I mean, look, the high court`s decision has unleashed a state-level legal battle royal all across the country. What are we looking at here now that we`ve had a little bit of time to digest exactly what the Supreme Court said?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Well, on one side, they said that it`s a federal law to decide who can carry a gun in this country, not on this -- not the states. And on the other hand, when it`s said, to preserve the agency over one`s body, it`s absolutely to the state level. And I think that we could agree with Buck is the -- at the end of the day, the voters have to decide what kind of state legislator they have.\par \par \uc2 The challenge, Chris, that we`re having is that so many of these areas are been highly gerrymandered that we are trying to see what -- you know what avenues there are to actually combat a lot of this legislation and a lot of these court orders because they are not necessarily the majority of people, even within those states that espouse these anti-abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And so that is one of the challenges that we`re finding. I have to say that there`s a lot of attorneys on the case on the progressive side suing the heck out of the gerrymandering, and you`re going to see a lot of them suing on the case of abortions. That`s exhaustive. I think that what it`s telling us is that fundamentally, we have to modernize our election system, that we have to revisit not only the issue of how redistricting is happening, but actually how do you expand the vote to eligible voters through automatic voter registration, and of these other pieces of stuff -- other pieces of policy that we know work in other countries.\par \par \uc2 If you look at Australia, if you look at New Zealand, modern -- the modern democracies where they encourage people not only to participate in to -- but in some cases demand it. And the nice thing about demanding automatic registration, you actually see the bipolarization of both extremes come down because you need to expand it so that everybody participates equally.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And I think in great democracies, Brendan, you see a belief in important institutions, right. But there`s this Gallup poll that was released the day before the court overturned Roe v. Wade and it shows confidence in the Supreme Court at a historic low, just 25 percent. That`s down 11 points in a year. I mean, you see the conservative majority ruling how it has this term, being criticized for taking a hammer to legal precedent, do you see this term hurting the views of the institution even further, and maybe even impacting how Americans view the party that put these justices in the majority?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Well, it`s certainly getting much more political than I`ve ever seen it. And I think that could have long-term political impacts. The Roe decision, I think is absolutely bad for Republicans nationally. There are a ton of Republican voters for whom this is very important and you can`t alienate them, and you need to stick by them. That`s why you`re seeing state after state, Republican legislatures are moving forward with what is probably bad national politics, banning abortion you know for a party that has struggled with women voters, this is a bad place to go.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I think this is damaging the credibility of the court. I think what you`re seeing though, is this is the first time in a long time you had a really solid conservative court. And so you`re starting to see a big shift. And it all feels like it`s happening really quickly. A lot of the court cases that we`ve seen in the last few weeks are not terribly surprising to me as a conservative, I don`t think that the \uc2 EPA\uc2  has the authority to regulate whatever it wants. Congress should be doing those things. I mean, these are basic things that conservatives believe, but they`re just all happening at once. The court has switched over and so now you`re just getting a taste of that. But we haven`t seen it in a while with such a liberal court for so long.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Ken, looking at this term, let`s talk about what quantified this rightward shift we`ve seen but also importantly, what other high- profile cases we should expect ahead.\par \par \uc2 KEN DILANIAN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Chris, what we saw this term was not only a conservative majority deciding cases in its favor. We`ve seen that before. What was different with this new majority is an entirely new approach to interpreting the Constitution not as a living document, but as one set in stone in the era of the people who wrote it. We saw that in the New York gun case where the court is saying now that the only relevant question on gun regulations is whether they are consistent with regulations throughout history, never mind that there was no such thing as AR-15 assault rifles and high capacity magazines in the 1860s.\par \par \uc2 And in the abortion decision, I was struck by a passage and Justice Kagan`s dissent, where she noted that the majority was interpreting the 14th Amendment as it was written in 1868. Not only were the people, who wrote it all men, women didn`t even have the right to vote at that time. And today - - and today in EPA`s ruling the court has rewritten the standard on how government agencies can regulate the air we breathe, the drugs we take, the products we buy in a way that will make regulation much harder.\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, Chris, the court will deal with some big and contentious issues. Next term, they`ll hear a redistricting case out of Alabama. That involves a key provision of the Voting Rights Act as well as two affirmative action cases challenging admissions policy at Harvard and the University of North Carolina. The justices will also hear a case concerning a web designer who won`t work with same-sex couples.\par \par \uc2 And they just agreed, Chris, to hear a case that could pave the way for state legislatures to set aside the results of elections and impose their own will. And four conservative justices, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , \uc2 Sam Alito\uc2 , \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , and \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  are already on record as supporting the so- called independent legislature theory, which holds that state legislators have the sole power to set the rules for elections and then that their decisions cannot be reviewed by state courts if they convince Amy Coney Barrett, they have a majority. And given what we`ve been going through over the 2020 election, and the ongoing threats to our democracy, it`s hard to imagine a more significant case, Chris.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It is indeed. Ken Dilanian, Maria Teresa Kumar, Brendan Buck, thanks to all of you. We`re going to stick with the High Court and its decision on the Remain in Mexico policy, now a major win for the Biden administration and migrants seeking asylum.\par \par \uc2 How this will shake up immigration process going forward? So we`ve got Gabe Gutierrez who`s done extensive reporting from both sides of the border here to join us next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A big win for the Biden administration out of the Supreme Court with bigger implications for our border and migration. The Supreme Court just ruled the Biden administration does have permission to shut down the Trump-era Remain in Mexico border policy. It required people seeking asylum at the southern border to wait in Mexico while their claims were decided.\par \par \uc2 And it comes as migration is in harsh spotlight after that tragedy out of Texas. 53 migrants found dead in the back of a big rig truck in sweltering heat willing to risk their lives to come to this country.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Gabe Gutierrez has covered this policy heavily and has reported from our border with Mexico. What are the implications, Gabe, of this ruling?\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, as you mentioned, this obviously comes at a heightened time of interest ahead of the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 You mentioned the death of those 53 migrants in San Antonio, actually and we`re getting breaking news that four other migrants were just killed in what authorities are calling a human smuggling incident near Laredo.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: A separate incident.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: A separate incident near Laredo. So there`s obviously a lot of interest in this. And what are some of the implications here? This ruling basically, you know, the Supreme Court was in an interesting situation here basically ruling on federal immigration policy. And yes, as you mentioned, the reaction is pouring in. Authorities in Texas, Lieutenant Governor \uc2 Dan Patrick\uc2  now saying that, you know, this will lead to increased migration at the southern border. He says that this will be devastating for the U.S. and for the state of Texas.\par \par \uc2 But we`re also hearing from advocates at the border. As you said, I`ve been down there to Reynosa, Mexico and also to Tijuana, Mexico within the last year and they said that this was a step in the right direction, Chris, because what -- you know, it isn`t officially over now, the Remain in Mexico policy, because now it goes down to lower courts and Title 42, which of course you`ve been covering on this show remains in place.\par \par \uc2 So there are some real questions on whether, you know, will this mean an influx of migrants across the border? That we don`t know right now. But the last few hour, we have been speaking with immigration advocates at the border. They see this as a very positive step in the right direction. The advocate that I was speaking to in Reynosa, Mexico in one of the tent camps that we visited last year, she says things have gotten increasingly more dangerous for those migrants in Reynosa, that they`re having to go through kidnappings, rapes, even murders in some of those migrant camps that have sprung up.\par \par \uc2 And now they`re having to build separate shelters to deal with the influx that growing number of migrants that were south of the border while Remain in Mexico was in place. Of course it`s that policy that you described also known as MPP, Migrant Protection Protocols, that was enacted during the Trump administration. Of course immigration advocates down there say that migrants were not protected. Instead they were threatened because of this policy, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Gabe Gutierrez, it`s good to have you here in New York. Thank you so much. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Sixty-seven years after the horrific abduction, torture and murder of Emmett Till, his family is calling for an arrest. A teen searching a Mississippi courthouse basement for evidence in his case has found an unserved warrant charging a white female in his 1955 lynching. Her name, Carolyn Bryant Donham, now in 80s and living in North Carolina. She is identified as Mrs. Roy Bryant on the document, who alleged that Till flirted with her, a white, married woman, and pointed him out to the two men accused of killing Till but who were acquitted by an all-white jury.\par \par \uc2 Six years ago, Carolyn Donham recanted those allegations for the author of the book, "The Blood of Emmett Till." As the "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reported then Professor Timothy B. Tyson spoke with Donham and said she said of her long ago allegations that Emmett grabbed her and was menacing and sexually crude towards her, that part is not true.\par \par \uc2 Till was just 14 at the time. Now as one relative said when the warrant was found, serve it and charge her. That arrest warrant was publicized at the time, but the sheriff at the time told reporters he did not want to bother her since she had two young children to care for.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, as we look ahead to 2024, we`re asking this. Will \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  run for re-election? The president has begun pitching himself to fellow Democrats as the strongest 2024 nominee, but how confident is his party?\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:42:44]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New NBC news reporting this morning reveals that President Biden has himself been pitching himself to Democrats as the best candidate for 2024. Fundraisers on the sidelines of recent events. People who have spoken to the president tell NBC News that Biden regularly makes the case that he`s the only one who can beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Sometimes even listing all of his potential Democratic challengers.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  chiming in on 2024 this week telling the "L.A. Times" that she will stay on Biden`s ticket if he runs.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now the reporter behind that NBC News story, Mike Memoli, and MSNBC political analyst Juanita Tolliver.\par \par \uc2 So, Mike, the newest approval rating we have for the president is 38 percent from Reuters. Given that number, is his message working? Does he really need to work hard to convince folks that he`s the likely and best nominee?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, we just have to remind ourselves how unusual this conversation even is. Typically, it`s not even a thought that the sitting president would seek a second term, but you give one number, 38 percent. That`s the president`s approval rating at this moment. That`s one concern for Democrat.\par \par \uc2 The other number of course that`s concerning for Democrats is 81. That`s how old President Biden will be on Election Day in 2024. Of course he is the oldest president in our history. And so that`s why this president is increasingly frustrated that this conversation is already so public and why the White House is really looking at 2022, the midterm campaign this year, as an opportunity to really reaffirm and demonstrate to the party that he is not just the best candidate to lead the party at this given moment, but also the best candidate who could stand up and beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in a rematch.\par \par \uc2 He`s going to be using the fall to test drive the message, but also to show that he still has the stamina to run for re-election, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, who else could the 2024 nominee be? Who`s he been talking about?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Well, it`s so interesting that one of the things here, we would assume if the president weren`t going to run for reelection, that his vice president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , would certainly potentially clear the field. That`s obviously not the case based on just how many Democrats are already talking publicly about potentially running themselves.\par \par \uc2 You have some of the candidates who ran in 2020. Senator Amy Klobuchar among them who would potentially run again. You also have members of the president`s Cabinet beyond Harris. Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  as a potential candidate.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:00]\par \par \uc2 You also have a group of Democrats who are going to likely be on the ballot this year, potentially have big wins in a tough midterm year, that that would help them build the case for running in 2024. I`m thinking of Governor Pritzker in Illinois who just said this -- didn`t rule it out to one of our NBC colleagues but also Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  in California. He survived that tough recall election. He`s on a glide path to reelection this year, and that would be a part of a new crop of Democrats who might be ready to run in 2024 even if Biden is in the race.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, Mike, first of all, thank you, but Juanita, what Governor Pritzker said is he thinks it is certainly possible that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  could face a primary challenge. Where do you put the chances of that and what do you think the circumstances would have to be?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Chris, I got to start with the midterms haven`t happened yet. I want everybody to kind of pump the brakes here. We got a long way to go and the midterms are going to be a big indicator about who else could be potentially running, but I do think that if these conditions continue to trend downward for the Biden White House, I wouldn`t rule out a primary challenger to a sitting president as unprecedented as it would be.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that the president has something on his side when he talks about I beat Trump in 2020, but to contextualize that, Chris, we got to think back that in 2020, voters were largely motivated about getting rid of Trump. That was the mandate. They knew they didn`t like what they were seeing. They knew that he was harmful. They knew that he was problematic and they didn`t want any part of that.\par \par \uc2 I heard it in focus group after focus group. And so in 2020 that was the mandate. And we saw that that energy went towards President Biden when he was elected. But considering those approval numbers that Mike mentioned, considering the fact that yes, the White House has seen a lot of historic economic growth, they`ve seen a lot of historic moments including today with \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , ascending to the Supreme Court. They`ve seen historic investments in infrastructure, people are still struggling.\par \par \uc2 And that is a reality that I keep coming back to because when President Biden and Vice President Harris assumed their offices, I said they need to make people`s lives markedly better than they were under the last guy, but with rising costs, with the pandemic that`s still hovering over all of our heads and not quite gone yet, with the reality that people again are still trying to make ends meet, I think that`s something that the president needs to focus all his energy on right now.\par \par \uc2 Don`t talk to me about 2024. Use every bit of energy you have to address issues that people are facing right now. Use every bit of energy that the White House has to address this highly partisan Supreme Court that`s been issuing rulings and judgments that are harmful for this country and attacks on our basic privacy.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I wonder if his best argument, Juanita, and I`m interested in what you think about this. I wonder if his best argument is to say I`m the guy who can beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . First of all, there has been a lot of back and forth about whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would run under the best circumstances, but number two, given what we`re seeing from the January 6th commission, will he even be able to run? I mean, is that the best argument to hang your hat on?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: I think that needs to be a large portion of the argument, right? Making the case for why Trump is not fit to serve in any electoral capacity. Emphasizing this not only attempted coup, but the ongoing threat that Trump presents to our democracy. Emphasizing and painting a very crystal clear picture which contrasts the Biden presidency with the Trump presidency is going to be clear because you better believe that people are taking all of that information in on top of what they`re experiencing in real time day-to-day.\par \par \uc2 Painting that picture of how destructive a second Trump presidency will be is going to be critical and emphasizing it for different demographic groups. Remember how Trump singled out immigrant communities, singled out black communities, singled out Asian Americans, singled out indigenous Americans, singled out Latino Americans.\par \par \uc2 Emphasizing that in a very deeply personal way absolutely will get this president far if he does seek the presidency in 2024.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Juanita Tolliver, good to see you. Have a great weekend.\par \par \uc2 And speaking of that, the stressed out Fourth of July weekend at airports everywhere. What you need to know if you`re going to fly the not-so- friendly skies this holiday is next.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It`s exhausting. It`s a pain in the rear end. Got to roll with it, though. You got to travel.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:24]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: U.S. stocks are on track for their worst first half of a year in decades as June comes to a close. At this moment the Dow Jones Industrial Average is down. What are we looking at here? Oh, only 69 points. It was down more than 500 earlier. But still, a drop of about 6,000 points from the first day of 2022 when the Dow closed at more than 36,000. Accelerating inflation and interest rates spurred losses in most major markets these last six months. The S&P 500, which is tied to retirement savings, dropped at an extreme not seen since 1970.\par \par \uc2 Also right now, chaos caused by flight cancelations and soaring gas prices have just about anyone getting ready to travel this holiday weekend stressed out. Well, you`re not alone. AAA expects close to 50 million Americans to travel. A headline in Vox put it like this, "Flying Will Be the Worst Part of Your Summer Vacation. With abnormally bad rates of flight delays and cancelations, air travel won`t stop sucking any time soon."\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Cal Perry is live from Reagan National Airport.\par \par \uc2 Cal, what are you seeing there? How is the airport preparing for what sounds like it`s going to be an insanely complicated weekend?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It sounds like a very accurate setup to the information I`m about to give you. As you said, close to 50 million people will travel this holiday weekend. And we are dealing with thousands of delays and cancelations. Over 3,000 flights have been canceled so far this week alone. Most of them on American and Delta. Those airlines are trying to make up for it by rescheduling flights, by rebooking passengers through various cities but that brings its own challenges as well.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 As you said, gas prices are at record highs. So people expected to fly more, but these delays and these airline ticket prices are preventing that from happening. The other thing that`s happening, Chris, around the country is there are no more rental cars. There is another run on rental cars just like we saw last year. So inevitably what is happening is people are getting rebooked, they`re going to cities they weren`t supposed to be in, and then they`re getting stuck at the airport because they don`t have a rental car.\par \par \uc2 I had a chance to speak to a passenger who is traveling today. I asked her her advice to other passengers because she travels frequently. Here`s what she had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Any tips you`ve got for people?\par \par \uc2 ANGELA FRAZIER, TRAVELING TO NEW ORLEANS: Get to the airport a little early. Make sure you have the -- well, the e-mail address to your airline because you can`t get nobody on the phone. And make sure you had downloaded the app on your phone, as well, so you can make those flight changes immediately.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Now separate from the travel nightmare that will be this weekend for millions, tens of millions of Americans, there`s a pilot strike today, by the way, on behalf of Delta. A few hundred pilots in five different cities. It doesn`t have anything to do with the snarling of travel right now. It has to do with labor issues. We`re talking about salaries and schedules.\par \par \uc2 But, Chris, it is a reminder that there is a pilot shortage that we are still dealing with from the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Chris, back to you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for us this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday at 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. Yasmin Vossoughian picks it up next. I`ll see you after the Fourth of July. Have a great holiday.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220701ei6u00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for June 29, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alicia Menendez \par \uc2 7,463 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: That is tonight`s "LAST WORD". THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the new subpoena sent from the January 6 committee to \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 , just a day after the bombshell testimony from Cassidy Hutchinson, and the pleas from the panel to get him to talk.\par \par \uc2 Plus, what we`re learning tonight about the very real security threats, just how much Trump knew and what happens next? Then more states take action on abortion after the Supreme Court decision, with Democrats pushing the president to do something more, as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. I`m Alicia Menendez in for Stephanie Ruhle. After days a very public invitations to come talk. The January 6 committee tonight has issued a formal subpoena for \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 . The move comes just one day after that devastating testimony from Cassidy Hutchinson, about the former Trump White House Counsel`s actions ahead of and during the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the committee writes, investigation has, "revealed evidence that Mr. Cipollone repeatedly raised legal and other concerns about President Trump`s activities on January 6th and in the days that preceded it." The panel note Cipollone did sit for an informal interview on April 13. But goes on to say, "unfortunately, however, you have declined to cooperate with us further, including by providing on the record testimony. We are left with no choice but to issue you the subpoena."\par \par \uc2 Earlier this evening, committee member Adam Schiff had this message for Cipollone.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF, (D) CALIFORNIA JAN. 6 SELECT COMMITTEE MEMBER: And he ought to show just a small portion of the courage that Cassidy Hutchinson displayed. He needs to come out of hiding. He needs to do his patriotic duty.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: As we mentioned Cipollone was prominently featured in yesterday`s testimony.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CASSIDY HUTCHINSON, FORMER TRUMP WH AIDE: Mr. Cipollone said something to the effect of, please make sure we don`t go up to the Capitol, Cassidy. Keep in touch with me. We`re going to get charged with every crime imaginable if we make that movement happen.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY, (R) WYOMING GOP CONFERENCE CHAIR: And do you remember which crimes Mr. Cipollone was concerned with?\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: In the days leading up to the sixth, we had conversations about potentially obstructing justice or defrauding the electoral account.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Hutchinson also testified about Cipollone`s efforts on January 6, to get the president to urge the supporters to end the violence. And she described him pleading with her boss, former White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  to intervene.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: I remember Pat saying to him, something to the effect of, the rioters gotten to the Capitol, Mark, we need to go down and see the President now. And Mark looked up at him and said, he doesn`t want to do anything, Pat. And Pat said something to the effect of, and very clearly had said this to Mark, something to the effect of, Mark, something needs to be done or people are going to die and the bloods going to be on your effing hands.\par \par \uc2 I remember Pat saying something to the effect of, Mark, we need to do something more. They`re literally calling for the Vice President to be effing hang. And Mark had responded something to the effect of, have you heard it Pat, he thinks Mike deserves it. He doesn`t think they`re doing anything wrong.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yesterday`s hearing is raising new questions about potential legal consequences for the former president, and for \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , who is already facing contempt charges after defying a subpoena from the committee.\par \par \uc2 According to Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony, both Meadows and Trump were well aware of the possibility of violence on January 6. She testified Trump knew people in the crowd were armed, yet demanded security be eased to allow more people to attend his rally. And she testified Meadows had been warned about weapons on the morning of January 6.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HUTCHINSON: I remember Tony mentioning knives, guns in the form of pistols and rifles, bear spray, body armor, spears and flagpoles. I remember Tony finishing his explanation and it taking a few seconds for Mark to say something to the point where I almost said, "Mark, did you hear him?" And then Mark chimed in and was like, "All right. Anything else?" Still looking down at his phone. And then he looked up and said, "Have you talked to the President?" And Tony said, "Yes, sir. He`s where he`s -- all right, good.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:19]\par \par \uc2 I was in the vicinity of a conversation where I overheard the president say something to the effect of, I don`t effing care that they have weapons. They`re not here to hurt me. Take the effing mags away. Let my people in. They can march to the Capitol from here. Let the people in. Take the effing mags away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Hutchinson also testified that her boss was hoping for a presidential pardon. Today (inaudible) and never planned. Not surprisingly, Cassidy Hutchinson`s credibility now being attacked by Trump`s supporters. Late today, her lawyers issued this statement saying, "Ms. Hutchinson stands by all of the testimony she provided yesterday, under oath to the Select Committee to investigate the January 6 attack on the United States Capitol."\par \par \uc2 Just a short time ago, the Reagan Library Committee Vice Chair \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  spoke out about the investigation, and its meaning for our democracy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: At this moment, we are confronting a domestic threat that we have never faced before. And that is a former president who is attempting to unravel the foundations of our constitutional republic. And he is aided by Republican leaders and elected officials who`ve made themselves willing hostages to this dangerous and irrational man.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel. Jackie Alemany, Congressional Investigations Reporter for The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , Luke Broadwater, Pulitzer Prize winning congressional reporter from the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , and \uc2 NYU\uc2  Law School Professor Melissa Murray, she was a law clerk for \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  on the federal bench before her nomination to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 It is good to see you all. Luke, let`s start with you. You and your colleague Maggie Haberman are reporting on the Cipollone subpoena tonight. He has been resisting formal testimony as \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has been ramping up the public pressure. What do we know about his willingness to cooperate now?\par \par \uc2 LUKE BROADWATER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CONGRESSIONAL REPORTER: Well, we know that the committee`s subpoena puts extra leverage on Mr. Cipollone to come in and testify. And my understanding is, as we speak, there are no negotiations that have started about what he would be willing to talk about where he to come in. Apparently, he`s not willing to talk about anything, direct conversations with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But some other topics perhaps could be on the table. That`s similar to an arrangement that other lawyers for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have reached where they have not talked about direct conversations with the President, but have been able to talk about other things.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  did come in and meet with the committee informally, months ago, but it was not transcribed. It was not under oath. And they really want to -- they think he`s such a key witness. You know, he was in the room for some of the biggest decisions in the build up to January 6, conversations about seizing voting machines, conversations, where Bill Barr offered to resign after he said there was not widespread election fraud of these conversations that Cassidy Hutchinson relayed where he and \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  are going in and out of the Oval Office and discussing the violence that was underway and whether Trump would call it up. So he`s an extremely key witness. They want him to testify. They want the American people to hear from them. And we`ll see whether this subpoena now puts the pressure needed to force him to come and make his comments public.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Jackie, I`m curious what you`re hearing from your sources on the Hill and in the committee. Cipollone was described by Hutchinson, as one of the last firewalls in the effort to stop Trump on January 6, was yesterday`s hearing part of the strategy then to justify a subpoena?\par \par \uc2 JACQUELINE ALEMANY, THE WASHINGTON POST CONGRESSIONAL INVESTIGATIONS REPORTER: In part was, the committee has had a behind the scenes very detailed strategy on how to force witnesses who have not so far cooperated with the committee to come forth and change their minds. And the timing of the hearing was in part due to some of the security threats, a concern from lawmakers on the committee that Cassidy Hutchinson might back out of her commitment to testifying publicly because of just how many security threats and how much pressure she was under. But also in order to potentially spur the cooperation of other people like \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As Luke noted, the committee has been in negotiations with him for quite some time now. And there have been times in the past where individuals in Trump`s orbit have simply needed a subpoena as cover in order for them to finally cooperate, they`ll step in, for example, was initially supposed to testify publicly. And he claimed it was due to being subpoenaed by the committee.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:13]\par \par \uc2 That being said, people that we spoke to who know Pat well said that they find it very unlikely that he`s going to testify that he`s a real believer in executive privilege. He thought the Don -- the court ruling on \uc2 Don McGahn\uc2  was wrong, which ultimately compelled McGahn to appear before the House and testify. So certainly curious to see how the negotiations and the parameters of the committee and Pat lawyers set pan out.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Jackie, it`s my understanding, you have some new reporting on Trump and Cipollone in light of the investigation, what is their relationship like now?\par \par \uc2 ALEMANY: Yeah, their relationship is not as amicable as some others who continue to work directly for him or even work closely with him at Mar-a- Lago. The former president does have a small staff around, some of them former staff from the White House. But Cipollone is not one of them. The two have remained cordial, though, and he has not been a public critic of the former president. That being said, he was the no man if there was one in the White House and the former president didn`t appreciate that. They -- he oftentimes tried it and told it and sort of dismissed what Pat was advising him and the legal advice that he was providing. But ultimately, you know, I think Cassidy Hutchinson made clear in her testimony yesterday, that to -- Alicia, certain extent, some of Pat Cipollone`s warnings worked.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Melissa Murray, I would love to hear from you on how you view this argument from Cipollone that he simply cannot reveal any of his conversations with former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, NYU LAW PROFESSOR: Well, Alicia, this is a long standing play from those in the Trump White House when they`ve been called upon to relay information about their time in the White House. They`ve said that this is limited because of executive privilege. Executive privilege does exist, but it`s not an unfettered and unqualified privilege. It is typically limited to those kinds of communications between the President and his close staff that relate to diplomacy, military matters, things that are really high level, sort of ordinary things, things that go to questions around the sort of general mindset or milieu around the Oval Office at the time. Those would not necessarily be subject to executive privilege.\par \par \uc2 So there`s going to be a fight over what Cipollone testifies to and what he`s offering, but it will be basically some of these long standing questions that we`ve seen about the scope and nature of the executive privilege that they`re asserting.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Luke, I believe you also have some new reporting on what DOJ did and did not know about Cassidy Hutchinson`s testimony. What was that?\par \par \uc2 BROADWATER: Right. So yesterday, as much of the world, us included, were sitting in, like stunned, astonishment as we watched a revelation after revelation come out from Cassidy Hutchinson at the witness table. So we`re the Department of Justice, prosecutors, we understand from some sources that the prosecutors working on this investigation, on the Justice Department side, were watching very closely, and they were surprised, very much surprised by the testimony they were hearing.\par \par \uc2 This gets to a dispute that`s been going on for a while between the Justice Department and the January 6 committee. The Justice Department wants the committee to turnover every transcript they`ve done with all of these 1000 witnesses every single one that they have transcribed. And the committee has resisted that for a number of reasons. They`ve said that "We will work with the Justice Department, but on our timetable, perhaps next month, when we finish these hearings, we will have more time to go through this request and accommodate the Justice Department."\par \par \uc2 And the Justice Department is -- some of the lawyers are feeling frustrated by that because they want to have these transcripts as soon as possible as they work on their cases. You know, and at the same time, some members of the committee have called on the Justice Department to work faster and be more aggressive going after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and some of his top allies. And so they`re saying, help us, help you. And so I think we will see eventually a deal here where these transcripts do come over, but it`s going to be -- it`s probably be a little more time before we see it come together.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Melissa, speaking of this relationship between the committee and DOJ, David Rohde of the New Yorker is offering insight into how the Justice Department is handling its 1/6 investigation and in light of all these hearings. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID ROHDE, EXECUTIVE EDITOR, THE NEW YORKER: There are weekly meetings where Attorney General Garland is updated by top officials in the Justice Department about the latest information and in the investigation as a whole, the sprawling investigation but, you know, including, you know, evidence against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [23:15:09]\par \par \uc2 There`s no decision yet on whether that, you know, there is enough evidence. But Garland is pushing. He signed off on a letter that asked for more for all the transcripts in the January 6 committee, that was a few weeks ago. And as you mentioned, there`s been a flurry of searches of subpoenas being issued, phones were seized. There is movement inside the Justice Department. And again, I`m hearing from former officials, this new evidence will change their calculus. But this was a big moment yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Melissa, what should we be watching for, for \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, I think, again, any kind of movement, any more incidences where we are seeing raids, for example, like the raid we saw in Jeff Clark`s home just the other day, or more subpoenas for phone records or things of that nature. But again, this has been and continues to be a very slow and deliberative process, by design, I think. This is a DOJ that decided that it was going to come in and restore morale, restore the institutional independence of this agency, and again, getting bogged down in what can be derived as a partisan witch hunt is the last thing they want. So they want everything buttoned up. All of the T`s crossed, all of the I`s dotted.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Jackie, this was a big week for the committee. What`s next in terms of the investigation and the hearings?\par \par \uc2 ALEMANY: That is a very good question. As of right now, in this very moment, there are no hearings scheduled for next week. But the following week, there are potentially two more hearings that are going to take place. One of those hearings being focused on the 187 minutes that Trump did not respond to the violence taking place at the Capitol. So from when that -- when pro-Trump, pro-rioters first reached security up until he finally issued a statement condemning them to go home in language that was not quite that forceful.\par \par \uc2 And then another hearing focused on far-right extremism, the culture of political violence on the right, that one expected to be led by Congressman Jamie Raskin and \uc2 Stephanie Murphy\uc2 . But again, it is quite possible that the committee is going to completely recalibrate especially in -- if \uc2 Pat Cipollone\uc2  ultimately comes forward and testifies behind closed doors, it`s going to give the committee a whole new hours of videotaped depositions to potentially use at their disposal. They`re also currently still conducting closed doors depositions just today. They had Sean Dolman, the CFO of the Trump campaign and who sat down and gave investigators some behind the scenes details for several hours, according to people involved with the investigation. So this is very much alive investigation and things are changing, really by the hour.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I think we saw some of that this week. Jackie Alemany, Luke Broadwater, Melissa Murray, thank you all so much for getting us started.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, one of our next guests is the former president was essentially sending a loaded missile to the Congress. The security issues raised by this week`s testimony just ahead.\par \par \uc2 And later, the impact the headlines of recent days are already having on the midterms, THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:08]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 STEPHANIE MURPHY\uc2 , (D) FLORIDA 1/6 COMMITTEE MEMBER: While the details are being disputed. The broader narrative is not being disputed. Mr. Ornato did not have as clear of memories from this period of time as I would say Ms. Hutchinson did, if that`s a fair assessment there. But we`re always happy to have folks who have recalled things to come back and talk to us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: After Cassidy Hutchinson`s stunning testimony, the Secret Service as its agents are also available to testify under oath. But there`s no word yet on whether former White House Deputy Chief of Staff Tony Ornato or the Secret Service Agent in charge of Trump`s detail on January 6 will do that.\par \par \uc2 We welcome back, Carol Leonnig, Pulitzer Prize-Winning Investigative Reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . She wrote the book on the \uc2 U.S. Secret Service\uc2 . And \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , West Point Graduate, Army Veteran, former \uc2 FBI\uc2  Special Agent and a distinguished Research Fellow at the Foreign Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 Carol, I want you to give us some background, who is Robert Engel, what do we need to know about the Secret Service during the Trump presidency?\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEONNIG, THE WASHINGTON POST INVESTIGATIVE REPORTER: Great questions and so concise as you, so cleanly are. First off, I`ll say, Alicia, the thing that you really need to know we all need to keep in mind about the Secret Service during the Trump administration is that, you know, President to President, there can be some loyalists that stick by a particular president. But \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  politicize the Secret Service in a way that no other president had before. The most similar situation might have been President Nixon, who tried to use Secret Service agents as spies on some of his political opponents. Candidates for president, he tried to install agency thought were loyalty him, to sort of catch them in the act of something bad, and report back to him.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:05]\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  politicize this agency by installing as his White House Deputy Chief of Staff, the former head of his Secret Service detail. That person, Tony Ornato eventually basically becomes the de facto director of the Secret Service. He`s the one who makes sure events, photo ops, special things that are going to help the President get elected, get done by the Secret Service. Bobby Engel was the Secret Services detail leader for the President, and essentially, at this point, working for Tony Ornato. I mean, not in name, but in reality, working for Tony Ornato.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I mean, Clint, I would love for you to just weigh in on how unusual that is. But I also want to talk about the fact that you have the right today challenging the veracity of some of yesterday`s testimony. But we`re not getting a lot of pushback on the actual security situation that day. And I wonder what you make of that?\par \par \uc2 CLINT WATTS\uc2 , FORMER FBI SPECIAL AGENT: Yeah, Alicia, I think they`ll probably be some actual written details, usually, in any sort of security plan for an event like January 6 will be some sort of operational planning, and actual write up, that can be referred to. And you`ll see some sort of a plan, setup of magnetometers were discussed during the hearing, which are a normal thing that someone would use to try and prevent weapons from getting very close to the President or anywhere near any sort of government buildings. Those plans should be somewhere and I think they would be something that would be subpoenaed by the committee if they wanted to get more details on what that plan was, and why it did or did not follow protocol.\par \par \uc2 I think, separately as Carol talked about, you know, any presidential administration, there`s quite a bit of bonding that goes in between a Secret Service detail and the very person that they`re willing to give their life too, to protect. I don`t think that`s unusual. But I do think is unusual is, in this Trump administration, how people are pulled out of acting government positions, pushed into what are really just White House staff positions. And it also really starts to erode the integrity of these institutions. That was a consistent theme throughout all of the Trump administration, whether it was the Department of Defense, law enforcement, whatever it might be. The Trump administration was always trying to bring them in to be their law enforcement, their military guys. And that`s what caused so many problems across the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2  in that four year period.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Carol, to this whole conversation about what is usual and unusual. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  points out the willingness of the agents to provide potentially critical details about the person they were protecting marks a rare turn for an agency that has historically prioritize the secrecy of presidents, even in the face of investigations.\par \par \uc2 Carol, just how unusual is it for the Secret Service to offer up any specifics about what it does?\par \par \uc2 LEONNIG: You know, I have to grin a little bit about this because over the decades, the Secret Service and many of its directors starting actually with the Whitewater and \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2  investigation under President Clinton, have derived it agents who share any details, any confidences they learn at the side or the shoulder of the president. The goal is smart. It`s -- you don`t want a president thinking that the guys who are right next to him are absorbing his secrets and gossiping about them later. You want the confidence of those individuals because you want those individuals close.\par \par \uc2 But I grin because the Secret Service doesn`t want any details shared except apparently those that are flattering to the President, and flattering to the Secret Service. That`s one of the stunners for me in exploring this agency. You know, they didn`t want any detail agents interviewed by \uc2 Kenneth Starr\uc2 , about whether or not the President had lied about being alone with \uc2 Monica Lewinsky\uc2 , which he had. And they don`t want people sharing any details about things that are unflattering to President Trump. But here now, we have an agency that says, wait a minute, we dispute Cassidy Hutchinson`s account, which she makes clear as secondhand. And we are willing to say that we were in a car with the President and no such attack took place. The President didn`t do anything except get angry that he wasn`t allowed to travel to the capitol as he insisted on doing.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to say one more thing about in some respects how while this story is salacious and lurid, and the source of amazing media treatment, right, including by my newspaper. The things that Cassidy Hutchinson said that she witnessed firsthand are the most dramatic, right? The President attacking a federal officer is a shocker, a shocker if true. But the President telling the Secret Service not to mag people who had firearms, so that he could speak to them, have a better rally and then send them to the capitol arm is really the undisputed stunner.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:10]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I would add a layer to that, Clint, which is all the talk about the Oath Keepers, hearing the word Proud Boy closer to the planning of the January 6 rally when Mr. Giuliani would be around. I mean, members of those groups are already facing charges of seditious conspiracy. Do you think this testimony then weighs in?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: I absolutely do. I think there are two parts to it. We already had seen from both the documentary footage, after of evidence over the last few weeks that the Proud Boys and the Oath Keepers, Rhodes and Tarrio had linked up in a garage. There was obviously some sort of coordination and planning. I think, in both of the indictments, when you read them, the text communications that have come forward through clearly show that they have some sort of an informant on the inside that`s providing them some sort of information, or they have worked with somebody as a cooperating witness for reduced charges. And then you start to expand that out. They`re -- the most key person to me, which was out in the open on January 5 and 6 is \uc2 Roger Stone\uc2 , showed up consistently there. And then we also saw discussions during Hutchinson`s testimony of \uc2 Michael Flynn\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So when you look at those people that were at the rally, those that were connected to these Praetorian Guard type groups that were guarding them and leading them into rallies at -- into and around the White House, into around the Capitol. I think those are the key linkages that were really missing up to this point, because they had not been flushed out in the indictment. So we`d seen out of the DOJ. So I thought this -- one of these things from the hearing those probably most important are these connections between the White House and these militia groups. I think that will get flushed out in the coming weeks. And that`s really where the ties are. We`re talking about an insurrection. We`re talking about violence. I`m much more interested in that and whether there was some sort of argument in Sudan with the president.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Also part of where the continued threat is. Carol Leonnig, \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the debate over Democrats response to voter expectations, what it could all mean for November when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:36:48]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ, (D) NEW YORK: I believe that President Biden should entertain expansion of the Supreme Court. I think that right now, we just need a fight. We need to fight and we need to show and demonstrate to the American people that then when they vote to give Democrats power, we will use it to the fullest extent possible to defend everybody civil, economic and human rights.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: In the wake of the Supreme Court overturning Roe vs. Wade, some Democrats are calling for immediate action. But there`s disagreement within the party about how aggressively they should respond.\par \par \uc2 With us for more, former Florida Republican Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , was a member of the Republican Conference for two years during the Trump presidency, and Democratic Strategist, \uc2 Xochitl Hinojosa\uc2 . She`s the Managing Director of Bully Pulpit Interactive, and the former Communications Director and Senior Advisor for the DNC.\par \par \uc2 Xochitl, after the Roe announcement, you had some polling showing Democrats with a seven point edge in the midterms. That same poll found that Democrats were more animated by this issue than Republicans. So then explain to me why the hesitation from some in the party to really go after this issue?\par \par \uc2 XOCHITL HINOJOSA, BULLY PULPIT INTERACTIVE MANAGING DIRECTOR: Well, I think that the polling is right, Alicia, I think that what will happen is that this will motivate our base, I think that whenever Democrats or any party is in power, what ends up happening is that there ends up being low enthusiasm. You have to spend money in order to get those voters out. You have to prove to them that you have delivered in the last two years or when you`ve been in power. So in this instance, what is happening is that this will definitely help our voters.\par \par \uc2 What I will say about the Democratic Party in this administration, is that while President Biden has said that he`s not for expanding the court, he`s doing everything possible to ensure that we are protecting a woman`s right to choose and on reproductive rights. And I think that he has taken aggressive action. He gave a speech, you saw the Vice President out there recently, and they will continue to be out there.\par \par \uc2 Number one thing that Democrats could do ahead of the midterms, though, is really talk about how elections matter. And what happened in the Supreme Court last week, is exactly a result of the 2016 election. And that`s -- they need to make a clear message ahead of the midterm, say, if you don`t turn out, it could get worse. We need to continue to put Democrats in power to ensure that this doesn`t happen and so we can restore Roe.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Carlos, let`s talk about the backdrop of this midterms. You have 61% of Americans have either little or no confidence in this Supreme Court. What does that mean, Carlos, for democracy?\par \par \uc2 CARLOS CURBELO\uc2 , FORMER FLORIDA CONGRESSMAN: It`s a big problem, Alicia. The court in the past had more credibility, more respect, more trust than the political branches of government. And now, this court that makes big decisions for the entire country is starting to feel like another partisan entity. So that is a big deal. I mean, it motivates people on both sides. So you will see some conservatives turning out to maybe reward Republicans for shaping the court. But I do give Democrats an edge when it comes to just a motivation and an urgency to vote. Because obviously they`re on the losing side, and that`s typically what happens to party whose policies lose tends to get energized.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:10]\par \par \uc2 I will say more broadly speaking, Democrats also have to worry about centrist and independent voters, especially in suburban America. And that`s where they should highlight the bipartisan wins that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has been able to sign into law, namely the bipartisan infrastructure bill. And, of course, the recently passed a gun reform bill, which is historic in nature for many reasons.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I mean, Xochitl, here`s the thing, I go back to that sound from representative Ocasio-Cortez, where she says Democrats have -- there has to be a fight, right? They have to have something that they are fighting for, and they have to be able to go into midterms, saying to their voters, we delivered something. Right? And beyond just policy wins, we understood that these rights were under attack. And here are the steps we took together and it can`t be amorphous, right? This just go vote message sort of begins to feel generic. I think it`s part of why you had Senator Warren coming out and saying, give us two \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  seats, and we`ll be able to deliver for you, right? It makes it much more specific. What do you think it is that this administration can actually do and can actually deliver around choice between now and midterms?\par \par \uc2 HINOJOSA: Well, I think that you had Secretary Becerra out there, and I think you`ve had administration officials who`ve talked about the tools that they`re going to use in the administration. I think that the President needs to ensure that this -- that he continues to talk about this issue. Yes, he gave one speech, but he does need to go out in the community and talk about and really listen to people about how Roe is impacting their day to day lives, what they can do. You heard Secretary Becerra talk about medication and abortion before and making sure that is available to people.\par \par \uc2 And you`ve also heard the Justice Department say that they`re going to take aggressive action. But I do think that President Biden does need to be out there a bit more when it comes to this issue. And it can`t be forgotten.\par \par \uc2 I will say, Alicia, and I think that`s one thing that people tend to forget is that the Supreme Court is not done here. We have a number of different cases that are going to come out shortly. We know that DACA is sort of in the courts right now. We don`t know what the future of DACA is going to be. So looking forward, not just Roe. There are a number of issues that are going to impact our values and democratic values. And the reason why they put us in office and this administration and Democrats need to be clear, listen, this could get worse, the Supreme Court could potentially take away DACA other things could happen. There are other consequences if you don`t vote for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Carlos, I only have about a minute left. But there was an incredible sound from \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  tonight saying we have to choose because Republicans cannot both be loyal to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and loyal to the Constitution. I wonder if you think that`s the choice, that`s before Republicans today?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Well, that`s certainly right, Alicia, and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has really become the conscience of the Republican Party holding all Republicans accountable, putting the truth out there and having them face it and say, are you going to accept this? Or are you going to turn to the truth and reject Donald Trump`s lie? So her voice is so critical in this moment. She has a big primary coming up in a few weeks. We`ll see what happens there. But regardless of what happens in her primary, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  has delivered a great gift to this country. The truth from a Republican, from a bonafide conservative, and she is really holding all of her colleagues accountable. Many are failing the test.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , \uc2 Xochitl Hinojosa\uc2 , thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, our own Yamiche Alcindor just talked to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  about the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe, why she is calling the move arrogant and ignorant? When THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:48:37]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: We`re hearing from \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  tonight at the Aspen Ideas Festival in Colorado. Former Secretary of State delivered scathing criticism of the Supreme Court`s reversal of Roe during the discussion with our own Yamiche Alcindor.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLARY CLINTON\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: It`s the most arrogant, misreading of history and law that you could ever find. It is so narrow and baseless. It was not only ignorant, but almost dismissive to the point of contempt for women`s lives and women`s choices and the difficulties that women of all, you know, backgrounds.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Joining us now from Aspen is Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News Correspondent and Moderator of Washington Week on \uc2 PBS\uc2 . Yamiche, powerful words there from the former Secretary of State. What else did you hear from her about this decision?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: Well, Secretary Clinton really delivered a full throated condemnation of this Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade. She said it would put women`s life at risk that really it was undermining women`s ability to choose what to do with their own body. She said it was dangerous. And I want to play a little bit more of what she told me about this decision. Here`s what she said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CLINTON: You`ll have those who are trying to go after other rights, and make -- you know maybe they`re going to go after IVF, they`ll go after IUDs they`ll go after contraception more generally that is going to cause a reaction. And people are going to say, is this America, where some faceless bureaucrat or some state legislator or some governor is deciding what`s right for me and my family? Then I think you`ll see the reaction to how long that takes, I can`t predict.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [23:50:24]\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: She also talks about the fact that really there could end up being a federal ban on abortion. And that women who are living in blue states, they should not feel protected. She also said that Democrats have to mobilize and really get ready to push back to restore in some places at least abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yamiche, there was so much in this interview, I wish we could play the entire thing in full but I want to make sure we get to this part, which was that Secretary Clinton was especially critical of Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , what did she tell you?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: She signaled out \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  by name. And, of course, we should remind people that his opinion that was attached to this decision overturning Roe v. Wade talked about whether or not -- what we actually talked about the court, undoing same-sex marriage, as well as contraception, even for married couples. So she was really saying that people need to pay attention to \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , that he is someone who is -- has a dangerous idea that he could possibly even have the other judges get along with him and that people`s rights can be taken away in other areas. She also said that women should not be penalized for not being in the 13th or 14th Amendment really talking about the fact that this original idea of looking at the Constitution, in its original form is just simply wrong in her opinion.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yamiche, I also know that you talked about 2024 election`s question of President Biden running for re-election. What were your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Alicia, this was really, really interesting. I asked her, a question that Democrats have been really asking -- have been asked all across this country, would you endorse President Biden, if he ran again, for President in 2024, the White House has been signaling that President Biden wants to run. And the her answer at first was not clear. She said, of course, he`s done great things. He`s our sitting president, and I had to push again and say, OK, but would you endorse him. And she said, of course, if he`s a sitting president, if he is the nominee, then I will endorse him.\par \par \uc2 And again, she was very praising him very, very much saying that he was doing a lot of good things. But it really tells you the fact that this is really a tension point in the Democratic Party. And it`s really remarkable, because you have a sitting president, whose own party now is wondering whether or not he might not run for president in 2024, especially of course, given his age, also doing the fact that some in the party say that they believe that he was a bridge, and that he was really just here to beat \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and that he will then let other people in the party into the office and let others run.\par \par \uc2 I should also tell you, I pressed her on whether or not she was running for 2024 because after this abortion decision, there are a couple of people who were sort of wondering whether or not Hillary Clinton 2024 might happen. She looked at me laughed and said, absolutely not.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: You pressed her on many things. The conversation was excellent, top to bottom. Yamiche Alcindor, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we should note that NBC Universal News Group is the media partner for the Aspen Ideas Festival.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re learning more about the deadliest case of human smuggling in U.S. history. What we`re hearing about the investigation and from family members of the victims, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:57:53]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GUSTAVO GARCIA-SILLER\uc2 , ARCHBISHOP OF SAN ANTONIO: That trailer was abandoned. And if that trailer was abandoned, the bottom line is that immigrants were abandoned. People.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The last thing before we go tonight, tragedy yet again in Texas. Dozens of migrants were founded in a sweltering tractor trailer on Monday in San Antonio. Today, the confirmed death toll reached 53. Officials are calling it, the deadliest case of human smuggling in the United States history. Fire Chief Charles Hood was on the scene Monday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLES HOOD, SAN ANTONIO FIRE CHIEF: We have 50 or 60 people that were in humanely stuffed in the back of a truck with no water, no air conditioning, no means for ventilation and they suffered, they suffered before they died.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: More victims, some of them children still being treated at hospitals. According to a Mexican immigration official, the driver attempted to escape the scene by pretending to be one of the survivors. But now the Justice Department says he and 