unch of subpoenas on it.\par \par \uc2 So, Senator Johnson has tried to deny, minimize and spin it.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Senator Johnson, how much did you know about what your chief of staff was doing with the alternate slates of electors?\par \par \uc2 SEN. RON JOHNSON (R-WI): I`m on the phone right now.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No, you`re not. I can see your phone. I can see your screen.\par \par \uc2 Can you explain what your chief of staff was doing? Does your chief of staff still work for you, Senator? Did you support his efforts to try to get those slates to the vice president?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: No, I had no knowledge of this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who`s the person --\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Well, I don`t -- you know, I had no involvement in an altered slate of -- slate of electors. I had no idea that it will be delivered to us, that delivered staff to staff, by chief of staff do the right thing, contact the vice president`s staff. They said didn`t want it, so we didn`t deliver it. That`s the end of story.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Who`s the person that delivered it to your office?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I have no idea.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK, heard that? Two days after Senator Johnson`s pretend phone call, he made an actual phone call to a local right-wing radio show and what do you know? His memory improved.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I was originally sent a text at 11:36 by -- your friend, I just met him, Judge Troupis. Six minutes later, I set up a text exchange between my chief of staff and Jim, it just says Jim Troupis meet Sean Riley.\par \par \uc2 We found out now, this came from Pennsylvania Congressman Mike Kelly`s office. We couldn`t remember who delivered this to us, OK?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: OK. Now, that`s a very different story. Now, for his part, a spokesperson for Republican Congressman Mike Kelly of Pennsylvania said: "Senator Johnson`s statements are patently false. Mr. Kelly has not spoken to Senator Johnson for the better part of a decade. He has no knowledge of the claims Johnson is making related to the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 So, we know now is that in his first attempt to swap this all way, the senator clearly lied. Now, a colleague in the House is saying he`s lying again, although who to believe between the two is a tough one.\par \par \uc2 And the middle of what should be a highly contested re-election battle this year in Wisconsin. The senator and the staff had many more questions to answer about their participation in the coup attempt.\par \par \uc2 Tim Miller is a former communications director for Jeb Bush`s 2016 campaign, now writer-at-large at The Bulwark, author of Why We Did It: A Travelogue from the Republican Road to Hell, which is just out now. Tim, it`s good to have you here in the studio.\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, WRITER-AT-LARGE, THE BULWARK: Hi, Chris, good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, you know, you work for -- you worked for politicians, right? They`re spinning and then there`s lying. There`s like shading the truth. He just pretty clearly just flat lied, staff to staff. No knowledge of this, like that wasn`t true.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:01]\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Well, throughout this whole process, Ron Johnson has been lying. You know, at The Bulwark, we published an article by a Wisconsin Republican county chair Mark Becker back before January 6th, between the election and January 6th, where Becker called Senator Johnson and said he was disappointed in him for going along with the stop the steal nonsense.\par \par \uc2 And Johnson said to Becker on that phone call, I just have to do what I have to do, it`d be political suicide to go against the president on this. We`re just going to wait and see how it turns out and everything will be fine.\par \par \uc2 So, he was lying that whole time between the election and January 6th, he knew. I think that there is reason to believe that Johnson was more involved in this that he`s even letting on now. Not just because he lied, obviously, but because what you`re hearing from other people in Wisconsin, from staff members.\par \par \uc2 And in addition to that, we keep learning this Mike Lee, it`s the same story with Mike Lee in Utah.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, Mike Lee is also another one. I think what`s important here is like this plot, which is like specifically on the fake electors, and transmitting them that Johnson is implicated in is the subject of like, multiple search warrants being issued by the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 Like, the Department of Justice is really investigating this as a straightforward criminal matter.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: For sure. And it`s going to be hard to believe that some of the Johnson or other Republican Senate staffers even from the more establishment if that really exists anymore, side are going to be on the receiving end of some of these e-mails.\par \par \uc2 Look, here`s the thing with Johnson and this way, it`s all it`s always hard to tell with these sorts of senators, like, he`s obviously lying. But in the old Watergate thing, these are just not very bright guys and things got out of hand, right?\par \par \uc2 So, it is kind of hard to tell the extent to what --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, totally, yes. But I also think Johnson is an interesting case to me. So, you got this part of it. I don`t know if you followed his vaccine stuff. It`s been -- some of the most like, aggressively irresponsible and flatly dangerous stuff we`ve seen from any elected politician in the country.\par \par \uc2 I mean, it`s really uptick when you rank it. He posted this with COVID-19 panel focusing on vaccine skepticism, he was hugely on the hydroxychloroquine. Like really dangerous stuff.\par \par \uc2 And it`s like, this is a guy -- this is not the senator from the plus 30 Trump`s state.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: No.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: He`s from the state that Biden won. And he acts like a guy who`s just never going to have to face swing voters the rest of his life.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Now, look, he`s a prime example -- not to plug the book. But what I write about a little bit about this book is bottom up.\par \par \uc2 You know, Ron Johnson is dealing with a lot of Republican -- going into Republican chicken dinners, going to Republican events. And he`s -- all this stuff is bubbling up from the ground up. This is --\par \par \uc2 Ron Johnson, remember, when he first ran, he`s like a business guy who`s going to be a non-ideal -- non culture war kind of business Republican. He knows what is going to -- what is going to make these voters tick. And now the mob is in charge of him.\par \par \uc2 And so, that is what`s happening on hydroxychloroquine. He was going out there and some of the Russia disinformation stuff and then had to back away from that when it kind of seemed right at the beginning of the Ukraine crisis that maybe the base was going to be more on the \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  camp, then kind of ended up being.\par \par \uc2 And so, Johnson, I think, on conspiracy theory, after conspiracy theory has been following his own base, and he`s got a close election this fall. But I think that right now, he doesn`t -- for whatever reason, he doesn`t feel like he has to worry about that. I think that he`s irrationally confident because of his own chamber and because it`s worked out.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes and your point is well taken, which I read about in the book about the how demand driven is, right?\par \par \uc2 But I also think with the vaccine stuff, what became clear to me and again, I don`t know what his diet of consumption is but it was like, as you watch him hit this, it was like, yes, he was pandering. But it also seemed clear and clear to me that like, he was just reading terrible information.\par \par \uc2 Like, he had brain worms. And that was really concerning to me, because you`re watching this guy out there being like, you know, in the same way of like trumping like, well, the Italians did this, and it`s like, oh, no, you`re a U.S. senator. And you`re up there being like, shove that hydroxychloroquine down your throat.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yes, apart -- oh, he`s getting bad information. And part of that is brain worms, but let me tell you what`s another thing that`s driving it and I saw this time and time again, when I was talking to Republicans, he`s so mad at the media and at the left for going after him and this sort of things and he`s bitter in a great -- which is crazy. You think that this is crazy. You`re a United States senator, like, I bet you`re rich, like everything is turned up Ron Johnson, what do you -- what do you have to be aggrieved about? But he is. He doesn`t like to be criticized. He doesn`t like the way that he`s been criticized.\par \par \uc2 And so that when people give them information that`s like, here`s how you can make the elites and MSNBC host look dumb, then then he`s like, you`re speaking my language.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s the two components of the nuclear reactor of modern reactionary politics is like huge amounts of privilege and genuine sense of grievance and victimization. And you put them together, you get \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , you get all of them, that`s the thing at the core of the reactor. And Ron Johnson is very much that.\par \par \uc2 Tim Miller, whose new book is Why We Did It: A Travelogue from the Republican Road to Hell. Great to have you here.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When we come back, what happens in an abortion clinic when a statewide ban goes into effect with the undoing of Roe look like at the last abortion clinic in the entire state of Missouri, after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:54:35]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Ever since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, the states where abortion is still allowed keeps changing hour by hour as bans go into effect in some places and then courts put a hold on bans in other states. As far as we can figure out right now, here`s where things stand.\par \par \uc2 Abortion bans are in effect in seven states, Alabama, Arkansas, Kentucky, Missouri, Oklahoma and South Dakota. Those bans were triggered by the Supreme Court decision and also, Wisconsin where a pre-Roe ban is once again the law.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:02]\par \par \uc2 More bans some near total are expected at least 15 other states, including Texas, Ohio and South Carolina, all states which already ban abortions before most people even know they`re pregnant. And Mississippi of course where the Supreme Court just allowed a 15 week abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 Two other states Louisiana, Utah already have laws in place to ban abortions. But those laws were put on hold by local courts today, which means abortion care is still available in Louisiana and Utah for now.\par \par \uc2 But Friday`s unprecedented decision by the Supreme Court left countless other people across the country in desperate need, as abortion services just suddenly closed down.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Colleen McNicholas is an Ob Gyn in Missouri, which was one of the first states to ban abortion after the Supreme Court ruling. She`s also the chief medical officer of the Planned Parenthood Clinic in St. Louis, which was the very last abortion provider left in the state until abortion became illegal on Friday.\par \par \uc2 And Dr. McNicholas joins us now. Thank you so much, Doctor, I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 DR. COLLEEN MCNICHOLAS, OB GYN, MISSOURI: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: First of all, just tell us what the legal situation -- how you heard about it on Friday. And what those consultations with your lawyers were about what it meant.\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLAS: Well, the truth is, Chris, that we have been planning for this moment for many, many years. For those of us who are practicing in states like Missouri, and other restrictive states across the country, we knew this moment was coming.\par \par \uc2 You know, Missourians have been navigating hoop after hoop, restriction after restriction for years, and, you know, at least for the last couple of years, have been fleeing the state to get care in places where they can do that in a dignified way.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, ironically, we were sitting shoulder to shoulder with Secretary Becerra in our last abortion clinic in Missouri, where we were talking about what we expected the impact to be.\par \par \uc2 You know, it was a conversation with the secretary, with Congresswoman Bush, with patients who have had abortions with us and with us as providers. And so, it was quite surreal to be ending up that roundtable with a call from the back of the room that said the decision has dropped.\par \par \uc2 And before we could even leave the room to process that, we heard that our attorney general implemented our trigger ban. And so, within a matter of moments, Missourians lost complete access to abortion care.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What is the state of the law? Is it -- is it a total ban on abortions in the state of Missouri?\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLAS: It is a total ban with very narrow medical exceptions. And of course, as I`m sure you`ve heard from other physicians, you know, medical exemptions to abortion bans really mean nothing. It puts physicians in an incredibly difficult position to have to decide when a patient is sick enough to get lifesaving care.\par \par \uc2 And so, we`re really going to see a tremendous impact, I think on maternal mortality broadly, as more and more states ban abortion.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What are you doing now for your patients who you can no longer provide this care to?\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLAS: You know, well, our affiliate is fortunate and that we just 15 miles away from the St. Louis Clinic, we had opened in 2019, a facility in Southern Illinois, the Planned Parenthood in Fairview Heights.\par \par \uc2 And so, we have already been for the last few years helping our Missouri patients, those who could travel just across the river to get that care in Illinois.\par \par \uc2 We`ve partnered with the independent clinic in our area Hope Clinic, to make sure that as a region, we are not only addressing the fact that folks need to come to Illinois, but we`re also addressing what I think is the real critical component in the short term, which is making sure they can get from their home, whether that`s 50 miles away, or 1,500 miles away to our clinic, and then back home.\par \par \uc2 This navigation piece is going to be critical as we`re looking to mass mobilize tens of thousands of people for basic health care in this country.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Are patients worried? I know they`re worried but about their legal exposure? Are they -- are those conver -- are you having those conversations? I mean, I know I don`t think the Missouri law criminalizes women who seek abortions, but it just doesn`t seem that far away.\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLAS: Well, even if there are laws or statements or promises from prosecutors and legislators in states like Missouri, that say they`re not going to prosecute women. We have no reason to believe that they won`t.\par \par \uc2 In fact, we already have seen examples of this. Pregnant people being criminalized for pregnancy outcomes, miscarriages and or self-managed abortion.\par \par \uc2 You`re absolutely right, we should be expecting that next, whether it`s criminalizing patients who are having abortions or providers who are doing those procedures for them.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Dr. Colleen McNicholas there in St. Louis, thank you so much for taking a little time away from your very important work to talk to us.\par \par \uc2 MCNICHOLAS: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 [21:01:04]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Chris. Chris, I have to ask you are you going to be there tomorrow night when we do our primetime recap of tomorrow`s January 6th hearing?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I am going to be there. I was in Los Angeles for the last one. But I will absolutely be here sitting at the table right one seat away from you.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220628ei6r00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for June 26, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton \par \uc2 6,443 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, MSNBC HOST: Thanks for watching SYMONE on this Sunday. I`m Symone Sanders, you can always count on us to tell you the truth here and have candid conversations and trying to make it make sense. So, join us on MSNBC weekends at 4:00 p.m. Eastern, and anytime over on Peacock, where I`ve got new episodes on the MSNBC Hub every Monday and Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 And hit me up on social media, y`all. You could find highlights and exciting things in the works for the show on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , and TikTok. But right now, I want to hand things over to my good friend, Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 . Rev, I am in L.A. I hear you have Congresswoman \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  coming up on your show, she`s running for mayor. I cannot wait to watch.\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Yes. Well, I`m excited to have \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 , and thank you as always, my dear friend, Symone. Good evening, and welcome to POLITICSNATION. Tonight`s lead, "FEARING THE FUTURE."\par \par \uc2 Right now, abortion rights supporters are contemplating next steps after last week`s pivotal Supreme Court ruling overturning Roe V. Wade. The ripple effects of that decision extending beyond America`s streets, still filled with abortion rights supporters and opponents for the third day straight. As demand for abortion drugs skyrockets over fears they`ll lose soon and finding those drugs inaccessible to millions of Americans. As Red State abortion providers never going to stop new legal regime, putting many in immediate danger and jeopardy. And as several companies pledged resources and support to workers who may now have to travel to a dwindling number of states, where abortion is still legal.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the dominoes have already fallen in at least nine states that have already banned abortions since Friday`s ruling, while others are just waiting for their bans to take effect. America remade by Roe`s reversal. That`s POLITICSNATION tonight. Plus, another round of Congressional hearings on January 6th approaches after the nation eyes were turn back to Donald Trump`s pressure campaign on Georgia`s Secretary of State to overturn his 2020 election results. I`ll talk to the Democratic candidate running to take the job about protecting voters of color from suppression and the big lie ahead of November midterms.\par \par \uc2 But first, joining me now, Congresswoman \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 , Democrat of California, currently running for Mayor of Los Angeles. Congresswoman, let me start with -- as abortion rights supporters game out their response to last week`s ruling overturning Roe. Some advocacy groups are concerned that this was a beginning of a rollback on privacy rights overall, citing Justice Clarence Thomas`s comments, suggesting that same-sex marriage and contraception should be, quote, reconsidered, as the court -- by the court, as the potential next positions they can go for. Now, as you as the potentially the next mayor of one of the nation`s largest cities, how are you preparing strategically for more court decisions that may impact your constituents?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KAREN BASS\uc2  (D-CA): Well, you know, I am deeply concerned about Clarence Thomas`s statements. But just the whole way that this court seems to be ready to implement a Republican agenda, never seen it as partisan as this. And I do think we need to be concerned. We need to be concerned about Board of -- Brown versus Board of Education. I mean, we don`t know how far this court is going to go. Well, I`m fortunate that I`m in California, where we`re doing the exact opposite, we`re preparing for a ballot initiative to enshrine a woman`s right to choose within our State Constitution and backing it up with funding, so women from other states can come here.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that we can`t take anything for granted, even in the liberal State of California that this is an opportunity for us to really scale up our organizing and our education of the public on how central the courts are. You know, Rev, a lot of times I think Democrats haven`t really paid a lot of attention to the Judiciary. And clearly, the right-wing has decided that that`s the way they`re going to consolidate their power in the future, which is taking over the judiciary.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:06]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And a lot of times, I think we forget that we must really get on the ground and organize, including around judicial issues. I mean, you and I have worked together on many of these through years, but we`ve not seen everybody as focused. Now, Congresswoman, in addition to the Roe decision, there was last week`s other big news story from the Hill, the Senate`s bipartisan gun bill, signed into law yesterday by President Biden after passing in the House the day before. Now, you voted in favor. What does it do for public safety in your city, in your district?\par \par \uc2 BASS: Well, again, you know, in Los Angeles and in California, we have very tough laws. So, if it reinforces, you know, what we`ve already done, but I do think that we need so much more in terms of gun control. And how ironic is it? You know, the same day that we were dealing with this issue in Congress, the Supreme Court essentially just opens the door even more for more guns, for people to actually carry guns, for their you don`t need to have permits for concealed weapons. And so, on one day, the court is going to declare they are, quote, unquote, pro-life, and then the day before, they`re going to do something that compromises life on the heels of the mass shootings that we have almost on a daily basis.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congresswoman, the police reform efforts spearheaded by you and Senators Corry Booker and Tim Scott in the aftermath of George Floyd`s murder failed to move forward last year, now you`re running to preside over a city, where crime and public safety have emerged as dominant issues in November`s mayoral election. Given the failure to secure police reform at the Federal level, what will you do as mayor? And you came out leading in the numbers, the most votes, but you had the runoff, what will you do as mayor to deal with police reform and public safety at the same time?\par \par \uc2 BASS: Well, absolutely. And the two are not mutually exclusive, as we very well know. As a matter of fact, to the extent that your police department is accountable and transparent, I believe that increases public safety, especially in certain communities that have historically had negative relationships with police departments. And so, one of the things that we have to do here is we really have to deal with the accountability. So, a few years ago, a ballot initiative was passed that I think actually undermines accountability. It`s a citizen`s commission, ironically, but when an officer needs to be disciplined, the chief of police can even call for termination, and the citizen commission, what their track record has been over the last few years is they basically do not hold officers accountable at all. So, that`s one of the first things that needs to be looked at.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, because I believe that we should invest tremendous resources in prevention, in proven crime prevention, violence reduction strategies, that`s where I want to put a very large focus and a lot of that went dormant during the time of the pandemic, which I believe was one of the contributing factors to an increase in crime in the Los Angeles area. Double down, triple down on prevention and intervention.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congresswoman, before we lose you, I`ve not heard much from Conservatives cheering the Roe decision about increased resources for state foster care systems.\par \par \uc2 BASS: Right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I know you recently took dozens of former foster youth to visit Capitol Hill. My understanding being that they`ll be shadowing Congressional lawmakers, sharing their experiences. Is it a stretch to assume that these visits have taken on an added dimension in the aftermath of the court`s decision on Roe?\par \par \uc2 BASS: Well, yes, absolutely. Because you`re talking about our most vulnerable population, children in the child welfare system. And this is a tradition we`ve been doing for the last 10 years. And what we work with with the young people, is we tell them how to strategically tell their life story in a way that educates lawmakers, and we`ve been able to make significant changes in the foster care system, legislative changes. And you know, in our city, one of the issues that we`re dealing with is the fact that we have 50,000 people a night who are sleeping on our streets, who are homeless. And children and the child welfare system is a whole category of people who are sleeping on our streets.\par \par \uc2 When they turn 18 or 21, we just cut them off. And of course, after a few months, they wind up unhoused. So, what we do in Congress is work to change the system, so that they don`t fall into homelessness in the first place. That`s how we work with the young people, and we`ve been able to make significant legislative changes over the years.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:11]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you, Congresswoman \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 . Joining me now is Georgia State Representative Bee Nguyen, who is also running for Secretary of State in Georgia. Thank you for joining me, State Representative Nguyen. Let me ask you this, and again, thank you for joining us tonight, two decisions from the court, wide-ranging implications for the nation. And I know, I would imagine at least that they are certainly wide-ranging impact on your state.\par \par \uc2 The first being, of course, Roe`s reversal, affecting thousands of women, specifically those of color in your State of Georgia, where 65 percent of reported abortions in 2019 were obtained by black women, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2  then. You have also now the court decision overturning New York`s handgun law. Of course, you`re Republican Governor Brian Kemp recently signed the law, allowing concealed carry of handguns without a state weapons permit. Now, as an aspiring Secretary of State, how do you pushback once in office?\par \par \uc2 REP. BEE NGUYEN (D-GA): Thank you, Reverend, for having me on your show today. And I think those are two critically important issues in Georgia. And in the State of Georgia, now that Roe v. Wade is overturned, we are a state that is poised to outlaw abortion. We`ve got a six-week abortion ban that passed in 2019. We don`t have a trigger law, so that means it will go through the court system, and we will likely see in the next few months that law being upheld in the books. We are a state where black women have the highest mother mortality rate in the nation, where they are three to four times likely to die from childbirth, more than their counterparts. Where over half of our counties don`t have OB-GYNs, where our governor has refused to extend Medicaid, leaving over half a million Georgians without health insurance.\par \par \uc2 So, this will have real implications on the State of Georgia. That means more people will die. That means more black and brown women will die, and more poor women will die. And that`s why it`s critically important to understand how this is critically connected and interconnected to protecting the right to vote. That is going to be our one weapon that we can use to elect people who represent our values, to elect someone like \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  who would automatically expand Medicaid if she were elected to our Gubernatorial race. And that means putting into place a legislature that can block further restrictions when it comes to abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Representative, just about all of the candidates endorsed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have lost in your state`s recent primaries. Most notably, perhaps the former Senator David Perdue`s nearly 40-point loss despite Trump`s support. Do you think the former president`s attempt to manipulate the 2020 election results in your state illustrated in last week`s Congressional hearing on January 6th, played a role in the losses with your state`s Republican voters?\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Well, we saw in 2020 that Georgians rejected the former president. And we saw in 2021 that Georgians rejected the two candidates that were endorsed by the former president. And we see that in a state like Georgia where we are a battleground states and the margins are so close, the people did speak. The people said, we reject that kind of radicalism. Where we need to do our jobs as Democrats is to remind the people in the State of Georgia that our current governor is a conservative governor who also supports the ban to abortion, who went to legislative session last year and expanded the right to carry firearms, even in the wake of the mass shootings with three bills that expands access in a legislature that has continued to chop away our voting rights. And so, our job is to remind Georgians that these candidates in Georgia, who are serving currently, they may not have been endorsed by Trump, but they certainly support him and they certainly support his -- parts of his agenda.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Representative, briefly, we`re out of time, but again, congratulations on your winning your democratic primary runoff. But you have to face now sitting Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  in November. Doing so, the first Asian-American woman to be a nominee for a statewide office in Georgia, why has Georgia emerged as such a battleground state in your view?\par \par \uc2 [17:15:01]\par \par \uc2 NGUYEN: Georgia is a place that is home to the civil rights movement. We have a very powerful organizing infrastructure of Georgians who have remained on the ground. When national media wasn`t paying attention to, as we were doing the work here in Georgia and our organizers have remained in Georgia, we know that we have faced uphill battles in the State of Georgia, but also in a place like Georgia, we are home to powerful civil rights leaders, who have required us and asked us and empowered us to carry on their legacy. So, we are a battleground state with a growing minority population. And the people in the State of Georgia want something different, and we have shown that in 2020, and we`ve shown that in 2021, and we will show that in 2022.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Alrighty, Ms. Nguyen, thank you for joining me. This week, the Supreme Court opted to take the power to control their bodies from millions of women. Today, I`ll explain how we can begin to fight for some of that power back. Then later, a critical call, \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders just made at their annual summit and how it might impact American voters come midterms. But first, my colleague, Richard Lui for today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC ANCHOR: Rev, a very good Sunday to you. Some of the stories we`re following, missiles hitting Ukraine`s capital for the first time in weeks. President Zelenskyy saying, the war is becoming tough to handle emotionally. The \uc2 U.K. Ministry of Defence\uc2  says Russia has now moved significantly closer to full control of the Donbas area with a likely capture of a key city there. An off-duty Providence Rhode Island police officer and State Senate Candidate was arrested and charged after this scene of his alleged assault against democratic candidate, Jennifer Rourke. She posted this video on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . Rhode Island Police say the officer was placed on administrative leave without pay, pending a review and criminal investigation.\par \par \uc2 And the Coast Guard rescued seven people Saturday. Lightning striking their 39-foot personal boat 100 miles offshore of Clearwater, Florida. The Coast Guard saying a helicopter aircrew hoisted five women and two men without medical concerns. More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:20:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: My rise up today is short and to the point. About the two dramatic and major Supreme Court decisions that came down this week, the decision on carrying a gun in New York is devastating and potentially dangerous. This ruling could not have come at a worse time. As we in the civil rights community have been working to de-escalate gun violence in the city, it has never been more important to Congress and for Congress to pass meaningful legislation to combat the epidemic that is gun violence. Not just New York but throughout this nation. And the Supreme Court`s decision overturning Roe versus Wade is a blatant attempt to bring us back to the dark ages.\par \par \uc2 It will disproportionately impact black women and poor women. It will only lead to unsafe, clandestine abortions and force women to carry unwanted pregnancies to term. This must be resisted aggressively. States must enact laws to protect women. I`m not just saying this as a civil rights leader as president of National Action Network. I`m saying this as a loyal, concerned citizen of this great country. And you know the ability to stay involved for so many years in social action stem from hope, the hope that things could be better, that we deserve better.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, moments like this week can break the mind because they show us that nothing is taken for granted, that even in a struggle, we have won an achievement that is already ours, can be instantly taken away from us. This is a moment that raises the concern. Will the struggle continue forever? The last few days I`ve been hearing one simple question, Reverend Al, what can we do? And my answer is, do something, do anything, don`t not do. You can be angry, sad, annoyed, or upset, but remember, those in favor of these rulings, don`t care if you are upset. In fact, they may be happy that you are.\par \par \uc2 What they don`t want you to do is mobilize. And so, find one thing to do, like the thousands who demonstrated across the country for the last 48 hours, or write a letter, raise money, register a friend to vote, study political candidates before you decide who to vote for. Maybe even consider running for office yourself. Join an organization, but rise up, rise up from your sofa, and do something. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Let`s dive a little bit deeper into the top topics of the day. Joining me now is New York Times Columnist Michelle Goldberg and former Republican speech writer, Elise Jordan. Elise, with these dramatic seismic decisions this past week, abortions and guns, was is it that Ketanji -- Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , walking into when she joins the bench? I mean, what impact can she have, if any? Let me go to break. We have some technical difficulties. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:35:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. We`re having some tech issues, so bear with us. Joining me now is New York Times Columnist Michelle Goldberg. Michelle, even before this week`s Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe, in one of your very well-written op-eds, you point out that, yes, most Americans believe abortion should be legal. And a recent Gallup poll showed that pro-choice sentiment is at a near record high. Yet, you say, after four years of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , more than two years of a pandemic and an unending right-wing onslaught, a lot of people with feminine sympathies are numb and exhausted. Please explain.\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, COLUMNIST FOR NEW YORK TIMES: Well, I -- you know, and I think it`s possible that this can change in the aftermath of this ruling. I can tell that it hit a lot of people hard, even though everybody knew that it was coming. And I note I think you saw this after the Dobbs` decision was leaked. There were people in the streets, but not in anywhere near the numbers of people that were on the streets after, say, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was elected or after George Floyd was killed. And when I talk to people, what I heard was both, that there was a tremendous amount of burnout, a lot of the major reproductive rights organizations have been in disarray and kind of racked by staff turmoil. And among some younger women, there`s a real disaffection with feminism after a few years in which feminism was really vibrant and kind of cool, for a lack of a better word.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Elise, with these dramatic seismic decisions this week, abortions and guns, what I was asking you before we had that little hiccup, is what is Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  walking into when she joins the bushing (PH), the bench and what impact can she have, if any?\par \par \uc2 ELISE JORDAN, FORMER REPUBLICAN SPEECH WRITER: Well, then she still will be one of nine Supreme Court Justices, but she`ll be one of three liberal justices. And you see in all of the liberal wing now going forward, will be exclusively female, which is quite a shift, historically, and it is historic in its own right.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:05]\par \par \uc2 But, you know, right now, there`s so much of a Republican overload on the Supreme Court. That it`s going to be difficult for the liberal justices to push their opinions forward.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Staying with you, Elise, President Biden signed the bipartisan Gun Safety Bill that passed both Houses of Congress this week. I want to ask you, what do you say to those who say this is too little? But first, I`ll tell you my take, I certainly think we need to ban all military style and assault weapons. I certainly think we should have background checks for everyone. But I also think that you have to do -- you have to start somewhere. This is a basement, not the roof as you`re starting to build a house for gun reform. What do you say, Elise?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: You know what, Rev, I was actually so interested to come on and hear your take, as someone who has worked for this legislation for so many years. And, you know, it might not -- it might disappoint a lot of the gun safety advocates who wanted more and they wanted bigger, and they wanted bolder, but at the end of the day, it represents 14 Republican Senators crossing party lines and saying, there is a problem. It is the most sweeping legislation in 30 years.\par \par \uc2 And while I would like to see high-capacity magazines banned, that, you know, caused so much of the killing when a gun is in the classroom, I do think that it`s a start of progress, and it`s admitting that we have a problem. And it`s going to be a longer background check on younger candidates for a gun. I think that that`s a start. I think that the mental health funding is great, just -- not just for in this area, but just overall, America needs more capacity to handle mental health in the country. So, I think that this has to happen incrementally, but you do have Republicans admitting there`s a problem, and that`s something that can be built upon going forward.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, it can be a start if we keep pushing. If we just stop here, then it becomes a major disappointment. Michelle, how do you explain that this week, we saw the Supreme Court ruled on guns and the Supreme Court doesn`t do Second Amendment cases very often. And then, Congress passed major gun legislation. And that, too, does not happen very often. And both Supreme Court decisions, both guns and abortion, pretty much against public opinion.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Well, look, the Supreme Court is not necessarily supposed to follow public opinion. Although these two decisions back-to-back, I think, underlie the fact the degree to which the Supreme Court has been an adjunct of this rapidly tightening, rapidly escalating crisis in this country of minority rule, where you have, again and again, presidents who are elected without the popular vote. First, \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  and then \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who were able to put people on the Supreme Court who are going to further subvert the will of the majority, further impose kind of very unpopular sectarian vision of, you know, what American freedom is supposed to look like on unwilling states.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, again, I think there`s such an irony about how, you know, as many people have said, you`re safer as a gun in this country than as a pregnant -- you know, that the guns in this country have more rights than women at this point. And I think it`s going to reach a crisis point, the more that you have a Supreme Court that makes any kinds of progressive legislation impossible, we still have a big environmental case to come, and imposes a very narrow, Christian nationalists` agenda on many, many people who don`t want to live under it.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, Michelle, I know you follow closely the January 6th hearings, and in fact, you have wrote that they have been so much better than you expected. And there are reports that former President Trump is also watching those hearings on T.V. and that he is furious that House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy decided to keep pro-Trump Republicans off the January 6th Committee. And that by that not putting anyone on to defend him, was that a bad tactical move on the Republican side?\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Yeah, I think it`s pretty clear at this point that it was a catastrophic move. And it`s not just that there is no one there to defend Trump, it`s that the entire success of these hearings is that because there are no pro-Trump Republicans on the panel, they have been able to avoid the really numbing, deadening back and forth droning on and kind of a deliberate point missing that you usually see in these bipartisan hearings, where you have senators who are -- I mean, sorry -- where you have members of the Congress who are grandstanding, who are trying to make a point, but aren`t necessarily trying to get to the truth.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:11]\par \par \uc2 Instead, this is unfolded like a \uc2 Netflix\uc2  docu-drama, right? This is unfolded with just such narrative precision and power. And enough that I think, you know, there aren`t a lot of minds in this country that could be changed, but I think they probably are around the edges.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Elise, we`re out of time, but briefly, NBC is reporting that President Biden and \uc2 G7\uc2  leaders have agreed to import -- to an import ban on Russian gold. The President is in Germany for the annual three-day summit. How much do you think that anything related to foreign affairs, Ukraine, China, Saudi Arabia, Iran, how much do you think they will play in the coming midterms, especially with the economic situation that we are at right now here at home?\par \par \uc2 JORDAN: You`re not much, frankly, unless there is something huge that happens in October, and October surprise of sorts. And if gas prices continue to rise, and inflation continues, then that`s really, you know, what is going to matter the most politically to American voters this upcoming midterm cycle.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Alrighty, Elise Jordan and Michelle Goldberg, thank you both very much. After the break, how community activists are adjusting their midterm tactics in response to the Supreme Court`s reversal on Roe versus Wade. You`re watching POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:45:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Protests continue to reverberate all over the country in reaction to Friday`s Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe versus Wade, consequentially reversing a nearly 50-year-old Constitutional right. Joining me now is \uc2 Deval Patrick\uc2 , former Massachusetts Governor and current co-chair of American Bridge 21st Century, and Shakya Cherry-Donaldson, executive director of 1000 Women Strong. Thank you both for joining me. Governor Patrick --\par \par \uc2 DEVAL PATRICK, CO-CHAIR OF AMERICAN BRIDGE 21ST CENTURY: Thank you, Reverend Al, for having me.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Thank you. And let me go to you first. With midterm elections just a few months away, Democrats are hoping to turn the anger and pain many are feeling following the reversal of Roe into a rallying cry for their base, warning that other freedoms could also be at stake. This also comes at a time when President Biden`s approval rating continue to fall for a fourth straight week, tying at a new low, according to a new Reuters/\uc2 Ipsos\uc2  poll published last week. Now, you have past experience with campaigns and now through the work in your organization, you also deal with grassroots public engagement, during the time when many are losing faith in democratic leadership, in what ways can people be mobilized to turn out to vote this November?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: So, first of all, Reverend Al, thank you for the question, thank you for having Shakya on, who is a terrific representative of the kinds of existing hubs (PH) close to people in neighborhoods and in communities, that I think more of us need to be trying to support. Look, more and more of what comes out of D.C. is unconnected to the lives that most of the rest of us live. And the Supreme Court has just joined that in -- as another example of that in particular in this decision, the same was true in the -- in the gun decision the day before. It`s leaving aside the really bad lawyering, the really bad jurisprudence that comes from Alito`s decision. It just doesn`t deal with the fact that, number one, poor people, overwhelmingly black and brown women and their families, can`t just hop in the car or hop on a plane or train and go to some place where a women`s full medical and health needs can be met, and aren`t banned by state law.\par \par \uc2 But secondly, it`s the totally cynical notion to say, well, these issues really should really be decided by state legislatures when this is the same court that has looked the other way when it comes to the hyper partisan gerrymandering and the vote suppression, the purging, and other ways in which Republicans, in particular, have engineered the outcome. So, I think people need to show up and overwhelm those barriers, they need to make a real stand this cycle and in every of the next several cycles, for this notion that the government really belongs to us, it should reflect the best of us, of all of us, and the best of who we are. And that means showing up, registering, and bringing others along to register and to vote in November.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Shakya, I want to ask you the same from your perspective as executive director of 1000 Women Strong, an organization dedicated to organizing around the issue and the issues that impact black women. In what ways can black women be mobilized to turn out this vote this December -- this November, rather?\par \par \uc2 SHAKYA CHERRY-DONALDSON, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR OF 1000 WOMEN STRONG: Thank you for the question, and it`s my pleasure to be here with you tonight, Reverend Al, as well as you, Governor Patrick. Since Friday, this has really been a weekend of mourning and acknowledging the huge stakes that have really come into play for this November and beyond with this election.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:08]\par \par \uc2 But the reality of it is that 1000 Women Strong and groups like us have been on the ground 365 every day, talking to voters, supporters and particularly to voters about what the stakes are for this election. So, since Friday, we have mourned the decision and recognized that the stakes are too high to let up now. So, us in coalition with thousands of other groups are committed to speaking to voters and mobilizing folks to the polls. And black women particularly as the core crux and base of the Democratic Party, it is important that not only to be show up and show out, but we are a crucial in creating what the future leadership of this country looks like, and policy to be back, systematic oppression, racism, that we`re still seeing. The legacy of this country, and honesty is the legacy of this decision that was made on Friday.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Back to you, Governor, in a concurring opinion to the Supreme Court ruling to overturn Roe, Conservative Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  called on the court on Friday to overrule a past landmark decisions that legalized the right to obtain contraception, the right to same sex marriage, and same-sex intimacy, now actively concerning that overturning Roe V. Wade will pave the way for other reversals. What should Americans expect out of this court?\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: Well, first of all, they shouldn`t be surprised at those -- at the concurring opinion of Justice Thomas, it`s not just his view. That has been the objective of the hard right in the Republican Party, of the so-called movement of the Republicans for a long time. You noticed that he stopped short of calling for the overturn of Loving versus Virginia, which was the case in the 60s that permitted -- or prohibited states from outlawing interracial marriage. On the same grounds, by the way, the same constitutional jurisprudence that is the foundation or was the foundation for Roe versus Wade. Look, the important thing I think for --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yeah, I`m glad you said that, because when we raised (INAUDIBLE) we`re not picking on him. It`s the same ground that he very clearly is saying one thing here, and avoiding dealing with what he has been able to have the right to do.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: That`s right. And I am not picking on him, I am saying that this whole notion that there is embedded in the Constitution, a fear, if you will, of personal liberty, that there are certain things the government does not and should not intrude on. And when it comes to the most intimate decisions that we make in our lives, that is exactly where governments should not be. And if that isn`t what the Constitution stands for, I`m not sure what else it does. And here`s the point, the decision on Friday, overturning Roe, was not whether anybody believes that abortion is right or wrong. That`s not it. It`s about who gets to decide.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That`s that.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: And when it comes to personal liberties, right, we don`t want any government, Federal or State, telling us how to make choices in our lives.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You`re right to choose.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: And that is what this decision opened up, and that`s what we`ve got to be focused on -- in, as we come up to November and beyond, for exactly the reasons that Shakya said.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, I`m going to -- I`m out of time. Shakya, we certainly going to have you back. Love to deal with people that`s on the ground. Governor \uc2 Deval Patrick\uc2 , thank you.\par \par \uc2 PATRICK: Amen.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And again, Shakya Cherry-Donaldson, thank you for all the work you both do. Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Darius Lee, a 21-year-old young man, was what we want to see in our community. He excelled at the St. Raymond`s High School in the Bronx, went on to even further being a basketball star with full scholarship at the \uc2 Houston Baptist University\uc2 . Tomorrow, I will join Reverend Michael Walrond, the pastor of First Corinthians Baptist Church in Harlem to funeralize Darius. Darius had come home, it was Father`s Day, it was June 19th. And he decided to go, as many in the neighborhood in Harlem, to some barbecue social event. Some gang-related activities some police are relating, the anger -- something broke out that had nothing to do with him and his friend. And nine people were shot and Darius died. Yesterday, his mother and family joined me at National Action Network, because we wanted to pledge our support for this family.\par \par \uc2 It is something that keeps happening, and it keeps happening, yes, because we need to deal with the conduct and behavior of some of the young people. Yes, it keeps happening because we need to deal with things if it turns out gang-related, but let`s get to the real primary point that keeps happening, easy access to guns. Young people, whether they want to be gangbangers or not, can`t shoot guns they can`t get. And we must stop the distribution of guns, we must stop the accessibility of guns, we must protect the Darius Lees of our community. All kids are not gangbanging, but all kids are at risk. And in the name of Darius Lee and others around this country, we`ve got to stop this.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5:00 p.m. Eastern. "AMERICAN VOICES" with Alicia Menendez, live from the Pride March in downtown Manhattan, I might add, that`s where my friend is right now, she starts right now.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:12]\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Missing you in studio. Thank you so much, Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220627ei6q00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION June 25, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui, Rick Tyler \par \uc2 7,048 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, MSNBC HOST: Read the concurring opinion, you all, it is what he said after the decision came down.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me say this: Vice Presidents Harris said, "The great aspiration of our nation has been to expand freedom, but the expansion of freedom clearly is not inevitable and it is clearly not something that just happens, not unless we defend our most fundamental principles."\par \par \uc2 We have to defend our most fundamental principles, you all. It is necessary if we want to continue to move forward.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for watching this edition of "Symone" on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 I`ll be back tomorrow. I`m Symone Sanders and right now, I will hand things over to my good friend, Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [17:00:46]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Thank you, Symone and good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: The focus on \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Right now, the conservative members of the Supreme Court are signaling that this Court will not be moved by the times in which we live, but driven by history, regardless of how distant.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday as expected, the Court issued its five-four conservative majority decision to overturn Roe versus Wade, in doing so upending 50 years of Federal protection for abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 In response, this weekend, our cities are erupting in protest. Here in New York, in Los Angeles, Atlanta, and in Washington where abortion rights supporters and opponents have surrounded the Supreme Court for the second day.\par \par \uc2 But also protesting are women in more than a dozen red states cleared by the decision to enact trigger laws banning abortion, six of which having already done so in the days since the decision, three others set to join them in the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 While gun advocates in those states and elsewhere are hailing another of the Court`s decision this week, to strike down the New York state`s law restricting concealed carry of handguns, potentially widening gun access nationwide and with it, gun violence.\par \par \uc2 Just as President Biden has signed the most significant gun control bill in a generation a day after its passage in Congress, in a rare moment of bipartisan compromise, coming after weeks of horrific mass shootings, including the tragedies in Buffalo and Uvalde. And yet, for all of that, some have already suggested that the Court`s decision on guns this week may cancel out whatever progress even this breakthrough represents, the impact of a historic week of decisions from our nation`s highest bench. That`s POLITICSNATION tonight.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Governor, first, our thanks for joining us tonight and certainly, on this weekend, obviously, I want to get to the Supreme Court decision this week overturning the state`s ban on concealed carry for handguns. But I have to begin, of course, with the Court`s decision yesterday as expected to overturn Roe versus Wade, and Federal abortion rights with it.\par \par \uc2 Now we`ve had abortion rights protests in the streets here in New York and elsewhere in the state, which you have pledged will continue to be a safe harbor for women seeking abortions, including those coming in from out of state.\par \par \uc2 But that presumes there won`t be potentially a national abortion ban attempted by Republicans, if they should regain control of Congress and the White House as some top Republicans have alluded to as being possible if not called for outright.\par \par \uc2 What is your response, Governor?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Well, Reverend Al, thank you for allowing me to talk about something that is hitting in my home state, New York State, your state so deeply.\par \par \uc2 And I was at those protests last night at Union Square, I was doing some interviews and I saw the anger and the frustration in these young women`s faces, and I said, I have to be out there with them and to give them some sense of consolation that here in the State of New York, there is no way in hell that we are going to go backwards and take away something that my mother`s generation fought for, I fought for, now, my daughter`s generation has to fight for. And I sure as heck didn`t think that my granddaughters` generation have to fight for as well.\par \par \uc2 So we`re on the frontlines, but as far as what we can do, Reverend Al, we already steps as soon as we heard about the leaked decision when we knew this was coming and we allocated over $35 billion to really beef up our abortion services, protect the providers make sure that there`s plenty of ability and capacity to take care of not just New York women, but as you said, that New York can be a beacon of hope, a safe harbor for people to come from elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:25]\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: But the specter you talk about, a nationwide ban? Well, that is more frightening than anything we can imagine and we have to be ready to deal with that in states like New York, and places that people have taken these rights for granted, and now, we`re prepared to take on that fight.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And you are saying right now on this show with people watching across the country that New York will remain a safe harbor for people from other states, as long as we do not have this national ban.\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Well, as long as I`m Governor, I can guarantee you that, that is for sure. And also, yes, that could be a scenario where if we have a change in the White House, a change in the House and the change in the Senate, and you know how that happens? With apathy, if people become complacent.\par \par \uc2 But I think the reverse has just happened. They don`t know what they`ve unleashed. And at the polls, and I`m not saying that`s their only solution, but it is the place of power. And in fact, just Monday this week, in the State of New York, we enacted nation leading reforms to make sure that the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Voting Rights Act of New York took place, I signed it into law, and make sure that everyone has access to be able to exercise their free right to vote in the event that we start seeing changes in other states where we could tip the majority from Democrats to Republican.\par \par \uc2 I served in Congress in the Republican minority and all they wanted to do was undo the work that our great President \uc2 Obama\uc2  did on healthcare. So I know what can happen if we lose control.\par \par \uc2 People should be not just marching in the streets, marching to the polls as soon as they can and mobilizing people. Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, while abortion rights advocates are disappointed across the country, President Biden earlier today signed the bipartisan gun safety bill that passed both Houses of Congress this week, after weeks of delicate negotiations in the aftermath of the shootings -- the mass shootings in Buffalo and Uvalde. Here is what he said on the occasion.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The number of people killed every day in the streets, their message to us was to do something. How many times have we heard that? Just do something.\par \par \uc2 For God`s sake, just do something. Well, today, we did.\par \par \uc2 While this bill doesn`t do everything I want, it does include actions I`ve long call for that are going to save lives.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, before we get to the Supreme Court`s ruling on New York`s handgun laws and your administration`s response to it, I wonder what does this gun bill do for New York that isn`t currently on the books at the state level, Governor?\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Well, we already have laws in place like the Red Flag Law, I just signed legislation to beef it up after the massacre in Buffalo. And Reverend Al, you and I were there, burying one of the mothers or one of the individual`s grandmothers who lost her life just grocery shopping, so we went back right away in New York State, beefed up our laws, we banned AR- 15s from teenagers, which was the law in the land that they could have them when they`re 18 years old. We did a lot of changes.\par \par \uc2 But also, what the Federal law does, and I thank President Biden for his leadership. You know, he`s absolutely right. People begged us, the victim`s family said do something and something has been done.\par \par \uc2 What we`re going to get is more resources to states to help us fund these programs, the Red Flag Law. I need more resources to be able to get the message out, how it works, more training. So I`m welcoming the resources from the Federal government to help us make sure that the laws that they`re doing in Washington have a difference.\par \par \uc2 Is it all the way -- are we done? No, of course not, we`re done. We`re never done. You know, the march toward justice, as you know, from your life`s experience is a long journey, but at least we can do something and say progress has at least started.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, and we want a lot more, but we have to start somewhere and this is, unfortunately, the first one we`ve seen in a generation. Certainly, we want a lot more, but we have to start.\par \par \uc2 Governor, I know you`re about to convene this special legislative session next week in response to the Court`s decision this week in our state`s Handgun Law, restricting concealed carry. What can you tell us about how State Democrats are planning to push back against the ruling?\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Yes, and I`m glad you said State Democrats, it reminds me that there are 36 Governor`s races up this year. The power base is shifting now to the State Legislatures and we need to elect more Democrats in those Houses, Governors and legislators. So I just want to make that point as well.\par \par \uc2 The reason I had to call back the legislature because otherwise they wouldn`t be back until January and there was a sense of urgency that we don`t allow people to, at random, just have a concealed weapon going on our subways, going into festivals -- I just left the Harlem Pride Festival a short time ago, I can`t imagine people be able to carry a concealed weapon.\par \par \uc2 So what we`re going to do, we read the decision carefully, we knew it might come and what I want the legislature to do is identify the sensitive places where concealed carry guns cannot be used.\par \par \uc2 In fact, the decision already said we cannot, for example, ban concealed weapons from the entire island of Manhattan, we get that, but we are going to go right up to the line, be as restrictive as we believe necessary to protect our citizens from further gun violence, pack their our own guns and be able to handle whatever situation comes their way.\par \par \uc2 Look at the statistics: New York State, random violent acts resulting in death, five per 100,000. The states where they have stricter or less restrictive access to guns where they can walk around with them, it is 25 per 100,000.\par \par \uc2 So New York State with our laws has made ours place safer. Respecting legal rights, but also saying, "I have a moral responsibility as a leader of this state to protect our citizens." So we`re going to have some very serious legislation enacted just in a matter of days, and I`m going to be proud of that. But again, the journey is not over yet. We have so much more to protect our citizens.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: No, it is a lot to do, even this morning at National Action Network`s weekly rally, we had the family there of Darius Lee, young college basketball star just happened to be at a gathering on Father`s Day, and was shot and killed. We are funeralizing him on Monday.\par \par \uc2 This is serious, and we need to take it seriously.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for your time, Governor Hochul of New York.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congresswoman \uc2 Nikema Williams\uc2 , Democrat of Georgia. Before running for elected office, she served as Vice President for Public Policy at Planned Parenthood Southeast.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, I`d like to start first, with your previous administrative experience at Planned Parenthood. You are also a member of the Pro-Choice Caucus.\par \par \uc2 Right now most of our major cities have seen some kind of protest, the epicenter, of course, being the streets around the Supreme Court. And I look at this moment with Civil Rights in mind, wondering how women`s health organizations like Planned Parenthood, push back not just against a Court decision, but against elected officials that agree with the Court`s decision and have teased expanding it.\par \par \uc2 Your response, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 REP. NIKEMA WILLIAMS (D-GA): So first, Reverend Al, thank you for having this conversation because I know that you know better than most how intersectional all of these issues are.\par \par \uc2 I am right here in Atlanta today, but I was at the Supreme Court yesterday, because people needed to know and hear that there were members in the body of the House of Representatives standing with them. Not everybody agree with this decision of the Supreme Court. I serve in the first majority -- the largest Pro-Choice Caucus ever in the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  and this is the majority of pro-choice members.\par \par \uc2 So we`ve already passed the Women`s Health Protection Act, but Reverend Al, what we know is that our issues are so intersectional. I serve in the seat that was once held by Congressman \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 , and so when I think about how we`ve been fighting to make sure that we have voting rights in place, it is because of things like this.\par \par \uc2 So I tell people all the time, whether you care about the climate, housing, reproductive freedom and justice, no matter what issue you care about, it is a link back to our right to free and fair access to the ballot.\par \par \uc2 So we still have so much work to do. This is the work that I`ve fought on the frontlines for 10 years, Reverend Al. And you know, I`m not that old. I`m a seasoned in my work, but not that old.\par \par \uc2 And so 10 years at one organization with Planned Parenthood Southeast, where I was on the frontlines in Alabama, Georgia, and Mississippi, so I am very much aware of the people that this will impact on the ground and we know that it disproportionately impacts Black and Brown women first.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, and one of the things I don`t think discussed enough is this also sets a precedent for states` rights, something the whole Civil Rights movement that I grew up in and I`m still on the frontlines of and you come -- you`re seasoned, but a lot younger than me, all of us can know from experience in history, that states` rights is always a threat to every -- a threat everybody`s rights.\par \par \uc2 And as you know, not all states in which abortion is legal publish racial data, but the latest numbers from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control\uc2 , compiling data from 29 states that did report racial data in 2019 found that 38 percent of women who obtained abortions in 2019 were Black, 33 percent White; 21 percent Latina. Again those not numbers from states that reported to the CDC.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:02]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: With that in mind, what is the conversation like among Black lawmakers, women and men in Congress right now? And what you as the Whip of the Democratic Women`s Caucus, what can you tell us is the conversation?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So one of the things that we know, Reverend Al, that I know from my experience on the ground with Planned Parenthood is only those public abortion providers have to report statistics to the State of Georgia. So we know that those numbers are skewed, because those people who have means and access are going to their private doctors.\par \par \uc2 So we know that one in four women regardless of their race, they will seek abortion care before they`re 45 years old. So when Republicans talk about overturning Roe and are gloating in what just happened, we know that some people in their family have sought abortion care, one in four women. So all they have to do is look around the dinner table.\par \par \uc2 But in Congress what we`re doing, especially as a Black woman in Congress, knowing how this disproportionately impacts us, I was proud to join with my colleagues led by Congresswoman \uc2 Ayanna Pressley\uc2  and Barbara Lee in writing a letter to the President, because this is a state of emergency for Black women, where we are already at number 50 in Georgia for maternal mortality.\par \par \uc2 Reverend Al, more Black women die within one year of childbirth in Georgia than in Third World countries, so that`s a crisis and we know that we still haven`t expanded Medicaid. So people still don`t have the healthcare that they need. But yet, we are forcing people to have politicians make personal private medical decisions for them, and not letting the doctors lead with the medicine that they know is best for their patients.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congresswoman, while there is time, talking to the Governor earlier about \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 , your mentor and predecessor, as you said, in your very district, obviously, I can only imagine what he would be thinking about this week`s Court decisions from the Court. But I can`t imagine that he would be in favor of a federally protected right being overturned after 50 years in the case of Roe.\par \par \uc2 What are your thoughts? You succeeded Congressman Lewis and knew him well?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Well, Reverend, I don`t have to wonder what \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  would have thought because I have been in a march with Congressman Lewis, when I was at Planned Parenthood at the State Capitol here in Georgia, where we marched around at the State Capitol, because there were abortion restrictions on the books coming up in the legislature, and Congressman Lewis led the rally here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 So I know that he wants us to find a way to get in the way. We know that this fight is far from over and it didn`t happen overnight and we`re not going to fix it overnight.\par \par \uc2 We have to make sure that right now, we`re getting people to the states where they can get the care that they need, so that people are not without access, but we know that we have to keep fighting. We have to continue to make sure that we`re pushing for it because this fight is not over.\par \par \uc2 We can`t back down and he told us that it`s never the wrong time to do the right thing and we`ve got to find a way to get in the way.\par \par \uc2 So this is our generation`s movement and we have to find a way to get in the way and make that good trouble, and I`m ready Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Good trouble. Congresswoman \uc2 Nikema Williams\uc2 , thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 After the break, a Texas Republican`s dream of succession, the citizens of their state are struggling under current leadership and they`re not the only ones. That`s this week`s "Got-cha."\par \par \uc2 And later, after the Supreme Court did away with abortion rights for millions of American women this week, my political panel will join me to discuss which party could be more energized by the decision ahead of midterms.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague Richard Lui in Washington, DC -- I`ll tell you why later -- with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, a very good Sunday to you.\par \par \uc2 Stories we are watching for you this hour, today in Washington, thousands rallied on the National Mall for the first ever Unity March led by the Asian-American community, partners included the LGBTQ+, Muslim, disability, Black, indigenous and Pacific Islander, Latino, and Arab-American communities. Issues included, socioeconomic, cultural, and racial equity.\par \par \uc2 A suspect killed two people and injured 14 in Oslo, Norway early Saturday morning. Police say they are investigating it as a terrorist attack. The shooting took place at the London Pub, a nightclub popular with the city`s LGBTQ community.\par \par \uc2 A Norwegian citizen of Iranian origin was arrested there. Norwegian authorities upgraded the threat of a terrorist attack to its highest level and canceled multiple Gay Pride events there.\par \par \uc2 And a Federal Appeals Court put the FDA`s new ban on sales of \uc2 Juul\uc2  e- cigarettes on hold while it looks into Juul`s request for an emergency review. This comes after the \uc2 FDA\uc2  said Thursday \uc2 Juul\uc2  failed to show the sale of its products would be up appropriate for public health.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:24:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As the January 6 Committee laid out more damning testimony this week, many Republicans were less concerned about the newly exposed threats to democracy than they were about the Democrats` priorities.\par \par \uc2 Here is what House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): It`s a political committee and the one thing I do know and the American public knows, gas has gone up $1.86 a gallon since the day \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  announced that Committee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Then there`s Texas Senator Ted Cruz who issued this tweet saying: "Inflation has gone up 8.6 percent since last May. President Biden and the Democrats` policies are failing the American people."\par \par \uc2 There is no doubt the economy is important, and while I would argue that we can handle more than one issue at a time, I could certainly understand that if Republicans wanted to make pocketbook politics a priority, but that`s not what happened when the Republicans in the country`s second most populous state met last weekend.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:25]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The Texas GOP came together to approve a platform that wasn`t looking ahead at how to strengthen the economy. Instead, it fixated on the past, rehashing Trump`s Big Lie, and saying President Biden was not legitimately elected.\par \par \uc2 There was no mention of inflation in the document, but plenty of ink spilled describing homosexuality as an "abnormal lifestyle choice." The platform also rejects any efforts to restrict gun ownership just weeks after a school shooting in Uvalde, Texas, left 19 students dead and two teachers also died, and it insist falsely that Texas has a legal right to succeed.\par \par \uc2 But while Texas Republicans are fantasizing about a Civil War reboot, their state has modern-day problems. The state rates 43rd -- 43rd -- in the nation and educational attainment according to a WalletHub study. The Texas power grid, which failed last winter remains vulnerable with one State Senator warning, there is no guarantee this summer`s record demand won`t lead to rolling blackouts.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, many Black and Brown communities in Houston still live under constant threat of flooding even five years after Hurricane Harvey and along the 1,200 mile border with Mexico, Texas State Republicans are actively working to make things worse.\par \par \uc2 I saw the situation firsthand last September in my capacity as National Action Network President when I met with Haitian asylum seekers in Del Rio to talk about the inhumane treatment they received, including being whipped by Federal Border Control agents on horseback.\par \par \uc2 Rather than offering state resources to help ease the humanitarian crisis, Texas Governor Greg Abbott this spring set up his own border inspections regime briefly threatening the North American supply chain already under intense pressure after two years of COVID disruptions.\par \par \uc2 It`s painfully obvious, Texas Republicans are not focused with voters` real life economic problems. In fact, they may be in touch -- they may not be in touch with reality at all, and is not just the Lone Star State, we see the same playbook at work in Governor DeSantis` Florida, Governor Kemp`s Georgia, and Governor Noem`s South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Where Republicans are in charge, bigotry, grievance, and neglect are at the top of the agenda.\par \par \uc2 So here is my suggestion to the GOP before you criticize Democrats for their political priorities: Get your own houses in order first.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:32:37]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Let`s dive a little bit deeper into the top topics of the day. Joining me are two political strategists, Democrat, Atima Omara and Republican Rick Tyler.\par \par \uc2 Let me go to you first, Rick.\par \par \uc2 What we saw this week regarding guns is very rare. On one hand, the Supreme Court doesn`t do Second Amendment cases very often and Congress doesn`t pass major gun legislation very often. Is this the new status quo, Rick?\par \par \uc2 RICK TYLER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, let`s see, Reverend, thanks for having me on.\par \par \uc2 I think the Court recognized that people do have a right to protect themselves with New York`s very strict regulations on people being able to carry a gun and protect themselves. Having said that, I think people should be trained to carry a gun.\par \par \uc2 But the Congress after 30 years has finally passed something that I think was actually quite useful, which was background checks, more intensive background checks on younger purchasers of firearms, and particularly a lot of money toward mental health, which is a serious issue and this needs to be funded. And also security of schools.\par \par \uc2 They also put in -- they encourage the states at least, to have Red Flag Laws and we`ll see how that goes. I know a lot of gun -- Second Amendment supporters are against that, but I think if there is enough -- if there`s - - if it makes -- if it protects the rights of gun owners to be able to get their guns back under false accusation and (AUDIO ISSUES) that they can do that until they prove that those guns should be taken away.\par \par \uc2 Because, look most gun owners are not crazy people to have guns, so I think on balance, it is pretty good progress in my point of view.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Atima, moving on to the abortion ruling, and I know you have a lot of experience with reproductive rights organizations. Now, the ruling does not make abortion illegal nationwide, but states can now make their own rules on abortion.\par \par \uc2 As I said earlier, we`re in the states` rights and states, including -- states can include the banning of the procedure of enacting restrictions. Now, your quick thoughts on the magnitude of this ruling and its political impact.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:05]\par \par \uc2 ATIMA OMARA: Well, I mean, it is horrific. As somebody who has worked for reproductive rights organizations and served on the board of reproductive rights organizations, and justice organizations, this is an absolutely horrific ruling that impacts over 50 percent of American people who seek abortion care, and it is just -- so for people who are following along at home, the Court historians say that this is the first time that a fundamental right has been not only overturned, but walked back as opposed to left as is or even expanded.\par \par \uc2 So it`s going to be quite interesting because right now, we are in a situation where quite a few states still have abortion rights are legal. But you know, what is going to happen when people leave in the states where now trigger bans are going into place in the last 24 hours, where are they going to go?\par \par \uc2 Those individuals are going to go to these states where it is, and there is going to be a lot of pressure on the system to support individuals seeking out abortion care, because it might be made illegal, but that doesn`t actually stop people from getting abortions and need them.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And as I keep bringing up the issue of states` rights because it was states` rights that gave us segregation, that gave us a lot of what the Civil Rights community that I was raised and still am out front with others in states` rights.\par \par \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , who wrote the decision couldn`t have gone to law school in some states, under states` rights, couldn`t have intermarried on the states` rights.\par \par \uc2 Rick, is it fair to say that by ending abortion rights, the Supreme Court lit a fire in the 2022 election? And now the question is, which base will be more energized in the elections for the midterm elections?\par \par \uc2 TYLER: Well, I hope this doesn`t surprise you, Rev, but I don`t think the ruling is going to have much political effect on the elections at all. And the reason is, is that most people made up their mind a long time ago, one way or the other, whether they`re on one side of the argument, or the other, or also that the decision has been known that it was coming for a long time.\par \par \uc2 And so it has kind of processed itself out and while some people are single issue voters, they just don`t amount to a lot on both sides. And so, you know, it`s been pretty equal.\par \par \uc2 I`m here in Washington, DC. I was there last night. And you know, the sides were pretty evenly matched, and I think that`s true in the country. Now, 16 states did codify Roe v. Wade into their State Law or their Constitutions, where women can get an abortion and the other states which are elected by - - popularly elected by legislatures in those states.\par \par \uc2 And I assume that those states would not have moved to ban abortion and the Governor having signed those laws. If in fact, it was a very unpopular thing to do.\par \par \uc2 So the country is divided on it, but we are essentially back where we were in 1973, because the Judges concluded that it`s not in the Constitution, it isn`t, and that the Roe v. Wade decision was flawed in many ways, including that it wasn`t in the Constitution. They never defined viability question, and the privacy argument was very weak.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And then, I guess that really says that both sides need to mobilize and get their side out, in big numbers.\par \par \uc2 Atima, I know you wrote an opinion piece titled "For conservatives ending legal abortion, it is just a pit stop." And so I want your thoughts on the following.\par \par \uc2 Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , who I had mentioned, writes in a concurring opinion, that the Supreme Court should reconsider Griswold, Lawrence, and Obergefell, the rulings that now protect contraception, same sex marriage, same sex relationships, and it can go on and on and on -- voting.\par \par \uc2 And like I said, interracial marriage -- all of this has been things that we`ve seen states having to deal with down in American history. How concerned should we be that this will in fact happen once we start accepting the state`s rights model?\par \par \uc2 OMARA: It`s absolutely going to happen because it`s been the plan of the conservative movement for 40 years that I lay out in my piece, it`s a multipronged 40-year plan.\par \par \uc2 Abortion is the first stop, as it were overturning it on the bullet train, some of us have willingly loaded on straight toward White, Christian, fascist nationalism.\par \par \uc2 And along that way, once you overturn abortion, yes, the same reasoning for protecting abortion takes away access to contraception, it takes away same sex marriage, the same precedent that allowed same sex marriage and access to contraception to be ruled on as a right will also put into question Brown v. Board of Education, the right to equal education; Loving v. Virginia, the right as you said, for interracial marriage in this country.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:11]\par \par \uc2 OMARA: So it`s been the plan, and even if you --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: None of which is in -- none of those things are in the Constitution either if that is the rationale of these.\par \par \uc2 OMARA: Yes, that`s absolutely the rationale and they`re questioning that, and using it within the, you know, the right to privacy clause doesn`t exist. And therefore, based off of the 14th -- they are questioning all of this that has been laid, the Civil Rights that we have enjoyed over the last 50 to 60 or so years.\par \par \uc2 And the thing that`s really scary about it is like lots of people in the movement, Democratic Party, I know yourself, Reverend, had since talked about this for years, have said that things like this are coming if we don`t get ready.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OMARA: And so you know, if you don`t believe it now, well, Alito put it in the draft opinion and Thomas made it official in the concurrent opinion, our Civil Rights beyond abortion rights are at stake.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rick, I`m out of time, but let me get the quick reply from you.\par \par \uc2 TYLER: \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has an interracial marriage. He`s not going to argue against his own marriage. He`s also an African-American, so I can say --\par \par \uc2 OMARA: He is --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: No, I am not saying he would argue, I`m saying that if you put it to the states, you could have state legislators decided it won`t be up to him, once you have a state right model, they don`t have to go back to him. This would be decided by state legislators.\par \par \uc2 OMARA: Based off of legal precedence that they`re already arguing. I`m not saying that he would, but I`m saying other people who are -- like based on that logic.\par \par \uc2 TYLER: No.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, you can`t make a Supreme Court decision and say, but don`t deal with these issues. They`ve opened the door and you can run a lot through that door, but I`m out of time.\par \par \uc2 Atima Omara and Rick Tyler, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up this, week`s abortion decision has many calling for Supreme Court reform. But what could that look like? I`ll tell you next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:45:29]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Protesters all over the country today continue to demonstrate their reaction to yesterday`s consequential Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe versus Wade, a move that reversed a half century of legal precedent.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Shaakirrah Sanders, contributor for SCOTUSblog.com. She`s the first Black Professor of Law at the University of Idaho, where she teaches Constitutional Law.\par \par \uc2 Professor, you currently teach in Idaho, a deeply red state where an abortion ban is set to come into effect next month. Now, there are no current abortion bans that attempt to prosecute women who crossed state lines to seek an abortion, however states could try in the future, according to \uc2 Drexel University\uc2  Law Professor David Cohen, who says: "There is no guarantee that an aggressive prosecutor might try to stretch the law as much as they can."\par \par \uc2 As a Law Professor in Idaho, what`s your reaction? And what`s your perspective on the legality of this?\par \par \uc2 SHAAKIRRAH SANDERS, CONTRIBUTOR FOR SCOTUSBLOG.COM: Well, first of all, Reverend Sharpton, thank you very much for having me on tonight.\par \par \uc2 I have been teaching at the University of Idaho for the past 11 years, and as you said, I teach courses on Constitutional Law, Criminal Procedure and the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 I have a lot of thought and perspective this evening. First, to your point earlier about the possibility of prosecuting individuals who seek to terminate a pregnancy in another state, once it becomes illegal in Idaho? Well, this would not be the only scenario where that type of prosecution could occur.\par \par \uc2 Idaho is currently surrounded by a number of states that legally allow for the buying and selling of marijuana. Moreover, there are other states that Idaho share a border with that allow for gambling and prostitution.\par \par \uc2 So what I will be paying attention to, should a prosecutor choose to target women exercising the right to terminate a pregnancy across state borders, I`d be curious whether we`d see similar prosecution of those engaging in other activities that Idaho has deemed illegal.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow. Now, how does the ruling overturning Roe versus Wade pave the way for other reversals? And what should Americans expect out of this Court?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, I think Americans should expect what they`ve seen from courts in our past. For example, and we`re all aware of cases like Dred Scott, Plessy versus Ferguson, and we may have often wondered, how is it that these cases could have been decided, given the language of our Constitution that, at least at the time, promised a fair amount of rights for individuals.\par \par \uc2 Yet, we saw in both Dred Scott, the court declining to extend citizenship rights to African-Americans who were enslaved, and not only those who are enslaved, but those who were free.\par \par \uc2 Later, in Dred Scott after us later, in Plessy, after we got a 14th Amendment, Equal Protection Clause, the Court says equality does not mean that the state is prohibited from separating people based on race.\par \par \uc2 So, I think a lot about that history at this moment in time.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, on Thursday, the Supreme Court also ruled that the Constitution provides the right to carry a gun outside the home, issuing a major decision on the meaning of the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of the fact that a majority of the Justices in the Highest Court in America went against public opinion on abortion and guns?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, what`s interesting to me when I think about these two particular issues is that we have had previous Supreme Courts decide that the right to keep and bear arms was really connected to one`s membership in the militia, and that was because of the text of the Constitution, specifically, the prefatory clause that talks about the need for a well- armed militia.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:18]\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: And yet, in the Heller and McDonald cases, the Court ignores that constitutional history to declare that one`s right to keep and bear arms is no longer connected to a militia. On the other hand, the Court continues to ignore the fact that individuals like me, born a few years after Roe, it is my history and tradition that this right is available.\par \par \uc2 But we must look to the original Roe opinion itself and understand its flaws. Its failure to discuss in the Courts analysis of history and tradition, the treatment of enslaved women and Native American women in particular.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, we just have 30 seconds, but I`d have to ask you quickly your thoughts on term limits for Supreme Court Justices?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, Reverend Sharpton, you know, it`s a difficult question. I`m not really sure what to think. And of course, I am an ambitious individual myself. And so should I find my ability to operate the Court or serve my government in that capacity, I certainly would want the lifetime tenure I think, that many other Federal Judges have enjoyed in the past.\par \par \uc2 So I`m not really quite sure if we have enough evidence that it is the length of the term that`s the problem or more so the process of choosing our Justices.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, I can only imagine what \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , the Incoming Justice must be thinking she is walking in to.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Professor Sanders.\par \par \uc2 Up next: My final thoughts.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:55:37]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: My final thoughts for today, short and to the point about the two major Supreme Court decisions that came down this week. The decision on carrying a gun in New York is devastating and potentially dangerous. This ruling could not have come at a worse time, as we in the Civil Rights community have been working to de-escalate gun violence in the city, it has never been more important for Congress to pass meaningful legislation to combat the epidemic that is gun violence, not just in New York, but throughout this nation.\par \par \uc2 And the Supreme Court`s decision overturning Roe versus Wade is a blatant attempt to bring us back to the Dark Ages. It will disproportionately impact Black women and poor women. It will only lead to unsafe clandestine abortions and force women to carry unwanted pregnancies to term.\par \par \uc2 This must be resisted aggressively. States must enact laws to protect women. I`m not just saying this as a Civil Rights leader and President of National Action Network, I`m saying this as a loyal, concerned citizen of this great country.\par \par \uc2 I will have much more on these topics tomorrow, including a rise up call for action.\par \par \uc2 Be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" with our Alicia Menendez, my colleague and friend starts right now.\par \par \uc2 [16:00:53]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220626ei6p00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for June 24, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Maura Barrett, Ron Mott \par \uc2 15,096 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [23:00:00]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Georgia Representative \uc2 Nikema Williams\uc2 , thank you very much for joining our coverage tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 REP. NIKEMA WILLIAMS (D-GA): Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Nikema Williams\uc2  gets tonight`s Last Word. The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: How do they have the right to tell me or any woman what she can do with her body?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I know plenty of people who have bad things happen to them. That`s not their choice.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And this is when they shouldn`t be allowed to make for themselves.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): It`s a slap in the face to women.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I want nothing more than to be a mother in my lifetime, but I want it to happen on my own terms.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like a betrayal. It feels like my country doesn`t love me.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. Tonight, protesters are out in the streets across the country after the unprecedented Supreme Court decision ending Roe v. Wade, 50 years after it became law. Demonstrations have been growing ever since the ruling was announced this morning.\par \par \uc2 Five of the courts Conservatives voted to overturn Roe. \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  whose draft opinion was leaked last month, wrote in his final decision that Roe was egregiously wrong from the start. The ruling automatically clears the way for abortion bans in 13 states, at least six states have already put them into effect. And there are concerns about what this may mean for court decisions on contraception and gay rights. Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  says those should be up for review as well.\par \par \uc2 We got a lot to cover tonight. But I want to begin with my friend and colleague, Maura Barrett, our NBC News reporter. She has been on the ground at the Supreme Court all day long. Maura, you have been among the protesters for hours. What are they telling you? And what`s their goal?\par \par \uc2 MAURA BARRETT, NBC NEWS REPORTER: Well, Stephanie, for nearly 12 hours, we saw this area head of the Supreme Court completely filled. It started off the day with a tension between two groups of protesters representing both sides of the arguments around abortion rights. And then it grew to fill this entire street that I`m standing in front of, nearly more than 1000 people I would say here at one point, you can see everyone kind of closing up shop here for the night.\par \par \uc2 But after the first couple of hours, we saw that initial elation from those supporters, those who oppose abortion rights, who were elated and celebrating and happy. And then after a few hours, they left and the streets were filled with hundreds and 1000s of people who were the ones that were hoping that maybe we`d see a compromise. They were maybe hoping that even though we were expecting to see this decision comes down. That`s not what they got today. And so we saw tears of joy and despair. We had very emotional heavy conversations. I wanted to hear some of those conversations, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: A lot of emotions have been going through my mind, anger, deep sadness. And a desire to turn this around, is going through my mind.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s a roller coaster or emotion. It`s complete and utter joy that was finally overturned.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Women are going to die between now and when we can get legislation passed, which is untenable.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARRETT: We had a lot of concern around that last sentiment, you heard about the access to safe abortion if women will get injured or die in these situations because that`s what they said they were fighting for. The right to a safe abortion. Another conversation you heard from Hannah (ph), the 66-year-old woman, the first person you heard from there, she fought for abortion rights when she was in high school back in 1973. And now she`s frustrated that she has to do it again.\par \par \uc2 Overall, I spoke with a lot of -- there`s a lot of young people hear, mostly young people, in fact, who were devastated to see their rights stripped away after thinking that it was a given. And I asked them, do you think that something can be done in Congress? Do you have hope with what we`re seeing in Congress, given that we saw President Biden call for Congress to pass this into federal law, for states to pass individual state legislation and one woman, Sky (ph), told me she`s 19 years old, and she said, well, what I`ve seen in the political spectrum and how divisive the country is right now and what we`ve seen Congress pass and how slow they work. She said she doesn`t have that hope right now. Today, though, as they closed out the protest just minutes before I came on the air. They said that they want to bring their anger, frustration and fury back out on the streets tomorrow and to the ballot box in November. Steph.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: To the ballot box in November. They want their voices heard. Maura, thank you for being there. I know you`ve been out there for hours and hours, stay safe and get some rest.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s get smarter with the help of our leadoff panel tonight, Katie Benner, Justice Department Reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Professor Melissa Murray of NYU Law School, she was a Law Clerk for \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  on the federal bench before her nomination to the Supreme Court, and former U.S. Attorney Joyce Vance who spent 25 years as a federal prosecutor. She`s also a professor at the University of Alabama School of Law.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:11]\par \par \uc2 Ladies, thank you so much for being here tonight. I feel like I need to start this program, sort of with a collective deep breath. There`s so much to get through. Melissa, when I think about the course of U.S. history, it`s been a journey to expand rights, give more rights to more people, as the great American experiment has evolved. Was today the first time the court took away a right that we`ve been given?\par \par \uc2 MELISSA MURRAY, NYU LAW PROFESSOR: So, Stephanie, first let me thank you for having us all on and for using women`s voices to surface these questions today. It has not been the case on all platforms. But yes, this is unprecedented. The court has typically chosen to expand the rights of individuals. That`s been the trajectory certainly since this century. But in this rather unprecedented move, the court has retracted rights that were extended almost 50 years ago. And again, this is not the end of it. Justice Thomas`s concurrence was not signed by any other justice. But he has laid out a blueprint for inviting litigation that would challenge other rights, including rights to contraception, same sex marriage and same sex sexuality, that will surely be taken up by members of the conservative legal movement going forward.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joyce, you join our show to bring legal expertise. But you`re also a dear friend of ours. And you`re a woman and a mother. What is this like for you, what`s on your mind?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Today has been one of those incredibly emotional days, especially I think, for the four of us, because we`re all women, who were used to looking at issues, difficult issues, through the lens of the news, and the need to help our communities and the viewing public understand these issues. So I think in some cases, we`re able to cloak ourselves in our expertise, and we insulate ourselves a little bit from the emotion. Today, that wasn`t possible. Because bottom Dobbs is a decision that sends this message to women, it tells us we cannot be trusted with making decisions about what to do with our own bodies. And no matter how you dress that up in law and history, that`s a relegation of women to second class citizenship.\par \par \uc2 So I think we all have this sense of loss today, this sense of pain. I suspect in the next few days and weeks, that`ll be replaced with a sense of purpose. But for all of us a sad day, and I think it`s appropriate for us to take the moment to acknowledge it. So thank you, Stephanie, for giving us that opportunity.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Thank you. When you talk about that sense of loss, is there confusion out there, that being pro-abortion rights means being pro- abortion? Isn`t it really about a woman simply wanting control of her body? Joyce?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: This issue has been politicized. The issue has been so politicized, that it`s become an issue. That`s something that it really is not about. I think you`re correct to say that this is about who makes decisions about women`s bodies. I know because I live in the deep south where there are many people who are profoundly against abortion, but nonetheless believe that that decision should be preserved for a woman to make on her own, that anything else really does devalue women as members of our society.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Katie, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  has promised to protect abortion rights. But can he, given his job and given the Supreme Court, what can he really do?\par \par \uc2 KATIE BENNER, THE NEW YORK TIMES JUSTICE DEPARTMENT REPORTER: Sure. So you`re right, the Justice Department`s ability to protect abortion is very limited in this case. What it can do is it can fight for the states that are decided to preserve the right to choose, so it can help protect the states and the Justice Department can help protect women who leave their states if their states have decided that abortion is illegal to travel across state lines.\par \par \uc2 Also the Justice Department in its long statement today, \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  said that it would also fight for the right for women to have medical abortions to the extent that it can. Now, one of the things that it can do is show that the -- because the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has said that certain medical abortion drugs are safe and efficacious. It can fight laws that are premised upon the idea that those drugs are not safe or efficacious. Anything beyond that, it would certainly have to look at the facts in order to figure out how to move forward and whether to move forward.\par \par \uc2 Please, keep in mind that for \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2 , if he had his druthers, he would be remembered as the attorney general who fought for civil rights above all else. And when I`ve spoken with him, including on the record this March, he was clear that he feels that the right to an abortion is central to a woman`s civil rights and something he`s extremely passionate about.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then help me understand why wouldn`t he have been doing this already, whether we`re hearing from \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  or the White House saying we are here to protect one is right. This isn`t -- it`s a shock that this happened today, but it`s not a surprise since we saw that draft last month, Melissa, why wouldn`t the Justice Department or the White House be taking action ahead of this day?\par \par \uc2 [23:10:14]\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: It`s only been a month, and it`s true that some more expeditious action could have been taken, but I think the White House has taken some steps, they convened a roundtable of experts on these issues. I was included in that conversation. I know that they have been investigating whether or not the prospect of federal funds can be directed to allow individuals who are federal employees to take paid leave in order to seek reproductive care. So they`ve been examining a lot of avenues. But frankly, this is an administration that has some really difficult roads ahead.\par \par \uc2 The President could certainly take executive action on this front, we saw President Obama do this on the efforts for climate change. We also saw how that turned out, there`s currently a case pending in the Supreme Court right now that has the seeds of the overturning of big parts of the administrative state and really limiting the effort to curb climate change because those executive actions were viewed as overstepping. And so I think one of the things the administration is trying to weigh here is if it takes steps under the authority of the executive branch, will that open the door for other losses at the Supreme Court on other avenues that they`re not willing to see right now. So there`s a lot in the balance. And I`m not being an apologist here for the administration in any way. But there are a lot of different things to weigh here. This is a really complicated and nuanced situation, both for abortion rights, but for other rights that are also on the table.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Melissa, correct me if I`m wrong, it sure feels like \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is running the show here. And the fact that he`s now raising things like banning contraception, or same sex marriage, are we in real danger of losing some of these other rights?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Well, Justice Thomas is the longest serving member of the court and he`s really bided his time, and now he`s seeing the seeds of his conservative project in full flower. Yesterday was his birthday and he gave himself the greatest gift of all, which was an opinion, basically allowing the court to expand the scope of the Second Amendment to permit public carrying of firearms.\par \par \uc2 Today, he got the opportunity to not only join the majority in overruling Roe vs. Wade, but also to write separately to say that in addition to overruling Roe, the court should in the future, reconsider the rights to same-sex marriage and contraception and the like. He`s very much I think the leader of the conservative wing along with Justice Alito. And the real question, I think, is what has happened to Chief Justice Roberts, who did not join in overruling Roe, although he joined the majority opinion to uphold the Mississippi law. He is, in many respects the Chief Justice in name only of this conservative court, he has lost control of that block.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joyce, prosecutors in 29 states have said they would not prosecute abortion care cases. On one hand, that seems like a big deal. But on the other hand, just because they say that they don`t have the law on their side, can anyone who lives in those states trust that? That`s just a prosecutor giving their word.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: This is an incredibly complicated issue, Steph. But what it comes down to it bottom is that for state prosecutions, when we`re just talking about state law crimes, the states that have now criminalized abortion, those prosecutions largely are vested in the hands of county district attorneys. And it`s a legitimate exercise of prosecutorial discretion for some of those county DAs to say, I have more important crimes to prosecute, I have limited resources, I have to set priorities. I`m setting my priorities in such a way that I might prosecute violent crime or fraud, and I`m not going to prosecute people who perform or receive abortions. And that`s certainly legitimate.\par \par \uc2 One of the problems here, you`re talking about trust, I think these DAs have taken this pledge. I know my district attorney in Jefferson County, Alabama is a signatory to that letter. And I believe that he means that. The problem is whether attorneys general in some states have the ability to come in and oversee that district attorney`s jurisdiction and whether those attorneys general might not choose to file cases on their own.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But right there, so what if that district attorney loses his job or get hit by a bus tomorrow? If you live in your district? Can you really trust what they`re saying?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: I think the point you make is a good one. For some reason, these district attorneys have chosen to publicly put themselves out there. I might have not been quite so public about my decision, but as you say, they could be voted out of office or they could simply wake up one morning and no longer be the district attorney. And that means anyone who has relied on this position is vulnerable and there are statutes of limitations for these crimes that permit prosecution particularly in states that have now made abortion tantamount to murder where those statutes could run for a very long time. So yes, people proceed at their own risk in relying on these promises that they won`t be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:23]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: There`s an old saying, your word is your bond. Does that not apply to Supreme Court justices? I want to play for you a little bit of what we heard from these conservative justices who were appointed by former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Senator, again, I would tell you that Roe vs. Wade decided in 1973. There`s a precedent \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . It has been reaffirmed so good judge will consider it as precedent of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , U.S. ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF THE SUPREME COURT: Senator, I said that it`s settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court. One of the important things to keep in mind about Roe v. Wade, is that it has been reaffirmed many times over the past 45 years.\par \par \uc2 AMY CONEY BARRETT, U.S. ASSOCIATE JUSTICE OF THE SUPREME COURT: As Richard Fallon from Harvard, said Roe is not a super precedent because calls for its overruling have never ceased. But that doesn`t mean that Roe should be overruled. It just means that it doesn`t fall on the small handful of cases like Marbury vs. Madison and Brown versus the Board that no one questions anymore.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So, Katie, to you first, and Melissa, I want you to weigh in, what were they just crossing their fingers under the table and saying sake. Are there, no repercussions? The reason we have hearings is to ask these specific questions. And clearly, they weren`t telling the truth, or at the very least, weren`t being straight.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: So I think that the idea of having hearings in order to really understand what can -- what, you know, people who are being considered for the bench truly think that is an antiquated idea. We see and hearing after hearing the people ask difficult questions, they find creative ways to basically repeat talking points. You know, to say that they believe largely in the idea of precedent. But if you look at the decision today, I think that all of the justices who decided they needed to overturn Roe versus Wade would say that there are sometimes cases in American history where the decision was just wrong. I think the big example is always Plessy versus Ferguson, it was important not to uphold that decision in order to go forth and have a more equal country. And I think that the idea that we see Supreme Court justices be considered, and they always tell the truth and tackle heart issues head on, is somewhat wrongheaded. I think everybody is playing the game of how do we get confirmed it`s a matter of Senate math. And it`s a matter of not saying anything that can be hard and fast, interpreted in a way that would pin somebody down.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Say whatever it takes to get across the line, Melissa?\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: Stephanie, if you listened to those responses carefully, they`re pretty anodyne responses. Roe versus Wade is a precedent. Richard Fallon set x. At no point did any of them ever say, when I get in the black robe, and I`m sitting on that bench, I will uphold Roe versus Wade. And Katie`s exactly right, they can`t say that because they want to be confirmed. They have to keep their conservative senators on board without having the liberal senators depart. And so they said what they said, it`s not exactly a lie. It`s obviously not as forthcoming as perhaps we would like, but it`s also not impeachable. These are individuals who are appointed for life on individuals like me, testified against them and said that they would be solid votes to overrule Roe versus Wade and we hoped that some senators were listening. Obviously, those senators were not and here we are.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: They`re appointed for life. \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , celebrating his birthday, overturning Roe vs. Wade, Katie Benner, Melissa Murray, Joyce Vance, it is always a privilege to have you on, but especially tonight. Thank you all for bringing your expertise and more importantly for being my friend.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, today`s ruling and the immediate impact for women around the country. One of our next guests will explain why she says there are now two different Americas.\par \par \uc2 And later, people already had a low opinion of the Supreme Court before today`s ruling where today fits into the history books. The 11th Hour just getting underway on a very important Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:24:04]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DR. KATHY KING, PLANNED PARENTHOOD OF WISCONSIN MEDICAL DIRECTOR: Today, I had to look people in the eye and turn them away when they were seeking abortions. The patients I see who have abortions are all of us, they`re your friends, your family, your neighbors, your members of our community of our state. All who deserve access to safe timely medical care.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It did not take long for today`s decision to create confusion and distress. In the state of Texas, the Attorney General released an advisory saying, "abortion providers could be criminally liable for providing abortions starting today."\par \par \uc2 With us tonight to discuss, \uc2 Amy Hagstrom Miller\uc2 , President and CEO of Whole Woman`s Health, the nation`s largest independent abortion provider and Mark Hearron, Senior Counsel at the Center for Reproductive rights. He also served as Senator Feinstein`s lead counsel for judicial nominations.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:00]\par \par \uc2 Amy take us through your day, your clinics in Texas had to immediately suspend services, what happened the moment this decision was made, because you`ve got women in many cases, girls, young women under the age of 18, having the most stressful day of their life heading in for an abortion, what happened to them?\par \par \uc2 AMY HAGSTROM MILLER\uc2 , WHOLE WOMAN`S HEALTH PRESIDENT AND CEO: So today, on the advice of counsel, we had to stop seeing the abortion patients. As soon as this decision came out, in order to protect our staff, in order to protect our patients, from a very aggressive administration in the state of Texas, we had to tell people that we couldn`t care for them. And we are completely committed to caring and completely trained and ready to provide safe abortion care, but for this kind of restriction, and but for this kind of decision that happened today from the Supreme Court. So while here in Texas, we have to say no, in our states, like Minnesota, and Maryland and Virginia, where we have clinics as well, we are preparing for an influx of people who are being forced to migrate out of the states where abortion has been banned, and travel across the country in order to get the safe abortion care that they need.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So what happened to those women today in Texas, when you turn them away? What did they tell you where they go?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: So, you know, people don`t have anywhere to go that`s close by. Clinics in Oklahoma have been shuttered by abortion bans now. Clinics in Louisiana, the same. Clinics in New Mexico are really at capacity. So we have been supporting people through our Wayfinder program at Whole Woman`s Health, to find their way to clinics outside of Texas. And we have a bunch of patients who just begged us to put them on a waiting list, if somehow, we`re going to be able to reopen and care for them. They`re begging, please come in. And our clinic staff as well, they are so dedicated and so committed in the state of Texas, and all the states where we have clinics, they`re just so tuned in to what people need in our communities. And, you know, they keep saying to us, we want to see as many patients as we can for as long as we can. So, so heartbreaking today that we had to stop care, and we were open in all four of our Texas clinics, and we had to stop providing abortion care services really came out and start sending people as far as Minnesota or Virginia or Maryland instead.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Marc I promise I`m going to get to in a second. That`s my question, then Amy. When you turned women away, if they said, all right, I`m going to get in my car, and I`m going to find the nearest clinic, how far are they going to have to go, how long would the drive be?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: So, Stephanie, even before this Supreme Court ruling today, already 30% of the people we are seeing in our clinic in Minnesota, which is about 21 hours` drive from the closest place in Texas, we`re coming from Texas, just because of Texas` SB 8 abortion ban. And now we`re seeing these abortion bans in multiple states in the South and the Midwest.\par \par \uc2 So once a person decides they don`t have the ability to continue a pregnancy, they`re going to seek an abortion. And these abortion bans don`t prevent unplanned pregnancy. They don`t help people plan their families. They simply ban people from care and forced them to migrate.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen people traveling all the way from South Texas from our community in McAllen, all the way to Alexandria, Virginia, that`s three days drive. We had a patient drive with her children. She took her children with her because childcare is very difficult to get for that many days in a row. And she had the abortion and got in her car and drove all the way back to Texas in order to make it to work so that she could keep her job. This is the reality of the people that we`re serving. People who are being forced to travel by car by bus. People who fly who have never even flown before, are trying to figure out how to fly and navigate all these things they haven`t done before. They don`t know what \uc2 Lyft\uc2  is, or \uc2 Uber\uc2  is or they may not have a credit card that allows them to check into a hotel. These people need our advocacy and they need our support at this time. And it`s going to be this way for some time into the future that we`re going to need to support people to get safe abortion care, and also be there in the communities for people who are going to be forced to carry pregnancies against their will and support community networks that can help with the maternal mortality and pregnancy outcomes that are going to impact our community for generations to come.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Marc, it was clear in Texas as Amy said their legal counsel advised them, they couldn`t continue abortion services, certainly they would want to. In other states how chaotic and confusing is this ruling just knowing whether or not it`s legal in your state?\par \par \uc2 [23:30:11]\par \par \uc2 MARC HEARRON, CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS SENIOR COUNSEL: Well, today`s decision, which is absolutely devastating, for at least half the country, abortion is poised to be banned, either and is immediately or will be very soon. Texas, for example, there is confusion about whether the pre-Roe ban has come back to life, this sort of zombie ban that isn`t actually anywhere on the statute books, and yet the attorney general is -- has come out with this extreme position saying that somehow, they`ve bans abortion anyway. And threatening criminal sanctions against providers like Amy, like Whole Woman`s Health in Texas, which I`m very proud to represent.\par \par \uc2 This -- the confusion is being sown everywhere. And look, if you`re in a blue state, and you think this isn`t going to affect me, this is terrible. I hate this outcome. I hate what this decision, but it`s actually not going to affect me, you`re wrong. And the reason is, think about -- look at what happened in Texas, after Texas passed a six week ban, that has been in effect for nine months now. As Amy was saying, the rush of patients out of Texas to Oklahoma, to New Mexico, to Kansas, that is creating backlogs of weeks, weeks in those other states, if you`re in a blue state, patients from half the country are not going to be coming to your state trying to seek abortion care that remains legal in your state. And it`s going to be hard to get an appointment there. That we also know this isn`t over.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`re out of time. But I want to ask before -- I want to ask you before we go, though, these red states that are making moves to ban abortion, when they ban it or strict it at the same time, are they then going to offer more financial services, more support to these women because many, many women who seek abortions, part of the reason is they cannot financially support a pregnancy, prenatal care is hugely expensive. Is this going to trigger any financial support coming from the state or an obligation of financial obligation from the fathers?\par \par \uc2 HEARRON: You know, I wish I could say that it would. But even if the states -- we know that the states that are acting to ban abortion are the same states that have rejected expansion of Medicaid. These are the same states that have limited resources available. But look, setting that aside, the state still should not be able to tell a woman that from the point of fertilization, from the point of conception, that she can`t decide what to do with her own body. I don`t care what kind of resources are available to that woman, that is a decision for women to make with their families, with their loved ones for themselves.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I`m not suggesting they are. My question was just those states that are making the decision that you can`t, what I`d like to know is fine, if they can`t, then what are you doing to support those women?\par \par \uc2 Amy, thank you so much for being here. Marc, thank you as well. You definitely made us smarter. I appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, American`s views on Roe do not line up with what we saw from the Supreme Court today. Democrats want to turn the outrage into votes in November. Is it going to work? When the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:38:17]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: In some recent NBC News polling 63% of Americans, nearly two-thirds of people surveyed said they did not want Roe versus Wade overturned, yet Republicans they`re doubling down. Former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is now calling for a nationwide ban as many in his party are celebrating the ruling.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in to discuss Jennifer Palmieri, the former White House Communications Director for President Obama, Tim Miller, Contributor to the Bulwark and former Communications Director for Jeb Bush, and our good friend, author and Presidential Historian Michael Beschloss. His latest work is Presidents of War.\par \par \uc2 Jennifer, let`s get right to it. We see protests in the streets across the country. Will this mobilize Democratic voters, young ones especially?\par \par \uc2 JENNIFER PALMIERI, FORMER WHITE HOUSE COMMUNICATIONS DIRECTOR FOR PRESIDENT OBAMA: Yeah, I think it can. But Democratic candidates have to lead the -- they have to lead the charge. I look to you can`t -- this, you can`t expect that it will happen organically. And I would point to \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  sort of an example of a candidate who is very much leaning into the abortion fight but also just concerned about the -- you know, the rollback of rights in general. She has a zombie law. So in Michigan a law -- so in Michigan, there is a law on the books that would ban abortion. If Roe is overturned, it`s in the courts right now. She filed a lawsuit against that earlier this year. She did a lot of press around this issue. She had one of your colleagues fly out to Michigan yesterday to be part of a roundtable, listening to pro-choice women Republicans and Democrats in the stage. She is not shying away from it, which is going right to it.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, these are -- this is an existential threat. That that right to American women, it is gone. There are other rights that are on the butchering blog and you have -- you can`t -- you just can`t shy away from it, you have to go and lead right into it.\par \par \uc2 Rather than giving them the rights that are on that are on the butchering blog and you have you can`t you just can`t shy away from it. You have to go lead right into it.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:09]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tim, help us understand the Republican calculation. This is a wildly unpopular decision that they`re celebrating as a huge win.\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, THE BULWARK CONTRIBUTOR: Well, it`s been a 50 year fight for Republicans. And I think that they`re obviously some in the pro-life movement --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: For some Republicans?\par \par \uc2 T. MILLER: Yeah, you`re right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: For some Republicans. You hold on a second -- you are a Republican for 20 years, right? Downstairs in my house, or my mother and father lifetime Republicans. None of the three of you, this has not been a fight for you, they don`t feel good about this.\par \par \uc2 T. MILLER: Right. And so that`s what I say, I think that there are a couple of parts of the Republican Party, right? I mean, the me and your parents were not in the Republican Party anymore. So a lot of the sort of the old, you know, George H.W. Bush matter Republicans in the suburbs have been pushed out of the party. And I think those are people that is important for the Democrats to talk to right now in the midterms, because it`s a two prong thing. They`ve got to mobilize the base, but also speak to the kind of Romney-Biden voters that helped put Biden over the top that Hillary didn`t get in 2016. So I think there are two elements to this.\par \par \uc2 The problem for the Republicans and political issue that they`re going to face is that, in some of these states, like what you have in Texas, you know, these five weak bands, these immediately -- immediate after fertilization bands, even mainstream Republicans don`t like that, you know, this is a very small part of the Republican base that is mobilized by that. And I think that if you look at a state like Georgia, Wisconsin, both very important swing states, the Wisconsin trigger laws going to go into effect, Georgia trigger law, going to go into effect, those are both very important states coming up this November, with big cities, Milwaukee, Madison, Atlanta, where there are a lot of voters that maybe have mixed views on abortion, that certainly are not going to be in favor of five week bands, that I think those are going to be places that the Democrats are going to have to fight aggressively to make Republicans pay for these more extreme measures that are being taken.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Michael, you know, who doesn`t have to fight aggressively or get reelected? Supreme Court justices. So well, we keep hearing about how unpopular this is, or how the court is really losing the faith of the American people, do these justices care? They`re on the bench for life, and they got exactly what they wanted.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL BESCHLOSS, NBC NEWS PRESIDENTIAL HISTORIAN: That`s exactly it. That`s the central question here. You know, look at the tone of that decision today, George Washington would have said, if you`ve got power, like a five person majority, you always exercise it with restraint, you`ll always try to bind the country together. That seems to be what \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  was trying to do, which was to introduce a change in abortion rights and narrow them. But what this decision, it sounds just like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , this decision was intended to throw scalding acid into the face of people who feel strongly about abortion rights, it was intended to shock. Compare that to, for instance, the decision on Brown versus Board of Education, 1954, saying that you took -- couldn`t do separate but equal anymore, you couldn`t have a segregated society.\par \par \uc2 Earl Warren said privately as he was talking to justices, you know, this is going to be a big social change to say that the government is now saying there has to be integration. You can`t do that with a five to four majority, you have to do it with language that`s channel introduced justice, with mercy. And so as a result, that was an overwhelming majority, for Brown versus Board of Education, held that social change to be accepted. But what this was, did this morning, I think, pushed our beloved bleeding country in the direction of almost civil war over the issue of abortion one day after it made another decision on gun safety. This is not a supreme court trying to heal. This is a Supreme Court of a radical majority. It`s essentially saying, look at how much power we have, even though we`re about a third of the country in terms of sharing our views. We`re going to just shove it in your face.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That might sound like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but doesn`t sound like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for life when it comes to abortion. He didn`t take such a strong stance until he realized the value of the evangelical vote. Yes, when he took the stand.\par \par \uc2 All three of our guests are going to stay with us. So stick around, we got to take a quick break. When we come back, public support for a woman`s right to choose goes back decades. We`re going to have more on the historical impact of today`s decision. And the consequences yet to come when the 11th Hour continues on this very important night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:49:21]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: But I was in high school in 1973, and I can say that the stories were out there. What happened to women. They needed an abortion to see us going back to knitting needles and the whole thing. I just -- I`m stunned. I don`t understand.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Two years after Roe vs. Wade was decided Gallup began asking Americans, should abortion be legal in all circumstances, legal in some circumstances, or always illegal. And as you can see on your screen right now, a huge majority of Americans have always wanted abortion to remain legal.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:02]\par \par \uc2 Let`s discuss, Jennifer Palmieri, Tim Miller, and Michael Beschloss, all still with us. Michael, is there historical precedent for a decision this unpopular?\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: Sure. But you`d have to go back to 1857 Dred Scott decision, but actually did lead to Civil War. And I`m by no means suggesting that this decision is the equivalent of Dred Scott. But sometimes there`s a decision from the Supreme Court, that is so shocking to a majority of Americans that it leads to consequences that we do not want.\par \par \uc2 This was almost nakedly cruel, the way that this was done this morning, was essentially saying to two-thirds of the American people, just as you`ve been saying, my dear friend, Stephanie, that even though a vast majority prefers to have abortion rights available much more easily, you know, we`ve got a cabal on the Supreme Court, you know, we`re now in charge. It`s only happened for a couple of years. But here we are, and at the earliest opportunity, we`re going to do this in the most angry and overwhelming way possible. That`s not the way it`s been done, usually in history. And when it`s done this way, just like 1857, it makes Americans distrust the Supreme Court sends its prestige way down.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: They don`t care what -- they don`t care about prestige or reputation, they got what they wanted.\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: With \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , in a sidecar babbling about we`re going after contraceptives and gay marriage next, that`s what we`re --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He doesn`t care if protests are going on. He got his wish.\par \par \uc2 BESCHLOSS: The purpose of this is the anger.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, it`s working. Jennifer, why haven`t Democrats codified Roe at any point in the last half century?\par \par \uc2 PALMIERI: Yeah. So there was an effort -- I worked with President Clinton, there was an effort early on in his presidency to do so, to codify Roe. And I would say it didn`t happen -- it didn`t happen under Obama, didn`t have an under Clinton method for two reasons. One, particularly under Clinton, there was still -- there was still disagreement within the party. You know, not all of Democratic members of Congress are pro-choice. That`s mostly changed now. And then, as it went on, I think people just thought it wasn`t ever going to happen, that they did not believe that Roe was actually in that kind of jeopardy. They did not imagine a day like today until it was too late. And with a Congress that we have now, you know, only 50 Democrats in the Senate, that`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That actually speaks to how determined anti-abortion activists have been over decades, working for this moment, in the last few years. I mean, they were almost laughed at by many others who thought this is never going to happen. This is settled law. Tim, is there actually anything to learn? For those who support abortion rights, is there anything to learn from the strategy that anti-abortion activists have had all these years? Because my God, nobody thought they win. And they just did?\par \par \uc2 T. MILLER: Yeah, absolutely. I looked at the deal with the devil, with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I mean, look, a lot of these folks came around to liking Trump. So we`re going to do a whole another segment about that. But in 2016, there were a lot of voters that did not like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , did not trust him. But he made a deal based on the Supreme Court, and he got enough votes to win not a majority, to win the electoral college in large part because of voters who had had been -- who cared about this issue, genuinely, in some cases, had been conditioned to care about this issue. This was part of, you know, putting on the team red jersey and other cases. And so I think that on the left, you did not see that in 2016. You did not see people saying, you know, we have to go out and vote for Hillary, you know, because the Supreme Court is too important. You know, you saw the opposite. You saw some people from the Sanders crowd that were refusing to support Hillary and throwing their vote away, or people who didn`t turn out for it.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I look, I think that there`s a good reason. I understand the frustration on the left for people who say, well, voting -- we voted, we won, and this still happened. But I think that there`s a lesson to be learned about the persistence and about, you know, prioritizing what is happening on the court and sometimes taking, you know, the good instead of the perfect, that certainly, you know, we wouldn`t be in this position had that happened in 2016.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But then here`s the question for those voters, does this make \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  more powerful and that he filled -- he fulfilled the deal, he got it done, or less valuable in that they`re done with him? They got their wish. They don`t need him anymore.\par \par \uc2 T. MILLER: I think that`s a really interesting question. I think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been slightly weakening over the past few weeks because of the power of the January 6 Committee and you could see that a little bit in some Republican polls and some focus groups we do at the Bulwark. I think that this could be a, you know, a shirt that he waves over his head, right, that make people feel like, you know, this heat, we picked him because he said he`d fight the left and he did fight and he won. And this might turn him from being a loser, which he is into a winner in some of their minds. I don`t -- we don`t know that yet, but I am a little bit concerned that this strengthens him. Obviously, that`s not the prime concern for people today. But it`s something to monitor.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We`ll see, there`s a whole lot of independence in this country. And they don`t think this is a winning day. Jennifer Palmieri, Tim Miller, Michael Beschloss, you made us all smarter tonight. I really appreciate you joining us.\par \par \uc2 Our special extended coverage of the end of Roe continues after the break. We`re going all night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This is something central to a woman`s life, to her dignity. It`s a decision that she must make for herself. And when government controls that decision for her, she`s being treated as less than a fully adult, human responsible for her own choices.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [00:00:49]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I`m Stephanie Ruhle back with you for another hour of our special coverage on the Supreme Court`s decision to overturn Roe versus Wade. The move reversed nearly 50 years of abortion rights in this country. This will likely be a weekend or possibly longer of protests in the streets, which we have already seen coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It`s complete and utter joy that it was finally overturn.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It feels like a betrayal. It feels like my country doesn`t love me and appreciate my body as a woman. I can`t even -- I can`t have a chat because I can`t say anything. It just -- it hurts.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I`ve had people in my family have abortions and they have felt their regret years and years down the line and they`re still dealing in facing with the trauma today.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: To see us going back to knitting needles and the whole thing, I just, I`m stunned. I don`t understand.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You cannot agonize over it. We have to get organized and just vote people out.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Unfortunately, people are going to die, women are going to die between now and when we can get legislation passed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: This ruling automatically clears the way for abortion bans in 13 states, at least six states have already put them into effect. We`ve got a lot to cover. So let`s get smarter and bring in our lead off panel. Dahlia Lithwick, Senior Editor at Slate and an MSNBC Law and Politics Analyst, Barbara McQuade, a Veteran Federal Prosecutor and former U.S. Attorney for the Eastern District of Michigan, she worked with the Department of Justice during the Biden transition and is a professor at the University of Michigan School of Law. And Josh Gerstein joins us, Senior Legal Affairs Contributor for Politico. He broke the story on the Supreme Court leak last month.\par \par \uc2 Dahlia, this current conservative Supreme Court has been in the making for decades. And while today might be shocking or jarring, should we be surprised? Wasn`t this the game plan?\par \par \uc2 DAHLIA LITHWICK, SLATE SENIOR EDITOR: Not only was the game plan, Stephanie, but it`s been telegraphed at every turn. This was so clearly decades in the making. This was an undertaking that goes back to the sort of misrevolution, we`re going to lose everything else. But if we take the court, we will ultimately win. And in a deep, deep way, I think when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took office, saying I will do one thing right for all the things I do wrong, and that is transformed the court, I think we should have believed him because this was all happening in plain sight. Nobody should be surprised.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Josh, as I said, just a moment ago, you were the reporter who broke the story when there was that draft opinion a month ago, this decision, how close is it to the leaked draft?\par \par \uc2 JOSH GERSTEIN, POLITICO SENIOR LEGAL AFFAIRS CONTRIBUTOR: It`s almost identical, Stephanie, you know, there may be a few small changes here and there, Justice Alito hewed almost exactly to the draft that we recorded about seven weeks ago or so. The only differences are he did add sections to this opinion, rebutting both the liberals the three justices who did the liberal dissent and Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , who did his own concurrence here and with Robert said, that the court should have allowed this Mississippi law that was at issue to take effect, but still left some form of a federal prosecution guarantee for abortion to be sorted out later. So there were some additions.\par \par \uc2 But I think, Alito, maybe he resented the fact, his opinion was made public, because it seemed like he went out of his way to really not change anything in it. Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Chief Justice Roberts was incensed, infuriated when that draft was leaked, when you got it. What`s happened with that investigation that he launched?\par \par \uc2 GERSTEIN: I don`t know the answer to that, Stephanie, beyond saying that I know that they`ve taken steps and there have been things that they have done to advance the investigation but precisely where it stands right at the moment, I`m not entirely sure.\par \par \uc2 [00:05:10]\par \par \uc2 I think it`s possible. You know, the court has such a rush of work that comes in the last few weeks of the term or the last few weeks before they go away for the summer, then I think it`s possible that after doing some initial work on the investigation, it was sort of effectively set aside. And the question is going to be whether the court decides to pursue it in earnest, particularly after a significant number of their staff will be leaving over the summer, the annual group of law clerks I think, probably go when the term change is over.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And now it`s been overturned. So the leak is history.\par \par \uc2 Barbara, let`s talk about \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , because it`s not just overturning Roe. Now, he wants to look at overturning other rulings, other rights we have been granted. What does that mean essentially? I mentioned it in the last hour. When you think about America`s historical arc, we`ve been expanding rights to include more people. And now we`re starting to take them back.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Yeah, you`re right about that, Stephanie. I think one of the things that`s so shocking about today`s opinion, even though as Dahlia said, it`s kind of been 50 years in the making, is it`s the first time the Supreme Court has overruled an opinion to take away a right that has been on the books for 50 years. And \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` concurring opinion, is very disturbing, because he suggests that, while we`re at it, we ought to take a fresh look at things like the right to contraception, and same-sex marriage, and even intimate sexual relations between consenting adults behind closed doors.\par \par \uc2 And the reason is, frankly, he`s the only one willing to say it out loud. Because if you look at the reasoning of the majority, in this case, it is all about this idea that the word abortion does not appear anywhere in the Constitution, and the idea of substantive due process, that there are some fundamental rights that are so basic, that they are protected in the Constitution, even though they are not specifically spelled out, if we`re going to go down that road, that`s the foundation for abortion rights. It`s also the foundation for all of those other rights. And so if that foundation is gone, then the whole house of cards comes down. And so I think he is really seeing the tea leaves.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But Barb helped me with this rationale. If one could argue you have the right to bear arms to protect yourself, why wouldn`t you have the right to protect your own body as a woman?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Well, I think you are reading the document the way it is intended to be read, Stephanie, in its entirety, you can`t read one section of it in a vacuum and say that it doesn`t -- you don`t look at the rest of it. That is the way until originalism came along in the 70s and 80s, the justices of the Supreme Court looked at it but now I think what they would say is, well, we pluck out the Second Amendment right here, it`s got the word keep in bear arms, we read that and so therefore, you know, ipso facto, there`s a right to bear arms, because the word abortion does not appear anywhere in the document. That`s not a right, and we are going to pull down Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 So, but I think the better way to look at the document is to say that you have to read the entire document. And when you think about, say, the Ninth Amendment, for example, which says rights, which are not enumerated, still exist, just because we didn`t think to write them down doesn`t mean they don`t exist anymore. And so they want to pretend those don`t exist. And say, because the word abortion doesn`t appear there, there is no such right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Dahlia, we are seeing, in some instances, a court that is clearly at odds with the majority of Americans, especially on issues like guns and abortion. But a moment ago, when we were showing a clip of protesters, one woman said, we`ve got to show up and vote them out. You don`t vote for a Supreme Court justice. They`re not going anywhere. So when people are out there protesting and saying they`re going to take action, what`s that going to look like? \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is not going any -- he`s not leaving that bench until he`s in a grave.\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: Right. It`s such a complicated question, Stephanie, because at one level, the impulses just vote, right? If all those people who didn`t vote -- voted, this could change. I think underlying that we have to just sit for a minute with the uncomfortable fact that not every vote is equal and that this is not actually a representative democracy because it was never intended to be a representative democracy.\par \par \uc2 And so you would have to strip out the Electoral College, the filibuster, you would have to strip out a massively mallet portions in it, where you have a 50/50 Senate but one side represents 41 million more people. All of that stuff, all of that minority, majority rule stuff that is baked into, right, the way government work means that just showing up and voting isn`t enough. And I think one of the lessons that people need to understand when they think about, you know, 70%, 80% of Americans hate this outcome, 70%, 80% of Americans hate the outcome and ruin the gun case yesterday.\par \par \uc2 [00:10:19]\par \par \uc2 But it`s not a one to one correlation between getting out and voting. This requires massive, massive democracy reform. And it requires kind of staring at a Supreme Court that in Shelby County, in Brnovich, in all the gerrymandering cases, and all the vote suppression cases, have increasingly made it hard for every vote to be equal. And so I always say, this isn`t a guns problem. It`s not an abortion problem. It`s a democracy problem. It`s a structural problem. And I think until we really, really start to think about, why is the filibuster getting in the way of voting rights reform? Why is it getting in the way of WHPA, The Women`s Health Protection Act? Because it was meant to, and that`s the stuff we have to think about fixing.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So how do you fix it?\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: Well, I mean, none of these things are unfixable, right? I think that there is meaningful effort to reform the Electoral College. There`s meaningful conversation about figuring out why the filibuster has precluded having, you know, reinstating the Voting Rights Act, which was passed by, you know, massive bipartisan agreement until the court struck it down. I mean, every piece of this can get fixed. But I think in a weird way, and I guess this is analogous to you know, what, what dogs is teaching us is that if you lived in Oklahoma or Texas, it was always hard to get an abortion. The Hyde Amendment always made it hard to get an abortion.\par \par \uc2 By the same token, this was never really one person, one vote, a fair democracy. This was never anything like what we think it is. And now`s a really good time to reckon with that, and to say, how do we fix gerrymandering? How do we fix vote suppression? We can do this, Steph. But it really requires thinking about boring, wonky system.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Josh, come September, President Biden`s pick, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  will join the Supreme Court, she will be on the bench. Between now and then, are we going to see the conservative justices tried to push a lot more of these type of rulings before she shows up?\par \par \uc2 GERSTEIN: Well, I don`t think they`re going to have that many more opportunities. But they do still have a bunch of cases, I think seven left to go that we expect to get next week, including, as Dahlia says some things that might sound pretty wonky and boring, but are very, very important involving the powers of regulatory agencies like the \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2 , you know, the conservative movement has always been sort of a two pronged movement, one on the social issues, and two, on trying to tear down the federal government`s regulatory state. And that part of the project is also very much alive and very much in the works and, you know, may even have more support at the Supreme Court than the social conservative agenda does. And we`ve got that case to be decided next week. And of course, the big case on the agenda for the fall, when Justice Jackson gets there, a pair of cases involving affirmative action might be the next big decision of the court that really grabs major headlines.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Barb, tell us a little bit about a case that`s going to go on in your state. We`re all watching Michigan, to see what happens with a law there that has been on the books since 1931. What can you tell us and why could it matter to the rest of the country?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Yeah, so in Michigan, we have one of those laws, sometimes referred to as a zombie law. It`s not the same as a trigger law, which was passed after Roe to come into place if Roe was ever overturned. It was already on the books passed, as you said in 1931. And it became moot in 1973 when Roe was decided. But it was still on the books. And so if Roe is eliminated, then that means that that law comes back to life that`s hence the name zombie.\par \par \uc2 But the law was the subject of a recent lawsuit Planned Parenthood and our governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  have each filed lawsuits challenging that under the Michigan Constitution. And so far, a judge has entered a preliminary injunction, saying that it appears that they may be likely to succeed on the merits and holding it in abeyance until she can decide the case on the merits.\par \par \uc2 And I think the lesson for the rest of the country is this idea that these battles now may need to be fought within the states, perhaps their state constitutional provisions that can protect a woman`s right to choose. It`s not explicit in the Michigan Constitution but there are some provisions there`s a due process clause and an Equal Protection Clause. And it`s -- the issue is about bodily autonomy, the same one that was used, by the way, to challenge vaccines and masking and shutdown orders earlier in the pandemic. And so there may be a lesson there for other states.\par \par \uc2 [00:15:25]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Dahlia, even before the ruling yesterday, the Supreme Court had the lowest approval rating, Americans had the least amount of confidence in the court that we`ve had since they have been monitoring this, that may be a really negative number, which it is, but at the end of the day, doesn`t matter. We might not have confidence in the court, but their rulings still stand and have a lot of power.\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: It`s a great question. And, you know, I think it`s important to just stipulate from the get-go, that we don`t want or that are slave to popular opinion, right? One of the reasons that we have lifetime tenure and political free, affords justices, massive, massive protection is because sometimes they have to do unpopular things. Think about Brown before, right?\par \par \uc2 Fundamental rights or fundamental rights, it doesn`t matter what the polling numbers show. All that said, I think both yesterday`s done case, and today`s abortion case, signal, not just that the six justices in this conservative super majority are speaking for, you know, 17%, 18% of the population that want these radical outcome, but that the justices are actually a part of this persistent effort, as they said, to shrink the vote. And the more you bless, you know, vote suppression laws, the more you bless, partisan gerrymandering, the more you say, oh, you know, this is too hard for us. It`s unjustifiable, and therefore, you know, states can continue to constrict the vote.\par \par \uc2 The Justice don`t just become a sort of minoritarian check. They become part of reinforcing -- persistently reinforcing minority rule. And I think that`s what we`ve kind of slid into without entirely reckoning with it, that now we have a court that is persistently making it harder for majority views and wishes and policies to be effectuated. That`s not necessarily doing something that`s constitutional. That`s just raw power.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Judges, they matter. Dahlia Lithwick, Barbara McQuade, Josh Gerstein, thank you all for joining us tonight. I really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, the battle for abortion rights is deeply personal for people all across this country. But for Congresswoman Jackie Speier, possibly even more, she`s here to reflect on today`s historic reversal from the Supreme Court and weigh in on what is next for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Plus later, as some states are working to fight back and protect access to abortion if they can, but are they prepared for the increase in new patients? Don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: This has nothing to do with the Constitution. This has to do with a cabal of people that think they can, I`m sorry.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I have to get rid of all of these. Who think that they -- how do they have a right to me or any woman what she can do with your body?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [00:23:19]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JACKIE SPEIER, (D) CALIFORNIA: I had a procedure at 17 weeks pregnant with a child I lost a baby. But for you to stand on this floor and to suggest as you have that somehow this is a procedure that is either welcomed or done cavalierly or done without any thought is preposterous.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Back in 2011, Congresswoman Jackie Speier was the first member of Congress to share her own abortion story on the House floor. She and her husband wanted to see their pregnancy through but the fetus was not viable and the procedure was necessary for her health. Since then she has fought for the right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 I want to welcome California Congresswoman Jackie Speier. Congresswoman, I`ve said it before I`ll say it again, thank you for sharing your personal story. It`s important, it`s valuable. I know you`ve spoken for the last 10 years about your experience. What was your reaction? What was this like for you today after the ruling?\par \par \uc2 SPEIER: You know, Stephanie, I went over to the Supreme Court early and the only people that were there were the protesters who were pro-birth, not pro-life. And I thought, you know, they had been tipped off. And I was disgusted by it. This is the greatest setback for women`s rights in the history of this country. It pains me to think that I have had this right and that my daughter won`t have this right.\par \par \uc2 [00:25:10]\par \par \uc2 I just can`t believe that we are at this point. And your earlier commentator said, well, you know, they were telegraphing that. Well, we thought maybe it was going to be a 15 week. We thought maybe the Roberts` position was going to be what was going to happen, but to have the whole law just dead is, it`s a gut punch.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Can we go back to what you just said a moment? Did I hear you right? When you went over to the Supreme Court this morning, you`re saying anti- abortion activists were the only ones there? You believe they were tipped off by the court?\par \par \uc2 SPEIER: I think they were tipped off, because it was quite early. It was just right after the decision came out. We were voting on the floor. I thought I was going to run over. And I thought this is odd. There aren`t any pro-choice people here yet. And these folks were there. So I do believe -- I believe they were tipped off. I don`t have any evidence of that.\par \par \uc2 But, you know, more importantly, the Supreme Court now has become a weapon of the far right. These two decisions, one on guns, and one on choice really sets us back in ways that I don`t think we can totally comprehend. On the one decision, they basically say the states can have this power about concealed carry. So everyone has the freedom to carry. On the abortion issue, they`re saying, we`re going to send it back to the states. And we`re going to force you to carry, its government mandated pregnancy. I mean, think about that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So if what you`re saying is the far right or the right controls the Supreme Court, Democrats have the Senate -- Democrats have the White House and Congress, what can you do right now to combat what`s happening?\par \par \uc2 SPEIER: So there`s a number of things we must do. First is to educate women throughout this country that medication abortion is \uc2 FDA\uc2  approved that it`s safe and effective. In fact, it`s used in 54% of the abortions that take place in this country. We have to get this out to them. It`s a pill you take, and it terminates the pregnancy for up to 11 weeks.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And can you get access to that pill -- hold on, can you get access to that pill in states like Texas and Oklahoma?\par \par \uc2 SPEIER: Yes. I believe you`re going to be able to access that pill. Now, you`re going to have to get it from a doctor who is out of state, but they can interfere with the interstate commerce and the Postal Service, I don`t believe. So there`s things we`re going to have to do to shore up protection for a health care professionals who want to do that.\par \par \uc2 There are some of my friends who are now going to get licensed in Texas for the specific purpose of being able to prescribe the drug. The other thing I think we did we`ve got to make it as easy on women as possible. I`m reaching out to the San Francisco airport personnel to see if we can create clinics that typically are medical clinics that are at airports, maybe that`s where we can beef up the ability there to provide those services.\par \par \uc2 The other thing we have to do is we have got to use the nuclear option on filibuster. And by that, I mean, we`ve got to get to more U.S. senators who believe as we do that choice is important that gun violence prevention is important. Get them elected to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  that will take us to 51 members of the Senate that can overturn the filibuster, and then we could do things like pass a Women`s Health Care Protection Act.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So that`s something you would look to do after the midterms. Today, you think you can get \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  on board for that after a ruling like this?\par \par \uc2 SPEIER: I don`t have a lot of confidence in getting them on board. Certainly, \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  came out and said that Gorsuch and Kavanaug lied to him. I mean, these are two Supreme Court nominees, three actually, that took an oath, swore that they were going to tell the truth, the whole truth and nothing but the truth. And they sat down and said that Roe was settled law that it was precedent, and within two years, they have absolutely turned it on its head. So maybe that is making him think, more willingly to maybe narrowly and the filibuster for this issue of women`s choice.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But they don`t face any consequences for being misleading in their confirmation hearings. \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  could say, I`m mad or I`m disappointed, they lied to me, but he can`t do anything about it. Is there anything that the President can do today, tomorrow to protect women in this country?\par \par \uc2 [00:30:08]\par \par \uc2 SPEIER: I think we have to galvanize support around the country. I have recommended to the President. We should have a summit on women`s autonomy and right to make these decisions on her own. Do it in Washington, bring women from across this country together, to talk about ways that we can guarantee that women have access.\par \par \uc2 You know, I chair, the Military Personnel Subcommittee, I am deeply concerned about all of the bases we have in the south, that now it`s going to require our soldiers and sailors and airmen to transport themselves 1000s of miles away to get abortions. They can`t get an abortion now at the military treatment facilities, but they can get it in the communities. Now, they`re going to have to go many states away. It`s going to affect readiness in the military as well.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: More talking and listening is very good. But decisive action changes things. The Supreme Court took decisive action today. And that`s impacted women across the country. Is there any action you would like to see the President take immediately?\par \par \uc2 SPEIER: Well, the President has looked at the options and I think he is determined that he doesn`t have the ability under an executive order to do more than make sure that the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has the medication abortion readily available everywhere. I think beyond that, we`ve got to put our heads together and see if there`s anything else we can do. Half the women in this country now, half of them are not going to have ready access to abortion services, because of this decision. And the states that have already utilized trigger laws, or will be passing laws that will prohibit access to abortion. So here we have one decision where you have the freedom to carry a concealed weapon, and then another decision that`s forcing you to carry a fetus to term.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And what can be done about it? Nothing. When the Supreme Court makes a decision, their decision is set. Congresswoman Jackie Speier, thank you.\par \par \uc2 SPEIER: But we could have beat them. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yes, that is an option. Congresswoman Jackie Speier, thank you for joining us this evening.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, has some states are working hard to protect abortion rights? We`ll be speaking to the Attorney General of the State of California about what is next for his state after the historic ruling when our special extended edition of the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [00:37:21]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 GAVIN NEWSOM\uc2 , (D) CALIFORNIA: I want to take this moment and sign legislation to push back against those Republican, state legislators and governors that seek to move forward with civil actions against people that wish to travel to the state of California seeking their reproductive rights and reproductive freedoms.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That legislation signed today by California`s Governor protecting anyone. Anyone seeking abortions in California from civil actions in other states. \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  also repeated California`s commitment to being a sanctuary for those seeking reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 With us tonight California Attorney General \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 . He`s running for reelection. Mr. Attorney General, unlike a lot of other states, you were not surprised you saw this coming. You have been preparing the state of California. What`s next there?\par \par \uc2 ROB BONTA, CALIFORNIA ATTORNEY GENERAL: We have a lot in the works. We`ve been preparing for months. But this moment, of course, hoping for the best but preparing for the worst. We had the preview with leaked draft decision in Dobbs. So we have an active legislature who have been incredible partners, who have 14 bills moving towards the governor`s desk. Of course, the governor signed today that bill that prevents any cooperation or assistance from the state of California on civil actions against individuals seeking abortion or providers providing abortion.\par \par \uc2 And there`s a bill that I`m supporting, sponsoring, that will also prevent cooperation and assistance in California by law enforcement when it comes to criminal actions being brought against individuals who are seeking reproductive health care and an abortion in California.\par \par \uc2 We have $125 million in the proposed budget that will be expanding access to reproductive health care, expanding access to abortion, supporting women in California and from out of state. We also have another bill I`m supporting that makes it clear that you shall not criminalize pregnancy loss in the state of California. And meanwhile, the legislature and the governor are working on, putting on the ballot for the voters of the state of California in November, a constitutional amendment enshrining in the California Constitution, the right to reproductive health care, reproductive freedom, the right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: For you and the governor, it was not a question of, if it was a question of when they were going to overturn Roe and you just laid it out you are well prepared for this. Why do you think so many other states aren`t and we`re hearing well, we got to get ready, we got to do something now, why are so many other surprise?\par \par \uc2 [00:40:03]\par \par \uc2 BONTA: I can`t speak for them. You know, this is part of who we are in California, what makes us special which it`s in our DNA, to stand up for our values to fight, to prepare, to have care and compassion for our people, to do everything we can to have their back. And knowing that a threat was coming that was on the horizon and getting nearer. We leaped into action. And we did it. I`m very proud to say, all hands on deck, from our leaders in the legislature in both houses, our governor, our \uc2 California Department of Justice\uc2 , my team as California Attorney General are advocates and activists in the nonprofit world, all working together, we had a future of an abortion Council created where we were preparing for this moment, starting months ago. Because we saw the tea leaves. We saw what was possible. Of course, we were -- thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then are you prepared for this massive influx of patients of women you`re likely to see over the next few months, do Californians want that?\par \par \uc2 BONTA: We are preparing for them. We are a haven when it comes to reproductive health care, a sanctuary. Anyone who seeks reproductive health care, who seeks an abortion, you can seek that in California. We have -- the right to an abortion is alive and well in in California. It is untouched by the Dobbs decision today. And we are a strong reproductive freedom state. So there will be many individuals unfortunately, who are being hurt and harmed by the trigger laws and other laws that will ban abortion in those states. We anticipate 26 states will ban abortion and the women and pregnant persons in those states will need somewhere to go. We invite them to come to California. It will be a huge influx. We are still preparing logistically and financially for that. We`re not working alone as a government. We also have our philanthropy, our private sector, also in support that share our values here in California. So we will meet the moment and we will rise to the occasion.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, you have got your work cut out for you sir. Attorney General \uc2 Rob Bonta\uc2 , thank you so much for joining us this evening. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 BONTA: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, she warned her fellow graduates and she was just a high school student about a war on her body and a war on her rights. The Texas valedictorian who sounded the alarm joins us next when the extended edition of the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Unfortunately, people are going to die. Women are going to die between now and when we can get legislation passed, which is untenable. And I don`t know how we`ve let it get to this point. It`s just very disappointing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [00:47:28]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PAXTON SMITH, FORMER LAKE HIGHLANDS HIGH SCHOOL VALEDICTORIAN: I have dreams and hopes and ambitions, every girl graduating today does. And we have spent our entire lives working towards our future and without our input and without our consent or control over that future has been stripped away from us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Paxton Smith gave that speech just over a year ago back when the most restrictive abortion law in Texas essentially banned the procedure around six weeks. Now, with the demise of Roe, Texas is one of 13 states ready to take those restrictions even further. Trigger laws expected to go into effect within 30 days banning abortions across the board. Even in cases of incest or rape.\par \par \uc2 Some clinics in the state warn abortion services have already ceased altogether. With me now, Paxton Smith, she`s the Texas high school valedictorian who swapped her speech to speak out against restrictive abortion laws. Paxton, thank you for being here tonight. Your speech is more relevant today than ever. What`s your reaction to the loss of Roe?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It`s been incredibly disappointing. But I think that the feeling that I`m feeling most is fear about what the future brings. As you know, I`m from Texas, I still live in Texas. And in my home state they are going to criminalize abortion within the next 30 days, which means that abortion providers could spend up to life in prison now for providing abortion care.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Is that what the majority of Texans want? You`re a college student at U.T. When you walk around campus, is that what your fellow students want?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: It`s certainly not what my fellow students want. Although grants in U.T. Austin is quite a liberal campus. But I would say in general, the majority of Americans do not want Roe v. Wade to overturn. The majority of Americans want abortion to be safe and legal and accessible.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The Supreme Court isn`t going to change their mind at least not today, tomorrow or the next day. So do you think young activist, young voters like yourselves in the state of Texas are going to take action after this decision was made? And after these more restrictive laws are going to be put in place very soon in Texas?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I think so. And I hope so, I know that my generation is definitely going to be taken to the polls and is going to be voting for politicians that favor our human rights and that will pass legislation that will value our human rights. What I`m really hoping for though is that people of my generation are going to take to the streets and are going to be consistent and their fight in the streets because part of what we`re seeing now is that people are going out and protesting for one or two days, and then staying at home and waiting for the next big event. But I think what it`s going to take to get this right back, it`s going to take a lot more coordinated and consistent action from the pro-choice side.\par \par \uc2 [00:50:25]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And what is the goal, protesting in the streets, you`re not going to change the mind of the Supreme Court? So what is your goal? What do you want to happen practically speaking?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I know that we`re not going to change people`s minds. We all have our beliefs, and we`re all setting our beliefs. But what I want to do is what I want to show that people in these positions of power, that we are not going to stand for them taking away our rights, we are not going to stand for that at all. And I want what I envision is people taking to the streets and demonstrating that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: How about in Texas? Have you ever -- I mean, after you gave that speech? How did your high school take it? How did your town take it? Could you get a meeting with Governor Greg Abbott?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: I did not get a meeting with Governor Greg Abbott. But the reaction to my speech was actually overwhelmingly positive. I received hundreds and hundreds of messages of support and love. And since giving that speech, I`ve received a lot of opportunities to continue my activism at the scale that I do now. And that`s something that I do every day.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You got those messages in that support from Texans or nationally, what I`m trying to get at is, if your voice, if your perspective is the future of your state, why does your state make the rules and restrictions that they do? And I`m obviously not accusing you of that, I`m trying to get inside the lone star state to understand why it is the way it is.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Right. Well, politicians are not necessarily making legislation that reflects the views of the people in the state. That`s not something that people of my generation can necessarily control, especially since we`re just now getting the right to vote. I just voted in my first election last semester, a few months ago. And I think it`s -- rest assured people are going to be taking to the polls, people are going to be vote -- be voting these people out of office the best that they can. But right now they are not working in favor of their constituents and in favor of their constituents` beliefs.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, Paxton Smith, thank you so much for joining us this evening. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Coming up, the complex story of the woman behind this bitter national debate that began half a century ago, when the extended edition of the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES, NEW YORK ATTORNEY GENERAL: We`ve organized providers as well as medical professionals in the state of New York and we are prepared to represent individuals. We`re prepared to train legal professionals as well as medical professionals in anticipation of individuals who might be prosecuted in their home state. We`re looking at expedition, we`re looking at subpoenas, we`re looking at all of that, we are ready. We will not again bow down to the radical right, we will stand up and protect a woman`s right to choose, protect the 14th Amendment. And not allow women to be treated like second class citizens.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [00:57:58]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 NORMA MCCORVEY: Once the Supreme Court decision was done in `73, I thought like, well, OK, we`re all safe and legal now. We can have control over our own bodies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go on this very late night, knowing Roe. Norma McCorvey better known as Jane Roe is a complicated person in our nation`s divided history. When Norma was just 16 years old, she was already married and pregnant, divorce by the time the baby arrived. She gave up custody to her mother. A few years later, Norma was pregnant again. She gave that child up for adoption. Then in 1969, when she became pregnant for a third time, Nora was dealing with addiction and living in poverty, and she wanted an abortion. NBC`s Ron Mott tells us her story.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RON MOTT, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Her quest began not as a movement for women`s rights but more simply an unmarried 22-year-old looking to end her third pregnancy in Texas, where the procedure was illegal unless the woman`s life was in danger. Despite her ultimate victory in the case, she had her baby, the legal process pushing well beyond nine months. The landmark ruling eventually propelled McCorvey out of the shadows of her pseudonym, and into the spotlight of perhaps the country`s most divisive social issue.\par \par \uc2 For a time she became the face of pro-abortion rights. But later in the mid-90s, she announced that she had become a born again Christian, and then an about face became an anti-abortion advocate appearing in ads.\par \par \uc2 MCCORVEY: I realized that my case which legalized abortion on demand was the biggest mistake of my life.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It was not until 2017 just before her death, when Norma McCorvey finally came forward with what she called a deathbed confession. She said she was never really against abortion. She was just paid to say that she was. A reminder, a stark reminder of the very complex and very personal story of Roe. A story that launched the bitter debate, one that tonight is clearly far from over.\par \par \uc2 [01:00:05]\par \par \uc2 We needed a deep breath. And on that very heavy and very serious note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late with us. It has been a privilege to be here with you.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220625ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with Lawrence O`Donnell for June 24, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow, Lawrence O`Donnell \par \uc2 6,997 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 DAHLIA LITHWICK, SENIOR EDITOR AND LEGAL CORRESPONDENT, SLATE.COM: So, I think that the court not only got out of step, deeply out of step with public opinion. By the way, not just on this, but on the guns case yesterday whereby similar margin. Americans hate this outcome.\par \par \uc2 But I think that the utter lack of solicitude for the reality of the trauma that is being imposed, both by mass shootings yesterday and by unbelievable terror of prosecution, of being spied on, of vigilantism, that rebounds from today -- the fact that the court didn`t even think beyond saying, yes, I guess there`s going to be some outcomes, not on us to worry about it. I think that that is what at the heart of what is so, so maddening today.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Dahlia Lithwick, senior editor and legal editor for slate.com. Dahlia, it`s a really important day. Thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 LITHWICK: Thanks, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Alright, that is going to do it for us for now. I will see you again on Monday. I`ll be back here for "The Rachel Maddow Show" Monday night. Now it`s time for THE LAST WORD with Lawrence O`Donnell. Good evening, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Rachel. It was, I believe, 53 long days ago when I was running through the hallways here with this leaked opinion that I couldn`t believe that I was holding. You had one and just the shock of having a leaked Supreme Court opinion. And then, getting it today was also shocking because the stuff that we and others pointed out in it, that could easily have been cut, was still in there.\par \par \uc2 The quoting, you know, these guys from the 1600 in England who thought witches should be tried and executed. The witch trial moral authorities are still in this opinion. They are still being quoted as a guidance for \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes. Justice Alito and the majority, they were not taking pains to make sure that this would be broadly accepted. That was not the goal of this work. This was just raw power, doing it because they could. And they`re not trying to bring anybody along with them.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s, I think, part of the reason that you`ve had so much discussion today about what`s next because if they`re willing to do this on this, which they knew would be the most divisive possible thing in the country, obviously, they would do it on things for which they think they had even more leeway to operate.\par \par \uc2 That, we don`t care element to this is, I think, is going to be a profoundly important part of the way this changes the country.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And when you think of the presidents who made this happen, beginning with \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , but more importantly now, George H. W. Bush, because he has \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  on the Supreme Court. There are no Reagan justice there now. But from George H. W. Bush to \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , to Ronald -- to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , it is just a statistic likelihood without even getting into their life histories.\par \par \uc2 It`s a statistical likelihood, but at least one of them has personally in some sense participated in an abortion, possibly by paying for it as is very common. And the idea that they actually really believed this stuff that they were saying on the campaign trail, and really wanted it to happen, is something I have never accepted about any one of them ever.\par \par \uc2 But the people they put on the Supreme Court didn`t get the memo that that stuff is just campaign talk.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It`s interesting, too, to see the right, to see the anti-abortion right, and see the Republicans certainly pleased with what happens today, but also worried about the electoral consequences of this because they are the dog that cut the car here, in a way. I mean, it`s one thing to crusade about the horrors of abortion and why we need to get rid of it and how terrible abortion is and how if you elect me, we`ll get rid of it, we`ll get rid of it.\par \par \uc2 Then you do get rid of it, and then you`re the person responsible for America being a country without right is no longer available to all American women. And that`s something -- that`s a kind of accountability that I`m not sure that they are prepared for. I was thinking about the fact that President Biden is going to the G7 tomorrow, right?\par \par \uc2 So, it`s going to be -- he`s going to be going to go talk to the leaders of Canada and France and the U.K. and Italy and Japan and Germany, and one of the things he has to tell them is, you know, oh yes, we just banned abortion in our democracy yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Wait, aren`t the American people against that 2 to 1? Yes, but \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , so, I mean, this is -- it`s hard to -- I think it`s hard to explain in democratic terms and I think it`s going to be hard to defend at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And he doesn`t have to tell them because it`s headlines worldwide and I think it was seven hours ago where I saw the tweet from the president of France, registering his objections to \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  decision, which only applies within the United States. I believe that`s the first time that`s ever happened that a president --\par \par \uc2 [22:05:00]\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I was looking at the Scottish premiere talking about this as one of the darkest days in memory for women`s rights. Like the United States is supposed to be leading on this. We`re not supposed to be like the Central American backsliding dictatorship, right? We`re supposed to be the beacon for the world in terms of rights, the expansion of rights and human liberty. And, yes, every other major democracy, every other mature democracy in the world is freaking out, looking at how -- what a basket case we are.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, you know, the solution is that boring sounding solution of voting, voting, voting, and the people who understood that are the voters who put those Republican presidents in office. They understood what those votes were about.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes. The other solutions are really practical about helping women become outlaws in order to continue to make decisions about their bodies and their lives and coming up with ways to protect women while taking them outside the law, in order to do what they need to do. And that is, that`s a very different country than we`re all used to.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And that story is just beginning tonight.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yes. Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Rachel. Well, I have read many Supreme Court opinions that I have disagreed with, but whose legal scholarship I have respected. This is not one of those decisions. The current Supreme Court is not reflexively worthy of respect. They have not earned it.\par \par \uc2 The current Supreme Court harbors the most corrupted Supreme Court justice in history who infamously ruled on a case involving his wife`s attempt to encourage people to commit election fraud. The court contains three justices who was names were handed to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  by \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Donald trump, a candidate who came in second in the presidential election of 2016, but then came in first in the Electoral College. He`s not the only second place candidate who got to be president and got to appoint a Supreme Court justice, including the justice who`s the author of this opinion.\par \par \uc2 The author of today`s opinion, \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 , was appointed by \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , who was the first president in history to get fewer votes than his opponent, but still win the Electoral College.\par \par \uc2 The current Supreme Court is not a product of democracy. It is a product of minority rule. It is the product of the corruption of constitutional processes by Senate Republicans who refused to even allow a vote on President Obama`s final choice of a Supreme Court justice. The Republican judges on the Supreme Court share a dangerous Trumpian characteristic. They are incapable of embarrassment.\par \par \uc2 The Trump appointed judges do not take their appointments to the Supreme Court with modesty, recognizing that most voters in the country voted against the president who nominated them. \uc2 Neil Gorsuch\uc2  shows no embarrassment in occupying a Supreme Court seat stolen from President Obama by \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and Senate Republicans.\par \par \uc2 I never said anything like this about the Supreme Court before the Trump years. I spent most of my life in awe of the Supreme Court. I always wish there were more justices on the court who agreed with me, but I always believed in the legitimacy of the electoral process and the confirmation process that delivered them to the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 The crisis of the very legitimacy of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  fills me with sorrow. The same sorrow expressed by the three justices on the Supreme Court who represent the views of a majority of the American people, the last line of the 66-page dissent, written by \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2 , \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , and Elana Kagan says, "With sorrow, for this court, but more for the many millions of American women who have today, lost a fundamental constitutional protection, we dissent."\par \par \uc2 They dissent from the majority opinions reliance on the moral authority on the subject of abortion of two English aristocrats, who in the 1630s believed that witches should be tried in court before being put to death. The Supreme Court opinion revoking a constitutional right for the first time in history relies on men who believed in witches and believed they should be executed, and it does so without embarrassment.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:03]\par \par \uc2 They dissent from the majorities belief that the 14th amendments guaranteed constitutional right to liberty applies only to the legally recognized liberties that existed in 1868 when the 14th amendment was written. \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  embraces that view without embarrassment, even though in 1868 he would not have had the liberty of interracial marriage that he now enjoys.\par \par \uc2 The dissent points out the 14th amendments ratifier`s did not give black and white people the right to marry each other. But 100 years later, the Supreme Court relied on the 14th amendment to make interracial marriage a constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 Twenty years after that, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  use that constitutional right in the state of Nebraska where interracial marriage was illegal until 1963. Interracial marriage was still a novel concept for Virginia Thomas` white relatives in Nebraska, in the Midwest, even after she married \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . In 1991, when \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` nomination to the Supreme Court was announced, Virginia Thomas` aunt and uncle spoke to the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " saying, "I can guarantee you I was surprised when I found out she was going with a black man," Ginni Thomas`s uncle, Ralph Knop, said from his farm in Iowa. "It was unusual for us, but he was so nice. We forgot he was black." Her aunt, Opal, added, "And he treated her so well, all of his other qualities made up for his being black."\par \par \uc2 Made up for his being black. They said that in 1991. In a country where most people thought it took other qualities to make up for being black, the Supreme Court found in the 14th amendment the liberty for \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  to marry the woman he loved. And today, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  cannot find the liberty for women in the 14th amendment. And he is not embarrassed about that.\par \par \uc2 Not long ago, the surest way to embarrass the Supreme Court justice would be to point out an inconsistency. Not anymore. The dissent hurls embarrassment at the majority opinion knowing that the majority created by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cannot feel embarrassment. The dissent says, "embarrassingly for the majority, early law in fact does provide some support for abortion rights. Common-law authorities did not treat abortion as a crime before quickening, the point when the fetus moved in the womb. And early American law followed the common-law rule. So, the criminal law of that early time might be taken as roughly consonant with Roe`s and Casey`s different treatment of early and late abortions."\par \par \uc2 We will have more on the embarrassing, amateur historians work in today`s Supreme Court opinion later in this hour with a professional legal historian. The Republican judges on the Supreme Court are now and never have been, and perhaps never will be, embarrassed by their cruelty.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES OF AMERICA: State laws banning abortion are automatically taking effect today jeopardizing the health of millions of women, some without exceptions. So extreme that a women can be punished for protecting their health. So extreme that women and girls are forced to bear their rapist child. With a child of consequence, it just stuns me.\par \par \uc2 So extreme that doctors will be criminalized for fulfilling their duty, to care. Imagine having young women having 