cess, that we need to make sure we are allowing families to decide how they want to live, and what healthcare decisions they want to have.\par \par \uc2 If we want to be a pro-life state, let`s stop putting guns in the hands of individuals who are shooting people, innocent children. We have to do a better job at protecting the families we have here in Tennessee, and stop the bad legislation that is coming down the line that will further -- stop other humans in this state for being able to make decisions about how they want to live their lives.\par \par \uc2 No longer will we have -- be able to rely on the Supreme Court and the federal government for giving basic human rights to all people. It is now in the hands of individuals in this state who no longer who wants to make this state back to what it was in the early 1900s.\par \par \uc2 We`re in 2022, we need to be working to make sure families have everything that they need, our education systems are fully funded and families have health care access.\par \par \uc2 Until we get there, we need to leave women`s rights alone and create a state where everyone can thrive.\par \par \uc2 But unfortunately, I`m expecting this upcoming session where there are going to be tons of legislation are going to hurt families. But I`m encouraging everyone watching right now to please engage more in January and go to the polls in August and November, because we have a chance to stop this and reverse this if we act now.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tennessee State Senator, not Representative, my apologies, State Senator London Lamar, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 If there is one person in the Senate you want to hear from tonight, it is Elizabeth Warren. She joins me next. So, don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:43:31]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. ALEXANDRIA OCASIO-CORTEZ (D-NY): This is a cementing of my minority rule in a lot of ways. And we`re going to have to fight a long road to get it back. But we`re not going to give up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, the Supreme Court has taken away the 50-year constitutional right to an abortion. Democrats in Congress need to see what exactly they can do, what laws they can pass to restore and protect the right of access to reproductive care across the country.\par \par \uc2 One senator pushing for that, Senator Elizabeth Warren, Democrat of Massachusetts join me now.\par \par \uc2 First senator, I know you`ve been very outspoken today in that protests, I want to just get what you`re thinking and feeling right now, you know, 10 hours after this decision was handed down?\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): So, look, I am furious. I am furious, because an extremist minority, six justices of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  think that they have the power and therefore the right to impose their personal moral and religious views on the rest of an unwilling America. That is wrong. And that is why I am in this fight. And that is why millions of people across this country are going to be in this fight.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court does not get the final word here. We will put a stop to this.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, what does that mean? Why have they not? I mean, I think a lot of people feel like they have at least for the short term.\par \par \uc2 WARREN: Sure they have for the short term. And that means we have to respond in the short term. That means right now, and you`ve been talking to people who are doing it all across this country, we need to be helping women who are pregnant right now, women who get pregnant next week or next month, and need help because they live in one of the states that is banning abortion.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:12]\par \par \uc2 WARREN: And we should be doing volunteering time, volunteering money. We also are calling on the President of the United States to use all of the administrative tools that are available right now.\par \par \uc2 Medication abortions are more than half of all abortions in the country, not a clinical procedure. But there are a lot of restrictions on access to that medication that are not medically necessary.\par \par \uc2 I want -- we`d like to see the president loosen those up. There are other places the president could act to dampen the ability of these companies to track women who travel out of state to an abortion clinic, to provide funds for people who are traveling and to look into the use of federal lands to be able to create opportunities for people who need access to abortion to get it.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s the thing we need to do in the interim term. The president could get that up and running in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 The third thing we need to do, though, is to be focused on the election coming in November. And we need to be laser-focused. Remember how we got here. We got here, not because the Republicans said, you know, let`s debate, abortion, and let`s go with majority rule. We got here because a concentrated small group, intensely focused on this issue, and they voted on it and voted on it and voted on it.\par \par \uc2 Now, this time, for the first time in November 2022, Roe vs. Wade is going to be on the ballot for everyone, Democrats, Republicans, independents, and this is our chance to be heard at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 Right now, we need to be asking every elected official up and down the ballot. Where do you stand on Roe vs. Wade? And if you`re not willing to speak out, then you`re not somebody I would want to vote for.\par \par \uc2 We also need to ask everyone who`s running for the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . Are you willing to get rid of the filibuster so that when we get another senator who is willing to make Roe vs. Wade the law of the land, we can actually get it done.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ve got a lot of plans here, a lot of work to do. Nobody is giving up. We take this anger, we take it and channel the energy of it into protecting the people across this country who need us.\par \par \uc2 You know, Chris --\par \par \uc2 HAYES: To that point -- yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 WARREN: You know who`s going to be hurt the hardest in this? I lived in a world where abortion was illegal. Now, people still got abortions. Well, to do women who could travel to another state, who could go to another country, they still had access. Other women still try. They ended up scarred for life. They ended up some taking their own lives, rather than facing a pregnancy that they could not bear.\par \par \uc2 Today, the consequences of this decision will fall hardest on poor women, it will fall hardest on women of color who already face higher maternal mortality rates. It will fall hardest on mamas who already are working three jobs to support the children they have. It will fall hardest on 14- year-olds who are raped by their uncles or their coaches. That`s who`s going to pay the real price today. And that`s why we have to decide as Americans.\par \par \uc2 Are we just going to say, well, now I despair. Now it`s over or we`re going to say Supreme Court doesn`t get the last word? In a democracy, we got the last word. It`s time to get in this fight and it`s time to make real change on behalf of everyone in this country.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator Elizabeth Warren of The State of Massachusetts on a brutal and long and difficult day. Thank you so much for making some time for us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Ahead, the fight for abortion access in post-rural America as the senator just alluded to. I`ll talk to the president of the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2  about what happens next, right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:53:59]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There are massive protests at this hour all across the country after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v Wade this morning.\par \par \uc2 64 percent of Americans opposed overturning Roe in a Marist Poll just last month. That`s a two-thirds majority, you don`t see for just about anything in this country.\par \par \uc2 And so, hundreds of people are out and protesting in New York City night gathering in on the streets of Denver, Colorado. On the west coast in Seattle, Washington outside the Supreme Court in Washington, D.C.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , president and CEO of the \uc2 Center for Reproductive Rights\uc2  has made securing abortion rights her life`s work, and she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Nancy, we`ve talked many times before, you`ve litigated on Roe and attacks on Roe for much of your career up to and including the Supreme Court. What`s your reaction to the ruling today?\par \par \uc2 NANCY NORTHUP\uc2 , PRESIDENT AND CEO, CENTER FOR REPRODUCTIVE RIGHTS: Well, it`s been a devastating day. I mean, the Supreme Court did what it`s never done in history, which is to take away an individual personal liberty and did so in a way that you know, makes women and their autonomies over their bodies and their ability to decide on their futures. Second class and unequal citizens as the dissents put it.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:12]\par \par \uc2 NORTHUP: So, it`s been a very devastating day. It`s been a hard day as our attorneys at the Center of Reproductive Rights have been on the phone with clients across the nation. We represent clinics, including the Jackson Women`s Health Organization, in this case in the Supreme Court, and they`re having to make decisions about what to do next.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s a very wrenching time. And first and foremost and in front is what Senator Warren just talked about, which is the impact it`s going to have on people.\par \par \uc2 I mean, today`s decision is going to change people`s lives. And there are going to be women who are simply not going to be able to get access to the abortion care that they would like to and are going to be forced to carry pregnancies to terms.\par \par \uc2 And other people are going to be caught up in this people who are having miscarriages. It is just an astonishing decision in which we have been thrown back more than 50 years.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Your organization serves as legal counsel for many abortion providers, particularly providers in states like Mississippi or Missouri, places where, you know, the governing party, the Republican Party is extremely hostile, is trying to make life incredibly difficult. Many of those states now have trigger laws in effect.\par \par \uc2 How should people be thinking about trying to work towards ensuring access to care for those populations, women and other pregnant folks in those states who are now living under this new regime within the day of how -- what can be done starting today to get them access to care?\par \par \uc2 NORTHUP: Yes, so as you pointed out, 13 states have trigger bans, some purport to go into effect right away, like in the state of Louisiana, and some might take you know, a week or two weeks or three weeks, depending on the laws.\par \par \uc2 But there are much that people can do. Number one, what you`re showing on your screen, people taking to the streets is a very important thing for everybody to do and to continue to do because the vast majority of people who want access to safe and legal abortion need to be made visible.\par \par \uc2 Second, they can support abortion funds, because abortion funds are helping to raise money they have for years, I`ve been doing this work for years to help people be able to pay for their abortion care and now, having to travel maybe hundreds or thousands of miles from their home, communities to get access to care.\par \par \uc2 They can make sure that their state legislatures in states like New York is going to become a state that is going to be very protective, just passed a slew of laws protecting the ability for people to come to the state of New York and for providers to provide here.\par \par \uc2 And also make sure that their members of Congress and the Biden administration know how important this is to them. So that the administration, as the president said today will be taking steps to ensure to the extent of executive powers access to abortion care.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Senator Warren just pointed out that half of all abortions in the United States are medical abortions, not surgical. Meaning they`re administered with drugs, those drugs have been approved by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  today. The Attorney General reiterating the fact that -- essentially, the supremacy clause means those drugs can be taken anywhere in the U.S. The \uc2 FDA\uc2  has approved them, they can`t be denied, it strikes me that that is going to be one of the next big fights.\par \par \uc2 Because obviously, it`s easier to move for women for whom this would work, it`s easier to move a packet of pills across state lines than to move a person to get abortion care.\par \par \uc2 It strikes me that the Republicans in those states are going to try to crack down on that and that is going to immediately be a front and center of very high-stakes fight.\par \par \uc2 NORTHUP: Absolutely. Medication abortion is the choice by about half of the people getting abortions in the United States today and it is safe and effective for up to 10 weeks, according to the \uc2 FDA\uc2  of pregnancy. And the \uc2 FDA\uc2  has said it`s safe to access by telemedicine.\par \par \uc2 So, this is going to be an important way that people can get access to abortion care in those first 10 weeks. And you`re right, states are going to try. They`ve also -- already some states passed laws banning telemedicine for medication abortion, which is, again, not what our health and safety regulations by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  say is a way that you should be able to access that care.\par \par \uc2 So, this will be a battle, but it`s going to be very, very important and people should understand, it`s a very safe method for being able to get abortions.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 . Thank you so much for making time for us tonight. I really do appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 NORTHUP: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is all in on this Friday night. And on a very, very, very, very dark day in many ways, I am at least glad to say that my colleague, Rachel Maddow begins her show right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Chris.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220625ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for June 24, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Josh Lederman, Julia Ainsley, Barbara McQuade,  Susan Del Percio, Symone Sanders, Ali Vitali, Cal Perry, Jonathan Capehart,  Eugene Daniels \par \uc2 10,734 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Questions right now, including what this ruling overturning Roe v. Wade could mean for rights ranging from privacy to gay marriage. But let`s start with what we know now. Scenes popping up all across the United States like this. These are the live pictures of protests outside the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 . People started gathering immediately once they announced that this decision was coming down. The crowds now are only growing and already no action required.\par \par \uc2 There are new restrictions on abortions ready to go in 13 different states. Look at those. Those are the ones highlighted on your screen. They have what are called trigger laws. They are triggered by the fact that the Supreme Court just overturned Roe. For example -- one example, Idaho`s law would make performing an abortion a felony.\par \par \uc2 We`re also going to dig into that passage from the decision from Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , where he writes "in future cases, we should reconsider all of this Court`s substantive due process precedents, including Griswold, Lawrence, and Obergefell." He`s talking about reconsidering decisions on gay rights and the right to contraception.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, NBC`s Julia Ainsley inside the growing protest outside the Supreme Court, Josh Lederman is at the White House where the president just spoke, and Barbara McQuade, former U.S. attorney, and MSNBC legal analyst. Julia, I`ve been watching that crowd grow bigger literally by the minute. What`s it like there? What`s the mood? What are people telling you?\par \par \uc2 JULIA AINSLEY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Well, here, Chris, if you saw that what we got from the leak just a few weeks ago, would take any steam or pressure off of this situation when the final decision came, you would be wrong. I was there that day. It is even more fraught today. We`re seeing a lot of intense emotions. Some women are crying, people who have had an abortion, some people who feel directly personally violated by this decision today.\par \par \uc2 Actually, the pro-life group has, by and large, left this area, Chris. A lot of them are here to celebrate early on. Now, they have moved off. But there`s still a large number of -- a lot of security. We`ve got barricades all around me here. It`s actually really brought the crowd into a really dense situation. And that`s where we are now.\par \par \uc2 I`ve been speaking to people on both sides of the issue. We`ve heard from women who said that they felt motivated the minute they saw this news on their phone to come here. Also, I`ve spoken to some, at least one person on the pro-life side who said that they think that this is now a cause for celebration and that they hope more states will start to enact laws that would restrict abortions. But as you can see, this is growing louder, bigger, and more fraught by the moment, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We will be checking in with you. Thank you so much for that, Julia. Barbara, I know you`ve had a chance to read through at least some of this opinion. And we`re going to talk about the far-reaching changes this could bring, the things that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  touched on from same-sex marriage to contraception. But let me start by asking you about abortion and how this will fundamentally change the landscape.\par \par \uc2 BARBARA MCQUADE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, for every woman of childbearing age, until today, she had a right to an abortion in this country. And suddenly that right has been yanked away. That means any state that wants to can ban abortion, making it a crime for the provider, or even for the mother. As you highlighted there, at the top of the hour, we`ve got about a dozen states with trigger laws and another dozen or so with zombie laws. And so for those fortunate enough to live in a state with protections, that life continues, but for others who will have to travel or face other hardships, they may be forced to have births they don`t want to give.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And that`s a range right from state to state to state. I mean as we just said, there are places where a doctor who performs an abortion now could go to prison, right?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: A doctor could. And I think we could even see mothers being subjected to criminal laws. And what`s tricky about this, Chris -- excuse me is that the laws sometimes make exceptions for the life of the mother or for rape or for incest. But how do you prove that? And so what doctor will take on that risk? It will have a chilling effect, I think of shutting down abortion providers in any state.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Josh, we just heard from President Biden, it was a forceful criticism. So what`s next for this White House? One of the things he said that struck me was this is not over.\par \par \uc2 JOSH LEDERMAN, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: That`s right, Chris. And certainly the White House --\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: More is done what has never been done before. Expressly take away a constitutional right. It is so fundamental to so many Americans that had already been recognized. This is a sad day for the country, in my view. But it doesn`t mean the fight`s over. We need to restore the protections of Roe as law of the land. We need to elect officials who will do that. This fall, Roe is on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LEDERMAN: Chris, President Biden, there, making an explicit political argument.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 We don`t often hear him talking that directly about elections and politics, particularly from the cross haul of the White House in a presidential address. But the president clearly making the point to people who are watching him that this is something that they`re going to have to elect people to restore the constitutional right to abortion that the Supreme Court has just said does not exist. President Biden saying there is no executive order he can sign today or any other day that would restore that right. That is something Congress has to do, and that people have to vote for lawmakers who are going to do that.\par \par \uc2 But there were two other very significant messages from the president today. One was about what his administration specifically will try to do. And we know that while the White House has been looking at those options for weeks now, ever since that draft ruling came out, these options are fairly limited. They are things that the White House and the Justice Department and other agencies can do around the margins to try to enforce other laws that may help facilitate women seeking access to abortion.\par \par \uc2 So, the president talked about the Justice Department protecting the ability for women to cross state lines to seek an abortion and in other places, talking about how the Justice Department will not allow states to essentially throw out the judgment of the \uc2 FDA\uc2  that certain abortion medications are legal and safe, and also what his administration is going to try to do to make sure people are not physically blocked from accessing abortion cites in places where it remains legal.\par \par \uc2 But then the third pillar of the president`s remarks that is also so important, had to do with the fear that many officials have right now about the possibility of unrest and even violence in the wake of this decision. President Biden is saying peaceful, peaceful, peaceful, encouraging people to use their First Amendment rights to express their dismay with this opinion but not to take it in a violent direction. That is something the White House is going to be watching very closely as the world and the country reacts to this Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes. And to his second point there, Barbara, I mean, for folks who look at the Supreme Court and think that it often settles a question -- a thorny question, the range of lawsuits that can come out of this abortion ruling is pretty extraordinary, right?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: It is. And I think, you know, this is unlikely to be the last word ever on abortion rights in America. It certainly is a seismic shift from the right that had been protected for the past 50 years. But there are other challenges that could come along. One that comes to mind is a religious challenge, the free exercise of religion. This law, the -- in these states are based on the idea that life begins at conception, but for many religions, life begins later, life begins at birth, or for those who are not part of religious faith, I think we could see those challenges. And those might actually have some merit with this court.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let`s talk very briefly if we can about the other rights that Judge -- Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  talked about that, he thinks to need to be reassessed. In terms of things like gay marriage, in terms of things like contraception, could we be very quickly seeing some action on those fronts?\par \par \uc2 MCQUADE: Well, we would need states to actually pass laws that ban those things. So I don`t know -- I don`t know that either is the popular desire to do that. Gay rights strike me as the one that might be the most endangering gay marriage. I suppose we could see a law enacted in some states. And that could come before the court. And I think based on this idea that there is no substantive due process right to privacy that could put the reasoning of the Obergefell decision in some jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: All right. Thank you so much, Barbara McQuade, appreciate that. Joining me now, New York`s Attorney General Letitia James, thank you so much for being with us. Obviously, we knew this was coming because of the draft that was leaked. But what does overturning Roe, first let me ask you, mean for you as a woman, and as a woman of color? Women of color, as we know, are disproportionately affected by these kinds of rulings.\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES, NEW YORK ATTORNEY GENERAL: This is a dangerous decision. It will up in the lives of individuals seeking safe abortions. It will harm individuals seeking safe abortions. And the reality is, is that there is no law. At the time of fertilization, the rights of women will not be respected. And no government is bound to respect the rights of women at the point of fertilization. It is a travesty.\par \par \uc2 But New York, I want individuals to know that New York is a state that will still allow safe abortions. And so I urge everyone to contact our office and also to contact Planned Parenthood. We will continue to provide safe abortions. We`ve established a fund in the state of New York to pay for individuals traveling to New York to seek abortions, for lodging, and for daycare. This is a state that respects the right of women to choose, that respects the autonomy of individuals over their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 And this is a political decision. And this lies at the feet of a Republican Congress. And ever -- and the only response to this is a political response. And that`s why I urge everyone to vote their conscience and to vote for the fact that this \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , this radical \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  has overturned 50 years of precedent that individuals seeking abortions have relied upon. It is unconscionable. And all that we can do is stand up and fight back by using the power of our vote.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I know that this is something that New York State has been looking at the whole idea of being a place as it has been in the past for reproductive rights refugees, for lack of a better term. What plans have you put in place? And do you believe that you are ready to help women who may need your help from other states where already as of now, their right to an abortion has gone away?\par \par \uc2 JAMES: We anticipated this decision. This decision from this righ-wing radical \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . And so we`ve been organizing advocates, we`ve been organizing medical professionals. We`ve been organizing the 20 largest law firms, not only in New York State but across this nation to respond to this decision. We are ready.\par \par \uc2 We want everyone to know that here in the state of New York we respect a woman`s right to choose. We respect the 14th Amendment, which will apply not only to women but also to the right to marry, the right to interracial couples to marry, the right to contraception, the right to privacy. It`s important that individuals understand that this will upend and dramatically transform the lives of Americans and women in this country. But here in the state of New York, again, we respect the right to choose, and we respect a woman`s autonomy over her own body.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I know that there have been, Madam Attorney General, talk -- there`s been talk of an amendment that would enshrine the right to an abortion in your state`s constitution. Where does that stand right now? And have you been having conversations with attorneys general from other states?\par \par \uc2 JAMES: Yes. Just yesterday, we had a meeting with the Vice President of the United States \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She met with some attorneys general in this nation to discuss efforts to address this decision in anticipation of this decision. In addition to that, we`ve been organizing with other -- with attorneys general all across this state and all across this nation to address this issue and to respond to this issue. It`s really critically important that we be prepared.\par \par \uc2 And because democratic attorney generals represent the majority of attorney generals in this nation, we are prepared to respond and to make sure that there are -- a woman`s right to choose is respected in various states across this nation and democratic states across this nation.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: If I`m right, I think that proposal from State Senator Krueger is an amendment that actually seeks to provide comprehensive protection from all types of discrimination that would be based on gender and sexual orientation race disability status. Given the way that Justice Thomas wrote about Griswold and Lawrence and Obergefell, has it changed anything about the way you would view an amendment like that or the need for it?\par \par \uc2 JAMES: So I know that the governor of the state of New York has called a special session. I do know that Senator Krueger has passed -- has introduced legislation which calls for a constitutional amendment to enshrine the right to have an abortion. And at this point in time, the legislative session is in discussion as we speak, and in New York, must pass the Equality amendment in the state of New York and I fully support it. And I fully support efforts by the state legislature to do so as soon as possible. It`s my understanding that the special session will be called next Thursday.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: New York Attorney General Letitia James, thank you so much. Appreciate you taking the time to talk to us. And joining me now, Democratic strategist, Basil Smikle, he is the director of the Public Policy Program at Hunter College, also, NBC News political analyst, and Republican strategist Susan Del Percio. Good to see both of you. Basil, we`ve seen pictures of the people protesting. There are protests in other places around the country. How can Democrats harness this outrage because clearly, it`s there?\par \par \uc2 BASIL SMIKLE, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Yeah, it certainly is there. First of all, to Tish James and Governor Hochul of New York, my friends that do an extraordinary work. And I think that`s really the crux of this is that elections matter. We used to use that line and it was viewed as somewhat trite. Voting for the lesser of two evils, we sort of dismiss the importance of what we do. But the truth is there is no better example of why voting matters than what we experience right now.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: How do you get that message out? How do you use it to animate people?\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: And I will tell you, one of the things we have to do is focus on state elections. Everybody`s talking about presidential, congressional, \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 , that`s important. But what happened during the \uc2 Obama\uc2  years, Democrats lost over a thousand legislative seats across the country. We flip -- the Republicans flipped legislatures. We have to go to the ground and focus on every election, contest every seat.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Because everything`s being pushed to the states whether it`s abortion today, gun laws, voting rights, all of that being pushed to the states. If we do not focus on state elections and flipping these legislatures and flipping governors` trips, we`re not going to make a dent in it. So the president`s right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: When you -- when you say we, who do you mean by we? And I asked this question because I covered a lot of that on the ground. I`ve covered state legislative races here for MSNBC. And what I almost always heard from the Democrats was we do not get the support from our national party and our state party that we see a Republican opponents get it.\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: That`s right. That has to change. The fact that -- the fact that all of this is being pushed to the states now, is not an accident. This is decades in the making. Flipping the judgeships under Trump, the fact that he got all of those Supreme Court justices, this is not accidental. We cannot be a party that has a strategy from one year to another. We have to have a multi-year decades-long strategy. If it that -- if not now, then when?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Susan, Republicans on the other side of this issue are as passionate as Democrats seem to be. But I guess the question is, did this happen because they have been more consistently passionate and that`s how this conservative Supreme Court came to be?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Pretty much, Chris. It`s basically something that has taken 50 years in the making. And I think Basil`s absolutely right, you have to focus on the states. But I will also add. We also have seen so little governance out of Washington, DC. I don`t care if it was a Republican administration, Democratic administration, how Senate flipping? They are not getting the job done. It should not be left to nine people on the Supreme Court every single time.\par \par \uc2 We are the United States of America. Half of us now do not have control or -- our government says we do not have control over our bodies. We need to guess -- I agree at the state level, I`ve been saying that for a very long time, as Basil knows, and you do, Chris. But damn, we have to be -- we have to get governance back in Washington because who -- otherwise, who are we? We should not have individual rights and individual states. We need to come together. And right now, my biggest fear is not only will we not come together, but we`re going to see a bit of political violence that we have not seen in over 100 years.\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: Right.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, that`s a conversation we should have. But I do want to, to your point about who would go along with this, there`s an NBC poll that was conducted just last month, early last month, Susan, 63 percent of Americans did not believe Roe v. Wade should be overturned. So that is Republicans. That is men. Some of those people, obviously, are Republicans. And I guess the question and it goes to what President Biden was just saying, could this alienate independents? Will the GOP potentially take an overall hit here?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: I think they will. I think nothing -- you know, we talked about the Republicans getting to this point in the judiciary as being a long-term effort. But that was because they came out because they wanted to fight for something. They wanted to fight to overturn Roe. Now, it`s up to the Democrats to mobilize to fight to get rid of those legislators who support banning abortion, even in the case of rape and incest. And that should happen at the city, state, and national levels for sure. But they`re -- I think independent women are now back up for grabs.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Basil, I want to take a look at the ages of the conservatives, all of whom voted to overturn Roe on the Supreme Court, some of whom may indeed serve for another, what 30 years? With so many, as you point out, so many decisions going on in the Supreme Court, is realistically really governorships and state legislatures, the only thing, at least in the near term, that people who are outraged by this can change?\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: We can expect these types of decisions for a generation because of exactly what you said, the ages of these justices. So, yes, what happens in the state -- at the state level really does matter.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes.\par \par \uc2 SMIKLE: This issue of states` rights and local control has been around for 100 plus years when there were folks that were concerned about women having rights, people of color having rights, so they always tried to create this local control as this issue and nationalize it. That`s exactly -- that`s all that we`re seeing right now. And so yes, what you do in the states really does matter if we`re going to be focused in -- on this era of local control.\par \par \uc2 I would also say this, that -- and Susan`s absolutely right in bringing this to the forefront. The fact of the matter is that there are a lot of people that voted for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  because they were saying, oh, yes, you know what -- it may not be as big a deal as we think it will be if the judge shifts flip, if the -- he gets a couple of Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 Right now, we can`t focus on racializing and urbanizing, and -- or getting them to do that. Cannot have these issues racialized and urbanized. There - - this particular issue affects a lot of suburban white women, as you -- and you talk about Republican women as well. You know, what are they going to do right now? And that is a -- that is a concern for Democrats as well. We need to be reaching out to those women as well. Because if we`re going to flip this narrative, if we`re going to turn the tide, we can`t allow Republicans to racialize and urbanize a lot of these issues. And suburban white women, quite frankly, that a lot of them have supported Trump, where are they right now?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: History will tell us, Susan, you know this better than I do that if people are happy with their lives, if they`re happy with their economic situation, then that helps the party in power, right? The economic situation was largely considered to be the issue that was going to impact the November elections, the midterm elections. And often when something like this happens, there is this outcry. But we have short-term memories. Months from now, do you believe that this will still bring people to the ballot box?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: I do think it will be a motivating issue, Chris. I also think that during the primaries that we`ve seen the question of Republicans going so extreme on abortion rights, meaning eliminating it in the case of rape, or incest in the life of the mother, has really put them into a really narrow box that is worth fighting, women do see that and men do see that as something worth the fight. It`s bigger than just what your tax bill is. Also, I like to add to that. You also have gun violence. That`s another issue, I think combined with Roe can -- that can really motivate those voters that Basil was just talking about, suburban white voters. There is something more and again, we can be going down a really bad road and we need some moral authority here.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Basil Smikle, and Susan Del Percio, thank you, always good to have you both on the program. Joining us now is Mallory Carroll, Vice President of Communications for the \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life American group. Their mission has been for decades to end abortion. And, Mallory, you and I spoke the day of the draft of the opinion was leaked and you didn`t want to assume anything you told me at the time. But now it`s here. What has this done to activate your base? And what`s the reaction you`re hearing over these past few hours?\par \par \uc2 MALLORY CARROLL, VICE PRESIDENT OF COMMUNICATIONS, SUSAN B ANTHONY PRO-LIFE AMERICA: This is a historic moment for the pro-life movement. We`ve worked so hard for 50 years engaging in the political process peacefully to change who represents us to change the law to challenge this unjust Supreme Court case. And now we have this -- finally, this -- the court has taken its thumb off the scale and is going to allow the American people to decide through their elected representatives where they should debate and find where they have consensus on where we`re going to protect unborn children. This is our opportunity to modernize our law. The United States is one of seven countries we have been up until this point because of Roe vs. Wade that allows abortion on demand up until the moment of birth alongside China and North Korea.\par \par \uc2 That`s the Democratic Party`s position. That is not consensus. And now we`re going to be able to debate and find consensus at the state level. And, of course, here in Washington as well, we`re very excited. We`ve been talking to women in the suburbs as you know. We`ve been talking to voters long ahead of this day in key battleground states across the nation. And we know that our message of consensus and protecting women coming alongside them loving them both and protecting their babies from the horror of abortion, that that`s a winning message, especially compared to the Democratic pro-abortion alternative.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I`m sure you just saw this poll. A majority of Americans, 63 percent did not believe that Roe v. Wade should be upheld. Only 30 percent wanted to see it overturned.\par \par \uc2 CARROLL: No, there`s been polls like --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Do you not think that the -- and we`ve seen that, we`ve seen it in these numbers, Mallory, that the number of people who support a woman`s right to choose has risen over the course of many years. Do you -- do you really believe that the majority of Americans believe as you do?\par \par \uc2 CARROLL: I do believe that. Yes, Chris. And I will say there`s polling all the time about American support for Roe v. Wade, but it`s not an accurate picture of where Americans would draw a line and support our children. People don`t realize that because of Roe v. Wade Doe v. Bolton, Planned Parenthood v. Casey that our judicial jurisprudence up until now has been to create a situation like we have, that is abortion on demand up into the moment of birth. We saw just yesterday, a late turn abortionist here in Washington, DC, Doctor Cesare Santangelo doing abortions at 28 weeks and beyond. Well past the point of viability.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 That`s the status quo in America has been up until today. And so when you ask Americans where they withdraw, why, and we find that most Americans support restrictions on abortion that Roe does not allow. So I just dismissed that poll out of hand, I think it`s more far more accurate to engage with people and ask them where it is that they would protect an unborn child growing in the womb, and also who they believe should be making those decisions. A majority say the elected officials who are responsible to the people who`ve elected them if they overstep, they can be quickly unelected, and there can be -- there can be consensus, there can be a correction, not unelected judge -- unelected judges.\par \par \uc2 In 1973, it was seven unelected men in a single day, who struck down every single state law that existed up until that point. And today, we have five justices saying -- five and a half -- five and a half saying that the American people can decide and that this can be a -- we can use the tools of democracy to allow this to play out. Consensus is going to look different. But we`ve got state laws going into effect today all across the nation allow to --\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Well, if I can interrupt you for a second, Mallory because I`m wondering what you have been doing since the draft ruling came out in terms of your outreach to states and what your plan is moving forward state by state by state.\par \par \uc2 CARROLL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I know I`ve talked to some of the legislative people who work on your side. And they`ve talked about the fact that there really are, they feel like 30 states in play, yes?\par \par \uc2 CARROLL: Right. Yes. And we`ve started this long before the draft. In fact, more than a year ago, when we knew that the court was going to hear oral arguments in this case and make a decision, my boss, we`ve been talking to governors across the country, seeing them as the real consensus builders having to lead the conversation in a lot of these states that that`s where the first action is likely going to be.\par \par \uc2 And we are expanding our team to replicate the success that we`ve had at the federal level to leverage politics and policy to stand up with people who say that they`re pro-life and who want to protect unborn children and women who want to pass consensus legislation at the state level to defend them politically, work with them once they`re elected, and hold them accountable for the promises that they`ve made.\par \par \uc2 That`s what we did at the federal level and what we`ll continue to do. That`s how we got into this position where we have. We spent the last 10 years working to elect a pro-life Senate, a pro-life president, that would give us a Supreme Court with justices such as the ones who are signed on to the draft and now to the final decision.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Mallory Carroll, thank you so much. I appreciate you taking the time to talk to us. Joining me now, Symone Sanders, host of "SYMONE," and former chief spokesperson to Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , who by the way, we`re expecting to hear from a little later on in this hour. Look, I think that Mallory lays out very clearly strategically, what they have done and what they plan to do. So when you hear that message from the anti-abortion side, what should be the message that who -- people who want a right to choose, what should they take away?\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, MSNBC HOST: Look, Chris, first of all, congratulations. It`s the first time I have been with you since you are now hosting a new spot.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I`m very excited for you and I tune in daily.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Look, I think for Democrats or even people who support a woman being able to make the decisions about her own body because that is in fact what we`re talking about, I think when they hear the guests that you just had on, they should hear that extremism. You know, in a number of states more than a half -- almost half the states in America have some trigger laws on the books, or abortion is poised to become the legal in half of the states here in our country.\par \par \uc2 That means women can be jailed or fined. Doctors can be jailed or fined for assisting with or giving an abortion. Other people can call on their neighbors and say I think that they are doing something nefarious. I think that they maybe are thinking about getting a portion. The -- this is the extremism.\par \par \uc2 And I think that is a place when you cited the NBC polling and there`s \uc2 Pew Research\uc2  poll, there`s every single polling entity has polling that supports that the majority of Americans do believe that Roe was settled precedent and that it should have remained in place. The majority of Americans, even people who do not themselves believe personally and getting an abortion, I do not think women should be thrown in jail for attempting to seek the health care that they need.\par \par \uc2 And that is what you`re -- what I would suggest folks across the country who are proponents of a woman being able to make -- women being able to make decisions about their own bodies. That is how they should be talking about.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So I was just having this conversation with Basil and Susan, Republicans, and again, they have made this an issue that people vote on for years. How do Democrats do that by November? How do they do it in their state legislature?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Look, Chris, the Republican Party apparatus in this country has for decades, longer than I have been alive, organized around the issue of ending abortion. They have not organized around the issue of, again, jailing women, jailing doctors, fining people, but that is in fact what their organizing has and will lead to.\par \par \uc2 So I think for Democrats and folks who are proponents of, again, a woman`s ability to make decisions about her own body you can`t out-organize 30 years of work overnight. And Democrats are going to have to make real investments at the state legislative level.\par \par \uc2 When I interviewed the first lady on my show, First Lady Dr. Jill Biden, she was my first guest, and she said people are going to have to pay attention to the state legislatures. People are going to have to elect people at the local level. I have never in all of my years of politics heard national Democratic leaders make such a forceful case from the highest levels, the White House, about state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 And so there is an entity that organizes for that. The Democratic Legislative Campaign Committee, they don`t usually get a lot of money, Chris. They`re not necessarily getting a surrogate program, they`re not getting an influx of attention, but that is what it`s going to take to compete with what the Republican Party apparatus has put in place for so many years.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: One of the things that we talk about a lot is that every four years when there is a presidential campaign we say this is going to be the year that so many young people are going to come out and it has very rarely happened, right? And certainly not in any consistent way. But this is something that uniquely, directly impacts young people and I wonder if this might be an animating issue that has, in a way that -- the Democratic Party, we have not seen it work in the past.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: It could be, Chris. It absolutely could be. And I think an animating issue for young people but animating issue for women. Abortion is not being outlawed for Democratic women or women that voted for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  only. It is women, period. So if you are a woman in this country and the health care that you need is in fact an abortion in many places in our great country you will not be able to get the health care that you need.\par \par \uc2 That is I think how Democrats frankly should be talking about this issue if they are to animate voters. I have been saying for the last couple of weeks, Chris, that, you know, Democrats used to say post -- you know, the forever First Lady \uc2 Michelle Obama\uc2  gave Democrats a battle cry. She said when they go high we go low. And for so many years that was the battle cry of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 I would like to offer up a new slogan, Chris. When they go low it might be time to go toe-to-toe and in so many places across this country the rights of women, the rights of young people, the rights of black people, the rights of the LGBTQ Plus community are under attack. We saw the Supreme Court yesterday extend rights for people who own guns and codify those rights and snatched rights away from women in this country.\par \par \uc2 Democratic leaders should be out in full force talking about how there is one party in this country that is going to defend democracy, that`s going to defend your civil rights, and that party is the Democratic Party. And then talk about how they are going to do that. I thought it was great that Speaker Pelosi came out so forcefully and early this morning. I thought the president`s remarks were very good.\par \par \uc2 I am elated to hear from my other former boss, the vice president, because she has been consistent and clear on this issue but it`s going to take more than that. Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  in Michigan has made this a central issue of her campaign. She`s talking about the roads in her reelection for governor and she`s talking about abortion.\par \par \uc2 Other governors across this country, candidates that would look to glean support from a wide array of people, might need to take notice and take note, and be willing to be clear and speak true. We are talking about jailing women, we`re talking about jailing doctors, we`re talking about fining people thousands of dollars for seeking the health care they need. That is not an America I would like to live in.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Symone, can you stand by? Because I want to get your reaction to something else that`s happening right now on Capitol Hill. You mentioned \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . I understand the House just made a critical vote.\par \par \uc2 Ali Vitali is standing by for us. Ali, what can you tell us?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris, with 14 Republicans joining Democrats, the House has just passed that bipartisan gun reform bill. Of course, that cleared the Senate last night. That was the biggest hurdle, finding something that Democrats could all get on board for in majority fashion. They were able to do that. 15 Republicans crossing party lines to vote for that bill last night.\par \par \uc2 And then of course the House just clearing it now. That means the next place it goes is to President Biden`s desk. And again, this is something that negotiators who were a key part of this legislation have said repeatedly but we should point it out here it is the most significant gun reform legislation that this body has done in several decades.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:08]\par \par \uc2 It includes things like Republicans priorities of bolstering mental health, as well as school safety but also Democratic priorities in making sure that the background check system is bolstered, especially for people 18 to 21, as well as getting rid of that so-called boyfriend loophole which stops people with domestic violence charges from being able to have a gun.\par \par \uc2 That`s something that we`ve seen in the data of course that shows that intimate partners are often the people who are victims of that kind of violence. So again a big day in this body as they are in the way that Speaker Pelosi did earlier with us talking about what`s happening at the Supreme Court but also trying to have this win on gun violence prevention that they have been working towards for weeks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Thank you for that, Ali.\par \par \uc2 Symone, I want to go to you and first let me ask you about the fact that they were able to get 14 votes. Not a huge number given the number of Republicans that are in the House. But it is bipartisan on both sides both in the House and the Senate. Your reaction in general?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I think this is a -- while it`s a dark day when it comes to women`s rights in America I think this is a good day for Congress and people should celebrate this legislation. Let`s just be very clear. This is not a bold piece of legislation. There is much more that can and should be done that this piece of legislation does not do but it is a start, Chris.\par \par \uc2 And I think people at home should remember that before there was the Civil Rights Act of 1964, then the Voting Rights Act of 1965 there were a number of smaller civil rights act bills that were passed in the years before. You have to take a first step before we get to big, bold legislation. And the fact that the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2  has done nothing on guns even after Sandy Hook when children were murdered in a classroom, Americans across this country stood up and said not today.\par \par \uc2 We need to hear from Congress. We need to act for our children, for our families, and for Uvalde, for Sacramento, for Racine, for Buffalo, and for Pittsburgh. That is what we have seen here from Congress. So first step, much more to go. But today folks should be proud.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But in keeping with the theme of what we saw today, right, at almost simultaneously there was a Supreme Court ruling in New York. And I`m just going to read from it, and it was Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , Justices Alito, Coney Barret, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh holding that the Second and 14th Amendments protect an individual`s right to carry a handgun for self- defense outside the home.\par \par \uc2 The mayor of New York said now it`s going to be the Wild West. So I don`t know if for folks who want more gun safety legislation that is one step forward two steps back or how they might quantify it. But just the idea that even as Congress is moving forward for more gun restrictions the opposite direction is being held for the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Look, this is not something I ever thought I would say but I do think it is time to take a real, hard look at the structure and the makeup of the Supreme Court. We have a Supreme Court that is currently rolling back rights for key portions of the population and then implementing a very dangerous and extreme, I`ll use the term, agenda.\par \par \uc2 It is insane to believe that people should be able to conceal carry into schools, into nurseries, into places of worship, into grocery stores. Taking away the ability of mayors and governors to make a decision, OK, given from -- based on representing their constituents I think is what has happened here. And how, again, we have rights for, it`s the right to privacy and a right to carry a gun but it`s not a right to be able to make a decision about your own body?\par \par \uc2 Make it make sense, Chris. And it doesn`t make sense. And so I do think that people who have long since talked about reform of the Supreme Court, Brian Fallon, who`s a good friend of mine, is one of those who started Demand Justice. I do think that while this is a proposal that the White House has roundly rejected, I know, I was there, OK. This is a president who is not for expansion of the court.\par \par \uc2 It might be, again I`m talking about going toe to toe, you cannot -- are we really talking about that we are going to allow ourselves to live in a country with the Supreme Court that is poised to not only roll back women`s rights and expand gun rights in America but roll back privacy rights, roll back the right to marry who you`d like to marry, same-sex marriage? Those are things if people read the concurring opinions from the decision today, they`re all now on the table. People should be concerned.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Symone Sanders, there will be no shortage of topics to discuss on "SYMONE" 4:00 on weekends here on MSNBC. It`s so great of you to come on the program. Thank you. And I look forward to seeing what you have to say tomorrow. Appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Now let`s go back to that far-reaching impact of this Supreme Court decision to overturn Roe v. Wade. We have a team of correspondents across the country gathering reaction.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:02]\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Cal Perry joins us from St. Louis, Missouri. He`s outside a Planned Parenthood office.\par \par \uc2 Cal, what are you hearing? I do see the protesters there.\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Protesters have started to arrive. This is going to become a focal point, a place where protesters are going to gather here in Missouri for the simple reason that this Planned Parenthood that I`m out in front of is the only location in the state of Missouri where women were able to come to receive an abortion. And I`m told by Planned Parenthood this morning they officially notified the state health department here in Missouri that they are ceasing operations, at least they are no longer going to provide that service to women in the state.\par \par \uc2 It was the attorney general here in the state of Missouri who basically codified that Supreme Court decision by making a proclamation just about 10 minutes after the Supreme Court decision came down. Missouri of course has what is called a trigger law. That is in 2019 the state legislature passed a law that said once Roe v. Wade is overturned, if it is overturned, that abortion would become illegal in this state. And it has become illegal.\par \par \uc2 We should remind our viewers now in the state of Missouri, it is a class B felony, that is five to 10 years if someone is found guilty of assisting or providing an abortion to a woman in this state. We spoke to Cory Bush, Representative Cory Bush earlier today who wanted to send a message to women who are in this state that they can go to Illinois this weekend. There were people here who had procedures scheduled. Those procedures that are now canceled, they can go to Illinois.\par \par \uc2 It sort of shows you where we are politically, Chris, when you have a governor for example in Illinois who is making a big deal about abortions continuing to be legal in that state but here in Missouri, a place where Planned Parenthood will tell you it was hard enough to begin with for women to find reproductive care before this ruling, now there is a concern that there could be a chilling effect beyond this decision that other freedoms as Planned Parenthood is saying could become at risk. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Cal Perry, thank you for that.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Sarag Warbelow, legal director of the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2  and I`m sure that you`ve had a chance to look at the ruling but I want to read from Justice Thomas`s concurrence which says, overruling Roe isn`t enough. For that reason in future cases we should reconsider all of this court`s substantive, due process precedents including Griswold, Lawrence, and Obergefell. That is of course the right to privacy, contraception, and marriage equality. Your reaction?\par \par \uc2 SARAH WARBELOW\uc2 , HUMAN RIGHTS CAMPAIGN LEGAL DIRECTOR: Yes. I do want to note, you know, that the majority opinion goes to great pains to say that that is not their viewpoint. But Justice Thomas`s concurrence really is a call to extreme folks within the United States to try to chip away at LGBTQ folks` rights, whether that`s legislators who are pandering to a far-right base, or individuals who think that they should be able to assert their personal view points over what is needed for the LGBTQ community. It`s deeply concerning the tone and message that that concurrence set.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Yes, one of the other things it said is we have a duty to correct the error established in those precedents. When you hear the right to marry being compared to an error what even goes through your mind?\par \par \uc2 WARBELOW: It`s really outrageous to suggest that what is supported by a vast majority of the American people, the right of folks to marry whom they love, should be placed at risk simply because one justice would have voted the other way or did vote the other way when he had the opportunity, that he would roll back precedent in order to restrict rights which of course is exactly what they did by eliminating Roe in other contexts as well.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So what does the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2  do as an organization? There is a lot of nervousness when you start talking about established precedents not being precedence at all. Particularly, obviously as it comes to gay marriage.\par \par \uc2 WARBELOW: Yes. You know, we`re going to continue to fight back in state legislatures. We`ve had an overwhelming number of negative bills introduced in the past several years. This year capping our previous historic high. Nearly 350 pieces of legislation designed to harm the LGBTQ community. We`re no strangers to fighting and we`re going to continue to do that. We`re going to use all of the tools in our tool kit to protect LGBTQ folks but we need people to join us, to stand up, to march. To make clear to the general public that we are proud to be LGBTQ people and we`re not going away.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today`s decision of course started from that case in Mississippi that was a crafted challenge, really, to Roe. Are there cases, specific cases in state legislatures that you`re keeping an eye on in terms of gay marriage?\par \par \uc2 WARBELOW: You know, right after the Obergefell decision there were legislatures who were trying to pass legislation that would reaffirm their constitutional bans and so, you know, we`re going to be keeping a really close eye on those efforts that may be renewed, fighting back against them.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:07]\par \par \uc2 And certainly of course we know that there are individuals who don`t want to perform their jobs even though their job is to issue marriage licenses to same-sex couples. Now the court has already said that folks have to do their jobs but we know that there will be pushback.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Sarah Warbelow\uc2 , thank you so much, from the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2 . We appreciate you taking the time to talk to us.\par \par \uc2 For more on this I`m joined by associate editor from "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and Pulitzer Prize winning journalist \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 . He is also the anchor of the "SUNDAY SHOW WITH \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 " right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 I also see Eugene Daniels is there joining us as well. I`m not sure if he`s all hooked up and can hear us yet.\par \par \uc2 But look, let me start with you if I can, Jonathan, because we`re just two days away from the seventh anniversary of the Supreme Court decision affirming the constitutional right of same-sex couples to marry. I know you remember it well. You were in D.C. then. I was covering the White House. You subsequently married your husband Nick. So I wonder what you thought when you saw what Justice Thomas wrote?\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 , MSNBC ANCHOR, "SUNDAY SHOW WITH \uc2 JONATHAN CAPEHART\uc2 ": I saw it coming. When the draft opinion, Alito`s draft opinion leaked a couple of months ago, and I read it, you could not read his opinion about why a woman does not have a constitutional right to choose an abortion without thinking about all the other rights that depend on the right to privacy that was guaranteed by Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And so I wrote a column with this lead. It wasn`t -- I did not know how much my same-sex marriage depended on a woman`s right to choose an abortion. And in reading, and I`m still reading, the final opinion, but the language is -- the final opinion is written by the same justice, by Justice Alito. A lot of the language is still there. And the alarm I had reading the draft is the alarm I have reading the final.\par \par \uc2 And to know that Justice Thomas, the most senior person on the bench, put in plain writing what anyone reading the final opinion would infer but putting in plain writing that Griswold, Lawrence, and Obergefell also should be reconsidered is to make it plain that the rights of LGBTQ people, the rights of married couples to get contraception, the right of same-sex adults being able to engage without the fear of criminal penalty in intimate contact, all of those things are now -- I`m not going to say up for grabs, but if cases that question the constitutionality of Griswold, of Lawrence, of Obergefell come to the court then that 6-3 conservative majority could stand poised to overturn those rights.\par \par \uc2 And as you pointed out, my husband and I, Nick, have been married for five years now. We were here in Washington on June 26th, 2015. I remember that day precisely, because that day, Chris, and you and I have talked about this, was the day that the United States, when America was at the high point of our ideals. Think about it. That morning the Supreme Court ruled in Obergefell that same-sex couples had the constitutional right to marry. The president then, President \uc2 Obama\uc2 , then went to Charleston and gave one of the most unbelievably powerful eulogies but also political speeches a president has ever made about who we are as a country and the role of race in our formation, and what bedevils us now and then he came home, came back to Washington, and as the sun went down on the nation`s capital on a day when an historic Supreme Court ruling was handed down, affirming same-sex couples` rights, constitutional right to marry, what did we see on the White House?\par \par \uc2 The White House was glowing in the rainbow colors. Think about what message that sent to the American people from the president of the United States. The message that sent to the world about who we are as a country, what we believe in, and here we are two days shy of the seventh anniversary of that. And now we`re looking at women, many women who were alive pre-Roe and have lived with 50 years of Roe v. Wade, looking at their daughters and their nieces and their granddaughters and young women they love and care about, now having less rights than they did when they were their age. Think about that.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:01]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I also want to bring in Eugene Daniels, White House correspondent and "Playbook" coauthor at Politico now with us from outside the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 We were talking just a few minutes ago to \uc2 Sarah Warbelow\uc2  from the \uc2 Human Rights Campaign\uc2  about what was written by \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and the idea that he believes that there is an error in the constitutional right for gays to marry. You and I spoke just a couple of weeks ago there in D.C. about plans for your wedding. What`s going through your mind right now, Eugene?\par \par \uc2 EUGENE DANIELS, POLITICO WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, we are -- I mean, both as a reporter who is covering this momentous occasion and someone who had to cancel a meeting with their wedding planner because of the news today to come out here and do my job, it`s a shaky day, it`s a weird day. When you talk to folks, the concern they`ve always had since Politico reported on that leaked draft and that leaked opinion was that this was going to be the next step.\par \par \uc2 President Biden talked about that today, Vice President Harris has talked about that over and over, that this wasn`t just about abortion. And at times they were talking about it in making people who aren`t somewhat -- isn`t someone who is going to get an abortion to care about this issue, that their privacy rights could be on the line as well.\par \par \uc2 And so today, you know, I was at home when it came down, me and my future husband were -- had to cry a little bit thinking about that moment while I`m working, while he and the rest of the country are ingesting all of this information because it is something that a lot of folks were not expecting.\par \par \uc2 Actually came here in an \uc2 Uber\uc2  to the Supreme Court and my driver started talking and she was a woman who was pro-life and she talked about how for years she was ready for this moment, didn`t expect it to happen so soon, and in this moment though she was a little happy, she wanted And I think when you talk to folks about the political implications of what happened today, that is the thing that you hear. Even from some Republicans that they have worried that the court may have overreached and that the political blowback may be something that they`re going to have to deal with not just in November but in 2024 and moving on because this is not just about as you guys have been saying and as my friend and our colleague \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2  so lovely lined out, it`s not just about folks who can become pregnant but also about the other rights to privacy.\par \par \uc2 And I will say in that piece, in that concurrent opinion that Thomas laid out, he did not put in there Loving v. Virginia, which is the case that gave interracial couples the ability to marry. He himself in an interracial marriage, myself in an interracial relationship, that is another one that could possibly be on the docket when we`re talking about the right to privacy in this country.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want you to put your White House hat on for a minute, if I can ask you to, Eugene, because we should also remind folks that when gay marriage became the law of the land, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was vice president and today on this issue, on abortion, he said this is not over. Do you sense the import? I mean, because we did have that leaked decision. Do you think that there is both the will and more importantly maybe the strategy in this White House, which has had a lot going on to take on these issues like abortion, like gay marriage?\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: It`s something that they are desperate to do. They know realistically that on this issue of abortion there`s not much they can do with the Congress that they have. They`ve known that for a long time but Vice President Harris has been talking to different groups to say what is it that folks are expecting from the White House. What are the different things that they should be focusing on.\par \par \uc2 I talked to a woman who was in a meeting with the vice president just last week and she said the vice president continued to think about and talk about apps that women use to track their periods and how that can be used to track women who want to have abortions, and what can the administration do to make sure that`s not an issue. And so they have the wherewithal I think in the administration to do so but they`re also very cognizant that there`s not an executive order that President Biden could sign that would give this right back to the American people.\par \par \uc2 There`s not an executive order that the president could sign that could enshrine gay marriage or same-sex marriage into law. That is not something that they can do. And so what we`re going to continue to hear from the White House as we get closer and closer to November is that it is up to these folks behind me to do something about this, to go and elect more people to Congress who are going to work on this issue, elect more pro- abortion members of Congress, elect people who believe in same-sex marriage so that they can enshrine that into the constitutional -- into the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:01]\par \par \uc2 And that is going to be something that they also know is going to come up in front of possibly this exact same court once again. And so a White House besieged with all types of different crises continuing, I see people with masks out here still in the middle of a pandemic, dealing with all of the international issues that they`re dealing with. And now this. It`s another place where they feel a lot of the time that their hands are tied, but they continue to say they`re going to fight and look for ways for the president to -- and vice president to kind of nibble around the edges on enshrining some of these things and protecting folks, especially leaning on the agencies to do some of that work.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Eugene Daniels, \uc2 Jonathan Capehart\uc2 , thanks to both of you. Good friends of mine. And I appreciate you talking the time to us on this day.\par \par \uc2 That`s going to do it for me this hour. Make sure you join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday at 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC but keep it right here on a very consequential day. Our coverage will continue with Katy Tur next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. I`m Katy Tur.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade and there is reason to believe that the body may not stop there. Contraception, same-sex marriage and same-sex relationships could potentially be next. In his concurring opinion, Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  wrote that the purported right to abortion is not a form of liberty protected by the due process clause. He goes on to say we should reconsider all of this court`s substantive due process precedents including Griswold, Lawrence and Obergefell.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220625ei6o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION WITH AL SHARPTON; June 19, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Symone Sanders; Al Sharpton; Ali Arouzi; Gigi Stone \par \uc2 6,200 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, MSNBC HOST: Also, hit me up on social media, you all. You can find highlights and news about exiting things in the works for the show on \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and TikTok.\par \par \uc2 But right now, I would like to hand things over to my good friend, Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 . Rev, I know you have an interview with Congresswoman \uc2 Sheila Jackson Lee\uc2 , coming up. I am very much so looking forward to seeing that today.\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Oh, yes. It`s going to be very informative. She has a lot to say. And thank you, as always, Symone, and Happy Father`s Day to Sean, from Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 Good evening and welcome to POLITICNATION. On this Father`s Day and this Juneteenth weekend, tonight`s lead, freedoms struggle.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the nations is in the midst of a day of reflection, the second federal observation of the black freedom struggle expressed by the holiday known as Juneteenth. It comes at the start of a potentially dizzying news cycle, starting this week, as the Senate holds out hope, that a tenuous bipartisan deal on guns can get a quick vote, while the momentum is there after weeks of mass shootings. And we`re bracing for the Supreme Courts spending public decision on the future of the federal abortion rights to come down within days. as the White House weighs its options to protect those rights, possibly through executive order.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s what will be the third week of public hearings in the congressional probe into January 6th, the next round, centering on Donald Trump`s pressure campaign to intimidate election officials in Georgia, to overturn the 2020 election results in that state. And we`re also waiting a hard confirmation that Ginni Thomas, wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , will indeed testify before the House select committee for her own efforts to undermine that election results. Lots to cover, let`s get started.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Congresswoman \uc2 Sheila Jackson Lee\uc2 , Democrat of Texas and a member of the House Judiciary Committee. Congresswoman, thanks for being with us tonight. Happiest Juneteenth to you in your constituents in Houston. Of course, I want to get to this national observance of our freedom struggle, but I have to start with January 6th. The next hearing on Tuesday will center on the testimony of Georgia Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , his top deputy Gabriel Sterling. Raffensperger, of course, has been pressured by former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to, quote, find the additional votes he needed to win the state in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Now, from your view, as a member of the House Judiciary Committee, what do you expect to hear Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SHEILA JACKSON LEE\uc2  (D-TX): Happy Juneteenth, Reverend, and also happy Father`s Day.\par \par \uc2 As a veteran of three impeachments, I believe that what the American people want is accountability. And what I have heard is a block-by-block presentation of the major actions of one person who led others, \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , to stop the peaceful transfer of power, to perpetrate potential threats to his life, of the vice president of the United States, never done before in least in 20th century, and to lead others, even though they are being prosecuted, many have testified in video, in their own trials, that they came to Washington at the encouragement and direction of the then existing president, to, in essence, overthrow, to stop, and to really injure members of Congress to avoid the peaceful transition of government. I believe we must have accountability.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, let me bring this up, Congresswoman, the Senate`s bipartisan deal on guns, and we`ve had mass shootings here in Texas, where you represent. It crafted this kind of agreement after shootings in Buffalo and in Uvalde right there in Texas. And it appears to be a question, Democrats and Republicans at odds largely over the question of gun rights for those with histories of domestic abuse.\par \par \uc2 Then in the House, I`ve read where some house Democrats, progressives, are disheartened not but just by what won`t be in this compromised bill but is focused bolstering school security, with some progressives think may have negative consequences, with more policing in minority and low income schools. Where do you come down on all of this, congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 LEE: Reverend, I believe that we need to start the carnage. All I can have in the back of my mind is a visit I made to Uvalde to be able to give comfort but also to talk about what are the next steps are and spending time hugging families and children that were 9, t10, 11 year olds that were crying, and then, of course, to hear about those innocent souls in Buffalo, New York, going to the grocery store, targeted because they were black, and the misinformation of the replacement theory, and then Mother Emanuel and Sandy Hook.\par \par \uc2 So, here is what I think is important. We did pass in the House a very effective bill, I was the lead sponsor, it`s a bill that I helped write, among with many other members. It`s called Protect Our Kids. We have the raising the age to purchase a weapon. We couldn`t get that in the Senate. We had regulation magazines, ending of ghost guns and bumper stocks, and we had the extreme risk orders among other, I think, important matters, and storage loss, simple, to just asked people to store their guns, to save people from suicide attempts, and children getting guns, and then children getting guns and doing evil things by taking those guns to school.\par \par \uc2 I believe, one start, one step is important. We`ll have to see will what they come out. The boyfriend loophole was in the Violence Against Women Act, and I implore those who are trying to make sure that the violence that happens against law enforcement and against women does not happen.\par \par \uc2 I think we`re going to have to see what the return product is, Reverend, and we`re going to have to be able to determine whether this mounts to the first step to begin stopping the violence of guns. If that is what the case is, I will interact with my progressive colleagues, my Democratic caucus colleagues, and we`ll come to decision in the House and how we should move forward.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congresswoman, I can imagine the pride you must feel for your role in getting Juneteenth, federally recognized as a holiday. In fact, last year you served as speaker pro temp of the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2  on the historic day the Juneteenth holiday bill passed the House of Representatives, on its way to the president`s desk for signing.\par \par \uc2 But now I have to wonder if any part of you is conflicted, though, about where the nation is at this time after the racist massacre in Buffalo last month and the LGBTQ pride events canceled this weekend after the aborted extremist attack and Idaho last weekend. How do you make sense of the progress represented by this holiday and what feels like social regression with regards to race and identity?\par \par \uc2 LEE: The nation cries out for healing. It cries out for repair. We can`t keep going like this where persons who deserve dignity, whether it`s the LGBTQ community, whether it`s African-Americans or Hispanic children in Uvalde, we can`t continue. And so I celebrate Juneteenth. In fact, the idea of Juneteenth was exactly this, to get the nation to pause for a moment understand the brutality of slavery. Slavery has been silenced. It has been unspoken about, and, frankly, that is not going to cure America, it`s not going to heal America, it`s not going to bring Americans together.\par \par \uc2 And so this gives a channel for discussion and education, I did this for a decade, introduced the first legislation with my colleagues, who I applaud and thank, glad to have been in the chair, glad to have had the leadership to do their support, and then, of course, the president of United States and our beloved Opal Lee in the state of Texas, Al Edwards, who started the state law.\par \par \uc2 But this is a statement that America has a race problem. When a young boy takes an automatic weapon and says that he is born into the replacement theory, then we must begin to put this forward and we must, Reverend, begin to put HR-40, the commission to study slavery and develop reparation proposals, understanding or knowledge will bring about understanding and we cannot continue this past way. I hope with Juneteenth we`ll have a pathway forward to do the healing of this nation.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, and I definitely see, you and I both were at the White House for the executive order on policing. We were talking to members in the west wing and the president about that commission on reparations, we must keep pushing forward.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:04]\par \par \uc2 Let me raise this. We`re waiting on the Supreme Court public decision on the future of Roe v. Wade, and with that federal abortion rights. I wonder what you`ve heard from your constituents, women in Texas, already living under strict abortion law and now poised to watch the court likely overturn abortion rights nationally. What are they saying, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 LEE: This is a tragedy. It`s catastrophic. They`re frightened. They`re outraged. African-American women in particular most burdened by the inability to seek reproductive rights or reproductive health in a way that will save their lives. Many of them, in times past, victim of maternal mortality, higher in black women, but just the care that comes through having abortion rights, and the choice of deciding what happens to your own body.\par \par \uc2 And they are stunned that the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  might I add Trump port (ph). These recent appointees would have the audacity to ignore the Ninth Amendment. They don`t ask for anything other than the right to privacy. Isn`t that conservative? Isn`t that what conservatives believe, that you individually have your own rights and yet they want to intrude on the bodies of women? They are frightened and I think that`s the most dangerous position to be in.\par \par \uc2 And we will see health care cut off that`s been given by Planned Parenthood and others, literally cut off. But I don`t know what the insanity of this court is. I have no reason to attack any one of the jurors. I want nothing but safety and security for them. But I think that this is a truly political decision, it came from nowhere, there was no harm or injury that they needed to care. There are cases that they`re looking at, the atrocious case from Mississippi that followed the most restrictive and strict law in Texas, and there will be more of those coming up, just like Governor DeSantis is outlawing vaccines.\par \par \uc2 This is a dangerous pathway and the federal government has always been the last bastion of freedom and justice and equality for all, where we`re going to go next, the legislature and votes, voting, and empowering people to change those who are in the elective office to be able to being rights back to people and the American people.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That is why I said this is a day of reflection, as we go to Juneteenth. Because the story of Juneteenth is, yes, those in Texas were finally told they were free. What we also must reflect on is the two years because of states` rights and the rebellion against the union, they were kept enslaved while others had already been free.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for being with us, Congresswoman \uc2 Sheila Jackson Lee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, for an update on the war in Ukraine, joining me now is NBC`s Ali Arouzi out of Kyiv, Ukraine. Ali, what is the latest there?\par \par \uc2 ALI AROUZI, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Reverend. Well, the battle on Ukraine`s eastern front continues to rage as the Russians make slow but steady gains in their bid to capture Severedonetsk, the capital of the Luhansk region. The governor of Luhansk region, \uc2 Serhiy Haidai\uc2 , say today that the Russians are moving in an enormous amount of reserves that they`ve taken from around the country in an attempt to capture that region in the coming days or weeks.\par \par \uc2 But the Ukrainians are also putting up a ferocious fight against the Russians. But as you know, they are outnumbered and outgunned by the Russians. The Ukrainian troops are saying there that they are constantly being hit by airstrikes, missiles and artillery strikes.\par \par \uc2 And even though the Ukrainians are saying that they are enormously grateful to the United States and Great Britain for the weapons that they`re supplying, it`s simply not enough. You will recall that the Biden administration pledged at a billion dollars worth of military aid in that aid package with 36,000 rounds of 150 millimeter ammunition, but the Ukrainians are saying 36,000 rounds is only going to last a week because the Russians are pounding them with about 50,000 or 60,000 rounds a day. And both sides are suffering incredible losses, Reverend. The Ukrainians are saying that they`re losing between 100 and 200 men on that frontline every day.\par \par \uc2 Today, we had an opportunity to speak to one of those soldiers on the frontline to get a description of what`s going on there. Let`s take a listen to what he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: The fighting was very intense and particularly I stayed a couple days, a couple weeks in Severodonetsk, it`s not severe but it`s very hard, a lot of artillery and mortar and multiple-launch rocket systems shelling of the industrial area of the city itself.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [17:15:27]\par \par \uc2 AROUZI: And, Reverend, this is showing all the signs of being a grinding, attritional war, that the Russians are using these methods to just wear down the Ukrainians with sheer brutality. The head of \uc2 NATO\uc2  said today that the world must prepare itself for years of war with the Russians, but it`s very important (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Ali Arouzi in Ukraine, thank you for that report.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, the principle of freedom is the foundation of June teeth. But beyond that, there are the lessons of truth and justice. In this week`s Rise Up, I will explain how we can apply those lessons in today`s political climate.\par \par \uc2 And later, a new study sheds new light on America`s love affair with guns. A panel joins me discuss how it could shoot down on any chance of real gun reform.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague Gigi Stone with today`s other top news stories. Gigi?\par \par \uc2 GIGI STONE, MSNBC HOST: Thanks, Rev. Here`s some stories we are watching at this hour. There were three people injured after a 70-foot yacht burned and sank in New Hampshire. That`s according to local authorities. The yacht got heading toward a marina in Newcastle when a passenger noticed black smoke below deck.\par \par \uc2 Three passengers and two dogs jumped overboard as the boat was engulfed in flames. People and the dogs were rescued by nearby boats.\par \par \uc2 Swimming`s international governing body today released a new rule effectively restricting transgender athletes from competing in women`s events. The new policy only prevents swimmers who transitioned before age 12 to participate in the women`s category. The new policy also calls for the creation of an open competition category that could include transgender athletes.\par \par \uc2 And Yellowstone National Park is set to partially reopen Wednesday after record flooding led the park to close for the first time in 34 years. Visitors had to be evacuated at the height of this tourist season as high waters washed out homes, roads and bridges in parts of Wyoming, Montana and Idaho.\par \par \uc2 I`m Gigi Stone Woods. We`ll have more of POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break. So, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:20:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Today, we celebrate the second Juneteenth as an officially recognized federal holiday. And while it is undoubtedly a festive occasion, it should also be a day of reflection not only for black Americans but for all citizens in this country.\par \par \uc2 The holiday memorializes the events of June 19, 1865 when a group of enslaved black people in Galveston, Texas were informed by federal troops that they were free. This was two years after President \uc2 Abraham Lincoln\uc2  had signed the emancipation proclamation releasing them from bondage, but in many parts of the confederacy, freedom didn`t come from the stroke of a pen but rather at the barrel of a union gun. Some whites simply lied to the black people they held captive as property about was happening or refused to release them until they were forced to.\par \par \uc2 Outside of the confederacy, slavery persisted legally, even after Juneteenth in states, such as Kentucky, Delaware, and even New Jersey, until the 13th Amendment abolishing the practice nationwide was ratified later that year.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, justice could be delayed but not denied. When I think about that history, I`m inspired by how far we`ve come but also troubled by how familiar it all feels. I hear echoes of that passed in the hearings of January 6th committee, which tell the story of a new generation of Americans willing to embrace a big lie and trample on the rights of countless fellow citizens in order to empower a select self interested we. It`s no wonder that the insurrectionists carried confederate flags as they defiled our United States Capitol, or that some in the Republican Party and the right-wing media would incite a moral panic over the mere mention of race in the classroom.\par \par \uc2 But if the lesson of Juneteenth was merely about our country`s racist roots, it would only be half a holiday. The true moral of the Juneteenth story is a righteous dream can only be deferred and never dies. The enslaved people of Galveston lived under a regime of unspeakable brutality. Their extraordinary feat was to hold on tight to their humanity until freedom came. Because they persisted back then, we don`t need to wait for an army now.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:04]\par \par \uc2 We can rise up and demand justice for ourselves.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. We have several topics to cover with my political panel. Let`s bring in former Republican Representative Joe Walsh of Illinois and Michael Hardaway, founder of Hardaway Wire.\par \par \uc2 Joe, January 6th committee will have their next hearing on Tuesday. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  reports they have emails that showed Ginni Thomas, wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , communicated with Trump Attorney John Eastman in the days leading up to January 6th. They say she may have been involved in efforts to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 The panel has asked Ginni Thomas to testify, and she says she looks forward to speaking with the committee.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:05]\par \par \uc2 What potential impact could her testimony have?\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. JOE WALSH (R-IL): Well, Reverend Al, it should have huge impact but it probably won`t. I mean, how horrible that the wife of a \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  justice is, in essence, an insurrectionist? And that should be huge news. That should be troubling news.\par \par \uc2 But Al, you and I know the former president of the United States is an insurrectionist and that`s what these hearings have made clear so far. Everybody around Trump knew the big lie was all a bunch of bull. Trump knew that and yet he continued to spread it.\par \par \uc2 My hope is that these hearings will have an impact, but talking to Republicans every day, Al, they`re not paying attention. They don`t seem to care, sadly.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michael, with inflation at a 40 year high, there is a lot of concern right now about the health of the economy. President Biden is urging Americans to stay optimistic. He told the Associated Press that a recession is, quote, not inevitable. This is as a new NBC report shows voters, Democrats, need to turn out in the election are really the ones hardest hit by this. Those with lower incomes, blacks, Hispanics and those under 40 are the ones that I`m referring to that are being hit the hardest. What should Democrats be saying to those voters?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL HARDAWAY, FOUNDER HARDAWAY WIRE: Rev, I would tell you that Democrats have failed to tell their story. Obviously, inflation in America is a problem but inflation across the world is a problem. Every major economy is dealing with this.\par \par \uc2 But if we`re going to have the conversation about this presidents economic record, let`s have the full conversation. You`ve got a 8.7 million jobs created by this president. He`s cut child poverty in half during his first year in office, and he has historically low unemployment, along with the fact that he`s the first president in about 70 years to pass an infrastructure bill. And so we have to have a full conversation about what is being delivered by this White House.\par \par \uc2 Now, I think they should do a better job and could do a better job in telling that story, but they have delivered, but they must address inflation and I think they`re doing the right thing --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So, you think, Michael, that the problem is messaging, that they`re not getting their message out, or the problem is they don`t have a message?\par \par \uc2 HARDAWAY: The problem is they need a stronger message. The problem is that they need to simplify and articulate the things they have delivered for the American public. No one knows that President Biden created 8.7 million jobs. No one knows that under this president, nearly job loss during the pandemic has been recovered. That matters to people and they have failed to tell that story.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes. And the question is, is it too late to tell it now when you`ve got gas prices that $5? Is anyone really going to hear that? But you`ve got to really come up with a strategy.\par \par \uc2 Let me go back to in a minute, Joe. Senators are hoping to pass a gun bill this week before the scheduled of July 4th recess. This comes as a new report by \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  shows there are more guns than people in the United States.\par \par \uc2 According to a new study of the global firearm ownership, this as gun manufacturers are encouraging more people to purchase firearms. How optimistic are you that new legislation will be passed to help address the country`s gun violence crisis?\par \par \uc2 WALSH: Hey, Al, I`m not, because I think these Republican senators went home and a very vocal minority of their base has gotten in their ears. We saw what happened to \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  down in Texas. I mean, he`s leading the Republican efforts on what really should be, and I say this as a proud gun owner and gun rights advocate, I mean, expanding background checks and red flag laws, those should be basics.\par \par \uc2 I mean, most gun owners support these reforms, but \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  was booed down in Texas for leading these talks. So, I think Republicans are going to get cold feet here.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And booed only for leading the talks. I mean, he was holding the line on some of the things that they would have wanted him to hold the line on. They booed at him because they don`t even want him in the conversation, which is now a healthy look to me toward trying to resolve this matter the way we must in terms of gun violence.\par \par \uc2 Michael, tomorrow, we are celebrating Juneteenth, as I`ve been talking about the whole show. And as for the entire month, we`ve been recognizing pride month, which I also brought up in this show.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:05]\par \par \uc2 Now, amid the celebration of diversity and inclusion, there is also fear. Many see what happened in Buffalo and recent arrests of militia members allegedly intent on disrupting a pride parade in Idaho. And they wonder whether their own festivities could be disrupted. What is your take on this mood of nervousness that seems to be gripping the country?\par \par \uc2 HARDAWAY: Well, the truth is America has come a long way, but we still have a long way to go. And as it relates to sort of the issues in Buffalo with the racially targeted killings there in South Carolina and others, it all comes back to one thing, which is the cancer white supremacy in America, which must be eradicated by this Justice Department.\par \par \uc2 And I would say that given the role of black Democrats have played in the victories the Democratic Party has enjoyed over the past decade or so, I would say to you that this needs to be at the top of the list for the attorney general to address white supremacy and those organizations in law enforcement and outside of law enforcement around the country that are targeting black and brown people and gay people and others who are just trying to live their lives.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Joe, I want to bring up something that caught my eye. Politico is reporting that Florida Governor DeSantis has already raised more than $100 million for his November gubernatorial race. This is in thanks in large part to some of Donald Trump`s biggest benefactors. You ran against Trump in 2020. What do these donations suggest about the party`s willingness to move away from Trump and possibly support a DeSantis candidacy in 2024?\par \par \uc2 WALSH: Al, I actually don`t think much. I think, privately, most of the big Republican donors don`t want Trump to run again, and Trump knows that, which is why I think Trump will come out within two months and announced that he is running for a reelection because he wants to put DeSantis and everybody else in their spot. He wants to freeze the field. So, I think that is what he is you going to do.\par \par \uc2 Look, AlI, this is still his party and he is just going to reassert himself. That is bad for the country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Joe Walsh and Michael Hardaway, thank you both for being on.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we celebrate this Juneteenth on Father`s Day. We sit down with an award-winning author and children`s musician to discuss black fatherhood. And tonight on MSNBC at 8:00 P.M., \uc2 Olivia Troye\uc2 , ex-aide to Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , sits down with Mehdi Hasan. Plus, former Ukrainian President Poroshenko, as the country he used to lead surpasses 100 days of war with Russia. Again, that`s tonight at 8:00 Eastern Time.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:40:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we always do on this very special day, I want to send out shout-outs to fathers everywhere but certainly black fathers and father figures. And for all the hats I`ve worn in my career, none has ever brought me more joy and satisfaction than being a father to my daughters, which is why I was so pleased when I looked up to see that a friend of the show, recording artist Pierce Freelon, a true renaissance, had sent me a copy of his latest children`s book, Daddy Daughter Day, a tribute to the bond between a father and his little girl as they spend a very special afternoon together, just the two of them.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Grammy-nominated recording artist and author of Daddy Daughter Day Pierce Freelon. Pierce, happy Juneteenth and thanks for coming back to the show. And, of course, I`m thinking back to Father`s Day last year when you and your children were kind enough to perform for us. You`ve got some talent in that house. And, frankly, it`s in genes. Your mother, Nina, herself a Grammy award-winning jazz singer, your father, the late Phil Freelon, who designed the Smithsonian`s African-American History Museum in Washington, and then there is you, a former city council member in Durham, North Carolina, an activist, a musician, and now a children`s author. Tell us about Daddy Daughter Day.\par \par \uc2 PIERCE FREELON, AUTHOR, DADDY DAUGHTER DAY: Yes, thank you so much for having me on, Reverend Sharpton. It`s an honored to be here. And, yes, Daddy Daughter Day is a celebration for all the girl dads out there. You know what that`s all about? And a special day, just me and my daughter, Stella, spending time together, kicking it at the house, building a fort at the crib, making smoothies, meditating, then we go outside, we use our imagination, and we play. We go to a protest. So, there`s that political piece in there, making sure our kids are conscious and aware in this country, which is important, as we know from watching your show. And then coming home, cooking a meal and laying her down to bed. It`s just a wonderful exploration of the day and a life of a girl dad, and that special bond between father and daughter that I care so deeply.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Pierce, my producer tells me that your daughter and I are somehow connected, and you have a photo to prove it. Let`s see it and give me the lowdown.\par \par \uc2 FREELON: Yes, yes. So, my daughters great grandfather is Reverend Dr. Samuel DeWitt Proctor, a wonderful preacher, Reverend, coming from your wonderful tradition. And my wife was like, you`ve got to tell Rev, you`ve got to tell him that. Stella`s grandfather is -- they call him DeWitt. That`s what they call him.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: He was one of the most prolific, respected ministers of the black church in history, Samuel DeWitt Proctor. That`s great. That`s a picture of him and I Reverend Willie Barrow, who was one of the leading female ministers and civil rights leaders at that time when I started at 12 or 13 under Operation Bread Basket. And, of course, there`s \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2 , who is the head of Operation Bread Basket.\par \par \uc2 Ironically, Pierce, Dr. Proctor, your daughter`s great grandfather, was the president of the college that \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2  went to. So, that was their bond.\par \par \uc2 FREELON: AGI pride (ph).\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, that`s right. That`s right.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you this. While I have you, the nation is observing Juneteenth for the first time. And I find myself reflecting on our history as a parent, and I`m pleased to say, a grandparent, I have one grandchild now. As a black father, what are you thinking about on this Juneteenth?\par \par \uc2 FREELON: Well, this Juneteenth coincides with Father`s Day. So, I`m thinking about my dad. I`m thinking about my dad, Phil Freelon. You mentioned he was the architect who designed the Smithsonian National Museum of African-American History Culture. And Juneteenth is really about celebrating that history, about cherishing that legacy of black struggle and black resiliency that is so such an important part of our story.\par \par \uc2 And if we don`t tell our own story, then somebody else will. It was Frederick Douglass who said what is the 4th of July to the Negro? We need to have our own opportunities to celebrate our legacies and our history and our culture. And I just thank God that my father and people like my daughter`s great grandfather, Samuel DeWitt Proctor, did such important work to tell our stories, to raise up our ancestors and preserve our legacies through their important work. So, those are the two dads in my mind today, my dad, Phil Freelon, and my wife`s grandfather, Dr. Proctor.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I`ve got to ask you this, Pierce. Do you have any plans at all to ever run for office again?\par \par \uc2 FREELON: Yes, I`m keeping the door open. I get that question a lot. And, you know, there are a lot of important things that I want to do in this life. And right now, I`m in the children`s space. I`m writing books. I`m working on podcasts and writing for television. And I think that`s a really important role for me as a black dad to play now when my children are young. But I`m absolutely open to getting involved in politics again. And it was the privilege of my life to serve on \uc2 Durham City Council\uc2  where I was born and raised, and I`m definitely open.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Pierce Freelon, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:50:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Last night, Tribeca Film Festival had its last showing. The documentary, Loudmouth, it is a documentary about my activism, when they compared the controversies when I became known in the 80s all the way until know where a lot of the same issues are there, and it may not be as controversial in some circles but have in others. But the story is -- I`m focal point, but the story is where we`ve gone and not gone in race in America.\par \par \uc2 It was a great night. I was honored to be the focus of the last film of the Tribeca Film season, and Robert De Niro was there. And Spike Lee, Oscar winner, film maker on his own right, who we grew up in Brooklyn, New York, together, and I was in many of his films. Spike and I go way back. I`ve been in a couple of his films, and for him to come and be with us.\par \par \uc2 John Legend, as I said, is the executive producer. Here, he is chatting with me and Fashion Designer Ayesha McSure (ph) about his hopes and dreams of the message he wants and we must continue to keep fighting. And, of course, the whole cast of the production team, a diverse team, welcomed Robert De Niro. And standing with Robert De Niro, you see the president of MSNBC came, and I was on that Rashida Jones came and saw the documentary herself, Kadar Masselberg (ph), and the whole team standing with her and Robert De Niro and Jane Rosenthal.\par \par \uc2 I think I say I`ll have to say that if we are going to do anything on this official observance of Juneteenth, we should reflect about what happened then in 1865, what was happening now, and talk about things that we talked about in that documentary, how from the racial attacks in `86 to George Floyd, to Buffalo is still there, and what can we do about it, how do we protect it, how do we deal with states` rights, which was what Texas used to prolong slavery the 1800.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:03]\par \par \uc2 Let`s make Juneteenth more than just a holiday taken off. Let`s turn it on and keep fighting against those aspects of white supremacy that still plague us.\par \par \uc2 Also, on Father`s Day, think of the fathers who did do the right thing in not only their homes but in their communities. And let`s have healing among us to realize that we all have flaws. My father left when I was young. I resented it for many years. But I`m so happy I reconciled with him and saw the strength and him. My father was an entrepreneur and a businessman even in the 50s, when it wasn`t popular to be.\par \par \uc2 So, let`s have a happy Father`s Day to my father and always on Father`s Day, I have to thank the man who became like my father, the godfather of soul, James Brown, who is always there. I thought about him last night as Tribeca gave me that honor. He would be proud. He always told me I could be something and I`m glad I believed him.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 That is it for me. We will see you back here next weekend at 5:00 P.M. Eastern Time on, of course, MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:02]\par \par \uc2 AMERICAN VOICES with Alicia Menendez will return next.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220620ei6j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL; June 15, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell; Michael McFaul \par \uc2 6,74