ary 6th committee. I want to present to him a question that Senator Sheldon Whitehouse has, who is going to be joining us later in the hour. Sheldon Whitehouse wants to know, where did that John Eastman letter really come from? Did John Eastman write it? Did someone write it for him? Or did the person who gave it to John Eastman have someone else -- where did it begin?\par \par \uc2 It`s a great question. And it`s one that I`ve overlooked until Senator Whitehouse focused on it. So, we will try to get some headway on that.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Yeah, the one member of the investigation you would like to ask about that is the one who is a distinguished constitutional law professor. And that`s the one you have got.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We are lucky.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: I better go.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Bye, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, now, it looks like Clarence Thomas`s wife is going to need a great F-ing criminal defense lawyer because she is now implicated in exactly the same legal jeopardy -- criminal legal jeopardy -- that provoked exactly that advice from one Trump White House lawyer to another lawyer, who was trying to commit crimes for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  election crimes.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the January 6 committee is reportedly considering how to deal with an issue that Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has been dealing with for failing to deal with for many years now, through political activism of Ginni Thomas, which may have repeatedly cross the line into criminal activism, in her attempts to violate election law and overturn the last presidential election.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " is reporting tonight that the January 6 committee has obtained email correspondence between Virginia "Ginni" Thomas, the wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and lawyer John Eastman who played a key role in efforts to pressure Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to block the certification of Joe Biden`s victory.\par \par \uc2 Last night, the vice chair of the January 6 committee, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , released video of Trump White House lawyer Eric Herschmann telling John Eastman the day after January 6th, to quote, get a great f`ing criminal defense lawyer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC HERSCHMANN, FORMER TRUMP WHITE HOUSE LAWYER: I said, good, John. Now I`m going to give you the best free legal advice you are going to get in your life. Get a great f`ing criminal defense lawyer. You are going to need it. And then I hung up on him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Virginia Thomas is now in the same legal boat that may now be a sinking ship, the same one as John Eastman and in need of the same kind of criminal defense lawyer.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:05]\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reports that the committee`s members and staffers are now discussing whether to spend time during the public hearings, exploring Ginni Thomas`s role in the attempts to overturn the outcome of the 2020 election. John Eastman once served as a Supreme Court clerk for \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . And Virginia Thomas is known to have stayed close -- in close contact -- with the former Thomas clerks.\par \par \uc2 Federal Judge David Carter ordered John Eastman to release documents that the January 6 committee, even though Eastman claimed attorney client privilege protect those documents. The judge ordered them release because, he says, that although he was acting as Donald Trump`s lawyer when he was advising \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , that Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  could simply disregard electoral votes on January 6th, during Congress`s official count of the electoral votes, Judge Carter said that because the attorney client privilege does not cover possible planning to commit crimes, the documents that the committee obtained from Eastman could not be protected by the attorney client privilege.\par \par \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  is the only Supreme Court justice who voted to block documents from Trump White House Chief of Staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  being delivered from to the January 6 committee at the time that \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  try to block those documents from being transmitted it was not publicly known that his wife was in frequent communication with \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and that her communications could be included in the documents that Justice Thomas was trying to hide from the committee.\par \par \uc2 That episode alone makes \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  the most corrupted Supreme Court to justice in our lifetimes. And it makes Virginia Thomas the most corrupted and possibly criminal wife of any Supreme Court justice in history.\par \par \uc2 Clarence Thomas`s Supreme Court career began in scandal at his confirmation hearing, when he was accused of sexual harassment. It may now be on the verge of ending in crimes. The possible election crimes and conspiracy crimes that may have been committed by his wife, who told \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , after January 6th that she was disgusted with Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . That was her word, disgusted, because Vice President Pence followed the law. Ginni Thomas was disgusted by that.\par \par \uc2 And after the certification of the electoral vote by the Congress on January 6th, Virginia Thomas wrote to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 : We are living through wet feels like the end of America.\par \par \uc2 Now surely, surely \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  knew how his wife felt after Joe Biden`s election became finally, officially certified by Congress on January 6th. Surely, Virginia Thomas did not share her most intimate feelings about the election only with mark meadows. Surely, she told her husband that we are living through what feels like the end of America. And still, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  did not recuse himself from the \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  documents case that potentially involved his wife`s own communications.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, what`s the wife of a Supreme Court to justice in need of a great criminal defense lawyer, we are living through what feels like the end of the Supreme Court as we know it.\par \par \uc2 Today, Marc Short, who served as Vice President Mike Pence`s chief of staff, said that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  knew from the beginning that he had absolutely no power to overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARC SHORT, FORMER CHIE FOF STAFF TO VICE PRESIDENT MIKE PENCE: I think what was very clear is the vice president has always been a constitutional conservative. And I think he knew what his constitutional duty was that day. He knew it from the beginning. And I think he was clear with the president and so is our office, clear about what we viewed his role as.\par \par \uc2 I think for any-limited government conservative, I don`t think you would want the notion of our founders would have thought that one person would be bestowed with that much authority, to overturn an election result.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  of Maryland. He`s a member of the House Select committee investigating the January 6th attack on the Capitol and he served as lead impeachment manager in the second impeachment trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congressman Raskin, thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 I want to begin with what the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  has breaking news tonight.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:01]\par \par \uc2 And that is, they are reporting that you are considering how to deal with these revelations about Clarence Thomas`s wife, including this latest revelation that she was in contact with John Eastman. That the committee is considering whether to include material about that in the public hearings that you`re having.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us about the committee`s approach to this evidence about Clarence Thomas`s wife?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Well, not much, Lawrence. You know, we are not commenting on specific witnesses and specific investigative lines. I will say that we are committed to getting all of the facts related to everything that happened, and delivering them to the people.\par \par \uc2 I just saw "The Post" article moments ago. I kind of hoped they would have led with the article about Judge Nichols smashing decision, which completely rejected the Steve Bannon`s attempt to attack his indictment by the Department of Justice and the contempt holding by Congress. But the court became one in a whole series of courts to reject an attack on the integrity of the select committee and the congressional investigation. So we feel, again, vindicated by that, of course -- none of these cases have made their way up to \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  or anyone on the Supreme Court yet, except for that original one where Thomas was the lone dissenter in that 8- 1 decision.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: If there are cases involving the committee or involving any aspects of the presidential election that make it to the Supreme Court, should \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  recuse himself?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, I mean, in the terrible ethics rules governing the Supreme Court, it is up to each justice to decide for himself or herself whether or not to recuse, based on a conflict of interest or an apparent conflict. It`s the only court in the land which essentially doesn`t have anybody overseeing their decisions. So, obviously, I would reserve judgment on any movements that we would make in terms of something like recusal. But I will tell you, as a legislator, I thought this is a problem for several years. And it is something that is moving through the House judiciary committee right now, to try to get the Supreme Court to bind themselves the way every other court in the land has a real procedure for asserting a conflict of interest, that would compel a judge or justice to recuse himself or herself.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let`s listen to what your vice chair, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , said on video about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was told was illegal. Let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): President Trump had no factual basis for what he was doing. And he had been told it was illegal. Despite this, President Trump plotted with a lawyer named John Eastman and others to overturn the outcome of the election on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: She said, he had been told it was illegal. Will you be presenting evidence in the public hearings of who told \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  that it was illegal?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Yes. In just a matter of hours from now, you will hear, just as you heard the people telling \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  as a matter of fact that he had lost the election by more than 7 million votes, and 306 to 232 in the Electoral College -- you heard that from the attorney general of the United States and you get it from his own campaign manager. You heard it from lots of people.\par \par \uc2 You are going to hear from multiple people who say that they heard that this thing was a big joke. I`m thinking of it as the big lie, the big rip off. And now the big joke. The big joke is, for more than two centuries, vice presidents have overlook the fact that they have the power to wave a wand and vaporize the votes of tens of millions of people.\par \par \uc2 Of course, that`s ridiculous. Nobody has ever thought the vice president has the power to just unilaterally extra-constitutionally reject the Electoral College votes that have come in through the governor certificates of ascertainment. And it was perfectly clear, as a matter of law as well as a matter of fact that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  lost the election and that it was over and that the vice president had no such powers.\par \par \uc2 When we got to the floor at 1:00 on January 6th, Vice [resident Pence had distributed a memo to all of us, explaining very carefully why he had no such power.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So will the testimony be that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was told that it was illegal for \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to do that? Or was he also told that it was illegal for you, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , to urge him to violate the law?\par \par \uc2 [22:15:08]\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: We are going to have to pay attention, closely, to the witnesses - - certainly, the former. I`m not sure about the latter. Of course, it doesn`t make any difference, because ignorance of the law is no excuse. It`s very clear, I think, to any reasonable person, that the vice president does not have the power to unilaterally pick the president of the United States. And I think \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  well understood that.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not he was told he could get in trouble for that, I don`t know. Again I`m not quite sure how relevant that is because all of us are bound for the legal consequences the constitutional consequences of our actions, regardless of whether or not we know exactly how much time we might do for violating our legal duties.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: As you know, the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  did its own examination of what was going on in the Justice Department in those final days of the Trump Justice Department, where there was that issue of elevating an obscure environmental lawyer to be acting attorney general, to carry out this plot. And it was that letter that is referred to, sometimes, as the Eastman letter, the letter that they wanted the acting attorney general to send to Georgia and other states telling officials they are to simply negate Joe Biden`s victory in those states and submit a Trump slate of electors for the Electoral College.\par \par \uc2 Senator Whitehouse has been trying to find out where the letter originated. Do we know whether John Eastman wrote the letter? Do we know whether it was written in the Justice Department, exactly where that letter originated?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, it`s providence is a matter of some curiosity, and there are different theories out there. So I don`t want to opine about it, because we are only dealing with actual facts, things that can be documented. I will say that it appears -- and I`m going to pay very close attention to the hearing tomorrow -- but it does appear that John Eastman rejected, perhaps around a year before, the very theory that he proceeded on, in 2020.\par \par \uc2 He had before taken the exact opposite position. So, whether he just had a change of heart and he saw the light when he thought this was the only opportunity to throw a Hail Mary pass, or whether someone else appeared, I don`t know. It will be interesting to try to follow that.\par \par \uc2 But in any event, John Eastman did become the legal architect of the Green Bay Sweep. He was the one saying, let`s throw everything at them. Let`s see what we can do in the Department of Justice to get them to cry corruption, to give Pence the opportunity and the pretext for rejecting Electoral College votes, we`re turning them to the states and then either just declaring a victory in the Electoral College for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or kicking the whole thing into the House of Representatives for a contingent election. He became identified with it regardless of whose original brain child it was.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah. And I just want to make it clear for the audience, it was Jeffrey Clark in the Justice Department who want to send that letter. And the question is, where did that letter originate from.\par \par \uc2 Before we go, I want to cover what Congressman Loudermilk said today after that video was released by your committee showing the tour that he led, which he originally said he didn`t do, on a time when no one was supposed to be -- no visitors were supposed to be allowed anywhere in the capitol complex, not just the capital building, but anywhere inside the campus. It`s that video.\par \par \uc2 Your committee released that video today. And Congressman Loudermilk said it was a totally innocent -- he came out today saying it was a totally innocent group of people. And in the spot where they appeared to be taking pictures of a staircase or a doorway that no one would take pictures of, it was actually some kind of gold seal there that was interesting and worthy of a photograph.\par \par \uc2 Do you know what is in the photographs that those people were taking? When they were with the congressman there?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: No. I have not seen those. I am not sure we are in possession of those. We should be clear that Congressman Loudermilk has not been accused of anything. We asked him to come and talk because we had interest in what he did know.\par \par \uc2 But that video raises a lot of questions in the minds of various people. And, of course, there was another video found of one of the people who is on the tour, on January 6th, and he was very much traveling with the MAGA crowd and had an interaction with another guy in the crowd, who had fashioned his American flag into a weapon, and said that it was for a special person and they chortled about that.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:21]\par \par \uc2 So -- but I certainly am very interested in this and think that there might be some evidence there. Of course, this is one small detail in a huge picture of lots of people being involved in the ultimate mob violence that beset the capitol.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, Rachel covered this extensively in the previous hour. And one of the man who was on tour with the Loudermilk next day on January 6th is on video threatening \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , Congressman Nadler, \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  and threatening them --\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: AOC.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yeah, threatening them with violence and that was all shown in the previous hour.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: You bet.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, Senator Sheldon Whitehouse has that question that he`s hoping that the January 6 committee can answer. And Senator Whitehouse`s home state of Rhode Island is not waiting for Washington to pass gun safety legislation. The Rhode Island legislature passed it late last night. Rhode Island Senator Sheldon Whitehouse will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:26:06]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  was the first to investigate the attempted coup in the Trump Justice Department in which environmental lawyer Jeffrey Clark posing as an election law expert suddenly tried to convince \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to appoint him acting attorney general, so that he could send a letter to government officials in key states won by \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , telling them the lie that the Justice Department found election fraud in those states and that Republican officials in those states should simply send Trump electors to the Electoral College, instead of the legitimate Biden electors.\par \par \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  Sheldon Whitehouse wants to know the true origin of the letter. It doesn`t appear that Jeffrey Clark actually wrote it himself. He seems to have received a draft of it from another recently arrived Republican lawyer at the Justice Department, who is also not an election expert.\par \par \uc2 Senator Whitehouse told "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " the other day, that it certainly could be a symptom of a much larger and more coherent plan then has currently been disclosed.\par \par \uc2 Senator Whitehouse`s state of Rhode Island is now the second state after New York to pass gun reform legislation since the mass murder at Robb Elementary School in Uvalde, Texas. The new legislation raises the legal age to purchase any firearms and ammunition in Rhode Island from 18 to 21. It bans the open carry of loaded shotguns in public, and bans ammunition magazines containing more than ten rounds of ammunition.\par \par \uc2 Members of the Rhode Island general assembly debated the legislation late into the night, last night, with protesters for and against the legislation in the gallery watching. Governor \uc2 Daniel McKee\uc2  will sign the new laws.\par \par \uc2 Today in Washington, Senator Whitehouse participated in a Senate Judiciary hearing in the impact of gun violence on children, where he defended Rhode Island`s new gun laws.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. SHELDON WHITEHOUSE (D-RI): We don`t allow people under 21 to buy beer. So why do they need to buy firearms and ammunition? That`s a little hard to explain, particularly in the wake of what we`ve seen recently. And for the life of me, I can`t imagine, as a gun owner myself, why you need open carry for a loaded long gun in this society. So, I think we are seeing some signs of progress at the state level.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Senator Sheldon Whitehouse of Rhode Island. He is a member of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Senator, let me begin where you were today in the hearing, on gun safety and Rhode Island`s experience overnight in passing these new laws.\par \par \uc2 What is this going to mean for Rhode Island?\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: Well, the issue of guns is just as passionate and emotional in Rhode Island as everywhere else. I thought it was a terrific moment when our general assembly, in full public, and in a very lively debate, came down and made three, I think, very sensible, important decisions that you`ve already described. So, I`m proud of my home state legislature right now.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And I want to get to your concerns about what was going on in the Justice Department in late December, when there was an attempted up there, with Jeffrey Clark trying to take over the Justice Department, with this letter that he was willing to send with his signature on it. But you suspect it didn`t originate with him.\par \par \uc2 What is it that you are suspecting about the origin of this letter?\par \par \uc2 [22:29:54]\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: Well for starters, Jeffrey Clark was described by his own Trump appointee colleagues as not competent. The body English in the judiciary committee hearing that I attended expressed real disdain for the guy by other Trump appointed folks in the Department of Justice. He ran the environmental section, which in the Trump Department of Justice, is a job that requires you to do nothing. And he had been lifted up to the position of acting civil division chief by the mere fact of vacancies as people left the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 So, this is not a guy with elections law experience, a lot of respect in the department, any background that would qualify him to write a letter like this. So finding out what a real source of the letter was, given that unlikelihood, I think becomes an investigative priority.\par \par \uc2 And I believe that that is being reviewed by the Department of Justice and by the January 6 Commission.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: And you have done extensive work about this vast network of a dark money funded, anonymously funded operations that work on the Republican side of these kinds of questions, on the Republican side of Supreme Court nominations, and on the Republican side of Justice Department interests in general.\par \par \uc2 Do you suspect that this kind of idea of just sending letters, telling legislators in states that they can simply throw out the Biden electors and replace them with the Trump electors that they have chosen themselves.\par \par \uc2 Do you suspect it originated out of that dark world?\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: It could well have. Both Jeffrey Clark and his associate in the Department of Justice, who may have produced the letter for him, instantly landed when they left the Department of Justice in dark money funded organizations.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s an important investigative thread to pursue. And speaking of important investigative threads to pursue, you will recall how Chief Justice Roberts went to DefCon 1 over the leak of the Alito abortion opinion and demanded investigations and said it was a betrayal of the court. If you look at the Ginni Thomas situation right now, that you were describing earlier, it seems to me that when you have the spouse of the Supreme Court justice now repeatedly connected with an insurrection against the country, and now connected with an individual who is so deeply in trouble that a White House -- a Trump White House legal counsel advised him to get a criminal defense attorney, that if you are going to go to DefCon 1 over the leak of a draft opinion, you might want to consider going to DefCon 3 or 4 or even 5 and start investigating within the court what they heck is going on here.\par \par \uc2 So, there is another big investigative gap in this space. And it`s the Supreme Court being unwilling to look at its own problems as regards Justice Thomas.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You know, we have used -- we`ve worn out the word "unprecedented" in the Trump era, beginning at some point in 2015, in that presidential campaign. But I have been struggling to find the words to impress upon this audience tonight how unprecedented the Virginia Thomas situation is.\par \par \uc2 The history of possible crime amongst Supreme Court justices` wives is exactly zero in its entire history, right up until this year, with Ginni Thomas` involvement in this insurrection. And she herself specifically advocating crimes, advocating the same kind of violations of law that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was advocating.\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: Yes. I think weren`t people supposed to be taken out on a barge off Guantanamo and shot without due process? The whole thing is pretty extreme.\par \par \uc2 But where it comes back to the court, and where it comes back to something that if you take the court at its own word, that it should be the only one to look into its situation, it should be the only one to clean up its mess, it seems to me that it is time to look at this mess because of Justice Thomas` vote that would have disabled or diminished the ability of the January 6 Commission to look into evidence that we now know would have led to his wife.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:44]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Should Justice Thomas, at minimum, if not resigning from the court, be publicly announcing right now his recusal from any cases that could involve the presidential election or the transition of power or January 6 in any way?\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: That would be a stunningly obvious and proper thing for him to do. The fact that it has not happened casts an additional cloud over the court. And again, the fact that Justice Roberts will go to DefCon 1 and call in the marshals and declare an investigation and say the court`s been betrayed because a document leaked and then do absolutely nothing on this set of facts is another very, very unfortunate situation for the court.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator Sheldon Whitehouse, thank you very much for joining us again tonight. Always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 WHITEHOUSE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up President Biden spent 40 minutes on the phone today with Ukraine`s President Zelenskyy pledging to stand by Ukraine in Vladimir Putin`s war of aggression. The president promised more military aid to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Former ambassador to Russia Michael McFaul will join us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:35:55]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: President Biden spent 40 minutes on the phone today with Ukraine`s President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 . President Biden pledged the United States, quote, "will not waver in our commitment to the Ukrainian people as they fight for their freedom."\par \par \uc2 President Biden announced another $1 billion dollars in military assistance for Ukraine today. In a new video tonight, President Zelenskyy thanked the United States for its support and praised the heroism of the Ukrainian people, saying, quote, "The Ukrainian army proved that courage and wisdom on the battlefield together, with the ability to tactically defeat the enemy gives significant results. Everyday, I fight for Ukraine to get the necessary weapons and equipment but courage, wisdom and tactical skills cannot be imported. Our heroes have it."\par \par \uc2 And joining us now is Michael McFaul, who served as the U.S. ambassador to Russia from 2012 to 2014 in the Obama administration. He`s an MSNBC international affairs analyst.\par \par \uc2 And Ambassador McFaul I apologize to you -- I will focus it to you in that this program has been unable to reach within our hours to Ukraine coverage while we have been covering, you know, covering the shooting and the mass murder in Uvalde, the January 6 Committee.\par \par \uc2 So, could you just bring this audience up to date on the state of the war and what we need to know about Vladimir Putin`s war of aggression in Ukraine tonight.\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCFAUL, MSNBC INTERNATIONAL AFFAIRS ANALYST: Lawrence, thanks for that big question.\par \par \uc2 First of all, I want to frame it big. If you think about the big objectives that Putin tried to achieve in Ukraine, he has failed in most of them. He failed to turn Ukrainians into Russians. He just call them Russians with accents. That unity did not happen. He failed to de-Nazify the country -- that is, to overthrow Zelenskyy. That did not happen. He failed to demilitarize the country. That did not happen. He failed to take Kyiv and other major cities. That didn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 But he is achieving success on the battlefield today in Donbas in eastern Ukraine. It`s a horrible, bloody war. It`s a war of attrition. It`s fought with artillery and rocket fire going back and forth. Dozens of Ukrainians and Russians are dying every single day.\par \par \uc2 They are making incremental advances, the Russians are. And that`s why President Zelenskyy needs more weapons, he needs more firepower from the west. And that`s why today`s call and the new billion-dollar aid package was a significant development in the war.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let`s listen to what General \uc2 Mark Milley\uc2  said about the state of the war today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GENERAL \uc2 MARK MILLEY\uc2 , CHAIRMAN, JOINT CHIEFS OF STAFF: The numbers clearly favor the Russians in terms of artillery. They do outnumber, they outgun and outrange -- you`ve heard that many, many times. And they do have enough forces.\par \par \uc2 But the Russians have run into a lot of problems. They`ve got command and control issues, logistics issues, they`ve got morale issues, leadership issues, and a wide variety of other issues.\par \par \uc2 So the Ukrainians are fighting a heroic fight. This fight down the Donbas has been going on since the 16 April. And the advances that the Russians have made have been very slow, a very tough slog, a very severe battle of attrition -- almost World War I like. And the Russians have suffered tremendous amounts of casualties.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Ambassador McFaul, is it now a matter of keeping the Ukrainians equipped as well as possible?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Absolutely. It has been from the very beginning. By the way, Lawrence, I would say it has been for years, right? Think about had we provided all these weapons years ago -- maybe this war wouldn`t even have started.\par \par \uc2 But today they need more weapons, they need more ammunition, they need longer fires, right? You heard General Milley say that. Right now, the Russians have the advantage on the battlefield. And they need our weapons and weapons from other \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries to correct the balance of power on the battlefield in Donbas.\par \par \uc2 And I think it needs to be clearly stated, there will be no peace negotiations while the Russian army is advancing. And right now, Putin`s army is advancing. And so, until we can stop that, until we can help the Ukrainians to stop that advance, there won`t be any peace in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Everyone should be following Ambassador Michael McFaul on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  as I do, because when we do not have time to cover what`s happening in Ukraine, Michael McFaul always is, every day. Thank you very much for joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:59]\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Thank you. Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Coming up, Vice President Harris held a meeting with legal experts on the possible overturning of Roe versus Wade. One of those experts was law professor Michele Bratcher Goodwin and she will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:49:49]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We all believe and know that our nation was founded on certain principles that we hold dear, principles of freedom, liberty, and self determination.\par \par \uc2 And I do believe that all of those principles are at stake when we look at the leaked draft opinion about what this decision may end up being. I do believe that we should prepare for the right to privacy to be attacked and under attack with this decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That was vice president \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  at a meeting she held with experts on the consequences of the Supreme Court overturning Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest, Professor Michele Bratcher Goodwin, was in that meeting with the vice president. The next decision announcement day at the Supreme Court is Tuesday of next week when the court could announce a ruling overturning Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Michele Bratcher Goodwin, chancellor`s professor of law at the \uc2 University of California Irvine\uc2 . She is the author of "Policing The Womb: Invisible Women and Criminalization of Motherhood".\par \par \uc2 Professor Goodwin, thank you very much for joining us tonight. What did you tell the vice president in that meeting?\par \par \uc2 MICHELE BRATCHER GOODWIN, \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF CALIFORNIA IRVINE\uc2 : In that meeting I spoke with the vice president about the historical arc of privacy, and how the constitution protects individuals from involuntary servitude and as well about the coming criminalization and civil punishments that we will be seeing as states implement the trigger laws. The trigger laws after this decision may end up banning abortions in half the states of the United States.\par \par \uc2 And also what this means in terms of technology. The tracking devices, that prosecutors may try to use, to determine whether women have gone out of state to be able to seek reproductive health care or abortions in other states.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Is there any historic, or constitutional precedent for the idea that a state can pass its own law, and then say that it is somehow enforceable when you are in another state?\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: So, you know Lawrence, what we see now is the coming of a different kind of Jim Crow, a kind of Jane Crow revisited. You know, during the period of Jim Crow, there were states that tried to enact any number of laws to suppress the citizenship of black people. And some of that also included tracking if they traveled interstate, and who they traveled interstate with.\par \par \uc2 And so, some of these kinds of actions won`t be new, but still nefarious, still harmful, and of course it is absolutely outrageous. We have seen the Supreme Court turn a blind eye in many ways on supremacy, such that states are seeking to enact laws that would trump federal laws. We have never seen that before, it is absurd. Or when states have tried that, they have always failed, but not in this new era.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What were the -- what would you say are the most important points that were discussed in the meeting from the several different perspectives that were offered?\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: I think the first thing is that privacy and liberty are at stake. And that one can`t trust the guardrails that Justice Alito in the leaked draft opinion says will still be protected, such as contraceptive access, or such as marriage equality, or even interracial marriage. There are already members of state legislators that are saying that they are going after those things next.\par \par \uc2 Also, the possibility of extraditions. States saying that if they believe that birth begins at conception, then going after doctors in other states who perform abortions under the theory that there has been murder, manslaughter, first degree murder, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 So these were amongst the issues that were talked about, and what this might mean for families that want to build using assisted reproductive technologies. How does that fit into this new future?\par \par \uc2 Many of the kinds of things that Justice Alito might have suggested we shouldn`t worry about, we really should, and I think the vice president wants to be prepared for that rightly so.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: You have written about what this kind of change in law can mean to girls. That is the word that is never used, and certainly no Supreme Court justice used the word "girls" when they were talking about this in argument because there can be 12-year-old girls, there can be 13-year-old girls who suffer incest, rape, and find themselves pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Talk about what this kind of change, and the repeal of Roe versus Wade by the Supreme Court, what it can mean to girls.\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: Well, I`m glad that you brought that up because in this leaked draft opinion, Justice Alito speaks nothing about rapes, speaks nothing about incest, but the new plateau of these antiabortion laws are that they make no exceptions for cases of rape or incest. So a girl who seeks to terminate a pregnancy after a rape could be punished for trying to do so.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:59]\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: And the Supreme Court has given no attention to that whatsoever. In many ways, you see the kind of horrific path and pattern that we have seen in other countries that we would separate ourselves from, that we`d say well those are countries that engage in human rights abuses, but that is where we will be.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Professor Michele Goodwin, thank you very much for joining us once again on this important subject. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GOODWIN: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tonight`s LAST WORD is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:55:27]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Programming note, I will join MSNBC`s special primetime coverage of the January 6 hearing tomorrow starting at 8:00 p.m. Eastern with the honorable Rachel Maddow presiding.\par \par \uc2 At 10:00 p.m., we will have our regularly scheduled edition of THE LAST WORD right here where we will no doubt continue the end of discussion of tomorrow`s public hearing of the January 6 Committee and possibly other breaking news of the day.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220616ei6f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for June 11, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Ali Arouzi, Milissa Rehberger \par \uc2 6,515 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, MSNBC HOST: We are going to talk about the Georgia Governor`s race and so much more.\par \par \uc2 You will not want to miss it, and later tonight at 7:00 p.m Eastern, I`ll be back for MSNBC`s special coverage with my good friend, Amon Mohideen, and my friend Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  who is coming up next.\par \par \uc2 [17:00:15]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Thank you, Symone and good evening, and welcome to POLITICSNATION. I`m coming to you live from my home, I tested positive for COVID, but so much was happening I wanted to be with us all to talk about it tonight, in no uncertain way.\par \par \uc2 So let`s start with the show. Tonight`s lead: Truth without reconciliation.\par \par \uc2 This week, the House Select Committee investigating the January 6 insurrection took direct aim at former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for his role in inciting the events of that day.\par \par \uc2 The primetime televised hearing painted a picture of a President who at best was unmoved by the chaos he helped create, and worse, was intent on capitalizing on the unrest to overturn the 2020 election, the layers of complicity run deep, at least, that seems to be what the House Select was suggesting when it revealed multiple Republican Members of Congress requested pardons from Trump after the 6th.\par \par \uc2 I was wondering first to call what happened that day a coup, and for this week`s presentation by the Select Committee, it has only served to confirm my initial suspicions.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, many of the Americans who most need to hear the truth about what happened in the tumultuous final days of the Trump presidency remained in the dark, as many of the largest conservative media outlets have chosen to play down or completely ignore the Select Committee`s important work.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Congresswoman Robin Kelly, Democrat of Illinois, who sits on the House Oversight and Reform Committee.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thank you for joining us tonight\par \par \uc2 REP. ROBIN KELLY (D-IL): Thank you for having me. I hope you feel you better.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I have no symptoms, I feel great. I just to make sure that I follow all precautions and don`t put anyone else in danger of infection, but thank you for your wishes.\par \par \uc2 Let me go right to it, after this tough week for the nation, leading us off tonight, I read your account of being in the House gallery on January 6 of last year. You feared then that you might be killed by the insurrectionists who stormed the Capitol. Now watching this week`s public hearing with the news that several members of the Proud Boys, an extremist group, they had been indicted now for seditious conspiracy just days before this, what went through your mind, Congresswoman, from your fear that you might die that day, to watching this hearing, and knowing that just a few days before these indictments had come down?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Well, first of all, thanks for having me on the show. And I think my colleagues did a fantastic job. They`ve had over a thousand interviews, 140,000 documents that they`ve reviewed. And, you know, I think I`m okay.\par \par \uc2 But then when I see the -- when I saw the film and the video they had it does bring it back again, you know, what we went through that day and really is still on our minds.\par \par \uc2 There`s a group of us, we call ourselves the gallery group or the balcony group, that touch base with each other almost every single day about this, just to make sure that people are okay.\par \par \uc2 And you know, the American people deserve the truth, and I think they`re going to hear it.\par \par \uc2 Yes, there are people we`re not going to affect, but 20 million people watched the other night, which is fantastic.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, at the core of these hearings is President Trump, who took my ear was exactly who I knew him to be after Thursday big night`s presentation. But even still, I was struck by the theme that the carnage that occurred on January 6th did not seem to move in to respond as lawmakers` lives were in danger at the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what are your thoughts about that?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: Well, I`m very disappointed in my car. Always for still covering up just saying it was a regular touristy day, very disappointing. It makes me angry to be honest about it, and it makes me not want to work with them.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:10]\par \par \uc2 KELLY: And it makes me not want to work with them. I have no respect for the former President and what he was a part of, in my mind putting us through. We could have lost our lives that day or have gotten hurt as five people did lose their lives, you know, if the police officer didn`t shoot the woman, that`s the only reason they paused, and they could get us out of the gallery was because of that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congresswoman, let me go to this. Right now, you have March for our Lives rallies in hundreds of cities across the country, in large part as a response to the mass shootings in Uvalde and Buffalo last month, and a lot of young people out there, I couldn`t go out, but a lot of our youth at National Action Network and other groups out there with these groups.\par \par \uc2 We are awaiting a Senate vote. At the end of the day, we need to change laws and that is why they are rallying. They are waiting on a Senate vote on the gun control package that the House passed last week, including your old bill to curb gun trafficking.\par \par \uc2 Now current wisdom is suggesting the package won`t pass the Senate. At the same time you have a small bipartisan group in the Senate working on a much smaller package, that something may have the votes to pass without touching items like assault weapons.\par \par \uc2 Will this time be different, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 KELLY: You know, it`s interesting because more people are saying they feel that it is different, and I credit the public for that feeling. They are hearing from so many people that we need to make changes. They are hearing from Democrats, Republicans, and Independents. They`re hearing from -- there was a group of Republican donors that said something to the senators that change needs to happen, because this is happening all over.\par \par \uc2 You know, you`re not safe, you know, in schools in the hospital, even at a cemetery. Every day going to the park, you know, walking to the store, it is affecting more and more people, and I think that it`s finally you would think after Newtown, it is finally affecting people more and more.\par \par \uc2 And I have hope. I know Senator Murphy is working so hard along with Senator Booker and Klobuchar, and others. They are working so hard. So hopefully, we will be able to get some things through. I have a little more hope this time, but hopefully it`s not going to be dashed.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you, Congresswoman Robin Kelly of Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Now joining me now is Mayor \uc2 Greg Fischer\uc2  of Louisville, Kentucky. He is the former president of the U.S. Conference of Mayors.\par \par \uc2 Nice to see you again, Mr. Mayor.\par \par \uc2 MAYOR GREG FISCHER, LOUISVILLE, KENTUCKY: Reverend Al, pleasure.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, in the wake of another series of mass shootings in the nation, hundreds of thousands of gathered at the March for our Lives demonstration today in Washington, D.C. and many sister marches also happening around the country. And you spoke today at the local march in Louisville, and also you have urged your citizens to attend the event downtown today.\par \par \uc2 Now, why was the issue of gun violence so important for you to address as the mayor of your city?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Well, Rev, it is tearing our country apart? I mean, the mass shootings get most of the publicity. But the facts are is that you know, a hundred people are killed every day in cities and rural areas in our country and another 200 are shot, and this just isn`t a city issue.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re all sworn to serve and to protect lives, and that includes the Republican Senate. And here is the irony of this, Rev.\par \par \uc2 Today, millions of people around the country is saying, we need commonsense gun reform, but there was one person that could make it all happen and he is the senior senator from Kentucky, the leader of the Senate Minority, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So we marched from City Hall over to the Federal Building, and just asked him to use the awesome power he has to change gun laws, just common sense gun laws that 80 percent of the people support and that the \uc2 NRA\uc2  members support as well.\par \par \uc2 So it`s just baffling why this isn`t taking place, but the fact that somebody can buy an assault rifle at age 18, and they`re not even old enough to buy a beer, or they can`t buy a handgun at 18, it just makes no sense.\par \par \uc2 So we`re demanding action. We need more than prayers and thoughts. That`s fine, but we need action.\par \par \uc2 So today, our city loudly shouted that we need action. We had people there from 14 months old to 87-year-old Reverend Charles Eliot, who whispered in my ear --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Oh, yes.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: "It was 16 years ago when I was marching with Dr. King and we`re still marching."\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes. A legendary figure. Right there though in Kentucky, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is one of your two senators.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right there in Kentucky, you cannot buy an alcoholic beverage or a beer at 18, but can buy an assault weapons and this does not touch the senator there who was family there. I mean, this is almost unthinkable.\par \par \uc2 But this week, the U.S. Conference of Mayors held their annual meeting in Reno, Nevada, filling in the conference room, and filing all in that filled it up more than 170 of America`s (AUDIO GAP) called the mass shooting protocol that included practical tips on dealing with the aftermath of mass shootings.\par \par \uc2 You expressed your frustration with lawmakers on the issue of gun violence, telling "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " that mayors have given up on any action from Republican states.\par \par \uc2 As a mayor of a blue city in a red state, can you elaborate more on that sentiment and also explain what mayors around the country can do at the local level to compensate for state politics that refuse to take any action on gun violence at all?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Well, we`re always going to keep pushing, but you know, when you`re blue city mayors go to their red state legislators and they laugh at us when we ask for help with gun reform, it is a sad day.\par \par \uc2 And here`s the reality, Rev. Our streets are being flooded with guns by people that think anybody should be able to have guns, and this is a political issue and I try to be bipartisan because that letter signed by the U.S. mayors was bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 But we have the Republican legislators and the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  blaming cities for all this gun violence. To me, they`re arsonists pretending to be firefighters. They flood our streets with gun, but yet want to complain about it.\par \par \uc2 And listen, from rural areas -- the rural areas, gun violence was up 25 percent year-over-year as well. This is not just a city issue, and you have to ask yourself on the Second Amendment, which of course, I support, but the framers of the Constitution, if they saw our children being massacred, if they saw a hundred deaths in in our country every day from guns, they would be aghast and they would say this has gone terribly wrong, we need to get this fixed.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, I want to move to another important story affecting your city while I have you.\par \par \uc2 Two neighbors of Breonna Taylor, who testified against the officer who shot into their apartment are asking the Judge to move their civil lawsuit outside of Louisville, because, quote, "Fatigue has biased the public in favor of police, flipping the argument made by the former officer ahead of his criminal trial."\par \par \uc2 It`s been over two years since the police unjustly shot and killed Breonna Taylor, and the lawsuits aiming to hold police accountable still remain. While I have you, what changes have been made in the wake of Breonna Taylor`s death? And are they effective? Just about three weeks ago, I happened to be sitting next to Breonna Taylor`s mother, when President Biden signed an Executive Order that included no-knock warrants.\par \par \uc2 But what on the city level has changed? And has any of that been proven to be effective?\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Yes, well, thank you for bringing that up and I was pleased to see you there and Breonna`s mom at President Biden`s Executive Order signing,\par \par \uc2 Look, the only way that you can honor the pain when a tragedy like that takes place is to have significant reform and that`s what you`ve seen us here in Louisville, and I think that`s why they invited me to the White House for that signing.\par \par \uc2 So whether it`s a Civilian Review Board, banning no-knock warrants, new training with the police, duty to intervene policies. These are all many of the dozens of reform activities that have taken place because I think the reality is, is that police community legitimacy is at a very tenuous point in our country right now.\par \par \uc2 So what we`re aiming to do in Louisville is to redefine what that relationship is all about. It starts with trust, because the biggest asset a police department should have is the community, but they must be seen as legitimate, and they must be seen as being accountable and constantly improving, so we`ve been on that track now for the last two years.\par \par \uc2 I want to give a big shout out to Breonna`s mom, Tamika Palmer. She has been a class act, working with us. We report to her the accountability measures that are taking place as well and she has done quite --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And a lot of that -- while there isn`t lot of that, what you and other mayors have to deal with is the public outcry that a lot of people criticize people like me that march do nonviolent activism, but if these issues were not raised like today, the tens of thousands that are marching about gun control, it does not set the climate for people like you to even try to get change.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:15]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  used to say that there are two types of leaders, those that are transactional and those that are transformative, and I think that it is the transforming masses that have changed the climate to try to make something move forward, even if it is in incremental steps.\par \par \uc2 FISCHER: Well, and that`s right and I can tell you, the police officers that I spoke to today appreciated these marches taking place, certainly here in our city, and around the country as well, because the situation is dangerous for police officers on the street right now when they are out.\par \par \uc2 And police officers also know that they have to be seen as legitimate, and that means everything needs to be transparent. There needs to be accountability so good police officers appreciate that.\par \par \uc2 Now, I`m glad to report, our reforms here are well underway and I wish this tragedy would have never happened, but we have to honor the pain by resetting the model for what great policing looks like in conjunction with the community.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you, Mayor \uc2 Greg Fischer\uc2  for being with us again.\par \par \uc2 Now, for an update on the war on Ukraine, joining me now is NBC`s Ali Arouzi out of Kyiv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Ali, what is the latest there?\par \par \uc2 ALI AROUZI, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Good evening, Reverend.\par \par \uc2 Well, today the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  President, \uc2 Ursula von der Leyen\uc2  made a surprise visit to Kyiv and had a meeting with President Zelenskyy. She said that the membership status to the bloc is probably going to be granted to them next week, and that`s a very important step for full membership for Ukraine to join the \uc2 European Union\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy said that the future of Europe hinges on that decision, and this is something as we`ve been talking about before that Ukraine really wants. They want to join the \uc2 European Union\uc2 , so a very important step for that country taking place today.\par \par \uc2 And on the Eastern front, Reverend, pounding of Severodonetsk continues. The Ukrainians say that they are hitting the Russians. They are causing losses to the Russians, but that they are taking at about 100 to 200 casualties a day and a senior adviser to President Zelenskyy today said in a rare admission that the Ukrainian troops have lost around 10,000 troops since the beginning of this conflict.\par \par \uc2 And today, Reverend, we attended the funeral of one of those soldiers who died in Severodonetsk in St. Michael`s Cathedral just behind me.\par \par \uc2 Let`s take a listen to what one of the friends of those soldiers told us this morning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)`\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: It is too great of a sacrifice to have a freedom to make our own choices and to live the life that we want to live. I don`t know, maybe, but this is the sacrifice we`ve made.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AROUZI: You know, and if these funerals, Reverend, that put a face on those figures that we`re talking about, and the Ukrainians here are digging in for more losses as they prefer this war to go into the long haul. They are taking a lot of losses on the Eastern front, but they`re determined to push the Russians back because as they always tell us, they`re fighting for their sovereignty, for their homes, for their families -- Reverend.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Ali Arouzi, thank you for being with us tonight from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: The January 6th Committee goes primetime with new details about what happened the day of the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 My panel joins me later to talk about what we learned and what the political impact will be.\par \par \uc2 First, my colleague Milissa Rehberger with today`s top news stories -- Milissa.\par \par \uc2 MILISSA REHBERGER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hi. Thanks, Rev.\par \par \uc2 Stories that we are watching this hour: Demonstrators in hundreds of locations across the country marched today in support of gun control legislation in the wake of recent mass shootings in Buffalo, New York, and Uvalde, Texas.\par \par \uc2 The march in Washington, D.C. drew thousands of participants. The events were coordinated by March for Our Lives, that is a student-led movement that arose after the Parkland Florida School shooting in 2018.\par \par \uc2 The average price for regular in the U.S. has topped $5.00 a gallon for the first time ever according to AAA. The national average price dropped 19 cents in the past week and it is up nearly $2.00 over the past year.\par \par \uc2 And voters in Alaska are choosing between former vice presidential nominee Sarah Palin and Santa Claus, or any one of nearly four dozen candidates in today`s open primary for the state`s lone seat in Congress. It is the first election in Alaska to choose a ranked choice voting system.\par \par \uc2 Santa by the way is a Democratic socialist candidate who legally changed his name. He hails from the town of guess what -- the North Pole.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:24:24]\par \par \uc2 REHBERGER: Before we go back to POLITICSNATION, we want to get some news happening right now in New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 President Biden is in state as a record breaking wildfire still continues to burn. Let`s listen in for just one moment.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: And so -- but, you know, when you asked for a major disaster declaration, Governor, I immediately responded providing millions of dollars in housing assistance and cash grants and funding for emergency responders, not because I am doing you any favor, it`s an obligation.\par \par \uc2 I think we have a responsibility as a government is to deal with the communities who are put in such jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 And today, I`m announcing the Federal government is covering 100 percent of the cost.\par \par \uc2 [APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 [17:25:12]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We will be bringing more emergency protective measures for the next critical months in this recovery, and then it`s going to be a strong bridge until we the Hermit`s Peak Fire Assistance Act, introduced by Senator Lujan and Senator Heinrich and Senator Fernandez, as well as Stansbury to fully compensate -- to fully compensate survivors for their total loss.\par \par \uc2 [APPLAUSE]\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We`re also providing funding and loans for small businesses, farmers and ranchers and we need to be sure this doesn`t happen again. We know the circumstances --\par \par \uc2 REHBERGER: That is President Biden speaking from New Mexico where a wildfire continues to burn talking about the future of that state.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , we`ll be back in a moment but first, here is Amon Mohideen with a look at his special show tonight featuring the Reverend.\par \par \uc2 AMON MOHIDEEN, MSNBC HOST: Hey there. Tonight, a very special edition of "Amon" starting at 7:00 PM.\par \par \uc2 What we learned from the first public hearing of the January 6 Committee, our special report on the Trump lies, the GOP cover up, and a whole lot more.\par \par \uc2 I`ll be joined by special guests Laurence Tribe, the Harvard constitutional law professor. All of that tonight at a special time of 7:00 PM Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:30:47]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 We go now to my political panel to get their take on today`s big topics.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Rina Shah, senior adviser for the Republican Women`s Progress and Dana Milbank, author and opinion columnist for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 Rina, the January 6th Committee says they have a mountain of information to be shared over the next couple of weeks, detailing how \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was center of a seven-part plan to overturn the 2020 election, and the panel promises to directly implicate Trump. And now, they have to deliver the facts.\par \par \uc2 What challenges does the Committee face to get their points across?\par \par \uc2 RINA SHAH, FOUNDER, RILAX STRATEGIES: First and foremost, this Select Committee must keep America`s attention the way it had debated at the other night.\par \par \uc2 That visual evidence that had never been seen before was so damning, but most of all, it was something that really made me feel as if I was watching a movie, as if I was watching television that had been created for me instead of actual footage of a day that I`ve now become intimately familiar with.\par \par \uc2 I remember the aerial shots of that day for months and weeks after we saw those shots and they said, "Okay, fine. There weren`t that many people." There were people who were naysayers saying, "Okay, these were just tourists. It wasn`t that bad. There weren`t that many."\par \par \uc2 But what we saw from the Committee`s footage in that unseen footage was on the ground, and that was truly telling of the exact carnage that happened that day.\par \par \uc2 The witnesses, of course, told us some things that we`d never heard before. Officer Edwards, of course, saying she was slipping in the blood of her colleagues. That was a line I was not prepared for.\par \par \uc2 But also let`s not forget the opening statements of the Chairman and the Chairwoman, the Ranking Member, excuse me, Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . Her words, they will go down in history. She said something to the effect of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will no longer be here one day, but to my Republican colleagues, your dishonor will remain.\par \par \uc2 That Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , I tell you, that is a line for the history books.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: No, I certainly concur with that.\par \par \uc2 Dana, in your latest article for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," you write that " ... beyond the January 6th Committee`s mandate to explain how so many people who began with honorable instincts ultimately retreated to join Trump, and there is probably no convincing them, nor the tens of millions they have deceived to correct course now."\par \par \uc2 "But the Committee`s work gives hope that the dishonorable will at least earn history`s rebuke."\par \par \uc2 Now, how will the House panel`s final report safeguard future elections? Are we doomed to repeat another January 6th like this attack?\par \par \uc2 DANA MILBANK, AUTHOR AND OPINION COLUMNIST, "THE WASHINGTON POST": Well, Reverend, we may be doomed to repeat it, regardless of what this Committee is doing, but what they`re doing is extraordinarily valuable regardless, because in their hundreds of hours of testimony, thousands of documents, they are providing the evidence.\par \par \uc2 They are providing the evidence, the own words of Trump`s former aides. We saw just you know, a little bit of a taste of that Thursday night, but we`re going to see and hear a lot more of that, of people who were close to Trump actually saying that he knew, they knew that he had lost the election, and yet they went through with this, fomented this violent riot anyway.\par \par \uc2 It is one thing for a Member of Congress to say that, even if it`s \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 . It is an entirely different thing to have the people who were standing there with the President threatening to resign, and I think we now know there were more people in the White House and in the administration who were confronting President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in real time and we`re going to hear more about that.\par \par \uc2 So even if FOX News didn`t let its viewers see that --\par \par \uc2 [17:35:02]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Including his own Attorney General that he selected. I mean, the head of his Justice Department, we saw the videotape of his testimony saying he told him that it was nonsense and I`m cleaning up the word.\par \par \uc2 But I am staying with you for a minute, Dana, let`s talk about new developments with Ginni Thomas, the wife of Judge \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " reports, Ginni who is \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 ` wife, pressed 29 -- two-nine Republican state lawmakers in Arizona to set aside Joe Biden`s popular vote victory, and choose presidential electors, choose them now, according to e-mails NBC has not confirmed these documents, but how do you see the latest revelations playing into a larger narrative around the final days of the Trump presidency?\par \par \uc2 MILBANK: Well, Reverend, it`s not surprising in the sense that we already knew. She had been lobbying \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , the President`s Chief of Staff. We already knew she reached out to a couple of lawmakers in Arizona, but now, it looks like she was spamming them.\par \par \uc2 So it really -- it raises the level of the activism we see. I mean, she was doing everything but breaking windows with a flagpole. Now, of course, this isn`t surprising, given what we know so far.\par \par \uc2 But it does increase the pressure on the Supreme Court, \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has shown no interest in recusing himself from potentially related cases, but his colleagues on the Court have to know that this whole situation is compromising even further the reputation and the legitimacy of the Court.\par \par \uc2 So hopefully, some uncomfortable discussions will be going on there to make \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  do the right thing, even if he won`t do it himself.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Rina, let`s turn now to the issue of abortion rights. President Biden says he is looking at ways to shore up abortion rights if the Supreme Court overturns Roe versus Wade in the coming weeks.\par \par \uc2 This, as Biden is receiving pressure from Senate Democrats for a national plan to defend a person`s right to an abortion. What options does the President have for addressing the concerns of abortion rights and the advocates that one day he was something before the midterm?\par \par \uc2 SHAH: Well, certainly this issue coming up this close to the midterms presents a real big quagmire, of course, for the White House. There is a challenge here that is purely political and that doesn`t have as much to do with the Supreme Court as we think it does.\par \par \uc2 The average American can think that this can be settled in the judicial branch, the executive branch, but the reality is, there are Statehouses across the country that are putting in jeopardy the lives of American women and their ability to have safe access to abortion, reproductive rights for American women are on the line in a way that I`ve never seen in my lifetime.\par \par \uc2 And the reality is this, is that if \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  chooses to act and do something when it comes to protecting access to safe abortions, then he really will put himself in danger of the backlash that could ensue right at the midterms. Republicans, well, we don`t know.\par \par \uc2 Are they going to mobilize in a way that says we`re not happy with what the White House is choosing to do? Or are Democrats going to feel super energized and say, let`s push back and let`s fight for this President in a way that we ought to despite things such as inflation, the economy looming large, and let`s not forget violence, gun violence being so hot right now, the issue endangering the lives of so many Americans around the country that want to see action from every level of government.\par \par \uc2 So, I think this is unfortunately a situation which the White House may be better off choosing to not act before the midterms. It brings me no happiness to say that, but it really could put the presidency in danger in midterms.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, I`m going to have to leave it there, Dana Milbank and Rina Shah. Thank you both for being on.\par \par \uc2 Coming up: More on the impact of gun violence in America.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:42:52]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As attention is drawn to Capitol Hill over the next weeks of January 6th hearings, I remind my viewers that along with the law enforcement present when the Capitol was breached, we also had federal workers, a sector with a large minority press, some still dealing with the trauma of that day.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, \uc2 Everett Kelley\uc2 , National President of the \uc2 American Federation of Government Employees\uc2 . Mr. Kelley, thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 EVERETT KELLEY\uc2 , NATIONAL PRESIDENT, AMERICAN FEDERATION OF GOVERNMENT EMPLOYEES: Reverend Al, thank you for having us today. And you know, I`m here at the Philadelphia \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  convention, and we`ll have a lot of discussions about this very same topic.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So let me ask you about that topic. I`ve seen your leadership and how you fought for your members. Are you worried about the security of your members or Federal workers in general, after last year`s attack on the Capitol and now, this series of public hearings on that attack? Are you concerned about your members?\par \par \uc2 KELLEY: Reverend Al, I certainly am. You`ve got to remember that I represent the VA Hospital. I represent Social Security workers. I represent BOP employees. I represent ICE employees. I represent the Border Patrol.\par \par \uc2 And sometimes, you know, the people that are really upset, they are upset because maybe a claim didn`t go their way that they wanted to go, and these people that`s on the frontline, these are the people that are the subject of an attack at any given time.\par \par \uc2 So, I am very concerned about the members that I represent.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Everett, I`d imagine a lot of Union Presidents right now are hearing from their members about our nation`s rising inflation. That rise now at a 40-year high according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  yesterday.\par \par \uc2 What are you hearing from your members?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:00]\par \par \uc2 KELLEY: Well, you know, Reverend Al, people have to understand that Federal employees make on an average of about $50,000.00 a year. Now if you are a \uc2 TSA\uc2  worker, if you are a canteen worker in the VA, you don`t even make that much.\par \par \uc2 Now, I am very grateful to this President that have made sure that no one makes under $15.00 an hour in the Federal government, thanks for that. But however, that`s just not enough.\par \par \uc2 You know, we have to really do something about this, because when you`ve got rising prices on gas, gas is over $6.00 a gallon now. You have the landlords that`s taking advantage of the people. You have consumers that`s paying the price for, you know, I`m going to say, the price gouging epidemic. That`s the problem.\par \par \uc2 And I think that if we can get behind the bill that Senator Warren has and make sure that corporate greed is no more and make sure that this bill is passed, and that consumers finally get a rebate for being ripped off through this price gouging, I think that`s where the problem really is, you know, not on all of the ring of crises, none of those things, you know, but the price gouging is one of the major problems, in my opinion.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, Everett, with yesterday`s news that the Biden administration will no longer require COVID testing for international travelers coming into the country, I wondered where you came down on that, given that the AFGE that you`re president of represents thousands of \uc2 TSA\uc2  agents. What`s your reaction to that?\par \par \uc2 KELLEY: Well, first of all, you know, I want to say, I`m really grateful for our \uc2 TSA\uc2  workforce. They have been right there on the work line every single day, along with others, like the VA and like the Border Patrol, and all those guys, right?\par \par \uc2 And my objective is this. You know, I want to make sure that the employees are safe. Now, that is one of the things that we have always advocated for. We understand that the vaccine is a health issue, and we want to make sure that our employees are healthy and are in a healthy situation, but we also want to make sure that the employees are safe.\par \par \uc2 When those people are coming through that line, we want to make sure that those people are safe. That`s why I`ve advocated for not only mask wearing of the employees, but also for the public, you know, because that`s also a health measure for the employees that I represent.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And that`s sort of -- well, and I`m out of time, but that`s sort of the thread you have to get through the needle where at one level, the politics of your union members, and you may have one thing, but at another level, you have to also challenge the administration and others to remember the membership in terms of their health, their life, and their earnings and you`ve been one of the few union leaders I`ve known in my capacity as National Action Network President that has been able to thread that needle pretty well.\par \par \uc2 KELLEY: Well, thank you, sir. I`m trying hard.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, well, thank you for coming on. I really want to talk to you about government workers, \uc2 Everett Kelley\uc2 , thank you and stay safe.\par \par \uc2 Still to come up, an update on charges in the police shooting death of Patrick Lyoya.\par \par \uc2 Plus my final thoughts.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:53:17]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we continue to cover the January 6 Committee hearings and the fight for gun safety, I want to briefly highlight a few other important stories that you may have missed this week.\par \par \uc2 Grand Rapids, Michigan: A police officer named Christopher Schurr has been charged with one count of second degree murder in the killing of Patrick Lyoya. He has pleaded not guilty to the charges.\par \par \uc2 The DOJ has launched an investigation into the Louisiana State Police for alleged use of excessive force and racially discriminatory tactics against Blacks and people of color. The move comes three years after Ronald Green, a Black man was beaten to death by White police officers during a traffic stop.\par \par \uc2 And finally, a New York based foundation has apologized for its role in Tuskegee in the Tuskegee syphilis studies.\par \par \uc2 For nearly 40 years, the Milbank Memorial Fund covered funeral expenses for Black men killed in the States, but in exchange, required families to consent to their loved ones` bodies being used for research purposes.\par \par \uc2 All of these stories are in the public eye because of protests, because of activism and that`s why today around gun violence, as we continue to deal with the criminal justice reform, we must remain active and we must protect the right to vote because we do not just need drama, we need the drama to bring about change and without legislative change, we will lead to frustration.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:07]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: So let`s keep marching, but let`s also make sure we vote and bring forward people that understand that we live in a nation that should not have 18-year-olds be able to buy beers -- cannot buy beers, but can buy automatic weapons and that we live in a nation where when you lose an election, you don`t lose your mind.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching.\par \par \uc2 I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 p.m. Eastern for another live out of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 American voices hosted by my friend, Julian Castro will start at the top of the hour right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:00]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220612ei6b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 CHRIS JANSING REPORTS for June 8, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Jansing, Juanita Tolliver, Sahil Kapur, Morgan Chesky, Steve  Kornacki, Joyce Vance, Ali Vitali, Tali Farhadian Weinstein \par \uc2 7,776 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC ANCHOR: Good afternoon, you`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS live from MSNBC headquarters in New York City.\par \par \uc2 11-year-old Miah Cerrillo survived the horrific attack on her elementary school in Uvalde, Texas two weeks ago but surviving it turns out can be a very difficult challenge. After watching her friends and her teacher gunned down in front of her eyes, she`s not sleeping much.\par \par \uc2 As you might imagine, her dad says she`s not doing too well. So it would be an extraordinary act of courage for anyone, let alone a little girl who should be enjoying summer break right now to put aside her own struggles and relive what she went through. But that`s exactly what Miah did, videotaping testimony for a House hearing on gun violence today. Here`s Miah and her dad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MIAH CERRILLO, SURVIVED UVALDE SHOOTING: There`s a door between our classrooms, and he went to there and shot my teacher and told my teacher good night and he shot her in the head. And then he shot some of my classmates and the whiteboards. When I went to the backpacks, he shot my friend that was next to me. And I thought he was going to come back to the room so I grabbed the blood and put it over me.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CERRILLO, FATHER OF MIAH CERRILLO: If there was something that you want people to know about that day and about you, right, or things that you want different, what would it be?\par \par \uc2 MIAH CERRILLO: To have security.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CERRILLO: Do you feel safe at school? Why not?\par \par \uc2 MIAH CERRILLO: Because I don`t want it to happen again.\par \par \uc2 MIGUEL CERRILLO: And do you think it`s going to happen again?\par \par \uc2 I come because I could`ve lost my baby girl. She`s not the same little girl that I used to play with in Randwick and do everything because she was daddy`s little girl.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: One of Miah`s classmates was 10-year-old, Lexi Rubio. Lexi was killed in her classroom just hours after receiving the good Citizenship Award and an honor roll award for getting straight A`s. Her parents testified about their daughter this morning and the life she will never get to lead. It was excruciating and heartbreaking to watch but it`s also something all Americans, those who support new gun laws and those who don`t, should see.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KIMBERLY RUBIO, MOTHER OF SHOOTING VICTIM, LEXI RUBIO: If given the opportunity, Lexi would have made a positive change in this world. She wanted to attend St. Mary`s University in San Antonio, Texas on a softball scholarship. She wanted to major in math and go on to attend law school. That opportunity was taken from her. She was taken from us. Somewhere out there, there`s a mom listening to our testimony, thinking I can`t even imagine their pain not knowing that our reality will one day be hers unless we act now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So the question now is whether those words will force lawmakers to actually pass new gun laws, something they failed to do time and time again? As recently as yesterday, the Democrats top Senate negotiator, \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 , said a deal could be done by the end of the week. But NBC News has learned that the group is now hung up on the issue of background checks for 18, 19, and 20-year-olds who want to buy guns.\par \par \uc2 Even if there is a deal, and that`s still a big if, it`s going to be pretty modest. Practically speaking, bigger ticket reforms, raising the age limit to buy assault weapons, for example. Those are off the table. So where are we right now? Punchbowl News puts it like this.\par \par \uc2 In the long struggle over guns and gun control, there`s finally a window open for compromise, albeit a small window with room for only a narrow accord. Why? Because for the first time in a decade, the political incentive structure has shifted. Democrats are working within the confines of a razor-thin majority in Washington, dropping their calls for big policy changes in favor of small progress. And some Republicans have accepted that opposing any and all changes to gun laws is both politically and substantively unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 And at this point, it`s worth reminding everyone exactly what we`re talking about here. No one is talking about banning handguns or even banning assault rifles like the AR-15, which was, let`s remember, designed for military use.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: It`s essentially the same as the M16 that soldiers carried in Vietnam, weapons of war that can make a hole in a human body the size of an orange. Dr. Roy Guerrero, a pediatrician in Uvalde who responded to the hospital that day testified about what kind of injuries this kind of weapon does to these children.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROY GUERRERO, PEDIATRICIAN IN UVALDE, TEXAS: What I did find was something no prayer, whatever relief. Two children whose bodies have been pulverized by bullets fired at them decapitated, whose flesh had been ripped apart, that the only clue as their identities was a blood spider cartoon clothes still clinging to them, clinging for life and finding none.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in NBC`s senior national political reporter Sahil Kapur, Anna Palmer is co-founder of Punchbowl News and Juanita Tolliver is an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Juanita, I want to stay on that for a while because we hear from people like Dr. Guerrero about the damage done to kids, and then some Republicans are claiming that these assault weapons are necessary. So I want to play what the argument is on the other side.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2 , (R-SD): It`s something that a lot of people, for purposes of going out to target shooting. In my state, they use them to shoot prairie dogs and you know other types of varmints.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KEN BUCK\uc2 , (R-CO): In rural Colorado, an AR-15 is a gun of choice for killing raccoons before they get to our chickens. That it is a gun of choice for killing a fox.\par \par \uc2 LIZ LANDERS, CHIEF POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT, VICE NEWS: Why does someone need an AR-15?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BILL CASSIDY, (R-LA): Well, if you talk to the people that own it, killing feral pigs and, you know, whatever, the middle of Louisiana. They wonder, why would you take it away from them?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So, Juanita, is the Republican message neutralizing the message from people like Dr. Guerrero? Democrats know from polls, they`re winning in the court of public opinion, so why hasn`t anything changed?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: It`s absolutely not going to neutralize what we heard from Dr. Guerrero because what the doctor did today was illustrate explicitly how these guns are being used against children -- against children in schools. And with his graphic experience with this, he`s laying it out for the public and for the Republicans who refuse to listen.\par \par \uc2 And -- but with this type of imagery, they`re not going to be able to look away anymore. They`re not going to be able to talk about oh, it`s for shooting animals, it`s for target practice because the reality is being used on children, it`s being used at grocery stores, it`s being used in churches.\par \par \uc2 And that is something that Republicans know they can`t move away from because during these hearings, while the doctor was speaking, recognize that Republicans were not in that room. They were at a conference trying to pivot the narrative away from guns, trying to talk about any and everything else that they think matters to voters, except for the true issue at hand.\par \par \uc2 And I just want to applaud the parents, the children, and the people who lost their loved ones for coming up and sharing these stories because this is what makes it not just a number featured in a headline, this will take it beyond the fact that it was 19 children and two teachers or 10 people at a grocery store. Because this is about their lives, this is about the impact of the loss and this is about the damage that these assault weapons are doing, and how Congress can take them.\par \par \uc2 Because the underlying message here today, Chris, is protecting children means more than protecting guns. And Congress has to act or it`s going to keep happening. And that`s something that we heard repeatedly from the witnesses today and Republicans won`t be able to neutralize that because it is a fact.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So lay out the facts politically, Anna, why can`t Democrats gain ground on this issue?\par \par \uc2 ANNA PALMER, FOUNDER, PUNCHBOWL NEWS: Well, I think as we laid out this morning, they are gaining small ground here when it comes to even having these negotiations as serious as they are. You have the House Republicans who have really kind of laid on a position that this is, you know, a Second Amendment rights issue.\par \par \uc2 But in the Senate, you`re having a much more substantive conversation around things like red flag laws and mental health. And yes, it is going to be a smaller package. That is just the reality of where things are in terms of the politics for Republicans. But I would say that you know, I`ve covered a lot of this issue throughout the years over the past decade and this is as serious of a conversation as we`ve seen in a really long time.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: But as serious conversation as well actually this time produce something? Because those are two different things.\par \par \uc2 PALMER: Yes, you are totally right. I -- we don`t know yet if this is going to come to a deal. I will say that there was a large number of senators, Republicans, and Democrats meeting in the Capitol today. The big question I have is there still -- we haven`t seen paper so it`s always the details that matter and it`s going to be also how much it costs. The mental health provisions that they`re talking about could cost up to $7 billion and that`s a lot of money and Republicans are going to want to find some offsets.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 PALMER: So as heart-wrenching and awful as this is and you see Democrats trying to continue to keep this top of mind whether it`s Matthew McConaughey yesterday at the White House and today with these hearings. But it`s really the details of this and continuing to make sure that Republicans stay at the negotiating table. We`ll have to see where this nets out at the end of the week and potentially into next week.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And, Sahil, as emotional as it was, it wasn`t just one-sided. We also heard from some gun rights activists, including Lucretia Hughes, who says new gun restrictions won`t work. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LUCRETIA HUGHES, DC PROJECTS, WOMEN FOR GUN RIGHTS: Our gun control lobbyists and politicians claim that their policies will save lives and reduce violence. Well, those policies did not save my son. The law that was being discussed are already implemented in cities across this country. We have decades of evidence proven they do not work.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 The Heritage Foundation\uc2  also said the laws being proposed would be legally suspect. They say they`ve proposed alternative ways to deal with gun violence. From what you`re hearing on the hill, what are those alternatives, and what are the chances that they get them through? In other words, things like mental health as opposed to actually dealing with guns.\par \par \uc2 SAHIL KAPUR, MSNBC SENIOR POLITICAL REPORTER: Well, there is a split among Republicans about this, Chris. If you ask Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2 , who`s leading the negotiations, and other Republicans, including Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , who`s supporting him, they are interested in encouraging states to pass new red flag laws which some Republican-led states like Florida have done.\par \par \uc2 This would essentially allow law enforcement or family members to petition a court to keep firearms away from people who are believed to be dangerous to themselves or others. There`s talk about doing something on background checks, there`s not going to be universal background checks even though that proposal has more than 80 percent public support.\par \par \uc2 They`re looking at this age group of 18 to 21-year-olds, specifically, whether they can include juvenile records into the background check system. A number of mass shooters have been a part of that age group. And if they`ve committed violent acts in their younger years, should they be allowed to have guns. Republicans are open to that. And then beyond that, there`s money for mental health and school safety on the table. That`s one category, Chris.\par \par \uc2 The second category is senators, I could -- I should say, Republicans with national ambitions -- with higher ambitions. I spoke to Senator Ted Cruz, Senator \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , in the last few days, and they`re not touching this negotiation. They`re keeping their distance from it.\par \par \uc2 They`re promoting this theory, unfounded, as it may be, in this case, that voters are worried any action that Congress could take would be a slippery slope to ending gun rights to taking away people`s guns. That is not what people are talking about here. Cornyn is emphasizing again and again, that that`s not what`s on the table.\par \par \uc2 And finally, if you look at the prevalence of mass shootings in the United States, they`re exponentially higher than in other developed countries. There`s no evidence that the U.S. has more mental health problems than other countries, what it does have is much easier access and availability of deadly weapons.\par \par \uc2 That does seem to be the key variable, Chris, driving this. But that doesn`t matter in terms of the politics of this so much because there is a strong pro-gun contingent in the country that is very well represented in the Senate through the structure of it and the fact that rural states have over-representation as sheer of population.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Juanita, you touched on this and I think it`s also extraordinary, especially when you`re talking about a little girl, but parents who just lost a child, who are able to get up and speak the way they did, make the cases that they did. There`s also been some debate, as you know, about showing pictures of the bodies of these children, with their parent`s permission, of course, but there are people who believe that will help galvanize public opinion because of their graphic nature.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, some conservative outlets have used graphic images of say, abortions to shore up what they want to be support for their position. Is that something that you think Democrats would, or should consider, again, if that`s something a parent wanted to do?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: When I think about that, my mind immediately goes through Mamie Till-Mobley, who decided to show the pictures of her son, Emmett Till who was lynched in Mississippi. And I want to remind you, Chris, like you said, that was a deeply personal decision that she made on her own. No one asked her to do it.\par \par \uc2 She knew that she wanted the world to see the impact of harm done to her son, the impact of white supremacy, and racism in this nation. And so if any parent felt compelled to do that on their own, with no controlling or encouragement from anybody else, then absolutely, it would make an impact.\par \par \uc2 But there is a fine line between making sure that you`re highlighting an issue in a substantive way versus producing traumatic images that essentially do a disservice to the individual who was harmed in their family. And so I want to reiterate. If any parent decided to do that on their own, that is one thing but anything short of, that would be truly concerning move to -- by any party involved.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Juanita, Anna, Sahil, important conversation today. Thank you so much. And while the debate over guns drags on, Uvalde families continue to bury their dead as we speak. Funeral Services are underway for a 10-year- old third-grader, Annabel Rodriguez. Her nine-year-old cousin was also killed in the shooting. NBC`s Morgan Chesky is in Uvalde for us. \uc2 Morgan\uc2 , hard to fathom how agonizing these weeks have been, and how agonizing they will continue to be for that community.\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chris, that`s absolutely right. You have the shock of what took place two weeks ago and then with the burial`s beginning. You have this whole other sense of loss that hits you yet again. And I think that`s what people here in this community are feeling when you see one funeral procession after another going right down one of the main streets, those hearses hitting hard every time you see them.\par \par \uc2 Of course, Annabel Rodriguez, her life celebrated today, a student Robb Elementary taken two weeks and a day ago, her family sharing that, of course, she will be so missed and that she loved the color blue butterflies and that they were very proud of her making the honor roll. Of course, that was one of the ceremonies that took place that fateful morning just minutes before that gunman entered.\par \par \uc2 Regarding this community and how they`re handling this, there is an increased call for accountability and for transparency. We heard Attorney General \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  say that that would be one of the focuses of the Department of Justice review here, Chris, but there are people that are waiting to see if any transparency will come out of the ongoing investigation. It began with DPS, it`s now under the view of the DA here, District Attorney Christina Busbee, who has yet to make a public statement about the status of this investigation.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Uvalde`s own mayor at an emergency city council meeting yesterday told me that he has asked repeatedly, not necessarily for sensitive information but just for any kind of briefing on where things stand and he has yet to receive one. At that city council meeting, Chris, important to note, noticeably absent, newly elected city council member Pete Arredondo, the school`s chief of police who has been silent since the shooting. Chris.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: \uc2 Morgan\uc2 , thank you so much for your ongoing reporting. And coming up, a clear message from one of the nation`s most progressive cities, crime and homelessness problems will push people to vote in big numbers. NBC`s Steve Kornacki is here standing by to take us through the results from California and across the country last night. And we`ll dig into the warning signs they give us about the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the abortion fight is getting a revival on Capitol Hill as more than 20 Senate Democrats called on President Biden to act on abortion rights. NBC News was the first to get its hands on that letter. We`ve got those details coming up. You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Today, there seems to be an election message to Democrats, courtesy of two of the country`s more famously progressive cities. In Los Angeles, businessman Rick Caruso, a Republican who switched to Democrat days before announcing his candidacy, captured more votes than progressive well-known Congresswoman \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 . This race now heads to a runoff.\par \par \uc2 In San Francisco, as the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  puts it, voters put an end to one of the country`s most pioneering experiments in criminal justice reform, ousting a district attorney who eliminated cash bail, vowed to hold police accountable and work to reduce the number of people sent to prison. That DA, \uc2 Chesa Boudin\uc2 , actually blamed the right for his loss last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHESA BOUDIN, SAN FRANCISCO DISTRICT ATTORNEY: The right-wing billionaires outspent us three to one. They exploited an environment in which people are appropriately upset. And they created an electoral dynamic where we were literally shadowboxing.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: NBC`s Steve Kornacki is with us at the big board after - I don`t know, a couple of hours of sleep. Take us through the big races, will you?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it was similar themes actually, I think animating both of these contests you`re talking about. Here`s what we`ve got right now in the LA mayor`s race. Now, there`s still a lot of votes that are going to be counted for this primary. But we do know as you say, Rick Caruso, \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 , they`re headed for a runoff.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be settled in the November general election. You can see the third-place finisher here, De Leon running sort of to the progressive left. So there`s an opportunity, at least in theory, for Bass to kind of, you know, get some of these other candidates -- to supporters of some of these other candidates kind of to her left or near her left flank for the general election.\par \par \uc2 Caruso, though, you know, has been spending heavily, has an unlimited war chest in those themes that he was stressing there about crime, about homelessness, about quality of life, certainly seem to have some resonance because this is better than at the outset of the campaign. You would have expected Caruso to do.\par \par \uc2 And again, it dovetails as you say, with what we saw in an even more Liberal city. In San Francisco here, here`s the recall and \uc2 Chesa Boudin\uc2 . Now, interestingly here, the comments he just played right there, the context for this -- I mean here`s how liberal, how democratic San Francisco is as a city.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Wow. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Here`s your 2020 election results. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  85, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , less than 13 percent of the vote, and yet you`ve got 60 percent of the electorate in this city choosing to recall the district attorney. So again, you know Boudin part is sort of a group, a batch of progressive prosecutors elected in cities around the country -- counties around the country over the last few years. You do think a result like this could have some resonance and could get noticed across the country.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Anything in the House races that give us a through-line that gives us some indication of what we should look for in November?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Yes, a couple of interesting things to keep an eye on here. Let`s start in California where I think this is the most interesting race here. David Valadao, Republican incumbent you`re seeing this is the Central Valley 22nd district. Now, the way they do these primaries in California, Democrats, Republicans, they all run on the same ballot. The top two vote- getters then meet up in November. So you see there`s one Democrat in the race, Rudy Salas. He finishes first. He`s on to the November general election.\par \par \uc2 But you see this Republican vote is really divided here and Valadao is leading among the Republicans but it`s only by about 1200 votes. Valadao voted to impeach \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  last year after January 6. And his top Republican opponent, Chris Mathys said I`m in the race because of that. I don`t think he should have voted to impeach \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . If you don`t think he should vote for me. Now, the way this happens in California, there`s still going to be some mail-in votes count in the next few days.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: There`s still theoretically room for Mathys to actually catch Valadao, although this does look like a decent pad that Valadao has. But still, you see that`s not a great performance for a Republican incumbent if he does hold on to second place here and get that general election matchup. Here`s what he`s looking at in terms of political terrain for the fall. This is a Democratic district Valadao barely won in 2020. He`s going to have to unite the Republican Party. It`s going to be a tough fight for him in the general election.\par \par \uc2 And then just quickly, Chris, one other surprise here, I think worth noting in Mississippi, this is the third congressional district of Mississippi, this is a Republican congressman, an incumbent. He is in second place right now. Not all the votes are counted. Mississippi is a runoff state, if nobody gets 50 percent, the top to head to a runoff. Guest, why did he run afoul of the Republican electorate? He voted for the creation of that bipartisan January 6 commission.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Oh.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: That became an issue that his challenger really played up. And look at this. Guest, it looks like very likely going to finish second here and go into a runoff in a couple of weeks, could have lost his seat in the primary.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Steve Kornacki, always fascinating stuff. Thank you so much. I want to dig deeper with former Democratic Senator Barbara Boxer of California, always good to see you. Look, you spent a lot of your life in the Bay Area. And one lifelong liberal who owns a San Francisco sandwich shop tells NBC News that the now recalled DA`s politics is no match for the reality of auto thefts, rising homicides, and crime as well as homelessness in the city. Let me play that.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I was -- oh, my God, I`ve been robbed on Mission Street. I was jumped on Sixth Street. The ideology that`s happening now is a little bit dangerous for the residents.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Let me reiterate. This is a progressive, so how should Democrats and progressives be reading these results from last night?\par \par \uc2 BARBARA BOXER, FORMER DEMOCRATIC SENATOR, CALIFORNIA: Well, hi, Chris. It`s so great to be with you. Let me say that I see something very interesting emerging from Democrats. They are rejecting ideological leaders in favor of pragmatic progressives. For example, you said very clearly what happened in that recall, that was a rejection of an ideological leader.\par \par \uc2 But down here in the Los Angeles area, we had a sheriff who came at it from the right-wing, and he`s forced into a runoff. And then you have our Attorney General Bonta, who was very progressive, but pragmatic, progressive, he wants to get things done. And he got a wonderful vote. So I think Democrats are saying we want pragmatic progressives. That`s what I think it.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Do you think there`s an exhaustion, they`re just tired of all the fighting and you know, they want someone who represents their views, but not at the cost of not getting anything done?\par \par \uc2 BOXER: Yes, exactly. That`s -- you know, the bottom line is -- I`ll give you an example. When I went to the Senate, I said I wanted to put 2 million acres into wilderness, the beautiful God-given wilderness we have here in California that`s disappearing. I couldn`t get 2 million acres, I got 1 million acres. And some of my supporters said, oh, you should just hold out. Well, I said, I`m not going to do that because I`ll never get it done. You`ve got to make things better.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I think if you look at our governor`s race where you know, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  ran away with it. And our U.S. Senator race, which you know, is the seat I used to hold it and Kamala held it, \uc2 Alex Padilla\uc2 , these are pragmatic people. They are quite progressive in their view, but they are going to make life better for people. They`re going to do everything they can. And that`s what I see happening across the board in the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So after we see these progressive criminal justice reform ideas rejected, at least in the case of that one race in San Francisco, let`s zoom out to 30,000 feet. I want to play for you what the president said about the primary elections just last hour.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I think the voters sent a clear message last night. Both parties have to step up and do something about crime, as well as gun violence.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: As well as gun violence. Do you see it through-line from the larger gun conversation that`s happening right now where Democratic politicians nationwide can sort of harness that outrage, the fury voters are feeling about crime as well, to somehow tie them together and propose solutions?\par \par \uc2 BOXER: Without a doubt. I mean, again, there`s pie in the sky solutions and there are real solutions that are backed by 70 percent of the people, 80 percent of the people, and more. So anyone whether they`re Republican or Democrat, call yourself a conservative, a liberal, a progressive, it doesn`t matter to me.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:06]\par \par \uc2 These are commonsense things, the background checks, that`s something that`s common sense. Raising the age to buy an assault weapon to 21. Look, I favored banning them. I voted to ban those weapons of war. If you can`t get that, settle for raising the age to 21 because unfortunately we have seen these young people lose their minds when they turned 18 and go get these weapons of war.\par \par \uc2 So, yes, I think -- I think there is a through line here. I think the whole issue of crime is important. We need to do all of the above. Yes, we need mental health help for people. Yes, we need sensible gun laws so that the wrong people don`t get these guns. And it goes on and on. And we can do these things. And it is a matter of just putting aside ideology and moving forward to make things better for the people.\par \par \uc2 And if I look, again, at California`s results, up and down the ballot, I see the progressives winning big. But they`re pragmatic, they`re not ideological.\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC HOST: Former Senator Barbara Boxer, always good to have you on the program, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 We`re now just one day out from another monumental day on Capitol Hill as we expect to hear from new witnesses and see brand-new video at tomorrow`s January 6th primetime hearings. We`ll speak with a legal expert about what`s at stake.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:21]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Just over 24 hours from now, the January 6th Committee will have its big shot to grab the attention of the American public and lay out its case. The committee`s investigation has been intense. They`ve interviewed more than 1,000 witnesses, received more than 140,000 documents and 98 known subpoenas have been sent.\par \par \uc2 Earlier committee member and Democratic Congressman \uc2 Pete Aguilar\uc2  was asked by our team if there was enough evidence to move on a criminal referral against the former president. Here`s his answer.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. PETE AGUILAR (D)-CA): That`s not the task before the committee. So the task before the committee is to tell the truth and to find out what happened on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: I want to bring in Joyce Vance, former U.S. attorney and an MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 So, Joyce, with all of this information that the committee has compiled, what do you expect is going on, right now in these last 24 hours of prep before primetime, and what does the committee need to do right out of the gate to grab America`s attention?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: The most difficult thing when you have as much information as the committee has is figuring out what you present to the jury, in this case to the American public, to compel them to understand the facts as a whole. So I suspect that there is still a little bit of scurrying going on to figure out just what they can get in, but Thursday night the most important task in front of the committee will be to get the public`s attention.\par \par \uc2 Of course, FOX News won`t be airing these proceedings. The committee will have to reach out and give people reasons that they can`t afford not to listen. We hear that they plan on giving an overview Thursday night and so I suspect we`ll hear their case for the public to engage them.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joyce, I want to share you a list that shows some notable depositions. It includes the former president`s family, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , former Trump and Pence officials. We heard what the congressman told our own Ali Vitali just now. What are you watching for when it comes to how these hearings will impact Trump world?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: So this is an interesting question because, of course, the Justice Department investigation is happening, I don`t want to say in parallel, but perhaps in tandem. And a big part of getting to the truth is getting central witnesses to cooperate with you. Witnesses who believe that everything is about to come out in the wash are far more likely to cooperate. So that`s one item that may be on the agenda for the committee this week, surfacing additional witnesses.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: There is a judge as you know who is ordering John Eastman, a Trump lawyer who wrote memos arguing that then Vice President Pence could overturn the 2020 election to turn over something like 170 documents to the committee. The judge pointed to one e-mail from December 20th, 2020, that he said included evidence of a potential crime.\par \par \uc2 So this committee is still actively gathering evidence even as it`s about to present these findings. So how does that work? I mean, I suppose it`s possible they could even find something enroute that might shake things up.\par \par \uc2 VANCE: You always pursue the evidence down to the final minute. But here Eastman has engaged in a deliberate practice of trying to delay, trying to keep the committee from getting access to his materials. One suspects that that makes them even more eager to have every last document. And, of course, that judge is involved in a civil case, not in a criminal case, so using a lower burden of proof to make these findings that suggest that there is a crime fraud exception that can permit the court to force the turnover of Mr. Eastman`s documents, even though he`s claimed that they`re shielded by the attorney-client privilege.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless it`s really something to hear a judge say that there is more evidence than not that the former president of the United States was involved in a crime.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Joyce Vance, I have a feeling we`re going to be seeing a lot of you in the coming days and weeks. Thank you so much for that.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow, there will be special coverage of the January 6th hearings. It starts at 7:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC and on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Putting pressure on the president, the new push led by two Democratic senators to take action on abortion rights. What they want to see next.\par \par \uc2 And a new lawsuit against \uc2 Meta\uc2  as one family blames \uc2 Instagram\uc2  for their daughter`s eating disorder, self-harm and thoughts of suicide. We`ve got the details on that case ahead as well.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching CHRIS JANSING REPORTS only on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:43:53]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Close to two dozen Senate Democrats are putting new pressure on President Biden to act to protect a woman`s access to abortion. NBC News was first to obtain a letter from this group led by Senators Elizabeth Warren and \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 . It calls for six specific executive actions from the president.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Ali Vitali is on the Hill, she broke this story for us. Hey there, Ali, what specifically does this group want to see?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, look, Chris, after a lot of weeks of asking what Senate Democrats and Democrats broadly could do to shore up abortion protections, now two senators are leading the way with an answer, pressuring the White House along with more than 20 of their colleagues on six specific actions that they would like the White House to take, asking President Biden to leverage a whole of government response through executive actions.\par \par \uc2 You see six of them there on the screen, increasing access to medication abortion, which is the most prevalently used form of abortion, it`s also \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  approved. Then you see other things on this list like providing resources to individuals who are seeking abortion in other states. That could mean things like travel vouchers or helping people pay for child care, any of the barriers that exist for people who are trying to access this kind of care and won`t be able to now that it`s becoming more restrictive.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:09]\par \par \uc2 Both on a state wide level and in anticipation of that official Supreme Court ruling that really could come any day now on Dobbs v. Jackson Women`s Health. That is largely expected based on a leaked draft opinion that we got a few weeks ago to topple the protections offered by Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 And, Chris, you and I both know, Senate Democrats tried and failed to shore up a federal codification of abortion protections several weeks ago. Now they`re looking for other options but really this list is tangible but it also shows us the other side of this coin which is that there`s not actually much that the federal government can do outside of a legislative pathway to shore up these protections if the Supreme Court is not going to continue to uphold them. Chris?\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Ali, thank you so much for that and for your great reporting.\par \par \uc2 Also right now, the latest on gas prices. The average for a gallon of gas today is more than $5 a gallon in 16 states and Washington, D.C. That`s double what it was just five days ago. On Friday, eight states were over that benchmark.\par \par \uc2 Prices are at record highs and they`re climbing fast. The national average now $4.95 a gallon according to AAA and it`s left people with some really hard decisions, like how much can I actually afford to put in my tank?\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We`re the ones hurting down here. You know, the poor people. The working class.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I`m working minimum wage job, so pretty much most of my paycheck goes to paying for my gas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: And if you thought gas prices were bad, get ready for more pressure as the cost of your electric bill climbs. The \uc2 U.S. Energy Information Administration\uc2  forecasts that the price for electricity will be up 4.8 percent over last summer. It`s already far worse than some places, the "Dallas Morning News" reports that rates in Texas have surged over 70 percent from last year.\par \par \uc2 Higher prices mean many folks won`t turn on their ACs and during extreme heat, that can cost lives. Today, 10 million people are under an excessive heat warning, in Arizona, southern Nevada, and southeastern California. Our climate unit says many cities in those regions and Texas could see five or more days of 100 degrees plus next week.\par \par \uc2 Olympic gymnasts and dozens of other women taking on the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , suing for a billion dollars. The case centers on how the \uc2 FBI\uc2  handled the Larry Nassar investigation. So will the bureau have to pay up and how much?\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:26]\par \par \uc2 JANSING: We`re following two major and really interesting lawsuits from people who are looking to the courts to channel their outrage into accountability.\par \par \uc2 First, there`s a group of 90 women, including Olympic gymnasts Simone Biles and Aly Raisman who are suing the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . They want more than $1 billion in damages for what they say is the bureau`s mishandling of its sexual abuse investigation into former U.S. Olympic team doctor Larry Nassar.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department did not immediately respond to our request for comment and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  declined to comment, referring NBC News to testimony from its director who told Congress, quote, "people at the \uc2 FBI\uc2  had their own chance to stop this monster back in 2015 and failed."\par \par \uc2 And then there`s the family suiting \uc2 Meta\uc2 , the company that owns \uc2 Instagram\uc2 , blaming it for their daughter`s eating disorder and self-harm. This case is unprecedented. It uses documents leaked last fall that revealed \uc2 Meta\uc2  knew \uc2 Instagram\uc2  was worsening body image among teenage girls. A spokesman for \uc2 Instagram\uc2  declined to comment on these lawsuits saying it`s active litigation.\par \par \uc2 With me now, MSNBC legal analyst Tali Farhadian Weinstein.\par \par \uc2 So, Tali, let`s start with this lawsuit against the \uc2 FBI\uc2 . Almost immediately following the news that the Department of Justice will not bring charges against the agent accused of mishandling the Nassar investigation.\par \par \uc2 I want to play, just to remind folks, what three of those women had to say about those agents last year before the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALY RAISMAN, OLYMPIC GYMNAST: The records established that Steve Penny, \uc2 FBI\uc2  Agent Jay Abbott and their subordinates worked to conceal Nassar`s crimes.\par \par \uc2 SIMONE BILES, OLYMPIC GYMNAST: We suffered and continue to suffer because no one at the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , USAG or the USOPC did what was necessary to protect us.\par \par \uc2 MCKAYLA MARONEY, OLYMPIC GYMNAST: What is the point of reporting abuse if our own \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents are going to take it upon themselves to bury that report in a drawer? They had legal, legitimate evidence of child abuse and did nothing. If they`re not going to protect me, I want to know who are they trying to protect.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANSING: What do you think? How strong do you think their case is?\par \par \uc2 TALI FARHADIAN WEINSTEIN, NBC NEWS LEGAL ANALYST: I think it`s heroic that they are bringing this case, Chris. And let`s give it some context. I want to start with what we just heard McKayla Maroney say, what is the point of reporting abuse if you`re going to do nothing. Somewhere between half and two-thirds of victims of sexual assault never go to the police, never go to law enforcement like the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , and I think that when they don`t get a response, it only makes it harder or discourages victims from coming forward in the next case.\par \par \uc2 And so what these women have said is well, we`re going to put ourselves in the driver`s seat. We could not get accountability through the criminal process. As you said, two weeks ago the DOJ said that they had decided that they were not going to prosecute these \uc2 FBI\uc2  agents for failure to act on the evidence that they got and so now they`re going to a different forum, where I think that they may actually have a really strong case because what they are trying to prove now in this civil case is different from what the DOJ was considering the charges that it rejected.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: So let`s talk about \uc2 Meta\uc2  lawyers who spoke with NBC News. They say this lawsuit makes unprecedented use of the \uc2 Facebook\uc2  papers, those are the ones that revealed the company`s extensive knowledge of the damage its products can do to teenagers, especially girls.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 How do you see that argument playing out on both sides of this suit?\par \par \uc2 WEINSTEIN: It`s really cutting edge. This lawsuit is a products liability lawsuit. Effectively it says that your product harmed us, the way you might say a gun manufacturer`s products or tobacco company`s products have caused harm. And I think that it`s a really important development in this frontier to try to get accountability, sort of echoes of the last one where victims are putting themselves in the driver`s seat and they`re saying if social media companies have not been held to account in the criminal process, there is a federal law that protects them from liability for the content that users put on their platforms and so now they are moving forward with a different theory.\par \par \uc2 And in a civil case like this one, we`re going to learn a lot in discovery that might make its way into motions about what exactly went on in cases like this one.\par \par \uc2 JANSING: Definitely two cases to watch. Tali Farhadian Weinstein, thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 And that`s going to do it for this hour. Make sure to join us for CHRIS JANSING REPORTS every weekday, 1:00 Eastern Time right here on MSNBC. But keep it right here. "KATY TUR REPORTS" starts next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you. I`m katy Tur. Miah Cerrillo should be two weeks into her summer vacation.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220616ei680002z\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for June 7, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Steve Kornacki \par \uc2 8,866 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 June 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Kevin McCarthy and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  get tonight`s "LAST WORD," once again, THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the polls are all now closed on another critical primary day. Results are coming in just as we`re coming on the air and Steve Kornacki is breaking it all down from the big board.\par \par \uc2 Then only two days away from the first January 6 hearing, new reporting about the former president`s plans that sent Secret Service scrambling that day.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the emotional and powerful plea from Uvalde native Matthew McConaughey to get something, anything done on gun safety, as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. We are 154 days out from the midterm elections and tonight we`re tracking all the results from the biggest primary day so far this year. Seven states held contest today from New Jersey to California where the polls have just closed. All eyes are on two key elections there. The race to be mayor of Los Angeles. The leading candidates sixth term Congresswoman \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  and billionaire developer \uc2 Rick Caruso\uc2 . And the ballot measure to recall progressive San Francisco District Attorney \uc2 Chesa Boudin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Let`s get straight to Steve Kornacki, it`s primary night so you know he`s going to be here at the big board waiting for us. Steve, break us down, who`s running? What are they doing? What are the numbers?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Stephanie, well the numbers right now are zero but within the next few minutes, we expect this map here of California to start lighting up like a Christmas tree. This is the way it works in California folks have been voting by mail for weeks now. This is different than some of the other states Remember we saw that slow count in Pennsylvania recently the mail-in ballots, not the case in California, they can process these, get these things ready to go and what happens is county after county really starting now because the polls have just closed in California within the next few minutes, county after county will start reporting out all of the mail ballots that they had received at least until Election Day.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to start seeing some big numbers. This is one of the places obviously we are going to be looking the most closely here. This is San Francisco. This is the recall effort against the district attorney \uc2 Chesa Boudin\uc2 . Obviously national significance here, Boudin, one of a batch of progressive prosecutors who have taken office in cities and counties across the country over the last few years. He has stressed what he calls decarceration policies. His opponents say, his critics say he is too lenient on crime.\par \par \uc2 San Francisco, obviously one of the most democratic, one of the most liberal cities, not just in California, but in the entire country. What would that say? Voters in a city like that choose to recall a prominent progressive prosecutor, when can we expect results in San Francisco? If that recall effort last year of \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  was any indication about 11:45 p.m. Eastern Time, that`s when we got that big batch of mail in votes from San Francisco in that -- in the gubernatorial recall election last year. So if that`s any guide, maybe about 11:45 tonight, maybe a little bit earlier, but again, when it comes in, we`re going to get a ton of votes. And we`re going to get them all at once. And as you mentioned, Stephanie, we are obviously also going to be getting a lot of results and keeping a close eye on this, the mayor`s race in Los Angeles. No votes yet. But again, same deal, city of Los Angeles, Los Angeles County, they have been processing. They`ve been getting those mail in ballots ready. They will get numbers reported out again, if last year is recall, the Newsom recall elections any indication it could be about 20, 25 minutes, and we will get a lot of numbers and maybe some early clarity here in this mayoral primary in Los Angeles.\par \par \uc2 Again, if nobody gets 50% that`s the magic number 50% then the top two finishers will go to a runoff, that runoff will be held in November. The polling is indicated \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2  and \uc2 Rick Caruso\uc2  are far ahead of the rest of the field. We will see what happens as we start to get those numbers.\par \par \uc2 We`re also going to be keeping an eye, Stephanie, on a number of House races in California, including areas and incumbent Republican, his name is David Valadao from the Central Valley of California. He voted to impeach \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  following January 6, he is facing the primaries. Congress are complicated in California Democrats, Republicans they all run on the same ballot, the top to advance to the general election. Well, there is a Republican who has stepped forward in that open primary against David Valadao making an issue of that vote for impeaching \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  trying to rally Republicans against him. We`ll be keeping an eye on that.\par \par \uc2 And by the way at this hour, speaking of Republican incumbents who may be in some danger in the state of Mississippi want to show you what`s going on there right now, to date this has been the biggest surprise tonight. Here`s Michael Guest incumbent Republican Congressman, leading his challenger here. Michael Cassidy by about seven and a half points.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:14]\par \par \uc2 Now, again, you got to finish over 50% in Mississippi to avoid a runoff. Why is Guest in such a close race in Mississippi, he`s the only Republican in Mississippi who voted in favor of establishing a bipartisan January 6 Commission. That`s been an issue in this campaign. And you can see that`s why he`s sweating it right now at this hour very much.\par \par \uc2 Again, Guest trying to win this, trying to avoid a runoff he`d need to finish over 50% and in the fourth district in Mississippi, this is Southern Mississippi, long serving 12 years and come and \uc2 Steven Palazzo\uc2 , incumbent member of Congress, you can see right now he`s clearly going to a runoff. He`s been scandals about his use of office funds, about office staff. He`s going to finish in first place here, as a weak -- weak first place finish for \uc2 Steven Palazzo\uc2  with his Republican primary.\par \par \uc2 The question now is which one of his opponents will face him in a runoff? It looks like Jackson County Sheriff Mike Ezell is slightly ahead of businessman Clay Wagner right now. But basically, you`ve got one incumbent member of Congress who is in grave danger here in Mississippi, certainly heading to a runoff. This is not a great position to be heading into a runoff in another incumbent member of Congress trying to avoid a runoff right now in Mississippi. That`s the drama that`s playing out right now as we speak. And again, we are waiting -- it`s a waiting game right now, Stephanie, I promise I will flag you as soon as we get any numbers from California because like I said, once we do, we could get clarity pretty quick in some of these big races.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When you get the numbers, we will come right back. But until you do. I want to bring in our lead off panel this evening, Democratic Strategist James Carville. He helped elect former President Bill Clinton in the early days. And Tim Miller, a contributor to the Bulwark and former Communications Director for Jeb Bush. And both of these gentlemen are favorite guests of mine.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Carville, to you first, what`s your reaction to what we just heard from Steve?\par \par \uc2 JAMES CARVILLE, VETERAN DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: The reaction -- I`m sorry, Stephanie, repeat it for me, I didn`t hear you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What do you think about what Steve just said?\par \par \uc2 CARVILLE: I think it`s fascinating. I`m right now located in Mississippi, Fourth Congressional District, so I didn`t vote in, and I wouldn`t have voted for any of them. But, you know, in election night, I mean, Steve Kornacki, is the guy I go to, actually I like him because he`s a horse degenerate. So maybe he`ll tell us who went to Belmont. But I just think he does a really good job of telling his viewers of what`s going on and really does a good job of explaining it. So I`m going to save a little bit later than usual tonight to see when he weighs in on California because maybe he`ll maybe give us a preview of the Belmont.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Steve, what do you think about that. I believe James just called you a horse degenerate.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: I`d argue if I could. In my Belmont pick got scratched a couple of days ago. No ethereal road, I`m looking for a new one. Give me a tip James, maybe.\par \par \uc2 CARVILLE: Mo Donegal -- Mo Donegal.\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: There you go., the hometown horse. I like it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, Tim Miller, let`s break down what Steve just told us because you`ve got a Republican who voted to impeach Trump. He`s at risk. You`ve got an incumbent Republican who voted in favor of the Jan. 6 commission. If both of those two gentlemen hold. What does that tell you about the state of the GOP?\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, THE BULWARK CONTRIBUTOR: You know, I don`t think that you can take broad pronouncements from what`s having these Mississippi races. Here`s what we know and what we`ve learned through a lot of the Republican primaries so far, is there`s about a third to maybe 40% of the Republican base that is all Trump all the time. And so I`m not surprised to see if you have a Mississippi congressman that voted for the January 6 committee that pro-Trump MAGA base would be able to get to 40 plus maybe some more.\par \par \uc2 You know, then you have another maybe 30% in these primaries that they still like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but or maybe ready to move on, maybe ready to go for a candidate they think might have a better chance of winning, you know, more practical Republican voters. And then you have some kind of crossover, depending on the race. So I think that what we`re seeing in Mississippi is going with a trend that we`ve seen in a lot of the other primaries throughout the country. Trump`s deal with a good hold on the party, but not a total hold depends on the situation.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Mr. Carville, California, considered a liberal state, San Francisco, one of the most liberal cities, the fact that you have got a progressive DA now getting recalled. What does that tell you about the state of politics there? Just last night, former Communications Director for President Obama, Dan Pfeiffer was here. I asked him about the San Fran mayor, and he said she`s doing a great job of pushing back against the far left, against the far left, you don`t hear -- it`s rare that I hear somebody in that seat when they say push back. The far left isn`t the next thing they say.\par \par \uc2 CARVILLE: Well, first of all, I`m going to brag on myself a little bit here. I think it was April 24 of 2021. I wrote an op-ed in the Wall Street Journal and recommended that Democrats get in front of the crime issue that they take ownership of it and as I recall my final line in the piece was you either own this issue, or this issue will own you.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:12]\par \par \uc2 Crime should be a Democratic issue. You know, people don`t realize this Senator Biden, President Clinton work together in 1994 Crime Bill and banned assault weapons. If there was an assault weapons ban in 1994, it was in effect for 10 years. In 1994, there were -- there were 400,000 assault weapons in the United States to date is 20 million. And now we think it`s progress. If you said, well, you got to pay 21 to have one, but for 10 years in this country, and bleeding, people hunting, they were going to target practice, they were in gun clubs. They weren`t buying assault weapons. And people United States needs to know that President Biden was instrumental, authored that bill, which was backed by then President Clinton, and for some reason, we`re losing sight of this. And we think it`s any kind of small victory. And I`m glad that Senator Murphy and I`m glad that Matthew McConaughey did today and passionate thing by Charles M. Blow, an hour before us.\par \par \uc2 But we did something as a country, and then we undid it, no one in the country realizes that. I don`t see why we can`t go big here sometimes and just ban the stupid things that no one needs them. Now, the only people that need this are people in the United States military. And this is ridiculous. But we`ve been through this. We did something and the Republicans aren`t dead. One of the great policy triumphs in crime and model lifetime, which is a long time, a long, long time.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tim, we`re going to talk more about guns in a little bit, but I want to stay and talk about tonight`s elections. Talk to us about California considered a Democratic stronghold. Will tonight`s midterms tell us more about where the state is headed?\par \par \uc2 MILLER: I`m sorry, Tim.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tonight`s primary, excuse me.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: Yeah, look, I mean, I don`t know that it`ll tell us about where the state is headed. But I think it is going to tell us a little bit about the Democratic Party is headed and I, you know, I`m out here in Oakland right now and socialize a lot of Democrats, despite my former standing as a Republican and the feeling among Democrats in California is a little bit of frustration, some of that stuff that James was talking about, I think that they feel like there`s a way to balance public safety with still being a liberal, still being a progressive, you can want to ban assault rifles that also think that, you know, cops should be protecting your neighborhood. And I think that there`s a feeling among Democrats that some on the progressive left, went overboard.\par \par \uc2 Chesa Boudin\uc2  is a big example, that in San Francisco, we saw the education, the school board members recalled here in San Francisco, and possibly, I think the most dramatic thing we`ll see what happens with Caruso in the mayor`s race in California. I mean, he was a \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2 , Republican, that`d be like me running for mayor of Los Angeles. So I think if he wins that, that that would be a real shock to the system. But even that he`s in the game, I think tells you that the Democratic base is ready to look for a more sensible reform oriented agenda. It`s sort of the rebellion of the Normie Democrats, right? They just want normal liberal stuff, the kind of stuff that James passed with Clinton back in the 90s. They don`t -- they feel like some of the things that have been pushed lately