othing that we can do?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Incredibly emotional moments. We`re hearing them day in and day out. We`re going to hear more tomorrow. But we`ve been hearing them for years. Gene, do you have any confidence that this time it will move the needle?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: No, I don`t. I have hope. I have hope -- look, the encouraging noises are coming out of the Senate, the bipartisan negotiations seems like they could actually produce a very, very weak bill, something that would encourage states to impose a red flag laws, something that maybe could slightly tighten background checks. And then they want to talk about mental health, which you know, good luck if you`re going to solve mental health. You know, the problem is the gun and nothing is directly going to be done about guns I hope against hope that at least they could raise the age nationally for buying assault rifle from 18 to 21.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:18]\par \par \uc2 But it looks doubtful that that`s going to come out of the Senate maybe, you know, surprise me, please. It is, you know, that said, if we can move one centimeter closer towards sensible, sane, life-saving gun policies, then, sure that`s better than staying where we are. We`ve tried doing nothing and that doesn`t work. So if they come up with just that much, you know, I say swallow hard, approve it and keep working at it and those -- and these family members shouldn`t have to do this. They shouldn`t have to come and face Congress in these moments of grief and 11-year-old girl want to come tomorrow to a hearing that is absolutely, that`s outrageous. That`s horrific, that they have to do this, in order to try to get through to Congress on this issue that is costing so many lives and causing so much tragedy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Mark, today we heard the reasoning, why AR-15s are useful, why they`re needed. We heard it from South Dakota`s \uc2 John Thune\uc2 . I want you to watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 JOHN THUNE\uc2 , (R) SOUTH DAKOTA: In my state, they use them to shoot prairie dogs and, you know, other types of varmints. And so, I think that there are legitimate reasons why people would want to have them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Varmints, that type of gun was used and killed those kids last week and hurt them so badly that that young girl was unrecognizable, except for her green sneakers.\par \par \uc2 MCKINNON: It`s stunning, Stephanie. I mean, I would love to see that Senator defend himself before one of the parents of those kids who were shocked by saying, yeah, we need these weapons of mass destruction to stop the menace of prairie dogs. This is like an emerging Republican talking point that we need them for varmints. I mean, it`s like a goddamn cartoon. The other one, so we need to stop raccoons and\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Feral hogs.\par \par \uc2 MCKINNON: Yeah, and feral hogs. Republicans, we need assault rifles for varmints. And that`s it. And McConaughey was so powerful today, because he humanizes. I think that`s the problem is that the senators are so removed from reality. They don`t realize that we`re talking about not just human beings, but 10 year olds, nine year olds, eight year olds, kids in elementary schools, who are being literally blown up so they can`t be recognized at anything other tennis shoe. And the thing that`s really disturbing to me, Stephanie, is the fact that 44% of Republicans now say we just have to accept the status quo of mass killings going forward, that`s just the way it is.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, Matthew McConaughey, he didn`t just speak at the White House today. He then went on Fox News and made that same emotional plea. Gene, do you think it`s more important that Fox News visit with a visit to the White House?\par \par \uc2 ROBINSON: On the Fox News visit may be more important, it really may be. He needs to reach the majority of gun owners who support sensible reasonable gun control, sensible reasonable measures to attack gun violence, who know about weapons, who understand the difference between, you know, what you need for varmints and weapons of war. So I think that`s that outreach is very, very important. But again, does it -- does even that get fed into the Republican caucus in the Senate, they seem to be completely sealed off from reality and public opinion has been for gun control in this country for a long time and then cannot be moved. It is a national disgrace.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Prairie dogs and varmints, that is the response to 19 children and two teachers being murdered less than two weeks after 11 people at a grocery store. Eugene Robinson, Mark McKinnon, I thank you both for joining us tonight. We`re going to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Before our special primary coverage continues at the top of the hour with Ayman Mohyeldin, we want to leave you with a little more of Matthew McConaughey`s important call to action.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCONAUGHEY: We got to take a sober, humble, and honest look in the mirror and rebrand ourselves based on what we truly value. What we truly value.\par \par \uc2 We got to get some real courage and honor our immortal obligations instead of our party affiliations.\par \par \uc2 [23:55:04]\par \par \uc2 Enough with the counterpunching. Enough of the invalidation of the other side. Let`s come to the common table that represents the American people. Find a middle ground, the place where most of us Americans live anyway, especially on this issue.\par \par \uc2 Because I promise you, America -- you and me, who -- we are not as divided as we`re being told we are. No.\par \par \uc2 How about we get inspired? Give ourselves just cause to revere our future again. Maybe set an example for our children, give us reason to tell them, "Hey, listen and watch these men and women. These are great American leaders right here. Hope you grow up to be like them."\par \par \uc2 And let`s admit it: We can`t truly be leaders if we`re only living for reelection.\par \par \uc2 Let`s be knowledgeable and wise, and act on what we truly believe.\par \par \uc2 Again, we got to look in the mirror, lead with humility, and acknowledge the values that are inherent to but also above politics. We`ve got to make choices, make stands, embrace new ideas, and preserve the traditions that can create true -- true progress for the next generation.\par \par \uc2 With real leadership, let`s start giving us -- all of us, with real leadership -- let`s start giving all of us good reason to believe that the American Dream is not an illusion.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220616ei670003h\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for May 31, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Morgan Chesky, Richard Engel \par \uc2 7,984 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: We`ll be presenting a special program field report with Paola Ramos that will focus on Latino voters in the upcoming 2022 elections. That is tonight`s "LAST WORD." THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:18]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, grief and unanswered questions. Funeral services have started in Uvalde. Exactly one week after the massacre at an elementary school, with new reporting tonight about the response.\par \par \uc2 And in the seven days since there have been at least 14 more mass shootings, what can actually be done to address America`s gun violence crisis.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the rare admission from a cabinet secretary, who was wrong about something so many Americans are struggling with as the 11th hour gets underway on a Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. Tonight, exactly one week after a teenage gunman took 21 lives at an elementary school in Uvalde, Texas. The very first funerals are getting underway.\par \par \uc2 While the murder victims are being laid to rest, the investigation into the shooting, and the police response at the school is only intensifying. There are reports that Uvalde police are no longer cooperating with Texas authorities.\par \par \uc2 NBC News reporting the school district police chief initially did cooperate but as yet to respond to requests for follow up questions made two days ago. Texas officials say the chief prevented officers from confronting the shooter for more than an hour.\par \par \uc2 Today, that very same chief was sworn in as a member of the city council. The Justice Department opening up its own inquiry into the police response.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Washington, there are some in Congress that believe they might be able to find common ground on some gun safety measures. The House Judiciary Committee plans to hold an emergency session Thursday on new legislation.\par \par \uc2 And on the Senate side, a bipartisan group met today on gun reform with another meeting scheduled tomorrow. Connecticut Democrat \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 , who has been working on this issue for the last decade, gave us an update on how those conversations are going.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): I have had the football pulled out from under me enough times before in these negotiations to be realistic about our prospects for success on the table our red flag laws, changes to our background checks, systems safe storage, investments in mental health, investments in school security.\par \par \uc2 It`s hard to find the path to 60 votes in the Senate for a ban on assault weapons. That`s the reason why it right now isn`t on the list of things that our group is considering.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: President Biden said he plans to meet with lawmakers about gun safety but no word on when that will happen. Meanwhile, lawmakers are talking in Washington about how to prevent this type of unthinkable tragedy. Both families and friends of the shooting victims are living with it facing new stark realities. As NBC`s Morgan Chesky reports.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 MORGAN CHESKY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): Tonight, heartbreak hitting home with hundreds saying final goodbyes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You just feel sad man like hits you hard.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: 21 funerals over the next two weeks, including services for 14-year olds today. Maite Rodriguez was remembered as a sweet soul. She wanted to be a marine biologist. Family of Nevaeh Bravo whose name is heaven spelled backwards said she`s lying with angels. Jose Manuel Flores`s father said he wanted to be a police officer because he liked protecting others. And Amerie Jo Garza, her family said called 911 that morning.\par \par \uc2 ALFRED GARZA, FATHER OF AMERIE JO GARZA: I just want to under her name, I just I just want her to be remembered as the type of person that she was.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: The Girl Scouts honoring her with one of their highest awards, the Bronze Cross given to those who risked their lives to try and save others.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Thanks to Morgan Chesky for that report. And let`s take a moment and pause. As we look at these images. They are personalized caskets made for each of the 19 young victims killed in that shooting. They`re being made free of charge, or the man you just saw on your screen. Trey Ganim.\par \par \uc2 The Texas man visited each family to learn more about their loved ones so he could create a unique final resting place. He says it helps these families in their healing process just a little.\par \par \uc2 Now I want to bring in our leadoff panel this evening Peter Baker, Chief White House correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , Katie Benner, Justice Department reporter for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  and Tali Farhadian Weinstein, a former federal and state prosecutor in New York. She clerked for \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  and Justice \uc2 Sandra Day O`Connor\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Peter, I want to start with what we heard today from New York Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , he gave us a reality check on whether the Senate is anywhere near passing anything on gun legislation.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:10]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Many of us do believe that the Senate has become a broken, undemocratic institution, largely driven by the filibuster, hopefully in the court of public opinion, will be able to put enough pressure on a handful of Republican senators to finally come to some common ground.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Common ground like I want to get real. The House is moving on their bills. But let`s look at this. The Senate is not back until next week, then there`s the Fourth of July, then there`s summer recess. In terms of is something going to really get done here, is this talk for real?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, it`s a good question. Look, the Senate is designed by the Constitution to avoid exactly the kind of rush to legislation that we`re talking about. They`re designed -- it is designed as an institution to avoid, you know, quick response to popular outrage.\par \par \uc2 The founders believed that there was something valuable in that. But obviously, in today`s political environment, there`s a great frustration with that, a frustration that doesn`t just slow things down to give it some thoughtful consideration. But it slows things down forever, and never get anything done. And that`s where we`re at.\par \par \uc2 And the truth is that, you know, there does feel like some, you know, positive momentum with Republicans talking with Democrats at this point. But nobody ever lost money betting against gun control passing in Washington in the last decade. If it didn`t happen after Sandy Hook, if it didn`t happen after Orlando, it didn`t happen after El Paso and Parkland, and so many others, then, you know, is it going to happen now?\par \par \uc2 And the question is, is the dynamic changed in a drastic way compared to before. Normally, you see this desire to do something, and then quickly fade with time because other stories come to the fore momentum saps, hoppier outrage diminishes, people move on. And the question is whether that will happen again this time.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then, the President has said, Peter, he`s going to meet with members of Congress, the likelihood that they`re going to want to play ball slim. I`m not saying none but slim. Given that, should he be focused on doing something on his own via executive order?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, I think what he will tell you is he has. I mean, that the White House has taken some actions already, Katie probably knows better than I do on things like those guns and other things that they can affect through regulatory action.\par \par \uc2 But the truth is, there`s a very limited menu of options to an executive at this point. The kinds of measures that would have any kind of impact on the massacres that we see almost all require congressional action require a law, and a president cannot pass a law by himself.\par \par \uc2 So yes, he`s going to probably offer to do some things on the margins. And they will be things that may, you know, may make some difference on some level. But the truth is the kind of things that most activists want, the kind of things that most people on the left anyway think are necessary, are not within the venue or purview of a presidential executive power.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Katie, take us to this Department of Justice investigation into the local police response at Rob elementary last week, what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 KATIE BENNER, THE NEW YORK TIMES JUSTICE DEPARTMENT REPORTER: Sure. So we saw that the \uc2 Texas Rangers\uc2  themselves were investigating their response. But they have stopped getting cooperation from Mr. Aaron Dondo, the chief of police of Uvalde Consolidated Independent Schools.\par \par \uc2 So what we saw as the Justice Department stepping over the weekend. Now, they cannot do this without invitation from local government. So it was actually the mayor who initiated the request, asking the Justice Department for help better understanding exactly what happened with this response.\par \par \uc2 What we can expect, as the department will issue a full report of their findings, they will not only give us some sort of tick tock of the decisions being made, and when, but they should help us understand why those decisions were made.\par \par \uc2 I think the reason why this is so important, is because as we`ve seen in local jurisdictions all over the country, state and local level, it`s very difficult to have oversight over the police and this should give local officials the top cover they need to do something.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Tali, what is the value in your opinion of this after action report on law enforcement? I mean, the people of Uvalde must be saying you`re not bringing our children back?\par \par \uc2 TALI FARHADIAN WEINSTEIN, FORMER NY FEDERAL AND STATE PROSECUTOR: Well, look, we do have to manage expectations, the support is not going to advise on gun safety measures going forward. It`s not going to tell us how to prevent another mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 But I do think it`s important for the people of Uvalde for the parents to understand what happened. And for all of us to think about how can law enforcement do better when there is another mass shooting, which seems inevitable and Stephanie, I`m particularly hoping that they`re going to answer the really hard question of why the police treated this as a barricaded suspect, rather than a situation where lives had to be saved, and why there were 19 police officers standing outside that classroom while those girls were making those phone calls to 911 saying we here alive. Please send the police in.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:00]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: 19 police officers while 19 children were inside that room. Peter, should we expect to see Democrats running on gun safety ahead of the midterms? Is this a good strategy?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, I`m sure they will, of course, but remember most Democrats are running a district they`re probably pretty favorable toward gun rights, to gun control, excuse me. And I think that the problem is not, you know, the districts where they have one on that issue, that problem or district where they have lost on that issue.\par \par \uc2 Republicans who have opposed gun safety measures or gun control measures over the years aren`t responding to national polls, which show a great deal of popular support for these measures. They`re responding to their own districts and their own constituents, who in many cases are not as welcoming to these kinds of measures and who certainly you see the gun rights part of their constituencies more active and more willing to vote and more willing to give money in the gun rights lobby, with the \uc2 NRA\uc2  playing a more active role than the other side of the equation.\par \par \uc2 So the political dynamics have always been, you know, very skewed in a polarized country. There are a lot of districts and a lot of states where control is very active, very popular, and therefore you see that politicians respond to that but there`s a lot of parts of the country where that`s not the case.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Katie, there`s some January 6 related news tonight as well. \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2 , longtime ally advisor to President Trump now been served with a federal grand jury subpoena. Is this the first concrete evidence we have seen of the Department of Justice criminally investigating Trump`s inner circle.\par \par \uc2 BENNER: I don`t think this is the first we`ve seen because we`ve also seen subpoenas crop up in other places, vis-a-vis, the organization of the rallies before January 6, and the scheme to send fake slates of electors to the National Archives and to Congress. But this to your point is the first time we`ve seen a subpoena issued to somebody who worked directly within the White House.\par \par \uc2 Now, Navarro is fighting this subpoena. He has asked the Justice Department`s U.S. attorney here in Washington, DC to work it out with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I`m pretty sure that legal advice, that legal recommendation is going to be ignored.\par \par \uc2 But what it does show us is that criminal investigators, the prosecutors are saying what exactly happened, especially around federal election laws that, you know, that we can dig into throughout the course of the year.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What do you think about this Tali? Work it out with DJT? Department of Justice ain`t doing that?\par \par \uc2 WEINSTEIN: You know, Stephanie, you and I`ve talked a lot about the scope of the DOJ investigation, how big is it? How narrow is it? I think we learned something about the scope today with the subpoena. I think we learned that the focus, as we had suspected is not just on the events of that day, but on who talked to whom and who talked to the President leading up to that day. So the planning and everything that went into what turned out to be the violence of January 6.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I have to ask you about this report circulating around the investigation into the League of the draft of the Roe vs. Wade opinion. We`re now learning that clerks, Supreme Court justice clerks, you were once one may now have to turn over their phone records, their all their personal information. How unusual is this?\par \par \uc2 WEINSTEIN: It is so shocking. And I think we really need to ask, what justifies this invasion of privacy. And what kind of example is the court setting. Because the leak was not a crime, maybe the closest crime that we could come up with is theft of government property. But the DOJ has said, we don`t prosecute that when it involves a leak, when it involves unclassified information, something that`s not tangible.\par \par \uc2 And so you have this investigation, not for a crime, maybe for a violation of norms, or some employment contract. And the Supreme Court has also said in a different context, that when your employer is the government, and they asked you voluntarily to answer some questions, or just turn some stuff over, that feels really coercive. Your job is on the line.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s course of whether you were the person who leaked, it might have been a clerk might not have been a clerk, or if you just object to all of this.\par \par \uc2 And I can tell you, Stephanie, as a prosecutor who has read lots of people`s emails and listen to their phone calls and seen their phone records, there are really good reasons why you would not want your employer, the government to see all of that even if you`ve done nothing.\par \par \uc2 So, I really wonder if this whole thing is really going to help the court put itself back together in the way that it wants to be put back together.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And meanwhile, there is no investigation into any of the communications around Ginni Thomas, wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 . Peter, here`s what I want to know. A dozen House Republicans have now introduced a bill that makes leaks from the Supreme Court punishable by up to five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 Yet, as we just discussed when it comes to gun violence, they have proposed absolutely nothing. Are Republicans banking on the fact that people aren`t going to care?\par \par \uc2 [23:15:04]\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, this is a performative bill. It`s not going to go anywhere, obviously a Democratic Congress, it`s meant to make a statement, it`s meant to make the issue the leak rather than what was the leak, right. Rather than the issue being about abortion rights and whether Roe v. Wade should or will be overturned. The issue is the leak and what was behind that.\par \par \uc2 The truth is we don`t know who leaked. We don`t know whether it was a liberal. We don`t know was a conservative. We don`t know what the motivation was. Was it meant to alarm pro, you know, abortion rights activist to understand what`s about to happen? Or was it meant to lock in the conservatives on a five-vote majority to not shift their vote from an earlier draft opinion, that`s Justice Alito, but we don`t know that.\par \par \uc2 And so the truth is, you know, this bill is kind of, you know, getting at a problem that they don`t really understand. I mean, look, it is and Tali knows this better than I do. It is very unusual to have a leak and it is corrosive clearly to a Supreme Court that is used to having very confidential communications. I can only imagine what this has done to their internal system of trust.\par \par \uc2 You know, from a reporter`s point of view, of course, we don`t want anything to be illegal when it comes to leaks. Everything should be leaked. Call me my numbers on the book.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Leaks work for us. Peter, Katie, Tali, thank you all for starting us off tonight. We`re going to leave it there.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, by now, everyone knows the problem. At least 14 more mass shootings since Uvalde and yet again, gun reform advocates are offering real solutions. Peter said it, there`s not a lot of options. We are going to hear a five-point plan for the President just ahead.\par \par \uc2 And later, the destructive advancement. Russia has made in the 97-day-old war on Ukraine, and the latest steps the U.S. is taking to try to slow it down. THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a very busy Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:21:29]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR JOHN TECKLENBURG, CHARLESTON, SOUTH CAROLINA: Every 16 hours in America, there`s a mass shooting. I don`t know about you, but I`m angry about it. I`m mad about it. I`m fed up. Enough is enough. And this entire issue of gun violence has to be addressed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Charleston`s Mayor echoing the growing frustration we are hearing everywhere. Enough is enough. Here`s the thing. Just days after that massacre at Robb Elementary, more than a dozen mass shootings were recorded this past three day weekend, including in Charleston, where that Mayor was speaking just right there.\par \par \uc2 So let`s discuss I want to bring in Igor Volsky. He`s executive director of Guns Down America. He`s also the author of "Guns Down: How to Defeat the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and Build a Safer Future with Fewer Guns."\par \par \uc2 I like the sound of that title, Igor. So how are we going to do it, President Biden said he is going to talk and work with rational Republicans to take some action on gun safety. Sounds great. But it`s he kidding himself?\par \par \uc2 IGOR VOLSKY, EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR GUNS DOWN AMERICA: Well, look, I think this is his choice, right? Remember, this is a president who told us that his 30 plus years in the Senate gave him the tools he needed to get big things done, right, to get Democrats and Republicans together.\par \par \uc2 And I think the argument I`m making that at a time when 90 percent of Americans all across the country, tell us that they support expanding background checks and all kinds of reforms, this president should be able to channel that popular support into political pressure on Republicans to get them to understand that they have to do something.\par \par \uc2 Look, it`s certainly not going to be easy. But why isn`t he criss-crossing the country building that kind of support? Why isn`t he bringing senators and Congress people back into DC so they could start those kinds of negotiations? Why isn`t he taking all kinds of executive actions that frankly, survivors have been asking him for to take for years? He needs to show us that this kind of cycle that a shooting happens, thoughts and prayers and nothing at all, that we as a country can break it. And I think my message to him is that, Mr. President, you`re the leader to do that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, so tomorrow, what can you do?\par \par \uc2 VOLSKY: Tomorrow, he needs to announce he`s opening a first ever White House Office of Gun Violence Prevention. He`s going to staff that office with a high level official who`s going to coordinate the government`s entire response to this pandemic. He`s going to announce that he`s going to embark on a tour of impacted communities listened to what Americans go through every single day, in terms of the impact gun violence has on them and on their families.\par \par \uc2 And he needs to announce that he`s going to be bringing stakeholders into the White House. He`s going to be bringing senators into the White House to start though it`ll continue really those negotiation processes. He needs to treat this issue with the kind of seriousness that he applies to so many other priorities. He knows how to do this.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Sounds like they might call you into the White House. You got a plan. I want to share what Arkansas Republican governor said this morning. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV ASA HUTCHINSON (R-AR): I think you`ve got to be able to talk about the AR 15 style weapons and whether that`s an 18 or 21 age, you have to at least have a conversation about that.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:12]\par \par \uc2 The challenge with the gun safety discussion is what makes a difference. You cannot just simply focus on a gun safety discussion, it`s got to also be about investment in higher levels of school safety.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Is that what progress on gun safety looks like? Sounds like?\par \par \uc2 VOLSKY: Well, look, we know what to do. It`s not a question as the governor`s suggesting, I mean, just today, you had the Prime Minister of New Zealand in the White House. And she reminded the country, I`m sure she reminded the President, that countries can choose to act to change these kinds of catastrophes, right. They can ban the sale of assault weapons. In this country, we have so many of them out floating in society. And so we need to ensure that we license and we register them in the same way, by the way, Stephanie, that we license and register fully automatic machine guns, right, that kind of regime worked when we did that back in 1986. And I think he can work again now.\par \par \uc2 You know, we need to regulate, I think the gun industry far more aggressively. We need background checks, we need licensing, we need registration, we need to invest in the communities closest to the pain of everyday gun violence, because those programs work. They work to reduce homicides.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s not a question of oh, we don`t know what to do, as he`s suggesting. We have the recipe. We`ve seen it replicated successfully in so many other parts of the world. Again, we just need our leaders, the very same ones, the president, Majority Leader Schumer, the ones who promised us this would be a priority for them. We want them to fight because believe me, when voters see them actually try to live up to those promises. They will be rewarded come the midterms.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, we`ve got the recipe, but our government`s not cooking. They haven`t done anything. So for you, this is your life`s work. What keeps you in this fight?\par \par \uc2 VOLSKY: Well, what keeps me in this fight is knowing that you can push our elected leaders, you can push other key voices be the corporation`s, for instance, right, who also have such an important role to play to keep their customers and their employees safe.\par \par \uc2 But there are different levers we can pull to try and make progress. And that`s been true by the way throughout our entire history. You look at the civil rights struggle. You look at the marriage equality struggle. It takes time, hard things take time, but believe me, they`re damn worth fighting for.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And you are in there fighting this fight. Igor Volsky, thank you for joining us tonight. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 VOLSKY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Coming out, the President wants to bring back plain speaking Joe, the \uc2 White\uc2  House`s new strategy for communicating in a complicated time when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:32:38]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , U.S. TREASURY SECRETARY: I think I was wrong then about the path that inflation would take. There have been unanticipated and large shocks to the economy that have boosted energy and food prices and supply bottlenecks that have affected our economy badly that at the time didn`t fully understand. But we recognize that now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And then the question is now what? President Biden reportedly rattled by his sinking approval ratings, and he`s looking to turn it around NBC News reporting. The President is pressing aides for a more compelling message and a sharper strategy, while bristling at how they`ve tried to stifle his plain speaking persona.\par \par \uc2 So let`s discuss. With us tonight, David Plouffe, former \uc2 Obama\uc2  campaign manager and senior adviser to the president and former Florida Republican Congressman \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , who was a member of the Republican conference for the first few years of the Trump presidency.\par \par \uc2 David, you worked with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , do you think we`re getting enough of his plainspoken self?\par \par \uc2 DAVID PLOUFFE, FMR. OBAMA CAMPAIGN MANAGER: Well, I think we`ll see more of it. I think that`s one of his strengths. You know, he`s going to hit the ball out of bounds every once in a while. But I think the way he speaks the way he connects with Americans, he puts complicated policy matters in plain English is a benefit.\par \par \uc2 But I think for Democrats, and this, quite frankly, Stephanie is less about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  than the individual Democratic candidates. You`ve got to turn this into a choice, not a referendum. And I think, you know, issues like gun control, issues like outlawing abortion, whether we`re democracy. Those are largely supported by the American people who also have questions in the economy or the inflation.\par \par \uc2 I think the question you ask is, if the Republicans gain power, what are they going to do about it? They`re not going to focus on your paycheck. They`re going to focused on \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is refuse to say he won`t pass a law to outlaw abortion at the federal level. There`ll be obsessed with making it easier to get assault weapons. They`ll be obsessed with conspiracy theories.\par \par \uc2 So yes, you can do a better job of talking about what you`re doing, what you`re fighting for, but particularly in our challenging political climate, and I`ve been in. If you do not make it a searing choice, you`re not going to like the election results on the other end.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Communications messaging is complicated. Carlos, let`s talk about the economy because new polling from NBC shows that only 33 percent of Americans approve of Biden`s handling of the economy.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:08]\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing. Inflation is a problem. There`s no doubt about that. However, the American consumer is forgetting. We were in financial crisis two years ago. We are in an economic recovery. There are good jobs. There are higher paying jobs. And while inflation is a problem, Americans also saved up a lot of money over the last couple of years. How does Biden tell this story? Because people aren`t happy?\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. CARLOS CURBELO (R-FL): Well, I think the President`s write stuff that they have to allow him to be himself to speak more often, even if you get a gaffe from time to time. That`s \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . That`s who he`s been his whole life.\par \par \uc2 His biggest accomplishment in Congress was the bipartisan infrastructure bill. Why? Because it was consistent with what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said when he ran for office, and he wanted to heal the country, bring the two parties together, he`s got another big opportunity now on gun reform, I think he`s being smart. Less is more right now encouraging \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  to engage Democrats.\par \par \uc2 If he can bring the country together on that issue, that will be very powerful. And it`ll be consistent with who he said he wanted to be. A lot of Democrats have wanted \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to be the enthusiastic champion of the most liberal policy agenda advanced by Democrats in a generation. I understand why, but that`s just not who Joe Biden`s been his whole life. And it`s not the way he campaigned. He was going to be different than Trump. He was going to heal the country. This gun issue has the potential to change the narrative and be powerful for Biden, if they get it right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Really interesting point. David, I want to share a headline from POLITICO tonight, quote, The White House has one problem that rules them all. Gas prices. Here`s the thing, David, no matter what the President does, he does not control the price of gas. And we are having to pay more for gas not just here, all around the world, because of the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Does the American consumer know or care because I remind our audience while people are mad about gas prices, we`re expecting a record number of vacationers travelers this summer.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Right, so people are going to pay the price to do what they`re going to do. But listen, I`ve been in the White House, when you`ve had high gas prices, not this high. And it`s -- you can explain all you want about why it is and what you`re doing to mitigate. And as you said Stephanie is very little a president can do. But you get to this sort of five, six, you know, there`s some places where it`s $7.\par \par \uc2 You know, it`s going to really put the electorate in a foul mood, and you add that to the other costs. So, I think the White House can go out there and say, here`s why it`s so expensive. It is not just a U.S. issue. This is true all around the world. Here`s what we`re trying to do to fight back, the President had an op-ed.\par \par \uc2 But you have to understand if even when people start casting votes by mail in early October, and gasses this high, and you know, and I hope it`s not for a bunch of different reasons, you know, the political environment is going to be incredibly challenging for every Democrat. That`s just a reality. And I don`t think Democrats can run away from that. They have to have scenarios.\par \par \uc2 Scenario one, gas prices recede, inflation gets a little bit better, people liked the trajectory, even if it`s still high. Trajectories matter in politics. I saw that with \uc2 Obama\uc2  in `12. Unemployment was so high, but it was coming down. Scenario B is trajectory is not good. And that is where, you know, if you don`t make this a searing choice, where people really think carefully about the change they`re prepared to make, you`re going to be in real difficulty.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do you agree David with Carlos that Biden has an actual opportunity here on gun safety. Cynics would say no way. Republicans are never going to play ball. But I`m embarrassed to say I almost forgot that infrastructure bill. Now law got done on a bipartisan basis. And while Democrats are not doing much to sell it, it`s a humongous win.\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Well, Stephanie I`d say this. I, you know, I do agree with the Congressman. I think it`s a necessity that happened for the country. The politics are secondary. I`m sure, you know, it is beyond comprehension, that we live in a country that allows this to happen time and time again.\par \par \uc2 And so something has to be done. I saw it, you know, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was appointed by \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  to work on this after Sandy Hook. He knows these issues inside and out. He knows the Senate inside now. So yes, it`s a necessity for the country.\par \par \uc2 But politically, of course, I think it would be something that most people in the country support, including most gun owners, including most Republicans support things like background checks and raising the age of AR fifteens from 18 to 21. So I agree the congressman.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Given that Carlos, is this a real opportunity for Republicans who want to break away from Trump on issues like guns, on issues like abortions, on anything that relates to Trump himself. Is there a clear lane for those Republicans now with these issues bubbling up?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Definitely. And I think, Steph, that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  knows that Republicans are out of step with most Americans on this issue and we know \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  cares about one thing, winning elections.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:04]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And he`s good at it.\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: He`s good at it. And that`s why he`s pushing Cornyn and other Republican senators cut a deal with Democrats. Let`s do something meaningful here. Take this issue off the table ahead of the November elections.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Wow. You`re saying there`s a chance. \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , David Plouffe, thank you both for joining us tonight. Coming up next, street by street fighting in a key city as the war intensifies in eastern Ukraine and a brand new shipment of U.S. aid headed there when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:45:09]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Russia is gaining ground in eastern Ukraine where soldiers are fighting block by block in some instances. Ukraine`s president Zelenskyy warns that Russia is using, quote, maximum combat power. NBC News correspondent Richard Engel has an update on the fighting from Donbas.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, NBC NEWS CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): At 1:00 a.m. Russian shells pounded this row of apartments and Slavyansk. Vitale Krevadenko (ph) a cobbler says after the explosion, he looked through the dust for his wife Nellie (ph). She started to shout my legs, my legs, she wanted water, but there was no cup. So I gave her water from my hands. Then she lost consciousness, he told us.\par \par \uc2 The sheets on Nellie`s (ph) bed are drenched in blood. With her picture in his hands and more blood by his feet, Vitale (oh) says he doesn`t know where his wife is now.\par \par \uc2 Downstairs American volunteer Dallas Casey had arrived. The six-year Army veteran who says he was compelled to help Ukraine defend its democracy was nearby.\par \par \uc2 Dallas and his team helped Vitale down from his apartment and to a car bound for a hospital without telling his wife is dead.\par \par \uc2 Dallas says the fighting is getting heavy. In nearby Severodonetsk, Russia has seized half the city.\par \par \uc2 DALLAS CASEY, AMERICAN VOLUNTEER IN UKRAINE: When we were in Severodonetsk, we were definitely being shelled heavily, buildings would get hit and we would go to those buildings immediately try to pull people out.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: And more help is coming. Ukrainian troops are pouring in, and the United States is pouring in more weapons.\par \par \uc2 (on camera): The \uc2 European Union\uc2  is also tightening its economic screws. Banning the import of Russian oil by ship, by pipeline is still allowed. The move is expected to cut Russian oil supplies to Europe by two-thirds by the end of the year, draining a key source of income for the Kremlin. Richard Engel, NBC News, the Donbas, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Thank you, Richard. All of that comes as the President said in \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  that more U.S. weapons would be headed to Ukraine. The President wrote this, in part, we will provide the Ukrainians with more advanced rocket systems and munitions that will enable them to more precisely strike key targets on the battlefield in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Let`s figure out what all that means and bring in Jason Beardsley, a decorated U.S. military veteran with over 20 years of experience in the Army and the Navy. He`s now the National Executive Director for the association of the Navy. So let`s start right there. What do more advanced rocket systems mean? What does that look like?\par \par \uc2 JASON BEARDSLEY, NATIONAL EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR ASSOCIATION OF THE \uc2 U.S. NAVY\uc2 : Yes, well, in real short summary, it means more range, and better precise targeting of Russian systems. So the Russian artillery systems are really what`s at stake here, when you get the right kind of rocket systems, the right kind of guided munitions, you can hit them from a further distance, and you can be more accurate, while also reloading and moving a lot faster.\par \par \uc2 So that`s the game changer. If you can do that quick and rapidly, while concealing your position, you have the advantage. So that`s the big takeaway in summary.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What could they mean for the war? Could they be a game changer?\par \par \uc2 BEARDSLEY: Yes, they have to be used effectively. But yes, again, one of the major things is the standoff or the distance you`re going to get from some of the systems that we can enable the Ukrainians to do that, that allows you Ukrainians to stay out of danger, while firing upon Russian artillery. It also allows them to do dynamic type striking.\par \par \uc2 So, if they have drone technology, and they`re able to spot all these targets, they can quickly get guided munitions, rockets that are launched and have guidance systems on board to actually hit those and to be a lot more mobile and a lot more nimble.\par \par \uc2 Right now what makes it difficult is when you`re firing artillery by itself, a lot of the artillery pieces take a lot of time to set up, then you`ve got to get those artillery pieces move. The multiple launch rocket system is sort of the answer to artillery. So it`s the United States developing technologies that will allow us to knock out Russian artillery batteries, and that Stephanie is what they`re using, what the Russians are using to pound the cities into oblivion. So that can be a game changer.\par \par \uc2 But again, this is a long war, it`s going to draw out. You`ve got to hit the supply lines of Russia. And I think Ukrainians are doing that. They`re being very smart strategically about where they`re fighting and when, so not an easy choice to make. So kudos to Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: More weapons for Ukraine. How do you think Putin is going to respond to this?\par \par \uc2 BEARDSLEY: I mean, I think his response is all over the place, right? He`s tried to threaten the United States in the West early on, the incompetence of his militaries kind of shining through. You can bet that they`ve got other problems inside the Kremlin, inside the, you know, the Dumas`s, and it`s not likely that he can do much more than he`s doing now which is trying to press the Belarusians to get their equipment on order.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:06]\par \par \uc2 They don`t have a lot of resupply. They don`t have great morale. Their officer cadre is not well trained. Their junior officer cadre has not well trained. They don`t have an NCO Corps. Russia, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , they`re doing everything they can, but it`s just not working for them. They didn`t have the kind of military that would have made this a success.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It might not be working big picture. But day to day, they are still inflicting a lot of pain on Ukraine, the fighting going on and Severodonetsk, that`s now being described as moving block by block. The mayor there said the city is completely ruined. Is that similar to what we saw happen in Mariupol? Because that was devastating.\par \par \uc2 BEARDSLEY: Sure is, and the Russians do like that advance where they deep penetrate into a city or a target and cause mass destruction, they hope to break the will of the people. Here the Ukrainians have had to make some very difficult decisions, how to do tactical withdraws from Severodonetsk to maybe put pressure down south and Mykolaiv and Kherson. That`s going to cause the Russians to have to react to the south.\par \par \uc2 So it`s not the best play, but it`s one that`s available to the Ukrainians now. And honestly, when Russia if they take Severodonetsk, it does not afford them some strategic advantage beyond just sort of an emotional plea for the Eastern Donbas region.\par \par \uc2 So, this is really Russia gasping but as you said, Stephanie, there are brutalities of war, the last that every day it`s hard to endure, the people of Ukraine have been through a ton. So getting this kind of equipment in their hands as fast as possible, that`s the most expeditious way of getting out of this.\par \par \uc2 And, and again, you and I talked about this before, but the real answer is trying to get on the front side of these and not have to be seven, eight, nine, 10 weeks into a conflict where we`re defending our allies. That`s a lesson for folks looking at Taiwan, by the way.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, every time you join us, you are taking us to school, and we appreciate it. You make us smarter every time we see. Jason Beardsley, thank you. Coming up, tennis star Andy Murray, getting personal not about sport, but about surviving gun violence. His call for change when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:56:48]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: In this country, we`re no longer surprised to hear about a gunman shooting up a school. We`re still shocked. But we`re no longer surprised. But in Scotland, this kind of thing doesn`t happen, or it didn`t until this morning.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Parents ran through the streets of Dunblane, rushing to find out if their children were still alive. Shortly after school began Thomas Hamilton, a man described by neighbors as an unpopular loner, burst into the primary school`s gymnasium. He was caring for handguns. Students in a nearby classroom heard noises they described as cracks, teachers told them to hide under their desks.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, Andy Murray on the need for change. Back in 1996, 16 children and one teacher were killed in the Dunblane primary school shooting in Scotland. It remains the deadliest mass shooting in modern British history.\par \par \uc2 Soon after tough gun laws were enacted. And eventually gun violence decreased significantly.\par \par \uc2 British tennis star Andy Murray is actually a survivor of that horrific school shooting. He was only nine years old at the time. In an interview with the \uc2 BBC\uc2  earlier today, he said how frustrating it is to see these tragedies continue to play out here in the US.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ANDY MURRAY, BRITISH TENNIS STAR: I get over there. Like it`s always talked about it being something political, but I just -- I don`t see how it is like, I don`t think anyone wants to see things like that that happening. And my feeling is that like, surely at some stage, you do something different, like you can keep approaching, you know, the problem, you know, by buying more guns and having more guns in the country. Like, I don`t see how that solves it.\par \par \uc2 So, but I could be wrong, you know, but let`s maybe try something different and see if you get a different outcome. But yes, it`s obviously unbelievably sad and disappointing. And, you know, just makes you feel angry and incredibly upset for the parents.\par \par \uc2 And I heard something on the radio the other day, and it was a child from that school, you know, and I experienced a similar thing when I was at Dunblane and a teacher like, you know, coming out and waving all of the children, like under tables and tell them to go and hide.\par \par \uc2 And it was a kid like telling exactly the same story about how she survived. And they were saying that they go through these drills, like as young children as like seven, eight year old children. And it`s like, how, like, how is that normal that children should be having to go through drills like, in case someone comes into school with a gun? I don`t get it. Just -- yes, she really, really upsetting and hope they make some changes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It shouldn`t be normal, Andy Murray`s call for change being heard across the world tonight. And tonight, my take away is what Jason Beardsley just said a few minutes ago, our response to Ukraine, while big has been slow. We have been playing catch up. This should be a lesson for us for what`s ahead, come Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, I wish you all a very good night and from our colleagues across the networks of NBC News. Thank you for staying up late with us. I`ll see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220616ei5v0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 24, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Ellison Barber, Vaughn Hillyard, Garrett Haake, Marc  Caputo, Greg Bluestein, Pete Williams, Dasha Burns, Irwin Redlener \par \uc2 8,825 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: There`s the music. It`s a Tuesday, voters are voting in Georgia, Texas, Alabama, and Arkansas. It`s SEC primary day. Where`s Paul Finebaum? Both Parties, chart a course for the November midterms.\par \par \uc2 Plus, we`ve got the latest on the ground in battleground Pennsylvania as the Republican Senate Candidates battle for control of the vote count in a razor-tight race that is heading for a recount. The key deadline takes place today.\par \par \uc2 And breaking just last hour, NBC News confirms that the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has been investigating an apparent plot by an Ohio man claiming ties to ISIS and a plot that he developed to assassinate former President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . We`ve got all the details coming up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd. As I said, voters are voting in SEC country and there`s the music. That`s right. It is a special Meet the Midterms edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY. We`ve got an SEC slate today. Primary elections in Alabama, Arkansas, and Georgia as well as runoff elections in the newest SEC state these days, Texas as they now have two schools added to that conference.\par \par \uc2 First polls will close in less than six hours and it just means more today because Georgia has emerged as one of the top battlegrounds in the nation for November and beyond. Folks, it`s not North Carolina, it`s not Texas, and it isn`t Florida anymore. Georgia is now the swing state in the Deep South and the nominees selected today are going to matter a lot going forward.\par \par \uc2 At the top of the ticket, former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  put his name and his money behind former Senator David Perdue`s sputtering effort to dethrone Republican Governor Brian Kemp. Both Perdue and Trump hold Kemp responsible for their 2020 losses in Georgia, a position as asinine as it is untrue, and have made that lie the tentpole of their campaign.\par \par \uc2 Trump doubled down on his seemingly doomed endorsement for \uc2 Perdue\uc2  again today. So if Perdue goes down, it would be the third gubernatorial nod from the former president to fall short. Be careful trying to challenge sitting governors, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The winner there will take on the soon-to-be Democratic nominee \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  and what may be a rematch of the ultra- tight Kemp-Abrams race from 2018. Both Kemp and Abrams seem to be looking ahead to that showdown already.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. BRIAN KEMP, (R-GA): We`re in a fight for the soul of our state and that`s why this family has been getting up every single day to make sure that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  is not going to be our governor or the next president.\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS, GEORGIA GUBERNATORIAL RACE DEMOCRATIC CANDIDATE: He`s refused to help the Georgians who need him most at the time when they need him most. It`s because Brian Kemp doesn`t care. But I care about Georgia and I invite anyone who cares about Georgians to come down here and help me win.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fascinating to hear Brian Kemp drop the P-word when saying preventing \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  from becoming governor or president. I think that`s a hint is how he may try to rally skeptical Trump supporters to his side in a general election.\par \par \uc2 Also today in Georgia, we`re going to see how \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  performs in his effort to run away with the Republican Senate nomination to take on \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  this November. We know he`s going to win. How big will his -- will his margin be and will some of those negative hits start to take a toll?\par \par \uc2 We`re also following a member on -- member Democratic primary for a relatively safe seat that`s based in Gwinnett County, and the Trump-back primary against Secretary of State and chief elections officer of Georgia, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 . He rebuffed pressure from Trump to commit a crime and as allies in the aftermath of the 2020 elections. So out in the -- we`ll see what that goes.\par \par \uc2 And out in the SEC West, we`re following up pair of Republican Senate primaries. In Alabama, there`s the open primary to replace the retiring Senator, Rick Shelby, which will leave the state with a member much more friendly to the right-wing fringe, perhaps look Katie Britt more in the Shelby mindset of things.\par \par \uc2 And in Arkansas, there`s a big-money effort to try to unseat Republican Senator \uc2 John Boozman\uc2  from the right by a former football player from Arkansas, who played briefly with the \uc2 New England Patriots\uc2 , Jake Bequette. He`s gotten quite a bit of financial support from the outside. The primary pressure is happening, even as Boozman has the all-important Trump endorsement. That`s a runoff state folks.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: So he can win tonight but be under 50 and then the world will be following that race in Arkansas. And finally, the premier runoff in Texas where Blue Dog Democratic Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  may end up defeated by progressive Jessica Cisneros in a race that is featured in an \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid on Cuellar`s house, questions of electability, border policy, and as of late, has been supercharged by the issue of abortion, thanks to the leaked Supreme Court opinion. So grab a seat and get comfortable, we`ve got a few hours until kickoff. So settle in for this pregame show.\par \par \uc2 Ellison Barber is in Georgia. She`s covering all angles in the Peach State today for us. Vaughn Hillyard is in Alabama, Garrett Haake is in Laredo, Texas, and also with us is one of our National Political Reporters here at NBC News, Marc Caputo. He also is based in Georgia tonight. But let`s start with Ellison.\par \par \uc2 Look, in some ways, this is truly a pregame primary day for what is going to be a massive general election fight in November for both the Senate seat and the governor`s seat. You`ve been following \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  most closely today, Ellison, what are you seeing on the ground today? What`s turnout look like?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes. I mean, you just look at the polls when it comes to \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and you touched on it that really we`re wondering, not necessarily will \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  win the Republican primary tonight but by how much. And speaking with him, I can tell you he says he`s a competitor. He doesn`t just want to win. He wants to win big. And when you look at the most recent poll, he is 58 percent ahead of his closest competitor. He held a rally in Athens last night where he played football and is still very much well-known and well-loved.\par \par \uc2 And as he was speaking at his rally, he mentioned President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  a number of times criticizing him and his policies on everything from immigration to inflation. One name that was noticeably absent from his speech last night was Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , the candidate he is likely going to be facing once we get through election night. So he was voting about an hour ago, and I had a chance to ask him a few questions and one of them I wanted to ask him about was, why the focus was on Biden and not Warnock? Listen to what he told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: You mentioned President Biden quite a few times, but you didn`t mention Senator Warnock by name. Do you think you`re running against Biden or you running against Senator Warnock?\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2 , GEORGIA REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE: Oh, you know, I`m running against Warnock because he voted with Biden. Everyone knows that --\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Do you see a difference between the two of them?\par \par \uc2 WALKER: No, I don`t. I`m calling them what, little Biden because all the votes are just like he`s voting for Biden and that`s what`s so sad. And everyone sees that, but nobody wants to mention it. But I`m not afraid to mention it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: National Republicans have already started to try and tie Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  with President Biden. Biden has an approval rating that is declining in this state and has been for a while. I get the sense that Republicans here know that Senator Warnock is generally well-liked as a person in this state.\par \par \uc2 We even see it in the most recent ad that the National Republican Senatorial Committee put out where they started off basically saying, he`s a good guy, we know he`s inspiring, but his policy. So it seems like we`re seeing \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  take an approach where he is trying to really link Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and it was something that I certainly noticed that the big name that was absent from his speech was the person he will be competing against. Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s an -- Ellison, terrific reporting, terrific analysis. I couldn`t agree more on how you played it. There`s no doubt \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  needs this to be a referendum on Biden and not on himself, which is something I know the Republican operatives up here fear. Ellison, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Let`s move over to Alabama where, frankly, if you`re looking for action tonight on what`s competitive and truly unknown of what`s going to happen, it`s all in Alabama. Vaughn, I do it`s sort of one of those -- I know it`s not the headliner because November, it isn`t -- it isn`t going to be that interesting of a bowl game. But wow, to date`s primaries are pretty interesting.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: This state always finds a way to bring us back whether it be \uc2 Roy Moore\uc2 , \uc2 Doug Jones\uc2 , Jeff Sessions, Tommy Tuberville, and now you`re looking at the three-way primary here. If nobody gets 50 percent here tonight, this will also go to a runoff four weeks from now. And when you look at these three individuals, you`re looking at \uc2 Mike Durant\uc2 , Katie Britt, who is a former aide to retiring Republican Senator \uc2 Richard Shelby\uc2 , and then that third name, Congressman Mo Brooks, who we actually just spoke with just a few moments ago here.\par \par \uc2 And the reason why Mo Brooks is turning head here is because, two months ago, Chuck, he lost Donald Trump`s endorsement when he plummeted in polling. Well, look where he`s at now. He`s got a really good shot to be in the runoff here that will most likely take on Katie Britt. I want to let you hear his response to me when I asked him about Donald Trump`s influence today.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Has Donald Trump`s influence waned in the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 REP. MO BROOKS, (R-AL): No, I don`t think so. Not at all. But no one has 100 percent influence there are varying degrees of influence in different parts of the country. In the state of Alabama by way of example, in Senate races, he`s endorsed -- two out of three times his endorses have not done well. One out of three times they have done well. We tend to be a pretty independent group in the state of Alabama.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: And this is a situation here where we`re likely expecting to head to a runoff. And I also do want to note here when we`re talking about the changing landscape of Republican senators in the \uc2 U.S. Congress\uc2 , \uc2 Richard Shelby\uc2  was one of those Republican senators who did not object to the 2020 election results. As of last night, Katie Britt told -- tells me that she would have done just that. So that means the most likely -- unless there`s a crazy democratic upset that the next U.S. senator from Alabama is going to be a Trump ally and questioning whether they would ultimately certify this 2024 results.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Vaughn, we got a bit of late-breaking news last night. \uc2 Mike Durant\uc2  has already said it almost -- I don`t want to say he`s conceding anything, but he said if he doesn`t get into the runoff, he would endorse Brooks, which I think tells you the sort of how things have broken in this primaries. Is that right?\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Right. There`s millions of dollars that have been spent to take down Mo Brooks and then \uc2 Mike Durant\uc2 . And that is why you -- largely you have seen Durant`s numbers plummet, and all these campaigns are telling me here. You know, there`s the McConnell and Shelby aligned forces that are trying to prop up Katie Britt here and that is what grow the ire of \uc2 Mike Durant\uc2  and led him to tell us last night that he would back Mo Brooks if this runoff is between Britt and Brooks.\par \par \uc2 Now to note, I asked Brooks that exact question here just in the last hour and he told me that he believes it could be a Durant-Brooks run off and Britt could miss the whole runoff herself. And so he would not endorse Durant because he wouldn`t want to find himself in that situation in the next four weeks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So did not reciprocate just yet. Very fascinating. We didn`t get to the governor`s race, which is equally as fascinating, but for different reasons and we`ll get to that later. Vaughn Hillyard --\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: We`ll be here tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Exactly. There`s a reason you`re there today. So, Vaughn, thank you. Now let`s go to South Texas. That`s where we find our Senior Congressional Correspondent, Garrett Haake. And, Garrett, this is the -- probably the biggest Democratic race tonight, though I might argue the runoff for attorney general in your home state of Texas, both of the Republican side and the Democratic side could be fascinating down the road. But why you`re there, is this House race, what do you learning? We`re trying to find out can a pro-life Democrat still survive in Democratic primary elections?\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes. Chuck, look, this race is a little bit of something for everybody. It`s got this idea of an abortion question, which is really energized. Jessica Cisneros, the challengers, followers that have energized her fundraising in the last couple of weeks.\par \par \uc2 But just from being down here of the last day, I don`t get the sense on the ground here in Laredo that is the issue going into the polling booth that is on the minds of voters. And it wasn`t exactly the runaway issue when I asked Jessica Cisneros about how she sees this race shaping up tonight. Listen to what she told me when I talked about the issue set that`s going to decide this race.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CISNEROS, TEXAS DEMOCRATIC HOUSE CANDIDATE: I think the issues are the same. You know, we`ve been running on health care that includes abortion care, we`ve been running on good-paying jobs here in the district, investment, and infrastructure here. But I do think that the sense of urgency has escalated in many ways and I do think that you know, like, for example, choice like is something that in the last few weeks, the urgency has just been escalated so much.\par \par \uc2 When we`re talking out there to voters, they want to know what are you going to do to make sure that I have a good-paying job to be able to provide for my family, that`s the number one concern. And two, you know, how are you going to ensure that I`m healthy enough to be able to provide for my family?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Chuck, health care, healthcare, healthcare, we`ve heard it from the mouths of every Democratic candidate going back, you know, really prior to the 2018 midterms that have been the key issue in so many of these races. But down here, we also have the specter of this looming \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation that Cuellar was involved in. He says -- his attorney says he`s not a target of it but the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has not said that.\par \par \uc2 And they raided his home and his office that has come up with voters that I`ve talked to. And also just the question of how long might be too long for a member of Congress. Cuellar has been here for more than 15 years.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: He is really associated with Laredo. He is an institution that works well for him in some cases. I`ve heard voters say they like that this is a guy who`s on the Appropriations Committee. He`s sending home money, he gets the district. But also perhaps time for a change. You know, Cisneros is out today. She`s not indoors. She was at this polling place. She has been basically everywhere. \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , no public events today. His campaign`s not holding an event tonight.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I don`t know how to read into that exactly, but I think it`s noteworthy that we`re not seeing him out here trying to really kind of close this thing out on the race`s last day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Garrett, very quickly, just in your experience in Texas, runoff turnouts, we know they`re lower than primary turnout, how much lower?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Substantially lower, Chuck. And it`s even more challenging now because you`ve got a new district here, this -- the redistricting plays into this as well.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: There`s this significant additional amount of Bexar County that`s the San Antonio suburbs in San Antonio City proper that we`ll be seeing both of these names on their ballot for the first time. The 28th District hasn`t had a runoff in modern memory. Just educating voters to know that they`re not done with this primary, there`s a challenge for both of these candidates.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. That`s why you would assume those with the deepest support, not necessarily the broadest might have the advantage today and all these runoffs in Texas. Garrett Haake, on the ground for us in Laredo, Garrett, thank you. Let me bring in Marc Caputo. Marc, let`s go a little big picture here. You spend a lot of time covering the sort of where the Republican Party`s relationship is with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on a given day.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be -- we already know this is at best a mixed bag rich set of results tonight for the former president, and at worst, a really sort of punch in the gut. I mean, he could have one guy he did endorse lose badly. The Secretary of State could lose that race. And then a guy he wants to endorse and then unendorsed gets into a runoff, essentially without his help. Are the Trump folks braced for this bad night?\par \par \uc2 MARC CAPUTO, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Oh, yes, sure. They`re not going to describe it as a bad night. As you know, with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , when he loses, he said he won and when he wins, he said he won. So in this case, Perdue who is more than likely going to lose to Governor Kemp is going to lose big and you`re not going to really hear Trump talk about that much. You might reference it.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, it`s going to be a \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  night for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He`s going to talk about how we recruited him to run for the Senate seat and how we won big and that`s what you`re going to hear about. There are the down- ballot races, the more interesting one is a secretary of state one, I should say, perhaps the more interesting one will be that one.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Correct.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Because it looks like \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  is going to do well. And if you would have asked us in 2021 how the former -- pardon me, the current Secretary of State would do. We would have told you well, he`s about to be the former Secretary of State.\par \par \uc2 Now, in his race against \uc2 Jody Hice\uc2 , it looks like he`s doing well according to polling. Most people expect it`s going to go to a runoff but some insiders here -- Republican insiders say it wouldn`t surprise them if Raffensperger somehow sneaked out a win and got above 50 percent. That`s probably less likely, but we`re going to keep an eye on it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Marc, you know, you`ve been reporting that there`s a -- that on one hand, they`ve been disappointed in Perdue`s campaign. We know the former president never takes any of the blame when this looked like an unwinnable race when he recruited Perdue, and it -- and it became more unwinnable as Perdue executed his campaign. But what does this mean going forward? Are they going to really limit their endorsements? I mean, are they going to stay out of the Alabama runoffs? Are they going 