uc2 I got an important programming note for you. My colleague, Kristen Welker, and I will be hosting another Meet the Press election night special. We cover the results all night long in all the states that are holding major primaries and runouts tonight. You can catch that special tonight starting at 8 p.m. Eastern. I think we have no race on a turn. It`ll stream on NBC News Now.\par \par \uc2 Up next, more election analysis as we dig into what tonight`s potential winners could mean for each party`s performance in November and beyond. And later, we got some new details from an accidentally unsealed court document that alleges a plot to try to assassinate former President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 . The suspect is in custody. The former president`s safety was apparently never threatened. We`re going to have the very latest ahead from our own Pete Williams. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back to our midterm primary date coverage. It`s SEC Tuesday. We just checked in with reporters on the ground for races in Georgia, Texas, and Alabama. Now we`re going to go take a deeper dive into all of those races. And we can`t forget Arkansas as well in what tonight`s potential winners could mean for each party as they face down the general election midterms in November.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to bring in a couple of smart people to dig deeper today. Joining me now, Jessica Taylor, she`s the Senate and Governor`s Editor at The Cook Political Report, and our Georgia politics guru, Greg Bluestein. He`s a political reporter at The Atlanta Journal-Constitution and an MSNBC contributor.\par \par \uc2 So let me start with Georgia, Greg, and just where we`re headed here. I want to spend more time previewing the generals that I do want to talk about tonight since we kind of have an idea. So, Abrams-Kemp, and I want to jump on a comment Kemp said last night, in which he talked about governor and president. And it looks to me -- and you tell me, but it looks like the Kemp world knows it`s got to get bring over whatever grumpy Trump supporters there are. And the threat of Stacey Abrams`s president may be what Brian Kemp wants to use. I assume that wasn`t an accident what we heard from him last night.\par \par \uc2 GREG BLUESTEIN, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: You`re exactly right. That wasn`t an accident at all. If there has been sort of a core central theme to Brian Kemp`s campaign, it`s that he and only he can beat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  because he`s the only Republican who`s ever beat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , he -- you know, suddenly reminds folks he couldn`t even be \uc2 Jon Ossoff\uc2  last year. So that`s his argument.\par \par \uc2 They`re hoping that hatred for \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  will overcome any sort of Maga backlash that Brian Kemp could face in the general election phase. It`s a big bet, but it is one that Republicans have kind of seated the ground for years. I mean, you know, folks running for dogcatcher for county commissioner in 2019 and 2020, would be railing against \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 . So the Republicans have turned her into this arch-villain that Brian Kemp hopes will motivate a unity sort of faction alliance against \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  in the general.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, Greg, what is the Abrams plan to sort of de-nationalize things? I mean, I do think it is deadly for a gubernatorial challenger to get caught looking national, too much. So what is -- what is her way of going local?\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: Yes. She was asked about whether or not she would welcome \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  -- President Biden into Georgia to campaign for and she said, of course, I would because he`s delivered billions of dollars in funding for Georgia over the last few years. But look, she`s going to focus on Georgia issues.\par \par \uc2 She`s going to focus on the high rate of incarceration, gun measures that rollback, gun restrictions, and localized issues without trying to play nationally because she knows also that Brian Kemp is going to -- every time she talks nationally, Brian Kemp is going to say she just wants to use Georgia as a stepping stone to the White House. That`s another -- that`s another attack line that has been used against her for years here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jessica Taylor, I want to move to the Senate race a little bit and insert Mitch McConnell`s role. And I want to group sort of both Senate races in the primaries tonight in Georgia and in Alabama. What`s interesting here is Trump and Walker -- excuse me, Trump and McConnell on getting on the same page in Georgia, and they weren`t nine months ago. McConnell tried desperately to get anybody but Herschel.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TAYLOR, SENATE & GOVERNOR`S EDITOR, COOK POLITICAL REPORT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And he did -- and he failed. But it does look like \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is at least using the McConnell talking points, which is don`t trash Warnock, just run against Biden.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Yes. In fact, you know, the clip I know you all showed earlier, it sounds like he`s running against Biden instead of Warnock. I mean, I think that clearly there is baggage that Walker has, and his opponent -- his Republican primary opponents have tried to exploit that, but they haven`t had any money to do so. Democrats are not going to suffer from that problem. So, I mean, this is going to be one of the most expensive races there. We`ll see whether it brings him down. I still think you know -- he`s a star in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: He`s a football legend. Heisman Trophy, winner the last time before this past year that they won the national championship. I think that still matters and just the environment, you know, Warnock is trying to run his -- he -- ads sort of separating himself from Biden in some of their policies, too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, I think about the four nominees, right? You have Warnock, Walker, both kinds of national figures now, you`ve Abrams national figure, Brian Kemp is the least national of the four big names. I want to move to Alabama here because \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  seems to be engaged in a primary that`s more concerned about a vote for Senate majority leader that it is about actually, you know, a Republican is going to be a United States senator from Alabama and the seat.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The two of them could end up being votes for somebody not named McConnell as the leader. One, Katie Britt, obviously would be. Is that what makes this interesting to follow nationally?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: It really is just what type of Republicans are going to be elected? If they`re more, you know, on the Ted Cruz, \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 , McConnell could be majority leader again, but he`s got a very unruly caucus. And that could happen with -- in Missouri if \uc2 Eric Greitens\uc2  wins. That can happen, you know, now that Ohio is over with J.D. Vance. So he can have some problems there. But, I mean, Britt is also -- I mean, she`s -- and this is what our opponents have attacked her for. She`s a Washington insider, she served as Shelby`s Chief of Staff, you know, to Trump --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They know her. McConnell know her --\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Knows her trust so we get why McConnell`s for.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Right. And I -- you know, very much I`ve talked -- sources I`ve talked to believe, especially with a party that`s lacking for women superstars, she could really be -- you know, they believe a really great woman there in the Senate for them as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Greg, let`s go back to Georgia today, massive turnout, and a lot of Republicans, at least on social media are wanting Democrats to eat crow over the new election law. Play a -- play truth detector here for us on this.\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: Yes, it`s a little more nuanced than that, especially because Georgia`s new voter overhaul mostly deals with mail-in ballots and not early in-person votes. And that`s where you`re seeing the surge. We won`t know for at least a week, maybe longer after we get the final details of mail-in ballots, but how much that -- how much the changes actually affected, likely Democratic and Republican voters.\par \par \uc2 But we have indeed seen a surge in early in-person voting and that could be because we`re seeing a migration away from Republicans voting by mail towards voting in person. And also, of course, one other reason is because there`s marquee matchups at the top of the GOP ticket in a way that there just isn`t for the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very quickly. What other primaries are you watching the night below sort of our headliners here? Obviously, Secretary of State, I count that as one of the headliners. What else are you watching, Greg? Inform me.\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: I`m watching to see how the Trump-endorsed candidates for these down-ticket races that will never hardly get a mention the AJC like insurance commissioner, like Attorney General, I`m watching how they fare because the former president put his name and brand behind them as well and we`ll see how they go up against entrenched incumbents.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Jessica, I -- my pet race of the day is on Arkansas.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:02]\par \par \uc2 JESSICA TAYLOR, COOK POLITICAL REPORT SENATE AND GOVERNORS EDITOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Because it`s one of those, \uc2 John Boozman\uc2 , Republican senator, we`ve all thought he was potentially vulnerable to a primary challenge from the right, and this Jake Bequette has gotten a ton of outside money help mostly from the Club for Growth and Richard Uihlein.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: And Richard Uihlein.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Who`s a Club -- who basically funds most of Club for Growth.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Although I don`t think the club is technically involved here. The chances that Boozman -- he`s going to be leading vote getter tonight. Chances he`s held under 50?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Maybe 30 percent. I still think --\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s pretty high. I mean, it`s not like a total out there.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Yes. I mean, I think he ends up somewhere, you know, 55 or so perhaps. I mean, he`s had to work for this and their ads show that they are taking it seriously. You know, he`s run ads with \uc2 Sarah Huckabee Sanders\uc2  that is going to be the next governor of Arkansas with Tom Cotton. But, yes, Boozman is not a flashy member of the Senate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And if he gets drawn into a runoff, it might be hard to find a path to victory.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: It would certainly -- I mean, the generational differences, I mean, Bequette is in his early 30s. Well, actually they both play for \uc2 University of Arkansas\uc2  football. We`ve got some SEC football players out there, too.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and I should give due where it`s due. The first reference test Tuesday was in Cook Political Report.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: From Jessica Taylor who is an SEC --\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: SEC super fan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: SEC super fan. Anyway, Jessica, Greg, thank you both. We`ll see you both a lot going forward.\par \par \uc2 And again, for all your primary updates, be sure to stay tuned to our MEET THE PRESS election special tonight. Like I said, we`re leaving no election unturned. In Arkansas, Texas, telling the Dem AG runoffs and the Republican AG runoffs matter in Texas. All of it will be streaming tonight on NBC News Now.\par \par \uc2 Up next, what we now know about an \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation into an alleged plot by a man with ISIS ties to attempt an assassination against former President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:32]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We`ve been following some breaking news here the last couple of hours after some dramatic court documents were accidentally unsealed. It involved an \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation into an apparent plot to assassinate former President \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Here`s our Justice Correspondent Pete Williams. He joins me now.\par \par \uc2 And Pete, again, well, let`s just start with the details. How did we learn this and how serious was and is the threat?\par \par \uc2 PETE WILLIAMS, NBC NEWS JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT: Well, we learned about it because "Forbes" Web site published an excerpt from a search warrant affidavit that was mistakenly unsealed and then immediately sealed earlier today, that first disclosed the evidence of this investigation.\par \par \uc2 How much of a threat was there to the president? I`d say the answer is zero because the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was constantly all over this man, who is appearing in federal court I think in just a few minutes in Columbus, Ohio, where he lives. He is from Iraq. His name is Shihab Ahmed Shihab Shihab. He entered the United States according to court documents in September 2020.\par \par \uc2 And Chuck, what`s apparent from reading the court documents we`ve seen so far is that the \uc2 FBI\uc2 `s initial interest in him was as somebody who was trying to smuggle people into the U.S. illegally. Not necessarily terrorists, just people he wanted to try to get into the U.S. from Iraq and he at one point talked to confidential informants, there were two of them very active in this case. Two informants.\par \par \uc2 He at one point said he would charge as much as $40,000 to get Iraqis who were not otherwise eligible to come into the U.S. into the U.S. illegally. And then as time went by according to the court documents, this man started to brag to the \uc2 FBI\uc2  undercovers that he had ISIS connections. That he was from the same clan as Al-Baghdadi, the ISIS leader, that he was involved in transporting explosives into vehicles in Iraq that he says were used to kill American soldiers there by blowing up those vehicles.\par \par \uc2 And then earlier this year, he started talking about wanting to bring in as many as six people from Iraq who he said were in a cell of ISIS that translates as the thunder, who wanted to assassinate the former president. And at one point, he actually flew down to Dallas. I think the \uc2 FBI\uc2  actually paid for that trip. He didn`t know it. The confidential informant picked him up and they drove to the former president`s residence where this man according to the court documents made videos with his cell phone.\par \par \uc2 And that`s about as far as it goes, Chuck. So I think it`s important to point out that obviously what the \uc2 FBI\uc2  says is that he was quite serious about this plot. But then he never actually had any means to carry it out. He never had weapons himself. There`s no indication that the people he was trying to smuggle into the U.S. ever got here.\par \par \uc2 Now you can well ask well, what would have happened if the \uc2 FBI\uc2  hadn`t stopped him. Would he ever been able to do any of these things? And of course that`s always an unanswerable question. But the \uc2 FBI\uc2  believes that he had pretty strong intent here. So he`s going to appear in federal court. He`ll face a whole wrath of federal charges this afternoon and then at that point, I would think more court documents will be unsealed. But he is in custody now and he`ll be appearing in court shortly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Pete, this seems to follow a pattern where I feel like you and I have done a bunch of these where there`s somebody the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has under surveillance for quite some time and they, you know, watch them as they commit more and more crimes for potentially some larger conspiracy. Maybe it`s a terrorist attack, in this case an assassination plot. Do we have an idea of how long the \uc2 FBI\uc2  was essentially surveilling-slash-working with this person?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Yes, it all began in April of 2021. He gets here in September of 2020 and then starting in April of 2021 is when he began meeting with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  undercover. So it`s been months that they have been keeping an eye on him. They`ve been meeting with him, they`ve been recording meetings with these confidential informants, monitoring his cell phones.\par \par \uc2 So I think it`s again important to say there was never any actual danger that anything was going to happen to the former president, but it does say a lot about what this man was thinking according to the bureau.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Also an interesting way we deal with international terrorism threats these days versus the conversation we have been having domestically as well.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Pete Williams on the justice beat for us. Pete, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, one week later, we may finally be inching closer to figuring out who has the true upper hand in that neck-and-neck Pennsylvania Republican Senate race as today marks the final day for counties to turn in their ballots to the states. Period.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:09]\par \par \uc2 We`re live in Philly with more. Are there more ballots to be counted? We`re going to try to answer that question after the break.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we turn our attention to today`s primary, there`s still some unfinished business from last week`s primaries. Specifically the Republicans that are in primary in Pennsylvania. Senate candidate David McCormick filed a lawsuit to get the undated mail-in ballots counted in this race against Mehmet Oz. McCormick trails Oz by nearly a thousand vote, but it`s close enough to trigger the automatic recount. That we know.\par \par \uc2 Pennsylvania secretary of state has until Thursday to make that determination. But today is the deadline for counties to send the results of their counts to the secretary of state. It all sets up for a potentially protracted legal fight in one of the most closely watched Senate races in the country as Republicans look to maintain a seat being vacated by the retiring \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dasha Burns who`s basically had to move to Pennsylvania to cover this year`s race joins me now from Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 So, Dasha, I know this is difficult to ascertain. But do we have a feeling that the vote count -- how many votes -- new votes do we expect counted today before the deadline? And then whatever we see today, the only other new additional votes are those votes under dispute with the courts. And is that it?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: OK, Chuck, we have got a bit of a confusing mess on our hands here. This is one of the most high stakes races in the midterms and it is stuck in a fight over paperwork right now essentially. So I`m going to ask you and our viewers to bear with me here because we`ve got two sort of separate but related things happening right now.\par \par \uc2 On the one hand, we are definitely heading towards a recount here. We know this is likely going to happen. Today is the deadline for unofficial results from the counties. Chuck, that doesn`t necessarily mean there are not more votes to be counted.\par \par \uc2 [10:45:03]\par \par \uc2 There are probably some provisional ballots left. Some challenge ballots left that won`t necessarily make it into today`s count. But what we`re going to get today, these results essentially trigger the automatic recount process. The secretary of state can look and say, OK, it`s under a thousand votes. That falls within that .5 percent difference that triggers the automatic recount.\par \par \uc2 Here we go. On the other hand, we have the lawsuit filed by the McCormick campaign to get those undated mail-in ballots counted. This lawsuit, Chuck, is based in a court ruling issued on Friday that actually has nothing to do with this election on its face. It`s about an election from last fall, November 2021, a county judge`s race. The court ruled that mail-in ballots that don`t have a date written on them by the voter, that they should be counted.\par \par \uc2 The McCormick campaign is arguing that that should apply to this race as well. The Oz team does not agree with this. In fact, they say that McCormick is taking from, quote, "the Democrats` playbook" here.\par \par \uc2 Also of note, the Pennsylvania Republican Party siding with Oz on this. The \uc2 RNC\uc2 , Republican National Convention, also siding with Oz on this. And the secretary of state has issued guidance just this morning telling counties to set those ballot aside, segregate them for now until we have some sort of result from this legal battle.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Counties, though, could still have their own votes, make their own sort of decision on how they want to deal with this. And voters trying to make sense of what`s going on here are probably losing their minds just like I am right now, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dasha, you did a terrific job. The bottom line, it sounds like this. Without those extra ballots, McCormick probably comes up short. Is that the bottom line?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The bottom line is any recount that has happened in the history of Pennsylvania, there have been six automatically triggered recounts. Three of them have been declined by the losing candidate, which, by the way, McCormick could do if he`s trailing by the end of today. Those three that have happened, none of them have changed the results. If this comes down to maybe 200 ballots or so difference, maybe, maybe we could see a recount, have an impact here.\par \par \uc2 But if it`s closer to 1,000 and all likelihood from the experts I have been talking Oz comes away with it in the end and taxpayers spend, you know, over $1 million, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. And we don`t know whether the next kind of recount, a hand recount, might be something the campaign would have to pay for. That could drag it out even further. Let`s see what we learned tonight.\par \par \uc2 Dasha, the never-ending Republican Senate primary still too close to call. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we have an update on some humanitarian efforts now underway in Ukraine three months after the Russians invaded.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY. `\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:38]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. It has now been three months since Russia began its brutal invasion of Ukraine. According to Ukraine`s armed forces Russian forces continue their offensive in the east. They`re intensifying their efforts to encircle two key cities in the Donbas region.\par \par \uc2 In his daily video address, President Zelenskyy warned of the difficult weeks ahead and said, quote, "No one has destroyed Donbas in the way that the Russian troops are doing now."\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a \uc2 UNICEF\uc2  report released this week estimates that 15.7 million people in Ukraine are in need of assistance, including three million children. This morning while meeting with leaders of Australia, India and Japan, President Biden called the humanitarian crisis a global issue and accused Russia of trying to extinguish Ukraine`s culture.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now on the phone from Lviv is an NBC News contributor. It`s Dr. Irwin Redlener. He is and his wife founded the Ukraine Children Action Program. It`s an initiative to provide mental health and educational support to children impacted by the war. You may recognize Dr. Redlener from our non-stop COVID coverage particularly when it comes to children and the impact of that virus.\par \par \uc2 But you`ve been in Lviv. You were there I know doing some work with the Polish government, Dr. Redlener. Just put it in your words what you`ve seen and underscore the displacement and the impact.\par \par \uc2 DR. IRWIN REDLENER, NBC NEWS CONTRIBUTOR: Yes. So, hi, Chuck. So, first of all, it should be just emphasized that the brutality of this invasion by Putin of Ukraine. It been unbelievable. Everything else is sort of secondary. But we do have a major unprecedented at least since World War II humanitarian crisis having to deal with people that are displaced from their homes. So right now Ukraine which has 42 million has got at least somewhere between 14 million and 16 million of its population that are either moved out of eastern Ukraine into western Ukraine or they moved out of the country entirely.\par \par \uc2 But in either case, we have many, many people with no real place to go or necessarily even know where they`re going to call home permanently. And I`ll tell you this, this is a, you know, situation where children are having a very, very serious future ahead of them because many of those children have been profoundly traumatized. They`re getting up, they`re being woken up in the middle of the night with missile explosions on their apartment buildings or in their communities, and their parents are getting them out as fast as they can.\par \par \uc2 Not every child survived but those that do are ending up in Poland, where we were last month, or somewhere in the western part of Ukraine without their materials, without their school connections, without their social connection. And it`s really, I am really worried about where this is going as far as the children are concerned.\par \par \uc2 But you know, you think about how many people are displaced. It`d be the equivalent of 110 million Americans, either leaving the country or having to leave their communities because that`s roughly the percentage that has been displaced by this war. So we got rough roads ahead, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 REDLENER: And this is why we really want to focus on how these kids are going to be helped.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m curious. You`ve seen -- you said you were in Poland, can they handle all this incoming? And what more help could they need to handle it?\par \par \uc2 REDLENER: Yes. So Poland has taken in close to four million refugees and the city of Warsaw, its population has increased from refugees between 15 percent and 20 percent.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:05]\par \par \uc2 Their school systems are overwhelmed. They don`t have enough Polish, I mean Ukrainian-speaking therapists for the children. People have been traumatized and there`s a lot that could be done. One of them is beefing up the ability of children to learn remotely, you know, not necessarily in classrooms. They only have about 20,000 of the 150,000 children who got to Poland, who are actually in classrooms.\par \par \uc2 So where are the rest of them going to school? And where are they getting services? And that`s a big challenge. And that`s really the (INAUDIBLE), this foundation that Karen and I set up, and this is what we`re going to be focused on. I think there`s a lot more that can be done.\par \par \uc2 I`ll tell you the one thing I heard from everybody in Ukraine, including kids, was a fear of the world forgetting about them. The world would forget about them if we didn`t keep up this pressure. And we can`t forget about these children and families. It`s just a horror story.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look, I know what a passion -- when you think about kids and whether it`s on health outcomes in this country or what you started up in Ukraine. I know this has always been a goal and a focus for your work.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Redlener, I appreciate you sharing a few minutes with us and I hope folks have a minute to check out the foundation that you helped co-found.\par \par \uc2 Thank you all for being with us this hour. Don`t forget to tune in to tonight`s election coverage. Our MEET THE PRESS primary night election special. It starts at 8:00 p.m. Eastern, streaming on NBC News Now which you can find anywhere you want.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC coverage continues with Katy Tur after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220525ei5o00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 23, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Blayne Alexander, Vaughn Hillyard, Mike Memoli \par \uc2 9,235 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: Brief reporters about the U.S. military strategy in Europe and beyond. And later, what`s going on with monkeypox? President Biden says everybody should be concerned about the latest outbreak. I`ll speak with the top White House National Security Official on biosecurity coming up.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Garrett Haake in for Chuck Todd. And another week means another big election night is almost here with primaries in Alabama, Arkansas, and above all, Georgia in addition to runoff elections in Texas tomorrow night. The big spotlight of the night will be on Georgia.\par \par \uc2 It`s a key battleground for Senate control in the midterms and a hugely important presidential battleground in 2024. And it has also been ground zero for President Trump`s lies about a stolen election. More on how that has shaken up these races in a minute.\par \par \uc2 But let`s start in my home state of Texas where the issue of abortion has become a key focus in a closely watched Democratic runoff. Back in March, neither incumbent Blue Dog Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  nor \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 - backed Jessica Cisneros could crack the 50 percent threshold in a race that also featured an \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid on Cuellar`s house and questions of electability and border policy.\par \par \uc2 But since then, Cisneros has tried to seize on the attention surrounding the Supreme Court`s draft opinion overturning Roe, putting the issue front and center against the self-described pro-life Cuellar. This is how Cisneros made her pitch to Chuck earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CISNEROS, DEMOCRATIC HOUSE CANDIDATE, TEXAS: In this moment, we are watching the fall of Roe and the erosion of our fundamental rights. And this moment we truly -- I mean, it necessitates a champion that`s going to fight for our people`s rights in this district. There`s so many key issues where he`s always sitting with Republicans, and he could become the Joe Manchin of the House. We don`t want \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  to be the deciding vote on the future of our fundamental freedoms and rights in this country. We just can`t risk that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Whether that single issue is enough to tip the scales in this Democratic race will be closely watched by Democrats around the country. And then moving to Georgia where a single issue has and really still is dominating the Republican primary for governor as former Senator \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , backed by former President Trump is challenging incumbent Republican Governor Brian Kemp on the issue of the president`s big lie that the 2020 election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 As you probably remember, Kemp and his Secretary of State refused to yield to pressure from Trump and his allies to overturn the 2020 results. And like Trump, Perdue blames Kemp for Republicans` 2020 losses in Georgia, including Perdue`s own Senate seat loss. Trump has sunk in $2.6 billion on behalf of \uc2 Perdue\uc2  but polls have camped above the 50 percent runoff threshold, perhaps showing just how hard it really is to defeat an incumbent governor.\par \par \uc2 But even if Perdue falls short, his share of the vote is going to tell us something really interesting. It`ll tell us how much of the GOP in Georgia cares about Trump`s election lies above basically everything else. And if you look at the results last week in Pennsylvania and the Republican front runners and other states like in Arizona, you see a pretty clear emerging portrait of a Republican Party and its embrace of the former president`s attacks on our elections.\par \par \uc2 We`ll get back to the runoffs in Texas in a minute but let`s stay in Georgia now with NBC`s Blayne Alexander and Vaughn Hillyard, both are on the ground in Atlanta with the latest on these races. So, Blayne, we`ve got dueling primary night or primary eve events tonight with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  stumping for Kemp and Trump holding a tele-rally basically dialing into an event for Perdue. What are their closing messages going to be here and how`s it looking for Kemp, we mentioned he was above 50 percent of the runoff threshold in the most recent polling?\par \par \uc2 BLAYNE ALEXANDER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Garrett, you know, unless the polling is just completely wrong, it does look like Kemp is on his way to a decisive victory tomorrow night. The latest polling shows him with 30 plus point lead over \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 . Now, as for what we`re going to hear from them, I think what`s notable is what we`re not going to hear from Governor Kemp, and that`s any sort of criticism of President Trump.\par \par \uc2 It`s something that he`s been really kind of doing all along the way. He`s not been talking much about Trump, he`s not talked about 2020, and he underscored that point today in a call with reporters essentially saying that he`s not going to criticize the former president. He`s just going to keep doing what he`s been doing all along, which is running on his record.\par \par \uc2 Now, that is a vastly different approach, of course, than what we`ve seen from former Senator \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , who has really pinned his entire campaign on 2020 and trying to cast Governor Kemp as the enemy, the villain that comes out of 2020. Now, I do think what we`ll hear from both is going to be this kind of shared message that both of them are going to be the best candidate to beat \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  in November, but from Kemp, it already looks as though he`s kind of looking past tomorrow and head to November, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Vaughn, for \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , it wasn`t supposed to be this way. I mean, this is a former senator. He was recruited by former President Trump. He had the backing of the Trump folks here. And now these recent polls have been down 30 some odd points. What happened?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: You know, he even acknowledged to me this the other day saying "we may lose on Tuesday," but he then went on to note, I can -- hold on a second, Garrett, well, was the way that he said this. I can get a damn guarantee that we will not lose by 30 points. It was an odd phrasing from the former U.S. Senator here who stands to lose two -- lose two different races in the course of 15 months. What went wrong?\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, there was a candidate that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  picked to run in this race who was not willing to put much effort into it here. You know, they are not even on the TV airwaves. This is a man who has self-worth of millions of dollars yet is put in only a few 100,000 of those on himself here into a race. This entire last week, his campaign has not been on TV airwaves here.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s important to know when we look at that Fox News poll, it suggested \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , it`s somewhere around 30 percent. That would go to suggest that there is that loyal base of Trump support in this Republican Party that we have seen in other races over the course of this last month. One of those was over in Nebraska, the governor`s race there, when the individual who is running, Herbster, who was running for the governor`s seat in that primary, he walked away with 30 percent of the vote and he was backed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . But what it does show is that there is a larger portion of the Republican electorate not only here, but across the country that are willing to move beyond 2020 and ultimately vote for a Republican candidate who runs on issues beyond the certification of the election.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And, Blayne, you kind of alluded to this here but whoever comes out of this, Kemp or Perdue, they`ve got \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , who they`ll be facing off with in November, very expensive, very high profile race, seems like some Republicans are already kind of looking ahead to that prospect and starting to target Abrams, who`s probably the Democrat, Republicans most want to defeat I think in a governor`s race this year.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: You know what, Garrett? It`s something that we`ve been hearing all along in this campaign. Her name was brought up some 20 times during the first debate and she wasn`t even on the stage. And I think that we`re already kind of getting a preview of what we`re going to see looking ahead to November in these months running up to November.\par \par \uc2 Here`s something I want to play a clip from you have a speech that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  gave in a democratic fundraiser over the weekend, that`s getting a lot of attention from Republicans in it. She says that Georgia is the worst state in the country to live, before going on to list a number of quality of life issues like maternal mortality and incarceration. Here`s a little bit of her remarks and then we`ll talk more on the other side. Take a look.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STACEY ABRAMS, DEMOCRATIC GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE, GEORGIA: I am tired of hearing about being the best state in the country to do business when we are the worst state in the country to live. When you`re number 48 for mental health, when you`re number one for maternal mortality when you have an incarceration rate that`s on the rise and wages that are on the decline, then you are not the number one place to do but -- to live in the United States. But we can get there (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Now, Garrett, as you can well imagine, Governor Kemp immediately seized on that, and tweeted, essentially saying that he believes that Georgia is the best state to live, work, and do business and he wants to fight to keep it that way. And I talked with his team and they said that he will very likely mention that during that rally tonight on stage with the former vice president. Now, Abrams` team has pointed me back to her tweets where essentially she doubled down on that kind of fleshed out those stats a little bit more and said in her words. Governor Kemp doesn`t care. But when I`m Governor, I will fight to make sure that all Georgians essentially have access to the best quality of life, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. Blayne and Vaughn, thank you both. And joining me now on set, we`ve got Abby Livingston, Washington bureau chief of The Texas Tribune, Republican strategist Matt Gorman, and Navin Nayak, President of the \uc2 Center of American Progress\uc2  -- for \uc2 American Progress Action Fund\uc2 . Abby, I`ll start with you. We`ve got Trump election lies on the right, we`ve got this abortion issue coming to front and center on the left in the runoff down in Texas, what are we going to learn about the salience of these two issues come Wednesday morning?\par \par \uc2 ABBY LIVINGSTON, WASHINGTON BUREAU CHIEF, TEXAS TRIBUNE: I think the thing I`m most interested in is particularly on in the governor`s race in Georgia, and in the South Texas, Cuellar race, do the benefits of incumbency. These are both two men who`ve been on the ground engaged with their voters. Typically, that candidate wins when challenged in a primary, but are the forces of politics, the changes that we`re seeing in real-time going to overwhelm those historical patterns?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Matt, for you now a guy who`s basically all the still valid guys in the party for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , he seems to have swung at a pitch in the dirt here with Perdue, right? He`s putting money into this campaign but hasn`t gone anywhere. Are we going to see the limits of what -- you know, this single issue for Republican voters is or where does this -- where does this race go?\par \par \uc2 MATT GORMAN, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: It seems to be that way. I mean, even Trump has backed away as Perdue has kind of struggled a little bit. And you`re right, there`s a 30 percent or so based on that party --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is it about Perdue struggling or is that about this issue, lacking the salience that -- with voters that it has with Trump?\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: I think it`s Perdue struggling. However, what I`m watching right now is the Raffensperger-Hice race, right, your Secretary of State. It`s gotten under the radar, but again, it`s just pointing to the same issue right? Raffensperger and Kemp are very aligned with that. So Hice has, you know, really aligned himself with that Perdue and Trump line. I don`t know if there`s going to be a lot of Kemp-Hice voters, but I`m curious to see if those margins match up.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: If those incumbents hold on, what does Trump do in the general? I mean, he`s basically said he almost would prefer \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  to Brian Kemp.\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: And you saw a loud chorus of Republicans really chastised Trump for that. And look, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  going to hopefully unify the Republican Party. However, I will say right, if she cannot win in 2018, in a historic democratic year, it`s going to be a lot tougher this time around.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Maybe that is the challenge, right?\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: 2018 was a great year for Democrats. She came to a point short. I don`t think anybody in politics thinks 2022 has the outlines of a great year for Democrats. What does that race look like for her in the fall and how realistic are her chances?\par \par \uc2 NAVIN NAYAK, PRESIDENT, \uc2 CENTER FOR AMERICAN PROGRESS\uc2  ACTION FUND: I mean, I think there`s a few things. One is that the state keeps changing.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That`s true.\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: So you know, Biden probably isn`t winning in 2018, he wins in 2020 and so things are kept moving in the right direction. And for Democrats, I think the other thing, which is going to be the key to 2020 overall is like, we`re about to see the most fundamental change in rights in this country with abortion being overturned.\par \par \uc2 And she has a very critical case to make as do a lot of governors -- Democratic governors across the country, saying I am the only thing that stands in the way of you losing these fundamental rights. And I think that`s going to be a case you`re going to see a lot of Democrats making, it`ll be more critical to claim governor.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: There`s a theory to all of this, that on abortion rights, it`s the party that overreaches more that gets punished, right? And that the idea of bans is scary to people who might even describe themselves as they`re not comfortable with Democratic language on abortion. The idea of a ban goes too far for them. Is that the needle that \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  needs to thread in a place like Georgia?\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: I mean, I think the Republican Party I mean, the Maga Republican Party right now is so far out of step with the mainstream Americans. 60 -- I mean, data after data, poll after poll shows that 60 percent, 65 percent of Americans support a right to abortion.\par \par \uc2 Not only are Republicans removing that right, you`re seeing exactly all these states are trying to pass laws that ban the right to an abortion that are going to start -- you`re going to see this criminalizing having an abortion, preventing women from going out of state to access this abortion -- I mean, you could -- if there`s one thing you can count on and even, you know, the fact that Senator McConnell couldn`t hold back saying that yes, it is possible we might actually ban this nationally, just goes to show you that that`s where the energy of the Republican Party is right now.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Abby, so when I get out of this chair, I`m heading to Laredo, I`m going to be down there covering the end of this Cuellar-Cisneros race. That is certainly the theory of the case that Cisneros wants to put forward, right? That this is an issue on what you can afford she says to have \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , someone who could be the Joe Manchin of the House on a question of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 Is that issue going to be enough for her? It`s not like she hasn`t had other help in terms of an \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation that`s connected to her opponent. I mean, there`s a lot of things that look like they could be breaking her way here at the last minute.\par \par \uc2 LIVINGSTON: I -- this -- Cuellar has taken a lot of hits in a lot of different directions, the timing of this one was brutal for him so it remains unclear. But it also must be remembered in democratic politics. This is potentially a general election seat in a highly Roman Catholic area and so the way it is discussed in the primary and how it isn`t discussed in the general are very open questions to make.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Democratic leadership have stayed with Cuellar on this much of the frustration of progressives, what do you make of that dynamic -- I mean where you`ve got sort of like even you know Speaker Pelosi, Whip Clyburn was down there, saying, we`re going to do everything we can to fight for abortion rights, except campaign against this guy in our party who`s against abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: I think that`s not surprising to me. It`s so rare for leadership to go against what someone in their caucus. I just think -- and they`re not going to be the kind of leaders that have ever said, you`ve got to be with us on every single issue. I think that`s actually one of the things that make the Democratic Party different right now than sort of Maga Republicans. Maga Republicans say you have to be with us on every single issue. I mean, Brian Kemp is totally aligned with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , except he wouldn`t overturn the election and find, you know, criminally go undertaken act to find 11,000 more votes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Matt, what`s the ad here for if Cisneros wins if you`re advising the Republican candidate down there, you`ve had Elizabeth Warren and \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , you`ve had the most progressive folks backing Cisneros, is that an opportunity for Republicans? It is definitely a blue-leaning district, got more blue in redistricting, but is having a progressive candidate there make that a more appealing opportunity for Republicans?\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Yes, I thought so even before the Roe kind of leaked opinion came down. And you`re right, if it`s a Roman Catholic District, what I essentially do is I -- a couple of weeks ago, Chuck Todd was interviewing \uc2 Nan Whaley\uc2  who`s running for governor of Ohio, and the -- after flat out, do you support any restrictions on abortion? And she said, no. She wouldn`t answer the question and wouldn`t commit to anything. I would say the exact same thing it Cisneros. And you`re seeing that as a way to -- for Republicans again on offense on the issue of abortion. When I was at the NRCC, we ran ads on that issue.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I want to go back into the questions of election denialism here, Matt. There`s a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  report they just put out. At least 357 sitting Republican legislatures -- legislators in closely contested battleground states have used the power of their office to discredit or try to overturn the results of the 2020 election. Produce -- probably not going to win tomorrow, but how deeply embedded in this culture of you know sort of like you could put election security on one end or like full-on election results denialism on the other in the Republican Party in the States?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Look, I think that`s going to be an issue that state legislatures, whether it`s the election stuff or even abortion, right? I got -- I want -- I`m curious if those 357 how -- of how many legislators in the entire country they are I -- just for perspective? But look, there`s a part of the party that believes this, it`s not going to go away overnight. But even as you see the 60 to 30 or so, percentage of coming to Perdue shows that this is waning, it`s not where it was a year ago, or even two years.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is there enough of the party to support \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 GORMAN: Well, in a -- in a five-person primary like it was and no one was dropping out, but you look in Nebraska, the governor`s pick one there, Trump`s didn`t, the same thing with well, we`re likely to see with Kemp. I think that`s more increasing. That share of the vote is increasing.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Navin, the -- hung up on this idea of overreach today. Is this the secret sauce for Democrats to survive this midterm, if it`s to say on abortion, Republicans are too extreme on to you know, throwing out your vote, Republicans are too extreme and to try to, you know, hammer that point -- to try to make every Republican gubernatorial candidate Mastriano?\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: Yes. I mean, I think the reality is elections are a choice. Historically, midterms are not. They are a referendum. And if it`s a referendum, I think Democrats recognize this is a tough cycle. But the reality is, every time you go into that ballot box, you are picking between two candidates and we now have a party that has been taken over. The ruling party has now been taken over by this extreme Maga movement.\par \par \uc2 And I think on issue after issue, the scary thing about the election stuff isn`t just January 6, 2020, it`s that all of these people are going to contest every election. You can`t tell me that Republicans aren`t going to contest the future election if they don`t like the results. And I think it`s this pattern of questioning, basic, you know, the idea that people`s vote counts, and I think you`re going to see them wanting to cancel people`s vote if they don`t like the outcome.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Eventually, we`re going to see somebody have to concede in that Pennsylvania Senate race too, which is going to be a very interesting moment for the Republican Party. Guys, we got to leave it there. Thank you all for your reporting and your expertise.\par \par \uc2 And as we look ahead to this week`s primaries, we`ve got some important programming notes. Georgia Democratic gubernatorial candidate \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , who you may have heard us discussing, joins my colleague \uc2 Joy Reid\uc2  tonight on the REIDOUT, that`s at 7:00 Eastern on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 And tomorrow, for all your election updates all night long, MEET THE PRESS will hold another election night special anchored by Chuck and Kristen Welker. That`s beginning at 8:00 Eastern, streaming on News Now, also featuring Yours truly, from Laredo. As I said, I`ll be heading there to cover the Texas House Democratic runoff, and I`ll be reporting from Cisneros headquarters.\par \par \uc2 Up next. The latest from Tokyo as President Biden rounds out his first presidential trip to Asia making some major news on a potential showdown with China over Taiwan. We`ve got those details after this. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If you didn`t want to get involved in the Ukraine conflict militarily for obvious reasons, are you willing to get involved militarily to defend Taiwan if it comes to that?\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: You are?\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: That`s the commitment we made.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. That was President Biden during a joint press conference in Japan and those comments are getting a lot of attention. Taiwan`s Ministry of Foreign Affairs welcomed the remarks while China`s Foreign Ministry warned, "no one should underestimate the strong determination, firm will, and strong ability of the Chinese people to defend national sovereignty and territorial integrity."\par \par \uc2 Soon after the president`s remarks, a White House official told NBC News that U.S. policy had not changed. The president will return to DC tomorrow, and we`ll have a slew of issues to tackle on his domestic agenda when he gets back including that ongoing baby formula shortage, rising gas prices, and a court decision on Title 42.\par \par \uc2 To break all of this down, I`m joined by my NBC News colleague, White House Correspondent Mike Memoli. He`s in Tokyo covering the president`s trip. So, Mike, this is not the first time the president has made remarks like this on Taiwan but this is the first time the president made them in Asia standing next to the Prime Minister of Japan. Is the White House trying to downplay the significance of what he said?\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, MSNBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, they sure are because you just heard an on-camera version of that walk-back statement from Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  a few moments ago, responding to our colleague Courtney Kube at the Pentagon to what we heard on from the White House yesterday.\par \par \uc2 But it`s so important, Garrett, that you played the full question and the full answer from President Biden yesterday because it is that full question, the context of comparing the way the U.S. might act with Taiwan versus how we`ve acted versus Ukraine. That makes it so much harder for the White House to walk this statement back and say that he was simply reiterating existing policy.\par \par \uc2 If you look at that existing policy, there`s the one-China policy in which we, yes, say that Taiwan is a part of China. But the Taiwan Relations Act, which Biden as a senator supported, the sort of terms of the Taiwan Relations Act are similar to what we`ve maybe been doing, you could argue with Ukraine, giving them the resources to defend themselves. And in that sub context of the question the president was asked, he indicated he would be willing to go further to do more for Taiwan than we`ve done for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Now, Garrett, the President had an event later that day, I was in the room and I was shouting, as many other reporters were attempting to get the president to sort of clarify what he meant. You can tell by my voice. Maybe my shouting wasn`t loud enough but I promise you he heard the other questions asked by my colleagues, and he did not do that.\par \par \uc2 As we now move into Tuesday, it is Tuesday here in Tokyo, the president has a number of opportunities, let`s say for the press to ask him additional questions. He`s going to be holding a number of bilateral meetings, that group meeting then of the leaders of the QUAD. And I think the full sort of story of whether this is a one-day story or a multiple-day public relations and international relations problem will be determined by what the president himself says later today.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right. On the one hand, there`s this idea of strategic ambiguity of keeping them guessing but another hand if you`ve got the president and his staff saying different things at different times, this could get really messy. Mike, when he gets back, it`ll amazingly still be Tuesday, the wonders of modern, you know, trans-Time Zone air travel.\par \par \uc2 The White House has got a ton on their plate when they get back, the baby formula crisis, inflation, whether they`re going to get something out of the Senate here on a reconciliation bill. I know they`re busy with the foreign policy side of this here but how are you -- how`s the White House looking ahead to all of those domestic challenges that are still going to be on their plate when the president touches back down here stateside?\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: Yes. Well, Garrett, up until about 2:00 Local Time here on Monday, that it was interesting the degree to which this trip had otherwise been defined by the way in which it was largely about domestic policy. The president doing a number of events trying to get home the message that his economic policies investment in, for instance, the electric vehicle market, leading to announcements like the ones he was able to make here with Hyundai, for instance, opening up a plant in Georgia or the Chips Act, which you know, they`ve been pushing hard to get Congress to move over the finish line, doing an event at \uc2 Samsung\uc2  as they plan to build a plant in Texas to do the same.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 MEMOLI: And so they have been trying to sort of tee up the domestic politics through their time here in Asia, but as you say, a number of issues in which the president`s going to be now having to deal with on a daily basis again. The biggest one, I think, is the Title 42 situation. Yes, there is now this injunction to prevent the White House from lifting it, but it`s not one that`s going to go away.\par \par \uc2 And it will lead to another sort of foreign policy challenge for President Biden because on the calendar just a few weeks from now is the Summit of the Americas welcoming, basically the entire Western Hemisphere, the leadership there, and it may not survive the test because of the questions that the president is facing on an international basis over his immigration policy as well, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We`re going to talk a lot more about Title 42 later in the show. Mike Memoi, shouting himself, of course, on behalf of the viewers. Mike, thank you. And in case you missed it, Chuck`s streaming show, MEET THE PRESS REPORTS digs into Taiwan and what the U.S. might do if China did invade. That episode called War Games is available on-demand now on Peacock. It`s cool.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, just when we thought COVID concerns were starting to fade, the president warns that everyone should be concerned about monkeypox. I`ll speak with the White House medical adviser about the real threat it poses after this. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. As COVID cases continue to climb nationwide, President Biden is trying to downplay concerns over another virus, monkeypox.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`ve had this monkeypox in the larger numbers in the past. Number one. Number two, we have vaccines to take care of it. Number three, there is thus far doesn`t seem to be a need for any kind of extra effort beyond what`s going on. And so I just don`t think it rises to the level of the kind of concern that existed with COVID- 19 or -- and the smallpox vaccine works for it. So -- but I think people should be careful.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: The \uc2 WHO\uc2  says monkeypox cases have been reported in at least a dozen countries now, although no deaths have been associated with this recent outbreak. And the president`s efforts to calm nerves over monkeypox came after he voiced concerns over it just a day earlier.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: Well, they haven`t told me the level of exposure yet, but it is something that everybody should be concerned about. We`re working on it hard to figure out what we do and what vaccine if any maybe available for it. But it is a concern in the sense that if it were to spread, it`s consequential.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I`m joined now by Dr. Raj Panjabi, senior director of the National Security Council, leading the White House`s Pandemic Office.\par \par \uc2 And Doctor, help me triangulate this a little bit. The president raised the alarm about monkeypox then he softened his statements a little bit this morning. What`s the reality of this outbreak and just how worried should Americans be about it?\par \par \uc2 DR. RAJ PANJABI, SENIOR DIRECTOR, NATIONAL SECURITY COUNCIL WHITE HOUSE PANDEMIC OFFICE: Thank you, Garrett, and thanks for having me on. The president is really ensuring we stay vigilant. We`ve taken infectious diseases seriously from the beginning of this administration. On day one the president restored the Pandemic Preparedness Office that I lead now, and we are monitoring and responding to infectious disease threats including this one.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to risk to the general public, we know that risk is low. And that`s because the current number of cases -- of the cases we`ve seen so far all have been less severe and we know that we have, as the president said, vaccines available for this disease. It`s not a new disease. It`s spreading in new ways, in new places and quite suddenly, but we have vaccines and we have treatments.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Can you talk about that spread a little bit? I think that`s one of the things that people have the most questions about. And you`re somebody who`s got firsthand experience treating this disease. How does it spread? How contagious is it or do we know with this current outbreak?\par \par \uc2 PANJABI: So the disease presents with flu-like symptoms in most patients and a rash can develop. It usually resolves in two to four weeks. It`s direct contact with the rash, the skin lesions themselves that is the most normal way or major way that this transmits from people to people. And that`s what we worry about.\par \par \uc2 So this is not asymptomatic transmission as we saw with COVID, which -- and it`s also not as contagious as COVID is. You have to have contact directly with the person who has the symptoms and as you can imagine, the pictures you`re showing now, the rash itself is quite apparent.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You`re the pandemic preparedness guy, we have a plan for this. What does a plan for a U.S. response look like should this become worse?\par \par \uc2 PANJABI: Yes, there are a few things we`re doing immediately. As soon as we knew there were new cases reported first in the U.K. a few weeks ago, we had already mobilized our colleagues at the departments and agencies, the CDC, \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2 , to start to work to procure sufficient amounts of vaccines and treatments. So that`s number one. Number two, we`re working with state and local actors.\par \par \uc2 I was just in touch with the doctors at the hospital in Boston this weekend to ensure that they have the testing capability available and also that they have the guidance of how to use the vaccine for those providers who are at the highest risk. And I`m happy to report that as of yesterday, they already have the vaccine available and are offering it to the health care providers who were exposed to the first patient.\par \par \uc2 So that`s what we have to do, Garrett, with this. It`s science-based to stop and end this outbreak. We`ve got to find those infected, ensure they get isolated and have the treatment and care that they need, and then ensure that those who are exposed get the vaccines they need. If we do that again and again, that`s what makes possible ending this outbreak. And that`s what`s worked before.\par \par \uc2 We had an outbreak in 2003 and the same approach of ensuring we`re identifying and supporting patients that -- we`re tracing their contacts and listing those who`ve been exposed and making sure we`re monitoring them, that`s what brought the 2003 outbreak in this country under control.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: This is effectively the smallpox vaccine that we use. Right? Is there any reason to think that for younger folks who weren`t vaccinated against smallpox this could be more serious, older folks less so. I mean, talk to me about the kind of relation to smallpox here and what people should know about that.\par \par \uc2 PANJABI: Yes, so think of monkeypox as a -- in the same family of viruses as smallpox but a much less severe type of disease.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:06]\par \par \uc2 So smallpox had a higher mortality rate. This disease has a death rate of less than 1 percent. And that`s in countries with weaker health systems. I worked in Liberia before this as you were mentioning, and, you know, we didn`t have access to vaccines there. We have access to vaccines and treatments here. And that makes the risks substantially lower.\par \par \uc2 The vaccine you mentioned, smallpox vaccine, is one type of vaccine that`s available for dealing of monkeypox. There`s also a vaccine that`s been licensed for both smallpox and monkeypox. That`s called Jynneos. And that is quite effective, especially when given within four days of an exposure to a patient with monkeypox.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Doc, I would be remised not to ask you about COVID while I have you here. We`re two years on now and I`m just curious if you worry about the distrust -- you talk about the science earlier, the distrust that some people have for the medical community during the pandemic, if it makes it harder for you guys as you`re trying to kind of keep battling with COVID now while with the other hand potentially worry about this other disease as well.\par \par \uc2 PANJABI: Well, on the one hand, it`s made people a lot more aware of infectious diseases. And, you know, even the conversation we`re having now is about how the virus transmits. Who can get more sick, how many deaths have caused, so the public is very aware. Of course with that awareness comes anxiety and fear, and I think the approach of this administration is science based, empathy based, and transparency based.\par \par \uc2 We have already, as I mentioned, started to work with local, state, and international health officials to make sure vaccines and treatments are available to patients. That we have testing available so we identify more cases. The CDC has put out an alert on Friday already to all doctors in the country to ensure that we`re finding cases and reporting them. And of course we`re working with international partners to figure out the source of this outbreak and so we can try to stop it.\par \par \uc2 You know, we will continue to remain engaged transparently with the public, and we want you to know that if you`re worried about this and you are afraid you have symptoms, reach out to your providers. We do have solutions for this. This is not like COVID where it took some time before we had countermeasures, vaccines and treatments. We have that now for this disease.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Great. Dr. Raj Panjabi, thank you for coming on and sharing your expertise with us.\par \par \uc2 PANJABI: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And coming up, the White House battles the fallout from another pandemic-related issue as a federal judge blocks the president from lifting migrant restrictions at the border. So what does President Biden do next?\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:40:44]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. A Louisiana judge ruled Friday that the Biden administration cannot currently take any steps to lift Title 42. The pandemic-era border policy that all but ended the ability for migrants to seek asylum in the U.S. stopping plans that would have gone forward today to lift restrictions.\par \par \uc2 The Biden administration released a statement opposing the decision announcing that the Justice Department would be appealing the ruling and arguing that the power to create public health policy should lie with the CDC, not a district court.\par \par \uc2 For now, the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2  said it would comply with the order but that it will also stick to plans to ramp up efforts to anticipation of lifting Title 42 at some point in the future.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Tyler Moran, a former senior migration adviser to the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 And Miss Moran, thank you for being here. This court injunction seems to block any efforts for now. What`s your overall reaction to Friday`s decision now that you`ve had time go through it?\par \par \uc2 TYLER MORAN, FORMER BIDEN SENIOR MIGRATION ADVISER: So first of all, let`s just back up and cover why this policy went into place. \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , Trump`s senior adviser, made multiple attempts to use it in the first years of the administration. Then in COVID it gave him the opportunity so the CDC did make the decision that there was a public health justifications to put it in place, though the public health authority is not an immigration authority.\par \par \uc2 So what the decision means is that there is a national injunction and likely Title 2 is going to have to be in place until next year. So what does that mean? It means that people cannot ask for protection. They can`t ask for asylum at the southern border. But it also means that it`s probably going to encourage more people to come to the border.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are pretending like this is a great border measure, but what it has done is just increased the number of people at the border ever since Trump put it in place because what is happening is that people are making multiple attempts to cross the border. One out of three people has tried to cross the border one or more times. The reason is, is because Title 42 suspends immigration law, there are no consequences for crossing the border unlawfully.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. So if we`re stuck with this potentially for another year or however long this, you know, court process takes, what should the Biden administration try to do next year? I mean, they seem to be seeking that middle ground of opening up, you know, the asylum program but they can`t get rid of this. So how do they navigate this?\par \par \uc2 MORAN: So in the first two weeks, President Biden issued an executive order that laid out his vision for the border and asylum, and so what they`ll do is they`ll continue to work on ensuring that they can process people in an orderly way at the border. There is a new asylum officer rule, which will mean that asylum claims can be processed in a year instead of four years. There are still will be some people who can apply for asylum.\par \par \uc2 Even under Trump about 50 percent of people were processed under Title 42 and the remainder were processed under immigration law. Some of those applied for asylum. Some of them were removed. And then there`s the regional work. We have the Summit of the Americas coming up and the administration is doing a lot of work to ask other countries in the western hemisphere to not only enforce their borders, but to create more legal migration to their own country and to beef up their own asylum systems.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Has Congress just totally dropped the ball here, too? I mean, it`s not the president`s job to make policy on the border.\par \par \uc2 MORAN: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s ultimately members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 MORAN: Absolutely. I mean, if Republicans want to have a debate about immigration, go for it, but I have been through the 2006, 2007, 2013, the DREAM Act, they have no interest in legislating on immigration. President Biden sent a bill on day one, a very reasonable, commonsense bill that included border security, a path to citizenship, funding for the western hemisphere, and he stood up in front of Congress at his first joint address and said, I like my bill. But I`m happy to listen to anyone. I will work with anyone. Come talk to me.\par \par \uc2 Did Republicans do that? They did not. Ultimately Congress has to fix this. Immigration law has not been updated in 30 years. That means there are very few legal ways for people to come. Besides asylum, which absolutely has to be restored, a lot of people now are coming because the COVID economy is pushing them north. There are certain industries that need workers and there is no legal way for them to come to take those jobs.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:06]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Our scale of what our big numbers for government spending has changed a lot in the COVID era, but DHS said they would need $2 billion to cope with lifting Title 42 in the expected influx of migrants that would come. What does it say about the scale of the problem here that needs to be solved?\par \par \uc2 MORAN: Well, first of all, you point out something, which is that we spend more on immigration enforcement than most other federal law enforcement combined. So we are spending an incredible amount on immigration enforcement. So I do know that in the Senate, Democrats are thinking about a border supplemental. And so rather than this nonsense of this Lankford- Sinema bill, which is moot now because the courts have basically suspended any ability to lift Title 42, they should focus on a border supplemental.\par \par \uc2 So DHS has enough resources, they have processing centers, they have technology. They are able to surge people to the border. And so that`s what`s needed. If Republicans are complaining about not having a plan and no order, we`ll give DHS the resources that they need.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I`m imagining part of my summer covering a combined COVID-border supplemental. It`s starting to come together in my head. We could see it.\par \par \uc2 Tyler Moran, thank very much for your expertise.\par \par \uc2 MORAN: Absolutely. Great. Thanks for having me on, Garret.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And still ahead, Ukraine lays down a hard line in negotiations with Russia as the Pentagon weighs sending military forces to guard the U.S. embassy in Kyiv. I`ll speak with the former U.S. ambassador to Ukraine, next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:50:11]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. In the last hour Defense Secretary \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2  announced that Western allies have agreed to provide additional military aid to Ukraine. It comes as Russia ramps up its offensive in eastern Ukraine as President Zelenskyy acknowledges the devastating toll this war is taking on his people. Zelenskyy says as many as 100 soldiers a day could be dying in the intense fighting in eastern Ukraine. The governor of the Luhansk region accused Russian troops of using scorched earth tactics.\par \par \uc2 And meanwhile, the "Wall Street Journal" is reporting that the Pentagon may deploy special forces to protect the U.S. embassy in Kyiv which reopened last week. A senior White House official and a Pentagon official tell NBC News that that planning is still very early and not under active consideration.\par \par \uc2 In a Ukrainian television interview over the weekend, a defiant Zelenskyy demanded that Russia return everything, everything seized during this war, and issued this blunt warning. Victory, he said, will be bloody and the end will certainly be in diplomacy.\par \par \uc2 All of this comes as a diplomat to Russia`s mission in the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . in Geneva resigned over the war in Ukraine, writing that he`s never been so ashamed.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by Bill Taylor, who served as U.S. ambassador to Ukraine during the \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  administration and he`s now the vice president of Russia and Europe at the \uc2 U.S. Institute of Peace\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, Ambassador Taylor, thank you as always for coming on. What do you make of Zelenskyy taking this message to the \uc2 World Economic Forum\uc2  and do you agree with his assessment here that it`s got to be victory and it`s got to be victory without territorial concessions to get through this?\par \par \uc2 AMB. BILL TAYLOR, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO UKRAINE: Garrett, President Zelenskyy is reflecting the strong view of the Ukrainian people, that they should not have to compromise, should not have to give up claim to their sovereign territory because they were invaded by the Russians.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: They are not willing to make that trade. They`re not willing to give the Russians that kind of a compromise. So, yes, he`s indicating that they will wait. They`re not going to go for a cease-fire that would basically give the Russians the ability to stay there. So, yes, he`s got a strong position.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We talked about this before but it`s so clear that the strategy even globally has moved from help Ukraine not lose to Ukraine has to win to do something like that. The other thing that Zelenskyy said that I was interested in is he talked about the prospect of maximum sanctions on Russia. I`ve asked Speaker Pelosi and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  who`ve both recently come back from Kyiv about additional U.S. sanctions, and they`ve been kind of coy about it.\par \par \uc2 I wonder what you make of the idea that sort of what`s left in the chamber in terms of sanctions that might still put real pressure on the Russian economy?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Of course the big thing, Garrett, is we know it`s the European purchases of oil and gas from the Russians. That`s what`s now funding the war machine frankly. That`s what`s providing Putin with the ability to keep this war going. He`s got big problems. He`s got to find additional weapons. He`s got to find additional ammunition. He`s got -- the big problem, he`s got to find additional troops.\par \par \uc2 And for all of those things he needs funds. And right now the big source of funds is coming from the sale of oil and gas to the Europeans.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s going to be the hard thing to get the allies to agree to change that prospect. I think that`s been the most challenging thing diplomatically from the word go here.\par \par \uc2 What do you make of this resignation of this diplomat here. Not a major figure but here you`ve got a Russian diplomat coming out publicly saying that he can`t work on this war anymore. Does that indicate that Putin might be losing more support within or is this likely to be just kind of a one- off?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: It`s more than a one-off, Garrett. Definitely more than a one-off. We`ve seen that resignation. We saw last week on a widely viewed TV show a commenter, commentator, who has been a pretty hardcore person saying we can`t win this war. We Russians -- he`s Russian. He was saying this on Russian TV apparently with the state approval because they control all the TV and he was saying we can`t win. We`re not winning. We are isolated. We`re isolated from the world. And he might have been arguing for an escalation but he was clearly making the case that the Russians are not winning this war right now.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: What do you make of the war crimes trial that we just saw in Ukraine, this conviction now of a Russian soldier? How symbolic is that? What kind of message does that send globally to see trials like this going on while this war is still going on?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: It sends a global message, you`re right, but it also sends a message to the Russian soldiers and the Russian commanders.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:02]\par \par \uc2 This demonstrates to them that they`re responsible, they`re going to be held responsible for the war crimes, for the atrocities, for the murders that they are perpetrating against Ukrainians. And for this soldier to plead guilty apparently not under duress and a lot of evidence against this soldier. It was very clear -- and they found this soldier guilty and they put him in jail for life. That message is going to other Russian soldiers. That message is going to the Russian chain of command, that the orders to kill Ukrainians, civilians, will be prosecuted. That`s a strong message.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I don`t want to ask you to skate too far out of your lane here, but I`m curious of what you make of President Biden seeming to offer a stronger defense upfront of Taiwan than was done of Ukraine. Obviously different types of allies, different types of situation globally. The White House is trying to clarify his remarks. But I wonder what you make of that generally and of the idea strategic ambiguity around what we would do there and how those two issues relate between Ukraine and Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: They do relate. Of course they relate. And President Xi of China is undoubtedly watching very closely what`s going on in Ukraine and how Ukrainians are fighting effectively against the Russians and President Xi notices these sanctions that you were talking about earlier against the Russians could very well apply to him. So he`s taking that lesson. But the engagement, the U.S. engagement, the \uc2 NATO\uc2  engagement on the military side has been very strong.\par \par \uc2 In terms of the weapons flow that are going into the Ukrainians, President Xi has to be worried about that as well and President Biden just made that worry for President Xi a little bit clearer.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Very interesting stuff. All right, Ambassador Bill Taylor, thank you as always for sharing your expertise with us.\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Glad to be here, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And thank you all for being with us for the hour. Chuck will be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC coverage continues with Katy Tur after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220524ei5n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May 23, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes \par \uc2 7,931 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JASON JOHNSON, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: We`re going to end up losing a great member of Congress no matter what who ends up winning.\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: Yes, no matter what happened. That is such a shame. They are two solid candidates, and one of them is going to be no longer in Congress. Jason Johnson, Greg Bluestein, great conversation. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 And that`s it. That`s tonight`s "REIDOUT." Thank you all for joining us from beautiful Atlanta. And ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Brian Kemp is it turncoat, is a coward, and is a complete and total disaster.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: As Republicans prepare to go to the polls in Georgia, it`s Trump versus Pence. The former president backing one candidate for governor, the former vice president backing another. Tonight, why the future of democracy in Georgia is on the ballot. And why for Trump it`s personal.\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I just want to find 11,780 votes.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Then, the impact of the threat to abortion as Texans head to the polls. And news tonight the January 6 Committee will only hold six public hearings next month. I`ll ask committee member \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  about that when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. We`re just 11 hours away from polls opening in tomorrow`s crucial primary election in Georgia. In the two marquee races, the Republican primaries for governor and for Secretary of State, two Trump back candidates are essentially running single-issue campaigns on the ex-presidents big lie of a stolen election.\par \par \uc2 You got former Senator \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , a close Trump ally who lost his Senate seat last year. He`s challenging the incumbent Republican Governor Brian Kemp. And you`ve got far-right Congressman \uc2 Jody Hice\uc2  who`s hoping to oust the current incumbent Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These races are notable not only as referenda on Trump`s attempted coup, but also to set the groundwork for the next one. Because out of all of Trump`s attempts to overturn the will of the voters and steal the election, it was the state of Georgia where he put his most effort.\par \par \uc2 Part of that, of course, has to do with how humiliated Trump appears to have felt by his defeat there. Not only because the election was widely seen as repudiation him and his movement, it had been years since a Democrat wanted, but also because he thought Georgia was a red Republican- controlled state. And in Trump`s mind, state Republicans were supposed to help deliver his win.\par \par \uc2 It all started almost immediately after NBC News declared Biden the winner, the apparent winner of Georgia. It was on November 13, 2020, and Trump`s meltdown began in full. He immediately started making baseless claims of widespread fraud in the state, which he says undermines his lost.\par \par \uc2 On November 19, the day before Georgia officially certified the election, Trump`s chief of staff \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  actually reached out to Secretary State Raffensperger presumably to get him to delay that certification, texting him "Mr. Secretary, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  here, if you could give me a brief call at your convenience. Thank you." It`s funny he doesn`t put the ask in text. Maybe he sort of knows.\par \par \uc2 As far as we know, Raffensperger didn`t return the call, and the next day, Georgia certified Biden`s victory as expected. Trump later went on Fox News to publicly lambaste Governor Kemp for not playing along with Trump`s scheme to steal the election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : They had judges making deals and they had electoral officials making deals like this character in Georgia who is a disaster, and the governor has done nothing. He`s done absolutely nothing. I`m ashamed that I endorsed him.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, a few days later, Trump actually called Kemp personally to pressure him into calling a special legislative session where state Republicans in the state legislature could throw out Biden`s victory and instead appoint a slate of pro-Trump electors, which you might remember, was also the basis of Trump lawyer John Eastman`s coup plot.\par \par \uc2 During this time, Kemp made an effort to publicly defend himself mostly honestly by just throwing the Republican Secretary of State Raffensperger under the bus. Get this. Here`s the statement. He releases it back then reading in part, "Georgia law prohibits the governor from interfering in elections. The Secretary of State, who is an elected constitutional officer, has oversight over elections that cannot be overridden by executive order."\par \par \uc2 Of course, we already know that Trump was targeting Raffensperger as well. On December 5, the same day that he called Kemp, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  tried to get through to the Georgia secretary of state again. Meadows texting him, "Mr. Secretary, can you call a White House switchboard for a call? Your voicemail is full."\par \par \uc2 The White House put Raffensperger who was clearly ducking their calls on the backburner because they then had a new strategy. The same week Trump made those calls, his lawyer \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  went in person down to Georgia, pushing baseless claims of voter fraud surrounding a nonpartisan election worker named Ruby Freeman, who became the target of a slew of intense false right-wing conspiracies which inaccurately claimed she was stuffing ballot boxes for Biden in the middle of the night.\par \par \uc2 And even though those accusations were debunked, there was no evidence of fraud, Giuliani went to Georgia and said this before the State Senate.\par \par \uc2 [2005:07]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUDY GIULIANI\uc2 , TRUMP LAWYER: This is a terrible, terrible constitutional crisis we`re in. You don`t have to be a genius to figure out what happened. I don`t have to be a genius to figure out that those votes are not legitimate votes. You don`t put legitimate votes under a table.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: No.\par \par \uc2 GIULIANI: Wait until you throw the opposition out and in the middle of the night, count them. We would have to be fools to think that. So, no need to push it any further, but there`s more than ample evidence to conclude that this election was a sham.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Again, all that completely bogus, right? There was nothing that Ruby Freeman did that was wrong. They caught her on some tape and made some huge deal about stuffing ballots. She wasn`t, OK. They counted the ballots, they counted them again. But Ruby Freeman`s story sadly does not end there. More on her in a moment. Giuliani`s claims were mostly laughed out at the state. His legal challenges of the election went nowhere, so Trump began working on yet another scheme. Again, all Georgia focused, right?\par \par \uc2 On December 28, few weeks after those hearings, the then acting-chief of the Department of Justice`s civil division, remember a guy named Jeffrey Clark, he`s plotting to have Trump installed him as the Acting Attorney General so that once in power, he could release a memo using the official letterhead and implied legitimacy of the DOJ to say there were election irregularities in Georgia, as well as other states, and to authorize the legislature to call itself intercession to throw out Biden`s victory.\par \par \uc2 Had that plan succeeded, it would have caused a full-blown constitutional crisis. I mean, it`s really hard to overstate the damage that would be done to our democracy for the Attorney General of the Department of Justice to falsely claim there was election fraud and say, hey, Georgia, you should know, you guys should call a special election because we just can`t say what happened there. Calling for legislators to go rogue and overturn the election.\par \par \uc2 Now, thankfully, the folks at the highest level of the Department of Justice understood this, and they threatened mass resignations, Trump and Clark back down. That was another failed plan. The clock was ticking. Trump was running out of time. So, by now, it was January and he finally made it through to Georgia Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  for that now- infamous call where he told Raffensperger to find the votes and griped about Ruby Freeman more than a