o like, this is the guy that`s supposed to be moving on. He`s like begging Trump not to be mad at him.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 MILLER: So, that`s the thing. Yes, there`s a little bit of wane in his popularity but he still has a hold on them.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:03]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. And I think that`s a great point. No one will defy him in some ways ends up being more meaningful in terms of the policy and in terms of the policy and in terms of the direction of American democracy than anything else. Tim Miller, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, with \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is split with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  extent -- will it extend beyond the campaign trail under the January 6 investigation. Committee Member \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  on new reporting about upcoming public hearings and \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  has nine-hour testimony after this.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Since last July, almost 11 months, the bipartisan January Subcommittee has been investigating the insurrection, they have received over 136,000 documents conducted over 1000 depositions in interviews, including most recently with Donald Trump`s lawyer and major big lie proponents seen earlier in the show, \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He reportedly met with the committee for nine hours on Friday. But now, as the committee is moving into a new phase of the investigation, the long- awaited public hearings where we learn about all they have uncovered. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  got its hands on a draft hearings schedule which shows six hearings scheduled for the month of June with only two during primetime hours.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  is a Democrat from Maryland. He serves on the January 6 Committee, and was the lead impeachment manager in the second impeachment trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  for inciting the insurrection. And he joins me now. Congressman Raskin, my sense is that you can`t comment on this sort of stuff, but I`ll ask anyway. Is the -- is the leaked schedule correct?\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): Hey, Chris, well, I`ll just say, in general, a lot of the leaks reporting, although not all of it has been correct. We`re very eager to get going with these hearings. You should know that the hearings are going to be about the events of January 6 and the causes thereof. But the whole question of recommendations for policy changes to fortify ourselves in our institutions against future attacks will come later.\par \par \uc2 So, I think there will be the opportunity for us to have hearings on other aspects of our charge under House Resolution 503 after this cluster of hearings.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I saw the news about \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2  and again, I don`t know what you can or can`t confirm. But it is striking to me in some ways when, as we near the end of this, the number of people close to the President that did end up cooperating with either invitations or subpoenas from the committee.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, everybody`s got a legal duty and civic duty to be there. And we have made ourselves perfectly clear that we will hold people in contempt and try to get contempt prosecutions brought against them for violating their legal obligations here. \uc2 Rudy Giuliani\uc2 , I should tell you, it`s not much news. But you know, he is he`s no Michael Tiger. He`s no Bruce Cutler as mob lawyers go.\par \par \uc2 But he definitely has a lot of information about what was taking place during those days. And, you know, I`m hopeful that he`s going to tell or telling us everything that he knows.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: The only thing in the world basically that I`m like genuinely an expert on is basically primetime political news programming. I have an interest in the hearings in that -- in a sort of professional capacity, obviously, but I do wonder like how you are thinking about them. Because people will talk about the Watergate hearings, and the thing I always say in response is look, back during the Watergate hearings, there`s like 50 million people watching Tonight Show every night. It`s just a different universe.\par \par \uc2 That`s true I think even the Benghazi hearings, you know, five or six years ago. Like, it`s a different universe right now in terms of how central to the attention one can make a thing. How are you on the committee thinking about that? What do you think is a goal of the public part of the hearing?\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Well, I`m with you on that. I think there`s something even kind of quaint about the phrase prime time because the, you know, those electrifying moments of television exchanges between legislators and witnesses are moments of real epiphany and conceptual breakthrough or moral breakthrough. Those will get replayed all over the internet, all over all of the channels and so on.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s not as if you need to be watching every moment in order to see everything that`s going to happen. It`s also the case that we can put out more material even than we`re going to produce for the hearings themselves and make it available to people. And those people who are real students of insurrection and coups in 21st century, America can study all of it.\par \par \uc2 You know, the thing about primetime that you could perhaps explain to me, Chris, is how networks decide when they`re willing to give up their regularly scheduled programming and when they`re not. And I think that I assume that that`s been part of the negotiation between our communications people and the networks.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I think that`s an interesting point. What do you -- do you think -- do you imagine in your head a set of people who are persuadable about the gravity of this who are as yet to be persuaded but can be persuaded by the public portion of these hearings?\par \par \uc2 [20:40:12]\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Absolutely. And the reason I know that is because I have heard, certainly from hundreds and perhaps thousands of people who wrote to me after the second impeachment trial to say, they were either independents, or they were Republicans, or they were teachers, they were professors. They were really watching to see what was happening.\par \par \uc2 And they found that the evidence of Donald Trump`s guilt was overwhelming, that we had refuted any and all of the frivolous legal and factual arguments that had been attempted by Trump`s lawyers. And so, I know there were tons of people who were in movement then and I think there are even many more people in movement now.\par \par \uc2 As we see, you know, what was an insurrection attempted coup that took place in January of last year, continues to be an attempt to delegitimize our constitutional order in our elections. And there are people like the Republican nominee for a governor in Pennsylvania today who participated in these events. I think that Mastriano rented three buses to take people down to Washington for the stop to -- Stop the Steal events, who are now being promoted up the ranks of the GOP.\par \par \uc2 So, the situation is far more dire than it even was then as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has continued to double down on the big lies and the polemics and the disinformation. And his party continues to go with them and to roll with these deranged theories.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Congressman \uc2 Jamie Raskin\uc2  who sits on that committee that will be shifting into public hearings very soon, thank you very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 RASKIN: Thanks for having me, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still ahead, the primary race in Texas where abortion rights loom large as the only remaining anti-abortion Democrat in the entire House caucus faces a runoff primary challenge. That`s coming up.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You may have seen this story about a tragedy in New York City this weekend. A 48-year-old man named Daniel Enriquez was shot and killed on a subway train in what appears to have been a totally unprovoked attack. The suspect remains at large after he fled when the train pulled into a lower Manhattan station, according to police.\par \par \uc2 It`s a brutal and horrifying crime. And in light of the spike in gun violence in recent years, as well as national interest in New York`s new Mayor Eric Adams, the story made headlines across the country understandably. There was another terrible tragedy here in New York that has not received as much coverage.\par \par \uc2 On Saturday 32-year-old David Reichman died after completing the Brooklyn half marathon. Police say he collapsed shortly before 9:00 a.m. after crossing the finish line at Coney Island. Reichman was transported to the hospital where he was pronounced dead. We don`t yet know the cause of his death. The medical examiner is still investigating. But we do know the Fire Department took 16 other runners to the hospital and that the race took place amid concerns and warnings about unseasonably warm weather.\par \par \uc2 New York Road Runner is the organizers of the half marathon, urged participants for repair for the forecast of heat. According to the National Weather Service, during the first hours of the race, temperatures were in the low 60s -- in the low 60s, low 70s, but the relative humidity was above 95 percent.\par \par \uc2 And by the afternoon, New York City saw a near-record high temperature for this time of year reaching 90 degrees in Central Park. Extreme heat, particularly when combined with extreme humidity is brutal on the human body, just a physiological fact. And it is getting hotter all the time around the world.\par \par \uc2 Just look at what has been happening in South Asia which also hasn`t gotten a ton of coverage. This March was the hottest March in India since the country began keeping records more than a century ago. Pakistan had its hottest April since records began. In some areas, temperatures have soared past 120 degrees, and the effects are awful and wide ranging. At least 90 people have died from the heat across India and Pakistan.\par \par \uc2 But also a melting glacier in the Himalayan Mountains triggered flash flooding, destroying houses and farmland, and causing this bridge in Pakistan to collapse. Increased demand and stress on the power grid in India is causing widespread power cuts. And the heat also scorched India`s wheat crop, leaving the country the world`s second-largest wheat producer to ban exports at a time when the war in Ukraine has increased concerns about wheat shortages.\par \par \uc2 Of course, extreme heat of this kind is just precisely what climate change looks like. It`s what it is. It`s here now. A new study from the British Meteorological Service shows that, "Human influence on the climate has increased the likelihood of extreme temperatures in the region by a factor of about 100." And that means a record heat event that would occur approximately every 300 years now occurs about every three years.\par \par \uc2 Having now lived through two years of a deadly pandemic, we`ve all seen up close the difference in attention for the kind of diffuse havoc wreaked by infectious disease and the gruesome spectacle of a mass murderer shooting like the one in Buffalo last weekend. Now, the climate crisis will produce its fair share of spectacles, but a lot of the damage is and will be more like the slow moving tragedy of COVID.\par \par \uc2 [20:50:14]\par \par \uc2 There`ll be more frequent and more severe heatwaves and devastation and death will follow. And this is an urgent crisis every bit as urgent is a very real threat posed right now by gun violence. And as a society, we must figure out how to focus our attention on it before it`s too late.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It was right around this time, like literally at this hour 21 days ago that we got that bombshell report from Politico on the leaked draft Supreme Court opinion which would overturn Roe v. Wade. And tomorrow, we will get our first real look at how that bombshell has reverberated and how it might play in the Midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 The lone anti-abortion rights Democrat in the House, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  of Texas, is defending his seat against 28-year-old immigration attorney Jessica Cisneros. Now, money has been pouring into this campaign since it started, over $7 million for the two candidates. According to the latest \uc2 FEC\uc2  filings,l Cuellar has raised $3.1 million, Cisneros $4.5 million. And in the days immediately following the leak of the draft Supreme Court opinion that would ban abortion, well Cisneros raised nearly three times what Cuellar pulled in, more than $342,000 to just over $136,000.\par \par \uc2 Cecile Richards is the former president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , co-chair of American Bridge 21st Century, born and raised Texan, now the National Finance chair for Beto O`Rourke`s campaign for governor. And she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Cecile, obviously, we`re in a strange interregnum period between the leak of that opinion and when it might come out. We don`t know if that`s going to be the final ruling or not. But it is very much focused, I think, everyone who cares about abortion rights, and even those who don`t think about a lot on the -- on it.\par \par \uc2 How do you view this race tomorrow night where you have the last Republican -- the last Democrat opposed to abortion rights facing a primary challenger who is pro-choice?\par \par \uc2 CECILE RICHARDS, CO-CHAIR, AMERICAN BRIDGE 21ST CENTURY: Well, I think Jessica Cisneros has been a fantastic candidate, and she`s generated enormous enthusiasm because of her support for women`s rights and worker`s rights. You have to remember though, Chris, too this is a district that was really -- it was drawn by Republicans. \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  was Rick Perry`s Secretary of State. They managed to draw this district, so it`s heavily centered in Laredo where he`s from, where his brother is the sheriff. So, he has that big advantage. But I just -- I think that she`s running a really strong race, and we will see.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, it`s going to be an interesting -- it`s an interesting test case. It`s the first time you`ve got this on the ballot since this has happened. And I guess the broader question, right is, look, this is obviously important to millions of people, women, but not just women, in the Democratic Party and outside the Democratic Party, people who are active voters and aren`t.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, there`s this question of what does it mean if this happens, this seismic reversal of a fundamental constitutional right. What does it mean politically? And I don`t think anyone knows the answer. But as someone who`s worked in this space for years, how are you thinking about it?\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: I think that`s exactly right, Chris. And I don`t think this one particular congressional run off is going to tell us because, of course, what`s happening now is people who live in states where abortion rights have always been secure, which is not the case in Texas, are now waking up and realizing all it takes is a Republican governor and a Republican- dominated legislature and you could lose all of your rights.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Pennsylvania is, of course, a perfect state to look at where they`ve now nominated a Republican nominee, who is for ending all abortion access in the state of Pennsylvania. And that is -- you know, this is where you look at independent voters, people who weren`t necessarily motivated to go vote -- who are going to vote this November because of this issue.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s true in a lot of the battleground states, Arizona, Georgia and Nevada. Suddenly, the thought that your state can become a state where safe and legal abortion is no longer available, is just beginning to sink in on voters.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. There`s reporting about Democratic Party allied groups spending $6 million early on Mastriano who you just mentioned. And it seems to me like, you know, this is as tangible as it gets. Like, as Josh Shapiro said on this program, and he`s running against Mastriano to be governor, you know, there will be a full abortion ban bill that will pass out of the Republican legislature in Pennsylvania, and it will either be signed into law by Governor Mastriano or vetoed by Democratic governor. And those are the stakes this fall like right away should Roe be overturned as it appears it will be.\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: Exactly, Chris. This is not a theoretical question. And you look at the voters that we need to energize, Democrats need to energize going into November, young people and women, and Mastriano his candidacy is going to do just that. And it is becoming very clear that the Republican rhetoric around abortion is not the -- not the question anymore. It`s what they have now promised to do in state to state.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s -- I think there`s going to be a lot more to happen in these next months to come as abortion bans begin to get passed throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, we saw one passed in Oklahoma incredibly severe. Also, the bands that are -- that are currently popular are some of the most aggressive, even more aggressive than anything pre-Roe which is another thing for people to keep their eyes on.\par \par \uc2 Cecile Richards, thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220524ei5n00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 20, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Yamiche Alcindor, Matt Bradley, Clint Watts, Garrett  Haake \par \uc2 9,466 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Plus, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  kicks off his first trip to Asia as president to rally and reassure American allies, as U.S. officials warn of a possible nuclear test by North Korea. Of course, North Korea is in the middle of a COVID battle. We're live in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 And Russia makes some games in eastern Ukraine amid a grinding battle that President Zelenskyy says has turned into a region -- turn that region into hell. What it means for the future of this war and the U.S.'s role in it?\par \par \uc2 Happy Friday. Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I'm Chuck Todd. Right now, both political parties are trying to reshape the political battlefield to their advantage as we approach the midterms. So what does that fight look like? And major issues from abortion to inflation and beyond. Well, we're going to dive into some brand new numbers out today from our NBC poll. For the last two editions of our poll, we've been asking respondents a battery of questions about what sort of qualities and attributes they would like to see that would make them more likely or less likely to support a candidate for Congress.\par \par \uc2 And looking at the numbers it becomes pretty clear, which is huge. Republicans and Democrats would like to lead the way. For Republicans, no surprise. But their most favorable terrain is on inflation and the economy. Among all voters the belief that government should cut spending to combat inflation. And increase domestic oil production to cut gas prices. That's pretty popular across the board.\par \par \uc2 And we know Republicans know this. Because guess what, it's all over their campaign advertising.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE LOMBARDO, SHERIFF OF CLARK COUNTY: \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2  are spending like drunken sailors and inflation is at a 40-year high. As Governor, I won't raise taxes.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Biden's bad decisions are costing Alabama families $5,000 a year. Well, I'm not going to take it.\par \par \uc2 REBECCA KLEEFISCH, FORMER LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR OF WISCONSIN: No amount of buy one get one free deal can overcome what \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  have done.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Thanks a lot, Biden.\par \par \uc2 KLEEFISCH: I'll cut taxes and waste to lower your costs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: For Democrats, their most favorable terrain is actually in positions held by Republicans. Looking at our MDC polls. Voters are not crazy about candidates who believe \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's big lie about 2020. They also don't look favorably on candidates who would like to overturn Roe v. Wade. We only have a draft opinion on Roe right now, but Republican state governments are giving Democrats plenty of examples on abortion rights under Jeopardy.\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, Oklahoma lawmakers passed the most restrictive abortion bill in the country. It's a Texas style measure that bans abortion after fertilization. Democratic ads are already leaning hard into abortion rights being on the ballot this fall.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: If Senate Republicans win in November, they will light women's rights on fire.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Jessica Cisneros is the pro-choice candidate who will fight for our health care and always protect our rights to make our own decisions.\par \par \uc2 SARAH GODLEWSKI, STATE TREASURER OF WISCONSIN: I'm at the Supreme Court where it looks like Ron Johnson is going to get exactly what he wants, overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 As your Senator, I will never sit by and allow our rights to be stripped away.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So bottom line here and it may be obvious, but we've got the data to prove it. It sure looks like Republicans are going to be fighting to make these midterms all about the economy and inflation. Well, Democrats are going to be fighting to make it a bit more cultural about abortion, and Trump. So let's dive in. Joining me on set to dig deeper into these numbers is NBC Washington Correspondent Yamiche Alcindor.\par \par \uc2 Democratic strategist \uc2 Xochitl Hinojosa\uc2 . And Republican strategist Brendan Buck, who was an advisor under former House speakers, Ryan and Boehner. Yamiche, I want to put out a couple other things up here. It's that it sort of reinforces the point we're making here just to understand on Trump and abortion rights for Democrats and on government spending and oil production.\par \par \uc2 First, let's put up full screen to best terrain for Republicans while willing to spell the candidates of 55-point advantage on inflation, using government -- downsizing government spending 52-point advantage on gas prices. Those are some big advantages. Here are some of the better sort of advantages for Democrats sort of reinforcing our point about Trump and Roe v. Wade here, minus 35 on Trump, minus 26 on Roe. That's still somewhat lopsided.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, MSNBC POLITICAL CONTRIBUTOR: That's still lopsided. And when you when you opened up by saying Democrats want to make this about cultural issues, and Trump and Republicans want to make it about the economy, I almost had to say, wait, is -- Chuck, did we get that flipped? Because obviously Republicans have been the ones wanting to talk about culture war, wanting to talk about race theory and abortion.\par \par \uc2 But now that they've sort of had these big wins, they don't really want to talk about those wins. And as a result, you have Democrats now reacting to them. When I've been out on the campaign trail, this is the thing that vexes Democratic voters, they say that Democrats are being so reactive, they're not being aggressive like Republicans, and as a result, it's only after things have gone bad. Only after you have election deniers being elected only after you have Roe v. Wade likely to be overturned.\par \par \uc2 Are they really getting sort of the fire in their bellies? Meanwhile, you have Republicans looking at the numbers saying, well, let's just argue at the idea that President Biden hasn't done enough on inflation. And you have of course, President Biden in Asia today making an international trip very domestic talking about \uc2 Samsung\uc2  Opening plant in Texas. He's going to be at Hyundai talking about their investments in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:04]\par \par \uc2 So, you see President Biden trying his best to will this but it really the numbers tell the story here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What's interesting here -- and Brendan, I'll start with -- to be it means Republicans, if they're disciplined, they got a winning message. But they get distracted by these culture wars.\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER ADVISER TO SPEAKERS RYAN AND BOEHNER: Ye. But yes, the things that show up in their ads, as you're saying is they put money behind are the issues that we're talking about. So, the abortion issue gives Democrats something when I think a few weeks ago, we would say they didn't really have anything. But the thing that they have to think about is, what are the issues that people are voting based on?\par \par \uc2 And you also asked in this poll, what are your top issues? The top issue was inflation. The second issue is jobs in the economy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: You know, abortion is higher than it was but people only vote on one or two things. And so the challenge for Democrats is how do you make this issue where maybe you have an advantage, the issue of the year. And that's a big, big lift, when inflation in the economy touches everybody.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Xochitl, we won't even -- if John Roberts pulls a rabbit out of his hat and preserves Roe, that would sort of complicate it seems the path that Democrats think might be the best one they have.\par \par \uc2 XOCHITL HINOJOSA, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, Roe escalates the state's -- the stakes in this election. And I think that -- and everybody understands that and you're seeing Democrats running as already and trying to make this an issue. But Democrats also understand that if they don't talk about rising costs in, you know, our day to day lives, then we aren't going to win the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 But I would also look at the map. If you're looking at the Senate map right now, it is very, very -- if I were a Democrat, I'd be very, very happy about it. Just because we are protecting seats, that where we have strong Democrats in place and states that Biden won. And if you're looking at what just happened in Pennsylvania right now, we are coming out united in support of Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 And while Republicans are still trying to scramble to see what is going on there. And one thing I want to say about your poll and Trump is that Democrats aren't going to be making this race about Trump. It is Trump that is going to insert himself into this race and actually help Democrats in the long run, because Republicans can't necessarily control him. And he does want to be out on the campaign trail ahead of the midterm elections, which is very problematic for Republicans right now,\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to put up a couple other full screens here. These were the most popular candidate qualities among all voters. So it gives you an idea of the general election. Here's the top seven. We put that up first. As you can see, actually, that's not the one guys, you put up number three, number four. I wanted number five on the full screen here. No worries. This is -- this is -- that's all right. That's why we number the things.\par \par \uc2 And you'll see here funding the police is number one. Controlling inflation, number two. Lowering gas prices, number three. Supporting the bipartisan infrastructure is number four, Yamiche. So, it takes to the fourth mount there, it's still not the right one guys. Let's full screen for it, sorry. I'm looking for number five, it's the one we're working on. You have to get down to the fourth most popular sheet before you get to something that is a Democratic success story. The top three issues are basically advantage Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: And not only do they advantage Republicans, but it really shows that the country in some ways isn't a place where it's sort of backtrack when we -- when we think about sort of the conversations, we were having about reforming the police for policing and sort of supporting the police to be the number one issue. It also tells you why President Biden has said over and over again that he wants to fund the police.\par \par \uc2 That, of course, has angered progressives. And I will say a lot of African Americans who he needs to vote, especially in places like Philly and Pittsburgh, which we were, you know, Chuck, because you see voters saying, OK, you're trying to pivot and when these Republican voters or these independent voters when you really need to be focusing on what we want. But in some ways, it tells you that is sort of the challenge that Democrats face which is that the number one issues are the issues, you know, policing the economy that people feel sort of comfortable and trustworthy on when it comes to Republicans.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let me put up the full screen number five again, here, Brendan, as you could see. I mean, this is -- you look at it and the best Democratic issues are in the bottom half of that list, the best Republican issues are in the top half at that point. I mean, it's -- it does sort of show you the path if Republican candidates can be disciplined.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: And the risk for Democrats is if you want to talk about an issue that's fourth or fifth, you look like you're out of touch because you're not talking about first or second.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: And I know \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wants to talk about the economy, but they don't have good answers for it either. And Republicans have lots of ammunition to use against them. I mean, my gosh, the thing that they were trying to pass all year long was even more spending that Republicans were able to say that we stopped them. So yes, if they can be disciplined, this is a cakewalk and that's why I think Republicans aren't going to try to change the conversation at all, they're very happy with the direction.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well and I do. I'm going to put up the negative side of this and this is to me, Xochitl, why we saw the words ultra MAGA. OK? Let me put this up. You know, after defunding the police is the least popular thing you can do, OK? That -- we've got that. But then the next four issues, Trump winning the election is a minus 35. A \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  endorsement is a minus 27. Right? Overturning Roe v. Wade minus 26.\par \par \uc2 A Trump endorsement minus 20. Then you get down to AOC and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , but it's sort of two through -- two through five there, Xochitl, cheap I see why there is this idea that, well, if you can create the -- do you want ultra MAGA policies?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:08]\par \par \uc2 I get. But that's a hard sell. It takes time.\par \par \uc2 HINOJOSA: Well, they -- you're pulling absolutely right here. That's exactly what the White House is seeing right now. They understand that if they talk about the far right and the far-right sort of taking over, if the far right takes over the ultra MAGA, takes over the House and the Senate, you will overturn Roe v. Wade. All these things will happen. We -- our elections will be at stake.\par \par \uc2 And if you put Republican Secretaries of State, they might try to overturn these elections. So what Democrats are doing and what the White House is doing right now is they're trying to raise the stakes, they're trying to tell people listen, don't stay home in the midterm elections. There is a clear choice between Democrats and Republicans right now. And if you don't go out and vote, you could end up with this ultra MAGA Congress that could impact you in your daily lives.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, we have -- we do have a primary coming up on Tuesday. We got Brendan here. He's Mr. Georgia. Camp winning as big as he's winning, Trump sort of throwing a temper tantrum about it. What does that mean for the general?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes. So we've seen a lot of candidates get nominated who were Stop the Steal or January 6. I don't think you pay a price in election, in Republican primaries for grabbing that position, but there's not much of a reward. And Brian Kemp is a pretty popular conservative governor. And \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  was running after him basically only on the idea that he didn't do enough to stop the steal.\par \par \uc2 That's not a very compelling message. And what you're seeing in Georgia is that while again, you don't pay a penalty for that, it's not very inspiring to Republican voters to just talk about what happened in 2020. And one interesting thing, I think, is actually the Secretary of State.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Raffensperger --\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Raffensperger.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He's got a heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: This is the person who was the face of fighting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on this election, and he's got a very good chance, he's running against a House Freedom Caucus member, very conservative, Republican. And he's got a very good chance to win because I think people are -- he's not running as a moderate. Don't get me wrong, but I think people are sick of talking about 2020.\par \par \uc2 And maybe the long-term consequence there for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is if he can't let that go, people aren't really interested talking about that anymore. It could -- it could be wrapped around his neck. So very positive signs, I think down in Georgia,\par \par \uc2 TODD: it is fascinating to watch what Republicans are trying to do. You know, they're running in the interests. He is running an ad and I show it to folks a little bit later, where they're like, we know Reverend Warnock has got a nice story. But -- and it's just sort of like, oh, wow, I mean, it -- Georgia seems is going to be, I think, a unique problem for Republicans and probably the Democrat's best chance to have a decent night.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: That's -- and it's really interesting because in some ways, you're talking about the person who was preaching at Martin Luther King's church. So, in some ways, they know that they have to be very --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: They can't make it personal.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Right. They can't make it personal. He is someone who is very clear about his sort of moral arc. And when he thinks that being said, I think what Brennan said about the idea that you're not hurt by questioning the credibility of American democracy, also, I think makes Georgia interesting because it was the place where you saw so many people, so many Republicans come out and be the face of the resistance to say, we're not going to get -- let an election be stolen.\par \par \uc2 We're not going to give you the 11,000 votes. And by the way, we're going to somehow miraculously put out the recording to show everybody what you're trying to do. I think it will be interesting to see sort of how \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  leans into that and whether or not in the end in the in these closing days whether or not he makes this sort of a moral argument about the why, you know, they might not want to talk about the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 It is all about civil rights, it's all about who they're targeting to take their votes away.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I tell you, Xochitl though, but if you have a ticket of camping, Raffensperger won. It does make it a little bit harder to hang that around camp and even \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 's neck. Doesn't it?\par \par \uc2 HINOJOSA: Well, I think that's right. But at the same time, I don't think that Reverend Warnock necessarily need that -- needs that. He's a strong candidate who's raising right now and he can on his own talk about what's at stake. I mean, that the Senate as a whole could hang on a seat like Georgia. Right? And so, he will make those cases he has a compelling story that NRSC is absolutely right. Thank you. NRSC.\par \par \uc2 And I think that you'll continue to see him as a strong candidate and Republicans know that they have a long shot with a seat.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I have to tell you, Brendan, actually it made me long for all politics was like hey, it's OK to like somebody on the other side of the aisle. It was sort of like -- it was so striking to actually see, it's like -- but I think that shows you how his personal story is connected to voters.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes. But \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 's best thing he has going for them. I hate to say this, go Dogs, is \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , not a very strong candidate, not someone who has any experience doing this. Someone who throughout his primary has kind of hidden from the press hasn't done his duck debates. When the spotlight is on him. I'm a little worried about how he reacts.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is the spotlight ever going to get on him completely because of Camp Abrams though?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Well, yes, I think so. There's a lot at stake. You know, the Senate could be decided by this and he is a high-profile person in the state.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:01]\par \par \uc2 You know, certainly the governor's race will draw a lot of attention, but this is going to be one of the primary deciders of who controls Congress.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there a number that Walker has to get -- if Walker doesn't hit 60, would you think that's a problem?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: That would be a big problem because I don't think anybody -- he's got someone like Latham Saddler, Gary Black running again. Some people who have pretty low name I.D. and have not really put up much of a fight against him. So, if he's not -- if he's not well above 60, I would -- I would be surprised. But I think the party is going to rally around him. I think they have to and I think he's going to have to rely on camp driving.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Xochitl, the biggest thing in the Democratic side of the island on Tuesday is actually the Cisneros square race, right? In many ways, what are you going to take away from it, depending on the winner?\par \par \uc2 HINOJOSA: Well, first of all, it is Texas, it is South Texas, and it's an area that has -- they're socially conservative. So I think it'll be interesting to see whether --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- close race, don't you?\par \par \uc2 HINOJOSA: I do think it is going to be a close race. But the reality is that (INAUDIBLE) has put a lot of money into this race. I'm not necessarily sure that Jessica Cisneros can, you know, can overcome that. But with that said, I do think that -- well, you know, the choice -- the choice message was front and center especially as her closing argument, which rightfully so in a place like Texas is should.\par \par \uc2 What I would just say in South Texas, is that that is a very socially conservative area, and I'm not sure Latinos will go for it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, this is quite a weekend. We got Elizabeth Warren for Mandela Barnes from Wisconsin. We got \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  for Jessica Cisneros, Texas. And by the way, we have Sarah Palin looking for Alaska votes in Georgia with \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  this week.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: It just tells you sort of how wild all of this has been and how much this is sort of just an interesting midterm season.\par \par \uc2 TODD: There's -- the \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  thing, I'll let it go.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Well, also when Brendan was talking about the fact that \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  is this sort of unknown person who hasn't gotten a real vetting. Maybe he won't and I was like, well, that also could be Trump. But of course, Trump wasn't running against the person who was preaching again at Martin Luther King's church though.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: -- \uc2 David Perdue\uc2  decided to run for the senate. Anyway, Yamiche, Xochitl, Brendan. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still to come. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  kicks off his first trip to Asia as president starting in South Korea. We're live in Seoul next. Later, its Dem versus Dem and one of the bluest states that redrawn congressional -- in New York. That set to be approved today is going to spark some serious drama on the Hill that will reach the leadership of the House Democrats. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:32]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. President Biden kicked off his five-day Asia tour with his first stop is Seoul, South Korea. His message so far in day one, manufacturing supply chains and the global economy. Obviously thinking about the midterms, a little bit and domestic politics. The president toured a \uc2 Samsung\uc2  semiconductor plant today, a model for a plant being built here at home in Texas, and he touted the company's commitment to invest and create new jobs in the United States.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Our two nations work together to make the best most advanced technology in the world. And this factory is proof of that. And that gives both Republican Korea and the United States a competitive edge in the global economy. If we can keep our supply chains resilient, reliable and secure.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The president also renewed his call to Congress to pass the Bipartisan Innovation Act, a bill that the administration believes will help the U.S. better compete with China. It has a ton of bipartisan support. This has just been bogged down by typical ridiculous Senate politics. NBC White House Correspondent Mike Memoli is traveling with the president. He joins us now from Seoul.\par \par \uc2 We also have Bonnie Glaser. Director of the Asia program at the German Marshall Fund. But Mike, let me get started with you. Obviously, you're in the middle of the night there. So we know. Tell us what's on the agenda tomorrow and where we go from here.\par \par \uc2 MIKE MEMOLI, NBC WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, happy Saturday, Chuck here from Seoul. Yes, indeed, the president will kick off later today sort of the more traditional official portion of the trip and North Korea is expected to be the focus of his meeting with South Korea's new President, President Yoon. He'll be laying a wreath also at a memorial for U.S. service members, of course, who died in the Korean conflict meeting with U.S. service members.\par \par \uc2 But you talked about this White House trying to focus a little bit on domestic politics while the president is here. I would say a lot about domestic politics. While the President is here, you've done these foreign trips with the president before, Chuck. You know, that often on the course of a trip like this, there might be an announcement about an economic package back home involving companies based in the country of presidents visiting.\par \par \uc2 But the president started his trip on Friday here in South Korea. And he -- before he leaves here on Sunday, he will end it with companies announcing not just investments in the U.S. economy, but plants in the U.S. Jobs that will be in the U.S. And we've also seen this as a White House, dealing first and foremost with inflation as a challenge now and they've tried to put that as much as they can on Putin's price hike, as they call it.\par \par \uc2 But remember so much of the supply chain crisis that it took up the last year and a half as well. It's something that they route in China, the disruptions because of COVID, the lockdowns that really snarled the global shipping and system for bringing goods to market here in the United States. So, that's really what the president is trying to put a spotlight on here. Yes, it's good to have alliances with democracies in this region, as well.\par \par \uc2 But it's also important to make things in America. That's something Biden talked about a lot on the campaign trail. And he's trying to bring that message home by saying these companies making investments in the U.S. will make us stronger at home, but also help the global economy as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Mike Memoli in the middle of the night on Saturday morning there. Mike, thank you. Let me bring in Bonnie Glaser here. And Bonnnie, I'm going to start with first North Korea and I actually want to start with COVID in North Korea. I know that we're -- there's a concern about a missile test. And it's sort of typical with the typical Kim Jong-un looking for a little attention right now.\par \par \uc2 But the fact is, this looks like we're about to have a human catastrophe in North Korea. What can the world do about this in this closed society that feels as if this thing is spreading, I think -- I saw a stat today, two million people may have it right now?\par \par \uc2 BONNIE GLASER, GERMAN MARSHALL FUND DIRECTOR, ASIA PROGRAM: North Korea has only recently acknowledged that it has any cases at all. And we don't know if the numbers that they are providing to the outside world are reliable. But it's hard to believe the COVID isn't rampant in North Korea. They don't have the health infrastructure or the vaccines to cope with it. So the world can provide humanitarian assistance and the new president in South Korea, I believe has already offered to provide assistance, especially in the form of vaccines and protective medical equipment.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me move to what I think the president's goal here. I mean, number one is as somebody who - I remember when President \uc2 Obama\uc2  went to Asia to unveil the Trans-Pacific Partnership and it seemed like it was going to be the beginning of what would become a check on China economically with the United States and some Southeast Asian countries and of course the domestic politics between \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  essentially eviscerated that treaty.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:10]\par \par \uc2 And now there's a baby step version of this, Bonnie. Can this be the beginning of an Economic Alliance? That is a check on China? Or is it too small of a -- of an idea?\par \par \uc2 GLASER: Well, the Indo Pacific economic framework that President Biden is going to launch in Japan is an important step toward writing rules in ways that favor open economies, and strengthen the rules based multilateral trading system. But it doesn't provide or won't provide access to U.S. markets which is what countries in the region want. They welcome the initiative, the interest in the United States and stepping up economically in the region, but they prefer the United States to return to the Trans- Pacific Partnership.\par \par \uc2 And indeed, many countries, I think, hope that the United States will.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think this is really the -- our intent and that we're just sort of taking a step here, seeing if we can get there domestically and then another step down the road?\par \par \uc2 GLACER: I think that the Biden administration has held his cards very close to its chest on this issue. There are obviously political risks in even talking about returning to the CPTPP. And so, I would not be surprised if even after the midterms, that it is not a policy that the Biden administration will pursue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, as someone told me, it is not going to be something, they're not going to pursue something that they want a vote in Congress to have to approve, on something like that. Let me move to the quad. And it's sort of the beginning of what it feels like a security alliance that could continue to grow. What is the best way to grow the quad and to make it more than just sort of an informal meeting of four countries with interest in worrying about China?\par \par \uc2 GLACER: Well, the quad, which includes the United States, Japan, Australia, and India, is now sort of in its second incarnation, it's really focused on providing public goods like vaccines to countries in Southeast Asia, climate is on the agenda, as well as issues like maritime cooperation. But there's more and more going on in the realm of security. And I think that there is potential to do more.\par \par \uc2 These countries are concerned about protecting their interests and supply chains, of course, but also, they are concerned about Chinese assertiveness and potential aggression in the region. And so, I think that there is a lot of potential to grow cooperation and also to include other countries not as members of the quad, but just as working with the quad countries in these working groups that they have set up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And before I let you go, I want to ask, and this is not something we told you we were going to ask about. So my apologies beforehand, but in the past, whenever a U.S. president was going to have a meeting with India, we always went out of our way to make sure we had some sort of event conversation whatever with Pakistan, that's not happening this time. Is this just another sign of just how far away we are?\par \par \uc2 The United States and Pakistan, how far apart they are these days compared to where they were even just five years ago?\par \par \uc2 GLACER: Well, yes, I think that the relationship with the United States and Pakistan has evolved, but more importantly, the relationship with India has evolved. President Biden has had many conversations with India's President Modi, as part of this quad, they are working together very closely. Obviously, Pakistan has been a thorn in the U.S.-India relationship.\par \par \uc2 But I think going forward, this is going to be an area where, you know, there are other -- this is one issue on which the U.S. and India agree to disagree. There will be others. But I think that the Biden administration felt that that phone call or heads up pre notification just was not necessary.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And it shows you how much as your -- as you put it. The U.S. and India relationship is growing closer all the time. Bonnie Glaser with the German Marshall Plan. Always appreciate getting your expertise. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 GLASER: Thank you\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next to Russia gets ready to turn its threats against Finland into action. They plan to cut off the nation's gas supply just one of many possible consequences. You could see unfold amid \uc2 NATO\uc2 's expansion. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Russia has threatened to retaliate against Finland for pledging to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  and now we may be seeing Russia attempt to make good on that threat. Finland's state-owned energy provider says Russia plans to cut off its gas supply to the country tomorrow. Russian gas exports account for just about 3 percent of Finland's total energy mix, according to European officials.\par \par \uc2 The company gave no reason for the move but the announcement came just two days after Finland and Sweden formally filed applications to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 . Finland would be the third European country cut off by Russia's state-owned gas company. Cut off Poland and Bulgaria last month. All three countries have refused to pay for gas in rubles.\par \par \uc2 Russian forces meanwhile appear to be making progress in their offensive in eastern Ukraine. One intelligence analyst says its troops have captured several villages in the Luhansk province. And President Zelenskyy says Russia is turning the Donbas region into hell. But while Russia may now control much of Mariupol, the protracted battle for the key port city may have come at a steep cost. According to the "\uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," the fierce fighting badly depleted its ranks and now we're seeing talk of Russians looking for older Russians to join the military.\par \par \uc2 Matt Bradley joins me now from Kyiv. \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2  is also here, our national security analyst and former \uc2 U.S. Army\uc2  infantry officer to talk about Russia's military strategy these days.\par \par \uc2 But, Matt, let me start with you and what we're hearing today, and give us some more context of what President Zelenskyy meant when he says the region is just a hell scape.\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes. I mean, we've heard this before. This is a region that has actually been at war since 2014. Remember it was only recently that the Russians made their push out since the actual invasion on February 24th to try to take the entire Donbas region and since 2014 they really had control over 30 percent of what we would consider the Donbas. And that's the Donetsk and Luhansk Oblast in eastern Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Now those 30 percent of that had been under essentially the dominion of Russia under the guise of Russian backed separatists in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 Now it looks like the Russians have really in earnest made a push to take that whole region. And this shouldn't be too much of a surprise because \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  himself had declared that this was his motive. Ever since he withdrew from close to where I am now here in the capital of Kyiv and in other places, in the northern part of this country, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and his military said that they would be concentrating on taking the Donbas region.\par \par \uc2 But now that they've tried, and this was weeks ago that they made this announcement that really their efforts have been so aborted, so faltering, it's gone to show that they haven't learned a lot of the lessons that they should have been taught from their attempts to take Kyiv and other places in the northern and central part of this country. Instead they had a really faltering effort to try to reconstitute their units and to try to make those efforts to retake the Donbas region, and instead it looks as though they went just headlong in with a lot of really damaged forces.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: And didn't try to re-constitute them in any way that would allow them to really make strong advances. So, so far just like around here in the capital, all those weeks and months ago their efforts there have been faltering but now we're starting to see some shifts. We could see -- we just what looks like the real victory over Mariupol, and the Russians have said that 1700 Ukraine troops from underneath that Azovstal steel plant, and even though \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  had declared victory over that southeastern city of Mariupol, it looks as though now they really can declare victory.\par \par \uc2 Now it looks as though they're going to actually be able to move forces from Mariupol to elsewhere in the Donbas region and really start to focus on retaking that. But we got to remember at the same time, Chuck, we haven't actually seen the full thrust, the full appearance of all of the material, all of that really big military equipment that had been promised from Washington, from London, from elsewhere in western Europe. None of that -- only some of it has actually made its way to the eastern part of the country so far and has been used in battle.\par \par \uc2 So we haven't seen the full effort by the Russians in the Donbas and we haven't seen the full effort of the Ukrainians, so we can probably see a lot of more violence and a lot of more civilian death in that region in the weeks and months to come. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, now we understand why President Zelenskyy is describing it as hell now and it looks like it could get worse.\par \par \uc2 But let me move over to \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2  because, Clint, it's one thing for the Russians to win battles right now in the east. The question is whether they're going to be able to hold this region. And we got reports today that they're looking for soldiers, you know, that they will take volunteers up to age 40 now which tells you that they're running out of soldiers, does it not?\par \par \uc2 CLINT WATTS\uc2 , NBC NEWS NATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: That's right, Chuck. They're definitely running out of soldiers. That's because they don't want to do mass conscription. Inside Russia part of that rule is around going from a special operation, that's a limited incursion the way that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  briefed as a defensive action to all-out war. He doesn't really want to do that.\par \par \uc2 I think big picture, though, it speaks to what Matt was talking about. Here in the east they just not underachieving their objectives, rewind 10 days, we see the Ukrainian military doing counteroffensives, taking out some of these swaths of what was Russian held territory, pushing a lot of the Russian artillery away from the center of the city so they can't reach it. Almost threatening this corridor right here.\par \par \uc2 This is a line of logistics and communication from this major Russian military base down to the Izyum access and the original plan, Chuck, was to go from Donetsk to Izyum, a much more bold strategy by where the Russia would try and flank many of these Ukrainian forces here in this pocket of Donbas. That has already failed again. Now that's really a second failure for all of these force there. And when you zoom in, the battle in the coming days and weeks is going to be much more limited.\par \par \uc2 When you watch some of those villages in this Luhansk region right here that are being taken, it seems that Ukraine is trading ground for time as we would say in the defense. If it's not critical you retreat back, you let the Russians take small gains. When they do that they burn more logistics and it opens them up oftentimes for attack. So you will see that they've taken small areas. But these are small movements. This is not the big bold maneuvers that are out there.\par \par \uc2 They'll try and secure pockets. The key town is a town called Sloviansk. It's about right here. Sloviansk is the corridor that if they can get there, they can start to bring out a much more limited encirclement of some of the Ukrainian defensive positions. But I am not sure they can do it. The troops at least on the Russian side, they don't believe in what they're doing, they don't believe in the plan, they don't have good teammates and good leaders. This is just a cobbled together unit. And the Ukrainians have all those things plus U.S. weapons coming in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say, so this is a case where if we have -- if the West has some patience it's going to be brutal, it's going to be bloody but it sounds like you'd rather be the Ukrainians here than the Russians in that over time you expect Ukraine to continue to make advances getting their country back?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Not only that, Chuck. I think this is -- the big debate right now is how far does Ukraine go and how far does the West will support Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 Because when you look big picture, the Russians are trying to set up two areas or breakaway republics which will become part of Russia. One is in around Kherson, they're talking about an independent Kherson Republic. Same in Zaporizhzhia Oblast, a region which they don't actually control Zaporizhzhia but this region to create this land bridge linking all of these places together.\par \par \uc2 However, for the Russians, the big problem, the Ukrainians are counterattacking in several locations. And in and around Melitopol which is about right in here, you also see Ukrainians fighting in the rear areas of the Russians, attacking some of these so-called Russian planted officials to take over these republics. So I'm not sure, big picture. When you watch what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  wants that's essentially what he wants is to bring this area here, he wants to unite all of this and try and bring it together over time. He may not be able to do it. And for the Ukrainians they've heard this story before.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Whenever \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  says OK, let's stop, let's negotiate, that's just pausing until he can rebuilt combat power and he's never stayed true on any of those. So for the Ukrainians I think they believe they need to advance all the way back to the territory that was once theirs.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. If you're on the Russian side of things, where's the point -- and if \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  came to his commanders and said I need to declare victory and go home, what's the best way to do it for him in the east?\par \par \uc2 WATTS: Yes, it's really in these provinces. And Chuck, just to put this in perspective, rather than doing the very stupid "Hail Mary" plan to try and go for Kyiv and surround half of the country, if Russia had just taken all its pores and poured into this area here, this is the Donbas region, he would already had it. He probably would had it in two to three days. Just all of that combat power focus he would have had it.\par \par \uc2 Now I'm not even sure he can take all of Donbas. This is the Luhansk region. You can kind of see that border right there. That is not that much more ground for them to take. But that's only half of the Donbas. Here, this is the border all the way out to the western part of Donbas, all of eastern Ukraine. The Ukrainians are fighting here, they're reinforcing, the weapons are pouring in, as I was talking about.\par \par \uc2 I don't know that he can get here which makes it very hard for him to trade off and say I'm done. And if he tries to take these areas down here and turned them into part of Russia, he would have essentially a Ukrainian insurgency in his rear area that he would have to defend. I'm not sure that they can last on that either. So it brings the question of what happens come July, August when sanctions are really kicking in? What can Putin really do here over time with 40-year-old soldiers that he's trying to reinforce in these broken units?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. How this all goes in the summer both with Western patience and whether the Russian people start to feel the pain is going to be fascinating to watch.\par \par \uc2 Clint Watts\uc2 , at the map for us. Clint, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, the former mayor of New York City has decided he wants to become a member of Congress. He's throwing his hat into what is becoming an extremely messy Democratic primary map for Congress all over New York state, mostly in the New York City region, as party leaders brace for a series of potentially brutal inter-party fights that will drag leadership into taking sides.\par \par \uc2 Those details are after this. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:46:29]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BILL DE BLASIO, FORMER NEW YORK CITY MAYOR: People are hurting. They need help, they need help fast, and they need leaders who can actually get them help now, and know how to do it. I do know how to do it from years of serving the people of this city and so today I'm declaring my candidacy for Congress in the 10th Congressional District of New York.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Former New York City Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  announced he's running for a New York City congressional seat. He did that on MSNBC earlier this morning, joining an extremely messy set of Democratic primaries that are going to put some prominent New York City lawmakers against each other.\par \par \uc2 De Blasio, he's running to represent the 10th District. Right now not the current 10th District. The new one. Because changes in the boundaries of the 10th District are part of the new New York congressional map that's slated to be approved by a judge later today and unlike other newly outlined New York City district where two incumbent Democrats are vying for one nomination, this district would be an open seat.\par \par \uc2 It will cover part of Lower Manhattan as well as parts of West Brooklynn. The 10th District was previously represented by longtime congressman and Judiciary Committee chairman \uc2 Jerry Nadler\uc2  but under the new map his Upper West Side will now be part of the 12th District. That would pit him against another longtime representative who's mostly got the Upper East, \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So suddenly it's a primary of the Upper West versus the Upper East. Meanwhile the head of the DCCC \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  finds himself roiled in a potentially messy inter-party fight with his Democratic colleague \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2 . And that's just a taste of the Democratic infighting in and around Capitol Hill that could set -- be set off by this new map.\par \par \uc2 We're going to have more on all of this messiness after the break. You're watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:43]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we mentioned before the break, the likely New York congressional map has exposed some deep cracks in the Democratic Party overall as some established New York incumbents are being forced to face off against each other, we have to bring in our senior Capitol Hill correspondent for this.\par \par \uc2 Normally, look, you love covering House races.\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, NBC NEWS SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But this one is going to potentially break up the party. \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  is in leadership, New York City Democrat, \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , a Hudson Valley Democrat, he's DCCC chair. This is a mess. Can it get resolved without Maloney losing his job as DCCC chair?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: \uc2 Sean Maloney\uc2  may get some help from the special master today when they released the final version of this map.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do we think it could change?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I think there's a possibility. I mean, the special master has not made any public comment except to say they are listening to all the incoming that they've been getting and they have been getting a ton. So it's possible you could see a little bit of --\par \par \uc2 TODD: And the real incoming has to do with how many congressional districts represented by African-Americans were broken up.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes. This is a huge part of it and this is what \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  has been most upset about. I mean, if you've been to New York City you know, it's a collection of tiny neighborhoods. Now whatever you think about the boroughs, it's the neighborhoods. And you've seen historically black neighborhoods get essentially cracked by this map, both in Brooklyn and in the Bronx and other areas.\par \par \uc2 And so that has been Jeffries' big focus. And so I think actually the civil rights angle to this is probably more like to push the special master back than \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  which district he's going to jump into. But he's got a powerful ally in this. Speaker Pelosi was asked three or four different ways about this yesterday by reporters and she has defended him and said that he can still do his job.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And run in the district where he lives.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, what other response could she have, though? I mean, I was sort of thinking about it. No matter -- you're in her seat.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you throw your DCCC chair under the bus five -- you know, we're not that long before the general election, right?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You can argue that's probably already one of the worst jobs in Washington to try to be holding together the Democratic caucus. And forget about expanding the map, but just trying to hold on to that majority.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I don't know who else could be qualified, who else would want that job if not Maloney who's got his own operation.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: For \uc2 Steny Hoyer\uc2  to oversee or something like that.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: My gosh, yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Because you just -- as you said, who would take it now.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes. I mean, it's going to be a very difficult assignment and made all the more difficult if, you know, you still got to have these member-on- member primaries that he's going to have to manage.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So it sounds like you think or perhaps based on reporting that if any change made that makes Maloney save face here he can grab it and say, no, no, no, this district looks good now and I'll do it. I can't wait to support \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2  over here.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You know, they could do a little carve-out line of just his house and put his house back in his -- you know, what was left of his original district and that might be enough. But he's faced so much blowback from --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ritchie Torres, a member of Congress.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right. Who's not somebody who goes out and starts trouble either. I mean, he basically tweeted all but accusing \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  of racism by suggesting that \uc2 Mondaire Jones\uc2  might somehow be a better fit for a different district which he had already won, right. So, I mean, this is an absolute debacle, and we haven't even talked about the two committee chairs who are going to have to run against each other in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And here's another part of this that people don't realize. If they're having to spend all this money, this is fewer dollars that these committee chairs would be giving to the DCCC, which is essentially free hard money getting a little political wonky here.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: But that's important dollars that suddenly are going to be poured into beating up each other.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And these are prime --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Nadler-Maloney?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: These are prime fundraising positions.\par \par \uc2 TODD: My god.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: These are people who have prime committee spots to raise money and to give to their colleagues, and if you're running a big expensive primary all through the summer, you're not doing that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: The irony here, Nadler and Maloney had to fend off progressive challengers in the last couple of cycles. They're not going to be alone in this race. I don't think. This is just going to be one of these real Upper West, Upper East, and I think progressives is going to come in there and watch out.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, and arguably the most liberal city of the country, there have been generations of Democrats waiting for either one of those two lawmakers to potentially retire and clear up --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Any inkling of that, by the way?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Of them retiring? I think there was --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Anybody who's thinking about retiring?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: The thought was perhaps that Nadler might have been looking towards the exit a little bit earlier on. He's had family health issues and things of that nature that made people think perhaps that he was getting near the end of his tenure. But he went quick to say that he was going to run in this district, even before Maloney had done so. There's been some, you know, back and forth this week that he didn't even give a head's up. That doesn't sound like somebody who's eyeing the exits.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And correct me if I'm wrong, and \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  obviously wouldn't say this, but if he wins that congressional district, does he become the member of Congress for Wall Street?\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: That's correct. \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 , the man of Wall Street.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Garrett Haake, happy Friday.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: We'll be busy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, we will.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We'll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC's coverage will continue with Katy Tur right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 KATY TUR, MSNBC HOST: Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220521ei5k00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for May 19, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Julia Ainsley \par \uc2 8,279 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAMON HEWITT, LAWYERS` COMMITTEE FOR CIVIL RIGHTS UNDER THE LAW: I said earlier that democracy is a promise. Democracy is also a choice. We have a choice when it comes to election administration. We can either strengthen democracy and make it easier to vote and administer elections, or we can make it harder. As policymakers you can make a choice, a choice in favor of democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Attorney Damon Hewitt gets tonight`s "LAST WORD." THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the new questions from the January 6 investigation, including whether a sitting member of Congress led a tour at the Capitol just one day before the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the next key primary now just days away, another Trump endorsement facing another test, with some in the GOP fuming over his picks.\par \par \uc2 And then the comedy of George Carlin, possibly more relevant today than ever before. My interview with Judd Apatow and Kelly Carlin on her father`s legacy, as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. 173 days until the midterm elections. Tonight, the January 6 committee wants to talk to another Republican congressman. The panel has asked Georgia Congressman Barry Loudermilk to come in for an interview about a capitol tour that it says took place on January 5, the day before the riot. The Capitol was closed at the time because of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to the Congressman the committee writes this, based on our review of evidence in the Select Committee`s possession, we believe you`ll have information regarding a tour you led through parts of the Capitol complex on January 5. The Committee adds it has evidence directly contradicting claims from Republicans who insisted that security video showed no tours before the attack.\par \par \uc2 Allegations about those tours were made just days after the riot by Democratic Congresswoman \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2 . Here`s what she said way back then.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKIE SHERRILL\uc2 : I also intend to see that those members of Congress who embedded him those members of Congress who had groups coming through the capitol that I saw on January 5, and reconnaissance for the next day. There`s members of Congress that incited this violent crowd. Those members of Congress that attempted to help our president undermine our democracy. I`m going to say that they`re held accountable and if necessary, ensure that they don`t serve in Congress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Late today, Congressman Loudermilk responded to the committee he said he brought constituents into a House office building on the fifth, but added they never entered the Capitol. He also asked her \uc2 Capitol Police\uc2  to release security tapes of that day. He had comes just as Axios reports former Trump Attorney General Bill Barr is in talks to cooperate with the January 6 panel. And POLITICO says House investigators have obtained official White House photographs, including some from that very day.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  will face his next big primary test in Georgia on Tuesday. He`s back and former Senator \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , who`s running to unseat current Governor Brian Kemp. Purdue is trailing big time in the polls. And NBC News reports Trump seems to have given up on supporting him.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, Perdue was asked about this new poll that showed him down about 30 points. And here`s what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAVID PERDUE\uc2  (R) GEORGIA CANDIDATE FOR GOVERNOR: We`re going to win Tuesday back here and empty. We`re not doing 30 points.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: If you don`t win, is that an indictment on your focus on the 2020 election in the former press?\par \par \uc2 PERDUE: I don`t think so at all.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: With that, let`s bring in our experts. A trio of MSNBC contributors. Eugene Daniels, White House correspondent for Politico. Jackie Alemany, Congressional investigations reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , and Greg Bluestein, political reporter for the Atlanta Journal Constitution. He is also the author of "Flipped: How Georgia Turned Purple and Broke the Monopoly on Republican Power."\par \par \uc2 Madame Alemany, to you first, how big of a development is this committee`s request to meet with Loudermilk?\par \par \uc2 JACKIE ALEMANY, THE WASHINGTON POST CONGRESSIONAL LINVESTIGATIONS REPORTER: It certainly is a big development. And I think that the revelations that Chairman Thompson and \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  outlined in their request calling in Barry Loudermilk today, certainly raise some eyebrows. First of all, why would House GOP lawmakers wait until this long to I think acknowledge that they were giving tours after previously saying that, you know, they had gone through all of the footage and that nothing had raised any alarms.\par \par \uc2 And then also now the question is what does the panel know exactly. Obviously, they`ve cited that they have this evidence. There`s a number of ways that investigators who have proven to be very savvy throughout the course of this investigation could have obtained it and what does it go exactly.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Loudermilk claimed in his statement today that it was just a family and that that family didn`t then breach the Capitol. But all of those claims are still not fact checked. We don`t know the full extent of what exactly the committee has.\par \par \uc2 But we do know that if they did go out on a limb here to make a request to a sitting member of Congress, they obviously have some evidence that that essentially points to what is the central argument they`re trying to make and are giving this Congressman the opportunity to respond to what they found.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Greg, Congressman Loudermilk is not someone many people know much about who is he? How close is he to the former president?\par \par \uc2 GREG BLUESTEIN, ATLANTA JOURNAL-CONSTITUTION POLITICAL REPORTER : Yes, you know, of all the members of the Georgia Republican delegation that we at AJC were scrutinizing for their role in the January 6 riot. Congressman Loudermilk was not high on the list at all. He challenged the counting of electoral ballots for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  on January 6, but he`s kept a lower profile than his colleagues like say, \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  or \uc2 Jody Hice\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He`s in a safely Republican district. He`s a former state lawmaker. He lives out in Atlanta`s northern exurbs. So he doesn`t really face any true, either primary challenge or challenge in the November election. But this is going to bring a lot more attention to a lawmaker who doesn`t mind staying out of the spotlight.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Eugene, we`re getting ready for the Committee`s public hearings with Congress already so divided and so dug in, what are the big revelations that we`re expecting to get? How are they going to impact Congress?\par \par \uc2 EUGENE DANIELS, POLITICO WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I mean, at this point, we`re hearing that the committee wants to hear from all of these other members of Congress that already set the pipe, right, to peening the leader of Republicans in the House that sets up a huge fight, it sets up a precedent, first of all, and that`s what Republicans have already promised lots of investigations if they were to win November.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s about what we know is that they`re going to continue to split apart, it`s hard to imagine that things can get worse on Capitol Hill with how members of Congress see each other on both sides, especially Democrats, who see Republicans as anti-democratic at this point, right? And see them as not participating in this investigation as making that even worse, right.\par \par \uc2 And we talk about Loudermilk. And folks wanting to know, why did you tell us that you whether it was a family or not gay people towards on the fifth as \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2  was talking about there. All of these things are probably going to come out because this committee has a huge task in front of them, and that -- they have spent months and months investigating and trying to keep leaks out our reporters, Jackie has been very good about getting what they can out of that come -- out of that committee.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, the Republicans have spent a year and change whitewashing what happened that day. And so that`s what the American people have been told. And so this committee, their job now was to make the American people pay attention and hear the truth of what happened that day.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: They`ve also spent months and months interviewing hundreds and hundreds of people. And they`re only getting to Bill Barr now. He`s a huge name. Why now Jackie?\par \par \uc2 ALEMANY: Yes, Stephanie, actually a source told us just this week that the committee was going after a few big fish to wrap up this phase of the investigation before they head to public hearings. That`s possible for a number of reasons.\par \par \uc2 But the -- primarily because the committee might have wanted to collect enough investigation evidence throughout the course of their investigation so that when they would bring in certain people they had already sort of a road map of we knew you did this on this day. Please respond to this. They`ve taken a very prosecutorial approach throughout the years long investigation. They`ve gotten -- they`ve gleaned a ton of evidence from low level staffers, people who have really been able to fill in a lot of the gaps that people like White House Chief of Staff, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and those who were closest to the former president and have bigger names than these lesser known staffers have.\par \par \uc2 The other reason is potentially that these negotiations unfortunately, just take some time. We`re not quite sure how long Bill Barr is attorney has been negotiating with the committee about the terms of his interview, but it`s certainly an important one, especially as the committee has been trying -- saying repeatedly that they are really trying to paint this full picture of the President`s mindset on January 6, to determine if this was in fact a dereliction of duty and whether or not he really worked to defraud the American people.\par \par \uc2 And Bill Barr is going to be essential to that as he knew Trump pretty well worked with him throughout the course of the administration, and also quit because the former president kept insisting that he had won falsely insisting that he had won the 2020 election. \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had and was furious When Bill Barr refused to say the same that was in December.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:04]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Eugene people get really pumped up when they hear that some of these Trump folks could potentially talk to the committee. But that doesn`t mean Barr is going to any -- to give any good information. The only time we`ve seen him share noteworthy info is when he`s trying to sell a book for himself.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: That`s true. You know what, one of the things that Democrats have been yearning for, right, is for four or five, six years at this point is like how do they take \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  down. I`m talking about Democrats out in the world, right voters, Democratic voters, not here on Capitol Hill, though there are many of them that fit that bill as well.\par \par \uc2 But what Jackie is saying is so true. At this point, if they`re saying that in, you know, in a month, we`re going to start seeing these primetime hearings, that means that a lot of the things that they wanted to know, they probably know, and so if they even bring Bill Barr in. It is probably unlikely he`s going to tell them something that they haven`t already heard of, as they`ve done these hundreds of different interviews after they`ve looked at all of these, you know, thousands and thousands of documents, and emails and conversations that they`ve had over the last few months.\par \par \uc2 And so, we should caution people who are getting so excited about this committee hearing that all of a sudden a bunch of people are going to go to jail, that is unlikely to happen. Congress doesn`t have the ability to throw people in jail, right? That is going to depend on what the DOJ does with some of the information they`re told.\par \par \uc2 There`s possibly set in -- possibly some information sharing being negotiated between the committee and between the DOJ. And so that`s what people should be focusing on what is the DOJ going to do and these committees. The committee hearings are going to be about what`s the story? What does the minute to minute they`re trying to reconstruct every single second of what President Trump was doing that day, who he was talking to and like you said, getting into his mind of how he was acting. And on January 6.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Greg, let`s jump on down to your state of Georgia, the big primary taking place on Tuesday. NBC reports, the following Republican unease is growing ahead of the next big contest on the calendar Georgia. In no other state has Trump`s heavy hand so divided the party. Explain this to us what`s at stake?\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: Yes, I think that`s 100 percent accurate. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has made Georgia maybe the biggest test of his influence this entire election campaign season. He`s issued 10 endorsements here, and only a few of them are shoo-ins (ph) only a few of them are incumbents who are set to win.\par \par \uc2 Some appear headed toward the fee that includes former Senator \uc2 David Perdue\uc2 , as we mentioned, is going after his challenging Governor Brian Kemp, others are facing uphill battles against entrenched incumbents or they`re in wide open races, that polling just doesn`t really show any sort of front runner in some of these contests.\par \par \uc2 Georgia could end up amounting to a significant blow to Donald Trump`s efforts to remake the Republican Party in his image.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then what is Trump going to do in the state of Georgia? When and if Kemp advances? He hates his guts?\par \par \uc2 BLUESTEIN: Yes, it`s not like he`s going to next Wednesday and suddenly start saying that he you know, I can`t wrong all this. Yes, exactly. And so it`s going to be a struggle for Brian Kemp to continue to navigate that because \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself has even said he`d rather see \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  as governor than Brian Kemp. That was at a rally in Georgia past September.\par \par \uc2 So it`s going to continue to complicate the Republican Party. But look, Brian kept wins as big as the polls show. He`s winning. And that`s not a foregone conclusion. But if he does, he`ll be able to say he has a mandate from Republican voters here in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 RUBLE: I cannot imagine \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  out there canvassing for \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , but you never know. Eugene, you`ve been reporting on how concerned Democrats have been about the midterms. Talk to us about the position they`re in right now because a lot has changed even in the last week.\par \par \uc2 DANIELS: Yes, absolutely. They have been for weeks and weeks getting really antsy about what`s going to happen in November, right as the primary season has been heating up. You see New York there -- in New York as the districts are being redrawn and pitting congressman against congresswoman, on the same side, incumbents against incumbents.\par \par \uc2 So they`re getting antsy, and most importantly, what they`re getting antsy about is that they don`t feel like they have enough to sell to the American people. I was talking to a Democrat today, who was saying that it`s not just that the policies haven`t passed, right. It`s not just that build back better hasn`t happened.\par \par \uc2 It is also that they haven`t figured out a message and a consistent message, right? You see President Biden go out on the road a lot more nowadays. That was something that \uc2 Cedric Richmond\uc2  who is now a former senior adviser to President Biden was telling me that they wanted to do they have to tell their story.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has gone out and has been doing that, but it`s not breaking through. It has been up it`s been over a year of allowing Republicans to kind of figure out what the narrative of November was going to be.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:00]\par \par \uc2 And so now Democrats are running an uphill battle. And so they`re looking around and they`re saying we can`t just tell voters that we did the ARP, that it was infrastructure, because voters are very much in what have you done for me lately, Mo Janet Jackson. They`re asking what is going on right at this moment. And they`re not seeing a lot of action.\par \par \uc2 And despite the fact that it`s a 50-50 Senate, it`s a House that is, you know, there`s a very tight majority there. Voters don`t care about that. What they see is that you have a president who`s a Democrat, you have a house that is run by Democrats and the Senate that`s run by Democrats. They don`t care about the filibuster. And Democrats haven`t been able to break through all of that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, then \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  could get up and channel Janet Jackson and saying to voters, it`s all for you.