l of these positions because no one actor can act alone and undo the election results. But acting in concert, should -- the elected, and actual insurrectionist running for secretary of state being elected, they could certainly put together to implement Trump`s plans to overturn the 2024 election.\par \par \uc2 No matter what position it is, democracy is on the ballot in 2022, and that is why I am running, and I need folks to help that, to make sure that we are protecting our democracy in every way possible.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Secretary Chapman, when do you think Pennsylvania will have a result in the Republican Senate primary?\par \par \uc2 CHAPMAN: Sure. So, the latest numbers that I have r that we have about 35,200 ballots, mail-in ballots, that are still being counted right now from ten different counties. So I am sure a lot of people forget about what was happening in the county regarding the 22,000 ballots who had the bar code error. I was told this evening that all of those votes have been counted. So they are complete with the count, the 22,000 ballots.\par \par \uc2 So the next step in the process is tomorrow at 9 am, election boards need to canvas ballots, and on Tuesday, by 5:00 p.m., counties will send me their unofficial results. And as we all know the statewide Senate race, the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  race, it is within that half percent margin right now for a potential recount.\par \par \uc2 So after I receive those unofficial results, I will make a determination as to whether or not there will be an unofficial, an actual automatic statewide recount in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 [21:15:09]\par \par \uc2 And so, we`ll make that announcement mid week next week Wednesday, or Thursday. That is where it is. I just need to let people let the process play out. You know, it`s important they count every vote, and that is what they are doing right now. They are doing their jobs to count every vote to make sure that every Pennsylvania`s voices heard.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And what would be the timetable for a recount of a recount occurs?\par \par \uc2 CHAPMAN: So, counties have to start by June 1st and it should be completed by June 7th. So it is about a week. But one thing to notice that counties, when they do the recount, they cannot do within the same method as how votes were counted the first time. So they have to use a different type of equipment for that recount.\par \par \uc2 But, the election code requires from June 1st to June 7th. So, a week.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Acting secretary of state of Pennsylvania, Leigh Chapman, and secretary of state Arizona, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , thank you both very much for starting off our discussion tonight. I really appreciated. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 CHAPMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up, what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was doing at the White House while his mob was attacking the Capitol to overthrow the presidential election, and as they were saying, hang \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 . The January 6th committee now has photographic evidence that could answer the question of what was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  doing. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:21:45]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Caught on camera. That`s the word Republicans must be feeling now.\par \par \uc2 Today, we learned the January 6 committee has on camera evidence against Republicans from then president of the United States to members of Congress. "Politico" is reporting that the January 6th Committee has obtained official White House photographs from the National Archives that, quote, may provide the committee with real time visual evidence of former President Donald Trump`s actions and movements as a mob of supporters battered their way into the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 Axios is reporting that former Attorney General \uc2 William Barr\uc2  is an active discussions to appear before the January 6th committee for a transcribed interview. The January six committee revealed today that it has evidence that Georgia Republican Congressman Barry Loudermilk led a tour of the Capitol the day before the January 6th attack.\par \par \uc2 In a letter requesting Loudermilk`s voluntary cooperation with the investigation, the committee said: You have invitation regarding a tour you lead through parts of Capitol the complex on January 5th, 2021. The committee`s letter made reference to public comments by other members of Congress, who believed they witnessed tours being given that seemed like reconnaissance tours. That`s when New Jersey Representative \uc2 Mikie Sherrill\uc2  called what she saw, and she described that on this program.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MIKIE SHERRILL\uc2  (D-NJ): I`m a former Navy helicopter pilot and I`ve served all over the world. So we are trained to alert to report things that are unusual or that don`t seem right and certainly when the capital is closed to visitors, to have those tours going through the Capitol, and then to see what happened on January 6th, I reported that, a record needed effort that went into planning January six went far deeper than many of us had anticipated.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The committee`s letter to Representative Loudermilk says, in response to those allegations, Republicans on the Committee on House Administration, of which you are a member, claimed to have reviewed security footage from the days preceding January 6th and determined that that there were no tours no large groups no one with MAGA hats on. However, the Select Committee`s review of evidence directly contradicts that denial.\par \par \uc2 Joining our discussion now, Glenn Kirschner, former federal prosecutor and MSNBC legal analyst.\par \par \uc2 Glenn, let`s begin with the first point raised, which is the caught on camera situation. The January 6 committee now has apparently official White House photographs of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  during the attack on the Capitol. I have seen many times in the White House the way the official White House photographers always behave, and they are just a constant presence in every room the president goes into. They become part of the wallpaper. They are clicking away nonstop at everyone in the room, with always a focus on the president.\par \par \uc2 So there is a lot of photographic evidence of that day.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:04]\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRSCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Yes, Lawrence, and the more vivid picture for the January 6 committee campaigned for us all when public hearings open on June 9th, what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was doing for 187 minutes while he angry mob, that he set on the Capitol, was attacking the Capitol, was hunting for politicians. It`s going to be really damning, because which we already know, and you know pictures have a way of driving it home.\par \par \uc2 What we already know is that the three hours, people were streaming into the Oval Office trying to convince the president to call off, let`s call it what it is, he is a attack, and he refused to do it. He said you know what, we even sent \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2  back into the Oval Office a second time to try to convince her father to call off the attack and he wouldn`t. Lawrence, I`m sorry, but we have to call that what it is. That was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  waging war with three hours on the Democratic process, trying to stop the certification of Joe Biden`s whip. And if we can see it with our own eyes, as captured in photographs, not only in testimony, which we will see beginning on June 9th, it`s going to be a powerful presentation.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let`s listen to what`s \uc2 Stephanie Grisham\uc2 , the White House press secretary at the time, what she said about what \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was doing that we will probably see in the White House photographs.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE GRISHAM\uc2 , FORMER WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: All I know about that day was that he was in the dining room, gleefully watching on his TV, as he often did, look at all the people fighting for me, hitting rewind, watching it again. That`s what I know.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Glenn, still photographs can tell that story.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yes, the word gleefully jumps out. It`s horrific that we were all watching it and that anybody could watch it gleefully, regardless of your political persuasion, your ideology, but you could be watching it gleefully, knowing you were the only person who could put a stop to it.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Capitol police\uc2  couldn`t put a stop to it, because the Capitol was deprived of enough federal law enforcement resources to repel the attack \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had launched. And watching it gleefully and refusing to put a stop to it, even though people were pleading with you to do just that, I think, Lawrence, once we see this with our own eyes in these public hearings, we see the crimes that were committed. We see the evidence that can prove those crimes beyond a reasonable doubt. The only next reasonable step for a responsible government is for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to be indicted for his crimes.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We also see from the January 6 committee that they apparently have video of Representative Barry Loudermilk getting a tour. They put that in writing in their letter to him after he had been part of a Capitol public denial for any of this, and that, for them to want to use it would have to be something in that video that is not just him showing a boy scout troop around the building.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yeah, and there is no innocent explanation, if Representative Loudermilk is proved to have lied and concealed the fact that he was giving tours. I hesitate to call them reconnaissance tours, but if he was giving tours of the Capitol to anyone on January 5th, when the place was shut down to visitors due to COVID protocols, there`s no innocent explanation for why he would hide that, why he would be hiding that for so long from everybody.\par \par \uc2 So I`ll be interested to see exactly what kind of evidence is produced.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And, Glenn, before we go, Attorney General \uc2 William Barr\uc2 , former attorney general, working out, it sounds like, an agreement to speak with the January 6 committee.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Yeah, let me start with the caveat. If Bill Barr`s word is to be credited, we know that he has been on his reputation rehabilitation tours, selling books, and he has said among other things that on December 1st, he told the president that your claim, Mr. President, of election fraud, is, to paraphrase Bill Barr, BS.\par \par \uc2 What that does, Lawrence, is it provides powerful evidence of Donald`s criminal intent every day moving forward from December 1st when his own attorney general said your claims of election fraud are BS.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:52]\par \par \uc2 And yet \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  continued to peddle them.\par \par \uc2 What does that do? It provides evidence of his corrupt intent.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Glenn Kirschner, thank you very much for joining us again tonight.\par \par \uc2 KIRSCHNER: Thank you Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up today, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  objected to the latest state law restricting abortion, this time, in Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Think about that for a second. From the moment of fertilization -- it`s outrageous, and it`s just the latest in a series of extreme laws (ph) around the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:50]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today, Oklahoma state legislature approved a bill that bans abortion beginning at the moment of fertilization. The bill says, quote, "Fertilization means the fusion of a human spermatozoon with a human ovum."\par \par \uc2 The bill also defines an unborn child this way. "Unborn child" means a "human fetus or embryo in any stage of gestation from fertilization until birth." The only exceptions to the absolute ban on abortion are to save the life of a mother, or in cases of rape and incest that have been reported to police.\par \par \uc2 The law allows citizen bounty hunters to sue anyone involved in any way in helping a woman obtain abortion services.\par \par \uc2 Today, Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HARRIS: Think about that for a second -- from the moment of fertilization. It is outrageous, and it is just the latest in a series of extreme laws around the country.\par \par \uc2 The strength of our country has always been that we fight to move forward. That we believe in the expansion of rights not the restriction of rights. So this, when and if it happens, will be an extreme step backward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The House Judiciary Committee helped a hearing on the impact of restricting abortion access when the Supreme Court overturns Roe versus Wade. Congresswoman \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. CORI BUSH (D-MO): When I was 17 years old, I was raped. Weeks later, I found out I was pregnant. Without the protections afforded to me by Roe v. Wade, I would have been forced to birth a human being that I could not take care of and the father would have been my rapist.\par \par \uc2 The emotional devastation of this encounter was traumatic. I didn`t know if it was my fault. I didn`t know what to do. Did I do something wrong? I hadn`t consented. But I blamed myself. The stress of possibly having a child at 18 was more than I could -- I could bear.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Representative \uc2 Lucy McBath\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LUCY MCBATH (D-GA): After which failed pregnancy should I have been imprisoned? Would it have been after the first miscarriage? After doctors used what would be an illegal drug to abort the lost fetus? Would you have put me in jail after the second miscarriage? Perhaps that would have been the time.\par \par \uc2 Forced to reflect in confinement at the guilt I felt, the guilt that so many women feel after losing their pregnancies. Or, would you have put me behind bars after my stillbirth? I ask, because if Alabama makes abortion murder, does it make miscarriage manslaughter?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining our discussion now is Dr. Iman Alsaden. She is the medical director for Planned Parenthood Great Plains, which includes Arkansas, Kansas, Missouri and Oklahoma. She is also an OB/GYN, a physician, and fellow with the Physicians for Reproductive Health.\par \par \uc2 Doctor, thank you very much for joining us tonight. Since your jurisdiction at Planned Parenthood includes Oklahoma, what is your reaction to a law saying that abortion is banned beginning at what they are calling the moment of fertilization?\par \par \uc2 DR. IMAN ALSADEN, MEDICAL DIRECTOR, PLANNED PARENTHOOD GREAT PLAINS: First, thank you for having me, Lawrence. I appreciate the opportunity to talk about this.\par \par \uc2 I agree with Vice President Harris. The law is ridiculous, it is cruel. We know that abortion is safe, essential, basic health care. And today, Oklahoma proved that they do not care about keeping us safe or healthy.\par \par \uc2 The interest of Oklahoma politicians that was proven today is that they are interested in controlling and criminalizing people who have uteruses, and the bodies of communities that are not white men, essentially.\par \par \uc2 [22:40:01]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So we are seeing a competition among Republican legislatures and Republican governors about who can come up with the most extreme abortion ban in a state. Oklahoma is a strong competitor here for this. But surely next week, there will be another one coming along that we can`t even anticipate now.\par \par \uc2 And this is the reality. Roe versus Wade is going to be overturned by the Supreme Court. And on that day, all of these laws become active and effective.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALSADEN: That is completely correct. I do think that there is a trend right now amongst Republican led states where they are prioritizing the control and coercion of people over the issues actually that should be addressed.\par \par \uc2 For example, Oklahoma ranks among the lowest in maternal mortality. One in five Oklahoman children live in poverty. Oklahoma ranks 39thj in the nation in gun violence and homicide.\par \par \uc2 We are looking at a states that is not focused on the right things. They are not focused on people`s health. They are not focused on facts, and science and medicine. They are focused on control.\par \par \uc2 For example, Representative Hamilton of the Oklahoma Senate wanted -- said that House Bill 4327 didn`t go far enough. He wanted it to also include ectopic pregnancies which are a known life-threatening emergency.\par \par \uc2 So we are dealing with medical misinformation that is rampant. It is uncontrollable. Medical misinformation kill thousands of people from COVID. And we are seeing medical information spread like wildfire in terms of abortion, which has proven time and time and time again to be safe, essential, basic health care.\par \par \uc2 And quite frankly, it is your basic human right to control your own body.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Dr. Iman Alsaden, thank you very much for helping us continue to cover this important story. Thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 DR. ALSADEN: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, another loss for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  today with Sweden and Finland officially applying to become members of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . That means that Sweden is finally breaking 188 years of neutrality to stand in opposition to Russia`s invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Ben Rhodes joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:42:28]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Relegated to the dustbin of bad pundit history now are the oft- repeated assertions for years that turned out to be based on zero evidence that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is smart. Vladimir Putin`s immoral invasion of Ukraine now takes its place as the stupidest military decision made by a Russian leader since \uc2 Joseph Stalin\uc2  aligned with Adolf Hitler at the beginning of World War II before switching sides after Hitler turned on Stalin.\par \par \uc2 The Putin plan to weaken \uc2 NATO\uc2  by invading Ukraine has only made \uc2 NATO\uc2  stronger. And now Sweden, a country that has maintained military neutrality for almost 200 years, is applying to join \uc2 NATO\uc2  as is Finland, which shares 800 miles of border with Russia.\par \par \uc2 Today, after meeting with Finland`s president and Sweden`s prime minister, President Biden said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Today my administration is submitting to the \uc2 United States Congress\uc2  reports on \uc2 NATO\uc2  accession for both countries so the Senate can efficiently and quickly move on advising and consenting to the treaty.\par \par \uc2 Let no one make a mistake the meaning of this historic day. In the face of aggression, \uc2 NATO\uc2  has not grown weaker or more divided. It has grown stronger, more united. With Finland and Sweden`s decision to request membership in \uc2 NATO\uc2 , it will be enhanced for all time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today the senate finally passed after a week of obstruction by \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , a $40 billion dollar aid package for Ukraine which includes military aid as well as aid funding for support of refugees, including English language instruction. The bill also includes $5 billion in global food aid to fill food shortages created by the collapse of Ukraine`s vast agricultural output.\par \par \uc2 11 Republican senators, including \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2 , \uc2 Joshua Hawley\uc2  and \uc2 Mike Lee\uc2 , voted against the aid package. Yesterday Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said, "For Senator Paul to delay Ukraine funding for purely political motives is to only strengthen Putin`s hand."\par \par \uc2 Today \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said the 11 Republican senators voting against the bill are, quote, using the same "soft on Putin" playbook used by former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:41]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: On Tuesday, on the senate floor, Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  proved that he has absolutely no comprehension about what war actually is when he said, "Yes, our national security is threatened not by Russia`s war on Ukraine, but by Congress` war on the American taxpayer."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Ben Rhodes, former deputy national security adviser to President Obama and an MSNBC political analyst. He is the author of "After The Fall: Being American in the World We`ve Made".\par \par \uc2 Ben, this is quite a historic day. It`s a thing we can say more than once a week these days, but to see Sweden after 188 years saying yes, we finally do want to step out of neutrality into an alignment, and to see Finland shares an 800 mile border with Russia saying yes, we now want to be part of \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This is a moment that we could not have conceived of in January.\par \par \uc2 BEN RHODES, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: You know, that is absolutely right, Lawrence. I mean you have to consider that Finland and Sweden made it through the Cold War maintaining neutrality. Sweden stayed out of World War I and World War II.\par \par \uc2 And Vladimir Putin`s invasion of Ukraine has precipitated this event. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has become the single most important force for the enlargement of \uc2 NATO\uc2 . And if you look at his strategic war aims at the beginning of this war, they have failed spectacularly.\par \par \uc2 He wanted to stop the expansion of \uc2 NATO\uc2  to Russia`s borders. He just precipitated the doubling of NATO`s border with Russia with that Finnish border with Russia.\par \par \uc2 He wanted to divide the west, which frankly had already been divided badly in the Trump years. Instead he has done more to unify the United States and Europe and democratic nations around the world than anything that has happened in recent years.\par \par \uc2 He wanted to subjugate and decapitate the Ukrainian government. Instead he has a Ukrainian population that has made clear that they will never submit to any kind of Russian subjugation.\par \par \uc2 And the Ukrainian government led by President Zelenskyy that has won the admiration of people around the world. So all his strategic war aims have already failed catastrophically just in these early months of what is likely to be a long and grinding conflict.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And Finland polling on joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  hovered around 20 percent to 30 percent for years. It now stands at over 70 percent. And that was, as you say, Vladimir Putin`s strategic outcome here.\par \par \uc2 When he had the troops amassed at the border of Ukraine, that was the last -- it turns out, that was the last moment where he had any negotiating strength at all. And he apparently didn`t realize that.\par \par \uc2 RHODES: No, he didn`t, Lawrence. And clearly he didn`t because he thought that a kind of lightning move with this massive military force around Ukraine would lead to the rapid collapse of the Ukrainian government. And once the Ukrainian people and government stood strong against that move, is (INAUDIBLE) he essentially failed but he`d lost his leverage.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s important to note, by the way, that those polling numbers mirror what happened in Ukraine, who used to have a minority of Ukrainians who wanted to join \uc2 NATO\uc2 , who wanted to orient this strongly toward the west.\par \par \uc2 It was after the annexation of Crimea and the initial Russian invasion of the Donbas region in Ukraine that you saw this significant uptake and Ukrainian support for \uc2 NATO\uc2  membership. That kind of popular sentiment, populist sentiment really ultimately led to the election of President Zelenskyy and we see now how important that is.\par \par \uc2 So time and again, you know, what \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has done is overplayed his hand in a way that has served to unify the Ukrainian people, it served to unify the Finnish people and it served to unify people across democracies, even as we are united in standing up for Ukraine as the vote in Congress suggests today we have a lot more work to be done within our own democracies if we`re going to win an enduring competition with authoritarianism.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So the president asked Congress for $33 billion for Ukraine. Congress says we will give you $40 billion. Including a majority of Republican senators saying we will give you $40 billion, more than you asked for.\par \par \uc2 What does that do for the Biden approach to Ukraine strategically going forward?\par \par \uc2 RHODES: Well, I think what is so important about this is that this is a significant amount of funding that could sustain Ukraine for the next six months. This is not like just a band-aid that we`re rushing assistance in. And by sending this signal, at a time when Ukraine in many ways has a significant amount of momentum in this conflict, although they have areas like Mariupol obviously, where they have lost territory.\par \par \uc2 By signaling this American commitment, not only are we going to be able to sustain the Ukrainian military and governmental effort but it sends a message to the rest of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , to the rest of Ukraine supporters that they should stay in this fight as well.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Ben Rhodes, thank you very much for joining us again tonight.\par \par \uc2 RHODES: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tonight`s LAST WORD is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:58]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: During today`s Senate Rules Committee hearing on election administration, civil rights attorney Damon Hewitt said this.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:48]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DAMON HEWITT, CIVIL RIGHTS ATTORNEY: This week marks the 65th anniversary of Dr. \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 .` famous speech, "Give us the ballot" where he warned us is about those who gained prominence and power by the dissemination of false ideas and deliberately appealing to the deepest hate responses within the human mind.\par \par \uc2 I said earlier that democracy is a promise. Democracy is also a choice. We have a choice when it comes to election administration. We can either strengthen democracy and make it easier to vote in administerial elections or we can make it harder. As policy makers, you can make a choice -- a choice in favor of democracy.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Attorney Damon Hewitt gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220520ei5j00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for May 16, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Steve Kornacki, Antonia Hylton, Vaughn Hillyard \par \uc2 8,098 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie rule starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:09]\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, the fight for democracy votes are still being counted in critical primaries with democracy on the ballot. The economy, racial tensions and trust are as well.\par \par \uc2 We`re tracking it all on this important primary election day as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on a Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Here we go. Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. 175 days until the midterm elections. And tonight you know what we are up to following key primaries in Idaho, Kentucky, North Carolina, Oregon, and in the place everybody`s watching the birthplace of democracy the state of Pennsylvania, where Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman has won the Democratic Senate nominations -- nomination just days after suffering a stroke.\par \par \uc2 His campaign says he does expect a full recovery. Fetterman will now face off against the winner of what has turned out to be a very competitive Republican primary with former hedge fund manager David McCormick, Trump back Dr. Oz and conservative commentator Kathy Barnette.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Oz is one of the many candidates endorsed by the former president, another \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 . Remember this name. He has won the Republican nomination for Governor of Pennsylvania. This man, he was at the Capitol on January 6. He refused to accept the results of the 2020 election. And he`s now going to be running come this November.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, we have just learned that North Carolina Congressman Madison Cawthorn called primary challenger Chuck Edwards to concede his race. We`ve got a lot to cover. So you know the one person who`s going to be here to do that the best Steve Kornacki at the big board. All right, Steve, here we are. It is your night. What do we need to know most at this moment?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Stephanie, I mean, it`s just the it is the suspense in this Pennsylvania Republican Senate primary. You mentioned John Fetterman is going to win the Democratic nomination who is going to oppose him? Look at this Mehmet Oz has been running in second place most of the night, but we`ve been watching that gap against David McCormick gets smaller and smaller. It`s down to 3,000 votes essentially right now, three tenths of one point separating Oz from McCormick, Kathie Barnette a little bit further back.\par \par \uc2 A couple of things to, and look at that, it just fell inside of 2,000 votes now. The margin of McCormick over Oz, a couple of things that are happening here. We were saying for the last hour or so that the Philadelphia suburbs which are going to account for about a quarter of all the vote in this primary. We`re very slow to report out their election day vote, the folks who went out and voted in person at the polls today.\par \par \uc2 We`re now starting to get the Election Day vote and a lot of it from these Philly suburbs and let me just take you through it and show you what we`re seeing. One of the big collar counties outside Philadelphia, this is the one where we have the most votes so far, Chester County.\par \par \uc2 You see here actually, this is good news for McCormick. McCormick is leading overall and he is leading the Election Day vote. We keep telling you there is a small sliver of ballots that were cast by mail in this thing, a lot of them got reported out early. This has been about who`s winning the election day vote, McCormick. And we just got another batch there from Chester. McCormick is leading the election day vote in Chester County.\par \par \uc2 In Montgomery County, another one of the biggies right outside Philadelphia, Barnette, Kathy Barnette actually is leading overall and leading the election day vote. One interesting thing to note here on the ballot in Pennsylvania, they list the key that it`s home county and then Barnette listed Montgomery County so did Dr. Oz, both of them list Montgomery. They are running one, two in Montgomery.\par \par \uc2 Then you to go to Bucks County, we have less of the same day voting here. But here it`s Oz who`s getting the same day vote and its Oz who`s leading so each candidate with a little something to brag about here in these Philadelphia suburbs. We`re waiting on a lot from Delaware.\par \par \uc2 But overall what this adds up to is, it`s McCormick and Oz then you can draw a line, and it`s Barnette statewide. And if you add up all of just the Election Day vote that we`ve counted the vote that was cast in person, it`s going to be like 90 percent of all the votes in this election.\par \par \uc2 Right now, let me get these numbers exactly right for you. The Election Day vote right now would be 32 percent -- it`d be 32 percent. I don`t know why this isn`t ready. Oz, 32, McCormick 30 -- that`s supposed to be a 30 --\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You think that says 32, Steve? I know planning. Does that say any of that?\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: OK. Oz is leading the Election Day vote critically here by one point statewide and 90 percent of all the votes that are going to be cast in this election are same day votes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What kind of volume are you predicting in terms of mail in ballots? I`m thinking back to the last general election when former President Trump was railing against mail and you didn`t see a lot of Republicans going that route Do you think we`re going to see high volume today\par \par \uc2 [23:05:07]\par \par \uc2 KORNACKI: Well, we don`t. And that`s why I`m putting so much emphasis on the same day vote. Because what happened in this race was that early on in the night, we saw some numbers that looked at odds with the polling, it`s because a lot of counties released their mail-in vote early on.\par \par \uc2 And what we were saying from that point was, OK, that`s going to be about 10 percent, the mail votes are going to be about 10 percent of the Republican primary. What about when they start counting up the Election Day vote, that`s going to look different, that`s going to be a lot bigger. So that`s why I think that`s key when I say that Oz actually has that now leads by a little bit more than the point on the election day vote. It`s why he`s continued to chip away ever so slowly at this McCormick leads statewide.\par \par \uc2 McCormick won the mail vote early in the night by nine points. Oz has been leading the same day vote by a little over a point. And he`s been getting closer and closer. The one wildcard to keep in mind, however, is in Pennsylvania, there are more than a dozen counties that have told us they do have some mailing ballots that they have not processed yet. They are not going to process tonight, and they`re going to process tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 We are not talking about hundreds of thousands of ballots, but we are talking about potentially thousands of ballots. So we could be in a situation where we get to the end of the night. We count up all the same day vote and it`s a very, very narrow margin between these candidates in a situation like that. There could be several 1000 mail-in ballots that we are waiting for a bunch of different counties to open up process and report out tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 And if that`s giving you any flashbacks to a week of November 2020, well, it certainly is for me, because that`s what happened back then, too.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, here we go. Again, Steve, stay close. Obviously, we`re not letting you go anywhere, not even a bathroom break for Mr. Kornacki tonight, but for now, I want to continue this conversation with Mr. Michael Steele, former chairman of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  and former Lieutenant Governor of Maryland, and former Democratic governor of the state of Pennsylvania \uc2 Ed Rendell\uc2 . He also served as the mayor of Philly and was the chair of the DNC.\par \par \uc2 I want to start down south with you, Mr. Steele, what`s your reaction to Madison Cawthorn conceding?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER RNC CHAIRMAN: Well, it`s kind of interesting in a couple of things. One, it says to me that Republicans can, can if they want to push back against crazy and push back against those who are outside of the mainstream, if you will, if they want to. Madison, obviously turn the corner on them. And this leads me to point number two, that the reality for him was he thought that he had a much longer runway to go out and behave and say, in a way, say things in a way that wouldn`t get him in trouble. That`s true up until he started telling little dirty secrets inside the party when he started talking about cocaine parties and orgies. All of a sudden, he`s right, proper, you know, Christian evangelicals got a little bit upset.\par \par \uc2 So the reality from Madison is a bit off a bit more than he could chew. The party came after him. And what the country should take away from that is all the other stuff that they countless, they don`t have to. They can step up and say no to the Trump lie. They can say no to the Marjorie Taylor Greene`s, the Matt Gaetz`s of the party, but they won`t. So, you know, they pick their poison, and it was Cawthorn.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, it was. Governor Rendell. Let`s talk about Pennsylvania. \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  is now one of two people who could get the job you once held. This is a man who was at the Capitol on January 6. He continues to be an election denier pushing the big lie. How high are the stakes are the Commonwealth of Pennsylvania, the birthplace of democracy?\par \par \uc2 ED RENDELL (D) FORMER PENNSYLVANIA GOVERNOR: Thanks to anonymous, for example, if Mastriano going to become governor, Republicans will probably hold both hands as they control the legislature. That will mean that Pennsylvania will criminalize abortion. That won`t be stunning.\par \par \uc2 The only thing that`s going to stop Pennsylvania from criminalizing abortion was no exception is Dr. Barrows (ph) elect as governor. I am fairly confident that he will get elected. Republicans did us a great favor. Michael, who`s right, they can get rid of crazy, but they chose nominee crazy to the most important office in the state of Pennsylvania. I think \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2  is a terrific candidate who will steamroll the election.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, let`s bring in one of Pennsylvania`s best political reporters Katie Meyer, she covers politics power and elections for WHYY and fun fact, she was once an intern of our own Steve Kornacki. Katie, what are you hearing about Mastriano`s win? What are people around the state and saying about him?\par \par \uc2 [23:10:03]\par \par \uc2 KATIE MEYER, POLITICAL REPORTER, WHYY: Well, we had a very last minute attempt by Republican insiders in Pennsylvania to coalesce support around Lou Barletta, who had been trailing Mastriano in the polls. And that really speaks to what Republicans in this state are thinking about. Not necessarily that they can`t support Mastriano and his policies, a lot of them, as Governor Rendell mentioned are kind of within the mainstream of the GOP at this point, like, getting rid of abortion, but because they have concerns about whether or not he can be \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I will just say as well, I mean, these are similar concerns to the ones that people had about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016. And he went on to beat \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  in the general election and in Pennsylvania. So, you know, you never know what`s going to happen in this.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Too true. Michael, let`s talk about Pennsylvania`s Democratic Senate candidates. What is it about John Fetterman that drew in voters and can he take it to November?\par \par \uc2 STEELE: The thing about John Fetterman is he`s every man. He is the neighbor next door. He`s the guy who helps you bring in the groceries. He`s, you know, he`s the, you know, the neighbor you go to when you got a problem. It comes off as this figure that is larger than politics beyond politics.\par \par \uc2 And it has translated very well for him around the state. He`s used his time as lieutenant governor. And I know a little bit of something about that job very, very well with my Governor friends across from me here.\par \par \uc2 But he knows very well how to connect to people to bring that narrative to the street, if you will. And he`s going to benefit I think mightily from this November. The Republicans are going to have a tall order kind of going after him and trying to create that separation with those voters, because he has a built in relationship that we haven`t seen in politics in a long time.\par \par \uc2 And Pennsylvania is a good bellwether, not just for, you know, Republicans to look at and see how they handle something like that, but for Democrats to understand how they can position themselves going forward in in upcoming elections.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Governor, what do you think of Fetterman`s win, tough for Republicans to say that Democrats are the party of coastal elites when Fetterman is your guy?\par \par \uc2 RENDELL: Yes, very hard. And John Fetterman is a great retail campaigner. We haven`t seen a retail campaigner as good as him for at least 20 years. I won`t tell you who that was. But in any, John Fetterman is incredible small groups. 