ill use that power in ways that would likely benefit Trump. The fate of American democracy may be on the ballot in Pennsylvania tonight.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC national political correspondent Steve Kornacki is at the big board. He joins us now with the latest numbers.\par \par \uc2 What do we got?\par \par \uc2 STEVE KORNACKI, MSNBC NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: Well, Chris, literally in the last seconds, we saw a swisher. This is the statewide leader board in the Republican gubernatorial primary in Pennsylvania and \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  now has taken the lead statewide here. \uc2 Bill McSwain\uc2 , a former U.S. attorney, running in second, Lou Barletta running in third.\par \par \uc2 Let me explain what`s going on because Pennsylvania is going to be the most complicated one to watch tonight but I think some revealing things just happened to explain this. We`ve been talking about how there`s this big disparity between Republicans and their reluctance to use mail-in votes and Democrats who use it very heavily in Pennsylvania. So primarily in the updates I`ve been giving you for the last hour or so, we had only been looking at mail-in votes being tallied across Pennsylvania. You saw McSwain doing well, you didn`t even see Mastriano on the leader board.\par \par \uc2 Now not every county in Pennsylvania is going to count these votes the same way in the same sequence. Many are going to count the mail-in votes first and then work their way through the same day we were seeing those mail-in votes early. Some though are going to count their same day first and then do the mail late tonight or even tomorrow. We are now starting to see the counties that are putting their same day vote out, and it is a strikingly different pattern that`s emerging.\par \par \uc2 Let me show you a few that have come in in the last few minutes. Again, we are looking at same-day vote now, we are not looking at mail vote. Same-day vote, here`s Mastriano 52 percent in Cambria County. Let`s go to -- this is close to Mastriano`s political base but again it`ll give you a sense.\par \par \uc2 Fulton County, small county but same day, 82 percent. This is his political base. Franklin County, same day, nearly 80 percent. Northampton -- Northumberland County, excuse me, same day, there`s Mastriano leaving Barletta. You`re getting closer to Barletta`s neck of the woods right there.\par \par \uc2 [21:05:02]\par \par \uc2 You`re seeing that if you want to call this violet or whatever color you want to call exactly the Mastriano here. You`re seeing that pop up where the same day vote is coming in. So he`s barely registering in the mail but he`s getting huge numbers in the places that are counting up the same day and already you see he moved into the lead now he`s moved into a two and a half point lead statewide. So I think we may be seeing something very significant begin to happen, as same day vote starts to come in in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Again, it`s going to be uneven. There`s going to be other places that report out that that mail vote here that could change things, but you`re getting a taste of how very different those two things are. And again, remember, probably like 90 percent of all the votes in this primary and that Senate Republican primary are going to be same-day votes.\par \par \uc2 So speaking of that, let`s take a look at where that Republican Senate primary stands now that we start to get same-day vote added in there. Again, David McCormick continues to lead. We saw he did well early in the mail vote in his political base in Allegheny County he`s done well in the mail votes that have been counted elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 But let`s take a look at some of those counties where we just showed you same-day vote has come in. Take a look at Cambria County actually, and you`re in western Pennsylvania here, and Mehmet Oz is actually leading -- McCormick doing well here, but Mehmet is actually leading with the same day vote out here.\par \par \uc2 And remember, as we said McCormick the only candidate in this in the Senate race with a western Pennsylvania identifier on the ballot with his name, but in the same day, he is trailing there in Cambria County. You see Barnette -- again, we just showed you how in the gubernatorial race, Mastriano is cleaning up here. Barnette in Fulton County, Oz, in Franklin County, Barnette in second place.\par \par \uc2 Take a look up here in Northumberland County actually, it`s a close one. McCormick and Oz, Barnette running a little bit behind. So when the Senate race here, again, we`re starting to see some day numbers. It`s not as clear-cut who that`s going to work totally in favor of here.\par \par \uc2 You see a couple of good spots there for us, couple good spots for Barnette. I think if you`re Oz or Barnette though, anytime your color comes up in the western part of the state, that`s a particularly good sign because again, as we say, we were wondering if there`s a McCormick geographic advantage there. But add that all up on the Republican side, McCormack continues to lead, Oz has edged closer, Barnette running a bit further back.\par \par \uc2 The other race to update you on here is that North Carolina congressional race. Madison Cawthorn in the 11th district. We`ll just check in out there because we got a lot of votes that are in right now and Cawthorn you can see has closed. Now, the difference here is close to about 2,000 votes.\par \par \uc2 The good news for Madison Cawthorn is that he`s gotten closer in this. The bad news is if you were to zoom in geographically to see exactly how that`s happened, basically what`s happened is along the Tennessee border, the rural counties along the Tennessee border, Madison Cawthorn really did a number on the same day vote and that brought him closer to Edwards.\par \par \uc2 Remember, this is Chuck Edwards. He`s a state senator. But Chuck Edwards state Senate district is down here. It`s Henderson County. It`s part of Buncombe County, and 