S CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, that`s exactly right, and speaking of down to the wire, as you were talking about with Steve just now, we`ve learned from the Barnette campaign that they have sent representatives to the Lancaster county board of elections office because there was a printing error there that resulted in only about a third of 21,000 ballots being able to scan properly.\par \par \uc2 [21:10:07]\par \par \uc2 So we know Barnette has had representatives there. We know Oz has sent representatives there, as has Mastriano. We haven`t yet heard from the McCormick campaign.\par \par \uc2 Now this just might mean that things are slowing down a little bit are slowed down by that hiccup there because this race is as close as Steve has been talking about, as we`ve all been talking about, you know, several thousand votes in Lancaster county might matter and that might delay the results here tonight.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, we`ve also know that there are about 700,000 Democratic vote-by-mail ballots that have been cast about, 200,000 Republican vote by mail ballots that have been cast, and we know from experience in that those also can take some time. So when we`ll actually know the results here is unclear.\par \par \uc2 But I`ll tell you there is a lot of enthusiasm in this room tonight. This is a candidate who did not have the Trump endorsement, like Oz did, who did not have the candidates -- the Trump campaign folks that McCormick has had and yet has been able to really come within striking range here, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right. Dasha Burns has been following that campaign for the last several weeks. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got NBC news correspondent Vaughn Hilliard who`s joining me from the campaign headquarters for Republican Senate primary candidate Mehmet Oz and if we don`t -- actually have him it appears although we see him there but I don`t think he sees us the magic of television.\par \par \uc2 Mehdi Hasan is the host of "THE MEHDI HASAN SHOW" on Peacock and MSNBC. Corbin Trent is former communications director for Congresswoman \uc2 Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez\uc2  in New York. He`s the co-founder of Justice Democrats, a progressive political action committee and they both join me now.\par \par \uc2 Corbin and Mehdi, let`s I guess start on the Fetterman story, Corbin. That was right -- well, it`s interesting. It was a matchup. You had \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  who very much was a kind of like sort of central casting centrist, both in sort of positions and vibe.\par \par \uc2 John Fetterman who is -- you know, doesn`t look like other politicians but a pretty resounding victory for Fetterman, even after the health scare in the last 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 CORBIN TRENT, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Yeah. I think, well, you know, for me that`s a pretty exciting victory for tonight. You saw a guy that`s running outside of the box he`s running behind things like Medicare for all and I think it`s exciting to see him win.\par \par \uc2 I think personally, it`s been my experience, when Democrats run to their sort of core values and they act like a party that understands people`s pain and understands what people`s lived experience are like, and they do better, and I think he`s got a good way of communicating with people that they trust. He seems -- I don`t know he seems like a man of the people or whatever you want to call that.\par \par \uc2 You know, I don`t know what that quality is, but I think he carries that in spades and people sort of gravitate towards that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 TRENT: For better for worse and I think in this case, it`s for better.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yeah, I think -- I think, Mehdi, I think it`s an interesting -- it`ll be an interesting test case because I think -- you know, we go back to that 2012 election where you saw Barack Obama beat \uc2 Mitt Romney\uc2  in what was actually a pretty tough political environment, right? Still coming out of for the great recession, polarizing the election on this sort of you know class dynamic like he`s a -- he`s the kind of guy that comes in and like liquidates your factory and sends your job overseas, and I`m the guy that came in and like saved GM.\par \par \uc2 That was effective and won just enough margins for \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  to win those states like Michigan, and I think it would be interesting to watch a kind of Fetterman McCormick race for instance or a Fetterman-Oz race along those same lines if he can kind of catalyze enough voters along those lines.\par \par \uc2 MEHDI HASAN, MSNBC HOST: Yeah, it`s a very good point. McCormick and Oz would instantly become one of the richest members of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  which is saying a lot given how rich United States senators tend to be.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 HASAN: Fetterman is an interesting character because we are told by centrist Democrats all the time that Democrats have to win back white working-class voters, that`s the plan. Well, who better than a guy who wears basketball shorts, hoodies, has tattoos speaks in very plain blunt language is the son of conservative Republicans and also wants to cancel student debt, wants a $15 federal minimum wage and legalize marijuana and prison reform. So there`s a lot there for that Fetterman campaign.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, the health scare on Friday with the stroke will be used against him by Republicans. That`s a given. But he will be a hard candidate to campaign against, as \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  found. Conor lamb should have been the favor, as you say central casting going into this race. But Fetterman beat him pretty roundly and easily in the end, even despite the health scare.\par \par \uc2 So I think the Fetterman candidacy will be a test case. I think there`s no guarantee of anything because our politics are very different now, Chris. As you know better than me than they were in 2012.\par \par \uc2 When you could, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , a b