e hands of white supremacists. It will be an opportunity for President Biden to reassert the core vision of what this country should be and speak out against the -- against the opponents of that vision.\par \par \uc2 Heather McGee is a board member of the nonprofit racial justice organization, Color of Change, and author of the fantastic book The Sum Of Us: What Racism Costs Everyone And How We Can Prosper Together. And she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Heather, you know, I thought of you when I was seeing some of the discourse around the manifesto and the discussion of the mainstreaming of the vision that is embedded in that -- in that -- in the shooters manifesto. This is the latest polling from December of last year, the National Opinion Research Center. 32 percent of Americans believe an effort is underway to replace U.S.-born Americans with immigrants for electoral gains.\par \par \uc2 And while I think that`s very, very high, because it should be a fringe view, it also points to the fact that there still remains this solid majority in this country, I think, that he`s willing to hear a message about us being bound to each other and reject the kind of zero-sum story that they`re being told so often.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:05]\par \par \uc2 HEATHER MCGEE, BOARD MEMBER, COLOR OF CHANGE: Well, that`s the hope, right? And that`s the reason why my book The Sum of Us is ultimately a helpful one. It`s why ultimately, when we understand that there`s somebody who is profiting from all of this pain, we can unite along our common shared interests.\par \par \uc2 You know, there is somebody who`s profiting from Black pain, as there has always been since the beginning of our country, right? And you`d have to ask those questions, who`s profiting, whether it`s the social media companies whose algorithm makes sure that fear and outrage drives clicks, and makes them more money? To obviously the cable news companies and the paid bullies in the corporate media like \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and Laura Ingram for whom this is their way to, you know, to become millionaires? To, frankly, the Republican Party, who has really mainstreamed and have this sort of white grievance as its core unifying ideology?\par \par \uc2 And ultimately, who wins when we have either a sort of mainstream version of this sort of white grievance that is held by the majority, according to the research of moderates and conservatives, the idea that black people take more from this country than we give, to the very fringe, right, the January 6, the great replacement theory.\par \par \uc2 Both of those together give a minority party more governing power than it should have in this country, this sort of uniting around white racial grievance. And who ultimately profits from that? The millionaires and billionaires who backstopped the party and whose interests are served financially by the Republican agenda.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I was just thinking about the fact that as we enter this primary season, it`s very hard to envision -- so, you know, when you speak, speak about what the agenda is right? Like, what would the first bill in a Republican Trifecta in 2024 be, right? So, you know, let`s say \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is reelected or \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , they have the house, they have the Senate. OK, HR1, S1.\par \par \uc2 I mean, I think it`s probably a national ban on abortion. But when you -- when you w talk about anything having vaguely to do with economics or policy, it`s like, I can`t name a single thing. Like I literally can`t name a single -- I have no idea. I mean, a tax cut. For some, it`ll be a tax cut.\par \par \uc2 MCGEE: Right. It will be more tax cuts, more deregulation, a ban on you know, different kinds of clean energy, you know, regressive taxes being raised on the working and middle class. It would be bans on history and our children`s freedom to learn. But ultimately, you`re right, it is frustrating when we see things like gas prices and inflation driving people away from the Democratic Party, when the Republican Party has absolutely no alternative agenda about what`s something that is ultimately being caused by these, you know, major global issues.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, when we think about what`s at stake here, I think we have to tie in the story that animates most people, right? What animates most people? They want to live in a place that`s safe. They want to feel like they belong. And that search for belonging has in many ways been, you know, the history of America, of different communities that have come here.\par \par \uc2 And what stops us from being the great city on a hill as a nation from sort of representing the great multiracial democracy that grand experiment is this very old zero sum lie. And right now, we`re seeing it being perpetuated from the highest reaches of the Republican Party, from our most-watched and profitable cable news network on the other side. And also, and we`re seeing it in these bands of books across the country and of our - - these attacks on our children`s freedom to learn.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what I think is really makes this tragedy, which is ultimately a moment to mourn, also a moment to mobilize against the forces that are profiting from this division.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Quickly, what do you think about the critique I`ve seen, which is that, look, Democrats and liberals talk too much about demographics as destiny, right? That their version of this narrative is, well, the country is getting less white, that means we win, right? So, like -- and that and that is heard by a certain segment of population as a kind of -- almost a kind of electoral threat.\par \par \uc2 MCGEE: Well, I mean, I don`t think that is something that most elected Democrats actually say in public, as opposed to the great replacement theory stuff that you hear all the time from the right-wing, right? I don`t think that`s the way that most good politicians see how they`re going to make it to 50 plus one.\par \par \uc2 I do think that we need to create a broad, multiracial, populist coalition that is willing to fight for one another instead of against one another. It`s that cross-racial solidarity that has always led to our greatest advances in American history, and it`s should be the backbone of the Democratic story.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Heather McGee, as always, great to hear from you. Thank you very much for making time tonight.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Monday night. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" starts right now. Good evening, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 RACHEL MADDOW, MSNB HOST: Good evening, Chris. Thanks, my friend.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220517ei5g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 16, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Garrett Haake, Emilie Ikeda, Dasha Burns, Antonia Hylton, Maria  Teresa Kumar, Eugene Scott, Brendan Buck \par \uc2 9,080 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Monday. The city of Buffalo mourning, 10 people killed in a grocery store rampage. Officials say the gunman was motivated by racist hate. What we know about the suspect the victims and the investigation ahead.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the rise in right-wing extremism in this country as some in politics stoke the rising hate for political gain, and the country returns to the seemingly endless political debate over what to do about guns.\par \par \uc2 And one day to PA, tomorrow is primary day in five states but it`s battleground Pennsylvania, where some last-minute twists are shaking up what was already one of the closest watched Senate primaries this cycle.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Garrett Haake in for Chuck Todd. As we learn more about the suspect and the victims in this weekend`s mass shootings in Buffalo, we find ourselves in depressingly familiar territory, asking what if anything could have been done to prevent this massacre and what can be done to prevent even more tragedies from happening in the future.\par \par \uc2 Police have now released the names of all 10 people killed, Saturday at the Tops Friendly Market. The youngest victim was 32 years old, the oldest was 86. Three other people were injured in the shooting and nearly all of the victims were black. Police say the 18-year-old suspect was dressed in tactical gear as he opened fire inside the supermarket and then he planned on continuing his rampage before he was stopped in what they`re calling a hate crime.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN FLYNN, DISTRICT ATTORNEY, ERIE COUNTY, NEW YORK: Well, obviously, from what -- all the evidence that we`ve gathered so far, the motivation at this stage appears to be pure hatred or pure racial hatred. Now, the -- there happens to be a penal law in the state of New York that is entitled a domestic act of terrorism motivated by hate. That particular crime right now is a crime that I`m focusing on.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Investigators say the suspect appears to have posted a 180-page manifesto, citing a racist conspiracy just days before the attack. New York State Police also say they investigated the suspect last June after he made a threatening statement about wanting to carry out a shooting. He was a minor at that time. He was taken to a hospital for a mental health evaluation and never charged for the crime. Meanwhile, the White House says President Biden will travel to Buffalo tomorrow to grieve with the community there.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by NBC`s Emilie Ikeda with the latest from Buffalo. I`m also joined by Erie County Executive \uc2 Mark Poloncarz\uc2 , and director of American University`s Polarization and Extremism Programming, Cynthia Miller-Idriss. She`s the author of the book Hate In The Homeland: The New Global Far Right. That paperback is out now.\par \par \uc2 So, Emilie, I`ll start with you. Just give me the latest here. What`s happening on the ground in Buffalo today in the wake of the shooting, and what`s the latest on this investigation?\par \par \uc2 EMILIE IKEDA, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Hey there, Garrett, unfortunately, new disturbing details coming out by the day. We know that the suspect had been in this area the day before the atrocious attack, scoping out the area, canvassing the area looking for where he would act out on this rampage. Remember, the police commissioner there told us that yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Now, we`re also learning from the Police Commissioner this morning that had this suspect not been stopped he would have planned on continuing his act of rampage down Jefferson Avenue continuing to target black people in this shooting, according to a commissioner speaking out to another news outlet earlier today. So believe it or not, there could have been even more bloodshed in this just an absolutely horrific tragedy.\par \par \uc2 Investigators have recovered three weapons on the scene. And one of the things I want to point out is police arrived on the scene within a minute of the call. Also, there was an armed security guard within the supermarket who did confront the suspect, slow the suspect down.\par \par \uc2 He did -- the police Commissioner told me actually shoot the suspect but because he was wearing tactical gear and just the assault rifle that he was wielding, the security guard was no match for the suspect as he was continuing throughout the story. He went on to kill even more people, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And, Emilie, what can you tell us about the victims of this shooting?\par \par \uc2 IKEDA: Well, these were beloved members of the community between the ages of 30 and 90 years old, friends, family, cousins, uncles, aunts. We know one -- the governor telling me that one of the people killed, he was simply trying to buy cupcakes for his son`s birthday.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 Another person, a grandmother who devoted her life to service, she ran a food pantry for a quarter of a century. We`ve talked about that security guard Aaron Salter, who confronted briefly the suspect he`s now being hailed as a hero. Another person, a driver who was shot and killed in the parking lot as he was trying to help someone put groceries in the car. So, just an absolutely atrocious act of hate, and one that you had mentioned is being investigated as a hate crime, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Mark, watching the rainfall there, it seems somehow appropriate. Talk to me about the mood in the community there today.\par \par \uc2 MARK POLONCARZ\uc2 , ERIE COUNTY EXECUTIVE, NEW YORK: Well, Garrett, it`s very somber here, Buffalo and Erie County. What we`re seeing is a community that`s reeling. We`re trying to come together to start the healing process. But we`re shocked that an individual from outside our community drove three-plus hours to our city of Buffalo to inflict as much pain as possible primarily on African-Americans, there`s no doubt that this was a targeted hate crime, domestic terrorism in our own backyard.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, Buffalo is now joining a long list of communities that have unfortunately dealt with mass shootings but there`s a much smaller list of those with regards to hate crimes and targeting African Americans. And buffalo is now part of that list. So our goal is to show the world that not only will we not be defined by the incident, but we will be defined by how we overcome it by supporting the families of lost loved ones, assisting those in need.\par \par \uc2 We have mental health counselors, as well as we`re going to be doing food distributions for people in the neighborhoods so that we can all overcome this and show that we`re stronger. If he tried to divide us he failed. We are united in our efforts to show that this community is stronger than one person coming in with a rifle.\par \par \uc2 And we will overcome it though we`ve lost, unfortunately, 10 individuals, many of them who are making a difference, not only in this community but other ones in New York State.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: How can President Biden help in that effort? What do you want to hear from him when he comes to Buffalo tomorrow?\par \par \uc2 POLONCARZ: Well, I think he`s going to come in and basically say that the nation mourns with you. I received a call from Secretary Mayorkas, the Homeland Security Secretary two days ago right after the incident and pledging the full support of the federal government for our efforts to not only prosecute but to heal.\par \par \uc2 And that`s exactly what we have is a two-track method of government now prosecuting through the Erie County District Attorney`s Office and the other departments in Erie County assisting with mental health counseling, food distribution when whatever`s needed in the community.\par \par \uc2 So I think the president is going to come here, I know he`s going to talk to some of the relatives of those who are deceased to help them grieve but also pledged the full support of the federal government to not only ensure that this individual is held responsible for his crimes but to help his community overcome what is a tragic, horrific event that will leave a scar on the city of Buffalo and Erie County for years to come.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I wanted to get on that government response a little bit. Play for you some of what \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , your governor told Chuck yesterday. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2 , (D-NY): Well, in many states legally, yes. In the state of New York, what he was able to buy was an AR-15. But he was able to enhance the magazine capacity. I mean, you can`t have that many rounds purchased legally here in the state of New York. But you just go over to Pennsylvania, go to a gun show.\par \par \uc2 Since my time in Congress, we`ve been trying to get a national response. And so individual states will do the best they can. And I`m going to be posing more gun laws on Tuesday, that pre-scheduled press conference even before this event occurred but we need other states to step up. We need the federal government on our side.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Do you agree with that sentiment here? What more can New York state do? What does the federal government need to do right now or better yet you know, last week?\par \par \uc2 POLONCARZ: Well, the federal government could have banned this weapon. It was previously banned. Remember, there was an assault weapon ban that expired, and unfortunately, since that, we`ve seen these horrible incidences across the United States. You often hear the argument well a good person with a gun can defeat an individual who has these types of weapons. Well, Mr. Salter -- a former retired Buffalo Police Officer Aaron Salter, a trained professional was unable to stop this assailant because the assailant was wearing body armor and wearing --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 POLONCARZ: And using a weapon which is designed for war. These weapons are not designed for hunting. I know a lot of hunters nobody uses an AR-15 to go hunting and if that`s how you`re going hunting, you`re doing the hunting wrong. They`re only used for one purpose, and that`s to kill as many people in a shorter period of time as possible. And Mr. Salter is a hero. He slowed down the assailant. Unfortunately, he took -- his own life was taken in the process. And this is a former trained professional, someone who was a Buffalo Police Officer for more than two decades, and he couldn`t stop him.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 POLONCARZ: It just goes to show we need to get these guns off the streets.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right. It doesn`t get more good guy with a gun than that. Cynthia, this shooting is just the latest now in a long line of racist shootings, motivated by white supremacist conspiracy theories and spread by online communities. What do you believe needs to happen to stop this pattern of racist violence like we saw in El Paso and in so many other cities?\par \par \uc2 CYNTHIA MILLER-IDRISS, DIRECTOR, PERIL CENTER, AMERICAN UNIVERSITY: Absolutely. I mean, we have been seeing this obviously, for quite a long time. But this specific conspiracy theory has mobilized terrorist shooters in Oslo and Christchurch, here in this country, in El Paso, and Pittsburgh, and now, here in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 And you know, what we`re seeing again, and again, is in this case is, unfortunately, textbook. Young men are radicalized in deep spaces online often through memes, through videos, through propaganda, that sharing false claims about scientific racism, and creating a sense of threat and then they calling on them to behave heroically, so -- what they believe to be heroic engagement.\par \par \uc2 We need obviously, gun -- you know, better gun laws, we need social media content moderation, but none of that`s going to work if we don`t reduce the susceptibility of people to racist propaganda, to begin with. And so, you know, people are going to believe this. If they`re not being taught otherwise in schools or at home, about structural racism and hierarchical systems of inequality, they get online and peep -- and propaganda offers explanations for them and tells them they`re a threat.\par \par \uc2 And so we -- you know, we`re in a moment in this country where we can`t even have conversations about race and we see the consequences of that in real-time right now.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So we should sit back for people who maybe haven`t followed this closely. This shooter`s manifesto talks a lot about what`s called the great replacement theory. It`s racist, xenophobic, anti-semitic idea that non- white people are somehow attempting to outnumber and replace white people in the United States. Talk to me a little bit about how you`ve seen this ideology grow over the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 MILLER-IDRISS: It has grown exponentially online. And I think you can track that to the rise of social media and to the way that online spaces, particularly for young men, are exploited in online gaming and meme sharing sites and other places that use video and other technology to embed humor, irony, satire, and share what are really hateful and dehumanizing ideas.\par \par \uc2 And so, we have seen that spread, it has been used in terrorist acts in many countries now at this point, and we`re seeing it here again and again. So that Great Replacement conspiracy theory is a conspiracy theory that has roots obviously, in long generations of racist belief systems here -- white supremacist belief systems.\par \par \uc2 But something about a decade ago has changed in it becoming a global rallying cry. The terrorist incident in Christchurch New Zealand, his manifesto was called the great replacement. I mean, we have been seeing this again and again and again over the last several years with a very delayed preventative response in this country.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So the -- last week, before this shooting happened, you wrote a piece for msnbc.com, in which you called out \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2 , the top-rated Cable News host in the business for perpetuating replacement theory. Then we had just this morning, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  tweeting the House GOP leadership has enabled white nationalism, white supremacy, and anti-Semitism. History has taught us that what begins with words, ends in far worse. GOP leaders must renounce and reject those views and those who hold them.\par \par \uc2 How problematic is it to have these ideas getting mainstreamed on the far right? I mean, you want to talk about sort of cultural solutions to this. These are folks with very large megaphones kind of flirting at the -- at the one end of the spectrum, maybe the least you could say, flirting with really dangerous ideas.\par \par \uc2 MILLER-IDRISS: Absolutely. I think there are two really dangerous things about it. The first, obviously, is what you just mentioned, that it`s mainstreaming and legitimizing the idea that there`s some kind of plot out there, some kind of nefarious plot by elites, by Democrats, in this case, in the case of the -- of the \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  type of illusion to it, to replace Americans with voters that would be more compliant by importing them, you know in -- you know, through immigration.\par \par \uc2 But what`s really dangerous -- I mean, in this case -- the buffalo case, it`s very clear this teen was radicalized not through mainstream media, but in his own words in online spaces but it makes it less likely that the adults around him. I mean, in our -- we have a guide for parents and caregivers that`s online, I can share, produce with the \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2  to warn them about online radicalization.\par \par \uc2 And the number one, our first bullet point under recognizing warning signs is about the great replacement theory. How less likely is it that parents or teachers, coaches, or other adults will recognize that as a warning sign if they`re hearing a version of it on cable news?\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Mark and Cynthia, we got to leave it there. Folks, you should follow Cynthia on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . There`s a lot of good resources there if you`re interested in reading more up on this, especially for parents.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, there were deadly mass shootings in multiple states this weekend. Democrat of Illinois, Majority Whip, Senator \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  joins me next on what is now decades of inaction from Congress on guns plus, the latest warning signs for Democrats in our new NBC poll, and later, we`re live on the ground ahead of some game-changing Senate primaries tomorrow. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. It`s not just Buffalo. Communities nationwide are grieving today after a weekend of gun violence took the lives of at least 14 people. As we said, 10 of those lives were lost in Buffalo where the black community was targeted in the city`s worst-ever mass shooting.\par \par \uc2 Then there was Orange County, California, Houston, and Chicago, 12 were killed in shootings that wounded eight others. Now, some elected Democrats are once again talking about doing more to address gun reform and racism. But folks, we have been here before, and it`s all been talk.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is the Senate`s second-highest-ranking Democrat, Senator \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  of Illinois. Senator, I think my first question is, do you think the country has just accepted this idea that mass shootings and gun violence are something we`re going to have to deal with every couple of weeks forever?\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN, (D-IL): Well, I think it`s a reality. It pains me to say it, but in the great city of Chicago, which I`m honored to represent, hardly a day goes by that someone isn`t shot and on the weekends, there was a shooting spree with 20, 30 people shot, seven or eight dead when it`s over. This has become commonplace, not just in Chicago, but certainly in that great city as well.\par \par \uc2 And you say to yourself, well, Durbin, you`re the chairman of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2 , you`re from Illinois, so what are you going to do about it? Well, you know the math. There`s a 50-50 Senate. 50 Democrats, 50 Republicans, it takes 60 votes to do anything significant or controversial.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 We don`t have 60 votes for any kind of gun safety legislation, even legislation accepted by the vast majority of people, whether it`s background checks to keep guns out of the hands of convicted felons and people who are mentally unstable, whether it`s getting rid of ghost guns, these guns that are being sold without any identification or serial number, the list goes on of common sense things we can do to make America safer, we cannot get virtually any Republican support for it.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: You don`t have 60 votes, but both you and majority leader Schumer, 14 months ago, one day after a mass shooting in Colorado took 10 lives, and a week after a shooting in Atlanta took eight said you were going to bring background checks to the floor regardless. And this is what you and the Majority Leader said at the time.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D-NY): This Senate will be different. The Senate is going to debate and address the epidemic of gun violence in this country.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Congress should do its job. Keep guns out of the hands of people who shouldn`t own them. We need to do more than reflect. We need to act. We need to show that we care and prevent the next mass shooting if we can.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I know you don`t have the votes, but you brought voting rights to the floor knowing you didn`t have the votes, you brought protections for abortion rights to the floor twice knowing you don`t have the votes. Where`s the fight? Where`s the showing people you care on this issue, Senator?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I can tell you we tried behind the scenes to work with members to member conversations and try to find some compromise, any compromise that moves us in the right direction. We`ve had no luck whatsoever. Buffalo was a horrible, miserable, hate film incident, which all of us across America really grieve for the people and families who were touched by it. But can we still bring anyone from the other side of the aisle across to join us in a bipartisan effort? I`ll see, I hope for it, I work for it but I`m very skeptical.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Why is it a good tactical idea then to have the big fight on voting rights or abortion rights to put those bills on the floor to put folks on the record, but to not do something similar with gun legislation? I mean, the Democrats broadly have not spent political capital on this issue.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I think our position on the issue is pretty well known. With only a couple exceptions in our caucus, the things that I`ve described about background checks, about ghost guns, people really understand where the Democrats stand and the Republican stand on. If there`s a decision that there`s going to be a vote to demonstrate that on the floor, I`ll support it. But I really want to continue to work to see if there`s any hope, any hope whatsoever, of getting Republican support for sensible gun safety.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I hear you. And you know, I do the reporting, I`m up there, I`m talking to Republicans too, and they`re not moving on this issue so I get it. On the Republican side, I did think it was interesting that you had Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  calling out her own party in a tweet this morning accusing Republican leadership of "enabling white nationalism, white supremacy, and anti-Semitism." What more would be helpful to hear from your Republican colleagues today on that part of this issue?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: The first hearing of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  that I chaired was with the superintendent, the Secretary in charge of the \uc2 Federal Bureau of Investigation\uc2 . He came forward and told us quite clearly the biggest threat to American terms of terrorism is domestic terrorism, its white nationalists, and white supremacist who are setting out to an agenda of violence.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve seen it play out over and over again. We saw it last year, January 6, 2021, on those who descended on the Capitol, and the message that they brought. We`ve seen it over and over again, sadly, repeated maybe by a lone wolf situation in Buffalo. This is a very real problem and I would hope that the Republicans could join the Democrats in saying whatever the political motivation far-left, far-right, whatever it happens to be, we`re going to condemn this activity.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: On a different topic, the Minority Leader, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , was in Ukraine over the weekend. He met with President Zelenskyy. On his way out, he got a call with reporters. He says the Senate is going to vote on this $40 billion package that`s -- this week. He`s confident it`ll get a bunch of Republican votes. You are the guy who counts votes on the Democratic side. Do you expect this to pass and do you expect it to send the kind of message that McConnell appears to hope that there is this broad bipartisan support for helping Ukraine in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: I was disappointed last week. I support that money for Ukraine. I want to send it as soon as the president suggested. We had a chance to do it last week. It was stopped by Republican Senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  of Kentucky. He demanded an amendment on the floor. It stopped our progress in place. We had to set put it off until this week. I`m sure we`re going to get back to it soon, very quickly, and I hope we can pass it quickly. I want this to always be bipartisan. We need to stand behind these brave people.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And it sounds like you`re going to get at least Republican leadership voting for that bill. That was McConnell`s message yesterday. I want to look ahead a little bit to the midterms here. The -- our new poll that came out over the weekend has the Democratic Party with a net negative rating of 19 points in the poll.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 It`s the worst it`s been in 30 years. Our viewers can see it on the screen the Democratic Party doing worse than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , worse than the Republican Party, and worse than the Supreme Court. To what do you attribute the branding part -- problem that Democrats have right now or is it -- is it more than a branding problem?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Well, I think it reflects the reality -- history`s reality that off-year elections are not coming to the president`s party. That`s the starting point. Secondly, we`re dealing with a transitional economy coming from the shutdown economy under COVID-19, reemerging, creation of jobs at record levels but with it comes the fires of inflation, every family`s feeling that. They want to know that Democrats are going to do something to change it.\par \par \uc2 The president used -- has used several tools in his kit. For example, releasing oil from the Strategic Petroleum Reserve was one, dealing with agricultural prices, and other there`ll be more jawboning if you will, those who are price gouging, and that`s happening at gas stations, it`s happening with baby formula. We`re finding a lot of that insidious activity taking place. So we need to be identified with that effort and helping families find an affordable place where they can deal with this economy.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is there more in the Senate`s toolkit quickly, Senator?\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: In terms of what we can achieve with the 50-50 Senate, it`s limited when it comes to policy but we continue to look for ways. The competitive bill that passed a bipartisan bill, that passed out of the Senate now in conference committee is going to give us a fighting chance to create good- paying jobs in the future. We`re sensitive to the economy, but it is difficult to fight the fires of inflation legislatively.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  of Illinois, the majority whip, the Chairman of the Judiciary Committee, perfect guests for this topic today. Senator Durbin, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 DURBIN: Thanks a lot.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: And up next, last-minute developments on both sides of the aisle in Pennsylvania. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:30:37]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. If it`s Monday, we`re less than 24 hours to polls opening in another round of midterm primaries. Now if Chuck were here, he`d either be quoting "Les Mis`" "One Day More," or maybe the Ramones. His eclectic taste notwithstanding, voters are voting tomorrow in Kentucky and in Oregon, North Carolina, Pennsylvania and in Idaho.\par \par \uc2 There was a big weekend in the closely watched Pennsylvania primary. Democratic frontrunner John Fetterman suffered a stroke. He says he`s on the way to a full recovery and according to him doctors say it appears he has no cognitive damage. His campaign now says Fetterman will not be at his team`s election night rally.\par \par \uc2 And on the Republican side, there`s new focus on Kathy Barnette`s past homophobic and anti-Muslim comments, and her appearance at President Trump`s January 6th rally in Washington, D.C., all this as she surges in the polls.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now with the latest on the ground from all over Pennsylvania is NBC`s Dasha Burns.\par \par \uc2 So, Dasha, let`s start on the Democratic side. What do we know about Fetterman`s condition and how is that likely to impact the closing hours of this race?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Garrett, the twists just keep coming in this race. Even in the last 24 hours multiple pieces of breaking news here. Yesterday we just found out that John Fetterman, you mentioned it there, he had a stroke.\par \par \uc2 We were actually at an event of his on Friday morning, a hundred or so people in the room, and well after start time his staffer came in and announced that he wouldn`t be there, that he is not feeling well. And we haven`t seen him on the campaign trail since. Well, it turns out that that morning he wasn`t feeling well and his wife Gisele made him go get checked out.\par \par \uc2 And Garrett, I hope you hear this, I hope my husband hears this, your wife is always right. Taking that advice might just save your life. OK?\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Got it.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: So thankfully he did get checked out and they caught a blood clot that had made his way to his heart causing that stroke. He is now recovering. He says he`s feeling much better. But he will not be at that Tuesday night rally. He`s going to still be in the hospital and his wife Gisele, the lifesaver, is going to be the headliner on Tuesday night, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right. Let`s talk about the Republican side. You`ve got new reporting verifying images of the ascendant Republican primary candidate Kathy Barnette at the January 6th "Stop the Steal" rally. What do we know about her participation there?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes, so right around the time we were getting the news of Fetterman`s stroke, I was looking on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and started to see images circulating of Barnette marching toward the Capitol on January 6th. Now we did know before this that she was there on January 6th. In fact she organized buses to the rally. But this is the first time that we`re actually seeing images of this. NBC News did verify.\par \par \uc2 Now in those videos, she`s marching towards the Capitol alongside known members of the Proud Boys that were later arrested and indicted on their alleged role in that riot. Now we talked to Barnette about this. She says that she views January 6th in the same way that the left views the summer of 2020 and the \uc2 Black Lives Matter movement\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 I asked her campaign to respond to these images. Here`s what they told me. They said Kathy was in D.C. to support President Trump and demand election accountability. Any assertion that she participated and/or supported the destruction of property is intentionally false. She has no connection whatsoever to the Proud Boys.\par \par \uc2 Now Barnette is fresh to frontrunner status. And just now the campaigns are doing their opposition research. They`ve been putting out statements and ads. I just had a chance to speak with McCormick, David McCormick, her rival in this primary. Here`s what he had to say about Barnette.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Do you think she is fit for office?\par \par \uc2 DAVID. MCCORMICK (R), PENNSYLVANIA SENATE CANDIDATE: Well, listen, I`ve got -- I know Kathy on the campaign trail. I respect her personal story. I mean, she`s been tested. She was tested 18 months ago when she ran for Congress and lost by 20 percentage points. And now what`s happening is she`s getting the spotlight and a lot of questions that should have been answered a long time ago that all the scrutiny that I got and others have gotten are now coming to the fore. And she has to answer all those questions, whether it`s about military service, career, whatever it is.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Do you think she`s answered them properly?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: Well, she needs to answer them, and I don`t think she`s answered them yet.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And Garrett, right now we`re packing up right after this. We`re headed to a rally that Barnette is hosting in Scranton. And we`ll see if at that event she does in fact answer any of these questions, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Dasha Burns, is there a candidate you haven`t talked to this in race? You`re killing it out there. And yes, you`re right, my wife is always right. Thank you, Dasha.\par \par \uc2 And Dasha will be one of the faces you`ll see tomorrow night when Chuck and Kristin Welker host a MEET THE PRESS election special streaming on NBC News Now. That starts at 8:00 Eastern.\par \par \uc2 And up next, with North Carolinians headed to the polls tomorrow, too, how abortion and other hot button issues are playing on the campaign trail there.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. And as we`ve been saying, tomorrow is primary day in North Carolina, too. So what`s on voters` minds there as they head to the polls?\par \par \uc2 As part of our county-to-county reporting project, we are talking to people about exactly that. In Anson, North Carolina, black voters struggling with the impacts of inflation and low wages say they`re frustrated with lawmakers in D.C. They say elected leaders are not doing enough to address their urgent needs.\par \par \uc2 NBC News correspondent Antonia Hylton is in Anson County, North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 So, Antonia, talk to me about what you`re hearing from these voters about whether they feel like they`re being listened to, if their issues are being addressed ahead of this primary?\par \par \uc2 ANTONIA HYLTON, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Garrett, people here feel like they`ve been completely ignored and they are feeling financially squeezed right now. Anson is a rural community close to the border with South Carolina, the kind of place where the plants that used to provide jobs have gone overseas in recent years. So people here tell me they`re mostly working low-wage jobs and they`re seeing the cost of everything around them go up.\par \par \uc2 But their income isn`t increasing to meet that. And they don`t feel like the Biden administration or even frankly leaders here at the statewide level are doing enough to bring them relief. And so many of the voters particularly black voters under the age of 40 here tell me that they`re not engaged with the midterms right now. Many of them haven`t voted yet early in the primaries and aren`t even sure they`re going to go out and vote tomorrow because they feel fed up with the entire system.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 In fact, I spent the day yesterday at a black-owned convenience shop and the owner there was telling me that his prices have gone up and he`s had to leave some of his shelves empty because he can`t raise prices on the people that he serves because they just can`t take it. Take a listen to a conversation that I had with one of his patrons there.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC STURDIVANT, ANSON COUNTY RESIDENT: People have this agenda, well, they say, I`m going to do this, I`m going to do that. But then when they actually get in the seat.\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: You just have lost trust?\par \par \uc2 STURDIVANT: Yes. Basically. Nobody never followed through and do what they say, you know what I`m saying? They have all the promises, if you vote for me, I`m going to do this, I`m going to make this change, I`m going to do this. It never gets done.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HYLTON: Garrett, interestingly, some of the non-voters who I met there yesterday told me that the one issue that might get them reengaged is abortion. They were aware of the leaked Supreme Court draft. Many of them while they don`t like abortion say that they do support choice and were concerned about the rollback in women`s reproductive rights. And so they were telling me that they may go out and actually reregister to vote and get reengaged ahead of November if they feel like they can get behind a candidate who they think will take serious action to protect abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 But right now, because of financial issues being their number one concern, they frankly just feel a bit disconnected and disrespected by people who they hope to see act on their behalf sooner, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, Antonia Hylton in Anson County, North Carolina. Thank you for that reporting.\par \par \uc2 And up next, as we`ve been saying, our new NBC News poll shows that the political environment for Democrats is looking pretty bleak this November. What, if anything, can change that trajectory? The panel is here next.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:45:21]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Welcome back. As we get ready for another round of primary elections tomorrow, the overall political environment for Democrats looking ahead to November is still very stormy. In our latest NBC News poll out just yesterday, just 16 percent of respondents say the country is headed in the right direction, with a whopping 75 percent saying the country is on the wrong track.\par \par \uc2 Those negative feelings extend to the party in power with President Biden and the Democratic Party`s numbers well under water less than six months out from the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now on set is Eugene Scott, national political reporter for "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 ," Maria Teresa Kumar, president and CEO of Voto Latino, she is also an MSNBC contributor, and Brendan Buck, former adviser to Republican speakers \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  and \uc2 John Boehner\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, Eugene, you saw these numbers here. When the wrong track numbers are this bad, what, if anything, can Democrats do to turn it around? They are going to be the guys who get blamed it seems like as we approach November.\par \par \uc2 EUGENE SCOTT, THE WASHINGTON POST NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: They certainly are. And one of the big criticisms of the Democratic Party from people on both sides of the aisle is not having a very clear message in terms of being able to articulate what they have done so far, which is not the same thing as not having done something, but when you talk to a lot of voters they really aren`t sure what it is that Democrats have accomplished and why it is that they should back them, you know, for two more years at least in Congress.\par \par \uc2 And I think that`s a real challenge that the Democratic Party are going to have to figure out what they need to do in terms of convincing people differently.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Maria, do you agree with that assessment, that it`s a messaging problem, not an accomplishment problem? Has enough been done that the Democrats can present to the American people come November?\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: You know, I was like to half joke and say for any mom who has a kid in school, they`re very excited that they`re no longer Zoom schooling. So it`s this progress that we have seen since a competent government has been able to get people back on track with the pandemic. Has it been perfect? Absolutely not. But we have shots in arms, we have businesses open, and we do have kids back in school.\par \par \uc2 The biggest challenge I think we`re finding is part of it is communication. Part of it is the far left of the party hasn`t received everything that they would like to see, but what we have seen is there was a recent poll about two weeks ago with ABC and "The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 " and they found that among independent voters and among young voters, they were leaning Democratic by 12 points, even though the Democratic Party itself and Biden was under water.\par \par \uc2 And I think it`s because we`re going to start seeing more issues driving this campaign whether it`s abortion, whether it`s access to voting rights, whether it`s gun violence. That is where the grassroots organization came together for 2018 and 2020, and again I think it`s going to be a grassroots movement that`s going to get the Democrats at least within striking distance despite the party itself.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: But when you look at the issue numbers, it is interesting because the coronavirus numbers -- Biden`s numbers are great but it seems like people don`t give him credit for it. And the abortion numbers do seem to be the kind of green shoot for Democrats. The issue moved from 3 percent to 10 percent on the list of Democrats -- voters rather most important issues. That`s before this opinion has come down.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: And I can tell you, like, our -- the work that we do when it comes to voter registration, trying to get people jazzed up about the primaries, no one is interested. We started serving connecting abortion to the voting booth, we`ve seen close to 40 percent increase in people actually registering to vote under that in Georgia, in Pennsylvania, even in Florida and Texas.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I wonder how it`s going to affect the (INAUDIBLE). Brandon --\par \par \uc2 BRENDAN BUCK, FORMER ADVISER TO SPEAKERS RYAN AND BOEHNER: Yes, so that`s the big challenge obviously Democrats have. Turn this election from one about inflation to something about other issues. Elections are usually only about one or two things.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Right now the election is about inflation, about the economy, about people not feeling well. If Democrats can make this about abortion or about gun control, then maybe they have a chance. But that`s a big bet. Maybe it`s the only bet they have. But you can`t make an election about five or six different issues. Right now it`s about cost of living and until that changes, they`re in bad shape.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Is there a risk for Republicans here. I mean, you look at all these states that put in place these trigger laws. I mean, Americans` abortion politics is actually somewhere in this mushy middle. You know, most Americans say they don`t want, you know, sort of complete access necessarily and they definitely don`t want these full bans.\par \par \uc2 Could there be a blowback for Republican politicians in some of these states that have put trigger bans and other really restrictive bills in place?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Certainly. I mean, what Republicans have long said is that if you strike down Roe vs. Wade you`re going to have states that put in reasonable limitations. And what you`re seeing in some places less than reasonable limitations on abortion. So that`s the risk. But again, as you said, I don`t think that this is a black and white clear win for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BUCK: There are a lot of Republican voters who are very animated by this as well. I think it`s largely awash if you even get to the point that it is about that this this election.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Well, it maybe, Eugene, the key here and maybe the special sauce for Democratic enthusiasm, because we did see that in our poll here that Democrats are starting to feel more enthusiastic about the election.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:02]\par \par \uc2 They`re closing that gap a little bit for Republicans. If Brendan`s theory in the case is right here and there`s only so many issues you can care about, maybe abortion isn`t the issue that, you know, closes the deal for Democrats but if it`s the one that sort of wakes people up that this is an election they need to pay attention to, that`s not nothing.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: Well, a lot of Democrats are certainly hoping so, and it`s important to remember that this is May and we don`t know what will be happening by the time, you know, most voters go to the poll. And we have seen in the past that things that people thought would motivate voters in the spring were like dead when it came to time to actually vote.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Right.\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: And so what happens in terms of whether there`s, you know, a leak of the draft actually moves I think will determine how severely people focus on this issue moving forward.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I want to talk about Pennsylvania a little bit. We could end up with a Republican ticket that was like all in on January 6th. I mean, these are all people who, you know, participating in some of the activities, not necessarily inside the building.\par \par \uc2 But, Brendan, I think about all the time is \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  has talked about trying to get the most electable people to the finish line here. It does not seem like that`s what he`s going to get in Pennsylvania. How big of a problem is that for Republicans?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Yes, it`s funny you say that. I ran into somebody in McConnell world last week. He was despondent.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Really?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: About the fact that -- yes. That Kathy Barnette was potentially going be their nominee. This is a race that I think both sides are trying really hard to lose. On the Democratic side, you have Fetterman probably going to beat \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 . When I was in the speaker`s office, we used to joke that \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  was the most electable candidate in the country. We used to joke we`re never going to beat him because everybody likes \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And yet Democrats are not going to nominate him probably. And then on the Republican side, you have Kathy Barnette. You know, there are often sort of kooky characters, with, you know, questionable backgrounds who won but they usually get ignored. And here we are in 2022 potentially the nominee for Senate in a swing state. So I hope that Republicans are still the favorite in this state but both sides are trying really hard to nominate probably the least electable of the possible candidates.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: What do we make of the Democratic field on this? I mean, I agree \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  on paper was supposed to be like the golden child Democratic candidate. He`s getting clobbered right now according to the polls. But all of a sudden Fetterman is off the trail. Pennsylvania is a state that everybody votes on election day basically. How do you see that playing out?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Well, I think it`s very similar. I`m going to take you guys across the Atlantic to the race that we saw against Macron and a white nationalist. And you have a lot of folks going neck to neck on that because they weren`t really enthusiastic. But the moment that the runoff came and you saw that it was either democracy or nationalism, that`s when a lot of the folks basically switched sides and they came out in droves for Macron.\par \par \uc2 I actually think that there is a group of Americans that are moderate Republicans and independents that are waiting in the wings. They`re not interested in the primary but if they have someone that is so anti- physicist, they`re fascist, running against any Democrat or independent, I think that there`s going to be a break. And it`s not because they like the independent but it`s because democracy, little D democracy is on the ballot this year.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s always been the Biden theory of the case which is compare me to the alternative.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Gosh, I would never know.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Not to the Almighty, right?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Right. Right. Well, that`s fair.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Like you want an alternative.\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Right.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, Eugene, when we wake up Wednesday morning, what`s the story going to be about the Pennsylvania primary? Like what -- open seat primaries can be very eye-opening to the dynamics that are going on in both parties. What do you think we might see?\par \par \uc2 SCOTT: I think one of the things people will be focused on specifically related to the Republican primary is figuring out how influential President Trump still is in Republican politics in this state. We talk about Kathy Barnette, you know, rising in the polls. Trump has come out and said that he doesn`t think she could win but he will supporter her if she does. And so ultimately I think people really want to see where the GOP is still going and how influential the former president remains in this party.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: His decision to endorse Mastriano in the governor`s race, feel like trying to hitch his wagon to somebody who is already winning.\par \par \uc2 Brendan, let`s talk about the Republican Party here. This Cheney-\uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2  new back and forth about great replacement theory, how we got into a place where the Republican Party are trying to like tap dance around this question of whether or not there is a white replacement effort going on in this country? How big of a problem is that for the GOP?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Well, it`s certainly not something you want to be talking about but maybe it`s something --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: When it`s some member of leadership --\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Maybe it`s something we need to be talking about. I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: In what context?\par \par \uc2 BUCK: Well, I think for a long time a lot of people wanted to say that there was no undertow of racism anywhere within our party, and I think at this point I have to admit of course there is somewhere within the party there`s racism. But then you have some members of Congress who are actually flirting with that.\par \par \uc2 And what we have is a situation where if you`re a member of Congress, you`ve decided that you`re going to go on an electoral path where it`s Republicans only, you don`t ever try to worry about the middle. When you get to a situation like that, you can`t cast aside any votes. Even if they`re racist, you can`t cast them aside.\par \par \uc2 And I think that is the dynamic that is flowing through a lot of the Republican Party. It`s not that anybody is embracing racism. But they don`t have the electoral margin to get rid of it. And I think that`s the problem we found ourselves stuck in when you take this Republican-only, play to the base approach and ignore the middle. I think you could cast that aside very easily if you would just embrace a little bit of the middle.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:04]\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Maria, I want to give you probably the last word here. I was struck in my interview with \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  when I asked him about guns and the idea that they were going to do something. I thought he was very fatalistic on it, that this is not an issue that`s going to get addressed in a 50-50 Senate, which I think anybody who can do the math can kind of see this, but I don`t sense there`s the fight there for Democratic voters or for Democratic politicians.\par \par \uc2 Can that be an issue that breaks through? Again, if we`re operating on a theory that there`s only so many issues that are going to matter to voters, can Democrats capture an anti-gun voter base to make a difference on this issue?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: I think that we have to look at folks like the, you know, (INAUDIBLE) Montana. He is a Democrat who has very much a constituency that believes in safe right guns. And so that`s something that they`re realistically in the Democratic Party they`re not going to want to touch. Is it good for America? No. I do think that we need to have sensible gun laws.\par \par \uc2 The fact that there are -- when we`ve talked about minority communities, we fail to recognize that we`re talking about 145 million Americans. We`re over 40 percent of this population. There`s nothing minority about us and when we walk outside and we are concerned that our children are going to get targeted because of the color of their skin, we need to have frank conversations on this changing demographic that we`re seeing, what it means.\par \par \uc2 And what I see in Trump and the extreme right and the Republican Party, it`s political expediency. They`re talking about a future that`s already in our classrooms. My children represent a majority-minority America. Your children represent a majority-minority America. And we are not having the difficult conversations of what that means and we need to, both parties need to embrace that.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So maybe the action is on hate, not on guns. It`s something. We`ll cover that in our second hour. No, I`m just kidding. Because that`s all the time we`ve got.\par \par \uc2 Maria, Brendan, and Eugene, thank you all for coming in.\par \par \uc2 And thank you for being with us this hour. Chuck will be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. And MSNBC coverage continues with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220517ei5g00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION WITH AL SHARPTON for May 15, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Yasmin Vossoughian, Richard Lui, Carlos Curbelo \par \uc2 8,638 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:00]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Authorities are still investigating but have made it clear this attack was racially motivated after investigators confirmed that the shooters may have left racist proclamations across his social media, allegedly targeting the Buffalo neighborhood for its racial makeup.\par \par \uc2 At least one weapon retrieved at the scene, confirmed to have hate speech imprinted on it and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  has announced that it will investigate the attack as possible hate crimes and an act of racially motivated violent extremism. The 18-year-old suspect charged with first degree murder awaits a felony hearing set for Thursday, according to the Erie County district attorney.\par \par \uc2 This evening, Buffalo is still reeling from the horror with vigils throughout the day, and City Mayor Byron Brown pulling no punches about the shooter`s motivation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MAYOR BYRON BROWN (D-NY), BUFFALO: This individual came here with the expressed purpose of taking as many black lives as he possibly could.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Earlier today, President Biden weighed in on the tragedy from the rose garden teasing up the federal response.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Jill and I, like all of you, pray for the victims and their families and a devastated community. I`ve been receiving updates from my team at the White House that is in close contact with the Justice Department. We are still gathering the facts where already the Justice Department has stated publicly it is investigating the matter as a hate crime.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Civil rights activists like myself as head of the National Action Network are sending thoughts, prayers and resources to the Buffalo. I`ve talked to some of the family members and want to help them as they face this tragedy that long after the cameras will live in their hearts, minds and souls, and we are going to help them with the funerals and help them pursue justice. More on that later.\par \par \uc2 But, to help the families we are also calling on the Biden administration to rededicate itself to containing the threat of white supremacist terror. More on that also in a bit.\par \par \uc2 But we first start on the ground tonight and Buffalo. Now, I want to bring in -- I want to turn in to my colleague, Yasmin Vossoughian, who is in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 Yasmin, what is the latest you`re hearing on the ground there?\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC HOST: Rev., you and I have been spoken when too many times about things that happened like this in this country. I believe the first time I was on with you, five years or so ago, it was I`m following a shooting of an unarmed black man. And much of that is now reverberating. Instances like that are reverberating in this community now.\par \par \uc2 And I just kind of want to show you a little bit of what is happening here, because this community has been gathering all day. You can see behind me, this community getting together, gathering, supporting one another, angry, Rev.\par \par \uc2 They are angry as to what is taking place. They are also celebrating the love that they have for one another as well and how they will rise up once again. But so much of Rev, what I have been hearing so far today has been the fact that this community feels as if they don`t matter. Feels as if they have been forgotten.\par \par \uc2 The resources are not here, but what happened yesterday is not the result of one instance. One incident and which an individual walked into the tops grocery store behind me, and fired indiscriminately shooting 13 people, killing ten of them, 11 of them of which are black Americans. But they are frustrated, because they feel as if there`s only one grocery store in this neighborhood. This is now shut down. They now have to drive miles to get groceries out further.\par \par \uc2 They`re providing transportation to get them to other stores. But it`s only a reflection of the lack of resources and investment that`s happening in neighborhoods like this. It`s a reflection of the 400 years of systemic racism that exists in this country, which you talk about all the time and you well know, obviously because the work that you do, and you`ve dedicated your life to it.\par \par \uc2 It is a reflection of the lack of resources that`s being given to schools in this neighborhood as well. They are angry. They are frustrated and they want to see change. And here`s to top it all off, Rev, they don`t have faith in the government getting things done.\par \par \uc2 They see the gridlock in Washington. They see people around this country that are trying to ban books, talking about the history of racism in this country. And they feel forgotten. They feel as if people are unwilling to address the history that faces black Americans in this country.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:01]\par \par \uc2 And because of that, that individual felt empowered to walk into the grocery store behind me and kill ten black Americans.\par \par \uc2 And so the investigation is ongoing. They`re looking into the history of mental health issues that shooter has. They were identified back in 2021, why it is those red flags were not raised. It was legally purchased, the gun that he did. He had three firearms on him, 30 magazines, I believe, when he entered the supermarket. The investigation will continue to go on. And as we learn more about the victims we will bring to you.\par \par \uc2 I actually just spoke also with someone who is inside that grocery store yesterday. She got separated from the father of her child and her eight- year-old daughter, London. And it was her birthday today, Rev. And she said all she cared about was seeing that her daughter was still alive. And her daughter hid behind some grocery shelves and she got so emotional with me because she said she did not know if she could survive.\par \par \uc2 And because another woman sacrificed her life lunging at the shooter, she`s alive today and she`s still mommy of that little girl, London. And so it`s stories like that wer`e continuing to hear throughout the day.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It`s so emotional reporting from Buffalo, and as I said I spoke with some of the families. Their emotions are certainly, certainly heartbreaking and understandable.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Senator Kirsten Gillibrand of New York, a Democrat. Senator Gillibrand, thank you for being with us here tonight.\par \par \uc2 You tweeted your condolences last night in response to the Buffalo shooting, writing that you will, quote, continue to fight my hardest to pass common sense gun safety measures and to confront and defeat the scourge of white supremacy, end quote.\par \par \uc2 Now, I`m reminded of when during the presidential run three years ago, 22 people were shot and killed in El Paso, Texas. The white gunman admitting to authorities that attack was racially motivated against Latinos.\par \par \uc2 You said then as a candidate that you would direct the Justice Department to crack down and infiltrate white supremacist organizations. In the aftermath of Buffalo, do you anticipate Congress, the Senate in particular, prioritizing this threat and legislation be enacted to combat it in the way you talked about?\par \par \uc2 SEN. KIRSTEN GILLIBRAND (D-NY): I do, Reverend. Since that time, I joined the Senate Intelligence Committee and I have had regular reporting on with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  is doing to trace, track down, arrest and break up these cells of white supremacists, these cells of extreme domestic violence and people who are just fueled by hate, by anti-Semitism, by bigotry, by racism.\par \par \uc2 And this shooting shows how not only divided this country is but how an 18- year-old man could have so much hate in his heart that he would drive over 100 miles with the intention of killing as many black people as he can find. That is a terrible reflection of where this country is today. And as you and I talked about it, this wasn`t started with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , but it certainly was elevated and exacerbated and created --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And given some legitimacy, I mean --\par \par \uc2 GILLIBRAND: And normalized by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That`s exactly the word and normalized. I said this one, I met the press, this young man was 15 years old when Charlottesville happened and the president of the United States at that time says they were fine people on both sides. So, impressionable kids reading racist stuff on tribes, on social media, and the president of the United States normalizing this.\par \par \uc2 GILLIBRAND: And you take -- on top of that, a president who normalizes it, you take on top of that social media platforms who have zero accountability to the American people who can create these rabbit holes of hate and disgusting rhetoric that would drive any 18-year-old person to hate his fellow humans so much.\par \par \uc2 This is a reckoning, Reverend. We have to hold social media platforms accountable. We need to regulate the internet. We need to figure out how we can create the possibility of a less divided nation and how we can lift up rights and equality and investment, just as Yasmin said, making sure these communities have the best public schools in the country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 GILLIBRAND: Making sure we are funding when we rebuilding the infrastructure through our bipartisan infrastructure bill, make sure it`s being invested in black communities, so members of that community can hired to do the work to make sure it is done in a way that gets rid of the racist past with the Robert Moses of the world who created racist architectures for cities that harmed low-income and black communities.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:01]\par \par \uc2 So, this is a long process, but it must be addressed head on at each place where it enables this man who also was able to buy all weapons he wanted without any red flags, because the \uc2 NRA\uc2  and the corruption in Washington and the greed and money in politics makes that so easy.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Gun control as well as dealing with biases, certainly part of it, and you`ve laid it out.\par \par \uc2 While there is time, Senator, the vote to pass abortions rights legislation 