 one terrorist threat is domestic. And it tends to be fueled by this insidious ideology of white supremacy. It is also related to this so-called replacement theory, which, unfortunately, is not just on the internet, you also see it on broadcast television, cable television shows.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:03]\par \par \uc2 We know Fox News has a number of folks that talk about replacement theory for African Americans and Jewish-Americans who are trying to replace white folks here in the country. It`s a ridiculous conspiracy theory and we have to condemn those kinds of comments. All folks, regardless of your political party, regardless of who you are, if you believe that we need to have facts governing this country and not let conspiracy theories stoke hate and rage, people have to understand that words have consequences. Words can lead to action. We`ve seen it in attacks in this country and all the places around the world and we`ve got to deal with it in a very (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, the shootings in Buffalo has cast a tragic shadow on what has been a weekend of solidarity for supporters of abortion rights. Demonstrators all over the country turned out to voice their opposition to the overturning of Roe v. Wade, which appears to be a very real possibility, according to a leaked draft of a Supreme Court opinion that I referred to.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know you have a personal family story about abortion. Can you share that with us, and explain why this issue is so important?\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Well, I will share some of it. It is a long story. I told the story on the floor of the Senate this week. But bottom line, it is a story that, unfortunately many, many people have. My former wife, who was pregnant with the child that we very much wanted, a medical emergency presented itself. But in the fourth month, our physician, her physician, I should say, basically said that there is no way that this baby is going to survive. Her water broken, there was no 