ey just want to do it.\par \par \uc2 Senator Peters, we mentioned you`re oldest chair of the Democratic Senate campaign. I want to get your thoughts on some breaking news this afternoon that the Democratic frontrunner running in this -- for Senate in Pennsylvania, Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman, has suffered a stroke. Fetterman has issued a statement saying he has been in the hospital since Friday but he`s feeling better.\par \par \uc2 Now, this comes as the Republican primary race has been shaken up. A deeply conservative newcomer, Kathy Barnette, is surging in the polls even in the face of attacks by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and a more establishment wing of the GOP. This race is setting off alarm bells for Republicans with political reporting. There is a rising sense of unease amongst party insiders that Republicans could end up with candidates who make a Senate takeover more difficult.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us about this situation in Pennsylvania and how would it play into the midterm picture nationwide?\par \par \uc2 PETERS: Well, certainly, first, let me say, I am certainly sending my prayers to that Lieutenant Governor Fetterman and his family.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:04]\par \par \uc2 I understand he will be underway to the full recovery but certainly we hope to really understand what he`s going through right now, and his family has got to be very difficult. But I understand he`ll be on the campaign trail soon. We will see what happens in the primary, who wins that primary.\par \par \uc2 But there is no question that Pennsylvania is central to our ability to hold the majority in the Senate and expand the Senate. We need to pick up seats. We have a very contentious Republican primary going on in Pennsylvania. They are beating each other up. We have got a surge of another candidate, as you mentioned, who is a very right-wing candidate.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Republicans themselves think that she is unelectable if she is the Republican candidate. And we are seeing that around the country. We are seeing Republican primaries where these candidates are just beating the heck out of each other trying to be the Trumpiest of the Trumpsters and making statements and taking positions that I think will come back to cost them dearly in the general election.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Let me thank you, Senator \uc2 Gary Peters\uc2  of Michigan, for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Coming, up New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  will be here to give us the latest on the terrible attack in Buffalo that killed ten people.\par \par \uc2 And more on lawmakers` efforts to protect the rights of women seeking abortion care. We will speak with one on the forefront of that fight in Georgia, next.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other top news stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev. a good Sunday to you. Some of the stories, other stories we`re watching for you this hour, COVID-19 cases in South Africa are surging. It is driven by two omicron subvariants, BA.4 and BA.5. Cases there grew from 300 a day on average in April to about 8,000 a day this week.\par \par \uc2 COVID cases in the U.S., now near an 83 million total. The virus has now killed over 1 million Americans.\par \par \uc2 And a so-called blue red moon will unbeaten display tonight, if you are North and South America. You will see a rare total lunar eclipse overnight. The earth will pass directly between the moon and the sun when this happens. We earthlings will see the reflection of red and orange hews of earth`s sunsets and sunrises for about 90 minutes. Enjoy that.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after this short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION. Joining me now, Congresswoman \uc2 Nikema Williams\uc2 , the Democrat of Georgia. She is the Chair of the Democratic Party of Georgia and sits in the seat once held by the late great \uc2 John Lewis\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, we are following the latest in the Buffalo mass shooting Saturday that left ten people dead, investigators are calling the attack a hate crime. You tweeted out your condolences to the families of victims and you said quote, we must take decisive action against white supremacy and extremism. What type of legislation would you propose to deal with, with this issue?\par \par \uc2 REP. NIKEMA WILLIAMS (D-GA): So Reverend Al, I think even beyond the legislation we have to start with calling things for what they are. We still have some people in this country that are not going to be willing to call this an act of white supremacy and extremism.\par \par \uc2 I am in a state that just passed legislation on the state level to say you don`t even need a license to get a gun in the state anymore. And this is what they`re calling constitutional hearing, but it`s really criminal hearing. So in a state, Georgia, where we are making it more difficult to register to vote and to vote at the ballot box to making it easier to get a gun. And this is unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman, on another battle front, you have been on the front lines of this abortion rights fight. You led a 118 of your colleagues in proposing that Congress condemn attempts to criminally prosecute people who perform abortions. Gender firming care and contraceptive care. Why is it important for this resolution to pass in the wake of the leaked Supreme Court draft opinion, setting the stage to overturn Roe v. Wade?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So Reverent Al, I`ve never lived in a time where Row v. Wade was not the law of the land where it was not established precedent. And I don`t want to go to a time where I have fewer rights than my mother and grandmother had. But those are the days that we are looking at.\par \par \uc2 The leaked Supreme Court draft decision was not a shout or surprise to me, because of