en, we need to make sure that we are being responsible in telling people, do not dilute the milk that you have. Don`t water it down. This could be temperamental to the lives of so many.\par \par \uc2 So as we are talking with our Republican colleagues, instead of being the party of no, how about they beat the party of the people that we are, and stand on the side of make sure that babies, that they, state that they care about so much, are getting what they need.\par \par \uc2 This isn`t for political points, this is about making sure that people get what they need. Infants who need formula in this country, but yet, they are still standing there and voting against everything that the Democrats do to deliver for the people of this country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now. In just over a week, Georgians will head to the polls to cast their ballots in the state`s primary races. Meanwhile, a judge in Georgia is expected to rule on whether quote, exact match, end of quote, registration rules and absentee ballot cancellation practices violate voting laws.\par \par \uc2 After the passage of Senate Bill 202, which makes it more difficult to vote by mail, what challenges could voters face and what can they do to prevent any issues?\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: So, Reverend Al, we`re seeing it in this primary. This is going to be our first test on this May 24th primary, on wet SB 202, the voter suppression bill, that Republicans in Georgia past will actually do.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing the numbers being impacted on people that return their absentee ballots already. And this exact match I did, that Republicans like to think is a big deal, it might not be a big deal for them. But when your name is Nikema and it might show up on a list with an Ne, instead of an Ni, that could deny me my right to a ballot in this country in this state of Georgia, if Republicans get their way.\par \par \uc2 So we have to continue to remind people of the laws, the new laws, that the Republicans have put in place. And we have to make sure that we`re continuing to educate people so that we get this done and in advance of the November elections. So this is our trial run, so that we know which issues come up, how to get ahead of it in November, and make sure that people have the information that they need to cast their ballot.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you for being with us, Congresswoman Williams.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAMS: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, we`re following breaking news out of Orange County, California. Police are on the scene of a reported shooting at a church in Laguna Woods. According to the sheriff`s office, multiple victims have been shot, one person has been detained and a weapon that may have been involved has been recovered. We`ll bring you more information as we get it.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is New York Governor \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Governor, thanks for joining us tonight on this difficult weekend for your state, your hometown in fact. Now, I know that President Biden reached out to you today. What can you tell us about the call and did he mention whether he would visit Buffalo ahead of his foreign travel (ph) later this week?\par \par \uc2 GOV. \uc2 KATHY HOCHUL\uc2  (D-NY): Well, thank you, Reverend Al. First off, for your outrage, you and I communicate within hours of this massacre. This white supremacist domestic terrorism case, as what they`re calling, you hear it calling weird, it is.\par \par \uc2 But I was very happy to receive a call from President Biden. And we talked for quite awhile about how painful this experience was, and he said, whatever he can do. And I said, Mr. President, you coming here would really make a difference because Buffalo is a scrappy blue collar town. And my grandparents were immigrants here. My parents lived in a trailer park. I mean, it`s a tough, tough town and (INAUDIBLE) Buffalo for a long time.\par \par \uc2 And I said, it would really mean the world to people in Buffalo. Just to have you here, Mr. President, to let them know this community matters. These are my neighbors. This is my hometown. And this is deeply personal that my friends were slaughtered going to the grocery store. So, I believe the president will come and I just appreciate so much for his outreach.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Governor, with the last 24 hours, you have said, in no uncertain terms that this was an act of white supremacist terror.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:04]\par \par \uc2 You said that to me on the phone when we talked a couple of times. You stood clearly on that. And you`ve calling on social media platforms to crack down on racist content. Where does that process begin?\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Well, it starts with us calling it out. I mean I should not be the only politician calling this out. I want to hear from people, Democrats, Republicans, black, white, brown, Latino, -- everybody should be saying, what it was and how this information is being fermented and people like -- it`s like a virus, spreading on social media and the dark web. And it has to be stopped after being identified. The key thing, Reverend Al, is to identify it the second it hits.\par \par \uc2 And that, in my opinion, is the responsibility of the people who own these platforms, the very profitable companies that make a fortune off of them, you can hire enough people and have enough algorithms to identify when key words appear and notify law enforcement before this happens again. So, that is something I believe should happen. I`m going to be pressing for this. And I want answers and I want them now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Governor, we`ve covered the threat of racist ideology, of course. There is also the debate around guns. While this attack was carried out with a weapon, I have heard you say it was legally obtained. The type of magazine the shooter used on this is banned in New York State. Do I have that right, Governor?\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: Yes you do. Basically, under New York, which has these toughest gun laws in America, and we`re proud of it, and I have to tell you one thing. Our laws in New York are being challenged in the Supreme Court as we speak. Literally in a matter of weeks we could hear that they`re going to allow, possibly, and I pray it doesn`t have -- allowed him carrying concealed gun so they could walk into at a grocery store with a concealed weapon and use it, or walk into a bar at night. So, that is what we`re monitoring and watching and truly hoping doesn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 But in terms of what happened with this case yesterday, in Buffalo, it was a gun, it was an AR-15, a low magazine capacity. It doesn`t have high capacity capabilities. That is illegal in New York. But what this person did and too many people are doing this, going to another state, like Pennsylvania, literally ten minutes from the perpetrator`s home, going to Pennsylvania and buy this capacity, high capacity magazine that allows him 30 shots from one magazine. You buy that, you add it to the gun, and now you have a military style assault weapon, which is absolutely illegal in our state.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Governor, while I have you, I would like to thank you again for relaying National Action Network`s pledge. As you know, I`m President of National Action Network that we would pick up funeral costs for those who lost their lives yesterday, for those families that need help. And I`ve talked to two of the families already before coming on the air. Because I don`t want them to have that extra burden, and I don`t want people running out around raising funds. I want the families to be directly beneficial, and I thank you for amplifying that.\par \par \uc2 But tell us how folks watching this can help that community to heal.\par \par \uc2 HOCHUL: And Reverend Al, that was so generous. I mean at a time when we were just reeling with pain, broken hearts, and a call from you saying tell the families not to worry. That was powerful. We have an office of victim services that is also -- I`ve asked them to deploy $2.8 million to help these families with their costs, of whatever they need from us. And we will also be there to help with any funeral costs up to $6,000. So, we`ll have you help us with the supplemental cost.\par \par \uc2 We also -- there is needs right now. Let me tell you, this grocery store had been in a food desert. It is shut down right now because it is an active crime scene. So, the people in this community, the ones who don`t have a car, they have to walk to the grocery store, I said, we have got a problem here. I said, I am calling on \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  to give free rides to people who live in these ZIP Codes if they need to go to the grocery store somewhere else.\par \par \uc2 So, those are the kind of hands on actions I`m taking. But people, continue to send your prayers, and I know it doesn`t feel strong, but I just left the church and they`re begging for prayers and interface service at Macedonia Baptist Church. So, send prayers, but also we are offering mental health support services of families -- of community like this under siege. They lose their love ones, and they feel their sense of security is evaporating. We have mental health service support on-call in the community. We are on the ground helping them as much as possible.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re looking forward to a visit from President Biden. But knowing that the country is helping us heal is really powerful. And thank you for your support, Reverend Al.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you, Governor Hochul of New York.\par \par \uc2 Police in Buffalo continue to investigate the suspect arrested in the shootings of ten people yesterday. Authorities say the shooting was motivated by racial hatred.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:02]\par \par \uc2 And we are working to confirm the authenticity of a 180-page paper that was posted online by the alleged shooter detailing the plot.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is we Talia Lavin, she is the Author of Culture War Lords, My Journey Into The Dark Web Of White Supremacy.\par \par \uc2 Talia, welcome back to the show. You came up to mind when this came about last night. And I remember you on before and your book is specifically about the intricacies of our white supremacy proliferate online. And we learn more about this Buffalo shooter as we learn more about him. How does he fit into the patterns you have observed monitoring hatred in the virtual world?\par \par \uc2 TALIA LAVIN, AUTHOR: It actually fits in quite precisely, Reverend. You know, the shooter in the manifesto, which I read, which I believe to be authentic based on the fact that it was posted before the attack and cited specific details of the attack.\par \par \uc2 You know, he describes sort of a melange of pastiche of racist influences. He described being radicalized on 4chan, which is a notorious sewer of the internet, where racist thought is inculcated.\par \par \uc2 And if you look at the manifesto, I mean, there are just pages and pages and pages of racist memes, anti-Semitic memes and he also cites several white supremacist mass shooters, including the Christ Church shooter and the San Diego shooter of a Synagogue. He cites these theories of, you know, these anti-Semitic theories and these racist theories that really just are absolutely in the DNA of the racist movement online.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, is the problem of online white supremacy hate language getting worse? Is it easy to spread misinformation and falsehoods today than, say, ten years ago?\par \par \uc2 LAVIN: I think the ubiquity of the internet and the ability to sort of connect people from around the world -- this is a transnational movement in a very real way, you know, I think that makes the problem more intense.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the substance of what they are saying, it is remarkable how consistent it has been across decades and even centuries, this idea that there is a Jewish plot against white people, that they are using black people for nefarious ends, particular paranoia about white people being replaced.\par \par \uc2 So, I would say, it`s like my observation -- my principal observation with white supremacist movement online is the methods of transmission may be noble but the essence of what is being said remains remarkably consistent and (INAUDIBLE) from the past.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, a lot of your research deals with the underground platforms most Americans have never visited. But I want to get your thoughts on the mainstreaming of hate on the political right. There are many examples, but I want to bring up Congresswoman \uc2 Elise Stefanik\uc2 , because Stefanik she represents an upstate New York district, and is a member of Republican leadership.\par \par \uc2 Now, last year the congresswoman was criticized by her own hometown newspaper, for running \uc2 Facebook\uc2  Ads, alluding to the great replacement theory. Apparently expels by the alleged Buffalo shooter. And just this week this congresswoman put out a tweet pushing the idea that the Biden administration is worsening baby formula shortage by providing food to migrant babies.\par \par \uc2 She also uses the term, pedo grifters, which seems to reference the QAnon conspiracy theory. Although our staff denies that`s what she meant. What is your take on what is going on here and how dangerous is it?\par \par \uc2 LAVIN: I mean, what we have seen, particularly over the last few years is a blurring and almost an erasure of the lines between the extremist right, the racist right, the right that will pick up a gun and shoot people in a church and a supermarket.\par \par \uc2 And the quote unquote mainstream Republican Party, what you see on Fox News, what you hear from officials like Stefanik who represent of the north country.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:08]\par \par \uc2 Many other Republicans, Senate candidates and elected officials, particularly, when it comes to so-called great replacement theory, which is the idea that, you know, if an areas plot of elites, sometimes specified as Jews, sometimes not, our sort of engineering immigration, encouraging interracial marriage in order to destroy and replace the white race.\par \par \uc2 And this is referenced directly in the shooters manifesto, but it`s also referred to, very openly, by Republican officials at all levels of leadership, and it is a building block for Senate candidates. I think this represents great danger.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Great danger.\par \par \uc2 LAVIN: I think it is very radicalizing, and it ultimately -- you know, they are only going in one direction. That direction is violence.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, Talia Lavin, we thank you for joining us again on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 After the break, more on the Pennsylvania primaries, new top contender. Plus, what Democrats need to do to try to defy expectations this primary season.\par \par \uc2 You are watching POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:50:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let`s bring in my political panel for their insight on today`s big topics. Joining me now, former Representative, \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2 , of Florida, and Adrienne Elrod, Democratic strategist and former Senior Aide to the Biden/Harris Campaign.\par \par \uc2 Carlos, just days before the Pennsylvania gubernatorial primary on Tuesday, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  endorsed front runner, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , a far-right extremist and an election conspiracy theorist. Who, if elected, would be tasked with certifying the state`s 2024 presidential election results.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, another far-right candidate is surging in the Senate primaries, Kathy Barnette, has a record of anti-Islam and gay statements. But she doesn`t have the endorsement of Trump. Who has backed Doctor Oz. Also, new reports of the states Lieutenant Governor, John Fetterman, who is the leading in the Democratic Senate primary race suffered a stroke on Friday and he is currently recovering. What is going on in Pennsylvania?\par \par \uc2 FMR. REP. CARLOS CURBELO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, Reverend, what is happening on the Republican side of the spectrum in Pennsylvania is actually pretty good news for Democrats. A lot of Pennsylvania Republicans I`ve spoken to in recent days says that Mr. Mastriano is totally unelectable in a Democratic -- the general election because he`s made so many outlandish comments because he is so far out of the mainstream. So, a lot of Democrats and Pennsylvania are actually welcoming this Trump endorsement because they believe this is a candidate who they can really beat.\par \par \uc2 In terms of Ms. Barnette and the Republican primary, here is an example of someone who is really taking advantage of the race to the bottom that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has started in the Republican Party when it comes to rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Barnette, who does not have Donald Trump`s endorsement, is speaking in ways and using rhetoric that is even more aggressive and more inappropriate, in my, view than Mr. Trump. So she is kind of beating him at his own game, and rising in the polls against a candidate, and Doctor Oz, who he endorsed.\par \par \uc2 So it really looks like, for the time being, all the turmoil on the Republican side of the aisle is going to benefit Democrats in Pennsylvania, despite this unfortunate health issue that the Democratic leading candidate has.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Adrienne, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  recently said he would be making an announcement on student loan forgiveness within weeks. He`s considering various proposals for debt cancellation, possibly with income based limits for legibility.\par \par \uc2 Some critics argue that this move doesn`t get at the root of the problem with college affordability and loan repayment. If you advise the president on this issue, what would you suggest? Cancel all student debt? Or forget a set amount.\par \par \uc2 ADRIENNE ELROD, FORMER SENIOR AIDE, BIDEN-HARRIS CAMPAIGN: Yes. I mean, Rev, I think honestly, the best thing to do here is to cancel student debt. I mean, certainly not working at the White House on advising him. But this is something that not only, as you pointed out, is crippling young voters, it is something that motivates young voters.\par \par \uc2 And it`s something that they`re going to be looking at going into 2022, and certainly going into 2024. It is -- the cost of college has gotten out of control. I mean, that age, myself here -- I graduated for college about 25 years ago. The cost has gone up so, so much. And I don`t even understand really how a lot of middle class families can afford to send their kids to college. A lot of them can`t.\par \par \uc2 And this is something that, not only by canceling this debt it will motivate a lot of young voters and turn them in a different direction. They will also get them off to the right start. It will have an overall significantly positive benefit on the entire economy.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Carlos, Republicans are causing a stir over baby formula. \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  reports Republicans are wrongly suggesting the Biden administration is sending baby formula to undocumented immigrants at the expense of American families amid a national shortage.\par \par \uc2 As part of a settlement of class action lawsuit, the government must provide food, including baby formula, and water to migrant children detained at the border. And it is unlikely that the amount of formula in stock at detention facilities would meaningfully and ease the shortage in any way.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:05]\par \par \uc2 Why are Republicans using migrant children as a scapegoat for this crisis?\par \par \uc2 CURBELO: Well, Reverend, the Republican playbook has been to insert immigration into almost any issue they can. They perceive the Biden administration as weak on this issue. Polling does indeed indicates that a lot of Americans don`t approve of President Biden`s handling of immigration. So Republicans want to tie him to that issue. And that`s why they`re talking about the shortage of baby formula milk in the context of immigration.\par \par \uc2 In reality, the reason or one of the reasons that we are suffering this crisis is because we have reduced a lot of our trade relationships with other countries. And we have one problem, with a facility in the United States, and we don`t have those trade relationships where we can import baby formula from other parts of the world.\par \par \uc2 So sure, you want to criticize the Biden administration`s immigration policies? That is fair. It seems most Americans agree. But to mix these two issues just doesn`t seem sincere.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Adrienne, inflation is a hot topic going into the midterms. And the black community is being disproportionately hit by it. Black households are now spending more of their post tax income on necessities like food and energy, according to the new bank of America report.\par \par \uc2 Also, gas prices hit another record high today. AAA says a gallon of regular is now $4.47. Breaking records for the sixth straight day. What could Democrats be doing now to help Americans that are feeling the financial squeeze? Briefly, please.\par \par \uc2 ELROD: Yes. Absolutely, Rev. I mean look, the bottom line is this, the economy is doing well. A lot of people are feeling record unemployment rates, and they`re feeling that -- good paying jobs have been created.\par \par \uc2 A lot of them enter the Biden administration but not everybody is feeling this. So I think President Biden has made it very clear, I acknowledge inflation as an issue, I certainly acknowledge that it`s an issue that affects -- not just proportionately effects people of color. But here is my plan to address it. And oh, by the way, every time I try to put more money in the pockets of American families in Congress, the Republicans obstructed a vote against that.\par \par \uc2 Rev, you`ve going to remember how gleeful Republicans where when inflation numbers started going up. This is what they want. And we have got to drive that contrast, to make it clear the Democrats are on the side of working families and Republicans are not.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: \uc2 Carlos Curbelo\uc2  and Adreinne Elrod, thank you both for being on tonight.\par \par \uc2 We want to update you on the breaking news out of Orange County, California. Police on the scene of reported shooting and a church in Laguna Woods. According to the sheriff`s office, one person has been killed and four victims have been wounded and they are on their way to the hospital. One suspect has been detained and a weapon that may have been involved has been recovered. We will bring you more information as we get it.\par \par \uc2 As we cover tragedies like the mass shooting in Buffalo, it is always important to remember that the victims are more than statistics. They are real people, with hopes and dreams that were dashed, who leaves behind friends and families, whose lives will never be the same.\par \par \uc2 Victims like Aaron Salter, a beloved retired Buffalo Police Officer, who is a security guard that confronted the assailant before being gunned down. And Pearl Young, a woman who ran a food pantry in Central Park for the past 25 years and was killed while shopping for groceries.\par \par \uc2 Catherine Massie, a civil rights leader and journalist, who use her voice to speak on issues of the black community including calling for more federal regulation on firearms. And Ruth White Whitfield, who stopped at the supermarket on a way home from visiting her husband at a nursing home.\par \par \uc2 I`m sure we will learn more about the other wonderful lives lost in the days to come, we should remember their names. And rise up so they did not die in vain.\par \par \uc2 I talked to the families today as my -- in my capacity as president of the National Action Network as we are making available whatever resources and finances as we can to help them with funeral expenses, because I`ve worked down for the last few years with victims that once the cameras are gone, and once we move on, they are still left with that hole that will never be filled. And we need people that are be there and organizations that will be there when the headlines are gone.\par \par \uc2 These are people`s mothers.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:01]\par \par \uc2 These are people, wives, husbands, these are real people. And they should be treated that way. And we need to make sure that they did not.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220516ei5f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION FOR May 14, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui, Cal Perry, Jim Cavanaugh, Rick Tyler,  Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 8,348 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, MSNBC HOST: ... next week on "Symone," I will actually be sitting down with Education Secretary, \uc2 Miguel Cardona\uc2  for an exclusive interview to talk about the Biden administration`s plans for addressing student loan debt and a host of other things. I can`t wait to bring that to you on Peacock and on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Make sure to check out my Peacock show this Tuesday, and my show right here on MSNBC next Saturday at 4:00 PM Eastern to see it all.\par \par \uc2 Well, that`s it for this edition of "Symone." I`m Symone Sanders. I will see you back here tomorrow at 4:00 PM Eastern, and on Peacock where I will have new episodes on the MSNBC hub every Monday and Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 But right now, I want to hand things over to my favorite friend, Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  -- Rev.\par \par \uc2 [17:00:42]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Thank you, Symone.\par \par \uc2 We have a great show today on POLITICSNATION with lots of great topics, but first we want to start with the breaking news out of Buffalo, New York.\par \par \uc2 Law enforcement sources say at least seven people have been killed in a mass shooting at supermarket, Tops. Police say the shooter in the incident is in custody, and two rifles have been recovered from the scene including one with a racist-term etched onto the weapon.\par \par \uc2 Police are investigating whether the suspect was motivated by White supremacist ideology and may have livestreamed the incident.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go now to my MSNBC colleague, Richard Lui with the latest information.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Rev, we are getting a lot of different indications right now. This is common at this stage. We are about two and a half hours into this incident in Buffalo, New York as you described, where we have a Tops supermarket where this incident happened, and from what you`ve described and what we`re learning. Yes, this suspect from what we are hearing right now, walking in wearing a military style gear. We don`t know whether that is pants, the whether that is a vest, whether it happens to be what he was wearing on his head. That detail not into us.\par \par \uc2 But wearing military style gear going into this Tops friendly market right there if you`re familiar with this Buffalo area, Jefferson Avenue. We are showing you some of the pictures from earlier on the scene of what happened at 2:30, began firing a rifle according to what the officials are saying on the scene at this very popular supermarket, very calm Saturday afternoon, probably a lot of families there shopping.\par \par \uc2 Now there are varying reports right now, Rev, in terms of how many people were killed, and the number injured. What we know is at least seven at the moment, this is just an indication that we`re still gathering more details just as law enforcement on the ground is doing the very, very same as they have their investigators there on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Let`s go to \uc2 Jim Cavanaugh\uc2  right now, MSNBC law enforcement analyst.\par \par \uc2 Jim, you know, we`ve got a lot of different pieces of information coming in at the moment. What do you know and what are you hearing from your sources?\par \par \uc2 Jim Cavanaugh\uc2 , can you hear me?\par \par \uc2 Our \uc2 Jim Cavanaugh\uc2  is one of our analysts here, retired A.T.F. Special Agent in Charge, former hostage negotiator for the A.T.F. Jim Cavanaugh, can you hear me, Jim?\par \par \uc2 JIM CAVANAUGH\uc2 , MSNBC LAW ENFORCEMENT ANALYST: Yes, Richard, you`ve just got me up here.\par \par \uc2 LUI: Perfect, perfect. We can now hear you, Jim. You know, a lot of -- you know, we`ve been in these situations before, Jim, and we`re getting very pieces of information, one that the suspect was killed. Now, we are hearing that he is in custody.\par \par \uc2 What do you know, and what are you hearing? Because I know that you also have your own sources there both on local level, as well as on the Federal level.\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: You know, from all preliminary indications, and everything has not been verified yet by NBC News, but, you know, the preliminary indications coming out is this is a vicious hate crime.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this guy is reported to have White supremacist markings on his rifle. You know, that can be a lot of things. They use a lot of various symbols, for example, 88, it means "Heil Hitler," because he is the eighth letter of the alphabet, but there`s a lot of other symbols in White supremacy, but something is on the rifle that is indicating that and apparently, he has posted some screeds. You know, White supremacist screeds, anti-Semitic talk online.\par \par \uc2 LUI: Right.\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: You know, he shows up in full fatigues. He`s in a predominantly Black area. We always look at targeting, Richard, for motive. You know, why does the killer want to kill these particular people at this particular place at this time? And you know what we`re going to look here, I`m sorry to say, this is going to look like and it probably will turn out to be a very vulgar hate crime just like the Mother Emanuel Church.\par \par \uc2 I mean, a White supremacist poisoned by the Internet, poisoned in his mind with hatred, you know gets access to guns, just boils over with hatred and goes out and kills eight American citizens for nothing, but hey, that`s the way it really looks like this one`s going to shake out. It`s just a horror story, a horror story. But we need to get rid -- find every little aspect of what is behind it.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:20]\par \par \uc2 LUI: Yes, Jim, I remember when we were covering that story. We do hope that that is not the situation, and yet NBC News not confirming all of these possibilities. We are we are hearing these reports that you`re sharing with us, Jim. And yes, this particular supermarket location, it is about three miles north of downtown Buffalo. It is a predominantly Black neighborhood.\par \par \uc2 It is a family neighborhood. It is a residential area. And these indications that we`re getting very, very early is what exactly law enforcement is doing on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Jim, if you can stand by, Rev, of course is going to continue. You have a quick one? You`ve got a quick one or two sentences here?\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: I`m sorry, say again?\par \par \uc2 LUI: Jim, do you have a last one or two sentences you wanted -- it sounded like you`re trying to get in?\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: Oh, no, I think we`ve got to find out what the detectives gleaned from this and whether or not there are going to be any Federal charges. It`s just a really horrible scene, a lot of heartache in Buffalo. So ...\par \par \uc2 LUI: Yes, again --\par \par \uc2 CAVANAUGH: We have to get some more --\par \par \uc2 LUI: Always appreciate it, \uc2 Jim Cavanaugh\uc2 . Thank you so much, MSNBC analyst, I appreciate your perspective, as well as the information. Again, at least seven dead, Rev, from what we understand right now that NBC is able to confirm. Of course, there are many reports right now, Rev, in terms of what is happening.\par \par \uc2 We will of course, keep you updated right here on MSNBC a little bit later in the hour, Rev. But for now, back to you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, we will certainly be waiting for those updates. I know that area very well. We have a chapter of National Action Network there, and I`ve been on the phone trying to find out what I can find out, but thank you, Richard. More updates soon. Don`t go anywhere.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congresswoman \uc2 Carolyn Maloney\uc2 , Chair of the House Oversight and Reform Committee. Congresswoman, first, welcome back to the show.\par \par \uc2 Before we begin, I, of course have to ask you your response to the shooting earlier today in Buffalo. We are still learning more details, what law enforcement says they are investigating whether the shooter may have been motivated by White supremacist ideology. What are your thoughts on this tragedy?\par \par \uc2 I think that we are fixing her mic. We can`t hear the Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 Let me get that adjusted. But as I said, preliminary reports raising serious concerns this may be a hate crime. All of us are checking our sources I know the mayor and many people there on the ground. But regardless of the motive, this amount of people dead is something that we all must deal with the terrific impact that this would have on us, as a nation, and certainly the community.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s go to Chicago. Let`s welcome the mayor of Chicago, Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Thank you for joining us Mayor Lightfoot. Let me go to what you promised that Chicago will be, quote, "an oasis." I want to deal with that. In Washington, there`s been marches all over the country around the right for women to have the right of choice and you promised that Chicago would be an oasis for those seeking abortions, should roe ultimately be overturned, and to that end, your administration is pledging $500,000.00 to provide for safe abortions for those seeking it in your city, including those coming in from out of state for procedures toward that end.\par \par \uc2 Can you explain why you want your city to take such a bold stand on this issue?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR LORI LIGHTFOOT (D), CHICAGO, ILLINOIS: Well, first of all, thank you, Rev, for having me on, and obviously very concerned about what`s going on in Buffalo, but we wanted to make sure that our city was a safe haven for all seeking reproductive healthcare services.\par \par \uc2 I`m deeply concerned based upon conversations with providers in my city, because as you know, many of the states surrounding us -- Wisconsin, Missouri, Iowa, likely Indiana and possibly Michigan, once Roe is struck down by the Court at the end of this term, all of those states have trigger laws, which means that abortion will be banned in those states.\par \par \uc2 Our providers are already seeing a significant uptick in services by women coming from Texas, so I can only imagine what the scale of increased demand for services in Chicago is going to be.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:06]\par \par \uc2 LIGHTFOOT: So we issued a Justice for All Pledge, and it goes far beyond reproductive rights, because if you look at what was written in that draft opinion, and I will add, a mean-spirited draft opinion from Justice Alito, what you see is his attack on the right to privacy and many rights that have been recognized by the Court over these last 50 years arise from the right of privacy.\par \par \uc2 Whether it`s same sex marriage, interracial marriage, banning sodomy laws, the list is long. So my concern is that we have to affirm as a City of Chicago that we are going to protect the rights of all of our residents against the hate that we`re seeing permeating and being embodied in laws in too many states across our country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, it sounds like your administration with this new initiative is trying to head off the rise of unregulated, potentially unsafe abortions. Are you worried about a resurgence of abortions underground, so to speak?\par \par \uc2 LIGHTFOOT: I`m very concerned about that. Just the panic that was set off with that draft opinion, what I heard from a lot of our providers is women are calling up in distress and panic thinking that Roe had already been struck down that they weren`t going to have access to reproductive rights - - reproductive healthcare.\par \par \uc2 So with the $500,000.00, it is a downpayment to help these providers meet what we anticipate is a surge in new cases. So, the money is for obviously providing the services themselves, but also for travel, for lodging, for time for recovery.\par \par \uc2 The need is going to be great, and I encourage any person of goodwill who understands that reproductive rights is a basic human right, to support your reproductive rights providers across the country, and certainly in my City of Chicago.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Mayor, I was pleased to read about a pilot program from your administration to directly help thousands of low income families suffering financially from the pandemic, you billed it as one of the largest cash assistance programs in the nation. What can you tell us about it?\par \par \uc2 LIGHTFOOT: Well, Rev, I came from one of those families that struggled every single week, every single month, trying to juggle the demands of utility bills, car payments, house payments, and so what I know in this time in my city, is that a lot of people are suffering economically.\par \par \uc2 So we put aside $31 million. We aim to support 5,000 families for one year with $500.00 a month to help bridge the financial divide up that they are experiencing at this point, but we also want to make sure that we really get to understand what the pain points are for these families. So that after one year, they`re going to have a lot of training on financial literacy, saving, how to build a solid financial base for the future because I want them to be in better shape after the one year than they were when they got into this.\par \par \uc2 And I`ve got to tell you, the demand is unbelievable. Our very first day of opening up the applications, and keep in mind, it`s only 5,000 families that we are going to be able to serve, we had over 90,000 people apply. So, the need is profound.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now finally, Madam Mayor, going back to your tweet from earlier this week, where you wrote, quote: "To my friends in the LGBTQ community, the Supreme Court is coming for us next. This moment has to be a call to arms." End quote.\par \par \uc2 Now several prominent right-wingers have come after you saying you intended to incite violence, with Senator \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  writing to the Justice Department suggesting your comments were part of a quote, "Coordinated campaign by Democrats to intimidate Supreme Court Justices." You`ve spoken to the irony of those charges coming from right-wingers who supported in many ways, the insurrection, I hope you might do it again for this audience.\par \par \uc2 LIGHTFOOT: Well, listen, the fact that you`ve got a \uc2 Marco Rubio\uc2  or Lauren Boebert and some of these other unhinged people who fomented the January 6th insurrection, the worst attack on our democracy since the Civil War, the fact that they have the audacity to challenge me when I am using terminology to make sure that our communities are wide awake and ready to fight for our rights, I`m not going to apologize for fighting for our rights, which are our responsibility under democracy.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:11]\par \par \uc2 LIGHTFOOT: But we`re going to do it the right way, the correct way, not like these folks have done, which is fomenting and supporting insurrection, lying about election fraud, and doing everything that they can to undermine the core tenants of our democracy.\par \par \uc2 I stand for the facts and for the truth, and for our rights.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Mayor \uc2 Lori Lightfoot\uc2 , many thanks for being on.\par \par \uc2 Now, back to Congresswoman Maloney. Congresswoman Maloney, we think we have your mic straight. Give us your response to the shooting earlier today in Buffalo.\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 CAROLYN MALONEY\uc2  (D-NY): Well, first Rev, I want to thank you for being on top of the shooting, on top of the hate crimes, and on top of sounding the alarm on the Republican attempts to do away with the right to reproductive rights in America.\par \par \uc2 I think that the crime is out of control in this country. We stand alone with these mass shootings. We need to pass gun safety laws and we need to do it now. And then with these hate crimes.\par \par \uc2 We do have punishment that is more than regular punishment, I think we should double whatever the punishment is, because the hate crimes are growing in this country and we need to hold people accountable for their actions.\par \par \uc2 This is outrageous that eight innocent people were murdered today, according to reports in Buffalo, and this is one of many. We`ve had roughly 40,000 people killed by guns in our country. One weekend recently in our City of New York, 27 people were hit, but they didn`t all die, but they were hit with gun wounds. So really, it is a crisis and one that needs to be addressed.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, and I know you and I have stood against hate crimes together, we did something together in your district, around the anti- Semitic killings in synagogues and working with \uc2 ADL\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: We need to do it again. We need to do it again on this one.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And we need to do it across the board, everyone needs to stand with each other against these hate crimes, and we`ll be following this tragic story out of Buffalo and bring our viewers the latest information as it becomes available.\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: We need to.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But I want to talk to you about some other stories.\par \par \uc2 You have mass actions across the country today, including here in Washington, supporters of abortion rights mobilized in several major cities after the Senate vote to codify Roe vs. Wade failed this week.\par \par \uc2 Every Republican senator along with Democrat \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , voting against the Women`s Health Protection Act. I guess, I wonder if Congress, despite its Democratic majority, is truly up to the task of defending women`s rights at the Federal level? Because I can assure you, it is a question I`ve heard from Black women, certainly, and with about half the states pretty much the entire South triggered to roll back women`s rights should Roe ultimately be dismantled.\par \par \uc2 Why should women believe that a viable Federal response is forthcoming?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: Because it comes down to votes, Reverend. We had the votes in the House because we had a Democratic majority to pass the Women`s Health Protection Act, and today I spent the day marching in the parade in New York, but in this last week, on Thursday, we marched over to the Senate in support of passing the Women`s Health Protection Act and we could not pass it because we did not have the votes.\par \par \uc2 And then later with Pelosi, we marched down the steps and announced that we will be working day and night to elect more pro-choice Members of Congress. We need a Democratic House, a Democratic Senate and a Democratic President that supports a woman`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 I tell you, they just, in their draft opinion that leaked, they overturned 49 years of precedent, which is a cornerstone of the Supreme Court. They have systematically been packing this Court with rightwing ideologues and we are seeing those votes come down now, against a woman`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 I would say, Reverend, that there is no democracy in this country if a woman cannot make decisions about the health of her own body, including her reproduction, and her reproduction rights.\par \par \uc2 This is outrageous. It`s an outrageous decision. We will continue marching, but it comes down to votes. We have to elect a majority of pro-choice representatives in this country in the midterms. Everything is on the line.\par \par \uc2 We have to really come down to votes and then they`re just starting now. Now, where are they going to go next? Let`s show and be clear that these abortion bans have the most harmful effects on people of color, the LGBTQ community, I`d say young people and people who are low income, and then where are they going to go next? They`re going to go after reproductive rights? They`re going to go after right to contraception. They`ll go after gay marriage. Who knows where they`re going to go in rolling back fundamental rights in this country?\par \par \uc2 [17:20:29]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, we`re out of time, but Congresswoman, briefly, Democrats would like to catch this abortion fight in electoral terms, arguing that the right to protect women`s rights, voters have to keep if not grow them majorities in the House and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 But a new Monmouth poll -- \uc2 Monmouth University\uc2  poll finds the economy and abortion are essentially tied 26 percent to 25 percent as the most important issue in the 2022 midterm elections, hearing that, which issue do you think will carry the most weight with voters six months from now, when they head to the polls?\par \par \uc2 MALONEY: I think they`re both important, and we have to really -- I went to Congress, I announced the day of the Casey decision in 1992, when they rolled back restrictions on -- and put more restrictions on abortion.\par \par \uc2 So I`ve always been a strong fighter for women and minorities, but we have to look at the economy, too. But Democrats have a strong record. We passed the American Rescue Plan that brought over $100 billion to New York City, the first infrastructure plan that brought $400 million in my district for moving up to 125th Street with the Second Avenue subway very, very important.\par \par \uc2 And we were pushing for the Build Back Better plan, we will continue working for that. We`ve created -- President Biden -- over eight million jobs. The GDP has been strong. Many economic indicators have been strong. We`ve got to work on this inflation, and we`ve got other things we`ve got to work on, but Democrats have a strong record of bringing everybody up and helping everybody with the economy.\par \par \uc2 But we need to fight on both areas, they`re both important. Certainly, abortion rights and certainly a strong economy, creating jobs, and opportunities for our people are extremely important, too.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, we`ve got to leave it there. Thank you, Congresswoman Maloney.\par \par \uc2 We now go to Ukraine for a quick update within NBC`s Cal Perry.\par \par \uc2 Cal, you`re in Kyiv where Ukrainian President Zelenskyy welcomed the delegation of Republican senators, led by \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  today. What is the latest on their visit as Ukraine continues to push back on racist forces?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, so this was a well-timed visit, Rev. We have of course, this $40 billion aid package sitting in Washington, D.C. \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  has held up that aid package. It is a bit of bureaucracy for Washington. Certainly, that`s the way that it was presented to President Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 You had this visit by the four senators, these are the minority leaders in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . And again, what is important about this visit is as you sort of laid out, fierce fighting in the eastern part of the country, and a need at least on behalf of the Ukrainian military for more military aid.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy asking that foreign leaders come with tangible military equipment aid and that`s what the topic of discussion was. As you said, the fighting in the East continues to be fierce. At least three civilians killed there today that sort of serves as a litany of days in a row where we`ve had civilians killed.\par \par \uc2 And then in the city of Mariupol, we continue to see the fighting in and around this steel plant where some hundreds of fighters remain including at least we understand, about 50 wounded fighters. There was some discussion today within \uc2 NATO\uc2  and \uc2 NATO\uc2  member, Turkey, about possibly evacuating that if there was some kind of ceasefire, but no word from the Russians as to whether or not a ceasefire is possible -- Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Okay, NBC`s Cal Perry in Ukraine, our many thanks.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, abortion right supporters are out in force across the country this weekend, as the Supreme Court appears poised to overturn Roe versus Wade and efforts in the Senate to save it this week proved unsuccessful.\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans are trying to silence protesters` voice. I`ll tell you about it in today`s "Got-cha."\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague Richard Lui has the latest on the deadly mass shooting in Buffalo, New York -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 LUI: Rev, yes, I want to get you updated. Now, we`re three hours in and according to three senior law enforcement sources that spoke to NBC News` Jonathan Dean, as well as others here at NBC News, at least seven people dead at the moment, confirmed. It could be more. There are varying reports that show eight, nine, and 10, but at the moment what NBC News can say is seven as well as those injured.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:09]\par \par \uc2 LUI: That number undetermined. This is happening at a supermarket in Buffalo, New York, a supermarket chain called Tops. Police saying the shooter in the incident, this is also new in the last hour, you and I were just talking about, is in custody, is alive and is not dead. He had two rifles that has been recovered from the scene, and one of those firearms had according to these three officials, a racist term, etched onto the weapon.\par \par \uc2 We will have more details on what that is a little bit later for you as we look into police now investigating whether that suspect was motivated by White supremacist ideology and may have livestreamed that incident.\par \par \uc2 So, they`re looking into that right now. Police trying to get the details. We are awaiting a law enforcement news conference and are continuing to monitor the situation right here at MSNBC and when we get more details for you, Rev, we`ll have it right here.\par \par \uc2 For now, we`re going to take a short break and back to more POLITICSNATION right after this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:30:17]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For this week`s "Got-cha," I`m calling out Republican politicians for their stunning hypocrisy on the issue of political protests.\par \par \uc2 Since the leak of the Supreme Court draft opinion, potentially overturning Roe versus Wade, many Americans have been understandably upset, and a few have voiced their displeasure outside the homes of the political figures that are responsible in their judgment and putting us in this tenuous state of abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 We`ve seen these protests with our own eyes, so we know what they are like. The demonstrators have been singing and chanting outside the houses of Justices Kavanaugh and Alito. The scenes have been noisy, but peaceful.\par \par \uc2 In Maine, people wrote messages in chalk on the sidewalk outside of Senator \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 ` home. She called the cops. For the most part, these events have been no more raucous than an elementary school recess.\par \par \uc2 But that`s not what you`ve been hearing from some Republicans like Senator Tom Cotton of Arkansas, who says quote: "Anybody protesting a Judge`s home should be arrested on the spot by Federal law enforcement."\par \par \uc2 Many of these Republicans are the same folks, who for over a year have been defending the action of those who stormed the Capitol on January 6th. Just this week, Federal prosecutors recommended an Idaho woman spend 60 days in jail for her role in the Capitol attack, which included shattering a glass door. In another case, a right-wing internet personality going by the name, "Baked Alaska," who livestreamed himself storming the Capitol suddenly withdrew his guilty plea to Federal charges, and now declares himself innocent.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, five Republican lawmakers subpoenaed by the January 6th Select Committee to share what they know about the insurrection have refused to cooperate.\par \par \uc2 In my capacity as President of National Action Network, and as a Civil Rights activist, I`ve demonstrated everywhere you can imagine from Bensonhurst, New York, where I got stabbed, to Puerto Rico where I spent 90 days in jail, to the D.C. Mall right here many times, to Sanford, Florida for Trayvon Martin, to Minneapolis for \uc2 George Floyd\uc2  and the list goes on and on.\par \par \uc2 Protesting is a powerful tool in democracy where every citizen can participate. I want to be clear that I support peaceful demonstrations. If some Republican lawmakers want to take a stand against political violence, they can begin by reckoning with what happened on January 6th. There is no comparison to what we`re seeing today.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:37:24]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 We`re following breaking news out of Buffalo where at least seven people have been killed in a mass shooting at a supermarket. Police are investigating whether the suspect was motivated by White supremacist ideology, and soon, police in Buffalo will be holding a news conference with updates. We will take you there live once it starts.\par \par \uc2 For now, let`s bring my political panel to tackle today`s big stories.\par \par \uc2 Republican strategist Rick Tyler, and "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " columnist Michelle Goldberg, both are MSNBC political analysts.\par \par \uc2 Michelle, let me start with you. We`re getting more details about the suspected gunman involved in an incident earlier today in Buffalo. Police are looking into whether the suspect was motivated by White supremacist ideology.\par \par \uc2 We know at least seven people are dead. The accused shooter is now in custody. What is your reaction?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, I`ve been reading the supposed Manifesto by the alleged shooter and it makes it very clear that he is a Nazi, a White supremacist that he was deliberately targeting Black people, that he wants to terrorize Black and Jewish people and immigrants, and I just think it`s worth reiterating that, you know, people in law enforcement and Intelligence have been saying for years now that the biggest domestic threat to the United States is White supremacist terrorism.\par \par \uc2 And we see over and over again that that is the case and we are almost becoming inured to it. You know, I think that a few years ago, this massacre would have been even more, you know, sort of even more world stopping than it is, but we`ve become used to a certain amount of sort of regular mass shootings in this country.\par \par \uc2 It shouldn`t change the fact that you know, if this is what it looks like, an act of White supremacist terrorism, we need, you know, sort of much more law enforcement focus on this kind of radicalization.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I agree with you and I would hope that it is a time that we see more action and more focus. And I also would hope that as we see if it in fact comes to be the case of a Nazi kind of Manifesto that you have referred to that Black and Jewish leaders and others stand together against all hate crimes when it is directed at your group or not, we`ve got to stop this.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:07]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Rick, I want to get your thoughts on this tragedy. We`ve seen a continuous escalation of racist fear-mongering from some parts of the right, including the Republican Party, a relentless focus on so-called Critical Race Theory.\par \par \uc2 If this does turn out to be a hate crime, shouldn`t that prompt some self- reflection on the part of some of those Republican lawmakers that act as though race or not be dealt with in this country?\par \par \uc2 RICK TYLER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, certainly, Al, I think we need a lot of self-reflection and there was a shooting yesterday at the Bucks- Celtics game where 17 people were shot. In fact, 20 people were shot over the course of three hours in two separate shootings, and that just seems to be you know, Tuesday or Wednesday. Well, it was Friday, just another Friday.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got to get a hold of what`s causing these shootings. Certainly, when you add racism, another horrible element of hatred, but people who -- we need to get guns out of the hands of crazy people. I own a firearm, I hope that no one is going to try to take away my firearm, a law-abiding citizen who carries a firearm, and that always seems to be what the solution ends up being, is we take guns away from law-abiding people, and then not take them out of the hands which they need to be taken out of their hands off, people who shouldn`t never own a firearm.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I certainly agree with that, but I also think that we need to deal with hate crimes, and I think that Black and Jewish and other leaders who have been targeted, our communities, should not be afraid to stand up to those forces.\par \par \uc2 I know when Jonathan Greenblatt out of \uc2 ADL\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Do you mind if I --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And the Rabbi President stands with people in our community, they get criticized, this is why we need to stand together. Michelle, you were getting ready to say something.\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: I just wanted to add that I think someone whose needs to be called out for this is \uc2 Rupert Murdoch\uc2  and FOX News, because, again, if you read this Manifesto, this Manifesto is, again, what we think it is, it talks again and again about race replacement and replacement theory, and what we`ve seen in the last few years is that this thing that used to be just confined to the White supremacist fringes has been mainstreamed by \uc2 Tucker Carlson\uc2  and then mainstreamed by some other various figures in the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 And I think that what we need is, you know, a sort of effort to, you know, you`re never going to get rid of hatred and vitriol and White supremacist ideology on the internet, but you can try to quarantine it and draw a line and denounce it, and that is something that, you know, kind of leaders of both FOX News and the Republican Party have really consistently failed to do.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, the mainstreaming of it is what is the most disturbing when you talk about the replacement, let`s not forget in Charlottesville, where people openly marched and chanted "Jews will not replace me, Jews will not replace us." And the sitting President of the United States said there were good people of fine people on both sides of this.\par \par \uc2 So we really need to deal with the mainstreaming of this kind of blatant racism if this ends up being what it is beginning to look like it is.\par \par \uc2 Rick, let me go to another subject as we continue to look into the shooting. The January 6 House Committee is waiting to see if they`ll get any testimony after issuing subpoenas to five sitting Republican lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 After this unprecedented move, the panel is considering its options for next steps if their G.O.P. colleagues do not comply. What does this latest development suggest about the direction of the Committee`s investigation?\par \par \uc2 TYLER: Well, I think it speaks to a much larger issue, which is very disturbing, which is the undermining of our institutions and their credibility. We now see the undermining of the Supreme Court, and now we`re seeing that -- we`ve seen the undermining of a fair and legitimate election, and now we`re seeing the undermining of the committee process.\par \par \uc2 But the problem is, it`s on both sides. You cannot have seven Democrats investigating essentially the other party, and so there`s a lot of mistrust. Now, this should have been handled differently from the beginning and I`ll admit the Republicans didn`t go along with that, which was to have an independent counsel or independent committee.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing and these investigative committees have got to get away from politicians asking people questions, just like the Supreme Court nominees get asked by Senators ridiculous questions that have nothing to do with their qualifications. It is all grandstanding. It`s all made for the TV, we`re going to see that when we have public hearings very soon.\par \par \uc2 And I think people, Republicans and Democrats and I don`t want to see the Republicans do the same to the Democrats, which they are now threatening to do if and when they become in the majority where they`re calling investigations even including calling what would then be the former Speaker of the House, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  to testify.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that`s a healthy way to run a democracy.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:15]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Michelle, in your newest article for "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 ," you write about how the death of Roe is going to tear America apart. You say in part, the demise of Roe will exacerbate America`s antagonisms creating more furious legal rifts between states than we`ve seen in modern times.\par \par \uc2 What can Democrats do? Or are you concerned it is already too late?\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: So I`m not sure what Democrats can do given the existence of \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , the existence of the filibuster and you know, the fact that we don`t have 50 senators to change it, right? So the chances of codifying Roe are very slim.\par \par \uc2 I do think it`s worth trying to pursue, you know, \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski, I believe, has said that they are open to a more bare bones version of codifying Roe. You know, I don`t really trust their good faith, but you know, but I think it is worth trying to do it. I also think that Democrats are going to have to do what Republicans did for decades and kind of begin the long march through the institutions.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Republicans focused for a long, long time on not just winning Court seats, but also winning State Legislatures, where they`ve been able to now -- where they`re poised to pass really draconian abortion bans the second Roe is gone, in some cases, they already have.\par \par \uc2 You know, but we`re in a really bad place, and I think our options for short-term relief are limited, which is why people are just thinking about ways to, you know, get women to