the end of the hour. And Patty LuPone deserves the Broadway and she`s going to get it.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: I love that.\par \par \uc2 I was on the subway they, thinking I could use Patty LuPone. No one`s wearing mask any more. But, yeah, I`m looking forward to that. You have a great show, my friend. I`ll watch it to the end.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Ali. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: All right.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Once again, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Trump Republicans have brought us to a place tonight where we have never been before. In the Trump era of our politics, which is still ongoing, when I`m about to say might be very hard to believe so I will say it slowly. In the 233 years that the House of Representatives has been in business, the number of House subpoenas issued in a year has usually been zero. That`s right. In most of the 233 year history, the House of Representatives issued zero subpoenas in those years.\par \par \uc2 The House Ethics Committee is the only committee that actually has ever subpoenaed a member of the House of Representatives and the House Ethics Committee did not even exist until 1967. And in most of the 55 years of the House Ethics Committee`s existence, the committee has not issued a single subpoena to a member of the House. That`s a typical year, in the House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 I`m trying, and I know I`m failing to convey here just how shocking, how monumentally historic it is that on this day in history, by those the House of Representatives including the Republican leader of the House of Representatives were subpoenaed by a House committee. That is not the ethics committee. The committee investigating the January 6th attack on the Capitol sent identical letters accompanying subpoenas to Kevin McCarthy, Jim Jordan, Mo Brooks, Andy Biggs, and Scott Penny.\par \par \uc2 Each letter to the members receiving the subpoena said: We have a solemn responsibility to investigate fully the facts and circumstances of the violent attack on the United States Capitol and issues relating to the peaceful transfer of power. A solemn responsibility.\par \par \uc2 I note in the age of Trump, with subpoenas flying around Washington constantly from the Mueller investigation beginning in the first year of the Trump presidency to the generous committee now. It is impossible for subpoenas to have shock value. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his children have been subpoenaed by the New York state attorney general, Donald Trump`s business records were subpoenaed by the New York attorney general and by the Manhattan district attorney who is now prosecuting Donald Trump`s business and his chief financial officer, as a result of what they found in the subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 Robert Mueller`s long forgotten subpoenas created an evidence trail that sent Donald Trump`s campaign manager to prison. Federal subpoenas in New York City sent on Trump`s fixer, \uc2 Michael Cohen\uc2 , to prison and exposed on Trump as and a coconspirator in that case. Along the way, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been impeached twice.\par \par \uc2 The January 6 committee`s subpoena power has unearthed electronic communications between the wife of Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and Trump White House chief of staff in which both of them were engaged in a criminal plot to overturn the presidential election with Clarence Thomas`s wife telling the White House chief of staff there are no rules in war.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:02]\par \par \uc2 And so, our shock reflexes are worn out and were worn out a long time ago. In Washington, just the word subpoena used to be terrifying.\par \par \uc2 I saw exactly one senator get hit with a subpoena in the years that I worked in the senate. In 1993, the Senate Ethics Committee subpoenaed the diary of the top Republican on the Senate Finance Committee, Bob Packwood of Oregon who`s been investigated by the ethics committee on multiple sexual harassment charges over a span of 20 years. Because I was working with the chairman of the Senate Finance Committee, Daniel Patrick Moynihan, we had endless dealings with Senator Packwood who was then considered a moderate to on some issues liberal Republican. He politely opposed us on tax policy but work cooperatively with us on many other issues. And there are two points to the Packwood story that are very important. In telling us how far Republicans have traveled since then and how they have lost their way on ethics and policy.\par \par \uc2 Bob Packwood was one of several pro-choice Republicans fully supportive of Roe versus Wade. In fact, Senator Packwood was even stronger supporter of abortion rights than many Democrats. Senator Packwood was endorsed by the National Abortion Rights Action League and one Planned Parenthood`s Margaret Sanger Award in 1979 when supporting abortion rights still took real political bravery. And no one said that Bob Packwood wasn`t a loyal Republican because he was pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 The second part of the Packwood story that is important tonight is that the lawsuit that he filed to block the ethics committee subpoena of his diary went all the way to the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  where a Republican appointed chief justice, William Rehnquist, the most conservative member of the court, refused to issue a preliminary order blocking the subpoena for the Packwood diaries which were then handed over to the ethics committee.\par \par \uc2 So, William Rehnquist then the most conservative Supreme Court justice of the modern era, appointed by \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , did not pause for a second in deciding to destroy the Senate career of Bob Packwood, Republican, who became the chairman of the Senate Finance Committee in 1995 as the ethics committee investigation of his diaries and other evidence dragged on. And when the investigation was complete, the Senate Ethics Committee voted unanimously to recommend to the Senate that they vote to expel Bob Packwood, the ultimate penalty that the ethics committee can recommend.\par \par \uc2 The ethics committee was equally divided between three Democrats and three Republican members, the Republican members of the committee were always in a position to block the investigation, to refuse to authorize subpoenas and they never did. They all felt a solemn responsibility.\par \par \uc2 The Republican members the Senate Ethics Committee approached the newest occasion with the same way the Democrats did. And in the middle of the multi-year investigation, Republicans won back the senate. So the chairmanship of the Senate of his committee shifted from a Democrat to Republican and the investigation continued.\par \par \uc2 And the Republican chairman of the ethics committee, who led the ethics committee to vote unanimously, to expel the Republican chairman of the finance committee, was Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 . And the Republican chairman of the Finance Committee, Bob Packwood, still respected as a legislator but now disgraced in as personal behavior to the right thing in the end, basing the inevitable and resigned from the Senate after \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  recommended his expulsion.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  knew that expelling Bob Packwood would lose a Republican seat in the senate. Democratic Senator \uc2 Ron Wyden\uc2  won the special election to fill that seat. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  wanted -- was expelling a Republican senator knowing that they would lose the seat to the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 At the beginning of the Packwood investigation two years earlier, there was not a single senator in either party who actually expected Bob Packwood to suffer any serious penalty from the ethics committee. But \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  did with the January six committee is doing now and simply follow the evidence without trying to hide any of the evidence and protect his fellow Republican.\par \par \uc2 Most senators, Democrat, Republican, did not want to vote to expel Paul Packwood.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:02]\par \par \uc2 But all of them knew how, that they would have to vote to expel Bob Packwood after \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  presented the findings of the ethics committee investigation against a fellow Republican on the Senate floor 27 years ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): A lot of people in the media and in the public think that we can`t handle the job of disciplining our fellow members and guarding the integrity of this institution. I don`t believe that. I never have. And if I did believe that to be true, I never would`ve agreed to serve on this committee.\par \par \uc2 The question before us today is really, whether we are up to the job. That`s the question before us today. Are we up to the job? Can we, through the instrument of the ethics committee, impartially and thoroughly investigate instances of misconduct by our colleagues? And will we give the committee the authority it needs to get the job done right?\par \par \uc2 If you believe the answer is yes, you will vote to uphold the unanimous decision of the ethics committee.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: That`s the way it used to be. Not anymore. No one was surprised by \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and the other Republican members of the Senate ethics committee followed the evidence and made a recommendation to expel a Republican senator based on the evidence. That`s the way everyone expected the ethics committee to work then. That`s the way of unexpected House and Senate investigations to work, follow the evidence and never lose sight of your solemn responsibility.\par \par \uc2 Today, Kevin McCarthy ridicule the January 6 committee after receiving a subpoena from them saying, it`s an illegitimate committee which is a lie. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  avoids commenting on activities in the House of Representatives but he will surely get around to ridiculing the January 6 Committee`s subpoenas. Exactly the kind of subpoenas \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  would have issued as the senator in the 1990s before he stopped believing that his oath of office conferred on him at the title of senator and a solemn responsibility.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Congressman Adam Schiff of California. He is chairman of the House Intelligence Committee. A member of the January 6th Select Committee and was a lead impeachment manager for the first impeachment trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chairman Schiff, thank you very much for joining us tonight and thank you for patience with that history lesson. It just strikes me on a day like today which is a historic day, as you know, it is a historic day, subpoenaing members of the house like this. To see the way it used to be and to see the way enforcement of ethics, enforcement of what -- kinds of things are trying to enforce. We`re a completely bipartisan effort in the house in the senate.\par \par \uc2 REP. ADAM SCHIFF (D-CA): I`m so glad that you did and I had never seen that footage before. It really is breathtaking to consider how far the Republican Party has gone from those days. \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  who could give a speech like that and then more recently, call in a personal favor with his Republican members, urging them to vote against a bipartisan commission to look into the first violent attack on the capitol to stop the peaceful transfer of power.\par \par \uc2 So there you have them, warning his colleagues, that was then toward do the right things, to do the ethical thing, saying that most people don`t think we can do the ethical thing. And today what is he doing? He calls in shifts to stop an investigation to January 6th but also another illustration of just how far the GOP has gone from those days where ethics into matter, Kevin McCarthy call recently lying about what he was saying on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 That`s not a scandal. The scandal is that if he wouldn`t lie he couldn`t occupy Republican leadership. You have to be willing to lie about something as fundamental to our democracy as whether the last election was legitimate, if you want to be part of GOP leadership now. That`s how much the Republican Party has changed either \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to listen to just a few seconds of Kevin McCarthy on this tape that was recently released. Just to show the kind of evidence you`re trying to get at. Let`s listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): The only discussion I would have with him is that I think this will pass. And it would be my recommendation you should resign. I mean, that would be my take. But I don`t think he would take it.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:03]\par \par \uc2 But I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I suppose one of your questions from that to get McCarthy could be, why should the president -- why should \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have resigned at that time? But when we look at evidence like that, we have to assume that you know an awful lot about what Kevin McCarthy did that day, what he said that day. What`s more are you trying to obtain from Kevin McCarthy through testimony?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Well, you`re right, we do know a lot about this. But most of what we know, much of what we know, we are here secondhand. And, of course, no one would be better position to testify about conversations that he had with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  than Kevin McCarthy. And indeed, he`s making reference there to what he would tell the president. What`s in fact did he say over the phone with the president? What was the president saying to him on January 6th, we are hearing from other members as we did earlier from Herera Beutler that the president irritated with McCarthy when McCarthy was begging to call it off said these people who are attacking the Capitol, these people apparently care more about the election than he does, or worse to that effect.\par \par \uc2 So there is a lot we would like to ask McCarthy. These other members we subpoena today, some allegedly involved or were involved in seeking pardons. Why do they think parties would be necessary? Or were involved in an effort to overturn the election that they -- judge of California recently proclaimed was likely a in criminal act violated multiple laws. So, these witnesses, clearly relevant information. If their oath of office means anything to them, it means cell -- subpoenaed to testify.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I think we both know seeing here that our audience listening to us right now does not expect anyone who received these subpoenas today to comply with them. Is there any reason that you have a greater confidence in that than our audience does?\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: You know, I would never want to second guess your audience, Lawrence. But part of the reasons why as you are pointing out, these circumstances are so impressive. It`s not just that we`re doing an investigation of all attack on the Capitol that is obviously completely unprecedented in terms of stopping an election result.\par \par \uc2 But the reality is that when we`ve asked members to come in, we have a path to subpoena them, even as recently as a Russian investigation and it at a time when I believe it was shared by Republican \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2  -- we had a Democrat and Republican with relevant information. We asked them to come in. They both did, to be interviewed by our committee.\par \par \uc2 They didn`t require subpoena, they didn`t try to fight it. And so, what`s unprecedented here is you have Republican members potentially involved in a plot to overturn the election. But also if they do refuse, already to refuse legal process, they`re willing to break the law to avoid having to come in to testify about what they know.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman Adam Schiff, thank you very much for leading off our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 SCHIFF: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, federal investigators are moving ahead with investigation into how classified documents ended up and Donald Trump`s club in Florida. Daniel Goldman and Tim O`Brien will join us next.\par \par \uc2 And later, Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  has what he called a simple question for Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): Hate on civil rights. Hate on women`s rights. Hate on reproductive rights. Hate on voting rights. Hate on marital rights.\par \par \uc2 Hate on equal protection under the law. Hate on liberty and justice for all. Hate on free and fair elections. Why are you such a hater?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  will join us later in this hour.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:23:12]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JAMIE RASKIN (D-MD): An attempted coup and a violent insurrection at the capitol interrupting the peaceful transfer of power are unprecedented events. And people have asked, does this set a precedent for the issue of subpoenas for members of Congress in the future if there are coups and insurrections? I suppose that it does.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is reporting that federal prosecutors are conducting a grand jury investigation into whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and Trump White House staff mishandled classified information after the National Archives discovered classified materials and 15 boxes that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  removed from the White House residence and took to Florida, before being forced to return the material to the National Archives.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports, prosecutors issued a subpoena to the national archives and records administration to obtain the boxes of classified documents, according to two people familiar with the matter. The authorities have also made interview requests to people who worked in the White House in the final days of Mr. Trump`s presidency. The prosecutors` moves show that the Justice Department under Attorney General \uc2 Merrick B. Garland\uc2  is at least willing to examine a matter that may ultimately touch directly on the president`s conduct.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Daniel Goldman, former house majority counsel during the first Trump impeachment trial, he is also the former U.S. attorney for the district of New York.\par \par \uc2 Also with us, Tim O`Brien, senior columnist for "Bloomberg Opinion", and author of "Trump Nation", both are MSNBC analyst.\par \par \uc2 And, Dan Goldman, let me begin with you and your experience with classified material. What does this investigation sound like to you?\par \par \uc2 DAN GOLDMAN, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Well, it is pretty remarkable to have 15 boxes that include a string, or a slew of classified materials, as you know, Lawrence, from working on the Hill.\par \par \uc2 [22:25:07]\par \par \uc2 When I worked on the house intelligence committee, we were in a secure location where classified information could remain. But there were still tremendous precautions that we had to take with any classified information. And the notion that it would just be thrown in moving boxes, and sent down to Mar-a-Lago is completely an accurate to anyone who regularly handles classified information.\par \par \uc2 So the question becomes, how did these classified documents get in those boxes? I tend to think that lower love royal stuffers, middle-able staffers, paid very close attention to those rules, and that perhaps more senior level political employees do not, or politicians themselves.\par \par \uc2 So I think that this investigation has to go directly to the people who pack those boxes, and to understand exactly what orders they had, what issues they brought to the attention of their superiors, and what they were told, so that the Department of Justice and the \uc2 FBI\uc2  can get a real story as to how classified information ended up in boxes. This is far more serious than Hillary Clinton`s emails.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I am very much out of my depth on unclassified information. I worked for a senator who served on the Intelligence Committee. But I was not on the Intelligence Committee staff. So I have never seen anything close to a classified document.\par \par \uc2 And, Dan, what I`m wondering about is, when those things are being moved, when they are in boxes, is there something that a staffer would see that would make a staffer realize, oh, this is a hot item here. This is classified. And at what point did the staff moving those boxes, those individuals, at what point do they face possible criminal liability?\par \par \uc2 GOLDMAN: The classified information relies heavily on markings, which is to say that you know things are classified, and what degree they are classified as because every single document has a marking of what the classification is. And in some cases, you know, secret or top secret, those words are written in diagonal, in huge letters, like you would see add a water stamp on a piece of paper. There is no possible way to look at a classified document and not know that it is classified.\par \par \uc2 So that is not an excuse. You cannot simply say, oh, I had no idea that there were markings on the classified documents. And so, you know, everyone knows that. And when these documents travel from one place to another, they are literally put in a bag with a lock. I am not kidding. You have a key and a lock for a bag where you bring the classified information.\par \par \uc2 So if you somehow lose that bag, nobody can get access to that information. That is how seriously these things are taken. So it is truly remarkable that we have this issue. And I think it is important that not only trying to figure out what information got out and where it may have gone so that they can cover their tracks on the security angle, but they are also interviewing people who worked at the White House, which means they are trying to figure out how these documents got to Mar-a-Lago.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNEL: Tim O`Brien, we have been working at these pictures of the boxes, we know who didn`t carry the boxes. There is no way \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the man you know so well, was carrying those boxes.\par \par \uc2 So when he tries to fall asleep tonight, he has to think about everyone who was carrying those boxes, and exactly how loyal are they to Donald at this point.\par \par \uc2 TIM O`BRIEN, MSNBC POLITICAL COMMENTATOR: Are they going to stay quiet as this proceed? I was just thinking, as my friend Dan was talking about secure locations for classified documents, and bags with locks on them, that this was a White House that was flushing documents down the toilets. That this was a White House in which aides were visually running around with scotch tape to tape back together documents that Trump had sort of just torn up at his desk.\par \par \uc2 I don`t think that there was a culture of document preservation to be kind in this White House. And I don`t think there was any loyalty in this White House. I think when push comes to shove, and they need to find out about who is in charge of moving them, why they were moved, and what they contained, all of which we still don`t know.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:48]\par \par \uc2 We don`t know what the subject matter was of these, and why these ended up at Mar-a-Lago rather than in the toilet. So there is a lot of a pattern we simply don`t know yet.\par \par \uc2 I also am not sure at the end of the day that there are going to be severe consequences for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in this one. You know, the president has a lot of leeway to declassify documents.\par \par \uc2 I think there is a lot of ways that he could get around this in the courtroom. I am assuming that the Justice Department has convened a grand jury because \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is always concerned about his investigations looking politicized.\par \par \uc2 And it reminds me yet again that if the DOJ has the time to convene a grand jury to look into this matter, why don`t they have the time to convene a grand jury to look into what January 6 committee is doing which has far more serious consequences around it.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC: That is the great question. Tim O`Brien and Daniel Goldman, thank you both very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Thanks Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  has a few questions for \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  that we don`t expect \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  to answer. Congressman Jeffries joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:30:58]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Supreme Court Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  said that peaceful protesters near the homes of the Supreme Court justices are bullying the justices and that Americans are quote, "becoming addicted to wanting particular outcomes, not living with the outcomes we don`t like," end quote.\par \par \uc2 Our next guest, Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  responded to Justice Thomas during yesterday`s House Judiciary Committee hearing.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES (D-NY): If Justice Thomas really wants to deal with bullying in America, or this problem of people supposedly unwilling to accept outcomes that they don`t like, I`ve got some advice for justice Thomas: start in your own home. Have a conversation with Gini Thomas.\par \par \uc2 She refused to accept the legitimacy of the 2020 presidential election. Why? Because she didn`t like the outcome. And so instead, she tried to steal the election, overthrow the \uc2 United States government\uc2  and install a tyrant.\par \par \uc2 That`s bullying. That`s being unwilling to accept an outcome because you don`t like the results. Because the former twice impeached so-called president of the United States of America lost legitimately to \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . How did she respond? Instead, she said, the Bidens to face a military tribunal in Guantanamo Bay on trumped up charges of sedition. You`ve got to be kidding me.\par \par \uc2 And lastly, let me ask this question of Brother Thomas. Why are you such a hater? Hate on civil rights, hate on women`s rights, hate on reproductive rights, hate on voting rights, hate on marital rights, hate on equal protection under the law, hate on liberty and justice for all, hate on free and fair elections. Why are you such a hater?\par \par \uc2 And you think you can get away with it, escape public scrutiny. Because you think that shamelessness is your superpower?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Chairman, a point of order.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Here is a news flash --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Mr. Chairman --\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: from the House Judiciary Committee. (INAUDIBLE) to the ground will rise again and truth will be your kryptonite.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Representative \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2  of New York. He is the chair of the House Democratic Caucus and member of the house Judiciary Committee and was an impeachment manager for the first impeachment trial of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 And that sounded like a moment that had been building for you for a while.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well that certainly is the case. I think Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  has been an outrage and an abomination from the very beginning. And I think many Americans across the country feel that way.\par \par \uc2 But the culmination was really twofold for me. One, this was a hearing where we were discussing and marking up a bill on the absence of a judicial code of ethics for the Supreme Court. Every other federal judge in the system has to abide by a code of ethics. The Supreme Court does not. They think they can police themselves.\par \par \uc2 We see it`s a runaway majority. That`s what the leaked opinion suggests. And then Justice Thomas had the nerve to lecture America about bullying and being unwilling to accept outcomes that we don`t like. It was just too much to take at that particular point in time.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And this is a judge who has ruled on a case involving his wife`s communications with the White House chief of staff, that it is against the law. There is one federal law saying that judges cannot -- the Supreme Court justices cannot rule on cases involving their spouses. He has already done that.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Yes and this is part of the challenge that we have here. That Justice Roberts has taken a position that Supreme Court justices effectively can police themselves. They cannot. And \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and the sordid affair with Ginni Thomas and her involvement in the January 6 insurrection and the effort to try to hide her records which is the case that Justice Thomas was ruling on is totally unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 And so I think as Democrats, we wanted to make the point that no Supreme Court justice, and certainly Justice Thomas, is above the law, above publics scrutiny or above public accountability.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:59]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Because they do not discipline themselves, the only discipline available for Supreme Court justices is impeachment.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well that`s the ultimate penalty that should be paid in cases of egregious violation where there`s been a constitutional crime committed. But I think the step that we have taken as House Democrats at this moment led by Chairman Nadler and Congressman \uc2 Hank Johnson\uc2  is to actually put a judicial code of conduct into place for the Supreme Court justices and then work to try to make sure that there`s teeth and accountability if there is a failure to comply.\par \par \uc2 So that was what the legislation that we were marking up at that particular hearing was all about. Of course, every single Republican opposed it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 28 women who were Princeton classmates of Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  have shared a letter expressing their outrage at his draft opinion overturning Roe versus Wade. The author of that letter will join us next, along with Cecile Richards.\par \par \uc2 [22:41:10]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reports half of the women in this country of reproductive age live in states where they will lose access to abortion when the Supreme Court overturns Roe versus Wade. 33.6 million women of reproductive age will lose the constitutional right to control their own bodies."\par \par \uc2 The now looming certainty of Roe versus Wade being overturned has pushed abortion policy to the top of voters` concerns, abortion policy is now tied with economic policy in a Monmouth poll as the most important issues facing voters.\par \par \uc2 In the last congressional midterm election, abortion policy was an important concern for only 9 percent of voters. It has now rocketed up to 25 percent with economic policy up 26 percent.\par \par \uc2 New Hampshire`s Republican Governor \uc2 Chris Sununu\uc2  is betraying the voters who reelected him last year when he said, "I`m a pro-choice governor. I`ve been pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 That is what Republican John Sununu said to win his reelection last year. He is now fully in line with the Republican Party on banning abortion and just signed New Hampshire`s first abortion ban into law.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOVERNOR CHRIS SUNUNU (R-NH): Look, I`m the first governor in 40 years to sign an abortion ban, right. Republican governors before me never signed that. I`m the -- I`ve done more on the pro-life issue, if you will, than anyone.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: 28 of Supreme Court Justice Samuel Alito`s college classmates have signed a letter that begins, "We the undersigned Princeton woman of `72 have been deeply shocked by the leaked Supreme Court draft authored by our classmate, Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We find it bitter indeed to see the draft Supreme Court reverse the strides we thought we were making as part of one of the first classes of Princeton women towards a world of equality and fairness for women of all races and social and economic positions."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now Cecile Richards, co-chair of American Bridge 21st Century, a former president of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . And also with us, Susan Squier, emeritus professor of gender and sexuality studies at \uc2 Penn State University\uc2 . She was a classmate of Justice Alito at \uc2 Princeton University\uc2  and wrote a letter protesting his draft opinion.\par \par \uc2 Thank you both very much for joining us. And Professor Squier, let me begin with you tonight. And what moved you to write this letter about your classmate?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN SQUIER, PROFESSOR, \uc2 PENN STATE UNIVERSITY\uc2 : Well, it was really very simple. I had already been planning to go to my 50th reunion. And I woke up the morning, I guess it was May 3rd and saw the news of the leaked document.\par \par \uc2 And I read it because I am trained as a literature and medicine scholar, so I pulled up the whole document, and I read it. And as someone who works with literature, I know this nuance and word choice. And I was appalled at what I saw.\par \par \uc2 So I got in touch with a couple of the women who went to Princeton with me and said this is not a time when we can do business as usual. We have to make something happen here. We have to do something. We weren`t sure what.\par \par \uc2 And then I finally decided I would draft a letter. I put it up on a \uc2 Google\uc2  document, and I started contacting women in our class asking if they were willing to sign.\par \par \uc2 And quite quickly, we got a lot of signatures, both from the class of `72 and the class of `73 which would have been the class in which Roe versus Wade turned 50. You know, they would have been the ones to celebrate that.\par \par \uc2 And it sort of happened very quickly. There were no negatives. There were no people who said they didn`t want to sign. Everyone was just, you know, thrilled that we were doing this and very urgently angry about it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Cecile Richards, we are seeing now that the estimate is that basically half of the women of reproductive age in this country will lose the constitutional right when the Alito draft becomes official -- the official opinion of the court.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:56]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And we now know that it could expand -- the deprivation of rights could spread all across the country if this same court decides that the constitution really does hold that all abortion is, in effect, murder.\par \par \uc2 CECILE RICHARDS, CO-CHAIR, AMERICAN BRIDGE 21ST CENTURY: No, I mean this is, beginning as you said -- it is beginning to dawn on people. This isn`t just an idle threat. This is actually the future that we are probably facing.\par \par \uc2 And it is more than half the country, 28 states are poised to ban abortion in some way, 13 of them very, very quickly. And one of the things that is interesting, I think Lawrence, is thought some of these states where this is going to happen are states that are hotly contested this November.\par \par \uc2 So you look at the state of Arizona where there is a 1901 abortion ban on the books that would also allow for jailing people, anyone who helped a woman get access to an abortion. That could go into effect immediately.\par \par \uc2 The state of Georgia, a six-week abortion ban that is already, you know, if Roe is overturned, will take effect immediately. This is going to increasingly rise up in the consciousness of people because abortions are no longer going to be available in more than half the country.\par \par \uc2 As you said, already people are taking notice. And as we saw this week, not a single Republican United States senator voted in support of protecting a woman`s rights to make her own decisions about her pregnancy. This is going to be an increasingly important issue to voters this fall.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Professor Squier, I was stunned by the shoddy scholarship in the opinion, especially quoting a couple of English aristocrats who were believers, both believers in witches, and believed that witches had to be prosecuted and put to death, and did their part as -- in the British judicial system to actually make sure that witches were executed.\par \par \uc2 Those are legal authorities in Samuel Alito`s draft.\par \par \uc2 SQUIER: Yes. When I read it I thought I wouldn`t have given this a C if he had been my undergraduate student. It was very badly sourced. He didn`t consider the context at all. And this from a man, I am sorry to say, who is trained as a historian at Princeton, as well as a political scientist.\par \par \uc2 For me, the really stunning thing was when you can watch the language from the Mississippi Gestational Age Act which I understand maybe settled law in Mississippi, but hasn`t yet been ratified by the Supreme Court as far as I understand it.\par \par \uc2 Now, I am not a lawyer. And that language seeps into the documents, so he is referring to unborn children rather than fetuses. I just found it stunningly duplicitous, and badly argued, and shoddy and embarrassing.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Cecile, what is your reaction to what we are seeing in the polling now on how serious an issue abortion has very quickly become in the congressional campaign?\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: Well, I think it is because, of course, all of these Republican candidates and folks who were in office are saying they are being asked, do you support these abortion bans? Do you support the repeal of Roe versus Wade?\par \par \uc2 And they are on record now. In fact we just launched a site at America Bridge so that people can see the exact quotes of what Republicans are saying. They are all on record now. And they are on the record saying that they would agree to an abortion ban.\par \par \uc2 Places like Pennsylvania, you know, a state where the gubernatorial candidates in the Republican primary are saying yes, we want to ban all abortions. And so I think it is no longer a Texas issue, it is no longer a Mississippi issue, it is an issue that is impacting folks all over this country.\par \par \uc2 And that`s you know, that to me, that is going to continue to happen as these abortion bans get passed, and go into effect.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Cecile Richards and Professor Susan Squier thank you both very much for joining our discussion tonight.\par \par \uc2 SQUIER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And we have a very special and slightly profane LAST WORD from the fabulous Tony award winning Broadway icon Patti LuPone. You are going to want to see what she said from a Broadway stage to an audience member about obeying the mask rule in the theater. The glorious fury of Patti LuPone is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:54:25]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Since Broadway theaters reopened after the COVID shutdown, I have ventured into exactly one Broadway theater to see the Tony nominated "How I Learned To Drive" with its brilliant Tony nominated cast.\par \par \uc2 Entering the theater, everyone had to show their vaccination cards and wear a mask throughout the play which everyone did without the slightest complaint, thrilled to be back in a Broadway theater.\par \par \uc2 Two-time Tony winner Pattie LuPone was just nominated again this week for a Tony for her part in a revival of Stephen Sondheim`s "Company". After a performance this week, the cast reassembled on stage to take questions from the audience.\par \par \uc2 It did not go well for an audience member close to the stage who didn`t follow the rule about masks.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:50]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PATTI LUPONE, ACTRESS: Put your mask over your nose. That`s while you`re in the theater. That is the rule. If you don`t want to follow the rule, get the (EXPLETIVE DELETED) out. I`m serious.\par \par \uc2 Who do you think you are that you do not respect the people that are sitting around you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We pay your salary.\par \par \uc2 LUPONE: You pay my salary. (EXPLETIVE DELETED) Chris Harper pays my salary.\par \par \uc2 Who do you think you are?\par \par \uc2 Just put your mask over your nose.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Chris Harper, the producer of "Company" issued a statement fully supportive of his Tony nominated star. And you can support Patti LuPone by buying tickets to "Company" at the Bernard Jacobs Theater.\par \par \uc2 Patti LuPone has been receiving standing ovations today from people all over the world. Standing in front of their computers watching that video. People who love the theater and will always love Patti LuPone.\par \par \uc2 Patti LuPone gets tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220513ei5c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May 12, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris Hayes, Glenn Kirchner \par \uc2 8,198 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST: And that denial continues even now. And so, we end tonight by taking a moment to honor these one million lives lost, people who mattered to their loved ones into their country. A tragic toll we cannot repeat and should never, ever forget. And that is tonight`s "REIDOUT."\par \par \uc2 ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 JOHN ROBERTS, ANCHOR, FOX NEWS: This is highly unusual to issue a subpoena to a sitting member of the House. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Subpoenas for five sitting members of Congress, including the leader of the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY (R-WY): I think that he has important information that needs to be part of any investigation.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Tonight, what this escalation means for the investigation as the hearings approach and what the committee now wants to know from Jordan, Perry, Biggs, Brooks, and McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 REP. ZOE LOFGREN (D-CA): We have information from other sources that lead us to believe that each one of these individuals who are our colleagues have information the committee needs to tell the full story.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Plus, Mark Elias on his court victory to block Florida`s racially gerrymandered congressional map. And what I learned from my bout with Coronavirus that could end up helping you when ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Chris Hayes. Today, the bipartisan committee investigating January 6 took the seismic step of subpoenaing the most powerful Republican the House Minority Leader Kevin McCarthy of California. That of course, is the guy who is poised to become Speaker of the House should Republicans win the house in the Midterm Elections.\par \par \uc2 They also issued subpoenas for four of his colleagues and in fact, their colleagues, Jim Jordan of Ohio, Mo Brooks of Alabama, Andy Biggs of Arizona, and Scott Perry of Pennsylvania. The latter four, in particular, are largely believed to have been key players in the ex-president`s plot to overturn the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 In case it`s not clear, this is obviously a really, really big deal. We sort of dug back through the history here. As far as we can tell, this appears to be the first time a member serving in Congress has been subpoenaed by Congress, outside of an ethics investigation, let alone the man gearing up to be speaker. And for months and months, House Democrats have been trying everything to avoid getting too precise at this point which is why it`s worth looking back at just how we got here.\par \par \uc2 In the days after the insurrection, it really seemed like Kevin McCarthy understood the gravity of the situation. It`s apparent in the since leaked phone calls with his fellow Republicans from the days following the attack when McCarthy privately told allies he was thinking of asking Trump to resign.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: And you asked if, you know, what happens if it gets there after he`s gone? Is there any chance? Are you hearing that he might resign? Is there any reason to think that might happen?\par \par \uc2 REP. KEVIN MCCARTHY (R-CA): I`ve had few discussions. My gut tells me no. I`m seriously thinking of having that conversation with him tonight. I haven`t talked to him in a couple of days.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: McCarthy appeared to take the attack so seriously that he actually deputized his own ally, a member of the Republican House leadership, Congressman \uc2 John Katko\uc2  of New York to negotiate with Democrats on an official bipartisan 911 style commission. And the idea is that the two parties would come together, and they would create a serious 50-50 commission where neither side had a partisan advantage.\par \par \uc2 And after months of painstaking negotiations, we covered them closely on the show, Katko reached a deal with the Democrats. But during those months in negotiations, McCarthy`s political calculations changed. It became clear that Trump had a firm grip on the party. So, McCarthy encouraged his own caucus to vote against the commission that he had essentially greenlit and left Congressman Katco to fend for himself, standing alone, while he tried to fact check his own party on the commission.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 JOHN KATKO\uc2  (R-NY): Now, another charge I heard was that a commission could be controlled by partisan staff hired unilaterally by the Commission chair. That is simply not true. There has been some concerns or arguments made about the criminal investigations. Make no mistake about it. This commission has nothing to do with the criminal investigations. This commission, by law cannot interfere with criminal investigations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s always going so strange about watching a Republican be like wait, my colleagues are lying about something? But no, no, no, that`s a lie. Now, Kevin McCarthy made a calculated decision. It`s clear to everyone, right? The facts of January 6, the (INAUDIBLE), the aftermath of the election, the insurrection, there were always going to be terrible for Republicans for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . So bad, in fact that his only hope was to try to turn the investigation itself into something illegitimate and partisan.\par \par \uc2 Katko`s compromised did end up passing the House. It actually got 35 Republicans on board which again not that many but more than would vote for it today, that`s for sure. But then the commission died in the Senate after \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  came out against it, whip votes against it, and Republicans filibuster.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:16]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL (R-KY): After careful consideration, I`ve made the decision to oppose the House Democrats slanted and unbalanced proposal for another commission to study the events of January 6. As everybody surely knows, I repeatedly made my views about the events of January 6 very clear. I spoke clearly and left no doubt about my conclusions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Oh, well, Mitch, you know, if you`re done with it, then I guess everyone should move on. By the way, the unbalanced line is just literally unsure, like mathematically it was balanced. That was the whole point. So, Democrats were not willing to let an investigation of the attack go. So, then House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  established using her power as Speaker of the House, a select committee to look into the insurrection to investigate it.\par \par \uc2 Now, that was less formal, the proposed commission, which would have involved both chambers of Congress. McCarthy, who was taking orders from Trump, decided to sabotage it. He then tried to appoint Jim Jordan to the committee, who of course, is now being subpoenaed for his very role in plotting the coup itself.\par \par \uc2 We know that Jordan spoke to Trump on January 6 multiple times. The committee he tried to join now says he is a material witness in the investigation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Congressman Jordan may well be a material witness. He is somebody who was involved in a number of meetings in the lead up to what happened on January 6, involved in planning for January 6, certainly for the objections that day as he said publicly. So, he may well be a material witness.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, obviously, it would be preposterous to put a member on a committee investigating an event that they were part of, right? Like, a juror who is involved in the crime setting the jury box doesn`t work. So, Pelosi vetoed his appointment, as well as Congressman Jim banks of Indiana who had spread Trump`s big live stolen election. In return, McCarthy took his ball and went home, pulled all of his Republican appointees off the committee.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: Speaker Pelosi has taken the unprecedented step of denying the minority parties picks for the select committee on January 6. This represents something that has not happened in the house before for a select committee by the historian. It`s an egregious abuse of power. Pelosi has broken this institution.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I mean, if we`re talking about precedent, no one had ever tried to appoint an alleged coup plotter to a committee to investigate the coup. It`s an interesting choice of words for McCarthy, considering the insurrection is of course, literally broke the institution, smashed it to pieces.\par \par \uc2 But of course, this was the party line now, right? The committee is a sham, its findings are illegitimate, we can safely sort of put it off in this other corner of the world. You there Republican voter don`t have to worry you`re pretty head about it. Of course, Pelosi did appoint two Republicans to the committee, so it`s bipartisan., \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  of Wyoming, \uc2 Adam Kinzinger\uc2  of Illinois.\par \par \uc2 Both immediately became pariahs in their own party. Cheney, once the third most powerful Republican in the House, was on the phone with McCarthy when he contemplating asking Trump to resign, lost her position and party leadership. The \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  even censured Cheney and Kinzinger for "participating in a Democrat-led persecution of ordinary citizens engaged in legitimate political discourse. The kind of political discourse where you take a weapon to a cop said and bang it repeatedly.\par \par \uc2 Now, to their credit, seriously, to their credit, Cheney and Kinzinger, whose politics I do not like at all, were not deterred. And the bipartisan committee has remained determined, united and its purpose. It`s been plugging away for nearly a year now in the face of Republicans stonewalling, stonewalling from Trump`s allies, evasions.\par \par \uc2 We have this situation now where less than a month before the committee is set to go public with hearings, there`s one last step in its investigation. And that`s to find out what their colleagues knew, and their colleagues won`t tell them. And so, they are going to do the unprecedented thing to issue subpoenas to the Republican obstructionist, including with Kevin McCarthy, the top Republican in the House, the man who helped block the initial commission in the first place.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2  is a Democrat in California. He`s a member of the House Intelligence Committee and the Judiciary Committee, and he joins me now. Congressman, I don`t think anyone would deny this is a very big a significant step. What is your reaction to it?\par \par \uc2 REP. ERIC SWALWELL (D-CA): Shouldn`t their oath have been enough, Chris? Every citizen`s responsibility when they witness a crime is to cooperate with authorities. This is the greatest crime against the Constitution we`ve ever seen, the most amount of arrests ever for a single a single act. And these individuals have information.\par \par \uc2 And so, shouldn`t their oath to the office that they serve when they raise their right hand and we`re sworn in on January 3 just three days before the insurrection, shouldn`t that have been enough? And it clearly wasn`t. They were asked to come in voluntarily, they refuse to do so.\par \par \uc2 And drastic times call for drastic measures. This was a day that we nearly lost our democracy, our freedom, our right to vote and have that vote means something. And if they don`t want to cooperate, then we`re going to have to press them with legal subpoena.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:20]\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, I mean, there`s always the don`t make a threat you can`t back up question too, right? So, now there`s legal subpoenas issued, you know, if those subpoenas are safely ignored, as appears to have happened with say, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , right, who was subpoenaed, was cooperating, stopped cooperating, was criminally referred to Department of Justice, nothing has been forthcoming. We don`t know why that is, but it hasn`t happened. Do you vitiate the power of the House to enforce its subpoenas if that`s the situation you end up in here?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: Well, Chris, again, if they`re not going to honor the subpoena, then I think the committee has already shown that they`re willing to keep pressing forward. So, that could mean yes, a civil case. You know, go up the courts of the civil case, or it could mean a criminal referral. It could also mean a referral to the Ethics Committee. I think they have a number of options.\par \par \uc2 But most importantly, if they don`t honor it, it means that they have something to hide. It`s an inference of guilt. And in a civil case, it would be -- it would be used against them that the only reason that they`re not coming forward, because an innocent person would, is because they have information that they want to conceal.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: There`s also this question, right, about the shoe being on the other foot. And this has always been sort of hanging over all this, right? The idea is that what binds the decisions the majority makes is the fact that it knows it won`t always be in the majority. We`ve seen Congress in the House move back and forth several times in the last 15, 20 years.\par \par \uc2 And there`s this threat that keeps happening despite the fact that I think there`s a real asymmetrical situation where you have like active insurrectionist in one party and not on the other, that punishment of their more aberrant members is going to be revisited upon the Democrats if, you know, the table is turned.\par \par \uc2 This is McCarthy threatening to strip you of your committee assignments back when the House voted to strip the committee assignments from \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2 . McCarthy replied when asked -- yes, McCarthy replied when asked. That meant he will use standard Democrats have created by stripping \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  and \uc2 Paul Gosar\uc2  of their committee assignments to remove Swalwell from Intelligence and Homeland Security Committees.\par \par \uc2 How do you think about the sort of threat of it`s going to be even worse when we have the majority?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: Yes, they`re going to do that anyway. And I told January 6 Committee members before this decision was made was that I hope you don`t believe you`re protecting me or Mr. Schiff or Miss Omar by not doing the right thing here because you don`t want them to do that to me or anyone else in the future. They`re going to do that, anyway. And I go into that with eyes wide open.\par \par \uc2 But if the shoe were on the other foot and Republicans were in power, we`re already out of balance as a country. We are already in a position now where they would not allow another peaceful transition of power. They`re trying to write the laws in a way that they would never have to give up power and they may not certify Donald Trump`s -- they may not -- they may decertify the next time \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  wins and have the votes to do it. And that really concerns me more than what retaliatory measures they would take against me or any of my colleagues.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: What do you think the goal -- you`re not on the committee, so you`re sort of one foot in, one foot out. But what should the ultimate goal here be in this next month as we approach the public hearings?\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: To tell the story of what happened with witnesses who can animate that for the public as to how close we came to losing our democracy, who was responsible? Kevin McCarthy said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  admitted responsibility to him. That`s why Kevin McCarthy is such an important witness.\par \par \uc2 So, who was responsible? What actions that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  take leading up to this? What did he know about violence that was going to occur? What actions did he not take that could have protected this from happening and could have protected the lives that were injured that day? And then what is the plan, knowing that Republicans are more comfortable with violence in voting going forward to make sure that a day like this never happens again?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Congressman \uc2 Eric Swalwell\uc2 , great to have you on. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 SWALWELL: My pleasure. Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: When we come back, at least five Republican members of Congress are attempting to hide what they know about the Trump coup. Next, former federal prosecutor Glenn Kirschner and what we already know about their conduct and what these subpoenas mean for getting them to talk.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. MO BROOKS (R-AL): Today is the day American patriot start taking down names and kicking ass. Now, our ancestors sacrifice their blood, their sweat, their tears, their fortunes, and sometimes their lives to give us their descendants an America that is the greatest nation in world history. So, I have a question for you. Are you willing to do that same?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That was Alabama Republican Congressman Mo Brooks at Donald Trump`s Ellipse rally on January 6 2021. And there`s no confusion given that tape which we all saw as to why the committee investigating insurrection would want to speak with him. But on that list of House Republicans subpoena today is at least one somewhat obscure name. That`s Scott Perry of Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 Now, he is one of the central figures in Trump`s plot to stay in power. Perry features quite prominently in the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  report on the efforts to overturn the election. Beyond amplifying Trump`s false election fraud claims and working to prevent his own constituents votes from being certified, "Perry acknowledged introducing Jeffrey Clark to Trump."\par \par \uc2 [20:20:18]\par \par \uc2 Now, Clark is a key figure. Remember, he was in the Department of Justice. He was the official who essentially tried to like pull off a coup at the Department of Justice where he would strong arm the Acting Attorney General into leaving or at least stealing the election by sending out these official letters to the Department of Justice, saying, "Georgia and several other states that it was investigating voting regularities and recommend that each state legislature call a special session to consider appointing an alternate slate of electors." Luckily, those letters never got sent.\par \par \uc2 Glenn Kirchner is a former federal prosecutor who spent 30 years in the Washington D.C. U.S. Attorney`s Office, and he joins me now. I guess we should just start, Glenn, with this fact which I think we can skip ahead to kind of the obstruction is that a subpoena is a serious thing in any context. When you get a subpoena, you don`t just like throw it in the garbage. And that`s the starting point for how to think about what happened today.\par \par \uc2 GLENN KIRCHNER, MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: You know, subpoenas are supposed to be taken seriously. They`re not party invitations, Chris. And we can have the usual conversation about OK, what are the enforcement mechanisms now in the event these five members of Congress defy these subpoenas.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve been through this so many times. It could either be that they are voted in contempt and referred for criminal prosecution to the Department of Justice. But DOJ`s batting average is not very good at the moment. They`re only one for four when it comes to indicting folks who have been referred for prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Only \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  has been indicted. We`re still awaiting \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , \uc2 Peter Navarro\uc2 , Dan Scavino. You then of course, have inherent contempt of Congress which is a long-dormant weapon in Congress`s enforcement arsenal. You know, will they pull out their weapon of inherent contempt and use it against some of their own members if they defy subpoenas? That would kind of be like just desserts.\par \par \uc2 And then of course, you`ve got civil enforcement, which can be a long, unwieldy process in the civil courts. So, you know, yes, they should be taken seriously. We have all the usual enforcement mechanisms, but it remains to be seen, you know, what will they do? Will they comply, or will they defy?\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, let`s start with Brooks, just because Brooks is so obvious. I mean, one of the things -- in the -- in the world of executive privilege, right, there`s an idea of waiving it if you talk about the thing at issue. And there`s a little bit, it seems to me, a kind of parallel here, which is a Brooks has been going around blabbing because, you know, Trump decided to unendorse him because he`s doing poorly in his primary. And he`s going around telling everyone who will listen that Trump still wants to overturn the election. And at that point, you think like, well, you seem to want to talk. Like, you should just talk.\par \par \uc2 KIRCHNER: Yes. And, you know, the view that I have about why they chose these five members of Congress because they could have chosen others. We`ve heard about these, supposed reconnaissance tours given to insurrectionists. We saw, you know, \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2 . He have the support for the insurrection. But they didn`t subpoena those folks. Instead, they subpoenaed these five.\par \par \uc2 I can only assume it`s because each one of these five brings something to the table about Donald Trump`s conduct and misconduct, not just their own. You just played Mo Brooks, inciting, you know, violence, quite frankly, expressly. But I think the other thing at risk right now, Chris, is that the legal landscape has shifted not so subtly in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Because a federal judge in California did announce in his finding that after litigating the John Eastman email issue, that there was evidence by a preponderance of the evidence, 51 percent, that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and John Eastman committed two felony crimes together. So, now, I suggest that ups the ante on the McCarthy`s of the world if they decide right to refuse to testify against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , when a federal judge has already announced by a preponderance there`s evidence that he committed crimes.\par \par \uc2 That actually constitutes two federal felonies in and of itself if you refuse to come forward and talk about the crimes that have been committed against the United States. Accessory after the fact, and misprision of a felony, which we haven`t talked about a lot. But that now comes directly into play, because a crime has been committed that is cognizable by a court of the United States. And you conceal it, which is what McCarthy and the others wouldn`t be doing if they refuse to testify, you`ve committed a three year federal felony misprision.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: We`ve also got evidence in some of the letters of just the degree of plotting that was happening, particularly this one sort of thread here with Scott Perry about the attempt to get pardons, which I think is also interesting because it sort of reflects a little bit of consciousness of guilt in the aftermath of this. This is reading from the committee`s letter to Representative Andy Biggs -- I`m sorry, not Scott Perry. This is Andy Biggs. Sorry about that.\par \par \uc2 That recent information from former White House personnel is identified an effort by certain House Republicans after January 6 to seek a presidential pardon for activities taken in connection with President Trump`s efforts to overturn the results of the 2020 presidential election. Your name was identified as a potential participant in that effort.\par \par \uc2 We don`t know that much about it. We`ve only sort of seen this a little bit obscured through reporting. But the seeking of Pardons strikes me as quite significant in terms of what it shows about their consciousness of the gravity of what they had possibly participated in.\par \par \uc2 KIRCHNER: Yes, exactly. You don`t need a pardon for a peaceful protest or a normal tourist visit. You need a pardon for a crime. And of course, granting a pardon is some indication a crime has been committed and the Supreme Court has said, receiving or accepting one is some admission of guilt.\par \par \uc2 And you know, when you boil it all down, you`ve got even the Trump children testified before the J6 Committee and members of Congress are not going to testify in what is virtually their own living room? I mean, that tells us that they are really wanting to cover something up.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, that`s a great point. If Don Jr. can Zoom in and, you know, talk at his very rapid clip. Certainly, members of Congress can do the same. Glenn Kirchner, thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is caught trying to dilute the power of hundreds of thousands of Black voters in his state. Marc Elias on the big redistricting win in court in Florida right after this.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Members we are back in session. As is obvious, we have members who decided they wanted to hijack our process today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, that was a scene in Tallahassee, Florida last month, a Democratic revolt as the Florida Legislature ram through a new congressional map drawn up by Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . And the complaint was that the DeSantis map reduce representation for Black Floridians by eliminating one congressional district represented by a Black Democrat and shrinking another.\par \par \uc2 Democrats took their case to court. And yesterday, a DeSantis appointed judge agreed, finding the map would, "reduce the impact of 370,000 Black voters in eight mostly rural counties," saying, "it diminishes African- Americans` ability to elect the representative of their choice."\par \par \uc2 And so as of today, the DeSantis map is blocked. And the question becomes what happens now. Marc Elias, the founder of Democracy Docket helped fight on behalf of Florida voters in that case, he has been in court pushing for fair elections across the country. And he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Marc, tell me what the Florida Map proposal was, why the lawsuit happened, and what the state court found.\par \par \uc2 MARC ELIAS, FOUNDER, DEMOCRACY DOCKET: Yes. So, Chris, thanks for having me. It was actually more outrageous than you would even think because the Florida Constitution prohibits drawing maps that diminish the rights of minority voters. And the Florida Legislature knew this. The Republicans in the State House and the Republicans in the state senate actually did an analysis that said, the DeSantis map will diminish the ability of Black -- of minority voters.\par \par \uc2 And that`s why they originally passed a different map. They opposed the DeSantis map. It is only after DeSantis vetoed that and said it`s my map or no map that you saw the spineless, gutless Republican leadership in the statehouse and the state senate say, OK, we`ll go with your map.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. So, they draw -- they draw essentially a constitutionally compliant map that doesn`t take away this one Black -- majority Black seat, right? And that`s when we`re talking about sort of selecting the representatives. That the language the Voting Rights Act. It`s been integrated into state constitutions as well, as they think about making sure that, you know, African Americans can elect representatives who are Black in majority and minority districts.\par \par \uc2 So, then DeSantis said no, you have to do this map that knocks out a Black representative from the congressional delegation because it advantages Republicans nationally more. What was the -- what was the mechanism of it? This is a state court finding, right? So, what happens now?\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: So, what happens now is, as you said, the trial judge, held a hearing and found that the map, violate state constitution and order to implement the map that our expert, Harvard Professor Steven (INAUDIBLE) had drawn that would preserve CD5 as it was.\par \par \uc2 The state has indicated that they are going to appeal. So, this will now will go up to the Court of Appeals or the Supreme Court of Florida where look, they have a very simple choice. They are either going to strike this map down because it is clearly violative of the state constitution, or they`re going to ignore the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the fact that the trial judge, as people have pointed out, including yourself, was appointed by Governor DeSantis, you know, is interesting, but he was applying the law as the state constitution as it clearly is spelled out in the state constitution.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Well, also in a lot of this litigation, we`ve seen that the court striking down a map can be just the beginning of the story. In Ohio, of course, similar situation, although they`re not a racial gerrymander so much as a partisan one. But that violated a state, I think, either statute or constitutional provision that says -- that bans partisan gerrymandering.\par \par \uc2 The court -- the state court said no, this map doesn`t work. Ohio Republicans basically barely tweaked it and they basically are trying to ram it through now. They`re reapproved the map previously rejected by the Ohio Supreme Court. The map is the same one a federal court has said it will impose if the state redistricting standoff isn`t resolved by May 28. It seems like their defiance of the state court in Ohio is basically going to let them win that standoff.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:23]\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Well, we`ll see. I mean, as you point out, they are in defiance of court order of the state Supreme Court. And we will see whether or not that court takes action on the motions to hold the state officials in contempt or not, because it`s really outrageous.\par \par \uc2 I mean, what`s happened in Ohio is the State Supreme Court struck down the map. The state Republicans are simply ignoring that by acting in bad faith and passing and repassing maps that the state Supreme Court has made clear are unconstitutional in hope to run out the clock. But we`ll wait and see. The court -- the State Supreme Court will have the last word on that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: So, in the case of Florida, you`ve got the timeline here. I mean, this will get appealed. Again, it seems pretty crystal clear, right? Like if you take a majority, minority district of an African-American -- predominantly African-American district away in a state that has not seen a significant decline in that population, like, you`ve clearly done something that pretty violative of the basic thrust of what we have post-Voting Rights Act, you know, standards for how -- for how to redistrict.\par \par \uc2 I guess the question is, how much faith do you have in the state court in Florida to do the right thing here, given that the trial judge was also DeSantis appointed?\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Yes, so look. What the Florida constitution says is that you cannot reduce minority voting opportunities to elect. There`s no dispute on the facts. There was a district that minority voters could elect their candidate of choice in the Fifth Congressional District. That district has now by anyone`s analysis been taken away. That will no longer be a district where minorities can elect their candidate of choice.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats agree on that. The Republican legislature agrees on that. I don`t even think \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  disagrees on that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: So, the law clearly requires for that map to be struck down. Now, this will now go up to the Court of Appeals or directly to the state Supreme Court. And those courts will face the same choice with the trial court. Do you abide by the Florida constitution or do you ignore it?\par \par \uc2 And, you know, I oftentimes say to you and to others that democracy is on the docket, because we are ultimately left with the courts as the last bastion to preserve rights in a country where Republicans have become increasingly lawless.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All right, Marc Elias who`s working on this and other cases, thanks very much. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 ELIAS: Thank you, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Next, Democrats already facing an uphill battle in the Midterms. And with abortion rights at the very top of voters minds, why is the Speaker of the House doubling down on reelecting a Democrat who opposes abortion rights? That race after this.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Today, one day after Democrats lost a major vote to codify Roe versus Wade at the national level, with the majority on the Supreme Court apparently poised to overturn that landmark ruling, Speaker of the House \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is once again reiterating her support for the lone member of the House Democratic Caucus who opposes abortion rights. Congressman \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  representing the Rio Grande Valley of Texas, has a Democratic primary challenger named Jessica Cisneros. And Pelosi is on team Cuellar.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): I`m supporting \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 . He`s a valued member of our caucus. He has not pro-choice, but we did need him. We passed the bill with what we had. And by the way, this is one of the first congresses that we have had with a Democratic president with a pro-choice Congress.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now I have to say, there is a little bit of tension between the message elect more pro-choice Democrats to prevent overturning Roe v. Wade. And also, don`t worry about this anti-choice Democrat, we have enough votes in the House. And yet the entirety of House leadership is giving money to or actively campaigning for this longtime incumbent Democrat to fend off a progressive challenger, who I should add, is pro-choice.\par \par \uc2 Abby Livingston is the DC Bureau Chief for the Texas Tribune and she joins me now. Abby, this was sort of a little under the radar I think in national politics. I`ve been paying attention to it. But now, the Roe v Wade leak of the Dobbs decision really puts a spotlight on this. And Cuellar is the lone remaining member and he`s bringing up the big guns of all leadership is down there helping him out.\par \par \uc2 ABBY LIVINGSTON, D.C. BUREAU CHIEF, THE TEXAS TRIBUNE: Absolutely. This could not have happened at a worse time for Congressman Cuellar. That said, I`m not surprised Pelosi is standing by him. She stands behind her members for a very long time. It usually has to become like a criminal situation which this is involved in. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  has become involved in the context of this race, but he has -- his attorney has told me and other reporters that he`s been informed by the \uc2 FBI\uc2 , he`s not a target.\par \par \uc2 Pelosi cares about one thing, power. That`s the name of her memoir, Know Your Power. She wants that gavel. And in the past, she and other Democratic leaders have recruited pro-life candidates and they haven`t come -- and he was one of the few incumbents left who is pro-life.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right. But I mean, let`s talk about the power questions. The \uc2 FBI\uc2  raided quasars apartment or his house, right? Now, again, this seems like a not great thing to have hanging over a candidate, so even if you take away -- take away abortion, you`re just thinking like, well, who do we want to come out of this primary?\par \par \uc2 Cuellar`s representatives have said like he`s not a target, but that seems like not the thickest assurance. Do we know anything about what the deal was with the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raid of the incumbent Congressman`s house?\par \par \uc2 [20:45:30]\par \par \uc2 LIVINGSTON: I have never worked the phones harder on a story and I have nothing on this. It was this is the strangest race I`ve ever covered. And I`ve covered hundreds of congressional races. And that happened in January. And that, again, was at a really bad time. And there are serious questions in the legal community over the propriety of the Justice Department doing it at that time. The name \uc2 James Comey\uc2  has come up in this context.\par \par \uc2 And so it is strange, but it had kind of died down. And so -- and then the Roe v. Wade thing blew up. And so, this is an incredibly hard race for \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  to win. I wouldn`t count him out. He`s a very good politician, but it is -- he`s had the kitchen sink thrown at him by the gods.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes, we should -- Jessica Cisneros, his challenger, it`s the second time I think she`s challenging him. She`s young, progressive lawyer. She was on the "MEET THE PRESS," and she was describing the fact that it`s not just on abortion, right? I mean, he is a sort of Mansion-esque figure in the house caucus, I think it`s fair to say, to the right, the furthest right of the caucus on everything. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CISNEROS (D-TX), CONGRESSIONAL CANDIDATE: The last thing we want is to hold on to a slim Democratic majority and then have someone like \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  who`s going to keep siding with Republicans, not just on this issue, but he`s done it on things like the Pro Act. He`s voted to, you know, fund the border wall. I mean, has an A rating from the NRA.\par \par \uc2 There`s so many key issues where he`s always siding with Republicans. And he could become the Joe Manchin of the House. We don`t want \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  to be the deciding vote.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, the question, right, of course, is it`s hard to imagine a Democrat other than Joe Manchin winning West Virginia. What is the district, the new line of the district in the Rio Grande Valley look like in terms of how swinging it is?\par \par \uc2 LIVINGSTON: It`s pretty swinging. I was around some Democratic operatives last night. And that`s the first question they asked me is what I thought. This is could be a tough race no matter who wins it. David Wasserman of The Cook Political Report has pointed to Cuellar as the more electable one in the fall.\par \par \uc2 That`s an open discussion. I`ve talked to Jessica Cisneros and (AUDIO GAP). So, I think it`s very much up in the air and very unsteady going forward in South Texas.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. I would -- I would say like the lack of clarity of why the guy`s house was raided by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  does really cast a pall over the whole thing that. Like, if I were just backing a horse want to hedge a little bit, but hey, that`s just me. Abby Livingston, thank you so much for your time.\par \par \uc2 LIVINGSTON: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: You may or may not have noticed, but I`ve been out for a few days. A huge thanks to Ayman Mohyeldin and Alicia Menendez for stepping in to host the show because last week, after successfully dodging it for two plus years, I did test positive for COVID. I`m thankfully feeling, I think sounding much better now.\par \par \uc2 But there`s something I learned while I was sick I think everyone should know about. So, stay right there. We`re going to talk about it next.\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: In 1991, legendary basketball star Hall of Famer Magic Johnson was going to die, or at least that`s what everyone thought. On November 7 of that year, Magic announced that he had contracted HIV. It was an awful, tragic, painful revelation that stun the world.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TOM BROKAW, FORMER NIGHTLY NEWS ANCHOR: Good evening. The news is so shocking and so unexpected. It is difficult to absorb it even as we report it. Magic Johnson has tested positive for the HIV virus. That`s the first step to AIDS. Magic Johnson, a basketball superstar is so beloved, so well known. His first name was all he needed well beyond the world of sports. Tonight, Magic announced that he is retiring from basketball because of his condition.\par \par \uc2 MAGIC JOHNSON, FORMER NBA PLAYER: Because of the HIV virus that I have attained, I will have to retire from the Lakers today. I think sometimes we think well, only gay people can get it, only -- it was not going to happen to me. And here I am saying that it can happen to anybody, even me Magic Johnson, it can happen to you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: All around the country, people were scared and upset. It was all everyone talked about the next day. Nightly News highlighted an AIDS hotline that was inundated with worried calls.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: At aids hotlines in Los Angeles and elsewhere, the phones were ringing off the hook. People asking all sorts of questions about the disease.\par \par \uc2 RANDI SWINDELL, AIDS HOTLINE VOLUNTEER: People are frightened. People who previously thought they were not vulnerable to this realize that they are.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Now, at the time, contracting HIV was essentially a death sentence. There were some drugs available that could sometimes delay the progression of the disease, but there was no cure. And that was a reality that Magic Johnson faced in 1991. And he did return to the court playing at the 1992 Olympics and in 1985. He played one more season for the Lakers. He went on to tour with the All-Star team. He of course became an entrepreneur, launched a successful media career.\par \par \uc2 And along the way, Magic Johnson went from being a man facing certain death with a world was preemptively warning to just a normal guy, albeit a very, very wealthy and famous one because thanks to a lot of research and the development of new drugs, HIV became something you could live with.\par \par \uc2 We have not eradicated HIV and AIDS. We have converted it from a death sentence to a manageable virus. With the minification is now available, people who contract HIV can live full regular lives. And Magic Johnson was one of the first, maybe the first highly visible people who demonstrated that to the world.\par \par \uc2 Now, more than two years into the COVID pandemic, after a million deaths just here in the U.S., millions more around the world, I`ve been thinking about that example because I think it`s a way to start to see a world where we can live with this virus too.\par \par \uc2 Now, as you probably noticed, I`ve been out for a few days. I had COVID, which is not a certain death sentence by any means, obviously. I`m vaccinated and boosted. The first 36 hours or so, I was pretty knocked out. I felt like having the flu. But then my doctor prescribed me the antiviral drug you may have heard about called Paxlovid. And I got to say, my symptoms rapidly improved.\par \par \uc2 Now, when Paxlovid first became available at the end of last year, there`s very limited supply and the drug was reserved for patients at high risk which made complete sense for rationing a scarce resource. But as with the vaccines, the problem right now in this country isn`t supply, its demand. Paxlovid is wildly underutilized and is just sitting on shelves.\par \par \uc2 Now, we should say there are limitations. It`s authorized under emergency use authorization. It has to be taken within five days at the onset of symptoms in order to be effective. So, you got to get it just about as soon as you test positive. There have also been somewhat worrying recent cases of patients relapsing after completing their five day course, which obviously needs to be studied further.\par \par \uc2 Right now, doctors are still limited by the \uc2 FDA\uc2  regulations. The agency authorized Paxlovid for people at high risk due to certain health conditions, like heart disease, and obesity, although the list is quite long and largely covers a really big chunk of the population.\par \par \uc2 But the fact that there are millions of doses of this drug just sitting unused is kind of insane to me. I mean, this was the therapeutic that we`re kind of waiting for, at least the first generation of something really does work, a drug that when taken right away, can really minimize the symptoms of this virus.\par \par \uc2 And I can tell you, I took it, it made a huge difference for me. It shows a lot of promise as a way to dramatically reduce the danger and disruption of a virus that it seems is just going to keep circulating forever.\par \par \uc2 A new poll shows that less than a third of Americans are worried about getting COVID while cases are once again rising around the country. People are over it and I get that. Almost no one wants to talk about COVID anymore, certainly not politicians. Basically, no one wants to like close down bars, and things like mask requirements are either struck down by courts or just dropped.\par \par \uc2 But the virus is not gone and we have basically abandoned any and all public health measures, non-pharmaceutical interventions to stop it from circulating. Now, the good news is that we now live in a world in which the overwhelming majority of folks can at least theoretically, be vaccinated and boosted. And everyone who comes down with COVID, again, could theoretically get this antiviral drug right away, feel much better, much faster, dramatically reduce risk.\par \par \uc2 If this were the reality, near 100 percent boosting rate, and universal automatic access to antiviral therapeutics, it would massively improve the situation for all of us. And again, it`s not unlike what happened with HIV. But for years, a cure seemed practically impossible. But slowly, with a lot of very hard work, we develop therapeutics that have made it completely manageable.\par \par \uc2 If treated properly, patients can be asymptomatic. And there was no like magic cure announced from one day to the next. It wasn`t Jonas Salk with polio. But even as the drugs were developed, there was also a multi decade fight to get those drugs to be made cheaply enough to be accessible to the world`s poorest people.\par \par \uc2 But today, we even have a highly effective medicine to prevent people from getting HIV in the first place. There are commercials for it all over TV. It`s as commonplace and as accessible as statins to lower your cholesterol.\par \par \uc2 Effective COVID treatments like Paxlovid at least now appears to be should be the same, right? I mean, the early days of HIV and AIDS, it was all focused on non-pharmaceutical interventions, safe sex to stop the transmission. We then develop tools to deal with it in a medical and pharmaceutical sense, right? So, we`ve got COVID now. We should be continuing efforts to develop more treatments and even better vaccines, and to keep educating the public about the importance of boosters and vaccination and the availability of Paxlovid.\par \par \uc2 But that`s not what we`re doing. Instead, Congress just put COVID funding on the backburner cutting it out of the package with funding for Ukraine. In Missouri, the legislature there recently passed a bill, and I`m not making this up, a bill making it illegal for pharmacists to tell patients that ivermectin and hydroxychloroquine are not effective treatments for COVID even though that has been shown by multiple studies.\par \par \uc2 So, if we`re going to let COVID circulate relatively unchecked, which appears to be the current policy consensus, well, then for the love of God, we need to do everything we can to use the tools that we have, which we have, we have developed, to reduce the risk and disruption it poses. That means really putting our back into the effort to develop the next generation of vaccines and drugs and getting once we have like Paxlovid to everyone who needs and wants it.\par \par \uc2 It is an abomination if Congress, particularly the Republicans in Congress who are balking at this, walk away from that basic duty.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "MSNBC PRIME" starts now with Ali Velshi. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220513ei5c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 12, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Ellison Barber, Ali Vitali, Courtney Kube, Carol Lee,  James Stavridis \par \uc2 9,612 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Thursday. Paying at the pump, rising food costs, inflation above 8 percent, market turmoil, rising interest rates, and recession fears. We are entering some uncharted economic waters on the most important issue to voters.\par \par \uc2 Plus, day 78 of the war in Ukraine still with no end in sight, as Russia threatens retaliatory action if Finland joins \uc2 NATO\uc2 , and Zelenskyy claims to make battleground games in the north.\par \par \uc2 And what Democrats plan to do next to tackle the abortion issue as a bill to codify Roe v. Wade fails in the Senate. I`ll speak with one of the co- authors of that legislation still ahead.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd. Back from our midterm trip to Pennsylvania. And we`ll be digging more into the midterms a little bit later. But we`re going to begin today`s program with the single most important issue for voters, obviously, the economy, which is rapidly entering some uncharted waters, with costs continuing to rise, gas prices at an all-time high, a hot war in Eastern Europe, and a whole lot of market uncertainty as policymakers try to cool down demand without tipping the U.S. into a painful recession.\par \par \uc2 The issue obviously brings with it enormous political fallout here at home, that`s obvious, could also rattle the West`s solidarity to counter Russia abroad. That`s of concern. And it`s why we start the show today with this question, though, what the heck is going on with this economy? And what should our leaders be doing about it? And how fearful should we be that we don`t know what the bottom looks like yet?\par \par \uc2 So let`s start with the numbers. It`s no surprise to anyone who`s been at a gas station or grocery store recently, that you`re paying more for the essentials, meat, eggs, and fuel, those rising costs are inescapable to anyone right now in America. AAA says that the average price of a gallon of gas right now in the United States is $4.42 for regular and $5.56 for diesel, both are record highs.\par \par \uc2 I can tell you in northeastern Pennsylvania, diesel was $6.50. It`s all up -- that`s up 40 and 50 percent -- 50 cents, respectively from just last month. When the price of gas and diesel goes up, it puts a pinch on everything, manufacturers, consumers, you, and the people you work for.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the pain out there, there`s a severe baby formula shortage hitting the country right now with shelves empty in stores. That is more about how those companies manage their supply, more so than it is anything else. But it only adds to the consternation that people feel right now about the economy. The president is expected to announce some action on the baby formula supply emergency later today.\par \par \uc2 President Biden said earlier this week that inflation is his top domestic priority. But what does that mean? The administration points to actions it is taking too like tapping the Strategic Petroleum Reserve and expanding the use of ethanol as a -- and fuel as a way to try to ease some pricing. But the reality is that so many of the forces fueling this issue are beyond the \uc2 White\uc2  House`s control. And Biden`s top economic advisors suggested that a lot of this price pay could be around for a while.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SARA EISEN, CNBC ANCHOR: Where do you think CPI is in a year from now?\par \par \uc2 CECILIA ROUSE, CHAIR, COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS: Well, that is -- you know, it`s a tough call. There`s a lot of uncertainty, but forecasters really do expect that prices will moderate over the coming year. So I expect it to be lower but you know, it`s not necessarily going to be linear. We can`t just focus month to month and we understand there are headwinds. But I do think it will be -- it will be lower than it is today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And folks, what makes our current situation so unique right now, is that all of this concern and uncertainty is coming amid strong job growth and low unemployment. That`s what makes us so weird. Wall Street likes to look for green shoots, but right now, even they aren`t finding much or they don`t like the green shoots that they see as economists, employers, workers, consumers, policymakers and elected leaders try to figure out what happens next and as President Biden told donors last night, inflation is going to scare the living hell out of everybody. I don`t know if it`s going to scare I would argue it is scary.\par \par \uc2 Ellison Barber is in Gainesville, Georgia. She`s taking a look at the human toll of these rising prices. We`re also got a pair of an economist with us. Joining me on set is Douglas Holtz-Eakin, the president of the American Action Forum, and a former CBO director under -- during the Bush years, CBO, of course, \uc2 Congressional Budget Office\uc2 , and \uc2 Betsey Stevenson\uc2 , a former labor department chief economist under President Obama, now, a professor at the \uc2 University of Michigan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But Ellison, I want to start with you. And again, you know, this is not about covering markets. This is about the day-to-day lives, people are living. You`re in Georgia -- this you know, this is not a lot of public transportation to mitigate fuel costs. What are you seeing and hearing?\par \par \uc2 ELLISON BARBER, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: People are frustrated. People are worried. We have been to three different counties, all of them politically important and very different politically. When we ask people who or what they think is to blame for the prices that they`re seeing at the gas pump and also in the grocery store, they gave various answers to that question.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 BARBER: But everyone we spoke to, they said the prices that they are seeing right now, especially at gas stations like this one, they say it is ridiculous. And for some of them, it is up-ending their budgets. Listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: Do you have an option other than driving?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN WHALEY, GWINNETT COUNTY, GEORGIA RESIDENT: I don`t. I do not. It`s my only transportation and I`ve got a little one, so it`s like I feel like all my gas is going -- or all my money is going towards gas at this point.\par \par \uc2 BARBER: How much of your salary does it end up cutting into?\par \par \uc2 JOSH, DEKALB COUNTY, GEORGIA RESIDENT: A lot of -- I think my groceries, got cheese, got everything that goes go in with this, so it is kind of hit me like crazy.\par \par \uc2 ALEX SPEIR, HALL COUNTY, GEORGIA RESIDENT: I work at the clinic down the road, and a lot of patients have come in and complained about it. How -- I`ve had so many people have to cancel their appointments because they can`t afford gas to come, get to their appointments.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARBER: I am a Georgia girl, I grew up in Atlanta. My parents live a little over an hour away from here in a more rural part of the state, and they don`t have a lot of doctors where they live. When they need to go to the doctors, they have to come here to Gainesville. It`s an hour and 15-minute drive each way. That is not an uncommon story. And rural communities around here, they`ve seen a lot of jobs go over the years, people are having to look further for work, they have to drive more to get to work.\par \par \uc2 And you touched on it, there`s not a lot of public transportation options here. There`s some in Atlanta, but it`s not that great. They`re in rural areas, it really doesn`t exist. And what people are telling us is they`re looking at what they have to pay to fill up their car to get to and from work, run basic errands, take their kids to school.\par \par \uc2 Then, they`re looking at their paycheck and they say that they really feel like they are having trouble affording the basic necessities. It is costing them so much money just to get to work. This is a real pain for a lot of people here in the big cities, in the suburbs, and especially in rural parts of Georgia, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ellison, I`m so glad you shared exactly how a lot of people outside the acylic corridor (PH). I really am concerned that people that mostly live in New York or the DC area just don`t appreciate how regressive rising fuel of a -- regressive tax rising fuel prices are. Ellison, terrific on the ground, people-focus reporting there.\par \par \uc2 So let me bring in my two economists here. Douglas Holtz-Eakin, good to see you here on set.\par \par \uc2 DOUGLAS HOLTZ-EAKIN, FORMER CBO DIRECTOR UNDER PRESIDENT BUSH: Good to see you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Betsey Stevenson\uc2 , you`re on satellites, I`ll let you go first with the first comment here. And it`s -- this is the conundrum is that a rising fuel price hits basically those with the fewest -- the lowest savings the most and hits them the hardest, it`s working people, may have to drive to two jobs. Working people have to drive farther. This is what I think makes this feel like such a rough period of time that we`re entering.\par \par \uc2 BETSEY, STEVENSON, PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF MICHIGAN: People are definitely frustrated from rising gas prices. And I mean, that`s because they see it every single day as they go to the pump and they fill up. So, you know, I know this is hitting everybody in their wallets in a way that`s making them unhappy, scared, and forcing them to cut back in other areas.\par \par \uc2 But I do think it`s worth pointing out this. The inflation we`re facing more broadly is not being driven by rising fuel prices, not being driven by this rising gas price, and unfortunately, this -- what`s happening at the pump has everything to do with Putin and it has everything to do with the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 I think the broader concerns about the economy is the inflation we`re seeing in other places and whether we`re seeing any kind of receipt -- you know, any kind of pullback in that. You know, we see obviously rent is going up. Rent is going up because the value of people`s homes are going up, they`re able to charge more and rent, and that`s going to continue to spill through these numbers as people sign new leases.\par \par \uc2 But one of the reasons why inflation is so awful is because it is very uneven. It hits some people harder than others. If you own your own house, inflation is not getting to you as much because you`re not paying higher rent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Correct.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: If you just signed a new lease, inflation really put a damper on what you can spend. Just like if you`re someone who drives two hours to work every day back and forth, inflation is hitting you a lot harder than somebody who can walk down the road to work.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And this gets at the class dividedness, right? Basically, those who can afford to live close, those -- and this is why I go to this Washington, New York Nexus here. I think, truly, this is a uniquely out-of-touch situation here for the acylic corridor.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: And it`s way worse than the numbers would suggest. If you look at the food, energy, and housing, that -- what you think of as the essentials, that`s 50 percent of the typical family budget. The average inflation rate and an annual rate since the president took office is 19.8 percent. That`s huge. And that`s what people are going through every day, all across this country. And so, obviously, they`re scared about the future. They want to know what`s going to happen next.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s what -- my question to both of you and Doug, I`ll start with you.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do we stall low unemployment? I mean at some point, does business pull back -- you know I think the next thing we expect is that OK, inflation means the business will pull back some investment that usually also translates into hiring fewer people.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: So, as it turns out, we have an incredibly strong labor market, low unemployment you know, 1.7 job openings for everybody out there who`s looking for a job. And that`s the flip side of the same coin is having the inflationary pressures. This economy is running so hot, and the labor market is so hot, that it`s generating inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 So, we got that, in part, because we did big stimulus in an already hot economy, did that 1951, we`ve got a six percentage point increases inflation, did it again last year. So can`t do that. And the key figure is \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , the chairman of the Fed whose job it is to tamp down on that red hot economy. That means slowing down the labor market. That means slowing down the housing market. But it also means we`re leaving the inflationary pressures. He`s got no good choices.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: You got inflation, or you got to slow things down a bit. And he`s trying to do with a latter in a way that doesn`t tip us into recession.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Betsey, how do we avoid a recession?\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: I think you avoid a recession by making sure that the Fed doesn`t act too quickly, doesn`t -- you know, as we think about slam on the brakes, they need to ease on the brakes, and we`re seeing them do that by right -- raising rates slowly. You know, there`s -- when you think about what we need to happen, we actually need more people to apply for jobs.\par \par \uc2 The prop -- the problem that`s causing high inflation is we can`t provide all the goods and services that people want. As a result, prices are getting pushed up. So what the Feds going to do is try to reduce demand. What the -- you know, there`s pressure on the government to do is reduce demand, by a sort of, you know, we`ve already seen them pull away from the support they were giving to families, so pulling away from that income, reducing demand.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: And that feels really counterintuitive to people because what they actually want is they want to see supply increase. They want to be able to buy -- you know, they want to be able to buy as much gas as they`re buying. They don`t want for everybody to pull back on buying gas, and that, to me, is what drives the price down. But at the end of the day, that`s what the Fed is going to try to do. It raises rates. Tried to convince people to buy fewer cars. They buy fewer cars. That tried to slow the rate of increase in the price of cars, and it lowers the demand for gas.\par \par \uc2 So that`s the process. And the question will be how quickly it happens versus how slowly whether or not they can realize, hey, we`ve slowed demand enough that we don`t need so far that would put us into a recession. I think the real uncertainty is that there`s -- problems all around the globe and they`re having to steer through, you know, really turbulent times with what`s happening in Europe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, and look, the two of you are not diplomats. I mean, you know, the fuel -- meaning the fuel -- the fuel issue could be solved by Saudi Arabia But that`s a -- that`s a whole another ball of -- a ball of wax that I want to get you. And Doug, do you want to jump in?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes, I think a theme underneath both our comments is that in the absence of a mistake, we`re not going to have a recession. I think the recession talk is way too big right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what is this going to look like?\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me -- tell me what the bottom looks like here? And is -- what are we seeing in the market -- as the markets so drop a little bit of a crypto thing and a little bit of a bubble issue on top of inflation?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Lesson number one, do not evaluate the economy based on the stock market.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Of course.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Never do that. Mark has been terrible recently.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: No question about that. Why? It was probably too high, to begin with. A lot of -- a lot of cash flowed into the financial markets, both because the Fed printed $5 trillion in the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Put it in financial markets. And because we send out checks and people got \uc2 Robinhood\uc2  apps and (INAUDIBLE) crypto is cool, and they do look game stop on the side. And now that`s going away, rates are going up and they`re coming back to reality. I`m not worried about the stock market. It always moves first and anticipates what`s going to happen down the line. It`s readjusting to the new reality. That`ll be fun.\par \par \uc2 I think the economy itself is going to grow solidly this year, solidly next year in the absence of something we don`t know about. And then there are only three names to worry about, Powell, and we pray that he does a good job, and land softly, Putin, and G. And I think the underappreciated part of this is China, which is a realist --\par \par \uc2 TODD: I agree that`s the least. Betsey, talk to the 60-something-year-old that was contemplating retirement and they`re looking at their 401k going oh, Jesus, I guess not this year.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: Well, you know, I think I would say take a longer-term look at it because boy, you`re probably in a better position today than you thought you were going to be five years ago. So --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You could say?\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: Let`s try to take a glass half full instead of a glass half empty look at this. So I think that`s part of what I would have said to them. What were your retirement plans a couple of years ago, because you haven`t lost everything? It`s just not where he thought it was going to be a couple of months ago, or six months ago. And I think the other thing to think about is to look at the other assets you own. Do you own a house? Because the house went -- price went up a lot.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 STEVENSON: So you`ve probably seen your portfolio is not doing as bad as you might think it`s doing. And I think if you`re 60 years old, you`re probably on track to retire when you want to, which is hopefully not too soon because we actually want to see labor force participation of people in their early 60s, you got a lot of live-in love to do, and I hope you`re really successful in your job for at least another five years, if not longer.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Doug, you know, I want to bring up the baby formula issue because that appears to be more of a -- of an issue of the just-in-time supply chain approach to economies.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I think this is a question that I wonder. Does the pandemic make a bunch of industries rethink how they do this? And, and maybe with baby formula, is it an argument that the government says hey, you got to have a -- this is not something we can -- we can have sort of go up and down based on, you know, one disruption screwing up everything here?\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: So point number one is, I think they`ve already rethought a lot of this just in time, particularly across the global supply chain.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: And that`s a business risk, it costs them a lot of money, they have every incentive to fix it themselves and certainly, with the baby formula, that`s a combination of an outage at an important supplier.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: And then the -- and the no inventories. We don`t have inventory.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that`s the point, it`s the no inventory approach to business.\par \par \uc2 HOLTZ-EAKIN: People are doing whole more inventories because they can find out the business requires it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Douglas Holtz-Eakin and \uc2 Betsey Stevenson\uc2 , thank you both, really appreciate it. I got -- sorry about that. I got some breaking news we got to get to from Capitol Hill because the January 6 committee is out. They`ve been obviously investigating the insurrection. They just issued subpoenas to five sitting members of Congress, including the House Republican leader, Kevin McCarthy.\par \par \uc2 This sets up quite the legal showdown here. So let`s bring in our Capitol Hill Correspondent Ali Vitali. So, Ali, there has been a debate for months in this January 6 committee whether or not to issue formal subpoenas to sitting members of Congress because it`s not clear they`ll ever be able to make them stick. But here we are.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Chairman Thompson has said in the past too that he doesn`t know if they`re actually able to do this, but nevertheless, the committee ultimately decided after several months of deliberation here to subpoena five House Republican lawmakers, including the top Republican Kevin McCarthy. You see these other members on the screen, Scott Perry, Jim Jordan, Andy Biggs, and Mo Brooks, all allies of former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And in the press release that we just got from Chairman \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2 . He says that these colleagues have brought information relevant to our investigation into the attack on January 6. He goes on to say before we hold our hearings next month, we wish to provide members the opportunity to discuss these matters voluntarily, they have issued letters asking all of these lawmakers to come before them and cooperate.\par \par \uc2 All of them have said no, some of them in the case of Mo Brooks have even preemptively said that they would say and fight a subpoena so we`re about to get into that legal period. But Thompson goes on to say that they urge their colleagues to comply with the law, do their patriotic duty, and cooperate with our investigations as hundreds of other witnesses have done.\par \par \uc2 And I thought it was striking these letters are in some cases pretty short. But the one to McCarthy just says we are left with no choice but to issue you a subpoena. So this tees up a huge legal battle, Chuck, the first time that this committee has actually issued subpoenas to their colleagues, and a rare, rare move here. We can`t think of anything in recent memory of another time that a House committee has subpoenaed House lawmakers, but here we are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali, this almost feels like a bit of a, surrendering in this respect. There`s no other way they think they`re going to get cooperation so they figure all right, let`s try the subpoena route because it -- I feel like everybody had explained to me that they thought the subpoena route would actually delay their ability to do the investigation, and perhaps run out the clock if control of the House of Representatives changes.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So is this an acknowledgment that there`s really -- they`re no, they`re not going to get cooperation so they`re just going to make this hurt?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: I think it`s an acknowledgment that they need to do this because they`ve got nowhere left to go if they want the cooperation here. Perhaps the headline of the subpoena the fact that this is so unprecedented or with very few precedents in this body, I think that is the point here because you and I both know, they are up against the clock on the midterms.\par \par \uc2 If Republicans were to retake the House, this committee goes away. But more than that, as soon as things reach the courts, they tend to slow down or just grind to a halt. Think of all of those criminal contempt referrals that are just sitting waiting to be taken up by the DOJ.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Once things get to the courts, that`s where the process gets really gummy, really slow, and I think that the committee was well aware of that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say do they have any sort of plan to expedite these court -- these court hearings?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes. Look, we just got this release. So if it`s in there, and I missed it on my first very, very quick scan of this letter, then I haven`t seen it.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But at the same time, you got to imagine that is where their heads are out here on the committee. They`re four weeks away from the first rounds of public hearings. This is something Chairman Thompson previewed to us that they were going to be reissuing letters in some cases to these lawmakers. Certainly, the question of the subpoena has been up in the air. But there are also other letters that were waiting for them to issue too. Chairman Thompson who told me a few weeks ago that they wanted to talk to Republican senators, we haven`t seen those letters yet, either.\par \par \uc2 And so while they`re still trying to make this the most compelling case to get these people to testify, now, actually issuing subpoenas, there`s also still other things that we`re waiting for them to put out there because they`ve told us that those are avenues that they want to explore. And again, for weeks until that first public hearing, they wanted to have their fact-finding done before that, and obviously now that we`re entering the legal arena, that`s going to be really hard.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, there`s three Republican senators today, Tommy Tuberville, we now got a call --\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Either that was by accident. Mike Lee, and obviously, \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  had a lot of --\par \par \uc2 VITALI: I have to be specific.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We now know of conversation. So there`s at least three.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali Vitali on Capitol Hill, thank you. Well, obviously keep an eye on the story as it develops. And as we figure out the legal fight, and what that looks like, it`s going to be up ahead.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, internationally, Russia is threatening retaliation as Finland look gets ever closer to joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  without delay and Zelenskyy claimed some battleground gains in northeast Ukraine, while Russia claims gains of its own in the east and the south. We`re going to get the latest on the fight in Eastern Europe next.\par \par \uc2 And later, President Biden hosted Leadership Summit without Southeast Asian nations at the White House as tensions intensify in the Asia Pacific region with China and the question about Taiwan. We`re actually going to take you inside something that`s never been seen on camera. It`s a full-day war game that imagines how the United States would react to war with China. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As the war in Ukraine now enters its 12th week, we`re seeing signs that Russia`s military may be weakening in some places just as \uc2 NATO\uc2  appears to be strengthening.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Finland`s leaders announced today that their country which shares an 800-mile border with Russia, plans on joining \uc2 NATO\uc2  without delay and Sweden is signaling that it plans on joining the alliance as well. Moscow says Finland now poses a threat to Russia and is vowing to retaliate. Although it`s still not clear how that would happen.\par \par \uc2 They`ve been threatened retaliation ever since Finland opened the door to \uc2 NATO\uc2  and Finland hasn`t blinked for what it`s worth. All of this comes as the UK`s Defense Minister Ministry tells us that Ukrainian forces have reclaimed some of the areas north of Kharkiv with Russian forces retreating east.\par \par \uc2 Pentagon officials say Russian forces aren`t even making significant progress in their offensive in eastern Ukraine. But despite those setbacks, officials and Mariupol say Russia continues attacking the steel plant where the last remaining Ukrainian troops in that city remain holed up.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in Courtney Kube. She covers Pentagon for us and she joins me now. Courtney, I wanted a sort of -- I`m a bit confused on the status of Russia`s forces. I understand, on one hand, there`s some success in reclaiming parts of the north but Russia has been in retreat to the south and the east. And it appears that they actually are making it slow, but they are making gains here. And perhaps it`s -- we`re trying to keep hope alive by talking about the North. Is that what`s going on.\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, MSNBC PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: Yes. So there are some areas where they`re making some progress. There`s a little city called Popasna, it`s in the east, they have taken that in the last 48, 72 hours. They are also pushing, as you said very, very slowly. Kharkiv is getting a lot of attention because that`s a city that was under dramatic assault for weeks and weeks and weeks.\par \par \uc2 And what`s critical here is that Ukraine has been able to push the Russians far enough back that for most parts of the city are now outside of artillery range. Artillery has been sort of the big weapon that we`ve seen in the East recently, largely because the Russians can`t fly but -- it`s a contested airspace. But being outside of artillery range really makes that city much safer and it`s significant for them.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the map that we`re showing right now, there`s a town called Izyum that you see there. That is an area where the Russians were trying to sort of have three lines of axes or lines of assault out of there. That`s where they have made a little bit of progress.\par \par \uc2 Again, it`s very slow, it`s definitely much, much slower than the Russians are believed to have thought they would be able to push forward towards the Donbass area, but it`s not insignificant. You know, if you consider that the Russians really are in this for months and months and months now, you know, they could make some actual progress towards the Donbass on its offensive.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, a constant concern that`s underneath this war has always been poking Putin, you know. There`s always been a concern, be careful poking him too much, whether it`s President Biden saying he had to go, things like this. There`s no bigger poke at Putin than Finland joining \uc2 NATO\uc2 , right? I mean, to me, this is, forget anything else. How seriously, is the Pentagon taking some of these threats now coming from Russia?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: So, as of now, the -- of course, the two big things that they`re watching that they`re the most concerned about are the use of some sort of a tactical nuclear weapon. As of now, they don`t have any indications that Russia is planning that or that`s really could work.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Medvedev`s throw it out there again, today.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: I know. I -- but I --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They keep saying it.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Exactly. They talk about it, but they aren`t seeing any movement of any kind of capabilities into the region. So that -- but that`s something that they are watching very closely. Chem bio is another one very concerned about it, still no indications that they`re planning on it.\par \par \uc2 I think the biggest concern, though, is any chance that Putin may decide to branch outside of Ukraine`s borders. And according to the officials I`m speaking with, there still are no indications that they want to do that. They`re still in -- and actually even Secretary Austin said this yesterday he doesn`t think they want to go to war with that with \uc2 NATO\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TODD: They don`t have the military capability to take on Finland`s.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Military a little of \uc2 NATO\uc2 , correct?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Right. Finland and Sweden you know, there`s a relatively small militaries when you look at, especially considering some of the other \uc2 NATO\uc2  members, but they are very small -- they are -- they are very adept, and they`re strong, and they`re well trained. And they`ve been training with \uc2 NATO\uc2  partners for a long time. So will --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, this is going to be an easy transition to that, you know.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Absolutely. They`ll fold right in. They have strong air capabilities and very strong naval capabilities.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are the concerns that they -- should Finland -- Finns be worried about cyber-attacks? I mean is that the type of realistic way that Russia is probably going to try to retaliate?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Cyber, I think is a big, one more information operations. So will that have a real impact on them? It`s not the -- it`s the kind of thing has been --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They have trending West for a while, though haven`t they?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Exactly. And it`s -- and they -- and look, they`re on the border, you said it yourself. They know -- they know what they`re going to be facing, they`ll be ready. It`s the kind of threats that they`ve been facing from Russia for years now, anyway, so I don`t have any sense that, according to the officials I`m speaking with, that they`re worried about this sort of tipping some sort of a tripwire. That it`s going to make Russia lash out with these decisions. But the reality is Putin is going to be pretty upset by this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is the biggest expansion of \uc2 NATO\uc2  that you can add, you know, since the initial expansion (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 KUBE: In the symbolism of Finland joining? Enormous.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Symbolism -- of all -- of all countries. It`s unbelievable. Courtney, good to see you.\par \par \uc2 KUBE: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thank you. Coming up, war games, serious ones. How the U.S. is gaming out its role in a potential conflict with China over Taiwan, and why the strategy is a lot more complex with potentially higher stakes than what we`re seeing in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As the White House continues its focus on providing Ukraine would the support it needs in its war against Russia, President Biden will turn its attention to another region today with his own security concerns. It`s Southeast Asia. The president will host Southeast Asian leaders at the White House at the U.S. seeks to assure our allies in that region that it is committed to their security amid a looming threat from China.\par \par \uc2 The timing of this meeting happens to come as our latest episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS" takes you inside something that`s never been seen on camera, a full-day war game exercise that imagines how America would react to a Chinese attempt at essentially invading and taking Taiwan. Here is a sneak peek.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: As you can see here on the map is a very large concentration of Chinese People`s Liberation Army forces at potential ports of demarcation for an invasion.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: We want to focus on a last-ditch effort to deter. This is a time to be sending the strongest possible message to Beijing both privately and publicly that there will be very severe costs if they actually go through with this.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: China has sort of seen our reaction to Ukraine, and we want to make sure that we are surprising them with how we react here.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:04]\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Hit the Americans as hard as we possibly can in the Western Pacific. Keep them out of the fight while we move on Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I would support early knockout punches that`s strong. I think we start very forcefully with the missile bombardment on Taiwan. I think we ought to bring the military to the mix.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, Stacie, high-level here. What just happened with move one?\par \par \uc2 STACIE PETTYJOHN, CNAS DIRECTOR OF DEFENSE PROGRAM: China has invaded Taiwan and began by attacking Taiwan`s outlying islands near the mainland, then it followed it with a large air and missile strike on Taiwan and on U.S. bases in Japan and on U.S. bases in Guam and the Northern Marianas. In response to that the United States followed up with bomber attacks on U.S. -- on Chinese ships and ports, and there was an air battle over Taiwan where American aircraft flying from the Philippines came in and engaged in combat with Chinese aircraft that were trying to bomb Taiwan.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So after move one, can you assess which team is winning?\par \par \uc2 PETTYJOHN: I think it`s a stalemate right now. China has strategically blundered by pulling Japan in, and the United States is still well- positioned to defend, but China also has a lot of its assets left and has a lot of power that it can apply.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me now on set is my colleague, NBC News correspondent Carol Lee. Also with me is Admiral \uc2 James Stavridis\uc2 , former Supreme Allied commander in \uc2 NATO\uc2  now an NBC News chief international security and diplomacy analyst and the man who helped us put together this partnership with CNAS.\par \par \uc2 Carol, take us into the years 2027. There`s a bit of a pretext that got us to the point where China and the United States ended up engaging.\par \par \uc2 CAROL LEE, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, exactly, and Chuck, the thing that was so striking about the war game was how significantly it escalated so quickly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So fast. This is over about a seven-day period. Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Over seven-day period. And the way in which that each team decided to approach, as you heard there, Michele Flournoy talking about deterrence and sending the strongest message possible. None of that worked with Ukraine and Russia, and it also didn`t work here, and the parallels that are there between the battle in Ukraine and the war game on Taiwan are really striking.\par \par \uc2 And so I think it was a really interesting exercise in the sense that it showed that it was -- even a weekend it would be a stalemate. How deeply the U.S. could get drawn into something like this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LEE: With direct conflict with China, and just in the region, it would be - - I mean, talk about World War III so that was fascinating.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It was. You know, Admiral Stavridis, the big lesson I took out of it and the big lesson -- we have two members of Congress, Democrat Mikie Sherrill of New Jersey, Republican \uc2 Mike Gallagher\uc2  of Wisconsin, both in the Armed Services, both really -- both veterans, so they really understood the concept, but they were struck, and I was struck by, boy, this would be a lot harder than rearming Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 This is a lot harder when you don`t have a \uc2 NATO\uc2  in Asia. That was the big takeaway I had from this. What have you learned from these exercises over the years?\par \par \uc2 ADM\uc2 . \uc2 JAMES STAVRIDIS\uc2 , NBC NEWS CHIEF INTERNATIONAL SECURITY ANALYST: Yes, and I`ve done many of them, Chuck, as you can imagine. This is something the U.S. Indo Pacific Command does constantly. Always updating, and Carol Lee is exactly right, which is currently drawing the lessons out of the Ukrainian conflict.\par \par \uc2 Two points really jumped out at me. One is the technical level of this, that you`re going to see as opposed to Ukraine. In other words, this is going to be maritime, air on air. It`s going to be cyber going in the background, which is certainly part of the conflict, so it`s going to be a very technological play.\par \par \uc2 And then secondly, Chuck, it`s leadership that`s going to matter here, and that`s the hard one to assess. Just as we blew it on Afghanistan, taking the Afghans would stand fight and we blew it on Ukraine, thinking the Ukrainians were going to fold, we`ve got to get this one right, and so I think a subtext for me that I am looking at is what is the fighting spirit and the will of the Taiwanese? That`s going to be quite determinative here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Here`s the thing, if I were to say the biggest question I have in my head through this whole exercise is, I think, and the hardest part about this exercise is putting ourselves into the Chinese ring, OK? The strategic mindset of the Chinese.\par \par \uc2 Look, this was a highly experienced Chinese experts that we had running the red team. But ultimately, you know, there is still a western bias that is in any western educated person`s head here, and I think that`s the hard part. We assume that China would be, OK, this is how we`re going to keep Japan out, and of course, we`re like all that did was draw Japan in.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:04]\par \par \uc2 But I imagine that`s the hardest part here is what is in Xi`s head and what do we know about the Chinese military`s?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: Let`s start with President Xi himself. He is a patient figure, and that is also of a piece with China in general. As opposed to that kind of impulsive activity that you see out of a \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 , the Chinese are patient, and Sun Tzu is their greatest military strategist, and pulling out his very slim but brilliant volume, "The Art of War," is well worth doing for anybody who wants to understand the mind of the Chinese military.\par \par \uc2 What I will say, Chuck, is if you dive into Sun Tzu, it`s all about deception. The best battle is the one you never actually fight because you`d surprised your enemy, but Sun Tzu also says -- and it`s worth knowing this -- went on dead ground fight. And that I think is something that has shot through China`s thinking about military.\par \par \uc2 I thought the first move, as you gave us a snapshot there, made a lot of sense with the one exception. My eyebrows went up significantly with China immediately striking those bases and instantly drawing the United States into war. I think they might take a more patient approach , but if you want to kick off a war game and move it fast, there`s nothing like a strike.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I`ll tell you this. The one other thing, Carol, I came away with here is, number one, water, it`s just harder.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That 90 miles may only be 90 miles.\par \par \uc2 LEE: That`s a big difference.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a big difference. But there is something else here, and I`m curious if you`ve gotten this in any of your reporting. Both Gallagher and Sherrill seemed to indicate to me, going, hey, we`d like to be more involved in things like this. This seemed to be an exercise that a lot of times the Pentagon will do these exercises and then will report to Congress what they need, and it`s, you know, like no, no, no. Us participating it`s a lot easier to understand what you need here.\par \par \uc2 It was a sense of like maybe the Pentagon has been going about this all wrong, they need to loop members in on how this works.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yes, open it up and --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Well, because a lot of what they do is going to depend on whether or not they have support for Congress. One of the things -- I mean, you heard the director of National Intelligence Avril Haines this week saying that China wants to take Taiwan or trying to do it by coercion, but they are determined and will do it by force if they needed to, and so, part of what the inclusions were from the war game was, should we be arming Taiwan in a different way?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LEE\uc2 : Should we be more involved? And those are questions that you have to deal with Congress for, and so bringing them in could be a little bit of a game changer.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, Admiral, let`s bring it to the real world right now. President Biden is with some Southeast Asian leaders. Here`s one of the big just sort of practical takeaways. Yes, we have Guam, yes we have Japan, yes, we have a base now in Australia. I was there for the opening up of it, when President Obama went there, and he realized how important that was.\par \par \uc2 Strategically, what allies would you like to see the United States have in Southeast Asia that could help protect the Taiwan?\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: I`m going to add one who is a strong ally, and I think we can depend on, and that`s of course Singapore, and as was indicated, Japan is very strong, and South Korea is very strong. So if you really look around the circuit, we`re missing one slot, and it`s the Philippine Islands, and don`t forget we had wonderful bases there. We fought hard there in World War II. We had Subic Bay, Clark Air Force Base. That, in many ways, was the centerpiece of our Western Pacific strategy, so if we could possibly get them back on board, we`ve had a very rough run, but they`ve just had an election. That would be the one I would like to add.\par \par \uc2 Final thoughts, Chuck, Vietnam is no friend of China.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No.\par \par \uc2 STAVRIDIS: That`s another distinct possibility. Cam Ranh Bay is one of the best natural harbors on the South China Sea, so there`s two to work on, I would say.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I guess, look. People in the 1940s probably did not expect us to be so close of allies with Japan. Perhaps people would be shocked if we started basing in Vietnam.\par \par \uc2 Anyway, Admiral Stavridis, Carol Lee, thank you both. Really proud of this episode, folks, and Jim, you were terrific in making this happen as well.\par \par \uc2 Be sure to check our entire episode of "MEET THE PRESS REPORTS." It streams tonight for the first time at 10:30 Eastern. That`s on NBC News Now. NBC News is home for news, and will be available tomorrow on demand in Peacock.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, a symbolic abortion bill fails in the Senate. What do Democrats do next? Democrat of Wisconsin, Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2  joins me ahead.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:47:58]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. It has been a busy day on Capitol Hill, as we broke just a few moments ago the January 6th Committee has subpoenaed five members of Congress, including the top House Republican, Kevin McCarthy, in its investigation. It comes as the committee says all of those members declined voluntary cooperation with the committee.\par \par \uc2 In addition, amid calls from their base to protect abortion rights, Democrats failed to secure the votes necessary yesterday to advance a bill that would ensure abortion access nationwide. The Women`s Health Protection Act would`ve insured abortion beyond what is protected by Roe v. Wade right now, invalidating some state regulations on abortion before 24 weeks. The vote failed 49-51 as Democratic Senator Joe Manchin joined Republicans against the bill.\par \par \uc2 Democrats also failed to convince two self-described pro-choice Republican senators, \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski, to vote in its favor as well. Murkowski said the bill allowed the abortion too late for her comfort, saying the legislation went, quote, "well beyond the precedent established in Roe and Casey." She urged lawmakers to vote for another bill she put forward that would simply codify Roe.\par \par \uc2 Democrats knew this bill was not going to ever get 60 votes no matter which one they put on the floor. Instead, they`re going to try to use it as a marker for where lawmakers stand on abortion ahead of the midterms, but voters across the country are expecting action as the threat of the Supreme Court decision looms.\par \par \uc2 So, what do you do now? I`m joined by Democratic senator from Wisconsin, \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , and Senator, given the state you come from and the immediate impact the -- what Roe would have come I can`t think of a better person to talk to right now. But first, I want to talk about the decision. You know, it seems as if we knew yesterday was a messaging bill, and there are two strategies one can take.\par \par \uc2 When you look at the final result, was it better to show that Democrats divided, or would have it been better to show the Republicans divided on this issue?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TAMMY BALDWIN (D-WI): Well, I would contend that this is a very substantive bill. It was around for a long time prior to this leaked decision. In fact, I teamed up with Senator Blumenthal to introduce it for several previous sessions because we saw all the states acting -- introducing bills that would limit access to abortion care.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:12]\par \par \uc2 And in some states this passed, in other states they didn`t. But we`ve had this bill for a long time. In terms of bringing it up at this particular moment, number one, it shows that we are engaged in this fight and this is only the first step in the fight given the anticipated opinion of the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And secondly as you noted, every senator is now on record as to whether they would strip away the constitutional right, the first time in history we`ve seen the court poised to peel back a constitutional right. We`ve always been expanding rights in our nation. Never looking backwards. So everyone is on the record. And now we organize. So we fight, we educate on the impact the Supreme Court decision will have on women and men, and then we organize for the elections.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you accept the premise, though, that you politically might have been able to show the Republicans as more extreme on this issue if you put a bill that allowed for some of the limitations that Roe and Casey have allowed for and yet they still wouldn`t support it. Right? Do you accept the premise that it would have painted them into a deeper corner?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: You know, I really dispute their characterization in going beyond Roe vs. Wade. What has happened is the states have limited and limited and restricted and imposed obstacles. These proposals that have come to pass have severely limited a woman`s right to choose. And they won`t stand up against that nor will they stand up against this anticipated Supreme Court case.\par \par \uc2 And when we want to talk about what`s extreme, you know, denying a teen who has been raped the access to abortion care, denying a victim of incest abortion care, denying a woman whose life or health has been placed in jeopardy because of, say, an ectopic pregnancy or something like that, the chance to live, that`s extreme.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about Wisconsin specifically, and I can`t help but get you to react to your Senate colleague that also represents the state. This is what he said according to "The Wall Street Journal."\par \par \uc2 This is Ron Johnson, it might be a little messy for some people but abortion is not going away, he said, saying that driving across state lines to Illinois would likely be an option. I just don`t think this is going to be the big political issue everybody thinks it is because it`s not going to be that big of a change.\par \par \uc2 So if you`re in Wisconsin, just drive into Illinois. Is that a real solution?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Well, first of all, let me tell you what the law would be in Wisconsin if Roe vs. Wade is overturned. In Wisconsin we have a law on the books, it was passed in 1894, sorry, 1849, one year after Wisconsin`s statehood. That`s where we`d be, that would be the status of our law in Wisconsin. And I think we can all agree that lots have changed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, at a time when women weren`t even allowed to vote in the state of Wisconsin. It might be worth bringing that up. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Women were property. They didn`t have independent rights of all sorts, let alone voting. You know, we`ve advanced quite a bit since 1849. And I have to say that the idea that women don`t have the same rights from state to state is ridiculous. But I would also say to voters that if our current Senator Johnson wants to send women to Illinois, we ought to send him packing this November.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you about what can be done in Wisconsin. I mean, it`s the most polarized state in the nation, and I look at a -- I look at this -- it feels as if that there`ll be nothing and there will be no protection, nothing at all. I mean, is there any sort of plan E, plan F here in the near term? Because you guys are sort of stalemated in the federal level. I mean, it just feels like this is going to be draconian in Wisconsin.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: I fear that it will. I really do. I will say that while our political situation in Wisconsin is overwhelmingly Republican legislature but a Democratic governor who can and has vetoed anti-choice measures that have gotten through the state legislature, what I will say is our general public, the people of Wisconsin are quite in tandem with the rest of the nation in terms of views about whether or not Roe vs. Wade should be overturned.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:23]\par \par \uc2 A significant majority believe that Roe vs. Wade should remain intact and a small minority believes that it should be overturned outright.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there any constitutional amendment, you know, the state of Michigan at least can put that on the ballot. I think they have a shot at getting something enshrined in their state constitution. Any path like that? Because, look, your legislature is gerrymandered in one direction and you have the governor, the Democratic governor that can sort of stalemate things. But is there a third path at all in Wisconsin?\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: The legislature has to pass in two separate sessions a bill to amend our state constitution and it goes to the voters of the state at the first election thereafter or an election thereafter.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s a long process. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Right. So what I will say is go to the ballot box, everybody who is concerned about this and a whole series of other issues where we have seen Republicans siding, you know, with -- well, siding with the idea of repealing a constitutional right, the first time in our nation`s history and setting in the United States tens of millions of women back.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: We need to vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator \uc2 Tammy Baldwin\uc2 , Democrat from Wisconsin, I really appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 BALDWIN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m closing in on my hour. I appreciate you all for being with us. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage will continue with Katy Tur after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220513ei5c00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for May 11, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Cal Perry \par \uc2 7,871 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022