ant.\par \par \uc2 But I think, Stephanie, if you rewind back to 16, what was one of the lessons of that presidential primary, Trump bullied everybody? OK, and I think what`s clear is if Trump runs again, in 2024, the first I think question everybody has to ask themselves who`s thinking about running is, can I stand up to this guy, and I think you`re starting to see more Republicans willing to do that.\par \par \uc2 I also think they think that Kemp is going to be a stronger general election candidate, these three governors, and he`s also got a healthy primarily. So this isn`t necessarily a profile and courage moment. But every week, we see Republicans being a little more willing to stand up to him a little bit. And I think you`re going to see more of that, because there`s going to be a big field, I don`t think Trump is going to clear the field. And I don`t think he`s going to like that race very much. Because I think people are going to punch him in the face. And when he punches back, they`ll punch back. And in 16, he just bullied the field. So I think there`s really interesting lessons here, not just for `22 but in terms of what this may mean for the `23, `24 presidential election cycle.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, Trump`s influence really depends on where you`re going in the country, West Virginia, for example, 50th, it ranks 50th in terms of infrastructure across the whole country. Yet the Republican congressman who voted to pass the infrastructure bill, that`s West Virginians didn`t like it. They voted him out in favor of another one who just continues to push Trump`s election fraud lies, and of course, was endorsed by Trump. So David Jolly, what does that tell you about Republicans per se, in the State of West Virginia, what matters to them?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Well, I think what it tells us something very important about Donald Trump`s imprint on the party, sometimes I think, when we just analyzed whether or not his endorsement mattered, we missed the bigger theme here, which is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has crushed traditional Republican orthodoxy and reshaped the party in his image. And so what you saw in that race was a candidate that reflects where today`s Republican Party is, particularly in West Virginia, and it is around these themes of populism, not of infrastructure investment and a state that might need it or policies that reflect the state. I think that`s probably the most important takeaway of where the Republican Party is today. It is a party that reflects Trumpism regardless of whether a candidate has Donald Trump`s support or not, the candidates that are winning are ones that reflect today`s new GOP orthodoxy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I want to share with our friend \uc2 Matt Dowd\uc2 , former \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  Strategist. He earlier today had some advice for Democrats. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:04]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, FORMER CHIEF STRATEGIST TO BUSH-CHENEY CAMPAIGN: I think Democrats ought to run a campaign based upon rights and freedom. They actually could run a campaign that says we`re the only one standing for all your rights, including Republicans, Democrats and independents. That`s the campaign. If I were the Democrats, I would run wringing their hands and bedwetting over inflation and the economy, obviously an issue have to deal with, but that`s not where they should be spending their energy and time. When they do that, they feed right into the Republicans hands.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Mr. Plouffe, do you agree?\par \par \uc2 PLOUFFE: Well, I think in part. So I do think, listen, right now, this is a very, very challenging political environment. So an issue with like rights, generally, abortion rights specifically. You don`t have a chance to alter that a little bit. I think, Stephanie, particular, let`s talk about Georgia, Arizona, Michigan, Wisconsin, Pennsylvania. These are massive battleground states. They have Senate and governor`s races, very open question. If Republicans were to do very well there, will abortion be outlawed in the States? So I think at some stage, you`ll see that.\par \par \uc2 So yes, and of course with data and with research, we understand the voters that that will be most persuasive. But that being said, you know, the economy is the central issue in every campaign I`ve ever been part of. So you have to have a message there. And it has to be an offensive message. So I think on rights, whether they be voting rights, civil rights, abortion rights, I think Democrats can play very offensively.\par \par \uc2 I think on the economy, it`s a little more defensive, but there`s a case to be made against the this brand of Republicans, to David Jolly`s point, so not going to invest in jobs. They`re not going to invest in infrastructure, they`re going to shower the wealthy with tax cuts, they`re going to cut education, they`re going to cut health care. So that`s going to be worse for you. We`re three economy generally. And bottom line specifically, but Democrats can`t ignore the economy. Because that`s all -- that`s going to drive a lot of vote. But there`s no question that Matthew is right, which is Democrats have to be a little bit more forceful about making the case and also like, they want to ban books. Most people don`t want to ban books. Most people want history to be taught. You know, we`ve got the public support on this side. And I think sometimes we get into turtle shell and are afraid to make our case.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Democrats need to be more forceful, appears to be the takeaway from this segment. I watched you get energized saying it and that`s where we saw David Jolly, give a yeah, pretty much. David Plouffe, David Jolly, thank you both for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, our 11th Hour one-on-one, my exclusive interview with the CEO of \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  on the future of remote work, why he thinks the business world can`t go back to 2019, when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:17]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  got crushed at the start of the pandemic with no one traveling and people quarantined at home, 80% of its business disappeared overnight. But as homework and travel blurred together over the last two years, the company recovered, went public and is thriving big time.\par \par \uc2 Today, \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  released a major relaunch of its website to match how people travel and look for places where they can live and work remotely. And the company is all in on remote work. They`ve recently announced their own employees can live and work from anywhere, forever.\par \par \uc2 And our 11th Hour one-on-one, I sat down with CEO \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2 . And, of course, an \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  and asked how the company envisioned what the future of work could look like not just for their employees, but for millions of us. No surprise, there reevaluation of work started during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHESKY\uc2 , \uc2 AIRBNB\uc2  CEO: Well, what happened was, people weren`t traveling for business. They weren`t crossing borders, and they weren`t there and visiting tourist attractions. But they were locked in their house sequestered and some people are like I need to get out of my house. So they`re getting in their cars. And they were traveling, say within a tank of gas, and getting with friends and families to \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 `s where they were living and working. And something remarkable happened. Suddenly, we weren`t just a travel company, because a fifth of our business was now longer than a month is not travel. Half our business was longer than a week. So suddenly, this whole new, like once in a century travel shift occurred and a wind kind of wind from to our face to our back.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So you transformed your company because we were forced into work from home because of COVID?\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And now here we are at the next phase.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Yes.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And you`re one of the first to say we`re not working from home for COVID anymore, we are working from home for good.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: We`re working from home. You can go to an office if you`d like, you can work in the United States. And if you`re in New York City, and you want to move to upstate New York, we`re not going to lower your pay, we`re not going to adjust it to the standard of living. And the reason why is I do think eventually within countries wages for people working on laptops are going to start to converge, and people are going to be moving around a lot more. And so we want to just leave what I thought was an inevitable trend.\par \par \uc2 My prediction is this is where the world`s going to be 10 years from now. And all the CEOs hauling their employees back to the office. That`s their prerogative. But here`s what I would say. The companies with the best talent will usually win, and if you are limiting your talent to a commuting radius, you`re not going to have the best people because the best people will live everywhere.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Why? Why do you think so many other CEOs are saying yes, our company has learned to function with all these people remote but we`re not optimizing productivity? Why did they see something different from what you see?\par \par \uc2 [23:50:10]\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Well, the world has completely changed since 2019. And I don`t think we can go back to 2019. Any easier than go back to 1950, A lot of people are looking at the big old companies to establish. What are they doing? I like to look at the young companies, because if you want to predict the future, you look at the next generation, and 20 years ago, tech companies popularized open floor plans, they popularized like onsite perks. And then what happened, older companies eventually started looking like younger companies, because they want to compete for that talent. Young companies are embracing remote work. They`re embracing flexibility. This is where the world is going. Or the company chooses not to embrace it, that`s fine. But this is where the world`s going to be 10 years from now.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Is it in some sort of bizarro way self-serving, because if people can work remote, there`s a chance they go and try and stay at \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  for months and months at a time?\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Yeah, of course it is. But to be clear, even if it wasn`t self- serving, I still would have gone with the policy. Now, why would I have done it even if it didn`t benefit \uc2 Airbnb\uc2 ? Because like everyone else, I want my employees to be happy. I want them to stay at the company. And I want the best talent to work here. And the best talent isn`t in New York, the best talent isn`t in San Francisco. The best talent is everywhere. I often ask myself, if the office didn`t exist today, would we invent it? And if we invented it, what would it look like and what it looked like it did before the pandemic? My instinct is the answer is probably no.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But innovation, connection, you really see that happening while you`re sitting in a living room in New York and somebody else`s in Chicago?\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: I think the proof is in the pudding. We haven`t been in the same room for two years. And last year, we made 150 upgrades to our app while being distributed and remote, we made the biggest change to our product in 10 years. And the biggest change our custom serves to 10 years. We did this all remote. And so that tells me something, it tells me that the world has changed. The way we work has changed. The way we`ve traveled changed. \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  changed with it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s talk Ukraine for just a moment when the war broke out, you made a lot of serious moves and announcement in the region. Talk to us about what you`ve done and what`s happening now?\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Well, when this crisis broke out, I think our first instinct was what I think a lot of people probably thought how can I help? And I think everyone probably like a lot of people want to help in the way they can. We just happen to have a lot of resources. So we announced the \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  in partnership with our host, Airbnb.org would house 100,000 Ukraine refugees. To date, we`ve housed over 20,000 refugees, and we`re just getting started.\par \par \uc2 The only other things, Stephanie, I will just say is something even more remarkable happen. People in our own community, on their own volition started booking \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  in Ukraine, with hosts they never intend to stay with just to give them relief aid. And over the course of a few weeks, I believe more than $20 million were sent to Ukraine, we didn`t take any commission. And this just speaks to something, in a world of darkness, in a world that destruction, I think kindness still exists in this world. And if you don`t believe that, all you`d have to do is look at our own data.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It sure does. Love is always the answer. \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2 , thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 CHESKY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2 , basically saying 2019 is no different from 1950s and kind of saying, OK, Boomer to all those CEOs out there pushing to get their employees back to the office, some of them may want to take note, \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  since they made this announcement, the company that only employs 6000 people has gotten a million hits on their jobs website. Who knows, this could be the way of the future.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, you don`t want to go anywhere, we`re going to set the record straight, little fact checking before we go on the gender pay gap after an Arizona Republican disputed its existence, little teaser is lying when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:43]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, a little fact check on the gender pay gap. A new video obtained by NBC News shows Arizona Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Blake Masters\uc2  protege of \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2 , disputing the existence of the gender pay gap. The video we are showing you is from a February candidates forum in Scottsdale.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS: Women are not paid less in America than men. It`s a left- wing narrative, this gender pay gap. When you control for the occupations, when you control for people taking time out to, you know, birth children, things are actually pretty equal. And men do the most dangerous jobs. Men are the ones who are the police officers. Not all of it, but you know, 80% of it in this country. Men are the ones who are doing risky, you know, fishing -- fishing crab in Alaska. And sometimes those jobs pay more. Sometimes those jobs pay more, and so I think we got to push back on the fake left-wing narrative that women don`t have equal rights in this country.\par \par \uc2 I want women to have equal rights. But we don`t need an equal rights amendment that would actually just be a Trojan horse to give the government more power to implement Kamala Harris`s national gender strategy, right? This is what the left does and we have to resist it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, there`s a lot to unpack there. But for facts sake let`s give it a shot. According to the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  in 2020 women earned 84% of what men earned. According to analysis of median hourly earnings of both full and part time workers. Based on this estimate, it would take an extra 42 days of work for women to earn what men did in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The Masters campaign did not immediately respond to NBC News when asked for further comment. But don`t worry, Blake, you can call me tomorrow, we`ll be here and still factchecking.\par \par \uc2 On that note, we wish you a very good night for all our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thanks for staying up late with us. I`ll see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220616ei5b0002y\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for May 11, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Neal Katyal, Ali Vitali \par \uc2 7,510 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:15]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 And we are going to be joined tonight by some of the people who joined us on the night that we first had our hands on this leaked Supreme Court decision, including Congresswoman \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And it`s interesting to get people`s reaction now from that night, when hands were shaking when they were reading this thing.\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: Yeah, I look forward to it, my friend. Have a great show.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you, Ali.\par \par \uc2 Well, once again today, the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  did what it is structurally built to do -- it crushed democracy. A group of senators who represent 186 million people were defeated in a Senate vote today by a group of senators who represent 144 million people.\par \par \uc2 The Founders debated whether the number of senators should be proportional to the populations of the states and in the end landed on a compromise position of two senators per state, which did not seem like an atrocious perversion of democracy at the time because the big estate Virginia had only 747,000 people. But today, Washington, D.C., which is represented by zero senators, has basically that population, 700,000 people.\par \par \uc2 When the founders agreed in the end to two senators per state the total population of the country they were forming was 2.7 million people. It was inconceivable to the founders at the time that the population of Brooklyn would be 2.7 million people as it is today. The Founders were designing a government for a country whose entire population was the same size as the population of Brooklyn today. It was inconceivable to the Founders that the unexplored areas of the continent would eventually include a state called Wyoming, with a population smaller than many of the country`s biggest cities.\par \par \uc2 And that even deeper into the unexplored territory, there would eventually be a state named by its Spanish-speaking settlers that would eventually have a population to rival the size of some of the most influential countries in the world and the economy of that state alone would grow to be the seventh largest economy in the world if it were a separate country. The founders would have recognized that the 40 million people of California being restricted to voting for the same number of senators as the people of Wyoming is a crime against democracy.\par \par \uc2 We long ago outgrew the Senate formula of two per state, which has evolved to allow outcomes in the Senate that are supported by a minority of the American people. Today, the Senate imposed the will of 30 percent of the American people at most, against the objection of 70 percent of the American people. The Senate can do that.\par \par \uc2 That was just another day`s work in a legislative body functionally disconnected from democracy. All Senate Democrats except one voted in favor of the Women`s Health Protection Act which has passed the House of Representatives and used the Roe versus Wade framework to guarantee women and girls the right to choose what happens to their own bodies in pregnancy. The final vote was 49 in favor of the bill, 51 opposed.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  who presided over the vote at the time ready to cast a tie-breaking vote if necessary said this:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KAMALA HARRIS\uc2 , VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I just presided over the Women`s Health Protective Act vote and sadly, the Senate failed to stand in defense of a woman`s right to make decisions about her own body. And let`s be clear, the majority of the American people believe in defending a woman`s right her choice to decide what happens to her own body. And this vote clearly suggests that the Senate is not where the majority of Americans are on this issue. It also makes clear that a priority for all who care about this issue, a priority should be to elect pro-choice leaders at the local the state and the federal level, because what we are seeing around this country are extremist Republican leaders who are seeking to criminalize and punish women from making decisions about their own body.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [22:05:03]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Republican Senators \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski who support Roe versus Wade voted against the bill today. They support their own bill which they say more closely mirrors the rights established in Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 Senator Murkowski released a statement today saying, quote: I strongly support women`s reproductive freedoms including the right to abortion established by Roe and Casey. The legislation before the Senate today, the Women`s Health Protection Act, goes well beyond the precedent established in Roe and Casey. It does not include the Hyde Amendment, which prohibits taxpayer dollars from being spent on abortions and has been the law almost as long as Roe. It does not include conscience protections for health care providers that refuse to perform abortions based on religious beliefs. It explicitly overrides the Religious Freedom Restoration Act for the first time. It also allows late term abortions without any notable restrictions.\par \par \uc2 After the vote, Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  said this:\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2  (D-NY): Elect more MAGA Republicans if you want to see a nationwide abortion ban. Elect more pro-choice Democrats if you want to see the right to choose a woman`s freedom available from one end of the country to the other. Elect more MAGA Republicans if you want no exception for rape or incest. Elect more pro-choice Democrats if you want to see women have the freedom to make decisions over their own bodies.\par \par \uc2 Elect more MAGA Republicans if you want to see a woman forced to carry the results of a rape or incest. Elect more pro-choice Democrats if you want to see women protect -- if you want to see women`s rights protected.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Democratic Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2  said this after the vote.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 PATTY MURRAY\uc2  (D-WA): We are not going to let Senate Republicans override women`s voices. They may have spoken out today. They may feel they`ve won today. But women of America, it`s time for us to stand up and fight, and that is exactly what we`re going to do. Use your voice, we will use our voices, we will fight back, and this goes to the November election. Every one of you has to be willing to stand up and fight with us. We will be your voice. We will fight for you, stand with us.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONELL: Join us now, Ali Vitali. NBC News, Capitol Hill correspondent.\par \par \uc2 Ali, the strategic question of the day is why didn`t the Democrats try to expand this vote to include Murkowski and Collins and Manchin -- and \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  by going more in the direction of the Murkowski-Collins bill?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Well, look, Lawrence, that was one of the open questions here. At the start of the week, when it was clear that this vote was going to be set up for Wednesday, one of the things I was asking Democratic leadership aides was, was the plan for this to fail in bipartisan fashion with more people voting in favor of it than against it or was the plan to just try to make it so that all Republicans would be on the other side of this and Democrats would have a more clear- cut line to run on in the midterms?\par \par \uc2 It`s clear now knowing the way that today played out, the way that they chose to go on this and the way that it was explained to me over the weekend by a Democratic leadership aide was they didn`t want to water down what they were voting on in order for it to be less meaningful to the base. Instead, they wanted to put forward the strongest version of the bill that they possibly could, all the while knowing that it was going to fail.\par \par \uc2 To them, they viewed this as a way to get people on the record ahead of the midterms and to hopefully energize their base. I do think that a lot of the polling that we`ve seen on this where the economy remains the number one issue for a lot of Americans and abortion and reproductive rights basically don`t rate on any of these polls and if they do they`re in the single digits, all of that at this point is out the window, because for both Republicans and Democrats alike, they`ve been running on this issue in theory for a long time but now they`re finally at a precipice where the landscape has significantly changed.\par \par \uc2 And as soon as the Supreme Court puts down this ruling, it will officially put Democrats and Republicans both in a place where it has been decided.\par \par \uc2 I do think though that as Democrats move forward and try to make this an election issue the way the vote went down today is really telling. It`s the exact same way that it went down in February when it failed. The same people who voted for it voted for it today. The same people who voted against it voted against it today, and it`s really telling because the landscape has significantly changed around this issue and yet it hasn`t changed the calculus in Congress.\par \par \uc2 It`s why you saw both senators there who you played as well as the vice president who I asked what comes next here, the only thing that can come next is looking to the states and making this an election issue.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2  apparently said something a little different about what might come next.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:04]\par \par \uc2 Let`s listen to what he said.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yeah.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Why are Democrats chose not to work with \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2 , or Murkowski and tried to get a bipartisan result?\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): I think that that effort is underway. The votes today are important I think in our baseline politically, but we`re going to continue to reach out and try to build whatever margin we have today.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, Ali, he sounds like he wants to reach out and work with Collins and Murkowski and which could get you to basically a vote of 52 in the Senate which would then have the power of majority and bipartisan.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: It would, but it also still wouldn`t get you to that magical 60 number where they would need to get in order to overcome a filibuster on this. I do think those conversations are happening in earnest but we`re presuming the 52 in that situation as well, and there are a lot of Democrats and frankly I`m interested in hearing what your next guest has to say on this where she would fall on this spectrum.\par \par \uc2 But there are a lot of Democrats who say that the way that the Murkowski- Collins plan is structured would still allow for a lot of loopholes at the state level where you would still end up with an America that looks like a lot of half of the states, allow abortion and a safe and accessible way, and then half of the states restrict and ban it and you end up sort of in the situation that you`re going to end up in when the Supreme Court ultimately decides and if it decides along the lines of that opinion that was leaked at the beginning of last week.\par \par \uc2 So the presumption that 52 Democrats would just get on board, that`s not necessarily what I`ve been hearing from senators in the halls of Congress who did view the Women`s Health Protection Act as the ideal that they wanted to vote on. Obviously, now, that seems like it`s on the dust heap because of the way the vote went today.\par \par \uc2 But at the same time, there might be other measures even aside from Murkowski-Collins that they could try to pursue there are some people who are floating doing this in a piecemeal fashion, trying to do smaller more targeted votes on reproductive issues. That`s something that could be explored going forward.\par \par \uc2 But I do think in having conversations with advocacy groups, people at the White House and of course people on Capitol Hill, there really is a sense right now that the federal government is pretty hamstrung if they weren`t going to do it this way. There`s not a ton that can be done otherwise, and that`s why a lot of people are not just looking to the states from an electoral capacity but also looking at the states and seeing if there are things that state attorneys general can do to better shore up reproductive access in the meantime, all of this in preparation for that ultimate Supreme Court decision later this summer, potentially June.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Ali Vitali, thank you very much for your reporting on this vote today and joining us tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And joining us now is Democratic Senator \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2  of Minnesota. She previously served as executive vice president of Planned Parenthood in Minnesota, North Dakota and South Dakota.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you very much for joining us again tonight you were with us on that shocking night when we were holding in our hands for the first time the leaked Alito first draft opinion and here we are with a vote in the Senate today that really is a reaction to that opinion. What happens next in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TINA SMITH (D-MN): Well, what we have in this country is a Republican Party that is hell-bent to tell women what it thinks they should be able to do with their own bodies. They are hell-bent to take away a fundamental freedom and autonomy that I think all women`s deserve and all women expect.\par \par \uc2 And so that is the fundamental reality and as you said in your opening, Lawrence, this is all playing out in a \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  where a minority of voters have an extraordinary voice in what happens in this country because of the way the Senate is structured. So what has to happen is that those of us that are in the majority, those of us that represent percent of the of the voters have to have more power in the Senate to be able to express the voices of those voters. That`s what my colleague Senator Murray was saying just a moment ago.\par \par \uc2 This is going to come down now to this election, it`s going to come down to organizing and turnout and persuasion and all the work that we have to do in a democracy, understanding that in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , majority does not rule. You have to have a super majority in order to be able to express the will of the people.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So, within a month or so, Roe versus Wade is going to be overturned by the Supreme Court. Will the Senate come back and vote on this again uh when it -- on the day that roe is overturned on the day that the judgment is announced by the Supreme Court or the day after?\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, you know, what I`m thinking about is how when Roe is overturned by the Supreme Court and let`s be clear Roe is going to be overturned because of the result of a multi-decade strategy by Republicans and their dark money donors, the \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  and others to accomplish this end.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:06]\par \par \uc2 When that happens, what`s going to happen is that there`s going to be a crisis of health care for women in this country. Women who need abortions for whatever reason are not going to be able to get what they need, and that is going to create a major crisis and the accountability for that is going to rest with the Republican Party and those Republicans that have refused to stand up for women`s fundamental freedoms, fundamentally the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  will do -- I will do -- Democrats in the Senate will do everything we can to clarify what the difference is but we don`t have the power to accomplish what the will is of the American public.\par \par \uc2 And that is why this election is going to be so, so important if you care about some of these -- this fundamental freedom.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Would you join an effort to pass the Collins-Murkowski version with 52 votes in the Senate and then, of course, that`s a meaningless thing to do because it would it would need to beat the vote threshold but then also ask those same senators to vote to change the Senate rule on the 62- vote threshold for just this one piece of legislation.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, you -- as you probably know, I think that the filibuster is an archaic remnant of an old Senate that uh that it is needs to be abolished and I think that we should get rid of the filibuster for voting rights, for abortion rights, for that the health of our democracy. I think that it is absolutely fundamental.\par \par \uc2 On this issue, though, I -- you know, I think that if you really look at what is in the Collins-Murkowski bill as it stands right now, it does not prevent the kind of abortion bans that we see playing out in state after state after state. I do not believe that it does the hard work we need to do to protect the fundamental freedoms that Roe protected and I don`t think it gets us where we need to get to.\par \par \uc2 So that is a big problem, along with the problem that as you said, you know, even if we had -- you know, plus one votes to pass Collins-Murkowski, that doesn`t get us anywhere because you need 60, and I see no evidence that we have 10 Republicans that would be willing to join us in an effort to protect reproductive freedom. And that`s why the election is so important.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So it`s sounding like the Democrats message on the day that Roe is overturned in the Supreme Court is wait till the next election and then wait several months after the next election for all those new Democrats presumably if you get them to be sworn in in the Senate so that you then might be able to pass something assuming the 60-vote rule could then be overruled in the Senate, at least for this one piece of legislation.\par \par \uc2 So, you`re asking, it sounds like you`re -- the Democrats will be asking for about a year they`re going to be saying to pregnant women in America for about a year, don`t expect anything from the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, I mean you`re touching on exactly what I`m talking about which is that this decision by the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  is going to -- it is creating a crisis of health care for women. It is unconscionable and the accountability for that rests with the Republican Party which is refusing to stand up for women`s fundamental rights. The only thing that I know how to do as a -- as a senator and also as a as an activist for reproductive rights as someone who worked at Planned Parenthood and as an organizer myself is to go out and organize voters to make sure that their voices are heard and that is our job right now.\par \par \uc2 I also would just note that there are many, many people and organizations that are now going to have to be pressed into action over these next months, some -- I mean, it`s starting right now, to do everything that we can to make sure that women have the capacity to access abortion care when they need it. And that is going to create as I said a crisis across this country, a health care crisis that we haven`t seen for many, many years.\par \par \uc2 So, it is -- and the thing that I just am focused on is like who is responsible for this and what are we going to do joining together to try to take action to make this place make our country a place where women have the fundamental freedom and autonomy to make decisions for themselves about their own bodies and their own lives.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator Smith, before you go I just want to ask you that -- sort of a personal question about how this feels to you now this Alito draft that we held in our hands together that first night, feeling the shock of it and I know you were still processing the shock of it, you`ve had over a week to live with it now, what does it feel like and what does it feel like to you with all your experience with Planned Parenthood and as a senator? What does it feel like to you that is coming? What is -- what is coming in this country in the next month or so when they make this decision?\par \par \uc2 [22:20:03]\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Well, you know, when you and I talked about this the night that we saw this opinion. It felt like a gut punch. It felt like uh yeah it was almost difficult to process because it was so stunning and so brazen and it`s disrespect for women and women`s ability to make decisions about their own lives.\par \par \uc2 And now that I`ve lived with it for a week, I have to -- I have to tell you -- I mean, today was a really difficult day for me personally. I was in touch with friends of mine who have worked so hard on these issues for so long and it -- I -- it just -- it just -- it is a hard, hard day to experience this.\par \par \uc2 But you have to persevere. You have to keep on working and when I wake up tomorrow, that`s what I will keep on doing, but it is -- as I said, I think the last we spoke, Lawrence, this is the first time that I can remember that we have seen the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2  in my lifetime take away women`s rights, take away people`s rights and this is a sign I think of the great dysfunction in our democracy.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator \uc2 Tina Smith\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us again tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SMITH: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And coming up after this break, Representative \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:25:55]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MURRAY: What we saw today is Republicans telling Americans all over this country and women in particular that their voice is more important than yours, that what they believe in is more important than your choice about your own body and your own family and your own future. Now is the time to lift up our voices and fight back.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Representative \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2  of California. She`s a member of the House Oversight Committee and the deputy chair of the House Progressive Caucus.\par \par \uc2 Now, Representative Porter, you were with us on that night when we were holding the draft opinion in our hands for the first time. It was kind of - - it was shaking in my hands I was so shocked that we possessed it first of all and then that we were reading it.\par \par \uc2 On reflection now that you`ve had more than a week to live with it and you saw what the Senate did today, what are your feelings about it tonight?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 KATIE PORTER\uc2  (D-CA): I think it is not my frustration, my anger, my disappointment hasn`t diminished one bit. In fact I`ve had more time to think about what the consequences of this decision are, not just in the immediate term for women who are who are worried about what this means for them, for families that are worried about what this means for them but thinking about the long-term economic consequences of this decision.\par \par \uc2 We saw \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  from the secretary of the treasury talking about what this decision will mean as women are forced to drop out of the workforce, are able to have to cut their education short. So I think my sense of frustration, my sense of rage, my sense of disappointment my anger as a citizen of this country that I feel like my own ability to control my health care is being taken away from me. And I think that we`ve we`re going to see the frustration and the rage the anger the disappointment, we`re going to see it keep building as people begin to really grapple with something that we thought we hoped would be unthinkable.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I want to go back to the point. You mentioned Treasury Secretary \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  pointing out the economic consequences of this for women. Most women, most -- a majority -- who use abortion services, already have one child. So what`s happening there in many, many cases is a decision about what is economically possible for them and what is possible for them given that they already have one child.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Absolutely. This is not -- abortion is not about whether p -- is that -- it`s not -- it`s not in contrast to motherhood. It`s not about people never becoming mothers. It`s about deciding when to start a family.\par \par \uc2 So the -- as you mentioned, the majority of people who have an abortion are already mothers at that time and some of them have several children. Some of them will have an abortion and will go on to have children later.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think this idea that it`s this choice that somehow Republicans are choosing mothers and motherhood and Democrats are choosing to not be mothers to choosing abortion is just wrong-headed. This is part of family planning. This is part of reproductive health care in the same way that things like birth control are.\par \par \uc2 And when we see Republicans start to invade our choices about abortion, we`re going to see them come for birth control next.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: President Biden has said that inflation is the number one priority for the Biden White House to try to get under control right now. As you`re out there in California talking to constituents during this re- election year for Congress, how does inflation compare to this newly important in the sense of the Supreme Court decision pending abortion issue? How do those two issues compare?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Well, I don`t think they compare. I think they actually reinforce each other. So the fact that things like inflation can happen and it become, become more expensive to feed your kids and to fuel your car is exactly why people need to be able to be in charge of how many mouths they`re going to have to feed.\par \par \uc2 [22:29:39]\par \par \uc2 So I think the fact that we`re seeing this jump in expenses, that we`re seeing people having to pay more in the grocery store, pay more at the pump, pay more for housing is a reason that people are saying I need to be able to make my own decisions about when and if to start a family.\par \par \uc2 So I don`t think we`re going to see them -- I don`t think it`s about comparing them or contrasting them. I think they reinforce for people just how big of a responsibility it is to take care of our family.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: All right. I want to take a look at a poll of Florida voters. This is a \uc2 University of South Florida\uc2  poll, about the causes of inflation. And they have at the top of the list, 87 percent say supply chain; 83 percent, COVID-19; 64 percent Biden administration; then comes war in Ukraine at 62 percent; Trump administration 49 percent.\par \par \uc2 I personally would move war in Ukraine way up there. How does that list look to you?\par \par \uc2 PORTER: I think it is missing a really important contributor here which is price gouging. So look, the evidence here is not tricky to understand. I don`t need a white board for this one.\par \par \uc2 When you are selling a product, and one of your inputs -- say you are selling gasoline -- and your input, which is oil, goes up in price, you might have to charge more. But if you are just covering the cost of your higher input, you would not see your profits double, quadruple.\par \par \uc2 And that is what we are seeing. We are seeing it with oil. We are seeing it with food products. So we are seeing these companies. They are not just passing along the cost of the higher input. They are not just passing it along their supply chain difficulties. They are using the supply chain moment to increase their profits by raising their prices well beyond the higher costs that they are facing.\par \par \uc2 So it`s -- nobody is taking away, I think, from the war in Ukraine, from supply chain issues, from labor shortages -- these are all real factors. And the price of fuel, of course, affects the price of many other goods.\par \par \uc2 But there is simply -- you cannot look at Wall Street`s profits and not come to the conclusion that this is one of the most profitable moments that Wall Street has ever seen and they are profiting off what they view as really an opportunity to engage in price gouging. And we have to crack down on that as we also try to solve some of these underlying inflationary factors.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Representative Porter, can you please stay with us across a break? Because I want to ask you something about what you did in your subcommittee. This may sound like something Congress does all the time because there`s been recent reference to it with the January 6th committee.\par \par \uc2 But you actually sent a criminal referral to the Justice Department for recommending an investigation of a Trump cabinet member.\par \par \uc2 I would really like to get the details on that the other side of this commercial break. Please stay with us.\par \par \uc2 [22:32:53]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And we are back with Representative \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 . I have a -- I can tell the story of your criminal referral to the Justice Department. But I would rather have you tell this story and how it came about.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that really stuns me about it is it centers on, among others, a Trump cabinet member who I forgot existed. I forgot who was secretary of the interior after the first one had to leave. But please tell us why this criminal referral is about.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: So, what happened here is the deputy secretary of the interior, the secretary of the interior at the time was \uc2 Ryan Zinke\uc2 , who is currently running for Congress. But the deputy secretary, his name was Bernhardt. He took a secret meeting with a developer named Mike Ingram (ph), trying to develop a project in Arizona, in a very environmentally sensitive area.\par \par \uc2 This was a secret meeting. It was never disclosed on any of Secretary Bernhardt`s calendars. It wasn`t disclosed to our committee when we were investigating.\par \par \uc2 A couple weeks later, this Fish and Wildlife career employee, 30 years, just doing his job, gets a call and is told that a high-level politico wants him to reverse his decision that this development would harm the environment.\par \par \uc2 And on October six, three things happened all at the same time. First, the \uc2 Army Corps of Engineers\uc2  announced they were reopening this permit process. They were going to reevaluate their decision. They have previously denied the permit.\par \par \uc2 Second, Mike Ingram and his 12 buddies of his donated nearly a quarter of a million dollars to the Trump victory fund and to the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And three, this Fish and Wildlife -- this whistleblower, this Fish and Wildlife person -- got a phone call telling him that he needed to reverse his decision. He got the phone call directly from the Department of Interior in Washington.\par \par \uc2 So, the facts here are really shocking. This is a quid pro quo. That`s what it appears to be. In return for making nearly a quarter of a million dollars of donations to the Trump victory fund and to the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , this developer was basically able to buy his way around environmental protection law.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:56]\par \par \uc2 PORTER: So the facts of this case are shocking and they are very, very serious. Quid pro quo is the most spurious (ph) form of corruption that there is. And officials need to know that just because they`re out of office, just because they tried to hide their tracks, doesn`t mean Congress isn`t going to do its job and conduct that oversight. And when we find crimes, we are not afraid to make the referral to the Department of Justice.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And you found all this in your subcommittee investigation, which you have now basically forwarded on to the Justice Department to see if they find criminal conduct.\par \par \uc2 PORTER: Yes. The chair of the Natural Resources Committee Raul Grijalva was working on this investigation. I took it -- you know, I took it over on the oversight subcommittee. We did it together. And I am really, really proud of how careful and thoughtful.\par \par \uc2 What we really tried to do here was get the facts. And then we`re making the criminal referral so that the Department of Justice can conduct the depositions and go through the formal legal process to reach their determination of whether or not criminal charges should be filed.\par \par \uc2 But given the evidence before us, we have a responsibility as congress members to see that the law is being followed. And so it does not appear that happened here and that`s why we need this unusual step.\par \par \uc2 This is the first time that the House Natural Resources Committee has ever made a criminal referral. And I think that speaks to just how egregious the conduct is here and just how important it is that we in congress are establishing the rule of law. And the expectation that administration officials are going to follow it.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Representative \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 , thank you as always for joining us again tonight. We always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, newly-released audio tape shows that \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  -- shows what \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  really thinks about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  cannot hear him.\par \par \uc2 And a newly-released email from Trump attorney John Eastman puts the criminal conspiracy to overturn the election in writing. \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:41:50]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: "\uc2 New York Times\uc2 " reporters Alex Burns and Jonathan Martin have released new audio recording of Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  criticizing \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and defending Congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  after the January 6th attack on the capitol.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SENATOR LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): He plays the TV game and he went too far here. That rally didn`t help, talking about primarying in Liz. He created a sense of revenge.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Lindsey Graham`s sense of revenge was on full display in the Supreme Court confirmation hearing for Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  when \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  said very plainly that he thought that the hearing should be revenge for the way that \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  was questioned by the committee, after \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  was publicly accused of sexual assault, and then found himself defending himself in his hearing by expressing his love for beer, among other things.\par \par \uc2 NBC News has confirmed that fake Trump electors in Georgia are cooperating with Fulton County district attorney \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2 ` grand jury investigation of possible violations of election law by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and others in Georgia. Trump election attorney John Eastman put the criminal conspiracy in writing according to emails obtained by Politico and emails from John Eastman to state legislators in Pennsylvania, urging them to change the vote count in favor of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , saying, "Having done that math, you`d be left with a significant Trump lead that would bolster the argument for the legislature adopting a slate of Trump electors perfectly within your authority to do anyway but now bolstered by the untainted popular vote. That would help provide some cover."\par \par \uc2 Joining us now \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  who served as acting U.S. solicitor general at the Department of Justice. He`s an MSNBC legal contributor.\par \par \uc2 And Neal, if I end my email to you with "this will help provide some cover", do you immediately call the \uc2 FBI\uc2  -- I mean who ends emails like that when they`re talking about anything other than covering up a crime?\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL, MSNBC LEGAL CONTRIBUTOR: Exactly, Lawrence. I mean at last -- at long last, we have emails finally we`re talking about, finally. And all of a sudden, Republicans are acting like words don`t count unless they were, I don`t know, notarized and you know, sent by carrier pigeon or something like that.\par \par \uc2 So, what Eastman is doing in these emails as a Trump lawyer is he is under the cover of math. He`s trying to throw out the votes after he knew Trump lost. So his basic thing in the email is, well, 4 percent of absentee ballots are generally thrown out. Here only 0.4 percent were thrown out. So, you should say throw out the extra 3.6 percent. And that will hand Trump a victory. Give me a break.\par \par \uc2 I mean there are a couple of problems with that. One is, I don`t know how many of the 4 percent were actually just votes cast by like \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and, you know, Trump people anyway.\par \par \uc2 And second, there is no electoral scheme anywhere, you know, at least in the United States, in which you do that kind of math. Just like, of course not. You go ballot by ballot. You look at whether or not there is election fraud with respect to any individual ballot. You do not do things like this.\par \par \uc2 [22:49:58]\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: And as you say, Lawrence, the tell here, is he says that will give us cover. Cover. This is a presidential election we are talking about, the most significant thing we do.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: SO does that email leave any doubt that John Eastman knows what he is advocating is illegal?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: It sure looks that way.\par \par \uc2 I mean, you know, you can try to explain it, you know. I`ve known John Eastman since we clerked together on the Supreme Court. He clerked for Justice Thomas, I clerked for Justice Breyer. I I think -- I thought he was -- I think many people thought he was a bit kooky, but people didn`t really think he was coupy (ph) but that evidently is what he has become.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: When you look at this issue of fake electors in Georgia, district attorney has now the cooperation of the fake electors in Georgia. You see this interstate conspiracy -- John Eastman is a part of -- this is all interstate communication, saying you know, here is how to give us -- here`s how to send to Washington a completely fake set of electors. And it is for more than one state.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: It seems like \uc2 Fani Willis\uc2  has almost the entirety in Georgia, they have an entire version of this conspiracy including the Donald Trump`s phone calls to the secretary of state.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: That is right, Lawrence. There`s two separate issues. One is the local investigation there with the local folks, including fake electors that, you know, were appointed. And that were potentially appointed by Republicans there. And that looks, potentially criminal.\par \par \uc2 And then the question you are raising is, how high does it go? Did any of these fake electors get contacted by Trump or Eastman? Or people close to him?\par \par \uc2 And the news today is that these fake electors are cooperating with the investigation in Atlanta. It`s not any surprise they are, I mean after all they did not sign up to participate in the crime. You know, all they wanted to do, I suppose was get together and fly to Washington D.C. and override the results of the securely run election.\par \par \uc2 So, how are they supposed to know that attempting a coup would create this kind of fuss?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I mean how is it different from impersonating an \uc2 FBI\uc2  agent?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: It is not. It is the same kind of basic problem, if they knew it. You know, obviously, they may have defenses on criminal intent and so on. But right now it looks bad for them and it looks bad for this overall what looks like an orchestrated conspiracy.\par \par \uc2 And you know, Lawrence, Judge Carter, a respected federal judge in California has already looked at the evidence and said it is more likely than not that not only John Eastman committed crimes but \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed federal felonies.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What is Merrick Garland`s role here?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Well, if there is a federal indictment, it has to be brought by the attorney general. And we don`t know exactly what the attorney general is doing. There is a school of thought that says that`s a good thing. They thought \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  is by the book, he is not leaking, and so on.\par \par \uc2 The other school of thought is he is such an institutionalist that maybe he is too afraid to go after Trump. You know, my hope is that, because he is such an institutionalist, because he cares so much about this country, the Department of Justice, the rule of law, he will look at all of this, he`ll look at what Judge Carter said, he`ll look at what the January 6 committee comes up with next month in the hearings and conclude as I suspect the evidence will be overwhelming that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  committed federal felonies.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And tonight`s LAST WORD is next.\par \par \uc2 [22:53:28]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Time for tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Let me ask you this. Have you made any decision in your own mind whether you feel Roe versus Wade was properly decided on without saying what that decision is?\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE CLARENCE THOMAS, SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: I have not made, senator, a decision one way or the other, with respect to that important decision.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE SAMUEL ALITO, SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: Roe versus Wade is an important precedent of the Supreme Court. It was decided in 1973. So, it has been on the books for a long time.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 NEIL GORSUCH\uc2 , SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: I would tell you that Roe versus Wade decided in 1973, is a precedent of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . It is a precedent of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 . It was reaffirmed in Casey in 1992 and in several other cases.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE \uc2 BRETT KAVANAUGH\uc2 , SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: Senator, I said that it is settled as a precedent of the Supreme Court entitled to respect under principles of stare decisis.\par \par \uc2 JUSTICE AMY CONEY BARRETT, SUPREME COURT OF THE UNITED STATES: Roe v. Wade clearly held that the constitution protected a woman`s right to terminate a pregnancy. Casey upheld that central holding and spelled out in greater detail the test that the court uses to consider the legality of abortion regulations.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [23:00:00]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tonight`s LAST WORD is "precedent".\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE" starts now.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220512ei5b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May 11, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ayman Mohyeldin, Michelle Goldberg, Charles Blow \par \uc2 7,615 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JOY REID, MSNBC HOST 17 journalists have been killed or murdered since the start of this year. Our deepest condolences to Miss Abu Akleh`s loved ones. And that is tonight`s "REIDOUT." ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES starts now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AYMAN MOHYELDIN, MSNBC ANCHOR (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 JOHN EASTMAN, TRUMP LAWYER: We have enough votes to barely get over the finish line.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: The so-called architect of Donald Trump`s coup in his own words. Tonight, the explosive new emails from John Eastman asking for ballots to be tossed to provide some cover for stealing the election.\par \par \uc2 Then, how the lawmakers working to overturn the vote in Pennsylvania may be on the verge of taking over the state`s elections.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the demands for answers as a Palestinian American journalist is killed in the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 And how did \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  get caught on tape endorsing \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Totally, he`ll maybe the best person to have, right? I mean, how mad can you get at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And the remarkable scene inside the Capitol where House members protested senators on their way to vote down a federal right to abortion. ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN (on camera): Good evening, everyone. I`m Ayman Mohyeldin in New York in for Chris Hayes. The ex-President knew the conclusion he wanted. He wanted to overthrow the results of a free and fair election. He wanted to install himself as the winner. He just needed to manufacture a way to get there. And that is where John Eastman comes into the picture.\par \par \uc2 Eastman, of course, is a lawyer. He`s a diehard Trump ally who was the architect behind much of the attempted coup. In fact, he even spoke at Trump`s January 6 rally at the ellipse, the one where Trump incited the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 EASTMAN: All we are demanding of Vice President Pence is this afternoon at 1:00, he let the legislatures of the state look into this, so we get to the bottom of it and the American people know whether we have control of the direction of our government or not.\par \par \uc2 We no longer live in a self-governing republic if we can`t get the answer to this question. This is bigger than President Trump. It is the very essence of our republican form of government and it has to be done. And anybody that is not willing to stand up to do it does not deserve to be in the office.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, so that`s what Eastman was saying publicly. But more notably is the work Eastman was doing behind the scenes. He authored the now-infamous coup memo where he actually urged then Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to literally throw out the results from seven states that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had won before he "gavels President Trump as reelected."\par \par \uc2 Now, in order for that scheme to have work, Trump and Eastman needed to get the states on board. They actually needed Republican legislators in those seven states to object to the official count, saying that Biden won. And that is where this latest revelation comes into focus.\par \par \uc2 According to a trove of Eastman`s emails first reported by the Denver Post and published by Politico, Eastman was corresponding with a Republican Pennsylvania state legislator, a guy named Russ Diamond. So, Eastman was basically trying to have Diamond come up with a way to overturn Biden`s 80,000 vote victory in Pennsylvania. And he suggested that the state just basically throw out some of the votes for Biden.\par \par \uc2 Eastman proposed a convoluted scheme in which the state Republicans would use Trump`s bogus claims of fraud as a pretext. And to do so, complicated math would ultimately result in a large number of absentee ballots for \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  being tossed out. Then Eastman writes, "having done that math, you`d be left with a significant Trump lead that would bolster the argument for the legislator adopting a slate of Trump electors. Perfectly within your authority to do anyway, but now bolstered by the untainted popular vote. That would help provide some cover."\par \par \uc2 So, Eastman is basically here saying that the states can just throw out Biden`s victory for any reason. But as he pointed out, they needed cover and official reason to do so. And that is the logic behind Trump`s big lie. The one that focused on election fraud -- by election fraud, excuse me, and that was basically to provide cover for the states to change those results and get him to be the winner.\par \par \uc2 Now, Eastman was essentially doing a slightly more sophisticated version of Trump`s call to Georgia Secretary of State. We all remember that one. The one where he basically told \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , find the votes I need to win. As one member of the bipartisan committee investigating January 6, Democrat Jamie Ryan basket of Maryland told \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , "Eastman wasn`t doing anything that Trump himself wasn`t doing. They were both trying to get officials in the electoral process to substitute a counterfeit for the actual vote totals."\par \par \uc2 Right now, at this moment, the committee is fighting a legal battle to obtain more of Eastman`s emails from the days leading up to January 6. Every new piece of information the committee finds about these coup plotters is damning. This is just yet another example of how they tried to use the levers of power to keep \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in the White House.\par \par \uc2 And it was mostly by sheer luck and quite honestly, the willingness of a handful of people to actually stand up to Trump to say that plot did not work. But Trump or perhaps even some future Republican is quite free to try again. And Eastman already wrote the blueprint for whoever wants to use it. As Congressman Raskin put it, "This shows the country one more strategic booby trap that was improvised by Trump`s team that can sit there for use by bad-faith actors in future elections."\par \par \uc2 Harry Litman is a former U.S. Attorney for the Western District of Pennsylvania and Deputy Assistant Attorney General under President Clinton. He is currently a legal affairs columnist for The Los Angeles Times and host of the Talking Feds podcast. He joins me now.\par \par \uc2 It`s great to see you again. Thank you so much for making time for us, Harry.\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FORMER U.S. DEPUTY ASSISTANT ATTORNEY GENERAL: So, as Greg Sargent puts it in the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 . He writes, Eastman advises the Pennsylvania legislature to use a complex formula based on treating mail-in ballots as illegitimate. To basically extrapolate that enough Biden votes are invalid to show Trump won the popular vote.\par \par \uc2 How would a scheme like that even potentially be challenged when you`re talking about tens of thousands of ballots that you are trying to invalidate?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: It would be a huge mess and that`s probably what he was pointing for. Although ironically, even under his bogus formula, they wouldn`t have gotten to where they needed to be. But look, we`ve been thinking of Eastman largely in terms of Pence. He`s the architect of the theory, and then he harangues Pence`s people after to saying they`re responsible for the actual riot.\par \par \uc2 This is before. This is in December. The other -- a judge has already held that Eastman probably committed crimes and held that what he was doing with pants was a coup in search of a legal theory. This is a coup in search of a different legal theory. And you`re really right, Ayman, to analogize it to the Raffensperger just give me 11,780 votes.\par \par \uc2 He basically went to Diamond in Pennsylvania and said, oh, do a lot of whereas, hereto forth, etcetera, and say you don`t have confidence in some of the absentee votes and therefore, throw them all out just enough to get over the line. Of course, his math is wrong.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Right.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: But that would -- that would have been the idea that state legislatures expressed some doubt. And now the apple carts upset and we start -- we start from scratch. That, again, was a whole different theory that he was applying before January 6. And those are the emails that we basically don`t have. We got these by chance through a Colorado sort of FOIA, but there`s a lot more that the committee hasn`t seen.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: OK, so, let me ask you about that. Because listen, I`m not the lawyer, you are. But Eastman referred to the Pennsylvania scheme as a "cover." And for me, that`s the kind of word. Again, I`m not a lawyer. But doesn`t that word basically give the whole game away when you are talking about a scheme and you`re saying it is a cover?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Yes, you don`t have to be a lawyer for that. And he`s done it in other places. So, cover exactly means -- that`s his equivalent of, I just need 11,107 -- 11,780. It`s reverse-engineered very plainly to get over a finish line either to then have the state do what it wanted or at least create the kind of chaos, you know, let all the chess pieces be upset that he was trying to do with Pence, and he still tried to do after.\par \par \uc2 This guy is shameless and there`s no theory he won`t endorse. But it`s important that this was a whole another one that he was trying to sell even before he focused on Pence.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And let me ask you really quickly. We have under a minute left. This is a point that Congressman Raskin has talked about. It`s something I`ve also talked about as well. This is basically a blueprint because if Trump wasn`t smart enough to pull it off in 2020, either him in 2024 or whoever replaces him, somebody who`s even more evil, more maniacal could pull it off. And this is just waiting for the next presidential candidate.\par \par \uc2 What is to stop them from doing so? Have we done anything in the last two years to prevent that scheme from be being executed by a future more savvy President?\par \par \uc2 [20:10:04]\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Yes, not yet. And again, the strategy is to create confusion and take it from there what we need to do is really clarify. It`s already illegal, Ayman. But if they really clarified the famously confusing electoral Count Act of 1887, that would make it clear that they couldn`t even get started with this sort of bogus scheme.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Harry Litman, thank you so much for your time. I greatly appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And I appreciate your insights.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  is a Democrat from Virginia. She sits on the bipartisan committee investigating the January 6 insurrection. She joins me now. Congresswoman, thank you so much for making some time for us. So, some damaging revelations in these emails published by Politico. Did you already have them? Have you already seen these emails in their contents?\par \par \uc2 REP. ELAINE LURIA (D-VA): Well, what I`ll say is, you know, we have over 100,000 documents that the committee has received. And you know, following our practice, I`m not going to comment on specific emails or documents that we have. But what I would say is, and I`ve said this before, these things are the tip of the iceberg.\par \par \uc2 You know, they paint a really clear picture as you just discussed of let`s just find the votes, let`s find some factor by which we can multiply the votes by and then do the math. I mean, that`s not how you do elections. You don`t just do the math, do some different equations to come out with results you want, then pick different electors.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, this whole scheme which he clearly personally admits is a cover, essentially, for stealing the election is, you know, it`s a key part of the story that we have to tell through the committee and that will, you know, touch on and go into much more depth when we have the opportunity to have hearings in June.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And can you tell us a little bit about the thrust of what the committee is hoping to learn from the emails that Eastman has not yet turned over?\par \par \uc2 LURIA: Well, you know, if every email we get from him is a treasure trove of, you know, details about, you know, how he`s going to try to coerce different state legislators, how to change the vote and, you know, make excuses for how you can use that as a cover to change the outcome of the election. You can imagine if through this FOIA request, there`s several that have been made public. There`s tens of thousands that are part of this litigation that`s ongoing to receive from Eastman.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, I think that we will discover a lot more. And just in my personal opinion, probably some very similar correspondence and maybe even more nefarious tricks to try to affect the outcome of the vote and use that to influence what, you know, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  -- what he was trying to get Pence to do on January 6.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes. And so it seems that there were, Congresswoman, a lot of co-conspirators in Trump`s attempt to wanted to push this coup through. But from how I read it, and I could be wrong, Eastman seems like he may have been one of, if not the most crucial person in this scheme. Is that your sense as well? Are you able to paint a clearer picture for us into the co- conspirators and where Eastman fit into that circle?\par \par \uc2 LURIA: What I can say is he was clearly a central figure in this plot, just the volume of emails that we know exist, and the types of people he was reaching out to, in this case, a legislator in Pennsylvania. You know, the volume, the content that we`re aware of from the previous discussion shows that, you know, he`s a key cog in this machine that was trying to, you know, piece together a different election result than what the voters had decided when they went to the polls that day.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And finally, I got to ask you about those public hearings. They`re set to start in about a month. What more does the committee need before that? And will you start those hearings regardless of whether you finish your work and trying to get to the bottom of this?\par \par \uc2 LURIA: So, we are planning to start the hearings in early June, around June 9th. And, you know, as we approach the hearings, we are still conducting interviews. We`re still receiving documents. There`s still information that`s important to the work of the committee. This opportunity in June is for us to lay out the work, the product to paint a picture with the information that we`ve received over the course of the last year of our investigation.\par \par \uc2 But we will continue to pursue the truth and more information, continue to talk to witnesses and you know, all of the information we gather collectively will go into our report later in the year.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, Congresswoman \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2 , thank you so much for your time. I really appreciated your insights.\par \par \uc2 LURIA: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: It is important to remember here that the attempts at election subversion have actually not stopped with the removal of President Trump. Many in the MAGA crew who tried to steal 2020 are now attempting to gain control over elections in states like Pennsylvania. In fact, the state representative who John Eastman was just emailing with about those strategies to overturn the election, believe it or not, he`s actually running for higher office in that state.\par \par \uc2 And he isn`t the only coup plotter campaigning for a position with more power over our elections. We`ll tell you about that next.\par \par \uc2 [20:15:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So, as we`ve been reporting, a new set of emails show that the ex-President`s top coup-plotting lawyer John Eastman was trying all he could to keep \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in power. The emails again, as we first mentioned, reported first by the Denver Post include this man, Pennsylvania State Representative named Russ Diamond who asked for Eastman`s help in "searching for legislative solutions to our current national predicament."\par \par \uc2 That national predicament you might wonder was the peaceful transfer of power after a legitimate election where there was no voter fraud. Now, that man Russ Diamond is running for the Republican nomination for lieutenant governor of Pennsylvania as we speak.\par \par \uc2 And he is not the only coup plotter that is asking for a promotion. You have state senator \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  who wants to be the Republican candidate for governor despite his involvement in trying to overturn Pennsylvania`s votes in the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 [20:20:29]\par \par \uc2 Now, Mastriano was also at the Capitol on January 6 during the riot after the police lines were breached. He then tried to use his position in the Senate to attempt an Arizona style audit of Pennsylvania`s election even after Joe Biden`s inauguration.\par \par \uc2 Now, Mastriano was the top Trump ally in state government working to find non-existent voter fraud. He was even subpoenaed by the January 6 Committee over his attempts to arrange for an alternate slate of electors in that state, though he missed that scheduled deposition.\par \par \uc2 So, even with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  staying neutral so far, Mastriano is MAGA-style politics have launched him as the current frontrunner in that race. And at a recent debate, he gave a preview of how Pennsylvania`s elections process would actually change if he got that position. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DOUG MASTRIANO\uc2  (R-PA), GUBERNATORIAL CANDIDATE: There`s seven or eight pieces of legislation I have to tighten up. But the most important thing is I get to appoint the Secretary of State. And that Secretary State is going to clean up the election logs. We`re going to reset. In fact, the registration, you have to reregister. We`re going to start all over again. I saw better elections in Afghanistan that in Pennsylvania. For me, this is no game.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Holly Otterbein is a national political reporter for Politico and previously worked at the Philadelphia Inquirer. She has been covering the various Pennsylvania races. She joins me now. Holly, it`s great to have you with us. I appreciate you making some time for us. So, you wrote about Pennsylvania Republicans and the scramble to prevent a MAGA candidate Mastriano from being the nominee for governor. How are they doing with that effort?\par \par \uc2 HOLLY OTTERBEIN, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, POLITICO: Yes, so remember the 2020 presidential election for the Democratic nominee. When \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  was, you know, winning states, Democrats freaked out. And then you had a bunch of candidates drop out and unite behind \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to defeat \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 That`s basically what the Republicans in Pennsylvania are trying for here with Mastriano. They`re very concerned about him becoming the nominee and ruining their chances in the fall. And so, top Republicans at the county level, local level, and gubernatorial campaigns have had conversations about hey, can we all unite around one person.\par \par \uc2 And right now, Mastriano is really benefiting from a splintered divided vote. There`s four top candidates for governor. There`s even more than that in the single digits. They`re all splitting up the vote. And Mastriano has this dedicated core base of like, 30 percent. That`s not going anywhere. And they want to try to basically, you know, do what the Democrats did in 2020 and unite behind one candidate.\par \par \uc2 But right now, it`s -- you know, I think that they might have limited success. Two of the candidates are scheduled to appear at a joint appearance tomorrow. We kind of know where that`s going. But I think it`s probably unlikely that you`re actually going to see all of them unite behind one non-Mastriano candidate at this point.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Can you shed some light? And I know you`ve reported that the PA Republicans, as you were just saying, worry that the party could be doing its chances in November by picking a polarizing candidate with limited appeal. What is it about him? For those of us that are not versed in Pennsylvania politics, what is it about Mastriano that gives even Republicans this moment of pause, let alone the statewide race?\par \par \uc2 OTTERBEIN: Well, look, he is the face of the movement in Pennsylvania to overturn the 2020 election. And like you said, he was there at the Capitol on January 6. He`s been subpoenaed by the Congressional committee investigating the attack.\par \par \uc2 Even if Republicans and many of them do, you know, like what he was trying to do there, they don`t want to be talking about those things in the general election this fall. They want to be talking about gas prices going up, inflation hitting record highs, crime going up across the country, right? They don`t want this focus to be on January 6 on all of these things that make \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2  so controversial.\par \par \uc2 And he`s also, you know, sort of a divisive candidate within the Republican Party itself. He actually voted for the legislation to legalize no excuse mail voting, you know, before that became something that you couldn`t do in the Republican Party. And so, a lot of Republicans are, you know, upset with him for doing that.\par \par \uc2 And they also think that he doesn`t have a real campaign operation. He`s spent less than $300,000 on TV ads. He doesn`t interact with the press at all. And so, they`re worried you know about him going up against \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , the current attorney general in Pennsylvania and the presumptive Democratic gubernatorial nominee who has just -- has a giant war chest and he has, you know, no real competition and has a very professionalized operation.\par \par \uc2 [20:25:23]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Really quickly and we`re almost out of time. Do we know why Trump has not yet endorsed a candidate in this race? I mean, he does loom large over Pennsylvania politics. He`s endorsed Mehmet Oz for the Senate race. We know it as a swing state, but he still has not endorsed a candidate. What gives you think?\par \par \uc2 OTTERBEIN: I mean, it`s always hard to get in the mind of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . The sort of humors in Pennsylvania are that his, you know, loyalties are a little bit split perhaps or he might not be that impressed with any of the candidates.\par \par \uc2 You know, one of the top candidates he actually endorsed in 2018 for another race but went on to lose. You know, you`ve got Mastriano who`s controversial in the party. You know, you`ve got some other folks. Maybe he hasn`t had, you know, perhaps the best interactions with.\par \par \uc2 So, again, I am reluctant to get into his head, but that`s kind of the, you know, what Republicans in the state are talking about.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, Holly Otterbein, thank you so much for joining us, Holly. I greatly appreciate your reporting and insights as always.\par \par \uc2 Still ahead, as the Senate fails to protect the abortion rights in this country, what is left for Congress to do? The next fight and the House members protesting in the halls of Capitol Hill after this.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: This morning, I woke up to the news of the killing of Shireen Abu Akleh. Shireen was a veteran journalist for Al Jazeera. She was a mentor, a colleague, and above all, she was my friend. Shireen was killed while she was doing her job reporting on an Israeli military raid taking place in the occupied West Bank.\par \par \uc2 Her killing sparked outrage and condemnation around the world. Al Jazeera said that the Israeli military targeted Shireen and killed her deliberately. The Israeli government acknowledged that it was conducting an operation in the area, but said that the Palestinian American reporter might have been killed by Palestinian militants during the gunfight.\par \par \uc2 Now, even though all the eyewitnesses around Shireen at the time, including nearby journalists reporting on that raid refuted the Israeli claim. The Israeli military actually later backtracked on that saying it is not possible to determine how Shireen was killed at this point.\par \par \uc2 Now, I`m not going to comment on who killed Shireen, but I am going to tell you who in my opinion should not investigate her death, and that is the Israeli military and the government. Time and time again, we have seen the Israeli military operate with impunity in the occupied Palestinian territories, and it goes unchecked.\par \par \uc2 No officers are held accountable. No justice is served. We hear as we did yet again today American officials condemn and call for investigations into Israeli abuses. But as history has shown, there is rarely, if ever, any justice for the killing of Palestinians, whether they are American or not, quite frankly.\par \par \uc2 And today, the spokesman for the Israeli military, Ran Kochav, seemingly tried to explain the tragedy by saying that Shireen "was filming and working for a media outlet amidst armed Palestinians. They`re armed with cameras if you permit me to say so."\par \par \uc2 Those were his words. They are not my words. Armed with cameras is exactly how journalists go to war. The only danger they pose is to anyone who would benefit from the horrors and injustices of war remaining hidden from the outside world. It is only because then those journalists on the ground there today who were armed with those cameras that we hear in New York and around the world know some of the circumstances of Shireen death.\par \par \uc2 And recently in Ukraine, an American journalist was killed covering the Russian invasion of that country. Did any one question or tried to imply that the journalist was in the wrong for what they were doing and why they were doing it?\par \par \uc2 Now, no one would expect the Russians to fairly carry out an investigation of the American journalist that was killed there. And if history is any indicator, no one should expect Israel to carry out this investigation impartially. Nobody would take that seriously.\par \par \uc2 Now, the killing of journalists, whether it`s in Mexico or Ukraine or in the occupied Palestinian territories, that must be condemned and investigated, but not by the countries accused of allegedly killing them, but by independent bodies who claim to uphold free speech and the right to a free press as fundamental human rights.\par \par \uc2 Here in this country, we owe that to our own citizens whose taxes are used to bankroll the Israeli military and the Palestinian authority to demand answers when Americans are killed even when it is at the hands of our allies.\par \par \uc2 Now, today, mourners actually escorted Shireen flag-draped body. It was covered with the flak jacket that she was wearing that was clearly marked press. Shireen believed in and die to protect that fundamental freedom. She was a fearless reporter who I got to know when I was based in Jerusalem.\par \par \uc2 In fact she actually took me under her wing and taught me so much about the region and the conflict as newsroom colleagues. We became friends. She was kind. She was gracious. She was hilarious. She was compassionate. Her humanity was unmatched and reflected in the people she covered and the stories that she told for nearly 25 years reporting on the frontlines of the Israeli-Palestinian conflict.\par \par \uc2 And while she became a household name across the Arab-speaking world, she also became a role model for millions of young children who aspired to be journalists in one of the most fraught regions of the world to be a journalist. Here she is featured in this AlJazeera 25th anniversary video in her own words.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHIREEN ABU AKLEH, JOURNALIST (text): I managed to overcome my fears in difficult times because I have chosen journalism in order to be close to the people. It might not be easy to change the reality but at least I was able to convey the people`s message and voice. I am Shireen Abu Akleh.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: That is who Shireen was. And that is how she will be remembered. And here is Shireen on a recent trip to New York during the holidays posing gleefully outside the Christmas tree right outside our studios at Rockefeller Center. And this is how I will remember my friend.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVE MEMBERS: My body, my decision.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: My body, my possession.\par \par \uc2 HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVE MEMBERS: My body, my decision.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, so this is a scene that does not happen in the United States Capitol. You have members of the House of Representatives basically crossing over to the Senate side to shout in protest at the senators who are about to cast a vote to decide whether or not abortion would remain legal in America after the Supreme Court is done with it.\par \par \uc2 The Senate though as expected voted against the Women`s Health Protection Act. And even though the vast majority of Americans, a whopping 61 percent actually support it, with Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  presiding, the vote came down 49 to 51. Every Republican including the West Virginia Democrat Joe Manchin voted against protecting the health of American women in this country.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by one of the House members who marched to the Senate side chanting, my body, my decision, Congresswoman \uc2 Sheila Jackson Lee\uc2  of Texas. Congresswoman, thank you so much for making time for us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Now that the Republicans have effectively killed this bill with Manchin on their side, what, if anything can Democrats do to protect women`s rights in this country?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 SHEILA JACKSON LEE\uc2  (D-TX): Well, first of all, thanks for having me. I think the announcement from that vote should be that the freedom of women in this nation has just been denied, that women do not have freedom, freedom over their own bodies, freedom over their own thoughts, over their own rights. And I think that that is going to ring loudly and clearly as we marched toward November 2022.\par \par \uc2 You know, it should be made very clear. Every single Republican voted against the Women`s Health Protection Act, a simple bill that would again, allow women to be treated as human beings with humanity, dignity and human rights and women`s rights being respected. So, Democrats are not going to allow this to be the case.\par \par \uc2 I happen to be one who supports the eliminating of the filibuster. And of course, Republicans have throated that. And so, the point should be made that we will -- incrementally, we will expand the knowledge of who has stopped you from getting an abortion, what has stopped you from getting an abortion. It is Republicans in the Senate, and it is potentially the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  where Alito in his opinion has said that constitutional rights are not necessarily fundamental. Can you believe it?\par \par \uc2 That means that we are going down a slippery slope, that the Constitution may be shredded in our very faces. That is not going to be allowed. You can see people are angry and anguish. We want to channel that into ensuring that Republicans don`t win and Democrats do win. We did not bring this on America. Republicans did. We did not bring this on Republicans. They did.\par \par \uc2 And so, we will put forward other legislative initiatives. We will hear the stories of women who are a victim, and we will educate Americans to ensure that the victor goes to women and women`s reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So, let me ask you about two of those things that you talked about both the filibuster and your legislative agenda because quite honestly, the house has sent several measures that were priorities in the President`s domestic agenda, including build back better, the Voting Rights bill. They sent those to the Senate to get something done on them only to see them stall out.\par \par \uc2 How can Congress function if Republicans -- if Republicans can just simply say they won`t vote for something and kill a bill legislatively while you also have senators like Manchin and Sinema who refuse to do away with the - - with the filibuster? Is the system now effectively dysfunctional?\par \par \uc2 LEE: I don`t want the American people to think that at all. And I frankly am not going to give up on Senators Sinema and Manchin. And Senator Schumer, Majority Leader Schumer is not going as well. And I think the American people`s voices collectively need to be raised.\par \par \uc2 And every senator will have to be held responsible for recognizing whether it is constitutional rights, the right to privacy is superior above any individual personal thoughts that might cause you one neck to want to get rid of the filibuster. The filibuster should have been gone for voting rights. The filibuster certainly should be gone for denying the Ninth Amendment and the right to privacy, and also the rights to fundamental constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:36]\par \par \uc2 Now, we`re going to introduce bills -- and by the way, we -- I have introduced a bill that the Senate has as well, and that is the anti- vigilante stalking bill. Because in addition to the whole HB-8, that is a Texas horrible bill that has now caused women to flee Texas to seek reproductive rights, they now have in essence metastasize the stocking part of it. That is the part that will allow someone to receive a bounty, a reward, money, if they stalk people who are seeking reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 And Oklahoma has just passed that bill. We want to criminalize it, I hate to say that, and provide enhanced penalties for anyone that does that. Add it to the federal anti-stalking bill. We want you to know that it is criminally illegal to stop a woman from seeking personal health needs and reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 But we`re going to keep putting forward bills, putting on the floor as necessary, when necessary that they can pass. I hope Republicans would want to vote against that. But as well, we want to make sure that the stories of victims or persons who have had the need, the personal need to address the reproductive rights, those stories need to be told.\par \par \uc2 And the question will be asked what side of history do you want to be on, the side of the Constitution, the side of biding freedom to women or not.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Congresswoman \uc2 Sheila Jackson Lee\uc2  of Texas, thank you so much for your time. I greatly appreciate you joining us this evening.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Next, the new tapes from after the January 6 Attack of Donald Trump`s biggest lackey in the Senate throwing his support behind \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . What he said after this.\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, so first, we got the tapes revealing exactly what Donald Trump`s allies in the House were saying behind closed doors after the January 6 insurrection. And actually, now, we`re getting a look inside the Senate and what they were saying. \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporters Jonathan Martin and Alex Burns, authors of the new book This Will Not Pass: Trump, Biden, And The Battle For America`s Future have just released an audio recording of Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina on January 6th just after the riot saying that President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is exactly what the country needed.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: We`ll actually come out of this thing stronger. Moments like this reset. People will calm down. People will say, I don`t want to be associated with that. This is a group within a group. What this does, there will be a rallying effect for the country says we`re better than this.\par \par \uc2 JONATHAN MARTIN, JOURNALIST: Biden will help that, right?\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Totally, he`ll maybe the best person to have, right? I mean, how mad can you get at \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: All right, so 60 months after the attack on the Capitol, when those words were uttered by Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , that position is clearly not the politics of most of the Republican Party, probably not even that of Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  himself.\par \par \uc2 Michelle Goldberg and Charles Blow are both opinion columnist for the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . Michelle writes about politics, gender, and social issues and Charles writes about politics and social justice. They both join me now. It`s great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Michelle, I`ll start with you. What do you make of these comments from \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  praising \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  behind the scenes because it`s a very different tune than what he has been singing in public about Trump and him being the future of our party, either we get with him or we`re out?\par \par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Well, look, it`s impossible -- I`ve completely lost my capacity to be, you know, either shocked or surprised. Republican hypocrisy I think it`s to be expected at this point. I mean, I do think it`s worth pointing out that, you know, when \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  says this is a moment to reset, that was a choice. This -- that could have been a moment to reset if the Republican Party had decided to cease it, had decided that that`s what they wanted to do.\par \par \uc2 They made a choice that it was more important to feed Donald Trump`s egos and fantasies than -- and, you know, to kind of drive the country even further into madness than to use that opportunity to kind of draw a line under the chaos of the previous four years.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So, Charles, we learned today through these John Eastman emails that he pressured Pennsylvania state lawmakers to throw out absentee ballots and give Trump a win. Does that surprise you when you see the degree to which this scheming was taking place behind the scenes?\par \par \uc2 CHARLES BLOW, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I don`t think it`s surprising as much as it is shocking and should be a warning for everyone heading into 2024. What he was asking the representatives, the officials in Pennsylvania to do is precisely what could be done if you had people who were open to the idea.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:18]\par \par \uc2 And what we see now is multiple people across the country who are people who believe that the election should have been overturned, who believe it \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won, or at least say that they believe that, are running for offices where we -- where we put them in position to do exactly what Eastman was asking to be done.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s less about me being shocked that they were trying to steal the election in 2020. I am very convinced of that. And every time that something comes out, it confirms that. What to me is -- everyone should be standing up saying hold on, wait a minute, is the idea that it could actually happen in 2024.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Michelle, let`s turn to the latest on the abortion rights debate in this country. Today, Joe Manchin did what Joe Manchin does which is basically vote with Republicans to reject a bill legalizing abortion nationwide. I want to play for you what he said before the vote. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. JOE MANCHIN (D-WV): The bill we have today to vote on, Women`s Health Protection Act, and I respect people who support it, but make no mistake, it is not Roe v Wade codification. It`s an expansion. It wipes 500 state laws off the books. It expands abortion. It`s just disappointing that we`re going to be voting on a piece of legislation which I will not vote for today. But I would vote for a Roe v Wade codification if it was today. I was hopeful for that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So, that`s -- that is what Joe mentioned does. You know, he just kind of like obfuscates a little bit some of the details of what this is really about. And so, it begs the question, how can Democrats say they are the stewards of abortion rights if their own party member defects and votes against it?\par \par \uc2 GOLDBERG: Well, look, I mean, I think that there`s a difference between being ineffectual and hypocritical. The Democratic Party, by and large, really are the stewards of reproductive rights in that, you know, the vast majority of Democrats support reproductive rights. They are, you know, appointing and confirming judges that will defend what is left of reproductive rights after Roe vs. Wade is overturned. They`re trying to codify reproductive rights at the state level wherever they can. So, I don`t think anybody should be comparing the two parties.\par \par \uc2 I don`t really understand the point of putting this up for a vote if you can`t get all the Democrats on board. I think, you know, there`s this idea that people are going to punish Republicans for this. But you know, the structure of the Senate means that Republicans don`t need to appeal to a majority of the country. That`s the reason that we`re in the situation that we`re in.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, this is all sort of theater because even if we did have a majority to pass this bill, we don`t have a majority to break the filibuster -- we don`t have enough votes to break the filibuster in order to actually enact it. And so, you know, as long as there were -- as long as there are members of the Democratic Party, Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  who put these, you know, sort of recondite procedural techniques ahead of being actually able to pass legislation, there`s frankly very little that, you know, Congressional Democrats can do.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Charles, in your recent piece about the Supreme Court, you write, "the court is a product of the framers of the Constitution. And for all their flashes of brilliance, they made some terribly flawed decisions about our government. That`s why originalist, those who believe that judgments by the court must conform to how the Founders understood the Constitution when it was written are so dangerous. The originalists know that they can turn that clock back. They know the horrendous history of the court, and they want it to rise again.\par \par \uc2 Some very powerful words there. But what are you most concerned about as we sit on the precipice of losing abortion rights in America and what that may mean for the originalist on the court?\par \par \uc2 BLOW: Well, there`s nothing to stop it, right. You know, the Supreme Court is, you know, a council onto itself. There are very few checks on it other than to replace members when they die or they resign. And so, there`s nothing really to stop them. They don`t bow to public pressure. They`re not elected.\par \par \uc2 And you now have people on the court, five of the nine are there because of people who didn`t even win the popular vote. They are pushing forward an agenda that doesn`t respect precedent in a way that even said before congress that they would respect. So, there`s nothing to stop.\par \par \uc2 And this is an invasion of privacy issue, at least for the courts on abortion. But there are a lot of other decision that are based on this issue of invasion of privacy.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BLOW: And what stops the court from going back?\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, that`s exactly the question I think everyone -- on everyone`s mind as this story continues to develop. Charles Blow, Michelle Goldberg, thank you so much for joining us. I greatly appreciate your insights.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Wednesday night. I`m Ayman Mohyeldin here in New York. Don`t forget to catch me on the weekends on Saturdays 8:00 p.m. Eastern, Sundays at 9:00. "MSNBC PRIMETIME" starts with my good friend Ali Velshi. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220512ei5b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 11, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Dasha Burns, Yamiche Alcindor, Vaughn Hillyard, Lauren  Mayk \par \uc2 9,364 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Yes, I can hear you.\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Wednesday. Here we are, another special Meet the Midterms edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY live from Independence Hall here in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, at the pivotal moment in these midterm primary elections.\par \par \uc2 Good day, I am Chuck Todd in Philadelphia. It`s a city rich in American history and a fitting place to talk about the future of our country less than a week before some of the most important primaries on the 2022 midterm calendar. Pennsylvania will be a key to Senate control in November and, of course, it is a major battleground in 2024. It may have replaced Florida and Ohio. It`s the state living up to the phrase Keystone if you could borrow a word.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, we were in the northeast part of the state focusing on the future of the Republican Party. We spoke to voters, officials, and one of the top Republican Senate candidates at the center of a very muddled and messy primary. Today, our focus is on the future of the Democratic Party. And the message the Pennsylvania Democrats appear to be sending is clear as a somewhat unorthodox politician appears to be pulling away in the Senate primary. We`ll see what his opponents think of that characterization.\par \par \uc2 This wasn`t expected to be a very a blowout if you will but according to at least one poll, Lieutenant Governor John Fetterman is up nearly 30 points over the more moderate Congressman \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  and Philadelphia State Representative Malcolm Kenyatta. We`re going to talk to both Lamb and Kenyatta live coming up.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got an exclusive look at the Fettermans, both he and his wife in a moment as well, because the question that is going to be on the minds of Democrats across the country, is whether John Fetterman is the future of the Democratic Party in some ways. His style, his focus whether his style of politics and potentially the way he talks to voters that are -- that respond by saying, oh, you`re not a typical Democrat, is that what the party needs to find at this moment when the party is clearly struggling politically?\par \par \uc2 Fetterman has campaigned with \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2  in the past, but today, he avoids the label progressive instead, preferring populists or just plain old Democrats. And he`s a film layer you face to voters, people need to realize this isn`t just a flash in the pan, he`s running for the third time statewide. He failed in a Senate run in 2016, he succeeded in a run for lieutenant governor in 2018 so in some ways, he`s paying his dues if you`re a rank and file Democrat.\par \par \uc2 But what we`ve discovered from talking to voters suggests that some of Fetterman`s appeal is because Democrats here like many places that we`ve gone and traveled to around the country, they`re simply tired of what they believe is the party`s current status quo. I talked to a group of Pennsylvania Democrats just a few hours ago that included one that`s backing Lamb, one backing Kenyatta and one backing Fetterman but they all had the same gripe. They feel like their party`s messaging is stale and doesn`t speak to them.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHRIS SCHMIDT, KENYATTA SUPPORTER: I think there are so many times where the Democrats, in politics in general, many times have this like messaging problem, where it doesn`t meet you where you are.\par \par \uc2 JAIME BASSMAN, LAMB SUPPORTER: Definitely a thousand times. The messaging of the Democratic Party needs to be completely overhauled from the top down.\par \par \uc2 TABITHA SSONKO, FETTERMAN SUPPORTER: The people who have showed up for years to support the Democrats, especially in the states, like Pennsylvania, are tired of showing up and not being responded to.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: The discontent among these democrats was palpable, and it appears they`re getting ready to air some of those frustrations at the ballot box if they come to the ballot box. We`ll discuss that later. Join on set with two of my colleagues who`ve been traversing the state, Dasha Burns, and Yamiche Alcindor. They`ve been on the ground doing what I`ve been doing here talking to voters, talking to candidates. Yamiche, let`s start out West. Is Fetterman the strong front-runner? Is that what you`re sensing too when you are out in the western part of the state?\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, MSNBC WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: That`s when I`ve been sensing. I sat in on a union town hall meeting with the \uc2 United Steel Workers\uc2  and they told me that John Fetterman is the person that has been connecting to rank and file union voters, people, of course, who were pulling away from the Democratic Party and where Republicans were making gains. They really do feel strongly that he`s someone who`s speaking their language, who also liked their style. You think about the shorts, you think about the sort of dress down style, and they are really excited about that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: But I also should tell you that I talked to the mayor of Aliquippa, who told me that Democrats, one, are running too many candidates that they need to really coalesce around a message, and coalesce around who they want to pick different races. So listen to a little bit about what he told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DWAN WALKER, MAYOR OF ALIQUIPPA, PENNSYLVANIA: Having good candidates, not running for all the same seats. And we spread that wealth out over different avenues like Congress, House of Representatives, Senator, Lieutenant, spread that out because you got -- in the Senate race, you got like three top Democrats and that are running for that one seat. The frustration part of the Democratic Party. That was so splintered. We need to get back to our roots, invest in good candidates who can do the job, and then stay with them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: And part of those roots, he says are talking to union workers and reminding people that Democrats were the ones who pushed for those unions to be established.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. You know, Dasha, I`ve had some Lamb people push back on our characterization that he`s the strong front runner and they`re pointing out. He`s got a lot of institutional support here in Philadelphia, we saw it. He`s sort of the, hey, this is the guy that can win as far as the sort of the democratic power brokers are around the state. Could we be underestimating Lamb right now?\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Look, he has institutional support now. Fetterman, in his first run in 2016, he told me their campaign barely had gas money.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And I think this says a lot about the direction of the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The fact that now he does have not only a lot of voters, according to the polls, but also the institution behind him went over --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, but the institutional support is still with Lamb, even though there are some people peeling off for Fetterman. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes. I think that`s -- I think that`s fair to say. You know, I quipped sometimes about voters in Pennsylvania, kind of feeling the vibe, right? It`s about a vibe -- it`s about a brand if you want to speak, you know, political terms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But there`s something about the way Fetterman approaches people that just hits home. I`ve heard people use the same language about him that I`ve heard voters use about Trump, frankly, not your typical politician. You know, he`s a real guy. He talks to people.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, you spent a lot of time with both John Fetterman and his wife, because the two of them run together at times. She`s a very important part of his campaign.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: She was a part of his initial reaction to the leaked Supreme Court documents, so let`s take a look at what you learned in that sit-down that you had with the Fetterman`s.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 BURNS (voiceover): Senate candidate John Fetterman`s road to Washington, swerves between left and middle.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN, LIEUTENANT GOVERNOR OF PENNSYLVANIA: I don`t want to be crass, but we need to keep making (AUDIO GAP) this country\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Trying to win back blue-collar Pennsylvania voters that swung to Trump but with a brand all his own.\par \par \uc2 If you walk into Washington day one to do one big thing --\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: Sure.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: What do you do?\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: What do I do? Well, again, that would be like picking my favorite child. I just -- I just couldn`t --\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I`m asking you a fair question.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: I just couldn`t do that. So again, I rattle off three priorities. Minimum wage, it`s a disgrace that it has to change. We have to codify Roe v. Wade. And third is voting rights.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Are you a progressive?\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: No, I`m just a Democrat that has always run on what I believe and know to be true.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: In his signature card hard hoodie, Fetterman wears in, not your typical Democrat type of brand. His approach is more at home with a commonwealth`s common man than with the party elite. As lieutenant governor for going to Harrisburg mansion for his car dealership turned home across the street from a steel mill in Braddock, where he had some choice words for his fellow Democrats in Washington.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: We are squandering, in my opinion, an enormous opportunity to do some transformative good through legislation that is being stopped by a senator like Joe Manchin. And I`m not criticizing him. I`m simply saying I would vote differently.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: It`s not just the candidate, but the couple that makes this campaign unconventional.\par \par \uc2 GISELE BARRETO FETTERMAN, SECOND LADY OF PENNSYLVANIA: Did you pick one already? OK, so you can grab the cake. I can take you two. Are they nice?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: If John is bringing the casual, his wife, Gisele, is bringing the polish and the personality.\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: Are you ready to go inside?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: Did you want to pick the cake? There`s two different kinds.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: She`s been front and center at rallies on social media and in campaign e-mails. How did that come about? How did you guys decide to really come as a package deal in this way?\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: I think we are. I mean, our family is the package deal, right?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: She`s a force in the community running several philanthropic efforts. She says she hates politics but hopes her story will transcend all that.\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: Hi, how are you?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Born in Brazil, Gisele came to the United States as an undocumented immigrant.\par \par \uc2 How much does that influence his position on the issue?\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: I think it does very much so. We`ve seen cases where I`m out with the children and people think I`m the babysitter and not their mother.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Gisele says she gets more hate mail than her husband.\par \par \uc2 You`ve said that you have three strikes against you. You`re an immigrant, a woman, and a cannabis patient. Do you worry that those three strikes might count against John in November if he wins the primary?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 GISELE FETTERMAN: I don`t think it should count. And if it does, it just means there`s more work to do, and we`ll continue to do it.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Those strikes may be home runs for the progressives in the party, but Fetterman is also trying to score with a waning working-class base.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: It`s like this idea that if you feel left behind or you feel that you`ve been ignored or you -- the party has moved on to something different or better in their minds like well, what`s wrong with us?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: The Fettermans together, hoping their brand can bridge the gap between old school and new school Democrats in a state with razor-thin margins.\par \par \uc2 JOHN FETTERMAN: Our first campaign, we barely had gas money and now we`re one of the most resourced -- well-resourced campaigns, you know, of a challenger nationally. So the only thing that`s changed is that we`ve been getting our message out and she`s a big part of the package.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dasha, very interesting there, by the way, it is -- Game Six is coming up here with the Sixers and the Heat.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: It`s silly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Obviously, the Sixers know their season is likely about to end. Oh, did I say that as a \uc2 Miami Heat\uc2  advocate here? But that is the noise you`re hearing in the -- in the background. Dasha, if there is some doubt about Fetterman that I`m sensing here in Philadelphia, it is about whether African-Americans will be enthusiastic enough to show up to the polls for him.\par \par \uc2 The incident -- the incident that happened when he was mayor comes up time and again with activist voters that I talked to here in Philadelphia. There`s a concern, Reverend Mark Kelly Tyler put it said the -- what super predator did to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2 , it suppress turnout in `16. That is the concern about Fetterman. How are they going to address this?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Look, they`ve been addressing it sort of in the same way from the beginning. I asked him the question. He didn`t really drip from the very same answers he`s been given at every debate. He talks about how he`s brought down crime when he -- when he was mayor, that gun violence went down. That`s what he`s been coming back with. I asked if he -- you know, should change that answer, that he kind of gave the same answer again.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I`ll tell you though, when I have been talking to folks in western Pennsylvania, especially people that kind of know the Pittsburgh area, the Braddock community, and including voters of color at some of Gisele`s events, for example, the free store where folks come and pick up goods for free, it doesn`t really come up with the voters I`ve been speaking with, with everyday voters. Activist, absolutely, but with everyday voters, it`s not a lot of people when I asked him about it don`t really know what I`m referencing. So it`s hard to say.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Here, it does feel like here folks know it, Yamiche.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know the incident.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Right. The issue is, of course, that Philadelphia is still the heart of the black voter -- voting in an -- in this state. And I think it`s very -- it`s a hard swing to ask African-American voters to forget about that. And it`s also hard -- when I talked to a lot of African American voters who say, we want candidates who also represent our ideals and we want candidates of color who don`t remind us of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And that`s the sort of risk there where you have African American voters who have told me that they`re -- they frankly, think that the transformational change that they were promised, a lot of them with the Biden administration, while they`re still supporting Biden because they won`t go to the Republicans, they`re still wondering, why aren`t Democrats more aggressive? I heard that time and time again, as I was talking to people saying why not policing and voting (INAUDIBLE) --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Can Fetterman overcome it -- I mean, look what he said his top three issues were to Dasha, if voting rights was part of it, can that help overcome some of the skepticism?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: It can possibly help overcome some of this activist -- skepticism, but democratic messaging in this state and with Fetterman, in particular, has to be the how are you going to get that done? Because I talked to you -- a voter who told me, you`ve already said that, like, if you give us this power, here`s what we`re going to do. If you -- if you give us this power, our lives are going to be infinitely better.\par \par \uc2 And they say, OK, we got \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , we got the first black VP, the White House press secretary is going to be African American, but how does that change my lived experience in America? I heard that question over and over again. So, Fetterman promising if you give me the power, I will do this isn`t going to be enough because they say we`ve already seen what it looks like in the hands that were tied at the White House when you had the Senate and the House and the President -- the presidency?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Real quick.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Well, that`s sort of the question at hand for the Democratic Party too, right? Is it going with what Fetterman is trying to do in trying to win back some of the voters in areas that Democrats lost those strongholds like Luzerne County that was blue until Trump? Is that the strategy or is it what Yamiche is talking about and really appealing to these voters and not just promising but taking action and getting stuff done?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, then there`s the other door three, which is, is there an and rather than an or, right?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Well, that`s I think what Gisele might be trying to bring to be honest to the campaign.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And you -- and you bring an end to that equation, which is a good transition to my next guest, who I think is campaigning as hey, I`m in and if he`s listening right now. Yamiche Alcindor and Dasha Burns, thank you both. Joining me now is a Senate candidate we`ve been talking about quite a bit here. It`s Democratic Congressman \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 . And, Congressman, I`m sure you just heard that.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: It is this -- and I think the reason why I hear from a lot of the institutional support for you, Congressman, is there`s this idea that you can do both, that you`ll be able to appeal to voters that are necessary for the Democratic coalition in Philadelphia, and also appeal to some of the same voters that Fetterman is doing. But you tell me, that coalition doesn`t look like it`s coming together yet. What do you say?\par \par \uc2 REP. CONOR LAMB, (D-PA): Well, Chuck, it is coming together. I wish you would share higher-quality polling information with your viewers than the one you lead this segment with. I mean, you`ve been doing this long enough to know how low quality that is. So what the numbers currently are, who knows, but the fact is, we`ve put in the work with voters in Philadelphia and all over the state.\par \par \uc2 And one of the things that they know, Reverend Tyler knows this, other know it -- other people know it is, on the day after the primary, you have to step out into a larger world in Pennsylvania and you do have to win swing voters, you have to win independents, even moderate Republicans. That`s not a luxury item. It`s something you actually have to have to be successful.\par \par \uc2 I`m the only candidate in this race that has ever done it and I`ve done it in red areas. President Trump came to a town last Friday for his rally that I won by 500 votes the year after he won it by 500 votes. I`ve done that in a lot of parts of western Pennsylvania and that`s what will make me successful as our nominee.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think this is more of a myth that Fetterman connects more with these voters than you do?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: I just think it`s totally unproven is my point. You know, I have been in three elections in a row where I had to win swing voters in order to win. John has only ever been in Democratic primaries, you know. Here in our state, you don`t get to vote separately for the lieutenant governor in the general election, and so he`s never faced a Republican head to head in a general election. This is the most important general election our state is going to have in a really long time. The entire balance of the Senate is there.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m just asking people to consider what it means to be proven. And having spent time in these areas, I`ve come by my beliefs, honestly, a lot of the swing voters we need are not big liberals. They liked the fact that I served Marines or that I was in law enforcement wherever the case, but they agree with us on things like Social Security, and Medicare. And that is what I would make the focus of my campaign. That`s how you win in a state like Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I had someone say to me that they think your biggest problem is something you can`t control and that is that your first name became congressman, and that there`s this palpable disappointment with Democrats right now in Washington and that that is -- that has been a bit of an anchor around your ankles simply because you`re a member of that group. Do you accept that theory?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: Well, I like to tell people that I am the biggest optimist in the entire Pennsylvania Democratic Party so I`m not going to say that you`re wrong about the feelings people have about Washington. But the fact is, when I first ran for office, it was in a district that was supposed to be impossible to win. The way I`m being outraised and outspent now is nothing compared to what happened to me in that first race. We were outraised and outspent 10 to one. It -- but we just did the hard work every single day of showing up and answering people`s questions and I`ve done that as a member of Congress as well. So I`ve really learned a lot about our community, and the way people care about issues.\par \par \uc2 And all I`m trying to talk to the Democrats right now is, let`s not throw away what made us successful. You know, when I was first elected, \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2  was the Speaker, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  was the majority leader, and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was the president. None of those three things are true anymore. And it`s because all around the country, we got behind candidates like me who could appeal to this larger world, and we won, so let`s remember that and get ourselves more numbers to do all the things we want to do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is there something that the party could do now that would help not just your chances, but the party`s chances by November? What do you need to see done? Let`s assume you`re the nominee here. Let`s assume that our friends of Franklin and Marshall really whiffed on this one. What do you need -- what kind of action do you need from the Biden administration to sort of back you up when you`re on the campaign trail?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: Well, I think -- you know, your previous question had a grain of truth in it, which is that people are a little mistrustful of Washington and disappointed in it right now. So I don`t think you need a message coming from someone who is not me or coming from Washington. What people need is to get to know me and see, first of all, I`ve spent almost my whole life in this state. I`m devoted to it. I`m not an out-of-state multimillionaire like the Republicans that we`ll be running against.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 LAMB: And as a result of that, I know what people are going through. I can`t tell you how many people I`ve talked to already, who their entire life is about the fact that they cannot afford sufficient childcare for their children. Or I go to a lot of senior centers and I play cards and bingo with them. They are enraged that the Social Security cost of living increases do not cover the true cost of inflation. Those are two things right there where a majority of Pennsylvanians, not just the majority of Democrats are with us, and that will be the contrast I`ll make in this race with a bunch of out of state multimillionaires that don`t know these issues and they don`t understand what people are going through.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On the issue of abortion rights, where do you think most Pennsylvania voters are?\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 LAMB: I think most want Roe v Wade to be upheld and stay the same. I think that is totally uncontroversial. I mean as I told you, I ran in a very, very heavily red district my first time, and my position, then as now is Roe v. Wade needs to remain the same, people need to be able to choose for themselves, whether to get an abortion, you know, and that includes even in the difficult later stages of pregnancy, where it is almost all having to do with medically -- medical uncertainties. And I`ve been elected three times with that position in a pretty red area, so I`m confident that`s where a majority of people are.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It does seem as if like both parties are trying to define the other is extreme on this issue. Is there -- you know, Roe v. Wade, do you think - - do you think you need to be saying what limits that you would put on abortion in order to meet voters where you think they might be on this issue?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: I think you know, my policy is anyone that wants to ask me a question can ask, and if that`s what someone asked me, I`ll tell them, you know, what`s in the Women`s Health Protection Act, which is, you know, after viability it has to do with the health and safety of the mother. So there is a reasonable medical standard to apply. But in general, I think a lot of people just want the same rule that we`ve had for 50 years to stay in place and they may not want to talk about it a whole lot more than that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 LAMB: But what`s radical about what the Republicans are doing, is they`re overturning something people have counted on for 50 years, just like they`re overturning their support for voting rights and just like they want to hold a vote on whether to eliminate Social Security and Medicare within the next five years. I mean, they make me feel like a conservative sometimes because I want to conserve these things that I think have been so vital to our national life.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If you win this primary, it`ll be because why? What would be the explanation for you winning?\par \par \uc2 LAMB: Because I`m not afraid to go anywhere and answer people`s questions and that`s something John Fetterman has been afraid to do. And I think whether it`s the gun issue that you talked about, or others, part of the way that you overcome some of this cynicism and mistrust is to show up and answer people`s questions, take their feedback and fight for every single vote. And that`s what I`ve been doing every day.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Congressman \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 , a Democrat from Western Pennsylvania, really appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective. Be safe on the trail, sir.\par \par \uc2 LAMB: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You got it. Up next, we got much more on the future of the Democratic Party. We`ll have another candidate vying for the Democratic nomination here. The third strong candidate that people have been buzzing about for a while, Malcolm Kenyatta will join me later in the show. You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY. We`re live from historic Independence Hall in Philadelphia. It`s a great backdrop. Is there a better one? I don`t think so. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. We`re in the heart of Philadelphia this afternoon. The home, actually of one of the candidates running in the Democratic Senate nomination fight, it`s state representative Malcolm Kenyatta. In fact, his district is just a little north of where I am right now. Kenyatta is trailing Fetterman and Lamb in most of the polling we`ve seen, but his supporters say he`s in touch with what voters want and speaks to more real- life experiences, particularly of younger and progressive voters on the ground. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: And it feels like his experiences guide his values and his policy. And I think he also represents -- he is someone that I feel represents me the best being from the Philadelphia area, being young, being a part of the LGBTQI community. All of those things, I just feel like he represents me in the most and the best, and we`ll fight for the things that I want him to fight for.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, the Democratic Senate candidate, current Pennsylvania State Representative Malcolm Kenyatta joins me now. And I`m going to talk to the motorcycle only goes over my voice --\par \par \uc2 MALCOLM KENYATTA, DEMOCRATIC STATE REPRESENTATIVE, PENNSYLVANIA: So that`s a \uc2 Miami Heat\uc2  fan with their --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They got their show that -- well, I hope so. I`m not going to stand in that way. Let me ask you this and why -- and I`m going to sound like I`m advocating for your campaign but here`s what I`ve heard from various voters, hey, it`s progressives are picking between maybe you are Fetterman, then there`s those that are worried about who can win in the general and they`re picking between you and Lamb. There`s a common denominator here. Why so far do you feel like you`ve not been able to view the progressive electability argument?\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Well, you know, I think so much of this race has been about trying to handicap who`s going to win and not enough about who should and why. In this moment, it`s not going to be about what the pundits say this flawed idea that the race has been about two white guys from Pittsburgh and that only they are electable in this race. If we do not have record turnout in places like Philadelphia, in southeastern Pennsylvania, we will lose.\par \par \uc2 We have somebody running from Philadelphia in myself, who has spent every single day talking about what I`m going to do to deliver for working families, and why it matters to have somebody who`s not just asking for folk`s votes, but who understands their life. I grew up in a working-class family here in North Philly.\par \par \uc2 My parents separated when I was young. I got my first job at 12 washing dishes to help my mom make ends meet. Move six different places by the time I graduated high school. Burry both of my parents by the time I was 27. And have been talking about things that I know and things that voters know here in southeastern Pennsylvania, but also all across the Commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You said something to me off-camera that others have said to me on this trip to0. It is -- there like so many Democrats don`t understand Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You were saying who basically really at the Democratic consultant class, probably more so than most. Explain.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: You know what, if you are -- if you are a cynic, there`s always a job for you in politics. But this idea that Pennsylvania is just there are parts of our state that are just so racist, that they would never give somebody named Malcolm Kenyatta from North Philly, I kept --\par \par \uc2 TODD: The James Carroll (PH) descriptor of Pennsylvania, we won`t get into it but you`re basically you feel like that`s a virus that`s going around amongst some Democratic strategists.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: And it`s from people who actually haven`t spent time going to places like Curwensville, where I just was last week, where a bunch of people are voting for me are excited about my candidacy, watch the debates, and saw me go up against two career politicians, and really layout in a clear, fresh way, what is at stake and what we need to do for their families. I`m talking about America`s basic bargain.\par \par \uc2 This idea that you can have one good job backed up by a union, that your kids can go to a good school, that if you get sick, you can go to the damn doctor and fill the prescription, and then you can retire with the level of dignity. It`s not going to be good enough for Democrats to say we`re not the Republicans. I think people get that. The question is why should people trust us to go to Washington and deal with the Senate that has not worked?\par \par \uc2 And if we want to change the Senate and the way that I think we need to, we have to change the senators and I represent that fresh vision. And unlike the Lieutenant Governor, I am a progressive. And so for progressives watching, there`s one progressive in the race. They can vote for me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Like I said, I`ve run across a lot of people who seemed to be rooting for you, wanted to support you, and they`ve decided you can`t win. Let me play for you Reverend Tyler. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REV. MARK KELLY TYLER, MOTHER BETHEL AME CHURCH PASTOR: He is a Philadelphian, he`s African-American, he`s young, a lot of people outside of Philadelphia are not, you know, necessarily OK with who he is. I mean, even though in the city of Philadelphia we love him for who he is and we`d support him no matter what. But that is an issue in the general election.\par \par \uc2 JAIME BASSMAN, LAMB SUPPORTER: So I really like Malcolm a lot and I also like \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  a lot. For Malcolm, I was just a little concerned that he wasn`t gaining enough momentum in terms of like the polling and the fundraising.\par \par \uc2 TABITHA SSONKO, FETTERMAN SUPPORTER: He just has not made enough of an effort and a presence in the centrist part of the state. I am looking for a candidate who really is exciting all of Pennsylvania, not just the typical parts that we talk about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: If I were in your shoes I`d react to that. Go ahead. Boy, that`s a lot of punditry, right. It feels like more of an analytical argument. It`s not about issues.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Yes, I think that, you know, there is a self-fulfilling prophecy in some of that. The candidate that you vote for is the one that can win. And in this moment there there`s so much at stake the real question is who -- when this election is over, when you`re not coming here to talk about Pennsylvania politics for a little, and the election is over, people need to ask the question of who do they trust to actually go to the Senate and be a productive disrupter to the status quo and to actually fight for them.\par \par \uc2 People know that if they want somebody who`s going to be a champion for working-class people there`s one working-class person in this race. His name is Malcolm Kenyatta. You can go to my Web site and support our campaign and vote for me in six days.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Reverend Tyler said something else about Fetterman and the reason he`s supporting Lamb, right. He chose Lamb over you. But was this electability argument but his concern about Fetterman is that if he`s the nominee what super predator did to \uc2 Hillary Clinton\uc2  among black voters in this city, meaning they didn`t go vote for Trump they just didn`t vote.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That that`s his concern. That it will be a wet blanket. And he`ll have a hard time convincing his congregation. You may, if you`re not your nominee, have a hard time convincing your fellow Democrats to do this. Is he right?\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: You know, if people go look at the debates I was very clear to the lieutenant governor that what he did was wrong. And John doesn`t have to be a racist to be wrong. But he was dead wrong. And the fact that we`re still nine years later and he cannot say two simple words, I`m sorry, is an incredible problem. But if folks look at the support that we`ve earned, the woman who replaced him as mayor of Braddock endorsed me. OK. And I was just in Braddock two weekends ago.\par \par \uc2 If you go around of Braddock outside of just that one little block that it feels like a lot of people go to, you`ll see a lot of Malcolm Kenyatta signs. And so I think folks have really -- folks who`ve been covering this race have done a real disservice by frankly not covering my campaign that much and deciding a long time ago that this race was only about two people from western Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 This is going to be about what happens here in places like Philadelphia. And there is a reason that the president trusted me to be the voice and face of his campaign all across the country and he doesn`t just ask me to go to black churches, OK. He was sending me to rural Iowa again and again and again, where they`ve never met somebody named Malcolm Kenyatta from north Philadelphia but people understood that I was the person who can speak with a level of urgency and authenticity about what is broken.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans on the other side are out of state, they`re out of touch, they`re out of their damns minds. And what people want is somebody who they know is going to fight to pass the (INAUDIBLE), raise the minimum wage, who`s going to fight for a woman`s right to choose. Who is going to be bold on issues like expanding the Supreme Court and who is going to be somebody who`s actually been a legislature, which the lieutenant governor has not been, and also who has the lived experience which none of these folks have in this race.\par \par \uc2 Time and time again we talk about how black voters are the heart of the Democratic Party. How this whole debate is about how do we win working- class voters. And then when a working-class black candidate runs, everybody says we don`t know if they can win. But on the Republican side they might be on track to nominate an African-American woman.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Woman. Yes.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: And so folks think the Republicans won`t vote for a black candidate. They might be voting for one in just a couple of days.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Malcolm Kenyatta, it`s great to have you on.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Be safe on the trail.\par \par \uc2 KENYATTA: Thanks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, by the way, we were supposed to have an interview with Kathy Barnette. She pulled out. We hope to have one before the end of the week.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the latest test of the power of the Trump endorsement. We`ve got results from those two Republican races we told you about that we are watching pretty closely.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:03]\par \par \uc2 You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY. We`re live right here. You see what`s behind me. Philadelphia. We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. If it is Wednesday it means, at least in an even numbered year, it means we got some election results to digest. As we told you yesterday we were watching a couple of primaries in Nebraska and West Virginia as the latest in former President Trump`s power and influence over the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 Well, Trump went 1 for 2 in last night`s primaries in Nebraska. The Trump- backed candidate \uc2 Charles Herbster\uc2  lost the Republican governor`s primary to Jim Pillen. Herbster who faces credible allegations of groping at least eight women which he denies got 30 percent of the vote while Pillen got close to 34 percent. So Trump`s endorsement certainly gave Herbster probably a little more of a chance than he should have had given those credible allegations.\par \par \uc2 So not a landslide loss for the former president who was in Nebraska last week and stood by Herbster despite those allegations, perhaps always thinking about allegations that are made against him over the years.\par \par \uc2 But Trump did get a win in West Virginia where his pick, Congressman Alex Mooney, beat fellow Congressman David McKinley in a member-versus-member fight in a redrawn district. Trump chose Mooney simply because McKinley did two things that Trump didn`t like. One is he voted to certify the 2020 election and created an independent commission to investigate January 6th.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:06]\par \par \uc2 But the real sin apparently was Trump told him he would endorse his he opponent if he voted for the bipartisan infrastructure deal. Well, he voted for it, Trump kept his word, Joe Manchin did endorse McKinley but it didn`t work. More evidence of how Trump is trying to reshape the GOP.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn Hillyard is in West Virginia. And Vaughn, you say people told you that Trump`s endorsement was the deciding factor for them at the end of the day especially in a district that actually was more of a home game for McKinley than Mooney.\par \par \uc2 VAUGHN HILLYARD, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: It was. And in fact when we were at the polls we tried to gather a little bit more from folks who were voting for Mooney. Why they were voting for Mooney. But frankly, Chuck, I wish we had a livestream going for everybody to watch our interactions because ultimately the answer came down to Trump and what Trump says, and that is exactly what Trump wants here.\par \par \uc2 You know, I think about a month ago, Chuck, we were talking about some of these GOP incumbents around the country, kind of the big 12. Individuals who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  perceived as opposition forces within this Republican Party. One of those individuals was David McKinley because as you said he voted to certify the 2020 election and he voted for what would have been an independent commission to investigate the January 6th insurrection.\par \par \uc2 Well, so far, one month later, two of them, they`re already down. \uc2 Fred Upton\uc2 , shortly after you had him on your show, he announced his retirement, bowing out of a race that would have been a competitive primary against a Trump-backed candidate and now David McKinley is gone. Of course we`re going to look a month ahead to Tom Rice and Nancy Mace. They`re the next ones up on the list in South Carolina. They`re also threatened to be ousted from this Republican Party as Trump looks ahead to his potential 2024 presidential run.\par \par \uc2 Of course Herbster, the other race there, three-point difference. That`s how close. A man who just in the last month was accused by eight women of groping them including a state senator on the record who said that he had reached up in 2019 up her skirt.\par \par \uc2 I want to also, though, this is sort of breaking to us right now, Chuck. Well, you`re in Pennsylvania, we`re here in West Virginia. There is a new video that we just obtained actually out of Arizona, another Senate race that we are going to be looking at that is still crowded. Trump has yet to endorse in this race but one of the candidates who`s already made a trip to Mar-a-Lago is Blake Masters, the other \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2  candidate along with JD Vance, who`s been heavily financed by the billionaire.\par \par \uc2 I want to let you listen here. This is the first time we are airing this, anybody is hearing this. This was Blake Masters back in February when he was asked the question about the future of the Equal Rights Amendment. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BLAKE MASTERS (R), ARIZONA SENATE CANDIDATE: Believe it or not, women are not paid less in America than men. It`s a left-wing narrative, this gender pay gap. When you control for the occupations, you control for people taking time out to, you know, birth children, things are actually pretty equal. And men do the most dangerous jobs. Men are the ones who are -- the police officers. Not all of it but, you know, 80 percent of it in this country.\par \par \uc2 Men are the ones who are doing risky, you know, fishing, crab fishing in Alaska. Sometimes those jobs pay more. Sometimes those jobs pay more. And so I think we`ve got to push back on the left-wing narrative that women don`t have equal rights in this country.\par \par \uc2 I want women to have equal rights but we don`t need an Equal Rights Amendment that would actually just be a Trojan horse to give the government more power to implement \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 ` national gender strategy, right. This is what the left does and you have to resist it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HILLYARD: Now, Chuck, in this video here, this is what we`re talking about when we`re talking about the new Republican Party of today. And there is much we could tackle here around what you just heard from Blake Masters. But one of them, I think it`s important and fair to note, there is a gender pay gap here in America.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, there is a disparity between maternal leave and parental leave in this country. There`s a systemic and cultural divide between what companies have long offered their employees. And we should note, there is no maternal leave required by the federal government here, meaning, oftentimes women feel forced ultimately to choose child care over their careers.\par \par \uc2 And of course there has long been a pay disparity because of the way in which women have been not as eager to be promoted in the workforce over time which hits at the root of that pay disparity. These are the issues here that this Republican Party is coming to terms with here in these months ahead, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it is yet another example of perhaps explains why the gender gap seems to only grow as we see this divide between the two parties more so particularly among gender and it may only grow in this midterm cycle.\par \par \uc2 Vaughn Hillyard, on the trail for us. Vaughn, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Up next, how the abortion fight could scramble next week`s primary races. We`re going to hear from a group of conservative keystone state voters about where they stand after this.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY. We`re live here in Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:48:12]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. The leaked Supreme Court draft on Roe vs. Wade last week has made it clear that abortion rights will be a key issue in the Pennsylvania primary next week, more so than even I expected, I`ll be honest with you.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what Republican voters I spoke with yesterday told me organically, unprompted about the potential repeal of Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE HERRING, PENNSYLVANIA VOTER: I am pro -- I`m anti-abortion. And I`d like them to speak out a little bit about where he actually stands on it. If you have a life inside of you and someone stabs you in the stomach and kills it, at what point is it murder and at what point it is nothing. And I don`t think any woman would tell you the life inside -- it wasn`t murder.\par \par \uc2 SANDRA FINLON, PENNSYLVANIA VOTER: No, but I think it should be the woman`s choice.\par \par \uc2 HERRING: Well --\par \par \uc2 FINLON: And I think they should --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 FINLON: I respect everyone`s views. But I definitely am strongly, I am definitely pro-life with exception.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are you going to go into the ballot box thinking about that issue?\par \par \uc2 FINLON: Absolutely. Depending on who it is. And I`ve already thought about that for our governor race.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Interesting. Yes.\par \par \uc2 FINLON: Because there were two governors that came out that said I am pro- life with exception, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So the exception mattered to you.\par \par \uc2 FINLON: Yes, the exception mattered.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s just a reminder of what voters are listening for. It`s not about pro-life, pro-choice. They`re listening for a lot of the details. And this is why the threats of full bans I think make this politically such a potentially problematic issue for the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 We are going to have much more on all aspects of that race, what could changes its trajectory in the final six days with somebody who`s been following this race a lot longer than us. When we come back.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY live from Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:53:08]\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. As deep as we like to say we take things in politics, we`re going to go deeper and more local here. I`m joined by Lauren Mayk. She covers politics for our NBC station here in Philadelphia, WCAU.\par \par \uc2 And Lauren, you`ve been covering this primary when there were more candidates running, when there were more better candidates running, before we even knew a TV doctor was going to be running. So here we are less than a week out. Are you prepared for Oz-Fetterman? Is this that what we`re staring at here or is this that fluid?\par \par \uc2 LAUREN MAYK, NBC 10 PHILADELPHIA REPORTER: Could be, although, you know, when you look at the latest polls coming out, it looks like you`ve got a three-way race on the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 TODD: At least on the Republican side. Kathy Barnette.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: On the Republican side.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me about Kathy Barnette.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes, Kathy Barnette, I have interviewed her and she has been running for quite a while. She got in this race before Oz, before McCormick. She`s been running for about a year or so. So she has been sort of slowly building a base. She also is closely tied to \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who is running for governor.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: So --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re almost an official ticket of sorts.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Of sorts.\par \par \uc2 TODD: An alliance type of thing.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: An alliance of sorts. She`s appeared in an ad for him. He has endorsed her. They appear at events together. So you can --\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re pretty out there on the Trump stuff. Not Mastriano and Barnette, right. They don`t believe in the election results, is that fair?\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes, I`ve had a conversation with her about, you know, about the election, and sort of went back and forth with her trying to get information about whether she believes that the election was stolen.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: As I`ve had with multiple candidates to be fair. That is a conversation that we had.\par \par \uc2 TODD: She leans into it a little bit more, though? Is that fair to say?\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes. Yes. And she previously ran for Congress in Montgomery County. So she also has experience as a candidate, which gives her a little bit more name recognition. But really, just, she has been sort of building this.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s interesting, in talking to the group of voters we talked to in northeastern Pennsylvania yesterday, I got this sense that both Oz and McCormick, there`s still skepticism.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:06]\par \par \uc2 Are they for real? Are they really Trump guys? Are they really American firsters? Are they really -- I mean, the one voter they wanted to know what Dr. Oz`s real abortion position was. He`s just not sure. That`s how what sparked that conversation for him. And it seems like Kathy Barnette is benefiting from those who are like, well, I know where she stands.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: She is benefiting from people looking for an alternative. And I`ll tell you the airwaves here in the Philadelphia area had been full of Oz and McCormick. So if you are looking for someone who is not them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: I think Jeff Bartos may have thought that he might have benefited from that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: But it looks like she is benefiting from that right now, although what we don`t know is whether it will ultimately benefit Oz.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Because you now have two strong candidates splitting an anti-Oz vote.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me about the Philadelphia Democratic machine. Is it not going to deliver for Cobor Lamb?\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes. \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  has gotten quite a few endorsements in the Philadelphia area. I know that you have talked to Malcolm Kenyatta. He is from Philadelphia. And John Fetterman, the lieutenant governor, has a lot of support here but \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  has gotten a lot of the official endorsements.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: The Democratic Party here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Quote-unquote, "machine," if you want to call it that anymore, you know.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes. Yes. So the question is, will they be able to turn out for him, will they have people on the street for him. Malcolm Kenyatta will have a strong union, SCIU is backing him. They will be on the street for him. The black clergy of Philadelphia is backing \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 . They are telling me that they are going to have people getting his message out. So we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m going to be fascinated to follow the results just from Philadelphia. It`s going to be fascinating.\par \par \uc2 Lauren Mayk, it`s been great to have you as a colleague on this race so thank you.\par \par \uc2 MAYK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`re going to be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. That I promise you. We`ll be back in studio. MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur, who will be carrying President Biden`s remarks right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220512ei5b00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for May 10, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Cal Perry \par \uc2 8,216 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: And those are tonight`s unforgettable Last Words. THE 11TH HOUR with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, a government guaranteed to a woman`s right to choose the latest developments in the battle over abortion rights. Then, as the campaign cloud of the former president is tested yet again, the politics of a Trump return to Twitter, why some Democrats are saying, bring it on. And as missiles fall on Odessa, a top U.S. intel official warns, Putin may be preparing for the long haul in Ukraine as THE 11TH HOUR gets underway on this Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, good to be back with you. I`m Stephanie Ruhle. And just a few hours from now the battle over abortion rights is moving to the floor of the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . Tomorrow, lawmakers are expected to vote on a Democratic bill that would make that those rights federal law. The first big political test comes just one week after the bombshell leak of that draft opinion which will overturn Roe versus Wade. And Senate Democrats are making it clear, they`re linking tomorrow`s vote to the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 CHUCK SCHUMER\uc2 , (D) NEW YORK MAJORITY LEADER: We are making sure that every, every senator will have to vote and every, every American will see how they voted the Republican Party, the MAGA Republican Party will suffer the consequences electorally when the American people see that.\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 PATTY MURRAY\uc2 , (D) WASHINGTON: We are going to remember everyone who votes against the right to an abortion. And this November, we`ll use our voices and our votes. We`re not going to let anyone forget.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: They want it on record for all our eyes to see. Meanwhile, Pennsylvania Senator Bob Casey, a Democrat who has long supported abortion restrictions, today he said he will vote with his party in favor of legislation to protect abortion access. The only Democrat who has not announced where he stands, not a big surprise, West Virginia Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 , but even if every Senate Democrat votes yes, the bill is expected to fail since it would lack the 60 votes needed to defeat a Senate filibuster.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2 , (R) KENTUCKY MINORITY LEADER: Safe to say there aren`t 60 votes there at the federal level, no matter who happens to be in the majority, no matter who happens to be in the White House. So I think the widespread sentiment of my conference is this issue, will be dealt with at the state level.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: This is also a big election night, we`re watching key Republican primaries in West Virginia and Nebraska, both with races testing Trump`s hold over the party. The polls have now closed in both states. In West Virginia Congressman Alex Mooney, endorsed by Trump has defeated fellow Congressman \uc2 David McKinley\uc2  in the primary for the state`s newly drawn second congressional district.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Trump could soon be back posting those very endorsements, and you guessed it, Twitter. Billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  says if his purchase of the social media platform goes through, he plans to reverse the ban on the former president who was kicked off the platform after the Capital Riots. We`ll have a lot more on that development later in the hour.\par \par \uc2 We`re also following the latest developments about the January 6 committee. The panel`s hearings begin June 9, less than one month from tonight. Political reports there are plenty of tapes of witness depositions, and that they could potentially become part of hearings.\par \par \uc2 And the two \uc2 New York Times\uc2  reporters who wrote that new book, this will not pass, are releasing even more audio of Republicans reacting to January 6. In this newly released recording, Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  talks of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  uniting the country and then he criticizes \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2 , (R) SOUTH CAROLINA: He`s misjudged the passion, he plays the TV game and he went too far here. That rally didn`t help talking about primarying Liz [Cheney]. He created a sense of revenge.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It was a lie. And I told you we had a lot to cover tonight. So with that, let`s bring in our experts Phil Rucker, Deputy National Editor at the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 . He just won a another Pulitzer Prize, this one for public service for his team`s coverage of January 6. Congratulations to you, Phil. Yamiche Alcindor joins us, NBC News Correspondent and Moderator of Washington Week on \uc2 PBS\uc2 . And \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , Department of Justice Veteran and former Acting Solicitor General during the Obama Administration. He has argued dozens of cases before the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We have got our experts on this important night. Phil to you first, your paper is reporting that Republicans are expecting voters to care more about inflation than they do about abortion even if Roe gets overturned. And at the same time, even though the abortion bill is expected to fail, Democrats want every senators vote on record. Talk about the two strategies?\par \par \uc2 [23:05:07]\par \par \uc2 PHILIP RUCKER, THE WASHINGTON POST WHITE HOUSE BUREAU CHIEF: Well, Steph, the democratic strategy here on abortion is clear, they think this could politically be a winning issue for them, in that, if the Supreme Court were to overturn Roe v. Wade, it could potentially galvanize women and other voters who support abortion rights, to worry about this election, to get out to vote and to mobilize behind Democratic candidates. And so you`re seeing Senate Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  calling for this vote tomorrow in the Senate not because he thinks they`re going to be able to get the 60 votes required to break that filibuster, the math just doesn`t work for him there. But because he wants to put Republicans on record, all of those Republican senators who are going to vote no. He wants them on record and he wants to be able to use it as a campaign cudgel against them in this fall`s November midterm elections to point out to voters that the Republicans are going to endanger right to an abortion, and suggest even further, that they might endanger the right to contraception, the right to same sex marriage and a host of other cultural issues, where Democrats believe they`re firmly with the popular opinion of the country.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Neal, let`s assume tomorrow`s vote fails. Let`s assume Roe gets overturned, what could the Justice Department do, especially if some states that are banning abortion are trying to prosecute women who want to travel to other states to get the procedure?\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL, FORMER ACTING U.S. SOLICITOR GENERAL: So first, Steph, I don`t want to give up on tomorrow`s vote. You know, I think the Dems have not been particularly strong and in calling for this vote tomorrow, as those clips you were showing shows. I think the case is two things. Number one, this draft opinion is the most radical decision in our lifetimes that will set women back in ways that we`ve never seen in our lives and, you know, Roes a seven to two decision with five of the seven justices appointed by a Republican president. So that`s point one.\par \par \uc2 And point two is Republicans already nuked the filibuster. It`s done. They did it for three Supreme Court justices, which happened to be three of the five votes in that leaked draft opinion, it looks like. So if you can nuke the filibuster for the far greater thing of appointing for Supreme Court justices, you should be able to not use it for the more modest thing of just reversing one of the decisions that these three justices wrote.\par \par \uc2 So now the greater power includes a lesser and it`s really ironic to me that \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  of all people is talking about 60 votes, since he`s the one who got rid of the filibuster in the first place on the far more greater thing of Supreme Court justices.\par \par \uc2 Now, with respect to your question about travel. Yes, some states are contemplating laws that would ban their citizens from traveling across the border, to get -- across state borders, to get an abortion in another state. You know, in a normal time, that would violate the constitution. There`s a unenumerated right to travel clause, right to travel, that people think it`s in the Constitution, but the radicalism of that draft opinion, last week, I think, would say that doesn`t exist. And so you would need I think a federal law or something like that, to try and sweep away to preempt the state travel restrictions will obviously be court cases as well, in a normal world. Those would be successful, but we`re not in a normal world.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We certainly are. Yamiche, last week, we talked to you when you were in Louisiana, and Mississippi talking to people about abortion. Tonight, you`re in Pennsylvania, one week ahead of that swing states primaries. Is abortion on voters radar?\par \par \uc2 I think we don`t have Yamiche`s audio at least -- I do not, YAMICHE, I think you`re muted.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, PBS WASHINGTON WEEK MODERATOR: Can you hear me now?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Here we go. I hear you now.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Of course, I`m like up late and -- so sorry about that. I`m in Pennsylvania talking to a lot of voters. I`ve talked to young black voters in Pittsburgh. I also talked to union workers, and sat in on a Town Hall for the \uc2 United Steelworkers\uc2 . And one thing that sticks in my mind from that town hall where union leaders were talking about the fact that workers` rights and health care should be top of mind. They said that the cultural issues are what the rank and file members, many of them who, of course, have now moved to the Republican Party after abandoning the Democratic Party largely after the messages of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . They said that those workers are focused on the three weeks and I said what are the three G`s and they say they`re God, Guns and Gynecology. So they`re the, you have this sort of a simple way to explain what voters are talking about to me.\par \par \uc2 We should also note though, that when you talk about sort of overturning precedent, I was sitting in on a meeting where a union worker said, OK, you think about Roe v. Wade in 1973, and it being overturned. They were also worried that the 1935 Wagner Act, which is an act that said that companies had to bargain and had to negotiate with collective bargaining had to negotiate with unions. They worry that that can be overturned because right essentially the Supreme Court is showing that they can overturn long standing precedent. So, that`s very interesting to hear that you`re seeing union members say, what the Supreme Court can do this to women`s rights, what else can they do to workers` rights?\par \par \uc2 [23:10:12]\par \par \uc2 But overall, workers are still very much, really very much leaning toward Republicans because of what President Trump and a lot of candidates are saying to them about inflation and about their jobs, and about culture. And Democrats are really scrambling to try to hold on to some of those union workers.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: God, guns and gynecology that has a bumper sticker I never want to see. But alas, here we are. Phil, we`ve been watching the results in tonight`s West Virginia primary, let`s talk about this, because the Republican that last is the one who voted for the infrastructure plan. And let`s remind our audience, West Virginia ranks one of the lowest, it`s like 48 or 49, in terms of infrastructure, who wins the super Trumper. So tell us what`s most important to the West Virginia voter? Is it standing with Trump or is it supporting things that the state needs the most?\par \par \uc2 RUCKER: You know, it`s interesting that that clearly, standing with Trump is more important, at least to the people who turned out today to vote in that Republican primary in West Virginia. But it`s a real change for that state because West Virginia Remember, it`s the place of Robert C. Byrd, who was famous in the Senate for years and years and years of bringing pork back home to his state, of bringing money for roads, for bridges. For an interstate highway system linking all of those mountain towns in West Virginia, he brought an outsized amount of federal resources to his state, and that`s how he kept winning reelection and being popular there.\par \par \uc2 But the political mindset of so many Republican voters has changed where fealty to former President Trump is paramount above almost any other issue. And so the other candidate who failed tonight was in fact advocating for the infrastructure program. He was advocating for a more bipartisan approach in order to get things done for his state. But that was not enough to carry the day with Republican voters who wanted fealty and allegiance to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yamiche, take us back to Pennsylvania, because this one`s a head scratcher. Republican, Pennsylvania Senate candidate David McCormick, he did not get Trump`s endorsement. After going to Mar-a-Lago kissing the ring asking for it, Dr. Oz did. But I want you to listen to his latest ad.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , (R) FORMER UNITED STATES PRESIDENT: Dave, I want to congratulate you. You`ve served our country well in some many different ways.\par \par \uc2 DAVE MCCORMICK\uc2 , SENATE CANDIDATE: You know why he said that, because it`s true. I`ve risked my life for America. And I do it again in a heartbeat. I came home and helped create hundreds of Pennsylvania jobs. I`m a pro-life, pro-gun, America first conservative. And damn proud of it. I`m \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  and I approve this message. And I`m asking for your vote to save this great country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Why is he still hanging his hopes on Trump and pro-life? How much has he talked about that? Because let`s remind our audience, David McCormack until very recently, this guy from Greenwich, Connecticut in New York City, his de facto campaign manager is his wife, Dina Powell, a huge advocate for women`s issues. She founded \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , 10,000 women business, and right now \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  is one of those companies contemplating paying their employees supporting them if they need to travel to another state to get the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Well, David McCormick, has been very vocal that he is pro-life. He said it multiple times, including during the debate just last month, saying that he does not believe that abortion should be legal unless it`s for very rare cases where a woman`s life is at risk. So it doesn`t sound like he`s making exceptions for other things like incest and rape. So it doesn`t it makes it very clear that in some ways he is tacking, much like the Republican Party is right now much like the Supreme Court decision that leaked poised Supreme Court decision.\par \par \uc2 The other thing to note is when David McCormick, is showing video of former President Trump and really trying to in some ways, give voters the idea that Trump has endorsed him. He`s doing what so many other Republicans around the country are doing. They`re trying to tell voters, I`m going to be Trump, like, I`m going to worship at the altar of Trump, I`m going to be as Trumpian as possible, please vote for me. Even if they didn`t get the endorsement. So you see, of course, Dr. Oz is the one here in Virginia -- in Pennsylvania that got the endorsement from President Trump, from former President Trump. But David McCormick is really trying to do as much as possible to keep himself close to former President Trump.\par \par \uc2 I should also know that Chuck Todd asked David McCormick straight on, how are you going to win this election without the endorsement of President Trump and he would not answer and sort of walked away. So that`s also tells you about the pressure that David McCormick is facing in the state.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Sure is. Phil, we also have new tapes from \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  after January 6, he seems to suggest that Biden will bring the nation together and he`s very critical of Trump. You know we heard a very similar thing from Kevin McCarthy and his tapes at any point do these displays of truth from Republicans, did they end up being a real problem for the GOP? Or do they just keep moving these to the side because you don`t hear these tapes on Fox News at night?\par \par \uc2 [23:15:17]\par \par \uc2 RUCKER: You certainly don`t, Steph. And it`s not just these tapes, remember the speech that \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  gave on the floor of the Senate, the night of January 6, where he basically said he was finished with Trump that this journey of a friendship he had with the president was over because of the way the President incited that deadly riot and attack on the Capitol. And yet a fast forward a few days, and \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  is on Air Force One, flying around with Trump trying to be his best friend again. And that`s the story of the Republican Party. There are these moments of truth that emerge from Republican leaders where they reveal what we all know, they really believe about Trump, which is that he`s unfit to be president, and frankly, kind of a crazy person. And yet, they won`t actually follow through with that, with actions and in their words, over the long run because of the power that Trump has politically and the connection that he has to so many millions of Republican voters around the country.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Neal, I know the January 6 hearings next month are hugely important. It`s how Democrats think they`re going to defeat Republicans in the midterms. But should they be focused on other elections? There are actual January 6 participants that are currently back in their hometowns running for local office. Should individual -- should those races be getting a lot more attention? Imagine the January 6 rider becoming an election official?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Absolutely, Steph. I mean, the idea that those folks will kind of come into our government. I mean, the whole point of the 14th Amendment in Section Three was to avoid having insurrectionists in our government, and yet, it seems like there`s a concerted effort by these people and vote deniers to, you know, to basically put them back into office. It`s part of a Republican strategy. And I just wanted to say one other thing on the whole question you were asking Phil and Yamiche about Trump`s effect on \uc2 Dave McCormick\uc2  and other candidates in a way it`s like, I mean, it`s -- that`s true for the primary, but I wonder about Trump`s sway in the general election. I mean, to me, Trump looks like the human equivalent of the decision to overturn Roe. It`s going to excite your face, but at the risk of horrifying everyone else.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, we`ll soon find out. Phil Rucker, Yamiche Alcindor, \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , thank you all for starting us off this evening.\par \par \uc2 When we come back, it is proven to be a lot more war in Ukraine than Putin bargained for. But this country`s top spy warns Putin is not about to back down.\par \par \uc2 And later, it`s what Trump supporters wanted to hear. But why Democrats might want them the former guys returned to Twitter, THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on this very busy Tuesday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:22:37]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AVRIL HAINES, U.S. DIRECTOR OF NATIONAL INTELLIGENCE: We assess President Putin is preparing for a prolonged conflict in Ukraine during which he still intends to achieve goals beyond the Donbas.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That warning from the country`s top intelligence officer today who told the \uc2 Senate Armed Services Committee\uc2  that Putin is now preparing for a prolonged conflict in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Tonight, the house agreed to send Ukraine nearly $40 billion in additional military and humanitarian aid as Putin`s forces continue to hammer Ukrainian cities. NBC`s Cal Perry is on the ground and Kyiv and filed this report.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Stephanie, civilians paying the ultimate price in the eastern part of the country. In the Donetsk Region, we understand that 13 villages coming under Russian shelling last 24 hours at least 12 people killed, another seven people wounded.\par \par \uc2 In Kharkiv to the northern part of the country, fierce on the ground fighting. Ukrainian soldiers taking back at least four villages, we understand from Russian control, with Russian troops pushing back in two separate villages.\par \par \uc2 And in the southern part of the country, we see Russia`s air campaign at least 25 missiles according to the Ukrainian president fired at the city of Odessa in just a two day period. At least one person killed there, another seven wounded. And what we understand was an airstrike on the mall.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian President going out of his way to talk to world leaders about what he says is a growing food crisis around the world and the \uc2 World Food Program\uc2  agrees with him. According to the Director, Stephanie, of the \uc2 World Food Program\uc2 , all of the grain silos here in Ukraine are full, enough food to feed some 400 million people around the world. But, of course, Ukraine cannot export any of it. The seven port cities along the Black Sea are under fierce fighting, if not heavy bombardment, like the city of Odessa. There are 44 million people "on the march to starvation." According to the director of the \uc2 World Food Program\uc2 , it is as he said, a crisis on top of a crisis when you factor in the rising food prices. Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Our thanks to Cal Perry. With that, let`s bring in Michael McFaul, former U.S. Ambassador to Russia and MSNBC International Affairs Analyst and author of the book From Cold War to Hot Peace: An American Ambassador in Putin`s Russia. And Julia Davis, Columnist for The Daily Beast, the Creator of the Russian Media Monitor, she watches Russian State TV, so we do not have to.\par \par \uc2 Michael, I want to start right there with what Cal was talking about. Russia now targeting the port of Odessa. Talk to us about how important it is to get this back open and what it will cause in terms of a global hunger catastrophe if it`s not?\par \par \uc2 [23:25:08]\par \par \uc2 AMB. MICHAEL MCFAUL, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO RUSSIA: Well, you just heard Cal`s excellent reporting, I think it`s one of the stories that are not getting enough attention about this horrible war. The ports closed, I just talked to one of the major port owners a couple of days ago. It`s going to take a long time to get it back online. All the other ports, Mariupol, of course, we know is under siege. And it`s literally starving people around the world.\par \par \uc2 So Mr. Putin`s war in Ukraine is not just killing people in Ukraine, he`s killing people around the world. There`s no reason he had to close the port of Odessa and the other ones around them. There were there were other military means to do that. He chose to do that because he`s trying to strangle the Ukrainian economy.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Is this Putin`s way of launching a war against the world?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: That`s a good question. I don`t know the answer. I have to say, \uc2 Avril Haines\uc2 , her testimony today that you just reported on was deeply disturbing to me. Because up until I heard Avril say that I used to work with her at the White House, and she`s the one of the best we have, her and \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2  at the CIA. We have the all-star team in charge of intelligence right now. But just a few days ago, we were all talking about -- and by the way, I watch Russian television too, not everybody has to, but as you`ll talk about remember, it was special military operation, (foreign language), right, in defense of Donbass, that was a new phrase they`ve been using on Russian television, to suggest that the war is just going to focus on Donbass. Avril today suggested otherwise. And that`s scary. That means that Putin is not done with this the Donbass he has plans to move further into Ukraine, whether he can or not, that`s a different question. Intentions and capabilities are two different things, but that he wants to, I found to be very disturbing today.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Julia, your reporting says that the grim reality for Russia is so obvious. Even Putin`s hardcore supporters have to admit his army has been a total embarrassment. What is Russian State TV saying?\par \par \uc2 JULIA DAVIS, CREATOR, RUSSIAN MEDIA MONITOR: Even the Russian state TV is now prepping the audience`s that this will be a long and painful conflict. They`re trying to draw the parallels to World War Two, and explain that the Ukrainian territory is so large, and the resistance is so fierce that this will take a long time. And also, there were clues and Putin`s own speech on May 9 when he claimed that the Russian troops are fighting on their own land when he referred to Ukraine. And as we know, they`re carrying out attacks still all over Ukraine. And this is unfortunately not going to and fast from all indications.\par \par \uc2 But even on state media, military analysts are admitting that Russian economy is not capable of maintaining this kind of a burden. And they were not prepared for a conflict of this magnitude, which is why it was called a special operation. Putin saw it as something that would turn out relatively painless and quick as Crimea was minimal resistance with minimal sanctions, that he would be able to finagle his way out of our wait for the West to move on. And now he couldn`t declare war in my opinion, that would be admitting defeat, because that would mean he is not capable to achieve the goals of this operation without declaring a full on war. So I doubt he will do that. But at the same time, it is war. And it unfortunately is going to drag on from everything that we`re seeing so far.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Julia, we covered something last night, I wanted to ask you about Reuters reporting that someone hacked into Russian Satellite TV on Victory Day, and sent viewers this message, "You have the blood of 1000s of Ukrainians and hundreds of dead children on your hands. TV and the authorities are lying. No to war.\par \par \uc2 People see these protests, OK, you cannot escape the growing number of dead soldiers. At what point are the Russian people going to say, enough is enough. We know you`re lying to us, end this war?\par \par \uc2 DAVIS: You know, the kind of regime Putin built even though people are protesting and they are speaking out, but the system is so brutally, crushing them, oppressing them, imprisoning them, and also silencing any news about it because he totally controls the media, that we don`t even hear about much of what`s going on. But there are definitely many signs that people are rebelling speaking out. There`s an enormous outflow of Russians leaving the country so people are doing what they can the word is getting in, a lot of people have VPNs, a lot of people have relatives in Ukraine and even on their news, they`ve started showing a lot more than what they did before. And they`re showing statements from our government officials that they`re, of course, misrepresenting in a different light.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:20]\par \par \uc2 But thinking people are clearly getting the message by now that what they`re witnessing is a brutal war. And some of them are motivated by fear not to do anything about it. Some of them are buying into propaganda, but there are Russians that are speaking out and protesting and we should definitely not overlook that. There was in fact, the news website called Lenta, where the former editors had posted a number of articles talking about the brutality of this war, and they fled the country themselves. But as a parting shot, they spread the news and spread the word. So hats off to them. Their names are Egor Polyakov and Alexandra Miroshnikova. And that`s just one example that the Russians are doing things to speak out and many of them realize brutal injustice of what Putin is doing to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ambassador, do you believe that Putin is starting to feel the pressure from within?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, I think, Julia there makes an excellent points about this Russian society`s way more divided about this war than I think is the conventional wisdom here in the United States. We look at these opinion polls, opinion polls in Russia`s dictatorship, like if somebody calls you on the phone from Moscow, and you`re sitting in Vladivostok and says, hey, my name is Volodya (ph), Natasha (ph), what do you think of Putin? There`s only one rational answer to that. I support him.\par \par \uc2 But if you look at the numbers, you look at, like opposition figures, look at the Navalny YouTube Channels, they have millions of viewers now. They`re rivaling Russian state television in terms of numbers of viewers, I think that tells you something bigger is going on.\par \par \uc2 By the way everybody should follow, Julia`s Twitter feed. That`s where I actually get most of my news about Russian news. So thank you for that public service that you do. That all said, that does not mean there`s pressure on Putin, which is to say, yes, there could be millions. There are 10s of millions of Russians that don`t support this war. But they don`t -- it`s not a democratic system. They don`t have a way to express that. And so far, I don`t see it putting pressure on Putin to stop the war, maybe in the future, but not yet.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Navalny`s \uc2 YouTube\uc2  channels, millions of viewers while he sits in prison. Ambassador Michael McFaul, Julia Davis, thank you both for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, during the 4267 days he was allowed on the social media platform. Former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  posted 57,000 tweets. Why some of his harshest critics are saying come on back, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:37:43]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK: It was not correct to banned on Trump. I think that was 1000 mistake because it alienated a large part of the country and did not ultimately result in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not having a voice. He is not going to be on Truth Social. Banning Trump from Twitter didn`t end Trump`s voice. It will amplify it among the right and this is why it is morally wrong and flat out stupid.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: He`s on Truth Social and not getting much traction. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said today he would reverse Trump`s permanent ban on Twitter if his deal to buy the social media giant goes through.\par \par \uc2 With us tonight to discuss Maria Teresa Kumar, the President and CEO of Voto Latino, also an MSNBC Contributor. Maria, always good to see you. Trump has already said that he will not return to Twitter. But if he does, who should be more worried Democrats or Republicans?\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, VOTO LATINO PRESIDENT & CEO: I would say the most -- the people that are most worried are the people that have been impacted by what Trump did on Twitter. And what I refer to that, Stephanie, is that the El Paso shooter was inspired by what the President said and sadly, got into a car and drove 10 hours to have the most -- the biggest terrorist attack in our nation`s history since the Oklahoma City, bombing. This man, if you recall, in 2020, killed almost 23 people because he had been inspired by what Trump had said about the Mexican invasion.\par \par \uc2 We all need to look at what happened in Pittsburgh with the synagogue where there was a massive killing, again, people that were inspired by President Trump. Words have consequences. He was using his position on Twitter and elsewhere to encourage violence, dismissiveness and to break up the country in ways that we have not seen. And so when \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  speaks, Stephanie, saying that we need to make sure that President Trump has a space to ensure that the rest of the country can hear him because he has followers. Those followers have a tendency of being extremely violent and causing harm. It translates from what is said online to real life violence. So I encourage \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  to talk to the most marginalized among us who are incredibly vulnerable to the hate speech that happens when Trump is on Twitter.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:14]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s talk about somebody else`s words. Trump`s former Defense Secretary \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 , he`s continued his book tour today, and I want to share a bit of what he said to our colleague, Nicolle Wallace.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , FORMER DEFENSE SECRETARY: Not everybody who served in the Trump administration was a bad person, and it goes back to, there were a lot of good people serving, not because they were there to serve \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , or were the Republican Party, they were there to serve the country. We need good people to serve. Because look, if good people don`t serve, who you left with?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, even if what he`s saying is absolutely right. Hallelujah. Praise the Lord. I want to know who is the target audience for Mark Esper`s book, for \uc2 Deborah Birx\uc2 ` book, for Bill Barr`s book, it`s clearly not Trump supporters. And people who don`t support \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  aren`t buying into any of this. They`re saying, why the heck didn`t you do anything when you were in office? Nobody wants to hear about it now. Who are they writing these books for?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: For themselves, and to the idea that it`s a very lucrative position to come out of an administration as volatile and divisive as Trump was, and to have a book deal and then go on tour. And shame on those folks that pay to listen to those lectures. Because, yes, good people can be in these visit positions of power. But if you don`t use it to warn the American public during the time in office, where he could have been removed for causing great harm, they are complicit. It`s oftentimes says, well, you know, people feel better at night, sometimes saying, well, I`m there for all the good reasons, but it`s not all good reasons. If you wait until the last, you know, until the very end, after all is done, and then you`re trying to make a buck. We could agree on that, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, let`s talk about the very end, before they left office. Because Esper, Barr, especially \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , they have all been enablers and apologist for a very long time. But in the end, when Trump wanted to overturn the election, did they save us from a lot worse? \uc2 Matt Gaetz\uc2  and Jim Justice were in those jobs -- Jim Jordan?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Well, I mean, we could say -- right, yeah, we can speculate. But at the same time, when it came, I`m not just asked for in that, but we also the Republicans, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , McCarthy, they were all recognizing that this president was unstable, but instead of giving the -- you know, using their authority and warning the American people that this man is unstable and said they`re either in book tours, or now, you know, backtracking and giving him \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  a space to potentially run again for 2024, a twice impeached president, one because he was trying to exploit a Ukraine in order to get and go after a presidential candidate, his opponent. And two, he was impeached because he was trying to overthrow a fair free election. He shouldn`t be eligible for office. Instead, they should make sure that the DOJ is doing their job and ensuring that he`s actually going to jail for the crimes that he`s committed against the country.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, don`t hold your breath on that one. Maria Teresa Kumar, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, what President Biden is saying today about the fight against inflation, the challenges ahead and what he might be able to do about it when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:51]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: I want every American to know that I`m taking inflation very seriously, and it`s my top domestic priority.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Despite President Biden`s attention to the issue, this is what you need to remember, presidents have very limited power to curb inflation. As the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  points out, Biden`s efforts have done little to tame inflation, which is running at its fastest pace in 40 years, the result of snarled supply chains that have led to shortages of goods, the war in Ukraine, which is driving up energy prices and rapid consumer demand. Consumer demand, that means we have disposable income and we`re out there spending.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Damian Paletta, Economic Editor for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 . Damian, we`re getting new inflation numbers tomorrow. But even without that data, we know inflation is bad. People are suffering. Wages are up, but prices are going up faster. So what is the President saying about it? What is he doing about it?\par \par \uc2 DAMIAN PALETTA, THE WASHINGTON POST ECONOMIC EDITOR: I mean, I think what he`s trying to do is tell Americans that he feels their pain, he understands, you know, what many Americans especially lower income and middle class Americans are experiencing and he`s doing everything he can to address it. Yeah, he`s talked a lot about, you know, cracking down on companies for price gouging, trying to break up monopolies. He wants to expand the Affordable Care Act to bring the price of health care down. He wants to invest in clean energy to make give Americans more options in terms of, you know, these really high gasoline prices.\par \par \uc2 So I think he`s, you know, saying he wants to do a lot, but a lot of these things are hard for him to do by himself. I felt like we were starting to make some progress on inflation before the Russian invasion of Ukraine, which obviously drove gas prices really high and created a major problems with wheat and grain all over the world. So I think, you know, that was like a terribly timed invasion on many levels. And it`s only now we`re kind of looking into the rest of the year, but the really high prices on a number of products and there seems to be no relief in sight.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Republicans want to capitalize on this. Florida Republican Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  today calling on the president to step down over inflation. But here`s the thing. What are Republicans offering on the other side? Biden`s Economic Adviser Gene Sperling reminded us tonight what`s actually in the Republican plan. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:09]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GENE SPERLING, SENIOR ADVISER TO PRESIDENT BIDEN: What`s the Republican plan? The only plan that we see out there is from Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2 , who is not just any senator, he`s head of the Republican Senatorial Committee, he put out a plan that was praised by the chairwoman of the Republican Party that basically called for a $2,000 tax increase on every lower moderate income family with children and virtually half of the smallest small businesses.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So I get people are not happy with the price of things under Biden, do they realize what Republicans are offering them on the other side?\par \par \uc2 PALETTA: Well, I think it`s interesting to watch how uncomfortable many Republicans are with Rick Scott`s plan. And as Gene Sperling pointed out, I mean, \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  has not any senator, he`s the head of the Senate, you know, the Republican campaign to get senators reelected. But you know, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is very uncomfortable with this plan, and so many others. I mean, unfortunately, as we know, that really -- the only way to combat inflation is through the Fed raising interest rates. And we`ve seen an incredible amount of stock market turbulence in the past few weeks that, you know, the NASDAQ, which is a lot of technology companies, has been falling sharply. And that might be a way to get inflation down as for the stock market to really, you know, shed many, many points. But that`s going to be painful for a lot of Americans too. And so there`s going to be, it`s going to be really hard for there to be a soft landing here. We`re going to have to kind of brace ourselves, maybe they`re going to be able to get gas prices down a little bit, but they reached a record high today. And I think, you know, neither party seems to be in complete control. And there`s a lot of finger pointing right now. And it`s really unfortunate, because a lot of Americans are suffering.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But there`s also a lot of economic winds, right? Look at jobs, look at wages, look at the deficit, for example, which in other times people care an awful lot about, except now when it`s going down, does the President get no credit for that?\par \par \uc2 PALETTA: That`s a great point. I mean, we are at 3.6% unemployment, which is amazing. It`s great. And we`re adding over 40,000 jobs a month for the last 12 months. And I`ve never lived through anything like that before. So yes, you know, inflation is rough on a lot of Americans. But the fact that so many people have jobs is tremendous. Stock market has come down a bit, but it was at a historic high. There`s a lot of positive things in the economy that we should kind of celebrate. And I think, you know, as we the next few weeks play out, and we see you have a situation in Russia and Ukraine goes, we`ll know a lot more about whether some of this inflation is just temporary or something that`s going to be longer lasting. But when you have 3.6% unemployment and so many Americans with jobs, and so many Americans who have great equity in their homes, like they didn`t maybe a couple of years ago, that gives us a lot more to kind of work with heading into something like this than the alternative.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: All right, Damian Paletta, thank you for making us smarter tonight.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, our 11TH HOUR one-on-one, my exclusive interview with \uc2 Airbnb\uc2  CEO and Founder \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2  on his prediction that work from home is here to stay.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BRIAN CHESKY\uc2 , \uc2 AIRBNB\uc2  CEO AND FOUNDER: My prediction is this is where the world`s going to be 10 years from now. And all the CEOs hauling their employees back to the office. That`s their prerogative. But here`s what I would say. The companies with the best talent will usually win. And if you are limiting your talent to a commuting radius, you`re not going to have the best people because the best people will live everywhere.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You`re going to want to catch our exclusive conversation tomorrow night with \uc2 Brian Chesky\uc2  right here on THE 11TH HOUR.\par \par \uc2 And right now we`ve got breaking news in the other primary we`re watching this evening. NBC projects, Jim Pillen has won the Nebraska Republican primary for Governor, Trump endorsed businessman \uc2 Charles Herbster\uc2  in that race, he`s a longtime Trump ally, who was in Washington with Trump`s family during the rally that took place before the January 6 attack. Herbster is the first candidate endorsed by Trump to lose a Republican primary in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, our friends of The Daily Show with their own unique take on former Trump officials cashing in, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:16]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ESPER: It`s important to our country. It`s important to the Republic, the American people that they understand what was going on in this very consequential period, the last year of the Trump administration and to tell the story about things we prevented really bad things dangerous things that could have taken the country in a dark direction.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Understand now. The last thing before we go tonight, cashing in, as we mentioned \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  who you saw right there is the latest Trump official to be criticized for waiting to share such important information until he was selling his own book. Well, Esper says, Trump suggested shooting Black Lives Matter protesters 