ed groper with the MAGA nod. Tonight, a new test of the Trump brand and what it all means for November. Then --\par \par \uc2 TRUMP\uc2 : I`ll take on anybody you want with regard to Ruby Freeman.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: An update on the election worker targeted in 2020. Plus, the fight for Roe. Senator Stabenow on what the Senate is trying to do now.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEBBIE STABENOW (D-MI): Women have a right to make our own health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: And is American democracy able to hold with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  returning to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, CEO, \uc2 SPACEX\uc2  AND TESLA: It was not correct to ban \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think that was his mistake.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: When ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ (on camera): Good evening from New York. I`m Alicia Menendez in for Chris Hayes. And it is Election Night in America. Tonight, there are major primary contests in Nebraska and West Virginia. It is the next big test for Trump endorsed candidates as the ex-president seeks to tighten his grip on the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 We all saw what happened last week in Ohio and Trump`s candidate J.D. Vance surge to an unexpected victory after Trump`s endorsement. And when Trump`s former aide Max Miller win a contested primary to replace Republican Congressman \uc2 Anthony Gonzalez\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gonzalez was of course one of just 10 Republicans who voted to impeach trump the second time. And Miller won despite being accused of slapping his ex-girlfriend, former Trump White House official \uc2 Stephanie Grisham\uc2 . He denies those allegations.\par \par \uc2 So, in many ways, the Republican Party is still the party of Trump. And boy has he backed quite a candidate for Nebraska`s governor. His name is \uc2 Charles Herbster\uc2 . He`s a conservative businessman who`s donated more than $1 million to Trump`s campaigns. He also attended Trump`s Stop the Steal rally at the Ellipse on January 6. You know, the one where he incited the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 And there are the sexual assault allegations from eight women that Herbster grope them, including when he served as a beauty pageant judge. One of the allegations comes from Nebraska Republican State Senator Julie Slama.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JULIE SLAMA, NEBRASKA REPUBLICAN STATE SENATOR: I was groped at a political event by someone who is not a member of this body and not a current or former officeholder.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: That`s Republican state senator Julie Slama speaking on the Florida legislature back in February. Thursday, she spoke up more saying in a statement she was not going to deny the truth that \uc2 Charles Herbster\uc2 , a candidate for governor, sexually assaulted her.\par \par \uc2 She told the Nebraska Examiner`s Aaron Sanderford it happened at this event in 2019 at the Douglas County Republican Party event called Elephant Remembers that brings in local politicians, donors, and everyday Republicans. Slama, a first-year state senator at the time, confirmed that Herbster reached up her skirt without consent and touched her. An unnamed witness backed up the story to the Nebraska Examiner.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The Nebraska Examiner reports another woman alleges "Herbster once cornered her privately and kissed her forcibly." The others allege, "Herbster touched them inappropriately when they were saying hello or goodbye to him or when they were posing for a photograph by his side." These allegations date back just five years from 2017 to now. All eight women say they were in their late teens or early 20s at the time of the assault. And the examiner says it has independently corroborated six of the incidents with an outside witness.\par \par \uc2 Herbster denies the allegations. He has called them libelous, fake news. He has filed a defamation suit against the state senator who says he reached up her skirt. And he is even running an attack ad arguing the accusations are part of a political hit job from his opponent, Jim Pillen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: And even after the supposedly incident, if she kept contacting Herbster, texts, calls, meetings, even invited Herbster to her destination wedding. Jim Pillen`s attack on Herbster built on lies.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Perhaps unsurprisingly, Trump has come to Herbster`s defense saying, "He is innocent of these despicable charges." Not too long ago, serious allegations like this would have been enough to end a campaign. But those days appear to be over. Allegations of sexual misconduct not enough to end Trump`s career and it`s his party now. And so tonight, a man who was at the Stop the Steal rally, a man accused of sexual assault by multiple women, is on the ballot with Trump`s endorsement to be Nebraska`s next Republican Governor.\par \par \uc2 [20:05:09]\par \par \uc2 Jane Kleeb\uc2  is the chair of the Nebraska Democratic Party and she joins me now. Jane, this is a close race as I understand it. The Nebraska Examiner has a new analysis of a forecasting model that gives Herbster a 44 percent chance of winning, Pillen a 42 percent chance of winning, Lindstrom a 14 percent chance. What does it say, Jane, about the Nebraska Republican Party that Herbster might still win this nomination?\par \par \uc2 JANE KLEEB\uc2 , CHAIR, NEBRASKA DEMOCRATIC PARTY: The reality is in red states, they are absolutely Trump states. And unless the Democratic Party is going to invest in red states, we are not going to be able to compete statewide. And we`re going to continue to get the worst of the worst of the Republicans.\par \par \uc2 It is absolutely bonkers, that we have somebody who not only has incredible sexual assault cases against him, but he is spreading, you know, lies about critical race theory, about the stolen election, of banning books. And all of the Republicans, even the so-called moderate in the race wants to ban abortions, not even making exceptions for rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 So, as a party, this should be a major wake-up call that we have to invest in red states, because we have good candidates, we have good messaging. But when you don`t have resources, the Democrats can`t win.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I promise Jane to loop back to that point. But before I do, we saw with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the candidate he endorsed in Ohio, Max Miller, allegations of misconduct are not a deal-breaker if the candidate has Trump`s endorsement. Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 KLEEB: I think because Trump has a complete lock on the Republican Party. There was a void, I think for so long of actual Republicans giving clear kind of a platform of the Republican Party in where their vision was going to go. And so, Trump gave them that.\par \par \uc2 And Trump really spoke to the worst of their values. He spoke to them being essentially the individuals who always want it to be the biggest, the best, and only care about their individuals.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The winner of tonight`s Republican primary, of course, has a solid advantage in a red state. I take your point about what national Democrats need to do what the infrastructure, needs to do in order to invest in these red states. I got to ask you, though, you`re leading the state party. What are Nebraska Democrats doing to make sure that a man accused of sexual assault isn`t in the governor`s mansion?\par \par \uc2 KLEEB: So, when we have 500 Democrats running across the entire state of Nebraska. We are obviously running an aggressive grassroots campaign to make sure that our voters have vote by mail, voter registration, all the things that a party infrastructure does. We`ll be also are not going to let up on the issue of choice.\par \par \uc2 And I think in red states, we cannot shy away from these issues. We still, of course, will talk about other issues to make the Republicans on the defense, things like property rights. The Republicans in our state continue to support the Keystone XL Pipeline, something that conservatives and Republicans rallied around at the grassroots level to be against because it was imposing on their property rights and a foreign corporation was using eminent domain.\par \par \uc2 So, we`re going to lift up issues like that to put Republicans on the defense, which is what we as Democrats nationwide have to start doing. We can only be on the offense all of the time. So, that`s something that we`re going to do. We`re going to put the Republicans on the defense, we are going to lift up the issue of choice, and we are going to constantly make sure that voters know that Herbster will bring our state in the wrong direction, and that people can vote for Carol Blood, the Democrat, who has all of the right issues and all the right direction for our state.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: \uc2 Jane Kleeb\uc2 , I know tonight was a busy night for you. So, thank you so much for taking the time to be with us.\par \par \uc2 KLEEB: Of course. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Symone Sanders grew up in Omaha and worked on the Sanders campaign in 2016, the Biden campaign in 2020, before working from Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She is the new host of "SYMONE" which airs weekend on MSNBC at 4:00 p.m. with new episodes on Peacock Monday and Tuesday. She joins me tonight along with Tim Miller, a veteran of Jeb Bush`s 2016 campaign, writer at large at the Bulwark, and the author of the upcoming book Why We Did It: A Travelogue from the Republican Road to Hell.\par \par \uc2 Symone, you are from Nebraska. And I think what can get lost in this conversation is this is not always what Nebraska has looked like, right? I mean, even at the time you were going to college, Republicans -- lots of Republicans in the state, they were fairly moderate, or at least that`s how they sold themselves. There wasn`t that much daylight between many of the moderate Republicans and the moderate Democrats.\par \par \uc2 How did we get here and what is the future look like for these deep red states as long as you have Trump controlling the Republican Party?\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, MSNBC HOST: Well, look, I think the story of Nebraska is the story of many places across the country. I`ve worked at governors raised in Nebraska in 2014. I have traveled across the country from Omaha to Scottsbluff more times than I ever thought would have been possible.\par \par \uc2 And I will tell you that voters in Nebraska are people who are concerned about -- again, you got a lot of farmland, so these are folks who are concerned -- these are small businesses. They are concerned about agriculture. They are concerned about the economy just like voters in places like Omaha and Lincoln, which are larger cities within the state.\par \par \uc2 [20:10:15]\par \par \uc2 I think what it`s really important to note about this race and particularly about the governor`s race in the Republican primary, every single woman who serves in the unicameral in Nebraska, everybody is a state senator, it`s a unicameral, one house, one chamber. Every woman who serves as a state senator in the unicameral signed on to a letter denouncing the Trump-back candidate for governor Mr. Herbster.\par \par \uc2 Every single woman, Democrats, Republicans, and independents. I think that speaks volumes. And if Republicans do advance the Trump-backed candidate to the -- to the general election, which is not a foregone conclusion, right? There to other folks in this race is really split. So, I really think that we`ll have to watch the numbers tonight.\par \par \uc2 If the truck back candidate, though, is advanced I do think for probably the first time in a long time, the Democratic candidate for governor in Nebraska who was a State Senator Carol Blood, currently, she has a fighting chance.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right. Tim, you`ve written extensively about Trump`s control over your party, including in your forthcoming book. It doesn`t look like his strangleholds ending anytime soon.\par \par \uc2 TIM MILLER, WRITER AT LARGE, THE BULWARK: No, look, there going to be a lot of primaries in a lot of states. We just went through this in Ohio where, you know, you had the Senate race in Ohio we have actual results from, so you can look at it, right? And there were four MAGA candidates, one had the Trump endorsement, and there was one of more of a traditional old school Republican, like the ones that I used to vote for that, you know, actually believes that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a legitimate president, for example.\par \par \uc2 You know, that guy finished in third place with about a fifth of the vote. The Trump-endorsed candidate JD Vance won. But the three others that lost were also for Trump. You see a similar situation in Nebraska, right, where it`s like Trump heavy versus Trump lights, you know, versus one more traditional candidate.\par \par \uc2 So, we`ll see how this breaks down in a three-way race. I think governor`s races are a little bit different because governors actually have to do something. They have a job. Voters want their governors to be able to, you know, be competent. And so, there`s a little bit more pragmatism in voting than for the Senate where voters I think, just want people to -- whatever, own the libs on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , is what the Republican voters are looking for. Democratic voters actually want their senators to do stuff.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, we`ll see what happens in the governor`s race. But regardless what actually happens in Nebraska, here`s the key thing. Here`s a concerning thing, Alicia. Of the 154 candidates Trump has endorsed, 25 have been involved in sexual scandals. 25 -- that 16 percent. My friends at the Republican Accountability Project looked into this. 16 percent, it`s like, look, one -- you know, two is a coincidence, three is a trend, 25 is an epidemic, right?\par \par \uc2 So, you know, even if Herbster loses tonight, there`s still 23 -- you mentioned Max Miller. There`s one that`s already made it through the primary, there are 23 others. And this is what, unfortunately, Republican base voters are looking for. And there`s only about a 20 percent part of the party that doesn`t want this as evidenced by the Ohio race. And if Herbster gets through, I think to Symone`s point, that 20 percent is who Democrats need to talk to if they want -- if they want to win races like this where extreme Republicans get through.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Here`s the thing. So, it`s not just the allegations, it`s the way that they are responding to these allegations. So, you have Herbster suing one of his accusers. I am struck by this Trumpian effort to use the legal process to go after women who come forward with allegations of sexual assault.\par \par \uc2 There`s the political piece of this, there`s also just the chilling effect this could have on people who are weighing whether or not it is worth it to come forward.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Oh, it`s disgusting to say the least, Alicia. And furthermore, I do think that it is a trend, OK. You know, Tim is talking about trends. Think about what is happening as it relates to Roe, for example. That is an assault on women, the criminalization of women. Juxtapose that with how some of our Republican friends out there respond to issues of sexual assault, respond to allegations of misconduct. There is a pattern here.\par \par \uc2 I do think that people have to wake up and have to call things out for what it is. And that is why I really do believe that in Nebraska specifically, this could be a case study for what could happen going forward in other states across the country.\par \par \uc2 What has happened in this particular race is not only did Mr. Herbster go after his opponents -- and people he`s viewing his opponents i.e. these women, who have -- he`s been credibly accused of sexual assault by, he ran ads. He had \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  come in and using video of the rally with him where former President Trump says I trust him and I believe in him. And if I didn`t believe in him, if I didn`t believe him, I would not be here.\par \par \uc2 This is just an ugly place to be. And I really think that regardless of who you voted for in the last election, folks have to be willing to call it for what it is and call a spade a spade.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tim, speaking to that ugliness that Symone just referenced there, one op-ed in the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  describes Herbster as someone who "sees conspiracies everywhere. Conspiracy is to destroy him, conspiracies to undermine Trump, conspiracies to unravel the very fabric of the nation. This sense of Republican injustice, it`s not new, but at the very least, it seems more paranoid, more amped up under Trump. No?\par \par \uc2 [20:15:19]\par \par \uc2 MILLER: No, definitely, that`s the case. And this is the problem when you`re trying to decide how to deal with this Republican Party right now. Because, you know, if I said there`s about a fifth of the party that wants to maybe move on for Trump, irrational, maybe a quarter if you`re -- if you`re being optimistic, unfortunately, you have about clear half of the party that not just with Trump, but has been fully engrossed in these conspiracies.\par \par \uc2 And those are the candidates -- and those are the voters that people like Herbster are trying to reach. They don`t believe that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is legitimate president. You know, they thought that the pandemic was a planp- demic being pushed by \uc2 Bill Gates\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And this is the sort of stuff that used to be kind of on the outskirts of the party. There were always conspiratorial candidates that were running in Republican primaries from way back, but they now have -- they now have grown and grown over time. Trump supercharged it. And now they`re a plurality of the -- of the of the electorate.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, how can, you know, if you`re a more mainstream candidate win when half of the party wants you to advance conspiracies and that`s why as we see this primary in Nebraska and we go all through primary season, we`re going to continue to see conspiracy-oriented candidates win. And that`s a problem. And that`s, you know, something that we need to break through that bubble, you know, or else we`re just going to keep having groundhog day over and over again with us.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Election Night in America. I am happy to spend it with you, Tim Miller and Symone Sanders. Thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Still to come, one of the main networks peddling election fraud conspiracy theories finally admits no voter fraud in Georgia and settles with the election worker at the center of their attacks. And later, Senator \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2  is here ahead of tomorrow`s critical vote on a federal abortion protection law and what Democrats plan to do if and when Republicans block that bill. That is all just ahead.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Looking back at the Trump election subversion push of 2020, you can make a pretty good case that the future of American democracy rested on the shoulders of one woman in Georgia named Ruby Freeman. She was a nonpartisan ballot counter in Fulton County, and she was being falsely accused by a number of conservative outlets of committing election fraud with her daughter by stuffing thousands of fake ballots.\par \par \uc2 The ex-President even named dropped her more than a dozen times in his now- infamous call to get Georgia Secretary of State to find votes to steal the election.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: We`re so far ahead of these numbers. Even the phony ballots of a Ruby Freeman, known scammer -- you know the internet, you know what was trending on the internet? Where`s Ruby? Because they thought you`d be in jail. Where`s Ruby?\par \par \uc2 It`s crazy. It`s crazy. That was -- the minimum number is 18,000 for Ruby, but they think it`s probably about 56,000. But the minimum number is 18,000 on the Ruby Freeman night where she ran back in there when everybody was gone and stuff. She stuffed the ballot boxes. Let`s face it. (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: And just days after that Ruby Freeman was approached by a publicist who previously worked with Kanye West, threatening her to admit to the crazy claims that she committed fraud. Freeman refused.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TREVIAN KUTTI, PUBLICIST: I cannot say what specifically will take place. I just know that it will disrupt your freedom. And the freedom of one or more of your family members. I would like to connect now on the phone Harrison Floyd who would be taking this situation to a detailed level for you to let you know exactly what is at stake.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Can you imagine? Well, almost a year and a half after all of this chaos and all of these lies, Ruby Freeman and her daughter finally reached a settlement with OAN, one of the conservative outlets that maligned her. And while the details were not disclosed in court documents, OAN did air this tiny 32nd-hour bad spot last night.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: We now provide you with this updated report from Georgia officials. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Georgia officials have concluded that there was no widespread voter fraud by election workers who counted ballots at the State Farm Arena in November 2020. The results of this investigation indicate that Ruby Freeman and Wandrea "Shaye" Moss did not engage in ballot fraud or criminal misconduct while working at State Farm Arena on election night.\par \par \uc2 A legal matter with this network and the two election workers has been resolved to the mutual satisfaction of the parties through a fair and reasonable settlement.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Meanwhile, the democracy that Ruby Freeman fought and defended in the face of threats still under attack. Ian Bassin is the co-founder and executive director of Protect Democracy, the group that represented Ruby and her daughter in their defamation suit. He joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Ian, what does it mean to have gotten the settlement with OAN for both Mr. Freeman and for our democracy?\par \par \uc2 [20:25:03]\par \par \uc2 IAN BASSIN, CO-FOUNDER AND EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR, PROTECT DEMOCRACY: Well, thanks Alicia for having me. I can`t talk about the settlement beyond saying that the matter has been resolved to the mutual satisfaction of the parties through a fair and reasonable settlement. But what I can say is why our clients brought a series of lawsuits to begin with.\par \par \uc2 There were really three goals, three reasons. First, justice. Our clients had their lives turned upside down as the result of being falsely accused of corrupting the election. They were at one point -- one of them was advised by the \uc2 FBI\uc2  to leave her home for her own physical safety. Under our system of laws, when someone has been injured like that, they are entitled to be made whole at a court of law. And that`s, that`s one reason our clients brought these cases.\par \par \uc2 The second is about truth and correcting the record because the lies weren`t just injurious to our clients lives, it were injurious to our democracy and to truth. And our clients wanted to make sure that the record was corrected not only for their own reputations, but for the sake of our democracy and for the sake of truth.\par \par \uc2 And then third, deterrence, because if these sorts of attacks are able to be conducted with impunity, they are going to continue and they are going to get worse. But if in fact, the law holds people accountable for intentionally spreading injurious, defamatory lies, that sends a message to others in the future that they need to comply with the rules of our First Amendment, of our media space and what good journalism is about, which is trying to produce actual truth.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: It`s just so wild that she was being accused of corruption by the people who were actually corrupt, and that what happened to her does not exist in isolation of trial in Dominion voting systems lawsuit against Fox News set for April of next year. How do you think Miss Freeman`s settlement with OAN plays into that trial?\par \par \uc2 BASSIN: Well, I think one thing that`s clear already is that defamation litigation is working as a method to correct injurious and intentional lies. So, for example, one day after the voting machine company \uc2 Smartmatic\uc2  sued Lou Dobbs and Fox News, Fox severed ties with Lou Dobbs. And I would speculate that Fox calculated that Dobbs was costing them potentially more money and liability on the air than it would cost them to buy out his contract.\par \par \uc2 DirecTV\uc2  has dropped One American News from its channel lineup. And just this week, it was reported that \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  apparently canceled a segment about a new conspiracy fell about the election. And they are too, I would speculate, that the fact that \uc2 Newsmax\uc2  settled with an employee of another voting machine company of defamation case probably had something to do with that.\par \par \uc2 Aaron Blake has an excellent piece in The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  today counting all of the situations in which defamation litigation is actually really helping address the problem of this sort of pervasive intentional lying in our information space. I think even before that case goes to trial in April, we`re already seeing the positive impact of simply applying the law that exists.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I mean, here`s the thing, The Big Lie is one piece of this, and we`re watching it play out in different ways in different states. So, in Michigan, Republicans replaced election officials who certified Joe Biden`s win in the Electoral College. Is there any way to protect future elections from partisan interference like this?\par \par \uc2 BASSIN: Well, I mean, first, we got to be -- we got to understand the plot that was afoot in 2020 because there is an attempt now to actually make that plot succeed in the future. And it involves, essentially five pieces. First, make it harder for people to vote. I mean, unfortunately, this country has a long history of voter suppression, racist voter suppression, and that`s -- that was part one of the plot in 2020.\par \par \uc2 The second was for the former president, the disgraced former president, to falsely claim victory the night of the election and allege that all of the late counted ballots were somehow fraudulent. The third was to file a series of spurious litigations that were designed to amplify those claims of fraud and delay certification. That was key. So, that`s step four. The former disgrace precedent could induce state legislatures like the one in Michigan to step in and substitute their own slate of electors for the one selected by the people of that state.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, finally, step five, get Congress on January 6 to accept that ultimate slate of electors. And what we are seeing is because that plot was thwarted at every turn by a powerful coalition of Americans who stood up for democracy in 2020, the disgraced former president and his allies are now trying to change different aspects of where it failed last time to make it succeed next time like changing officials in Michigan.\par \par \uc2 The thing that is standing in the way of that right now, changing laws in Michigan, etcetera, is Democratic Governor \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , who`s up for reelection in November, is standing in the way of hyper-partisan changes to election rules that would potentially corrupt the election. So, keeping \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2  in office is really important.\par \par \uc2 And then second, there were key Republicans in office in Michigan who stood up against pressure in 2020. And that`s why \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is intervening in some of those state legislative races now to get those people out. We need those Republicans who believe in democracy to stay there to hold strong and for the people of Michigan to make sure that Trump isn`t able to stack the deck with people who are loyal to him.\par \par \uc2 [20:30:05]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Ian Bassin, thank you so much for bringing us the story. Still to come, as protests continue over the possible overturning of Roe, a look at the already perilous state of maternal health in America. That is next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Protests continued in Washington D.C. today outside the Supreme Court amid the widespread fallout from leaked draft opinion that would overturn Roe versus Wade. And as a powerful minority are trying to make it so that Americans would be forced to give birth, we are seeing more examples of how difficult and even dangerous it is to have a child in this country.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:16]\par \par \uc2 Just feeding a baby is a major challenge for so many right now. There is a dire shortage of baby formula nationwide. 40 percent of the most popular brands out of stock as of the end of last month. Supply chain issues and a recall by one of the largest manufacturers led to the empty shelves.\par \par \uc2 And giving birth has become significantly more deadly in recent years. The maternal death rate in the United States jumped 20 percent in 2020. And it is nearly three times higher than any other industrialized nation.\par \par \uc2 Today, at a Senate Banking Committee hearing, Secretary of the Treasury \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  mince no words arguing that overturning abortion rights would also harm the nation`s economy.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JANET YELLEN\uc2 , SECRETARY OF THE TREASURY: I believe that eliminating the right of women to make decisions about when and whether to have children would have very damaging effects on the economy and would set women back decades.\par \par \uc2 Roe v Wade and access to reproductive health care, including abortion, helped lead to increased labor force participation. It enabled many women to finish school that increased their earning potential. It allowed women to plan and balance their families and careers.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: And many people believe that the anti-abortion forces will not stop at overturning Roe. Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  of Connecticut told \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2 , he thinks that when Republicans are back in power, they will end the filibuster to get what they want. When the opportunity presents itself, there`s no doubt in my mind that they`ll change the rules to pass a bill criminalizing abortion federally.\par \par \uc2 Tomorrow, Democrats will get their chance to take action to protect the rights enshrined in Roe before they are gone. But will they even get 50 votes? More on that next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB CASEY (D-PA): I don`t think we should be banning abortion and the only legislation now that is in front of us is this bill which would prevent a ban like that from going forward. I was surprised last week in light of the opinion that Republicans have already prepared to introduce legislation and actively across the country.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Today, Senator Bob Casey came out in support of Democrats bill to make Roe versus Wade national law. It is a notable shift for the Pennsylvania Democrat who is relatively conservative on abortion rights and who said he was undecided on this bill as recently as last night.\par \par \uc2 Right now, the only Senate Democrat who still has not announced where he stands, Senator \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of West Virginia. He will have to decide by tomorrow when the Senate votes to advance debate on the bill. Even if mentioned supports it, the bill is expected to fail in the face of Republican opposition.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2  is a Democrat from Michigan, a state with an old law on the books which would make abortion a felony if Roe versus Wade is overturned. And she joins us now. Senator, thank you for being with us. Talk me through the goal of tomorrow`s vote.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEBBIE STABENOW (D-MI): Well, at least it`s wonderful to be with you. And tomorrow in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , each one of us is going to have the opportunity and the responsibility to vote yes or no on whether or not we support a woman`s freedom to make our own reproductive decisions. That`s the vote.\par \par \uc2 For the folks that say our freedom should be taken away, they`re on the side of saying politicians should decide for each of us what should happen in our most intimate health care decisions that there should be government regulation on women for these most personal decision.\par \par \uc2 So, tomorrow, we`ll know. Tomorrow, there will be the vote. And then we will go forward and continue to shine a light on what is happening across this country all the way until November when we have an opportunity to change it by electing a bigger majority in the Democratic Senate and keeping the \uc2 U.S. House\uc2  and electing great people across the country.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Senator, understanding that most of the opposition to this is coming from Republicans. What would you say to your still undecided colleague \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: Well, I appreciate Joe will have to decide for himself. He comes from a very -- certainly a very conservative, very different state from mine in Michigan. I think for me, though, I want to keep focus on what has really brought us here. What has brought us here, Republicans for years, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  for years and years and years, working hard to set up this situation, right-wing members of the \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2 , putting others on the federal benches that are right-wing, supporting those in state legislatures, where Republicans have been chipping away at our freedoms now for years.\par \par \uc2 I mean, there`s no mistake about it. We are here because of the Republican Party and the fact that they are willing to say that they as politicians should be making our decisions about what we`re going to do in terms of our most intimate reproductive decisions.\par \par \uc2 And so, I find that incredibly outrageous. And 50 years of severed law should be enough for all of us in the United States, and tomorrow is an accumulation of what the Republicans have been doing for years.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:28]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Senator, I want to get personal for just a moment, if you`ll allow me. You know, I`m born `83. I was born into a post Roe world. I have two daughters. I am watching all of this unfold with them in mind. You were a young adult when Roe was decided.\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: Right.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tell me what you remember from that time. What did the decision mean to you? And what does it mean to you now to watch this all unfold?\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: I have lived through -- I mean, I was in college when Roe was passed. And I had friends in situations where they had to decide what to do or whether or not to go to another state. I mean, this also affects women very differently. If you have means you have more choices than if you are a poor woman. Disproportionately, women of color are affected by this because of their circumstances, their lack of other kinds of choices.\par \par \uc2 But I`ve watched that. I watched what happened before Roe. And what happened when we were told as a country that in America, that we have a right to privacy, that we have the freedom to decide our most intimate health care decisions. I`ve had people in my extended families struggling with situations, wanting a baby, coming to the end of their pregnancy, and then finding horrible things happen and their own -- their life is on the line.\par \par \uc2 We all know of situations of someone being raped or incest, you know, 12- year-old or 14-year-old. Why in the world should government, should politicians step in front of the family, step in front of the woman with her faith, her doctor, her family, and say that they should make those decisions, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  should make those decisions, other Republicans should make those decisions. Really? I mean, that is really quite extraordinary. And that is the fundamental question that we`re talking about is who decides? Who decides?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Senator, we only have about 30 seconds left. But part of the reason I do want to talk to you tonight is because what we`re watching play out in your state in Michigan. You have your A.G. Dana Nessel saying she`s not going to enforce that 1931 abortion ban that is still on the books. You have Governor Whitmer sued to vacate the ban last month.\par \par \uc2 It all makes me wonder how much of this -- as you try to riddle this out federally, how much of this is just going to fall to the states?\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: Well, we know right now that if Roe v. Wade is overturned, there are 26 states that have laws like Michigan that will kick in in terms of banning abortion services, and that 17 of those like Michigan have no exception for rape and incest. And so, this goes right to the States.\par \par \uc2 Thank goodness we have Governor Whitmer in Michigan and our Attorney General Dana Nessel and they`re challenging under Michigan Supreme Court. Hopefully, the Michigan Supreme Court will in fact rule that this is not constitutional under our Constitution. But we continually then still have a problem which is we have Republican majorities in the State House and State Senate who will run immediately to try to overturn it.\par \par \uc2 So, it ends up coming back to the fall elections in terms of who`s in the majorities in the Michigan State Legislature, who`s in the governorships, who`s in the majority in the U.S. House and U.S. Senate. Ultimately, this is about all of us voting and in speaking up, by the way, all along the way.\par \par \uc2 And I can`t think of a more important time. Do we want a robot back the clock 50 years -- 50 years for women in this country or are we going to continue to move forward as a country and respect women and respect our own capacity to make our own decisions?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Senator \uc2 Debbie Stabenow\uc2 , thank you so much for spending some time with us.\par \par \uc2 STABENOW: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Still to come, is Donald Trump`s Twitter ban are about to come to an end. What the replatforming of the former president would mean for the Midterms. The Republican Party and democracy for this.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: There was a brief moment after the January 6 attack on the Capitol where everyone seemed to understand how potentially dangerous social media platforms could be when they were weaponized to try and end American democracy. Even Republican leaders like Kevin McCarthy understood it.\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , just four days after the insurrection he "told other GOP leaders, he wished the big tech companies which stripped some Republican lawmakers of their social media accounts as \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and \uc2 Facebook\uc2  and done with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , saying, can`t they take their \uc2 Twitter\uc2  accounts away too?\par \par \uc2 But now tech billionaire \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  who is in the process of purchasing \uc2 Twitter\uc2  wants to give \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the person who bears probably the most responsibility for the Capitol riot, his old platform back.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ELON MUSK, CEO, \uc2 SPACEX\uc2  AND TESLA: I do think that it was not correct to ban \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . I think that was -- that was a mistake because it alienated a large part of the country and did not ultimately result in \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  not having a voice. He is now going to be on Truth Social. I guess the answer is that I would reverse the ban.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:55:16]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: What could this mean for American democracy? Quinta Jurecic is a contributing writer at The Atlantic and a fellow at the \uc2 Brookings Institution\uc2 . She wrote an article for The Atlantic in May 2021 about Trump`s deplatforming titled, The Ringmaster Is Gone. And she joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Quinta, I was reading your piece again and it is as relevant today as it was when you wrote it in 2021. Your response to Musk`s claim there that deplatforming Trump did not ultimately curb his online influence and reach.\par \par \uc2 QUINTA JURECIC, CONTRIBUTING WRITER, THE ATLANTIC: I think that`s just not true. There`s -- you know, it`s a little hard to put data to such a sort of a fuzzy question. But there have been studies, \uc2 Zignal Labs\uc2 , which looks into these kinds of things has done -- you know, looking into the media mentions of Trump after his deplatforming, and you see a really sharp downturn.\par \par \uc2 You know, if you look at Trump`s social media endeavors, like Truth Social which \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  mentioned, that`s not doing particularly well. It really seems to be floundering. Trump`s blog, which he tried before that, which was sort of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  but he was the only one using it, he shut that down after less than a month because it wasn`t getting enough readership.\par \par \uc2 So, I think it`s pretty clear that, you know, removing him from \uc2 Twitter\uc2  definitely led to a decrease in his influence. Now, (AUDIO GAP) to what extent we can trace that to his loss of his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account as opposed to his departure from the presidency. But the two certainly do seem to be linked.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Understanding that it`s hard to track influence, can you track disinformation, misinformation that he was putting out or is that equally hard to quantify?\par \par \uc2 JURECIC: Again, it`s a little tricky. I`ll point to \uc2 Zignal Labs\uc2  also. So, they looked at a misinformation around the election, around the 2020 election in the period after Trump left \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and saw a really sharp drop off. And I`m referring to a study here that was conducted immediately after he left.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s a little hard to trace that to Trump specifically because when Trump was removed from \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , there was also a huge content moderation on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  of a lot of accounts that were linked to QAnon, for example. So, it`s a little hard to say, you know, what`s Trump, what was all those other accounts. But again, after he leaves, you do see that downturn.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Musk offered an alternative solution to a permanent ban today in his remarks. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Even after he egged on the crowds who went to the U.S. Capitol, some of them carrying nooses, you still think it was a mistake to remove him?\par \par \uc2 MUSK: I think the -- if there are tweets that are wrong, they should -- and bad, those should be either deleted or made invisible. And a suspension, a temporary suspension is appropriate but not a permanent ban.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Tweet deletion, a temporary suspension, is any of that adequate?\par \par \uc2 JURECIC: I think what we`re seeing here is, you know, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  said he wanted to purchase \uc2 Twitter\uc2  to return it to sort of its free speech roots, but he didn`t like the way it was going about content moderation. And I think what we see here when he says, you know, I`m potentially open to hiding tweets that are in his words wrong and bad, which is a pretty vague standard, you see that, you know, he`s -- he is implicitly embracing the idea that we don`t want all speech on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . We do want some amount of moderation.\par \par \uc2 And I think what he`s really doing here in terms of saying, well, maybe not a permanent ban but hide some tweets, that`s really just haggling over the price. He`s essentially acknowledged that, you know, \uc2 Twitter\uc2  should be engaging in some level of control over its platform, including potentially tweets by the president himself.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I`ve got about 30 seconds. If Trump does return to \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , the implications of that?\par \par \uc2 JURECIC: I think his \uc2 Twitter\uc2  is a very powerful tool. Not as powerful as it was when he was president, of course, but I wouldn`t be surprised if it played a significant role in the Midterms, you know, blasting out news, attacking opponents. It would certainly up and I think where the Midterm seem to be right now.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Quinta Jurecic, thank you so much for being with us.\par \par \uc2 That is ALL IN on this Tuesday night. I`m Alicia Menendez. As always, you can catch me on "AMERICAN VOICES" which airs every Saturday and Sunday nights 6:00 to 8:00 p.m. Eastern. "MSNBC PRIME" starts right now with Mehdi Hasan. Hi, Mehdi. addie.\par \par \uc2 MEHDI HASAN, MSNBC HOST: Hello, and thank you, Alicia. And thanks to you at home for joining us this hour. As Alicia said, this is MSNBC PRIME airing at this hour Tuesday through Friday. "THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW" continues to at this hour on Mondays. I`m glad to have you with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 And this hour, polls have just closed in the great state of Nebraska in that state`s primary election. Voters today chose nominees for Congress, for Governor, and other statewide offices in Nebraska. And as you know, Nebraska is a pretty red state.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220511ei5a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 10, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Dasha Burns, Andy Mehalshick, Lisa Sylvester, Yamiche  Alcindor \par \uc2 9,246 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Tuesday and it`s an election year. It`s a special MEET THE MIDTERMS edition of MEET TH PRESS DAILY. We are live from battleground Pennsylvania, on the banks of the mighty Susquehanna River.\par \par \uc2 It`s fun to get the music, right? Good day. I`m Chuck Todd. I`m here in Luzerne County, behind me, Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania. We`re on the ground here in Pennsylvania with one week to go until some of the most important primaries on the 2022 Midterm calendar. We`re talking to voters, candidates, and officials in a state that could very well decide which party controls the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in November. And of course, Pennsylvania might be the most pivotal battleground in 2024.\par \par \uc2 The Senate race features primaries in both parties that are crowded, contentious, and ideologically diverse. And they`re very reflective of the national issues facing each party right now. For Republicans, the testing of the power of Trump. For Democrats, is the latest round of the moderate versus progressive fight.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be in Philly tomorrow for more on the Democratic side of these primaries, but today, here in northeastern Pennsylvania, we want to talk more about the Republican Party and Trump`s power in it. It`s our focus here in battleground Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 And we might get some additional clues about the current trajectory of the GOP because Republican primary voters are voting right now in Nebraska and West Virginia, a pair of GOP races that will put Trump`s power and populist stances and influence to the test.\par \par \uc2 Both states feature races, where the former president is backing what some believed, is a more flawed candidate while others are sticking with a more establishment candidate. It could shed some insight on the big Republican race right here where television personality Mehmet Oz has the former president`s backing in the Pennsylvania senator race, but he has struggled to break free from the primary pack.\par \par \uc2 Oz is a newcomer to politics, has had to dive deep into the Maga world after having a completely different political identity while appearing on television. Oz and his Republican rival David McCormick are on the airwaves big time. They`ve been spending millions attacking each other`s Maga bonafides.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time, it`s political commentator and newcomer, Kathy Barnette, who may be the spoiler here. She has seen a late rise in polls despite spending a fraction of the money, perhaps benefiting from Oz and McCormick fighting each other. We`re going to talk to Barnette live on MEET THE PRESS DAILY tomorrow. So, all of the Pennsylvania Republicans are trying to out-Trump each other despite only Oz having Trump`s formal backing.\par \par \uc2 This morning, what was interesting, I was at a campaign event where McCormick stopped to talk to me for a few minutes. The event itself was about the need for better-trained workers and didn`t hear much red meat at all. You didn`t hear about any of the primary, all the silly Trump stuff. But then when I asked him how he played to win the nomination without Trump`s endorsement, well, this is all he had to say.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do you win this without Trump?\par \par \uc2 DAVID MCCORMICK, PENNSYLVANIA REPUBLICAN SENATE CANDIDATE: All right, listen. I`m not doing any more interviews but I --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Oh, no. How do you win without Trump? You got to -- you met --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: All right, thanks, guys.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No?\par \par \uc2 MCCORMICK: See you, Chuck Todd.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Thank you, David.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: For what it`s worth, we had a robust conversation about whether immigration or they need to do a labor workforce here in northeastern Pennsylvania. That was some substance there. After that, though, when Trump`s name came up, they had to go. So look, we got a lot to talk about here on the ground.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now my NBC News colleague, Dasha Burns, who`s basically been spending most of her time in this part of Pennsylvania as part of our MEET THE PRESS county to county midterm project, also with us is Andy Mehalshick. He`s the local political Guru here at our NBC affiliate WBRE in Wilkes-Barre. And in a moment, we`re going to be joined by Lisa Sylvester from our NBC affiliate WPXI in Pittsburgh, on the other side of the state. She`s had the benefit of moderating both a Republican and Democratic Senate debate in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Dasha, you`ve been on the ground here. Let me start with you. The fascinating part to me about McCormick`s event today is what wasn`t said.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think I was the first one to bring Trump`s name up. And fair -- and fairness, it was not -- in fact, he didn`t say the word Republican until the very end. It was a very -- if you didn`t watch any of the TV ads, you`d have -- it was just -- it was almost discombobulated.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes. I mean, that`s a very different picture to what we`ve seen on the airwaves, to what we`ve seen at the rallies, and to what we`ve been seeing from all of these candidates. Like you said, Oz has a Trump endorsement but all of the other candidates are trying to represent the Trump brand. And when you talk to voters, which we`ve been doing nonstop -- listen, I called -- as soon as the Trump endorsement happened, I called voters and every single one of them was scratching their head. They didn`t understand why Oz was running. They didn`t understand why he was running in Pennsylvania. He was from --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Would you`ve got that same reaction that had -- he endorsed McCormick?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Interesting.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: No. I don`t think so. They know him as a celebrity doctor who talks about products on television.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 BURNS: They do not know him as a politician, as somebody who understands the issues. And they don`t see the Trump alignment there other than the celebrity. And when we watched the first debate, every single time he brought up the Trump endorsement, people groaned. The groans grew louder each time he brought it up.\par \par \uc2 And the voters that we watched that debate with came out saying, these two front runners we`re seeing their names everywhere, but there are these mega-rich guys, I don`t really understand how they can connect with me and represent me. So they came out of this a little bit disillusioned.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Andy, I think, I -- if I -- one thing I did hear McCormick say a lot was how close -- how many ties he has to the state of Pennsylvania. Seventh Generation Pennsylvanian, he said at one point. Obviously, he thinks he A, has to continue to establish that. But is that the way to sort of keep knocking us?\par \par \uc2 ANDY MEHALSHICK, WBRE-TV REPORTER: I think it is because I`ve covered several McCormick events over the last several weeks and that is a common theme.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: My farm -- I own a farm in Bloomsburg, Pennsylvania, right in central Pennsylvania. He`s the native homegrown guy. I`m not the guy who parachuted in from New Jersey.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He named checked all the places he played high school sports, right?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, he`s been very careful with that title.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Entitle -- when you`re talking about in -- anywhere in Pennsylvania, especially in northeastern Pennsylvania, Shikellamy, Shickshinny, Bloomsburg, Catawissa, small towns, they are the folks who are the Trump supporters, will be driving through the highways and you`ll see the Trump flags. You`ll bring America back, that kind of thing. So yes, he`s getting keynotes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think Oz and McCormick have had either one of them actually tapped into the Maga base? I mean is it -- is it a turnout that those folks are sniffing them both out here?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I don`t think they have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Again, anecdotally, I don`t think they have because it`s so close. And I see Kathy Barnette and we were talking -- you know, people I`ve spoken to on the street said that she is resonating her message with folks, is she the real conservative Republican in this race in Pennsylvania. But you mentioned before you`re talking about the celebrity of Dr. Oz. I think people do get a good vibe -- maybe they get a vibe from Oz because he`s like a Trump-type personality.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: No, Trump wasn`t a lawmaker. He was involved -- he was a celebrity. So they`re trying to think is this the same kind of a blueprint to success?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dasha, you`ve been talking to a bunch of voters.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What have you learned?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I don`t think that`s necessarily sticking though. Look, when you look at the polls, Oz 18 percent, McCormick 16 percent, that`s not a whole lot of the vote. 40 percent --\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, it`s not -- this is a big undecided. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: 40 percent are undecided, Chuck. That is a massive number of people. One week out, what do we got, 40 are undecided? We went door-knocking with two ladies. They call themselves Trump gals. They are huge Trump fans. And they`ve been door-knocking for three weeks for Carla Sands and another underdog in this race. They love Trump. They don`t get the Oz thing.\par \par \uc2 They think he made a mistake. They`ve knocked on doors in Westmoreland County, deep, deep red. They said they have not yet met a single Oz supporter. And they were themselves surprised by that. And when we went door-knocking with them for you know, a couple of hours, every single door that opened was an undecided voter.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very interesting. You know, one thing that I think some of us overlooked in `16 and none of us overlooked at -- in `20 is, Andy, and I`m sure you -- Trump`s political organizations quite strong in Pennsylvania. They did a bunch of voter re-registration between 16 and 20. Are you seeing evidence that he`s able to flip that switch for us yet?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I think he can. I know. Well, you`re -- well, you`re a week away from the May 17 primary. And it seems you know in the political world, that`s like a blink of an eye. However, because the race is so close, if you have all these undecided which is what I`m hearing on the street, undecided, even there -- those supporting OZ are not a -- or of McCormick are not attacking \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They`re saying we don`t -- we respect him. We don`t respect his decision. Do I think he can flip the switch? Absolutely. We were talking before air back in 2016 when \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  took the ride down the escalator, OK?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: And I could tell you why here in northeastern Pennsylvania, there was a long line of people saying they need to laugh at him right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: But he resonates - -his message. He`s speaking about what we`re talking about at the dinner table.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m going to -- about to bring in Lisa here and I want to get some of her impression from the other side of the state. But, Andy, I got to ask you this just in case I don`t get another chance. Oz, Fetterman, in northeastern Pennsylvania, who would -- if -- you know who would play better here?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: That`s like Rocky Balboa.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: The sequel, first, second, the third fight who would fare better? Again, anecdotally, I think right now, at this point in time, again a week before --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I think Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fetterman could over-perform an average Democrat right now.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Yes, right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s Oz. Is it easier against Oz or McCormick?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Easier against Oz I believe.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Because he`s from Braddock, Pennsylvania. He`s a local guy. He speaks the language of the Democrats -- but also a little bit of conservatism in there also. So he`s not your traditional Democratic candidate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. This is the story from the east part of the state. Let`s go to the other side of the state where I have my colleague, Lisa Sylvester. Lisa, you`ve moderated both debates so you get an idea, obviously of what`s resonating -- who`s resonating the best in this Republican primary out there.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 I think of Beaver County. I know that`s Trump country. Who`s playing best in Beaver County?\par \par \uc2 LISA SYLVESTER, WPXI-TV ANCHOR: Well, it really comes down to celebrity versus sincerity. So, Oz obviously has the celebrity factor. He`s got the wow. People know him. They know his name from the years of being on television. But I got to tell you, coming out of the debate, the one person that a lot of people were saying, hey, let me take another look at was actually Kathy Barnette. And you have actually seen her numbers rise since the debate. And the reason why I think is because she comes across as more sincere.\par \par \uc2 So if you are looking for the America First candidate, a lot of people are taking another look at her because she doesn`t just sort of speak the talking points, you really get the sense that she lives them, that she has a history, that she has believed and has held these beliefs for a while. With McCormick and Oz, it`s a little bit of a head-scratcher because you`re like, well, maybe this is what they really believe but maybe it`s not, especially when you see the attack ads.\par \par \uc2 And the other thing too is Kathy Barnette, if you look at just the amount of money spent in this race, she has spent nowhere near the amount of money that McCormick and Oz have spent, yet she is resonating. And I think it`s because she`s doing very much a grassroots, 1500 miles traveling across the state, and people see her as sincere and they see her as authentic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Lisa, I know we have a big satellite delay. I really appreciate you giving us a few minutes there. Dasha, let me bring it back to you. What she just described with Kathy Barnette is what I hear people describe John Fetterman to, right? It is sort of the hey, they`re not traditional, but they`re speak -- it`s this -- you know, there`s a new paradigm.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: And it`s the language that was used about Trump.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I`m hearing voters say the exact same things, not the typical politician about Fetterman and about Kathy. She has an unconventional story and she really has been doing that grassroots work. She has been all over Pennsylvania. And in many ways, her talking points when you actually listen to her match up very closely with what a lot of the Trump base wants to hear. And like I said, some people are impressed that someone with a lot less money, with a lot less name recognition, people liked an underdog.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Trump was outspent left and right in `16. People forget this. On every level, he was outspent. So if you do catch enough traction on primary, you don`t need to be the biggest spender right, Andy?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Right. And I could tell you back -- going back to 2016, who would have thought in northeastern Pennsylvania, a rich guy from New York with a lot of money who`d never been involved in politics, far as running for office can win in Luzerne County, and flipped his county, which as we all know, is a bellwether state in the whole country? And we`re doing shows here. Why? Because we saw it in 2016. Can it happen again? Can he flip it to Oz this time around? I think he can.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s not out of the realm of possibility. But as Dasha was talking before, there`s different dynamics -- and I think people also anecdotally, they don`t want your typical politician. We`ve seen that, been there, and done that, what`s coming down that somebody who really knows what we`re feeling.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Well, that`s why I think McCormick isn`t necessarily running away with it because if the Republican Party couldn`t chisel a perfect candidate, right?\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, he looks like a perfect 2012 candidate.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And I say this with no disrespect. He looks exactly like Mitt Romney`s Republican Party and that would have been a winner.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: At the same time, the question I have is for those 40 percent of undecided voters. At the end of the day, will they say, listen, there`s not enough time to do all this research?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Trump says this guy`s it. You know, I talked to one gentleman who owns a bar, Dave Magill. They call him Mogie. He owns Mogie`s Irish pub in the western part of the state. He told me he flipped from Democrat to Republican to vote for Trump. He still considers himself a Democrat, but he voted for Trump. He wants a Republican victory because of the economy, he blames Biden for inflation for his business suffering.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But he told me he`s undecided but Trump might be the final factor because he said Trump picks winners.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Andy, before I let you go, I got to ask you about \uc2 Bob Casey Jr\uc2 . voting the way he`s voting on abortion rights. Now, I -- you`ve been covering politics slightly longer than I have but the Casey name in pro- life Democrat this was -- you know, when I thought of the picture of pro- life Democrat in the political dictionary that you and I grew up with, Bob Casey Sr. was on it.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s why Jr. was recruited, why he won by so many points. How will that play here? How does his shift on abortion rights play here? And is -- what is the line? Are you going to get punished more for being thrown out a right ban or for being for fewer exemptions?\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I think it will be a factor with the abortion issue for sure. I remember -- I can`t remember what presidential convention it was. And Bob Casey would not allow --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, we`ll leave it.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: To come to -- into the rooms.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I think it was `96, right? Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I believe, 1996.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I`ve been around a little bit longer as well, but I`m not sure who`s been around longer, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re close, I know.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: But will it play big time? I think so, too. I mean, as you saw Casey defeated Barletta several years ago. Casey is a juggernaut in Pennsylvania. He`s a respected individual, well-liked. But I think on the abortion situation, and real conservative folks are going to say, we respect it -- we respect him. We don`t respect his decision on this abortion issue. I think it could may be a big factor.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What was interesting to me today, and granted, it was a -- this was an event that McCormick was having at a technical school wherein the -- in the -- essentially the energy capital of the state this area right now so it`s understandable, but not a bit of any of that social conservative red meat, not a lick of it. Are you seeing the rhetoric on the trail heat up at all on abortion rights or no?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, I got you.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: No, that -- and that`s been the big surprise to me. On the Democratic side, yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right, but not on the Republican.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: But not on the Republican side. Even the voters I`ve been talking to have been repeating what you`re hearing from Republican politicians talking about the leak, the issues with the Supreme Court, not about the actual contents of that leak. I`m not hearing that from voters. I`m certainly not hearing that from the candidates.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I get the sense. Dr. Oz does not want to have a detailed conversation about abortion.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I don`t think so.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: If I do hear about abortion, I hear about it from voters saying wait a minute, that Oz guy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is he really is --\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Is he really pro-life?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: And your -- when you watch political ads, we know what they are. It seems it didn`t -- we know what the beast is. But there`s not denying what you`ve said. Whether you`ve changed your mind, there`s not denying what you said. And I think a true conservative is what I know in northeastern Pennsylvania and hearing about the Keystone State that matters to them. Your word, what -- how -- it`s such a big issue how do you change that quickly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: If you said any words --\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s hard for some people to understand how you switch on an issue like that.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s one thing on trade policy, right? It seems a little bit different for people.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: I say one of these deals was --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: That -- not with abortion, though.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Andy, it`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Same here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Get --\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: My pleasure and honor to you\par \par \uc2 TODD: Get the Intel -- get the Intel from you here.\par \par \uc2 MEHALSHICK: Thank you for having m.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dasha, you`ve been doing terrific work. So thank you both.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And to Lisa Sylvester out there at WPXI, I really appreciate your help as well. Up next. One of the reasons we picked Luzerne County as a place to watch in 2022 is because it is representative of a sea change in American politics where long-standing loyalties to the Democratic Party have shifted to the right.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to speak with two Pennsylvania officials who have had an up-close and personal experience with this changing electorate. They both join me here on set after this short break. You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY live from the Keystone State.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: As we mentioned at the beginning of the show, we`re in beautiful Luzerne County, Pennsylvania, one of the areas of the state that has grown increasingly Republican over the last few election cycles. Luzerne went for Trump in 2016 and again in 2020 after going for Democrats in the previous four presidential elections. The County also voted for Senator Bob Casey`s Republican challenger in the 2018 Midterms. So we`ve been watching this shift go on for most of the decades.\par \par \uc2 So, what does that shift continue to look like here in Lucerne and what can it tell us about the larger sea change in American politics ahead of next week`s primaries? I got a couple of guests here who`ve been dealing with this changing electorate. Two politicians have had a front-row seat to these changes, the current independent state senator and a former Democrat, John Yudichak, and the mayor of Wilkes-Barre, George Brown. Mayor Brown, Senator, good to see you both. Mayor, let me start with you.\par \par \uc2 GEORGE C. BROWN, MAYOR OF WILKES-BARRE, PENNSYLVANIA: Sure.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, this used to be a pretty strong democratic area, a lot of Union Democrats, culturally conservative but economic populist. We`ve watched this shift. Tell me -- tell me about it on your own -- through your own lens.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Sure. Well, Wilkes-Barre still is a strong democratic city. The majority of voters are democratic in Wilkes-Barre. But when you talk about the county, it`s more of a 50-50 split. And in the last elections, we saw when President Trump ran against Hillary, he had more of a commanding lead with the Democrats when he ran against Mr. Biden, about 5000 less Democrats than he had when he ran against Hillary. So, it really depends, I think, on the candidates that are running.\par \par \uc2 And whether you`re a Democrat or Republican, that`s how they decide which way to vote as far as the candidate. Do they feel a relationship with their candidate, do they feel that the values that candidates putting out is what they`re looking for. So -- but Wilkes-Barre still is a predominantly Democratic city.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Senator, you`ve got elected as a Democrat, you decided to leave the party. You didn`t go to the Republicans, you want to be independent. And these days, it`s tough to be in the middle. You -- trust me I`m aware of left and right don`t like that these days. Tell me in your own words why you did it.\par \par \uc2 JOHN YUDICHAK, INDEPENDENT STATE SENATOR, PENNSYLVANIA: Well, I think the party that captures the middle, whether that`s the middle class or the middle of the road voter is the one that can govern. We have the two bases of the respective parties, the Republicans and the Democrats. Their base, they`re bankrupt. They`re driven more by outrage and hate against one another than they are about rallying around behind the city, the Commonwealth, or the country. That`s why I moved from the Democratic Party.\par \par \uc2 I grew up -- my father was a united mine worker, my mom worked for Ask Me, a public-sector union. Blue-collar roots are very, very deep in my family for generations. When the Democratic Party -- particularly at the national level, not only started to dismiss those blue-collar voters but to demonize them, that`s when I decided I need to get to the --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Where did they demonize them? I mean, could they --\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: I think --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Policy-wise, you get -- I`ve had progressives will argue, hey, who`s actually advocating for a better policy for these folks, and they take --\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: I`ll give you a great example.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, go ahead.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: I`ll give you a great example. Chuck, I was a big advocate for an energy project here in Luzerne County, a $6 billion project that`s going to be transformative for the region, $25 billion economic impacts, and also right on the environment, but because it was tied to natural gas, 50 percent of the Senate Democratic Caucus voted against -- voted against legislation that would provide an incentive to this company.\par \par \uc2 And not only did they vote against it, when we had a rally with Union building trade workers, union building trade workers fighting for their jobs. The rallying cry of the progressives on the far right was your jobs don`t matter. And when you tell a man or a woman that their job don`t matter -- that their job doesn`t matter, you`re telling them they don`t matter. That`s when I decided I need to get to the center to get away from the extremes on both the right and the left to try to rally the middle, middle class, and the middle-of-the-road voters to get back to common-sense policies in this commonwealth.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mayor Brown, how would you -- tell me how do you -- this energy sector has been pretty important to economic development around here. Obviously, there`s a -- there`s concern about climate change and there`s some concern about -- what would you say to national Democratic leaders about both the importance of that, and how do you pitch climate change proposals that don`t scare people around here?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, let`s be honest. There is a change in the climate. People can debate that. They can say yes and no. I firmly believe there is.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 We`ve held rallies in Wilkes-Barre on Public Square addressing the climate change. We have to take a realistic approach to this. There is a problem. We have to make sure that we address it. But at the national level, the leaders at the national level have to do this. Naturally, as a city, I will get to follow whatever they put out if I believe in my heart it`s the right way to go. But it is serious, it`s real. And I -- also, I do implore our congressmen, our senators, our president, to take this seriously and make sure that we take the proper stance here and the proper methods.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How would you sell it? How would you -- how would you appease, Senator -- the senator, Yudichak here.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: How would I?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Appease. You know, the concern about the issue of, hey, these regulations are there, you know, that the half of the Democratic Party essentially doesn`t want to use government investment for the energy sector. What would you say to the national level?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, I think that it`s going to be necessary down the road. It`s going to have to happen. Senator Yudichak and I, we`ve agreed on several key issues over the last three years that I`ve been in office, and the Senator has been a wonderful help as far as securing funds for us for different projects we have going on during the city.\par \par \uc2 So, we are like in several ways as far as the major policies that we want to push through. You`re exactly right. I mean, we have to get together in unison and work on this. We have to do something about the energy problem. Right now, $4.59 a gallon, that`s not going to cut it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. I keep seeing $6.50 for diesel. That is just scary.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: When you see that number there. I`m curious, Senator, when you move to -- when you -- when you got rid of the D and you put the I there, is it makes it harder to legislate? Does it make -- you know that`s the one you know that there`s a huge desire. Voters say they want more independent candidates.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But it`s hard to let -- it`s hard to function when you don`t have somebody --\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: It`s a great question. As a student of politics as you are --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: The conventional wisdom would be yes, it makes it much harder from the middle and from the independent party. That wasn`t the case. Some of my biggest legislative achievements have been in the last three years because I was one independent, two in the Republican majority, and three have many Democrat friends like Mayor Brown here in the city of Wilkes- Barre. We`re able to cross the aisle. That is really what voters want to see. Can you accomplish tangible things?\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately, Pennsylvania is closed primaries, and independents like myself can`t vote, so only the extremes dominate the primary election. And I think that`s what you see playing out in states that have closed primaries. If you had a more robust primary and open primary, you would be able to have that debate, and legislators would not have to be looking to primary opponents. Rather, they`d be looking to be appealing to general election voters.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Give me the general election in a Senate race that would be the most difficult for you to decide to pick.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: The -- we try --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Meaning, you do -- tell me the two candidates that if they face each other, that`ll make your harder.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: In this Pennsylvania --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: Pennsylvania primary? I think that you mentioned it earlier. I think Oz, Fetterman --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Beats up to see if you breathe them.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: It will be a very tough decision for me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why?\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK:: I think now someone on the Democratic side like \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2 , who would represent this area very well, who just got the endorsement from the Philadelphia Inquirer, a rather progressive newspaper. They said he`s a candidate that could add value versus a candidate like Fetterman, who just adds a vote. I think both candidates, Oz and Fetterman, are more about celebrity than they are substance. So that would be a very difficult challenge for voters in the Northeast between Oz and Fetterman.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mayor Brown, who would -- who would do best here? Come you`d think could do the work better?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: I feel it would come down to \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  -- Congressman Lamb, and also, Dr. Oz. Dr. Oz has the name the publicity, the money. Congressman Lamb has using Congress and Marine Corps experience. I think I liked some of his values that he`s put forth. So I would say that as a battle between Congressman Lamb and Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I appreciate it. I have a feeling I got much better political handicapping from the two of you than from anybody we`re dealing with today. Good to be talking to both of you. Good to meet you.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Welcome to Wilkes-Barre.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What Chamber of Commerce weather you brought here? We got to give the mayor a credit for that.\par \par \uc2 YUDICHAK: The mayor gets all the credit out.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: Well, what we did looks out --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: We knew you`re accompanying --\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is -- I mean, not a --\par \par \uc2 BROWN: When Lester was here --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Literally, not figuratively, literally not a cloud in the sky if you can find it.\par \par \uc2 BROWN: When Lester was here two years ago, we couldn`t do anything for him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Same Picture?\par \par \uc2 BROWN: But rain it was there.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Rain, all right. I`ll let him know. Lester now knows you know -- gets more love here. All right, Mayor Brown, John Yudichak, thank you both.\par \par \uc2 Up next, we`re going to head to the other side of the state, back to Beaver County, another place that we put on our county-to-county spotlight and another place where Republicans are making inroads into what used to be traditional Democratic-leaning union workers. You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY live here in Wilkes-Barre, Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:27]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Since taking office President Biden has vowed to be the most pro-union president in American history. Just last week he met with the leader of Amazon`s labor union and in Ohio he called unions the single best workers in the world. Union members, that is.\par \par \uc2 Union workers have long been a key voting bloc for Biden and Democrats. But Republicans have made some major inroads with these voters as well. In some places really scrambling the political terrain and battlegrounds across the country. We saw it in eastern Iowa a few months ago. We see it right here in Luzerne County, Pennsylvania, and we see it where we find my colleague NBC News Washington correspondent Yamiche Alcindor who is in Beaver County, Pennsylvania. A place that was heavily, heavily a Democratic pro-union county.\par \par \uc2 And Yamiche, it`s still pro-union, but it`s not so pro-Democratic.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, NBC NEWS WASHINGTON CORRESPONDENT: That`s certainly right, Chuck. And last night, I was about an hour north of Pittsburgh, right on the border of Ohio, and listening to a town hall with the \uc2 United Steel Workers\uc2  talking about sort of the things that are important to them which is worker rights and retirement, but also talking about the divisions among them, the differences.\par \par \uc2 Listen to what Bernie Hall, one of the leaders in that union, had to say about those differences.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BERNIE HALL, UNITED STEEL WORKERS DISTRICT 10 DIRECTOR: I think union members are no different than anybody else, right. They don`t live in a vacuum. So it`s not just about, you know, labor issues. They also are concerned about women`s rights. They are also concerned about education. In some parts of the state, gun rights are a big issue. But at the end of the day, typically what we find is working people vote their paycheck.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:01]\par \par \uc2 You know, what`s going to pay my bills, what`s going to keep a roof over my head, what`s going to put my kids through school.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: So Bernie Hall was really trying to make the case in that meeting that workers should really be voting their pocketbooks. But he also made the case that they cannot be thinking about the political atmosphere where they`re voting. And he was talking about the Supreme Court case and the poised decision to possibly overturn Roe v. Wade. He said if the Supreme Court can overturn a law from 1973, what about the 1935 Wagner Act that said that companies had to organize and had to sort of negotiate rather with the unions that were helping employees get better wages? So it was a real interesting connection to hear him make that case.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Yamiche, I`m curious. And look, if the answer is no, it`s no, did you see any evidence -- any of the Senate candidates sort of making a play for any of those workers last night?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: John Fetterman was very, very popular among that crowd. They were saying that he came, he talked to rank-and-file workers. It might be the shorts. It might be the fact that he feels more like them. But he apparently made a very, very good pitch to them. And they feel good about that.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you, though, that one union leader said something to me that had stuck in my head ever since he said it. He said it`s really going to come down to the three G`s among the rank-and-file and that is God, guns and gynecology -- Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s an interesting third G that we`ve not heard before. The reason I asked about, and I`m curious on the Republican side, we have -- they`re closed primaries and I know there aren`t a lot of registered Republicans among labor union members, but it is something that is growing. No evidence of any Republican candidates trying to win these votes?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Well, Republicans are doing -- a lot of the work that`s being done for Republicans I`m being told is on the media. It`s sort of what information that people are taking in. So I was listening to union leaders talk about how do we balance what our workers and our fellow union workers are listening to on the radio. Basically they`re saying that the message from conservatives are that Democrats want to take your gun.\par \par \uc2 The Democrats are going to be against abortion. So he shouldn`t be voting for them for that. So they are really in some ways going with -- leaning into the cultural issues while on the other side, you heard union leaders really try to explain to a lot of their members and really try to strategize about how to make union workers understand that workers` rights, retirement, the infrastructure bill, some of the things that President Biden has passed that that is what`s in their best interest.\par \par \uc2 But I have to tell you, you could feel the tension in the room and the culture it seems to be working. Except where I said John Fetterman, who was in some ways meeting them with the culture, the way he dresses, the way he talks.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Really trying to give a message to working-class Americans.\par \par \uc2 I will also say that they were handing out these forms, which really had X`s by all the Republicans and checkmarks by all the Democrats trying in a subtle way to say that the Democrats have your back and the Republican do not.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Well, I`ll tell you, boy, the evidence just keeps piling up as to explain why so many national Democrats have turned away from \uc2 Conor Lamb\uc2  and have started focusing on John Fetterman here. I think they do see him as their best candidate to win this Senate seat.\par \par \uc2 Yamiche Alcindor, I`m going to see you tomorrow on the other side of the state. We`ll meet in Philadelphia. Thanks for your work out there in the western of the state.\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Can`t wait.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, President Biden tries to ease fears of surging inflation nationwide. Vulnerable Democrats face some anxious voters in their district.\par \par \uc2 Congressman \uc2 Chrissy Houlahan\uc2  will join us next. I told you I have seen $6.50 diesel. $6.50. She`s in a competitive Pennsylvania district that Democrats are trying to hang on to in November.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching a special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:41:53]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. While much of the focus here in Pennsylvania this November will be on that vacant Senate seat, thanks to retiring Republican \uc2 Pat Toomey\uc2 , Democrats here and nationwide are also facing the increasing likelihood of losing control of the House. Last hour President Biden laid out his plan to address the issue that`s become perhaps the Democrats` biggest liability and the top concern for voters this year. Inflation.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: I want every American to know that I`m taking inflation very seriously, and it`s my top domestic priority. My plan is to lower everyday costs for hard working Americans and lower the deficit by asking large corporations and the wealthiest Americans to not engage in price gouging and to pay their fair share in taxes.\par \par \uc2 The Republican plan is to increase taxes on the middle-class families, let billionaires and large companies off the hook as they raise profits and raise prices and reach record profit amounts.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m joined now by Congresswoman \uc2 Chrissy Houlahan\uc2 , Democrat from Pennsylvania, right here in Pennsylvania, whom the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee says holds one of the most competitive seats this November. I`m sure that is not music to the congresswoman`s ears.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, it`s good to see you. Thanks for spending a few minutes here with me.\par \par \uc2 REP. CHRISSY HOULAHAN (D-PA): It`s nice to be with you. Thanks for joining us in Pennsylvania.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So, look. $6.50 diesel. Right. You just -- you see it. It really stings. Right? $4.50 is bad enough for regular gas. You see that. So you see this everyday reminder of this inflation hit. Look, you heard the president`s remarks. What more do you want from him? What more do you want from the administration right now?\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: Sure, and thanks for having me. And you`re not wrong. Those are difficult prices to see and something that we all collectively need to address in whatever capacity we can from each of our roles. And so what I would say for my role is the message I would like to project to you is that Democrats in this Congress have for the last several years been able to stop what could have been a catastrophe, what could have been a depression of the likes of which we haven`t seen in a long time.\par \par \uc2 We didn`t experience that. And as a result of the American Rescue Plan and the Infrastructure Act and the CARES Act and a number of other things, we`ve been able to layer on the help that has allowed our economy to be able to survive. And now we are absolutely experiencing some of the other side effects of that help that we`ve provided.\par \par \uc2 But we do very much need to recognize, we are in a place where we now in my community in Chester County where I`m sitting and talking to you right now have 3.2 percent unemployment, a really good economy. And we certainly need to address the fact that gas prices are a problem and grocery store prices are a problem. But we also need to recognize that we are still here standing two years after a literal pandemic.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, there`s two things that the administration could do arguably. Look, the Fed, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has what it can do with interest rates.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:01]\par \par \uc2 But there are two things that could be done with policy. One is, you mentioned the unemployment rate. It means we actually have a labor shortage. We need to open the borders a little bit. Obviously, politically, that`s a different conversation. The second is to repeal some of those tariffs on Chinese products.\par \par \uc2 Are you for a spaghetti at the wall approach right now by this Biden White House? Hey, pull some tariffs, add some immigrant labor, whatever it takes to lower cost of living.\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: Well, what I certainly am for some of those things. As an example, I did encourage the administration to make sure that we open the Strategic Oil Reserves, which we did. I have encouraged the administration to encourage the fact that all of our pipelines and oil that had been pre- pandemic be activated for one reason or another, we`ve reactivated the rigs that were put into a dormant position were reactivated. So I have done that.\par \par \uc2 In terms of immigration, yes, I absolutely believe that we have a real problem with not enough people to fill our jobs. Whether they are on our mushroom farms or in our \uc2 QVC\uc2  headquarters which is here in my district. We do need to have comprehensive and bipartisan immigration reform. And I really do think that`s something, frankly, that the Congress can do that I`ve been encouraging my colleagues across the aisle to do alongside with me.\par \par \uc2 So you`re right, each one of us has a different role to play. The administration has their role. The Fed has theirs and the Congress has ours. And I believe that we, as Democrats, are working very, very hard to make sure that we`re working on behalf of all of the people, the working people of our communities and our economy to make sure that we are instead of taking things away from them, that we are instead providing opportunities for us to survive and to thrive.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me your midterm message. Look, between the issue of reproductive rights and the fact that women may actually have rights taken away from them that were once granted. You have the inflation issue. They`re not two issues that easily go together when you`re making your elevator pitch, when you`re making your campaign messaging. How do you plan to do it?\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: So my community is that purple place in Pennsylvania. And that`s why you`re probably sitting here talking to me. It is that place that has 40 percent Dems, 40 percent Republicans, and 20 percent independents.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: And we want to see our government work for us on behalf of us. We are a pragmatic people and we want to work on what I call endearingly, affectionately the no duh agenda, which is making sure we provide terrific health care opportunities for all of us, making sure that we have terrific educational opportunities for us all. Great jobs that treat one another with decency and civility.\par \par \uc2 All of those things, but the other thing which you kind of alluded to in terms of choice, we want to make sure that we`re protecting the rights that we already have and the fact that we are all equally able to be successful in this economy and in this society. And so the no duh agenda is the kitchen table kind of conversation. But overarching that is the civility and decency that I think we all demand from one another. And choice is part of that conversation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you think you`re going to have a hard -- you know, Democrats had a hard time selling the economy last year when there was -- seemed like it was a lot more positive news. So you`ve got to sell this hey, look, we`re the party that can, you know, we got handed another crisis, third straight Democratic president that gets handed a crumbling economy, is able to sort of turn it around.\par \par \uc2 But at same time, you`re going to have a voter that says, boy, I don`t like the way Biden is managing this economy, but I don`t like the direction of the Supreme Court. Are you worried about losing that voter, that the voter may care more about the pocketbook than what the Supreme Court is doing?\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: So I think that our community is a very -- as I mentioned a very pragmatic community. And it`s weighing its options very, very thoughtful voters here. And so my job is to make sure that I`m communicating what we brought home to our community. And as we began this conversation, what we brought home for our community is schools that are open, one of the highest vaccination rates in the entire country here in this area, in this district.\par \par \uc2 We brought home PPP loans, emergency disaster loans, shuttered venue loans, restaurant loans, we brought home the opportunity for teachers to be able to be vaccinated. These are all of the things that we have as messages that have allowed our economy to be still open at this point in time. And so that alongside with some of the larger overarching issues of choice and freedoms I think are what I hope is the right message and the strong message to say that as I mentioned Democrats are very much about unifying people whereas I really believe that Republicans, at least the ones in this race, in my particular race, are about dividing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Do you have a preference in the Democratic primary for Senate?\par \par \uc2 HOULAHAN: I do not. I very much look forward to finding out who that person is. That gentleman is. And I very much look forward to throwing myself a thousand percent behind support of that candidate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Fair enough. Congresswoman \uc2 Chrissy Houlahan\uc2  from a pretty important swing district in the suburbs of Philadelphia, thank you for coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:08]\par \par \uc2 This special edition of MEET THE PRESS DAILY, we`re live from Luzerne County, it continues after this short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Tomorrow, MEET THE PRESS DAILY will be heading south past Allentown and King of Prussia to Philadelphia for much more on next week`s primary elections in Pennsylvania where folks are a little bit more on the D side of things. But before we bid Wilkes-Barre farewell, here`s some conversations that our own Dasha Burns had with voters in the area about next week`s big primaries.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 MELANIE PATTERSON, CAMPAIGN VOLUNTEER: It always says this is the race, the Senate race that Dr. Oz is in and you get a reaction from people right away. You know, no, we don`t trust him. That he`s gone too woke.\par \par \uc2 DASHA BURNS, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: And what about you? I mean --\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: Yes. Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: A man that you admire, Trump did endorse Oz, did that have any impact on your view of him?\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: I thought he made a mistake. That was a major error.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: I agree 100 percent.\par \par \uc2 PATTERSON: And thought he`s getting the wrong advice, you know, from people. And that`s what everyone is saying. He`s getting the wrong advice. Who advised that?\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Trump, the guy that you changed parties for endorsed Oz.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Does that influence your influence your opinion?\par \par \uc2 DAVID "MOGIE" MAGILL, OWNER, MOGIE`S IRISH PUB: It does in the sense that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in my opinion picks winners. I mean I don`t think -- he knows he`s electable and who is not. And that has a lot to do with how you can move your party forward.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 [13:55:09]\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Dasha, I guess if I were to say what did I come away with so far on this trip, it`s there`s a lot of clarity on the Democratic side all of a sudden. I mean, almost everybody seems to expect Fetterman to be the nominee. Nothing but a muddy picture on the Republican side. Trump`s endorsement of Oz muddied things up. It did not clarify.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Pennsylvania is not Ohio. As you say, Pennsylvania is not Ohio. And here, you kind of have like the ink blot test, right. You can see MAGA in whatever candidate you look at. So if you are a die-hard Trump fan of Pennsylvania, you kind of have your pick right now. But the reason I think Pennsylvania is going to be biggest Trump test yet is because of this resistance to Oz. If Oz does win, that`s going to say a lot about the Trump influence.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And if Oz wince here, it really is -- that means that 40 percent undecided was waiting for the Trump patsy.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: Ultimately said, OK, I`ll do it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, it`s very interesting and no matter what, we`re going to have quite the general. So you don`t get to leave. I think northeastern Pennsylvania becomes even more important as a swing area.\par \par \uc2 BURNS: I`m renting a condo in Luzerne County.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, they`ll be happy, that`s for sure.\par \par \uc2 Dasha, nice work. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 We`ll see you all in Philadelphia tomorrow. We`ll have exclusive profile of John Fetterman and his wife. A fascinating interview that Dasha has. I`ll also have a Senate hopeful from each party, live on set with me, Kathy Barnette and Malcolm Kenyatta, will join us in Philadelphia. You won`t want to miss it.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s coverage will continue with Katy Tur after this break. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220511ei5a00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle for May 9, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Alicia Menendez, Cal Perry \par \uc2 7,729 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  recused himself from any Supreme Court cases involving the communications of his wife or the presidential election, generally since his wife was a participant in it at the highest levels with the White House Chief of Staff.\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER, FMR. ATTORNEY GENERAL: Yes, that is a no brainer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And that is what former Attorney General \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  told us earlier in this hour. THE 11TH HOUR WITH STEPHANIE RUHLE starts now.\par \par \uc2 [23:00:59]\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: -- gets underway on this Monday night. Good evening, I`m Alicia Menendez in for Stephanie Ruhle. Ukraine has now been fighting Russia`s unprovoked invasion for nearly three months. Today, \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  was trying to defend his war. This was Victory Day in Russia when the nation celebrates victory over Nazi Germany. It`s Russia`s biggest holiday.\par \par \uc2 And during today`s observances, Putin tried to justify the war with false claims of Nazis in Ukraine and so called aggression toward Russia. Yet, amid the images of troops marching in formation, and the military hardware on display, Putin did not declare a new offensive in Ukraine and did not declare victory. While in Ukraine, President Zelenskyy offered up his own message to the Kremlin.\par \par \uc2 Today, a senior U.S. defense official called Russian efforts on the battlefield incremental is somewhat anemic. Despite that, Russia continues to hammer Ukraine with airstrikes this weekend targeting what Ukrainian officials say was a school being used as a shelter, at least 60 percent to be killed.\par \par \uc2 NBC News reports that night President Biden told Democrats Putin is a very, very, very calculated man, but that he doesn`t have a way out right now.\par \par \uc2 Earlier today, the President signed an updated version of the Lend-Lease Act. The program originally used to speed weapons to Britain during World War II will now help get weapons into the hands of Ukrainian fighters.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , U.S. PRESIDENT: Everyday Ukrainians pay with their lives, the cost of the fight is not cheap. But caving to aggression is even more costly. That`s why we`re staying in this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Biden has called on Congress to approve another $33 billion for Ukraine. House Democrats say they`ll begin working on that funding legislation tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 Let`s begin our coverage of the war with NBC`s Cal Perry live for us tonight in Kyiv. Cal, it wasn`t just Victory Day in Moscow, Russian troops also mark the day in Ukraine. Tell me more.\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, we had this kind of bizarre scene on the eastern part of Mariupol, where we`ve not seen a heavy fighting last few weeks, where Russian officials who have changed the street signs there to Russian took part in a small parade. This was obviously a piece of propaganda used by Moscow. It coincided with that large March that we saw in Moscow.\par \par \uc2 But bizarre because the fighting on the other side of the city is so fierce, you continue to see that sort of stronghold that steelworks facility where hundreds of fighters are still in that basement area, we understand that a number of them are wounded.\par \par \uc2 And so to have these scenes play out on the other side of the city is a juxtaposition that a lot of Ukrainians just cannot wrap their heads around. On the other side of the country, you have a continuation in the east of violence the school that you mentioned hit on Saturday night and airstrikes there. At least 60 people were sort of trapped in the rubble at some point but again, it`s hard for rescuers to reach that area just as it was, for example, that theater in Mariupol because the fighting is heavy, because the airstrikes from Russia just continue.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Cal, do we have a sense of the status of that steel plant in Mariupol?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Not a good one as for an hour by hour basis. We know that as of yesterday, the government was still in communication with fighters there. We think most of the civilians if not all of the civilians have finally been extracted from that site. They`re on their way the last of them to Zaporizhzhia, but we just don`t know how long the Ukrainian fighters can hold out and they`re not sharing with us how many there are remaining in that plan for what they say are operational security reasons.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:01]\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot I think of speculation that the Russians are close to taking that plant because they have swung a whole lot of troops to the eastern part of the country to the Donbas region leaving just a few 1,000 in the city of Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Cal, I wonder which area of the country are Ukrainian officials most worried about at this point?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Well, certainly Mariupol because they don`t have a good grasp of how many people have died there. Officials think it could be in the tens of thousands, in the eastern part of the country in the Donbas region, in the Luhansk area that`s where that school is the fighting is fierce.\par \par \uc2 I should say the Ukrainian government is really proud of gains that`s making in the Kharkiv region, that`s to the north, that Kharkiv, you see there, region east of where I am from the north of the country, six villages have been liberated by Ukrainian forces.\par \par \uc2 So the Ukrainian army is on the move in the northern part of the country. It seems like they`ve stalled the Russian invasion to the east. And then in the southern part of the country, its cities marred by massive bombardment, you know, the city of Maricopa has basically been wiped off the map.\par \par \uc2 The city of Odesa, I should say, in the last six hours has come under cruise missile fire, at least seven rockets landing in the city of Odesa. So a lot of fear about what could happen in Odesa as well because it is, of course one of those black sea port cities,\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: NBC`s Cal Perry live for us in Kyiv. Ukraine. Cal, as always, thank you.\par \par \uc2 With that, let`s bring in our experts Peter Baker, Chief White House correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . He spent years as the Moscow bureau chief for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  closely watching the rise of Putin. Jason Beardsley, a decorated U.S. military veteran with over 20 years of experience in the army and navy. He`s now the National Executive Director for the Association of the Navy. And William Taylor, former U.S. ambassador to Ukraine is also the \uc2 United States Institute of Peace\uc2  Vice President for Russia and Europe. Gentlemen, it is good to see you all.\par \par \uc2 Peter, I want to start with you. Wasn`t a lot to take away from today`s victory day in Russia. But we did hear this from the U.S. Ambassador to Russia, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AMB. JOHN SULLIVAN, U.S. AMBASSADOR TO RUSSIA: It`s also difficult to know what President Putin is planning, it`s difficult to speculate because his decision circle is so small. If I were a betting person, I would say it`s most likely that President Putin is doubling down on his special military operation in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Peter, Putin tried to defend the war today. Why do you think he chose to refrain from making any major declarations about what is next for Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Well, I think first of all, of course, the reality on the ground is such that it makes any kind of pronouncement along those lines pretty hard to sell. Even in a country where the Kremlin controls the media where the information is under the state auspices. There`s a lot of reality still there in Russia. They see the body bags coming home. They hear the stories from their loved ones on the frontlines. They know what`s really going on, at least a lot of them do.\par \par \uc2 And I think that it would be hard for him to sell a story of victory at a time when there`s clearly nothing like that.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, I think I don`t think I`ve answered what Ambassador Taylor thinks, because obviously, he knows that part of the world really well. I think that he enjoys being unpredictable. I think he probably enjoys having tweaked us for the last number of days, thinking that something big was going to come and then watching us get all worked up and then showing Ah, see, I didn`t do what you thought I was going to do. I`m not, you know, so easily categorize.\par \par \uc2 But that doesn`t mean that something big can`t happen. It doesn`t happen to happen -- it doesn`t have to happen on Victory Day for him to decide. He`s going to escalate or in some way, make this war even more deadly and devastating and it`s already been. So we get worked up about the holiday. But it`s the days after the holiday we should probably be thinking about.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Ambassador Taylor, Peter Baker wants to hear from you. And that means so do I.\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM TAYLOR, FMR. U.S. AMBASSADOR TO UKRAINE: Alicia, Peter, it`s great to great to be able to talk to you, I think Peters right. I think Peter`s right. President Putin had two bad options. People were debating whether he would choose one or the other that is doubled down or back down, declare victory and try to settle it down. He couldn`t really do either. He didn`t have a choice. There was no good option for him.\par \par \uc2 So he chose no good option. He is afraid, Alicia. He`s afraid of his people. He doesn`t want to ask them to sacrifice. He knows that they`re already starting. Peter`s right. They`re already the people in Russia are already beginning to feel the pain. And they`re beginning to bury their sons and fathers and brothers when they come back from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 So Alicia, Putin is worried about his standing with the people, a dictator or not, he`s got to worry about the people.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Jason, I want you to take a listen to what we heard today from a former adviser to President Zelenskyy. Here it is.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 IGOR NOVIKOV, FMR. ADVISOR TO UKRAINIAN PRESIDENT ZELENSKYY: I`ve got that sense that Putin blinked today in you know, there were no ultimatums. There are no victories announced, it looks like you know, they don`t know what to do. He`s scared. You know, with the Lend-Lease bill signed, I think it`s Ukraine`s to win now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Jason, do you agree?\par \par \uc2 JASON BEARDSLEY, NATIONAL SECURITY EXPERT: Yes. And by the way, both Peter and William have it right here and really Putin didn`t blink today.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:03]\par \par \uc2 He`s been blinking for the entire invasion, every step of the way. He`s been beleaguered and hampered by logistics, poor supplies, bad leadership. They tried to knock out the Ukrainian communication networks. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2  stepped in with Starlink. They tried to knock out Ukrainian weapons, the United States stepped in with howitzers with all kinds of ammunition and munitions. So that`s when -- that the Ukrainian forces have been fighting. They`ve been creative. They`ve been using guerilla style warfare plus combined arms strategy. They are really on the cusp of being able to open up a seam in this battlefield. That doesn`t mean this is over soon.\par \par \uc2 Putin as the two guests already suggested, he did what he could today a show of certain let`s call it theater, it was farcical. Everybody knows that. The reason he didn`t brag and complete this or call this a war, it`s a bit of a technicality. He does not know he cannot rely on the Russian people by declaring a general mobilization because that will put them in a whole different worlds. Right now he`s trying to limit this and try to save face as much as possible.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, Ambassador Taylor, it`s in such sharp contrast to what we are seeing from President Zelenskyy, that powerful video walking the streets in response to Russia`s Victory Day talking about being a free people. What are you hearing from your contacts in Ukraine about how the government views the war at this point?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Alicia, President Zelenskyy used his words, he mobilizes his people, and they mobilize him. He`s -- He really represents them. The Ukrainian people are strong, are tough. They`re determined to be free. They`re interpreted to be independent. And he is leading that. He is -- he grows on that. He absorbs that. And he projects it and it`s a very positive projection. So it is a great combination President Zelenskyy and Ukrainian people that are so tough.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Peter, we also saw evidence on Russian media of anti-war sentiment. There was hacking on Russian TV instead of a display of the usual channels and message took over the screens that read, quote, the blood of thousands of Ukrainians and hundreds of murdered children is on your hands. The Kremlin has made it a crime to express anti-war sentiment, people have been arrested. Could a protest like this hack prompt the Kremlin to crack down even harder?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Certainly, yes, absolutely. They`re very, very jealous of the information space they have over the years, repeatedly cracked down on any information, you know, source that got in their way as they saw. They`ve been incredibly repressive since this war began with these new law threatening 15-year lifestyle, or 15-year sentences in prison for using the word war to describe the special military operation. They have arrested hundreds, if not thousands of people over the last few months.\par \par \uc2 They`ve successfully, you know, tamp down a lot of the street actions up until now. But the more and more, you know, information gets through. And more and more, you know, Russians feel that they are losing, you know, their loved ones for a cause they don`t necessarily believe in. I think the more dangerous it becomes for him. Ambassador Taylor`s right that there isn`t backed up the popular, you know, will that matters even in a dictatorship. That`s what happened in Afghanistan, what happened with Chechnya, there was, you know, people at home saw the cost of war in their own communities that you can`t hide funerals.\par \par \uc2 And I think that, you know, over time, you know, he is worried about losing that public support, which is why he`s playing the nationalist drum so heavily in order to try to keep up, you know, some sort of sense of patriotic duty in fighting a war that many Russians don`t understand because it`s against remember, they`re a Slavic brothers, these are not against Chechens, or Afghans or people they consider to be foreigners Ukraine. Ukrainians to them are like, you know, fraternal relatives, and they -- and I think it`s very confusing for a lot of Russians why they`re at war in a family like that.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: All right, Jason, to that point, tonight you had the President expressing concern about Putin not being able to find a way out of this war. What Is the President worried about here aside from the conflict not ending soon?\par \par \uc2 BEARDSLEY: Desperation. What we`ve seen from the beginning is because Putin hasn`t been able to decisively defeat the Ukrainians on the ground, you`ve seen these aerial bombardments of the cities, the atrocities and Bucha, places like that. You`re going to see more of that the longer Russia is in play.\par \par \uc2 So I think what President Biden is worried about is essentially adding to that. So the real key here, President Zelenskyy has got to mobilize his forces, the new munitions that are being surplus inside of Ukraine, get them to the front lines and combine their strategy with the so far guerrilla style sort of hit and run tactic warfare. The Ukrainians have been doing and they need to hand Russia some decisive blows.\par \par \uc2 Russia right now they`re creating their bodies on site. They`re trying not to push bodies back to Russia. So they are trying to hide this from the people.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:01]\par \par \uc2 But information is leaking out. And again, all credit to two Ukrainians for one, putting this effort together and to the White House for getting the weapons in here. The big lesson is they should have been in before the invasion. We had six months of previous intelligence. So here`s an opportunity for the President to get in front of the next kind of occasion Taiwan, looking right across the street to China.\par \par \uc2 The time to do weapons supplies is not in the middle of a fight. It`s prior to it. And by the way, the Green Berets, the Special Forces have been successful since 2014 training the Ukrainians on these tactics, which is why they`ve operated differently than they did in Donbas region prior to that. This is going to be success.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Ambassador Taylor, this all comes as we heard this alarming news from the Pentagon today, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We certainly have seen indications that Ukrainians are being moved from Ukraine into Russia. I can`t speak to how many camps or what they look like, but we do have indications that Ukrainians are being taken against their will into Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Ambassador your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 TAYLOR: Alicia, war crimes. This forced movement of people who don`t want to these are Ukrainians. They want to go into Ukraine. They want to go into where free Ukraine is. And Putin is moving them, is almost kidnapping them, taking them back and putting them into what people are calling filtration camp. We`re talking about reeducation. We`ve heard this, Alicia. This is terrible that we`re going back to this.\par \par \uc2 So this just indicates why Ukrainians have to win. Why the Ukrainians have to be able to beat the Russians and their counter attacks are an indication that they can do this. They`ve succeeded on the ground. They succeeded on the sea with their own missiles, on their own anti-ship missiles, they can do this. They need to win so that these atrocities stop.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Jason, I take your earlier point about the need to have gotten these weapons there sooner given where we are given that we have already sent billions in weapons. What else do Ukrainian forces need now?\par \par \uc2 BEARDSLEY: Well, they`ve gotten a lot of good intelligence from the United States, communications is huge. You can`t fight on multiple fronts without those robust networks. That`s where again, Musk with the Starlink network came in and patched a gap that`s made Ukraine very effective in this. What they need now is to be able to integrate the new weapons. The United States is training them in other countries, that`s been great. It`s now getting those forces into play, because as Ambassador Taylor suggests, the opportunity is now to break the will of Russia, the only way to do that is you got to knock out their armor, you got to knock out their artillery.\par \par \uc2 They`re doing that now a steady supply of that has helped. But again, I think the United States needs to be smart here. We`ve got our own issues trying to compete in the naval fleet against China, against Russia. So it`s time to look at how do we get in front of these rather than behind them.\par \par \uc2 And I would suggest that the White House has got a great opportunity, while the American people are very supportive of a strong defense. It`s better to do this and avoid a war than to get inside of one and have to end it.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Peter, to that sense of urgency, the President is pushing Congress to immediately approve more aid to Ukraine. He says it needs to be done before trying to secure and more COVID funding, the House taking up the measure tomorrow. What should we be watching for?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Yes, what`s striking, of course, as the President knighted states came to Congress said I need $33 billion to help Ukraine with his military humanitarian aid, and Congress is bidding him up. They look like they`re going to add another $7 billion to which indicates, by the way, I think a certain bipartisan consensus here in Washington, which is rather remarkable given everything else. They`re telling the United States we not only support what you`re doing in terms of helping Ukraine, we want to do more.\par \par \uc2 So that`s going to be I think, on the table starting tomorrow. The question is whether the Republicans and Democrats have completely agreed on the specifics of the bill that they want to move forward. They don`t want to let this get bogged down by tying it to this COVID bill, which is tied up in an immigration fight this unrelated because they do feel there`s some pressing need to get this out there that if they don`t do that, there`ll be a slowdown on the weapons that Jason Beardsley talking about and that`s something neither Republicans nor Democrats won.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Our thanks to Peter Baker, Jason Beardsley and Ambassador William Taylor. Coming up. Critics of Roe argue abortion is an issue states should get to decide. But those critics may not be planning to stop there.\par \par \uc2 And later the world`s addiction to oil has been blamed for fueling autocratic governments for years. Could the tragedy of Ukraine finally end the dependency? THE 11TH HOUR just getting underway on a Monday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:24:15]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: You`ve just said that you believe life begins at conception. If there is legislation brought to you to ban contraception. Would you sign it?\par \par \uc2 GOV. TATE REEVES (R), MISSISSIPPI: Well, I don`t think that`s going to happen in Mississippi. I`m sure they`ll have those conversations in other states --\par \par \uc2 TODD: But you`re not answering the question.\par \par \uc2 REEVES: As is always the case with thing -- well, that`s always the case. There`s so many things that we can talk about.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Justice Alito is leaked opinion overturning Roe v Wade has many fearing what`s next. Take for example, Mississippi`s Governor refusal to rule out a ban on contraception. Some Democratic candidates for state attorneys general now promising to help. Just today New York`s AG announced new legislation to boost funding for women`s clinics in the state.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:05]\par \par \uc2 With us tonight, Harry Litman, former U.S. attorney and former Deputy Assistant Attorney General and Victoria DeFrancesco Soto, the dean of the Clinton School of Public Service at the \uc2 University of Arkansas\uc2 . She is also an MSNBC political analyst.\par \par \uc2 Harry, if Roe v Wade is overturned, there is already concerned access to contraceptives could be next, what else is on the table, how much of this is going to play out in the courts?\par \par \uc2 HARRY LITMAN, FMR. U.S. ATTORNEY: On the table is a really vast array of things we`ve come to take for granted, Alicia, same sex marriage, into race, marriage, private, consensual behavior, even the ability to educate your children. All of these things are completely put into play by the Alito leak.\par \par \uc2 Now, the leaked draft says, Well, this is only about abortion. But judges don`t declare winners or losers. They give reasons, they give principles. And just as you can say, as to abortion, well it used to be a crime in the 18th century. That`s why we can overrule it today, you could say the exact same thing, exact same thing about marrying someone of another race, marrying someone of the same sex.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s just completely at the grace of these five, whether or not that something new like that will come up and be shot down. And of course, as the Mississippi Governor makes clear, there`s a lot of people in the country who will want to be pushing them in that direction.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Vicki, as I listened to Harry lay out everything that could possibly be on the table and lay out the power of these five justices. I have to wonder to what extent is the politically galvanizing argument here about health care, access to abortion, access to Plan B, contraceptives, and to what extent it`s about a broader argument on judicial overreach and the variety of rights that could be impacted that will actually motivate voters?\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA DEFRANCESCO SOTO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Right. And I think that what we`re seeing here to take a step back, Alicia, is a shifting of a rallying point for so many decades, essentially, since 1973. We`ve seen Roe v Wade be a rallying point in the GOP and a momentum. And we`ve reached this point.\par \par \uc2 So when we`re looking at these other things that Harry put on the table, the question is, will they be sufficiently effective in rallying folks to push forward, right? I think that that is the question. And I think it`s also going to differ by region, right.\par \par \uc2 So when we`re talking about the curtailment of rights, and taking back these rights, we see it at the national level in terms of the justices acting in this way. But I also think that we need to keep our eye on what`s happening in our region in our individual state, for example, what we`re seeing in Mississippi right now, so I think it`s an overall issue that we need to keep our eye on in terms of judicial overreach, but also what is happening at the state level in the courts, but also the legislature.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Harry, I want to shift gears a little talk about the 1/6 investigation. The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  has more on the role that \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  played in advancing Trump`s efforts to overturn the election. The paper reports quote, Meadows granted those peddling theories about a stolen election, direct access to the Oval Office and personally conducted some with the President. He pressed the Justice Department to investigate spurious and debunked claims. He also pushed the Justice Department unsuccessfully to try to invalidate the election results in six states through federal court action. Has been nearly five months now since the House found him in contempt. So why hasn`t the Justice Department charged Meadows yet?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: And by the way, it`s not just that he`s also with Trump the entire afternoon of 1/6, and even after he`s repeatedly told don`t do it, he goes ahead, that report is really harrowing, why not? Well, some have posited that there`s a memo that makes it makes -- it seem as if maybe there`s a claim that he doesn`t have to show up, because he`s so close to Trump, that there is so called testimonial immunity.\par \par \uc2 The courts have rejected that, Alicia. But the idea might be that it`s giving the department pause before charged him with a crime. But what I`ve suggested they ought to do is re subpoena him tomorrow and have it only apply to things that have nothing to do with his official duties. For instance, the campaign and everything about the 1/6 rally because the argument if it exists back there is that it`s only for our official duty.\par \par \uc2 So then I think that could slap an immediate new content referral on him and I think that might be an easier road for the Department of Justice. Short answer is we don`t know and it`s a real issue because there`s so much that he is up to his elbows in.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: All right, \uc2 Eric Holder\uc2  suggesting a Trump indictment could be coming. Here`s what he had to say earlier tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ERIC HOLDER, FMR. ATTORNEY GENERAL: By the end of this investigative process, you`re going to find that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has done the necessary things to meet all of the elements of a variety of statutes, and also will be have shown to have the requisite intent in doing those. But if nothing else, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was a norm breaker and he has taken us to a place where a consideration of a prosecution of a former president and his closest allies considerations of the prosecution so all of those people is totally is totally appropriate.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Harry, your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 LITMAN: You know, Holder is an institutional so am I. I basically coming around as he is to the view that the only thing worse than prosecuting Trump is not prosecuting him. He continues to absolutely propound The Big Lie, whip up all his supporters and maintain the same toxic effect on the country. I just giving him a pass on all of this. Wow, what a message about the rule of law.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Harry Litman, as always, thank you so much for being with us. Victoria has agreed to stick around. Coming up, members of Congress are about to go on the record over abortion rights. We`re going to preview a critical vote destined to fail on THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:55]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. KIRSTEN GILLIBRAND (D-NY): We need to make sure that every single voter understands that the Republican Party and \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  does not believe that their daughters, that their mothers., that their sisters have rights to make fundamental life and death decisions. We are half citizens under this ruling.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Senate Democrats warning of Republican efforts to restrict abortions nationwide after \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  left the door open to a nationwide ban, telling USA Today it was quote, possible. The Senate plans to vote on a bill to provide protections for abortion rights on Wednesday. The vote is expected to fail. Democrats don`t have the support to pass the 60 vote threshold.\par \par \uc2 Senator Lisa Murkowski one of the last potential Republican supporters of the bill says she will vote no.\par \par \uc2 Still with us Victoria DeFrancesco Soto, and joining us MSNBC political contributor Matthew Dowd is also a former \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  strategist and founder of Country Over Party. Matt, this vote on Wednesday, it`s expected to fail, why is it still important that they hold it?\par \par \uc2 MATTHEW DOWD, FMR. CHIEF STRATEGIST TO BUSH-CHENEY CAMPAIGN: Well, it`s going to fail. But I also always think politics is always about pointing out and pushing through where you want the debate to be about. And I think Democrats would be smart to make this entire campaign over the next six months of referendum on rights and freedom, not only about pro-choice, but also about voting rights.\par \par \uc2 And so I think holding a vote, even if you lose begins to build resonance for this in the coming campaign. But there`s no doubt this is going to go down. But I think at this point in time, what we`re talking about from now until November 2, no the first week, first Tuesday of November, is what the campaign is going to be about and Democrats want to make this about that.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right and Vicki, there`s going to be the lived experience that accompanies that larger argument. The LA Times wrote about how even with Roe v Wade in place, low income women struggle to get abortions in Texas, you`ve been covering this years long effort to restrict abortion access in that state. Talk to us, how do we get here?\par \par \uc2 SOTO: The disproportionate burden as you point out, Alicia, is going to be on for women and especially women of color. And I think that the one strategy going forward and looking at all of the odds being stacked against the Democrats and seeing that something is likely not going to get passed in the Senate, looking at the Supreme Court, looking at various state legislators is looking at public opinion. And I have one word, it`s overreach.\par \par \uc2 I think that we have really reached a tipping point where we know that first of all, the general American public is supportive of a woman`s right to choose. But beyond that, I`m really zeroing in on two key groups. A, independence, where we see two-thirds of independence, especially women don`t support this total ban on abortion or near total ban, as well as Republicans.\par \par \uc2 So really interesting among Republicans, it`s very few that you have that extreme case, most Republicans and this is from a recent Pew poll shows that it depends on the length of pregnancy. But what we`re seeing is this rhetoric of a very extreme stance, not even in the case of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 So I think that this overreach that we`ve seen is going to pull over certain segments that had been teetering before, and also mobilize those segments of the Democratic base, namely those women of color.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, Matt, to this point that Vicki is making about overreach, you have McConnell saying a nationwide abortion ban is possible. He has got to know that is not where a majority of Americans are.\par \par \uc2 And so I wonder Is this him trying to cater to the base ramp up midterm turnout? And then he doesn`t actually go through with it on the other side of it. Is this something that could come back to haunt him?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: I think \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  knows in his heart, that if this debate in November is about choice, and is about abortion, the Republicans lose.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:00]\par \par \uc2 I think \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  knows that they want the Republicans want this debate to be about the economy and inflation, because in a debate if the election becomes wholly about that Republicans win. And so I think this is \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  clearing the deck for now, just saying that going on moving on knowing that he has to make that argument.\par \par \uc2 But as Vicki said, this is this is a great issue for Democrats to both motivate and persuade because of the vast majority of the country. The issue is, is will Democrats do it? Do it well, and be able to adjust the debate and move the debate from a debate about inflation to a debate about rights? And that really is the fundamental question for November. Can they move this to a broad enough and (INAUDIBLE) enough debate to move the election off the economy?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I got to ask you, since you both have ties to Texas, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  points out quote, Governor Greg Abbott said on Thursday that he would seek to overturn a 1982 court decision that obligated public schools to educate all children, including undocumented immigrants.\par \par \uc2 Vicki, it brings back your point about overreach, it makes me think about California and the way we watch this go down in California in the 90s, when you had a Republican governor who pushed very hard on these same questions of public services for immigrants, could we see a similar swing back in Texas?\par \par \uc2 SOTO: I mean, if this makes it up to the Supreme Court, Alicia, potentially, it would. And let me just point out the irony that 2001 was the first state in the nation to allow in State College tuition for dreamers.\par \par \uc2 So I mean, I think what we see here is Greg Abbott, has really been one of the most outspoken folks in terms of abortion and abortion restriction. That fight is being won for him. Now he`s turning to another issue that has really been mobilizing the base. And this is I think, where we`re going to see Greg Abbott really take the stand. He`s won other battles. And the border issue is one that he knows, as we saw with the Trump presidency, in the Trump campaign, can really fire up the base.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yes, I mean, Matt, McConnell may want this to be about the economy, but he`s got Greg Abbott, you know, screaming about undocumented immigrants. He`s got \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  picking fights with \uc2 Disney\uc2 . There`s a lot of noise in the national atmosphere. Is McConnell going to be able to make this about the economy?\par \par \uc2 DOWD: Well, I mean, that`s what he has to try to do. But I as I listened to all of this, how far afield we are from what the Republican Party in Texas was 20 years ago, when \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  was governor. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  could not get elected Governor of Texas as a Republican today. He was pro- immigrant. He didn`t want to argue about a bunch of the social issues at the time here in Texas. He wanted to talk about and focus on the economy and education.\par \par \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 , in just in two decades, we`ve moved from \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  to Greg Abbott, vastly different political parties today, and that`s where we are. Greg Abbott knows if he wants to run for president, which all odds are that he does. He`s trying to make cultural issues. He thinks he`s got this election won. He`s moving on to the next election which he wants to run in, which is for President United States.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Victoria DeFrancesco Soto, Matthew Dowd, thank you both so much for spending some time with us. Coming up, our next guest says Western greed for oil helped prop up \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  for years. We`re going to ask historian Ruth Ben-Ghiat if a new plan to phase out Russian oil is too little too late. THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:48:15]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD STENGEL, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Maybe the lesson of sanctions is to get in early and go bigger instead of do a kind of a drip, drip, drip approach. We`ve done a lot already. You know as I`ve said you before Jose the biggest sanction of course is the EU cutting itself off from Russian oil and gas that`s bigger than all the sanctions that we can apply.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Despite the heavy reliance on Russian oil from several G7 Member States, leaders promised to phase out or ban imports during a virtual meeting yesterday with Ukrainian president Zelenskyy. In a statement, the leaders promised to ensure that we do so in a timely and orderly fashion and in ways that provide time for the world to secure alternative supplies.\par \par \uc2 With us tonight, Ruth Ben-Ghiat, history professor at \uc2 NYU\uc2  and the author of the book, "Strongmen: Mussolini to the Present." Ruth, you recently tweeted about Putin and other kleptocrats saying Western greed for their oil and money prop them up for years. Ruth, will these new sanctions make a dent?\par \par \uc2 RUTH BEN-GHIAT, PRFOESSOR OF HISTORY, \uc2 NEW YORK UNIVERSITY\uc2 : I hope so they`re actually getting to the heart of Putin`s regime because Putin isn`t just a dictator. He`s a kleptocrat. And his whole regime revolves around control and plunder of national resources above all oil and gas.\par \par \uc2 And in a kleptocracy, \uc2 Gazprom\uc2 , for example, the oil -- the energy conglomerate is treated as a personal tool by Putin and the oligarchs. And its profits are actually kind of plundered. It`s called exfiltration, where the profits are not going back into Russian society, but they`re taken out into the leader and his cronies offshore accounts.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:00]\par \par \uc2 So if you want to strike it this whole system of thievery, you have to strike at do you have to do what the \uc2 EU\uc2  is planning to do.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: OK, but given that, Ruth, let`s talk timeline, what is a, quote, timely and orderly fashion mean? Especially when you have some of them saying, we`re not going to do it immediately. We`re going to phase this out. Does that give Putin time to come up with a contingency?\par \par \uc2 BEN-GHIAT: I think it would it but there`s already a stumbling block and that \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2  and Hungary just want to go along with this, and there`s nothing surprising about this. But in fact, you know, the whole thing is we`re having a huge reckoning of the relationship of dirty energy, and the enemies of democracy. And for too long, we have made these deals with dictators out of, you know, a thirst for energy for low energy prices.\par \par \uc2 And the problem is, you know, in the past, especially the Germans, nobody wants to pay the price at home. And, you know, \uc2 Viktor Orban\uc2 , he just got reelected on a ticket where he said, I`m never going to, you know, back sanctions on Russian energy, because that`s going to write raise prices for home. So, he actually played this nationalist card.\par \par \uc2 And so it shows the intelligibility of this, you know, he takes \uc2 EU\uc2  money, but he`s very much you know, Putin`s ally, and now he`s holding up this whole thing, and that gives Putin time to have more of a contingency, which is not good for the cause of Ukraine and ending the war.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Meanwhile, earlier today, Council on Foreign Relations, President Richard Haas warned of what Putin might do if the rest of the world keeps standing firm against him, take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RICHARD HAAS, COUNCIL OF RELATIONS PRESIDENT: So if you have a losing hand, you don`t want to walk away from the table that punctuates it, you just keep the game going. So my guess is Putin that I was going to play for time, a low key open ended, military struggle. So there`s no one who can say you lost and you could say I didn`t lose. This is still ongoing, we are going to prevail. He at least has the talking points.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Ruth, how does that match with your understanding of Putin as a strong man?\par \par \uc2 BEN-GHIAT: You know, when I watched this, quote, Victory Parade today, it came to mind like this late communist spectacle with this kind of frozen, rigid leader, and this, you know, these platitudes, this gaslighting, because these are very old themes. He talked about that Russia is the victim of the West. And the war as a defense of Russia has just been grafted onto these old themes.\par \par \uc2 And the thing is, you know, what, an irony that you had parades of tanks and soldiers, when you know, Putin`s misguided war has cost 24,000 soldiers, 1,000 tanks, but the public, when you have a regime that is propped up by repression and propaganda, what you need is the appearance of victory, not actual victory. That`s what Putin cares about. And he`s in this war to maintain himself in power.\par \par \uc2 And so just going along and gaslighting the Russian public, which is doing quite successfully his popularity, ratings incredibly rising, and so I could see that what that scenario making sense to him.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Ruth Ben-Ghiat, as always, thank you. Coming up, the big award for a pint sized hero in Ukraine when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:10]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: The last thing before we go tonight, Patron`s presidential metal. You may remember we recently told you about patrolling. He is a bomb sniffing Jack Russell Terrier, saving lives in Ukraine credited with detecting more than 200 Russian explosive devices since February. He has been gaining fans all around the world.\par \par \uc2 But now he has received a medal from President Zelenskyy himself for dedicated work in the Ukrainian army. Patron`s owner who is part of the Civil Protection Service was also honored.\par \par \uc2 Canadian Prime Minister \uc2 Justin Trudeau\uc2  with Zelenskyy in Kyiv for the ceremony yesterday, but Patron seemed suspicious of him.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translator): And medal for the dedicated service, a service dog called Patron.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Look at that tail wagging. Trudeau checking his pockets for cheese treats for Patron there. It has been an exhausting few days for that famous pup.\par \par \uc2 Next, Belarusian opposition outlet tweeted a photo of Patron snoozing during a press conference over the weekend. Seems that even heroes need a rest. A very good dog to take us off the air tonight.\par \par \uc2 And on that note, a reminder you can always catch me on American Voices, Saturdays and Sundays, 6:00 to 8:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, good night.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220616ei590002w\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE LAST WORD WITH LAWRENCE O`DONNELL for May 9, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Lawrence O`Donnell, Claire McCaskill, Dahlia Lithwick \par \uc2 7,167 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donne