ls but there is another possibility out there. There`s two Republican senators in the Senate who broadly support abortion rights. One of them is Lisa Murkowski. And she thinks this bill goes too far. I want to read what she said in a statement in February when this bill failed the first time. She wrote.\par \par \uc2 The fact that my choice is between this bill or nothing at all shows how insincere Majority Leader Schumer is about protecting women`s rights, failing to conduct any outreach reducing this important issue to nothing more than a designed to fail show vote is a disservice to women across America.\par \par \uc2 I know you don`t agree with that. But is there room perhaps for compromise with senators Murkowski and Collins on some other piece of this ever?\par \par \uc2 HIRONO: As far as I knew, both of those senators have supported Roe. So you know, for all this time, they -- I don`t think they took the position that roe went too far. So no, I do not agree with their positions now. No room for compromise, no room for a compromise bill here. All this bill does is protect a right a constitutional right that women in this country had for almost 50 years. That`s what this bill does is hardly what I call radical, Garrett.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I want to ask you about Speaker Pelosi. She just put out a Dear Colleague letter this morning kind of talking to her members about how they will talk about this issue. And in it, she writes Republicans have made clear that their goal would be to seek to criminalize abortion nationwide. Make no mistake once Republicans have dispensed with the precedent and privacy and overturning Roe, they will be taking additional basic human rights.\par \par \uc2 Obviously, this is after \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  said that a national ban on abortions wasn`t off the table if Republicans take back power in November. Do you agree with the speaker`s outline of the stakes here, not just on abortion rights but potentially on things like marriage 