ominates.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: I want ask you, Matt, while you have the floor here, about Donald Trump`s reaction to the abortion news from last week, which is basically been silence.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I`ve been following it pretty carefully. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s fascinating it to me. I mean, this is something that he ran on. He appointed three of these justices. If this decision is what it appears to be, he can claim as much credit as anybody and I have never known \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to pass up an opportunity to claim credit for something. What`s going on here?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Sometimes Trump is very shrewd, and he`s been asked about this in some interviews. I know it`s hard to believe but -- and he said, look, it`s not the final decision. Wait and see. And I think from his viewpoint, and the point of view of many Republicans and conservatives, they feel as though the Supreme Court has burned them before. So they`re just going to wait. Look, this is a leaked opinion. We know there were five votes in February, but until it`s officially released, we`ll see, we`ll hold the champagne.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Juanita, Democrats say Republicans just don`t have the guts to own this.\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: They don`t have the guts to own it because we know this is not about life. This is about control. It`s about harm, it`s about furthering their authoritarian mandate, which they have been running on. And I think Democrats have to tie this to that broader frame. This is the same as the efforts to censure basic black history being taught in the school. This is the same as attacks on LGBTQ people and children, and looping in abortion into that same frame is going to carry this message forward.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: If it`s official, Trump will own it because it`s about `24 politics. Right? And --\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: No benefit to Trump for owning it for `22. He wants to own it for himself for `24?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: Well, what I`m saying, he`s going to -- the pro-life movement is part of his movement, is part of the Republican Party. And so he`s going to need to rally that base in `22 and `24. I think it`s literally a case of him just waiting and see. Remember he always loves to see, we`ll see.\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: It`s weird.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: We`ll see.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: It`s also one of those things that \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  was always more comfortable doing than was \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , right. \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is a true movement conservative. He lived and breathed in this space. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  didn`t.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: But remember \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was pro-choice for most of his life.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Also true.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: For most of his pre-political life. And I want to just add on to one thing you said, which is this is how \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  is framing it, too, as if they`re going to do this, just wait until what they do next. What rights will they strip away next? Pelosi just put out a letter an hour ago about that and said that to her Democratic lawmakers, like we need to fight on this because, A, it`s not over and B, we don`t know what`s next.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Jake, you`re not as comfortable with Supreme Court politics. But I know you love the Senate counting here. What do you make of the Schumer strategy to put this Women`s Health Protection Act on the floor again knowing he doesn`t have the votes to pass it?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: He has no choice. I mean, he really has no choice. He has to fight the good fight. I mean, the majority, vast majority of Democrats want to codify Roe v. Wade. Let`s be clear. Not 50, though. There are not 50 Democratic votes to codify Roe v. Wade so this isn`t an issue of well, if they just got rid of the filibuster they could pass this thing. No. There are -- Joe Manchin is not for this piece of legislation. There are not the votes to codify.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Casey might not be either.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Bob Casey of Pennsylvania, a longtime pro-life lawmaker, is probably not. We haven`t heard from him directly on this. They don`t really have many places to go right now. The House passed it, Senate can`t pass it, they`re going to have just throw up their arms and continue to talk about it.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, Juanita, when I interviewed Mazie Hirono earlier in the show, she said that`s one step one. Step two is take it to the polls. We`re starting to see some Democratic lawmakers do this already. I want to play for you part of an add that \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2  is running online right now on this issue. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Three men, one agenda. Fulfilling Mitch McConnell`s decades-long crusade to criminalize abortion. They all supported New Hampshire`s abortion ban and now with Roe v. Wade on the verge of being overturned, these three men and their one agenda mean a woman`s fundamental rights, our freedoms, hang in the balance.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: No punches pulled there. Do you think that kind of thing is something we`ll, A, see more of and will be effective?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: I think it absolutely is going to be something we see more of because Democrats have to paint that explicit picture of what is to come next. I appreciate that she also named McConnell in there with him talking about a federal ban on abortion. This is not going to stop, especially when the right and the Republicans have dedicated decades to taking this basic right away from women and anyone with a uterus.\par \par \uc2 I also think that is going to be effective and I want Democrats to continue to illustrate what day-to-day life will look like. People don`t have money to drive or fly hundreds of miles to access abortion. They don`t have child care to be able to do that. All the same things that Republicans are also taking away and blocking in Congress. But we are absolutely going to see more of this.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So, Matt, are we going to see Republicans lean in and engage in the nitty-gritty on this fight or are we going to see them say, we`d rather talk about inflation, you know, the other issues that have brought them to the cusp of what could be a very effective midterm campaign for them?\par \par \uc2 [13:55:11]\par \par \uc2 It`s sort of like, do you really want -- here`s the culture war fight you`ve kind of always wanted. Do you want to have that fight or do you want to stick with what got you as close as you apparently are?\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: I think candidates are going to have to be ready to answer the question of where they stand in a post-Roe politics, in the new politics of abortion in this country and they should be able to make their positions known with clarity and with fluency. That`s hard to ask of a lot of politicians, right. And so some of them won`t be able it do it. But I think there are others who will. And the truth is, you know, we`re talking about the pro-choice base, there`s also a pro-life base.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 CONTINETTI: And you need those voters to come out in the fall if you want a chance to recapture Congress. And so the Republicans can`t shy away from this issue. They also need to recognize, too, that as the current polling shows, the most important issue facing the electorate is inflation, inflation, inflation.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: Jake, you get the quick last word here, but are you sort of looking at for who the next Todd Akin is, who maybe takes this a little bit too far or?\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Yes. These are really complicated questions. It`s not only rape, it`s not only incest, it`s things like ectopic pregnancies where like the life of the mother is truly at risk, where do you stand on that? How do -- people need to find answers for that and quickly because those things are very salient with a lot of people.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: So a lot of folks who talked about this in broad terms are going to have to get real smart real fast.\par \par \uc2 SHERMAN: Yes, very quickly. Yes.\par \par \uc2 HAAKE: All right, folks, we`ve got to go. Jake, Juanita, Matt, thank you all for joining us for this conversation. And thank you all for being with us for the hour.\par \par \uc2 Chuck will be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY live as we said from Pennsylvania. MSNBC coverage continue with Katy Tur after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220510ei5900001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION WITH AL SHARPTON for May 8, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Jay Gray, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,685 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:00:00\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION, and Happy Mother`s Day. Tonight`s lead, mothers of the moment.\par \par \uc2 Right now, I want to thank the women that have and continued to shape me, the nation, and the world. Earlier today, First Lady Jill Biden was in Ukraine joined by its first lady, her unannounced visit to the country, coming halfway through her tour of the refugee crisis in Eastern Europe, while her husband, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , met virtually with Ukrainian President Zelenskyy and the leaders of \uc2 G7\uc2  nations to discuss new sanctions on Russia.\par \par \uc2 And here in the U.S., Congress is girding itself for votes on President Biden`s $33 billion aid request as early as this week and for the escalating battle around abortion rights after last week`s leak of that Supreme Court draft opinion potentially overturning Roe versus Wade. Senate Democrats are preparing for a vote this week to codify Roe that is unlikely to pass. As Senate Republicans led by Minority Leader \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  say that a national abortion ban could be, quote, possible.\par \par \uc2 Out in the streets, protests continue across the country nearly one week after the leak, but civil rights activists like myself who are distressed by both the disproportionate impact on women of color and the legal battles that we know will follow this one.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Congresswoman \uc2 Joyce Beatty\uc2 , Democrat of Ohio, Chair of the Congressional Black Caucus. Congresswoman, thank you for joining us tonight.\par \par \uc2 I can only imagine tactically the position that you and your Congressional Black Caucus colleagues find yourself, in knowing that Roe versus Wade is under threat, thinking about your black women constituents who already suffer from disproportionate deficit in reproductive care while bearing a maternal mortality rate three times higher than their white counterparts, according to the Center for Disease Control. As chair of the CDC, would have the conversations between members been like this week, and have you heard from your constituents on this matter?\par \par \uc2 REP. JOYCE BEATTY (D-OH): Well first of all, Reverend Al, thank you for having me on, happy Mother`s Day to all.\par \par \uc2 Yes, we have been speaking out as members of the Congressional Black Caucus, male and female, because we know this will have a devastating, disproportionate effect on women of color, especially black women. In 23- some states our constituents are contacting us because if this is overturned, it would immediately ban abortions in 23-some states, even including my state, of Ohio.\par \par \uc2 So, we are speaking out. We have a chair of the pro-choice caucus. Congresswoman \uc2 Ayanna Pressley\uc2  has joined with me and others, Congressman Brenda Lawrence over the women`s caucus, Congresswoman Barbara Lee, and even our male members of the CDC. Because this devastating effect, people could be incarcerated, whether that`s the doctor, the nurse in participating. We know that 56 percent of black people live in the south, so we know it will happen whether it is incarceration, whether it is the legal process, it will disproportionately affected black folks.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congresswoman, what`s your response to the news this weekend, 19 Democrats wrote a letter to Speaker Pelosi Friday calling on her to hold a vote on legislation that would increase police funding to address rises and crime. Given that we still don`t have police reform legislation, isn`t unrealistic that some black voters might ask why this instead of that? And do you anticipate President Biden or Democrats making another push behind priest reform before November?\par \par \uc2 BEATTY: Well, absolutely, and I can certainly speak for the Congressional Black Caucus and where I stand. We are not giving up on police reform.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:00]\par \par \uc2 We need to make sure that we go back to the table. And while I want to make it clear, the House did pass the George Floyd Justice and Policing Act. It did not get through the Senate, to get to the president`s desk.\par \par \uc2 But we`re not giving up. We have met as members of the congressional black caucus, as leaders in the Democratic caucus with the president. The president has been very positive, and I am very hopeful that he will step up with an executive order.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And let me be clear, we`re not saying to be anti-police. We`re saying that we can do both and not one or, and we certainly want to see police reform.\par \par \uc2 BEATTY: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman --\par \par \uc2 BEATTY: Absolutely, and I think that`s so important it is about making sure that we re-channel funds into our communities, that we set up neighborhood commissions, that we get mental health advisers, but we also need to make sure that those bad police officers aren`t rehired. We need to make sure that all of those points in the George Floyd Justice in Policing don`t go forgotten.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That`s right. We`re a few days from the second anniversary of George Floyd`s killing, and I`ve been to your district and with you as hundreds come out as we -- I had to do a eulogy there for a police shooting victim.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you this. Can you comment on the significance of Jill Biden`s visit to Ukraine earlier today? As you and your colleagues prepare to vote on the president`s aid request for Ukraine in its defensive war with Russia, what is your feeling about Doctor Biden`s trip?\par \par \uc2 BEATTY: I think Dr. Jill Biden being there sent a strong message when we look at the number of women, and the number of children. I mean, it is unthinkable that Russia would go into a medical center and now a school with so many people every day losing their lives. I think that it gives them hope and courage that we are standing with them, I think this week we will see more aid going, and more sanctions because this is just unthinkable. I`m pleased that Dr. Biden was there.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman, what other concerns do you want to see President Biden addressed by executive order if need be before November, I`m thinking about voting rights as well as reform?\par \par \uc2 BEATTY: Voting rights.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: What is your response?\par \par \uc2 BEATTY: Well, let me just tell you, voting rights is at the top of the list, we have still been working in the Congressional Black Caucus. Just last week, we had an executive meeting where we are looking at if we can even get preclearance for all 50 states and get that through the Senate. We`re going to come out with that, we are not giving up on voting rights. It`s at the top of our list.\par \par \uc2 I think we have to also look at what`s student debt is looking like. We hear a lot from our constituents about that. We know there is legislation that`s looking at providing financial assistance for those with student debt. We know the president has also talked about providing assistance for those.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a full play. We are continuing doing this off-year presidential election. Many of our primaries are underway. And I think the good thing is Democrats understand that elections have consequences.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me ask you about one thing that is critical to me. As we look at this battle around women`s right to choose, and whether this leaked opinion will lead to the Supreme Court, in many ways, debunking the Roe v. Wade decision of 1973. This can potentially lead to a whole new model of state`s rights deciding basic rights for people, which could include LGBTQ rights, civil rights, voting rights.\par \par \uc2 Are you concerned that we are now in a nation that is beginning to see forces try to lean us back towards a state right model with the national government doesn`t interfere and protect us against state`s rights?\par \par \uc2 BEATTY: Oh, absolutely. And I think that`s why it is so important for us to speak up, to speak back, get people registered and vote. We must put all of our energies and here, because you`re right, once we`ve opened up Pandora`s box with this, it will be other rights for women, civil rights issue you mention, interracial marriage rights, LGBTQ.\par \par \uc2 So, we have a lot to lose. We recognize that. And that`s why we`re fighting to make sure that we vote, and we vote in those judicial races.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:00]\par \par \uc2 It is so important that we have a pipeline. That was very fortunate for us that we had Justice \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 . So we`re very pleased that we now have a black woman in the highest court in the nation. We can`t stop there. She can`t do it alone.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you Congresswoman \uc2 Joyce Beatty\uc2 , Chair of the Congressional Black Caucus.\par \par \uc2 Now for a quick update on the war in Ukraine, joining me now is NBC`s Jay Gray. He is in Lviv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Jay, first lady Dr. Jill Biden made a surprise Mother`s Day visit to the southwestern corner of Ukraine, which has been under attack for the last ten days. And she met with the Ukrainian first lady. What is the reaction you`re seeing from Doctor Biden`s visit today?\par \par \uc2 JAY GRAY, NBC NEWS SENIOR NATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Reverend Sharpton. First of all, the first lady of Ukraine called the visit by Dr. Biden a courageous act, noting that the area where they spent a little bit of time was an area that had suffered attacks over the last ten days on a regular basis.\par \par \uc2 The first lady said that she had to be there on Mother`s Day, said that it was important that she show the world that the United States is behind Ukraine, that the United States supports Ukraine, also said that the war must end, and wanted to make sure that she got that message across.\par \par \uc2 On the ground, residents here in Lviv say that they are proud that the first lady made the trip and they`re also very thankful. I spoke with a member of the city council here in Lviv today, and she said that the American people have been a tremendous help in this effort. She also said that they along with the government will share in the Ukrainian victory that she believes will be coming soon.\par \par \uc2 Overall, you can sense from people on the ground here in Ukraine that they will really appreciative that First Lady Dr. Jill Biden was on the ground and ventured into Ukraine, not to staying on the border. That was very important to them, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Jay, Russian troops continue to advance in Ukraine ahead of their annual May 9th holiday commemorating the Soviet victory against Nazi Germany. What more can you add to the Russian Day reaction there in Lviv?\par \par \uc2 GRAY: There`s clear a lot of tension across Ukraine right now, just worried about what`s going to happen as we move into Victory Day. There have been air raid sirens across the country today, including here in Lviv earlier. Quiet right now, just after midnight.\par \par \uc2 But, again there`s a lot of people that under concern that Russian troops on the ground here are frantically trying to give \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  something that he can claim as a triumph on this Victory Day. As you know, that`s a day every year where he likes to flex the military muscle of the Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 I can also tell you that today in the town square here, there was an exhibition honoring the fallen, not only soldiers, but also civilians who have died as a result of the Russian invasion here. When asked about it, the residents here say is, tomorrow brings nothing to celebrate, there is no victory, that they will celebrate victory when they`ve expelled Russia from the country. But they do mourn those who have been lost. They say this is a sad time for their country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, NBC`s Jay Gray in Ukraine, thank you for giving us that update.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, a salute to women in our lives this Mother`s Day. And later, we`ll speak with the reproductive justice activist about why the Supreme Court`s draft opinion on Roe threatens more than just abortion access.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague, Richard Lui, with today`s other top new stories. Richard?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC HOST: Rev. a very good Sunday to you. Some stories we`re watching for you this hour, gas prices in the U.S. closing in on a new high. According to AAA the average price of a gallon of regular is $4.32, just one penny shy of the record set on March 11th. That`s up 13 cents from just a week ago. Experts say the war in Ukraine and the impending summer travel season are fueling that spike.\par \par \uc2 Churchill Downs\uc2  say they saw a record amount of betting in yesterday`s Kentucky derby. Nearly $274 million were wagered on derby day, which ended with the second biggest upset in the derby`s 148-year history. The winner, Rich Strike, faced eight in one odds, they longest among all 20 horses.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend Al Shapton right after this short break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:15:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: On this Mother`s Day, I want to honor the women who show up for their ship children in a profound way, those parents who take the time to care and nurturing young people. This day is not just for these unsung heroes but also to the other women who are our mother figures.\par \par \uc2 I have had the privilege of being surrounded by so many powerful women in my life, like my beloved mother, Ada Sharpton, who passed away ten years ago. Though I miss her greatly, I feel her presence in the strangest ways. She was not only my mother but she was also my best friend. No matter what good I do is because of her. No matter the mistakes I`ve made, she always kept me going. She taught me that life isn`t about not stumbling but getting back up when you stumble, admit your mistake and go on and remember those who helped you along the way.\par \par \uc2 She is one of the reasons why I fight as hard as I do on behalf of mothers of the movement who lost their children to police misconduct and racial violence. They are bonded by this shared pain of burying their child.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:01]\par \par \uc2 I`m here to remind you to remember those mothers who are sitting at tables with empty chairs because their children were taken from them.\par \par \uc2 I was joined just yesterday by one of those mothers, Gwen Carr, mother of Eric Garner, at our weekly rally of the National Action Network. We gave out flowers to the mothers in the community who may not have kids at home to give them flowers or who just needs encouragement in the community.\par \par \uc2 As we celebrate our mothers, let`s also offer grace to those women who may have had to make a tough decision over abortion. Right now, the Supreme Court is poised to overturn rights women had with their own bodies for half a century.\par \par \uc2 We must rise up to protect the right for every woman to have the choice of whether to have kids of their own. Even if someone can`t have children, they still can play an important role of helping to shape those who are coming up after them.\par \par \uc2 We appreciate what you all are in our lives. It is because of you that we all can rise up knowing we have your support, so, Happy Mother`s Day. We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 The possibility of Roe versus Wade being struck down is a hot topic right now within the black church as Christians discuss how their faith informs their stances on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Reverend Erika Forbes, a reproductive rights activist, and Monica Simpson, Executive Director of Sister Saw, a national activist organization dedicated to reproductive justice for women of color.\par \par \uc2 Reverend Forbes, you had to abortions as a teenager, one at 14 and the second at 18. How did that experience, and walking into the abortion clinic shaped your opinion on the matter especially that you later became a licensed interfaith minister in Texas?\par \par \uc2 REV. ERIKA FORBES, INTERFAITH MINISTER: Thank you for having me. Those two abortions saved my life. It was without a doubt pivotal moments in my lifeline, because had I not had access to those clinics at such a crucial time in my life, my life would have been decimated. I would not be sitting here before you right now had I not had both of those abortions.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Monica, other black organizers are working to make sure blacks people`s needs are not ignored because as the Supreme Court appears to be said to strike down Roe versus Wade. An activist points out to NBC, that low income patients and people of color already have to navigate a health care system that can be in attentive and discriminatory, which means they would be even less likely to get an abortion if Roe is overturned, worsening a cycle that perpetuates poverty for black people.\par \par \uc2 Now, other marginalized communities were impacted by this as well, such as queers, trans and gender nonconforming individuals. Will you explain how intersectionality of race and sex, gender, is at the crux of this abortion issue?\par \par \uc2 MONICA SIMPSON, SISTERSONG EXECUTIVE DIRECTOR: Absolutely. Thank you so much for having me today. And what`s real about intersectionality and what is important about reproductive justice is that when we think about not just the right to have an abortion but the ability to access an abortion, the ability for us to think about ways that our race is black folks and folks of color impact the ways that we make decisions in this country.\par \par \uc2 The fact that we are working against a system of white supremacy, as we think about fighting for abortion access is deeply connected to this issue as well. And so we can`t look at our lives or even think about our organizing from single issue lens. We have to think about the ways in which all of our identities, all of those systems of oppression that we unfortunately have to live under and navigate in this country, come together when making a very personal decision about whether or not to continue a pregnancy or not.\par \par \uc2 So, reproductive justice is a movement that was started by black women because they understood that it was important for black women`s voices and for folks of color to move our experiences and our needs to the center of the conversations around sexual health and reproductive health in this country.\par \par \uc2 And so what it is so real right now, as we are fighting this fight, right, this is something that this draft that was leaked and something that unfortunately we knew was coming, because our opposition has been working very hard over the past couple of decades to move us to this moment where Roe v. Wade is in peril.\par \par \uc2 And so it is important for us when we think about this, again, especially with folks of color, that we see the fullness of what this really means for us right now when you think about the spike for abortion acts. That`s means that have to think about not only codifying the right for that but also ensuring that we create all that is necessary for us to be able to access that light.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Reverend Forbes, yesterday at National Action Network, I had \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , President of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , first black woman, and we were talking about how opponents have used abortion rights to people that are religious to support their argument. They`ve used religion for why it should be illegal.\par \par \uc2 A new report from the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  shows that while three quarters of white evangelicals want to mostly ban abortion, less than a third of black Protestants support similar restrictions.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:09]\par \par \uc2 Overall the study finds most Americans and most believers support some exceptions to any abortion ban, such as cases of rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 In the most recent data from the CDC in 2019, black women had the highest rate of abortion with 24 abortions per 1,000, and black women are three times more likely to die from a pregnancy-related cause than white women. How does race and religion influence someone`s view of abortion?\par \par \uc2 FORBES: Well, quite frankly, race has played a part in, historically for black women, around issues around reproductive justice. We know that during times of slavery, black women were mutilated, abused, in the name of learning about the women`s reproductive system.\par \par \uc2 And so race and reproductive justice are intricately linked because from the time we were brought here as slaves, we were poked on, pulled on and our rights to our bodily autonomy either didn`t exist or barely existed. So, race and abortion are intricately linked and we`re seeing that through line play out right to this day.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Monica, the Senate will hold a vote next week to codify Roe versus Wade, although the chances of it passing are very slim. How does your organization plan to continue to help women if the Supreme Court does move to end legal abortion protections?\par \par \uc2 SIMPSON: You know, what`s interesting to me when I think about what`s happening at the state level, at our state level, all the way up to the federal level right now, it`s really, really hard to trust and to have respect for institutions right now that are constantly putting our lives and our ability to make our own decisions about our bodies, just putting those on the line every single day.\par \par \uc2 We`re seeing this from Roe, we`re seeing this with voting rights, we`re seeing this with LGBTQ issues, like it is just going across the board that we are having to fight to hold on to the very protections that we have fought so hard for so many decades ago.\par \par \uc2 And so what we`re going to continue to do is to organize and mobilize our people around these issues. We`re working from a state level all the way up to the federal level to ensure that people understand why it`s important for them to be connected to this issue, understanding whether or not you are in line to do the work around voting rights or whether you`re connected to economic justice or environmental justice, that this is a decision that we all need to be playing close attention to and we all need to be organizing around.\par \par \uc2 We are also moving to do a national action at state capital in D.C. for Juneteenth. We are talking about bringing black bodies and black power together and to ensure that black people have a voice and an opportunity to talk about what is happening here and put our faces, our bodies on the line for this work. Because we know that our communities are the most impacted by this when we think about laws like Roe being reversed.\par \par \uc2 And so we are moving people into direct action against, towards that national action for Juneteenth in D.C. We`re also doing local actions every single day, almost at our state level, so we encourage people to be connected to their organizations on the ground in their state. But this is the time for all people to be directly connected to this issue. We have to talk about in our churches. We have to talk about it in our families. We have to talk about it across the board because our lives are at stake.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Reverend Forbes, we`re out of time but I want to ask you this. Do you feel that as we move towards dealing with this issue, that the intersectionality of it is important of getting not only women`s groups but LGBTQ and civil rights groups and groups that deal with immigration across the board?\par \par \uc2 Because if this is the case, if we do see the Supreme Court rule, this will then send all rights, potentially all rights back to the states to decide. And that is a threat to everyone. Do you see it important enough that all the different constituency groups that may be different silos come together to stop it here, otherwise it can come to every other area of concern of civil and human rights in this country going back to a state`s rights model?\par \par \uc2 FORBES\uc2 : Absolutely. Listen, the issue at stake is justice. And if we don`t act, every single individual act right now get out there in the streets and protest and fight for justice, you better believe that if it goes back to state`s rights, every single social justice issue will be at stake.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:10]\par \par \uc2 It`s an all hands on deck, it`s sounding the alarm, we are in crisis now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All hands on deck, all feet on the ground. Reverend Erika Forbes, Monica Simpson, thank you both for being with me.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, former New York City Mayor \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2  knows a thing or two about political hardship. He joins us next with some advice for President Biden and the Democrats, ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 You are watching POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: With inflation rising at home and war raging overseas, Democrats have been bracing for a difficult midterm election season. Now abortions has emerged has another divisive issue as the Supreme Court appears poised to overturn Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 It certainly does not help that President Biden`s approval rating continues to hover near the lowest levels of his presidency. But my next guest says that there is still time to turn it all around.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, former two-term Mayor of New York City \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 , a Democrat, whose piece in the Atlantic magazine is titled, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  can learn from my mistakes.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:02]\par \par \uc2 Mister Mayor, good to have you here.\par \par \uc2 You know, out of everything your wrote in your piece for the Atlantic I was most struck by this line that, quote, I`m quoting the article, keeping things together even in the midst of a crisis isn`t the same as moving things forward. It does not answer the public`s yearning for a sense of direction and it certainly doesn`t make the case for continued Democratic control of Congress, end of quote.\par \par \uc2 Now, how does that explain Joe Biden`s struggle as a communicator as you illustrate in this piece?\par \par \uc2 FMR. MAYOR BILL DE BLASIO (D-NEW YORK CITY, NY): Yes, Rev. You know, what I`m trying to say there is that given how much people are hurting, this is been a very tough few years. There is a lot of fear, there`s a lot of confusion out there. And in that context people don`t just need some good policies. You know, that`s not enough. They need to sense that we`re getting somewhere, that we`re going get out of this.\par \par \uc2 And I think what I did sometimes as I mistook policy for popularity. I worked hard on the policies. I think someone of them are right. But I didn`t think enough about what I was doing to reassure people, to show them the way forward, to give them hope.\par \par \uc2 What I see with \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is a man of great personal empathy and great policy intellect but there`s a disconnect that reminds me a lot of what I did wrong because I don`t think he`s speaking to the fullest of what people are going through right now. I don`t think he`s speaking to them at their kitchen table.\par \par \uc2 I saw, you know on they -- on Friday, I think it was in Ohio, Biden did an event that was supposed to be about the economy. He spoke about a lot of different pieces, China and competition and technology, and I was listening after, while I didn`t know what he was telling me. He was on the tarmac on Tuesday morning after the shock of the draft opinion when Roe v. Wade came out, he said a lot of very intelligent analytical things. He didn`t say the one thing I think a lot of people wanted to hear, which is I will fight like hell to protect the women`s right to choose.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, what really, really impressed me is that you talked candidly about how your popularity with New York voters diminished in your second term, and you felt like you lost touch with voters in the street. And this is after you did what you did with stop-and-frisk, when we all march about it, you did pre-K. I mean you did a lot of the policies things everybody wanted. But -- and you won reelection by a huge margin but then your popularity waned.\par \par \uc2 And then for you to come out now and admit that, and say that, and say, and I made mistakes, President Biden tries to reverse his poll numbers. Now, you`re saying, learn from my mistakes. I think, first of all, I have a lot of respect for you admitting your mistakes and then coming forward such a public way. You were just mayor, what, six months ago?\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO: Not even. And, Rev, thanks for that and I tell you I felt moved to write the piece in The Atlantic because I don`t want to see \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  continue to make that mistake. I do think there`s time to turn things around. We`re in a very dynamic moment, and the Roe v. Wade decision that`s just thrown a huge strategic new angle into all of this, which I think will energize a lot of voters, particularly woman voters to come out. That`s a new factor that I wish it didn`t exist, but it does favor Democrats in many ways, I believe.\par \par \uc2 But what I was trying to say to Joe Biden`s, just like during the campaign in 2020, I think he made some very powerful adjustments. So, I think from early in his campaign which wasn`t so strong to later, he made a lot of the right adjustments. He communicated much more clearly. He won back faith that might have been lost, I think it`s time to do that, and then some. Because, in fact he is pointing us in the right direction but I actually think the message does not meet the vision.\par \par \uc2 For example, he put forward a very bold plan to tax corporations on the wealthy. This is still one of things that animates people across the political spectrum. They want to see that fairness. But he talks about it in bits and pieces. He doesn`t make it a central driving theme. The kitchen table issues, the cost of living number one, I think too often he sounds a bit defeated instead of sounding confident.\par \par \uc2 Look, folks want a leader who`s going to be strong and show empathy. That`s that beautiful FDR --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And that they feel people. I member, James Brown, the godfather of soul, used to tell me, Reverend, people can fear you before they hear you.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you this though.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:00]\par \par \uc2 In the article you lay out some key areas that you think Democrats can win in November, prescription drugs, tax increases of corporations which you mentioned. As Democrats plot their cause over the next months, where does the abortion rights fight fit into the electoral strategy?\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO: So, I think it`s almost a one, two punch, if you will. I think we have historically -- Democrats have historically won and differentiating themselves right at the kitchen table on health care, on the most basic things, jobs, education, all the things that really come home to people, particularly the whole economic reality, because historically Republicans were not believable in terms of helping working class people. Democrats were.\par \par \uc2 There`s still truth and that and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , as a communicator, can do that. He can connect the working class people across all ethnicities and say look I want tax corporations on the wealthy, I want to lower your prescription drug costs, your insulin costs. Yes, real things you can put before him. Congress has to act also to help him, and they can. But the second punch is the question of abortion. Because now you see the militancy and the extremism of the Republican Party, and now you see them snatching away women`s rights, stealing rights away.\par \par \uc2 One of the things we know that gets people going, gets them voting as when they feel something emotionally. Voting is not an intellectual exercise. It`s an emotional exercise. For millions and millions of American women, they woke up on Tuesday morning to hear that their rights were being stolen before their very eyes. I think that`s truly -- if ever there was a voting issue, that`s a voting issue. That`s where right think Democrats bring out an energy we didn`t have before.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I need to ask you this. Crime is rising all over the country, including in New York City. And while you were mayor, crime went down, at the same time you brought stop-and-frisk down. You supported the stop-and- frisk movement -- ending stop-and-frisk when you were running. You marched with us, and you did that when you were in.\par \par \uc2 But now crime is rising. And your successes having to deal with crime and deal with the fact that many of us that are still saying you cannot have police just go unchecked and not accounted. How do you get the ballots? You just were a mayor just a few months ago. How do you deal with rising crimes without going too far in terms of letting police feel that they can go back to the kind of street unit, Giuliani-style that we saw in New York, in cities around the country? Because it`s not only New York, in every major city, we`re seeing crime tick up.\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO: Rev. such an important issue. Do you remember so many people later on realize the crime bill was wrong?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: In `94, yes.\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO: Right, but remember in recent years --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Tens of thousands of people went to jail needlessly for long periods of time.\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO: And it was in response to a real serious challenge, but it was the wrong response, it turned out. And now you have people in recent years falling all over themselves saying they`re wrong. Let`s not make that mistake again. We are going through something very difficult. There`s a lot of pain and a lot of fear out there when it comes to crime.\par \par \uc2 But as recently as 2019, we prove not only can you get rid of the broken policy of stop-and-frisk, we reduced arrest greatly. We reduced incarceration greatly. This was all done with absolute buy-in of the \uc2 NYPD\uc2 . I remember Bill Bryan was saying to me one time early in my administration, you cannot arrest your way out of problems. You need to bring the community into the solution with the police. We cannot lose that lesson.\par \par \uc2 So, neighborhood policing, restoring that notion of bond between police and community, which certainly was hurt after the murder of George Floyd, and it`s been hurt during COVID, getting past the dislocations of COVID, in general, focusing on things like the Cure the Violence Movement and the great efforts, like occupy the corners that National Action Network helped lead, that is the way forward.\par \par \uc2 We actually had proven by 2019 that we had a lot of the key elements. Let`s not lose them and forget them. Let`s work our way back to them. That is what I say.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Before I let you go, briefly, your thoughts on the Republican field and on the control by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  of the Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 DE BLASIO: Well, I would say he keeps proving -- every time, I think, progressives and Democrats and journalists who feel that way, say, look, there is cracks in the armor, maybe he doesn`t have the control, we keep getting evidence, yes, he does. I think we should assume at this point it`s Donald Trump`s party, but that is, in many ways, not, if you will, all bad, because a lot of the candidates that he is backing are not electable in a lot of states because they are just so extreme.\par \par \uc2 And I think the Roe v. Wade issue is now going to put even more of a point on that.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:03]\par \par \uc2 You know, were a lot of those swing House districts are, in those red states that are going to see their laws made draconian overnight by this decision. So I think the combination of Trumpism plus the extremism of the Republican Party on the question of abortion, that is really going to profoundly affect what happens in November.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: \uc2 Bill de Blasio\uc2 , thank you for coming in tonight.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts, stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we in the last hours of Mother`s Day, I again thank and remind myself of my dear mother, Ada Sharpton, passed ten years ago, and I feel her presence often.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:00]\par \par \uc2 But at least I was able to outlive her and do the eulogy at her funeral. And I stay in touch with my father. We have a healthy relationship. He is still a very active and productive businessman.\par \par \uc2 But I think about those that did not have that opportunity for their children to bury them. They had to bury their children, which is why I spend a lot of my time on Mother`s Day calling and texting people like Gwen Carr, mother of Eric Garner, like Wanda Cooper Jones, the mother of Ahmaud Arbery, and even back 20 years to Kadijatou Diallo who I text today, the mother of Amadou Diallo, and Sean Bell`s mother Sabrina Fulton, the mother of Trevon Martin.\par \par \uc2 Just think about how they had to bury their child where in the cause of humanity, usually, the children buried their parents. So, it`s a sad day for them and it`s a day that reminds me that we`ve got to fight for mothers not to have empty seats at Mother`s Day dinner because of those that should be making sure they`re protected and not the cause of the mother`s grief.\par \par \uc2 We will be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [18:00:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me, thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5:00 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220509ei5800001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; International\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for May 7, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Symone Sanders, Cal Perry, Cori Coffin, Juanita  Tolliver, Susan del Percio \par \uc2 7,139 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:20]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: Her body is not a battleground.\par \par \uc2 Right now, I hope that this is the rallying cry and the wake-up call that too many of our lawmakers appear to have needed because I don`t see how this leaked draft opinion from our conservative majority Supreme Court reportedly signaling the demise of Roe vs. Wade and with that, Federal abortion rights, does not consume Washington ahead of midterm elections, and reports from some battleground states say that is already happening.\par \par \uc2 The debate over abortion rights affecting campaigns on both sides with less than six months left to go. Of course, no official ruling has been produced from the draft opinion and nor will there be one for weeks, but the lines have already been formed, with some Republicans, of course, more incensed by the leak itself, while Democrats scramble to find ways to protect abortion rights through legislation, reinvigorating perhaps the conversation around ending the filibuster finally, because there is no discernible end to have this reported opinion will impact others.\par \par \uc2 And Civil Rights activists like myself are preparing for battles to come. So can we expect to see that reflected in enhanced turnout on either side, with some Republicans encouraged that the Court is sympathetic to the escalating culture wars or will some Democrats be able to channel this moment with that support from concerned voters ahead of November?\par \par \uc2 That`s where we start tonight, as always, with the perfect guest. Joining me now Congresswoman Brenda Lawrence, Democrat of Michigan. She is the co- chair of the Democratic Women`s Caucus, and the original co-sponsor of the Women`s Health Protection Act.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thank you for joining us tonight. You`ll forgive me if I`m thinking about what this moment means politically for Democrats, but President Biden calling on voters to elect more of them to Congress in November, ultimately giving him abortion rights legislation, he can then sign into law and congressional Democrats, I`m pleased to say reiterating their call for the demise of the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 But at baseline, I`d imagine the party wants to express solidarity with abortion rights supporters. Can Democrats build on that solidarity going into November midterms, and whose base is most activated by the potential demise of Roe and Planned Parenthood versus Casey, and the more than two dozen states poised to roll back abortion rights if those rules are overturned?\par \par \uc2 REP. BRENDA LAWRENCE (D-MI): You know, we have lived in a society almost the majority of my life -- almost the majority of my life, can you hear me?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, we hear you.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Majority of my life, I`ve had the right and protection to choose. My granddaughter just graduated from college and she is going to have a different life than mine if this past what they are proposing, that was leaked. But the Women`s Health Protection Act, it calls out those politicians who talk about women`s rights, women`s protections, and it causes us to be able to confront those who are running to represent us in Congress.\par \par \uc2 And you ask who is going to be the loudest? Let`s be very clear. There has been this debate on the rights of women when it comes to their health and the right of choice, but understand something, a lot of people have been quiet because they said I`m not going to fight that fight because we have Roe versus Wade.\par \par \uc2 You strip that away, you`re going to see a whole level of active engaged voters, not just women who will say, "How dare you take away my right." I`m a woman who have two beautiful children, have two amazing pregnancies, but I`ve never had an abortion, but I`ve never been a victim of incest, of rape. I never had to look my husband and I say, "Do I die or do I give birth?"\par \par \uc2 [17:05:05]\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: And in any of those cases, a woman should have the right to make that decision, and the privacy part of that is critical. Because once you start chipping away at that, we know so many other issues that have been passed by the Supreme Court into law can be revisited as we are seeing this debate happening right now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, that that is the most disturbing or among the most disturbing things to me is because we`re talking about going back into states` rights model of government, but Congresswoman should Roe be overturn, does it mean that pro-abortion rights Republicans like \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski, were in effect, lied to by conservative Justices, like \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2 , who they voted to confirm despite his voting record on abortion right cases.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: They were absolutely lied to. Remember, majority of the conversation they had was public. It was part of their confirmation hearings, they were absolutely lied to. And we are seeing this precedent of public people who we elect in place in positions of power when they have the mic, they`ll say anything, but then when they get in office, they do something totally different.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I was so much in support, call them out, make them take a vote, make the Senate take a vote. Are you really going to vote to take away the right of choice from women? Are you going to take away a law that has been in effect, in essence, taking away from the public?\par \par \uc2 And I know that we have the Women`s Health Protection Act, and we are pushing for that, as you said, I`m an original co-sponsor. But this is a time where I want to say this. What they`re voting for is not to ban abortion, but to ban safe abortions, and some of them say "Oh, we are never going back to those days." We already are hearing testimony where states right now that put forth so many barriers that women are driving a thousand miles of state crossing state lines just to get a safe abortion.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congresswoman, you joined me last month in Michigan, in Grand Rapids to be specific where I was giving the eulogy for Patrick Lyoya, whose police shooting death was documented in part, of course, by the police body camera, which brings me to this new Police Accountability Bill that you`ve introduced in Congress in response to his killing, because I know that body camera footage is at the heart of the Oath Act. Do I have that right, Congresswoman?\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Yes, you do. And Reverend Sharpton, I`ve always had so much respect for you, but to see you in that room at a time where our community was broken, and to stand there and to call out that which was not right, and just as simple thing as save a man`s name, who murdered Patrick.\par \par \uc2 And so the Oath Act, we know that bodycams are being used around the country, but the only time you hear about them being reviewed is when something bad happens. And so the Oath Act, which I have introduced would require police to review the footage from every bodycam as a training exercise. Let`s use the example of what happened to George Floyd, and few people recognize that that was a training moment.\par \par \uc2 That officer was training new police officers how to apprehend and how to deal with certain situations in the field.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Correct.\par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE: Unfortunately, George Floyd died in that, but if they were using that bodycam and saw an officer sitting on the neck of a human being, they can say: Look, this is inappropriate, you should never do this again and it is against the rules. And if you do that, you`re going to be on your own because you`re going to be charged with murder.\par \par \uc2 Now think about the transformation versus these unfortunate acts by police, bad police officers because we have, thank God, the majority of them are good. But think about the opportunity to be proactive instead of reactive.\par \par \uc2 So you can catch behavior that is happening in your police force, because there is no supervisor riding around with police officers. The only record we have of their day-to-day is the footage from their bodycams.\par \par \uc2 And so let`s be proactive and put this into law where we will mandate that police officers use the footage for training purposes and to remediate and make sure our police are doing the job that we expect them to do.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, thank you, Congresswoman Brenda Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 Let`s bring in former senior adviser and chief spokesperson to Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , Symone Sanders, I am proud to have her as a colleague right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: She is the host of a brand new show airing right before POLITICSNATION. Symone, how does it feel to have your first show in the books? I`ve been doing this show now 10 and a half years, but I remember the first two or three days, how does it feel?\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, MSNBC HOST: It feels good, Rev. I finally got the first one over with. So it`s like, okay, we`re going to do this again tomorrow. I`ve been in rehearsals for a number of weeks, but nothing is like the first show. So I appreciate everyone for tuning in, and I am so glad I get the lead into you.\par \par \uc2 I love you so much, Rev. So this is just a true honor.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, I love you back, and they didn`t give me a lot of rehearsals, but I rehearsed on the air, so that`s a little different. That`s good.\par \par \uc2 But let`s go to this topic of abortion. I want to get your perspective, of course, on turnout, and how midterm campaigns will be affected by this reported ruling.\par \par \uc2 You worked on the Biden campaign and you worked for Vice President Harris. So when President Biden made his pitch to voters incensed by this thread calling on them to grow his majority in Congress producing a path to Federal abortion rights legislation, is it unrealistic to believe that this reported ruling will enable Democrats to galvanize voters, particularly women voters, in a way that makes it possible for them to be galvanized?\par \par \uc2 You been in the field, as I said, you worked campaigns, so you know, on the ground all over this country, will this galvanize voters in your opinion?\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: Well, Rev. I think it depends, right?\par \par \uc2 So there is definitely an opportunity in the suburbs, definitely an opportunity, I think, in rural parts of the country, and in many big cities across, you know, America.\par \par \uc2 The question becomes, what exactly is the pitch? I know a lot of people, Democrats or Republicans, people who say I do not identify as a party at all, who do not think that women or doctors should be thrown in jail for seeking the healthcare they need, and women or doctors in aiding people in getting that healthcare, in the form of an abortion.\par \par \uc2 And I think that that is maybe an argument that can get a lot of people on board, pointing out the cruelty is the point. I think it`s something we may see from Democratic campaigns across the country.\par \par \uc2 But I had \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , House conference -- Democratic House Conference Chair on my show earlier today, and he said that calling out -- calling, you know, some of his Republican colleagues radical or Republicans across the country radical, it wasn`t necessarily the strategy for Democrats, but they were just telling the truth.\par \par \uc2 And I think that folks are going to need to do more than just put labels on it. Folks are going to need to paint the picture and not put it in the lens of Democrat versus Republican. This is about women. Period. These are about doctors. Period. Regardless of who you voted for, who you pray to or where you live.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And it`s about women`s rights, but it`s about all rights, because once you get into a states` rights model, you can then go after Civil Rights, voting rights, affirmative action, immigration rights. I mean, basically States rights was what the Civil War was about. This has a far reaching effect, and I think that Democrats would be wise to bring that up. Everybody has skin in this game.\par \par \uc2 But let me go to this, you interviewed First Lady Jill Biden, right, before she left for her tour of Eastern Europe. She`ll spend Mother`s Day in Slovakia. After meeting earlier today with Ukrainian refugees at a public school in Romania, as the President pushes Congress for $33 billion in aid to Ukraine, votes on which could come as early as next week says Speaker Pelosi, will those votes in favor, be there for them, Symone.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: I really don`t know, Rev. I don`t know if the votes in favor will be there. I think that Americans watching across the country, people watching across the world, they know the images that they`ve seen coming out of Ukraine. They have seen these images of mothers, children, and young children who are just clinging to their last possessions, fleeing their homes because an unnecessary war is being waged in their home country.\par \par \uc2 But even given that, you know, Congress has not necessarily ponied up and been ready to give President Biden the money that he has asked for. So, I think folks really need to watch the meeting that the President is going to have tomorrow with the \uc2 G7\uc2 , the First Lady will be at the Slovakia-Ukraine border tomorrow as well. I think watching that, paying attention to that.\par \par \uc2 And then Monday, Monday is Victory Day in Russia. What does that mean? What is \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  going to do? We cannot know for sure, but I think it is absolutely something people have to have their eye on because it will be consequential.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:05]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, well, Symone Sanders, I know you have a glass of champagne there. You know, I only drink tea, but good luck and see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 SANDERS: See you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now for a quick update on the war on Ukraine, joining me is NBC`s Cal Perry. He is in Kyiv, Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Cal, a humanitarian win in the city of Mariupol today. The Vice Minister, the Prime Minister of Ukraine confirming all women, children and elderly civilians have been evacuated from the steel plant. What else can you add?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: Yes, fairly remarkable when you look at the video of the fighting, when you hear witness accounts of the fighting. This window opened up, a very short window.\par \par \uc2 And according to the Deputy Prime Minister and the President, it seems like now they`ve gotten the majority of the civilians out of that site. It`s possible there are still men of fighting age there who are medics, the President talking tonight about getting the medics, the doctors, and the wounded soldiers out of there.\par \par \uc2 We still don`t have a good, accurate assessment of how many wounded soldiers are there. We think it`s in the hundreds. But information is very, very difficult to come by for obvious reasons, the fighting there has been fierce.\par \par \uc2 When it comes to Victory Day, you heard Symone there talking about Victory Day on the other side of Mariupol. You have a cleanup effort on behalf of Russian officials, according to Ukrainian officials, Russian officials and soldiers cleaning up the streets there for what could be some kind of commemoration of Victory Day, some kind of propaganda taking place in that city and alerts across the country, Rev.\par \par \uc2 We are you know, anticipating that there could be an uptick in cruise missile fire. It was a busy week in the City of Lviv. They took a number of cruise missile hits on infrastructure targets there, train infrastructure targets, power infrastructure targets. There is now a fuel shortage across the country because of some of these strikes.\par \par \uc2 So a lot of, I would say nervousness about Monday and what we could see across the country here -- Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Cal Perry in Ukraine, thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the Supreme Court`s draft opinion leak on abortion has people on both sides of the aisle talking, but I`ll explain why we must pay attention to what the G.O.P. is planning next week in this week`s "Got-cha."\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague, Cori Coffin with today`s top news stories -- Cori.\par \par \uc2 CORI COFFIN, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Thanks, Rev. Good Saturday to you.\par \par \uc2 Stories we are watching this hour, the death toll of an explosion at one of Cuba`s most luxurious hotels has now risen to 26 people. Dozens more were injured during a powerful gas explosion at the Hotel Saratoga in Old Havana on Friday.\par \par \uc2 Reports indicate that a gas tank parked outside the historic hotel was ignited causing an explosion, which destroyed multiple floors of the building.\par \par \uc2 The Cuban presidency said the dead included a pregnant woman and a child.\par \par \uc2 Three Americans were found dead at the Sandals Emerald Bay Resort in the Bahamas and a fourth has been airlifted to a local hospital. Authorities say there are no signs of foul play, but they`re waiting for the autopsy report to determine the cause of death there.\par \par \uc2 And gas prices in the U.S. are reaching record levels. The national average of a gallon of gas has reached $4.27. That is just three cents shy of the all-time record and 12 cents higher than last week.\par \par \uc2 Experts blame the war in Ukraine for the rising energy prices worldwide.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:22:33]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It`s been five days since the leak of the Supreme Court`s draft opinion potentially overturning Roe versus Wade and many supporters of abortion rights are still understandably in shock. For perhaps the first time in American history, a right the government gave Americans half a century ago may suddenly be snatched away.\par \par \uc2 We are hearing a lot of smart analysis about what the end of Roe versus Wade might mean, but if you really want to understand where things are headed, listen to Republicans when they tell you, take Texas Senator Ted Cruz who is very open about his intention to change this country from one that protects abortion rights at the Federal level to one that imposes bans on abortion in every single state.\par \par \uc2 Senator Cruz said this week he has supported numerous Federal bills restricting abortions and will continue to vote for them in the future, adding that contested policy issues should be resolved through democratically elected body.\par \par \uc2 Make no mistake, overturning Roe versus Wade could be just the beginning of the war on abortion for many Republican lawmakers, and when they start passing nationwide bans, they might do it claiming it`s what voters have demanded.\par \par \uc2 But let`s take a look at those democratic bodies Senator Cruz mentioned because we know that five of the nine Justices sitting on the Court were appointed by Republican Presidents who lost the popular vote in their initial run for the White House.\par \par \uc2 They were confirmed by a Senate where at present, the Democratic half represents 41 and a half million more people than the Republican half, and where California and Wyoming have the same number of senators, even though California`s population is 68 times larger.\par \par \uc2 At the heart of the abortion debate is an effort by a small, but impassioned minority to impose this morality on the majority and to disguise that effort as just the normal functioning of our democracy, but nothing about the numbers I just cited are normal.\par \par \uc2 Our system of representative democracy is badly out of whack even before we get into the filibuster of voter suppression of Senate Minority Leader McConnell`s policy are blocking Democratic Supreme Court appointees by any means necessary.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:17]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Some Republicans don`t want you to focus on these things, which is why they spent much of this week shouting about the leaker, but if we listen to what they say more quietly, and especially if you watch what they do, it is not hard to see what their real game is, and that`s what we need to fight.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 As we continue our conversation on the leaked Supreme Court memo on Roe versus Wade, I want to focus on two aspects. The impact that this possible ruling would have on the Black community and as we approach the midterm elections, what are Democrats -- what can they do to defend his vital constitutional rights?\par \par \uc2 [17:30:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Joining me now is Democratic political strategist, Atima Omara, who has a lot of experience with reproductive rights organizations and Professor Michael Eric Dyson of \uc2 Vanderbilt University\uc2 . His most recent book and is selling, is called "Unequal."\par \par \uc2 Atima, you have a deep background of working on Democratic campaigns and have worked extensively on abortion rights with abortion rights group and the day after the Supreme Court draft decision to overturn Roe versus Wade was leaked, we saw a group of Democratic senators marching down the stairs of the Capitol to declare their support for abortion rights and to express their solidarity with protesters.\par \par \uc2 But how much leverage do the Democrats have in this fight? What is at stake if they don`t fight back? And could this become yet another Democratic priority that could face a dead end in the Senate?\par \par \uc2 ATIMA OMARA, DEMOCRATIC POLITICAL STRATEGIST: Yes, it is a major priority because it is a medical emergency for most Americans, and you had mentioned what the impact would be on the Black community.\par \par \uc2 Abortion rights access is primarily important to all Americans, but specifically to Black and Latina women, and pregnant people who are having trouble always accessing healthcare in this country because of cost and racism. And then when you look at something like reproductive healthcare, it gets criminalized more and more and more over the last 50 years through anti-abortion restrictions. So, it complicates access for them even further.\par \par \uc2 What Democrats can do, I think are four things. One is the U.S. Senate Democrats were put in there to lead because of people in communities of color, specifically Black women. And those folks gave them the White House and Congress because of Georgia, Michigan, and Arizona. They need to figure out how to codify Roe, they need to figure out how to end that filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  is a no, \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  even though she`s pro-choice is not going to end the filibuster on principle. But you know, you have to look at somebody like a \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  who maybe she could drop her faux outrage enough to actually codify abortion instead of pretending she is surprised about Kavanaugh. Lisa Murkowski has said she is willing to vote to codify abortion law.\par \par \uc2 But then let`s look at the state as well, because that is where the fight is going to go. California is now talking about putting a constitutional amendment on the ballot. You`re looking at New York, they already codified Roe into their State law in 2019, with the Reproductive Health Act. Blue states like that should be -- blue states they call them -- where Democratic legislatures should be looking more into what they can proactively do to codify Roe.\par \par \uc2 If you`re in a red state --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, that`s why I hate to bring up what some of us told people to get rid of the filibuster around voting rights, if we have done that, we wouldn`t be faced with this dilemma now.\par \par \uc2 Professor Dyson, your book "Unequal: The Story of America" talks about racial inequality permeates every facet of American society. How does the abortion debate fit in to all of this?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL ERIC DYSON, PROFESSOR, \uc2 VANDERBILT UNIVERSITY\uc2 : Yes, it is a great point, Rev.\par \par \uc2 Look, you know, I`ve heard people say this is the first time in American history that a right that has been given has been taken away. No, this is why you`ve got to study Black History. This is why you`ve got to study American History.\par \par \uc2 Free Black people could vote in the 1600s, mid-1600s. Slavery got into full effect and that right was taken away, then they were given rights to vote in the early 1700s into the mid-1700s, then that right was taken away. Then reconstruction came after slavery, and then Black people could vote and then after 12 years, they took that right away.\par \par \uc2 We have often been subjected to the mercurial arbitrary expression of power by political forces, and so, this is yet another installment.\par \par \uc2 If we look at the long view of history, as we do in our book "Unequal," you will see that Black people and other allies have been fighting for time and time and again. Yes, this is a setback. Yes, this is undeniably painful, but this is the long game. This is the long ball we`re after, and what we have to do is to insist, as our other guest has said that our representatives to be fair, and represent what we`re talking about in terms of equality and justice in America.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now staying with you, Dr. Dyson, on that historic point.\par \par \uc2 This week, a University of North Carolina Constitutional Law Professor said that he has never seen the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  reverse course on a civil rights once it`s been granted and overturning Roe versus Wade could open the door to years of legal battles on other Civil Rights and I think that`s something I was saying earlier.\par \par \uc2 People don`t understand once we go back to State rights model, it can you know sweep the table so to speak.\par \par \uc2 Could we really see a reversal of say, decisions in supporting same sex marriage or even interracial marriage? Can you foresee more lawsuits challenging other Civil Rights, racial Civil Rights coming up in the future?\par \par \uc2 [17:35:22]\par \par \uc2 DYSON: Yes, that`s a great point. We know that now at least two of the sitting of Justices want to be empowered soon, \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  and \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  are interracially married, so they`d have to vote against their own interests, I doubt if that comes about.\par \par \uc2 But you`re absolutely right that these other rights are not sacrosanct. They are not safe. They are not spared. The executioners of, you know, robe or judgment and decision, and what we have to do is to remember that the fight for justice is an ongoing one.\par \par \uc2 What you have done your entire career with Reverend \uc2 Jesse Jackson\uc2 , you know, \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2 , \uc2 Joyce Beatty\uc2 , all of these figures have reminded us that we have to grasp hold of freedom. It`s not a once and for all thing. It`s a constant reaffirmation of the fundamental principles of democracy.\par \par \uc2 And yes, if we`re not careful, this radical reactionary Supreme Court will put at risk and make vulnerable so many other rights that we fought for. But again, if we go back to the motto of African-American people fighting for voting rights, this ain`t the first time that a right granted has been rejected. We`ve got to learn and study our history.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Atima, as a political strategist, what in your opinion can be done by lawmakers and activists to protect American democracy and civil liberties when considering what`s at stake here?\par \par \uc2 OMARA: Well, Democrats have always said that they want folks to actually mobilize around abortion access for the midterms, and they have to mobilize themselves. And as part of what I was saying that blue states and Democratic-led legislatures can actually pass proactive legislation.\par \par \uc2 In red states, they can still represent the majority of people in this country over two-thirds who support abortion access by blocking legislation or introducing it, even if Republicans shut them down. Those folks need to be represented, people who support abortion in this country need to be represented. Across the country, if you`re a candidate, we`re at high season for primaries right now, a candidate should be putting it on their websites where they stand on reproductive rights, campaign literature, and working with activists.\par \par \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  suspended her campaign for a couple of days this week to direct people to give money to abortion funds, which will be vital access. The activists lead those organizations to make sure those who cannot fund abortions or cannot pay for their abortions are funded and able to do that.\par \par \uc2 So those are just some of the things that should be done, just to make sure that we`re expanding access and protecting access in this country, and the Democrats really want to see people come to the polls for them, then they need to be vigorous in their defense, regardless of where they are in this country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Dr. Dyson, on Atima`s point, this morning at National Action Network weekly Saturday rallies, we had the President of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , speak to us and she talked about how there needs to be an intersectional movement here, women`s groups and Civil Rights groups and immigration groups, because everyone is being threatened by this.\par \par \uc2 And I think that this goes to one of Atima`s point is this may be a kind of wake-up call for elements that may not always work together to start saying, wait a minute, we all are at risk here. This is not just women, or in some that this is all about White women. No, it is about women, all women, disproportionately Black women, but it`s also about all of the Civil Rights that all of us have fought for, and I think that that is the point that Miss Johnson, the President -- the first Black woman, by the way, President of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , raised eloquently this morning in Harlem.\par \par \uc2 DYSON: Yes, sir. No, that`s absolutely right. You know, that old story, you know, they came for somebody else and I didn`t speak up. They came for another group, I didn`t speak up, then they came for me, nobody was left.\par \par \uc2 So the point is that we have to always be our brothers` and sisters` keeper. It is an intersectional reality that race, class, gender, sexual orientation affects me. I was recently in Atlanta on a radio and a Black woman called and said, "I can`t believe you`re an ordained minister, and you`re speaking about pro-choice." I said, "Ma`am, I am an ordained minister because I believe in the sanctity of life, and these Republicans are concerned about conception and birth. They are not concerned about life. They are against life."\par \par \uc2 And so we have to see that even if you`re a conservative, Black, pew- sitting Christian, you have much more in common with women who deserve the choice over their bodies than you have with reactionary Christians who say they follow the same God.\par \par \uc2 Martin Luther King Jr\uc2 . said I look at these White people sometimes, and I wonder who their God is. We are all in the same boat together, and if it goes down, we`re going down together.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well you have, as both of us, as ordained ministers, you`re given a choice even if we think you make the wrong choice, you`re given a choice.\par \par \uc2 Atima Omara, Dr. Michael Eric Dyson, the book is "Unequal." Thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Next up, abortion rights` advocates took to the streets in Houston, Texas today to voice their opposition to the possible end of Roe versus Wade. I`ll ask my political panel whether the energy can make a difference in the midterm.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:44:14]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Now, let`s have my political panel to weigh in on today`s big topics. I`m joined by Juanita Tolliver, Democratic strategist and Susan del Percio, a Republican strategist. They are both MSNBC political analysts.\par \par \uc2 Juanita, the Senate will hold votes, a vote next week to codify Roe versus Wade, although the chances of it passing are very slim. If the Supreme Court votes to strike down the landmark abortions rights decision, as suggested by an initial draft majority opinion written by Justice Alito that leaked this week, how should Democrats handle this his issue going into the midterms? Will it motivate Democrats to go to the polls?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:06]\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Rev, it absolutely is going to motivate Democrats to go to the polls, especially when you have 70 percent of the country that believes that Roe should stand as law of the land, and I think that with that motivation, you`re going to see the massive turnout. You`re going to see pro-choice voters being vocal, as we saw this week.\par \par \uc2 The rally started immediately, the response was immediately and strong. But to keep that going, Democrats need to keep a steady drumbeat about what is at stake if Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 So it`s the first step to tell people to, yes, go out and vote, elect more Democrats, but you better believe Democrats also need to demonstrate the voters how they are fighting for them in real time.\par \par \uc2 So this vote by Schumer will get people on the record, but what else -- what other efforts are Democrats taking to create a carve out in the filibuster, which we know both Senate Democrats and Republicans supported just a few months ago?\par \par \uc2 What other efforts are Democrats taking to expand access to medical prescriptions for pills to have abortions? What other efforts are they doing to fight for this in real time because while it will turn out voters, you better believe those Democratic voters are also going to be asking, "What are you doing for me in real time to protect my rights? What are you doing to protect us and make sure that poor people, pregnant women, and pregnant people don`t die as a result of Roe being overturned?" Because we know abortion will continue, it`ll just be extremely unsafe.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes, no, the issue is really safe abortions. Abortions will continue. Your turn now, Susan, "\uc2 The New York Times\uc2 " writes, "Republicans appear determined to recast their position on the issue as one of moderation and avert the gaze of voters away from the anti-abortion rights agenda."\par \par \uc2 Does this week`s news hurt Republicans who have so far appeared to be in a strong position going into the midterms?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I think it does, Rev, because let`s not forget, there are a lot of Republican lawmakers out there that basically hid behind Roe v. Wade. They said, yes, I`m pro-life, but I respect the law of the land. They usually were for rape, incest, life of the mother. And now we`re seeing that all disappear.\par \par \uc2 So it`s going to put a lot of legislators on the record, not just in the Senate, but around the country. And I would like to add just to what Juanita said, you`re not only going to see a lot of Democrats, you`re going to see a lot of women.\par \par \uc2 Women`s rights are being taken away. We had a right, it is being taken away. We are no longer able to take control of our bodies. So, I absolutely do think that that will be a mobilizing factor and it will not just be Democratic women, it will be suburban women who are independent, right of center, or even some who are right and believe that there should be some exemption and moderation within the abortion laws.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Susan, let`s stay with you. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was in Pennsylvania last night to campaign for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  candidate G.O.P. candidate Dr. Oz. However, some Trump supporters expressed their disapproval of the talk show host. Let`s take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 DONALD TRUMP\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Right here in Pennsylvania, that starts with nominating the man who has by far the best chance to win Pennsylvania`s \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race this fall, my friend, Dr. Mehmet Oz.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now you can hear booing from the crowd at the mention of Dr. Oz`s name. This comes as Trump`s former Secretary of State \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2  said Friday on a call for one of Oz`s competitors that he poses, quote, "national security concerns." He being Oz, due to his ties to the Turkish government and military.\par \par \uc2 Now a spokesman for Dr. Oz responds in part, "These are pathetic and xenophobic attacks."\par \par \uc2 What do you make of Dr. Oz`s candidacy and Trump`s endorsement of him, Susan?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: Well, Trump`s endorsement is important in a Republican primary, and there is no doubt we`ve seen in the polling, it has helped Dr. Oz.\par \par \uc2 The thing about Dr. Oz is like J.D. Vance is that he is a bit of a fraud. He has been able to say I was pro-choice, now he is pro-life. He has flip flopped, and the voters in Pennsylvania are really paying attention to this race.\par \par \uc2 So I think you know, Dr. Oz is going to have a very uphill battle, and I don`t think that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is going to be able to necessarily get him over the line.\par \par \uc2 David McCormick is a very strong candidate, his wife worked for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , and I think that you`re going to see a big move -- as a matter of fact, we already are in the advertising campaign of David McCormick.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, Ukraine is bracing for a bloody weekend ahead of Russia`s Victory Day commemoration on Monday. How concerned are you that if matters get worse in Ukraine, the U.S. will have more of an active role in the war?\par \par \uc2 [17:50:10]\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: I`m very concerned, Rev, because we know Putin is not going to stop his atrocities. He is not going to back down. And there are reports that apparently, on that May 9th Victory Day parade, he is going to try to highlight some of the atrocities they`ve committed in Ukraine in Mariupol, for example, were apparently Russian troops on the ground changing street signs to Russian, forcing any Ukrainians left behind to help with the cleanup effort.\par \par \uc2 And I think that is only going to further temper the U.S.` position in providing additional aid, additional resources. But we all know that the clear line that the White House has drawn and \uc2 NATO\uc2  has drawn is, is that they will provide aid to Ukraine, but no other action will take place unless Putin goes into a \uc2 NATO\uc2  allied country.\par \par \uc2 And so I think that is going to hold, but additional resources are going to be sent to Ukraine, and that`s going to put it right back front and center in the U.S., too.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita Tolliver and Susan del Percio, thank you both for your time.\par \par \uc2 My final thoughts after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we focus this week on the Ukraine war, and the Supreme Court`s leaked draft on abortion, I want to make sure you did not miss on other important stories like the Judge in Oklahoma who ruled that a lawsuit seeking reparations for the 1921 Tulsa massacre can proceed bringing hope for justice for the three remaining survivors.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:16]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And the \uc2 U.S. Justice Department\uc2 , who has reclassified WNBA star Brittney Griner as wrongfully detained in Russia. The new classification in her case will mean that the U.S. will take a more aggressive approach to release her from prison instead of waiting for her case to go through the Russian Courts.\par \par \uc2 In Minnesota, a Judge in the Federal Civil Rights cases of the four former Minneapolis Police officers for the killing of George Floyd has accepted the plea deal of Derek Chauvin and will sentence him to 20 to 25 years in prison. Chauvin would serve more time in prison than he faced under the State sentence.\par \par \uc2 And we found out this week that one of the men convicted of murder in the pursuit and fatal shooting of Ahmaud Arbery spoke with his former boss, the local District Attorney at least 16 times by phone in the days and weeks after the killing.\par \par \uc2 And history is being made at the White House as \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2  will take over as Press Secretary for the President, becoming the first Black woman and LGBTQ person to take on this high-profile job.\par \par \uc2 So yes, lots going on, but please remember that the nexus around abortion, the State`s rights, which is the real threat we should all be paying attention to as \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , the head of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2  said at National Action Network`s rally this morning, that the target may be women, but the injury will be all that have Civil Rights as always something, that is a threat when we go to States rights.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION. Our top guest is Chairwoman of the Congressional Black Caucus, \uc2 Joyce Beatty\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "American Voices" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 [18:01:02]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220508ei5700001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News\par \b \uc2 The 11th Hour for May 6, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Kelly Cobiella, Cal Perry \par \uc2 8,022 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [23:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Thank you so much for joining us. The 11th Hour with Stephanie Ruhle starts right now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, intense fighting at that steel plant in Mariupol, civilians still trapped as fears mount over escalation ahead of Russia`s Victory Day holiday.\par \par \uc2 And the First Lady shows support for allies in Eastern Europe. Ukraine`s Ambassador to the U.S. is here on the fight ahead for freedom and democracy.\par \par \uc2 Plus, the road ahead for abortion protections after a week of protests over the leaked draft opinion, the new push to get lawmakers on the record, as the 11th Hour gets underway on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening. Once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. The next 72 hours may be pivotal for Ukraine in its fight against Russia. Ukrainian forces continue to resist Moscow`s drive to capture key cities in the East. But this evening, the nation is bracing for the possibility of a brutal new assault ahead of Russia`s so called Victory Day on Monday, when Russia commemorates its victory over Nazi Germany in World War Two.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, fighting at the Mariupol steel plant has only intensified as the city is completely devastated. NBC`s Kelly Cobiella has the latest from Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 KELLY COBIELLA, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Tonight, Russian forces are still trying to break the last pocket of resistance at Mariupol steel plant.\par \par \uc2 It won`t take long, this Russian backed fighter said. The United Nations describing the plant as a hellscape with 200 civilians and hundreds more wounded fighters holed up. Today, 50 more civilians were rescued, evacuated by the \uc2 U.N\uc2 . and Red Cross and now in Russian controlled territory.\par \par \uc2 Russian flags are now flying in the city of Mariupol amid fears Moscow was planning a victory parade next Monday. Although the Kremlin denies it. Today, a defiant President Zelenskyy said Mariupol will never fall because there`s nothing left. Russia has already destroyed it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Our thanks to Kelly Cobiella for that report.\par \par \uc2 President Zelenskyy says diplomatic efforts are underway to rescue Ukrainian troops that are still trapped in that steel plant. While President Biden will meet with Zelenskyy virtually on Sunday along with other U.S. allies.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this evening, the President signed off on an additional $150 million in military aid for Ukraine. He said the U.S. has nearly exhausted the assistance Congress approved back in March and called our lawmakers to vote for more.\par \par \uc2 Also, tonight, President Biden reportedly not happy about leaks concerning U.S. intelligence sharing with Ukraine. NBC News reports that while on the phone with intelligence officials, Biden`s message was that his disclosures are distractions from the objective and conveyed the leaks need to stop.\par \par \uc2 And as all of that unfolds here in the U.S. First Lady Jill Biden is spending this Mother`s Day weekend in Eastern Europe. Today, she visited U.S. troops supporting \uc2 NATO\uc2  forces. We`ll have much more on her visit later in the hour. But I want to begin with NBC`s Cal Perry who`s live for us again in Kyiv. Cal, what are Ukrainian officials -- I`m glad to have with us tonight this morning where you are. What are of Ukrainian officials telling their people ahead of Russia`s Victory Day on Monday?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: They`re warning people here across the country. They`re telling people to be on high alert that they should abide by the curfew and we are hearing new announcements of curfews, rolling curfews in cities across this country. In the last two days, there have been two dozen cruise missiles fired from the Black Sea at targets across Ukraine. A number of them have been shot down according to officials over Kyiv. So this is certainly a city I think that is at war. It`s certainly a country no question, the eastern part of the country is engaged in a fierce war. But officials are warning that Victory Day we could see an uptick of rocket attacks. \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  could use this as an excuse to make a point.\par \par \uc2 We know there will be parades in Moscow. We`ve seen the video in Kelly Cobiella`s peace about cleaning up in Mariupol. Perhaps they will do something there. They have been making residents in Mariupol, clean up the streets, change street signs to Russian to get to get prepared for some kind of publicity event. That`s what people here are preparing for. Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Over the last few weeks, though, a lot of Ukrainian refugees who left the country have been making their way back in. Are they pausing? Are officials telling them don`t come back right now ahead of what could come on Monday?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: They are. Started with the mayor of Kyiv, telling people to stop and think about it to pause their return five -- excuse me, 7.7 million people internally displaced 15 percent of them according to the United Nations trying to make their way home. Two reasons officials are telling people to stop and rethink about it. One is Victory Day, and we`ve been talking to people on the street who say that they have friends. They have family who are outside of the country, some of them inside the country, but in the western part, they`re waiting to see what Victory Day brings.\par \par \uc2 The second reason that officials are asking people to pause is the infrastructure doesn`t exist here to support these people. There`s power out in a lot of places. There`s no water so many buildings have been destroyed, so it is a refugee crisis and perhaps a refugee crisis where we will see an uptick in violence on Monday. Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:09]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But a reminder to our audience, if you`re wondering why so many refugees want to come back, remember, many of them left their husbands, their sons inside the country fighting and they just want to get home. Cal, thank you. Please stay safe where you are.\par \par \uc2 And now I want to bring in our experts this evening, Peter Baker, Chief White House Correspondent for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 . He spent years as the Moscow Bureau Chief for The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , closely watching the rise of Putin. Retired Four Star U.S. Army General, Barry McCaffrey joins us. A decorated combat veteran of Vietnam, and a former Battlefield Commander in the Persian Gulf. And Michael McFaul, former U.S. Ambassador to Russia and MSNBC International Affairs Analyst. His book titled, From Cold War to Hot Peace: An American Ambassador in Putin`s Russia.\par \par \uc2 Gentlemen, thank you. We need you all after the week we just had. General, Russia is now focusing on the east and Mariupol, what are you expecting we`re going to get out of Russian forces over the next few days? Yes.\par \par \uc2 GEN. BARRY MCCAFFREY, \uc2 U.S. ARMY\uc2  (RET.): Well, Stephanie, let me just put it in context, the war has clearly turned in the favor of Ukrainian armed forces. Russia is in a strategic dilemma that there`s no easy way out of their militaries proved itself being competent or leadership`s and amateurish. They`re trying now to match on a single access to attack the Ukrainian from the East. We are giving them better technology, not adequate yet probably, but a lot of 155 artillery with its long-range ammunition. And the Mariupol defenders, who have fought valiantly for a couple of months now have sucked in a lot of the Russian combat power and destroyed it in the south, throwing the whole -- the whole effort to control the land bridge and to seize the West Coast, and to include Odessa and the (inaudible). So, the situation is unclear, the Russians aren`t giving up. Monday, May 9, may well be some dramatic signal by Putin. But I might add a cautionary word for the Russian Armed Forces don`t have a big parade in Mariupol, you will be a vulnerable target.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Peter, give us a bit of a history lesson. Why specifically is Victory Day so important?\par \par \uc2 PETER BAKER, THE NEW YORK TIMES CHIEF WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Yeah, obviously, more than the United States, Russia found itself, you know, redefined, transformed by World War Two. So even to this day, 75 years later, you know, is still an important part of their national psyche, the national identity, that victory over Nazi Germany remains, you know, part of their annual tradition, this may date -- this is not maybe May 9 Victory Day, you know, celebration and parade is something that is a big, big deal there.\par \par \uc2 So for Putin to sort of target that date, if he`s doing that is meant to appeal to Russian national pride, as meant to once again sort of identify this war against Ukraine, as if it`s part of some sort of -- remember denazification is what he called it. The idea that they`re fighting against the same kind of enemy that they fought against way back in World War Two, that somehow the Ukrainians are equivalent to the, to the Nazis of that era. So that`s why it matters. And it`s -- we don`t know for sure what they`re going to do, if anything on May the ninth, but that`s why there`s so much attention to it, because it`s such a big part of the Russian, you know, Russian culture, Russian history.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But Ambassador, isn`t Putin running the risk of looking silly. He could put on a big show for the Russian people who have no idea what`s actually happening there. But the U.S. and \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies, the West, we know what`s happening. Putin is not winning. It would be a joke to celebrate victory day. Isn`t he afraid of looking like a fool to all of us?\par \par \uc2 AMB. MICHAEL MCFAUL, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO RUSSIA: Yes and no. So they are going to celebrate victory day no matter what. May 9 is always celebrated. I was there twice as the ambassador. It`s a very big event. It`s a quasi-religious event and legitimize the Soviet Union. And for Russia, it still plays that role.\par \par \uc2 By the way, Ukrainians also used to celebrate this victory day. they fought and died in World War Two, as well, as well as many other nationalities within the former Soviet Union. But remember, you`re right, he say the outside world, we know when he`s winning or losing. I think he`s already lost the war of Ukraine. I think quite definitively. He may win a few more battles. He`s lost the war. But to his people back home, they live in an information bubble. They don`t know that. They`re not seeing these photos from Mariupol like we are. They`re not seeing any of that and he can tell them anything he wants, and they will believe at least a portion of Russians will believe them on that day.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: General, President Zelenskyy has asked officially that Russia be placed on a U.S. list of nations that sponsor terrorism. And I want to share what \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  had to say about that.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:02]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): If Russia is not listed as a state sponsor of terrorism, tear up the list.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: I mean, I`m a mere civilian. I hear that and I say drop the mike, Nance. You`re right. But you actually know how this works. Should they be?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Oh, no question. I listen to that interviews, really first straightforward. Russia is fighting for their -- now for their public survival. Putin can`t laugh as a leader in Russia, if there`s a continuing military disaster on the ground in Ukraine. So part of that arming Ukrainian armed forces, part of that a big part in the longer run where the economic sanctions, and do they halt defense production? Do they turn the political and military elites against Putin? But a good bit of it is political, can we make Russia a pariah state. And part of that is labeling them as a state sponsored terrorism, which they clearly are not just in devastating attacks on Ukrainian civilians and infrastructure, but also murdering his opponents, both in Russia and abroad. So it`s a move we ought to take.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So Ambassador, let`s say we do that. Let`s say we give them that label. What does it mean? What happens?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, as the General just said, it makes them a pariah state, it makes it hard for anybody to financially transact with them. It makes companies think twice about investing again, there. And once you get on that list, it`s really hard to get off that list. By the way, there`s four countries on right now. It`s North Korea, Iran, Syria, and Cuba. And if Cuba is on the list, surely Russia qualifies.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Peter, I want to talk about this intelligence sharing, I want to share what the Pentagon said, when it tried to explain the type of intelligence we give to Ukraine. Watch this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PENTAGON PRESS SECRETARY: We provide them what we believe to be relevant and timely information about Russian units that will allow them to adjust and execute their self-defense, to the best of their ability. The kind of intelligence that we provide them, it`s legitimate, it`s lawful, and it`s limited.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Here`s the thing, this information is not coming out of nowhere, Biden is not happy about it. So what`s the biggest concern about the consequences of the leaks, because it`s not just about the media organizations reporting on it. It`s coming from U.S. intelligence. Peter?\par \par \uc2 BAKER: Well, look, I think there`s a certain degree of pride on the part of, you know, U.S. security agencies that they have has to hand in Ukraine success so far in beating back the Russians, and perhaps there`s a desire for, you know, Americans to know that the United States has played a role in that, then they have something to do with. I don`t know, myself, I -- my colleagues are doing stories on this, and I haven`t been involved with those stories. So I haven`t talked to the sources that they`ve been engaging with.\par \par \uc2 But it certainly, you know, American, you know, intelligence has been an important factor in this war, have been a force multiplier, you can argue, right? In other words, if you are there on the ground, and getting the kind of high level information about where units are, what their capacities are, what their movements are, what their logistics are, that gives you a real, you know, heads up, and a real advantage that a smaller country normally wouldn`t have. The latest reports suggest that not only have they been able to take out mobile command centers that have resulted in a dozen generals being killed, they will also receive health United States and taking out that flagship of their as the most VA, and that`s a big, big deal.\par \par \uc2 So from the point of view of the Russians, obviously, you know, you can argue that it inflames them and tells them that United States is more involved in this than perhaps President Biden wants them to understand and makes them more likely to retaliate or take some sort of provocative action on the grounds that they are, in fact, at war with the United States, even if we`re not directly firing guns at them, ourselves. And that`s, I think the risk that`s probably on the minds of people at the White House is a desire not to escalate, because this war could still get a whole lot nastier.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: General, do you think it`s a problem that we`re -- we are reporting about this intelligence sharing, does it make the government`s job harder?\par \par \uc2 MCCAFFREY: Well, you know, it`s hard to fault the press for juicy stories. And I think they`re, you know, watching Admiral Kirby, who was very carefully choosing his words, and they -- there are councils of lawyers all over Washington to rule on is this act belligerent, or is it defensive? Some of it is nonsense. Obviously, we should support Ukraine. We`re sending them lethal hardware to kill Russian soldiers. It`s hard to imagine why we wouldn`t assist them in locating a capital ship bombarding the whole country out in the in the Black Sea. So there, I think he was carefully using his words.\par \par \uc2 But look, at the end of the day, we want to protect sources and methods. We don`t want the Russians to know who we`re listening to. We intercept transmitters if you`ve got 20 transmitters in one spot you got to headquarters. Obviously, we should provide that to them.\par \par \uc2 [23:15:11]\par \par \uc2 And I think the disconnect they`re carefully using language is, did we tell them to go kill the general? No, we went after his headquarters and got 200 of them about four or five days ago. And missed the Chief of the General Staff, unfortunately, so you know, Russia has been a fix. We should be less concerned about it and 100 percent support the Ukrainians in our fight for freedom.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ambassador, you all are educating us at this top level of who`s winning, who`s losing the direction we`re headed in. But take us to your context. Your friends in Russia and Ukraine, we`re now 73 days in. How have their lives changed?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Steph, yeah, I`ll get to that in a minute. But I feel very strongly about what you`re just talking about.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: We can get it.\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Yes, we should get -- we should, because I just talked to Ukrainians about it today and Senior White House official today and Russians today. And yes, we should help the Ukrainians with intelligence. But no, with all due respect to my friend, Peter Baker, we should not be talking to Peter Baker and his friends about it and the \uc2 U.S. government\uc2 . One, it makes it sound like a proxy war. That`s exactly what Putin wants to tell the world, not just as people but the world that this is a proxy war.\par \par \uc2 Number two, the White House most certainly did not approve this leak. And number three and talking to my friends and Kyiv today who worked for President Zelenskyy, it makes them look weak. Why is that in the American national interest. So I would urge those that wanted to have a prideful day to talk about our intelligence, do your job and stop talking about it.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Lose Peter Baker`s number is the takeaway in this segment, stop talking to him, don`t call him, forget his email. All right, then tell me about -- tell me about the state of things for Ukrainians and Russians that, you know, because we keep talking about the war in this big sense. But take to us personally.\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: What for Ukrainians just horrible. It`s great that Kyiv is open again, and people are walking on the streets. But if you`re President Zelenskyy, you feel helpless, watching your citizens die in Mariupol. And that`s the feeling of helplessness as the Russians continue to kill civilians in Ukraine. And then Russia, you know, I don`t want to pretend that I speak to all Russians, but I talk to Russian elites in Moscow, pretty awful. pretty often. This is a horrible time. As one of my friends said in Moscow, there is no winners from this war, not Putin, not the generals, not the KGB guys, not the oligarchy, not the middle class, only losers, and they`ll continue to lose until this horrible tragic war ends.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ambassador Michael McFaul thank you so much. General Barry McCaffrey, thank you. Peter Baker, sorry, no more scoops for you. Thank you all for joining us this evening. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, Ukraine`s Ambassador to the United States here to discuss the First Lady`s trip to Eastern Europe. Why it matters and fears Russia may escalate attacks at Victory Day.\par \par \uc2 And later, what is next for Democrats plans to protect abortion rights? They want to put everyone on the record, but will it make a difference? The 11th Hour just getting underway on a Friday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:05]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SYMONE SANDERS, FORMER BIDEN CAMPAIGN SENIOR ADVISER: Since you are going to the Slovakia-Ukraine border, I`m wondering have you spoken with Mrs. Zelenskyy?\par \par \uc2 JILL BIDEN, FIRST LADY: Well, I`ve gotten a letter from Mrs. Zelenskyy. And I`ve also spoken to the First Lady of Poland and she gave me her list of things that they needed. So you know, I think it`s, I mean, we`re in a conversation but it`s not really, you know, one-to-one. Although, I did talk on the phone with Mrs. Duda.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: First Lady Jill Biden has kicked off the first leg of her four-day trip to Eastern Europe. She spent time in the U.S. military base in Romania serving food and talking to troops and military leaders. Tomorrow she`ll head to Bucharest to visit a public-school hosting refugees. The First Lady is the latest high profile official to visit the region to demonstrate America support for Ukraine and \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies. For more we want to welcome Ukrainian Ambassador to the United States. \uc2 Oksana Markarova\uc2 . Madam Ambassador, thank you for joining us tonight. How important is it for the first lady to visit the region and see firsthand what`s going on?\par \par \uc2 AMB. OKSANA MARKAROVA, UKRAINIAN AMBASSADOR TO THE UNITED STATES: Thank you for having me. It`s very important. I cannot even explain how much it will mean for Ukrainians who are outside of Ukraine and we have more than 5 million outside primarily women and children. So it`s going to be very good that the First Lady who`s paying so much attention here, to education, to children, that she will be able to see and talk to them firsthand and hear the stories of why so many Ukrainians were forced to flee the country. Because, again, it`s very important that on this weekend, the Mother`s Day coincide also with the 77th Anniversary of the end of the World War Two and yet we are again in the situation when a brutal war returned to Europe from the uncivilized country. That has taken our country, who has no -- for no reason. So it`s going to be a great sign of support.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:06]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The brutality continues in one area, specifically Mariupol. We`re seeing Ukrainian forces continue to keep up the fight at that steel plant. What can you tell us about what`s going on there?\par \par \uc2 MARKAROVA: Thank you. Yeah, brutality continuous everywhere. But I think Mariupol is such a symbol now of everything that Russians are doing to, you know, Kharkiv, Chernihiv, Odessa, to other places, but Mariupol has been encircled for the past two months. And Russians are using every cent they have on that city and our brave, brave defenders in Mariupol, in this Azovstal Steel Plan, they`re defending the civilians, which many are still there, without access, again, to food, gas and electricity without no resupplies, they`re still holding high the Ukrainian flag, and they`re not given up. But the whole Ukraine has not given up. So we call on everyone who can help us not only to organize the humanitarian corridors, more to get all the civilians out, but also to do everything possible, to save our defenders, to get the wounded out, to get our grave defenders out from that area.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Do you have any specifics on what`s going on with those civilians that are trapped there? It has been weeks?\par \par \uc2 MARKAROVA: It has been months, you know, and they are trapped there. After 95 percent of Mariupol has been destroyed, residential areas, schools, everything. Russian specifically targeted civilian infrastructure and civilians there and the only refuge they have there is actually the underground or the steel plant, where they are guarded by the Ukrainian defenders. But still, you know, they`re surrounded there without access to everything without access to medical supplies. So the faster we can get everyone out, the faster Russians will stop line and actually organize the corridors and stop shooting, because they do it even right now, stop shooting and trying to destroy the plant, the faster we can save this lies.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Mother`s Day isn`t the only big day we`re looking ahead to Monday is Victory Day for Russia, a hugely symbolic holiday. Are you concerned about Moscow ramping up attacks leading up to it?\par \par \uc2 MARKAROVA: Yes, well, you know, the Sunday, which is May 8, is when all civilized world is going to mourn and rejoice that the World War Two was over. But I think we all will be thanking the Lord, how -- what went wrong? How is it possible that 77 years after we all said never again, Russian Federation attacked us eight years ago and now subjected the country to a full-fledged war with all this war atrocities, which are equal to what Nazis were doing to Europe 77 years ago.\par \par \uc2 So, I think May 9 for Russia, you know, it`s a shame day for them, not a Victory Day, because how can they celebrate a Victory over Nazism when they`re doing now exactly what Nazis were doing. And again, and they have engaged in this brutal war in the middle of Europe was no reason whatsoever, just because Ukraine was peaceful, just because Ukraine made a civilizational choice to be democratic to be European. And that was a threat enough for Russia to attack us in 2014, to illegally annexed Crimea and occupied less in Luhansk and the reason to start a full-fledged war 72 days ago.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: 72 days ago, I know I`ve heard you say before, this isn`t just a war against Ukraine, this is a fight for freedom and democracy. But here we are day 72, are you worried that you`re going to lose the hearts, the minds the attention of those from the West, and if you lose our attention, things could get really isolating really quickly?\par \par \uc2 MARKAROVA: If this loses that attention, it`s not going to be just the loss of Ukraine. I think, you know, it`s very important for all of us who believe in freedom, democracy, who believe that the board is a sacred who believe that countries can decide for themselves how they want to live and make their own mistakes if they have to.\par \par \uc2 If we do not stop Russia, while still in Ukraine, if we do not say decisively that it`s not OK to wage a war in the 21st century against a peaceful country, then we all lose, then the whole global rule of law, and the whole global security architecture will prove totally inefficient. So I think, you know, we cannot lose attention to it. Like Ukrainians will not get tired in defending our country, even though it`s very hard. And it`s heartbreaking and we`ve lost already so much, but we will not surrender, and we will not get tired and I think the world, the civilized world should not get tired insane and standing up for the principles that are so important for all of us, for freedom and democracy.\par \par \uc2 [23:30:15]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Madam Ambassador, thank you for joining us this evening. I appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MARKAROVA: Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Coming up, our friend Charlie Sykes is here to break down what he calls a dramatic change for Republicans on abortion restrictions when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:35:10]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. ELIZABETH WARREN (D-MA): The plan starts by getting everybody on the record. You know, you got to be willing to stand up in public and say which side of this you come out on. And it`s both hands. It`s making sure that everyone knows where people stand who are in office. And everyone knows where people stand who are running for office, because we don`t have enough people to get this done right now that this is the day to get in the fight so that come November, we have enough people in office who will protect the rights of a woman to choose.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Senator Warren turning up the heat following the leak of that draft Supreme Court decision that would overturn Roe v. Wade. Senate Democrats are planning a vote on abortion protections next week. It comes as Republican state legislatures around the country have been pushing even more extreme abortion bans.\par \par \uc2 As Charlie Sykes writes in the bulwark, "This reflects a dramatic change in the position that pro-life Republicans held until about five minutes ago. So let`s discuss. With us tonight, Betsy Woodruff Swan, National Political Correspondent for Politico and Charlie Sykes himself, Editor-at-Large for The Bulwark and an MSNBC Analyst.\par \par \uc2 Charlie, break it down for us. What do we need to know about this shift and Republicans?\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE SYKES, THE BULWARK FOUNDER AND EDITOR-AT-LARGE: Well, I mean, for years, there was a consensus among Republicans that if you will, were pro- life you supported restrictions on abortion, that it would always include exceptions for rape, incest and the life of the mother. That was the position that \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2  took. That was the position that national right to life organizations took. That was the position that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  himself took as recently as 2019. But what`s been happening has been the radicalization of the base people pushing the limits saying, no, we should have no exceptions whatsoever in places like Louisiana, it will be a complete ban would treat mothers as murderers.\par \par \uc2 So this is a dramatic shift. And, you know, up until now, though, it`s been a free shot for many Republicans, because it`s just been theoretical. So they could put out the press release, they could appear in front of pro- life groups and say, you know, I am the purest pro-lifer here. But now in the last several days, you realize, no, you`re firing with live ammunition, and there are real consequences. And the reason why this is a huge problem for Republicans is all you have to do is look at the polling. And the number of voters who support bans on all abortions with no exceptions is vanishingly small. It`s only about 8 percent of the electorate, even in places like Texas, it`s a very small minority of people that do not think that you should have an exception for rape and incest. But now that`s on the ballot. And many of these legislators and politicians are on record as saying that yes, you should be compelled to carry a child to term if it is the result of incest or rape. And that`s going to be a very difficult position for them to defend. And it`s one of the reasons why for the last, you know, 40 years, they haven`t tried to do that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Certainly with suburban women. Is that that, Betsy, why Democrats want to put this on the big screen, if over 60 percent of Americans think that abortion should be legal in all or most cases, if this thing gets taken to a vote, are you going to see those numbers translate?\par \par \uc2 BETSY WOODRUFF SWAN, POLITICO NATIONAL POLITICAL CORRESPONDENT: It`s something that certainly creates a really challenging moment for elected Republicans. In some ways, many members of the Republican Party feel a little bit like the dog that caught the male truck when it comes to the possibility of Roe v. Wade actually being reversed this year or in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 For decades, Republicans have capitalized on their base as opposition to abortion rights and use that as a way to simultaneously excite social conservatives, excite evangelical Christians without delving into an issue that corporate America ever chose to weigh in on or get involved on until in a very limited way only very recently. But now all of a sudden, it`s no longer a hypothetical discussion. It`s no longer something that`s complex and a little bit down the road. The issue of Roe v. Wade itself is actually very much up in the air. Of course, the piece of this that did change that was never complex or hypothetical was abortion access as state level laws governed it.\par \par \uc2 Of course, we know around the country, a host of red states have taken steps to essentially make it impossible in several of these states, or almost prohibitively difficult for people to get abortions but nationally in terms of the supreme court politics, it`s been a real quick change real fast, and you can tell just from looking at the way Republican members of Congress are talking about it, that they`re kind of scrambling to figure out what exactly there, what their message should be in this moment.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:05]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Charlie, I`ll tell you personally the moderate Republican women in my life, I`m pretty sure the reason none of them are taking my calls this week is because they don`t want me to ask them about it. So do you think Elizabeth Warren, well, all of her points might be correct. Do you think as a progressive icon, she`s the right messenger for Democrats right now, because the group they could snag are moderates?\par \par \uc2 SYKES: Well, look there are a lot of messengers out there. And I think both parties are going to be struggling to figure out how they are going to frame this. You know, for example, only a small percentage of voters want to ban abortions with no exceptions, but also a very small percentage of voters believe that there should be abortions with no exceptions for the full nine months. Now, if some Democratic politicians take that position, that is obviously going to give Republicans a talking point here.\par \par \uc2 The problem with this issue, and obviously, I`m not telling you something you don`t know, is that people have very -- the American people have very nuanced views about abortion. There is strong support for legalized abortion in the first trimester. But those numbers drop off dramatically in the second and third trimester. So, you know, there is there is room for a centrist compromise, but you know, in our current environment, that is just not going to happen, because what`s going to happen will be that the absolutist will drive the debate on both sides. So it is going to be interesting, you know, who is going to be able to cast this, who`s going to be able to frame this issue, but there`s no question about it. This is a, you know, huge problem for Republicans and I think that the, you know, the analogy that Betsy mentioned, you know, the dog that caught the car is exactly right, because, you know, I`ve worked with these politicians for years. And they all just, you know, operated under the assumption that this would never actually happen. And they all understood what the blowback would be. And now suddenly, it`s about to happen.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, then, Charlie, maybe this is the moment for the radical center, though it`s threatened to for years to finally rise. We`ll soon find out. Betsy Woodruff, Charlie Sykes, thank you both so much.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, I recently sat down with the one and only Terry Crews. We have a one on one to get his take on toxic masculinity and how he has had to change his life and redefine what a man even means, when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:09]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You should recognize my next guest, actor, entertainer, superstar Terry Crews. He has been known for his roles in countless shows and movies like Everybody Hates Chris, Brooklyn Nine-Nine, The Longest Yard, and America`s Got Talent. But you may not know he`s also been on a decade`s long quest to understand the true meaning of toughness. In his newest and deeply personal memoir, he writes this, "Man wield power in this world because of our size and our strength. And because society gives it to us whether we deserve it or not. What I`ve learned is that to be a true man is to be the ultimate servant. With any talent or advantage that life has given you whether by birth or circumstance, your duty is to use that advantage in service of others."\par \par \uc2 And with that, I am so pleased to welcome my dear friend Terry Crews, his new memoir, Tough: My Journey to True Power.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Terry, I am -- I`m so grateful that you wrote this book. When people look at you, they clearly see a tough guy. But talk to us about your upbringing, because you grew up in Flint, Michigan.\par \par \uc2 TERRY CREWS, ACTOR: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: When the GM plants closed, when you saw what it looked like with huge job loss, gangs on the rise, drug use, you actually grew up in a home where there were some domestic abuse. How did your childhood shape your idea of what a tough man was?\par \par \uc2 CREWS: First of all, growing up in that era, the 70s and 80s, in Flint, Michigan, you`re talking about the demise of the auto industry and the rise of the crack epidemic, which created this vacuum. People went right from the factories into the drug game. And it was ruled by violence. I learned in the culture that was in, in the world that I was in, that he who was the most violent one in my house, my father -- my earliest memory is my father knocking my mother out. And I was like, hey, man, it`s your world. And men have the say, and when I went out in the street, people were getting beat up, people were getting shot, people were getting killed, and people were getting away with it. That was the thing that I learned, and I saw that. And I knew that I would have to be very, very -- just very violent in order to survive and you have to understand this too. It was a very competitive world and the competition that I was in was a matter of, it was always me versus them.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What made you change your perspective on what it is to be truly tough? You grew up seeing one thing and becoming another.\par \par \uc2 CREWS: First of all, you know, what was wild is that I -- that level of competition and me wanting to make it that hard, it just be this this, you know, ruler and ruled by fear rather than love. It gets you really far as a man. I mean you become really successful.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:09]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yeah, for male.\par \par \uc2 CREWS: On the external, the alpha, you are the king, you`re the one. You`re king of the hill. And what was the -- the problem was, is that my internal success never matched my external. I had created an image, but my wife was the highlight. She said, I`m out. When we were 20 years married, this was 2010. And I`d had this success and all this stuff. And she was like, Terry, I don`t know who you are, because she had married the image. But she didn`t marry the man. And let me tell you something, that even the process of being so transparent in this book, and telling all of my stuff, is an attempt to tear down that image that I was hiding behind, and keep it down, so that anybody can call me on my stuff. Because I knew that thing, that double life that I had was absolutely, it was going to kill me. Straight up, it was going to be the end of me, because I wasn`t -- I had no integrity. I wasn`t a real man. And I thought I was better than any woman - - that all the women in my life, simply because of the things that I learned. It was like, hey, you`re the man, you don`t love your wife, you own your wife. And it was so wild. I didn`t appreciate my family. I possessed my family. These are the things I was taught. These are the things that a lot of men are being taught. And I had to reveal the truth.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So you`ve gotten better as a person but has society. We all just watched, Will Smith stand up for his woman, and smack Chris Rock in the face.\par \par \uc2 CREWS: Let me tell you, there was a time, and this is why I say this, I`ve done worse than Will that some man basically insulted my wife, I picked him up, put them on in his head on the concrete. And my wife was screaming, no, what are you doing? Stop. But I blacked out. And I mean, blacked out. I was stomping this guy in the ground. And she said, she pulled me to the side after it was all over because I almost got shot by the cops, the police came, and all my brute strength, I was using on this guy. And it was overkill. It was too much. And my wife at the end of the night, she pulled me to the side, she said, hey, man, you can never get violent like that. At the minimum, you`re going to get sued. And at the maximum, you`re going to get killed. And she said, she was pregnant with my son at the time. And he`s 16 now, but she was like, don`t you want to be here, but your son. You want -- you have to be here for him.\par \par \uc2 And I said, but I`m a man. And this is what I have to do. Like, if you don`t like it, I`m sorry, but I`m going to make sure -- she`s like nothing, I`m fine. But it`s you who`s got the problem. And I`m just sitting there going, what, and little did I know that by that moment, that I`ve controlled myself, fine -- once I finally learned it, when my agent sexually assaulted me, I had learned, I had already been through years of therapy. And I was able to control myself when I -- when she knew the old guy who was sure to kill this man. And some kind of way, I was able to control myself and really put myself together through the therapy. And the promise that I made to her. It saved my life.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Then if there`s one thing that you could change, impact that you could have, would it be to change how we define masculinity?\par \par \uc2 CREWS: Absolutely. I think this is what the book is about. First of all, tough, for me growing up was always the ability to throw punches. But real toughness is the ability to take -- to take the punches and to endure and to grow and to protect.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: And to keep loving.\par \par \uc2 CREWS: And to keep loving. It`s tough and to do the work like to actually do the internal, you know, surgery that you have to do. I like to say that we as people come assembly required and most men feel like I`m fine, just the way I am. But we have to work on ourselves.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Just because we`re broken doesn`t mean we can`t be fixed.\par \par \uc2 CREWS: Right.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Terry Crews, so good to see you. So good to see you.\par \par \uc2 CREWS: Thank you for letting me be here.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Coming up next, inspire America when the 11th Hour continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:48]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, inspiring America. This week NBC is celebrating remarkable individuals who remind us of the healing power of community. One of those being honored is fashion industry icon Christy Turlington, after facing potentially life-threatening complications during childbirth, she now travels the world brings support to pregnant women with her nonprofit, Every Mother Counts. Here`s some of her conversation with Savannah Guthrie.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SAVANNAH GUTHRIE, NBC NEWS: Something about that moment, you go from the ecstasy of having your first baby, to the trauma of having to go through a medical procedure that was painful, and you did not plan. And that moment, gave you a mission.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTY TURLINGTON, FASHION ICON: It really did. Definitely, took a little time and I started to talk to other women. After I came home and more and more women were sharing their stories with me like oh, you know, my child was in the NICU or I had a similar experience or I enriched or I needed a transfusion, right? Like there were all these stories that I hadn`t heard until after I became a mom. And that`s too late, I think.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: You started to do your homework, you started researching maternal health. And what did you find?\par \par \uc2 TURLINGTON: I learned that around the world, hundreds of 1000s of girls and women die every year from pregnancy and childbirth related complications and that shocked me, like that seems like something that happens in a different time.\par \par \uc2 GUTHRIE: It does. It feels like some old-fashioned Victorian book you might read.\par \par \uc2 TURLINGTON: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: You can see all the honorees during tomorrow night`s special Inspiring America, the 2022 Inspiration List, airing on NBC at 9 p.m. Eastern and MSNBC at 10 p.m. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 And before we go, I have to give a huge congratulations to our dear friend Chris Jansing on her new role as anchor of the 1 p.m. hour of MSNBC reports. We cannot wait to see her. And a huge thanks to the one and only Monica Lewinsky tonight. I vowed to not make it on air unless I completed today`s Wordle. Well, Monica took to \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and helped me complete it.\par \par \uc2 On that note, I wish you a very good night from all of our colleagues across the networks have NBC News. Thanks for staying up late. Have a great Mother`s Day and I`ll see you next week.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220616ei560002v\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 The Last Word with Lawrence O`Donnell for May 6, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ali Velshi, Alicia Menendez, Glenn Kirschner, Joyce Vance, Cal  Perry \par \uc2 4,530 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Last Word with Lawrence O'Donnell\par \uc2 LWLOD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [22:00:00]\par \par \uc2 ALI VELSHI, MSNBC HOST: It`s time now for THE LAST WORD and my friend Alicia Menendez is filling in for Lawrence tonight. And if you`re confused about time changes, you saw Alicia on TV at 5:00 a.m. this morning. So, don`t be puzzled. It`s still the -- it`s the same day and you`re doing a second show, 15 hours, whatever it is.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENEDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Ali Velshi, someone tweeted at me, "Who do you think you are, Ali Velshi?" And I think that is a joke I have already made to you. Have a wonderful night my friend.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: You, too, my friend. Have a good one. We`ll see you tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: A new poll confirms that the extreme Republican majority on the Supreme Court way out of step with the nation -- 66 percent of Americans support Roe v. Wade and the constitutional right to have an abortion. Red state Republicans are pushing for even more extreme anti-choice legislation.\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana, the Republican legislature advanced a bill classifying abortion as a murder allowing prosecutors to criminally charge patients with murder. The bill would grant constitutional rights to a person, quote, "from the moment of fertilization." Raising alarms with the bill, if signed into law, could restrict access to in vitro fertilization and emergency contraception.\par \par \uc2 So, will there be a murderer`s row for those who take the morning after pill? It sounds crazy, I know, because it is. This is why thousands of people, thousands of women, have turned out across this country to protest having a constitutional right taken away. A new political video imagines an America where abortion is illegal.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Listen, everything is going to be okay. All right? Just let me do the talking, okay?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Evening, ladies. License please. Where are you headed?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: We`re just out for a drive.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Headed to the border?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Oh no, no. We were just going up to the --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Hey there! What`s your name?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Grace.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Are you pregnant, Grace?\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Mom.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Step out of the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: She does not have to get out of --\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Yes, she does.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: No, no, no.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Show me your hands on the wheel please. Grace, step out of the vehicle.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Mom? Mom!\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: On the wheel.\par \par \uc2 UNKNOWN: Grace. Grace. It`s okay.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Sound impossible or hysterical? Well, some states where abortion would immediately be outlawed are considering laws to prosecute those who travel for abortions. In March, the Missouri House of Representatives blocked a Republican sponsored bill that would allow individuals to sue anyone helping a patient cross state lines for an abortion.\par \par \uc2 If Roe v. Wade is overturned, legislations like this could gain more support. Here`s the thing. Many have pointed out it is not just abortion. The draft Supreme Court decision opens the door for ending even more rights, from health care to interracial marriage, to marriage equality.\par \par \uc2 An extreme right-wing politicians know five extreme right-wing justices on the court have got their backs. But will they come for next? In Texas, Republican Governor Greg Abbott says he will seek to overturn a 1982 court decision that ruled the public schools must educate all children that is including immigrant children. Throwing school children out of their classrooms probably not something the majority of the American people want, but it`s possible. Republican lawmakers don`t need a majority of the voters anymore. Just the majority of the Supreme Court. Conservatives achieving the very thing that they always claim they hated, making policy from the bench. Leading off our discussion tonight, Danielle Holley-Walker, the dean and professor of law at \uc2 Howard University\uc2 . Professor Hawley Walker, what do you fear from this draft opinion and what could be next?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE HOLLEY-WALKER, DEAN, HOWARD UNIVERSITY LAW SCHOOL DEAN: Thank you so much for having me on this evening. I think we have seen with the Louisiana bill that you just spotlighted, how fast this train can move down the tracks once Row and Casey are overturned.\par \par \uc2 I think what we`ve seen in the Louisiana law is we have many states like Louisiana that have what are called trigger laws, which basically allows them to outlaw abortion the minute that Roe is overturned. And that`s what Louisiana has and we see them blowing past what they`ve had with this committee vote out of committee to vote in Louisiana House to basically criminalize abortion and move beyond finding abortion providers to actually targeting women who are seeking health care and charging them with murder.\par \par \uc2 And so, when we hear experts and people who are raising the alarm around this draft opinion, it`s not just histrionics.\par \par \uc2 [22:04:53]\par \par \uc2 And it`s really important that everyone who is watching us tonight understands that the broad language that`s in the draft opinion really does put on the table everything from the right to interracially marry, the right to prevent forced sterilization, all of these things that are protected by the right to privacy and by our 14th Amendment right. That could all be up for discussion.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Stay on that point, walk us through what would a reversal of Roe v. Wade mean for other landmark Supreme Court decisions?\par \par \uc2 HOLLEY-WALKER: So, for example, there are many rights that we think of as being protected under the constitution. And I think it`s important to remind people that Roe was affirmed in Casey. And Casey is also an opinion that protects the right to privacy and abortion.\par \par \uc2 But once you start saying that if it`s not written in the text of the constitution and it has to be deeply rooted in the American law, which is what the draft opinion says, it really takes us back as Justice Alito and the draft opinion does to the 18th and 17th century. And if you think about how many rights were not protected for many people in this country, including women, people of color, or LGBTQ community, all of those rights are now on the table when you say that the rights that are protected under the 14th Amendment are only the ones that are deeply rooted in American history.\par \par \uc2 And as Linda Greenhouse and others have pointed out today, it also sweeps away a lot of the progress that`s been made on women`s equality. I think what we saw in that ad is really the specter of women and our healthcare being criminalized by states. And without the protection of Roe, that is really what`s on the table.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: I want to read you this tweet from Congressman Adam Schiff. He says, "I don`t care how the draft leaked. That`s a sideshow. What I care about is that a small number of conservative justices who lied about their plans to the Senate, intend to deprive millions of women of reproductive care. Codifying Roe isn`t enough. We must expand the court." Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 HOLLEY-WALKER: You know, I think this is something that really has to be thought about when we`re asking what do people want to mobilize around to stop what we`re seeing from this draft opinion? I agree that the leak is not important in the sense that what is critically important is protecting rights that have been protected for a long amount of time.\par \par \uc2 Row has been in place for half a century. So, these are settled rights, and also to think that only rights that we`d be protected by are constitution are ones that would have been there before. Most of us had recognized constitutional rights is something that is deeply troubling.\par \par \uc2 I think when Congress Schiff is talking about the idea of federal legislation, federal legislation is honestly, that would be a backstop, that would be a last resort, but it is not the same thing as protection of constitutional rights in the sense that before the federal legislation passed this week at (inaudible) states like Louisiana doing what we saw there, and also federal legislation can be overturned as we have seen.\par \par \uc2 And what would need to be done to pass federal legislation would include probably ending the filibuster, which would mean if we end the filibuster to pass federal legislation protect abortion rights, then in the next Congress they could absolutely pass another law that would take away those same rights.\par \par \uc2 So, I think the constitutional protection of these rights is incredibly important to everyday citizens, women, and everyone who cares about liberty around this country.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Professor Holley-Walker, thank you for walking us through this as dark as it may be. Joining us now, Ana Marie Cox, columnist for "New York Magazine," Maria Teresa Kumar, President and CEO of Voto Latino and an MSNBC contributor, and MSNBC legal analyst Joyce Vance. Maria Teresa, you work with voters. You mobilize voters. Are Republicans going to take this right away without a fight?\par \par \uc2 MARIA TERESA KUMAR, PRESIDENT AND CEO, VOTO LATINO: Absolutely not. I think we forget that one of the things that we were able to do the day after, Alicia, that President \uc2 Obama\uc2  was sworn into office, we had the Women`s March. And it wasn`t just in the United States. It was global. And it wasn`t just in blue states. It was red ruby states.\par \par \uc2 People marched and they decided that not only were they going to knock on doors, they were going to volunteer, they were going to donate, they were going to run for office. And in 2018, I call that the dress rehearsal. That all of a sudden, we had the most diverse members of Congress in our nation`s history because Americans mobilized.\par \par \uc2 We live in a multi-cultural democracy and what the Republicans are trying to do is that they`re trying to take away the rights of a majority of Americans and allow it to be relegated to have just a few individuals of the Republican Party control it.\par \par \uc2 Whether we`re talking about controlling our bodies, access to the voting booth, burying books, bullying trans children, taking away immigrant rights. You name it, and that`s what they`re trying to do. They`re trying to create control of a minority few.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:01]\par \par \uc2 But what 2020 taught us was that when you start rattling the cages of women, we not only stand up, but we do the work to ensure that we are saving the country.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yes. Ana Marie, the point of Maria Therese just made right there. The culmination of this Republican project. It comes on the era of Trump. There`s no longer any question, if there ever was, no fiction about Republicans are all about. Your thoughts.\par \par \uc2 ANA MARIE COX, HOST, SPACE THE NATION PODCAST: Yes. I guess the Republicans knew themselves better than we knew them right away, right? Like they have no illusions about being a party with any kind of moderate wing. Like they are catering to who they are. And I just think it`s taken Democrats a long time to figure out who we`re dealing with and hopefully this has kind of ripped everything away.\par \par \uc2 Maybe even \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  knows that they are lying to her. I don`t know what would actually get her to that point. But we know who we`re dealing with now. I would also say that women have been saying this for a long time, right? Like women have been warning this is coming for a long time.\par \par \uc2 And, I do believe in our ability to get out on the streets. But, I also think we need to start planning. Like, I live in Texas right now and I love Texas. I love tacos. I love bluebonnet, but I also now worry about my health. So, I am going to start donating what I can to abortion funds. And anyone who really wants to be in this fight, do that right now. Do that right now because women are going to die because of this if it happens. Although, I guess, we`re all kind of thinking it`s going to happen.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Yes. Joyce, Ian Millhiser tweeted, "It strikes me as an unfair structural imbalance in our constitutional system that Democrats have to win enormous super majorities in the popular vote to be able to change federal laws, but Republicans only need to file a lawsuit." Your reaction to that Joyce.\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: Well, I think, Ian is dead on the money here. And look, there are a lot of structural problems that we`ve identified over the last four years. We now appreciate, for instance, that the Electoral College is a process that makes it very difficult for Democrats, even when they have a majority of the popular vote to take control. So, we have the structural issues that we have to deal with, but the reality is that Democrats never took the Supreme Court as seriously as the Republican Party did when it came to voting for the presidency.\par \par \uc2 The Republicans did a great job of convincing their voters that it mattered, that taking control of the court mattered, and of course, that resulted in \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  who did what Republicans would view as an excellent job of conserving court resources so that President \uc2 Obama\uc2  wasn`t able to get a final appointment to the court, and \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  was essentially shut out while giving \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  three votes.\par \par \uc2 That`s why we find ourselves where we are. So, ultimately, the lesson that has to be learned is that court politics are for keeps. Presidential elections are about the future of the Supreme Court. And now we find out, we are in a difficult situation that it will be tough to get out of because of the structural issues.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Maria Teresa, I want to look back to this idea of extreme power. Your read on Texas Republican governor, Greg Abbott, thinking he can use this Supreme Court to go after education for migrant kids?\par \par \uc2 KUMAR: Well, I encourage him to see what happen when Pete Wilson tried to do that. And Pete Wilson, that`s where I would say, honestly, that politicized me and politicized Senator \uc2 Alex Padilla\uc2  and politicized our vice president, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , and so many others because we were living under a place in California that just by we see (inaudible) being brown, you are racially profiled.\par \par \uc2 Imagine having a seven-year-old child right now having -- pulling them out of the classroom and having an authority ask them if they`re an American citizen. How frightening is that? This opens up not just whether, you know, you have a piece of paper. But it goes against the very fabric of our country because you`re encouraging racial profiling among the youngest among us.\par \par \uc2 And it didn`t go well against Pete Wilson and I can`t imagine it going well for Greg Abbott. Part of the reason, and get this, this is the fun part, in Texas you have 23 percent of the electoral base is Latino -- 52 percent of the classrooms are Latino. The more important part though is that 92 percent of them are American born.\par \par \uc2 So, the majority of young people that are about to turn 18 and when ballot is on that -- is on the ballot, are going to be young Latinos who are eligible voters and they`re not -- this is not going to sit well with them.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: A potent power voice, and to your point, yet reconstituted California politics. We could see very much that same realignment in Texas. Ana Marie, I got to ask you before we go, this idea of extreme power. Where do you see them trying to use it next?\par \par \uc2 COX\uc2 : Where won`t they? I mean, it`s true. Like, this is now a slot machine for them. So just bring things up to the Supreme Court. They know they can do it. They know that these justices are no longer constrained by what they used to tell people, right?\par \par \uc2 [22:15:00]\par \par \uc2 So, I mean, I see we`ve already had attacks on trans kids, that`s what`s going to continue. I do think that immigrants are going to suffer. I think they`ll find new and exciting ways of making women`s lives harder. Basically, that it is going to pose any kind of threat to the white male, you know, patriarchy, needs to be concerned.\par \par \uc2 And I agree that there`s going to be a backlash to all this. I agree that people are going to mobilize and in collection, and I agree that what Greg Abbott wants to do, which is unpopular with the vast majority of people, but we need to start thinking about what is going to happen, like win these -- like it`s not going to happen fast enough, but our ability to mobilize is going to be outpaced by the ability of the Supreme Court and the Republicans to pass these trigger laws.\par \par \uc2 So, I really want people to think about what they`re doing in their communities right now, who they can reach out to? What kind of support they can offer each other? This is the time to come together. One of the best things about the Trump era -- I can`t believe I just used those words all together in one sentence, but I`ll just finish the sentence.\par \par \uc2 Was that a lot of us did find support with like-minded people. This is a chance words and, I feel like a news those words together in one sentence. Let me finish the sentence. A lot of us did find support with like-minded people. This is a chance to do that again.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Ana Marie Cox, Maria Teresa Kumar, thank you so much for joining me. Joyce Vance, I`m going to see you in just a little bit. Because coming up, Democrats are fired up about reproductive rights, but their ability to hold their majorities in Congress still likely dependent on how voters feel about the economy.\par \par \uc2 Today brought more good jobs numbers, but inflation still high. We`re going to talk to Austin Goolsbee about that, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:20:00]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): Sometimes it really just takes something as appalling and at such an assault on privacy on precedent on the constitution on personal liberty, the rights of women and family. It`s really not country, that bring people focus into -- this is what your vote means and the elections and how it affects the courts and how it affects you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: That was House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  on MSNBC earlier today. We are now six months out from the midterm elections and the attack on reproductive rights is galvanizing Democrats. But the public`s perception of the economy could still be the single most important factor in Democrat`s keeping their majorities in the House and the Senate when voters head to the polls in November.\par \par \uc2 Of course, reproductive choice is an economic issue for many families. Something Republicans never acknowledged that Democrats will surely remind them of. Another economic front, today brought good news on jobs. The labor department reported that the U.S. added 428,000 jobs last month while the unemployment rate remained 3.6 percent. The economy has regained nearly 95 percent of the million jobs lost at the height of pandemic related lockdowns before President Biden took office.\par \par \uc2 Despite these strong job numbers, inflation remains a concern for voters according to a new CNN poll. Forty-six percent of registered voters say the Republican Party`s views on the economy are closer to their own. That`s compared with 31 percent for Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now, Austin Goolsbee, the former \uc2 chairman of the Council of Economic Advisers\uc2  for President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . He`s currently a professor at economics at the University of Chicago`s School of Business. It is good to see you. You know, earlier this week, Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said, quote, "the economy is strong and is well positioned to handle tighter monetary policy, but I`ll say I do expect that this will be very challenging. It`s not going to be easy."\par \par \uc2 So, Austin, for those of us who thought we could skip Act X, what does that mean and what do you see as the state of the economy?\par \par \uc2 AUSTIN GOOLSBEE, FORMER COUNCIL OF ECONOMIC ADVISERS CHAIRMAN: Well. I`ll tell you, Alicia, it depends which part of the economy you`re looking at. The job market is red hot. Looks fabulous. That the unemployment rate is already 3.6 percent and we still added more than 400,000 jobs in the month is almost jaw-dropping. I mean, that`s a fabulous number.\par \par \uc2 And normally, when the unemployment rate is that low, it`s hard to add that many jobs. At the same time, you`ve got inflation made worse by the war in Ukraine and what is happening in commodities. Made worse by COVID. And that we shifted what we were spending away from services and on to physical goods and that over loaded the supply chain and that led to a bunch of problems.\par \par \uc2 So, what the Fed is trying to do is navigate a little bridge. And I guess it`s kind of like if you got in the shower and you want to take a nice temperature shower, you`ve got some burning hot water is coming from part of it, and then there`s some cold water coming in and the Fed is trying to balance those off and raise rates to cool some of it without sending us into recession. Let`s hope they can do it.\par \par \uc2 I think if COVID starts to fade, they have a better chance to be able to do it. But that`s where we are. It`s kind of mixed up. Some of it looks great and some of it looks not as great.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Right. You sort of began to answer this question, but I want to dig into it and make sure that I understand what it mean. How much of this is really in the administration`s control? How much can they do actively to improve the problem? And how much of it is just, you know, as you said, COVID, the war in Ukraine, outside of their control?\par \par \uc2 [22:25:00]\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes. Mostly it`s not under their control and that`s what drives people crazy. In the White House, there is no, as I said, you know, you go all through the basement, there`s no switch down there. We`re like oh, if we had only flipped the switch then it would be fixed. Mostly, for inflation, it`s the Fed that`s the tip of the spear.\par \par \uc2 And that`s because the Fed can control the interest rate. Very abruptly they can change it, they can monitor conditions, they can go up, they can go down. And so we rely on the fed to be the first line of defense against inflation.\par \par \uc2 In the White House, it`s much slower moving. It`s got to go through Congress for the most part. They got to pass legislation. That could takes months or years and I don`t see that -- much of that happening to deal with inflation now. There are certain things they can do, for sure, on the geopolitical fronts and they can release from the strategic petroleum reserves to have a small impact on fuel prices in the short run.\par \par \uc2 You can address bottlenecks through the Defense Production Act and you can do everything you can, but everything you can do when you`re in the White House isn`t that big because 95 percent of what happens in the economy has nothing to do with Washington. It`s being set in the private sector. It has to do with the nature of demand and the supply chain problems that we`ve been facing for a year now.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Also, I would layer on to that challenge the fact that the answer you just gave, which is completely illegitimate answer, is a process answer, right? And that is not going to fly out on the trail.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes. Right.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: So then what explanation can Democrats give, do Democrats give to voters who may be frustrated?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes. Look, the first thing to note is partly, you just got to grin and bear as the -- if you`re the political leadership, that that economic timetable is not on a political calendar. And that`s just reality. But I think the things that you will likely see them emphasize are, number one, the job market is great and if anybody wants a job, now is the time to get a job. Wages are up, and hopefully, they will get real incomes rising more. Two, if we get relief on COVID, I do think there is a chance that you start to see relief on the inflation front. That is people stop spending their money on physical goods and go back to spending it on services, you know, as I say, it`s not me, but a lot of people stop going to spend money on haircuts and, you know, going to the dentist and a bunch of services.\par \par \uc2 Going to restaurants, going on vacation. If they shift back to spending on that, I think you will see relief on the inflation front. And then you will likely see the White House and the Democrats as they go into the fall, into the midterm election, emphasizing, look, this was a humongous problem that landed on us, but we`ve worked our way through it and they`ll hope that`s the persuasive argument.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Awesome. Thank you for that self-deprecating joke. I needed that today. Austin Goolsbee, as always, thank you for your time.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Great to see you.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Coming up, today a judge ruled \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  can remain on the ballot in November. And \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  making robocalls against \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  as a special grand jury investigates Trump`s crusade to find 11,780 more votes in Georgia. We get to all of it, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 2230\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020220507ei5600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 ALL IN for May 6, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Ayman Mohyeldin, Courtney Kube \par \uc2 7,614 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: All in with Chris Hayes\par \uc2 CHAYES\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [20:00:00]\par \par \uc2 JASON JOHNSON, MSNBC HOST: I`m going to scream. You`re both wrong though. The winner of the week is \uc2 Karine Jean Pierre\uc2 , our dear friend, formerly MSNBC. She is now the White House Press Secretary. She`s a former Dora Milaje as you can see her here defending then-candidate \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . She is one of the nicest best people. She started from the bottom. I`m so happy to hear that the crew is succeeding. I`m really happy for her.\par \par \uc2 Juanita Tolliver and Fernando Amandi, thank you so much. And that`s tonight`s REIDOUT. "ALL IN WITH CHRIS HAYES" starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 AYMAN MOHYELDIN, MSNBC HOST (voiceover): Tonight on ALL IN.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: I was just flabbergasted that not only was the idea proposed, but the people, people in my department were working on it.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Donald Trump`s defense secretary reveals just how fascistic and barbaric the former President and \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  wanted to get. Tonight, Congressman Jamie Raskin on the Esper revelations.\par \par \uc2 And Rudy Giuliani`s no-show and his refusal to comply with the January 6 Committee. Plus --\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: Ms. Green, did you advocate to President Trump to impose martial law as a way to remain in power?\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 MARJORIE TAYLOR GREENE\uc2  (R-GA): I don`t recall. What today`s ruling in the \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  case means for her campaign with the attorney who took her to court.\par \par \uc2 And as Democrats and activists organize, these are the people legislating what happens inside a woman`s body.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Shame on all of you. Shame on you killing babies. You`re murderers. You`re murderers.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: ALL IN starts right now.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Good evening everyone from New York. I`m Ayman Mohyeldin in for Chris Hayes. It has been 14 months since \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  left the White House. And just when we thought we knew everything about how dangerous, how unhinged, or how downright stupid this man and his administration were, along comes another book this time by this man, \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He was Trump`s second Secretary of Defense. And you probably last heard of him back in 2020 when Trump fired him on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  right after the election. And in the past year and a half, we have learned more about the circumstances of his ouster. Trump and Esper, you know, they were on the outs for much of 2020 and after Esper publicly at the time disagreed with the ex-President`s proposal to use the military to quash protests across this country after the murder of George Floyd.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing. Esper`s last straw, it may have been courtesy of this man, Johnny McEntee. He`s a close Trump ally. He`s a former football player who actually ran the powerful White House personnel office. In fact, McEntee was sometimes called the deputy president because of the authority that he wielded within the Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 And about a month before Esper was actually fired, McEntee`s office reportedly circulated this memo about him as first revealed by journalist \uc2 Jonathan Karl\uc2  in his book Betrayal. The memo outlines more than a dozen reasons as per should be removed, and chief among them publicly opposing the President`s direction to utilize American forces to put down the riots just outside the White House.\par \par \uc2 The memo also cites among other concerns Esper`s removal of the Confederate flag from military installations, and the fact that he had promoted Lieutenant Colonel Alexander Vindman, the national security official who of course became a star witness in Trump`s first impeachment trial.\par \par \uc2 And now, well over a year since he was fired, Esper is outselling a memoir on his time in the administration. And as in the case with every other tell-all book about the ex-president, it is full of some shocking previously unreported allegations.\par \par \uc2 According to a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  report on the book which has not yet publicly been released, "Trump asked Esper about the possibility of launching missiles into Mexico to destroy the drug labs and wipe out the cartels maintaining that the United States` involvement in a strike against its southern neighbor could be kept secret. Now, when Esper raised various objections about this, Trump said that we could just shoot some patriot missiles and take out the labs quietly, adding that no one would know it was us.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me be clear here for a second. Trump wanted to bomb Mexico, our ally, our southern neighbor in what would certainly be an act of war. And he wanted to do this clandestinely, possibly in such a way that somehow blamed another actor other than the United States. And according to Esper, one of Trump`s top advisors, \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  also proposed sending hundreds of thousands of troops to the U.S.-Mexico border.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Who suggested that we send a quarter-million U.S. troops to the border?\par \par \uc2 [20:05:03]\par \par \uc2 MARK ESPER\uc2 , FORMER U.S. SECRETARY OF DEFENSE: \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 . We`re in a meeting waiting for the President to come out. We`re standing around the resolute desk and he`s behind me and this voice just starts talking about caravans are coming and we need to get troops to the border, and we need a quarter-million troops.\par \par \uc2 And I think he`s joking. And then I turned around and I look at him and his deadpan eyes. It`s clearly he is not joking.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And according to another report from the times on the book, Miller also proposed that the military secure the head of the dead ISIS leader Abu Bakr al-Baghdadi who had just been killed by U.S. forces. And then he went on to recommend dipping it in pig`s blood and parading it around to warn other terrorists.\par \par \uc2 That would be a war crime, Mr. Esper shot back. Esper is clearly eager to paint himself as a maverick in this administration. He was willing to stand up to Trump`s worst impulses during the time he served. But there may be some revisionist history at play here. Yes, while Esper did publicly defy Trump by using the military to quash protests, he was actually seen as little more than a rubber stamp for Trump schemes.\par \par \uc2 As NPR, noted, he even earned the derisive nickname Yesper for his unwillingness to disagree with those around him in the White House. And then there`s the matter of the book itself. You know, here`s the thing about this for me. Esper knew before the election that Trump wanted to bomb Mexico. He knew this. And that his top a \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2  wanted troops to parade around a dead ISIS leaders had dipped in pig`s blood.\par \par \uc2 Didn`t millions of voters have the right to know what kind of president was seeking reelection? Might that information have been relevant before now trying to sell a book?\par \par \uc2 Courtney Kube is an NBC News Correspondent covering national security at the Pentagon. She joins me now. Courtney, it`s great to have you with us. Thank you for making time for us tonight. So, another Washington insider here trying to sell a book. Secretary Esper wants to be seen as a strong line of defense against Trump.\par \par \uc2 And perhaps he was one of the few adults in the room, an expression that we heard time and time again, with officials like him. Does that reflect the reality of his tenure there or was it a little bit more complicated than what is being perceived right now?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: So, it`s much more complicated. And part of it is the reason that you just mentioned in that report by NPR and by others. Many people at the Pentagon, both when he was the Secretary of the Army, and then when he fleeted up and became the secretary of defense, many people at the Pentagon, his compatriots, believed -- they called him Yesper.\par \par \uc2 They believe that he was someone who was rarely going to push back. And the one thing that I would say, from reading parts of this book so far is that he was pushing back at times, according to some of the anecdotes, but he was largely pushing back in them against some of the people surrounding President Trump and less against President Trump himself.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s hard to know whether that moniker of Yesper really was accurate. Another thing that I would point out, though, about \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  is, you know, he came in as a Secretary of the Army. And then of course, we had general -- Secretary Mattis who was fired in December after Secretary Esper had been at the helm of the army for a number of months already.\par \par \uc2 And then it was widely known that \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  basically lobbied or campaigned for the job to be Secretary of Defense. At that point, President Trump had been in office for some time for more than a year. It was well- known sort of how he operated, how his administration operated, and yet \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2  still wanted to take over the Pentagon and serve as a cabinet secretary under President Trump`s administration.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, he knew what he was getting into. And even when he moved up into the job of Secretary of Defense, the nickname Yesper, it`s still persisted throughout the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So, you know, Esper has said, Courtney, that there were in fact discussions within the Pentagon to send troops to the border for the so- called caravan that the administration was constantly hyping up. Do we know how far they got in those discussions or deliberations?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: So, according 