urse, we now have text messages actually showing Congresswoman Greene trying to bring up martial law with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  through his chief of staff at the time. But despite that, but despite that revelation, today, a Georgia judge ruled against the bid to disqualify Green from running for office. And just a short while ago, Georgia Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  agreed, meaning that Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  will in fact remain on the ballot come this November.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by one of the leaders of the organization that filed that lawsuit on behalf of Georgia voters. Ben Clements, he`s chairman and senior legal advisor for free speech for people. Ben, thank you so much for making time for us and joining us. Let me get your reaction to what happened today.\par \par \uc2 BEN CLEMENTS, CHAIRMAN AND SENIOR LEGAL ADVISOR, FREE SPEECH FOR PEOPLE: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And I want to read -- my pleasure. I want to read to you and our viewers from some of the judge in his initial decision on your lawsuit, he writes in part, the evidence in this matter is insufficient to establish that Congresswoman Greene, having previously taken an oath as a member of Congress to support the Constitution of the United States, engaged in insurrection or rebellion against the same or gave aid or comfort to the enemies thereof under the 14th Amendment to the Constitution. What say you about that line?\par \par \uc2 CLEMENTS: Well, thank you. Obviously, we are very disappointed and strongly disagree with the judge`s conclusion that there was not evidence to establish that she engaged in insurrection. But let me just briefly talk about where I think the judge did get it right because between his decision and the earlier Federal Court decision rejecting Greene`s effort to block our lawsuit, these two decisions really validate our legal theory in this case and others in every respect.\par \par \uc2 The judge accepted that if she engaged in insurrection, she`s disqualified under the 14th. Amendment. He recognized that if she`s disqualified, that is grounds to exclude her from the ballot. He rejected arguments that we would need a criminal conviction before she could be disqualified and excluded. And he agreed with us that we did not need to show that she engaged personally in any violent act. We only needed to show that she engaged in some voluntary act to aid or assist the insurrection.\par \par \uc2 So, so far so good, but it`s on that last point in applying that standard that the judge went astray. We rejected, for example, the evidence that when she said the day before the insurrection that this will be our 1776 moment, he rejected the evidence that that was, in fact, a call for violent resistance to the peaceful transfer of power, instead accepting her truly incredible testimony that she was referring only to lawful means to object.\par \par \uc2 And the very compelling evidence that that`s what she meant that you just played where she said, we cannot accept a peaceful transfer of power, we cannot allow that to happen. Now, according to your clip, that was stated sometime in late December. But even though it`s before she`s sworn in, it is compelling evidence that when she said that she did things she said the day before the insurrection, including this as our 1776 moment, it was indeed a call for violent resistance. And the judge inexplicably ignored that evidence that you played.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes. And it`s incredible that not only did he ignore that, but then was willing to accept her saying, I don`t recall, I don`t recall, and just deflecting, deflecting in what I think anyone in this country would know was a bad faith act on her part. This is a woman who has boasted about the role and connections and communications she has had with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  about January 6.\par \par \uc2 Do you think it would have made a difference if your teams had the evidence of greens text messages to Trump`s White House Chief of Staff about martial law?\par \par \uc2 CLEMENTS: Well, it might have made a difference. But let me say a little bit more about that. Because as you played, we cross-examined her about whether she had urged the president to impose martial law. And she said she did not recall. Now, even before we had the actual text messages shown that she had made that request, that was an utterly incredible statement that I do not recall whether I urge the President of the United States to impose martial law. It was just totally unbelievable on its face.\par \par \uc2 And so were many, many others of her claims to not remember, and yet the judge accepted much of her testimony in his decision. Normally, when a witness refuses to answer questions, or evades questions, won`t directly respond to key questions, normally, a judge or a jury will draw an inference from that, that if the testimony were -- if the answers were given truthfully, that they would be incriminating, that they would hurt that person`s case. The judge failed to do this here.\par \par \uc2 Now, we did in fact, obtain the actual text showing that she urged the president to impose martial law or at least discussed it with \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  the day after the trial when it became public. And we submitted that right away. We submitted it to the court and an effort to supplement the record but that`s another piece of evidence that the judge simply ignored.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Absolutely incredible. Ben Clements, thank you so much for joining us. I appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 CLEMENTS: Thanks. Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Ahead, some more new reporting that U.S. intelligence aided in the sinking of the Russian warship. Tonight, the President is speaking out about leaks from the Pentagon and what they mean for this war. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:33:51]\par \par \uc2 AYMAN MOHYELDIN, MSNBC HOST: We are learning more about the Ukrainian military`s effectiveness in its fight against the Russian invasion, particularly in the sinking of the Russian Black Sea flagship, the Moskva.\par \par \uc2 According to U.S. intelligence, excuse me, according to U.S. officials, intelligence shared by the U.S. actually helped Ukraine sink the cruiser Moskva. And it is not the first time American intelligence has actually helped the Ukrainians in their fight against Russia.\par \par \uc2 Last month, NBC News also learned that American intelligence "paved the way for Ukraine to shoot down a Russian transport plane carrying hundreds of troops in the early days of the war".\par \par \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  has also reported that American intelligence may be the reason Ukraine has been able to kill as many as a dozen Russian generals.\par \par \uc2 And yesterday, in a press briefing, Pentagon spokesman John Kirby denied that the U.S. has an active role in Ukraine`s targeting decisions.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOHN KIRBY, PRESS SECRETARY, PENTAGON: The United States provides battlefield intelligence to help Ukrainians defend their country, we`ve talked about that quite a bit.\par \par \uc2 We do not provide intelligence on the location of senior military leaders on the battlefield or participate in the targeting decisions of the Ukrainian military.\par \par \uc2 Ukraine combines information that we and other partners provide with the intelligence that they themselves are gathering on the battlefield.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:07]\par \par \uc2 KIRBY: And then, they make their own decisions, and they take their own actions.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: And so, all of this raises a very important question, if the U.S. is providing real time intelligence that is actually leading to the killing of Russians and their generals, as well as the sinking of their flagship military vessel, what sort of implications does this intelligent sharing have on our role in this war and Russia`s possible response?\par \par \uc2 Helene Cooper is a Pentagon correspondent with the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  where she has been covering this. She joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Helene, it`s great to have you with us. Thank you for making time for us.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with what we do know, do we know the level of detail that the U.S. is providing or sharing in terms of intelligence with Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 HELENE COOPER, PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT, THE NEW YORK TIMES: Hi, Ayman, thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 We know a little bit we don`t know all of it by any stretch of the imagination. The Biden administration has really ramped up the amount of intelligence that it provides to Ukraine. It was very -- the administration of the United States for years has been much more delicate about this intel sharing for fear again of inflaming Russia into doing exactly what Russia did.\par \par \uc2 Once \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  invaded Ukraine, however, a lot of the gloves came off. And since the invasion, Russia`s invasion of its sovereign neighbor, the United States has been carrying a lot of active battlefield real time intelligence with the Ukrainians. And it goes everything from the information, for instance, on the general story that we did this week.\par \par \uc2 In this case, the administration is sharing with Ukraine, where Russia`s mobile tactical headquarters are. And these are the headquarters, the battlefield headquarters that move frequently as Russia advances and retreats in this war.\par \par \uc2 And the U.S. is giving the Ukrainians the location have these gleaned from, you know, various different sources and methods that we don`t have that much fidelity on.\par \par \uc2 But they`re sharing this information and they say -- and these are the -- when you`re looking on the generals point, head tactical headquarters, or where generals tend to hang out, that`s where your senior officials are, they`re not going to be sitting in a convoy where they`re not going to be actually fighting, but they`re going to be in these headquarters systems.\par \par \uc2 So, and then, Ukraine goes and they -- you know, they attack these headquarters, and that`s how they`ve managed to kill not all -- 12 Russian generals so far in this war, which is an astounding number.\par \par \uc2 Now, not all of them were killed due because of U.S. intelligence. But we are told that many of them, you know, were.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, and I was going to say I think I read the too, a dozen generals is about how much of the U.S. lost in 20 years during the Vietnam War, not just in the first two months as Russia has.\par \par \uc2 Let me ask you about your reporting within the Pentagon. Are there any concerns within the Pentagon among the American military brass about how they think Russia might respond to this revelation?\par \par \uc2 I mean, are there growing worries that Russia might conclude that we have crossed the line and that this may pull us further into the conflict, perhaps a direct conflict with Russia?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: You know, that`s a -- it`s such a good question. And it`s something that we all as news organizations and NBC as well, we wrestle with day in and day out.\par \par \uc2 On the one hand, it is our job as journalists to report American strategy as it unfolds. On the other hand, none of us want to start a war.\par \par \uc2 The reality is that for all of the -- let me choose my words carefully now, for all of the indignance we hear from the -- a lot of the officials publicly, we are not reporting -- or we have not reported, in my opinion, anything at this point that all the parties of the war are not already aware of.\par \par \uc2 Russia is aware of the fact that the United States is supporting Ukraine. \uc2 Lloyd Austin\uc2 , the Defense Secretary said in Poland right after he and \uc2 Tony Blinken\uc2  went to Kyiv, to beat meet President Zelenskyy.\par \par \uc2 He said outright, that the goal of the United States is to weaken Russia so that they can`t do something like this again.\par \par \uc2 President Biden has been clear he`s now put 33 billion into backing Ukraine in this war. So, we`re describing some of what`s going on. But the bigger picture is clear for anyone to see.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:08]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Helene Cooper thank you so much incredible reporting. As always appreciate your time this evening.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Still to come, if the Supreme Court overturns Roe, abortion rights laws would be decided by the states. But who are the people that would actually make those decisions? We`re going to show you, just ahead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. SUSAN DELEMUS (R-NH): Shame on all of you. Shame on you killing babies. You`re a murderer. You`re a murderer. You`re a murderer.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:45:17]\par \par \uc2 MIKE PENCE, FORMER VICE PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: The truth is today most on the left believe abortion is a moral good to be celebrated and encouraged. But we`re here to declare on this momentous week with one voice, Roe must go and to encourage you once more to just pray. Pray for all the members of the \uc2 Supreme Court of the United States\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And in these days, especially pray that those five might have the courage of their convictions and send Roe versus Wade to the ash heap of history where it belongs.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Since the Supreme Court`s draft decision overturning Roe vs. Wade was leaked earlier this week, national Republicans as you saw there have been testing out their abortion ban messaging.\par \par \uc2 You had the former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  reading from the approved notes. But should the Supreme Court ended up overturning Roe as the leak indicates it would? The decision of whether abortion will become a crime will be made at the state level. That is where the animated base of the anti-abortion crowd will be making future reproductive decisions for hundreds of -- hundreds of millions of Americans.\par \par \uc2 You have people like Ohio State Representative Jean Schmidt, who has proposed a near total ban on abortion, including in instances of rape.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. JEAN SCHMIDT (R-OH): Rape is a difficult issue. But if the baby is created, it is a human life. And whether that mother ends that pregnancy or not, the scars will not go away. It is a shame that it happens. But there is an opportunity for that woman no matter how young or old she is.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: The bill that Schmidt is introducing would make performing an abortion punishable by up to a year and a half in jail but the woman would not be prosecuted.\par \par \uc2 However, this week, after that draft decision leaked, Republicans in Louisiana advanced a bill that would classify abortion as a homicide, and it would actually allow patients themselves to be criminally charged.\par \par \uc2 Now, it is an extreme extreme position. But it seems to be exactly how New Hampshire State Representative Susan DeLemus feels based on this video that was shot yesterday. Watch.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CROWD: Shame on you.\par \par \uc2 REP. SUSAN DELEMUS (R-NH): Shame on you. Shame on all of you. Shame on you killing babies. You`re a murderer. You`re a murderer. You`re a murderer. You`re a murderer. Shame on you. I`m a murderer. I murdered on my own baby. Shame on you. Shame on you. Shame on all of you.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So, New Hampshire is a state where Republicans control both chambers of the state legislator and the Office of the Governor. So, what is the plan on the national level?\par \par \uc2 Democratic leaders are facing massive questions about what they can do to push back. We`re going to break that down, next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [20:53:02]\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 NANCY PELOSI\uc2  (D-CA): Instead of people going off on what -- the Republicans would like us to talk about the leak, no, we`re talking about your life, the wife of women in our country, and how we have, again, a calibration of all of this in Roe v. Wade, and how we must have it be enshrined as the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: So, this afternoon, Speaker of the House, \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  laid out the challenges facing Democrats in a post-Roe world. What steps can the party take to protect women across the country and make sure the conservative justices on the Supreme Court cannot continue to peel away hard-earned freedoms in this country?\par \par \uc2 Next week, Senate Democrats will hold a vote to codify Roe as federal law. But it seems clear at this point at least that they lacked the votes to pass the measure without breaking the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Now earlier this week, House Intelligence Committee Chair Adam Schiff advocated a solution that at this point has come from the party`s most progressive members.\par \par \uc2 He tweeted out "codifying Roe isn`t enough, we must expand the court" but are suggestions like this simply wishful thinking or is there actually something tangible that Democrats can and should do at this moment?\par \par \uc2 Meagan Hatcher-Mays is the Director of Democracy Policy at Indivisible, a progressive advocacy organization. Brian Fallon is the executive director of the progressive judicial advocacy group Demand Justice. He`s a former Senate aide to Senator \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  and a former director of the Public Affairs in the DOJ under President Obama, it`s great to have both of you with us.\par \par \uc2 Realistically here, Brian, let me start with you. What can Democrats do between now and the Supreme Court`s official decision to try and firm up abortion rights across this country?\par \par \uc2 BRIAN FALLON, CO-FOUNDER, DEMAND JUSTICE: I think it`s important to understand that we didn`t find ourselves in this situation overnight. This has been a decade`s long project of the legal conservative movement. And it`s going to take some time to respond to the situation that we`re in.\par \par \uc2 So, I don`t think that this, unfortunately, is a problem that can be solved in the next six weeks between now and the end of this current Supreme Court term.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:08]\par \par \uc2 FALLON: But I think that Senate Democrats are taking a good step in putting this bill to codify Roe on the floor. It`s not going to succeed.\par \par \uc2 And so, I think what then becomes important is, yes, campaigning on this issue and electing more Democrats, but Democratic candidates for office cannot just assume that people will be organically motivated to come out and pull the lever for Democratic candidates without them doing more.\par \par \uc2 And by doing more, what I think they need to do is clearly articulate a vision of what will happen if Democrats gained seats in the Senate, what will happen if Democrats are able to hold the House? What will they do?\par \par \uc2 And I think that there needs to be three steps, at least three steps that are (INAUDIBLE) commensurate with the problem that we`re facing here.\par \par \uc2 Yes, get rid of the filibuster, yes, codify Roe. But then take that step that Adam Schiff is talking about to add seats to the Supreme Court. Because even in a scenario where you passed the Women`s Health Protection Act, this Supreme Court has proven itself so politicized, it will inevitably strike that bill down too.\par \par \uc2 We need a plan that directly addresses the root of the problem, which is this utterly politicized core. So, I think that Democrats it`s incumbent on them to communicate to voters that they have a plan that is of the right scale that can truly solve the problem.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: You know, Meagan, what -- the stuff that Brian talked about are very important, but what steps can President Biden take that don`t require 60 or even 50 Senate votes? Is there anything that he could do in the short term before we get to the midterms, before we even get to next week that he could do to shore up women`s rights in this country?\par \par \uc2 MEAGAN HATCHER-MAYS, DIRECTOR OF DEMOCRACY POLICY, INDIVISIBLE: Yes, that`s a good question. And in this situation, probably not. I think that Brian is right that in the short term, this is going to be very hard, and it`s going to be a very grim reality that we`re living in between now and November.\par \par \uc2 There are many things that I`m sure \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is looking at right now to see if there are ways that he could use his power as the president to protect abortion at the federal level, but we have a big hurdle in our way. It`s called the Hyde Amendment. It prevents federal money from being used on abortion services. So that makes it a lot more complicated.\par \par \uc2 And frankly, executive action can be undone either by a future bad president, whether that`s \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or somebody just as bad as \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . Or again to Brian`s point, can be undone by the Supreme Court who has shown itself to be over eager to gut abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 So, really, we should all be thinking about what we can do in this moment. And the best thing we can be doing in this moment is fighting to win in November to protect the majority that we`ve got and expand it with Democrats who are willing to do what`s necessary to restore the right to an abortion and protect it going forward.\par \par \uc2 And that also means, by the way, excising sort of like old school Democrats, like there`s one Democrat left in the House who is pro-life, and he`s got to go.\par \par \uc2 So frankly, one of the best things folks could be doing right now is send Jessica Cisneros, $5.00 or $10.00, so that we can get rid of \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  so that we are guaranteed we do have a Democratic majority that \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  is not that deciding vote on whether or not Roe should be codified.\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, let me ask you, Brian, about the anticipated mobilization of the base. You already see Republicans as we played earlier, Vice President -- former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  using this to say this is important for the base, a historic moment.\par \par \uc2 Who do you think is going to have the net positive here in terms of animating the base to come out for the midterms as a result of this?\par \par \uc2 FALLON: Well, I think that in general, when you have -- when you`ve lost something, the pendulum tends to swing the other way in favor of the people that are fighting back.\par \par \uc2 Republicans are at this moment here, because they`ve succeeded in building an evangelical wing in their party over the last 30 to 40 years. That is, in many ways single issue voters on this question of abortion.\par \par \uc2 And for the longest time we`ve seen in polls, you know, over the last several years, that the general public was strongly supporting abortion rights and strongly supporting the Roe ruling, and did not believe that it was truly at risk, believe that some of the rhetoric was overheated.\par \par \uc2 And now, we`ve arrived at the moment where I think a lot of people are god smacked that this is truly happening, I don`t think they should be. But I think that there is going to be an awakening in this country.\par \par \uc2 But again, I don`t think that Democratic candidates for office can assume that that will all that that outrage and that shock will translate necessarily into mobilized voters for them in November, unless they clearly articulate that they have a solution.\par \par \uc2 And I think Meagan is absolutely right, when Democratic leaders go down and campaign for \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  who is a vote on the other side of this issue, it sort of sends mixed signals about how committed are Democrats in Congress to vindicate.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:02]\par \par \uc2 MOHYELDIN: Yes, I was going to say Democrats got caught flat footed but at the same time, they were also lied to by some of these justices who publicly in hearing said that Roe versus Wade was settled law of the land and clearly, now they -- it seems at least based on this leak they think otherwise.\par \par \uc2 Apologies Meagan, we`ve run out of time, but I am sure this is a conversation we`re going to continue to have in the days weeks and months ahead.\par \par \uc2 Meagan Hatcher-Mays, Brian Fallon thanks to the both of you for joining us this evening.\par \par \uc2 And that is ALL IN it for this week. I`m Ayman Mohyeldin. Don`t forget, you can watch my show "AYMAN" every Saturday at 8:00, Sundays at 9:00 p.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. You can stream new original episodes on Fridays on Peacock.\par \par \uc2 "MSNBC PRIME" with my friend Ali Velshi starts right now. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220507ei5600001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 6, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Matt Bradley, Betsy Woodruff Swan, Ruth Marcus,  Michael Steele, Joshua Johnson \par \uc2 8,506 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:00:09]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Friday it`s the economy. A strong jobs report closes out a topsy-turvy week of news on the economic front for the administration as it tries to grapple with surging inflation, interest rate hikes, wild market swings, and of course, the messaging challenges that go with it. All ahead of the midterms.\par \par \uc2 Plus, government officials confirmed to NBC News that U.S. intelligence indeed help Ukraine sink the flagship missile cruiser of Russia`s Black Sea Fleet, as U.S. continues to ramp up its involvement in the war.\par \par \uc2 And later, governors across America prepare for a world without Roe. I`ll speak with one Democratic governor, who state just passed a law, protecting and guaranteeing access to abortion as it braces for an influx of patients from neighboring states with fewer access.\par \par \uc2 Good Friday to you. Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd here in Washington where the Biden administration faces some difficult questions on the most important issue for voters which continues despite all the other news this week to be the economy. How does it slow inflation without slowing down the economy? And politically, how does this administration sell a cooler economy when it struggled to sell the hot one that it in here -- that it began with?\par \par \uc2 This morning, the Labor Department reported that 428,000 new jobs were created last month beating economist estimates with the unemployment rate remaining at just 3.6 percent. This is a robust jobs market folks. And right now President Biden is on his way to the Cincinnati area to sell his economic agenda. All of this comes as earlier this week, the Fed took a major step to try to tame the surge in inflation raising interest rates by a half a point.\par \par \uc2 It`s the largest single increase in more than two decades. Raising concerns that its efforts to slow down its economy could potentially trigger a recession. It`s all about the soft landing. It leaves the President and his party in a perilous position as they face the prospect of a cooler economy. And voters who were already unhappy with their handling of the issue, even when the economy was going gangbusters.\par \par \uc2 Just 40 percent of Americans approve of the president`s handling of the economy. According to our March NBC News poll, there`s no evidence that really that has changed. Majority of Americans, 56 percent Expect a recession in the next year. According to a recent CNBC poll that uses the same polling firm. This morning, labor secretary \uc2 Marty Walsh\uc2  acknowledged the administration must improve its messaging on this critically important issue at this critically important moment.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MARTY WALSH\uc2 , UNITED STATES SECRETARY OF LABOR: It`s not just about telling a good story on T.V. so people feel good about themselves in their home, when inflation is high, or when they`re working in a job quite honestly, they`re not happy about. We have to -- we as an administration and when I say administration, I`m talking about myself and other members of the administration, because the president`s out there every day, need to do a better job of talking to the American people about actually what we are doing on their behalf.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: So joining me now is \uc2 Andrew Ross Sorkin\uc2  he`s a co anchor of CNBC Squawk Box and a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  columnist. And also with me is Austan Goolsbee, he was served as the chair of the Council of Economic Advisers under President Obama. He`s now a professor at the \uc2 University of Chicago`s Booth School of Business\uc2 . Andrew, let me just start with you. Just straight up. How would you describe the state of the American economy right now?\par \par \uc2 ANDREW ROSS SORKIN\uc2 , CNBC HOST: Well, I would say that the state of the American economy is actually rather strong, but set against this problem of inflation. So, you see the jobs numbers, the jobs numbers look good. In a normal world, you would say, this is a great economy. The problem is that wages just aren`t keeping up with costs. And so, you would -- you would turn around and say, well, actually, maybe it`s not such a great economy.\par \par \uc2 And that is the conundrum. And that`s the conundrum the president faces. And that`s the conundrum that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  faces.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me what markets are looking at. Why are markets reacting the way they reacted this week, which is pretty pessimistic?\par \par \uc2 SORKIN: The markets are finally coming to a realization and why it`s turned out to be such a surprise in the last few days, I think is a real question. But there`s a realization that the only instrument, it`s a blunt instrument that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has, is to effectively tamped down demand, that by default whatever they do, they are trying to tamp down demand, which means they have to turn the economy in a very perverse way, almost negative, because the truth is that we still have a huge supply problem.\par \par \uc2 Whether it`s semiconductors, the cost of gas, because there`s not enough of it, whether it`s -- whether it`s a late labor unto itself. And so, if you can`t control the supply, the only thing you can do is try to effectively hurt demand and that`s what they are effectively trying to do.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:04]\par \par \uc2 TODD: So Austan, walk me through this and how we know if this is working, right? And is it as simple as we see the -- our grocery bill get cheaper? Is that really how we look at this? What`s the Fed going to be looking at to decide, hey, our interest rate hikes are working?\par \par \uc2 AUSTAN GOOLSBEE, PROFESSOR, UNIVERSITY OF CHICAGO`S BOOTH SCHOOL OF BUSINESS: Yes, I think it depends who you are, you know, are you a consumer or are you the Fed? I think the Fed is in a tough spot. They`re trying to balance out the economy on one hand, roar and hot it -- with the unemployment rates 3.6 percent and we still added over 400,000 jobs in a month. I mean, in previous times that would be almost unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 You wouldn`t think you can add that many jobs when the unemployment rate is at low. At the same time, they want to see the inflation rate coming down. So, it`s not -- it`s not going to be that the prices actually shrink. That will be negative inflation or deflation. But this five, six, seven percent growth rates over a year absolutely, the Fed wants to see those coming down. Now, if we get continued good fortune on the COVID front that people up -- for whatever reason, even as the numbers have gone up, there isn`t the same level of fear.\par \par \uc2 It is conceivable that we could get out of the COVID era of the economy, let`s say by the end of the summer. In which case, it might relieve some of those supply problems that Andrew was talking about, that people could go back to spending money on services, instead of physical goods where we`re having this supply shortages. And if that happened, prices might come down without there being a recession.\par \par \uc2 But if not, and if the war escalates, and we see commodities prices and gas prices, and those things continue to go up, the Fed is going to risk putting us into recession to try to keep the inflation from growing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Austan, if you were appointed the inflation czar at the White House, and I`ve talked to a few who think actually, like COVID, that this should be something that somebody`s portfolio ought to be the spokesperson of. So, let`s say you got that task. What else would you be focused on? The Fed is the Fed, right? There`s not much you could do other than to jawbone the Fed, if you will, when you`re in the administration.\par \par \uc2 What could the administration do to help relieve realistically to help relieve this pressure?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Not a lot. If you`re going to take a job as the inflation czar in the White House at a time of inflation, you better just not be afraid to take a punch because that`s mostly what you`re going to do. We -- the reason we normally think of the Fed as being the tip of the spear, of dealing with inflation is they can move that they have channels that can move more rapidly than fiscal policy.\par \par \uc2 I think, for sure, you can work the edges, like in fuel, you can release the Strategic Petroleum Reserve on a temporary basis, you can address shortages, the defense production act to try to relieve some of those shortages. But mostly, this is not under the control of the federal government. And fiscal policy is nowhere. They`re not going to do any fiscal policy. So, I don`t -- I don`t -- I think that`d be a pretty tough job.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So what you`re saying is essentially, it`s the Fed or bust right now?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes, it`s the Fed or bust. Hopefully not both. It`s \uc2 the Fed\uc2  or bust.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Hey, Andrew, this is a global issue, this inflation story. Meaning, how much can -- if we`re the only one acting and you tell me, our central bank`s going to act all over the world and is this going to be a concerted sort of Western effort here? Or are we alone right now and trying to tame this?\par \par \uc2 SORKIN: Well, everybody`s trying to tame it. The problem is, we`re actually probably in a better position than most. We`re the cleanest of the dirty shirts in the laundry today. And in Europe, right now, they face an even greater conundrum in large part because if you think that fuel prices are high here, they`re much worse there. And again, that`s even harder to control. All of this comes back to the supply issue which is how can you get supplies?\par \par \uc2 Whether it`d be gasoline, semiconductor chips, labor, to effectively be cheaper, and to be working. That is a function of landing the plane on COVID, by the way, because you see what`s happening in China right now, that`s creating its own supply disruption. So, there are things that can be done but they`re not short-term things. They all require some spending, and I don`t think that they actually will solve the problem immediately.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: The other conundrum is when you start to spend immediately in certain ways you actually create more of the inflation problem. So, we`re having a debate in this country right now about student debt, for example. And I know there`s a lot of people who think we should or leave student debt, you will leave student debt. And in a very, again, perverse way, it might actually create more inflation. So, all of this becomes so super complicated.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Austan, I was going to ask you about student debt, Andrew brought it up. I mean, for -- as fiscal policy, this seems like the wrong time to do it. I understand the political pressure the president`s under.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Yes, look, I haven`t -- a lot of this is coming from Congress. And there are many different proposals. I -- as you know, I haven`t been a big fan of blanket writing off of everyone`s debt because I think if you go look at the numbers, there are a lot of high-income people with medical school debt, business school debt, and that sort of thing that -- it feels like you might have a better policy to aiming somewhere else.\par \par \uc2 I still think one of the fundamental things that happened here in COVID, is that Americans normally spend most of their money on services and because of COVID, they haven`t been able to spend money on services. And they went out and the demand for T.V.s and Peloton`s and exercise equipment and houses and a whole bunch of physical goods broke the supply chain because it`s never been higher.\par \par \uc2 If we can get out of COVID and we can go back to spending on -- I mean, I`m not spending on haircuts but, you know, getting people getting their hair cut and going to the dentist and going on vacation and restaurants that will put some -- that will give some relief on the supply chain. And we got to just hope that the Fed which is doing what it should do to kind of move back to a normal position that they just don`t do that too -- with too much emphasis that it -- that it drives us to recession.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t know about you, Austan. But the less hair I got, the more I have to get haircuts, right? Like, you know, because you don`t -- it`s the struggle.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Once you lose enough that will no longer be through.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Trust me, I`m just hoping to make it to my sixth decade with this head of hair. Andrew, the issue of watching a spiral on wages and prices. We saw wage growth, still not keeping up with inflation. But there`s also that trigger we don`t want to get to where suddenly we end up with -- I guess stagflation? How do we avoid it?\par \par \uc2 SORKIN: Well, that -- and that`s -- and that is the other potential problem that we might face. Which is to say, if interest rates go up too much and - - which means that the credit card bills go up because the interest rate is up and your mortgage rate is up and there`s just less money to go around. And then companies frankly, can`t hire on the other side, then you`re in like the worst of all worlds.\par \par \uc2 And so, the -- when people say, land the plane or we`re going to have a soft landing. It means that it got to be a landing of some sort. It`s just how hard is it? And I think when \uc2 Jay Powell\uc2  said soft dish, I think it was always the line, soft dish.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Ish.\par \par \uc2 SORKIN: You rarely -- you rarely hear a Federal Reserve Chair say that aloud. But that`s why people are anxious. That`s why people are worried about what`s going on in the markets. The market is an indicator of where the economy or how the economy should look not today, but in 12 months from now. So, think of it as a preview.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say Austin, very quickly, at best stability and inflation is a year away if everything goes well?\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: I don`t think so. I actually think if we can get past COVID, the new inflation that is the months of the summer, you could see inflation go down pretty significantly. And then we`d be feeling a lot better about the -- about the landing. If we continue to see the new months of inflation coming in hot, then that`s going to push the Fed to act more aggressively. And then I think all bets are off.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. The most important thing I learned here is Austin needs to have a weekly barber visit. And if we do that, we can fix this economy. \uc2 Andrew Ross Sorkin\uc2  --\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Stop bragging about your hair do their, Andrew. Not fair. Not fair. Anyway, thank you both. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 GOOLSBEE: Nice to see you. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Let me turn now to Ukraine. NBC News was first report that its U.S. intelligence that was shared with Ukraine that helped their military target and sank Russia`s flagship last month. Dealing Russia`s military significant and embarrassing blow. According to U.S. officials, Ukrainian forces asked about a ship in the Black Sea and U.S. intelligence identified it as the Moskva.\par \par \uc2 Well, in response to the report, Pentagon Press Secretary John Kirby said the U.S. did not provide specific targeting information for the ship and was not involved in the decision to strike the ship or in the operation itself. On those non-denial denials.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Meanwhile, on the ground in Mariupol, the U.N. General -- Secretary General says that nearly 500 civilians were able to evacuate the steel plant and the surrounding area as the city continues to be ravaged by the Russian military. President Zelenskyy is calling the situation an example of torture, with starvation being used as a weapon of war. Let me check in with Matt Bradley. He`s in Ukraine for us. And, Matt, it`s interesting to see these reports come out.\par \par \uc2 Of course, it`s the Ukrainian military that acted on the operational intelligence here. And this is really kind of an open secret that \uc2 NATO\uc2  and U.S. intelligence is being shared. It`s sort of, you know, is there a point of making this more public? Is it a way to taunt the Russians here? How is it being received to have all of this be so public?\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, MSNBCFOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Well, I mean, the Russians have said that they have no confirmation of that. They`ve sort repeatedly denied it, this ship was actually sunk. So, I mean, for the Russians, this is just a big face saving effort. I mean, everything that they`re doing is trying to conceal the truth from their public and trying to make it seem as though they`re winning this war.\par \par \uc2 And, you know, Chuck, we`re about to see a massive exercise in just that coming up on Monday, May 9th. That is this Victory Day when the Russians will be celebrating the anniversary of their defeat and their Western allies, including the U.S. has defeated the Nazis back in World War II. So, this is something we`re going to be putting up yet again, another kind of massive charade for their public back at home, who are -- they must becoming increasingly skeptical of what this war is about, and how much the losses that they`re tallying up are.\par \par \uc2 I mean, some Western analysts are saying it`s as much as 15,000 Russian troops, which is, you know, more than twice the number that the U.S. killed in -- we had killed in Afghanistan and Iraq. So, for them, for the Russians, for \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  all of this is about deflection including this issue with the boat.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Well, what he says on Monday is obviously going to be of interest to a lot of people. Matt Bradley in Ukraine for us. Matt, thank you. Coming up. The other large issue of the week. How the nation`s governors are scrambling to prepare for a post Roe World. The Democratic governor of Colorado, \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  joins me next.\par \par \uc2 And later, according to former Pentagon chief \uc2 Mark Esper\uc2 , President Trump proposed launching missiles into Mexico to destroy drug labs run by drug cartels. Yet another bizarre twist in the political times that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has given us. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:45]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As this week`s leaked Supreme Court draft opinion reverberates around the country, governors are racing to prepare for a post Roe world. A number of red states have moved to restrict abortion access even more. Oklahoma`s governor signed a Texas style six-week abortion ban into law. Meaning it allows citizens to try to enforce that law. Louisiana`s legislature advanced a bill that would abolish abortion in the state and classify it as a homicide, criminalizing abortion procedures.\par \par \uc2 And at least three more states, South Dakota, Nebraska and Indiana have indicated they would move to further limit abortion rights once Roe is officially overturned in total. Only about a half of all states, mostly these days led by Democrats are expected to keep abortion legal and accessible. 16 of them, plus D.C. have already codified abortion rights into law. And now they`re bracing for an influx of patients from surrounding states.\par \par \uc2 I`m joined now by the Democratic governor of Colorado, \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  who just last month signed into law on abortion access bill, as neighboring states moved to restrict it. You know, Governor was thinking about this today. When you look at Wyoming and Kansas and Utah, three states that border Colorado, all three are going to have less access for women on this issue for reproductive rights, making Colorado likely a Western almost sanctuary state of sorts.\par \par \uc2 What do you -- what do you need to do to prepare for that likelihood?\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS (D-CO): Well, you know, Republicans and I do mean Republicans because this has unfortunately become a partisan issue. By the way, Colorado`s first law decriminalizing abortion was signed into law by a Republican governor, John Love in the 60s. But now what we`re talking about, frankly, Republicans are just obsessed with taking away freedom and micromanaging how and when people have families, really one of the most intimate decisions.\par \par \uc2 It`s a scary time for many Americans, particularly in areas where their freedoms are being stripped away by their elected leaders.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, you I`ve been one of the few Democrats to I think, frame it this way. That it is --it is an issue of freedom, government control, you know, when it does seem when you sort of realize that we`re actually having government tell women when they have -- when they have to stay pregnant. And I think sometimes people, you know, get their backup when you frame it that way. But that`s essentially, where we`re headed.\par \par \uc2 What other freedoms are you concerned about could get rolled back?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Yes, I mean, it`s really just terrific in some of these states, Chuck. I mean, if a woman is forcibly impregnated or raped, the government is saying, we`re forcing you to have that child. This is on the same continuum as what we`re seeing in other states where they`re trying to micromanage what words you use in our schools, you can`t say gay, that sort of activity. This is just not the role of government. Let people live.\par \par \uc2 I mean, people are very savvy, they can -- should control their own bodies of course. They also should be able to control and have a say over what their kids are saying at school and not have the state interfere with that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What is a better way to protect the -- I mean, it does -- look, doing this via the states is going to mean that essentially, if a woman lives in Utah, they`re going to have less rights than if they live in Colorado. And it just seems astonishing to me that that we`re going to have a judiciary that is going to allow that to happen. I mean, would you like -- at some point, I think people are going to want to see this codified in Congress.\par \par \uc2 You were in Congress. I guess this is going to be an impossibility. What would a codified law look like on the federal level?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: Yes, of course, I would support the codification. What`s really scary here is there will also be a human toll to this. When you criminalize abortion, you put doctors in jail, you put women in jail, you don`t end abortion, you just make it a lot more risky. And many of these states, it`ll be returned into the era of back alley abortions with under qualified or unqualified but well intended people performing them at great risk to the mother outside of medical facilities. It`s dangerous. People will die.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 POLIS: I mean, this is not just some legal framework we`re talking about. There`s no question that if this decision moves forward people will die.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If I`m not mistaken you you`ve started to send a message to corporations who may be uncomfortable doing business in states that make it harder for them to hire employees. Do you see an opportunity for Colorado to become home to more of these corporations?\par \par \uc2 PLEITGEN: Yes. We`re open for business when Governor DeSantis attacked \uc2 Disney\uc2  because of their political advocacy for LGBT rights. I was frankly, just taken aback. And we said, you know, in Colorado, I don`t care if your organization is conservative or liberal. I don`t care what your health care plan does. But we have a home for you. We don`t interfere or we don`t retaliate based on your politics.\par \par \uc2 That`s a very authoritarian mindset. That`s what Putin does. That`s what Chavez did and Maduro. This is not what America is about. You have the freedom to have your political opinion, whatever it is. And of course, the state is not going to have retribution against you because of that. So, I think that`s a sweet spot for Colorado`s competitiveness. We`re attracting companies from across the country, across the world, in part because of that. Whether we get Disney World or not, we`ll see. But lots of companies are coming to Colorado.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to talk to you about the economic situation. Look, you were early on advocate wanting to see the White House focus more on the cost of living issues. What is realistic that government could do and how much of it. I don`t know if you heard our economists that were just found essentially like, look, you know, it`s the Fed or bust here that everything that government is doing is around the edges. Is that -- is that what -- we -- the message we have to send?\par \par \uc2 POLIS: You know, they missed a big solution. And I hope you can bring it up with the next time because the two biggest things I think our federal government can do against inflation, is get rid of tariffs, certainly not raise tariffs on solar imports from some of the East Asian countries. That would be disastrous because energy prices affect everything. Reduce tariffs and immigration reform. We have an artificial manufactured labor shortage in our economy right now.\par \par \uc2 We can fix it through the rule of law with immigration reform. So, immigration reform, rolling back tariffs that will solve a large part of inflation. Here in Colorado, we`re cutting property taxes, we`ve cut the income tax twice. We`re trying to do everything we can to make sure families can hold on to more of their hard-earned money.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, good luck telling politicians to sell opening the border and lowering tariffs on China right now. But the fact of the matter is, what you suggest is the -- is exactly what every other economist has suggested as well. Anyway, Governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , Democratic Colorado. Good to see you, sir.\par \par \uc2 Still to come. The power of Trump`s endorsement put to the test yet again next week. And a primary contest shaping -- shaken up by multiple allegations of groping. My political panel joins me here on set to discuss that and a lot more. That`s next. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:31:34]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Tuesday`s primaries in Ohio and Indiana kicked off what`s going to be a busy stretch in the midterm calendar as battles rage on the left and right about the future of both political parties coming up this Tuesday. We`ve got a pair of notable contests on the Republican side. They`re not big states, but Trump has got something on the line.\par \par \uc2 In Nebraska all eyes are on the party`s gubernatorial primary features a Trump endorsee \uc2 Charles Herbster\uc2  who faces multiple groping allegations. May feel like they`ve been growing and expanding which Herbster continues to deny. Herbster joins a notable group of Republican primary candidates this cycle who are also facing allegations of assault or inappropriate conduct. Think Missouri Senate and Georgia Senate.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in West Virginia, we`re following a contest on the right that could answer the question who is the ultimate kingmaker in wild and wonderful West Virginia politics, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  or Joe Manchin. Yes, they are on opposite sides of the Republican primary. And this redistricting induced member-on-member Republican House primary Trump is backing Congressman Alex Mooney, and Manchin has thrown his support behind Congressman David McKinley.\par \par \uc2 Let me 