id there was probably election fraud in that loss, even though it`s a pretty safe Democratic district.\par \par \uc2 And she`s rocketed from the strengthened -- you see her campaign, just incredibly charismatic, dynamic speeches. If you go down the list, and keep going to find somebody who`s running on their electoral experience or things they did in office. They`re not running in this race.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. Dave Weigel whose trailer newsletter of the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is absolutely indispensable. You should definitely subscribe if you haven`t. Thanks so much.\par \par \uc2 WEIGEL: Thank you so much.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Still to come, the U.S. surpasses a once-unthinkable milestone, one million lives lost to COVID-19. Atlantic writer Ed Yong won a Pulitzer Prize for his incredible coverage of the pandemic and he joins me on how we got to this point and where we go from here, after this.\par \par \uc2 [20:50:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Back on March 30, 2020, it was a few weeks into the pandemic. We were all trying to understand and make sense of what was coming at us. Dr. \uc2 Deborah Birx\uc2 , the White House Coronavirus Task Force Coordinator did an interview on "THE TODAY SHOW" where she gave a fatality projection.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Dr. Fauci said yesterday, we could see millions of cases in this country and as many as one to 200,000 deaths. Do you agree with that analysis? Is that a worst-case scenario or something that we should prepare ourselves as potentially likely?\par \par \uc2 DR. DEBORAH BIRX, FORMER WHITE HOUSE CORONAVIRUS TASK FORCE COORDINATOR: So, in the flu models, the worst case scenario is between 1.6 million and 2.2 deaths. That`s the projection if you do nothing. So, we`ve never really done all of these things that we`re doing. We`ve put them into a model. We`ve looked at the Italy data with their self isolation, and that`s where we come up with. If we do things together well, almost perfectly, we could get in the range of 100,000 to 200,000 fatalities. We don`t even want to see that.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: Well, that -- I know. But you know, you kind of take my breath away with that, because what I hear you saying is that`s sort of the best-case scenario. If everything works and people do the things you`re asking them to do, maybe you can hold the deaths to one to 200,000 in this country.\par \par \uc2 BIRX: Well, the best-case scenario would be 100 percent of Americans doing precisely what is required. But we`re not sure based on the data that you`re sharing from around the world and seeing these pictures that all of America is responding in a uniform way to protect one another. So, we also have to factor that in, cities that don`t social distances, that don`t stay at home that believe you can have social interactions, that believe you can have gatherings of homes of 20 and 10 people even. That is going to spread the virus even if everyone looks well.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HAYES: It`s really striking to wash it again. Isn`t it 100,000 to 200,000 lives lost in this country if we do things almost perfectly. I remember that. I actually remember poring over models from England in February and how much of a shock it was to see the numbers. Now, a little more than two years after the interview, one million people are dead.\par \par \uc2 And look, this is the part in the copy where I try to put that in context. There`s no actual way to put it in context, honestly. It`s a number and a toll on a society-wide experience that in many ways I have found rebels against context against us truly grasping it.\par \par \uc2 That`s something Ed Yong has been trying to do at the Atlantic chronicling our pandemic journey, recently writing that in just two years, COVID has become the third most common cause of death in the U.S., which means it is also the third leading cause of grief in the U.S. And he joins me now.\par \par \uc2 You have covered this as well as anyone in the country. You`ve been a cherished resource on this show. And I`ve been reading your writing about the morning here and trying to reckon with it, I guess at an individual level. How do you think about this particular milestone?\par \par \uc2 ED YONG, STAFF WRITER, THE ATLANTIC: I think it just marks a monumental national tragedy. I think we should see it as the disgrace that it is not only because the number is so extraordinarily high, and so disproportionately high given the advantages and the supposed preparedness that America had going into this.\par \par \uc2 It`s also -- it`s also a tragedy, because we have collectively normalized to it so much that that so many people could have been -- the fourth and fifth deadliest months of the pandemic so far were January and February of this year. And yet in those months, the narrative was largely about how things are going to be OK. You know, mild variant, blah, blah, blah. Like, we`re going back to normal.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:18]\par \par \uc2 People were still dying in those months. And those people left behind loved ones who are grieving the people who they lost at a moment when society told them to go back to normal. I just got a call with the Marked By COVID group and listening to grievers, share their experiences. I`ve listened to dozens and hundreds of such accounts. People are not OK.\par \par \uc2 The consequence of one million deaths is about at least nine million people who had a hole ripped in their lives, and a hole that is not closing. Even for people who lost loved ones two months ago, their wounds are raw, their grief is prolonged. And we need to do much better at giving them the recognition, validation, and grace that they need to even begin to cope with the losses that they`ve experienced.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: I think that the thing that I keep coming back to -- I think a little bit as I watch the war in Ukraine play out, right, it`s just the human ability to acclimate, right? The human ability to like, soldier on, you know, watching people in, you know, just the most dire catastrophes, right, in Ukraine and posting social media messages and finding humor and soldiering on admits the worst kind of devastation, that I think I had some vision of catastrophe that was more sort of like singular and spectacular in some way than what this has been.\par \par \uc2 Which is just that like this sort of weird, surreal juxtaposition between disruptions to life, grinding levels of death and grief, and some ersatz normalcy all just sort of moving next to each other for a very long period of time.\par \par \uc2 YONG: I agree. This isn`t like a war or a hurricane or, you know, an earthquake. You cannot see the devastation around you. I look out my window and things look OK.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Right.\par \par \uc2 YONG: It does -- I can`t see the nine million holes in people`s lives. I can`t see the traumatized healthcare workers. You know, the ruin inflicted by the pandemic is harder to see. You actually have to talk to people and empathize with their stories, which is something I think we are constitutionally a bit bad at.\par \par \uc2 There`s also the fact that the deaths were not spread equally, the disproportionately affected Black and Brown communities, all the people, poor people, sicker and immunocompromised people. And America is nothing if not excellent at ignoring mass death when it affects the most marginalized and vulnerable populations. This is something that I think contributes to that moral tragedy, and I think is one of the reasons why people have normalized so much.\par \par \uc2 The people in positions of power in politics, in our industry, in the media, were among the first and earliest to get vaccinated. And the minute they`ve -- a lot of them felt that they were safe, they declared that it was safe with no regard for the couldn`t -- the dramatic residual risk that some groups still bear, or that long-term accumulative costs that aren`t going away, like, the grief that is befalling to people I`ve been talking about.\par \par \uc2 We need to refocus our attention on the people who suffer the most. And I`m not sure that we`re that`s what we`re doing. I`m not seeing that.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes. You know, it`s interesting, because I think back to the flu pandemic of 1917. And the fact that, you know, one of the -- you know, we never -- most people didn`t learn about it, right? There was a big book about it in the -- in the early aughts by John Barrett. It`s great book that, you know, it was a sort of, oh, my God. It was a larger deaths toll than World War I. Why do we never hear about it?\par \par \uc2 And now here we are, at the end of this period, and it`s like, oh, they just didn`t talk about it because they were so traumatized. Like, they wanted to forget it. And suddenly, that would seem inexplicable at the beginning of the pandemic. I`m watching it happen in real time in our lives.\par \par \uc2 YONG: Right. You know, when 100,000 people die, the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  had a big splashy front page, and it built the losses as incalculable. Well, what`s 10 times incalculable? Apparently, you know, we`re not even thinking about that question, right? We`re, we`re sort of moving past it.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is one thing that gives me hope that -- one thing that suggests that we weren`t just forget, which is just that so many people have been affected. I have seen so many grassroots groups, I want to just talk about Marked By COVID, sprang up in the wake of this tragedy. People united in their grief and their losses who are trying very hard to make sure that their loved ones are not forgotten, that the lessons from these years are not forgotten. And I think that there is enough -- there are enough of them that I wonder if that`s sufficient to make us remember.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Yes.\par \par \uc2 YONG: I would I would hope so. But, you know, it shouldn`t be on the people who have lost the most. It shouldn`t be on the people who are still mourning their parents or their children to carry this burden of refashioning our society. You know, it should surely be on the rest of us.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: Ed Yong, as always, it`s a great pleasure to have you on. Thank you very much.\par \par \uc2 YONG: Thanks, Chris.\par \par \uc2 HAYES: That is ALL IN on this Thursday night. "MSNBC PRIME" with Ali Velshi starts now. Good evening, Ali.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220506ei5500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for May 5, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Ali Vitali, Charlie Sykes \par \uc2 8,671 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: Unease within the GOP as it faces the potential midterm fallout over Roe v Wade being overturned. And right-wing activists are pushing for a nationwide ban on abortion. The electees are getting nervous. This, as abortion politics takes center stage and it contentious democratic runoff in the Lone Star State.\par \par \uc2 And nine-term incumbent, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2 , is trying to hang up despite his self-described pro-life stance. His Democratic rival, Jessica Cisneros, will be here ahead.\par \par \uc2 And where we stand on day 71 of the war in Ukraine as the White House denies giving Ukraine intelligence designed to target and kill Russian generals as bloody battles reportedly rage on in Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 Good Thursday to you and Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd. For decades, Republicans loudly pushed a rollback the right to an abortion but now that the Supreme Court seems poised to actually deliver on that promise that Republicans have been making for decades to overturn Roe v Wade, suddenly, Republican lawmakers are trying to change the conversation, signaling a growing level of anxiety inside the party particularly among those that actually face the voters that the issue could complicate their plans to take back Congress this November.\par \par \uc2 Just take a look at what we`ve already seen from some Republicans since the court`s draft Alito opinion leaked. Former Nevada Republican Attorney General \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  now running for the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  went out of his way to specifically note that the people of Nevada voted to make abortion rights legal and the matter is settled law, no matter what the court does. Translation, he doesn`t want to have a debate about abortion rights in the state of Nevada, where a large majority are in favor of it.\par \par \uc2 South Dakota Senator \uc2 John Thune\uc2 , who`s lone called Roe to be overturned, and his back pro-life causes in the Senate told CNN that he doesn`t know if abortion is a party issue. Many other Republicans have directed their comments on the leak of the draft rather than its content.\par \par \uc2 And then you have the NRSC, which is the GOP Senate campaign on. They`re trying to urge Republican candidates to put out statements that carefully tread around the issue. In a polling memo, the group argued that pushing a 15-week ban could be politically popular when coupled with hitting Democrats for their position on the issue.\par \par \uc2 The problem is that these kinds of political messages are going to run into a conflicting reality, that all made sense in a world where Roe v Wade was law of the land. But if this draft opinion is handed down, 13 conservative states will automatically ban all abortion, with little or no exceptions. This is not about 15 weeks it`s about legal or not legal. Several other states have six-week abortion laws in place should Roe be overturned and those are basically bands. People don`t know it -- six weeks, whether they`re pregnant or not. Oklahoma just passed one of those, by the way, this week.\par \par \uc2 And then there`s places like Michigan and Wisconsin which have laws that are so old from the 19th century, that are on the books that actually banned abortion completely and those would become automatic law should Roe v Wade go away.\par \par \uc2 And just last night, Louisiana Republicans voted to advance a bill that would classify abortion as homicide, which then would allow prosecutors to criminally charge abortion patients. Folks, in a post-Roe world, the debate won`t be about limits on abortion. What week is it? Things like that. Late- term, the things like this, as Republicans have long argued. It`s simply going to be about whether abortion is illegal or illegal for millions of Americans. And it`s a debate that Republicans are signaling pretty loudly right now that they want to avoid.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is our Capitol Hill Correspondent Ali Vitali, \uc2 Charlie Sykes\uc2  is the founder and editor-at-large of The Bulwark, he`s also an MSNBC Contributor, and The Cook Political Report, House Editor Dave Wasserman, also with us the Washington Post Caroline Kitchener. She`s a politics reporter who focuses on reproductive rights.\par \par \uc2 Ali, let me start with you. You`ve been on the front lines of chasing Republicans who -- let`s admit it, it`s hard to find many elected Republicans that want to talk about Alito`s opinion. And surprisingly, not many of them want to seem to celebrate it. And you would think -- after years of pushing for judges that would overturn Roe v. Wade, you would think there would be a celebratory atmosphere with some of these elected Republicans, but I haven`t seen it.\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, MSNBC CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I haven`t seen it either here. It`s almost as if the theory of overturning Roe was better for politics in campaigning for Republicans than actually seeing it settled by the Supreme Court, which is what we`re likely heading towards in a matter of weeks.\par \par \uc2 For Republicans here on the Hill, the conversation that we`ve been having as we`ve been asking them and we have, whether or not they would push forward their own kinds of codifications at the federal level once this decision comes down from the Supreme Court. The ways in which they`ve engaged with that question have really been fascinating because in many cases, they`ve sidestepped it all together instead, just focusing on the leak.\par \par \uc2 In other cases, people like Senator \uc2 Kevin Cramer\uc2  said at one point, he imagined that pro-life activists would push for federal restrictions on this. That`s not him saying that Republicans are going to move forward on this quickly, there doesn`t really seem like there`s an appetite for it.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But I would also add just as the reality check we`re doing on Democrats here who are trying to codify this at the federal level in the other way to protect access to abortion, Republicans would run into the same numbers of issues likely because they`re not going to have 60 votes, even if they retake the majority in the Senate come 2022.\par \par \uc2 So, the number`s reality here would stop that from making it a reality, but I think the more important trend that you touch on in your lead, there does really seem to be a divide happening right now between national Republicans who are here in Washington speaking to the more national audience, and then state Republicans and conservatives who have been working, frankly, for years to make these laws possible so that they could be challenged at the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And then, of course, seeing now what the court is likely doing, you`re watching other conservative states like Louisiana, Texas, South Dakota, and others who are trying to push forward on the abortion issue, and then some.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`m just curious, Ali, have you seen it, Senator \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  introduced a bill to create a new floor on the viability of 15 weeks in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Not that I have seen yet. But I also think when you talk about the NRSC, and of course, that`s where \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  is focusing all of his energies ahead of 2022, their polling tells Republicans that they need to be compassionate on this issue. And at the same time, it`s hard to look at this as anything other than when Republicans start talking about restricting abortion, that`s a conversation that means taking things away from people, the option away from people.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time, too, I think the thing I`ve been keeping in mind with this NRSC polling is it`s all stale. It`s all done in a world where a lot of people may not have been focusing on Roe as an important issue because it wasn`t. In theory, it was settled. And for the past 49 years, that has been the case.\par \par \uc2 And so sources that I`ve been talking to on both sides of this, Republican and Democrat, you`re sort of moving into uncharted territory because for both Republicans and Democrats, this has been theoretical, the threat to Roe, from both sides. Now, it`s actually happening, and we don`t know yet what that`s going to look like.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me bring in Caroline Kitchener here. You`ve been reporting on what`s been going on in the States. And it does feel as if elected Republicans on a federal level are trying to compartmentalize what`s happening in the States. I mean, to hear \uc2 Rick Scott\uc2  arguing, hey, the Democrats are the ones that are radical, they don`t support any limits at all. And you`re going well, wait a minute, look what we`re seeing in Louisiana? I don`t -- I don`t think this is going to be the debate they think it`s going to be?\par \par \uc2 CAROLINE KITCHENER, NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER, THE WASHINGTON POST: Well, we are seeing states push more and more extreme legislation. I mean, I think what happens if -- yesterday in Louisiana, it really shows, you know, the -- how far they might be willing to go. I mean, it -- this was a bill that, you know, would make it a murder charge for a woman.\par \par \uc2 And that`s really something that we haven`t seen before, Chuck, you know, up until this point. It`s really been about punishing the people surrounding the abortion procedure. It`s been about the providers. It`s been about the various people who facilitate the procedure. But, you know, here, we`re seeing them actually moving forward on a bill that would -- you know, could potentially, you know, make this a crime for the woman who receives the abortion. So I think that`s a really significant change that we`re seeing.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you see already -- Caroline, I`ve seen a few legislators, all of a sudden, the reality of Roe`s being eliminated and you`re already seeing, and it`s been in a couple of places, but I`m curious if you`re picking up on it? Well, maybe there should be exceptions for X, Y, and Z, that all of a sudden, the reality that, oh, this is actually going to become law in our state maybe we rush to judgment here.\par \par \uc2 KITCHENER: I mean I really have not seen that yet. I mean, I spend a lot of time in my reporting talking to state lawmakers, and anti-abortion lawmakers all over the country, and they are just so energized by what`s happening right now. They -- you know, this is something that they have been working on for decades, and you`re actually seeing a trend of getting rid of exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 You know, in Texas, there`s only an exception for medical emergencies. And even that, the way that it`s played out, I mean, even ectopic pregnancies, doctors are concerned that that might not even -- they`re so worried to do the procedure because they`re worried you know, does this actually qualify as a medical emergency, when of course, we know that it does. But there`s a lot of fear, I think is the point among providers about what they can do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Charlie Sykes\uc2 , look, you were -- you were a well-known, you were conservative talk radio host in Wisconsin, extraordinarily influential. The issue of overturning Roe v. Wade, I`m going to be a bit -- I come at this a bit cynically, I don`t think many of these elected Republicans when they were saying it believed it and they felt comfortable saying it because they didn`t think it was ever actually going to happen. Am I being too cynical?\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 CHARLIE SYKES\uc2 , FOUNDER & EDITOR-AT-LARGE, THE BULWARK: No. I mean I think Ali made a really good point that it`s been theoretical up until now. And so, this was -- this was a -- this was a free shot for Republicans. It was also a free shot for Democrats. I mean, you know, in Wisconsin, we have a law on the books criminalizing abortion, and Democrats never repealed that because I don`t think they ever thought this day would come.\par \par \uc2 But you know, in -- on Capitol Hill, Republicans may be able to dodge answering questions about this, but at the state level, it becomes a very, very urgent question. So, here in Wisconsin, we have a governor`s election and Attorney General`s election this year and it`s a binary choice.\par \par \uc2 48 hours ago, if you would have asked me, I would have said that the odds overwhelmingly favor Republicans winning those races but now it becomes really an upper down on the criminalization of abortion without any legislative action. If Republicans win the governorship and the attorney general position here in the state of Wisconsin, that law goes into effect if Roe is overturned.\par \par \uc2 And the other big question in terms of the energizing of the base and I think it`s too early to know is what will the litmus test among Republicans be we`ve seen how these things can move very, very quickly? And the elimination of all of the exceptions is an example of that. This is a base lead party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 SYKES\uc2 : This is a party that is responsive to what the grassroots says and is reluctant to get crosswise with them. And if the Republican base demands radical restrictions on abortion, you are going to see more legislation like this and you will see pressure at the national level, particularly if in 2025, you have a Republican president and a Republican Congress to do something about that. And I think that`s very unpredictable right now how intense that will be.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dave Wasserman, break this down for me on some election terms. Look, the national polling is overwhelmingly in favor of Roe but I -- obviously, it`s different depending on the state. And if you take out New York and California, you probably see a more narrow divide among the other 48 states on this issue. Where do you expect this to become more problematic for the Republicans, and does it surprise you at how nervous they`re acting right now?\par \par \uc2 DAVID WASSERMAN, HOUSE EDITOR, COOK POLITICAL REPORT: Well, Chuck, just go back to the 2020 exit poll, 28 percent of Trump voters supported legal abortion in all or most cases, and that`s a significant chunk of the Trump base. I think this has the potential to drive a wedge into that base, particularly in the Midwestern states where that percentage of Trump voters was even higher.\par \par \uc2 Keep in mind, in 2016, he downplayed social issues like abortion and gay marriage and played up trade and immigration issues where he had a lot in common with those white-working class voters, including women. And then on top of that, consider that the energy level on college campuses before this decision leaked was pretty much dead.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WASSERMANN: And now it has the potential vote to very much come alive, particularly for state races, and we`ve got 36 governorships on the ballot this fall, that has the potential to mitigate democratic losses at the federal level. I think the reelection stock for people like \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , who`s got \uc2 Michigan State University\uc2  in her district.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 WASSERMAN: Or \uc2 Marcy Kaptur\uc2  in Toledo has gone up.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So you see this as the -- one of the things I`ve wondered here is that is this a, essentially an equalizer when it comes to base energizing, that the Democrats were lacking that sort of fear issue to get the -- you know, at the end of the day, we know this fear motivates a base more than hope. And the Democrats now have their fear issue, right, Dave?\par \par \uc2 WASSERMAN: You know, in March, the NBC News poll said at all right, 67 percent of Republicans rated their interest in the midterms as a nine or 10 out of 10 versus just 50 percent of Democrats. We almost never see an enthusiasm gap that wide. Now, I don`t think that gap will close entirely, but it will close significantly as a result of a ruling. And if that is the case, you know, that has the potential not only to lift Democratic governors as a check on Republican legislatures in a bunch of key states but also could cut Democrats` losses in the House, probably not enough to save their majority but still important.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Charlie Sykes\uc2 , Wisconsin is ground, to me, ground zero. And my goodness, considering how polarized that state is, and in many ways, Wisconsin, as you could argue, is exported their polarization quite expertly to the rest of the country. I feel like it`s going to be the last state that settles its abortion divide.\par \par \uc2 SYKES: Well, I don`t think we`re going to settle that abortion divide, and I mean it. It is going to be intensified.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 SYKES\uc2 : This -- again this has been a very theoretical debate, free shots on both sides but it`s going to die. I don`t see any other way -- I don`t see how it does not dominate this November`s contest. And then if Republicans take control, they will have to figure out, do they pass some sort of a restriction or did they let the old law go into effect? Yes, again, we`re a very, very closely divided state.\par \par \uc2 And to Dave`s point, there were a lot of Trump voters who voted on non- cultural issues. This seems like it`s going to be front and center, and obviously, there`s a lot of headwinds involving inflation, and other things that are going on here that might not be dispositive. But Democrats had a real enthusiasm problem here in Wisconsin. I think they were hoping that the opposition to Ron Johnson would be enough to turn them out.\par \par \uc2 But there`s no question that in order for Democrats to win in Wisconsin, you have to have a massive turnout from the college towns, massive turnouts from Milwaukee, the Madison area, and they need to continue to make inroads into the suburban areas. And this is exactly the issue that I think will cause a real problem for Republicans in suburban -- in the suburban WOW counties in Wisconsin, Waukesha, Ozaukee, and Washington counties.\par \par \uc2 So, there`s got to be real anxiety that once again, they use the grossly overused clichAc. They`ve been chasing this bus for a very long, long time, and the dog finally caught it, and they`re not sure what to do with it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Caroline, there`s an unintended consequence that a doctor friend of mine pointed out to me, which is the states that create these strict laws that govern reproductive health care, what will follow are a whole bunch of people who are OB-GYN who say, maybe I`m not going to practice medicine in Louisiana, maybe that`s not, and all of a sudden, you`re getting low-quality health care in these places that put these restrictive laws in place that may criminalize medical care.\par \par \uc2 KITCHENER: That`s absolutely true, Chuck. The American College of Obstetrics and Gynecology, actually, just last week, released a report on this. You know, it`s a -- it`s a really high number of these residency programs are just not going to be able to train people and abortion procedures.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, that is going to have huge ripple effects for the number of doctors available across the country who can do this procedure. And that -- you know that affects not just Republican-led states that are banning the procedure, that could also affect you know, how many providers are available in states like California and New York and Connecticut?\par \par \uc2 You know, one thing that you`re really seeing a lot of our, you know bills from these Democrat-led states that try to open this up and say, you know, we`re going to let you know, more people become abortion providers. But you know that`s just not going to be possible if people can`t get the training.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Ali, let`s go to the Democratic side of the aisle here. They have control of Congress but in theory. But not to do anything on this. Is there any path that they have of actually passing anything that might protect some access to abortion?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: No, because right now, what they`re trying to do is effectively a messaging bill. And there are some Democratic senators that will cop to that because the realities are what they are, in terms of the numbers. They don`t have the ability to do this by a simple majority, by getting rid of the filibuster on this issue. There`s not the political will for that.\par \par \uc2 And then, on the other side of this, to do it through the regular process would require 60 votes, and they certainly don`t have that. So what we`ve just heard in the last few minutes is that \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is going to hold a vote on this next week, it`s going to be Wednesday, most likely, and what they`re going to do is put senators on the record here.\par \par \uc2 There has been some tinkering around the edges with the Women`s Health Protection Act. The version that failed in February is pretty similar to the version that they`re going to put on the floor next week. It has some less non-binding language at the top, but the crux of it is very much still the same.\par \par \uc2 One of the hang-ups though, that we`re going to be watching to see if maybe they end up getting potentially Murkowski or Collins on board, the only two really curious Republican senators on doing something like this, they would have to remove one of the religious exemptions from the bill. The reason they have it in there right now, as it`s been explained to me, is that they want to make it basically unassailable in court.\par \par \uc2 Again, there`s no court to make it unassailable from because it`s not actually going to become law, but that one piece could be a sticking point that makes this just most Democrats voting for it and all Republicans voted against it. And by the way, I would say \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  doesn`t like he`s going to vote for that either so it`s not even all Democrats who are likely to vote for that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look, in a Roe -- in a world where Roe existed, this creates all sorts of wedges and divides on the Democratic side. In a world where Roe doesn`t exist, you see where suddenly, all of those attempts go away, and all the wedges and problems show up on the right. Anyway, Ali, Charlie, Dave, and Caroline thank you all for providing your expertise. Coming up.\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: How the abortion fight has taken center stage in one democratic runoff, it is an exception for what it`s worth. House candidate Jessica Cisneros who`s running against a nine-term incumbent, \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  joins me next.\par \par \uc2 And as we go to break, a look at the markets right now. The Dow is tanking, shall we say, it comes a day after the Fed raised interest rates yesterday by half a percentage point. A lot of this has to do with the perception now that Wall Street believes this shaky economy is now locked in retail sales, not so great, tech getting hit hard, this sort of post-pandemic economy is a reality, and this is Wall Street`s reaction to it.\par \par \uc2 This is the biggest for whatever it`s worth, this is a pretty large drop for one day. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we mentioned, the debate over abortion rights is now center stage in this year`s midterm contests and it`s mostly going to be an issue with Republicans. But there is one place where it`s a democratic debate if you will, and it`s down in Texas where longtime incumbent \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  is under fire from his opponent, Jessica Cisneros, and some progressives for his self-described pro-life stance on abortion.\par \par \uc2 Democratic leaders in the House for what it`s worth are standing by the embattled moderate. House Majority Whip \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 , the third-highest ranking Democrat in the House, rallied support for Congress Cuellar in San Antonio yesterday. Shortly before taking the stage, he even defended his support in an interview with my colleague Garrett Haake. Take a listen.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GARRETT HAAKE, MSNBC SENIOR CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: Is there still room in the Democratic Party for a self-described pro-life member?\par \par \uc2 REP. JIM CLYBURN (D-SC): I hope so. I hope so. We ought not to have fitness tests on our body. We`d never have had and we should not stop now. If this district has anything like it was, pretty odd to me, it gives us a much better chance of winning a seat than anybody else.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: All of this comes three weeks out from Election Day with Cisneros and Cuellar set for a heated runoff rematch in the 28th congressional district down there.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: And, Jessica Cisneros, an immigration lawyer and Democratic candidate for Texas joins me now. Ms. Cisneros, I really appreciate you coming up.\par \par \uc2 JESSICA CISNEROS, DEMOCRATIC CANDIDATE FOR TX-28: Of course, happy to be here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me start with, you heard Congressman Clyburn`s remarks there. And I understand you believe that his position on abortion should be enough for them to pull their support. But you`ve run on abortion rights before and the voters know that he is a self-described pro-life candidate. Why do you think voters have re-elected him with that abortion rights stance?\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: I mean it`s a challenge going up against someone who has been entrenched for a very long time. \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  has been in office at one level or the other, longer than I`ve been alive. And for me to at that time, be a 26-year-old first-time challenger with running a completely 100 percent -- 100 percent grassroots campaign, we`re not taking a single dime of corporate PAC money and come within three percentage points of defeating \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  last cycle, we just had to come back and finish the job this time around.\par \par \uc2 We had our election -- our primary election on March 1, we were just 800 votes difference -- there was an 800-vote difference between our campaigns so we`re getting closer and closer on March 1. The voters elect -- they -- the majority of voters in the district voted for a pro-choice candidate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: And for us, it`s about trying to turn out those voters on the May 24 runoff election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Talk about -- let me ask you this, there seems to be the district is a bit more divided on abortion rights than maybe your typical Democratic- leaning district. How much of that is more conservative religious beliefs among Latinos in general? What do you ascribe that to?\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: I feel like people have just taken Henry Cuellar`s word that this district is as conservative as it is when it comes to this issue. I mean, we`ve been running on it since the last cycle as you said, back in 2019, when I first decided to run. We`re bringing it up again the cycle because we have noticed and I think this week really points out the urgency of protecting not just Roe but so many of our fundamental freedoms and civil liberties that stem from this kind of case law.\par \par \uc2 And I know that it`s important to voters because I`ve been out there talking to them myself. I remember when \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  was the only Democrat to vote with Republicans against codifying Roe last year, we had a phone bank right after that, we didn`t plan it that way, it just so happened, and the first few voters that I got on the line were talking about, you know, they were telling me about how upset they were that \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  had sided with Republicans on this issue.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You heard \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  there. He said he doesn`t think there should be a litmus test. On this, you believe there should be a litmus test. Why? What would you tell \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: In this moment, we are watching the fall of Roe and the erosion of our fundamental rights. And this moment, we truly -- I mean, it necessitates a champion that`s going to fight for our people`s rights in this district. And I`ve always said that this race truly goes beyond South Texas.\par \par \uc2 The last thing we want is to hold on to a slim Democratic majority and then have someone like \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  who`s going to keep signing with Republicans, not just on this issue, but he`s done it on things like the PRO Act. He`s voted to you know fund the border wall. I mean, has an A rating from the \uc2 NRA\uc2 . There are so many key issues where he`s always siding with Republicans. And he could become the \uc2 Joe Manchin\uc2  of the House.\par \par \uc2 We don`t want \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  to be the deciding vote on the future of our fundamental freedoms and rights in this country. We just can`t risk that. And if you`re watching this, and you want to help us out, we`re 19 days away from our election, and we need all the support we can get.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I -- it is not -- I`m sure it`s not lost. I knew that you`ve seen in some of these primary matchups that have put the progressive wing and the establishments against each other. The establishment has come out on top. You saw that in Ohio. How do you plan to buck that trend?\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: I mean, again, we`re getting closer and closer. We were -- there was just a one percentage point difference in our race during the March 1 Democratic primary. We know this is a winnable race. And we`ve seen it happen before. I mean, Murray Newman and \uc2 Cori Bush\uc2  did it last cycle, and we`re planning on doing it this cycle as well.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you say to \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  who says that he -- it`d be he clearly is endorsing Cuellar because he thinks Cuellar can hold the seat. And there`s concern that you cannot hold the seat. That you`re going to be considered too progressive for this district. Make your case.\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: Sure. People in this district aren`t voting for me because I am progressive. They`re voting for me because I`m putting forth policies that are actually going to enact change in this district. This district, for example, my hometown has a 30 percent poverty rate. We`ve historically had very low rates of people that are insured. I mean, people are coming out and voting for us and are you know, giving this entrenched incumbent the rest of his life because we need change.\par \par \uc2 [13:30:00]\par \par \uc2 And I plan on delivering on that. And I really hope that the Democratic leadership doesn`t stand in the way of the change that South Texans want to see. There are so many people rallying behind this campaign. We`ve been able to nickel and dime our way to -- with support, with votes, with donations, and go toe-to-toe with this very entrenched corporate incumbent. I guess it just shows the power that we have here in South Texas. And this is the kind of race where we`re centering people, we are centering the campaign around people as well with our voter outreach efforts to keep this district safely blue in this midterm election.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Very quickly, if you get elected, President Biden may be calling you and saying, hey, you`re representing folks on the border. What do they want in a border policy? What do they want when it comes to Title 42? What would you tell them?\par \par \uc2 CISNEROS: To repeal this Trump relic. It`s not surprising that \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  is supporting keeping Title 42 because he`s known as Trump`s favorite Democrat. He voted with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  70 percent of the time during Trump`s administration. So it`s not a surprise that he`s supporting, you know, keeping Title 42 instead of repealing it.\par \par \uc2 I am an immigration and human rights attorney. I was born and raised in this border district in my hometown of Loredo. I can put forth, I`m excited once elected to shape our border policy in a way that takes into consideration the joys and the challenges of living here on the border. So whenever we do win, I am more than happy to take that call from President Biden and help.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jessica Cisneros, one of the two Democratic candidates in the 28th Congressional District, it`s a run-off that we will have later this month. Appreciate you coming on and sharing your perspective with us.\par \par \uc2 Up next, as the U.S. passes that grim milestone of one million COVID deaths, one lawmaker in Capitol Hill is pushing for more research into treat long COVID. The Democrat of Virginia, Senator Tim Kaine, who himself suffers from long COVID, joins me to discuss that and a lot more.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:35:35]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Senate Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is promising to hold a vote on the Women`s Health Protection Act. It`s a bill that would codify Roe v. Wade despite the incredibly slim odd it passes a 50-50 Senate. This is on top of all the other legislative priorities the Senate is passing, passing the president`s Ukraine aide, request securing most voted fund and then passing a budget.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is increasing interest rates as Americans and markets continue to worry about rising inflation, another pocketbook issue.\par \par \uc2 So for more on this, I`m joined by Democratic senator from Virginia, Tim Kaine.\par \par \uc2 Senator, I want to start with, look, when you first got into politics, you were a self-described pro-life Democrat, but there was sort of this -- I would call, it was personally pro-life and maybe legally pro-choice, and I guess that probably would describe your position. Is that fair?\par \par \uc2 SEN. TIM KAINE (D-VA): It is, Chuck. I think about 30 percent of Americans have the position I do. We`re personally pro-life and we live that way, but we think people ought to be able to make their own reproductive decisions. And so, it`s a huge swath of the American public even if it`s not necessarily that much are represented position in Congress.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes, is this -- you know, it feels like this is something that I see already \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  isn`t going to vote for this version of the bill because there`s some fine -- piece of fine print that she thinks is not protected there. Is this something that politically is even possible to get 51 people to agree on what the -- I mean, it feels like -- it seems impossible that you guys are going to agree on a floor?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, I think to get 60 votes right now in the Senate is virtually impossible, Chuck, because the Republicans want to overrule Roe vs. Wade. There are a couple of exceptions, \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  and Lisa Murkowski, but most of them want to return to pre-Roe, where states get to decide if they want to criminalize women`s reproductive health care decisions. So we can`t get to 60. You might be able to see us get over 50 for some version of a clear statement that, you know, Griswold, Roe and Casey are good law and Americans have a constitutional right to make their own reproductive health care decisions. So we`re going to -- we`re going to make the effort to do that beginning next week.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I want to get -- I want you to share your story in a few minutes about your fight with long COVID, but I want to ask quickly about the economy. I had a pretty high-level Democrat over the weekend say to me, I wish the White House had a inflation czar the way they have a COVID czar, and get out there and sort of acknowledge that, yes, we are dealing with inflation. This is not politically caused. This was -- you know, we got a lot of reasons for it.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: But here`s what we`re doing every day to work on it, and apparently this idea was shot down by the White House. What do you think of it?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, Chuck, it reminds me, I`m older than you but I think it was Nixon or \uc2 Ford\uc2  that had the whip inflation now buttons, the wind buttons that people would wear.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: And I can`t remember if they had a czar, but they made it a front and center priority. I think that explains what the Fed is doing with interest rates. We have an economy that job growth is strong, wage growth is strong, GDP growth has been pretty robust, and I think it`s going to continue to be, but we do have an inflation worry. There are things we can do in Congress.\par \par \uc2 For example, if we were able to pass a reconciliation bill that would bring down childcare and health care cost, we could really help people with two big pocketbook items. But I also think that what the Fed is doing under Jay Powell`s leadership is trying to, without choking off the positive growth that we`re seeing in jobs especially.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Can we narrow down and reduce the inflation challenge.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Tell me what it`s like to live with long COVID? What is it like today?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Yes, so it`s like today, I mean, even as I am talking to you, I got COVID when we were passing the CARES Act more than two years ago, and it was early, and I got hit with non-standard symptoms but one of them was this -- like a light switch went off and all my nerve started to tingle. It`s like I`ve got, you know, my skin dipped in an Alka-Seltzer that`s fizzing. And it never has gone away. So my other symptoms went away quick, this fizzing sensation, others are experiencing it. It`s not painful. It doesn`t stop me from working or exercising or sleeping. But it`s just weird and it`s 24/7 and it`s been going for two years, and at this point I guess I kind of have to assume it`s probably never going away.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:02]\par \par \uc2 TODD: What kind of -- you know, and I`m sure you`re probably more aware of how many people have similar stories as yours, how many have you come across and what kind of medical research has gone into this?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: So, Chuck, tons of people, as soon as I shared my story, you know, my office has become a bit of a magnet for people who want to share theirs. And I decided to share my story after about six or eight months. I`ve been to a neurologist. I kind of felt like I understood what was going on, but I was running into others with much more serious symptoms who are not being believed.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: And when they were not being believed, I thought, you know, if I am on the committee and I say hey, look, I believe you because I -- it`s real, then that would give them some comfort but then I can also on the committee try to drive toward solutions. So here`s what`s going on, in the American Rescue Plan, we put more than $1 billion into research for causes and cures. And interestingly, Chuck, this one, we may know cures before we know causes.\par \par \uc2 So for example, my nerve tingling thing, there are other cures for nerve tingling not related to COVID, well, maybe those cures could work for people who have it connected to COVID. People with long COVID often have racing heart, irregular heart rates, well, there`s other cures for that. Would that work for somebody who got that because of COVID?\par \par \uc2 So we`re doing research but the Long COVID Act that I put in recently is sort of four big pillars, gather patient data, more research into causes and cures, spread the information to providers, employers and families, but then also let`s provide support for those, for example, who might be so debilitated that they actually are disabled under Social Security or they need an accommodation to be able to work.\par \par \uc2 And so I am very focused on this because I am living with it, and dealing with it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: But there are many who are really suffering under it and we owe them answers and we owe them treatments.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, you talked about getting medical professionals to believe their patients. You`re almost describing what many times people who get Lyme disease go through. Right? Their doctors are like, we can`t find it. We can`t find it.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Absolutely.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do you make sure insurance companies don`t deny benefits based on these symptoms that again are not as well-known yet?\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Well, Chuck, it`s a really good question. And you`re right. The research that we`re going to do is going to answer some questions not just about long COVID but about other conditions that may have followed viral infections because Lyme disease and others often have a tail of neurological and other consequences. We want to research that, too.\par \par \uc2 But you`re right, the coverage issues are fascinating. What if you got COVID at the start of COVID before there was a lot of testing and so you never really got a test, but you did have COVID? Then you`ve been vaccinated, so now you have antibodies in your system, but somebody could say well, those antibodies, they are from your vaccination, they don`t prove you had COVID.\par \par \uc2 So making a claim for insurance, establishing a disability, asking an employer for an accommodation to help you work consistent with what you can do. These are all thorny questions, and that`s what the fourth pillar of my bill would do, patient support to help folks who have been through these tough two years. We just passed a million deaths. I mean, my god, who would have ever believed that two years ago.\par \par \uc2 But it`s millions more that are going to be not dying of COVID but having to live the rest of their lives with long COVID. And I think this bill which does, I think have some significant bipartisan support, particularly on the funding side, you know, can help us provide some of that support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, look, I have seen your interviews on this, and I felt as if the more people pay attention to this, I think you`re right, people may have been shamed by doctors saying they are imagining things and things like that, you`re not somebody that does this. This is happening to you. I appreciate you coming on and sharing your story, Senator.\par \par \uc2 KAINE: Absolutely. Thank you, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. Senator Tim Kaine, Democrat from Virginia.\par \par \uc2 Up next, the bloody battles are raging in Mariupol, as the U.S. denies giving Ukraine intel with the singular intent of killing Russian generals. Where we now stand on day 71 of the war in Ukraine. That`s ahead.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:47:20]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Any moment now we expect to hear from the Pentagon spokesperson, John Kirby. That briefing comes as "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " is reporting that the U.S. is providing specific intelligence that`s helping Ukraine target and kill Russian generals. According to "The Times," the targeting is part of a classified effort by the Biden administration to provide real-time battlefield intelligence to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 NBC News cannot confirm that reporting and the National Security Council has decided to put out a statement that calls the headline of the story misleading and it`s framing irresponsible. The spokesperson for the NSC Adrienne Watson goes on to write, "The United States provides battlefield intelligence to help the Ukrainians defend their country. We do not provide intelligence with the intent to kill Russian generals."\par \par \uc2 All this comes as intense fighting had continued at the steel plant in Mariupol with the last remaining Ukrainian forces in that city have refused to surrender. I`m going to speak with the former deputy commander of the United States European Command after this break.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:51:53]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. Joining me now is retired Lieutenant General Steph Twitty. He`s a former deputy commander of the United States European Command. He`s now an NBC News military analyst.\par \par \uc2 I got to start with the news about whether we`re providing intelligence to target Russia generals. It is -- it feels like the statement from the government is a bit of a distinction without a difference basically saying, look, that isn`t the motivation for this intelligence sharing, it`s about helping Ukraine in the battlefield but targeting generals, if the Ukrainians are doing this, there`s a motive to this, is there not?\par \par \uc2 LT. GEN. STEPH TWITTY (RET.), U.S. EUROPEAN COMMAND Well, Chuck, first of all, I would tell you I would have to agree with the government. I`ve been a recipient of this intelligence for 40 years having served in the military and what the U.S. is doing I must say is unprecedented because normally we share our intelligence with our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies and, as you know, our Five Eyes partners. So opening up this intelligence and serving -- helping the Ukrainians, that is different and something that I have not seen in my 40 years in the military.\par \par \uc2 But what I will tell you is it is making a difference on the battlefield obviously for us to share that intelligence, whether it`s going after the command-and-control systems is what I would think that the Ukrainians are doing and as you know most of the command and control and operation centers, most of those have generals in them. And so in a round-about way, yes, you`re probably killing some generals by going after command-and- control systems, but they`re also going after other things, whether it be logistics or the artillery or the missile systems.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: So I would put this in a greater context of they`re going after things to really cripple and weaken the Russian military.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let`s look at this from the flip side, General. Being former head of the European Command, I`m sure you thought you knew everything there was to know about the Russian military. Are you surprised at certainly how poorly their leadership structure has performed?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Not really, Chuck. I`ve spent a lot of time in Ukraine. My first tour in Ukraine training and assisting the Ukrainian forces was in the 1996 and I saw then the Ukrainians themselves supports the wall had come down a couple of years prior but the Ukrainians were still under the old Soviet system of training and fighting, and throughout that time, those three months I spent in Ukraine, I had U.S. forces there and they are amazed at the autonomy, the accountability, the morale that the U.S. service members bring to training and to the fight.\par \par \uc2 When you took a look at the Ukrainians back then, it was the senior leaders leading operations where we had noncommission officers leading operations.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:09]\par \par \uc2 And so I saw a vast difference in the way U.S. soldiers fought and the way Russian-type, Ukrainian soldiers fought during that time frame. And I will tell you --\par \par \uc2 TODD: So --\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Go ahead, Chuck.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So it sounds like, General, it`s inevitable that Russia was going to have a morale problem the second they essentially hit a hurdle?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Well, it`s more than just a training. First of all, the Russian soldiers need to know why they`re fighting. And as you know, you`ve heard the stories, they don`t quite know why they`re fighting. And when you call it a special operation, that is not telling your soldiers in honesty what they`re going into battle for. They need to know why they`re fighting, and they need to have the will to fight.\par \par \uc2 That carries a long way when you`re dealing with combat operations. Don`t underestimate the will of the fight. And part of it is their leadership being honest with their soldiers so they`ll no why they`re fighting.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you believe, knowing what you know about the Ukrainian military, that they can win this war with everything we`re providing them, or do you think we`re going to need to do a little bit more to get them over the top?\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: Yes, I think we`re going to have to do a little bit more. You probably heard, this is a battle of wills, this is a battle of attrition. And what you`re seeing now in the east you`re seeing that attrition start to fall out here, where the Russians will win a battle, a small battle, that you come back, and you wake up the next day and Ukrainians have taken back over that particular city or that particular ground support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 TWITTY: It will continue to slog through this unless we get some overwhelming combat power to the Ukrainians for this fight to make a difference.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right, General Twitty, I`ve got to leave it there. It`s a pleasure to get your expertise on the air, so thank you.\par \par \uc2 And thank you all for being with us this hour. We`ll be back tomorrow with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. MSNBC`s coverage will continue with my friend Chris Jansing after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220506ei5500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE 11TH HOUR for May 4, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Stephanie Ruhle, Cal Perry \par \uc2 7,979 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The 11th Hour with Brian Williams\par \uc2 BRIWIL\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 LAWRENCE O`DONNELL, MSNBC HOST: Kevin McCarthy, once again, gets tonight`s LAST WORD. "THE 11TH HOUR" with Stephanie Ruhle starts now.\par \par \uc2 STEPHANIE RUHLE, MSNBC HOST: Tonight, a potential reversal on Roe reshaping the midterms. The President raises the alarm on what could happen next. Will that be enough to get voters to the polls?\par \par \uc2 Plus, more hypocrisy caught on tape. The newly released recording of Kevin McCarthy`s harsh words for the former President and wondering whether to remove him from office.\par \par \uc2 And in Ukraine, fierce fighting outside that steel plant in Mariupol, while some civilians that still remain trapped, those who escaped share their harrowing stories, as THE 11TH HOUR we`ll get underway on this Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, once again, I`m Stephanie Ruhle. House lawmakers investigating the Capitol riot have now heard from one of the highest profile witnesses so far, the former president`s eldest son \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Jr. According to reports he appeared voluntarily before the January 6 committee yesterday. NBC News says he spent two hours with the panel via zoom. One source describing the session is pretty uneventful. He`s the latest Trump family member to testify. \uc2 Ivanka Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Jared Kushner\uc2  and Don Jr.`s fiance, Kimberly Guilfoyle have all spoken previously to the committee.\par \par \uc2 Also tonight, there`s brand new audio of House Republican Leader Kevin McCarthy, recorded on a call with other leaders of his caucus, just two days after the riot, it was obtained by the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 . In it, McCarthy called Trump`s actions atrocious, and was looking for a reason to talk to President-elect, Biden.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 KEVIN MCCARTHY, HOUSE REPUBLICAN LEADER: What the President did was atrocious and totally wrong. From the standpoint, we`re 12 days away. I mean, the one point I`d make with Biden -- if you have an impeachment and you`re stuck sitting in the Senate, and he needs cabinet members, he`s got Secretary of Defense, he`s got a lot of things he`s got to have moving. And, if you think from a perspective, you put everything else away, this country is very, very divided. Impeachment divides the nation further and continues to fight even greater. That`s why I want to reach out to Biden, I wanted the President to meet with Biden, but that`s not going to happen.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: That phone conversation also included a mention of the 25th amendment to remove Trump from office.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY AIDE: I think the options that have been cited by the Democrats so far are the 25th Amendment, which is not exactly an elegant solution here.\par \par \uc2 MCCARTHY: That takes too long too. It could go back to the House, right?\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Meanwhile, protests and rallies continue in front of the Supreme Court after the leak of that draft opinion that would overturn Roe vs. Wade. Today, President Biden again warned that a whole range of privacy rights could now be under threat.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , (D) U.S. PRESIDENT: This is about a lot more than abortion. What are the next things that are going to be attacked? Because this mega crowd is really the most extreme political organization that`s existed in American history, in recent American history.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Senator Majority Leader \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  is preparing to hold a vote on a bill that would protect abortion rights nationwide. But Senate Democrats admit the odds of getting it passed slim to none.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. DEBBIE STABENOW, (D) MICHIGAN: The House has passed the Women`s Health Protection Act already. And we would love to pass it in the Senate. We have something called a filibuster, which means you have to have at least 10 Republicans that are joining us. This is going to be pretty tough to get to 60. So the question is whether or not we could get an exemption or not. That only happens if women across the country are weighing in with their senators and both Republicans and Democrats.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Earlier today Republican Senator Lisa Murkowski of Alaska was asked about that bill.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. LISA MURKOWSKI, (R) ALASKA: I did not support it last time because I felt it was over expensive. If there is something new that is on the table today. Obviously, we take a look at that.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Schumer is hoping to hold that vote sometime next week.\par \par \uc2 We got a lot to cover. So let`s bring in our experts Jackie Alemany, Congressional Investigations Reporter for \uc2 The Washington Post\uc2  and an MSNBC Contributor, Yamiche Alcindor, NBC News Correspondent and Moderator of Washington Week on \uc2 PBS\uc2 . And former U.S. Attorney Joyce Vance, she spent 25 years as a federal prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 Jackie, let`s start with Don Jr. What does the committee want to get from him? And then let`s get real. What are they likely going to get from him?\par \par \uc2 JACKIE ALEMANY, THE WASHINGTON POST POLITICAL REPORTER: Yes, Stephanie. Don Jr. is the latest member and the closest person really to the former president that was interviewed by the select committee investigating January 6 and they`re interested in speaking with him for a number of reasons.\par \par \uc2 [23:05:11]\par \par \uc2 One, as is already publicly reported and acknowledged by the Committee. He was one of the characters throughout that period leading up to January 6, that was testing \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  in touch with Meadows and others about some of the plots to overturn the results of the election. But more importantly, perhaps he was one of the witnesses who was actually in the Oval Office. According to a document that was released by the select committee earlier this year on the morning of January 6, with Trump and his top aides.\par \par \uc2 He had arrived at the White House with Kimberly Guilfoyle and a handful of others according to this document, that the committee was able to receive for records requests. And it was during this time period that Trump made that call to Pence that Don Jr. Would have potentially witness where he was making a final effort to pressure Pence to overturn the results of the election. It`s all but, you know, certain that the committee was looking for Don Jr.`s firsthand take on what that call was like, what he witnessed and the disposition and attitude of his father and then conversations that he had with his dad in the aftermath, especially as he was one of the people who was trying to urgently get in touch with \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  to ask his dad to condemn this, not repeated for television.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joyce, let`s talk about this new leaked audio of Kevin McCarthy and remind our audience while Republicans are apoplectic about this leak from the Supreme Court, it was Republicans that taped Kevin McCarthy and have now leaked out that audio, as much as it might infuriate \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to hear Kevin McCarthy say these things, anything on those tapes that are going to impact the committee`s investigation?\par \par \uc2 JOYCE VANCE, FORMER U.S. ATTORNEY: The tapes are remarkable, because it`s almost like they`re two Kevin McCarthys, and on the tapes, we hear the good Kevin McCarthy, the elected official who understood what the right thing to do was. But again, we know that in the real world, Kevin McCarthy never had the courage to do it, never had the courage to lead his party in a way that could have brought the country back together.\par \par \uc2 And we also hear even on the tape, Stephanie, just to be frank, someone who`s more interested in positioning the party politically in a way that permits it to forget events that took place two days earlier. This is on the eighth, he`s already talking about meeting with Biden trying to move forward and forget January 6, but these tapes buttress the committee`s argument for hearing from McCarthy, it`s clear that he`s privy to conversations, that he has relationships with people that there is information that he can shed, most importantly on whether the former president truly understood that he had lost the election. Because as you`ll recall, Trump`s intent and his knowledge is very central in all of this. It defies belief and a judge in California has already ruled that Trump knew he had lost the election and was in fact trying to steal it himself. McCarthy`s testimony could shed further light on that.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yamiche, you are down in Louisiana, one of the 13 states with those trigger laws that will ban abortion. If the Supreme Court overturns Roe, what are women they`re telling you.\par \par \uc2 YAMICHE ALCINDOR, PBS NEWSHOUR WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Women here in the south are telling me that they are devastated and gutted by the idea that Roe v. Wade is poised to be overturned. I`ve been talking to women who are the head of these abortion clinics both in Louisiana here in Shreveport, Louisiana, which is one of only three clinics in a state that has been inundated by the way with women fleeing and going from Texas, which has an abortion ban starting at six weeks. And also, I talked to the director of the only abortion clinic in Mississippi who says she has no faith that Congress is going to be able to help women. I also interviewed this remarkable woman today, her name is Lillian Newton, she has had three abortions. And she talked to me about what life would be like if she was not able to access that health care. Take a listen to what she told me.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LILLIAN NEWTON, PATIENT ADVOCATE: Had I not had abortion access when I was at the age of 24, 25 debating my life path, I would have been completely lost. It would have had a negative impact on almost every aspect of my life. Mental Health was specifically I don`t know how I would have made it through an unplanned pregnancy while managing my mental health. I don`t know if I would have made it through an unplanned pregnancy, managing my mental health. I physically was not healthy enough to carry a child and was not making the best life decisions at the time.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: Now, she tells me that she grew up protesting at the very clinic where she got her abortions. And by the way, Stephanie, she is now working at that very clinic because she said that clinic really, she said saved her life, saved the -- saved her from really having to go through a pregnancy that just she said she did not want. And she told me that she`s really worried that these trigger alarms that are going to go in effect are really going to lock women in this whole region outside of abortion here.\par \par \uc2 [23:10:22]\par \par \uc2 If this law goes into effect, if Roe is overturned, women can have to drive as much as 11 or 12 hours to states like Illinois, and Maryland from Louisiana, and Mississippi to access abortion right. So a very, very concerning situation here.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Mental Health, what about financial health, Yamiche, 75% of women who get abortions are living at or below the poverty line? If a place like Louisiana puts in place a ban? Are they then going to offer accompanying financial support to help women, pregnant women carry to term, it`s very expensive to have a baby?\par \par \uc2 ALCINDOR: I mean, that is a key question because all of the clinics that I that I went to, the directors there, the women who are leading those clinics say that they are overwhelmingly servicing women who are living below the poverty line that they are servicing women of color who simply cannot afford to raise a child that they did not want to have. And I should also tell you that Lillian who was brave enough to go on camera and tell her story, she said that she felt privileged that she was able to access abortion care, didn`t have to wait in line because that very clinic right now it has a waiting list of over 300 women, Stephanie. So right now when you try to get an abortion in the state, you can`t just walk into the office, you are going to be waiting through a number of obstacles before you can access that care.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So Jackie, Senate Democrats are planning a vote on abortion protections next week. Help me out here. Why is it a slim to none chance they will get something passed? The majority of Americans, not majority of Democrats, the majority of Americans support upholding Roe, why can`t Congress get something done?\par \par \uc2 ALEMANY: There is a creeping and quiet recognition that Democrats really do have limited tools at their disposal to actually do something from a legislative perspective here. Congress could of course guarantee abortion access if they`re able to pass this bill next week nationwide by codifying Roe v. Wade protections into law. But the conversation that we`ve had time and time again, when it comes to getting anything done in Congress is that because Democrats have a razor thin majority, and that to Democrats, in particular, Joe Manchin and \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  have made it clear that they`re still not in favor of eliminating the filibuster, that it would require 60 votes to get any laws to codify Roe v. Wade, ultimately pass. There have been some alternative discussions taking place in the White House on the Hill about potential executive orders or regulatory actions that the White House could take. But then there`s a concern that if Republicans do ultimately take back Congress, they`re going over the presidency, eventually, they either would, in return, nuke the filibuster in order to overturn a lot of the changes made or in these Republican controlled states, enact laws and wage legal battles to undo and reverse some of the executive actions that the White House could potentially take. There`s some other various mechanisms that White House officials are currently exploring. But again, a lot of these are -- a lot of these options right now are a longshot.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: But beyond Democrats, Jackie, Republicans in swing states in suburban America, they`re not prepared to protect abortion rights?\par \par \uc2 ALEMANY: You know, it is actually a conversation that you`re -- I wonder if the public is noticing House Republicans, many of them are reticent to weigh in on because as you said, a majority of Americans, including some Republicans don`t want this to happen. And had had always thought that it was had been putting it off time and time again. Two have the conservatives on the January 6 Select Committee have conspicuously not weighed in on this issue either. This is something that a lot of Republicans don`t want to touch. And it just so is politically convenient that at least on the House side, Republicans don`t have as much of a say right now whether anything gets done, but I doubt we`re going to see much Republican support here outside of maybe \uc2 Susan Collins\uc2  or Lisa Murkowski, who have so far expressed some disillusionment with the process and disappointment with justices Kavanaugh and Gorsuch who, in their opinion had made it clear that that they would not be in favor of this actually happening in the courts, and they could potentially support this. But again, that`s still not 10 votes from Republicans that it would take to pass this through without eliminating the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, you can`t hide and say nothing if \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2  brings it to a vote.\par \par \uc2 Joyce, again, President Biden warned that this draft ruling puts privacy rights at risk. We`ve heard about the threat it could mean to same sex marriage. What else could be impacted?\par \par \uc2 [23:15:09]\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Well, because abortion rights rests on this rubric of due process and privacy rights and their other rights that the court has enforced based on that same sort of analysis, the thinking is that everything on that branch of the substantive due process tree could ultimately be at risk. And the primary targets, Stephanie, has to be contraception. There`s a Supreme Court case, Griswold v. Connecticut that made it legal. This is sort of shocking to think back to that in our lifetime. It wasn`t legal for a married couple to access contraception. But it`s Griswold v. Connecticut, the case that does that. In her confirmation hearing Justice, Amy Coney Barrett, declined to say that she believed that Griswold was good law, that could be squarely in the sight of Republican activists who may try to bring a test case and see if that can be the next one of our cherished rights that will fall.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Joyce, before we go, a judge has denied Donald Trump`s request to suspend that $10,000 a day fine that he`s been having to pay for the last week or so because he wouldn`t give up documents. So in theory, he`s been racking up this bill since April 26. But what`s the likelihood Trump`s actually paying any of this?\par \par \uc2 VANCE: Probably slim to none at this point, Stephanie, of course, this is the case that the New York Attorney General is pursuing. It`s a civil action looking into Trump`s business practices. And he was supposed to turn over documents. In fact, his lawyer had stipulated that he would do that in late April, come the deadline, they turned over nothing said that they didn`t have anything and objected to the subpoenas, which they`d already agreed to comply with. So the judge imposed this fine, although the New York Attorney General, at least to date hasn`t acknowledged that she`s collecting, she will be entitled to these sorts of civil contempt actions are meant to coerce testimony in this case to coerce the documents out of a party. And as long as Trump continues not to turn them over, the Attorney General has a right to go to court to enforce this order. And this judge who I think is angry based on judge and groans order where he says it`s sort of remarkable that that, you know, Trump has this obligation to detail how and why and who searched for these documents, and to certify that they came up empty handed, and they did none of that.\par \par \uc2 Back on April 26, his lawyer said she`d file an affidavit under oath explaining what happened. That still hasn`t been filed in court, as far as I`m aware., Stephanie. So I think the President -- the former president is in trouble on this one.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Yet another day, same story. Trump doesn`t give up his documents. Trump doesn`t pay up, but he needs to.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to leave it there ladies, Jackie Alemany, Yamiche Alcindor, and Joyce Vance, thank you for starting us off this evening.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, as more civilians escaped Mariupol, Russia now targeting critical sites that keep Ukraine`s lights on and the trains running.\par \par \uc2 And later, a once unthinkable milestone has arrived, 1 million COVID deaths in the United States. We`ll look back on over two years of a pandemic with a frontline doctor. THE 11TH HOUR, just getting underway on a Wednesday night.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:23:06]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE (through translation): You can`t imagine how scary it is when you sit in the shelter in a wet and damp basement, which is bouncing, shaking.\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: What did your father tell you when you left? What did he say to you?\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE (through translation): He said we`d meet each other soon.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: It`s hard to hear that. They are just some of the hundreds of civilians who have now been evacuated from the steel plant in Mariupol, Ukraine, but many more may still be trapped there as the Russian attack gets even more intense. Our own Cal Perry following today`s developments from Kyiv, he joins us live. Cal, we are now going into day 71, Mariupol has been described as decimated, obliterated, but still there are civilians there. What`s the latest?\par \par \uc2 CAL PERRY, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: So the latest is that steel plant, which is actually a compound, it`s a complex, it`s a number of buildings, not one building. According to the mayor, Russian troops have broken into that complex. They`re inside the outer perimeter that existed there. And they are fighting just above where the civilians are.\par \par \uc2 At one point today, the government lost communications with the people inside that steel plant. So it`s possible that what you saw there from our colleague, Matt Bradley, interviewing the civilians, those could be the last civilians to make it out of there. That may have been the last window. Russia has actually started pulling back some of its troops from Mariupol. I think there`s real concern that this could be it. This could be the end of that last sort of stand out. And what happens to those civilians, we just don`t know. We know there are also hundreds of wounded Ukrainian troops in and around the basement of that steel plant. What happens to them? We just simply don`t know as Russian forces now look like they`re completing their takeover of the entire city. Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Talk about this reporting we`re hearing about Ukrainian trains being targeted, possibly, the electricity grid?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Yeah, so look, yesterday we had over 18 cruise missiles fired at targets across the center, and western part of the country including five drones in addition to those missiles. They struck, as you said railway targets, not just the stations but the tracks.\par \par \uc2 [23:25:09]\par \par \uc2 There were then 14 trains delayed sitting there, unable to go anywhere. That`s a pretty obvious message that Russia is done with weapons, making their way to the front via rail. The rail system here has kept alive. I think the supply chains to the eastern part of the country to that front. Well, Russia has done a pretty good job of taking out at least a dozen or so of those targets. Then in addition to that, you have these power substations that were taken out, so the power in Lviv was out for most of the past 24 hours, it`s starting to come back, but spotty in places. But again, that grid is going to be quickly overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, when you talk about the 7.7 million people who were internally displaced, almost 15% to 20% of them are trying to make their way home. Well, they`re coming home not just to oftentimes bodies buried in their gardens and their homes completely destroyed. But they`re coming back now to an infrastructure that cannot support them, a place that doesn`t have food, water or power, to be able to support people who are trying to return to their home. So you get sort of a picture of how Russia is not just killing people on the front here, but making it a livable for the people who remain.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Cal, you have been covering this war since the beginning. Last week, when President Zelenskyy was on the cover of Time Magazine, it talked about one of the most important fights he`s in, the fight to keep this war in the attention of the West, to have the Western media continue to focus. But look at the last two days, when Roe versus Wade headlines crossed, Americans quickly shifted Ukraine into the rearview mirror, you are a reporter on the ground. Tell us why it is so important that we cannot lose interest there.\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Because the losers of this war are the Ukrainian people, are civilians who are being killed in this country who are not necessarily on the front. And there are a great deal of Ukrainian -- Ukrainians dying on the front, people who weren`t soldiers, people who were just civilians one day, and then soldiers the next. And young Russian soldiers who may not have known what they were doing here, who may have been lied to. And we`re hearing those stories. I mean, the humanitarian catastrophe that`s going to play out here in Europe, and in places like Africa, where there just won`t be the food supplies because of what`s happened here in Ukraine. It is for all those reasons and more.\par \par \uc2 You hear the Ukrainian President say, he wants leaders to arrive here, not with cakes, not with presents, but with real tangible things like weapons. This is a man who spoke to the last American President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and told him we need weapons to fight the Russians, and was not just sort of laughed at, but it became an international incident. And that call became a piece of American and Ukrainian history. Well, now this country is at war and Europe is at war with Russia. And NATO`s question about his existence is now coming up. And you have a steel factory where there are children in the basement and the Pope is begging for both sides to stop.\par \par \uc2 My point is, a long way of answering your question, none of these things are going to change in the next month, in the next few months. And if President Zelenskyy is right, and we go to this slow, cold war in the east, none of that`s going to change. But the interest needs to stay the same if civilians here are going to be able to rebuild their lives, and frankly, there`s going to be a push for peace. The interest has to remain the same. Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Are you worried that the WEST will lose interest? You`ve just experienced the last two days of us probably not calling on you for hits all day long on MSNBC, like we had been for the last six weeks?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: Yeah, not just the West America. I mean, I think we`ve seen how American foreign policy can change on a dime. It will be my question, one day, if I get to speak to President Zelenskyy, I`ve been asking government officials, do you worry that -- that the dedication that America is showing is not going to be there in the future? And of course they do. They were a great deal. They see the midterms coming in the United States. I mean, they see the political landscape and how it can change. And again, for people here for Ukrainians, this is a fight for their lives. This is a fight for their homes. I spent most of the day yesterday touring an area outside the capitol that was completely destroyed, again, where bodies are still buried on the side of the road. And so for these people, this is about their existence. This is about their future. And so yes, people here are worried and again, they want long term commitments.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, you know this better than anybody else, your relies on Russian gas, right? So if interest wanes here, what happens with that? Is Europe able to stand up Ukraine? People here are very afraid of that absolutely.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, Cal Perry, I am grateful that you are there. I am grateful for what you do. Thank you and please stay safe.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, the battle for Ohio`s all important Senate seat taking shape. We`ll ask Michael Steele and Juanita Tolliver if Democrats can sway some of those Republicans who voted against JD Vance, there was a lot of them, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:34:14]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. \uc2 TIM RYAN\uc2 , (D) OHIO: \uc2 Matt Dolan\uc2 , who was the anti-Trump candidate in the Republican primary, got 23% of the vote. He said the election was legitimate. He didn`t go just Trump`s ring. And he got 23% of the vote and those voters are going to be \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  voters, we`re going hard after them.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Democratic Congressman \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  now running against Donald Trump`s pick in the Ohio Senate primary JD Vance, but in a crowded field, that endorsement still earned Vance less than a third of the vote, twice as many Republican primary voters cast their ballots for somebody else. So the question now, who gets their vote next?\par \par \uc2 With us tonight to discuss Juanita Tolliver, a Veteran Political Strategist to progressive and causes. And Michael Steele, former Chairman of the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  and former Lieutenant Governor of Maryland.\par \par \uc2 [23:35:06]\par \par \uc2 Michael, could \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  be right? He might not get all 23% of that vote. But could he get votes from Republicans who voted against Vance?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL STEELE, FORMER RNC CHAIRMAN: Sure. I mean, he`s going to have to work it, though. I mean, the -- you know, it doesn`t translate one for one from a primary -- from a Republican primary, which largely has hard, right, Senator, right, sometimes voters. So he`s going to have to work in edge those voters closer to himself. So it`s going to matter how he sounds on a lot of issues in this race. And keep in mind that, you know, Dolan had the benefit of a little bit of a surge at the end there. But again, what were those voters who -- what is the composition those voters were -- why didn`t they swing towards Mandel or and certainly JD Vance. And so that if he can figure that little part of the formula out, I think he can pull a good portion of those voters to his side, and make that mad dash against JD. Because JD is not the popular candidate in this this race. He wasn`t in the primary. But for that last minute endorsement, Mandel probably would have kicked him out and beat him.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, Trump may have put them over the top, but he certainly didn`t -- you wouldn`t call Trump a kingmaker, not in this case, and not in Michigan, Juanita. We saw an upset there when a Democrat won a congressional district only -- this is the only ever time, this has ever happened. It has always been held by a Republican. Now, of course, the Republican chevy has said some extremely offensive things about rape. Listen to this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED MALE: You know, having three daughters and I tell my daughters well, if rape is inevitable, you should just lie back and enjoy it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Lie back and enjoy it. Is this a warning for the GOP, some of those fringe candidates are just too much even for their voters?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Absolutely, Stephanie. And you saw the women`s reactions. And so in the moment that he said it, because it is that disgusting, that ignorance and just harmful. And it just shows that if you call out this type of behavior, voters are going to respond to it. And that`s what Carol Glanville did, she walked the line where she raised the tension about that statement. She called it out for what it was, and challenged him in real time. But once she had the attention of Republican voters and voters in general, she pivoted back to the issues that matter to them. Her platform was about the economy. It was about jobs. It was about education.\par \par \uc2 But what she did in standing up for what is right and standing up for against that statement resonated with voters on both sides of the aisle. They want to see her punch back. And honestly, I think that`s the lesson for all Democrats, you cannot ignore the harmful statements, the conspiracy theories, the lies coming from the right, you need to challenge it in real time and then pivot again back to the issues that matter to voters.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Mr. Steele, assuming it holds what kind of Supreme Court decision reversing Roe mean for Republicans in swing districts, right? The flavor Republicans you used to be.\par \par \uc2 STEELE: Oh, let me tell you, there are a whole lot of angina going on in D.C. Let me tell you, \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  is not a happy camper. This was not the conversation he wanted Republicans to have over the summer and going into the fall. It was not what Republicans in swing districts want to have to go stand in front of a very, very an agitated, angry and animated crowd of women and men on this issue.\par \par \uc2 So that, you know, you saw it yesterday, when McConnell stood at the podium, and like Peter, on Holy Thursday night denied anything related to the subject, you know, at hand, it`s like, no, I, you know, abortion, what abortion law, what law, you know, and you know, we`re just going to worry about what happened in the league, and the country`s like, nah, that`s not the conversation we`re having right now.\par \par \uc2 And so Republicans are going to have to figure out how to pivot into some kind of answer here. Because you now have you -- you`ve gotten your dream. You know, this has been a an effort in the making for a long, long time. I`ve been in a lot of those meetings. So now you`re here, and a lot of us at the time was like, OK, what about childcare? What about supporting these women? What about programs that they`re going to need? If you say, you have to carry the child to term and with the draconian laws that are being put in place right now? What the hell do you think is going to happen with a 10 year old girl who`s been abused by her father? She can`t stay in that house. She`s now pregnant with his child and you`re telling her she`s got to carry to term but you`ve got no resources available for? That`s the summer of 2022 for Republicans and McConnell and a lot of them are not happy with how this has played out with the Supreme Court, particularly given those cases that forced this issue right into this cycle.\par \par \uc2 [23:40:20]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Juanita, are they going to be ready to answer those questions? If Republicans are insisting that a woman has to carry her pregnancy to term, 75% of women who get abortions are at or near the poverty line. Are Republicans going to make some sort of commitment to say, I`m going to financial -- we`re going to make a financial commitment to these women, or to start requiring child support starting at six weeks pregnant, having a baby is expensive?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: It`s expensive, and having a baby that you don`t want is harmful and multiple levels. And absolutely, they are not going to have any response to this because this isn`t about people. This isn`t about caring about people or the children themselves. This is about control. This is about exerting control over basic rights and a basic freedom to women and pregnant people to access abortion. That`s all this is about. And now Republicans have no cover to hide behind that. That`s why you saw \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  him and Han as Michael laid out, he has nothing to say about this, because they`re ultimately the dogs who caught the car. And now they`re like, oh, but we actually don`t want this. They do want it. And they`ve been plotting for this for decades, Stephanie. They`ve been filling the court with \uc2 Federalist Society\uc2  approved judges. They`ve been forcing through nominations, like we saw with the restaurants multiple times under Trump and McConnell`s leadership in the Senate. And this is now what they`re dealing with.\par \par \uc2 And back to Ohio. Remember, in that -- those primary candidates all came out and said, we are prolife, we support this move for by the Supreme Court if this is the decision that`s ultimately rendered. But that`s the other thing that \uc2 Tim Ryan\uc2  is going to be up against, because he`s made it absolutely clear in the right way that this is about freedom for women. And that`s going to turn off some of the even some of those anti-Trump voters. And so when he thinks about this conversion rate, he is to calculating the fact that those anti-Trump voters and most of the Republican primary voters are probably anti-abortion too. And so he`s going to have to make a choice about just how hard he`s going to go after them when the reality is that abortion is going to be a deciding factor in that election to.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Summer is about to heat up. Juanita Tolliver, Michael Steele, thank you for making us smarter tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we`re going to mark the milestone we have all been dreading when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:47:13]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Back on May 24, 2020, \uc2 The New York Times\uc2  dedicated its front page to the incalculable loss of 100,000 people to COVID-19. Tonight, we have hit a grim and dreaded other milestone. More than 1 million Americans have lost their lives who COVID. The pandemic as the AP writes, leaving empty spaces in homes and neighborhoods across the country, whether we aware of them or not. It is almost impossible to wrap your mind around the pain of the past two years. But I think back to something \uc2 Mount Sinai Hospital\uc2  Chaplain Rocky Walker told me during the early days of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROCKY WALKER, MOUNT SINAI HOSPITAL CHAPLAIN: In the middle of all this sadness, there is -- there`s so much goodness that people are -- people are displaying not just other co-workers which they amaze me. But the patients themselves, the ones that are not intubated that can talk.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie, you have no idea how many times in a day, a grieving family member will stop crying for their family member who we`ve just told them has died. And they stop to thank us, you`re seeing some of the most wonderful things happening in one of the most darkest times.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Humanity in the face of darkness. With us tonight, Dr. Stephen Sample, he`s an E.R. physician at Memorial Hospital and Health Care Center in Jasper Indiana. And Kristin Urquiza, Co-Founder of the nonprofit Marked By COVID.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Sample, you have devoted countless hours to trying to save people from COVID. We`ve now lost more than a million people. What do you think about tonight?\par \par \uc2 DR. STEPHEN SAMPLE, EMERGENCY PHYSICIAN IN JASPER, INDIANA: You know, I don`t even know that I have the words to express it. I don`t know, when we start to get to numbers like a million gone. I don`t know that anybody has even the real capacity individually to understand that. And so we go back to our individual losses. Because the bigger the number gets, the more unreal it gets a million people in two years. It is insane. And it compounded by the fact that so many of those came. Most of those have come post vaccination. It is -- it`s awful. And we haven`t really had time to sit and even sit with it because then Ukraine went to war and now the Supreme Court and so there`s always something occupying us to kind of delay our collective grief, I think.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Kristin, after you lost your dad to COVID, you turned and devoted yourself to teaching other people about the virus. What are you thinking about tonight?\par \par \uc2 KRISTIN URQUIZA, CO-FOUNDER, MARKED BY COVID: Thinking about how this was preventable. Thinking about the literally hundreds if not 1000s of grieving individuals that I have met over the course of the last couple of years and how much pain and suffering is out there behind doors and hidden from view as it seems like so many want to move on, when we need to pause for a minute and recognize these losses a million people gone, that is not normal nor is it OK.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:31]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Kristin, how has this virus changed you?\par \par \uc2 URQUIZA: I`m not the same person I was two years ago. I`ll never be the same person. I am really - I describe myself as a high functioning not OK person. What I have really learned over the course of the last couple of years is that in a pandemic, money talks and big business matters a lot more than people`s lives. And that`s not the world I want to live in.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Wow, Dr. Sample, how has it changed you? You spent a lot of time frustrated hours treating people that we`re still denying COVID existence?\par \par \uc2 SAMPLE: Sure. I think I`m an E.R. doctor. So we`re all a little cynical by nature we have to be. But I think I have to really work on my own cynicism, and my attitudes towards fellow man, because I`ve seen a lot of stuff that has been really painful to me, as a physician, you know. I have been told in exam rooms, that I`m either uninformed or lying directly to the patients about their conditions, even while I go next door, and break bad news to the family, you know, and it also makes every single cough and bad cold that checks into my emergency department. It also sends a little shiver up my spine and you wonder, oh, my gosh, is the second wave here, is the third wave. Now, I think we`re on the sixth or seventh wave that starting to rise, but certainly, certainly it has affected the way I look at people around me and I don`t like that about myself very much right now.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Has it changed the way you practice medicine? Did you ever think in the last two years, you don`t want to do it anymore?\par \par \uc2 SAMPLE: Yeah, I`ve thought a lot about I don`t want to do it anymore. And, you know, emergency medicine and medicine in general is going through struggles pre-COVID. And all of those struggles have been compounded, just everything that has been brewing in the background has come right up to the forefront, between doctor and nurses shortages. But yeah, it`s hard. It`s hard to want to go in every day. It`s nice. It already has been nicer now, because recently we have been in more of a, you know, sort of a downward swing. And the people that we are seeing with COVID aren`t as terribly sick as they were. But it`s always there in the back of your mind. Are we going to do this again?\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Kristin, you lost your dad, and how isolated and alone you must have felt. Yet at the same time you are grieving with 1000s of other people, what has the sort of the collective grief been like for you?\par \par \uc2 URQUIZA: I mean, it was so isolating. I wasn`t able to be with my dad. And it felt like nobody was talking about the fact that these were real people that were dying. I felt alone. And when I first spoke out, it was such a healing piece of healing action for myself, because people started to come forward and say, oh, my gosh, you finally said what I was thinking of stealing that I was robbed, that this was preventable, that this shouldn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 Our collective grief, though, has not been recognized on the scale that it needs to be recognized in order for us to really grapple with what`s happened to us, not only individually but as a society. And that`s partially why Marked by COVID is working for permanent recognition through a COVID Memorial Day, permanent memorials, but also a COVID commission to ensure that we codify lessons learned from this experience and pass that on to future generations, so this never happens again. And we can`t push this pandemic into the memory hole like we did the 1918 pandemic. We just can`t.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: What was your dad named Kristin?\par \par \uc2 URQUIZA: He was Mark.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, Mark, we`re thinking about you tonight. Dr. Stephen Sample, Christin Urquiza, thank you both for joining us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, she argued, she has not found the right to be honored this way. Didn`t matter. She`s about to be a Hall of Famer anyway, when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 [23:55:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, we always have time for this woman. This lady earning the right and many others the Rock and Roll Hall of Fame 2022 inductees were announced earlier today. The list includes Pat Benatar, Duran, Eminem, Eurhythmics, Lionel 