ctable consequences".\par \par \uc2 Helene, is this just posturing? Or does the Pentagon view this as a credible threat from Putin?\par \par \uc2 COOPER: We don`t know at this point. They certainly don`t like the weapons that the Americans have been sending to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is the first official sort of demarche that we`ve seen on this, but that hasn`t so far stopped the Biden administration. We have more. We`re looking now at sending -- the United States has been sending javelins and stingers and is now upping back to the helicopters, as well as talking about -- looking at artillery.\par \par \uc2 So, this is -- the Russia`s \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  certainly don`t like this. But I don`t think you`re going to be seeing the United States stopping at any point, you know, in the next -- in the near future. And it`s -- particularly now that President Biden has announced this latest tranche of 800 million in weaponry median aid to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:11]\par \par \uc2 COOPER: So, I expect to see more complaints coming from Russia. But whether or not this -- I don`t think you`re going to be seeing anything from the Biden administration that sort of stops at this point in time or from \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Helene Cooper, thank you so much for your reporting. Please keep us up to date as you`ve been doing.\par \par \uc2 COOPER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Coming up, from his outrageous new redistricting map to his abortion ban photo-op. Why everyone should be keeping a close eye on Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , after this quick break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Democrats are rightly worried about what the redistricting process will mean for their chances in November`s midterms. Republicans are in a position to do some pretty dramatic gerrymandering, which could give them the power to win a majority of congressional seats with a minority of the votes.\par \par \uc2 In Florida, Republican Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  recently rejected the Republican-led legislature`s map and he proposed his own even more extreme gerrymander, which would give Republicans an enormous 20 to eight advantage in House seats. This is in a state \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  won with barely more than half the vote back in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And DeSantis, is making sure to shore up his own base before Florida voters go to the polls in November. But he`s not just contented to destroy voting rights for minorities.\par \par \uc2 Just yesterday, he signed an extremely restrictive 15 week abortion ban, which is guaranteed to face legal challenges.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now on set is Marc Caputo, a national political reporter for NBC News. And in my opinion, an expert on all things Florida politics.\par \par \uc2 Mark, it should be hashtag because Florida politics. Can we steal that?\par \par \uc2 MARC CAPUTO, NBC NEWS NATIONAL POLITICAL REPORTER: Sure. Anytime.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Let`s do that. So Mark, the current split in Florida is Republican seats, there are 16 and 11 Democratic Congressional seats, right?\par \par \uc2 So, then, we picked up because of the new census, which happens every 10 years, we`ve picked up one extra seat in Florida for a total of 28.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis`s proposal for his map is going to be 20 Republican seats to eight Democratic seats. How`s that going to fly?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Well, it`s probably going to fly in that it`s going to pass the legislature next week.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Well, that`s no big surprise though, it`s a Republican (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Another way to phrase it is that right now, there`s a five seat Republican edge, right? And now, it`s going to go to 12. It`s just quite a big jump.\par \par \uc2 Now, part of that is because of demographic shifts in Florida and the way in which people kind of self-segregate, politically speaking.\par \par \uc2 But some of that also to your point looks like a partisan gerrymander and some of our reporting that we`ve done, that I`ve done, some Republicans in the legislature privately acknowledged, like, yes, there`s some partisan gerrymandering going on here.\par \par \uc2 [20:40:02]\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: \uc2 Charlie Crist\uc2 , who is the congressman from the St. Peter`s Burg area, his seat. He`s leaving that seat by the way to run for governor. His seat is on the Peninsula. And what they`ve done is they`ve actually taken Democrats or people from Democratic areas in the south of the Peninsula. And they`ve put them across the Tampa Bay into Tampa. And that makes his seat or his former seat -- soon to be former seat, more Republican and the Democratic seat just more Democrat. So, that`s one of those shifts.\par \par \uc2 And the other one is in North Florida. There is a congressional district held by Lawson, the Black congressman, the Fifth Congressional District goes from Jacksonville to Tallahassee, that`s just eliminated.\par \par \uc2 So, those are two Democratic seats that are probably just gone.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Yes. And we`re going to get to that fifth district seat. But I mean, people should understand, right, in Florida, the governor getting involved in a redistricting map, that doesn`t happen.\par \par \uc2 Well, frankly, it really doesn`t happen any other states, but to have a governor that`s actively involved, where he says, you know, what, I`m going to veto that proposed map that comes from his own party folks in the legislature. And he says, I`m going to do it. It`s rare, right?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: We haven`t seen it before in the state. This is what my third redistricting that I`ve done. And or second, third actually. I know, I`m getting too old, it`s the gray hair, right? Yes, normally, the governor is in charge of the legislative maps he can -- he can -- or better said, the governor is not in charge of legislative maps. He`s in charge of the congressional ones whether to veto or not, but usually they don`t care. Because it`s not their problem.\par \par \uc2 You know, the one of the legislature passes, fine. What made DeSantis different this time is he started off the process with a proposal, very similar to the map you see now. The legislature looked at and said, look, we can`t do this, because we think it violates the Florida Supreme Court rulings on what are called the Fair District amendments, right? Which voters approved in 2010. And part of that was that district that we just talked about, Lawson`s district in North Florida. So they --\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Because it`s supposed to give people that are underrepresented the opportunity to have a seat at the table. And that`s the reason why the voters said let`s go ahead and allow those types of maps to occur, right?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Yes. That was one of the reasons that the Court said to draw the map this way. It`s kind of complicated because of the way in which that seat was drawn and redrawn. The Fair Districts amendments also said, look, you can`t draw districts to intentionally favor or disfavor an incumbent or political party.\par \par \uc2 And what DeSantis did is he drew his initial map legislature and said, look, we can`t do this, they passed their maps, they said, you know, I`m going to veto it and hold them back in.\par \par \uc2 Now, the legislature is basically looking at essentially the same maps. And a lot of them are thinking, look, this is probably not going to pass muster, it`s probably going to wind up in courts.\par \par \uc2 But you know, maybe \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is right, maybe what are called minority access seats, that seat in North Florida, maybe because of new \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  rulings, maybe that`s not going to pass \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  constitutional muster.\par \par \uc2 So you know what, this is going to be his problem. Let him get hauled into court, let him get to pose, let him deal with it.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Well, here`s the deal, though. Just recently, in March, just a month ago, a federal judge actually ruled that there was a lot of bad voter suppression going on in Florida. No big shocker for those of us that are following what`s happening in Florida. But does DeSantis really think that this is going to pass constitutional muster if this proposed map gets in front of these judges?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: I think he believes that it`s going to pass in the federal sense when it comes to striking down that minority access seat. The big question that we have is when it comes to that potential partisan gerrymander that I mentioned in the Tampa Bay region, St. Petersburg and Tampa, whether that passes Florida State Supreme Court muster, but that also depends on which court winds up taking jurisdiction over this.\par \par \uc2 Redistricting is very complicated. It`s very convoluted. And now you have possibly two courts. The question is who`s going to take charge? And who`s going to do the map drawing, who`s going to do the rejecting?\par \par \uc2 PHANG: You mean like the federal court or the state court? Is that what you mean?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Correct.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Now, listen, the Florida Supreme Court has a number of DeSantis appointees, right?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Right. That is the difference between now and 2015. The last time they ruled on this, there`s now three DeSantis appointees on the Florida Supreme Court. It`s a seven-member court.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: So, I want to now move on. I mean, listen, I am a moving target for \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . I am a minority. I am a woman and I`m a Democrat. So, I got some problems, right?\par \par \uc2 So, let`s talk about why I`m also still on the radar for someone like \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 . And that`s because he just passed that 15 week abortion bill, that photo-op was pretty unappetizing, having those little kids hold up those signs. But that`s also due for a legal challenge.\par \par \uc2 Tell me why you think that it actually could not survive. Putting aside what happens with Roe v. Wade, with \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  in June, the Florida Supreme Court actually has prior law that protects women`s right to access under this privacy analysis, right?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Right. Well, the Florida constitution actually has a right of privacy specifically enshrined in it. And previous Florida Supreme Court rulings have used that in protection of certain abortion rights. And that`s different between the state Supreme Court or better said, the state constitution and the federal constitution.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Now, let`s be clear, it`s a woman`s right to privacy to be able to protect her private right to access to abortion. It`s pretty much what it is.\par \par \uc2 [20:45:01]\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Right. That`s not what that jives with the court rulings now, that`s not the exact text of what`s in the Constitution.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: The old constitution.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Right. Well, or the current constitution. But it -- now, the conservatives have tried to remove that from the state constitution that takes a vote of the people 60 percent to do that. So far, they failed.\par \par \uc2 One of the reasons they`ve tried to remove that is because that right to privacy in the state constitution protects based on prior state Supreme Court rulings, a woman`s right to have an abortion.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Well, listen, it doesn`t mean then that if something happens with \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2  in June, and it gets kicked back to having the states determine on an individual level, whether or not they`re going to allow right an access to abortion, you know, whether that`s actually going to stand -- constitutional withstand constitutional muster, if every state is able to determine what it`s going to do, right?\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Yes, I mean, in the end, the question we have back to your original point, or your point you made a moment ago, is it`s a kind of a different state Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 So, to what degree are they going to respect the prior court opinions when it was more of a liberal or a Democratic leaning court? I don`t know.\par \par \uc2 The other question I have is kind of politically speaking, is, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  is up for reelection. He`s raised $100 million so far.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: And we all know that he`s gunning for a 2024 presidential nomination.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: There`s that as well. But the question is, is there this sleeping giant that we in politics have long presupposed that if abortion is made illegal, that lots of women and lots of moderates, moderate men are going to rise up and say, you know what, this is too much, we`re going to come out and vote against another.\par \par \uc2 Some evidence that Florida voters actually support abortion rights, in 2012, the legislature put a measure on the ballot, asking people in Florida to ban public funding of abortions, which really didn`t happen, but nevertheless, they put it on there.\par \par \uc2 Florida voters voted against that by 10 percentage points, which is another way to say that is Florida voters actually voted to allow for public funding of abortions, sort of allowed to continue again, it wasn`t really happening.\par \par \uc2 So, that`s one example we have, but the question is, is there -- is there this kind of sleeping giant or the sleeping mass of votes and voters or women voters, a moderate voters, pro-choice voters, so to speak, who will come out and vote or not? Like we`re going to find out.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Yes, but the problem is, Marc, you and I both know the amount of time it`s going to take to see if that sleeping giant`s concept works, there`s going to be so many women that are not going to have access, and we`re going to have something like abortion tourism, right? Where people are going to have to go to a state where they`re going to be able to seek this relief.\par \par \uc2 Marc Caputo, thank you for being here with me. Thank you for the insight, and we appreciate your time.\par \par \uc2 CAPUTO: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Coming up next, Herschel Walker`s Senate campaign takes another be wielding turn. After skipping his own debate, he`s now going on T.V. to ask The Nation Elie Mystal to hang out?\par \par \uc2 Well, the man of the hour Elie Mystal is going to join me next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [20:52:30]\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Yesterday, the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2  voted unanimously to require Republican presidential candidates to skip debates? Held by the nonpartisan \uc2 Commission on Presidential Debates\uc2  which has run the process for decades, \uc2 RNC\uc2  Chairwoman \uc2 Ronna Romney McDaniel\uc2  said "The \uc2 RNC\uc2  will find newer, better debate platforms for Republican candidates to make their cases."\par \par \uc2 Platforms like Fox News, perhaps because that`s where we found Georgia Republican Senate candidate \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , he`s been making most of his national appearances on Fox News after skipping his state`s first debate ahead of next month`s Republican primary. And it was from that safe space on conservative T.V. that Walker took a shot at writer Elie Mystal who wrote this piece critical of Walker`s candidacy this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HERSCHEL WALKER\uc2  (R), SENATORIAL CANDIDATE OF GEORGIA: It doesn`t matter what they say, I represent the people of Georgia, and I`m not going to do what people tell me to do. And I`m not going to have you to bully me because you can`t.\par \par \uc2 But what I would love to do for you to take the time to come down to Georgia. We sit down and break bread because I`m about bringing people together, not separating people. What it seems like you are is to separate people because of color, that`s not \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Elie Mystal is the Justice Correspondent for The Nation, whose new article is titled The Herschel Walker Senate campaign is an insult to Black people. He joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Ellie, when this came out last night, I tweeted, I`m driving, there was a lot of responses. First question to you is, what are our snacks for that road trip and who`s in control of the mix tape?\par \par \uc2 ELIE MYSTAL, JUSTICE CORRESPONDENT, THE NATION: Don`t get me wrong, Katie. I would love to Snap into a Slim Jim, pop down I-95, taking through way down, threw on some -- I would probably go with just because of the season, I probably go with Jesus Christ Superstar. I do a great chi effects (PH) which will be fun for the entire road trip, but I won`t.\par \par \uc2 Because of the -- because the bottom line is, I will not waste the time of Georgia voters helping \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  distract himself from their issues, all right?\par \par \uc2 I will debate or have lunch with or break bread with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  the moment after he talks to the senator from Georgia, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  about actual issues affecting Georgia voters.\par \par \uc2 All Walker is trying to do is distract people from the fact that he doesn`t have a policy plan, literally cannot articulate an energy policy or a COVID policy or any policy that matters for Georgia voters.\par \par \uc2 [20:55:07]\par \par \uc2 MYSTAL: And he won`t even sit down for his own party`s senatorial debate. But sure, he wants to talk to a guy from New York.\par \par \uc2 I mean, look, he wants to talk to a guy from New York. What are we going to? I mean, look, if he wants to talk about, you know, the efficacy of the screen pass in the face of an all-out blitz, we can have that discussion.\par \par \uc2 But if he wants to talk about being Senate, a senator from Georgia, he needs to talk to Georgians, which is what he`s afraid of doing.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Well, you know, the shotgun formation is a personal favorite of mine. So, Elie, here`s the deal, though. You`re not just some guy, right? Walker predictably -- there`s a reason why he addressed what you -- what you said and what you wrote about. He did not respond, though, to the merits of your argument.\par \par \uc2 Bottom line, what is your case against \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 ?\par \par \uc2 MYSTAL: So, again, it`s a misunderstanding that he has. And I think that a lot of Republicans have, as if liking somebody is important -- is an important qualification for their political career, it`s not.\par \par \uc2 I don`t need to sit down and have dinner with him because I don`t care if he`s a nice guy, right? My article about him wasn`t even really about him. It was about his Republican handlers. It was about the white conservative handlers that dredge this guy up out of Texas, which is where he lives, not Georgia, dredge them up to run against \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Why? Because \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  is Black. And in 2020, Black voters in Georgia prove that they have the electoral might and muscle to put candidates over the top. That`s what they did for Warnock. That`s what they did for \uc2 Jon Ossoff\uc2 , all credit to \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2  and LaTosha Brown for registering Black voters in Georgia, and so what does the Republican Party do? Do they have an outreach to Black voters in Georgia? No.\par \par \uc2 All they come forward with Black voters in Georgia is voter suppression. That`s all you get from the Republicans if you`re a Black voter in Georgia. Tthey want to suppress your vote, they want to make it so that you have to sit in a very long line before you can vote, then they don`t want to let people -- they want to make it so people can`t bring you water while you`re in the line to vote.\par \par \uc2 Why does \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  want to break bread with people who are voting in Georgia? That would be a nice use of his time. Go hand out bread and water to people who are standing in line to vote in Georgia. I bet he won`t.\par \par \uc2 I`ll tell you why he won`t because he -- because he knows that the more people who vote, the less likely he is to be senator. And that`s the whole game, right?\par \par \uc2 To distract, to throw up -- to throw up bank shots, to call me out on in front of -- as you put it, the safe space of Hannity, as opposed to getting down on the ground and dealing with his potential constituents. That`s what he`s afraid of doing.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: How much longer is this I`m going to hide out in the basement of Fox News is going to last? How long is that going to work for him? Because he will eventually have to address the critical issues, the core issues, the kitchen table issues that are confronting not only all of us in the United States, but the people in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 I mean, Walker, he won`t even show up for that debate we`ve talked about, he`s been accused of abuse by his ex-wife, which he blamed on his struggles with mental health. He has basically said, I don`t have to do this until I actually get the primary win.\par \par \uc2 But you and I both know, he`s never going to stand a chance against the brilliance of somebody like \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 . So, is this just a throw away for the GOP at this point?\par \par \uc2 MYSTAL: It`s going to work for Republicans until voters start punishing them for putting forward these clown candidates, right? And that`s what we haven`t seen so far from the Republican base, right?\par \par \uc2 So, you can -- you can go on -- you can go on this network, and you can find a bunch of former Republicans who now want the Democratic Party to start acting like the Republican Party, but they`re not doing the work in their own party of stopping these people at the primary stage, right?\par \par \uc2 Because the Trumpers have completely taken over that party. The inmates are running the asylum and it`s the crazy people now who are in charge of that party.\par \par \uc2 Until the Republican voters, other place like Georgia, other place like Iowa, other place like Arizona until the Republican voters stop voting for these yahoos (PH), there will continue to be yahoos.\par \par \uc2 Right now, there is no downside from Walker hiding from the voters of Georgia because the voters of -- because the Republican voters of Georgia apparently do not care about his actual policies.\par \par \uc2 And let`s be clear, this is the same state that sent the inimitable \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2  to Congress, right? So, Republicans voters in Georgia are clearly not particularly -- they don`t really care about what these people are going to do. So long as there`s an R behind their name. And they feel like those people will own the lives, that`s like their -- that`s their -- that`s what passes as policy for these people, which is -- which is a problem.\par \par \uc2 So, until enough voters kind of rise up to stop them, we will be where we are.\par \par \uc2 [21:00:02]\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Elie Mystal, we`ve run out of time. I should have just actually just had you do the entire segment. I`m still going to drive though, just in case you change your mind. Thanks for being here.\par \par \uc2 MYSTAL: When \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  beats him, we will go down and we will have dinner with \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2  and talk about energy policy. Let`s see how that goes.\par \par \uc2 PHANG: Thanks, Elie. That is ALL IN on this Friday night.\par \par \uc2 Don`t forget, you can now find me on my own show every Saturday and Sunday morning at 7:00 a.m. Eastern right here on MSNBC. We hope you`ll join us.\par \par \uc2 "The Rachel Maddow Show" with Mehdi Hasan starts now. Good evening, Mehdi.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document CHAYES0020220416ei4f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for April 15, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Matt Bradley, Courtney Kube, Ali Viali, Navin Nayak,  Rick Tyler \par \uc2 9,315 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 April 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:03]\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Friday, Russia readies its next offensive following a major military setback as its flagship missile cruiser sinks. CIA Director \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2  warns of Russian desperation, including the potential deployment of so called tactical nuclear weapons. All of this as the Western allies tried to predict the Kremlin`s next move. One of Putin`s fiercest critics, dubbed Russia`s most wanted man joins me ahead.\par \par \uc2 And later, health officials keep it leery eye on a rise in COVID infections as we head into a holiday weekend. What it all means for the future of public health policy. All that`s coming up. Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I`m Chuck Todd in Washington where national security officials are trying to answer a key question. How will Russia respond to recent setbacks in Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 How desperate is Putin for some sort of symbolic victory? Just days after Russia`s withdrawal from Kyiv, Ukraine`s capital is once again on high alert. NBC News is obtained this surveillance video that appears to show a suspected Russian missile strike on a factory in Kyiv that manufactures anti-ship missiles. And official from the region says three missile strikes were carried out in the region today and that civilians in and around Kyiv remain in danger.\par \par \uc2 Moments ago, by the way, the Pentagon has now confirmed that it was indeed Ukrainian missiles that struck the Russian Flagship of its Black Sea Fleet yesterday. The ship has since sunk. Despite Russia`s failure to capture Kyiv in the worst first 50 days, President Zelenskyy is warning that his nation`s capital may very well be the target as this war moves forward. He told the Atlantic, they can return to the center of Ukraine and even Kyiv. It is possible. Now is not yet the time of victory.\par \par \uc2 We have a very small window of opportunity, as Kyiv itself remains on high alert, Russian forces are preparing for a major offensive in the eastern part of the country as they continue their brutal assault in the south on Mariupol. Ukrainian officials say Russia has begun using long range bombers for the first time to attack that besieged city. Folks, we don`t know, think about all the accusations of war crimes and genocide.\par \par \uc2 We have not truly seen everything that has happened in Mariupol. \uc2 U.N\uc2 . aid groups say people in the city are being starved to death. And after President Biden pledged an additional $800 million in military aid to Ukraine, the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  is reporting the Moscow has sent a formal diplomatic note to the United States warning it to stop arming Zelenskyy`s troops. So, what is Putin trying to "prebut here?"\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, CIA Chief \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2  caution that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  may be particularly dangerous right now, given his military struggles in this war.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 WILLIAM BURNS, CIA DIRECTOR: Given the potential desperation of President Putin and the Russian leadership, given the setbacks that they`ve faced so far, militarily, none of us can take lightly the threat posed by a potential resort to tactical nuclear weapons or low-yield nuclear weapons.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And realize \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2  is probably -- as I believe the most senior American official to meet face to face with Putin in the last six months. He went over there in the end of 2021, essentially letting Putin know, we knew everything they were preparing to do. So, he`s got a little extra insight than most people in this policy realm.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Matt Bradley from Kyiv, Courtney Kube is at the Pentagon and the former Deputy Assistant Secretary of Defense for Europe, and \uc2 NATO\uc2  policy in the Obama administration Jim Townsend is also joining us. Let me start with Matt, though. So, here we were, Matt, thinking that -- all right, Russia was going to try to concentrate its forces. But whether it`s a Putin reaction to what happened in the Black Sea, whether it`s frustration overall, whether it`s fear that his own public is starting to get an idea that the Russian military is not performing very well, it seems like Kyiv is back in the crosshairs.\par \par \uc2 MATT BRADLEY, NBC NEWS FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Yes. It looks like he was kind of back on the menu. But really, the reason why is because it sounds as though the Russians hit a factory that was producing the very same type of Neptune missile system that American authorities now believe was the one that sunk the Moskva, that flagship -- ship that was in the Black Sea and that sunk at port just last night.\par \par \uc2 So, that was a really big deal here for Ukrainians not just because, you know, it was a strategic victory sinking that huge ship, but because it was a technological victory. The Neptune missile system was essentially -- well, it`s a Soviet model that was greatly improved and produced here in Ukraine. So, unlike a lot of Ukrainian weapons that are used now on the front line, this is Ukrainian technology, and it was used to crush this massive ship in the Black Sea. One that had been terrorizing Ukraine for the last several months. Chuck?\par \par \uc2 [13:05:06]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Matt, I`m curious. It`s a holiday weekend for a lot of religious folks. Whether Jewish, for Passover, Christianity and Easter. Are you seeing Kyiv -- are you seeing any religious celebrations being attempted right now in Kyiv?\par \par \uc2 BRADLEY: Well, I`m glad you asked, Chuck, because I am standing in front of the central synagogue of Kyiv. And actually, Passover, one of the most important Jewish holidays of the Jewish calendar. It just started just a couple of minutes ago, and we were down in the basement of this synagogue where everybody was preparing Matzo, they had Manischewitz, they were preparing for that holiday that really just started just a couple of minutes ago.\par \par \uc2 We weren`t allowed to stick around because of course, having cameras inside goes against the idea of, you know, not using electricity during these high holy days. So, we said, we`re out here. But we spoke with a lot of Jews inside and at a cemetery earlier today where one who was killed by the Russians was being buried. Now, there`s a lot of irony here because of course, Putin`s adventures here in Ukraine, the stated goal of that is to "denazify" Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The Ukrainian Jews that we`ve been speaking with today, they say, they don`t need to be denazified. And of course, the irony goes past that, you know, that President \uc2 Volodymyr Zelenskyy\uc2 , he said that he was the president of the country, he`s part Jewish. And actually, some of the Russian bombardments hit the Babyn Yar Memorial. That was the site of one of the most -- one of the worst massacres by the Nazis in this country.\par \par \uc2 And of course, Ukraine still has a really important and dynamic Jewish community and Jewish life here. It is one of the largest Jewish populations in Europe. And historically, it`s been the source. It`s incubated so many important spiritual and intellectual movements within the global Jewish community like Hasidism or Zionism. So, it really is quite rich of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to irrigate himself to say that he is de not certifying Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, the evidence of Vladimir Putin`s anti-semitism only grows it seems sometimes by the day. Matt Bradley in Kyiv for us. Matt, thanks very much. Let me go to Courtney Kube at the Pentagon. And over the last couple of days, we`ve wondered, I think -- I think I was speaking to you, I was like, we have the ability to confirm via satellite footage at some point. So, Courtney, is that what`s likely happened here as we were able to comb through our satellite footage, and indeed see that this ship was hit with two missiles?\par \par \uc2 COURTNEY KUBE, NBC NEWS PENTAGON CORRESPONDENT: So, the defense officials aren`t saying exactly how they know. But they are now confident according to an assessment, a U.S. assessment that there were two Ukrainian Neptune cruise missiles that struck the ship. And a lot of questions that we`ve been asking here is if it was struck by cruise missiles, why didn`t the air defense system, this ship it -- in and of itself is largely used for defense -- air defenses?\par \par \uc2 Why didn`t it -- its own air defense systems defended against these incoming missiles? So, senior defense official is saying what they don`t really know yet at this point, but there are a couple of working theories. One is that there may have been a maintenance period going on right now that their air defense systems weren`t working because of that. Another is they simply may have been shut off at the time.\par \par \uc2 And a third is it`s possible that these Neptunes were coming in at such an angle that the Russian ship air defense systems just did not work. That being said, what is clear now, the Ukrainians have been saying that they fired these Neptune missiles at the ship. The U.S. wouldn`t confirm it. Today, they are saying yes, in fact they -- their -- these Neptune missiles did strike. Some additional questions that we still don`t have answers to and we may not really get is, what was the casualty?\par \par \uc2 What was the toll among Russian sailors who were on the ship? We know there were somewhere in the neighborhood of four to 500 Russian sailors on the ship at the time that it was struck by these missiles. There have been indications that some lifeboats came in, and were able to take some of them away, but we still don`t have a good sense of what -- how many Russian sailors, Russian service members may have been impacted by this before the ship sank.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Courtney, what are your sources in the Pentagon telling you about what they think Russia is trying to do now on the offensive?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: So, there -- there`s this still is, you know, a refitting and a resetting period of time where they -- the Russian forces that were involved in some of these major axes over the last 50 days or so. They`re getting reset and, you know, refitting their weapons, getting ready for the next sort of phase of this offensive. But the big question is, when will that begin? Will they wait until the -- all of these Russian forces that have moved out of the country and are getting ready for this next phase.\par \par \uc2 Will they wait until they`re all ready or will they start trickling forces in slowly over the course of days and even weeks? There`s a big deadline that everyone is looking to and that`s May 9th.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:03]\par \par \uc2 KUBE: That`s a -- it`s a symbolic day for the Russians, their victory day. There`s a lot of speculation that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  and the Russian military want to have some sort of progress before May 9th. If they want that they need to start moving right away. That is a very tight timeline. So, we`re all waiting to see what happens in the next couple of days. If we see any kind of ground movement back in the country.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Courtney, are the -- it`s interesting, what you noted on the Russian ship and that it was possible they were in the midst of maintenance and they didn`t see it coming. Is this a particularly vulnerable time for Russian troops? Is this a window that perhaps Ukrainians -- I heard President Zelenskyy use the phrase that there`s a small window here. Is this a particularly vulnerable time for the Russian troops?\par \par \uc2 KUBE: It absolutely could be. Most of them are inside Russia. So, we haven`t seen Ukraine take too many strikes on the Russians inside Russia. The question is, will we start seeing more of that? Now that they`ll have things like artillery, they`ll have even more capable the ability to do that if the Ukrainian military decides to do so.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Courtney Kube at the Pentagon for us. Courtney, thank you. Let me bring in Jim Thompson -- Jim Townsend. He was in the Obama administration, Assistant Secretary of -- for European Policy, for Secretary of Defense for European and \uc2 NATO\uc2  policy. So, let me ask specifically about what \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2  said, because it raised my antenna, because \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2  probably has had more time to think and study Putin than really anybody else in the administration right now.\par \par \uc2 He`s had plenty of face to faces with him. So, when \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2  says he`s worried about the use of a tactical nuke, I take it pretty seriously. What would we be watching for if Russia is indeed thinking about this?\par \par \uc2 JIM TOWNSEND, FORMER DEPUTY ASSISTANT SECRETARY OF DEFENSE, EUROPEAN AND \uc2 NATO\uc2  POLICY: I think the first indications would come from intelligence. There are certain moves that any military that has nuclear weapons would do in terms of pulling that nuclear weapon out of storage. Whether it`s going to be dropped by a plane or shot from artillery tube, there are some things that you look for that are telltale signs of vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Communications, as well. As well as the unit itself that`s beginning to move. So, you can bet right now, \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2  has the satellites and other ways in which we collect intelligence just really looking down hard on those sites to see if we can see some movement. Today. You know, we haven`t. This nuclear saber has been rattled before and the \uc2 Intel\uc2  community has been watching for those signals. They haven`t seen anything.\par \par \uc2 But obviously, Bill thinks that times coming close. The frustration levels going up, and that this might be a time when Putin gives the orders. So, that`s what they`re going to watch for.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How should we read Russia officially sending us a warning about arming Zelenskyy?\par \par \uc2 TOWNSEND: I think we take that very seriously. And a number of us have been waiting for that letter to show up because we knew that arming his opponents there in Ukraine is something he`s not going to stand for too long. Particularly as the flow of equipment going in has increased the sophistication as well as the ability of Ukraine to be able to use it. And, you know, I think back to Vietnam, you know, when the supply was going to the Vietcong in the south, they were going through Laos, Cambodia, Ho Chi Minh trail, you know, that was something that upset us too.\par \par \uc2 And we went in and started attacking though Chi Minh Trail and trying to stop that resupply. And so, this kind of thing for Moscow, they want to try to stop it too. The problem for us is he might target the Ukrainian side of the movement of supplies, trucks that are leaving Poland and going into deeper into Ukraine. But the problem is if he starts targeting in Poland, he`s attacking bases or transport in Poland. That`s attacking a \uc2 NATO\uc2  ally. That`s an Article Five and that`s going to bring us right in.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, Jim, it seems like a both Russia and the United States were all tiptoeing not wanting to technically violate, you know, whether it`s, you know, non-\uc2 NATO\uc2  territory versus \uc2 NATO\uc2  territory. It seems like he`s sending this warning. But as you say, you expect him to attack supply lines within Ukraine. Putin does not want to draw the United States in to this military conflict. Correct?\par \par \uc2 TOWNSEND: Right. He doesn`t want to draw us in, but he might feel that the west is afraid to be drawn in. In other words, perhaps he`s thinking that he could get away with a pinprick strike in Poland. If he wanted to send one missile over the border, he might do something small thinking that \uc2 NATO\uc2  would say, well, look, this was something small, you know, we don`t have to invoke Article Five here.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:03]\par \par \uc2 TOWNSEND: We can do something else. He might think he can get away with something small but very symbolic and also something that might frighten the European allies where they might say, oh, my God, this is -- this is getting out of hand, we`re seeing spillover into Europe. We can have this happen. He might hope, Putin might hope this is what will happen. So, we have to be very concerned about that letter because there`s some hidden benefits to Putin in there if he feels he can do something and get away with it.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. I mean, we have to assume this letter is his pretext, right? He`s basically going to say, hey, I warned you. Now, it could be cyber, it could be an attack on the west. Let me ask you this. Can you imagine \uc2 NATO\uc2  not responding especially now we`re on the verge of perhaps, including Sweden and Finland into \uc2 NATO\uc2 , I would imagine we`re not -- we`re not flexing the alliances` muscles very well, if they allowed a pin pitch -- a pin prick strike against Poland, correct?\par \par \uc2 TOWNSEND: That`s exactly right. That`s -- that will be the debate at \uc2 NATO\uc2  around the table. That will be one of the issues being discussed. How does this make \uc2 NATO\uc2  look if we don`t rise to that challenge but on the other hand, the talk about proportionality. Should be invoking Article Five, is that proportionate if there`s just one missile or a salvo that might go into a military target within Poland that is doing trans shipment?\par \par \uc2 There will be allies who are going to make the case that this is not something we want to get involved in. Think about Hungary, for instance. Perhaps Turkey, perhaps Greece, that they might come in and say this is not worth a potential war with Russia, to go to war, because of these few strikes. And that`s the worry about \uc2 NATO\uc2  is around that table there`s a lot of other views that have to be aired out.\par \par \uc2 And decisions are made by consensus. So, one ally could say, I am not in favor of this. And \uc2 NATO\uc2  doesn`t do anything.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You`re describing a situation where you think Putin could drive a wedge into \uc2 NATO\uc2  still.\par \par \uc2 TOWNSEND: Putin has been trying to drive wedges in the \uc2 NATO\uc2  for years. That has been what he`s wanted to do is to not just separate the allies and undercut \uc2 NATO\uc2 , but certainly separate the United States from Europe too. So, he`s been doing everything he can to stoke division. And I think he misjudged the alliance when he started the invasion. He felt the alliance wouldn`t stick together.\par \par \uc2 So, I think in this case, he might harbor a hope that if he can do something to embarrass the alliance, that that would be something that would be added value to him. So, the alliance has to be unified and strong. The section is going to have to really exercise its leadership and President Biden too. This is a testing for the alliance. Let`s hope he doesn`t decide to do those pinprick strikes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Jim Townsend, a former assistant secretary of defense during the Obama administration, whose focus and portfolio with \uc2 NATO\uc2  and Euro. Jim Townsend, thank you. Up next, we`re going to have more on what`s in store in this new phase of the conflict. He will join here on set by one of Putin`s biggest critics who`s been a target of the Kremlin himself. That`s ahead, you`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 [13:21:18]\par \par \uc2 BURNS: We`ve seen some rhetorical posturing on the part of the Kremlin about moving to higher nuclear alert levels. So far, we haven`t seen a lot of practical evidence of the kind of deployments or, you know, you know, military dispositions that would, you know, reinforce that concern. But we watch for that very intently. It`s, you know, one of our most important responsibilities at CIA.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. That was a little bit more there from the CIA Chief \uc2 Bill Burns\uc2 , warning yesterday that Russia could still pose a nuclear threat to the United States and the rest of the world simply because of Putin`s own mindset. Well, joining me on set is a Putin critic, \uc2 Bill Browder\uc2 . He was a key figure in pushing for the passage of the Magnitsky Act. It`s a law that allows the United States to sanction individuals involved in human rights abuses.\par \par \uc2 He`s out with a new book titled Freezing Order; A True Story of Money Laundering, Murder, and surviving Vladimir Putin`s wrath. \uc2 Bill Browder\uc2 , it`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 BILL BROWDER\uc2 , PUTIN CRITIC: Good to be here.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, it`s -- it is -- your safety seems to me to be more at risk today than it has been in a while because you have noted \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  at least cared symbolically of what the west thought of him. And that that gave you at least some protection. You`re a high profile to target you. Now that though he doesn`t care what the west thinks, I`m just curious, how are you feeling about your personal safety?\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: Well, you`re right. I mean, so back in the day -- so, I`ve been in my own private war with \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  for the last 12 years. And after the Magnitsky Act was passed, he made it, you know, he went after me, you know, every way possible. Death threats, kidnapping, arrest, warrants, et cetera. But he always wanted to attend the G20 Summit and host international sporting events and all that kind of stuff.\par \par \uc2 And so, taking me out in an obvious way would have probably jeopardize some of that. But now he`s got both feet in the criminal world. It`s -- this is all exponentially raised the risk.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You may be as well schooled in how he money launderers and how he launders money, how the Russian oligarch system works than anybody that`s not in the American government. What do you make of the sanctions protocol? How effective is it? And can it be more effective?\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: Well, sanctions are like medicine. It depends when you apply the medicine to the disease, how bad the disease gets. We could have applied a tiny fraction of these sanctions if we had done them before the invasion. It might have changed Putin`s calculus completely, because Putin had - he assumed completely, probably accurately beforehand that we would do nothing because we did nothing for the last 20 years. We -- he invaded Georgia, nothing.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, it was a -- it was a correct gamble on his part.\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: Exactly correct. He had so much evidence that we were going to do nothing that he said they`re going to do nothing again. And so, if we have just done something, some little thing I was arguing -- so oligarch sanction, five oligarchs before the invasion, just so you can see that we`re serious, he might not have done this. But now that we`re in in, it`s a totally different thing. Putin doesn`t know how to back down, he only has a forward gear, no reverse gear.\par \par \uc2 So, he`s not going to back down. So, what are sanctions for now? Sanctions are simply and only for depleting him of the financial resources to pay for this war.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that to me is what it should be all about. Making -- basically making it impossible for him to access any of these resources any way possible and the oligarchs as well, right?\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: Absolutely correct. So, we`ve sanctioned his onshore money, the central bank reserves and by sanctioning the oligarchs, we`re sanctioning his offshore money that he could use for this war. But there`s one huge elephant in the room which is OK, we`ve dealt with his assets but he`s still got the cash flow coming in every day. A billion dollars a day from the sale of oil and gas.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:07]\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: He spends a billion dollars a day killing with his military machine killing Ukrainians. And mostly Europeans give him a billion dollars a day for oil and gas. That`s got to stop.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What we see, it looks like the E.U. is trying to ratchet that up a little bit better. But, you know, they`re -- as I say, people -- their citizens in Germany don`t like to pay high gas prices either. And you do -- you don`t want to create political instability either in the \uc2 NATO\uc2  alliance, and frankly, we have it in this country. France has it in there. There is a lot. How do you walk that line?\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: Well, there`s one easy fix to this whole thing which nobody seems to be talking about, which is -- we`re talking about high gas -- oil and gas prices. Well, there`s one huge player in the oil and gas industry which is Saudi Arabia. Why doesn`t Saudi Arabia turn on the taps and pump out an extra one or two million barrels a day?\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re in cahoots with Russia. I mean -- and do you think we should be holding them more accountable for essentially, they`re helping Russia strangle the world right now financially.\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: They are. And -- I mean, Biden gave Mohammed bin Salman a free pass after killing Jamal Khashoggi. And it didn`t sanction him. Well, we might as well get some payback for that now which is, they should turn on the taps right now. And we have some leverage over them. It`s not as if we have no leverage and we should be exercising that leverage in the maximum way possible.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Any of these oligarchs going to be come a Brutus?\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: No.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Why is that?\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: Because Putin can take them out with a flick of his eyelash.\par \par \uc2 TODD: They`re all scared of him.\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: Terrified. You should see these guys in the presence of Putin. They act like all the most powerful, alpha male type guys in -- on their own. You put them in a room with Putin, and they become these subordinate little animals.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Are any of them -- I mean, I did hear from some that said, well, there may be a whole bunch of oligarchs that want to talk. Are they weak enough? Where they become intelligence assets?\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: Absolutely not. Under no circumstance.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You wouldn`t trust anything they had to say.\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: No, I wouldn`t -- I -- if they`re talking, they`re doing so at the direction of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  to give us misinformation. They would never betray Putin because there`ll be killed in an instant f they do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How would you try to get the information of what`s actually happened to Ukraine into Russia and to the Russian people?\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: Well, in any way possible. \uc2 YouTube\uc2  still operates, they`ve got this telegram messaging service. That`s how they do things which we should do shortwave radio again. We should do everything we did during the Soviet times. We should be dropping leaflets in. Wherever possible way we can get information and we should get information in because he`s -- the one thing that will cause the Russians to take \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  out is if they see that he is the loser, that he actually is today.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right. Well, it feels like U.S. policy says they want to make sure he`s losing this war, that he pays a price for this war. He`s trying to figure out how to probably get out of this in some form without looking like he`s been humiliated. Do you have an idea what that looks like?\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: There`s only one thing I know about \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is that he always escalates. And so he`s not going to try to get out of this. He`s just going to go crazy. And what I dread is that there is going to be a massive civilian casualty of some sort. Much, much, much more worse than anything we`ve seen so far.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`ve been using this quote, there`s a health public health official in Ohio who said about pandemics. You never look back and regret what you did, you end up regretting what you didn`t do. Do you fear that if Putin escalates, we`re going to regret not doing something soon?\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: Yes. I think they were -- I think that we should -- and this is not popular to say here in Washington but we should have a no-fly zone there. And everyone says, what about World War III? We`re already in World War III. And from my perspective, if we show Putin strength, that`s the one thing that could cause him to moderate not because he is trying to compromise.\par \par \uc2 GUTFELD: You`re from the side and you`re not alone in this. There are some people that say he only understands one type of conversation and that`s force.\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: And that`s correct. And anybody who`s not saying that just doesn`t understand him. And I know so many experts on Russia who are not really familiar with Russia, who are saying other things but you talk to \uc2 Garry Kasparov\uc2 , you talk to \uc2 Mikhail Khodorkovsky\uc2 . You talk to \uc2 Alexei Navalny\uc2 , you talk Vladimir Kara-Murza who has been arrested by Putin recently. You talk to anybody who knows Putin, it`s only force.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. All those people you`ve talked about live under the threat of death. That`s from the Putin regime. \uc2 Bill Browder\uc2 , as you do to, please stay safe.\par \par \uc2 BROWDER: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Thanks for coming on and sharing your perspective. Coming up. Renewed questions about the future the Democratic Party leadership, is the end of an era appears to be approaching. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:33:32]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. California Democratic Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  is responding to new questions about her mental fitness after the "San Francisco Chronicle" published a report saying four senators, three former Feinstein staffers and an incumbent California member of Congress told them that her memory is rapidly deteriorating.\par \par \uc2 Feinstein is 88 years old and was first elected to the Senate in 1992. According to the "Chronicle" Feinstein initially declined to be interviewed for the story but yesterday she broke that silence telling the paper`s editorial board, "I meet regularly with leaders. I`m not isolated, I see people. My attendance is good, I put in the hours. We represent a huge state. And so I`m rather puzzled by all of this." She also said she does not plan on stepping down before her term ends in 2025.\par \par \uc2 The panel is here to talk some politics. Joining me now on set is (INAUDIBLE), Democratic strategist and president of the Center for \uc2 American Progress Action Fund\uc2 , Republican strategist, MSNBC political analyst Rick Tyler, and my NBC News colleague Ali Vitali who covers Capitol Hill.\par \par \uc2 Ali, I`ll simply ask you. You know, there`s -- you know, the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  regularly has folks that are up there in age. It`s been something when I got here, it was Strom Thurmond that we`d have these conversations about. There`s the conversation that everybody has on Capitol Hill and then there`s a published report like this. How surprising was this published report?\par \par \uc2 ALI VITALI, NBC NEWS CAPITOL HILL CORRESPONDENT: I think that that`s the moment where the conversations that happen on Capitol Hill sort of merge with the public conversation then because this is something that people have whispered about in the halls of Congress for some time now. I so think it also kind of comes back to the idea of what is the basics of being a senator. You see Feinstein in this statement saying I see people, I have attendance.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 Someone said to me today she`s not not doing the job, which is to say she`s showing up, she`s taking votes, she`s voting with a thumbs up on the things that Democrats need her to vote with a thumbs up on. But at the same time is it too much to ask that your senators can do more than that? And so that I think is the open question here.\par \par \uc2 There`s also the other piece of this where some people who would defend her are making this a question of gender saying that even though we`ve seen this with lawmakers before when it`s men like Senator Storm Thurmand or Ted Cochran, we seem to have a little bit more of an allowance for it.\par \par \uc2 What people are saying with Feinstein here is, are we just asking this of her because she`s a woman of a certain age, that`s part of this conversation, too, whether or not we`re being unfair on the gender piece.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. Navin, I -- here`s what I lament here is her legacy is unbelievable.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK? When you think back to the Harvey Milk assassination and her leadership here. You don`t want to see her leaving the stage become the dominant part of her legacy.\par \par \uc2 NAVIN NAYAK, PRESIDENT, \uc2 CENTER FOR AMERICAN PROGRESS\uc2  ACTION FUND: Yes, and that`s the part that`s actually pretty disappointing about this story which is these are people who in the story say they`re very concerned for her, they have all this respect for her. Why not go have a conversation with her?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Go to them or people around her first.\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: There`s ways to do this with more integrity. And to your point, I actually do think -- it`s hard not to respect her. She`s got such an unbelievable career both as mayor and they she stepped in that role. Obviously as a senator now. I find it really unbecoming that there are still these people who are, you know, say they respect her and like her, and feel the need to say these pretty nasty, harsh things and pretty demeaning. Like some of the -- you know, the lines that were in some of those stories, and how she`s being portrayed, not the kinds of things you should go talk to the press. I understand why the press is poking around on this. But I`m really surprised that it was sort of colleagues of hers in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It is interesting. You know, Rick, the one incumbent senator who`s running in their late 80s is \uc2 Chuck Grassley\uc2 . And he`s -- it`s been interesting, his polling is not good even though Republicans are doing just fine in Iowa. That`s not the issue. This does matter to voters. This really does, and I`ve seen it. Again, Grassley`s numbers are surprisingly soft and it appears to be the age.\par \par \uc2 RICK TYLER, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: I hate these stories like we all do. Right? And I think Navin is exactly right. It`s unbecoming, it`s undignified. And it lacks integrity because with Senator Feinstein it`s a power play. But for my opinion, they`re all too old, they all served too long and frankly they do too much. It`d be nice if we had a lot of old senators who did very little from the conservative point of view because most of what they`re getting done has not been good for the country. So there you go.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Look, it is a fair point. I mean, this brings up, you know, we don`t want to chase folks out. At the same time, you know, I do understand why there`s some cynicism. The Democratic Party right now, its entire leadership structure, \uc2 Chuck Schumer\uc2 , \uc2 Dick Durbin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 TYLER: Is old.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Number one and number one, both over the age of 70.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All three leaders in the House, 70 or 80. The president of the United States, come on. Harris is the youngest basically of the elected leadership, and nobody else. She`s the only one under 70.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Yes, but we also cover the 2020 campaign where age was not not a factor during that and there were plenty of people who were trying to come in with the fresh perspective for progressivism and voters said, no, thank you, I`ll take \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 . They had that choice. It was on the table. They said we don`t care.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, I think the age of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sort of negated that issue.\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: I also think, you know, this goes a little bit to the functionality. I mean, no one is going to put a finger to Speaker Pelosi and how focused she is, how competent she is. I mean, she will go down as one of the greatest speakers for sure in that role. And, you know, the age is just -- so I just think there`s less to do there and I think there`s a really impressive crop.\par \par \uc2 I mean, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ended up defeating a lot of people who were in their 40s and 50s. And so I don`t know that there`s that much of a concern from that point. I think this just might just be a moment in time for the party.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It is. And it`s -- again it`s a tricky thing because if you start, again, to bring your point, if you start questioning \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2 , by the way, the president of the United States is going to start getting this stuff and that`s something the party doesn`t want to have to deal with those questions here.\par \par \uc2 I want to shift gears a little bit and get to what Greg Abbott is up to, Rick. Is there a line -- you worked for Texas Republicans. I understand the politics he`s playing here. The overreach risk, the sort of using a publicity stunt, to drop folks off literally right outside the store, is there some risk here that he`s playing too much to the 2024 political primary and not enough for the state of Texas?\par \par \uc2 TYLER: In what regard? I mean --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, just sort of pushing this so far and looking like it`s just nothing but -- using people for political prop.\par \par \uc2 TYLER: And give me the example, maybe on the fallout.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Dropping -- yes, what he`s doing here. You don`t think that`s a publicity stunt that goes too far?\par \par \uc2 TYLER: Well, I think we got a lot of that in politics, but it`s not new. I mean, history is replete with people -- with political stunts. His job apparently is to win over the Republican base and then to be viable in the presidential election.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:01]\par \par \uc2 TODD: You think this is a 2024 play, not really a 2022 play?\par \par \uc2 TYLER: It`s both. Like he`s the governor of the largest state. His legacy would be secured if he ended there. But he clearly wants more. Otherwise why do publicity stunts?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Ali, have you seen this play at all on Capitol Hill, this one?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Well, they`ve been on recess, right? They`re gone for this week and for next. I think at the same time, though, with something like this, immigration has always been such a galvanizing issue. It`s the thing that Trump regularly would turn back to when things were going really badly in Washington. It`s the thing that would stir the base back to him.\par \par \uc2 I don`t know that anyone else can out-Trump Trump on immigration, though, and if this is a 2024 primary play, I don`t know that Greg Abbott is going to seem more Trumpy on the border and build the wall than \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is, right. So for all of these conservatives who are playing this shadow primary, whether it Kristy Noem in South Dakota on LGBTQ Plus issues, whether it`s Greg Abbott on immigration, all of them are going to run into the same thing, which is that you can`t out-Trump Trump. And so where does that leave you? If he doesn`t run, sure, it`s an open field for that mantle but if he does, I don`t think they`re going to get to the outside of him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`d be no. Is there a safe space for a Democrat on a battleground state on immigration right now?\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: Yes, I think there is. I mean, I think Democrats -- nothing has really changed in the problem writ large in the sense that people have thought for decades that our immigration system is broken. Democrats have been saying that. Democrats have tried for decades without any support from Republicans. You have to go back to 2013 when they were able to pass a bipartisan bill in the Senate, that now all of the Republicans (INAUDIBLE) because the party has pivoted to a very different party now.\par \par \uc2 This is a MAGA Republican Party. None of them are going to touch this issue. I think Democrats have a set of commonsense solutions that do involve securing the border, pushing more resources to help the agents around the border and actually finding a path to citizenship, which the American people still support.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it`s interesting here, though, 2013 drove a wedge inside the Republican Party on immigration.\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I`d argue right now there`s no divide on the Republican Party on immigration. The divide, look at this Title 42 conversation. That seems to be pretty upsetting to some progressives that you see more and more Democrats going, you know what, whoa, we need to reinstate that.\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: Yes, I think that is an unfortunate that we got entrapped by the policy that Trump put in place a couple of years ago. I think -- I don`t think this is a place where Democrats have to be on their back heels. I think we`ve --\par \par \uc2 TODD: At this point they`re acting like it, though, right?\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: No, I agree. I actually think that the solutions we have been offering for a decade around actually fixing our immigration system and doing it in a humane and fair way are still actually overwhelmingly popular. And the people that are animated by what Greg Abbott is doing or where Republicans are they`re never voting for \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 . They`re never voting for, you know, Senator (INAUDIBLE).\par \par \uc2 TODD: But independents look like Republicans on the immigration issue. You know, if independents look like Democrats on abortion, independents look like Republicans on immigration.\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: Right. And I think Democrats have to continue to be strong. Obviously, you know, I think the way that Republicans try to drive a wedge here is creating an open borders policy, which is obviously farcical and not anything where the Democratic Party is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Rick, the other -- going on your side of the aisle, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his endorsements and how they sort of seem to wreak the havoc that I think he wants, right. He became the center of attention in Pennsylvania last week. Now he`s the center of attention in the Ohio Senate primary. I have this theory that a Trump endorsement -- what you don`t want is Trump`s wrath. The endorsement, that might actually be a problem. As long as you can stay out of his crosshairs, is that almost the best place to be?\par \par \uc2 TYLER: Sticking on the (INAUDIBLE) model, right, just be close enough but just far enough away. And that`s what they`re all trying to do. But (INAUDIBLE) purposes, he doesn`t really -- he`s not out to save the Republican Party. He doesn`t care about putting the Republican Party in the majority.\par \par \uc2 TODD: He doesn`t care who the best nominee is in Ohio, right?\par \par \uc2 TYLER: No. In fact, he would not want -- I`ll just say this. He will not want a Republican to beat \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  or whoever the Democrat --\par \par \uc2 TODD: If it`s not Trump.\par \par \uc2 TYLER: Of course, because that would mean that person was a greater man or a greater woman than he is because he beat the Democrat whereas he lost. So he`s making -- all he wants is to be the -- as you say, the center of attention.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 TYLER: That`s his sole purpose. He gets in the middle of these primaries. He`s about -- I think there`s about been 60 races now on the NRCC`s target list and he`s going to endorse every single one of them.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And not always in the way the party wants.\par \par \uc2 TYLER: No. And he`s not going to pick the winners either. As I say, it`s not about winning, it`s about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  being in the center of attention, and are you loyal to me or not loyal to me?\par \par \uc2 VITALI: But also look at the kind of person that he`s endorsing. He`s endorsing the outsiders to the political game, the people who say the incendiary thing that people --\par \par \uc2 TYLER: Yes, but they have to be loyal.\par \par \uc2 VITALI: Of course. But they are the Trump model of person. The only difference is the political gravity rules apply to them and they don`t apply to him.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, if Pennsylvania and Ohio have anything in common, he leaned to the celebrities.\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: I was going to say --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. He sort of leaned to the celebrities.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 NAYAK: I think the reality is that, again, it comes back to Trump is a little bit of a side show to what`s really happening in this party is that the party and the base has moved dramatically in the last decade. It a different Republican Party.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:02]\par \par \uc2 All of them are trying to remake themselves around this MAGA brand whether it`s Trump or without Trump, they still have to tow that line.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Well, and we didn`t even get to all of the shifts to right on abortion policy that it seems like if the state legislature is in there they`re doing it, I`m very curious to see what happens when voters noticed these changes.\par \par \uc2 Navin, Rick, Ali, it`s good to talk a little politics. Good to have you on set.\par \par \uc2 Up next, new COVID concerns as cases spike ahead of the holiday weekend. Will new restrictions be put in place?\par \par \uc2 You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. It is Easter weekend and the first night of Passover, which means millions of Americans are traveling to celebrate with friends and family. This packed travel day comes just as the CDC announced earlier this week it will extend the transportation mask mandate for 15 more days after the original expiration date of this coming Monday, April 18th, telling Americans it`s continuing to monitor Omicron variants across the country.\par \par \uc2 This perhaps is just simply a let`s get through spring break and see what happens. U.S. cases overall are still relatively low but the moving average only rising a little more than 2 percent in the past two weeks. But cases are trending upward. The CDC reports that there`s been a 30 percent increase in new cases from last week to this week, and some areas are seeing even worse outbreaks.\par \par \uc2 Right here in Washington, D.C. cases are up 86 percent over the last two weeks and in New York the positivity rate for tests has reached 5 percent with cases up 72 percent in the last two weeks. But guess what hasn`t risen? Hospitalizations. And that may be the metric that matters the most.\par \par \uc2 Up next, \uc2 Michael Osterholm\uc2  joins us next with his perspective on what this all means. Keep it here.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:52:20]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As COVID cases grow ahead of this holiday weekend, I`m joined by \uc2 Michael Osterholm\uc2 , director of the Center for Infectious Disease Research and Policy at the \uc2 University of Minnesota\uc2 . He`s been advising the Biden administration COVID transition team. He did that last year, advises a lot of organizations, I believe the \uc2 NFL\uc2  and plenty, so, Dr. Osterholm, it`s good to see you.\par \par \uc2 So let me just start with this.\par \par \uc2 DR. \uc2 MICHAEL OSTERHOLM\uc2 , DIRECTOR, CENTER FOR INFECTIOUS DISEASE RESEARCH AND POLICY, \uc2 UNIVERSITY OF MINNESOTA\uc2 : Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What metrics are you looking at to decide whether we should start to put in restrictions or not?\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: Well, unfortunately the only reliable metric we have right now is hospitalizations, and as you`ve talked about it, Chuck, it`s a lagging indicator, meaning that it`s often several weeks after the fact when transmission occurs. But we`ve seen a turndown in the number of people being tested and access to testing, more and more people testing where people are being tested but not reporting results, and so I don`t have a lot of faith in a lot of the numbers that are coming out right now, the number of cases. And so this is a challenge for us and it`s not just true in the United States. We`re seeing that in a number of countries around the world.\par \par \uc2 TODD: No, I`m with you, I think all these Washington, D.C. positives are because there`s a culture of testing in the federal government that we don`t have in other places. Is the waste water testing basically -- is that a reliable metric and is that a more reliable metric than trying to -- since we don`t have a testing system that`s accurate?\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: Well, it is surely one method. I look at it almost like a triangular in a forest fire with three different fire towers in the old days. It`s, one, by itself doesn`t give you enough measurement to really say with certainty what`s going to happen. So I think surely the waste water data are very helpful, but I think the message we have to get across here is why are we trying to count cases so we know what to do?\par \par \uc2 Well, if we can`t count cases, then we have to accept the fact that in many instance we`ll be behind the eight ball on this issue and that`s exactly what`s happening right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: You know, it seems as if at the end of the day, and maybe I`m channeling my inner libertarianism here. We know what to do. We know to get a vaccine. We know to get a booster. We know that if you want to cut your chances you should wear a mask in a crowded place. Do you think given the public`s exhaustion that that`s the best the federal government can do these days, is like, look, we`ve told you everything that works and what we`ll do is we`ll let you know whether there`s a spike in your community or not?\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: Well, you know, I would go one step further and say not only might that be your libertarian view, but it`s only the practical advice we have.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: And I think that`s what we have to understand is the public is done with this pandemic. They already have moved on. The virus just hasn`t moved on from them. So I think the best we can do is just what you just said.\par \par \uc2 [13:55:03]\par \par \uc2 TODD: How do you keep yourself safe? How do you live your life?\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: Well, you know, first of all, I accept the fact that I very well may eventually contract COVID, but just as you discussed just now, I have my four doses of vaccine, and like the Gridiron Dinner, which I think in many ways signals a success, the fact of the matter is, there were a number of people there who got infected, who are older, who would be at high risk for a serious illness, hospitalization even death, and to my knowledge we`ve not seen one serious illness come from that.\par \par \uc2 That has a lot to do with the fact that the vaccines may not prevent cases but they surely can have a big impact on serious illness, hospitalizations and deaths. And so that`s what we have to keep striving for. Now the real, you know, curveball on all of this, that you and I have talked about for two years, is what is the next variant going to bring?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: What does that mean? We have these two new variants, BA.4 and BA.5 which now appear to have an increased ability to evade immune protection. That -- if that happens, you know, we`re going to be back in a whole new ballgame. But for now vaccination, vaccination, and as you said, use adequate respiratory protection. That`s the best we can do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And the fact that there weren`t hospitalizations, does that tell you that really that maybe our safeguards are as good as we have, that you might contract a cold, that`s something you`re going to have to live with?\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: Well, in that case, definitely. But let me just be really clear. And you know, there are three really prominent words that have become first and center in my vocabulary. I don`t know. And I think we have to understand that, that we are going to see --\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: -- ongoing transmission, and that`s a problem.\par \par \uc2 TODD: \uc2 Michael Osterholm\uc2 , whose advice is something that a lot of American organizations turn to to figure out how to manage this pandemic. It`s good to hear your perspective so thank you.\par \par \uc2 OSTERHOLM: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that does it for us this hour. We`ll be back Monday with more MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I hope you enjoy this holiday weekend with friends and family. And if it`s Sunday, we`re going to be there, MEET THE PRESS. It`s on your local NBC News station. Don`t miss it.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC`s coverage continues with Katy Tur who`ll be interviewing the State Department`s spokesperson \uc2 Ned Price\uc2  right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSNMPD0020220416ei4f00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW for April 14, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Maddow \par \uc2 7,847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 April 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: The Rachel Maddow Show\par \uc2 TRMADS\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [21:00:08]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS HAYES, MSNBC HOST: That is "ALL IN" on this Thursday night.\par \par \uc2 THE RACHEL MADDOW SHOW starts right now.\par \par \uc2 Good, evening Rachel.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Chris. Thank you, my friend. Much appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 And thanks to you at home for joining us at this hour.\par \par \uc2 It is a landmark decision today by the National Republican Party. This is something Chris was just covering.\par \par \uc2 I think it`s a landmark decision that the Republican Party has said that they will no longer participate in presidential debates. It will go down as a landmark decision I think in political history, but I think it was probably inevitable.\par \par \uc2 I mean, the modern presidential debate structure that the Republicans are now opting out of that they`re deciding to kill off with this decision today, is a process that puts journalists formally into the electoral process. Journalist served as moderators of the presidential debates. And that`s what the Republicans have decided that they can no longer abide.\par \par \uc2 It really is just an amazing thing for the Republican Party to have decided this today with this unanimous vote at the \uc2 Republican National Committee\uc2 , but honestly, you really could see it coming if you are looking in the right direction. I mean, once the president of that party, the leader of that party declared that the free press of the United States was at his words the enemy of the people, once the Republican Party decided that American free press is bad, that journalism something they can do only without, that they`re against as a party.\par \par \uc2 Well, this is where that ends, right? They`re not going to allow some formal role for the protests in the electoral process. They`re not going to do debates with journalist asking questions, not when they`ve decided that as a party, they`re against journalism. They`re against the press, the press is the enemy.\par \par \uc2 I mean, we`ve seen all of these other sort of norms around this, traditions around this fall by the wayside without must commentary. The leader of their last president wouldn`t even do the White House correspondents dinner which is a comedy show.\par \par \uc2 Under the previous president, they had the White House spokesperson stopped doing briefings for the process. They invited rally-goers to scream and curse at reporters and camera crews who were on site covering the president`s rallies. This is -- it`s been clear where they have where they`ve been going with this.\par \par \uc2 But, yeah, Republican set formal day there will be no more presidential debates. Not with their candidates. This is a landmark thing, but it was I think it was a quick and direct trip to that landmark, once Republicans under Trump declared that journalism is the enemy and as far as they`re concerned it must be stopped.\par \par \uc2 So, I don`t know that there`s much more to said about that. What`s done is now done. But let the record show that`s exactly why it happened.\par \par \uc2 There`s a lot of interesting things, I think some other landmark things in the news today. Today, former President Trump`s domestic advisor \uc2 Stephen Miller\uc2 , he had fought a subpoena from the January 6th investigation for months. He had even sued them to try to avoid testifying, but today, he testified to the January 6 commission. We`ll have more on that later, January 6 investigation, forgive me.\par \par \uc2 "The San Francisco Chronicle" today published a brutal, very long piece today quoting lots, and lots of across -- Democratic sources in California and the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 . All claiming to various degrees that Senator \uc2 Dianne Feinstein\uc2  of California is mentally no longer up to the job of being senator. The claims are basically that presumably because of age, she is too out of it too frequently to continue to hold the job.\par \par \uc2 Now, again, these are unnamed sources and Senator Feinstein`s office is vociferously denying these claims. She`s also being defended very aggressively by fellow senators and by House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . Fellow Californian, fellow San Franciscan.\par \par \uc2 But this is the second article along these lines about Senator Feinstein. The first was a little over a year ago from Jane Mayer in "The New Yorker Magazine". After this "Chronicle" piece today, there has not been widespread reaction to these allegations yet in Washington. Presumably that`s because the Senate isn`t in session right now, I`m assuming that there is no senator who wants to stick their head over the parapet on this one.\par \par \uc2 But this is a really sensitive subject, not just for Senator Feinstein, but for a bunch of other senators. I mean, yeah, she`s 88 but there are six other U.S. senators who are in their 80s right now. So, like I said not much reaction yet on Capitol Hill to this very difficult reporting on Senator Feinstein in "The San Francisco Chronicle" about her mental state, particularly about our short term memory.\par \par \uc2 But this is a pretty real depth charge by an institution that does not really like contending with this issue at all, and for which this issue looms large if we are honest about it.\par \par \uc2 [21:05:02]\par \par \uc2 So, we shall see how that emerges. It`s worth keeping an eye on. It`s worth reading that piece if you haven`t yet.\par \par \uc2 And one other note, it was -- it was just about this time last night here on the show when we started to get more credible but still unconfirmed reports about this ship, reports that the Russians most important naval vessel in the war against Ukraine had been hit or damage somehow, or might be out of commission.\par \par \uc2 Well, today, that ship, the Moskva, which is the Russian for Moscow, that ship sank. Over the course of the last few days while this has been covered, you`ve probably heard the Moskva described as the flagship as Russia`s fleet in the Black Sea. Being flagship means that it`s the most important, usually the most capable, highest profile ship in any fleet.\par \par \uc2 In this case, the Moskva was gigantic. I mean, if the Ukrainians claim that they sank it with a line to see missile are correct, the Moskva might be the largest ship ever sunk by a missile. It certainly the largest warship of any kind some since World War II.\par \par \uc2 This is a guided missile cruiser, it has a crew of 500, it`s more than 600 feet long, and it`s now gone. The Russian ministry of defense is now confirming that it has sunk.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to have some expert help here in a minute just to get to the practical significance of that development, as well as the new news today that new Europe might pull out, perhaps, the biggest weapon that they have against Russia. This is absolutely unthinkable, even just earlier in the course of the war, but Europe today started to consider an embargo, an outright ban on Russian oil.\par \par \uc2 This is the -- this is as big as it gets in terms of punishing Russia. Russia is a petrol state, they assume they can have their way in the world because nobody what they did, no matter what`s violence or nefarious ends they pursued anywhere in the world, other countries would have to let it slide because nobody can forgo Russian oil.\par \par \uc2 Well, after 50 days of Russia bombarding Ukraine, and Ukraine begging the world, begging Europe in particular to cut Russia off, to stop funding the Russian state by buying their oil, today, Europe finally started to draft an embargo that would cut off the Russian state, by stopping buying their oil. They said it couldn`t be done.\par \par \uc2 But, Europe has started that process today. There is no larger economic measure that could be taken against Russia, oil and gas is the existential lifeblood of their sclerotic, backward economy. If it is cut off, Russia as we know it will cease to exist. We will have expert help on that, and much more coming up tonight.\par \par \uc2 But I want to start us off tonight, here at home, with a headline that is maybe not a surprise but it has come sooner than any of us have expected. Here it is tonight from "The Associated Press". Quote: Abortions stop in Kentucky as groups seek to block new law. Just read that one more time, abortions stop in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 At the end of last month, Republicans in Kentucky passed a bill to make it harder for women to get abortions in that state. Previously, Kentucky had a law in the books that you couldn`t get enough abortion there if your pregnancy was more than 20 weeks along. This new law passed last week ruled it back even farther, said that you can longer get an abortion in the state if you`re pregnancy is 15 weeks along or further. They will get back from 20 to 15 weeks.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky Republicans, of course, are not the first to pass that kind of legislation. They will not be the last. But unlike in most of the other states with Republican-controlled legislators, Kentucky has a governor who was a Democrat. And after the Kentucky Republicans passed that 15-week abortion ban to the legislature last month, the state`s Democratic governor, \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2 , he vetoed it.\par \par \uc2 But last night, the Republican-led legislator in Kentucky voted to override the governor`s veto. And the practical consequence of that is very important for our country, because it means that that new abortion ban in Kentucky is now in effect. There is no grace period, there is no planned implementation date for the future for, it the law takes effect immediately. It`s operational today, right now.\par \par \uc2 And it turns out, it`s much more than just a 15-week ban. There is already only two clinics that provide abortions in the state of Kentucky, they`re both located in Louisville. Tonight, both of those clinics say that because of the way that the law is written, not just because of the 15-week ban, but because of the other restrictions and the bill that are designed to make it basically impossible for anyone to perform any legal abortion in the state, those two last clinics in the state tell us tonight that there is no way for them to comply with this new law, which again, went into effect today with the veto override.\par \par \uc2 So the last two clinics in Kentucky are announcing that, as of now, they had to close their doors. They are no longer able to provide abortions because of this new law as of today. Which means, if we are talking about landmarks, today is the landmark because today, for the first time since Roe v. Wade, Supreme Court decision in 1973, today, the first American state has gone dark. To outright block all legal access to abortions in the state, full stop.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky is the first one. It`s the first state with no legal access to abortion since Roe versus Wade in 1973.\par \par \uc2 Now, as you know, as we`v