par \par \uc2 Imani, The \uc2 Washington Post\uc2  writes this about Judge Jackson`s historic confirmation. Jackson`s presence will go a long way toward President Biden`s stated goal of a court that looks more like America. But it won`t impact for now one that is ideologically stacked for conservatives, caught in a political cross Fire as intense as any before and facing a host of issues that will stoke rather than dissipate partisan dissatisfaction, end of quote.\par \par \uc2 How do you expect Justice Jackson to fit into the overall makeup of the court when it`s still a 6-3 conservative court?\par \par \uc2 IMANI GANDY, REWIRE NEWS GROUP SENIOR EDITOR, LAW & POLICY: I think it`s fairly clear that she is going to be on the liberal wing of the court with other justices, Sotomayor and Kagan. And I do understand that people are hesitant to really get as excited as they should be about Justice Jackson now because of the fact that we do have the six conservative federalist society capture justices who seem to vote in lock step.\par \par \uc2 But what people have to remember is that this is a long game. Right? Conservatives have spent decades packing the court, stacking the courts with conservative ideologues. And so right now what liberals and Democrats are trying to do, and what Biden has been successful in doing specially with respect to lower a pallet courts, is to try to make the courts look more like what the makeup of the country looks like and to nominate and appoint judges and justices who will be able to connect the law, these sort of high-brow constitutional principles to people. And Justice Sotomayor is very good at doing that and I expect that Jackson will be amazing at that as well.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, one of the issues Judge Jackson will face when she becomes justice -- Associate Justice Jackson is abortions. This weekend, murder charges were dropped against a 26-year-old Texas woman who was arrested for what authorities called, quote, the death of an individual by self-induced abortion.\par \par \uc2 Though it was unclear whether, Ms. Herrera was unclear whether was accused of having an abortion or whether she helped someone else get a termination. Now, in a statement released a short time ago, the star county district attorney said, and I`m quoting, in reviewing applicable Texas law, it is clear that Ms. Herrera cannot and should not be prosecuted for the allegation against her. The issues surrounding this matter are clearly contentious. However, based on Texas law and the facts presented, it is not a criminal matter, end of quote.\par \par \uc2 Despite is D.A.`s decision, the arrest itself raises questions and concerns about the growing focus in red states like Texas on targeting not only abortion providers but of those seeking abortions. And the issue seems likely to be taking up again by the Supreme Court in the future.\par \par \uc2 [17:45:01]\par \par \uc2 Your thoughts?\par \par \uc2 GANDY: Well, we don`t really know what Judge or Justice Jackson will do on abortion, because she hasn`t really had an opportunity to dig into those issues but I suspect that she will fall on the Kagan, Sotomayor, you know in the \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2  legacy, and \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  as well. I expect her to fall on the side of Roe vs. Wade should not be overturned. Obviously, she won`t be on the bench win that decision comes down in a couple of weeks but I expect her to be in that vein when it comes to abortion politics.\par \par \uc2 The issue with respect to what`s going in Texas is that it is a continuation of the criminalization of people for having bad pregnancy outcomes. This is not new. This is something that has been going on for quite some time. The difference is that anti-choicers, they anti-choice advocates and politicians and activists know that they have won. They know that Roe is going down. They know that they`ve captured the court. They know that even though the American people don`t want Roe 