nd after a string of successes of local reformers, we are now in a period of inevitable reactionary backlash. That`s what we`re living through.\par \par \uc2 Now, well, fear-mongering about crime and violence is politically resonant, even when it doesn`t reflect reality, even as crime is going down, it sure as heck is more destructive, and more resonant when crime actually is on the rise.\par \par \uc2 And to be clear, it definitely is. This is not really in dispute anymore. I mean, over the past few years, homicide rates have spiked dramatically, in some cases, to record levels.\par \par \uc2 And while during the pandemic, other kinds of crime, property crime declined during the worst stages when people were just staying home. A lot of that was because there were just fewer people on the streets. And now seems like many of those rates are increasing, as well.\par \par \uc2 Now, there is a question about what`s going on? Why did crime spike in the 90s? And then go down? We don`t really know what is driving the rise in crime right now.\par \par \uc2 Answering that question in a rigorous, clear-eyed way is going to be crucial in both designing policy to address it, to reduce the very real harm it causes, and also to avoid so many of the mistakes that we made in the 1990s, when the Tom Cotton kind of rhetoric created mass incarceration and the generation and generations of harm that followed.\par \par \uc2 Because you see, conservatives are pointing to the few gains that have been made on criminal justice reform as proof. Look, this is what happens. Crime rises. When you listen to liberals, we reformed bail practices, so people don`t rot in a cell because they can`t pay their way out. We reform policing or let nonviolent offenders out of prison.\par \par \uc2 Look, it`s a simple and appealing message. But I will also say, it is one with a pretty testable hypothesis. Again, if we want to think rigorously here.\par \par \uc2 Republicans and conservatives argue that cities that reform their policies, whether by cutting police funding, or the number of police on the street, would lead to more crime, would see a rise in homicide and other crimes.\par \par \uc2 And we`ve got a lot of data to see if that`s true or not that testable hypothesis. And one example for instance, a place where it is true is the city of Austin, Texas, which cut its police budget and did see a spike in homicides last year that happened.\par \par \uc2 But guess what, so did Columbus, Ohio, which actually increased the number of uniformed police officers between 20 and 21.\par \par \uc2 In Baton Rouge, Louisville, the Baton Rouge and Louisville and Toledo and Tucson, city leaders publicly rejected calls from activists to defund the police or reduce police budgets. And in fact, police funding increased in all of those cities, often by million dollars and yet, violent crime surging all of them last year.\par \par \uc2 [20:35:09]\par \par \uc2 There are many more examples like this. The spike in interpersonal violence and in crime is happening everywhere, big cities and small, it is happening in very conservative places. It is happening in liberal places. It is happening under so-called reformed prosecutors who are attempting to be more selective about who gets thrown in jail and how long the sentences they asked for are.\par \par \uc2 Like San Francisco`s Chesa Boudin who is recently facing a recall election. And it`s happening under Sacramento`s \uc2 Anne Marie Schubert\uc2 , who is just an old-school tough-on-crime prosecutor.\par \par \uc2 In fact, Schubert is running for California Attorney General on a pretty old school reactionary (PH) law and order platform, even as their own city has seen a rise in crime. Just experienced this tragic mass shooting earlier this week, which left six dead and 12 more injured.\par \par \uc2 And it`s not just California, John Pfaff, professor of criminal law at \uc2 Fordham University\uc2  looked at data from 69 Police Department