 home in the middle of Miami.\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: Weren`t their due diligence checks made?\par \par \uc2 GONZALEZ: Well, they`re supposed to be due diligence check made and obviously not all of them were done.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN (voice-over): The program which ended last may save millions of real jobs. But prosecutors say alleged criminals kept it from helping even more.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO TAPE)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Ken Dilanian and joins us now with more. Ken, you have got to be a special flavor of Craven to steal from taxpayers in a once in a lifetime pandemic. This PPP program was a finite amount of money. If you took money, somebody else wasn`t going to get any. What was it about this program that led to all this fraud? There was even internet tutorials teaching people how to scam the government.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: That`s right, Steph, and thanks for highlighting this. You`ve been doing a great job on this COVID fraud problem. This program was designed on the honor system, that`s the bottom line. The lenders were instructed to take the borrower`s word for everything they represented, the size of their payroll, how many employees they had, whether they had a felony conviction, whether they were qualified, they just shoveled the money out the door.\par \par \uc2 So for a lot of these fraudsters, it was like they found piles of cash on the street, literally, like they couldn`t believe their luck that banks were just sending them millions of dollars. In some cases, I mean, people steal 17, 18, $20 million as part of this program by just simply bombarding these lenders with fake companies and fake employees.\par \par \uc2 And they spent the money on Lamborghinis and Ferraris and mansions and yachts, and probably a lot of other things that we haven`t found yet. Because the reality is that a lot of people have been caught, but many, many more people will not be caught, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: OK, then the billions and billions in fraud that people know about, they`re concerned about, is this going to stand in the way of Congress being able to get more COVID relief money approved with so many people knowing there was so much misuse of the almost $5 trillion that went out?\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: I think common sense tells you it will give some people pause, some lawmakers pause and maybe it should. I mean, it feels like the country -- we as a country have not come to grips with what a massive scandal this was that bureaucrats and the Trump administration design these programs in a way that they can be plundered. And we`ve known about this for some time.\par \par \uc2 NBC News is calling attention to it. But these cases have been out there, Stephanie, you`ve covered it. I`ve covered it. Billions of dollars gone. Where are the congressional hearings? Where`s the outrage? Maybe we should get our house in order before we start, you know, sending billions more dollars out and really, we should make sure that it`s not going to get stolen.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Are they actually answering for that because while it was the Trump administration, and while Trump was on sort of this spree to get rid of all different inspectors, generals who would be overseeing these kinds of things at the same time, Congress didn`t staff the Cares Act oversight committee.\par \par \uc2 DILANIAN: That`s right. And Congress wrote the law that said hand this money out quickly and no, there has not been accountability for that. And while that, well, Trump did get rid of some watchdogs, the \uc2 Small Business Administration\uc2  Inspector General was warning just months after this law was passed, that he was seeing fraud. He was warning the Trump administration. He later talk to the Biden administration. There really wasn`t very much done until much, much too late, Stephanie.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Didn`t seem like months after, it seemed like weeks he was writing white papers saying fraud is going to be everywhere. We need to do something. And we didn`t.\par \par \uc2 Ken Dilanian, I`m so glad you`re here and covering this really important stuff. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 Coming up next, \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  back at the White House for the first time since he left office. He says Democrats have a story to tells. So how should they tell it will ask his former Press Secretary Robert Gibbs from THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:48:41]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FMR. U.S. PRESIDENT: Vice President Biden. Vice president -- that was a joke. I heard some changes had been made by the current president since I was last year. Apparently, Secret Service agents have to wear aviator glasses now. The Navy mess has been replaced by Baskin Robbins.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 A lot of smiles in the White House. Former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  returned to the White House for the first time since he left office to show his support for a Biden administration initiative to fix a coverage glitch in the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: That ladies and gentlemen is what happens when you have an administration that`s committed to making a program work. And today. Today, the Biden-Harris administration is going even further by moving to fix a glitch in the regulations that will lower premiums for nearly 1 million people who need it and allow 200,000 more uninsured Americans get access to coverage.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Let`s welcome Robert Gibbs, former campaign senior advisor and White House press secretary under President \uc2 Obama\uc2 . Robert, I watched you smile while we saw President \uc2 Obama\uc2  speaking people fired up when they see him there talking about the Affordable Care Act, the huge achievement that it was.\par \par \uc2 [23:50:09]\par \par \uc2 But does \uc2 Obama\uc2 `s presence alongside President Biden translate into votes? The midterms around the corner?\par \par \uc2 ROBERT GIBBS, FMR. OBAMA WHITE HOUSE PRESS SECRETARY: Well, I certainly think it helps with certain voters, it helps with Democrats who are reminded of the things that each of these to accomplish during eight years of the \uc2 Obama\uc2  Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 And I think, look, today`s event is important, as you talked about, this is about reducing health care costs for Americans. That`s top of mind for every voter right now, in this country, it`s the leading issue. And every day, this White House and President Biden are talking about reducing costs on American families is a day they`re talking about an issue that`s top of mind for voters.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Money is always top of mind. So just think about this. We`ve got unemployment at 3.6 percent and dropping. The cruise industry just had its best week ever. We`ve got a White House housing -- white hot housing market, yet consumer sentiment today is as low as it was in 2009, after the financial crisis.\par \par \uc2 So clearly, inflation woes seem to be the only thing on our money minds. So what does Biden do? He`s got a lot of good facts, but not many good feelings. How does he solve for it?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Right. Well, Stephanie, I think like we faced in 2009, and 2010, you can`t Jawbone your way into people feeling better, you`re not going to dazzle them with statistics. But I do think continuing to do things that show action and cutting costs.\par \par \uc2 Just last week, the House of Representatives passed a bill to cap the price of insulin on prescription drugs, garnering even a dozen Republican votes. I think it`s things like that working on gas prices, trying to keep them low, cost of living kitchen table issues, doing not just telling, I think is what is going to get this White House back on track seven months before an election.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: \uc2 Obama\uc2  also -- President \uc2 Obama\uc2  also weighed in on the midterms today, and I want to share what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 PETER ALEXANDER, NBC NEWS WHITE HOUSE CORRESPONDENT: Mr. President, what do you say to Democrats worried about the midterms? What do you tell the Democrats worried about the midterms?\par \par \uc2 OBAMA: We got a story to tell, just got to tell it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: So there you go. Your point, if you`ve got a story to tell, tell it where should he do that, for example, the bipartisan infrastructure bill, that`s now law. We don`t talk about it much. And it was huge.\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yes, I think there`s no doubt. I think, you know, I think you`d like to see President Biden out on the road talking a lot about these issues, highlighting where that money is going to rebuild roads and bridges to make infrastructure better. Again, how do we get into -- how to Democrats get into a fight on Capitol Hill about making prescription drugs more affordable, making college more affordable, making gas prices more affordable, all of those things.\par \par \uc2 I think is a contrast that this White House and Democrats are going to have to put in front of the American people if they`re going to be successful. If not, they`re going to drive by, they`re going to see gas prices, they`re going to see the receipts at the grocery store, and they`re going to be reminded that things are more expensive. So I think it really is not just about telling it`s doing, it`s showing, it`s that action.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Well, former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  was also sort of joking, but reminding us sort of America loves regular Joe joking about ice cream and aviators. But can President Biden tap into that regular Joe brand, when right now he`s a wartime president?\par \par \uc2 GIBBS: Yes, it`s certainly difficult. And I think his travel outside of Washington is likely been curtailed in a whole lot of meetings in the Situation Room and discussions with foreign leaders. But I think as we get closer to this election, and I think even now, as much as the president can get out as much as he can bring that sort of plain spoken, you know, sort of Pennsylvania Joe to the American people, I think those are his best days. I think when he`s empathetic, when he understands the plight of Americans and talks about the plan. He has to deal with it. That`s when it feels good.\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: Scranton, Pennsylvania, his hometown. Robert Gibbs, always great to have you here. I appreciate you joining us.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, another Republican led state approves in your total abortion ban ahead of a key Supreme Court decision. This matters. And we are all over it when THE 11TH HOUR continues.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [23:58:50]\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: The last thing before we go tonight, reproductive rights in a last minute addition to the agenda with little discussion and no debate. The Oklahoma House voted overwhelmingly to approve legislation making performing an abortion illegal abortion rights supporters went to the state capitol to protest.\par \par \uc2 Under the bill those who perform abortions could face up to 10 years in prison and a $100,000 fine. It is a near total ban on abortion, except to save the life of a pregnant woman in in medical emergency, and has already passed the state Senate and is expected to be signed by Republican governor \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Reproductive rights had been under attack in Republican led states across the country ahead of the Supreme Court`s coming reconsideration of the landmark Roe vs. Wade decision.\par \par \uc2 The State of Colorado however, has taken action on the other side to guarantee the right to an abortion and other reproductive rights. Democratic governor \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2  said this at the signing of the Reproductive health Equality Act yesterday.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. JARED POLIS (D) COLORADO: This bill codifies a person`s right to make reproductive health care decisions free from government interference. In the state of Colorado, the very serious decision to start a pregnancy or to end a pregnancy with medical assistance remains between a person, their doctor and their faith.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 RUHLE: This matters, a decision from the Supreme Court on the future of Roe vs. Wade is expected this summer, America will be watching.\par \par \uc2 And on that very serious note, I wish you all a very good night. From all of our colleagues across the networks of NBC News, thank you for staying up late with us. I`ll see you at the end of tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document BRIWIL0020220406ei4500001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 MEET THE PRESS DAILY for March 30, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Chuck Todd, Richard Engel, Keir Simmons, Michael McFaul \par \uc2 8,797 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 March 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Meet the Press Daily\par \uc2 MSNMPD\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 CQ-Roll Call, Inc. All rights reserved. Prepared by CQ-Roll Call, Inc. \par \par \uc2 [13:00:00]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 CHUCK TODD, MSNBC HOST: If it`s Tuesday. New attacks hit Kyiv in Northern Ukraine in the exact same areas Russia claimed it was easing its assault. As new U.S. intelligence warns Putin might be losing his grasp on the reality of this conflict.\par \par \uc2 Plus, President Biden speaks with President Zelenskyy as talks between Russia and Ukraine continue. We`ll speak with the former president of Ukraine about the future of this conflict as he navigates this crisis from the middle of a war zone.\par \par \uc2 And later, what the current political environment means for the president and his party heading into November, we`re going to head into the board for a brand new edition of the MEET THE PRESS MIDTERM METER along with new numbers from our NBC News poll.\par \par \uc2 Welcome to MEET THE PRESS DAILY. I am Chuck Todd. We just received a readout about President Biden`s call this morning with President Zelenskyy. According to the White House, the two leaders spoke for just under an hour. They discussed specific defensive support, financial and humanitarian aid, and another round of sanctions to hit Russia and perhaps Belarus.\par \par \uc2 The conversation came about 24 hours after President Biden spoke with his European counterparts about the current state of the war in Ukraine. And as it`s become increasingly clear that Russia`s words are not matching its actions, I know, a shock to the system there, despite Russia`s vow to drastically reduce military activity around Kyiv and another region around Ukraine, the shelling has continued. You`ll see that in our coverage today.\par \par \uc2 U.S. Officials are saying it is evident that Russia is not withdrawing troops, it is simply repositioning them. This comes as the latest rounds of peace talks between Russia and Ukraine wrapped up in Turkey. Ukrainian negotiators say the two sides discussed security guarantees that would be enforced by outside countries.\par \par \uc2 Russian negotiator is saying there was progress, but no major breakthroughs. But as the West anticipates Moscow`s next move, it`s becoming apparent that Moscow itself may not be sure what its next move is either. Over the past 24 hours, Russia has hinted at off-ramps, repositioning forces, and yet renewed its air assault.\par \par \uc2 One Russian negotiator told reporters that Ukraine has stated a willingness to meet Russia`s core demands, but we actually still don`t know what those core demands actually are. They change. And a U.S. official provided NBC News with de-classified intelligence suggesting that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  is actually being misinformed by his advisors about how badly its military is performing. And that there`s a persistent tension between Putin and his ministry of defense. Essentially they`re delivering him their own version of fake news. Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  addressed this intelligence a short time ago.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: 14 seconds we`re going to --\par \par \uc2 ANTONY BLINKEN\uc2 , U.S. SECRETARY OF STATE: One of the Achilles heels of autocracies, is that you don`t have people in those systems who speak truth to power or will have the ability to speak truth to power. And I think that is something that we`re seeing in Russia.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Joining me now, Richard Engel is in Kharkiv, Ukraine, Keir Simmons is in Istanbul where the negotiations between Russia and Ukraine had been taking place, and I`m also joined by Michael McFaul, the former U.S. Ambassador to Russia and NBC International Affairs Analyst.\par \par \uc2 Let me start in the war zone with Richard. Richard, I`m not going to give anything away here. About an hour ago, I was speaking with the former President Poroshenko, with still daylight and we heard -- you know he was - - he`s in Kyiv and he heard shelling as he was talking to me. He`s in the northern part of the city. No evidence to him that anything is in retreat. What do you see?\par \par \uc2 RICHARD ENGEL, MSNBC CHIEF FOREIGN CORRESPONDENT: Well, we are seeing that Russia is suffering losses. We went to a village just on the outskirts of Kharkiv earlier today. And to get to the village, we passed a -- what had been a Russian position and it was completely decimated. And it had been decimated by Ukrainian artillery. They said they used the Javelins, and then they closed in and finished the position off with a small arms fire.\par \par \uc2 There were about 120 Russian troops in this position, numerous armored vehicles. We counted a dozen bodies, Russian soldiers killed, on the ground. And they left in a hurry all of their equipment, all of their food, their ration packs were all just spilled out on the ground. And Ukrainian troops were there trying to salvage whatever thing they could that was useful. Because the Ukrainians knocked out this Russian position and you could see the burned-out Russian vehicles with the big Z on the -- on side of them, the Ukrainian forces were able to liberate a nearby village and they were able to push the Russians out and we saw families were coming out for the first time today.\par \par \uc2 And we spoke to one family that has been living in a bunker beneath the house for 27 days, and today was the first time they went outside into the -- into the daylight. And we`re surveying what was left of the village. The village has been significantly damaged. So these are exactly the kinds of images that it seems that \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  isn`t being told about, that his generals are too afraid to tell him about. And it is the kind of images that is -- that are giving Ukrainians a tremendous sense of confidence and a tremendous sense of momentum.\par \par \uc2 [13:05:00]\par \par \uc2 The attacks are continuing. The Russian attacks are continuing here. They`re continuing in Kyiv, but they don`t seem to be taking territory. It`s been quite a bit of time now, quite -- several weeks in fact, since we have heard about a major Russian advance that has succeeded in taking new territory.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Richard, given that they can`t take any territory outside the -- they`re -- basically the coastal south and East that they`ve been in attacking is there -- it feels like if you`re in the Ukrainian position here during negotiations, you`re not -- they are the ones that have the advantage here because they might think, hey, if we keep pressing, we can start taking territory back.\par \par \uc2 ENGEL: That`s exactly what the Ukrainian soldiers we were talking to today told us. That they`re watching the negotiations, but they`re not holding their breath. The fact they don`t trust the Russians is a given, but they`ve not -- they don`t feel they`re ready to negotiate yet. They feel they are continuing to take territory, they think with more support, with more weapons, the weapons are still coming in, that they can push the Russians to an even weaker position. They don`t want to give up any territory.\par \par \uc2 And when you speak to Ukrainian soldiers, they feel they have the win to their back. They want to take back Crimea, they want to take back Donbass, they hope that this war will lead to the collapse of \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2 . These are still early days. We are only 30-plus days into the fight, but right now, the Ukrainian troops feel that they have the momentum. That could be a dangerous feeling. They could be feeling overconfident and that could lead them to make mistakes. But right now, based on what we saw with our own eyes just on the outskirts of this village today, their confidence seems to be -- seems to be merited.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And that likely will influence the negotiating process. Richard Engel, who is in Kharkiv right now, giving us the firsthand report, Richard, thank you. Let me move to Keir Simmons who`s still in Istanbul. First of all, Keir, when is the next meeting scheduled? There was this idea that the two foreign ministers, Russia`s and Ukraine`s, would meet face-to- face, that would be step two of whatever progress everybody claimed they made, but the reality is that it doesn`t look like any progress was actually made. What can you tell us, Keir?\par \par \uc2 KEIR SIMMONS, MSNBC SENIOR INTERNATIONAL CORRESPONDENT: Yes, I think 24 hours later, it is looking a little less hopeful than perhaps it looks yesterday. I guess that really is to be expected. There isn`t another meeting, as far as I know, scheduled. Certainly, not a meeting scheduled between the two foreign ministers or indeed between President Zelenskyy and President Putin, not scheduled yet.\par \par \uc2 Of course, the Russians have seemed a little more open to that idea. I think what you`re seeing, Richard there talking about no retreat on the battlefield if you like, what you`re seeing is both sides kind of retreat into more uncompromising positions. So the Ukrainians are repeating that they would want to see the Russians go back to the positions they were in outside Ukraine before this conflict started.\par \par \uc2 The Russians at the same time, saying that they -- for example won`t negotiate over Crimea, as the Ukrainians have suggested because they wrapped Crimea into their constitution. So there are always going to be enormous hurdles here. There is, of course, the question about whether either side is really ready to do any kind of a deal. And I think we`re already seeing that play out right now.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If -- you know this was-- I saw President Erdogan was given a lot of credit on this. I`m just curious as we`ve watched the last 24 hours, you know, is there -- is there any sort of explanation of actions not meeting the words?\par \par \uc2 SIMMONS: Well, I think there are those who, Chuck, believe -- there are many who believe that you just need to get to a ceasefire, you just need to get to a peace deal because you need to stop the bloodshed. And then there`s the overarching questioning over what kind of a deal would it be and how ensuring would it be? And then it goes into all of those questions about you know how much territory would Russians be allowed to keep and a lot kind of thing.\par \par \uc2 And then you have the question about President Putin and I think it`s interesting when it comes to President Putin. We heard Dmitry Peskov become a spokesman in a briefing today talked about Crimea but most he says I don`t want to comment on the negotiations. Again and again, you hear the Russians say they don`t want to have public negotiations. It`s interesting to interpret that.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to interpret that this way, Chuck. In the end, what Russia thinks is what President Putin thinks. And many of the officials around him will wait to hear what President Putin thinks about for example these Ukrainian proposals before they kind of step out and make their own statements about them because they`ll want them to marry with what President Putin has to say.\par \par \uc2 [13:10:00]\par \par \uc2 So, fundamentally, President Putin`s view is crucial. And we did hear from the French. President Macron had a call with President Putin. We heard from the French and interestingly they described the Russians as very tough about Mariupol right now. So that suggests that the French, who spoke recently to Russian President Putin are getting a very tough message back, and that may give you a picture of where the Russians are really at.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Keir Simmons in Istanbul for us, some terrific reporting there, Keir and you`re setting me up with Mike McFaul. All right, Mike. Let`s start with the -- what we just heard there about we have sort of two what I might argue, you know, differing views of sorts of what`s happening are, number one, that Putin isn`t getting factual information by how badly his troops are performing outside of Mariupol, I guess would be a way to put it, and then, of course, hearing what Macron heard and what Keir did there. Square that. If you were right in your assessment cabled back, how would you examine where Putin`s head is at right now?\par \par \uc2 MICHAEL MCFAUL, FORMER U.S. AMBASSADOR TO RUSSIA: Well, we`re all guessing, Chuck, so let`s remember that. And even when I hear intelligence reporting suggesting that Putin is not getting information, remember that Putin is a very hard target for the intelligence community so we need to bracket that as speculative intelligence. Here`s what I would be saying if I were writings that cable.\par \par \uc2 Putin right now is bound and determined to take Mariupol. They couldn`t do it by force in a kind of conventional way, so now they`re just bombing it into oblivion, they are killing civilians, they are kidnapping children if you believe President Zelenskyy, but they want to take the city and then want to make your map red across the bottom, right, and that will be their minimal position when they go into negotiations about where the borders are.\par \par \uc2 Until that`s done, there`s going to be no peace treaty, there`s going to be no ceasefire. That`s my strong prediction. There it is, look at the map. They want to connect the red around Crimea to the red in Donbass. And then maybe they`ll begin to negotiate. Before then, they won`t stop.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So -- but if you`re Zelenskyy and you`ll hear this in my interview with Poroshenko as well, the success the Ukrainians are having in the rest of the country, I think makes it a harder pill to swallow that you somehow have to negotiate away whatever territorial gains they made in connection to the Donbass region and near Crimea and near the -- and near the coast there. That seems to be a pretty big hurdle, no?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Correct. I mean, we`re a long way away from a settlement. And there are three big issues. The first one is about the security guarantee in return for neutrality, right? That is really hard. That has not been settled. Zelenskyy wants an article 5-like \uc2 NATO\uc2  guarantee for his country from the \uc2 UN Security Council\uc2 . That is a huge hurdle. By the way, that would be 67 senators who would have to vote on that treaty. I don`t think that`s possible.\par \par \uc2 Number two, where are the borders, Chuck? You`re absolutely right. When I listen to Zelenskyy, and I talked to government officials in Ukraine pretty much every day, I hear a willingness to agree, to disagree about where the borders are, but to agree that they would not try to change them through force. That`s a kind of strategic ambiguity. You know.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Referendum? Things like that? Or --\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Sorry.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Referendum? Is that the idea? Is that what Zelenskyy may just be throwing out there? It feels like he`s looking for a way to punt the future of the Donbas region and Crimea, not accept anything definitive, right?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Correct. That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: The referendum, just to be clear, is about the change in the constitution. Because right now the constitution says you know Ukraine`s on their \uc2 NATO\uc2  aspirations. So Zelenskyy has been clear about that that in order to do neutrality, there has to be a referendum on that so that the Ukrainian people would own neutrality, not just Zelenskyy. But the borders are very hard because Putin doesn`t want to go back to the pre-war borders, which I think would have been difficult, but not impossible for Zelenskyy to agree to disagree with. He`s trying to take new territory.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: And as you rightly said, that is going to be a very heavy lift for President Zelenskyy to agree to.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And then, McFaul, there`s the larger issue here, which is there`s an opportunity here if we have enough patience, perhaps, to really squeeze Putin. Why let up?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, ultimately, I think that`s a decision for President Zelenskyy. And let`s be -- let`s remember, he even mentioned this when I spoke to him a couple of weeks ago, he said it many times before.\par \par \uc2 [13:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Imagine, Chuck, being President Zelenskyy and watching what Putin is doing to your people in Mariupol, that is where the pressure comes in. We think it`s irrational, horrific, Barbaric, and it is all of those things, but it creates pressure for negotiations. And so that`s the trick. Absolutely they`re -- you know Putin didn`t make some magnanimous decision, oh, we`re going to pull back on Kyiv because we want to be nice to them. No. Russian soldiers were losing to Ukrainian soldiers. That`s called defeat.\par \par \uc2 TODD: That`s why they offered it. Right.\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: That`s why they -- that`s why they backed off. But at the same time, to win in Kyiv, to win in Kharkiv, means you have to suffer more casualties, civilian casualties in the south and that`s what puts some pressure on Zelenskyy to negotiate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And which is also why as you`ll hear a few minutes from former president Poroshenko, he wants to put more pressure on the West to help Ukraine even more. Ambassador Mike McFaul --\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Well, he`s absolutely right about that, Chuck. I just want to -- before you get to that, we got to do more to put more pressure on Moscow through sanctions and more weapons to help the Ukrainians fight to a stalemate. There will not be negotiations without a stalemate.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me ask you this, Mike. Is there a way -- if the Ukrainians have successfully, for now, protected Kyiv and Kharkiv, should \uc2 NATO\uc2  come in and make sure those -- make sure that security is guaranteed? Is that a -- is that a line that you think we`re -- we`d be willing to cross or not?\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: It depends on how you cross it, Chuck. It`s exactly the right question. I don`t think \uc2 NATO\uc2  is prepared to deal with a no-fly zone, in fact, I`m confident that they aren`t. But remember, we`ve been talking -- we`ve been talking a lot about these anti-aircraft systems, anti-missile systems. My understanding is there`s a lot more that we could ship in to help secure the skies above places like Kyiv and Kharkiv. And that`s what we should be focused on doing right now as fast as possible.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And if we could do that, the Ukrainian military can concentrate on pushing the Russians back to other places. Mike McFaul, I`m a little bit over, but I appreciate you coming on, as always. And sharing your perspective with us. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 MCFAUL: Thanks for having me.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Up next, what I`ve been hinting at, my exclusive one-on-one with the former president of Ukraine. And what he`s seeing from the frontlines of the fight for his country. He`s in Kyiv. And later this hour, President Biden is scheduled to address the current state of the pandemic here o America and received his second booster shot to both by the way. We`ll take you to the White House for those remarks and the shot up ahead. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:20:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. As we mentioned earlier, Russia said it would de- escalate conflict in and around Kyiv yesterday, but the pictures you`re seeing right now tell a radically different story. These were taken in Chernihiv earlier today and after what Ukrainian officials call yet, another night of continuous shelling even though the northern town is part of the so-called area of de-escalation.\par \par \uc2 Former Ukrainian President \uc2 Petro Poroshenko\uc2  knows a thing or two about negotiating with the Russians. He was elected president two months after Putin annexed Crimea in 2014, illegally. Here`s our interview which we conducted a short time ago while the sun is still out in Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: And joining me is the former president of Ukraine, Mr. Poroshenko. Mr. President, thank you for spending a few minutes here.\par \par \uc2 PETRO POROSHENKO, FORMER PRESIDENT OF UKRAINE: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Let me just start with what you are seeing in Kyiv right now, you`re standing in front of a bombed-out building there and the supposed promise that Russia made in Turkey during negotiations that they were backing off their military onslaught of Kyiv. What have you seen in the last 24 hours, sir?\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: I see exactly the same as you see. This is a civilian object, civilian buildings, which were attacked and destroyed by Russian cruise missiles. And this is a very bright demonstration about the daily -- de- escalation a la Putin.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: Speaking a foreign language. And if you -- I`m not sure that microphone is working very efficiently but you can now hear the artillery fire of the Russian artillery and Russian multi-rocket launch system which happening just maybe seven kilometers from here, from Russian artillery position fighting and shelling on the civilian object. We are now on the northern border of Kyiv, and to the Russian position, again, this is just six kilometers. This is just a demonstration the war is at a very high level.\par \par \uc2 And today is exactly five weeks of the heroic fighting of all Ukrainian people. And to me, and I do exactly as doing any democratic President in my shoes, we are protecting, defending the Ukrainian people. We are together with our troops stopping the Russian aggression.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So given that what was deemed as a potential start of a breakthrough in peace talks with -- between the Russians and Ukrainians, the fact that they can`t -- they`re not even abiding by what they say, how do you -- how do you negotiate with somebody that is yet, to be honest with anything they have promised?\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: Several recommendations. Condition number one, please don`t trust Putin. I am the president who had negotiations with Putin for five years. He never keeps his words.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: And they -- it`s starting because two days before the aggression and the war started, he promised not to launch the aggression. Point number two, don`t be afraid of Putin. And point number three, Ukraine never gave up. And the territory, integrity of Ukraine cannot be part of any compromise. These are the demand for our constitution that I`m very much happy that all Ukrainians together, starting from me and pretty sure with the president and talks all the way around, confirming that this is not possible. From our partners in the world, we need more sanctions and more weapons.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Crimea and the Donbass regions, can it be part of the negotiation if Russia truly does want peace?\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: (Speaking a Foreign Language) can`t be a negotiation for the global security if Russia (INAUDIBLE). Russia has absolutely the same, zero reason to demand Crimea or Donbass (Speaking a foreign language). And if we allowed Putin to speak ultimatum language, the war never ended and Putin never stopped.\par \par \uc2 [13:25:00]\par \par \uc2 And we think that it is absolutely unacceptable when we accept the president to change the border by force in the 21st Century. Definitely, this is unacceptable. Definitely, Putin should withdraw his troops. Definitely, we can do that with the strong support of the free world. Please launch exactly, like, you do in World War II. (INAUDIBLE). Please launch a second tranche, everything but soldiers, from sanctions to an embargo, and with this situation, I`m absolutely confident that Putin cannot keep going and Russian troops would be withdrawn from Ukrainian territory.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I was just going to say you think the fact that Russia wanting to negotiate is proof of the weakness now. They know they can`t take the country, they know they can`t take Kyiv so they`re desperate to come away with something. Should that be read by the Ukrainian side as hey, we can win this war and we can push him out of the Donbass region?\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: First of all, I never -- we never give up. And first of all, we never have any doubts that Ukraine will win. But please don`t be -- don`t underestimate the Putin tactics. Putin, when he said he withdraw his troops from Kyiv, this is not true.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: This is the -- again the de-escalation a la Putin. He just take it for the regrouping (INAUDIBLE) for the new attack against Kyiv. We in Ukraine, and as the first -- as the fifth president don`t have any illusion that Putin will continue to attack us. And that`s why the shortest way to peace, shortest way would be to supply weapons to the Ukrainian arm forces.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What are --\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: So that`s peace.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What aren`t you getting from the United States and \uc2 NATO\uc2  that you would like?\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: First of all, we -- kind of weapons. We need anti-aircraft missiles because we cannot give them the airspace above the civilian object you see here, above the nuclear power station, and this is definitely we should -- significant element for our victory. We definitely need antitank rocket launchers, we need tanks and armed personnel vehicles and this is not a fortune because I can tell you as a supreme commander in Kyiv of Ukrainian arm forces for five years. (INAUDIBLE) and maybe a 1,000-ton personnel carrier, this has completely changed the whole picture for us.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: Please, help us to save you.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Final question is this. Can this war really end peacefully with Putin still in power in Russia?\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: Difficult question.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I know.\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: I believe, yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK.\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: I believe, yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: How?\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: All right, this situation -- we don`t know when it can be possible, but if it would be a hell sanction, if it will be hell embargo, the conflict inside Russia, bringing Putin to the decision to withdraw Russian troops from the Ukrainian territory. But definitely, it can -- well be the stable security but definitely, this can`t stop the war. Our solidarity, unity of Ukrainian inside the country can bring Putin to the decision to stop the war. I strongly believe in that.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Mr. Poroshenko, your words strike the heart and head really well over here. I appreciate the time and appreciate you telling us what you`re seeing and what you`re experiencing. Thank you, sir.\par \par \uc2 POROSHENKO: Thank you very much, indeed.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Coming up, from the war abroad to the politics here at home. What our new NBC News poll data shows about the challenges facing the president and his party heading into the midterm. We`re going to head to the big board for the MEET THE PRESS MIDTERM meter, right after this. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS DAILY.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [13:32:53]\par \par \uc2 TODD: Welcome back. There`s the music, you know what that means, we`re doing a little midterm preview.\par \par \uc2 It`s "Meet the Midterms" time, time to re-meet our midterm meter. This is the tool that our political unit has cooked up using our NBC News poll data in history and currently to understand and visualized which way the political winds are blowing by looking at the key factors that influence the performance of a president`s party in a midterm election.\par \par \uc2 You have the issue of direction of the country, the president`s job approval rating and the generic congressional ballot here. And just a reminder, right, these are big changes in a midterm 2010, 63 House seats. The Democrats held the Senate, 94. Both flipped `06, both flipped `14. The Senate flipped `18. The House flipped there as you could see.\par \par \uc2 And what we`re labeling as a conceptional or the two times essentially since 1990 that the incumbent part gained seats, you can make a case it belongs in decent rather than exceptional, but again we`re dealing with it within the overtone window.\par \par \uc2 So where are we right now? Well, let`s take a look. The current wrong track in our poll, 71 percent. What`s really striking is third straight poll where the direction of the country has been above 70 percent. Only one been other time we`ve had that in our poll. It is not a good time. And if you have 71 percent, forget -- look, 65 percent wrong track is a bad number, 71 percent, that`s why it is in the shellacking category.\par \par \uc2 Let`s look at presidential job approval rating. All right? When \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  had midterms that went their way, their job approval rating was right around 50 or north of it. All right? When you start dipping below 45, you`ve got a problem, and you`re going to have midterms that are in the bad to shellacking ratio. President Biden`s at 40. \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  was at 39 percent in 2006. We know how that turned out. So you see that`s also in shellacking territory.\par \par \uc2 Then let`s look at the generic ballot. What you`re going to see here is a narrowly for the Republicans, 42 to 44, and you`re going to say that`s competitive. No, no, no. In national polling, it`s extremely rare because the way gerrymandering has worked, the way Democrats are packed into districts, you know, they have more, 85 percent districts and the Republicans. The bottom line is, generic ballot, if the Democrats are losing it means they`re going to get walloped.\par \par \uc2 [13:35:05]\par \par \uc2 Last time in 2010, Republicans had a two-point advantage. That translated to 63 seats. So in summary right now, you`ve got three, essentially three numbers here that all point into dangerous territory for the Democrats and the incumbent party. Presidential job rating at 40 percent, wrong track over 70 percent, and the Republicans leading in the generic ballot.\par \par \uc2 Not a good time, but when we come back, we`re going to show you, if you`re a congressional candidate, what`s more likely, what`s an issue that will more likely help you and what an issue that will less likely help you, and we`ll tell you the one issue that could change all of this in a heartbeat.\par \par \uc2 That`s what next. Don`t miss the panel. We`ve got a lot more of this poll to digest. You`re watching MEET THE PRESS.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 TODD: We`re back. We have more new numbers out today from our latest NBC News Poll. It`s some insights into what kind of qualities voters want and don`t want in their candidates as we approach the midterms. What stands out most of all from this data is this. Voters say they`re far more likely to support a candidate who backs the funding of police. That was a 64-point margin. Same goes for a candidate who supports expanding domestic energy production.\par \par \uc2 Those are two key pillars of Republican messaging right now. And they`re at the top of the list of what voters want in a candidate. But the next three most popular candidate attributes are all Democratic priorities. The bipartisan infrastructure package championed by President Biden, the president`s health care and prescription programs, and then the one issue that could change the trajectory of the midterms, "Roe v. Wade`s" constitutional right to an abortion.\par \par \uc2 So joining me now on set to go through this even more, Amy Walter, publisher and editor-in-chief of the Cook Political Report, Republican strategist Brad Todd and Jeh Johnson, former Homeland Security secretary under President Obama.\par \par \uc2 So let`s start with the midterm landscape, Amy. When you look at -- the numbers are the numbers, but it`s also what`s the issue environment and what works. And when you see those top two things.\par \par \uc2 AMY WALTER, COOK POLITICAL REPORT PUBLISHER AND EDITOR-IN-CHIEF: Right.\par \par \uc2 [13:40:00]\par \par \uc2 TODD: This is not where the Democratic Party is right now.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Well --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Maybe where President Biden wants to get to, but it`s not where the party is.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: But it`s not where the party is. And the number one issue, of course, is also the economy. Right? I mean, you pointed to attributes, but if you say what is the number one issue, it`s the economy. And the president`s approval ratings on the economy, that`s a pretty grim number if you`re Democrats, 33 percent.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Seven straight -- let me give you this. Seven straight polls we`ve conducted.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Each time, each poll, the number has gone down.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: It`s gone down. Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: On the economy. Each time, seven straight declines.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: So could be at minus 30 on the number one issue. That`s a really big problem. Also, with the --\par \par \uc2 C. TODD: I have to interrupt.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Because the one place he is right upside up.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: His could be on --\par \par \uc2 TODD: He`s on TV right now talking about COVID and getting his booster. Here he is.\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: It means that COVID-19 no longer controls our lives. That`s what it means. Cases are ticking up, as we thought they might. But now, thanks for the foundation we`ve laid, America has the tools to protect people, all the people. And you know, as we`ve done from day one, my administration is making it easier than ever for America to access these tools.\par \par \uc2 Today I`m announcing the launch of COVID.gov. COVID.gov. Excuse me. A one- stop shop where anyone in America can find what they need to navigate the virus. Free vaccines and boosters, free at-home tests, high-quality masks, and for the latest information about the level of COVID-19 spread in your community, you can get that immediately.\par \par \uc2 Now, you`ll also able be able to find our new test-to-treat locations, which I announced in my State of the Union Address. These are pharmacies and other places in your neighborhood where you can get tested, and if you`re positive, you can get life-saving treatments all in one stop. We`ve already stood up 2,000 test-and-treat sites across the country. We`re also nearly at -- we have nearly 200 sites just to serve military families and veterans communities as well.\par \par \uc2 What we`ve done throughout the pandemic, we`ve ensured these locations are at the hardest hit, hardest to reach communities as well. The bottom line, no longer will Americans have to scour the internet to find vaccines, treatments, tests or masks. It`s all there. And just go visit COVID.gov. COVID.gov.\par \par \uc2 Now, let me remind you, when I took office about 14 months ago, the pandemic was raging, the economy was reeling, and the deficit was soaring. Most schools were closed. We didn`t have enough vaccines, the unemployment claims were sky high, and then we got to work and we delivered enough vaccines for every American months ahead of schedule. Effective treatments, at-home tests that are free and accessible.\par \par \uc2 Over 99 percent of our schools are open again. Businesses are open again. Because of how we responded, we created more jobs last year than ever before. 6.7 million jobs. And by the way, we did it while cutting the deficit from the largest one-year deficit reduction in American history. Say that again. The largest one-year deficit reduction in American history. But none of that happened by accident.\par \par \uc2 We were able to do it because we coordinated across the government, partner with state and local leaders, governors on both sides of the aisle, and the private sector to leverage every resource we had to fight against this virus. We left no stone unturned. And we`re able to do it because Congress worked with us, and provided us the necessary funding. But now, just as we have reached the critical turning point in this fight, Congress has to provide the funding American needs to continue to fight COVID-19.\par \par \uc2 We`re already seeing the consequences of congressional inaction. Take monoclonal antibodies, for example. They`ve helped save lives. This isn`t partisan. It`s medicine. But Congress hasn`t provided enough money to keep purchasing these monoclonal antibodies. We`ve had to cancel planned orders and cut the supplies we`re sending to the states. Without more funding, we`ll start to run out of them by the end of May. The end of May.\par \par \uc2 We`ve also had to scale back our plan to purchase more preventive therapies for Americans who are immunocompromised. Critical tools to protect the most vulnerable among us. Without more funding, we risk running out of the supply by this fall. The same is true of testing. It took months to ramp up our testing capacity. The Omicron, we saw how vital -- with Omicron, we saw how vita it was. And we have enough tests on hand to weather the surge.\par \par \uc2 [13:45:02]\par \par \uc2 Without funding we`re not going to be able to sustain the testing capacity beyond the month of June. And if we fail to invest, we leave ourselves vulnerable if another wave of the virus hits. Look, on vaccines and most important tool in this fight, we`re also running a risk. Yesterday, the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , the \uc2 FDA\uc2  and the Center for Disease Control and Prevention, the CDC, authorized a fourth shot for those 50 years and older.\par \par \uc2 We know boosters are critical to providing additional level of protection. That`s why I plan to get my second booster today right here after I`m done speaking. If you haven`t gotten your first booster, please don`t wait. Do it today. Those who are 50 and older, as well as those who are immunocompromised can now get even more protection than they have from the initial first doses.\par \par \uc2 We have enough supply to give booster shots to those newly eligible individuals, but if Congress failed to act, we won`t have the supply we need this fall to ensure the shots are available free, and easily accessible for all Americans. Even worse, if we need a different vaccine for the future to combat a new variant, we`re not going to have enough money to purchase it. We cannot allow that to happen.\par \par \uc2 Congress, we need to secure additional supply now. Now. We can`t wait if we find ourselves in the midst of another surge to act. It will be too late. And we also need this funding to continue our efforts to vaccinate the world. We have commitments we made. It`s critical for our ability to protect against new variants. There`s no wall you can build high enough to keep out a virus. Congress need to act now, please.\par \par \uc2 Let me close with this. I`ve worked so hard to get our lives -- we worked so hard to -- we, all of us, have worked so hard to get our lives back. We`re summoning every ounce of American resilience, pulled every lever of our government. Called upon the goodness, decency and patriotism of the American people. Together we turned an unthinkable pain into an unextraordinary progress and purpose.\par \par \uc2 Americans are back to living their lives again. We can`t surrender that now.\par \par \uc2 Congress, please act. You have to act immediately. The consequences of inaction are severe. They`ll only grow with time. But it doesn`t have to be that way. We`ve proven what we can do when we work together. So I urge Democrats and Republicans to get this done with urgency. Let`s stand united. Let`s continue to pull together. Let`s get this done.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, and God bless you all, may God protect our troops. And now I`m going to get my second booster shot.\par \par \uc2 TODD: All right. We are going to watch him get a second booster. Some of us at this table now realized we`re in a --\par \par \uc2 WALTER: We`re in that category.\par \par \uc2 TODD: It`s the first time we`re in the vulnerable group.\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 TODD: Brad, are you there now?\par \par \uc2 BRAD TODD, REPUBLICAN STRATEGIST: I`m 50, I had cancer, and my first shot was J&J.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: Like I need to be --\par \par \uc2 TODD: Double boosted. And in fact let me bring up this, you know, of all the things we tested, the one place where the president has decent approval ratings is on his handling of the virus now.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And yet it`s the one most disconnected from his job approval numbers which tells you how compartmentalized I think the public has put the virus versus presidential performance.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Right. The salience question is key. And that was one of my other favorite questions that you asked about the issue of the economy and inflation versus the war in Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 TODD: We need people to narrow it down.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 TODD: What do you expect of your commander-in-chief to -- what do you want him to focus on?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: And 70 percent almost.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes. I knew it was going to be one way, but I was surprised it was 3 to 1.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Yes. So.\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: You know, it`s the economy, stupid, as James Carville said. And politicians always ignore that at their peril. And I don`t think that people believe this administration is focused on the economy and on prices and on inflation. And they see another tax increase coming and right or wrong we voters don`t think tax increases help the economy, and I think we`re about to compete over, for three or four months on a new tax increase plan that will unite Democrats but it will separate them from swing voters because it`s a tax increase.\par \par \uc2 JEH JOHNSON, FORMER DHS SECRETARY: So this poll reaffirms for me something that I -- we`ve all known I think for a while now, funding the police, Biden primary voters supported 79-4, which means that candidates, Democrats, candidates for office want to be on the Eric Adams place --\par \par \uc2 TODD: You define the most interesting divide within the parties that we found right now, is that divide. There is sort of -- the Warren-Sanders wing of the party versus the Biden wing of the party, and it`s -- which of course is mostly dominated by African-American.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Right.\par \par \uc2 [13:50:00]\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: Right. But most Democrats, most Democratic candidate for office want to be in a position to say, yes, I fund the police.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: I think that can manifest itself in public safety on -- regarding the border this year. If the administration drops its Title 42 rule on using COVID rules to deny entry for asylum, I think that will move that public safety argument to the border. I mean, all accounts, there have been a million encounters returned under Title 42 in the last six months. Mr. Secretary certainly is the expert on this.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I know something about this.\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: Yes.\par \par \uc2 TODD: So react to that. Is he -- do you think -- what do you think? I want to use your expertise.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: So border is a tough issue. I am waiting to see when and if they drop Title 42 for the southern border. It is the, frankly, a way to keep the numbers down, although these numbers are pretty high. Just for perspective --\par \par \uc2 TODD: By the way, summer is about to come.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: And -- well, typically the largest months are March, April.\par \par \uc2 TODD: OK. We`re just -- we`re in it, yes.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: We`re surging right now. Just for perspective, we`re about to hit one million in six months. My highest year was more than 68,000 in the entire year, and that politically felt like the world was coming to an end.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: So these are very, very large numbers. They are unsustainable in my view. And you make a good point, that if Title 42 is lifted then the public health debate may move to the southern border.\par \par \uc2 TODD: If there is one issue in this poll where you would say, OK, what if I went into a coma and I woke up a day after the election and you told me, hey, you know, Republicans only picked up about 10 House seats and Democrats held the Senate, my instant reaction would be, "Roe" got overturned in June. Those numbers were striking in here, too. And it is sort of you could see it`s like the Democrats` last life line appears to be what?\par \par \uc2 WALTER: It is something we don`t know yet how motivating of an issue it is. We can see it in the data that suggests it is.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: And at the same time there`s still like wet blanket of the economy, of inflation.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Right.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Just of -- I know we`re not supposed to use the word malaise anymore, but I`m going to use it just as -- and it`s like that people are just in a bad way, right? That things are very much off on the wrong trend.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Still COVID grumpy.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: We`re still -- there are people who are in a bad mood.\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: They want to blame their leaders.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Right. And so that -- but I do think that inflation, economy issues still has a dampening effect, if you will, on that. I`ll be curious, though, because we haven`t seen for all of the movement we`ve seen in the states to restrict abortion, even in a state like Texas which is more red than the nation as a whole, but even in Texas you haven`t seen a rise in that issue to the top in the way that you would expect it to be.\par \par \uc2 TODD: And the question is, we have not seen -- when we talked about what`s happened in our lifetimes, we`re not seeing a Supreme Court sort of take away -- take away a perceived right. That exists what that reaction will be. My instant analysis is that the biggest beneficiary will be Democratic incumbent governors. But what`s your sense on this?\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: Well, I think first, let`s be clear it`s important to talk about what a tough year we`re talking about, and what`s going to talk about the Dobbs case which involves restricting abortion after 15 weeks of pregnancy. If you look at the polls in depth, only 18 percent of Democrats believe third trimester abortion ought to be legal at all. But that`s the law of the land in most liberal states and it`s also Planned Parenthood`s position.\par \par \uc2 We`re about to have a very nuanced discussion about abortion policy. When you ask most Americans, do you want "Roe v. Wade"?\par \par \uc2 TODD: Do you believe we could have a nuanced discussion about abortion policy?\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: I do. I do. I do.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t think we can have a nuanced discussion about anything anymore.\par \par \uc2 (CROSSTALK)\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: I trust the American people. I`m a political consultant. So --\par \par \uc2 (LAUGHTER)\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: I think if you ask people what do you mean, you want to keep "Roe v. Wade," people who say yes, what they mean is access to abortion very early in pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Correct.\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: And this -- once we have the Dobbs decision, it`s going to be about 15 weeks. That`s the Mississippi law that`s going to be upheld.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I understand that you believe if you can have the debate about when you have an abortion, that`s always been the case.\par \par \uc2 WALTER: Versus not having it at all.\par \par \uc2 TODD: Versus not having it at all.\par \par \uc2 B. TODD: But we`re going to have that debate for the first time this time.\par \par \uc2 TODD: I don`t know if that`s the case. That`s the curiosity I have. Jeh?\par \par \uc2 JOHNSON: I look at these numbers and I was born in 1957 and I am a child of the `60s, so for me the norm was Kennedy and Johnson having super majorities in Congress, all the same party. Divided government is now the norm. During Obama we