als had fought hard enough to take over that town, but now Ukraine has taken over it back.\par \par \uc2 Officials say they really have the blue and yellow flag over the town, Defense Department officials say Russia`s advance towards Ukraine`s capital remains completely stalled, Russian troops are still more than nine miles beyond the northwest part of Kyiv, and more than 18 miles from the eastern edge of the capitol, which is basically exactly where they were a week ago.\par \par \uc2 There are reports of Russian soldiers suffering from frostbite, because they have not been outfitted with the proper gear. The Pentagon said today that the Russian military has lost more than 10 percent of the combat forces that were set to invade Ukraine U.S. officials say that for the first time Russian forces have begun shelling the port city of Mariupol not from the ground, but from the water, which is a sign that their ground forces continue to struggle to gain and maintain new ground.\par \par \uc2 And so, for the people in Ukraine trying to beat back Russia`s assault on their nation, that is the good news. But for the people who are still in Ukraine, every day is a repeat of the same nightmare.\par \par \uc2 NBC`s Gabe Gutierrez has this report, tonight.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEOTAPE)\par \par \uc2 UNIDENTIFIED FEMALE: It seems unreal.\par \par \uc2 GABE GUTIERREZ, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT (voice-over): It`s become a good bleak existence. This is a dark frigid basement where a dozen families crammed together each night, huddled by candlelight, fueled by defiance.\par \par \uc2 VICTORIA FILOTAVA, CHERNIHIV REGIONAL CHARITY ARATTA DIRECTOR: When the bombing is going, you never know where it will drop.\par \par \uc2 GUTIERREZ: Victoria Filatova works for a charity in Chernihiv, that`s in northern Ukraine near Belarus. Russian troops outside of the city, but it`s hanging on. So are other cities like Mariupol and Kharkiv.\par \par \uc2 FILOTAVA: This is not a war between Ukraine and 