ntry, even though Russia continues to bomb by the cities and cause heavy damage, what are you hearing about where things are tonight?\par \par \uc2 ALI AROUZI, NBC NEWS CORRESPONDENT: That`s right, Ali. That`s in the suburbs of Kyiv, some of the Russian troops have made advances and occupy small parts of summers in Kyiv and in northwest, in Bucha, but in other parts in the west of the city, Russian troops had made advances, but the Ukrainians fought back hard as, as you mentioned in your report and took back certain areas in the eastern parts of Kyiv.\par \par \uc2 [21:10:08]\par \par \uc2 And that`s because, Ali, Kyiv is a city on a heel, it`s heavily fortified, it`s streets and roads are very net narrow, making it very hard for Russian tanks and heavy armor to maneuver their way around there. And, of course, because they`ve been held met by fierce resistance by the Ukrainian forces trying to defend their capital, where President Zelenskyy is still standing, and putting out these very strong messages to his armed forces and to the people.\par \par \uc2 So, despite a big push by the Russians trying to encircle Kyiv, they haven`t been able to do it. That is one of their main objectives, to take the capital, and other major strategic places like Mariupol.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Let`s talk about where you prefer a second, the images that we have seen this evening out a Mariupol, and literally for the last several days, make the place look like hell on earth. You`ve recently spoke with someone who`s made it out of there just in the last few days.\par \par \uc2 AROUZI: That`s right, Mariupol is the most hard hit and destroyed city in Ukraine, they have taken the brunt of the Russian artillery, missiles, and bombs, and because it`s such a strategic place for the Russians. It would create a land corridor to the Donbas region, if they were to take Mariupol, it would connect Russia to Crimea, and that would be a huge victory for the Russians. They haven`t been able to capture