hipped across the world from Mariupol. If they`re not going to be able to do that, that`s all going to economically strangle the city.\par \par \uc2 So, I devastating situation, not only from a humanitarian point of view, but from a financial point of view for this country, as well.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Ali, what a thing to hear them make a suicide pact if they didn`t think they would make it out of the city. Thank you for your amazing reporting. Ali Arouzi is live for us in Lviv, this late night.\par \par \uc2 In just a minute, I`m going to be talking live with someone who just escaped from Mariupol with his family, that interview will be after this. But, first, I want to get some thoughts from a military analyst on what we witnessed today on day 27 of Russia`s invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Rob Lee. He`s a former Marine Corps infantry officer, and an expert on Russian defense policy. He is currently a senior fellow at the Foreign Policy Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Lee, thank you for joining us. It`s good to see this evening.\par \par \uc2 I want to ask you about a senior pentagon official telling reporters, today, that the Russian military has lost more than 10 percent of its combat power that was sent into Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [21:15:05]\par \par \uc2 There is no indication that reinforcements are been brought in from Russia. We do know that they`ve been trying to recruit people into the military. Talk to be about this idea of losing 10 percent of your fighting force.\par \par \uc2 In military terms, that`s actually a turning point.\par \par \uc2 ROB LEE, FORMER MARINE CORPS INFANTRY OFFICER: Yeah, it`s significant because -- he wants to see cities, he wants to circle cities, probably Odessa, and they want to further degrade the Ukrainian military. In every single, white rushes facing attrition, and the more it sets, and the more limitations they have. Can they actually take big cities of Kyiv which would require additional casualties?\par \par \uc2 So, you know, the figures we`re seeing are been backed up by open source information on the nimble of destroyed or captured vehicles, walls indicating that Russia is going to really struggle in further operations, for weeks and weeks at a time. And we`re facing a number of limitations at this point.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: The Kremlin does a report on how many Russian troops have died, they do give out numbers, but they tend to be very low, we get much higher numbers on the Ukrainian government. We had a very middle of the road estimate from U.S. intelligence.\par \par \uc2 But, yesterday, a pro-Kremlin news outfit briefly reported that 10,000 soldiers have been killed. Suddenly back reference was quickly removed by the website. What do you make of that? These numbers sound really remarkable for a major, long standing army like the Russian army.\par \par \uc2 LEE: Yeah, it`s hard to tell. It could be just a hack. It could be an influence operation for the Ukrainians, who obviously know what they`re doing in terms of intelligence. Or it could`ve just been a mistake and the actual factual information that was published, it`s hard to tell.\par \par \uc2 But we know the Russian military is taking the heavy casualties, we can see this from destroyed vehicles, from destroyed convoys, sustaining heavy casualties, and it`s significant because it`s not like you think Russia has faced in the few wars they fought, right? In Georgia, a few hundred deaths. In 2014, 2015, Ukraine, it was only hundreds, in Syria, it was low hundreds.\par \par \uc2 So, what they face at after week in Ukraine this time was greater than the last three wars combined. That was weeks ago, and now, the figures are obviously much greater. So even if that figure, I don`t know the veracity of it, but it`s somewhere in the ballpark of what the actual casualty figures are, and we talk about that, that`s a substantial share of the Russian invasion force. So, again, not kind of attrition, it