of Russian troops on the way to Zaporizhzhia. There were a lot of different nationalities. The Russians said that they were Slavic people, but they were a lot of other nations, not Slavic, on the troops.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Tell us about your home in Mariupol, what happened to it?\par \par \uc2 SHEVCHENKO: Our neighbors were killed, 4:00 a.m. in the morning do -- the rocket hit their window. My flat was without any windows, as well as other flats. I left Mariupol four days ago. I don`t know what`s there, possibly, the houses are burned down.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: We`ve been hearing from people from Mariupol who are not able to be in contact with those people they have left behind, their friends in their family, because as you said they are out of electricity and gas.\par \par \uc2 Have you been able to speak to anybody? Obviously, you`ve got friends and family who are still in that city.\par \par \uc2 SHEVCHENKO: Yeah, actually, today, my friend called me. He was in a district where a lot of Russian troops who were. It was very scary, and at this moment the only choice he had was to walk by foot through this shelling area, to get to the C district -- it is really dangerous to.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: What do you think it`s going to happen to that city? The Russians have not succeeded in taking control of it, yet, but they have devastated it, and they continue to keep shelling.\par \par \uc2 SHEVCHENKO: I guess it will burn down, to ashes, because the first days without the Russians, they wanted to invade the city, to have a victory over people inside. But, at the latest, I understood that they just wanted to destroy it.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: What do you -- what do you plan to do now? You got out with your family, what happens next?\par \par \uc2 SHEVCHENKO: It is hard to tell because, we had a lot of information about Belarus troops, possible invasion, and in this case, it will be harder for Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 [21:30:02]\par \par \uc2 I have even my family. Really, I don`t know what will be next. No idea.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: How is your family doing?\par \par \uc2 SHEVCHENKO: They are good. We have a parent with us. We took her with us to this trip out of the city. I have here my grandfather his 80 years old. He is disabled so he cannot walk. It was really tough trip, it`s because 1,000 kilometers.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Wow. Artur, thank you for taking time to join us tonight. It is a middle of a night for you in western Ukraine. We hope you and your family stay safe, and I`m sorry for what you`ve had to go through.\par \par \uc2 Artur Shevchenko is a programmer who`s escape the city Mariupol with this family. We appreciate your time tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, we are going to go to the Senate, where the Supreme Court justice nominee \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  has been carrying questions from Republicans for the last 12 hours. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [21:35:48]\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: On day two of her confirmation hearing, Judge \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 , President Biden`s nominee for the Supreme Court, descriptive judicial methodology that she uses as a guide while she`s on the bench. She gave that answer today in response to questions from senators on the Judiciary Committee yesterday about her judicial philosophy.\par \par \uc2 By going to -- going on to outline the three steps in her methodology, she offered the senators a pretty thorough understanding of her approach to any case before her as a judge. A useful way for America to get to know Judge Jackson who could eventually be the first black woman on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 So, it was notable, if not entirely surprising, when a couple of Republicans senators decided to veer into this line of questioning.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. \uc2 LINDSEY GRAHAM\uc2  (R-SC): Did you know that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  actively filibustered Janice Rogers Brown?\par \par \uc2 KETANJI BROWN JACKSON\uc2 , SUPREME COURT NOMINEE: I did not know that.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Did you know that he told "Face the Nation", if Bush nominates her for the Supreme Court, I can assure you that would be a very, very, very difficult fights, and she probably would be filibustered. Is that news to you too?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: Did you notice that people from the left were pretty much cheering you on?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: A lot of people were cheering me on, Senator.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: That`s true. That`s true.\par \par \uc2 Did you know a lot of people from the left were trying to destroy Michelle Childs? Did you notice that?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Senator, a lot of people were supporting various people for this nomination.\par \par \uc2 GRAHAM: So you`re saying you didn`t know there was concerted effort to disqualify Judge Childs from South Carolina because she was a union- busting, unreliable Republican in disguise? You didn`t know that all those people were declaring war on Judge Childs?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Senator, I did not.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Senators \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina, or Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  from South Carolina used his time today to ask Judge Jackson about the other black jurists who were not sitting before them. When Republican senators did turn to her actual judicial record, they fixated on her time representing Guantanamo prisoners and her decisions and child pornography cases.\par \par \uc2 Judge Jackson rejected the implication that she`s been lenient on defendants in those cases. But there are a lot of questions about Republican accusations about her sentencing decisions, the relevance of her time as a public defender. I know who to ask about them.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Janai Nelson, president and director counsel of the NAACP Legal Defense and Education Fund.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Nelson, thank you for being with us tonight. I am puzzled by Senator Graham and Senator \uc2 John Cornyn\uc2  of Texas` line of questioning today, which is asking Judge Jackson to offer her opinions about the treatment and the process that other black jurists went through. What is the point of that line of questioning?\par \par \uc2 JANAI NELSON, PRESIDENT AND DIRECTOR COUNSEL, NAACP LEGAL DEFENSE FUND: It was assumed to be the playbook for many of the Republican senators during that hearing, and that was to air their grievances about either previous hearings or about other potential nominees that the perhaps would`ve supported more than they support the very eminently qualified \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Either way, it was an appalling display of disrespect to ask her to opine on the treatment of other black women in the process. In many ways, it was pitting black women against one another and asking her to offer her opinion about the treatment or the vetting process. And she really had no responsibility for it. And they wrongly characterized an assault on these two individuals.\par \par \uc2 However, it turned out, in terms of whether they moved forward or didn`t, had nothing to do with the qualifications of \uc2 Ketanji Brown-Jackson\uc2 , which was the only subject that was relevant at today`s hearing.\par \par \uc2 So what we saw was a very nasty exercise in trying to create discord among professional, highly qualified women, black women, who should not be maligned in any way.\par \par \uc2 [21:40:12]\par \par \uc2 And it was very disappointing to see them try to play that tactic, and certainly inappropriate for a hearing of the sort.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Let me ask you about Senator Ted Cruz`s decision to ask her -- ask Judge Jackson, about a reference to Nikole Hannah Jones`s piece of journalism, called the 1619 project. He was holding up books from a Georgetown school on which he`s a board, a private board, of which is a member of the board, and he was trying to pin her down on critical race theory.\par \par \uc2 Tell me about what you extend observed in that exchange.\par \par \uc2 NELSON: Yeah, it seems that he thought that we would all be impressed by the theater and the promise that he brought in to try to link her to this 1619 Project and to the renowned acclaimed award winning journalist, and I should say LDL climate, Nikole Hannah-Jones.\par \par \uc2 I think that Judge Jackson explained quite clearly why she referenced the 1619 Project because it is part of the zeitgeist around issues of race currently, and she raised it in a speech to students who would clearly be interested in it. We know that the 1619 project is a very popular project and text because of the number one "Times" bestseller that`s occupied the front for many weeks.\par \par \uc2 So, she mentioned in the speech, she referenced the author, the actual organizer of the project, Nikole Hannah-Jones, but that has nothing again, to do with her qualifications as a judge. It has nothing to do with the decisions that she wrote or any of the judgments that she`s issued, or any of her professional work.\par \par \uc2 Now, it`s not to say that it`s off limits to questions someone`s speech or oratory writings outside of third judicial background. But to ascribe a negative intention to it, or to a scribe of particular viewpoint to it was again, beyond the pale, and completely unjustified.\par \par \uc2 What we saw today was an attempt to assail the character of \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  because her record itself is so wholly unassailable, they`re grasping at straws. They`re trying to find some way to make her seem unqualified and they failed miserably.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: Janai, may I ask you to stay with us. I want to dip into these hearings right now. They are still continuing.\par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Marsha Blackburn\uc2  of Tennessee is now questioning Judge \uc2 Ketanji Brown-Jackson\uc2 . If you don`t mind staying with me, let`s listen.\par \par \uc2 SEN. MARSHA BLACKBURN (R-TN): She`s a mom and grandma mom, and she is -- this is a question that is important to her, to look at life. And Senator Feinstein talked about that issue with you a little earlier today.\par \par \uc2 You`ve also said today that it would be an appropriate for you to show your views on political issues are issues that may become -- may come before the court, like abortion. But I want to go to you on something you said when you were in private practice. You made your views on pro-life and the pro- life movement very clear. In fact, you attacked pro-life women.\par \par \uc2 And this was in a brief that you wrote. You describe them, and I am quoting, hostile, noisy crowd of in-your-face protesters, end quote. And you advocated against these women`s First Amendment right to express their sincerely hailed views regarding the sanctity of each individual live.\par \par \uc2 And I`m a pro-life woman, 79 percent of the American women support restrictions of some type on abortion. And so, I find it incredibly concerning that someone who is nominated to a position with life tenure on the Supreme Court holds such a hostile view toward a view that is held as a mainstream belief that every life is worth protecting.\par \par \uc2 So, how do you justify that incendiary rhetoric against pro-life women?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Thank you, Senator.\par \par \uc2 [21:45:01]\par \par \uc2 The brief you are referring to was a brief that I filed on behalf of clients who were clients of my law firm. This is in I believe, goodness, 1999 or 2000 -- maybe 2000 or 2001. I was an associate at a law firm and I had appellate experience because I had just finished my Supreme Court law clerk position.\par \par \uc2 And in the context of my law firm, I was asked to work on a brief concerning a buffer zone issue in Massachusetts at a time there were laws protecting women who wanted to enter clinics, and there was a First Amendment question about the degree to which there had to be room around them to enter the clinic.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: Right, I understand all of that. I am asking about the rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Senator, I drafted a brief along with the partners in my law firm who reviewed it, and we filed it on behalf of our clients.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: OK.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: In -- to advance our clients` arguments that they wanted to make in the case.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: Let me ask you this, when you go to church, and knowing they`re pro-life women there, do you look at them thinking of them in that way, that they are noisy, hostile, in your face? Do you think of them, do you think of pro-life women like me in that way?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Senator, that was a statement in a brief made for my argument. It`s not the way that I think of or characterize people.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: All right. Thank you for the clarification, on that because I think even zealous advocacy doesn`t allow that type of rhetoric on a free speech issue.\par \par \uc2 You know, Roe v. Wade, let`s talk a little bit about that. It`s come up a touch today. In my opinion, that was an awful act of judicial activism, and it has cost the lives of over 63 million unborn children.\par \par \uc2 And nearly 50 years later, the shameful ruling remains binding precedent, but the battle is being fought in the courts. As you know, and as we discussed when we visited the Supreme Court, it`s reconsidering whether the Constitution protects the right to an abortion in Dobbs.\par \par \uc2 And, if you`re confirmed, you will be in a position to apply the court`s decision in Dobbs. Whatever that decision is going to be, and you`ve talked about following precedent, and what the court decides.\par \par \uc2 So, do you commit to respecting the courts decision if it rules that Roe was wrongly decided, and that the issue of abortion should be sent back to the states?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Senator, whatever the Supreme Court decides in Dobbs will be the precedent of the Supreme Court. It will be worthy of respect in the sense that it is the precedent, and I commit to treating it as I would any other precedent.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: There`s one other thing, one of the central issues in the Dobbs case is about whether the Constitution protects the right to an abortion. Let`s talk about that.\par \par \uc2 Can you explain to me, on a constitutional basis, the courts decision in row and where is abortion protected in the Constitution?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Senator, abortion is a right that the Supreme Court has recognized in the -- its one of the kinds of rights that is an enumerated, it is in the same category of writes that the same Supreme Court has recognized --\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: But the text of the Constitution does not mention abortion.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: That is true.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: That is true. That is correct.\par \par \uc2 So you agree that the Constitution does not mention the right to an abortion, and yet, through one of the most brazen acts of judicial activism, our Supreme Court created the rights, through Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 [21:50:01]\par \par \uc2 This is why Americans -- this is why it`s so many women who have talked to are really concerned about who sits on the federal bench. We need in justice who will adhere to the text of the constitution. We`ve talked a little bit about that today, as you`ve talked about the historical context.\par \par \uc2 And I don`t want justices who are going to invents writes out of whole cloth, to serve a political interest.\par \par \uc2 Let`s move on. I want to -- when you are at Harvard, your thesis entitled "The Hand of Oppression, Plea Bargaining Processes". In that piece, you argued that judges have, and I`m quoting, I brought this up yesterday, personal hidden agendas that influence how they decide cases.\par \par \uc2 So what`s personal hidden agendas do you harbor, or do you think other judges harbor?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Thank you, Senator, that lined to the extent that it appears in my thesis, was written by someone who had not gone to law school, and by his senior in college, who had spent a summer at an internship working and making observations in the context of criminal justice internship. It is not a view that I hold.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: What lead you to that belief?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: I am thinking back. It`s been 30 years -- but the summer before my senior year, I interned at a public defenders office in New York, and I was part of a team of lawyers and interns on this team, and they were handling misdemeanor cases. And I --\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: So, it was from an internship experience.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes, before I became a lawyer.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: All right. Let`s go to something else. Senator Cruz was discussing with you, and I think this is important, it`s something that parents all across the country are worried about, and that is the progressive indoctrination of their children, and the pushing of critical race theory in the schools.\par \par \uc2 You told Senator Cruz that as a board member of the Georgetown Day School, you didn`t have any control over what was taught at the school. No control over curriculum, and you weren`t aware and that the school was pushing CRT.\par \par \uc2 But, I would think that there would have been parents that came to you and said, have you seen these books, are you aware that this is being taught? Being on that school board.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: No, Senator.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: No one ever mentions it.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Georgetown Day School is a private school. It`s not a public school.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: I`m fully aware.\par \par \uc2 And you mentioned that you said, that you meant -- you thought he meant that critical race theory isn`t being taught in public schools, correct?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Yes.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: But, we know that`s not the case. We know that CRT is been pushing our K through 12 schools. And I`ve got a letter here, Mr. Chairman, that Senator Tuberville and I wrote last year to the \uc2 National Education Association\uc2  on this issue. I`d like to submit that to the record, because it`s relevant to our discussion.\par \par \uc2 SEN. DICK DURBIN (D-IL): Without objection.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: And we had I manage the NEA for adopting pro-CRT materials during their annual organizing meetings, and proposing to spend $127,000 in teachers` dues to combat opponents of CRT. And there are countless reports of CRT being pushed in the public school system.\par \par \uc2 A lot of parents that I work with every day, every week, don`t have the funds to send their child to a private school. They are relying on the school systems, the public school systems, and it is important to them to have a Supreme Court that is going to protect parental rights to teach these children, as parents see fit to have their children taught. I will tell you, too, with Georgetown Day School, I found it astounding that it teaches kindergartners, five-year-old children.\par \par \uc2 I`ve got grandchildren, and they teach them that they can choose their gender.\par \par \uc2 [21:55:03]\par \par \uc2 So is this why you were praising, when you applauded, and I`m quoting you, transformative power of Georgetown Day Schools progressive education. Do you believe our school should teach children that they can choose their gender?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Senator, I`m not remembering exactly what quote you are referencing, but Georgetown Day School.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: It was in a book, and you gave the quote.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Georgetown Days School is a private school that --\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: Yeah, I`m asking if you agree that schools can teach children that they can choose their gender.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON: Senator, I`m not making comments about what`s schools can teach.\par \par \uc2 BLACKBURN: Let me ask you this then, United States versus Virginia, the Supreme Court struck down VMI`s 