l like when I`m used to.\par \par \uc2 [21:10:04]\par \par \uc2 Who are these folks, and where they motivated by?\par \par \uc2 PERRY: There is a Trumpian feel to this demonstration. There is, exactly as you`ve laid out, this want to be connected to America and a movement that is viewed as a larger movement. This is not, as you said, representative of what is happening in Canada as far as vaccines, as far as mask requirements. But it is representative, in a broader movement, and a broader frustration.\par \par \uc2 A lot of these folks who came out here are just frustrated. It may not necessarily be about the vaccine, it may not be about the masks, but it is definitely about the political system that they find themselves in. And so, as this kind of moves west, there is a kind of yearning to be heard.\par \par \uc2 I just want to mention, Ali, we talked visually about the Trump rallies, we have so many of the same visuals here, we have the Trump flags, the red hats with him writing in white for the truckers. It`s designed after those Trump`s logos. There are t-shirts being sold.\par \par \uc2 So, you have that atmosphere that is very eerily similar to what we saw in the United States five or six years ago.\par \par \uc2 VELSHI: And you`ve seen not a lot, because you are a lot of those rallies.\par \par \uc2 Cal, good to see you. Thanks again for being with us tonight, keep us posted if anything happens. Just wave into the camera, we`ll come right back to you.\par \par \uc2 MSNBC news correspondent, Cal Perry, on the Canadian side of the Detroit- Windsor Ambassador Bridge right now.\par \par \uc2 In addition to the immediate pressing issue of these protesters blocking supply lines, and occupying Canadian cities. There is actually a bigger and more interesting question, here, about democracy. I want to return, for a second, to the photo I used to start tonight`s show.\par \par \uc2 If those protesters in Canada were out in the streets, without their trucks, this would 