\uc2 Starting with the problem, in addition to being a black woman, Biden`s pick will maintain the remaining liberal minority of the court. Of course, that is minor considering they are still outnumbered two to one by conservatives on that side of the bench, thanks largely to Donald Trump`s presidency.\par \par \uc2 But the news also gives the right an election year gift in the form of the inevitable nomination fight theater we can expect from \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  and the Senate Republicans coming as the Court is expected to rule on no less the future of reproductive rights in America and affirmative action, and both parties try to rally their bases around those fights.\par \par \uc2 We`re looking at all angles of the week in the court and on Capitol Hill tonight on politics nation, plus more election drama, Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  announcing her run for re-election and setting off speculation through her own silence on whether or not she would seek the speakership again as Democrats brace for a possible loss of their majority.\par \par \uc2 Huge week in Washington folks.\par \par \uc2 We begin tonight with the Court fights to come in the near future. Joining me now is Congressman \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , Democrat of New York, and chair of the House Democratic Caucus.\par \par \uc2 Congressman, thank you for joining us.\par \par \uc2 REP. HAKEEM JEFFRIES\uc2  (D-NY): Good evening. Good to be with you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, after this very busy week of news from the court and on the Hill starting off with the Court, the news that Justice Breyer`s -- of his imminent retirement, the complaint from the right over President Biden insisting that black women fill the seat with important Court decisions on abortion and affirmative action on the horizon, with all of that context, who does the Supreme Court issue favor politically in this midterm election year -- Democrats or Republicans?\par \par \uc2 I mean, whose base is more energized by that fight?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, Republicans and right-wing conservatives are always going to try to use a judicial nomination to energize their base, but I think this is a uniquely different circumstance.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court has moved so far to the right, in terms of rolling back civil rights protections, and they are the reason why we have to act legislatively in terms of voting rights protection legislation because of a prior decision that struck down the original 1965 Voting Rights Act in part.\par \par \uc2 I think that people of goodwill, Democrats and Independents across the country, and even many suburban Republican women understand that there needs to be an appropriate balance on the Supreme Court, that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is going to nominate the most qualified person to sit on the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 And all things being equal, I think it will be a wonderful thing in this country for that person to be an African American woman as well.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I remember when he made that commitment at the South Carolina debate, I was there and the next morning, he and Congressman \uc2 Jim Clyburn\uc2  came to National Action Network`s Ministers Breakfast.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:10]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: He was very clear, even before he won the nomination that if a vacancy became available, what he was going to do.\par \par \uc2 But, Congressman, let me go to this, with the news this week that \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  will seek re-election rather than retire from the U.S. House, do you expect her to pursue another term as House Speaker?\par \par \uc2 Now, her silence on that subject has, of course added to the speculation that she may not seek the Speaker`s gavel has Democrats brace for the midterm election losses, if not the loss of their majority. And there has been debate whether a new generation of Democratic leadership is needed, as the party charts its future.\par \par \uc2 Of course, as House Democratic Caucus Chair, you`ve been touted as the frontrunner in that new generation. Your response, Congressman?\par \par \uc2 I mean, the folks in Brooklyn, on Rogers Avenue, want to know, are you interested in running if the Speaker does not run to keep the gavel?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Right now, I think our interest is in getting things done, you know, for the American people, in terms of completing the work around Build Back Better, doing something about inflation, and the price increases that we`ve seen all across the country related to supply chain disruption and dynamics connected to the COVID-19 pandemic, continuing to fight to crush the virus, build upon the work of the American Rescue Plan, as well as doing something around voting rights.\par \par \uc2 Now, I think Speaker Pelosi`s decision to run for re-election is consistent with our approach, which is all hands on deck at the moment to make sure that we hold the House Democratic majority, if not increase it, because the stakes are so incredibly high in so many areas.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And that was certainly not a "No, I will not run," so I will take it as you may run. Now, that leads me to the voting rights, which I must addressed at this -- as we have this conversation.\par \par \uc2 Last week, I reiterated my call as the President of National Action Network that President Biden enact whatever further executive orders he can to protect voting rights ahead of the midterm elections and beyond, and that we deal with voting rights legislation passed in the House continually dying in the Senate, what would you like to see contained in those kinds of orders that would be most helpful for the nation?\par \par \uc2 And would you like to see the Voting Rights Act broke down in pieces like they`re talking about with Build Back Better, and have the Senate vote on different pieces?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, it is important for the Biden administration and the Department of Justice to do everything possible within its executive power to protect the right to vote, and I expect that they will do so. But as you indicated in the latter part of your question, Reverend Sharpton, I still think there is a pathway in the Senate to get some meaningful voting rights legislation done.\par \par \uc2 Now, the difference between the two parties is pretty simple. Democrats want to make it easier to vote, and harder to steal election, the radical right wants to make it harder to vote and easier to steal an election.\par \par \uc2 And so we`ve got to deal with two things moving forward: Reform the Electoral Account Act because it is clear that it needs to be tightened up, so that you can prevent the type of thing that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was endeavoring to convince \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  to do, steal the election, and we also have to figure out some common ground with Joe Manchin in particular, as it relates to access to voting, early voting, making sure that ID laws aren`t discriminatory, as we`ve seen in some places where a student ID even from a state university is not acceptable, but a hunting license is apparently acceptable in terms of voter ID. We have to eliminate those disparities.\par \par \uc2 We have to also make sure that we don`t criminalize things such as providing water to people who have been waiting on line, hour after hour after hour, because you don`t provide adequate polling sites in certain neighborhoods intentionally.\par \par \uc2 So there are a whole host of provisions that I think can be explored, and I`m cautiously optimistic that we`ll be able to find common ground and get something done in advance of the midterm election.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, lastly, of the elements that made up the President`s tall social spending plan, otherwise known as I said, Build Back Better, which can we expect to see the Democrats give priority to this year, individual pieces of legislation that can pass in both Houses or voting rights?\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: Well, I think we have to do both. We can walk and chew gum at the same time, pursue the voting rights protection agenda, but also makes sure that we can get things like the Child Tax Credit over the finish line, the $150 billion in creating and preserving affordable housing is incredibly significant.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:14]\par \par \uc2 JEFFRIES: We`ve got great leadership from Chairwoman \uc2 Maxine Waters\uc2  in that regard. We have $5 billion in break the cycle of violence funding set aside to be able to engage in violence, disruption, and interruption, as well as supporting productive community-based activities for young people that might otherwise fall into an unproductive way of life.\par \par \uc2 We`ve got to invest $150 billion, at least in standing up our home care system, both for those loved ones who are elderly and need to be cared for with compassion, as well as our home care workers.\par \par \uc2 So there is a lot that`s a part of the Build Back Better Act that I believe we are going to be able to get over the finish line in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, glad to have had you tonight. And, Congressman, you know, I believe in consistency. We have the Majority Leader of the Senate from Brooklyn, and his name is Chuck Schumer. If Miss Pelosi gives up the gavel, there`ll be nothing wrong with having the Speaker of the House from Brooklyn, and I`m glad you didn`t turn me down.\par \par \uc2 You didn`t accept, but you didn`t say no, and I`m telling that, I`m tweeting that to all the brothers and sisters on Rogers Avenue.\par \par \uc2 Thank you as always, for being with us, \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Imani Gandy, senior editor of law and policy for Rewire News Group. Thank you for joining us tonight, Miss Gandy.\par \par \uc2 IMANI GANDY, SENIOR EDITOR OF LAW AND POLICY, REWIRE NEWS GROUP: Thank you for having me.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I always learn something -- I always learn something when you are on with us, and of course, I have to start with this week`s prime legal news. Associate Justice \uc2 Stephen Breyer\uc2  retiring from the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  after 28 years. President Biden says he will stick to his campaign pledge to nominate a black woman, many conservatives of course seeing red over it.\par \par \uc2 Tell me a little about some of the top candidates to replace Breyer, who are they and what could they bring to the Court?\par \par \uc2 GANDY: Well, that`s a really great question. And one of the things that I find most sort of distressing about the discourse around the Supreme Court nomination is that somehow, President Biden is going to pick any black woman off the street or any black woman that the majority of people on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  can name and identify. That is not the case.\par \par \uc2 The women that are on the shortlist, the sort of shortlist that is known amongst legal scholars to be the shortlist are very qualified. Ketanji Jackson-Brown or Brown-Jackson, excuse me, for example, now sits on the D.C. Circuit Court of Appeals. She was elevated to that position when Biden tapped \uc2 Merrick Garland\uc2  to become Attorney General.\par \par \uc2 There is obviously \uc2 Sherrilyn Ifill\uc2 , who would follow in the glorious footsteps of Thurgood Marshall --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And has a similar background with Thurgood Marshall.\par \par \uc2 GANDY: Exactly.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Because I think that one of the things we hear in these law professors and people like Sherrilyn, that don`t come off the Federal bench like Marshall, and I believe Earl Warren didn`t come off the bench.\par \par \uc2 GANDY: There have been several Supreme Court justices who didn`t come off the bench, there have been several who are there -- there was even one who didn`t graduate from high school.\par \par \uc2 So I just have an -- I would like to name another couple more people, Candace Jackson-Akiwumi is on the Seventh Circuit. She is the only person of color sitting on the Seventh Circuit Court of Appeals right now. These are women who are eminently qualified, and as a black woman who went to law school and who was consistently underestimated, who was consistently viewed as a diversity pick, or an affirmative action pick, I know what it`s like to have to be really twice as smart, to work twice as hard, to read twice as much to be viewed as even half as competent as whatever random white guy is sitting next to me in a Torts class.\par \par \uc2 So the idea that these women are going to be somehow affirmative action higher simply because the parameters of the search have been widened. And I would say that they`ve been widened and not narrowed, because you have to remember, something like 114 out of 121, Supreme Court Justices have all been white men. It was a prerequisite to be a white man.\par \par \uc2 So I think it is disingenuous to now say a prerequisite that we diversify the Court and add a black woman to the bench is somehow affirmative action. I find that very, very troubling.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, assuming this confirmation goes as planned, we are still talking about a six-three split between conservative and liberal Justices, essentially gaining another liberal dissent, certainly not a tiebreaker, and certainly not a liberal majority, which I would argue is needed at this time. I would like to have both, but I`m not a legal scholar, so I admit to having to still evolving point of view on expanding or reforming the Court.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:06]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But Biden has shown a willingness to at least study the matter. Do you think, there is still a momentum for making changes to the Court?\par \par \uc2 GANDY: I truly don`t think that there is. I think Biden showed a willingness to study the matter, but I think the result of that permission demonstrated that he is not really willing to do much about it.\par \par \uc2 I think that -- you know, I think he is sort of beholden to this need for bipartisanship, this idea that if he were to expand the Court, it would be some radical leftist thing to do and that would bother the Republicans that he seems to think still want to work with him.\par \par \uc2 So no, I don`t think that there is a lot of stomach for expanding the court, but what I do think is important about sort of swapping out Justice Breyer for a black woman jurist is that it adds another voice to the Court, besides \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , who is focused on the way the law impacts people.\par \par \uc2 Justice Breyer was a great Justice, particularly in reproductive rights, and I don`t want to detract from that at all. But in terms of the ways in which, for example, abortion restrictions hurt low income people, hurt black and brown women, black and brown people, \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  is the person who is constantly making that connection. She has been making that connection in the dissents that she has been writing in the Texas SBA bounty hunter case.\par \par \uc2 So the idea that there would be a black woman on the bench, who would also be able to bring her experience to bare as being black in America that is going to change the tenor of the dissents that come out, and I think that she and \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  and Elena Kagan are going to be writing dissents that hopefully in future generations will become majority opinion, and it is going to be key to have a black woman`s voice, a black person who understands what it`s like to be black in America which Clarence Thomas, clearly does not, or at least does not want to admit that he does.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Justice Breyer will still be a part of the Court`s looming decision on Mississippi`s 15-week abortion ban, which has been cast as a bellwether for the future of Roe versus Wade. Do you think we are still in a situation where Roe could essentially be overturned later this year?\par \par \uc2 GANDY: Absolutely, and it might not even be later this year. There has been some scuttlebutt among, you know, legal scholars and court watchers, that perhaps this retirement in January, it is very uncommon for a Justice to retire in January. They usually do it closer towards the end of the term.\par \par \uc2 So you know, we are in a situation where we might be having confirmation hearings, and we might confirm a new Justice while Breyer is still sitting because in his retirement letter, he said he will continue to sit until his replacement is nominated and confirmed.\par \par \uc2 So it`s not going to affect any of the cases that are on the docket. It`s not -- I do think that we are in for a flat out reversal of Roe versus Wade, or at least a gutting of it to the point where it is no longer effective in at least half of the states.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Imani Gandy, thank you for joining me tonight.\par \par \uc2 Coming up, President Biden has a chance to make the Supreme Court look more like America, but that`s just the beginning of the real fight and the fight for real justice from the nation`s Highest Court. I`ll explain in this week`s "Got-cha."\par \par \uc2 And later hundreds of \uc2 NYPD\uc2  police officers paid their final respects to slain Officer Jason Rivera on Friday. Inside of this, his widow sent a message to Manhattan`s new DA, but is she delivering it to the right person?\par \par \uc2 Stay with us. But first, my colleague Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, a very good Saturday to you. Some stories that we are watching for you this hour, breaking news in the northeast, more than 68 million people under winter weather alerts today, over a hundred thousand people in the Northeast are without power right now says poweroutage.us.\par \par \uc2 A powerful nor`easter, the cause of all of this, 10 Eastern states right now were hit by snow, coastal flooding, and high winds. Massachusetts declared a state of emergency along with Maryland, New Jersey, New York, and Rhode Island. Residents were warned to stay off roads. Airlines canceled more than 3,000 flights due to the weather.\par \par \uc2 Today`s launch of COSMO-SkyMed Second Generation, that was cancelled as well.\par \par \uc2 And President Biden says he will move U.S. troops to Eastern Europe and \uc2 NATO\uc2  countries as tensions with Russia rise. U.S. diplomats are working with their Russian counterparts to reduce concerns over Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Russia deployed an estimated 10 or rather 100,000 troops along the Ukrainian border this week.\par \par \uc2 We will have more POLITICS NATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:24:02]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For this week`s "Got-cha," I want to address this pivotal moment for the Supreme Court.\par \par \uc2 As we mentioned earlier, President Biden will get the chance to nominate a new Justice, and I`m cautiously optimistic that we will soon see the first black woman ascend to the Highest Court in the land. But even if that happens, conservatives will still have a six to three majority, and what the conservative justices are planning on doing with their majority will affect all of us for generations.\par \par \uc2 This week, the Supreme Court announced it will take up the constitutionality of affirmative action to cases involve students, mostly Asian-Americans, who say they were discriminated against by \uc2 Harvard University\uc2  and the University of North Carolina in favor of black and Hispanic applicants.\par \par \uc2 Now, lawyers for the schools deny their policies are discriminatory and studies show white women are the greatest beneficiaries of affirmative action. A fact you rarely hear about from critics of the practice.\par \par \uc2 [17:25:12]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: At the same time, experts warn black and Latino students would suffer disproportionately from the elimination of affirmative action, not only because it would make it harder to get into top schools, but those minority students who are admitted will find smaller communities of peers with similar backgrounds to support them and help them succeed.\par \par \uc2 A Gallup poll just last year shows 62 percent of Americans support affirmative action programs for minority groups, the highest in 20 years -- over 20 years. So you might wonder why, with all of these problems facing our country, the High Court has chosen to focus in on this issue now.\par \par \uc2 The answer is, conservatives are trying to seize a historic opportunity to transform this country created by the dirty tricks of Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2  who denied former President \uc2 Obama\uc2  the opportunity to appoint a Justice to the Court and then rammed through one of his own just days before an election under \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The addition to targeting affirmative action, the conservative packed court is targeting abortion rights, a ruling due this summer. It could roll back Roe versus Wade just months before its 50th birthday.\par \par \uc2 They are also intent on expanding gun rights in a country already dangerously armed to the teeth. As historic as Biden`s Supreme Court nomination will be, it alone will not stop the Supreme Court`s extreme rightward turn. That`s why Democrats must continue to focus on turning out the vote in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 If Democrats can`t hold on to the Senate in November, Biden`s first Supreme Court appointment may very well be his last for more than a generation. Republicans have made the Supreme Court their top priority, but their success in packing the bench may also be a curse as the Justices move the court dangerously outside of the political mainstream of the country it is supposed to represent.\par \par \uc2 It is up to Democrats to make sure Republicans pay the price and pay a political price for their overreach.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICS NATION. Let`s continue the conversation with my political panel.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now Susan del Percio, Republican strategist and Juanita Tolliver, Democratic strategist. They are MSNBC political analysts.\par \par \uc2 Susan, let`s start with Justice Breyer`s retirement announcement. President Biden has vowed to choose a black woman and because Democrats hold the tie- breaking vote in the Senate, her confirmation seems assured.\par \par \uc2 However, that hasn`t stopped some conservatives from calling this quote, "affirmative action pick." And Minority Leader McConnell has warned against a quote "radical nominee." Can Republicans make this into a partisan battle ahead of the midterms?\par \par \uc2 SUSAN DEL PERCIO, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: I don`t think they will be able to, Rev. I mean, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  ran as someone who said he was going to put a black woman on the Supreme Court. So President Biden, given the opportunity is going to nominate one, that is very acceptable, and that`s what he should be doing.\par \par \uc2 I`m also of the belief that when you win the presidency, you get to put your person up. And in this case, I actually think it is a really wonderful choice that he is putting someone, a black woman in there, because it is someone who is going to add a different life experience.\par \par \uc2 And that`s what Justice Breyer was about. He was about trying to make the law practical for everybody. So this is, I think, a really wise choice by President Biden, and you`re going to hear the right go and say every which thing they can, and maybe they have a delay here of a day based on some rules or whatever, but this is going to sell through as far as the Democrats go. There is no reason it shouldn`t.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, a black woman nominee for the Supreme Court will be historic, but will it be enough to energize voters during the midterms? Black voters? Or will it be overshadowed by the lack of progress on other issues of concern for black voters like voting rights and police reform?\par \par \uc2 JUANITA TOLLIVER, MSNBC POLITICAL ANALYST: Look, Rev, this election of a nominee and placing a black woman on the Supreme Court is historic, but you`re right, it is not a direct exchange with essential legislation like voting rights or provisions of the Build Back Better Act.\par \par \uc2 But what it is, is President Biden`s opportunity to say to black voters, hey, here is what I delivered for you, especially black voters in Georgia who delivered two senators to allow the conditions for this nominee to move through the Congress as easily as unobstructed as she can.\par \par \uc2 I think that Biden is smart also to make this pick, an announcement during Black History Month. I think he is smart to include Civil Rights leaders and Vice President Harris in the selection process, and you better believe that Civil Rights advocates are going to continue to apply pressure about how Biden should still deliver for them, because this is not a one and done, but it is going to be a mobilizing factor because the pomp and circumstance that just celebrates and surround this historic moment is not going to be forgotten by November.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:03]\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: I think it is going to be something that helps black voters understand why they should continue to turn out and vote for Democrats, while Democrats also have an opportunity to continue to deliver on some of those other provisions because voting rights is not done, Build Back Better is not done, but this gives them a chance to emphasize what they stand for and what they are fighting for to deliver for black voters and voters across the country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And Juanita, I`ll note that you withdrew your name from consideration.\par \par \uc2 Susan, a Media Matters study has found that at least 50 Republicans and Independent candidates running in the midterms have expressed some level of support for the QAnon conspiracy theory that has led even a former Trump Homeland Security Department official to voice his concerns.\par \par \uc2 How worried should we be about conspiracy theorists running for office, mostly under the banner of the G.O.P.?\par \par \uc2 DEL PERCIO: It`s frightening, and what is worse is some of these people are going to get elected. Hopefully the ones who run in swing districts or swing states and win a primary and run in General will not be elected, but from a deep red state and or a deep red district, you`re going to see some of these crazy people and I do mean they are crazy.\par \par \uc2 If you believe in some of these things, you`re a nut and should not be in Congress or a dog catcher. You should not be serving the public. You are a danger to the public, and I think the more Republicans especially with a National Defense background or Intelligence background can talk to the dangers of people associated with these groups and what these groups are about, the better we will be as a country.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Juanita, last topic, foreign policy. Russian President \uc2 Vladimir Putin\uc2  has deployed nearly 100,000 troops near the Ukraine border. President Biden warned the Ukrainian President that Russia could invade next month.\par \par \uc2 According to "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " without firing a shot, Mr. Putin has forced the Biden administration to shelve other foreign policy priorities and contend with Kremlin grievances the White House has long dismissed. In particular, reversing Ukraine`s westward lean in the post-Soviet period.\par \par \uc2 Now, what is the U.S. political impact of what is going on with Russia at the moment?\par \par \uc2 TOLLIVER: Look, you`re exactly right and the fact that Putin has pulled Biden all the way into this because Putin`s move here is to force the U.S. out of Europe. He does not like the fact that Ukraine is a democracy, he wants to expand his authoritarian control and Biden is stepping forward to do as much as he can to support Ukraine economically to support them. It is to some degree militarily to prevent that from happening.\par \par \uc2 Putin fully understands that what he is doing right now is creating this tense environment, especially with the U.S. among its \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies, and what does not help in this debate is the fact that you have members of the G.O.P. saying, why are we supporting Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 You have members of the G.O.P. saying that they are pro authoritarianism and pro Russia`s expansion, and that the U.S. shouldn`t have a role in this, as though the fall of one democracy does not have harm on democracy globally because the same energy that we`re seeing from these Republican members supporting Russia`s move here, I think one can be defined as a legacy of Trump and how he fawned over Putin, but two, it highlight their anti-democracy narrative and frame that they`re even using back here in the United States.\par \par \uc2 And so I think what Biden must continue to do is support Ukraine because losing this moment, backing down in this moment only signals weakness in the future of democracy globally.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Susan del Percio and Juanita Tolliver, thank you both for being with us.\par \par \uc2 Next, a tragic week for law enforcement officers across the country raises new questions about reforms to policing and criminal justice.\par \par \uc2 Be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:42:40]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Homicides in the United States have spiked since the start of the pandemic, particularly here in New York City, where the murder rate has increased by 40 percent. This past week, we have tragically seen police officers as the victims of homicide as Wilbert Mora, the second \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officer shot alongside fellow officer Jason Rivera died on Tuesday from his injuries.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of New York City police officers lined Manhattan`s Fifth Avenue on Friday to honor him. Officers dressed in blue uniform with white gloves, carried the flag-draped coffin into a funeral service held in Manhattan`s iconic St. Patrick`s Cathedral, and on Thursday, in Houston, Texas where three police officers were shot.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Cedric Alexander, former member of President Obama`s Task Force on 21st Century policing, and MSNBC law enforcement analyst. Thank you for joining me today.\par \par \uc2 CEDRIC ALEXANDER, MSNBC LAW ENFORCEMENT ANALYST: Thank you for having me. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  is set to travel to New York next week to meet with New York City`s new Mayor Eric Adams to discuss the administration`s comprehensive strategy to combat gun crime. In your point of view, what do you think both the administrations should give priority to when combating gun violence in New York City and the rest of the country?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Well, you know, let`s consider the fact. You take the loss of lives of those two young officers in New York City, two young men who appear to have grown up in that community, decided they want to be police officers. When you look at the deaths of more recent shootings and deaths of police officers since the beginning of this year, Reverend Al, and I think there is something to be said about their conversation that needs to take place.\par \par \uc2 And first of all, what needs to be done, what support from the Federal government can be lent to these cities who are doing everything that they can. Mayor Adams walked into the city with a great deal of issues. That was one that he took on his watch, crime being one of them, alone under this cloud of the pandemic that we all are still living through and I think what`s going to be very important here is to be able to talk about what resources, what additional moneys not just for more police and more training, but what are we going to do different with more police --\par \par \uc2 [17:45:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Because there has a balance there, Cedric.\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Yes, exactly.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The National Law Enforcement Memorial and Museum published an end of the year fatalities report, noting that law enforcement related deaths are up by 55 percent. The same report found that violence against police officers are also at 32 percent in terms of an increase.\par \par \uc2 So you have 55 percent increase in law enforcement caused deaths and 32 percent of law enforcement deaths. Why do you think that is?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Well, there is something that`s going on in the environment. I think there are a number of things. Certainly, we see this uptick during this pandemic that we`re in, but we can`t blame it on the pandemic solely because everything gets blamed on the pandemic, because many of these issues were already merging, quite frankly, emerging in our communities across this country before the pandemic and got exacerbated.\par \par \uc2 Here is what I think is critically important to remember, during this time right now, in many of our states, and I think you would have to agree, Reverend Al, is that bail reform is playing a real difficult role in this. Certainly, there needs to be bail reform in this country, but there is an appearance now with the number of violent criminals that have been on the street here very recently that have shot and killed police officers and innocent citizens across this country, where people who should have been arrested and contained.\par \par \uc2 Now, we cannot totally bring this one to Judges because Judges do not write the law for the states. So state legislators really need to reconvene and give police, give judges, give the system some support that they need in order to keep these very violent criminals off of the street, while your federal, state, and local law enforcement work together to try to contain and look not just as short term results, but also long term of things I think that`s going to have to be done such as dealing with poverty and poor education and poor healthcare, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, and reimagining how we deal with policing. A second trial is underway in Minnesota, involving three former Minneapolis police officers who failed to intervene in the murder of \uc2 George Floyd\uc2 . "The Washington Post" is reporting today that the three former officers have been trained to verbally and physically intervene to stop a colleague from using unreasonable force and broke department policy when they didn`t stop Derek Chauvin from kneeling on Floyd`s neck. This, according to a police supervisor, who testified Friday.\par \par \uc2 How can leaders around the country balance protecting law enforcement such as the two young \uc2 NYPD\uc2  officers who were tragically shot and we all mourn for them and stand with their families, but also they must, at the same time enforce ways to hold police accountable for aggression and violence of their own?\par \par \uc2 ALEXANDER: Well, here is what has to happen here. One thing is very important, Reverend Al, you`ve got to have policing community working together, or we don`t have good public safety, one cannot operate without the other.\par \par \uc2 Clearly, there`s work that needs to be done in police departments around this country. We`ve been referring to it as reform, but we`ve been hearing very little of it here in recent months, but we can enforce the law, we can reform, we can look at who it is that we`re recruiting and who we are training, how they`re being supervised, how they`re being managed.\par \par \uc2 And also, we look at the culture in policing in some of these respective departments and how do we help make them better? Because here is the one thing about policy, you can have a policy that states that Reverend Al, if you see me do something wrong, you should stop me.\par \par \uc2 But quite frankly, that is something that also that has to be at the heart. That is something you can`t train. That is something you learn along the way, and your families, your churches, your schools, communities, et cetera.\par \par \uc2 But police are doing the very best that they can under the circumstances. There`s a lot of good police officers out there, a lot of good departments like \uc2 NYPD\uc2 , a lot of great leadership just like Mayor Eric Adams, who are doing their very best that they can to keep officers safe, and we`ve got to be able to keep them safe to be able to do their job and keep them well- trained and well-supervised so that they can provide the type of public safety that is necessary for our cities across America.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I think many of us want to see that done, but I think again, you`ve got to deal with the question of guns. I think that Mayor Adams and all of us have spoken how you do that has to be balanced, and I think some prosecutors are saying that some of the crimes that are not as violent or not violent at all are crimes that we need to look at differently and deal with getting guns -- it is guns that have killed these policemen.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:06]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This young man that killed both of these officers in Harlem had a backup of 40 rounds he could have shot. That`s where the attention must be and no one is addressing guns and how they continue to inundate our communities. That`s the balance we have to deal with as well as keep policemen following their own rules and laws.\par \par \uc2 Cedric Alexander, thank you for stopping by POLITICS NATION.\par \par \uc2 My final thoughts after the break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: In 1991, President George H.W. Bush nominated \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  to the Supreme Court. During the Senate hearings, a young black woman named \uc2 Anita Hill\uc2  came forward and testified against the nomination and talked about some inappropriate behavior that Mr. Thomas had done according to her while serving in another post.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:12]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: As we saw the election many years later of the presidential election of 2020 come into being, and we saw that the head of the \uc2 Senate Judiciary Committee\uc2  that grilled \uc2 Anita Hill\uc2 , grilled her and many of us felt an over the top way. The Chairman of that committee was a Senator from Delaware named \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Many years later, in 2020, as I said, that same \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  was running for President of the United States, and had to clarify and talked with Miss Hill, and in many ways, brought back some of that grilling, and some of those statements.\par \par \uc2 Well, it seems ironic that the same presidential candidate who became President that had to make amends with a black woman who will be the President that will put a black woman on the Supreme Court. That`s why I was always taught you never give up the fight. You keeps going. And you`ll see people change, and turn and go to ways you would want.\par \par \uc2 That`s why I wrote this book about "Righteous Troublemakers," people that fought never got credit, but got to turn the culture and thinking around of this nation, get my new book, "Righteous Troublemakers," and you will understand why some of us never give up.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICS NATION. I will interview Transportation Secretary \uc2 Pete Buttigieg\uc2  on his brand new comprehensive national roadways safety strategy and specifically its impact on communities of color.\par \par \uc2 That is Sunday at five right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Alicia Menendez picks up our news coverage now.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:43]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220130ei1t00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION January 22, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,715 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (17:00:00)\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST, POLITICSNATION: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: The year to reset, which is what I`d imagine \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wants and the nation certainly needs. Because right now, the nation`s faith in government is waning, while the muscles of our democracy continue to atrophy.\par \par \uc2 This week, the second year of the Biden administration began with the President`s press conference, taking Republicans to task for obstructing the nation`s progress, which, of course, they did by blocking voting rights legislation in the Senate again, with the help of Democrats unwilling to move on the filibuster, after weeks of maneuvering and the President`s own charm offensive.\par \par \uc2 And so again, Democrats must recalibrate and ask themselves what voting bills can they get passed while Republican lawmakers at all levels continue to resist and ask themselves, what voter restrictions they can get away with?\par \par \uc2 Watching all of this are black voters and Civil Rights leaders like myself, exhausted with the process and ready to demand executive orders. We`ll unpack the domestic week that was.\par \par \uc2 And shortly, I`ll ask the Chair of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee about the escalating tension between the United States and Russia over the country`s brinksmanship on Ukraine. Is confrontation inevitable? All of that tonight on POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get started.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 , Democrat of New Jersey, and Chair of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for joining us tonight. I know it was a bruising week for Democrats and certainly President Biden, who opened up the second year of his presidency with thundering condemnations of Republican obstruction, but watched as the Senate was unable to pass voting rights legislation due in no small part to imposition from members of his own party.\par \par \uc2 Critics on both sides have insisted that this administration needs a reset, as national weariness over COVID and the economy has pulled its poll numbers down. Where can the President stand to reverse course? And where should he stay on the one he`s on now?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ\uc2  (D-NJ): Reverend Sharpton, good to be with you. Look, the vote the other night in the Senate on voting rights was a tough disappointment. As someone who advocated for the legislation and spoke for it, I was extremely disappointed to see that 16 of our fellow Republicans who in 2006, led by Leader McConnell at the time voted to extend the Voting Rights Act. Somehow, a few years later, found no reason to join us.\par \par \uc2 Had those 16 joined us, we would have passed the bill without changing any rules and we would be on our way to ensuring the franchise for every citizen in this country.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to continue that fight. Some of our Republican colleagues during the course of that debate said they were for this element or that element of voting rights. Well, I think it`s time to test them on that. At the same time, we need to use the Justice Department to ensure the voting rights of people.\par \par \uc2 As it relates to the broader question about, you know what the President needs to do? I think, you know, he needs to build upon the successes he had in the first year. We forget that he entered into a raging pandemic, and now 200 million Americans are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 We forget that he created 6.4 million jobs in the first year of his administration, the greatest rate. We forget that we passed the American Rescue Plan that really helped families in tough times and save literally thousands of businesses, small and midsized businesses across the country.\par \par \uc2 He`s got to build on that, particularly on the economic equations, where families are hurting, particularly with rising costs, deal with a supply chain, and make sure that we can educate our children in the midst of a pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Those are some of the critical issues I hope that he will pursue, as he unpacks maybe the Build Better Back program.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator, and clearly a lot of what you said has not been given the proper attention, a lot of solid achievements. And one of the things that I know when you talk about voting rights and issues of Civil Rights in general, you`ve been consistent. I know you well. We`ve worked together talked down through the years since you were a senator from my bordering home state.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:05]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But one senator today, the Arizona Democratic Party formally censored your colleague, Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  for her refusal to support filibuster reform, and with that impeding passage of the Voting Rights legislation.\par \par \uc2 I see that and I keep coming back to raw politics, Senator, because I don`t see how the party expects to hold on to its majority, with so called moderates clinging to rules that make voter protections impossible.\par \par \uc2 And to be clear, we`re talking about the voters of color that the party needs. At what point do Senate Democrats start holding their colleagues accountable in some way, as apparently the Arizona state party is doing now.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, you know, every senator, Reverend Sharpton, had an opportunity to cast their vote. And of course, you know, Senator Sinema would cast a vote different than mine, I wish she had cast a vote to change the rules.\par \par \uc2 She did cast a vote for the legislation, which got no Republican votes, therefore couldn`t break our 60-vote filibuster, but was unwilling to change the rules of the Senate for our exception on voting rights.\par \par \uc2 And as I said that night, you know, at the debate, as Dr. King, you know, so clearly, and poignantly pointed out to us when he was alive, that voting rights is the essential of all rights. Without it, you cannot ultimately have a voice into who governs you, what the economy will be, what healthcare will be like, what education will be for your children.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, we`re going to continue to go back to our colleagues and press them on these issues. You know, but here`s the challenge. When you are divided 50/50 in the Senate, 50 Democrats and 50 Republicans, if you lose a Democrat and somehow they go to the other party, you`re no longer in the majority, and not able to do absolutely anything then.\par \par \uc2 So that is the challenge we have. I know it`s not one that our communities want to hear, but it is the reality of what we face.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Senator, before we run out of time, I have to ask you as Senate Foreign Relations Chair, are you in favor of military action if Russia ultimately invades Ukraine? Are the United States and our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies are concerned that it will do so?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, no, I`m not in favor of U.S. military action in Ukraine. I am in favor of giving the Ukrainians all of the wherewithal as President Biden is doing. As a matter of fact, the 200 million of the critical defensive equipment, including Javelin missiles that can stop Russian tanks arrived in an expedited fashion. There`s more of that to come.\par \par \uc2 I have offered a bill which we are trying to get Republican support for along with some of their ideas on what I call the mother of all sanctions to send a clear message to Putin before he makes a decision. This would be a huge mistake for not only you, but for the Russian people.\par \par \uc2 So I think that is the way to go in terms of trying to ensure both Ukrainians` ability to defend themselves and a global community that says to Putin, there will be devastating economic sanctions beyond those that we`ve seen in the past, and it will be a real consequence to the Russian economy.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Do you think sanctions will get Putin`s attention? Do you think that that will give him pause -- a cause to pause?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Reverend, I think there are a couple things that will get his attention. Number one is, all of this equipment that is flowing into Ukraine, and already, prior to this was flowing into Ukraine and all of the training, he has to think about how many of Russia`s sons will be lost in an invasion of Ukraine, and how many of those coffins is the Russian people willing to accept for an invasion of Ukraine, which was not instigated by Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 Secondly, you know, the sanctions we`re talking about are the typical sanctions. That`s why I call it the mother of all sanctions. Our sanctions would go not only directly to Putin and his oligarchs and military leaders, it would also go to the Russian economy, in sectoral sanctions on its extraction economy, which is its primary engine of resources.\par \par \uc2 It would sanction Russian sovereign debt, and potentially it would unplug Russia from the International System of Financial Transactions called the SWIFT system just to mention a few. These are the types of sanctions that originally brought Iran to the negotiating table. I think it`s the type of sanctions that could get Putin`s attention.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:10]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: In addition to all of the incredible material and supplies we are providing the Ukrainians, which would change the calculus, maybe he can succeed in a military venture, but the question is, at the cost of how many Russian lives?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator Menendez, thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, my political panel, Republican strategist Shermichael Singleton, a contributor to "The \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ," and former chair of the Maryland Democratic Party, \uc2 Maya Rockeymoore Cummings\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Maya, let me start with you. As I mentioned at the top of the show, so many of us are rightfully frustrated that in the same week we celebrated \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  Holiday and marked the President`s first year in office, we saw efforts to pass voting rights legislation all but collapse in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 NBC News polling shows black support for Biden has fallen 19 points since April 2020 to just 64 percent. The White House is now considering executive orders to protect minority communities` access to the ballot, as well as to reforming policing, another issue of great importance to black Americans. Can Biden fix these problems with the power of the presidential pen, Maya?\par \par \uc2 MAYA ROCKEYMOORE CUMMINGS\uc2 , FOUNDER, PRESIDENT AND CEO OF GLOBAL POLICY: Well, I think that he has some options there, and you`re absolutely right, that African-Americans are very frustrated, they didn`t get police reform, they haven`t gotten voting rights reform, and we`re facing existential threats in terms of our ability to participate in our democracy here.\par \par \uc2 But Biden does have the power of the pen. He does have, you know, these executive order options. I`m not so sure that he is willing to actually execute on police reform with regards to the executive order. But you know, certainly on voting rights, he`s already done it.\par \par \uc2 He did it last year, where he issued an executive order seeking to, you know, beef up the national voting website to expand voting access for military members, and some other minor issues. He can do it again, but this time, no more commissions, no more studying this or that. This is about actually having an executive order with some staying power that would actually make an immediate difference in the election of the 2022 and 2024.\par \par \uc2 So things that he can possibly look at is like, for example, in Section V of the NVRA, National Voter Registration Act that passed in 1993, there was a requirement, we also known as the Motor Voter Act.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right.\par \par \uc2 CUMMINGS: There was a requirement that there be a simultaneous registration of anybody that comes into the DMV to get a license, and yet, we`ve created an opt-in system in many states around the country, make it opt-out, meaning that the simultaneous component of that law actually becomes real, make it an opt-out system where everybody is automatically registered as soon as they come into the DMV, and if they want to opt out later, they can.\par \par \uc2 Another thing that they can do, we have all these Federal aid programs that are administered through the states like Medicare and Medicaid, and you know, various other programs. Use the administrative data from those programs and release them to the state so that they can automatically register those people to vote as well. That`s within the purview of the President.\par \par \uc2 Other things that he can do in terms of limiting the actions of the states. You know, he can order states not to limit access to absentee ballots, not to limit access to early voting.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, we are in unprecedented times. We are still in the midst of a global pandemic. There are things that they can do with teeth, and put it under the Department of Justice Civil Rights Division Election Department, it will be good.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And of that is in the purview of the President, all that you`ve named.\par \par \uc2 A quick follow up. As we mentioned, Maya, today the Arizona Democratic Party voted to censor Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  for blocking voting rights legislation. In a statement, they said and I`m quoting, "While we take no pleasure in this announcement, the ADP Executive Board has decided to formally censor Senator Sinema, as a result of her failure to do whatever it takes to ensure the health of our democracy."\par \par \uc2 You`ve served as the head of a state party. Is this an effective strategy with dealing with Senator Sinema? Briefly, please?\par \par \uc2 CUMMINGS: Well, let me tell you, it certainly is unprecedented. But it also is -- I`ve got to tell you, it`s serious, because I can guarantee you the party did not do that without the agreement of the entire delegation from that state.\par \par \uc2 So that means that the Democrats in Congress actually agreed and so Senator Sinema is to take this as a very serious warning.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:02]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me move on to the G.O.P. because after all, there are not just two Democratic senators that oppose voting rights right now, but also every single Republican -- every one of them, including lawmakers who supported the exact same policies in the past.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to Minority Leader Mitch McConnell talking about the issue this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-KY): Well, the concern is misplaced because if you look at the statistics, African-American voters are voting in just as higher percentage as Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Just as higher percentage as Americans. Shermichael, what`s your reaction to Senator McConnell`s comments?\par \par \uc2 SHERMICHAEL SINGLETON, REPUBLICAN POLITICAL ANALYST: Rev, thanks for having me. I mean, actually, Senate Minority Leader McConnell was one of the 17 Republicans that Senator Menendez mentioned back in 2006, during the \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  years that actually voted to renew the 1965 Voting Rights Bill, and you`ve seen a switch within the party, including among many of those very Republicans who are still in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 I can`t tell you why. I really can`t tell you why, Rev. But from my perspective, as a conservative, this is what I think should occur.\par \par \uc2 I do think that Republicans should work with Democrats to extend voting times. I do think that Republicans should work with Democrats to have more voting precincts available, particularly in heavily populated areas.\par \par \uc2 As I told a friend, Rev, a week ago, I wouldn`t wait in line for 15 hours. I certainly know that someone who has been working all day certainly wouldn`t wait in line for 10 hours. And so I think that there has to be some steps that we can take, Rev, in a bipartisan fashion that are not, Republican that are not Democratic, but that really sort of strengthens and sort of provides a healthy balance, if you will, to the voting process.\par \par \uc2 And that is critical because as we saw throughout the pandemic, as I hope we`ll see going forward, people are interested in being able to participate in the voting process, if given the proper access.\par \par \uc2 And I`m not saying that we should make it easier, I`m not saying that we shouldn`t have some type of voter laws that are available, you want voter laws that`s fine, just make sure that getting an ID is easily available.\par \par \uc2 And so I think, Rev, there are steps that we can take to strengthen the process as far as security is concerned, but while also making it easy for people to be engaged in the civic process, which is very healthy for the country.\par \par \uc2 I`ll just add quickly, Rev, when I was younger, I used to go vote with my grandparents. That`s one of the ways I fell in love with politics. I think we need to sort of continue that tradition of having people bringing their kids and their grandkids to just watch how critical this process is, whether you`re Republican or Democrat, is really, really key and critical to continuing the tradition of keeping our Democratic Republic alive.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, that`s why we need to fight because when I was young, my grandparents couldn`t vote. But, maybe the reason they changed the votes is as the senator said, he sees African-Americans as not regular Americans or other Americans.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you this, Shermichael, Senator McConnell was assuring black voters they have nothing to worry about this week, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was proposing a special police force to monitor election, something that does not currently exist anywhere in the country.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis, of course hasn`t ruled out a run for the White House in 2024, much to the chagrin of the former President Trump, is this kind of rhetoric -- is this what it takes to rocket to the top of the Republican Party right now?\par \par \uc2 SINGLETON: Well, Rev, I certainly don`t support any type of measures or steps that could be perceived as intimidating to voters. Now, it is one thing to say, hey, Democrats, Republicans, let`s have nonpartisan commissions in states across the country to make sure that the process is occurring fairly and legally.\par \par \uc2 That`s one thing to say you`re going to have an equal number of Democrats and equal number of Republicans making sure that everything is working the way that it should.\par \par \uc2 But to have people at precincts, Rev, potentially intimidating people, I just don`t think that`s a good thing. I know a lot of people have played a lot of politics with this particular issue, and I don`t think we should. Again, it`s important for us as the beacon of freedom across the world, a beacon of democracy across the world to showcase what a good and fair election process and safe election process can look like.\par \par \uc2 And we have to get away from the extremes, Rev, and be willing to work together collaboratively in order to do that. And unfortunately, I just don`t think we`re seeing enough of that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes. All right, \uc2 Maya Rockeymoore Cummings\uc2  and Shermichael Singleton, without his tie or his piano tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just ahead, we have this week`s Got-cha, a wake-up call for Democrats, what the party must focus on this year to stop the steal in 2024.\par \par \uc2 And the Roe versus Wade verdict has been handed down for 49 years today, coming up I`ll ask one of the country`s top activists whether the landmark abortion rights decision will make it to 50.\par \par \uc2 But first my colleague, Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:21]\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, good Saturday to you.\par \par \uc2 Some stories we`re watching for you this hour. The first American shipment of what is being called lethal aid to Ukraine touched down in the capital of Kiev. Tensions with Russia continue to escalate.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  and his Russian counterpart meeting in Geneva yesterday. There were no though significant breakthroughs in diplomacy talks.\par \par \uc2 COVID cases in the United States top 70 million, the number of deaths due to COVID now stands at more than 868,000 as cases appear to have peaked.\par \par \uc2 And nearly 500 residents about 150 miles south of San Francisco had to evacuate due to a wildfire. Officials say the unusual winter blaze is less than 10 percent contained. The Big Sur Fire also moved unusually fast for this season.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after a break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For this week`s Got-cha, I want to give Democrats a friendly, but firm reminder that all politics are local. A lot of the attention on voting rights now is understandably focused on the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  where legislation to protect the vote is floundering, and on the 19 states where Republicans have passed 34 new suppression laws according to the Brennan Center.\par \par \uc2 But let me warn you tonight, the fight to stop the steal in 2024 will be on the midterm ballot this year. Let me explain.\par \par \uc2 Just this week, the District Attorney in Atlanta asked for a special Grand Jury to help investigate President Trump`s efforts to overturn the 2020 election results in Georgia, including his call to the Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  asking him find 11,780 votes, which fortunately, he refused to do.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing, even as criminal probes are getting underway, Republicans are fighting to make sure Trump`s next perfect phone call gets a friendly reception all over the country.\par \par \uc2 The G.O.P. is moving aggressively to get right-wing partisans elected as Secretaries of State. According to "The Hill," the Republican State Leadership Committee, which handles Secretary of State races raised a total of $33 million in 2021. Meanwhile, the Democratic Association of Secretaries of State raised just $1 million during the first six months of last year.\par \par \uc2 Nationwide, 26 Secretaries of State positions will be on the ballot this year, 14 of which are currently held by Republicans and 12 by Democrats, and it only takes a few races to massively change the balance of Electoral College electoral power, because in many states, Secretaries of State play a decisive role in determining how election results get certified.\par \par \uc2 One way to stop all this would be comprehensive voter protections, like those Senate Democrats could pass right now if they unite behind eliminating the filibuster reforms to our electoral counting system would also be useful.\par \par \uc2 But one of the easiest solutions would be to beat Republicans at their own game. Right-wing figures like \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  have been encouraging followers to run for state and local offices for a while and it`s been having a major impact. Democrats need to start doing the same thing.\par \par \uc2 Republicans think their efforts to co-opt local politics from School Board to the Statehouse we`ll be able to fly under the radar until it is too late for anyone to do anything about it, but some of us have been paying attention to their schemes, and now, we`re getting the word out.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:32:38]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Forty-nine years ago today, history was made in the Supreme Court by a ruling that changed many women`s lives for generations.\par \par \uc2 On this day in 1973, a decision in the case of Roe versus Wade ruled that the Constitution of the United States protects a woman`s right to an abortion without government interference. But now, many women rights activists, such as my next guest, believe we may be witnessing the beginning of the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Cecile Richards, activists and former president of Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Richards, many thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 CECILE RICHARDS, ACTIVISTS AND FORMER PRESIDENT OF PLANNED PARENTHOOD: Sure.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: On Thursday, the Supreme Court declined to challenge the Texas Abortion Law, which instills a precedent for the abortion ban to take place and allows the ban to stay in effect for now.\par \par \uc2 In a strongly worded dissent, Justice Sotomayor called the Texas law quote, "a disaster and a grave disservice to women in Texas."\par \par \uc2 This week, we also saw Arizona become the sixth state to introduce this Texas-style abortion ban as this push is happening in conservative states throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 Cecile, do you think there is any hope left for Roe versus Wade? Or is this the end?\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: Well, Reverend, obviously the Supreme Court has yet to make a decision in the Mississippi case. If they decide in favor of the Mississippi abortion ban, that essentially does mean that Roe is no longer the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 So if they are good -- they could still do the right thing. You mentioned the Texas abortion ban and it is really important because not only they refuse this last week, the Supreme Court to make a ruling or to intervene in a clearly unconstitutional ban that bans abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, which is of course before many people know they`re even pregnant.\par \par \uc2 This is the third time the Supreme Court has refused to intervene in Texas. For folks down there, it has been now almost five months that access to safe and legal abortion is practically nonexistent. You could definitely say that Roe is no longer the law of the land in Texas.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:07]\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: And as you say, not only Arizona, but we estimate that probably 26 states are poised to either completely or largely ban access to safe and legal abortion.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow. That`s over half the country. Now, this is a highly contested partisan issue and the Republican Party has been focused on for years on trying to curtail abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 A new poll shows that more than two-thirds of Americans are against overturning Roe. And if it will overturn, 59 percent of Americans say they want more permissive abortion laws. Will we see this issue as an energizer for both bases ahead of the 2022 midterms?\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: I think it will be a motivator. I mean, this is a right that people have had for nearly 50 years, and I think it`s important what you point out, Reverend, this did not happen -- this abortion ban in Texas or in Mississippi didn`t happen because the voters of those states or the people in those states rose up and said we need to ban abortion.\par \par \uc2 This happened because the Republican Party, in an effort to excite their extreme right base, has done everything they can to ban abortion, and so that -- it is going to be a motivating issue, I believe. But, you know you were talking earlier about voting rights.\par \par \uc2 It is not a mistake or an accident that the same states that are banning abortion are trying to prevent people from going to the polls. They are trying to prevent expansion of access to affordable healthcare. This is the Republican Party agenda.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is, I hope, a wake-up call for anyone who has thought this couldn`t happen, it can. It can happen with a Supreme Court that was appointed largely by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And this November, we have the opportunity to send a message to the Republican Party that this country is not going to go back to a day when people couldn`t make their own decisions about pregnancy. The Republican Party wants those decisions made by government and by politicians, not by women, and that is not what the American people want.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, research has repeatedly shown that abortion rates are higher among black and brown women. So reduction in access to those services affects these communities disproportionately.\par \par \uc2 Can you talk to us about that? And what alternatives exist for these women?\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: Sure. I mean, look, I think the interesting thing is, you know, looking at what happened in the 50 years since Roe, it has greatly expanded the opportunities for all women to finish school, get a job, pursue a career, and most importantly, plan their families.\par \par \uc2 We just had huge economic benefits, healthcare benefits, and disproportionately has benefited black women who had the least access to healthcare in many of these states. And that, of course, Reverend is the problem, I mean, one of the cruel parts about what the Republican agenda is.\par \par \uc2 If abortion is banned, and women have to somehow get to some other state that falls, of course, disproportionately on women with low incomes, young women, teenagers, people that live in rural areas, and black and brown women.\par \par \uc2 And that is -- you know, if you`re working at a job at a minimum wage job, or perhaps you`re working at a place where you can`t get time off, there are a lot of folks who can`t leave their kids with someone else and leave for several days, travel hundreds of miles to get access to safe and legal abortion.\par \par \uc2 It`s one of the cruelest parts of this law, and I don`t think it`s an accident. The Republicans have not cared about the wellbeing of women, of women of color in this country, and this law -- these abortion bans are a classic example.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Cecile Richards, thank you for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 Coming up the Haitian migrant crisis last fall caught our attention then, but the plight of the black immigrant in America remains an issue now. My next guest explains.\par \par \uc2 And did you know that you can hear the latest news and updates from all of your favorite MSNBC host including me anytime, anywhere on any device with tune in. Scan the QR code to hear now.\par \par \uc2 Be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:43:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Despite having one of the largest Haitian immigrant populations in the country, the State of Florida just watched the first Haitian- American member of its congressional delegation be sworn in this week as Democrat Sheila Cherfilus-McCormick became only the second Haitian-American ever to serve in the U.S. House entering the chamber as House Democrats try to hold on to their thin majority in 2022, and many of those Democrats in the House of color are just trying to hold on to their districts.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Congresswoman Sheila Cherfilus-McCormick, Democrat of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thank you for joining us tonight and congratulations on this -- congratulations on this momentous occasion.\par \par \uc2 According to a new study from the \uc2 Pew Research Group\uc2  this week, about 4.6 million black Americans are immigrants. That`s about one in four. But Pew expects that even that may double if nearly 10 million -- they may be up to 10 million by 2060, with most of that immigration presently coming from Jamaica or Haiti.\par \par \uc2 Of course, most of the media framing around this issue of immigration focuses on Latin America and even East Asia. But I think many Americans were troubled as they watched as we all did, those confrontations between border agents and Haitian refugees at the Texas border. I even went down there, and even yet another debilitating earthquake and political instability that drove many Haitians to seek asylum.\par \par \uc2 Is our immigration system sensitive enough to the black immigrant experience? And are we ready for the explosive growth this Pew study suggests?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:16]\par \par \uc2 REP. SHEILA CHERFILUS-MCCORMICK (D-FL): Well, thank you so much for having me and our country is definitely not ready. We see that what happened at the border and what is still happening at the border, it is inhumane for us to have an immigration policy, but it`s not being applied equitably to black immigrants.\par \par \uc2 When you see so many Haitian people who are being expelled back to Haiti, some of which, who were living in South America for a long time, and didn`t have a chance to actually articulate their critical fear, and why they should even have humanitarian parole. That`s where you see the in justices that are happening with Haitian and with black immigrants.\par \par \uc2 But this is not something that just started, we have a history of the United States of treating black immigrants very differently. And until we start righting the wrongs of black immigrants, with black America, and actually address the social justice issue that we have in our country, we`re going to see this continued and precipitate into all black affairs, especially when it comes to black immigrants.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I think you said it right there, there is a social justice issue. When I went down and saw the Haitians under that bridge, it was deplorable, and I was heckled by some for going, but it is deplorable.\par \par \uc2 I saw some pictures from your swearing in this week, the official swearing on the House floor, then the photo session with you, your family, various members of the Congressional Black Caucus there in front of Rosa Parks` statue in our Capitol, which is why I`m profoundly curious as to what your morale looks like, coming into office this week after Senate Democrats failed to pass voting rights legislation, due in no small part to a small number of Democrats who refuse to let go of the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Where is your mind right now at the end of your first official week? And what do those internal Black Caucus conversations sound like right now?\par \par \uc2 CHERFILUS-MCCORMICK: Well, it is very strong, and our morale is strong, because what this shows is that the fight is far from over. We need everything that we have because when we look at the cumulative effect of what is going on with voting rights, the cumulative effect of the rise of even white supremacists coming into black areas, and trying to intimidate, when we look at the cumulative of even the states who are putting forth more voter suppression laws, we realize that this fight really is not over.\par \par \uc2 It`s far from over in fact, and so we`re dedicated even more to fight even harder. Being demoralized is not an option for us. That is our freedom. That`s our liberty for not just us, but for our children. So if anything, we`re incentivized even more to keep pushing to ensure that we have fair elections in 2022 and in 2024.\par \par \uc2 We will not stand for anyone to intimidate us, nor to treat us less than and the comments about African-Americans voting just as much as Americans is an indicator of where we are today and what we have to push through.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman, before we lose you, I`d love to hear your reaction to this bill pushed by your Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in his race for the G.O.P. presidential nomination, effectively banning public schools and private businesses from teaching anything that would make white people feel discomfort or guilt over race.\par \par \uc2 I know it passed your state Senate`s Education Committee this week. Do you think it will ultimately pass though?\par \par \uc2 CHERFILUS-MCCORMICK: I`m hoping that it doesn`t pass. If anything, it is more than disheartening. The fact that he is trying to erase the history of America in which black people were treated so terribly, it allows them to do it again, and to live a lifestyle that isn`t true.\par \par \uc2 And so we can`t allow anyone to erase history, because that puts us in jeopardy of it happening all over again. So I do believe that it is all part of a cumulative scheme so that certain groups, especially the Proud Boys feel incentivized and they feel motivated, and that Trump`s base feels motivated and that they will going through this election cycle trying to get every single seat in any way that they choose to do it because they feel like they`re covered.\par \par \uc2 So we have to stop this movement and this process that they`re doing, because they`re hitting us on every single side. Even during my election, we found Proud Boys throwing up the white supremacy sign in black communities at the African-American Library. We`ve never seen that kind of activity before, and I strongly believe that this is a precursor.\par \par \uc2 So we have to be working harder than ever as Democrats, as black people, as anyone who has an alliance and coalitions that believe in freedom and equity for everyone. We have to work harder than ever right now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, new Representative Sheila Cherfilus-McCormick. Thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 Up next on POLITICSNATION, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:53:57]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This week, I joined millions around the globe in mourning the passing of my friend, Andre Leon Talley, an icon in the fashion industry and a trailblazer in our culture, and a committed activist and a friend of this show. I also give condolences to the one and only Regina King who lost her son.\par \par \uc2 But I also want to condemn the shooting in New York, in Harlem, 10 blocks from the headquarters of National Action Network of two policemen last evening, one died, one laying in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 I was very proud when our youth department, The Huddle at National Action Network e-mailed me in the middle of the night saying they wanted on their own to do a vigil in front of the hospital and pray for the families of those fallen officers because it is just as wrong to brutalize police as it is when we stand up and fight police brutality.\par \par \uc2 And I was proud as the Huddle team led by my daughters Dominique and Ashley and Reverend Stephen Marshall and others stood out in the bitter cold to say, if we can march and have vigils when police are wrong, we must condemn then the community when they had done wrong and this is wrong, and it is wrong.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:19]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We must be morally consistent, and that is this thing that I`ve tried to emphasize all through my being a public figure. That is why this book I have out now, "Righteous Troublemakers" that came out earlier this week talks about people that were unknown, that were not household names, but were morally consistent and did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 They were making good trouble and they were righteous about it, and you can`t be righteous if you`re inconsistent.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Alicia Menendez picks about news coverage now.\par \par \uc2 [17:59:41]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Thank you so much, Reverend Sharpton.\par \par \uc2 Hello, everyone. I`m Alicia Menendez. And we begin this Saturday with new developments in the 1/6 investigation and new revelations that the former President was willing to seize voting machines after losing the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 As found in a draft of an executive have order obtained by POLITICO, a never issued order from Trump instructs the military to collect voting machines and deliver them to the Director of National Intelligence for quote, "assessment."\par \par \uc2 [18:00:10]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2022 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020220123ei1m0002t\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 Politics Nation January 22, 2022, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Richard Lui \par \uc2 6,722 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2022\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2022 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2022 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 (17:00:00)\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST, POLITICS NATION: Good evening and welcome to POLITICS NATION.\par \par \uc2 Tonight`s lead: The year to reset, which is what I`d imagine \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  wants and the nation certainly needs. Because right now, the nation`s faith in government is waning, while the muscles of our democracy continue to atrophy.\par \par \uc2 This week, the second year of the Biden administration began with the President`s press conference, taking Republicans to task for obstructing the nation`s progress, which, of course, they did by blocking voting rights legislation in the Senate again, with the help of Democrats unwilling to move on the filibuster, after weeks of maneuvering and the President`s own charm offensive.\par \par \uc2 And so again, Democrats must recalibrate and ask themselves what voting bills can they get passed while Republican lawmakers at all levels continue to resist and ask themselves, what voter restrictions they can get away with?\par \par \uc2 Watching all of this are black voters and Civil Rights leaders like myself, exhausted with the process and ready to demand executive orders. We`ll unpack the domestic week that was.\par \par \uc2 And shortly, I`ll ask the Chair of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee about the escalating tension between the United States and Russia over the country`s brinksmanship on Ukraine. Is confrontation inevitable? All of that tonight on POLITICS NATION.\par \par \uc2 Let`s get started.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Senator \uc2 Bob Menendez\uc2 , Democrat of New Jersey, and Chair of the Senate Foreign Relations Committee.\par \par \uc2 Senator, thank you for joining us tonight. I know it was a bruising week for Democrats and certainly President Biden, who opened up the second year of his presidency with thundering condemnations of Republican obstruction, but watched as the Senate was unable to pass voting rights legislation due in no small part to imposition from members of his own party.\par \par \uc2 Critics on both sides have insisted that this administration needs a reset, as national weariness over COVID and the economy has pulled its poll numbers down. Where can the President stand to reverse course? And where should he stay on the one he`s on now?\par \par \uc2 SEN. BOB MENENDEZ\uc2  (D-NJ): Reverend Sharpton, good to be with you. Look, the vote the other night in the Senate on voting rights was a tough disappointment. As someone who advocated for the legislation and spoke for it, I was extremely disappointed to see that 16 of our fellow Republicans who in 2006, led by Leader McConnell at the time voted to extend the Voting Rights Act. Somehow, a few years later, found no reason to join us.\par \par \uc2 Had those 16 joined us, we would have passed the bill without changing any rules and we would be on our way to ensuring the franchise for every citizen in this country.\par \par \uc2 So we`re going to continue that fight. Some of our Republican colleagues during the course of that debate said they were for this element or that element of voting rights. Well, I think it`s time to test them on that. At the same time, we need to use the Justice Department to ensure the voting rights of people.\par \par \uc2 As it relates to the broader question about, you know what the President needs to do? I think, you know, he needs to build upon the successes he had in the first year. We forget that he entered into a raging pandemic, and now 200 million Americans are vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 We forget that he created 6.4 million jobs in the first year of his administration, the greatest rate. We forget that we passed the American Rescue Plan that really helped families in tough times and save literally thousands of businesses, small and midsized businesses across the country.\par \par \uc2 He`s got to build on that, particularly on the economic equations, where families are hurting, particularly with rising costs, deal with a supply chain, and make sure that we can educate our children in the midst of a pandemic.\par \par \uc2 Those are some of the critical issues I hope that he will pursue, as he unpacks maybe the Build Better Back program.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator, and clearly a lot of what you said has not been given the proper attention, a lot of solid achievements. And one of the things that I know when you talk about voting rights and issues of Civil Rights in general, you`ve been consistent. I know you well. We`ve worked together talked down through the years since you were a senator from my bordering home state.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:05]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But one senator today, the Arizona Democratic Party formally censored your colleague, Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  for her refusal to support filibuster reform, and with that impeding passage of the Voting Rights legislation.\par \par \uc2 I see that and I keep coming back to raw politics, Senator, because I don`t see how the party expects to hold on to its majority, with so called moderates clinging to rules that make voter protections impossible.\par \par \uc2 And to be clear, we`re talking about the voters of color that the party needs. At what point do Senate Democrats start holding their colleagues accountable in some way, as apparently the Arizona state party is doing now.\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, you know, every senator, Reverend Sharpton, had an opportunity to cast their vote. And of course, you know, Senator Sinema would cast a vote different than mine, I wish she had cast a vote to change the rules.\par \par \uc2 She did cast a vote for the legislation, which got no Republican votes, therefore couldn`t break our 60-vote filibuster, but was unwilling to change the rules of the Senate for our exception on voting rights.\par \par \uc2 And as I said that night, you know, at the debate, as Dr. King, you know, so clearly, and poignantly pointed out to us when he was alive, that voting rights is the essential of all rights. Without it, you cannot ultimately have a voice into who governs you, what the economy will be, what healthcare will be like, what education will be for your children.\par \par \uc2 And so, you know, we`re going to continue to go back to our colleagues and press them on these issues. You know, but here`s the challenge. When you are divided 50/50 in the Senate, 50 Democrats and 50 Republicans, if you lose a Democrat and somehow they go to the other party, you`re no longer in the majority, and not able to do absolutely anything then.\par \par \uc2 So that is the challenge we have. I know it`s not one that our communities want to hear, but it is the reality of what we face.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Senator, before we run out of time, I have to ask you as Senate Foreign Relations Chair, are you in favor of military action if Russia ultimately invades Ukraine? Are the United States and our \uc2 NATO\uc2  allies are concerned that it will do so?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Well, no, I`m not in favor of U.S. military action in Ukraine. I am in favor of giving the Ukrainians all of the wherewithal as President Biden is doing. As a matter of fact, the 200 million of the critical defensive equipment, including Javelin missiles that can stop Russian tanks arrived in an expedited fashion. There`s more of that to come.\par \par \uc2 I have offered a bill which we are trying to get Republican support for along with some of their ideas on what I call the mother of all sanctions to send a clear message to Putin before he makes a decision. This would be a huge mistake for not only you, but for the Russian people.\par \par \uc2 So I think that is the way to go in terms of trying to ensure both Ukrainians` ability to defend themselves and a global community that says to Putin, there will be devastating economic sanctions beyond those that we`ve seen in the past, and it will be a real consequence to the Russian economy.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Do you think sanctions will get Putin`s attention? Do you think that that will give him pause -- a cause to pause?\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: Reverend, I think there are a couple things that will get his attention. Number one is, all of this equipment that is flowing into Ukraine, and already, prior to this was flowing into Ukraine and all of the training, he has to think about how many of Russia`s sons will be lost in an invasion of Ukraine, and how many of those coffins is the Russian people willing to accept for an invasion of Ukraine, which was not instigated by Ukraine?\par \par \uc2 Secondly, you know, the sanctions we`re talking about are the typical sanctions. That`s why I call it the mother of all sanctions. Our sanctions would go not only directly to Putin and his oligarchs and military leaders, it would also go to the Russian economy, in sectoral sanctions on its extraction economy, which is its primary engine of resources.\par \par \uc2 It would sanction Russian sovereign debt, and potentially it would unplug Russia from the International System of Financial Transactions called the SWIFT system just to mention a few. These are the types of sanctions that originally brought Iran to the negotiating table. I think it`s the type of sanctions that could get Putin`s attention.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:10]\par \par \uc2 MENENDEZ: In addition to all of the incredible material and supplies we are providing the Ukrainians, which would change the calculus, maybe he can succeed in a military venture, but the question is, at the cost of how many Russian lives?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Senator Menendez, thank you for being with us tonight.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, my political panel, Republican strategist Shermichael Singleton, a contributor to "The \uc2 Boston Globe\uc2 ," and former chair of the Maryland Democratic Party, \uc2 Maya Rockeymoore Cummings\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Maya, let me start with you. As I mentioned at the top of the show, so many of us are rightfully frustrated that in the same week we celebrated \uc2 Martin Luther King\uc2  Holiday and marked the President`s first year in office, we saw efforts to pass voting rights legislation all but collapse in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 NBC News polling shows black support for Biden has fallen 19 points since April 2020 to just 64 percent. The White House is now considering executive orders to protect minority communities` access to the ballot, as well as to reforming policing, another issue of great importance to black Americans. Can Biden fix these problems with the power of the presidential pen, Maya?\par \par \uc2 MAYA ROCKEYMOORE CUMMINGS\uc2 , FOUNDER, PRESIDENT AND CEO OF GLOBAL POLICY: Well, I think that he has some options there, and you`re absolutely right, that African-Americans are very frustrated, they didn`t get police reform, they haven`t gotten voting rights reform, and we`re facing existential threats in terms of our ability to participate in our democracy here.\par \par \uc2 But Biden does have the power of the pen. He does have, you know, these executive order options. I`m not so sure that he is willing to actually execute on police reform with regards to the executive order. But you know, certainly on voting rights, he`s already done it.\par \par \uc2 He did it last year, where he issued an executive order seeking to, you know, beef up the national voting website to expand voting access for military members, and some other minor issues. He can do it again, but this time, no more commissions, no more studying this or that. This is about actually having an executive order with some staying power that would actually make an immediate difference in the election of the 2022 and 2024.\par \par \uc2 So things that he can possibly look at is like, for example, in Section V of the NVRA, National Voter Registration Act that passed in 1993, there was a requirement, we also known as the Motor Voter Act.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Right.\par \par \uc2 CUMMINGS: There was a requirement that there be a simultaneous registration of anybody that comes into the DMV to get a license, and yet, we`ve created an opt-in system in many states around the country, make it opt-out, meaning that the simultaneous component of that law actually becomes real, make it an opt-out system where everybody is automatically registered as soon as they come into the DMV, and if they want to opt out later, they can.\par \par \uc2 Another thing that they can do, we have all these Federal aid programs that are administered through the states like Medicare and Medicaid, and you know, various other programs. Use the administrative data from those programs and release them to the state so that they can automatically register those people to vote as well. That`s within the purview of the President.\par \par \uc2 Other things that he can do in terms of limiting the actions of the states. You know, he can order states not to limit access to absentee ballots, not to limit access to early voting.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, we are in unprecedented times. We are still in the midst of a global pandemic. There are things that they can do with teeth, and put it under the Department of Justice Civil Rights Division Election Department, it will be good.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And of that is in the purview of the President, all that you`ve named.\par \par \uc2 A quick follow up. As we mentioned, Maya, today the Arizona Democratic Party voted to censor Senator \uc2 Kyrsten Sinema\uc2  for blocking voting rights legislation. In a statement, they said and I`m quoting, "While we take no pleasure in this announcement, the ADP Executive Board has decided to formally censor Senator Sinema, as a result of her failure to do whatever it takes to ensure the health of our democracy."\par \par \uc2 You`ve served as the head of a state party. Is this an effective strategy with dealing with Senator Sinema? Briefly, please?\par \par \uc2 CUMMINGS: Well, let me tell you, it certainly is unprecedented. But it also is -- I`ve got to tell you, it`s serious, because I can guarantee you the party did not do that without the agreement of the entire delegation from that state.\par \par \uc2 So that means that the Democrats in Congress actually agreed and so Senator Sinema is to take this as a very serious warning.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:02]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let me move on to the G.O.P. because after all, there are not just two Democratic senators that oppose voting rights right now, but also every single Republican -- every one of them, including lawmakers who supported the exact same policies in the past.\par \par \uc2 Take a listen to Minority Leader Mitch McConnell talking about the issue this week.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SEN. MITCH MCCONNELL\uc2  (R-KY): Well, the concern is misplaced because if you look at the statistics, African-American voters are voting in just as higher percentage as Americans.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Just as higher percentage as Americans. Shermichael, what`s your reaction to Senator McConnell`s comments?\par \par \uc2 SHERMICHAEL SINGLETON, REPUBLICAN POLITICAL ANALYST: Rev, thanks for having me. I mean, actually, Senate Minority Leader McConnell was one of the 17 Republicans that Senator Menendez mentioned back in 2006, during the \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2  years that actually voted to renew the 1965 Voting Rights Bill, and you`ve seen a switch within the party, including among many of those very Republicans who are still in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 I can`t tell you why. I really can`t tell you why, Rev. But from my perspective, as a conservative, this is what I think should occur.\par \par \uc2 I do think that Republicans should work with Democrats to extend voting times. I do think that Republicans should work with Democrats to have more voting precincts available, particularly in heavily populated areas.\par \par \uc2 As I told a friend, Rev, a week ago, I wouldn`t wait in line for 15 hours. I certainly know that someone who has been working all day certainly wouldn`t wait in line for 10 hours. And so I think that there has to be some steps that we can take, Rev, in a bipartisan fashion that are not, Republican that are not Democratic, but that really sort of strengthens and sort of provides a healthy balance, if you will, to the voting process.\par \par \uc2 And that is critical because as we saw throughout the pandemic, as I hope we`ll see going forward, people are interested in being able to participate in the voting process, if given the proper access.\par \par \uc2 And I`m not saying that we should make it easier, I`m not saying that we shouldn`t have some type of voter laws that are available, you want voter laws that`s fine, just make sure that getting an ID is easily available.\par \par \uc2 And so I think, Rev, there are steps that we can take to strengthen the process as far as security is concerned, but while also making it easy for people to be engaged in the civic process, which is very healthy for the country.\par \par \uc2 I`ll just add quickly, Rev, when I was younger, I used to go vote with my grandparents. That`s one of the ways I fell in love with politics. I think we need to sort of continue that tradition of having people bringing their kids and their grandkids to just watch how critical this process is, whether you`re Republican or Democrat, is really, really key and critical to continuing the tradition of keeping our Democratic Republic alive.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Well, that`s why we need to fight because when I was young, my grandparents couldn`t vote. But, maybe the reason they changed the votes is as the senator said, he sees African-Americans as not regular Americans or other Americans.\par \par \uc2 But let me ask you this, Shermichael, Senator McConnell was assuring black voters they have nothing to worry about this week, Florida Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  was proposing a special police force to monitor election, something that does not currently exist anywhere in the country.\par \par \uc2 DeSantis, of course hasn`t ruled out a run for the White House in 2024, much to the chagrin of the former President Trump, is this kind of rhetoric -- is this what it takes to rocket to the top of the Republican Party right now?\par \par \uc2 SINGLETON: Well, Rev, I certainly don`t support any type of measures or steps that could be perceived as intimidating to voters. Now, it is one thing to say, hey, Democrats, Republicans, let`s have nonpartisan commissions in states across the country to make sure that the process is occurring fairly and legally.\par \par \uc2 That`s one thing to say you`re going to have an equal number of Democrats and equal number of Republicans making sure that everything is working the way that it should.\par \par \uc2 But to have people at precincts, Rev, potentially intimidating people, I just don`t think that`s a good thing. I know a lot of people have played a lot of politics with this particular issue, and I don`t think we should. Again, it`s important for us as the beacon of freedom across the world, a beacon of democracy across the world to showcase what a good and fair election process and safe election process can look like.\par \par \uc2 And we have to get away from the extremes, Rev, and be willing to work together collaboratively in order to do that. And unfortunately, I just don`t think we`re seeing enough of that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes. All right, \uc2 Maya Rockeymoore Cummings\uc2  and Shermichael Singleton, without his tie or his piano tonight, thank you.\par \par \uc2 Just ahead, we have this week`s Got-cha, a wake-up call for Democrats, what the party must focus on this year to stop the steal in 2024.\par \par \uc2 And the Roe versus Wade verdict has been handed down for 49 years today, coming up I`ll ask one of the country`s top activists whether the landmark abortion rights decision will make it to 50.\par \par \uc2 But first my colleague, Richard Lui with today`s top news stories -- Richard.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:21]\par \par \uc2 RICHARD LUI, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Rev, good Saturday to you.\par \par \uc2 Some stories we`re watching for you this hour. The first American shipment of what is being called lethal aid to Ukraine touched down in the capital of Kiev. Tensions with Russia continue to escalate.\par \par \uc2 Secretary of State \uc2 Antony Blinken\uc2  and his Russian counterpart meeting in Geneva yesterday. There were no though significant breakthroughs in diplomacy talks.\par \par \uc2 COVID cases in the United States top 70 million, the number of deaths due to COVID now stands at more than 868,000 as cases appear to have peaked.\par \par \uc2 And nearly 500 residents about 150 miles south of San Francisco had to evacuate due to a wildfire. Officials say the unusual winter blaze is less than 10 percent contained. The Big Sur Fire also moved unusually fast for this season.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICS NATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  right after a break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For this week`s Got-cha, I want to give Democrats a friendly, but firm reminder that all politics are local. A lot of the attention on voting rights now is understandably focused on the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  where legislation to protect the vote is floundering, and on the 19 states where Republicans have passed 34 new suppression laws according to the Brennan Center.\par \par \uc2 But let me warn you tonight, the fight to stop the steal in 2024 will be on the midterm ballot this year. Let me explain.\par \par \uc2 Just this week, the District Attorney in Atlanta asked for a special Grand Jury to help investigate President Trump`s efforts to overturn the 2020 election results in Georgia, including his call to the Secretary of State \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2  asking him find 11,780 votes, which fortunately, he refused to do.\par \par \uc2 But here`s the thing, even as criminal probes are getting underway, Republicans are fighting to make sure Trump`s next perfect phone call gets a friendly reception all over the country.\par \par \uc2 The G.O.P. is moving aggressively to get right-wing partisans elected as Secretaries of State. According to "The Hill," the Republican State Leadership Committee, which handles Secretary of State races raised a total of $33 million in 2021. Meanwhile, the Democratic Association of Secretaries of State raised just $1 million during the first six months of last year.\par \par \uc2 Nationwide, 26 Secretaries of State positions will be on the ballot this year, 14 of which are currently held by Republicans and 12 by Democrats, and it only takes a few races to massively change the balance of Electoral College electoral power, because in many states, Secretaries of State play a decisive role in determining how election results get certified.\par \par \uc2 One way to stop all this would be comprehensive voter protections, like those Senate Democrats could pass right now if they unite behind eliminating the filibuster reforms to our electoral counting system would also be useful.\par \par \uc2 But one of the easiest solutions would be to beat Republicans at their own game. Right-wing figures like \uc2 Steve Bannon\uc2  have been encouraging followers to run for state and local offices for a while and it`s been having a major impact. Democrats need to start doing the same thing.\par \par \uc2 Republicans think their efforts to co-opt local politics from School Board to the Statehouse we`ll be able to fly under the radar until it is too late for anyone to do anything about it, but some of us have been paying attention to their schemes, and now, we`re getting the word out.\par \par \uc2 I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:32:38]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Forty-nine years ago today, history was made in the Supreme Court by a ruling that changed many women`s lives for generations.\par \par \uc2 On this day in 1973, a decision in the case of Roe versus Wade ruled that the Constitution of the United States protects a woman`s right to an abortion without government interference. But now, many women rights activists, such as my next guest, believe we may be witnessing the beginning of the end of Roe.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Cecile Richards, activists and former president of Planned Parenthood.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Richards, many thanks for being with us.\par \par \uc2 CECILE RICHARDS, ACTIVISTS AND FORMER PRESIDENT OF PLANNED PARENTHOOD: Sure.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: On Thursday, the Supreme Court declined to challenge the Texas Abortion Law, which instills a precedent for the abortion ban to take place and allows the ban to stay in effect for now.\par \par \uc2 In a strongly worded dissent, Justice Sotomayor called the Texas law quote, "a disaster and a grave disservice to women in Texas."\par \par \uc2 This week, we also saw Arizona become the sixth state to introduce this Texas-style abortion ban as this push is happening in conservative states throughout the country.\par \par \uc2 Cecile, do you think there is any hope left for Roe versus Wade? Or is this the end?\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: Well, Reverend, obviously the Supreme Court has yet to make a decision in the Mississippi case. If they decide in favor of the Mississippi abortion ban, that essentially does mean that Roe is no longer the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 So if they are good -- they could still do the right thing. You mentioned the Texas abortion ban and it is really important because not only they refuse this last week, the Supreme Court to make a ruling or to intervene in a clearly unconstitutional ban that bans abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, which is of course before many people know they`re even pregnant.\par \par \uc2 This is the third time the Supreme Court has refused to intervene in Texas. For folks down there, it has been now almost five months that access to safe and legal abortion is practically nonexistent. You could definitely say that Roe is no longer the law of the land in Texas.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:07]\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: And as you say, not only Arizona, but we estimate that probably 26 states are poised to either completely or largely ban access to safe and legal abortion.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow. That`s over half the country. Now, this is a highly contested partisan issue and the Republican Party has been focused on for years on trying to curtail abortion rights.\par \par \uc2 A new poll shows that more than two-thirds of Americans are against overturning Roe. And if it will overturn, 59 percent of Americans say they want more permissive abortion laws. Will we see this issue as an energizer for both bases ahead of the 2022 midterms?\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: I think it will be a motivator. I mean, this is a right that people have had for nearly 50 years, and I think it`s important what you point out, Reverend, this did not happen -- this abortion ban in Texas or in Mississippi didn`t happen because the voters of those states or the people in those states rose up and said we need to ban abortion.\par \par \uc2 This happened because the Republican Party, in an effort to excite their extreme right base, has done everything they can to ban abortion, and so that -- it is going to be a motivating issue, I believe. But, you know you were talking earlier about voting rights.\par \par \uc2 It is not a mistake or an accident that the same states that are banning abortion are trying to prevent people from going to the polls. They are trying to prevent expansion of access to affordable healthcare. This is the Republican Party agenda.\par \par \uc2 And so, this is, I hope, a wake-up call for anyone who has thought this couldn`t happen, it can. It can happen with a Supreme Court that was appointed largely by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And this November, we have the opportunity to send a message to the Republican Party that this country is not going to go back to a day when people couldn`t make their own decisions about pregnancy. The Republican Party wants those decisions made by government and by politicians, not by women, and that is not what the American people want.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, research has repeatedly shown that abortion rates are higher among black and brown women. So reduction in access to those services affects these communities disproportionately.\par \par \uc2 Can you talk to us about that? And what alternatives exist for these women?\par \par \uc2 RICHARDS: Sure. I mean, look, I think the interesting thing is, you know, looking at what happened in the 50 years since Roe, it has greatly expanded the opportunities for all women to finish school, get a job, pursue a career, and most importantly, plan their families.\par \par \uc2 We just had huge economic benefits, healthcare benefits, and disproportionately has benefited black women who had the least access to healthcare in many of these states. And that, of course, Reverend is the problem, I mean, one of the cruel parts about what the Republican agenda is.\par \par \uc2 If abortion is banned, and women have to somehow get to some other state that falls, of course, disproportionately on women with low incomes, young women, teenagers, people that live in rural areas, and black and brown women.\par \par \uc2 And that is -- you know, if you`re working at a job at a minimum wage job, or perhaps you`re working at a place where you can`t get time off, there are a lot of folks who can`t leave their kids with someone else and leave for several days, travel hundreds of miles to get access to safe and legal abortion.\par \par \uc2 It`s one of the cruelest parts of this law, and I don`t think it`s an accident. The Republicans have not cared about the wellbeing of women, of women of color in this country, and this law -- these abortion bans are a classic example.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Cecile Richards, thank you for being with us this evening.\par \par \uc2 Coming up the Haitian migrant crisis last fall caught our attention then, but the plight of the black immigrant in America remains an issue now. My next guest explains.\par \par \uc2 And did you know that you can hear the latest news and updates from all of your favorite MSNBC host including me anytime, anywhere on any device with tune in. Scan the QR code to hear now.\par \par \uc2 Be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:43:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Despite having one of the largest Haitian immigrant populations in the country, the State of Florida just watched the first Haitian- American member of its congressional delegation be sworn in this week as Democrat Sheila Cherfilus-McCormick became only the second Haitian-American ever to serve in the U.S. House entering the chamber as House Democrats try to hold on to their thin majority in 2022, and many of those Democrats in the House of color are just trying to hold on to their districts.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Congresswoman Sheila Cherfilus-McCormick, Democrat of Florida.\par \par \uc2 Congresswoman, thank you for joining us tonight and congratulations on this -- congratulations on this momentous occasion.\par \par \uc2 According to a new study from the \uc2 Pew Research Group\uc2  this week, about 4.6 million black Americans are immigrants. That`s about one in four. But Pew expects that even that may double if nearly 10 million -- they may be up to 10 million by 2060, with most of that immigration presently coming from Jamaica or Haiti.\par \par \uc2 Of course, most of the media framing around this issue of immigration focuses on Latin America and even East Asia. But I think many Americans were troubled as they watched as we all did, those confrontations between border agents and Haitian refugees at the Texas border. I even went down there, and even yet another debilitating earthquake and political instability that drove many Haitians to seek asylum.\par \par \uc2 Is our immigration system sensitive enough to the black immigrant experience? And are we ready for the explosive growth this Pew study suggests?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:16]\par \par \uc2 REP. SHEILA CHERFILUS-MCCORMICK (D-FL): Well, thank you so much for having me and our country is definitely not ready. We see that what happened at the border and what is still happening at the border, it is inhumane for us to have an immigration policy, but it`s not being applied equitably to black immigrants.\par \par \uc2 When you see so many Haitian people who are being expelled back to Haiti, some of which, who were living in South America for a long time, and didn`t have a chance to actually articulate their critical fear, and why they should even have humanitarian parole. That`s where you see the in justices that are happening with Haitian and with black immigrants.\par \par \uc2 But this is not something that just started, we have a history of the United States of treating black immigrants very differently. And until we start righting the wrongs of black immigrants, with black America, and actually address the social justice issue that we have in our country, we`re going to see this continued and precipitate into all black affairs, especially when it comes to black immigrants.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And I think you said it right there, there is a social justice issue. When I went down and saw the Haitians under that bridge, it was deplorable, and I was heckled by some for going, but it is deplorable.\par \par \uc2 I saw some pictures from your swearing in this week, the official swearing on the House floor, then the photo session with you, your family, various members of the Congressional Black Caucus there in front of Rosa Parks` statue in our Capitol, which is why I`m profoundly curious as to what your morale looks like, coming into office this week after Senate Democrats failed to pass voting rights legislation, due in no small part to a small number of Democrats who refuse to let go of the filibuster.\par \par \uc2 Where is your mind right now at the end of your first official week? And what do those internal Black Caucus conversations sound like right now?\par \par \uc2 CHERFILUS-MCCORMICK: Well, it is very strong, and our morale is strong, because what this shows is that the fight is far from over. We need everything that we have because when we look at the cumulative effect of what is going on with voting rights, the cumulative effect of the rise of even white supremacists coming into black areas, and trying to intimidate, when we look at the cumulative of even the states who are putting forth more voter suppression laws, we realize that this fight really is not over.\par \par \uc2 It`s far from over in fact, and so we`re dedicated even more to fight even harder. Being demoralized is not an option for us. That is our freedom. That`s our liberty for not just us, but for our children. So if anything, we`re incentivized even more to keep pushing to ensure that we have fair elections in 2022 and in 2024.\par \par \uc2 We will not stand for anyone to intimidate us, nor to treat us less than and the comments about African-Americans voting just as much as Americans is an indicator of where we are today and what we have to push through.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Congresswoman, before we lose you, I`d love to hear your reaction to this bill pushed by your Governor \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  in his race for the G.O.P. presidential nomination, effectively banning public schools and private businesses from teaching anything that would make white people feel discomfort or guilt over race.\par \par \uc2 I know it passed your state Senate`s Education Committee this week. Do you think it will ultimately pass though?\par \par \uc2 CHERFILUS-MCCORMICK: I`m hoping that it doesn`t pass. If anything, it is more than disheartening. The fact that he is trying to erase the history of America in which black people were treated so terribly, it allows them to do it again, and to live a lifestyle that isn`t true.\par \par \uc2 And so we can`t allow anyone to erase history, because that puts us in jeopardy of it happening all over again. So I do believe that it is all part of a cumulative scheme so that certain groups, especially the Proud Boys feel incentivized and they feel motivated, and that Trump`s base feels motivated and that they will going through this election cycle trying to get every single seat in any way that they choose to do it because they feel like they`re covered.\par \par \uc2 So we have to stop this movement and this process that they`re doing, because they`re hitting us on every single side. Even during my election, we found Proud Boys throwing up the white supremacy sign in black communities at the African-American Library. We`ve never seen that kind of activity before, and I strongly believe that this is a precursor.\par \par \uc2 So we have to be working harder than ever as Democrats, as black people, as anyone who has an alliance and coalitions that believe in freedom and equity for everyone. We have to work harder than ever right now.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, new Representative Sheila Cherfilus-McCormick. Thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 Up next on POLITICS NATION, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:53:57]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: This week, I joined millions around the globe in mourning the passing of my friend, Andre Leon Talley, an icon in the fashion industry and a trailblazer in our culture, and a committed activist and a friend of this show. I also give condolences to the one and only Regina King who lost her son.\par \par \uc2 But I also want to condemn the shooting in New York, in Harlem, 10 blocks from the headquarters of National Action Network of two policemen last evening, one died, one laying in critical condition.\par \par \uc2 I was very proud when our youth department, The Huddle at National Action Network e-mailed me in the middle of the night saying they wanted on their own to do a vigil in front of the hospital and pray for the families of those fallen officers because it is just as wrong to brutalize police as it is when we stand up and fight police brutality.\par \par \uc2 And I was proud as the Huddle team led by my daughters Dominique and Ashley and Reverend Stephen Marshall and others stood out in the bitter cold to say, if we can march and have vigils when police are wrong, we must condemn then the community when they had done wrong and this is wrong, and it is wrong.\par \par \uc2 [17:55:19]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We must be morally consistent, and that is this thing that I`ve tried to emphasize all through my being a public figure. That is why this book I have out now, "Righteous Troublemakers" that came out earlier this week talks about people that were unknown, that were not household names, but were morally consistent and did the right thing.\par \par \uc2 They were making good trouble and they were righteous about it, and you can`t be righteous if you`re inconsistent.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICS NATION.\par \par \uc2 Alicia Menendez picks about news coverage now.\par \par \uc2 [17:59:41]\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Thank you so much, Reverend Sharpton.\par \par \uc2 Hello, everyone. 