saying, save us. What`s happening here? And so it`s fascinating to get the detail. But does it feel like one of those things that where we could have as drama writers filled in the blanks.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Right. I mean, I think the thing that we couldn`t have known was that we would get to see all this material, right?\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: It`s one thing to know that \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  is texting with the rally organizer and the rally organizer says to him, we need direction from you. It`s getting out of hand. Thus, confirming that the rally -- the people organizing the rally which turned into the violence were taking their direction from the White House.\par \par \uc2 And we think we thought that White House was directing this thing. But actually seeing it in black and white, seeing members of the conservative media who are saying Trump had nothing to do with this on the day of, saying Trump needs to stop this because they absolutely knew he was responsible in the moment. I just never thought we would see this stuff and now, here it s.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: I think we by now have the behavioral guarantee that stupid people will put everything in writing. And so -- and so you will eventually, you will eventually get to see these things.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: If it was ever written down or typed out, it will come back. That`s exactly right.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Yes, it will. Thank you, Rachel.\par \par \uc2 MADDOW: Thank you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Well, several deeply reported books have documented Donald Trump`s rage. We have had angry presidents before. But we have never had a rage-a-holic like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . We can only imagine how high his rage is peaking to now after a federal judge who he appointed has dismissed Donald Trump`s frivolous lawsuit to block the Treasury from handing over Donald Trump`s tax returns to the chairman of the House Ways and Means Committee, Richard Neal.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump`s rage at the judge he appointed, and who he probably believed he owned is accompanied tonight by his rage at the news that his long time accountant at the Mazars accounting firm, Donald Bender, has been granted immunity to testify to the grand jury in Manhattan investigating Donald Trump`s businesses. "Washington Post" says that Mr. Bender, quote, could serve as a human road map through Trump`s tax returns.\par \par \uc2 And all of that rage comes on top of the more than 24 hours of rage \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has been experiencing about his last White House chief of staff Mark Meadows` texts revealing that Fox hosts and congressional Republicans were all trying to tell him what to do, when the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  was being attacked on January 6th, because they were all as their texts show, absolutely certain that the most ignorant and incompetent president in history was sitting in the White House with absolutely no idea what to do. That`s what their texts show about what they thought about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 And how livid is \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tonight about the public revelation that the cowardly \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 . on January 6th finally said what \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 . may have been trying to say for his whole life.\par \par \uc2 [22:05:15]\par \par \uc2 But because he is \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 ., he was not allowed to say it to his father. He was allowed to say it to a guy working for his father, \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He has to lead now. That`s what \uc2 Donald Trump Jr\uc2 . told \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  about his father when the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  was being attacked. He has to lead now.\par \par \uc2 That is a cry from the deepest part of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  Jr.`s shallow heart, the hopeless cry from a cowardly son to a cowardly father who has never known how to lead. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has never known how to lead anyone, not his children, not his company which is now under criminal investigation, with one officer of the company already indicted, and certainly not his country.\par \par \uc2 If \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  were still allowed to use \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he would no doubt be filling \uc2 Twitter\uc2  tonight with hateful nickname for the Federal Judge Trevor McFadden in the district court in Washington, D.C., who dismissed Donald Trump`s lawsuit trying to prevent the Treasury from following the law and giving his tax returns to House Ways and Means Chairman Richie Neal. Judge McFadden, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  appointed, said that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , quote, is wrong on the law. A long line of Supreme Court cases requires great deference facially valid congressional inquiries. Even the special solicitude accorded former presidents does not alter the outcome. The court will therefore dismiss this case.\par \par \uc2 Judge McFadden began his opinion at the beginning, the beginning of taxation. Congress first levied an income tax 1862 at the height of civil war. Under that law, the public could access and inspect any tax return. Judge McFadden reviewed the special law that allows only the chairman of the House Ways and Means Committee and the chairman of the Senate Finance Committee to request copies of any tax return from the \uc2 IRS\uc2 . \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  tried to have the law found unconstitutional on its face, a claim Judge McFadden dismissed.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  falsely claimed that Chairman Neal was abusing his power just so he could make Donald Trump`s tax returns public. In the final line of Judge McFadden`s 45-page decision, he said, it might not be right or wise to publish the returns. But it is the chairman`s right to do so. Congress has granted him this extraordinary power, and courts are loath to second- guess congressional motives or duly enacted statutes. The court will not do so here and thus must dismiss this case.\par \par \uc2 Chairman Neal and his staff will not be able to enlist the help of Donald Trump`s accountants to provide a road map through Donald Trump`s tax returns since Donald Trump`s accountant is already doing that under oath with immunity to a grand jury in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 Donald Bender recently testified to that grand jury and prosecutors interviewed Rosemary Vrablic, a former managing director at Deutsche Bank that oversaw hundreds of millions of dollars in loans to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . "The Washington Post" reports, quote, "The appearances by Bender suggest prosecutors are seeking information about Trump`s finances from a small circle of outside partners who handled details of Trump`s taxes and real estate deals, Bender and Vrablic were never Trump employees but think knew more about this company`s inner workings than many employees did.\par \par \uc2 Donald Bender has been forced to testify in the past about Donald Trump`s tax returns including in Donald Trump`s frivolous lawsuit against our first guest tonight, Tim O`Brien who \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  sued for libel when Tim O`Brien reported in his book that Donald Trump`s -- that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  had lied about his wealth. Tim O`Brien, of course, won that lawsuit.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now are: Tim O`Brien, senior columnist for "Bloomberg Opinion". He is the author of the book "Trump Nation". And Neil Katyal, former acting U.S. solicitor general and MSNBC legal contributor.\par \par \uc2 And, Tim, I just want to begin with you tonight on this development today about who we now know is who is talking to the grand jury. This is the accountant Bender under oath with immunity to the grand jury in Manhattan, the same accountant who is under oath in your case.\par \par \uc2 TIM O`BRIEN, SENIOR COLUMNIST, BLOOMBERG OPINION: That`s right.\par \par \uc2 [22:10:01]\par \par \uc2 We deposed Donald Bender. And we deposed him because we wanted to find out what he knew personally about the guidance he received from \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the Donald Trump Organization in preparing Donald Trump`s financial statements and tax returns.\par \par \uc2 You know, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  routinely floated this statement of financial condition or SOFC that he would give sometimes give a reporters. He didn`t actually provide it to me until we litigated. But even his own accountants would say we can`t sign this off on this document because it couldn`t comport with general accounting principles. In other words, we`re holding our noses and looking the other way.\par \par \uc2 And Bender was the lead representative for Trump`s accounting firm. He worked directly with Allen Weisselberg and Trump preparing a number of financial documents that Trump used to get loans and report his own taxes.\par \par \uc2 And I think the really important thing here is the Manhattan DA is pursuing criminal charges against Trump. They need to prove intent.\par \par \uc2 There is more in Bender`s possession possibly and definitely in his knowledge about whether or not Trump directed him to do things, that go beyond just the financial documents themselves. If Bender is a material witness who can show that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was told he might be breaking the law and went ahead and did it -- and went ahead and did it anyway and directed them to do that if they wanted to keep their jobs that, is an important moment in this whole case.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , let`s consider Judge McFadden`s opinion tonight. This is the district court level. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  can appeal it, surely will appeal it. But it`s a federal judge, a Trump appointee, who is crushing every single argument made by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his lawyers.\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL\uc2 , MSNBC LEGAL ANALYST: Absolutely, Lawrence, Trump is getting used to that. He loses every case he is brought. That is what happened in December and January. But now all the tax return cases which he lost already unanimously at the Supreme Court before. And today`s decision I think is really important.\par \par \uc2 It`s a Trump appointed-judge, Judge McFadden, a former Trump DOJ official who says basically Trump, read the law. The law is really clear. Congress can get these tax returns. And laws really clear in the Supreme Court that we don`t accept someone even former president`s innuendo for the motivation for the House of Representatives and trying to get this information.\par \par \uc2 So, it`s an important win for the House. In another way, it`s unremarkable. Trump continues to insist that law must silence opponents and hide misdeeds and he continues to lose. And Trump`s only remaining strategy here is to delay, and it seems that the courts have had enough of him. That`s what the decision today is about.\par \par \uc2 And it comes as a really bad time for Trump, as you were saying, Lawrence. It`s not just the text that`s have come out from Fox news hosts that are saying, are saying stop this on January 6th which are incredibly powerful and compelling. It`s pretty hard, I think, to argue that the horror of January 6th was a liberal dilution when you`re on record as the Fox News hosts are, texting pleas to the president saying, please make it stop.\par \par \uc2 But second, it`s also coming, the text today emerging from his own family. It`s not just Fox News. It`s Don Jr. sounding like, I don`t know, like Roman or Kendall on "Succession" or something. Some sort of cry for help. To just plea and say make it stop.\par \par \uc2 And, boy, I mean, these two things together, you know, really signal, you know, just what hot water \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is in right now.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Tim O`Brien, you know \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . He gets this -- this opinion today, from a judge he appointed saying, not only do you have to follow this extremely clear law, which has never been questioned in the past, that your tax returns do have to be handed over to the chair of the Ways and Means Committee. But on the final page, it also says, he can basically do whatever he wants with them, including make them public.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: And Donald Trump`s going out of his mind because he assumes that every judge he appoints is in his pocket. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  rolls like an old 19th century ward boss or a mobster. He thinks all of these people owe him. And he got them jobs and therefore they have to defer to him.\par \par \uc2 He thinks the same way about the Supreme Court by the way. And I think he can come in for a very rude shock. After they appeal these last two rulings on his tax returns and they go to the court and the court affirms lower courts rulings.\par \par \uc2 This is -- he is so thuggish that he is routinely surprised that members of the bar and members of the court don`t do his bidding.\par \par \uc2 [22:15:06]\par \par \uc2 And the common thread linking the ruling last Thursday from the D.C. Circuit saying that January 6th Committee was allowed to get his tax returns, and the ruling today saying that House Ways and Means can get the tax return is that the Congress has a long standing and valid right to check the presidency, to scrutinize the presidency and to police it for abuses. And that what they`re doing right now in seeking the returns are well within their powers and serve a good public purpose.\par \par \uc2 In both rulings, they also invoked not only precedent but history. And I think there is -- this is weighing on these judges, that we are at this precipice right now. And that all we have is the rule of law and institutions to guard us from the abyss.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2  and Tim O`Brien, thank you both very much for starting us off tonight. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`BRIEN: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Coming up, while Republicans were texting \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  during the attack on the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 , \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2  was thinking about what weapon he might be able to use to protect his colleagues if the attackers entered the House chamber. Congressman \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2  will join us next.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Here`s the chairman of the House Select Committee investigating the January 6th attack on the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 , on the House floor tonight before the vote on holding \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  in contempt of Congress.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2  (D-MS): If you are making excuses to avoid cooperating with our investigation, you`re making excuses to hide the truth from the American people about what happened on January 6th. You`re making excuses as part of a cover-up. There was a steady stream of communication between certain members of Congress and Mr. Meadows about matters central to our investigation.\par \par \uc2 [22:20:10]\par \par \uc2 We have questions about those communications. We will pursue those questions. And we won`t let the facts be buried by a cover-up.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The vice chair of the committee, \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2 , outlined possible crimes committed by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY\uc2  (D-WY): Mr. Meadows` testimony will bear on another fundamental question before this committee. And that is whether \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  through action or inaction corruptly sought to obstruct or impede congress` official proceeding to count electoral votes. This committee is entitled to Mr. Meadows` testimony and it will inform our legislative judgments.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congresswoman Cheney revealed more of the texts from if Republican members of Congress sent to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  during the attack on the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 CHENEY: Here are a few others from Republican members. Quote, it is really bad up here on the Hill. Another one, the president needs to stop this ASAP. Another one, fix this now.\par \par \uc2 But we know hours passed with no action by the president to defend the \uc2 Congress of the United States\uc2  from an assault while we were trying to count electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 HANNITY: That was at the rules committee meeting this morning.\par \par \uc2 While Republicans were sending those texts to \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was doing nothing, our next guest, a highly trained former Army Ranger and combat veteran was protecting and reassuring his colleagues in the House chamber and planning to use his pen as a weapon if the attackers made it into the House chamber.\par \par \uc2 Joining us now is Democratic Congressman \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2  of Colorado. He is a member of the \uc2 House Armed Services Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Thank you very much for joining us tonight, Congressman Crow. I know the vote on the Meadows contempt is coming up on the floor very soon. We appreciate your time. Thank you for joining us right now.\par \par \uc2 What have you been feeling as you have been reading those texts that were sent to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  by your Republican colleagues while you were on the floor there trying to think about how to protect the lives of your colleagues?\par \par \uc2 REP. JASON CROW\uc2  (D-CO): Hi. Thanks for having me, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 Well, I`ve been angry. Frankly, I hope America is angry too. As there was a mob descending on the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  not just attacking their representatives, attacking \uc2 Capitol police\uc2 , attacking the staff and journalists, brutally beating Metropolitan and Capitol Police, but undermining their vote, actually trying to overturn the votes of the American people.\par \par \uc2 And the other end of the mall was utter disregard by \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and others, people ignoring pleas for help. People should be really upset about that. People should be angry. If they`re not, they`re not paying attention in the way that they should be.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Let`s listen to what Chairman Thompson said today about who the Republican members are who were sending those texts. Their names have not yet been revealed. He didn`t reveal them. But this is what he said.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 REP. BENNIE THOMPSON\uc2  (D-MS): There won`t be any surprise as to who they are. They`ve been very public with their defense of what happened on January 6th. And obviously, it was their belief that election was fraudulent and stolen.\par \par \uc2 (END AUDIO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman Crow, one of the themes that`s clear in these texts to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  is that the people sending them believed that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  could control the attackers of the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  and that they would follow his orders to stop what they were doing. They also seem to believe that Trump left to his own devices would do absolutely nothing.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Yeah. I think that`s right. Think about it this way, Lawrence. After 9/11, the U.S. Capitol was under attack. The Pentagon was attacked. The Twin Towers were attacked. There was a mass evacuation of the federal leadership from Washington, D.C., from the \uc2 Capitol\uc2 , from the White House, from the Pentagon.\par \par \uc2 We moved our command structure. We moved our elected leaders out of Washington, D.C. so fast forward to January 6th of this year. The \uc2 Capitol\uc2  came under attack by an insurrectionist mob. In just a couple miles away at the White House, there was zero effort to move \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 [22:25:01]\par \par \uc2 That tells a lot as to who was directing and controlling that mob, and where the threat was directed and where the threat was emanating from. It speaks volumes frankly.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: We have another communication to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  -- involving \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  before January 6th, saying that the National Guard was ready, and that the National Guard would be ready to protect the pro-Trump people and that there would be reinforcements for the National Guard.\par \par \uc2 So they were thinking in the White House about the National Guard before January 6th but when the attack happened, no one was lifting a finger about the National Guard or anything else.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Yeah. But are we surprised by the fact this they would be willing use the military for the political purposes at least trying to? I don`t think they would have been successful because I think the senior commanders of our military that I have a great faith and confidence in would not have allowed that happen.\par \par \uc2 But if you start with the assumption that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and the key enablers and the people that were supporting him and helping put this effort together like \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  and others, if you start with the assumption they don`t care about American democracy, they do not care about the right to vote, they care about nothing other than their own power and staying in power. They would be willing to do anything and use anybody in anything to accomplish that.\par \par \uc2 So if you use that assumption, that will lead you to where you need to be and what you need to know about \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and his enablers in almost every instance.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Congressman \uc2 Jason Crow\uc2 , I know you have to go to the House floor now to vote on the \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  contempt of Congress. Thank you very much for joining us tonight during this breaking news situation. Really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 CROW: Of course.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Steve Schmidt\uc2  will join us next on how Republicans are failing what one member of Congress calls the moral test of our time.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [22:31:01]\par \par \uc2 REP. LIZ CHENEY\uc2  (R-WY): All of my colleagues, all of them knew that what happened on January 6th was an assault on our constitution. They knew it at the time. Yet now they are defending the indefensible.\par \par \uc2 Whether we tell the truth, get to the truth, and defend ourselves against it ever happening again is the moral test of our time. How we address January 6th is the moral test of our generation.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is \uc2 Steve Schmidt\uc2 , former Republican strategist and co-founder of The Lincoln Project. And Steve, on the house floor they are very soon going to begin the vote on contempt of Congress against \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 What have we learned in the last 24 hours with the revelation of these text messages, about what Republican members in the House were really thinking while the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  was being attacked? And what they are saying today and what they`re saying with their votes today. In effect, trying to defend \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 STEVE SCHMIDT\uc2 , CO-FOUNDER, THE LINCOLN PROJECT: Well, good evening, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 First off, it`s the members of Congress along with the Fox News hosts. What they`re saying at its core is that they have really unfathomable disdain and contempt for their viewers, for their voters.\par \par \uc2 They think they`re worthless. They are unworthy of the truth. They lie to them with abandon and with cynicism. And you just look at it through this prism.\par \par \uc2 They have killed hundreds of thousands of people with the lies about a pandemic, about a disease that can be controlled. That even if you get it the vaccine can prevent you from dying from it. You look at the disproportionate number of deaths out across the country.\par \par \uc2 You look at the lies about democracy. And we know that Brian Kilmeade, Laura Ingraham, \uc2 Sean Hannity\uc2 , scores of members of Congress, they knew exactly what they were looking at in real time.\par \par \uc2 They knew that they were watching an incited mob storm the Capitol of the United States in an act of insurrection trying to disrupt the counting of electoral votes.\par \par \uc2 They knew that this was Trump incited. That he alone could stop it. That it had raged out of control and in this last year fundamentally has been spent.\par \par \uc2 But the first moment of the presidency, was trying to tell us that we saw something that we didn`t see. But, of course, we all saw it. We saw that Barack Obama`s crowd size is bigger and we saw it play out on television this murderous mob come to do violence teaming with fascistic elements, extremist elements, militias, Proud Boys and the sordid list goes on.\par \par \uc2 So at the end what we find out through this for certain is that the people on Fox News, they don`t believe a word of what they say, of what they preach, what they sell for billions of dollars.\par \par \uc2 The cynicism is completely exposed. I suppose there are some people in the country that look at the money in Joel Osteen`s (ph) wall and say goodness, it`s a miracle, you know. But look, at the end of the day, this stuff speaks for itself.\par \par \uc2 [22:34:41]\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Steve, one thing I`m struck by in all of the texts, this is universal, in every one of the texts that has been read from a Republican member of Congress to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 , to the Fox hosts -- all of them -- to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2  to Donald Trump`s own son to \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Every one of those people on January 6th during the attack they all are convinced of one thing. \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  has no idea what to do. Therefore, I must try to tell him what to do through \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In other words, every one of them knew that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  was unfit to serve as commander in chief while the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  was being attacked.\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: Well, for sure that`s true, right. Look. You have to think about this, I think, like