somewhere in those first three months. So let`s say by March. We will be seeing this in carefully structured televised hearings with the committee.\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: Yes. I mean I think I`d like to see these committee hearings play out in prime time where the maximum number of the American people can see it, where people who aren`t riveted to politics 24/7 can look and understand what`s at stake here with regard to the future of American democracy. What`s at stake with regard to the truth. The cynicism of the people that have been lying to them.\par \par \uc2 So this is going to be an enormously important event in this -- in this country. And it`s going to frame a really important decision as we`re five years out from celebrating the 250th anniversary of the birth of this country.\par \par \uc2 So yes, it`s a profoundly important moment and this committee is really going to shape, I think, the discussion for many, many months to come as we enter into `22.\par \par \uc2 The dialogue is going to be different than it has been over the course of `21 because of this committee.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Steve Schmidt\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight. We always appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 SCHMIDT: You bet. Good to be with you, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, a grim anniversary became even more grim today when schoolchildren in Connecticut had to stay home because of threats to their schools. Connecticut Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  joins us next.\par \par \uc2 [22:39:28]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Nine years ago today, 20 children and six adults were murdered at Sandy Hook Elementary School in Newtown, Connecticut. Today students in Newtown remained at home due to anonymous threats that have plagued the school for years.\par \par \uc2 In a letter to parents, the Newtown superintendent of schools writes, quote, "Aside from the emotion this day evokes, we often have to deal with calls or anonymous threats that raise levels of stress for the entire school community. In recent days schools across the state continue to face threats following the tragedy in Michigan."\par \par \uc2 Here`s what President Obama said when he reflected on the mass murder at Sandy Hook in the final days of his presidency.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BARACK OBAMA\uc2 , FORMER PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Every time I think about those kids, it gets me mad.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Today President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2 , PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: For me and for Barack, the shooting at Sandy Hook Elementary School was one of the saddest days we were in office those eight years.\par \par \uc2 [22:44:52]\par \par \uc2 We came close to legislation but we came up short. It was so darn frustrating. And it`s still frustrating now for you and me and so many others.\par \par \uc2 There are three common sense bills to reduce gun violence that the Senate should pass now, right away. Long overdue.\par \par \uc2 I know our politics is frustrating, can be frustrating. It`s particularly frustrating now. But we can`t give up hope. We can`t stop.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Joining us now is Democratic Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2  of Connecticut.\par \par \uc2 Senator Murphy, thank you very much for joining us on this very difficult day, difficult day for you and your state.\par \par \uc2 Can you tell us anything more about the threat situation at Connecticut schools today?\par \par \uc2 SENATOR \uc2 CHRIS MURPHY\uc2  (D-CT): The threats are not exclusive to Sandy Hook School. There have been threats throughout the state that have caused many of our secondary and elementary schools to close.\par \par \uc2 I mean listen, it`s not surprising, unfortunately, when you have a high profile mass shooting like the one we saw in Oxford. You have copycats. And when the highest levels of the federal government do nothing to substantially condemn or stop these acts, it sends an implicit message of endorsement to other young men whose brains are breaking and contemplating these kind of actions or at the very least contemplating making a prank call.\par \par \uc2 So I think that it`s perfectly explainable these threats because of the inaction from Congress. So listen, Sandy Hook is a community that is resilient. In many ways it`s bounced back. But in many other ways, it will never be the same. This is obviously a really difficult day for the community even without these threats.\par \par \uc2 And then to have kids at home continually worrying about their safety, it is just layers of trauma on top of trauma that this country should find unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: What is this day like in Connecticut and in Newtown, this grim anniversary day once every year?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: You know, I`ve become very close with many of the families and parents of those that were killed in Sandy Hook. I try to reach out on the night before knowing that a lot of them sort of like to stay quiet on the 14th.\par \par \uc2 I will tell you for me it doesn`t get easier. It doesn`t get easier. In part because every year that passes without action is another year that I know a whole new generation of kids is going to face this reality.\par \par \uc2 And you don`t have to have a shooting at your school to be affected. You know, my two kids -- a seventh grader and a fourth grader -- go through active shooter drills. This is a generation that, you know, in some ways expects to have a shooting occur on their campus.\par \par \uc2 So, you know, in Connecticut, this is a day that I think it`s tougher, not easier over time. Those family, you know, they relive the nightmare every day. But this is certainly a time and a day where it is particularly acute.\par \par \uc2 And it`s a day for all of us to just remind ourselves this happens nowhere else but the United States of America. And that our cause is so righteous that it will prevail. And I appreciate Joe Biden`s words today to keep us to task.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The methods used to raise the debt ceiling this week and pass that through the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  appear to outsiders to be a change in Senate rules which technically it isn`t.\par \par \uc2 But it certainly appears as though to get the debt ceiling passed, an accommodation was reached to allow the Democrats to do that with only 50 votes. In fact, it was just a reversal to the way it used to be done back in the 1990s and other times when there would never, ever, ever be an objection to the motion to proceed to the debt ceiling vote.\par \par \uc2 And so that`s what they got out of the way. But to people out there who think voting rights are the most important thing the country is facing, to other people out there who think the same thing about gun safety laws where they`re always being told, sorry, we don`t have 60 votes, what do you say to them on a day like today when they`re watching the Senate bend, when they`re watching the Senate part the sea so that the debt ceiling can go through but never, never consider doing that on the issue of gun safety?\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Well, I don`t know that I can say it better than you, Lawrence. I mean what we showed today is that the Senate is willing to change the rules for something important. And 50 Democratic Senators are willing to change the rules for something important.\par \par \uc2 Now in this case, that important thing was the full faith and credit of the \uc2 United States government\uc2 . But my lord, what is more important than the safety of our kids?\par \par \uc2 [22:49:53]\par \par \uc2 I want America to pay our bills but ask those parents in Sandy Hook whether they would trade a temporary debt default for another ten minutes with their dead first graders.\par \par \uc2 I mean nothing is more important than the safety of our children and so, yes, this is a day to be reminded that there are moments when 50 Democratic Senators and even a few Republicans are willing to change the rules to do something important.\par \par \uc2 And Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  from Georgia gave one of the most stirring, most powerful speeches I`ve ever seen behind closed doors today in our caucus making this point. And maybe, just maybe some of our colleagues listened to it and will have an open mind as to how we proceed on the rules of the Senate moving forward.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Senator \uc2 Chris Murphy\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us on what I know is a very difficult day for you. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 MURPHY: Thanks, Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 And coming up, California and New York are going to go after guns using the same legal tools that Texas is now using against abortion rights. That`s next.\par \par \uc2 [22:50:59]\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: The \uc2 United States Supreme Court\uc2  is so far refusing to stop the new Texas that allows anyone to sue abortion service providers in Texas. California`s Governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  has announced that California will do whatever the state can to help women in Texas and other states where abortion services are being restricted. He said California will become a sanctuary for them.\par \par \uc2 And this week Governor Newsom announced this. "I have directed my staff to work with the legislature and the attorney general on a bill that would create a right of action allowing private citizens to seek injunctive relief and statutory damages of at least $10,000 per violation plus costs and attorney fees against anyone who manufactures, distributes or sells an assault weapon or ghost gun kit or parts in the state of California.\par \par \uc2 New York`s attorney general Tish James said she will follow California`s lead.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 LETITIA JAMES, NEW YORK ATTORNEY GENERAL: When I heard about that, I said to my team, we need to follow his lead. And the reason why that is, is because gun manufacturers and gun distributors in this country are immunized. No liability whatsoever.\par \par \uc2 They are the only industry that is protected in this country, and given the carnage, and given the fact that this is the ninth anniversary of Sandy Hook, I am sick and tired of prayers and individuals whose hearts go out to all of those who have lost lives. We can do something about it.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: Rejoining our discussion is \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , former acting U.S. solicitor general and an MSNBC legal contributor.\par \par \uc2 And Neal, I was so struck reading Gavin Newsom`s announcement. It just mirrors the Texas law almost word for word.\par \par \uc2 NEAL KATYAL\uc2 , MSNBC LEGAL CONTRIBUTOR: Absolutely. And it`s the right move. So what the Supreme Court did in this decision a couple of days ago is green-light essentially this Texas vigilante provision.\par \par \uc2 It said Texas got it wrong in the details because it included licensing officials, but the bottom line was the Supreme Court said these vigilante provisions are ok. That you can -- you as the state could enact a law that says that individuals can sue other individuals for pursuing their constitutional rights.\par \par \uc2 Now, if that sounds crazy to you, it`s because it is. And it was crazy to, for example, Chief Justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2  who is not exactly, you know, some liberal or something like that.\par \par \uc2 And I think what Tish James did today is absolutely right. If we`re going to have this in Texas for abortion, then we should have it in New York and California and other states for guns. Indeed why stop there.\par \par \uc2 You know, corporate donations are protected by terrible \uc2 Citizens United\uc2  decision. Let`s empower citizens to sue corporations that give money to campaigns. Individuals sometimes skirt campaign finance laws, why not create a cause (ph) of action allowing vigilantes to sue other people who they think are violating the campaign finance laws.\par \par \uc2 This could go on and on. It`s a nuts decision. It`s a nuts decision but if we`re going to live with it, then they`re going to have to live with the consequences in states that aren`t, you know, politically in their pocket.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So would the Supreme Court get itself out of this bind because I think it`s very clear. We know that the Republican majority in the Supreme Court would not like to see this kind of law applied by California.\par \par \uc2 Will they get themselves out of this if and when they just completely overturn Roe versus Wade and then thereby, making the Texas law irrelevant?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: No, that doesn`t get them out of it because the decision`s rhetoric and the reasoning of the decision was you basically can have these vigilante provisions, and it wasn`t an abortion-specific ruling. As the chief justice said, this is going to be for any constitutional right.\par \par \uc2 So they can`t get themselves out of this with overturning Roe versus Wade. The only way to get themselves out of it is overturning this treacherous decision, dangerous decision that the Supreme Court rendered a couple of days ago.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: So Neal, this -- California will go forward with this and these cases will eventually, presumably, ripen to the Supreme Court. It might take, what, a couple of years?\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Correct. It might take two to three years to get to the Supreme Court, but given what the Justices said, I don`t think there is a way out of this box without overturning this horrible decision that they`ve rendered a couple of days ago.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: \uc2 Neal Katyal\uc2 , thank you very much for joining us tonight. We really appreciate it.\par \par \uc2 KATYAL: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 O`DONNELL: And the House is voting right now on the contempt of Congress resolution against \uc2 Mark Meadows\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR" will bring you the results of that vote.\par \par \uc2 That is tonight`s LAST WORD.\par \par \uc2 "THE 11TH HOUR" starts now.\par \par \uc2 [22:59:57]\par \par \uc2 CHRIS JANSING, MSNBC HOST: Good evening once again. I`m Chris Jansing.\par \par \uc2 Day 329 of the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document LWLOD00020211215ehce00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION WITH \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2  for December 12, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, Yasmin Vossoughian, Steven Romo \par \uc2 7,354 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 December 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:02]\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): I mean, there have been, I think, multiple bodies, but just -- the wreckages is extensive as we continue to try to get through it.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Do you have anything from the National Service Survey (INAUDIBLE) that they are seeing in swift, anything like that sort of view.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: We have the national weather service here that could answer any questions.\par \par \uc2 CHRISTINE WIELGOS, WARNING COORDINATION METEOROLOGIST, NATIONAL WEATHER SERVICE PADUCAH, KENTUCKY: Good afternoon. My name is Christine Wielgos from the Warning Coordination Meteorologist at the National Weather Service in Paducah, Kentucky. And we have Mr. Steve Eddie, who is our meteorologist in charge at the National Weather Service in Paducah.\par \par \uc2 And for the last two days, we have had several teams out surveying the damage. Of course, as you all know, it expands a good number of miles, so, it`s a lot of ground to cover. But I did do an aerial survey yesterday and I can tell you that the track is continuous from south of Casey in Fulton County all the way to at least Beaver Dam, where we finally turned around.\par \par \uc2 So, the damage that we have surveyed so far indicates that the rating is at least an EF-3. However, we have world renowned experts on the surveys right now and also experts within the National Weather Service that are looking at the data and we`ll be looking at possibly upgrading that rating as well.\par \par \uc2 On behalf of the National Weather Service, our hearts go out to each and every person that has been affected by this tornado. It not only affects us here locally and our offices but across the entire Weather Service where we`ve had numerous people come to us and say, how deeply sorry they are for everything that`s happened here. But as we get more information, we`ll definitely release that to you. We have lots of surveys we continue to do over the next couple of days and a lot of work to do still.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Is that EF-3 for Mayfield or -- which part of the county that you --\par \par \uc2 WIELGOS: That`s the highest rating that we`ve found along the entire trek in our area. Our area serves parts -- EF-3 (INAUDIBLE). And there are locations along the path, Casey, Dawson Springs, Arlington, Bremen that are definitely harder hit areas.\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: How long is that path in miles?\par \par \uc2 WIELGOS: We have not figured out that because it`s the -- the survey has not been completed. As you go south into Tennessee, that`s another weather office that is covering that, and then you go east of Muhlenberg County, and the National Weather Service of Louisville covers everywhere east of Muhlenberg County. So, again, there are surveys ongoing for the last couple of days and we still have to go figure out exactly how long the path is.\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Great. We`ll just take one more, and we`d say that it`s at least a three and look around. I`m not a meteorologist, but I expect we`ll find it`s much more severe. So, yes?\par \par \uc2 REPORTER: Obviously, this is historic for Kentucky, but are you concerned that this kind of extreme weather could be the new normal and that this is a reflection of climate change?\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: Yes. Right now, I can`t process whether this is a new normal or not. We`re just trying to identify the dead, locate the living, reunite families, try to give people a place to stay and something to eat, get them their medicine, meet their needs. All the rest of it, over time, we could talk about. And what I`d say is please just focus on our people and the help that they need and they deserve.\par \par \uc2 As we get more information, as we get concrete numbers, we will be transparent about them. We will get them to you as soon as we can. Please understand we`re 40 hours in in the midst of rubble but we`re tough people. We`ve got a ton of help.\par \par \uc2 Thank you to the federal government. We could not ask for a stronger response. Thank you to our federal delegation. We`re going to get through it and we`re going to get through it together.\par \par \uc2 YASMIN VOSSOUGHIAN, MSNBC ANCHOR (voice over): The governor there wanting to end on a positive note, thanking everybody for their support and the federal government, of course, as well talking about the fact that he spoke with the president three times just yesterday, also hearing from, of course, Department of Homeland Secretary Mayorkas as well as the FEMA administrator.\par \par \uc2 Very much seeming like they are still very much in the beginning stages of this thing, of the rescue and recovery mission there on the ground in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 I want to bring in Elizabeth Zimmerman quickly, former FEMA Associate Administrator for the Office of Response and Recovery. Elizabeth, thanks for sticking with us through that. I appreciate it. I think it`s important for folks to hear from you right now. Help us wrap up all that we heard.\par \par \uc2 And I think probably one of the toughest things to hear is how early on they are in this mission in this rescue and recovery mission. The governor asked repeatedly for how many people had lost their lives, how many homes have been lost and not necessarily having hard and concrete numbers on that.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:01]\par \par \uc2 When will we -- at what point will we have a better understanding of the magnitude of destruction of this storm from homes and businesses lost to lives, families that have been destroyed?\par \par \uc2 ELIZABETH ZIMMERMAN, FORMER ASSOCIATE ADMINISTRATOR, FEMA RESPONSE AND RECOVERY: Yes. Thank you very much, Yasmin, for allowing me to be with you today. I just want to say my heart and prayers go out to all those people impacted by these events. These tornadoes are big. We`re 40 hours into it. And as the governor mentioned, the death toll will come in over the next course of the next days, could be a week, a couple of weeks because it has to be verified through the medical examiner and those things within the Commonwealth of Kentucky, how they notify the people that have the families and the process for that.\par \par \uc2 So, it will take time. It`s not going to happen again overnight here, as well as knowing the number of homes. I know that people are both in Kentucky and the folks from the federal family that are there looking at the area, looking at maps of what was there in Mayfield and all the other communities that were impacted. The aerial flyovers that have been happening and to be able to assess what homes are missing, what blocks are gone, what businesses, what infrastructure was there and what`s been damaged. So that, too, will take time to happen, but it`s -- the good news is that it`s happening right now. There`s people on the ground taking care of those things that need to be done.\par \par \uc2 VOSSOUGHIAN (voice over): Elizabeth Zimmerman, we appreciate you sticking with us through this and your voice on this, as always. Thank you.\par \par \uc2 That wraps up the hour for me, everybody. I`m Yasmin Vossoughian. I`ll be back here next Saturday and Sunday, 3:00 P.M. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 I`m going to turn it over to my colleague, Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 , and "POLITICSNATION."\par \par \uc2 REV. AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening, and welcome to POLITICSNATION. We start off again tonight with our thoughts on those in Kentucky and surrounding states devastated by this weekend`s extreme weather in the region. Search and rescue efforts continue in multiple towns as the confirmed death toll from the storms rises to almost 50.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  saying earlier today that his state alone may end up losing more than 100 of its residents. President Biden remains in touch with state leaders as resources and relief are sent. And satellite imagery shows just how devastating Friday night`s tornadoes were. We`ll be sure to update you with any new developments over the next hour.\par \par \uc2 But, first, tonight`s lead, priorities in order. Right now, I wonder what Democratic Party leadership must be thinking after a bruising year that resulted in a legislative breakthrough on infrastructure. That breakthrough itself made possible by Joe Biden`s historic cross-racial win last year. Speculations about Democrats` midterm election prospects are cautious, at best. And that includes with the black voters that gave him and them the white house in the first place and the Senate.\par \par \uc2 So, they should expect the perception that I and so many other civil rights activists have had over the past year, which is that while we understand the existential threat posted by climate change and that our infrastructure will determine our ability to meet needs of this century in which we find ourselves, through no fault of our own do black and brown voters and public servants find themselves fighting the very same battles of the last century. And that is why federal voting rights protections must be enhanced to stem a nationwide flood of state-level restrictions. I cannot believe that the Democratic Party leadership doesn`t get that. So let me begin tonight by asking them.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, chair of the Democratic National Committee, \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 . Chairman Harrison, thank you, first of all, for joining us tonight. I`d like to --\par \par \uc2 JAIME HARRISON\uc2 , CHAIR, DEMOCRATIC NATIONAL COMMITTEE: Great seeing you, Rev.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I`d like to start by asking, as a Democratic voter, what are the party`s three biggest priorities with 2022 three weeks away?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Well, Rev, I think you said it so artfully, as you always do.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:04]\par \par \uc2 We have to work and really tackle this issue of voting rights. It`s the most existential -- most important thing that we have to do over the course of the next few months as we move into this election.\par \par \uc2 You know, we`re going to get, and we`ve gotten so much done in terms of the president`s agenda, in terms of infrastructure, tackling COVID through the American rescue plan. We`re going to be able to help alleviate some of the pressures from families in the Build Back Better agenda. But all of that pales in comparison to the issue of voting rights. We have to make sure that we ensure that all Americans, regardless of what you look like, who you pray to, who you love, that all Americans get an opportunity to exercise their right to vote because it`s the great equalizer.\par \par \uc2 I just spent a good bit of time this week talking with folks from the administration about the importance of this, and they get it. They get it. And they`re working on it. As I tell folks, sometimes you don`t see stuff in -- it`s not all over \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and everything else but there are a lot of conversations being had right now in developing the strategy and making sure that we can find a way to get around the filibuster and to focus on getting this bill to the president`s desk, or a series of bills to the president`s desk, so that we can make sure everybody can go to the polls in 2022 with an assurance they can cast their ballot and their ballot will count when it`s been cast.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I hear that. You and I talk about it. You are always open, take my calls, meetings. I`ve talked to the White House, talked to the president. But people are frustrated. I`m frustrated. It sounds like lip service because, as we talk, and as we do things -- and you are right, some things have been done in some very key areas. There`s no question about it. But as this voting question is there, and we see states changing the laws as we are talking, they`re not waiting on the White House or somebody to try to talk. They`re changing it as we go. This frustration will lead to a massive frustration that will really affect the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 My next question simply, the Democratic Party cannot expect to win nationally in many state races without minority support. Of course, Republicans in nearly every state are going after that support through hundreds of voter restriction bills and Democrats in Congress can`t get federal voting rights bills passed due to a Republican filibuster, a filibuster. That`s the word. And that is to my chagrin. Even some moderate Democrats still support this filibuster.\par \par \uc2 So, if you need votes to win that you can`t protect, because of a rule that makes the bills impossible to pass, at what point do you give priority to candidates that back reform, if not, removal or add to push for filibuster removal to the party platform, because otherwise I do not see how this gets solved, Mr. Chairman.\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Reverend al, I think you have come to it. Listen, at the end of the day, we know we`ve got 50 votes in the Senate. When we get all 50 of our Democrats to vote for it, then we can get Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  to break that tie. But we also know in the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  in order to get most things done, it takes more than 50 votes. It actually takes 60 votes.\par \par \uc2 So, in essence, in order for us -- and this is the challenge. I`m going to straight, no chase it. The challenge is this, that either we have to get rid of the filibuster in order to get voting rights done or we have to find a carve-out in the Senate rules in order to get it done so that we can bypass the filibuster. And that is one or two routes that we have to take.\par \par \uc2 Now, I know Senator Tim Kaine is leading in the effort right now to see if we can find a carve-out specifically targeted to voting rights. And Senator Manchin and a number of other folks are a part of those discussions. If we are successful at doing that, then we can bring this legislation to the floor of the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2 , and then hopefully get it passed so that it gets to the president`s desk.\par \par \uc2 Now, if it does not get done, then we do need to make sure that we can elect more Democrats to the \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  going into this next cycle who are willing to make the modifications to the Senate rules to make sure that we can protect the thing that is most sacred to Americans which is their right to vote. It`s a great equalizer, and we`ve got to make sure that we`re doing everything within our power to do that.\par \par \uc2 But we know -- you and I know this, Reverend Al. We`ve had these conversations before.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:00]\par \par \uc2 Right now, it takes all 50 United States senators on the Democratic side in order to vote to even get into the discussion about what we do with the filibuster. And we just are not there yet in terms of having all of those centers lined up. That is not to say that people aren`t trying.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: But they need to either find a way to modify -- whatever it takes, we need to get those votes, and we need to run candidates that will say specifically that they are for the filibuster being eliminated or reformed and it needs to be in the party platform.\par \par \uc2 But Chairman, briefly, please, Democrats will effectively still be campaigning against \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  next year. His role in David Perdue`s the primary challenge against Georgia Governor Brian Kemp only this week is an example of how much power he still wields nearly a year out of office. But I read in The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  this week about how that influence is now extending into down ticket races as well.\par \par \uc2 Now, as Trump supporters, conservative activists, some of which were actually at the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  in January, are now running for the very same voter administration offices they alleged were in on the steal last year. What`s your response to that?\par \par \uc2 HARRISON: Rev, I have asked all of our state party leaders to make sure that their number one priority is to fill every seat that we can, with a Democratic candidate. Now, I know one of the hardest jobs is a state party chair or chair in general is candidate recruitment. It is hard to convince people to open their lives up to all of the chaos and the mess in politics these days, so all of the vitriol.\par \par \uc2 But the one thing if folks are focused on saving democracy, and I say this to people who are listening to me today. If you`ve been thinking about running for office, run for office. Get in contact with your state party chair and your state and let them know that you are interested in running because we need to fill every seat with a good Democrat who is interested in saving our democracy and representing the people who desperately need good leaders right now.\par \par \uc2 And so that is our push to make sure that we fill every slot that we can to make sure that we put voter protection staff on the ground to begin to build up a shield around the voters in this country. And not just Democratic voters, all voters, all voters need to be able to exercise their right to vote and that`s really, really important. And if it`s just a Democratic Party doing it, then we`re going to play that role.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Well, I thank you for being with us. And as I said, you remain open as we really, really have been putting pressure, and I hope some of the moderate Democrats are watching so they get a little view of what you get from us on the phone and Zoom calls for the frustration. And you have been committed to this. We`re going to keep the heat on because we`re talking about saving our democracy. Thank you, DNC Chair \uc2 Jaime Harrison\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now, I want to bring in Congresswoman \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 , Democrat of California. Congresswoman, thank you for being with us.\par \par \uc2 You are the former head of the Congressional Black Caucus. I don`t know anyone that has been more straightforward than you. Do you still have any belief, any hope that Congress will do anything to protect voting rights across the country before the 2022 midterms?\par \par \uc2 REP. KAREN BASS\uc2  (D-CA): Oh, I absolutely have to have that hope, Rev, because I don`t know what we do next year when you have states that are actually saying, if we`re able to get over voter suppression and win that they want to have the right to overturn the election. It`s just crazy. I mean, when this happens in other countries we call it the closing of democratic space. We issue sanctions and cut foreign aid. So to have it happen here in the United States is just egregious. We absolutely have to get voting rights on President Biden`s desk this year this year, this session.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, let me raise another issue now. If the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturns Roe versus Wade and if reports are accurate that more than two dozen states may follow up and ban abortion, the state of California has revealed a plan to make the state a sanctuary for those seeking reproductive care, including possibly paying for travel and lodging for people from other states. What are your thoughts on that?\par \par \uc2 BASS: Well, I know that this is something that is being discussed at the state level. We`ll have to see. The interesting thing is that a number of those states that would outright ban abortion also did not extend Medicaid. So, they don`t basically provide health care for their population. It`s really important that we make sure that all Californians have access to health care and then the idea that people would be able to come into our state to receive health care services is sure a sad statement on those states.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:13]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You are now running to be mayor of Los Angeles in 2022. And if elected, you will become the first black female mayor of that city. A big part of your campaign is fighting the rising crime in Los Angeles. 200 robberies were reported in the last week alone. And also we experienced, you and I and others, a terrible loss with the killing of Jacqueline Avon, wife of rock n` roll hall of famer, one of my mentors, Clarence Avon. I knew her, and you did, too. What are your specific plans in terms of public safety, policing and criminal reform?\par \par \uc2 BASS: Well, first of all, it was such a tragedy and such a blow to the city to lose Mrs. Avon, especially in the manner in which her life was lost. If people commit egregious crimes like that, they have to be held accountable to the full extent of the law. And you know, Rev, we have worked for so long for police reform, for criminal justice reform that that does not mean that we have to just absolutely sacrifice to the point where people do not feel safe.\par \par \uc2 The number one job of the mayor is to protect Angelinos. And we have to do that in every means possible. At the same time, we have to make sure that police departments are accountable, that there`s transparency. We can have safety and justice at the same time. And you know what happens when crime spikes, and it does, crime goes up, crime goes down, you know, over the years, that what happens especially when it comes to some communities, the entire community is held account accountable for the actions of a few. And that`s where you get into abuse and overstep.\par \par \uc2 And so we have to be safe. We values to have justice, police reform, all of those go hand in hand. It isn`t one or the other.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Representative \uc2 Karen Bass\uc2 , thank you for coming on.\par \par \uc2 The pandemic is taking its toll on the most vulnerable among us, our kids. What we can do to help them rise up against the challenges they face, after the break.\par \par \uc2 And later, The Supreme Court`s youth and willingness to overturn Roe versus Wade has some asking if term limits should be imposed. My political panel weighs in.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague, Steven Romo, with today`s other top news stories. Steven?\par \par \uc2 STEVEN ROMO, MSNBC HOST: Thanks, Rev. An update now on the deadly tornadoes that ripped through six states this weekend. At least 40 people have been rescued so far from what`s left of that candle factory in Mayfield, Kentucky. The building was flattened by a tornado while more than 100 workers were still inside. More than 30 people are still reported missing. Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  fears that death toll though in his state could reach triple digits. When talking about the damage at a press conference moments ago, he said this.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BESHEAR: To the people of America, there is no lens big enough to show you the extent of the damage here in Graves County or in Kentucky.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROMO: Well, dozens have been confirmed dead across five states now. At least two young children are among those victims. Tens of thousands of people across Kentucky and Tennessee are also still without power right now.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, in Illinois, a search and recovery effort is still under way at an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse. That`s where six people were killed as a tornado hit that facility. Local authorities confirm at least 45 people managed to escape.\par \par \uc2 Two storm-related deaths have been reported in Arkansas. One of the victims there has been identified as a male nursing home resident in his 80s.\par \par \uc2 President Biden vowed to help any of the states affected by those tornadoes. FEMA is on the ground in Arkansas, Illinois, Kentucky, Missouri, Mississippi and Tennessee. He`s saying that they will provide federal guidance and assess the damage.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, more POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  coming up right after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: For this week`s Rise Up, I want you all to do something to protect our children`s mental health. That is because this week, the surgeon general of the United States is calling attention to widespread mental health challenges among youths across the country. Some of those issues existed before the COVID-19 pandemic but have now been exacerbated, according to a new advisory issued this week.\par \par \uc2 The surgeon general, Vice Admiral Dr. Vivek Murthy, says it will take a whole of society effort to address. Here is a quote. If we seize this moment, step up for our children and their families in their moment of need, and lead with inclusion, kindness and respect, we can lay the foundation for a healthier and more resilient and more fulfilled nation, end of quote.\par \par \uc2 The advisory states that mental, emotional developmental or behavioral disorders have been reported in about 20 percent of children ages 3 to 17 years.\par \par \uc2 [17:30:00]\par \par \uc2 And many are not receiving adequate treatment. Inequities also persist. Black children under 13 are nearly twice as likely to die by suicide as white children. And youths with socio-economic disadvantages are two to three times more likely to develop mental health conditions than their peers, according to the advisers.\par \par \uc2 Those in rural areas who lack access to educational and mental health services are at higher risk of mental health challenges during the pandemic. As well as racial and ethnic minority youth who have experienced race-based hate due to the pandemic or live in communities that suffered an outsized number of COVID-related deaths.\par \par \uc2 So, what steps recommended by the advisory can you take to address your mental health and youths` mental health? First, recognize mental health is an essential part of overall health and empower youth to manage difficult emotions. Then in your capacity as an employer or family member, an educator or if you are involved in community or philanthropic organizations, try to ensure every child has access to high-quality, affordable and culturally competent mental health care. Also do your best to address economic and social barriers.\par \par \uc2 To quote Dr. Murthy, if we each start re-orientating our priorities to create accessible space in our homes, schools and workplaces and communities for seeking and giving assistance, we can all start building a culture that normalizes and promotes mental health care. I`m going to do my share, and I hope you do too.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:35:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back. We have a lot to get to with my political panel. Joining me now is Democratic Strategist Deshundra Jefferson.\par \par \uc2 Deshundra, The Washington Post is reporting that in a letter addressed to Speaker Pelosi and Majority Leader Hoyer, 22 moderate Democrats pled that more needs to be done by the party to address the economy ahead of the midterms. Democrats feel that voters blame them and the president for the supply chain and truck driver shortages launching great concern that Republicans will sweep the midterms if more action is not taken. What do you think?\par \par \uc2 DESHUNDRA JEFFERSON, DEMOCRATIC STRATEGIST: Well, I think it`s more than the supply chain disruptions. It really is inflation which we saw on Friday hit, what -- its highest level since 1982. People are not feeling the recovery. The stock market is doing fairly well. Income is up. But people are still feeling that it`s not impacting them. They are looking at their own pocketbooks and realizing, hey, I`m paying more money than I did last year for basics, like milk, eggs, gas prices.\par \par \uc2 And to be quite frank, looking ahead at the midterms -- I mean, looking at Virginia this past cycle, the way we botched Virginia, I don`t trust Democrats doing any better nationally with our messaging. We really have got to talk about the kitchen table issues which I don`t think is really penetrating. I don`t think Democrats have done a good job of that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Deshundra, the Supreme Court on Friday allowed Texas` draconian abortion ban to stay in effect which allowed no exception for abortions, including cases of rape and incest. How close are we to seeing the overturn of Roe versus Wade become not just hypothetically a nightmare but a reality?\par \par \uc2 JEFFERSON: I think if anything brings people on the midterms, it might be, you know, galvanizing pro-choice supporters. That might be the Democrats` ticket to getting more people out to midterms. Listen, this ban, Republicans have been plotting this out for who knows how long. I think that this -- there`s some legal maneuvering go on that the justices will allow the clinics to sue to overturn the ban. To me, it`s this legal maneuvering. It`s not going to make much of a difference because they`re going to have to go through the same process they`ve already been through. And when it goes to appeal, I believe it`s the 5th U.S. circuit, they`ve already said that the Texas law is constitutional.\par \par \uc2 I say let the sandwich (ph) trials begin, because one of the things that frightens me most about that law is that any private citizen, anywhere in the country, can raise concerns, could give a civil lawsuit saying that, you know, Deshundra Jefferson aided and abetted Jane Doe to get an abortion. So, that`s what really makes me nervous. That`s going to be a slippery slope. That`s why I say, I mean, this is the (INAUDIBLE). Who knows what direction this is going to go in. But the way I`m looking at this, it makes me as a pro-choice advocate very, very nervous.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: It`s very, very, very frightening. The January 6th -- let me go to this, while I have you. The January 6th House select committee issued another batch of subpoenas. The committee wants to hear from an array of people, from one of Trump`s aides, Max Miller, who is now running for Congress, to some of the protests and rally organizers who spearheaded the events from that day.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:00]\par \par \uc2 Clearly, they are trying to see if there`s a connection between communication in the White House to their supporters that gathered outside the \uc2 Capitol\uc2  that day. Once they hear from this new bunch, what do you predict will come out of this?\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I`ve got to be honest, Rev, it could be \uc2 Bennie Thompson\uc2  right now, okay? I mean, he might be -- the Democrats, the Republicans here (INAUDIBLE) of \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 . I mean, they`ve already indicated there are going to be public hearings next year. I -- and I look forward to it. The American public has a right to know what happened on January 6th.\par \par \uc2 And I think, you know, a lot of people are quietly nervous about what`s going to come out. How is it going to make them look? I mean, he`s already gone on record saying that over 250 people have come before the committee, either voluntarily or involuntarily. But most of them have been voluntary.\par \par \uc2 You know, January 6th -- someone who worked on the Hill, someone who spends a lot of time on the Hill, I never thought I`d see something like that happen. It`s frightening, and it was a direct assault on our democracy.\par \par \uc2 So, I think with that these new subpoenas, they are really starting to connect the dots, and I wouldn`t be surprised if we see, you know, even more before the New Year.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Thank you, Deshundra Jefferson.\par \par \uc2 Okay, we`re not taking a break. In a moment, the future of Atlanta Mayor- Elect Andre Dickens joins me to discuss his plans for police reform in the city and how to tackle voter suppression in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 But, first, my colleague, Steven Romo, with another update on today`s top news stories. Steven?\par \par \uc2 ROMO: Thanks, Rev. A lot to keep you caught up on. We`re watching updates come in on the massive rescue and recovery efforts now underway in six states after those deadly tornadoes ripped through the central U.S. More than 50,000 residents are still without power now in Kentucky. This comes as state officials fear that the death toll will climb, likely surpassing 80.\par \par \uc2 In Mayfield, recovery efforts are ongoing as that tornado struck a candle factory while more than a hundred workers were still inside. 40 employees have been rescued so far and dozens are still reported missing.\par \par \uc2 In Edwardsville, Illinois, authorities say recovery efforts at a damaged \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse are expected to take days as crews sift through rubble. Already six are confirmed to have died there. President Biden has vowed to provide assistance to those states dealing with the destruction caused by those storms.\par \par \uc2 Now back to POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:45:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Welcome back to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Atlanta`s new mayor will take the oath of office in just a few weeks. Despite the GOP`s efforts to divide and exclude in the state, Democratic mayor-elect Andre Dickens says he wants to unite and collaborate. He joins me now from Atlanta.\par \par \uc2 Good evening, Mayor-Elect Dickens, and congratulations. As you know, National Action Network, my civil rights group, has an office in Atlanta and everybody there, Reverend Sailor and Damon Bayum (ph) and Ms. Edith (ph) told me you were going to win, and you did, so congratulations.\par \par \uc2 As I mentioned earlier, the Georgia`s so-called Election Integrity Act, which clearly targets voters of color, passed this year. The law allows Republicans to restructure county election boards and take over boards they say are underperforming.\par \par \uc2 At least six counties have gone through redistricting. For example, the Spalding County Board of Elections went from having a black Democratic majority and black election supervisor to a white Republican majority. How could these and other voting -- voter suppression tactics impact the city of Atlanta in the future? And is there anything you can do about it?\par \par \uc2 MAYOR-ELECT ANDRE DICKENS (D-ATLANTA, GA): Yes, thanks, Rev, for having me on the show. Listen, this is downright voter suppression. Let`s call it exactly what it is. They ran scared after the state of Georgia went blue for the first time in a very long time. We were instrumental in making sure that Biden was elected, that Warnock and Ossoff were elected and that has them very, very scared.\par \par \uc2 And so they created a bill that has now -- it`s called SB-202 and it affects the entire state. And I`m going to be very vocal about it, just like I am right now, that the state lawmakers that created this bill wanted to suppress Democratic and black votes. And I even saw it in our municipal election that I just won just a few weeks back. And that is the precursor to what they are trying to make happen next year in the governor`s race and Senate race that Warnock is going to have to face.\par \par \uc2 What I saw two weeks ago was it was difficult for people to get a mail-in ballot or absentee ballot during COVID. They are requiring senior citizens to have to mail in a request with their driver`s license copy, get something sent back to them and then mail back their ballot with their driver`s license copy. Now, how many seniors have access to a printer? How many people have access to this technology?\par \par \uc2 So, what I saw in my election was half the people did the first part, did not get the second part and so they were confused about, well, where`s my ballot? It wasn`t counted. Did it go in? Check the internet to see. These are just ways and tactics to suppress the vote and I`m going to be vocal about it now that I`ve seen it firsthand.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, as a native of Atlanta and long-serving city council member, you are no stranger to the city`s issues.\par \par \uc2 [17:50:00]\par \par \uc2 Through your safe streets plan, you focused heavily on public safety and reforms for Atlanta`s police department. You also are a strong proponent of community of community policing. How do you believe these reforms will help the people living in Atlanta?\par \par \uc2 DICKENS: Yes, so I want to make sure we have a balanced approach to public safety, to talk about safety and justice. Because we have to be smart on crime but also be very well aware that a social justice movement is afoot and it`s necessary.\par \par \uc2 So, part of this is, we have lost about 450 officers in the past few years. So, we do need to have a level of policing to man our streets from violent crime. But we also want to make sure that we don`t have our police officers responding to non-violent situations.\par \par \uc2 We need to have specialists to deal with homelessness, to deal with mental health challenges, people having behavioral health expressions that don`t require a badge and a gun and a taser to be the best answer. We need to continue down a path of our pre-arrest diversion and issues that divert people from having to deal with a police officer that`s very necessary for violent crime, but not so necessary for non-emergencies like I mentioned before.\par \par \uc2 So, having a specialist to deal with that, to get people to the proper care that they need if they`re experiencing a mental health crisis or something related to homelessness or something thereof. I`m talking about community- based policing, where we have officers get out of their cars and walk the beat and be able to be in conversation with folks at the gas station, grocery stores and shopping centers.\par \par \uc2 We need to reduce the number of unnecessary police interactions on the wrong side of a gun, could be another case that we all don`t want to deal with across this nation. And I don`t want that to happen in Atlanta, Georgia, where we can and will get this right.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, let`s talk about more -- let`s talk about equity and inclusion. You`ve been an outspoken critic against the Buckhead cityhood movement, which aims to separate the city`s most wealthy neighborhood entirely from Atlanta. They recently set up a physical location near Buckhead Village. Meanwhile, you`ve pledged to create more affordable housing in underserved parts of the city. Talk more about that and your other plans for equity.\par \par \uc2 DICKENS: Yes, so, to me, the enemy of Atlanta is poverty, generational poverty. Everyone knows Atlanta has a black middle class and upper class and we have a lot of black businesses and I was born and raised here. I grew up in a part of the city that gave you only a 4 percent chance of making it from poverty to the upper middle class. So, we do have a problem with income, immobility and income inequity.\par \par \uc2 And so I`m really committed to making sure that Atlanta is the place that solves for equity. So, part of it is building mixed income housing, affordable housing to the tune of 20,000 units of housing over eight years. And so this is going to enable people to be able to afford to live in this city, teachers and firefighters, and just hard-working individuals that are now being priced out, because the rents and mortgages are going up, because Atlanta is so attractive.\par \par \uc2 But the other side of the equation, besides housing that we will build and create, the other side is income because affordable housing, what`s affordable is all about your income. So, I`m all about making sure that we have great-paying jobs in this city. In Atlanta, I don`t care how many buildings these billionaires build, I want to know how many Atlantans are working in your buildings, having great-paying jobs. So, that`s why I created technology training programs that have trained hundreds of people to get middle-income jobs in just four to five months.\par \par \uc2 This is about putting people on pathway out of poverty. And so when we talk about Buckhead city movement, a part of our city wants to separate from us. This is impossible. We can`t let that happen. And I`ve been very vocal and I`m fighting it, making sure that we are one Atlanta, with one future. And it`s got some undertones to it that I`m not going to allow to stay ugly.\par \par \uc2 We`re going to call it out for what it is, and we`re going to make sure that we stay this city that has been a beacon of light for the rest of the nation with regards to the civil rights movement, human rights. We`re going to make sure Buckhead sees that this city is safe and that it`s run well and we`ll do that together all across the city.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right. Well, I`m very happy to have you on. Atlanta Mayor- Elect Andre Dickens, thank you and please stay in touch with us as you take office.\par \par \uc2 Up next, my final thoughts. Stay with us.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:55:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Let us continue to pray for those in Kentucky and the surrounding states impacted by these tornadoes for their families, for the survivors, and for all that have been directly impacted by this. And let us also take the challenge of emailing and calling your U.S. senators around the question of voting. There is clearly those on the far-right that are committed to undermining the Democratic principles of voting in this country. It goes beyond the black community and the brown community, though it is disproportionately there.\par \par \uc2 By their trying to control election commissions and those that will count the vote, we`re talking about nullification. We`re talking about deciding who can vote by making it difficult, like the mayor-elect of Atlanta just said, for seniors and others to even get a ballot. They`re talking about having the state commissions appointed so that they count the ballots. Either way, you can`t have a democratic country where people can`t vote easily and then one side counts the votes that`s cast. Let`s stop this.\par \par \uc2 That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here next weekend at 5:00 P.M. Eastern.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:00]\par \par \uc2 Alicia Menendez picks up our news coverage now.\par \par \uc2 ALICIA MENENDEZ, MSNBC HOST: Thank you so much, as always, Reverend Sharpton.\par \par \uc2 Hello, everyone. I`m Alicia Menendez.\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020211213ehcc00001\par }\page {\par\fs20\uc2 News; Domestic\par \b \uc2 POLITICSNATION for December 11, 2021, MSNBC\b0\par\par\uc2 Al Sharpton, David Jolly, Steven Romo \par \uc2 6,975 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2021\par \uc2 MSNBC: Politics Nation\par \uc2 MSLIV\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC. ALL RIGHTS RESERVED. Copyright 2021 ASC Services II Media, LLC. All materials herein are protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced, distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the prior written permission of ASC Services II Media, LLC. You may not alter or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the content. \par \par \uc2 [17:00:17]\par \par \uc2 AL SHARPTON\uc2 , MSNBC HOST: Good evening and welcome to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Before we begin tonight, I want to start off with breaking news and extend my concerns and condolences after last night`s severe weather in Kentucky and surrounding states, left thousands homeless and killed dozens of people.\par \par \uc2 Kentucky alone is grieving the loss of more than 70 people, and that death toll is expected to rise as rescue efforts continue.\par \par \uc2 President Biden says he is in touch with State leaders and directing emergency resources to the region.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 JOE BIDEN\uc2  (D), PRESIDENT OF THE UNITED STATES: Jill and I pray and I sincerely mean this, pray for those who have lost loved ones, and for those who are uncertain of the fate of their loved ones, and debris that you see scattered all over the hurricanes` path.\par \par \uc2 They lost their homes, they lost their businesses. And it`s a tragedy. It`s a tragedy and we still don`t know how many lives are lost or the full extent of the damage.\par \par \uc2 But I want to emphasize what I told all the governors, the Federal government will do everything -- everything it can possibly do to help.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: We are keeping an eye on this tragedy and we will keep you updated during the hour, but first fighting words.\par \par \uc2 Right now the prime political battles of what will soon be 2022 all seem to have converged in Texas. And this weekend, the Supreme Court set the stage for perhaps the most controversial of those conflicts over abortion rights, ruling that challenges can proceed to Texas`s near total ban on abortion.\par \par \uc2 But for now, it remains law guaranteeing that next year`s midterm elections will take place in the shadow of an abortion battle 50 decades in the making. And of course, a voting rights fight going back to Jim Crow, where again, the Lone Star State has been confronted by the Biden administration.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Justice announcing its second suit against Texas in as many months, this one over the State`s Republican drawn voting maps, which the Justice Department contends were drawn expressly to prune minority representation.\par \par \uc2 And guess what? It is true. And my next guest is in the crosshairs, because what is happening in Texas is not just an assault on the black and brown residents, but also their representatives. We must now fight for their voters and their borders.\par \par \uc2 Joining me now, Congresswoman \uc2 Sheila Jackson-Lee\uc2 , Democrat of Texas. Thank you for joining us, Congresswoman.\par \par \uc2 The week began with the Department of Justice suing your state over its voting maps, which we`ll get to because the week ended, of course, with the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  allowing Texas`s six weeks abortion ban to remain in effect, but also allowing abortion providers in your state to legally challenge the ban, setting the stage for a protracted fight in the lower courts as midterm elections loom.\par \par \uc2 Now what impact has this law had on the women in your Houston district, and as a Democrat, do you see this issue driving women Democrats of either agenda to the poll?\par \par \uc2 REP. SHEILA JACKSON-LEE (D-TX): Reverend, thank you so very much for having me, and if you would just allow me as well to join you with my deepest sympathy and concern for the people in Kentucky, as a Member of Congress to join in to say that every resource will be utilized to help them.\par \par \uc2 But there is an explosive catastrophe in Texas. It is a tragedy of catastrophic proportions. And frankly, Reverend, it is a disgrace to the Constitution, and the law of the land.\par \par \uc2 The law of the land is Roe v. Wade. It is precedent. And tragically, we are allowing the trickery of state legislators to abuse women in their most difficult time. Women with their faith leader, their family, and their health provider should be the ones that make decisions about the next steps for them during a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 [17:05:09]\par \par \uc2 JACKSON-LEE: What has happened in Supreme Court, the Court that was bought by \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the conservative, lopsided six-three Court, the Court that should be expanded is that they ignored something that is sacred for those of us who are lawyers.\par \par \uc2 We are taught in first year Law School that precedence is standing as law of the land. It is to be respected. Under the 9th Amendment, Roe v. Wade became precedent, and two of the jurists that were asked Kavanaugh and Judge Amy Barrett indicated that they believed in precedent.\par \par \uc2 Isn`t it a shame? So what has happened in Texas, women are fleeing, but poor women cannot flee, and California now is trying to devise a fund that poor women can come and seek medical help.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Wow.\par \par \uc2 JACKSON-LEE: Poor women are being stalked, providers are being stalked because of the bounty law. So, it is catastrophic, and we are going to fight and yes, I think they will be voting for themselves, and they will vote Democratic in 2022.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Congresswoman, your state is popular with Merrick Garland`s Justice Department. The second of two D.O.J. voting rights lawsuits against Texas came down this week. This one citing the recent Republican drawn voting maps, and contending that they dilute black and Latino representation across the State.\par \par \uc2 And at the same time, Garland also called on Congress to restore the Justice Department`s authority to command preclearance for electoral changes to certain jurisdictions. Now, that would be passing the \uc2 John Lewis\uc2  Protection Advancement Act.\par \par \uc2 I mean, this is beyond just a black voting rights issue or brown issue. We`re talking about the basic part that this country must do to preserve its democracy. What effect would that have on Texans, Americans as we prepare to go to the polls next year?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON-LEE: I think what is most tragic, Reverend, is that we were not able, the many new faces that came to the \uc2 United States House of Representatives\uc2  in particular, are the Senate and State Legislature, were not able to be defeated at the ballot box.\par \par \uc2 So unfortunately, our friends, the Republicans took to writing people out. Took to the most insulting form of disrespect, which is to write you off, to ignore you. And they did that in Texas, where 95 percent of the growth was Hispanic, African-American and Asian, not one seat gained.\par \par \uc2 Both seats that were drafted and drawn and approved by the Governor of the State of Texas were seats that would elect Anglo-persons.\par \par \uc2 Now, everyone has a right to choose a person of their choice, but they have denied that to African-Americans, Hispanics, and Asians as well.\par \par \uc2 And the Freedom to Vote Act and the important firefighting, put out the fire, stop the fire John Robert Lewis Bill could have been the source of support.\par \par \uc2 In fact, thank you to Attorney General Garland, they using what we call Section 2. That means they have to come in when the house is on fire.\par \par \uc2 If we had had the preclearance, if we are able to vote for that bill that is now stuck in the Senate, we would not have had any of this because none of these plans would have --\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And preclearance is taken out by this Court when they dealt with the Shelby versus Holder.\par \par \uc2 But Congresswoman, I want to leave you some time for a topic I know you`re passionate about in light of that horrific school shooting in Oxford, Michigan just two weeks ago. I know you`ve sponsored legislation specifically to enhance gun safety, specifically, with regards to the storage of firearms.\par \par \uc2 What can you tell us about this bill, the Kimberly Vaughan Act?\par \par \uc2 JACKSON-LEE: People are hurting, Reverend, thank you so very much, hurting so much that for the first time in history, the prosecutor arrested the parents. And what is so shocking for those who have lost their precious children is that these parents bought a gun and then presented the gun in a non-safe place.\par \par \uc2 They say it was in a drawer. Someone says it was somewhere else. But it was accessible to this 15-year-old that obviously had challenges.\par \par \uc2 My bill says enough is enough. That it is required by gun sellers to have the words "Gun Storage Saves Lives." Tax credits will be given to those who have guns and sell storage devices, and as well penalties will occur if you do not store your gun and someone their life.\par \par \uc2 [17:10:02]\par \par \uc2 JACKSON-LEE: You know in the Second Amendment, it is never written in the right to hold a gun or to have a gun that you can be unsafe with a gun or that your gun usage or the usage of that gun by a child can then be protected under the Second Amendment.\par \par \uc2 It was wrong what happened in Oakland County in the Oxford School, it was wrong, and now, there are four dead precious children only because of the parents who bought a gun in that store.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Very, very sad, tragic, horrific, and should be dealt with.\par \par \uc2 Thank you, Representative \uc2 Jackson-Lee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We are continuing to monitor the aftermath of the deadly tornadoes that tore across the Midwest last night. In Kentucky alone, the Governor fears the death toll may be over a hundred people.\par \par \uc2 We will bringing you the latest in just a moment and throughout the evening right here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 But first I want to bring in MSNBC political analyst and former Republican Congressman, David Jolly and founder of Woke AF Nation and co-host of the podcast "Democracy-ish," Danielle Moodie.\par \par \uc2 Danielle, we mentioned the Supreme Court and the battle over abortion taking place right now. It is worth noting that three of the conservative members of that Court, Gorsuch, Kavanaugh and Barrett were all appointed by former President Trump, and are all under the age of 60.\par \par \uc2 With such a young group of Justices granted a job for life, there is a possibility that the Court will remain conservative majority for long chunk of our lives.\par \par \uc2 Is it time, Danielle, for term limits?\par \par \uc2 DANIELLE MOODIE, HOST AND FOUNDER, WOKE AF NATION: Absolutely, Reverend. I mean, here is the thing that people don`t understand is that we have lost the Courts for generations. Right?\par \par \uc2 When you talk about the agents, when you talk about the fact of how important elections are, and the thing is, is that Democrats frankly don`t talk enough about the Courts when we are in midterm elections, when we are headed into presidential elections. It is something that we don`t pay attention to.\par \par \uc2 And guess what? Republicans have been paying attention to it since the passage of Roe v. Wade, since the passage of the Civil Rights Act. Every single policy that has given people equity in this country has given people access has come through the Courts and Republicans have known that and that`s why they`ve had this plan for 40 years.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Yes. You know, David, there are calls for Republican Congresswoman Laura Boebert to be removed from her Committee assignments, after her anti-Muslim comments about Democratic Congresswoman, \uc2 Ilhan Omar\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The resolution to remove Boebert was sponsored by Congresswoman \uc2 Ayanna Pressley\uc2  and co-sponsored by several progressives, including \uc2 Pramila Jayapal\uc2 , and, of course AOC.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, on Tuesday, Boebert tweeted a photo of her and her young sons holding guns in front of a Christmas tree just a week after four kids were shot dead in a school in Michigan that I just finished speaking with Congresswoman \uc2 Jackson-Lee\uc2  about.\par \par \uc2 To troll your fellow Representatives is one thing, to be insensitive and malicious is another. What do you see happening here, David?\par \par \uc2 DAVID JOLLY, MSNBC CONTRIBUTOR: Look on the photo, it is cruel, and it speaks to the heart of the Congresswoman and the American people can judge her for that. In terms of her Islamophobic rhetoric, it is not hard to pass a condemnation within the House recognizing that Islamophobia is not just anti-Muslim, it`s anti-American, it`s antithetical to every American value that we should embrace.\par \par \uc2 And so I do think it is right for the House to pass some type of condemnation or censure, and look, I don`t think Boebert should be in the House. So if she is removed from the committees, I think that`s a net good.\par \par \uc2 But I do think as Democrats go down this road, as they approach some of these Republican Members of Congress and their violations of ethics and bringing ill-repute upon the House, Kevin McCarthy has already said during a floor speech, we`re going to do the same thing to you, and they will. Republicans will do this.\par \par \uc2 So look condemnation, absolutely whether or not to remove them -- remove her from a Committee, I think Speaker Pelosi has to make a judgment call in that regard.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Danielle, I have to bring this up. Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  this week met with a group of black women from a coalition focused on Civil Rights issues, \uc2 Melanie Campbell\uc2 , Ebonie Riley, Senior Vice President of National Action Network, among others.\par \par \uc2 The meeting was a space for black female leaders to talk to the VP not just about voting rights, but about other issues that impact black women nationwide.\par \par \uc2 [17:15:00]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: What do you think she could be doing to support the black community more? She has been given a lot of negative publicity, negative articles, many of us feel unfair. What do you think she could be doing more?\par \par \uc2 MOODIE: You know, Reverend, I want to say this is that I appreciate the fact that the Vice President met with black women leaders, but we have to understand something here, which is that black women are the base of the Democratic Party. And so while I appreciate the fact that the Vice President is meeting with these leaders, the entire administration, including the President of the United States needs to be meeting with these leaders.\par \par \uc2 It is not the sole responsibility of Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  because she is a woman of color to be uplifting women of color, when women of color have uplifted them to the White House. That`s how they got there in 2020.\par \par \uc2 And so it`s not her responsibility alone, it is the administration in its entirety, it is a responsibility alone to get at the issues that affect black people across this country, particularly black women that vote for them 98 percent of the time.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: David, New York`s Attorney General wants \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  to sit for a deposition on January 7th is all part of a civil fraud investigation into whether the \uc2 Trump Organization\uc2  purposely altered the valuation of their properties.\par \par \uc2 Trump`s lawyers are pushing back and his spokesperson says the investigation is a witch hunt and a political persecution. What do you see happening here?\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Yes, this is a common opportunity for fraud among people like \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 . You undervalue assets when you`re paying taxes and you`re obligated to pay some type of revenue on them, you overvalue them in terms of the marketplace and the sale point, and so the, New York State Attorney General is saying look, that is fraud. That is outright fraud.\par \par \uc2 As you pointed out, Rev, it`s a civil case and very importantly, the Manhattan District Attorney Cy Vance has a criminal case, and so the question is, if Tish James can get \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  under oath on this fraud claim, even though it`s civil, that creates a record that Cy Vance might use in the criminal space and go after \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  on the same type of fraud, but with criminal implications.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: David Jolly and Danielle Moodie, thank you for coming to POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 JOLLY: Thank you.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Coming up, one of the former President`s best people is taking another step to prove his loyalty, it is probably a step in the wrong direction.\par \par \uc2 And later, Trump`s next coup has already begun. January 6th was just practice. Is the G.O.P. in much better position to subvert the next election? I`ll have a guest who thinks so.\par \par \uc2 But first, my colleague, Steven Romo with an update on today`s top story. Richard -- Steven, I`m sorry -- Steven tonight.\par \par \uc2 STEVEN ROMO, MSNBC CORRESPONDENT: Yes, Rev, thanks.\par \par \uc2 Right now we are watching the latest developments from those deadly tornadoes that swept across the Central U.S. last night. More than a hundred people are feared dead as search and rescue efforts continue across six states right now.\par \par \uc2 And Kentucky, at least for tornadoes reportedly touched down. The National Weather Service estimates one of them had winds of 150 miles per hour. More than 70 people were killed. The town of Mayfield was virtually destroyed.\par \par \uc2 And rescue efforts are still underway at a flattened candle factory in that town, where 110 people had to shelter in place when that tornado hit. Governor \uc2 Andy Beshear\uc2  says he fears that dozens of workers may have been killed in that factory and at the death toll is expected to rise.\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 GOV. ANDY BESHEAR\uc2  (D-KY): The devastation is unlike anything I`ve seen in my life and I have trouble putting it into words.\par \par \uc2 When it was safe to travel this morning, I flew to Mayfield. My first stop was that candle factory. A hundred and ten people working in it at the time the storm hit. They rescued 40. There are at least 15 feet of metal with cars on top of it, barrels of corrosive chemicals that are there. It`ll be a miracle if anybody else is found alive.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROMO: And this deadly weather was so widespread, it stretched from Arkansas and Mississippi through Missouri and Tennessee up into Kentucky and Illinois last night.\par \par \uc2 In Edwardsville. Illinois, at least two deaths have been reported at an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse after a direct impact from a tornado there caused one of the walls of the building to collapse, also caused all that roof damage you see there, 30 workers managed to escape on their own, three others have been rescued from that building so far.\par \par \uc2 In Monette, Arkansas, at least one person was killed at a nursing home when the roof collapsed during the storms. At least five others were injured and that building was destroyed.\par \par \uc2 And just moments ago, President Biden sent his condolences to the victims of those tornadoes and pledged to help the States affected.\par \par \uc2 [17:20:10]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 BIDEN: We`re going to get through this. We`re going to get through this together and the Federal government is not going to walk away. This is one of those times when we aren`t Democrats or Republicans. It sounds like hyperbole, but it`s real. We are all Americans.\par \par \uc2 We stand together as the United States of America. And so I say to all the victims, you`re in our prayers, and all those first responders, emergency personnel, and everyone helping our fellow Americans that this is the right thing to do at the right time and we`re going to get through this.\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ROMO: And it may not be over yet, an estimated 12 million people are still at risk in the southeast for severe weather. Heavy rain and high wind alerts are also in place for cities across the Mid-Atlantic and northeast.\par \par \uc2 More POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  coming up after this break.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:25:01]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: And now for this week`s Got-cha. We return to the swamp to congratulate one of its most emblematic residents, California Congressman \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2 , top Republican on the House Intelligence Committee, and perhaps Donald Trump`s biggest congressional cheerleader for the last five years, and who announced this week that he would be leaving his nominal public service job to become the Chief Executive Officer of the Trump Media and Technology Group.\par \par \uc2 And what is this semi-serious collection of words you ask? Well, on paper, it will be a social media company, reflecting the MAGA point of view, freed of the constraints of \uc2 Twitter\uc2  and other outlets that have muzzled our last President since the insurrection, capable supposedly of going toe-to-toe with big streaming studios like \uc2 Netflix\uc2 , in terms of production value.\par \par \uc2 Like you, I can only wonder what an ardent dairy farmer, who still managed to sue the fake \uc2 Twitter\uc2  account of a cow knows about running an entertainment and technology company, especially one with the ambitious target of amassing more than 80 million followers and $3 billion in profit in just the next four years.\par \par \uc2 But our questions may have to wait, because apparently, both the \uc2 Securities and Exchange Commission\uc2  and the \uc2 Financial Industry Regulatory Authority\uc2  have their own with the S.E.C. filing a report this week, confirming their investigation at the same time, Nunes was breaking his big news.\par \par \uc2 And that report revealed inquiries into the Special Purpose Acquisition Company, Digital World, which has received multiple inquiries from both regulators since the October announcement that it would be acquiring Trump media to take it public. Of course, that announcement came was weeks after it told regulators that it had nothing in mind when it was formed.\par \par \uc2 "The \uc2 New York Times\uc2 " finding that the shell company`s Chief Executive had been talking with Trump about the potential deal as early as March, then there is the question of exactly who are the unnamed investors that put up $1 billion into this venture.\par \par \uc2 According to a recent Trump Media press release, but among those we do know of who invested when the company announced it would go public are Wall Street firms that cashed in when stock prices jumped, and then cashed out just as quick leaving of course, sensible retail investors like Congresswoman \uc2 Marjorie Taylor Greene\uc2 , who like many who have yet to cash out of this venture, has already lost money and is poised to continue to do so.\par \par \uc2 Certainly, Federal regulators should find that like Trump University, Trump Foundation, Trump casinos, and of course, Trump stakes, there is something rigged or overcooked, but to you, Mr. Nunes, let`s go back to you because that is something tragic, and how fitting your next post is not just yourself, but the party at this point in which Republican lawmakers now have retirement options for many of the constituents they represent, either take the old school route and become a lobbyist or work for Trump`s post pre-presidential campaign to make sure he has enough cash in 2023 or if he loses in 2024.\par \par \uc2 But given Trump`s track record with his best people, I probably don`t have to do too much, but wait. So good luck \uc2 Devin Nunes\uc2 , and when you hear "You`re fired," just remember, I said this first, I got-cha.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:33:25]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: The January 6th insurrection and Donald Trump`s coup attempt was unprecedented and ultimately unsuccessful. But over the past year, there has been a concerted and meticulous effort to make sure he does succeed next time.\par \par \uc2 In an expansive piece in "The Atlantic," staff writer, Barton Gellman lays out all the groundwork that Republicans have done to ensure they take power next election whether Americans vote for them or not, and Barton Gellman joins me now.\par \par \uc2 Let`s start with the main point of the article, Mr. Gellman. I mean, one of New York`s mega most influential pastors, Reverend Walrond called me and told me you have to read this article, the whole article, and I did.\par \par \uc2 And you say it plainly, January 6 was just practice. And I`m quoting you and you say, Trump`s next coup has already begun. Just explain what you mean there.\par \par \uc2 BARTON GELLMAN, STAFF WRITER, "THE ATLANTIC": What I mean is that Trump and his allies have identified every obstacle they had to Trump`s attempt to overthrow the last election and have gone about methodically rooting out those obstacles. So, if it wasn`t official who refused to go along with a demand to change the count, that official has been hounded out of office, or opposed for re-election or where they have changed the law so that official no longer has any power over elections, and this is going on systematically around the country.\par \par \uc2 [17:35:09]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: You know, I`m very disturbed that it seems like Democrats don`t see the urgency of what we`re talking about. We`re talking about absolutely changing the whole democratic structure of this country, or at least what it may have never lived up to me, but at least it touted.\par \par \uc2 The Justice Department is suing both Texas and Georgia, arguing new restrictive voting measures that says target black and Hispanic voters. However, we know voter suppression is just one strategy to subvert the election.\par \par \uc2 As you point out, more than a dozen states have new laws moving authority over elections from governors and career officials to legislators. Can you explain how this strategy will help Republicans seize power?\par \par \uc2 GELLMAN: Well, it`s a little bit like changing referees in the game and putting in a referee that works for you when you`re about to play the game at the election. It is one thing to suppress the vote and to reduce the number of people on the other side who get to vote, and that`s the traditional Republican vote suppression method.\par \par \uc2 This is even more dangerous, because it affects who counts the vote. And you have -- let`s just take one example, the State of Georgia, \uc2 Brad Raffensperger\uc2 , the Secretary of State refused to certify that Trump won the election when Biden actually won it. What`s happened, Trump has endorsed someone to run against him. The State Legislature has censured him. The State Legislature has removed him from being a voting member on the Board of Elections of the State.\par \par \uc2 So that even if he wins re-election, he won`t have any power to certify or decertify an election next time and they have changed the rules so that the legislature can fire county election authorities if they`re not counting the votes the way they like.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Which means to nullify the votes. I mean, that is nullification. Now, now one of the most interesting parts of your piece centers around who the insurrectionists are, those people you call foot soldiers. Tell us briefly about the demographics of that group, and how G.O.P. leaders using these people to further their goals.\par \par \uc2 GELLMAN: What`s different about this group is it is the first time in a hundred years that we`ve had a large mass political movement of people who are prepared to use violence, who are in the middle class, who are in the middle years of their lives, who are in the upper side of income and education in this country, and there have been some fascinating work done at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2 , where they traced back the insurgents, the ones who`ve been arrested and charged with crimes on January 6th, and it turns out that they are most likely to come from counties in which the fraction of white people in the population has diminished.\par \par \uc2 If white people are on the decline in a county, that county was much more likely to send an insurgent to Washington.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: I want to finish with what you wrote toward the end of your piece, you say, quote, "There is a clear and present danger that American democracy will not withstand the destructive forces that are now converging upon it. Our two-party system has only one party left that is willing to lose an election, the other is willing to win at the cost of breaking things that a democracy cannot live without."\par \par \uc2 What do we need to do to save democracy? What can the current administration do?\par \par \uc2 GELLMAN: Well, the first and foremost thing is to treat this situation as an emergency. This is not normal times. The whole point of norm breaking that Trump is so famous for is that it is abnormal, that you can`t respond in normal ways.\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  gave a speech in which he said election subversion is the greatest threat to our democracy since the Civil War. That implies that he should be acting with comparable urgency and he has not.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Barton Gellman, thank you for joining me tonight.\par \par \uc2 Up next, colleges and universities around the country are being forced to acknowledge their part in America`s ugly history of slavery. It`s all thanks in large part to student activists like these, their fight for recognition and reparations at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 . We`ll talk to them in a moment.\par \par \uc2 But first, Steven Romo joins us again with another update on the deadly weather in the Midwest -- Steven.\par \par \uc2 [17:40:00]\par \par \uc2 ROMO: Thanks, Rev. Those updates continue to pour in. Multiple tornadoes with paths hundreds of miles long spread across six states late last night causing mass devastation and killing at least 70 people. In Kentucky, 11 counties sent ambulances to the City of Mayfield, that is after a tornado flattened a candle factory there with a hundred people reportedly inside at the time, many of those workers are still unaccounted for. Rescue efforts are ongoing.\par \par \uc2 In Arkansas, at least two deaths have been confirmed, at least three people were killed in Northwestern Tennessee, and at least one person was killed when a tornado hit an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse in Edwardsville, Illinois.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be following all the latest developments on this tragedy and we`ll bring you updates on the rescue and recovery efforts throughout the night here on MSNBC.\par \par \uc2 Now, more POLITICSNATION with Reverend \uc2 Al Sharpton\uc2  coming up.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Steven Romo, thank you for that update.\par \par \uc2 Now to a story out of \uc2 Georgetown University in Washington\uc2 , D.C., where students are pressing the school to move faster to help the descendants of 272 enslaved people who were sold to Louisiana plantations by the school to pay its debts in the 19th Century.\par \par \uc2 The university promised to start funding community projects two years ago, after a student referendum and protest calling for reparations, but students say nothing has happened since.\par \par \uc2 Is \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2  breaking its promises to the descendant community?\par \par \uc2 Joining me now is Nile Blass, the Student Body President at Georgetown University, and Julia Thomas, a current student at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , and a descendant.\par \par \uc2 Julia, I want to start with you. You are a descendant of Mr. Sam Harris and Miss Betsy Ware-Harris who were trafficked and sold as slaves in 1838 by \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , and now you`re a student at the school. Tell us about it.\par \par \uc2 JULIA THOMAS, STUDENT AT \uc2 GEORGETOWN UNIVERSITY\uc2 : So it is definitely an interesting experience, but I think mainly, I feel a sense of pride and purpose and that purpose being to honor the lives and legacy of the GU-272 and the other enslaved individuals and advocating for reparations for their descendants, and I`m honored to be here today and be able to do that.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, in Nile, in October of 2019, the university said it would commit at least $400,000.00 annually to fund community-based projects to support descendant communities. You pointed out that many students assumed that would start happening immediately, but the University says the reconciliation fund effort was delayed by the coronavirus pandemic, and that it has been helping descendants and that reconciliation fund will begin considering grant proposals this spring, even though they didn`t specify how exactly these funds would be allocated. Is that enough to get this started?\par \par \uc2 NILE BLASS, STUDENT BODY PRESIDENT, GEORGETOWN UNIVERSITY: No, I think that when you`re dealing with a debt and a labor that was free to the university that built its over a billion dollar endowment that we have specific details for it, it`s not enough to be vague or inconsistent in the payment of it.\par \par \uc2 I think when we`re talking about reparations, we`re oftentimes, even in the Georgetown context, we`re treating it as a charity or something that`s being done to a privileged few, when in reality, this is the debt owed. This is someone not paying the type of work that you were doing to build an institution for centuries. So that`s not enough.\par \par \uc2 I think we deserve more clarity, and I think when you speak to descendants, not just Julia, but even others, there`s a lack of clarity there. And I think that if Georgetown were delayed as a result of the pandemic, that`s something that should have been clearly and distinctly articulated to the student body and to the community at large, and it wasn`t.\par \par \uc2 And that failure of communication isn`t the responsibility of the Georgetown community to try to reconcile or try to view charitably, they said nothing. So, we interpret that as nothing. And until we see something, not just an announcement, but real economic contribution, then we kind of have to continue considering it nothing.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Julia, you only discovered that your ancestors had been sold by the university in 2016. You told "The Washington Post" that you wished your family had known about it sooner before your grandfather died.\par \par \uc2 In coming to \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , tell us more about this specific purpose you feel.\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: So my grandfather was a very family-oriented man. He was very proud of his family, and it saddens me that he never got to know where he truly came from. So I think, my true purpose is to honor his legacy and to fight for our family and to fight for the rest of the descendants to get what is owed to us, which is reparations from \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Nile, your Class of 2022 is now the only group of students to have directly witnessed that original organizing for reparative justice. Do you feel it`s now another level of urgency?\par \par \uc2 [17:45:10]\par \par \uc2 BLASS: I think I do. I think for better for worse, when we`re dealing with university or college settings, there`s a convenience in the four-year cycle. So even if there`s a lot of work that comes with activism or social issues, if people graduate out, and there is no institutional knowledge passed down to previous classes, then there`s a lesser push, because you`re not building off of the foundation.\par \par \uc2 I think this year with working with Julia and working with the other members of the Class of 2022, it is our last time to articulate knowing what that promise was, and I think that especially we`re talking about \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 , as a case study for dealing with race relations and injustice in general, if a predominantly white institution is able to substantively and consistently and with a mind to include the descendants in leadership positions deal with their history, then the rest of the country can.\par \par \uc2 So in being that example, especially in the racially tensioned country that we are now, we have now or never, and I don`t think that graduating in six months, if this doesn`t happen, I view that as a failure of my leadership and then likewise, a failure of Georgetown`s leadership.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Julia, are you in touch with any other school or university on this topic?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: I`m not. I`ve been working with Nile and other students on this topic heavily. But I haven`t heard from any other university on this topic.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Now, Nile, this money is being raised through fundraising, not from student fees like students wanted. Why is that?\par \par \uc2 BLASS: I think it`s because Georgetown and other institutions like it -- because even when we`re talking about the terminology of reparations, it is complicated, and it is uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 I think when we`re talking about the history of the United States, in general, it makes people uncomfortable.\par \par \uc2 But when we look to the student body, and we look to the people who originally crafted that referendum, the reason it was so important is one thing that I referenced in the previous question, that this is not charity.\par \par \uc2 This isn`t fundraising for a community that isn`t inherently owed something. These individuals and their labor, so both social, cultural, and economic, contributed to a university that now brings in millions of dollars to fund itself and this auxiliary institutions.\par \par \uc2 It is not acceptable to frame that as a favor and not as a commitment to pay something that is owed.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: Julia, what else can Georgetown do apart from this money? What else can they do?\par \par \uc2 THOMAS: Well, I would like to university to start having open and honest conversations with more descendants. Georgetown communicates with about 10 descendants. We know there are thousands of descendants.\par \par \uc2 There is actually groups of hundreds of descendants who have found and elected their community leaders. Those leaders have reached out to Georgetown and Georgetown refuses to engage in conversation with those leaders.\par \par \uc2 I don`t feel comfortable moving forward on any action with Georgetown until they bring more descendants to the table.\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: All right, well, we`re going to keep in touch and watch where this goes. Thank you Julia Thomas and Nile Blass.\par \par \uc2 My final thoughts after the break and stay with MSNBC for updates on the deadly tornadoes that hit parts of the Midwest and South last night.\par \par \uc2 You`re watching POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:51:45]\par \par \uc2 (BEGIN VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 ALAYNA ALBRECHT-PAYTON, DAUNTE WRIGHT`S GIRLFRIEND: I tried to hold him and I was just screaming his name and I was just trying to have him talk to me, and just kept saying "Daunte," like, "Daunte, say something. Please," like, "Talk to me."\par \par \uc2 (END VIDEO CLIP)\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That was Daunte Wright`s girlfriend, Alayna Albrecht-Payton, calling how she tried to save his life after he was shot during a traffic stop.\par \par \uc2 This Wednesday, the trial of Kim Potter got underway in Minnesota. Potter, a 26-year police veteran shot and killed the 20-year-old Dante Wright during a traffic stop in the Minneapolis suburb of Brooklyn Center. She faces two felony counts of first and second degree manslaughter.\par \par \uc2 This week, jurors heard from Dante Wright`s mother. They also heard from police officers. And the defense who claims Kim Potter meant to fire her Taser instead of a gun.\par \par \uc2 I remember that we were listening to summations in the trial of Derek Chauvin in the murder trial of George Floyd when we got word that a young 20-year-old had been killed by a police officer just 10 miles away. Brooklyn Center is just 10 miles away from that courthouse, and I remember saying you would think on a day with summing up arguments in a trial that had went all over the world about police brutality, police would have been on their best behavior.\par \par \uc2 And then how does a 26-year veteran, I wanted to know, mistaken the size, weight, and color of a gun for what they thought was a Taser.\par \par \uc2 This young man, unarmed, this young man dead.\par \par \uc2 I was asked by the family to come and I did. I mourned with the family, and I did, preached the eulogy at Daunte`s funeral and I pledged to stay with that family who I prayed with virally on the first day of the trial.\par \par \uc2 All they want, all the people in Brooklyn Center want, and all I am praying for is justice. Whatever is fair, and just let it be. But let it be fair and just.\par \par \uc2 But let me say that even with all else that is going on, our fight around voting rights and our fight around dealing with the questions that continue to arise, we cannot put on the back burner police reform. There must be accountability for police in this country. And if no one else is going to keep saying it, I`m going to say, yes, we must protect the right to vote. And yes, citizens have the right to expect those that protect and serve us to protect and serve us and not keep coming with situations that are unexplainable and seemingly happens disproportionately in black and brown communities.\par \par \uc2 So with all going on, we all keep our eyes in Brooklyn Center to see what happens in the case of Daunte Wright.\par \par \uc2 We`ll be right back.\par \par \uc2 (COMMERCIAL BREAK)\par \par \uc2 [17:59:46]\par \par \uc2 SHARPTON: That does it for me. Thanks for watching. I`ll see you back here tomorrow at 5:00 PM Eastern for another live hour of POLITICSNATION.\par \par \uc2 Alicia Menendez picks up our news coverage now.\par \par \uc2 [18:00:00]\par \par \uc2 THIS IS A RUSH TRANSCRIPT. THIS COPY MAY NOT BE IN ITS FINAL FORM AND MAY BE UPDATED. END\par \par \uc2 Content and programming copyright 2021 MSNBC.  ALL RIGHTS  RESERVED.  Copyright 2021 VIQ Media Transcription, Inc.  All materials herein are  protected by United States copyright law and may not be reproduced,  distributed, transmitted, displayed, published or broadcast without the  prior written permission of VIQ Media Transcription, Inc. You may not alter  or remove any trademark, copyright or other notice from copies of the  content. \par \par \uc2 Document MSLIV00020211212ehcb00001\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\